// The Coach. Lexicon, meeting contributions and speeches.
window.COACH={"longLessons": [{"id": "long-1", "title": "The anatomy of the polished contribution", "teach": "Jason's five minutes at the Curriculum Advisory Committee looked spontaneous. They were not. They followed an architecture so stable that he could fill it with almost anything and still sound authoritative: thanks, acknowledgement, standing, common ground, a frame with comparative evidence, a summary, and a closing line. What he lacked was the sixth slot, a proposal with content. Your task is the reverse of his: keep his architecture and put your substance into it. The scaffold is not decoration around the idea. It is the delivery system that decides whether the idea is heard at all.\n\nEach slot does a specific job on a highly educated room. Thanks and acknowledgement work through reciprocity, one of the most robust findings in social psychology and the first of Cialdini's principles of influence: people who have been credited are inclined to credit in return, and the leaders who have just delivered bad news are the people whose nod you most need. Acknowledgement also tells the room that you have read the emotional situation correctly, which is a precondition for being trusted with the practical one.\n\nStanding works on status. Academic and bureaucratic rooms sort speakers quickly, and the sorting is driven partly by cues that have nothing to do with the argument; the halo effect, documented by Thorndike in 1920, means that a judgement of competence in one dimension spreads to others. A line of earned authority early buys you a more charitable hearing for everything after it. Common ground does something subtler. By stating a purpose nobody can dispute, you establish yourself as an ally of the room before you ask it to change course, and you make later disagreement with you feel like disagreement with that purpose.\n\nThe frame is where priming, in its defensible sense, enters. Semantic priming, the way an earlier concept speeds recognition of related ones, is well established; the more theatrical claims of social priming are not, and a room containing psychologists will know that. The usable point is modest: the vocabulary you introduce in the frame shapes how the proposal is categorised when it arrives. If the frame speaks of the Board's three objections as design problems, your proposal is heard as a design solution rather than a complaint.\n\nThe proposal and close exploit the serial position effect: items at the beginning and end of a sequence are recalled better than those in the middle, a finding that goes back to Ebbinghaus and was measured cleanly by Murdock in 1962. This is why Jason's opening and his closing line were what people repeated afterwards. It is also why your first contribution failed. You led with the solution, in the middle of the meeting's emotional arc, with no frame, and you ended on detail. The room remembered the speakers who began with people and ended with a line.\n\nTwo rules govern the evidence slot in rooms like this. Use only well-established ideas, the ones a sceptical professor would wave through, because a single contested claim invites a correction that will be remembered instead of your proposal. And never invent a figure. If you do not know the number, describe the pattern: 'several jurisdictions have moved to a common assessment outline' rather than a statistic you half-recall. An academic will forgive vagueness stated as vagueness; he will not forgive a wrong number, and neither will the minutes.\n\nTiming across the slots is uneven. Thanks, acknowledgement and standing together should take no more than forty seconds. Common ground is one sentence. The frame and evidence take perhaps a minute. The proposal takes the largest share, as much as two minutes. The close takes thirty seconds. In a three-minute contribution that proportion keeps the ritual short and the substance long, which is precisely where you will outclass a speaker who has mastered the ritual and nothing else.\n\nFinally, the architecture is itself a memory aid. Because the slots occur in a fixed order, you need to remember only what goes in each one. Under adrenaline, that is the difference between speaking and searching.", "model": "Thank you, Chair. || I want to thank *Helen* and her colleagues for the clarity of this morning's briefing, | and particularly for setting out the Board's *three* objections in writing. || That can't have been an easy message to carry. | For what it's worth, | I've led a specialist music program since 2021 and sat with this course since its first draft. || We all want the same thing: | a course that *students* can take with confidence in 2028. || As I read the Board's paper, | its objections are *design* problems, not problems of purpose, | and design problems have solutions. || I'd like to put *three* on the table. | ... || So, Chair, | three fixes, each mapped to an objection, | each ready for wording today. || The Board has told us *what* to change. | It hasn't told us to *start again*.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Take a matter currently before a committee you sit on. Deliver a three-minute contribution that visibly passes through all seven slots in order, keeping thanks, acknowledgement and standing together under forty seconds.", "seconds": 180}, "checks": ["The first forty seconds contained thanks, acknowledgement and standing, and no more.", "The proposal occupied the largest share of the time.", "No figure was stated that I could not source on the spot.", "The last sentence was short enough to be quoted in the minutes.", "A listener could name the three parts of my proposal afterwards."]}, {"id": "long-2", "title": "Specific thanks that sound sincere", "teach": "Ritual thanks are audible as ritual. 'Thank you to the presenters for their work' carries no information, and a room of practised listeners discounts it to zero; worse, it marks you as someone filling the opening while composing the real remarks. Specific thanks carry information. They show that you listened, that you understood what was difficult, and that you are capable of precise judgement, which is exactly the quality you will ask the room to trust when you reach the proposal.\n\nThe test of a sincere thank-you is that it could not have been said about any other presentation. It names a person, names a thing they did, and, ideally, names why that thing mattered. 'Thank you, Helen, for putting the Board's objections in writing before the meeting, because it means we can respond to the text rather than to our memory of it' does all three. It also does something strategic: it fixes the Board's written objections as the agreed object of discussion, which is where your solutions are strongest.\n\nChoose what to thank with care. Thank decisions, not effort. Effort is the vocabulary of consolation and, said to senior leaders, can sound faintly patronising; decisions are the vocabulary of peers. 'Thank you for the decision to bring this to us before it went to schools' credits judgement. Thank the hardest thing, not the easiest. Carrying a rejection to the committee that wrote the course is hard; reading slides is not. And where possible thank something that leads into your argument, so that the opening is not a detour but the first step of the frame.\n\nName people correctly. In SCSA and university settings, use the form the chair has used: if the chair said 'Dr Nguyen', so do you; if first names are the convention, use them, but never mix forms in a single sentence. On Zoom, where names are displayed, there is no excuse for a wrong name or a mispronunciation, and both are noticed. If three leaders presented, either thank all three by name with a distinct item each, or thank them collectively and single out one item. Thanking two of three is worse than thanking none.\n\nKeep it short: one to two sentences, never more than fifteen seconds. Lingering turns gratitude into flattery, and a highly educated room is acutely sensitive to flattery because it is a technique they use themselves. Deliver it to the person, not to your notes. In the room, look at them for the length of the sentence; on Zoom, look at the lens, not at their tile, because looking at the tile reads as looking down.\n\nTwo refinements separate the expert from the merely courteous. First, thank other members, not only presenters. 'I want to pick up Margaret's point about assessment load, because I think it's the key to this' is a form of thanks and a form of alignment at once, and it is far rarer in committee than thanking the presenters. Second, know when not to thank. If four speakers before you have thanked the leaders, a fifth round adds nothing; acknowledge that it has been done ('I'll add my thanks to those already given') and move on. The economy itself reads as confidence.\n\nThe underlying mechanism is reciprocity, but you should not think of it as a lever. The room will detect an instrument. Think of it instead as accuracy: you are recording, briefly and exactly, what was done well. Accuracy is sincere by definition, and it sounds like it.", "model": "Thank you, Chair. || Can I start by thanking *Helen*, | and through her the *curriculum team*, | for something specific. || You put the Board's objections to us *in writing*, | ahead of today, | and in the Board's *own words*. || That was a decision, | and it was the right one, | because it means we can answer the *text* | rather than our anxieties about it. || I'd also like to thank *Professor Lim* for her point a few minutes ago | that the Board's concern is *assessment comparability*, | not content. || I think that's the hinge of this whole conversation, | and I want to build on it.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Recall the most recent presentation you heard in a meeting. In under twenty seconds, thank one presenter and one other member, each for a named decision or point, and make the second thank-you lead directly into an argument.", "seconds": 30}, "checks": ["Each thank-you named a person and a specific decision or point.", "I thanked a decision, not effort.", "The thanks took fifteen seconds or less.", "The final thank-you led straight into my argument without a gear change."]}, {"id": "long-3", "title": "Standing: credentials without arrogance", "teach": "In a room of academics and senior officials, standing is not boasting; it is information the room needs to weight what you say. Jason gave his because he understood that a hybrid committee of fourteen people does not know who most of its members are. You have more relevant standing than almost anyone at that table: you run the program the course will be taught in. If you do not say so, the room will not infer it, and your practical proposals will be heard as one teacher's preferences rather than as the judgement of someone who will have to make the course work.\n\nThe art is to state standing as relevance, not rank. Arrogance is credentials offered for their own sake; authority is credentials offered as the reason a particular observation can be trusted. The grammar shows the difference. 'I've been teaching for twenty years and I'm a professional musician' is a list. 'I've run a specialist program since 2021, so I've watched what this kind of assessment does to a Year 11 timetable' is a warrant: it tells the room why the next sentence is worth having.\n\nChoose one credential, two at most, and choose the one that bears on the point. For assessment design, it is your role as Teacher in Charge and the number of years you have taught senior courses. For a performance requirement, it is twenty-six years as a professional jazz bassist, which no academic at the table can match. For governance or process, it is chairing a school council or serving as a festival association's treasurer. Never give all of them. The full list sounds like a curriculum vitae; the single apt credential sounds like expertise.\n\nPitch matters. Use the plain past or present tense and plain verbs: 'I run', 'I've taught', 'I've chaired'. Avoid evaluative words about yourself entirely: not 'extensive', not 'significant', not 'deep'. Let the facts carry the weight and let the listener make the evaluation. Delivery should be level and slightly quicker than the surrounding sentences, as if the credential were a necessary aside rather than the point. Dropping your voice at the end of it, rather than lifting, prevents it sounding like a request for approval.\n\nThere are reliable ways to soften without hedging. Frame the credential as limitation as well as authority: 'I can only speak for a metropolitan specialist program, but in that setting...' This is the academic convention of stating your sample, and academics hear it as rigour, not weakness. Or credit the experience to your students and colleagues: 'what my colleagues and I have seen with three cohorts'. Both moves increase credibility while removing any suggestion of self-regard.\n\nWhen should you skip standing altogether? When the room already knows you, as in a department meeting or a council you chair; restating it there is faintly absurd. When a previous speaker has just stated the same kind of authority, and yours would sound competitive. When you are in a short procedural exchange and the credential would outweigh the contribution. And when the person you most need to persuade outranks you in the same domain: stating your teaching experience to a professor of music education invites comparison. There, borrow standing instead, from a body of practice ('teachers across the specialist programs are telling me...') or from the evidence itself.\n\nOn second and later contributions in the same meeting, do not repeat standing. A brief reference is enough: 'as I said earlier, from the program side...'. The room has already placed you. Repetition would suggest you do not think it has.", "model": "For context, | I've run the specialist music program at a large metropolitan school since 2021, | and I've taught senior courses since *2005*. || I mention that only because it means I'm the person who will have to make this course work on a *timetable*, | in a real school, | with real *Year 11s*. || And I can only speak for that setting, | a specialist metropolitan program. || Colleagues in regional schools will see things I can't. || But from where *I* sit, | the Board's concern about assessment load is *well founded*, | and it's also *fixable*.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Prepare three different one-sentence standing statements for three different points (assessment design, a performance requirement, governance). Deliver each in turn, choosing the single credential that warrants that point.", "seconds": 60}, "checks": ["Each statement used one credential, two at most.", "Each credential was followed by why it made my next point reliable.", "I used no evaluative words about myself.", "My voice fell rather than rose at the end of the credential."]}, {"id": "long-4", "title": "Common ground and the positive frame", "teach": "Every polished contributor at that committee table reached, sooner or later, for 'the best outcomes for students'. It works because it is unanswerable. Nobody at a SCSA committee will argue for worse outcomes, so the speaker who names the shared purpose first places himself on the side of the room before saying anything contentious. The problem is that it is also the most worn phrase in Australian education, and said without care it sounds pious: a moral posture adopted in place of an argument. Your aim is to get the effect without the piety.\n\nThe first technique is specificity. Generic purpose is cheap; specific purpose is a commitment. 'The best outcomes for students' could be said about any course in any meeting. 'A course that a Year 11 student in Kalgoorlie can take in 2028 with the same confidence as a student in Mount Lawley' says the same thing, but it names a population, a date and a standard, and it quietly introduces equity of access as part of the shared purpose. A specific purpose also does work later: it becomes the test against which you measure your proposal.\n\nThe second technique is to make the common ground descriptive rather than moral. Pious phrasing tells the room what it ought to care about; descriptive phrasing tells it what it has already shown it cares about. 'This committee has spent two years on this course' is a fact, and it establishes shared investment without any sermon. 'Everyone here wants this course to survive the Board' is a description of the room's interest, not an exhortation. Facts about the group are hard to resent.\n\nThe third technique is to include the other side in the common ground. The Board rejected the course; the leaders delivered that news. A clumsy positive frame sets the committee against the Board. A skilled one includes the Board: 'The Board and this committee want the same thing, a course that can be assessed fairly. We disagree only about whether this draft does that yet.' This narrows the dispute to something technical, which favours the person in the room with technical solutions. That is you.\n\nThe positive frame is the broader version of the same idea. You present the situation as a problem with a solution rather than as a loss. This is not optimism; it is a choice of category. A rejection can be described as a verdict or as feedback, and the category determines what kind of response feels appropriate. If it is a verdict, the natural response is grief or appeal. If it is feedback, the natural response is revision, and revision is what you have notes on.\n\nKeep the common ground to a single sentence. Its power depends on its being self-evident; if you argue for it, you imply it is in doubt. Deliver it with the least emphasis of anything in your contribution, almost as a given, and then pause briefly before the frame. The pause marks the shift from what we share to what I see, and it lets the room assent silently before you ask anything of it.\n\nAvoid three phrases entirely: 'at the end of the day', 'it's all about the kids', and 'we're all on the same page'. Each has been used so often that it now signals the opposite of thought. And never claim a purpose on behalf of a group you do not belong to: 'what teachers want' from a single teacher sounds presumptuous to a room that includes teachers. 'What I hear from colleagues across the specialist programs' is accurate and does the same work.", "model": "I think there's one thing everyone at this table, | and I'd include the *Board* in this, | agrees on. || We want a course a Year 11 student in *Kalgoorlie* can take in 2028 | with exactly the same confidence as a student in *Perth*. || That's the standard. || And measured against it, | the Board's paper isn't a *verdict* on two years of work. || It's *feedback*, | quite precise feedback, | on three design questions. || The Board and this committee aren't on opposite sides. | We disagree, narrowly, | about whether this draft meets the standard *yet*.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Take a contested decision in one of your committees. In under forty-five seconds, state the common ground as one specific, descriptive sentence that includes the opposing party, then recast the situation as feedback rather than a verdict.", "seconds": 45}, "checks": ["My common-ground sentence named a population, a date or a standard.", "It described what the group already shows it cares about rather than what it ought to care about.", "It included the party the room might see as the opponent.", "I used none of the three banned phrases.", "I paused after the common ground before moving to the frame."]}, {"id": "long-5", "title": "Evidence and comparison in speech", "teach": "Jason's most persuasive move was not his thanks; it was his habit of saying, in effect, 'other states have dealt with this'. Comparison with other jurisdictions is the native argument of policy rooms. It converts a matter of opinion into a matter of precedent, it signals breadth, and it reassures a cautious body that it would not be acting alone. You can use it as well as he does, but the standard of accuracy is higher in speech than in writing, because you cannot footnote and the room cannot check until afterwards, when an error will be remembered far longer than the point it supported.\n\nKnow your comparators and name them correctly. In senior secondary curriculum, the relevant bodies are the NSW Education Standards Authority (NESA), the Victorian Curriculum and Assessment Authority (VCAA), the Queensland Curriculum and Assessment Authority (QCAA) and the SACE Board in South Australia, with the International Baccalaureate as an international reference point. Use the name the room uses; in Western Australia, people will say 'NESA' and 'the VCE' without expanding them. Cite only what you have read in the current documents. Syllabuses and assessment requirements change, and a confident account of a superseded Victorian study design is worse than no account.\n\nSpeak evidence in three moves: claim, source, relevance. 'Queensland requires a common internal assessment structure across schools for its senior music subjects; that's in the QCAA syllabus; and it's exactly the comparability mechanism our Board is asking for.' The claim should be the smallest true statement that supports your point. The source should be named, not described ('the QCAA syllabus', not 'some interstate documents'). The relevance should connect back to the Board's objection, because evidence that is merely interesting is evidence the room will file and forget.\n\nCalibrate your certainty aloud. Experts mark how sure they are, and educated rooms reward it. 'My understanding is' signals that you are reporting your reading; 'as I recall' signals memory; 'I'd want to check the detail, but the structure is' signals that you know the shape and not the specifics. These are not hedges, which weaken a claim you hold; they are calibrations, which describe accurately how you hold it. The difference is audible. A hedge is attached to everything; a calibration is attached only where it belongs.\n\nResearch needs even more care than jurisdictional comparison. In a room with education academics, cite only work you have actually read, cite it by author and gist rather than by statistic, and prefer findings that are broadly accepted over striking ones. Practice evidence, what happens in schools, is your distinctive asset and should not be apologised for. 'In three cohorts, the students who struggled with the composition portfolio were not the weaker musicians; they were the ones without access to software at home' is evidence of a kind no academic at the table can supply, and it lands precisely because it is observed rather than reported.\n\nKeep the evidence slot brief. One comparator, well chosen and accurately stated, outweighs four mentioned in passing; a list of jurisdictions sounds like a literature review and invites someone to ask about the fifth. Twenty to forty seconds is enough. If someone challenges the detail, concede gracefully and redirect to the principle: 'You may be right about the weighting; the point is the common structure.'\n\nNever invent a figure. Not a percentage, not a number of schools, not a year. If the room needs a number, offer to supply it after the meeting, which is also an excuse for the follow-up email in which your proposal will be restated.", "model": "It may help to look *east*. || My understanding is that Queensland, under the QCAA syllabus, | sets a *common* structure for internal assessment in its senior music subjects, | so that a performance task in one school is recognisably the *same* task in another. || I'd want to check the current weightings, | but the *structure* is the point. || That is *exactly* the comparability mechanism our Board says this draft lacks. || And from the school side, | I'd add one thing the documents won't tell you. || In my experience, | students who struggle with a composition portfolio | aren't the *weaker* musicians. || They're the ones without the *software* at home. || So comparability isn't only a moderation problem. | It's an *access* problem too.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Choose one comparator jurisdiction whose current documents you have read. In forty seconds, state one claim, name its source, connect it to a live objection in your committee, and add one piece of practice evidence of your own.", "seconds": 40}, "checks": ["I made the smallest true claim that supported my point.", "I named the source specifically.", "I calibrated my certainty only where it was needed, and nowhere else.", "I stated no figure I could not verify.", "I connected the evidence explicitly to the objection on the table."]}, {"id": "long-6", "title": "Making a solution stick", "teach": "Your notes on how to fix the Board's objections were sound. They failed because they were delivered as content, and committees do not adopt content; they adopt things that have handles. A proposal sticks when it can be referred to by later speakers in a few words, recorded by the secretary without interpretation, and adopted without anyone needing to remember its details. Five techniques give it that shape.\n\nName it. A named proposal can be discussed; an unnamed one can only be summarised, and summaries drift. The name should be short, neutral and descriptive: 'a common assessment outline', 'the two-pathway model', 'a moderation sample'. Avoid names that carry a verdict ('the obvious fix') or your ownership ('my proposal'), because the room will not want to adopt either. Use the name at least three times: when you introduce it, when you explain it, and when you close. By the third use, others will start using it, and when they do, it is theirs as well as yours.\n\nNumber it. 'Three changes' is a promise of structure that the listener can hold before hearing a single detail, and it gives the secretary a list. Cowan's work on working memory suggests that people hold about four chunks at a time, so three is a natural limit for a spoken proposal; four is possible if each is brief. Announce the number, deliver the items with the number attached ('first', 'second', 'third'), and restate the number at the end. Map each item to one of the Board's objections where you can, so the structure of your solution mirrors the structure of the problem.\n\nAttach it to a respected speaker's point. The most efficient way for an idea to travel in a committee is under another person's authority. If Professor Lim has said the Board's concern is assessment comparability, your proposal becomes 'a practical way of doing what Professor Lim has described'. This does three things: it credits her, it makes disagreement with you awkward for anyone who agreed with her, and it gives her an incentive to support the proposal, because it extends her argument. Choose the speaker the room has visibly agreed with, not merely the most senior.\n\nOffer minute wording. Most proposals die between the meeting and the minutes, because the secretary must turn a spoken discussion into a recorded outcome and will reach for whatever is easiest to write. Make yours the easiest. 'Chair, if it helps the minutes, the wording might be: that the committee ask the curriculum team to prepare a common assessment outline for the Board's next meeting.' Spoken wording should be in the form of a resolution or action: subject, verb, object, owner, date. Keep it under thirty words and say it slowly.\n\nMake it the default. In committee, the proposal that becomes the default is the one that will be adopted unless someone objects, and objecting costs more than assenting. You make yours the default by assuming its adoption in the phrasing of the close ('unless anyone sees a difficulty, perhaps we could take that as the direction'), by proposing it as a next step rather than a decision ('could we ask the team to draft this, so we can see it?'), and by making it small enough that agreeing is easy. A draft is easier to approve than a policy.\n\nNotice what none of these involves: no increase in argument. The case for the proposal can stay exactly as it is. What changes is the proposal's form, from a thought you had to an object the room can pick up.", "model": "So I'd like to propose *three* changes, | one for each of the Board's objections, | and I'll call them together a *common assessment outline*. || *First*, | a single performance task structure across all schools. || *Second*, | a moderation sample of *five* recordings per school, | not the full cohort. || *Third*, | a composition portfolio that can be completed on *school* equipment. || I think this is really a practical way of doing what *Professor Lim* described a moment ago. || Comparability, | built into the *design*. || And Chair, | if it helps the minutes, | the wording might be: || *That the committee ask the curriculum team to draft a common assessment outline for the Board's March meeting.* || Unless anyone sees a difficulty, | perhaps we could take that as our *direction*.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Take a solution you have recently suggested in a meeting that went nowhere. Re-deliver it in ninety seconds with a name, a number, attachment to a named colleague's point, spoken minute wording under thirty words, and a default-making close.", "seconds": 90}, "checks": ["The proposal had a short neutral name, used at least three times.", "I announced the number of parts and restated it at the end.", "I attached it to a specific earlier point by a named colleague.", "My minute wording had a subject, verb, object, owner and date, in under thirty words.", "My last line assumed adoption unless someone objected."]}, {"id": "long-7", "title": "The third-speaker close", "teach": "In competitive debating, the third speaker does not introduce new material. The job is to rebuild the debate around a small number of clashes, show that each falls the speaker's way, and end with a line the adjudicator will carry into the decision. Jason closes like this, and it is why he is remembered: whatever he contributed, he ended by telling the room what the meeting had been about. The person who summarises the debate owns it, because the summary becomes the shared memory of the discussion and, frequently, the basis of the minutes.\n\nThe third-speaker close has three parts. First, a summary of the discussion in your terms. Not a neutral recap, which merely repeats, but a reorganisation: 'I think we've heard three concerns this morning: comparability, load and access.' Those three categories are your categories, and they happen to correspond to the three parts of your proposal. The summary credits other speakers by name, which makes it generous rather than self-serving, but its structure is yours. Whoever chooses the categories chooses the conclusion.\n\nSecond, the resolution of each concern by your proposal, one sentence apiece. 'The common task deals with comparability; the five-recording sample deals with load; school equipment deals with access.' This is the moment where your practical solution is transformed from one contribution among many into the answer to the meeting's questions. It works because the room has already accepted the summary; the resolution follows almost as arithmetic.\n\nThird, the quotable line. This is the sentence that will be repeated in the corridor and at the next meeting. It should be short, ten to fifteen words, and built on a recognisable rhetorical figure. Antithesis is the most reliable: 'The Board hasn't asked us to change what the course is for; it's asked us to change how we show it.' Chiasmus works when the content allows it. A concrete image, a student, a timetable, a recording, works better than an abstraction. The line should restate your frame, not introduce a new idea; its job is to compress, not to extend.\n\nDeliver the close more slowly than the rest of the contribution. Drop your pitch at the end of each sentence and leave a full stop before the final line, so the room knows something is coming. Say the line once, cleanly, and stop. Do not paraphrase it, do not explain it, and do not add 'so that's my view'. The silence after the line is what makes it quotable; anything you add dilutes it and returns the room's attention to you rather than to the idea.\n\nIn a committee, unlike in a debate, the close often needs a practical tail: the wording for the minutes or a next step with owner and date. Put the tail before the quotable line, not after it. 'So, Chair, the wording I'd suggest is... And I'd end with this.' Then the line. If you put the procedural material last, it is the procedure the room remembers.\n\nTwo risks. The summary can sound presumptuous if you are not the last speaker, so frame it as provisional: 'From what I've heard so far...' And the quotable line can sound rehearsed, because it is. Reduce that by placing it after a brief pause and delivering it at conversational volume rather than projecting. A line delivered as an afterthought sounds like a discovery; the same line announced sounds like a performance.", "model": "So, Chair, | from what I've heard this morning, | I think the Board's paper comes down to *three* concerns. || *Comparability*, | which Professor Lim set out so clearly. || *Load*, | which Margaret raised. || And *access*, | which I'd add. || The common task deals with the first. | The moderation sample deals with the second. | School equipment deals with the third. || If it helps, | the wording might be that we ask the team to draft the outline for March. || And I'd end with this. || The Board hasn't asked us to change what the course is *for*. || It's asked us to change how we *show* it.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Recall a recent meeting discussion with at least three contributors. In sixty seconds, summarise it into three categories of your own choosing, crediting speakers by name, resolve each with your proposal, give a procedural tail, and finish with an antithetical line of no more than fifteen words.", "seconds": 60}, "checks": ["My summary used my own categories and credited at least two people by name.", "Each category was resolved in a single sentence.", "The procedural tail came before the final line, not after it.", "The final line was fifteen words or fewer and restated my frame.", "I said nothing after the final line."]}, {"id": "long-8", "title": "Holding the floor while you think", "teach": "The three leaders who briefed the committee did something you noticed precisely: they filled gaps smoothly while recalibrating. Nobody heard them search, because what filled the search was language that sounded deliberate. This is a learnable skill, and it is the one that most distinguishes experienced public speakers from intelligent but less practised ones. The audience cannot hear you think; it can only hear what you say while you think. If what you say is 'um' or a false start, it hears uncertainty. If it is a restatement, a signpost or a measured aside, it hears control.\n\nRestating is the most useful gap-filler, because it buys time and adds clarity simultaneously. 'Let me put that another way' or 'to put it plainly' allows you to repeat your last point in simpler terms while the next one forms. Educated audiences often welcome it, since a second formulation helps them consolidate the first. The rule is that the restatement must be shorter than the original. A longer one sounds like circling.\n\nSignposting is the second. 'There are two parts to this.' 'I'll come back to the timetable.' 'The harder question is the second one.' Signposts sound like planning, and they are; they are also time, a second or two in which you can locate the next idea. You can signpost before you know exactly what the two parts will be, provided you know the territory well enough to find them, and in your own field you do. Practise holding a number you have committed to: it is a discipline that forces structure under pressure.\n\nThe measured aside is the most sophisticated. It is a brief, relevant, slightly lower-pitched digression that carries information and sounds reflective: 'And I say that as someone who has had to explain this to parents.' 'That was, incidentally, Margaret's point as well.' The aside sounds unhurried because it is optional; by choosing to spend a second on it, you imply you have time to spare. Used once in a contribution, it is a mark of ease. Used three times, it becomes a tangent, and you know your own tendency to follow those.\n\nPausing is the foundation beneath all three. A silent pause of a second or two is not a gap; in a committee, it reads as considered thought, especially if you hold eye contact or keep your gaze steady on the lens. A filled pause, 'um' or 'er', reads as searching. The difference is not the duration but the sound. Train yourself to close your mouth when you need time. Silence, held calmly, is the cheapest gap-filler there is.\n\nNow the physiology. Adrenaline, the sympathetic response, produces predictable effects: a faster heart rate, shallow upper-chest breathing, a dry mouth, a rise in vocal pitch and an urge to speak faster. Each degrades fluency. Breathing is the one you can control directly. A slow, extended exhalation, longer than the inhalation, engages the parasympathetic system and lowers arousal; two or three such breaths while the previous speaker finishes will steady you. Breathe low, into the abdomen, so that you have support for the end of sentences, which is where nervous speakers trail off.\n\nDeliberately slow your first sentence. Adrenaline compresses time, so what feels slow to you will sound normal to the room. Keep water within reach and sip before you speak, not during. Sit back in the chair rather than leaning forward, which affects both posture and voice. And give your hands something to do, a pen held still or the edge of the table, so that the energy has somewhere to go that is not your speech rate.", "model": "The second objection is the harder one, | so let me take it *slowly*. || ... || The Board says assessment *load* is too high. || To put that plainly, | they think we're asking students to do *too many things* for one course. || ... || And I say that as someone who has had to explain the current load to *parents*, | so I have some sympathy with the Board. || There are really *two* parts to this. || The *number* of tasks, | and the *size* of each. || I'll take the size first, | because that's where the easy gain is. || ... || A full-cohort recording is the expensive part. || A sample of *five* per school would do the same moderating work | at a *fraction* of the load.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Ask someone to give you an unfamiliar curriculum question, or draw one at random. Speak to it for ninety seconds without a single filled pause, using at least one restatement, one signpost with a number you then honour, and one measured aside.", "seconds": 90}, "checks": ["I used no 'um', 'er' or false starts.", "Every restatement was shorter than the original.", "I honoured the number in my signpost.", "I used one measured aside and no more.", "I took two slow exhalations before I began."]}, {"id": "long-9", "title": "Memory under pressure", "teach": "What impressed you about the leaders was their recall: coherent, sequential, every detail present. What impressed you about the room was that everyone recalled what the others had said, while your contribution vanished. Both are memory problems, and both have the same solution: structure. Unstructured material is hard to retrieve under stress and hard for listeners to encode; structured material is easy for both. The seven-slot scaffold is, among other things, a retrieval system.\n\nStart with the constraint of working memory. Cowan's research suggests that people can hold about four meaningful chunks in mind at once, considerably fewer than the 'seven plus or minus two' often attributed to Miller, whose paper was more nuanced than its popular summary. Under adrenaline, capacity falls further. You therefore cannot hold a three-minute contribution in your head as sentences. You can hold four or five keywords, each of which unpacks into a slot.\n\nThe jotted keywords should be cues, not content. A keyword works if, when you see it, a sentence or two arrives unprompted. 'Helen: written objections' unpacks into the thanks. 'Kalgoorlie 2028' unpacks into the common ground. 'QCAA common task' unpacks into the evidence. 'CAO: 1 task 2 sample 3 equipment' unpacks into the proposal. 'For / show' unpacks into the closing line. Five cues, one line each, on a card or the top of your agenda. Write them while the previous speaker is talking, in the order you will use them.\n\nThe scaffold does the rest. Because the slots always occur in the same order, you never have to remember what comes next; you only have to remember what goes in each slot, and the cue tells you. This is why experienced speakers can give long impromptu contributions apparently without notes: they are using a structure so familiar that it no longer needs to be held consciously. The method of loci, the classical memory technique of placing items along an imagined route, works on the same principle. The route is fixed; only the items change.\n\nRecall of other speakers' points by name is a separate skill, and it is one of the most powerful moves in committee, because it combines memory, attention and generosity. Jason did it, and it is part of why he seemed to be listening even while dominating. Do it deliberately. Keep a column on your agenda or notepad, and as each person speaks, write their name and a three-to-five-word summary of their point in their own vocabulary: 'Lim: comparability, not content'. Then, when you speak, cite two or three of them by name. The room will be impressed, and the people you cite will be disposed to support you.\n\nEncoding matters as much as retrieval. If you want the room to remember your contribution, give it the features memory favours: a small number of named items, concrete images, a distinctive phrase, and placement at the beginning and end. These are the same features that help you remember it. Abstract, unnumbered, middle-heavy material is forgotten by speaker and audience alike.\n\nRehearse the structure, not the words. Before a committee meeting, take each likely agenda item and run the seven slots aloud in two minutes, without writing anything except the five cues. Do it two or three times with different content. The aim is not to have a speech ready but to have a structure so practised that, in the meeting, your attention is free for the room. A rehearsed speech is brittle: when someone says something unexpected, it breaks. A rehearsed structure absorbs whatever arrives.", "model": "I want to bring together three things we've heard. || *Professor Lim* said the Board's concern is comparability, | *not* content. || *Margaret* said the load on Year 11s is already at its limit. || And *David*, | online, | made the point that regional schools can't run a full-cohort recording day. || ... || Those three points, | taken together, | describe *exactly* the course the Board would approve. || A common task, | for Professor Lim's comparability. || A sample, | not the full cohort, | for Margaret's load. || And a portfolio done on *school* equipment, | for David's regional schools. || That isn't my proposal. || It's *yours*, | assembled.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "In your next meeting, keep a names column of each speaker's point in under five words. Afterwards, from five keywords only, deliver a three-minute contribution through all seven slots that cites at least three other speakers by name.", "seconds": 180}, "checks": ["My notes contained no more than five keywords for my own contribution.", "I cited at least three other speakers by name with their points accurately stated.", "I moved through the slots without looking for what came next.", "I did not read any sentence from my notes."]}, {"id": "long-10", "title": "Hybrid meetings: getting and holding the floor", "teach": "The meeting you described had eight people in the room and six on Zoom, and the person who dominated was online. That is not an accident. Hybrid meetings change the economics of attention. Online members have a visible queue, the raised hand, and a frame that places their face at the same size as everyone else's, while in-room members compete for the chair's eye in the ordinary way and often appear on screen as small figures around a distant table. A speaker who understands both channels can use each; one who understands neither is heard last, if at all.\n\nBegin with the chair. In Westminster and Australian meeting practice, as set out in Joske and Renton, members speak through the chair and the chair controls the floor. In a hybrid meeting, the chair is managing two queues, and most chairs default to the Zoom raised-hand list because it is visible and ordered. In the room, you need to make yourself part of that ordered queue. Catch the chair's eye early and indicate, with a small raised hand or a note to the secretary, that you wish to speak. Better still, ask the chair at the start of the meeting, or at a break, whether they will alternate between room and screen. Few chairs will refuse, and the question establishes you as attentive to process.\n\nTiming your entry matters more than in a fully in-person meeting. Entry immediately after the presenters is high-status but crowded, and the dominant online member will usually take it. The more effective moment is after two or three contributions, when the discussion has generated points you can cite but before it has settled on a direction. Enter when the room has started repeating itself; that is the signal that a new contribution will be welcome. Do not wait until the chair is summarising. By then, the frame is fixed.\n\nIf you are online, the raised-hand function is your queue, and you should use it early, since the chair may take hands in order. Keep your camera on throughout the meeting, not only when you speak, so that you are present to the room rather than appearing from nowhere. Position the camera at eye level, slightly above if possible, light your face from the front, and look into the lens rather than at the screen when you speak. Use a headset or a good external microphone: poor audio is the single largest reason online contributions are discounted. Unmute a second before you speak so that your first word is not lost.\n\nIf you are in the room, remember that the online members can barely see or hear you. Speak towards the room's microphone, sit where the camera can see your face, and state your name at the start so online members know who is speaking. Your in-room advantage is presence: the chair and leaders are physically there, and a brief word before the meeting or at the break counts for more than any contribution.\n\nCompeting with a dominant online member without fighting him is mainly a matter of building on him. Do not criticise his length or contradict his framing; the room likes him. Instead, cite him, briefly, and redirect: 'Jason's point about other states is right, and I'd like to make it practical.' This makes your contribution the logical successor to his and positions you as the person who turns his rhetoric into action. If the chair has allowed him repeated turns, it is reasonable to ask, through the chair, whether those who have not spoken might be heard first. Phrase it as process: 'Chair, I wonder if we might hear from those in the room who haven't yet spoken.'\n\nThe chat is a secondary channel. Use it to post your minute wording or a link to the document you cited, never to argue. Chat that adds material is useful; chat that comments on others is visible to everyone and permanent.", "model": "Thank you, Chair, | and thank you for coming to the room. || It's *Dijon Summers*, | for those online. || I want to pick up *Jason's* point about other states, | because I think he's *right*, | and I'd like to make it practical. || Jason pointed us to Queensland. || What Queensland actually *does* | is set a common task structure. || That's something we could draft for *March*. || I'll put suggested wording in the *chat* now, | so colleagues online can see it on the screen | while we discuss it. || And Chair, | if I may, | there are a few people in the room we haven't heard from yet | who teach this course *every day*. || It might be worth hearing from them before we close.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Rehearse an entry into a hybrid meeting where a dominant online member has just spoken. In sixty seconds, identify yourself for online members, build on his point by name, make it practical, mention a chat posting, and make one process request through the chair.", "seconds": 60}, "checks": ["I stated my name for online members.", "I cited the dominant speaker positively and redirected rather than contradicting.", "I spoke towards the microphone and camera, not only to the room.", "My process request was framed as a question to the chair.", "Anything I put in the chat added material and argued nothing."]}, {"id": "long-11", "title": "After being ignored: reclaiming the idea", "teach": "It happens in every committee: you make a practical suggestion, it passes without comment, and twenty minutes later someone else makes the same point and is thanked for it. The common response is either silence, which concedes the idea, or a pointed reminder that you said it first, which wins the idea and loses the room. The expert response reclaims the idea gracefully, in a way that gives it a second chance and attaches it to someone whose authority will carry it.\n\nFirst, diagnose why it was not heard. Usually the reason is not the idea but its packaging: it arrived without a frame, in the middle of the discussion, without a name, and ended on detail. Occasionally the reason is timing: the room was still absorbing bad news and could not yet hear solutions. Occasionally it is status: the speaker before you was more senior, or you were on the less visible channel. Each diagnosis calls for a different re-entry. Packaging failure calls for the scaffold. Timing failure calls for patience: wait until the room has moved from grief to problem-solving, which you will hear in the shift from 'how could this happen' to 'what do we do'. Status failure calls for alignment.\n\nThe re-entry itself should never reproach. Do not say 'as I said earlier' in a tone that suggests the room was inattentive. Say instead, 'I want to come back to something I mentioned earlier, because I think it connects with what Professor Lim has just said.' This makes the return to your idea a response to the present discussion rather than a grievance about the past. It also links your idea to whatever the room is currently attending to, which is the only thing that will make it heard the second time.\n\nIf someone else has since made your point, thank them for it. This is the counterintuitive move, and it is the strongest. 'Margaret has put that more clearly than I did earlier, and I'd like to build on it.' You lose nothing, because the room will now attribute the idea to both of you, and you gain a co-sponsor who is disposed to support the elaboration you are about to offer. The person who argues about authorship looks small; the person who shares it looks secure and usually ends up being remembered as its source.\n\nAlignment with high-status speakers is the general form of the same move. In any committee, some people's views travel further than others. Learn who they are: usually the chair, a senior academic, a long-serving member, or whoever the leaders visibly defer to. Identify the point they have made that is closest to your proposal, and present your proposal as the practical form of that point. 'Professor Lim described the principle; I'd like to suggest a mechanism.' This is not flattery. It is a genuine connection, made explicit, and it lets your proposal travel under her authority.\n\nBefore the meeting is better still. A brief conversation with the chair or the most respected member, five minutes before the start or during a break, in which you outline your proposal and ask what they think, frequently converts them into its advocate. They will raise it themselves, or welcome it when you do. This is ordinary practice in senior governance and diplomacy, and it is not manipulation, provided that you are genuinely seeking their view and are open to their improving the idea.\n\nFinally, the written follow-up, which is the subject of the next lesson, is your second channel. If the idea was not taken up in the meeting, it can still be placed in the record.", "model": "Chair, | I want to come back to something I mentioned earlier, | because I think it connects directly with what *Professor Lim* has just said. || Professor Lim described the *principle*, | comparability by design. || I'd like to suggest the *mechanism*. || And I should say | that *Margaret* put part of it more clearly than I did, | when she spoke about load. || So let me build on both. || A common task structure, | for Professor Lim's comparability. || A sample of five recordings, | for Margaret's load. || If it would help, | I'm very happy to work with the team on a *one-page* draft | for the next meeting.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Recall a suggestion of yours that was passed over. In forty-five seconds, re-enter without any reproach, link the idea to a high-status speaker's principle, credit anyone who made a similar point since, and offer a small practical next step.", "seconds": 45}, "checks": ["I used no phrasing that implied the room had failed to listen.", "I linked my idea to a named high-status speaker's point as its mechanism.", "I credited anyone who had since made the same point.", "I ended with a small, concrete offer."]}, {"id": "long-12", "title": "The follow-up in writing", "teach": "The contribution in the room is half of the work. The other half is done the same day, in writing, and it is the half that most committee members never do. A short email to the chair or the secretariat after the meeting does what no spoken contribution can: it puts your proposal into the written record in your words, before the minutes are drafted, while it is fresh, and in a form someone can act on. In a body like a SCSA committee or a university academic board, the minutes are prepared by the secretariat from notes, and the secretariat will be grateful for anything that makes that job easier and more accurate.\n\nSend it the same day, ideally within a few hours. The minutes are often drafted within a day or two; a follow-up that arrives after the draft has to ask for a correction, while one that arrives before it simply informs it. The timing also signals seriousness: the person who writes the same afternoon is the person who will do the work.\n\nAddress it to the right person. If the chair asked for proposals to be developed, write to the chair and copy the secretariat. If the matter is now with an officer, as when the leaders are taking the Board's feedback away, write to the officer and copy the chair. Do not copy the whole committee unless the chair has invited that; a reply-all memorandum looks like campaigning and can embarrass the leaders. One recipient, one copy, is the usual form.\n\nThe structure is fixed and short. A subject line that names the meeting, the item and the proposal: 'CAC 14 October, item 3: common assessment outline'. A first sentence of thanks, specific, and one that refers to the meeting rather than flattering the recipient. A second paragraph restating the proposal in the same name and numbered form you used in the room, so that the email confirms the memory rather than competing with it. A third paragraph that offers the suggested minute wording, explicitly marked as a suggestion, and states the next step with an owner and a date. A final line offering practical help. The whole should fit on one screen without scrolling: under two hundred words.\n\nWrite in the register of the institution. Use the formal names of bodies and documents, the same form of address you used in the meeting, and the passive or third person only where the institution's own minutes would. Avoid any suggestion that the meeting reached a decision it did not reach; if the committee only agreed to consider your proposal, say 'agreed to consider', not 'agreed'. The secretariat will check, and an overstated email is worse than none.\n\nCredit others. If your proposal combined points made by Professor Lim and Margaret, say so in the email. It is accurate, it makes the proposal look like the committee's rather than yours, and it is far more likely to be adopted on that basis.\n\nAttach nothing unless you said you would. If you offered a one-page draft, attach it or say when it will follow; if you did not, do not send a document the recipient has not asked for. A short email with a clear offer will be read; an email with three attachments will be filed.\n\nThis email is also your safety net. If your contribution was not heard in the room, the written follow-up gives the proposal a second hearing, in a more favourable channel, and in your own words. Many good ideas in committee are adopted not when they are spoken, but when they are written down by the person who will carry them.", "model": "Subject: CAC 14 October, item 3: common assessment outline\n\nDear Helen,\n\nThank you for this morning's briefing, and particularly for circulating the Board's objections in writing beforehand; it made a constructive discussion possible.\n\nFollowing the discussion, and building on Professor Lim's point about comparability and Margaret's about assessment load, I suggested a common assessment outline with three elements:\n\n1. A single performance task structure across all schools.\n2. A moderation sample of five recordings per school.\n3. A composition portfolio that can be completed on school equipment.\n\nIn case it is useful for the minutes, a possible wording: 'That the Committee consider a common assessment outline for presentation to the Board at its March meeting.'\n\nI would be glad to work with the curriculum team on a one-page draft by 7 November, if that would help.\n\nKind regards,\nDijon Summers", "exercise": {"kind": "write", "task": "Write the same-day follow-up email for the last meeting in which you made a proposal. Keep it under two hundred words, with a subject line naming meeting, item and proposal, suggested minute wording, and a next step with owner and date."}, "checks": ["The email was under two hundred words and fitted on one screen.", "The subject line named the meeting, the item and the proposal.", "The proposal used the same name and numbering as in the meeting.", "It credited at least one other member by name.", "It claimed no decision the meeting did not make."]}], "longScenarios": [{"id": "long-a1", "room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee, hybrid, 14 members: 8 in the room, 6 on Zoom", "briefing": "The Executive Director, the Principal Consultant for the Arts and the senior project officer report that the SCSA Board has declined to approve the new course the committee has spent two years developing. They take the committee through the Board's reasons in sequence. The Board saw too much overlap with existing ATAR and VET offerings. It judged the assessment load, at seven school-based tasks plus an external practical and a written examination, heavier than comparable courses. It was not persuaded that schools outside the metropolitan area could staff and resource the course. And it wanted a clearer account of where the course leads after Year 12. The Executive Director stresses that the decision is 'not a verdict on the quality of the work, but on its readiness for the system as it stands.' The Principal Consultant adds that the Board has left the door open to resubmission, though without a date, and that the Authority will not carry the work forward without a clear direction from this committee. The chair thanks the three of them and opens the floor.", "before": [{"who": "a university academic on Zoom", "said": "Thank you, Chair, and may I say at the outset how much I admired the candour and the composure of that report; it cannot have been easy to deliver. I have sat on curriculum bodies in two states and on accreditation panels for the better part of twenty years, and I can assure colleagues that this is not an unusual moment in the life of a good course. What matters now is that we hold on to why we began: a senior pathway that recognises the full breadth of what young musicians actually do. Other jurisdictions have wrestled with exactly these tensions. I would simply urge us not to lose heart, to stay true to our principles, and to trust that work of this quality will find its time."}, {"who": "a principal of a regional senior high school, in the room", "said": "I will be blunter, if the committee will allow me. The resourcing objection is the one that worries me. I have one music teacher, and she covers three year groups. If this course needs a recording studio, a second specialist and a full ensemble to deliver, it will be a metropolitan course in all but name, and the Board will be right to say so. I want this course. But I need to hear how a school like mine could offer it with what we actually have, or I cannot in conscience ask the Board to look at it again."}], "notes": ["Overlap: a one-page matrix mapping every unit outcome against the existing ATAR and VET courses; keep what is distinct (creative practice, production), cut or merge what genuinely duplicates.", "Assessment: seven school-based tasks down to four, by combining performance and reflective portfolio into one integrated task each semester; keep the external practical.", "Resourcing: core-plus-options structure, with a core a school with one specialist teacher can deliver; staged rollout through four to six pilot schools, at least one regional.", "Pathways: written statements from the universities and TAFE on how they would recognise the course, obtained before resubmission.", "Working group of five to redraft against the four reasons, report to the next meeting, aim for the Board's first meeting next year."], "task": "Respond to the Board's rejection with a contribution that turns its four reasons into a plan the committee adopts.", "seconds": 270, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Chair. And thank you to the Executive Director, the Principal Consultant and the project officer, not only for the clarity of that report but for giving us the Board's reasons in the order the Board weighed them. That sequence is the most useful thing we have been handed today.", "acknowledge": "Let us be plain about it: two years of this committee's work has been declined, and that is a real disappointment for everyone at this table and on the screen.", "standing": "I come to it as Teacher in Charge of Music at a large specialist music school, a teacher since 2005 and a working jazz bassist for twenty-six years. I would be one of the people delivering this course on a Monday morning, so I have read the Board's reasons as a delivery problem.", "common": "And I think we can all agree on this: we want a course that reaches students in every kind of school, not only schools like mine, and we want it approved.", "frame": "Our colleague from the university reminded us why we began, and he is right that this moment is not unusual. But I would put the question a little differently. The Board has not rejected the idea; it has given us four specific objections, and four objections are four tasks. The principal's point about one teacher covering three year groups is, to my mind, the test every redraft should pass.", "proposal": "So I propose a four-part response, one part per objection. First, overlap: a one-page matrix mapping every outcome against the existing ATAR and VET courses, keeping what is distinct and cutting what duplicates; second, assessment: seven school-based tasks reduced to four, by combining performance and reflective portfolio into one integrated task each semester, with the external practical retained; third, resourcing: a core-plus-options structure, with a core that a school with one specialist teacher can deliver, and a staged rollout through four to six pilot schools, at least one of them regional; fourth, pathways: written statements from the universities and TAFE on how they would recognise the course, gathered before we resubmit, not after.", "close": "In short, the Board has told us what it needs to see, and we can show it. We do not need to defend the course; we need to answer the Board. I suggest the minutes read: 'That a working group of five members redraft the course against the Board's four stated reasons and report to the next meeting, with a view to resubmission at the Board's first meeting next year.' I am happy to convene it, and I would welcome our regional principal on it."}}, "why": "It converts the Board's four reasons into four numbered tasks, a structure the room can repeat without notes. It borrows authority from both earlier speakers, honouring the academic's sense of purpose and adopting the principal's test, then hands the minute-taker exact wording with an owner and a date."}, {"id": "long-a2", "room": "SCSA ATAR Music Syllabus Review Committee, in person, 11 members", "briefing": "The Principal Consultant presents the findings of the syllabus review. Teacher surveys and focus groups report that the Year 12 content is overloaded, that the designated works are taught to the examination rather than understood, and that schools offering the jazz and contemporary contexts feel the resources are weighted toward Western art music. The review team proposes replacing the designated works with broader areas of study from 2029, so that teachers can choose their own repertoire within published parameters. She is frank that the proposal divides the field: 'Half of the people we spoke to asked for exactly this, and half told us it would be the end of a shared standard.' She asks the committee for advice on whether to proceed, modify or hold, noting that the Authority must settle the direction before the draft syllabus goes to consultation next year. The chair invites discussion, reminding members that the committee is advisory and that a clear recommendation will carry more weight than a range of views.", "before": [{"who": "a senior lecturer in musicology, in the room", "said": "I want to begin by commending the review team. To have gathered that much testimony, and to have presented it without smoothing over the disagreement, is exactly what a review ought to do. I think the deeper question is one of epistemology: whether we want students to know works, or to know how works are known. The literature on curriculum design has been moving toward the second for some time, and there is something rather beautiful in the idea of a teacher choosing repertoire that speaks to her own students. I would not want us to cling to a list for the sake of comfort. Equally, I would not want us to lose the common conversation a list makes possible. It is a genuine tension, and I am sure the committee will hold it wisely."}, {"who": "an experienced ATAR Music teacher from a metropolitan independent school", "said": "My concern is practical. If every school chooses its own repertoire, how does an examiner write a question that is fair to all of them? And how does a new teacher, in her first year with a Year 12 class, know what depth is expected? The designated works are a burden, yes, but they are also a map. Take the map away and you advantage the schools with experienced staff and penalise everyone else."}], "notes": ["Keep a short designated list (two works per context) but rotate it on a four-year cycle so teachers are not locked into the same works indefinitely.", "Add an area-of-study component alongside it, worth a defined share of the written exam, using unseen excerpts so examiners can write questions fair to every school.", "Publish a resource pack for each designated work, including jazz and contemporary works first, to address the weighting complaint.", "Release sample examination questions twelve months before first examination; offer a funded professional learning day for teachers new to Year 12."], "task": "Give the committee a clear recommendation that resolves the split in the field.", "seconds": 240, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Chair, and thank you to the Principal Consultant for a report that did the hardest thing a review can do, which is to tell us that the field disagrees and to show us exactly where.", "acknowledge": "This is a decision that will shape how several thousand students meet music in their final year, so it deserves a clear answer rather than a balanced shrug.", "standing": "I teach Year 12 Music at a specialist school, I have prepared students for the examination in more than one context, and I have played and taught jazz for most of my working life, so I feel both halves of that divided field in my own practice.", "common": "I think everyone here wants the same two things: teachers free to teach music their students care about, and an examination that is fair to every school.", "frame": "Our colleague in musicology framed it as knowing works or knowing how works are known. I agree, and I do not think we have to choose. Our teacher colleague called the list a map. The question, then, is how small the map can be while still letting a first-year teacher find her way.", "proposal": "So I recommend what I would call a 'list and lens' model, in four parts. One: keep a short designated list, two works per context, rotated on a four-year cycle; two: add an area-of-study component, assessed through unseen excerpts, so that examiners can write questions fair to every school regardless of repertoire; three: publish a resource pack for every designated work, starting with jazz and contemporary, which answers the weighting complaint directly; four: release sample questions twelve months before first examination, with a funded professional learning day for teachers new to Year 12.", "close": "That gives the half who wanted freedom a genuine lens, and the half who wanted a standard a map they can still read. A small list, well chosen, is not a cage; it is a common language. I would propose the minutes record: 'That the committee recommends a modified model retaining a reduced, rotating designated list alongside an area-of-study component assessed through unseen excerpts, with resources and sample questions to be developed before consultation.'"}}, "why": "It refuses the false binary the room has inherited and gives the compromise a name, 'list and lens', that people will use in the corridor. Each of the four parts answers a specific concern already voiced, so both earlier speakers can support it without conceding anything."}, {"id": "long-a3", "room": "SCSA Moderation Reference Group, Zoom only, 12 members", "briefing": "The Manager of Assessment and Moderation explains that the Authority intends to replace in-person consensus moderation meetings with digital sample submission from 2028. Schools will upload a stratified sample of student work, and trained moderators will review it remotely and return a judgement on whether the school's marks are aligned with the standard. He sets out the rationale clearly: travel and relief costs, the difficulty regional teachers face in attending, and inconsistency between meeting groups that depend on who turns up. 'We are not abandoning professional judgement,' he says. 'We are trying to make it less dependent on geography.' He acknowledges that practical courses raise particular questions about how performance evidence will be captured and judged, and invites the group to advise on safeguards before the model is finalised at the end of the year.", "before": [{"who": "a deputy principal from a large metropolitan school", "said": "Thank you, and let me say that the rationale is compelling, particularly for colleagues in the regions who have carried the cost of travel for years. I have watched moderation evolve over three decades, from the days of paper folders on trestle tables, and I have always believed that the heart of it is the conversation between professionals about what quality looks like. If we can preserve that spirit in a digital form, I think we will have done something genuinely valuable. I would encourage the Authority to be bold and to trust teachers to adapt, as they always have."}, {"who": "a dance teacher from a regional school", "said": "I welcome not driving six hours each way. But the consensus meeting is the only time I see another dance teacher all year. It is where I learned how to mark. If it becomes an upload portal, the small-school teachers lose their professional learning, and the moderation becomes a compliance exercise. And a phone video of a performance in a gym is not the same evidence as a live performance."}], "notes": ["Keep one regional consensus day per year as funded professional learning, separate from the compliance judgement.", "A statewide recording protocol for practical evidence: single unedited take, fixed camera position, audio captured from a set distance, file format specified.", "Moderator feedback within four weeks, with annotated exemplars returned to the school, not just a pass or adjust judgement.", "Pilot the digital model in the arts learning area for one semester and evaluate before statewide rollout."], "task": "Support the change while securing the safeguards practical courses need.", "seconds": 210, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Chair, and thank you to the Manager for setting out not only what is changing but why, and for naming practical courses as a particular concern before any of us had to raise it.", "acknowledge": "For many teachers, moderation is where they learned what a standard looks like, so a change to its form is a change to how the profession reproduces itself. It also changes what the profession sees of its own standards, which is why the safeguards matter as much as the savings.", "standing": "I lead a music department in a selective program, where most of our evidence is performed rather than written, and I have moderated practical work in that setting for some years.", "common": "I suspect we all agree that geography should not determine the quality of a teacher's judgement, or the cost of exercising it. The disagreement is only about method, and method is something we can design.", "frame": "Our colleague from the metropolitan school spoke of preserving the spirit of the conversation. Our dance colleague told us exactly where that spirit currently lives: in one room, once a year. So the question is not whether to digitise, but how to keep the learning when we remove the meeting.", "proposal": "I would suggest four safeguards. First, retain one regional consensus day each year as funded professional learning, separate from the compliance judgement, so the conversation survives; second, a statewide recording protocol for practical evidence: a single unedited take, a fixed camera position, audio captured at a specified distance, and a set file format, so a gym and a concert hall produce comparable evidence; third, moderator feedback within four weeks, with annotated exemplars returned to the school rather than a bare judgement; fourth, a one-semester pilot in the arts learning area, evaluated before statewide rollout.", "close": "With those in place, the Authority gets its consistency and teachers keep their apprenticeship. Moderation should not only check the standard; it should teach it. I would propose we minute: 'That the reference group supports digital moderation subject to a retained annual consensus day, a statewide recording protocol for practical evidence, feedback with exemplars within four weeks, and a one-semester arts pilot.'"}}, "why": "It supports the Authority's aim, which makes the safeguards hard to refuse. It turns the dance teacher's personal loss into a structural proposal and gives the room a sentence about teaching the standard that names what is really at stake."}, {"id": "long-a4", "room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee, hybrid, 12 members: 7 in the room, 5 on Zoom", "briefing": "The Executive Director informs the committee that the Authority intends to withdraw a General course in the arts learning area from the end of 2027, citing statewide enrolments that have fallen for several consecutive years and the cost of maintaining a syllabus, support materials and moderation for a small cohort. She is careful to say that the decision is not yet final and that the committee's advice will go to the Board with the recommendation. The Principal Consultant outlines the proposed transition: students currently in Year 10 would be the last cohort able to begin the course, and schools would be directed toward existing courses or endorsed programs. 'We know that for some students this course is the reason they stay engaged,' the Executive Director says. 'We are asking how to make the change responsibly, not whether those students matter.' The chair invites advice on the transition and any alternatives.", "before": [{"who": "a curriculum leader from a metropolitan government school", "said": "Thank you, Executive Director, for being so open about where this sits in the process. I think we all feel the weight of a withdrawal, because behind every enrolment figure there is a young person for whom the arts are a lifeline. I have seen students in my own school transformed by courses like this one. If we lose it, we lose something that does not show up on a spreadsheet. I would hope the Board hears that, and I would hope our advice speaks to the value of the arts in the lives of young people who are not headed for university."}, {"who": "a teacher on Zoom from an outer-suburban school", "said": "I teach this course. My enrolments dropped because the course was timetabled against Physical Education and General English two years running, not because students stopped wanting it. I suspect that is true elsewhere. Before anything is withdrawn, I would like to know whether the decline is about demand or about timetabling. If it is timetabling, withdrawing the course treats the symptom and loses the students. I would also want to know how many schools stopped offering it, as opposed to how many students stopped choosing it, because those are different problems."}], "notes": ["Request a breakdown of enrolment trends by school type and region before the recommendation goes to the Board, testing the timetabling explanation.", "Alternative to withdrawal: fold the course's distinctive content into an elective unit within an existing General course, preserving the pathway at lower maintenance cost.", "Guarantee a teach-out for the current Year 10 cohort, including continued moderation support through to the end of their Year 12.", "Provide a short professional learning module so teachers of the withdrawn course can redeploy into the replacement offering."], "task": "Shape the committee's advice so the Board decides on evidence and with a responsible transition.", "seconds": 220, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Chair. Thank you to the Executive Director for telling us where the decision sits before we spoke, and to the Principal Consultant for a transition plan that already protects the current Year 10s.", "acknowledge": "A withdrawal is never just administration; for the students who choose this course, it is often the reason they turn up.", "standing": "I lead a music department and I have spent much of my career building programs that depend on timetabling decisions made well above my head, so I recognise the pattern our colleague on Zoom described.", "common": "I think we are united on the principle: the students in this course should not lose a pathway because of how a timetable fell, and the Authority should not maintain a course no one chooses.", "frame": "Our colleague spoke movingly about value that does not show up on a spreadsheet, and I agree. But I would add that some of it may show up on a spreadsheet we have not yet seen. If enrolments fell because of clashes, then the decline is a symptom of timetabling, not a verdict from students. That is a testable claim, and we should test it before we advise.", "proposal": "So I propose a three-step response. One: before our advice goes to the Board, the Authority provides enrolment trends broken down by school type and region, so we can see whether this is demand or timetabling; two: if the course is to go, we offer an alternative to outright withdrawal: its distinctive content becomes an elective unit within an existing General course, which keeps the pathway open at a fraction of the maintenance cost; three: whatever the decision, a guaranteed teach-out for the current Year 10s, with moderation support through to the end of their Year 12, and a short professional learning module so teachers can move with their students.", "close": "That gives the Board evidence, an option and a safety net, which is more than a yes or a no. Courses can be withdrawn; pathways should be redirected. I would suggest the minutes read: 'That the committee requests enrolment data by school type and region before advising, and recommends that any withdrawal be accompanied by an elective-unit alternative, a guaranteed teach-out and a redeployment module for teachers.'"}}, "why": "It honours the emotional argument while moving the room to the factual question the second speaker raised, which is the one the Board will care about. The three steps are sequenced so the committee advises on evidence first, and the closing line gives a principle the Board can adopt."}, {"id": "long-a5", "room": "SCSA Assessment Standards Committee, hybrid, 10 members: 6 in the room, 4 on Zoom", "briefing": "The Director of Assessment introduces a draft standard on the authentication of school-based assessment, prompted by the widespread availability of generative AI. Under the draft, every ATAR course would be required to include a supervised, in-class component in any extended written task, and schools would need to keep evidence of the process by which work was produced. He frames the problem with care: 'The question is no longer whether a student could have had help. It is whether we can stand behind the mark.' He concedes that the draft was written with essay-based courses in mind, and that its effect on practical, creative and portfolio-based courses has not been fully worked through. He invites the committee to advise on how the standard should apply across learning areas before it is released for consultation.", "before": [{"who": "a professor of education on Zoom", "said": "Thank you, Director, for a thoughtful and timely introduction. I have been writing about assessment integrity for some years now, and I would say that we are living through the most significant disruption to assessment since the arrival of the internet itself. My own view is that the answer will ultimately lie not in surveillance but in redesign: in assessments that ask students to do things only they can do, to show their thinking, to reflect authentically. I would hope that the standard, whatever its final form, signals that direction of travel, and trusts the profession to find its way there."}, {"who": "a head of English from a metropolitan school", "said": "We have already moved to supervised drafting in class for our major essays, and it works, but it has cost us three weeks of teaching time across the year. I support the standard. I would just ask that it allows flexibility in how the supervised component is delivered, because a single rigid rule will hit some courses much harder than others. A pilot in each learning area before the rule takes effect would tell us where the costs fall."}], "notes": ["Tiered application: a supervised component for extended writing; for practical and creative courses, a process portfolio (dated drafts, recordings of rehearsal stages) counts as authentication.", "Short oral defence (five minutes) for a random sample of students per task, used as verification rather than assessment.", "Authority to publish two exemplar authenticated task designs per learning area with the standard.", "Review the standard after one full year of operation, with data on teaching time lost."], "task": "Advise on a standard that protects integrity without distorting practical and creative courses.", "seconds": 230, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Chair, and thank you to the Director for a framing I expect we will all borrow: whether we can stand behind the mark. That is precisely the right question.", "acknowledge": "This standard will change how every senior teacher in the state designs assessment, and it will do so quickly, so it matters that we get its edges right now. It will also tell students, very directly, what the Authority believes counts as their own work.", "standing": "I lead a music department where much of what students produce is composed, rehearsed and performed over weeks, and I have spent many years watching student work develop in front of me, which is itself a form of authentication.", "common": "I think everyone here wants the same outcome: marks the Authority can defend, without courses losing weeks of teaching to compliance. None of us wants to return to a world where every task is an examination in disguise.", "frame": "Our colleague in education argued for redesign over surveillance, and our colleague in English asked for flexibility. I think those are the same point seen from two sides. In practical courses, much of the redesign has already happened, because process is visible. The standard simply needs to recognise it.", "proposal": "So I propose what I would call 'authentication by design', in four parts. First, tiered application: a supervised component for extended writing, and for practical and creative courses, a process portfolio of dated drafts and rehearsal recordings that counts as authentication in its own right; second, a five-minute oral defence for a random sample of students on each task, used to verify, not to assess; third, the Authority publishes two exemplar authenticated task designs for every learning area alongside the standard; fourth, a review after one full year, including data on teaching time lost.", "close": "That way the standard protects the mark without dictating a single method, and it rewards courses for designing integrity in. The best evidence that a student made something is the record of them making it. I would suggest the minutes read: 'That the committee supports the draft standard with tiered application by course type, recognition of process portfolios, sample-based oral verification, published exemplars, and review after one year.'"}}, "why": "It joins the eloquent principle and the practical concern into a single named model, which makes it feel like the room's own conclusion. The quotable line about the record of making is the kind of sentence that ends up in the consultation draft."}, {"id": "long-a6", "room": "SCSA Senior Secondary Assessment Working Party, in person, 13 members", "briefing": "The Principal Consultant reports that the Authority is considering a cap on the number of school-based assessment tasks in every ATAR course, in response to student wellbeing data and repeated feedback that Term 2 has become a period of continuous assessment. The proposed cap would apply uniformly across courses. She acknowledges that practical courses currently assess performance at several points across the year and would be affected most. 'A uniform rule is simple to explain,' she says, 'but simple rules have a way of landing unevenly.' The Authority wants advice on whether the cap should be uniform, how integrated or ongoing tasks should be counted, and how to address the Term 2 peak without simply moving it to Term 3. The chair notes that the working party's advice will inform a paper to the Board early next year.", "before": [{"who": "a school psychologist representing a professional association", "said": "I want to thank the Authority for putting student wellbeing at the centre of this conversation, where it belongs. The research on assessment anxiety is sobering, and those of us who sit with distressed students in Term 2 see its effects every week. I would say that any reduction in load is a step in the right direction, and that we should resist the temptation to carve out exceptions until the principle itself is secure. Young people need us to show that we have heard them, and a cap is a powerful way of saying so."}, {"who": "a head of science from a metropolitan school", "said": "I support a cap, but I would point out that the load students feel is not set by any one course. It is the stacking: five courses each scheduling a major task in the same three weeks. A cap per course will not fix that unless someone looks at the calendar across courses. Some of our students had four major tasks in one week this year, from four different departments, each of which was well within its own limits. No single course caused that."}], "notes": ["Count an integrated task (e.g. performance plus written reflection) as one task for the purposes of the cap.", "Allow ongoing practical assessment, recorded across the semester, with two formal checkpoints only counting toward the cap.", "Schools required to publish a single cross-course assessment calendar for each year group by the end of Term 1, with no more than a set number of major tasks in any one week.", "Authority to publish an assessment outline template that shows integrated tasks clearly."], "task": "Advise on an assessment cap that reduces load without distorting practical courses.", "seconds": 200, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Chair, and thank you to the Principal Consultant for warning us, before anyone else could, that simple rules land unevenly. That honesty makes this conversation much easier.", "acknowledge": "Student wellbeing in Term 2 is a genuine problem, and the Authority is right to act on it. The data is clear, and students have told us so in their own words. But a cap defined badly could remove the very assessment that builds performance skill, and leave the actual pressure untouched.", "standing": "I lead a performance-based music department, so I see both sides of this: students under real pressure, and courses where skill is built by being assessed often and lightly.", "common": "I think we all agree that the aim is less pressure on students, not merely fewer lines on an assessment outline.", "frame": "Our colleague from the professional association asked us to secure the principle first, and I accept that. Our science colleague then identified where the pressure actually comes from: the stacking across courses. If that is right, a cap is necessary but not sufficient, and its definition of a task matters as much as its number.", "proposal": "So I propose a cap with three conditions. First, an integrated task, such as a performance with a written reflection, counts as one task, not two; second, practical courses may assess ongoing performance across the semester, with only two formal checkpoints counting toward the cap; third, and most important, every school publishes a single cross-course assessment calendar for each year group by the end of Term 1, with a limit on major tasks in any one week. The Authority could support all three with a template assessment outline that makes integrated tasks clear to students and parents.", "close": "That keeps the principle intact, protects how practical skill is built, and addresses the Term 2 peak where it is actually created. Students do not feel a cap; they feel a calendar. I would suggest we minute: 'That the working party supports a uniform cap, subject to integrated tasks counting as one, ongoing practical assessment with two counted checkpoints, and a mandatory cross-course assessment calendar for each year group.'"}}, "why": "It accepts the wellbeing principle outright, which disarms the strongest voice in the room, then uses the science teacher's insight to locate the real fix. 'Students do not feel a cap; they feel a calendar' is short enough to be quoted back in the Board paper."}, {"id": "long-a7", "room": "SCSA WACE Endorsed Programs Committee, hybrid, 13 members: 8 in the room, 5 on Zoom", "briefing": "The Manager of Endorsed Programs explains that an audit has found inconsistent records for school-delivered ensemble and performance programs that students use toward the WACE. Some schools could not produce attendance logs, and hours claimed varied widely for apparently similar programs. The Authority proposes tighter requirements: individual hours logs signed by a supervising teacher, a minimum of documented rehearsal time, and annual re-endorsement for each program. He is measured in tone: 'Nobody doubts that these programs are worth a great deal to students. What we need is to be able to show it.' He acknowledges that the paperwork burden will fall on teachers already running ensembles outside class time, and asks the committee how the integrity concern can be met without discouraging schools from offering the programs.", "before": [{"who": "a senior policy officer from the Department, on Zoom", "said": "Thank you, Manager, for a very clear account. I think the audit findings are a healthy reminder that the credibility of the WACE rests on the credibility of every unit of credit within it. I would say that accountability and generosity are not opposites; the best systems manage both. I have every confidence that schools will rise to the new requirements, as they have done with every reform, and I would hope that the committee can send a message of support to the Authority for taking integrity seriously."}, {"who": "an instrumental music teacher from a metropolitan school", "said": "I run four ensembles before and after school. If every student needs an individually signed hours log, updated weekly, and the program has to be re-endorsed every year, I will spend more time on paper than on music. Some of my colleagues will simply stop claiming the credit for their students, and the students will lose out. The students who most need these credits are often the ones whose only structured commitment outside class is the ensemble, so the cost of losing teachers here would be borne by them."}], "notes": ["Use the school's existing roll-marking system for ensemble attendance and generate the hours log from it, rather than a separate signed sheet.", "Authority to publish a statewide template program description for common ensemble types, so schools adopt rather than write their own.", "Multi-year endorsement (three years) with an annual attestation, rather than full annual re-endorsement.", "One transition year in which schools move to the new records without students losing credit."], "task": "Propose a way to meet the integrity concern without driving teachers out of the program.", "seconds": 210, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Chair. Thank you to the Manager for presenting the audit findings without overstating them, and for naming the paperwork burden yourself.", "acknowledge": "The integrity of credit matters, and so do the teachers who give up their mornings so that students can earn it. If the requirements are designed badly, the cost will not fall on the Authority or on schools in general, but on the teachers who run ensembles before and after school, and then on the students who lose the credit because those teachers stop claiming it.", "standing": "I lead a music department that runs a large number of ensembles outside class time, and I have personally signed off more rehearsal hours than I could count, so I know exactly where this burden will land.", "common": "I am sure we all want the same thing: credit the Authority can stand behind, delivered by teachers who keep wanting to offer it. Nobody at this table is arguing for weaker records; the only question is who carries the cost of better ones.", "frame": "Our colleague from the Department said accountability and generosity are not opposites, and I agree. Our instrumental colleague showed us how they could become opposites in practice. So the question is how to get better records without creating more work.", "proposal": "I would suggest four measures. One: generate the hours log from the school's existing roll-marking system, which most schools already use for ensemble attendance, rather than a separate signed sheet; two: the Authority publishes a statewide template program description for common ensemble types, so schools adopt a description instead of writing their own; three: endorse programs for three years with an annual attestation, rather than full re-endorsement every year; four: one transition year, so that no student loses credit while schools move to the new records.", "close": "That gives the Authority consistent evidence, and gives teachers less paperwork than they have now, not more. The best record is the one the school is already keeping. I would propose the minutes read: 'That the committee supports stronger evidence requirements, to be met through existing attendance systems, statewide template program descriptions, three-year endorsement with annual attestation, and a transition year.'"}}, "why": "It aligns with the Authority's integrity aim and then solves the practical objection by reusing systems that already exist. The proposal is easy to adopt because it costs the Authority nothing and reduces work for schools."}, {"id": "long-a8", "room": "SCSA Examinations Advisory Committee, hybrid, 11 members: 6 in the room, 5 on Zoom", "briefing": "The Manager of Examinations reports that, from 2028, the practical examination window for performance-based ATAR courses is proposed to move earlier, into the final weeks of Term 3, to allow more time for marking, review and the reconciliation of practical and written results. She explains that the current window is compressed against the written examinations and that examiner availability has become difficult. 'We are trying to protect the quality of judgement,' she says, 'and at present the calendar is working against us.' She acknowledges that schools will lose several weeks of preparation, that the window will collide with school concerts, festivals and other commitments in Term 3, and that regional candidates already face significant travel. She asks the committee to advise on how the change should be managed.", "before": [{"who": "a university music academic in the room", "said": "Thank you, Manager. I have examined practical candidates for many years, and I can only endorse the concern for the quality of judgement. Examining is a demanding art, and examiners who are rushed are examiners who make mistakes. I would also say that the students will adapt; young musicians are remarkably resilient, and in the profession they will face far less forgiving schedules than this. I think we should support the Authority and trust that schools will find a way."}, {"who": "a regional school principal on Zoom", "said": "My students already drive four hours for their practical examination. If the window moves into Term 3, they will be sitting it in the same fortnight as our regional arts festival, which half of them perform in. I would like the committee to look at where students actually sit these exams, not only when. Moving the window without looking at venues just shifts the burden onto the families who already carry the most. A regional venue would make more difference to my students than any change of date."}], "notes": ["Two-window model: an early window for most candidates and a short late window for schools with documented clashes.", "Regional examining venues in at least two regional centres, so candidates travel to examiners less often.", "Timetable released by the end of Term 1, so schools can schedule concerts and festivals around it.", "Authority to publish guidance on aligning school-based practical tasks with the earlier window."], "task": "Advise on managing the earlier practical window so quality improves without unfairness to schools and regional candidates.", "seconds": 200, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Chair, and thank you to the Manager for being candid that the calendar is working against the examiners. That is an honest diagnosis and a fair reason to act.", "acknowledge": "Moving a high-stakes examination by several weeks affects preparation, school events and families, so this is not a minor administrative change. For regional candidates, who already travel the furthest, the effect of a change like this is magnified, and they are the students least able to absorb it. We should design for them first and the rest will follow.", "standing": "I prepare candidates for this examination each year, and our department's Term 3 calendar of concerts and festivals is one of the heaviest in the state, so I can see the collisions from here.", "common": "I think we all want examiners with time to judge well and candidates with a fair chance to perform at their best. Those two aims are compatible, provided we design the window with the furthest-travelled student in mind.", "frame": "Our university colleague is right that rushed examiners make mistakes. Our regional principal has given us the other half of the problem: it is not only when students sit, but where. If we solve for time and ignore place, we move the unfairness rather than remove it.", "proposal": "So I propose three measures. First, a two-window model: an early window for most candidates and a short late window for schools with a documented clash, such as a regional festival; second, examining venues in at least two regional centres, so that examiners travel once rather than dozens of students travelling separately; third, the timetable released by the end of Term 1, together with guidance on aligning school-based practical tasks to the earlier window, so schools can plan their year around it rather than react to it.", "close": "That gives examiners their time, gives regional students a shorter road, and gives schools a calendar they can build on. Fairness in a practical examination begins with the drive to the venue. I would suggest the minutes read: 'That the committee supports the earlier practical window, subject to a short late window for documented clashes, regional examining venues, and release of the timetable by the end of Term 1.'"}}, "why": "It supports the Authority's quality concern and adds the dimension of place, which nobody else had turned into a proposal. The concrete date and the regional venues give the Authority something it can act on immediately."}, {"id": "long-a9", "room": "SCSA Music Examination Panel and Reference Group, hybrid, 9 members: 5 in the room, 4 on Zoom", "briefing": "The Principal Consultant announces that the written examination in Music will transition to an online, computer-based format, in line with the Authority's broader move to online examinations. The aural component would be delivered through headphones on each student's device rather than played through speakers in the examination room. She sets out the benefits: consistent sound quality, the ability for students to control replay within set limits, and faster marking. 'The format will change,' she says, 'but the construct we are assessing must not.' She is frank that notation input remains the hardest problem: students who have learned to notate by hand may be disadvantaged by unfamiliar software, and the Authority has not yet settled on a solution. She invites advice on implementation, particularly on notation and on readiness in schools.", "before": [{"who": "a head of department from a metropolitan independent school, in the room", "said": "We were part of an early trial for online examinations in another learning area, and I would say it went better than anyone expected. Students adapted quickly. I am supportive. My concern is only with timing. Schools need at least a full year with the actual platform, not a demonstration version, before the first live examination, or the first cohort will be the test cohort. We also found the devices themselves were the weak point, not the students."}, {"who": "a university music academic on Zoom", "said": "I would like to thank the Principal Consultant for her clarity, and above all for that line about the construct. It goes to the heart of the matter. Assessment in music has always negotiated between the sounding art and its written representation, and every technology, from the printing press to the recording, has shifted that balance. I think we are witnessing another such shift, and I would encourage us to embrace it thoughtfully, with an eye on what is gained as well as what is lost."}], "notes": ["Notation: keep handwritten notation for the first two examination cycles, on a scanned answer booklet used alongside the online paper; review afterward.", "Headphone delivery solves room acoustics; specify a minimum headphone standard and supply loan sets to schools that cannot meet it.", "Full platform, with real aural items, available to schools for mock examinations at least twelve months before the first live examination.", "Paper fallback mode for any school with a documented technical failure on the day."], "task": "Advise on implementing the online Music examination so the construct stays the same.", "seconds": 190, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Chair, and thank you to the Principal Consultant for a line that should be printed on the platform: the format will change, but the construct must not.", "acknowledge": "This is the first time the aural and written examination will be taken this way, so the first cohort deserves a fair start. And for students who have spent five years learning to hear and write music in a particular way, a change of tool in their final examination is not a small thing.", "standing": "I prepare Year 12 students for this examination, and I have watched many of them become fluent in notating by hand, so I can see exactly where a change of tool could become a change of test.", "common": "I think we all want what the Principal Consultant described: a better-delivered examination that measures the same musicianship it measures now. None of us wants a student's mark to depend on how quickly she can find a quaver in a drop-down menu.", "frame": "Our university colleague placed this in a long history of shifting technologies, and that is a helpful perspective. Our colleague who trialled online exams told us what the history means in practice: give schools the real platform early, or the first cohort becomes the trial.", "proposal": "So I propose four implementation conditions. First, notation: retain handwritten notation for the first two examination cycles, on a scanned answer booklet used alongside the online paper, and review after that; second, headphones: specify a minimum standard and supply loan sets to schools that cannot meet it, because the acoustic benefit is only real if the equipment is equal; third, the full platform, with genuine aural items, available for mock examinations at least twelve months before the first live paper; fourth, a paper fallback for any school with a documented technical failure on the day.", "close": "With those conditions, the Authority gains everything the online format offers, and no student is assessed on their typing rather than their ear. Change the screen, not the skill. I would propose we minute: 'That the panel supports the transition, subject to handwritten notation for two cycles, a headphone standard with loan sets, full platform access for mock examinations twelve months ahead, and a paper fallback.'"}}, "why": "It adopts the Principal Consultant's own phrase and builds the proposal around protecting it, which makes the conditions feel like loyalty rather than resistance. The four conditions are short, distinct and easy to minute."}, {"id": "long-a10", "room": "University Academic Board, Courses and Accreditation Committee, hybrid, 16 members: 10 in the room, 6 on Zoom", "briefing": "The Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Education) and the Dean of Education report that the external accreditation panel has granted the Bachelor of Music Education only conditional accreditation. The panel found that the program's professional experience component lacked structured progression across the years, that partnership agreements with schools were informal, and that the program could not yet show evidence of graduate impact in classrooms. The Dean is direct: 'We are not at risk of losing the program this year. We are at risk of losing it in two years if we do not act now.' The conditions must be addressed in a report to the accrediting authority within twelve months. The Deputy Vice-Chancellor asks the committee, which includes external members from the schools sector, to advise on the response before a working party is formed.", "before": [{"who": "a professor of music on Zoom", "said": "Thank you, Deputy Vice-Chancellor, and thank you, Dean, for a frank and helpful report. I have led accreditation responses at two universities, and I would say, with respect, that conditional accreditation is far more common than people outside the sector realise. It is a conversation, not a judgement. What distinguishes this program is the musical depth of its graduates, and I would hope that whatever we do in response, we do not trade that depth for compliance. Our task is to tell our story better, with confidence and with evidence."}, {"who": "the Associate Dean (Professional Experience)", "said": "The conditions are fair. Our placements are arranged school by school, often through personal contacts, and that has worked because people are generous. But it is not a system. We have no agreed mentor training, no consistent progression from first year to final year, and no way of tracking graduates once they leave us. Our graduates tell us informally that they are well prepared, but informal is exactly what the panel has criticised. We need something the panel can read and inspect."}], "notes": ["Formal partnership agreements with a defined group of schools (say six to eight), each with trained mentor teachers and an agreed placement sequence.", "A progression map: observation in first year, co-teaching in second, small-group teaching in third, full teaching load in the final placement.", "Recognise mentors through professional learning hours and a stipend or reciprocal access to university resources.", "Graduate impact: an annual survey of graduates and their principals at one and three years, plus a small set of case studies."], "task": "Advise the committee, as the schools-sector external member, on a response that will satisfy the accreditation conditions.", "seconds": 240, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Deputy Vice-Chancellor, and thank you, Dean, for giving us the panel's conditions in plain language and for being clear about the timeframe.", "acknowledge": "Conditional accreditation for a teacher education program is a serious matter, because the schools that will employ these graduates are relying on it. It is also an opportunity, because the conditions are specific and we have a year to meet them.", "standing": "I sit here as a schools-sector member: I lead a large specialist music department, I have been teaching since 2005, and I have mentored pre-service teachers from more than one university in that time.", "common": "I think the whole committee shares one aim: graduates who arrive in schools as excellent musicians and ready teachers, and a program the accrediting authority has no reason to doubt.", "frame": "Our colleague on Zoom urged us to tell our story better, and I agree that the program's musical depth is a strength. But the Associate Dean has told us something more important: the story is good, but the system behind it is informal. The panel is not asking for a better narrative; it is asking for structure.", "proposal": "So, from the schools' side, I would propose four commitments. First, formal partnership agreements with six to eight schools, each with trained mentor teachers and an agreed placement sequence; second, a progression map: observation in first year, co-teaching in second, small-group teaching in third, and a full load in the final placement; third, recognition for mentors, through professional learning hours and either a stipend or reciprocal access to university resources, because goodwill is not a system; fourth, graduate impact evidence: an annual survey of graduates and their principals at one and three years, with a small set of case studies.", "close": "That answers every condition with something the panel can inspect. Partnerships built on generosity have served us well; partnerships built on agreements will keep the program. I would suggest the minutes record: 'That the committee recommends the working party develop formal school partnership agreements, a placement progression map, mentor recognition and a graduate impact survey, for inclusion in the report to the accrediting authority.' I would be glad to sit on the working party and to offer my school as one of the partners."}}, "why": "It speaks from the position only an external schools member holds, which gives it authority the academics cannot claim. The four commitments map onto the panel's conditions, and the offer to become a partner school makes the proposal concrete immediately."}, {"id": "long-a11", "room": "University Faculty Board, Arts and Humanities, hybrid, 18 members: 11 in the room, 7 on Zoom", "briefing": "The Executive Dean reports that the university's portfolio review has recommended closing the jazz major within the Bachelor of Music, with no new intake from 2028. She sets out the reasons: intakes have been small for several years, the cost per student is high because of one-to-one tuition and ensemble staffing, and the review found overlap with the contemporary music major. She acknowledges the program's reputation: 'Its graduates are on stages and in classrooms across the state. That is not in dispute.' The Head of the Conservatorium adds that staff have been informed and that students currently enrolled will be supported through to completion. The Faculty Board is asked to advise the university's Academic Council on the recommendation, and the Executive Dean signals that a credible alternative would be considered if it addressed cost and overlap.", "before": [{"who": "a senior lecturer in jazz performance, in the room", "said": "I want to thank the Executive Dean for her words about our graduates. I have taught in this program for many years, and I could tell you a story about every one of them. Jazz is not a niche; it is the great improvised art form of the last century, and it has shaped every popular music that followed. A university that closes its jazz program is making a statement about what it values. I would ask the Board to consider what kind of institution we want to be, and to resist the logic of the spreadsheet."}, {"who": "an associate professor in music technology on Zoom", "said": "I have some sympathy with the review. The first year of the jazz and contemporary majors already share a lot in practice, and our students do not always understand why they are separated. If there is a model that keeps what is distinctive about jazz without running two parallel majors from day one, I would be interested to hear it. I would add that our cost per student would look very different if the first year were shared, so the numbers in the review may overstate the saving from closure."}], "notes": ["Instead of closure: a shared first-year core for jazz and contemporary students, with specialisation from second year, removing duplication.", "Group tuition for part of the one-to-one load in first year, reducing cost while keeping individual lessons at advanced levels.", "External partnerships for performance units: the festival association and established jazz venues provide performance opportunities and some ensemble direction.", "A two-year review with agreed enrolment and cost targets, rather than immediate closure; an industry advisory group to support recruitment from schools."], "task": "Offer the Board a credible alternative to closure that addresses cost and overlap.", "seconds": 260, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Chair. Thank you, Executive Dean, for acknowledging the program's graduates so directly, and for leaving the door open to an alternative. Thank you too to the Head of the Conservatorium for making sure current students are protected.", "acknowledge": "A recommendation to close a major is a heavy one, and it affects staff, students and the music scene this university feeds.", "standing": "I come to this as Teacher in Charge of Music at a specialist music school that sends students into this program, as treasurer of the association that runs the state's schools' jazz festival, and as a professional jazz bassist for twenty-six years. I see the pipeline into this program from the beginning.", "common": "I think we all agree on two things: the university needs a program it can afford, and the state needs somewhere to train its next generation of jazz musicians and teachers.", "frame": "Our colleague in jazz performance made the case for the art form, and I share every word of it. But the Executive Dean has told us what would change the decision: an answer on cost and overlap. Our colleague in music technology has just pointed to the answer on overlap. I would like to build on that.", "proposal": "So I propose a 'shared core' alternative in four parts. One: jazz and contemporary students share a common first-year core, with specialisation from second year, removing the duplication the review identified; two: group tuition replaces part of the one-to-one load in first year, with individual lessons retained at advanced levels, which reduces cost where it is highest; three: external partnerships for performance units, with the festival association and established venues providing performance opportunities and some ensemble direction; four: instead of closure, a two-year review against agreed enrolment and cost targets, supported by an industry advisory group to strengthen recruitment from schools.", "close": "That keeps the specialisation, removes the overlap and lowers the cost, which is the alternative the Executive Dean asked for. Closing the major solves a budget problem once; reshaping it solves it every year. I would suggest the Board advise: 'That the Faculty Board recommends to Academic Council that the jazz major be retained under a shared-core model, subject to a two-year review against agreed enrolment and cost targets.'"}}, "why": "It takes the Executive Dean's own conditions as the brief and answers them, rather than arguing values against numbers. It combines the advocate's passion and the sceptic's insight into a single named model, which leaves the Board with something it can actually recommend."}, {"id": "long-a12", "room": "University Admissions and Pathways Committee, hybrid, 12 members: 7 in the room, 5 on Zoom", "briefing": "The Pro Vice-Chancellor (Students) presents a proposal for a new portfolio and audition admission pathway into the creative arts degrees, allowing applicants to be admitted without an ATAR. He argues that the ATAR is a poor predictor of success in creative disciplines and that the current system excludes talented students from schools that do not offer the relevant ATAR courses. 'We want to admit the students who will thrive here, not only the students who examine well,' he says. He acknowledges concerns from some faculties about academic preparedness, consistency of judgement between audition panels, and the perception that the pathway lowers standards. He asks the committee to advise on the design of the pathway before it goes to Academic Council.", "before": [{"who": "a professor of fine arts on Zoom", "said": "Thank you, Pro Vice-Chancellor, for a genuinely visionary proposal. I have long believed that the creative disciplines are poorly served by a single numerical rank, and I am delighted to see the university take this step. We have seen similar pathways open doors elsewhere, and the students who came through them have often been among the most original. I would encourage us to be generous in spirit, and to see this not as an exception to our standards, but as a fuller expression of them."}, {"who": "the Associate Dean (Teaching and Learning) for Humanities", "said": "I support the intention. My concern is first-year retention. Students admitted on talent alone sometimes struggle with academic writing and research in their first semester, and if we admit them without support, we will be setting them up to fail and then blaming the pathway. The other risk is consistency. If one panel admits a student another would reject, the pathway will lose credibility very quickly with schools, with parents and with our own staff, and it will not get it back."}], "notes": ["A published assessment rubric for portfolio and audition, with criteria and weightings available to all applicants.", "Every audition panel to include one external member from the schools sector, to improve consistency and community trust.", "A credit-bearing academic skills unit in first semester for pathway entrants, embedded in their discipline.", "Track pathway cohorts against ATAR entrants for three years (retention, grades, completion), reported annually to this committee; regional applicants may audition by video."], "task": "Advise on a pathway design that is open, consistent and protects first-year students.", "seconds": 230, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Pro Vice-Chancellor, for a proposal that names the problem clearly, and for listing the faculties' concerns yourself rather than leaving them for us to raise.", "acknowledge": "Changing who a university admits is one of the most consequential things it can do, so it is right to design this carefully before it reaches Academic Council. It also sends a message to every school about what the university values.", "standing": "I lead a specialist music program in a public school that auditions every applicant, so I have spent years designing and defending audition processes in front of parents, and I know where they are strong and where they are fragile.", "common": "I think everyone here wants the same outcome: students admitted because they will thrive, and a pathway the community trusts.", "frame": "Our fine arts colleague asked us to see this as a fuller expression of our standards, and I agree. The Associate Dean has told us what that will require: a standard that can be seen, and support for the students it admits. In my experience, audition pathways are judged on two things: whether the criteria are visible, and whether the students succeed.", "proposal": "So I propose four design features. First, a published rubric for portfolio and audition, with criteria and weightings available to every applicant; second, each audition panel includes one external member from the schools sector, which improves consistency and builds trust; third, a credit-bearing academic skills unit in first semester for pathway entrants, embedded in their discipline, which answers the retention concern directly; fourth, the committee tracks pathway cohorts against ATAR entrants for three years, on retention, grades and completion, and regional applicants may audition by video so that geography is not a second barrier.", "close": "That gives the pathway an open door, a fair panel and a soft landing. A pathway is only as credible as the evidence it produces. I would propose the minutes read: 'That the committee supports the pathway, subject to a published rubric, an external schools member on each panel, a first-semester academic skills unit, video auditions for regional applicants, and three years of cohort reporting to this committee.'"}}, "why": "It draws on a kind of expertise the academics in the room lack, which is running auditions that parents can challenge. The design features meet each faculty concern, so the visionary proposal becomes one the sceptics can also vote for."}, {"id": "long-a13", "room": "University Teaching and Learning Committee, in person, 14 members", "briefing": "The Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Education) introduces a new assessment policy limiting every unit to a maximum of three graded assessment points, with a strong preference for fewer, larger tasks. The rationale is staff workload, student over-assessment and the integrity risks of many small online tasks. The Director of Learning Design explains that units with frequent assessments will need to be redesigned within eighteen months. The Head of Music raises an immediate concern: performance units currently assess students at weekly or fortnightly juries, and the policy would eliminate them. The Deputy Vice-Chancellor responds calmly: 'The policy is about graded points. It does not forbid feedback. But I accept that we have not yet thought about performance disciplines in enough detail.' He invites the committee to suggest how the policy should apply.", "before": [{"who": "a professor of education in the room", "said": "I welcome this policy wholeheartedly. The research on over-assessment is unequivocal, and the shift toward fewer, richer tasks is consistent with everything we know about deep learning. I have seen students drowning in micro-assessments that teach them to comply rather than to think. I would gently caution colleagues against seeking exemptions; every discipline believes it is the exception, and if we accept that argument, the policy will not survive its first year."}, {"who": "a senior lecturer in performance from the music school", "said": "Our juries are not over-assessment. They are how a performer learns to perform under pressure, in front of a panel, many times before it counts. If they become ungraded, students will stop preparing for them. If they stay graded, we breach the policy. I am not asking for an exemption, I am asking for a design that works. The difference between a jury and a micro-quiz is that the jury is the learning. I would like the policy to recognise that difference without opening the door to everyone else."}], "notes": ["Make juries a hurdle requirement (attendance and performance completed, pass/fail) rather than graded points, with written feedback each time.", "A single graded performance portfolio at semester's end that draws on jury recordings, counting as one graded point.", "Programmatic assessment across the year: track progress across linked performance units rather than grading each unit separately.", "Learning Design to produce one exemplar performance unit under the policy within six months, before other units redesign."], "task": "Suggest how the assessment policy should apply to performance units without exempting them.", "seconds": 200, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Deputy Vice-Chancellor, for drawing the distinction between graded points and feedback. I think that distinction is the key to this whole question, and I want to build on it.", "acknowledge": "A policy that applies to every unit in the university will shape teaching for years, so it is worth taking a few minutes to get the hard cases right. Performance disciplines are among the hard cases, and if we solve them well, the solution is likely to help other practical disciplines facing the same question.", "standing": "I lead a performance-based music department in a selective school, and I have run jury-style assessments for students for many years, so I know how much they depend on stakes and how little on grades.", "common": "I think we all accept the principle: fewer graded points, more meaningful feedback, and less time spent marking small tasks.", "frame": "Our colleague in education warned us against exemptions, and he is right. Our colleague in performance said she is not asking for one. I would put it this way: the question is not whether performance is an exception, but whether juries need to be graded at all to keep their stakes.", "proposal": "So I propose a three-part design. First, juries become a hurdle requirement: students must complete each one to pass the unit, with written feedback every time but no grade, which preserves the pressure without breaching the policy; second, one graded performance portfolio at the end of semester, drawing on recorded jury performances, which counts as a single graded point; third, over the longer term, programmatic assessment across linked performance units, tracking progress across the year rather than grading each unit separately. To make it concrete, Learning Design could produce one exemplar performance unit within six months, before other units redesign.", "close": "That gives the policy full compliance and gives performers every rehearsal they need. Stakes come from the panel, not the grade. I would suggest the minutes read: 'That performance units comply with the policy by treating juries as hurdle requirements with written feedback, culminating in a single graded portfolio, with an exemplar unit to be developed by Learning Design within six months.'"}}, "why": "It accepts the policy and the warning against exemptions, then finds the design that satisfies both earlier speakers. 'Stakes come from the panel, not the grade' gives the committee a principle it can apply to other disciplines with the same problem."}, {"id": "long-a14", "room": "University School of Music Advisory Board, hybrid, 10 members: 5 in the room, 5 on Zoom", "briefing": "The Head of School reports that the faculty has asked the School to discontinue its Saturday pre-tertiary program for young musicians at the end of next year. The program runs ensembles, theory and individual tuition for secondary students, and has operated for many years. The faculty's concern is cost recovery: fees cover only part of the expense, room bookings conflict with other uses, and the program is not counted in any of the university's performance measures. The Head of School is visibly reluctant: 'I am bringing you the faculty's position, not mine. I am asking you for the argument and the plan that might change it.' She notes that the faculty would reconsider if the program could approach cost neutrality and show its value to the university.", "before": [{"who": "an emeritus professor of music on Zoom", "said": "Thank you, Head of School, for your honesty, which does you great credit. I was involved in the early years of this program, and I remember the first Saturday when a handful of nervous teenagers arrived with their instruments. Many of them became our students, and some became our staff. It would be a sad irony if a university that has given so much to the musical life of this city were to close the door through which so much of its talent first walked. I hope the faculty will remember its history."}, {"who": "a parent representative on the board", "said": "Families would pay more if they had to. The fee has not risen in years. But many of us chose the program because it is affordable, and if the fee doubles, the students who most need it will drop out first. I would support a fee increase if it came with scholarships for families who cannot pay it. My own child would not be in music at all without this program, and I know several other families in the same position."}], "notes": ["Fee review: move to a tiered fee, with a modest increase for most families and funded scholarships for students who need them.", "Partner with a secondary school (or two) for ensemble rehearsal space on Saturdays, freeing university rooms.", "Measure the program's value: track how many participants apply to and enrol in the university each year; report it to the faculty as a recruitment metric.", "Seek a sponsor or philanthropic partner to fund the scholarships specifically."], "task": "Give the Head of School the argument and the plan to put back to the faculty.", "seconds": 220, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Chair, and thank you to the Head of School for telling us plainly whose position this is, and for asking for a plan rather than a protest.", "acknowledge": "Closing a program that has run for this long would be a real loss to the city's young musicians and, I suspect, to this university's future intakes. And the families involved will hear about it long before the faculty explains it.", "standing": "I lead a specialist music department in a public secondary school, and a number of my own students have attended this program on Saturdays, so I have watched its effect from the other end.", "common": "I am sure we all want the program to continue, and to continue on a footing the faculty can defend.", "frame": "Our emeritus colleague reminded us that this program is the door through which much of the university's talent first walked. That is the argument. The faculty has told us what it needs to hear: cost recovery and measurable value. And our parent representative has told us how far fees can move without losing the students who need it most.", "proposal": "So I would suggest a four-part plan. First, a tiered fee: a modest increase for most families, with scholarships for those who need them; second, a sponsor or philanthropic partner to fund those scholarships specifically, which protects access without subsidising everyone; third, rehearse ensembles in a partner secondary school on Saturdays, freeing university rooms and reducing the conflict the faculty raised; fourth, and most important, count the program: track each year how many participants apply to and enrol at this university, and report it to the faculty as a recruitment measure.", "close": "That gives the faculty a program close to cost neutral and a number it can value. If this program is the front door, we should be counting who walks through it. I would propose the minutes read: 'That the Board asks the Head of School to present the faculty with a revised model comprising tiered fees, sponsored scholarships, partner rehearsal venues and an annual recruitment report, with a costed proposal by the end of next term.' I would be glad to approach a partner school."}}, "why": "It takes the emeritus professor's history and turns it into a metric, which is the only form in which the faculty will hear it. It answers the parent's concern before the plan can be dismissed as pricing families out."}, {"id": "long-a15", "room": "Board of the incorporated association that runs the state schools' jazz festival, hybrid, 9 members: 5 in the room, 4 on Zoom", "briefing": "The President reports that the festival's principal sponsor has advised that it will not renew its agreement for next year, citing a change in its community investment strategy. The sponsorship has covered a significant share of the festival's operating costs, including venue hire, adjudicator fees and travel for regional bands. The Festival Director outlines the immediate effect: without replacement funding, the festival would need to either reduce its program or increase registration fees substantially. 'We have been here before,' the President says, 'and we have always found a way. But we will not find it by waiting.' She invites the board to discuss options, noting that registrations open in early Term 1 and that schools will need to know their costs before then.", "before": [{"who": "a board member who is a retired school principal, on Zoom", "said": "Thank you, President, and thank you, Festival Director, for laying it out so clearly. I have been involved with this festival for a long time, and I have seen it survive worse. What has always carried it is the goodwill of teachers and families who believe in what it does for young people. I am confident that goodwill is still there. I would encourage us to be optimistic, to reach out to our community, and to remember that the festival is bigger than any one sponsor."}, {"who": "a board member who is a jazz educator and performer, in the room", "said": "We cannot cut the regional travel support. If we do, the regional bands simply will not come, and we stop being a state festival. Whatever we do, that has to be protected. The regional schools already raise money for the bus, the accommodation and the relief teacher. A fee rise on top of that would be the end of their participation, and once a school drops out, it rarely comes back. We would be a metropolitan festival with a state name."}], "notes": ["Three-year budget with three scenarios (no replacement sponsor, partial, full), presented to the next board meeting.", "Tiered registration: modest increase for metropolitan schools, frozen fees for regional schools; announce before registrations open.", "Approach venues for in-kind support in exchange for naming or acknowledgement.", "Grant applications: identify two state or local government funding rounds closing in the next six months; replace some interstate adjudicator travel with local panels."], "task": "As treasurer, propose a financial plan the board can act on before registrations open.", "seconds": 240, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, President, for the plain account, and thank you to the Festival Director for setting out exactly what the sponsorship has been paying for.", "acknowledge": "Losing a principal sponsor is a serious blow, and we have only a few months before schools need to know what the festival will cost them. If we wait for certainty, we will be setting fees after schools have already set their budgets.", "standing": "As treasurer, I have been through these accounts line by line, and as a teacher who brings bands to the festival, I know what a change in fees means for a school's budget.", "common": "I think we all agree on what must survive: a festival that is genuinely statewide, affordable for schools, and financially sound. Every option we consider tonight should be tested against those three things.", "frame": "Our colleague on Zoom spoke of the goodwill that has carried the festival, and he is right; it is our greatest asset. Our colleague in the room has told us what the goodwill must protect: regional participation. So the question is how to cover the gap without losing either.", "proposal": "I propose a four-part financial plan. First, a three-year budget with three scenarios, no replacement sponsor, partial replacement and full replacement, which I will bring to the next meeting; second, tiered registration: a modest increase for metropolitan schools and frozen fees for regional schools, announced before registrations open; third, approach our venues for in-kind support in exchange for acknowledgement, which reduces cost without touching the program; fourth, applications to at least two state or local government funding rounds closing in the next six months, and some interstate adjudicator travel replaced with local panels, which saves money and supports our own profession.", "close": "That protects the regional bands, keeps the festival affordable and buys us time to find a new sponsor without pressure. Goodwill keeps a festival alive; a budget keeps it standing. I would propose the minutes read: 'That the treasurer present a three-year budget with three sponsorship scenarios to the next meeting, and that the board adopt tiered registration with regional fees frozen, pursue in-kind venue support, and apply to two funding rounds within six months.'"}}, "why": "It speaks with the specific authority of the treasurer, which makes the plan credible on its own terms. It protects the one item another member made non-negotiable, which secures his support, and the closing line marries the optimism already in the room to a budget."}, {"id": "long-a16", "room": "State arts funding body, Music Advisory Panel, hybrid, 8 members: 4 in the room, 4 on Zoom", "briefing": "The Director of Investment reports that the funding body is moving from annual project grants to a multi-year organisational investment model from 2028. Under the new guidelines, organisations must demonstrate governance capacity, audited accounts and an outcomes framework to be eligible. The Director acknowledges that small youth music organisations, which typically run with volunteer boards and part-time staff, may find the new requirements difficult to meet. 'We are trying to give organisations stability,' she says, 'but we are aware that stability can look like a barrier from the outside.' She asks the panel to advise on how the model can support smaller organisations in the youth music sector without diluting the governance standard. She adds that the panel's advice will inform the final guidelines, which are due for release in the first half of next year.", "before": [{"who": "a member who is a senior arts administrator, on Zoom", "said": "Thank you, Director. I think the move to multi-year investment is long overdue, and I commend the funding body for its courage. In my experience, organisations that are given stability flourish; they plan, they invest in people, and they take creative risks. I would say the governance standard is a gift, not a burden, because it asks organisations to become the institutions they aspire to be. Smaller organisations will rise to it."}, {"who": "a member who runs a regional youth orchestra", "said": "We have a volunteer board and one part-time coordinator. An audited account costs us more than some of our grants. If the new model requires it, we will not apply, and the funding will go to the organisations that already have staff to do the paperwork. We are well run. We have never missed an acquittal. But we cannot afford to prove it in the form the guidelines require, and I suspect half the youth music organisations in the regions are in the same position."}], "notes": ["A tiered governance standard proportionate to size: reviewed rather than audited accounts below a set income threshold, consistent with the Associations Incorporation Act 2015 (WA) tiers.", "A capacity-building grant in the first year to help small organisations build an outcomes framework.", "A simple outcomes template for youth music organisations, published by the funding body.", "Allow consortium applications, where small organisations share administration and apply together."], "task": "Advise the panel on how the new model can include small youth music organisations without lowering the governance standard.", "seconds": 220, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Director, for being frank about how stability can look from the outside. That is a rare admission in a funding guideline, and it makes this conversation possible.", "acknowledge": "A change of this scale will decide which youth music organisations can plan beyond a single year, so the threshold matters a great deal. If the threshold is set without them in mind, the organisations with the least administrative capacity, which are often the ones closest to young people in the regions, will simply stop applying, and the new model will have narrowed the field it was meant to stabilise.", "standing": "I chair a school council, I am treasurer of an incorporated association that runs a statewide festival, and I lead a music department, so I know what governance costs a volunteer board in time and money.", "common": "I think the whole panel shares the same aim: well-governed organisations, and young people in every region with access to music.", "frame": "Our colleague on Zoom called the governance standard a gift, and I agree it can be. Our regional colleague has told us the price of that gift for a small organisation. The question is not whether the standard should exist, but whether it should be the same for every organisation regardless of size.", "proposal": "So I propose a proportionate model in four parts. First, a tiered governance standard aligned to size: below a set income threshold, reviewed rather than audited accounts, which mirrors the tiered approach the Associations Incorporation Act 2015 already takes; second, a capacity-building grant in the first year to help small organisations develop an outcomes framework; third, a simple outcomes template for youth music organisations, published by the funding body, so no one starts from a blank page; fourth, consortium applications, so that small organisations can share administration and apply together.", "close": "That keeps the standard high and makes the door the right height for the organisations walking through it. A standard should measure governance, not size. I would propose the panel advise: 'That the funding body adopt proportionate governance requirements, including reviewed accounts below an income threshold, a first-year capacity-building grant, a published outcomes template and provision for consortium applications.'"}}, "why": "It draws on his governance roles to speak credibly about compliance costs, and it grounds the tiered model in a statute the panel will recognise. The closing line separates governance from size so neatly that it reframes the whole question."}, {"id": "long-a17", "room": "Joint meeting of two youth music association boards, hybrid, 12 members: 8 in the room, 4 on Zoom", "briefing": "The two Presidents jointly present a proposal to merge their associations, one running youth orchestras and the other youth jazz ensembles. They explain that both face rising venue and insurance costs, a shrinking pool of volunteers and overlapping membership, with many families involved in both. A merged organisation would share administration, insurance and a single coordinator. The orchestra association's President says: 'This is not a takeover. It is two families deciding to share a house.' Both acknowledge the risks: loss of identity, especially for the smaller jazz association, and uncertainty about how the board would be composed. Members of both boards are asked to give their views before a formal resolution is drafted for their respective general meetings. The two Presidents ask members to be candid, noting that neither association is in immediate financial danger and that there is time to get this right.", "before": [{"who": "a board member of the youth orchestra association, in the room", "said": "Thank you to both Presidents for their leadership in bringing this forward. I have been part of the orchestra association for many years, and I believe deeply in what music does for young people. I think a merger could be the beginning of something wonderful, a community in which young musicians move freely between traditions and learn from each other. We should approach it in a spirit of openness and trust, and I am sure the details will fall into place."}, {"who": "a board member of the youth jazz association, on Zoom", "said": "My worry is that the jazz program will become a subcommittee of an orchestra organisation. We are smaller, and in a merged board we would be outvoted on everything. I would need to see how our program's identity and budget are protected before I could support it. Our families joined a jazz program, not an orchestra program with a jazz option. Without written protections, I fear the identity that brought them to us would fade within a few years, and with it the reason they came."}], "notes": ["A constitution with two program streams, each with a ring-fenced program budget and a program committee, under one board.", "Board composition guaranteed for the first three years: equal representation from each association, with an independent chair.", "A one-year joint operating trial (shared coordinator and insurance) before formal merger.", "Draft constitution prepared with reference to the Associations Incorporation Act 2015 (WA), with legal review, for the general meetings."], "task": "Propose a merger structure that both boards could support.", "seconds": 230, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Chair, and thank you to both Presidents for bringing this forward together. Presenting it jointly has already shown the kind of partnership you are proposing.", "acknowledge": "A merger asks volunteers to give up something they have built, so it is right that we take the risks as seriously as the benefits. Both associations have their own histories, and those matter to the families who built them.", "standing": "I am treasurer of an incorporated association that runs a statewide jazz festival, I chair a school council, and I lead a music department whose students play in both orchestras and jazz bands, so I see these two communities from both sides.", "common": "I think every member here wants the same outcome: more music for young people, at a cost families can bear, run by volunteers who are not exhausted.", "frame": "Our orchestra colleague spoke of a community in which young musicians move freely between traditions, and that is a fine aim. Our jazz colleague has told us what that community must guarantee: that the smaller partner is not outvoted out of existence. The details will not fall into place on their own; they have to be written into the constitution.", "proposal": "So I propose a 'two streams, one house' structure, in four parts. First, a constitution with two program streams, each with a ring-fenced budget and its own program committee, under one board; second, guaranteed board composition for the first three years, with equal representation from each association and an independent chair; third, a one-year joint operating trial, sharing a coordinator and insurance, before any formal merger; fourth, a draft constitution prepared with reference to the Associations Incorporation Act 2015 and legally reviewed, ready for both general meetings.", "close": "That gives both associations the savings of a merger and the safety of a trial, with the smaller partner protected in writing. A good merger is one where both families still recognise their own rooms. I would suggest the minutes read: 'That both boards endorse a one-year joint operating trial, and commission a draft constitution providing two ring-fenced program streams and equal board representation for three years, to be presented at the next general meetings.'"}}, "why": "It takes the President's metaphor and gives it a structure, 'two streams, one house', that both boards can repeat. The protections for the smaller association are specific enough to win over the most anxious voice in the room."}, {"id": "long-a18", "room": "Board of a not-for-profit community music school, in person, 9 members", "briefing": "The Chair and the Executive Officer report that the local government has reduced its annual grant to the school by a third, as part of wider budget savings. The grant has funded subsidised lessons for students from low-income families, as well as part of the rent on the school's premises. The Executive Officer presents a forecast showing a deficit next year unless costs are cut or new income is found. 'The council has been clear that this is not a reflection on us,' the Chair says, 'but it is a reflection of their priorities, and we will need to show them ours.' She asks the board to discuss options and to agree a direction for the Executive Officer to work on before the next meeting.", "before": [{"who": "a board member who is a local business owner", "said": "Thank you, Chair, and thank you to the Executive Officer for such a clear forecast. I think this is a moment to remind ourselves of who we are. This school has been part of the community for a long time, and people love it. I believe that if we tell our story well, people will step forward. Perhaps a gala concert, or a campaign. I am confident that our community will not let us down."}, {"who": "a board member who is a music teacher at the school", "said": "We cannot raise fees across the board. Most of our students are on subsidised lessons, and if we raise their fees they will leave. Whatever we do, I would ask that it protects the subsidised places first. Many of those families came to us because the fee was within reach, and a rise of even a few dollars a lesson would be enough to lose some of them. Those are precisely the students the council grant was meant to support in the first place."}], "notes": ["Request a meeting with the council to present data on how many local families the subsidy serves, and seek partial restoration or a multi-year commitment.", "Tiered fees: modest increase for full-fee students, subsidised places protected.", "Negotiate shared use of premises (with a local school or community centre) to reduce rent.", "Apply for state arts and community grants that fund access programs; a single annual concert as both fundraiser and evidence for funders."], "task": "Propose a direction the Executive Officer can work on before the next meeting.", "seconds": 200, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Chair, and thank you to the Executive Officer for a forecast that shows us exactly where the gap is and when it arrives.", "acknowledge": "A one-third cut to the grant puts subsidised places and the roof over our heads at risk, so we need a direction tonight, not just a discussion. And the families on subsidised places will feel any decision we make before anyone else does.", "standing": "I lead a music department in a public school with a large instrumental program and I am treasurer of a festival association, so I deal with budgets like this regularly, and I know that the first decisions shape everything after them.", "common": "I think we all agree that the subsidised places are the heart of what this school does, and that they must be the last thing we cut, not the first. Whatever we decide tonight should be judged by whether those places survive it.", "frame": "Our business colleague said we need to tell our story well, and he is right. The Chair said we need to show the council our priorities. Those are the same thing: the story the council needs is a number, the number of local families our subsidy serves.", "proposal": "So I propose four directions for the Executive Officer. First, request a meeting with the council to present that number and ask for partial restoration or a multi-year commitment; second, tiered fees: a modest increase for full-fee students only, with every subsidised place protected, as our teacher colleague asked; third, negotiate shared use of premises with a local school or community centre to reduce rent; fourth, apply to state arts and community grants that fund access programs, and hold one annual concert that serves both as a fundraiser and as evidence for funders.", "close": "That covers the gap from four directions and keeps the subsidised places off the table. Councils fund numbers, but they remember names, so let us bring them both. I would suggest the minutes read: 'That the Executive Officer prepare, for the next meeting, a council presentation on subsidised places, a tiered fee proposal protecting subsidised students, options for shared premises, and a schedule of relevant grant rounds.'"}}, "why": "It converts the general call to 'tell our story' into the specific evidence the council needs. Protecting the subsidised places, as the teacher requested, makes the plan easy to agree, and the four directions give the Executive Officer a clear brief."}, {"id": "long-a19", "room": "Board of a community youth jazz ensemble program, hybrid, 8 members: 5 in the room, 3 on Zoom", "briefing": "The President reports that, following the release of updated state guidance on child safeguarding, the organisation's insurer has advised that the program's policies must be revised before renewal. The main changes are a requirement for two adults to be present at all rehearsals and lessons, a code of conduct signed by all volunteers and tutors, and a documented process for reporting and responding to concerns. She is clear that the changes are right in principle: 'The safety of young people is not negotiable.' She notes, however, that the program relies on volunteer tutors, many of whom are working musicians who give one evening a week, and that some sectionals run with a single tutor. She asks the board to agree how the program will comply before the renewal date.", "before": [{"who": "a board member who is a lawyer, on Zoom", "said": "Thank you, President. I want to say first that I support these changes without reservation. The history of child safeguarding failures in this country has taught us that good intentions are not enough, and that institutions must build systems that protect children even when individuals fail. I commend you for treating this seriously. I am sure that our volunteers will understand, and that the culture of this program, which has always been one of care, will carry us through."}, {"who": "a board member who coordinates the tutors", "said": "I support it too. But practically, if every sectional needs two adults, I need to double the number of tutors on a rehearsal night, and I am already struggling to cover the roster. Some sectionals will simply have to stop. Our tutors are generous, but they are working musicians with gigs and teaching of their own, and I cannot ask them for a second night. If the answer is more tutors, I do not have them."}], "notes": ["Restructure rehearsal nights so sectionals run in adjacent rooms with open doors and a roaming second adult (a parent volunteer roster), satisfying the two-adult requirement.", "A parent volunteer roster, with Working with Children Checks, to provide the second adult.", "A one-page code of conduct and reporting flowchart, signed at the start of each term; a short induction for all tutors.", "Nominate a safeguarding officer on the board; review compliance after one term."], "task": "Propose a way for the program to comply in full without losing its sectionals.", "seconds": 200, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, President, for bringing the insurer's advice to us early, and for stating the principle before anyone could suggest it was negotiable.", "acknowledge": "Child safeguarding is the one area where an organisation like ours cannot afford to be approximately right. It is also an area where a policy that cannot be implemented on a wet Tuesday night is no policy at all.", "standing": "I lead a school music department where these requirements are part of every rehearsal and tour, and I chair a school council, so I have had to put policies like this into practice rather than only approve them.", "common": "I am sure we all agree: the young people in this program must be safe, and the program must keep running so they have somewhere to play.", "frame": "Our colleague on Zoom reminded us that good intentions are not enough, and that systems must protect children when individuals fail. Our tutor coordinator has told us the practical cost. I think the answer is to design the rehearsal night so that compliance does not depend on doubling the tutors.", "proposal": "So I propose a four-part plan. First, restructure rehearsal nights so that sectionals run in adjacent rooms with open doors, and a roaming second adult moves between them, which satisfies the two-adult requirement without a second tutor in every room; second, a rostered group of parent volunteers, each with a current Working with Children Check, to provide that second adult; third, a one-page code of conduct and reporting flowchart, signed by every tutor and volunteer at the start of each term, with a short induction; fourth, a nominated safeguarding officer on this board, and a review of compliance after one term.", "close": "That gives the insurer full compliance, gives the tutors no extra roster burden, and keeps every sectional running. Safety should be designed into the room, not added on top of the roster. I would propose the minutes read: 'That the board adopt the revised safeguarding policy, implemented through an adjacent-room rehearsal model with a rostered second adult, a termly signed code of conduct and reporting flowchart, and a nominated board safeguarding officer, with review after one term.'"}}, "why": "It accepts the principle without qualification, which protects him from being heard as resisting safeguarding, and then solves the tutor coordinator's practical problem. The plan is specific enough for the insurer and simple enough for volunteers."}, {"id": "long-a20", "room": "University Academic Council, hybrid, 20 members: 13 in the room, 7 on Zoom", "briefing": "The Vice-Chancellor and the Provost present a proposal to merge the School of Music into a new School of Creative Arts, together with theatre, visual arts and design. They explain that the merger would reduce administrative duplication, create a single budget line for the creative disciplines and support more interdisciplinary teaching. The Provost acknowledges the concerns already raised by music staff: the potential loss of a distinct identity and profile, pressure on specialist resources such as practice rooms and instrument collections, and the risk that one-to-one tuition will be seen as an anomaly within a larger school. 'The aim is not to make music less visible,' the Vice-Chancellor says, 'but to give the creative arts a stronger voice in the university.' Council is asked to advise on the conditions under which the merger should proceed.", "before": [{"who": "a professor of theatre on Zoom", "said": "Thank you, Vice-Chancellor and Provost, for a bold and imaginative proposal. I have long believed that the boundaries between the creative disciplines are more administrative than artistic. Some of the most exciting work of our time happens where music, theatre and visual art meet. I am excited by what a School of Creative Arts could become, and I would encourage colleagues in music to see it not as a loss but as an invitation to collaborate on a larger stage."}, {"who": "the Head of the School of Music, in the room", "said": "We are not opposed to collaboration; we do it already. Our concern is that our specialist resources and the way we teach will be judged by averages designed for other disciplines. If one-to-one tuition is treated as an inefficiency in a merged budget, it will be cut, and without it there is no music degree. Practice rooms and instruments are not overheads; they are the laboratory. They need to be protected explicitly, or the first budget round will treat them as discretionary."}], "notes": ["A protected specialist teaching model written into the merger's terms: one-to-one tuition and ensemble staffing recognised as essential, not discretionary.", "Retain the 'Conservatorium' or School of Music name as a public-facing identity within the new school, for recruitment and external profile.", "Specialist resources (practice rooms, instrument collection) held in a dedicated budget line for at least three years.", "An external review at the end of year two, reporting to Academic Council, on enrolment, profile and teaching quality in music."], "task": "Advise Academic Council on the conditions under which the merger should proceed.", "seconds": 240, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Vice-Chancellor and Provost, for setting out the music staff's concerns yourselves, and for making it clear that the aim is a stronger voice, not a quieter one.", "acknowledge": "A merger of this kind changes how a discipline is funded, governed and seen, so it is right that Council sets its conditions now, not afterward.", "standing": "I sit on this Council as a schools-sector member; I lead a specialist music department that sends students to this university, and I have worked as a professional musician for twenty-six years, so I am thinking about how this looks to the students we recruit and the profession they will join.", "common": "I think we all want the same thing: a stronger creative arts presence in the university, and a music degree that remains among the best in the country.", "frame": "Our colleague in theatre called the merger an invitation to collaborate, and I share her enthusiasm. The Head of Music has told us what the invitation must not cost: the teaching model on which a music degree depends. So the question is not whether to merge, but what must be protected in writing before we do.", "proposal": "I would propose four conditions. First, the merger's terms recognise one-to-one tuition and ensemble staffing as essential to the music degree, not discretionary, so they are not judged by averages from other disciplines; second, the School of Music name is retained as a public-facing identity within the new school, for recruitment and external profile; third, specialist resources, including practice rooms and the instrument collection, are held in a dedicated budget line for at least three years; fourth, an external review at the end of year two, reporting to this Council, on enrolment, profile and teaching quality in music.", "close": "With those conditions, the university gains a stronger school without weakening one of its strongest disciplines. A merger should change the address, not the instrument. I would suggest Council resolve: 'That Academic Council supports the merger subject to the protection of the specialist music teaching model, retention of the School of Music identity, a dedicated budget line for specialist resources for three years, and an external review reporting to Council at the end of year two.'"}}, "why": "It supports the merger and frames the debate as conditions, which leaves the leadership nothing to oppose and gives the music staff everything they asked for. 'A merger should change the address, not the instrument' is the kind of line Council members repeat in the corridor."}, {"id": "long-b1", "room": "State arts funding agency, youth and community music assessment panel, hybrid: six assessors in the boardroom, three on Zoom, chaired by the Director of Investment", "briefing": "The Director of Investment, Helen Okafor, tells the panel that from next year the agency will close its three-year organisational funding stream for youth and community music and fold it into a single competitive project round, open twice a year. She frames the change as a response to a flat appropriation and to an internal review which found that the multi-year stream had become, in its words, 'a closed shop' that new applicants could not enter. She is candid that the panel's advice will shape the guidelines but will not reverse the decision. 'We are not asking whether. We are asking how, and how to do it without breaking the organisations that hold the sector's young players together.' She invites each assessor to give practical advice on transition, eligibility and assessment criteria, and notes that the draft guidelines go to the minister's office in six weeks. Two current multi-year recipients are regional youth orchestras.", "before": [{"who": "Patrick, a former arts company executive director, on Zoom", "said": "Thank you, Helen, for your candour, which is rare and welcome. I have sat on both sides of this table for twenty years, and I would say this is exactly the kind of courageous reform the sector needs, provided it is done with care. Other jurisdictions have walked this path, and the lesson from each is that transition is everything. We must be bold but not reckless, contestable but not chaotic. Ultimately the question is whether we trust the sector to rise to the challenge, and I do. I would simply urge us to keep the young people at the heart of every decision we make."}, {"who": "Margaret, a regional community music coordinator, in the room", "said": "Can I be blunt about what this means outside the metropolitan area? Our youth orchestra runs on a part-time coordinator funded through the multi-year stream. A twice-yearly project round means we spend our coordinator's hours writing grants instead of running rehearsals, and if we miss one round we lose staff we cannot rehire. Project funding pays for the concert. It does not pay for the forty Tuesday nights that make the concert possible. I am not against contestability. I am against pretending that a rehearsal year is a project."}], "notes": ["Bridging year: current multi-year recipients get 12 months' tapered funding while the new round opens.", "Separate 'core activity' category inside the project round, up to two years, assessed on continuity and retention of players.", "Two-stage process: short expression of interest first, so small and regional groups don't write full applications to be rejected.", "Publish success rates by region after every round."], "task": "Advise the panel on how to structure the transition and the assessment so that ongoing youth ensembles survive a project-based model.", "seconds": 240, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Helen, for telling us plainly what is decided and what is still open; it lets us spend our time on the part we can actually improve. And thank you, Margaret, for the line I suspect we will all remember: that project funding pays for the concert, not for the forty Tuesday nights that make it possible.", "acknowledge": "This is a significant change for organisations that have planned around certainty, and some of them will hear it as a threat to their existence. We owe them guidelines that are fair in their effect, not merely in their wording.", "standing": "I come at this as someone who runs ensembles rather than funds them: I lead the music department of a large specialist music school, I have taught since 2005, and I have played professionally in this city for twenty-six years, much of it alongside people who learnt in exactly these youth and community programs.", "common": "I think everyone here wants the same two things: a round that is genuinely open to new applicants, and a sector in which a young player's ensemble does not vanish between one funding decision and the next.", "frame": "So the question for us is not whether contestability is good, which Patrick has argued well, but what a contestable round should measure when the activity is continuous by nature. A rehearsal year is infrastructure, and most arts agencies, here and nationally, already separate multi-year investment from project grants for that reason.", "proposal": "I would put forward a three-part transition package, and I will call it the continuity provisions. First, a bridging year: current multi-year recipients receive twelve months of tapered funding while the new round opens, so no one falls off a cliff in the first cycle. Second, a distinct core activity category within the project round, of up to two years, assessed on continuity and retention of participants, which is Margaret's forty Tuesdays turned into a criterion. Third, a two-stage process, a short expression of interest before a full application, so small and regional organisations do not spend a coordinator's month on an application that was never going to succeed.", "close": "That gives Helen a round that is open, as Patrick wants, and survivable, as Margaret needs: contestable for projects, stable for practice. The test of this reform will not be who wins the first round, but who is still rehearsing in the third. I would ask that the minutes record that the panel recommends the continuity provisions, a bridging year, a core activity category and a two-stage application, for inclusion in the draft guidelines going to the minister's office."}}, "why": "Dijon turns Margaret's memorable image into an assessable criterion and credits Patrick's principle without borrowing his vagueness, so the practical package arrives already owned by the room. The named provisions and the draft minute give Helen something she can carry to the minister's office intact."}, {"id": "long-b2", "room": "National music education peak body, Western Australian consultation roundtable, twelve members around one table, facilitated by the national policy officer", "briefing": "The national policy officer, Dr Ruth Calloway, tells the roundtable that the peak body is drafting a national position statement on music education in schools, to be put to education ministers' officials next year. She acknowledges that previous statements 'were warmly received and quietly shelved', and says the board wants this one to contain no more than five asks that a department could actually implement and report on. 'Advocacy that only describes the value of music has had its day. We need to say what should change on a Monday morning.' She outlines the draft's current headings, the value of music, equity of access and teacher supply, and asks each state roundtable for concrete, implementable recommendations, particularly on access and teacher supply in government schools. She adds that officials will discard any recommendation that cannot be measured with data departments already hold. Western Australian input closes in a fortnight.", "before": [{"who": "Ian, a university music education lecturer", "said": "I would like to support Ruth's diagnosis. The research base on the value of music participation is substantial, and we should cite it, but that is not what ministers lack. What they lack is a costed picture. My worry is teacher supply. I am hearing from every state that we are not replacing the specialist secondary music teachers we are losing, and primary generalists consistently tell us they lack confidence teaching music. Any statement that does not address the pipeline is decorative. I would want supply named as the first priority, not the third."}, {"who": "Leanne, a primary school deputy principal", "said": "From a primary school's point of view, the ask has to fit the timetable and the budget we actually have. I cannot create a specialist music position from nothing. What I can do is release a staff member for professional learning, partner with a secondary school, or book an instrumental service if one exists in my area. The statement will land with principals if it gives us options at three levels of cost, rather than a single ideal that only well-resourced schools can reach."}], "notes": ["Tiered asks, no cost, low cost, funded, exactly as Leanne said.", "No-cost tier: secondary specialist school partners its feeder primaries in a combined ensemble (we already do this).", "Low-cost tier: PL release for primary teachers to co-teach with that secondary specialist.", "Funded tier: paid practicum placements in specialist music schools; a pathway for professional musicians to retrain as teachers.", "Each ask needs a measure departments already collect."], "task": "Propose concrete, implementable recommendations on access and teacher supply for the national statement.", "seconds": 210, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Ruth, for the Monday morning test; it is the most useful sentence I have heard in an advocacy consultation for some years. And thank you, Ian and Leanne, because between you, you have given us the two halves of the problem: Ian the pipeline, and Leanne the budget line.", "acknowledge": "We should be honest that a fourth statement that is warmly received and quietly shelved would cost the sector credibility it cannot easily recover.", "standing": "I lead the music department of a large government school with a specialist music program, I have taught since 2005, and each year we run a combined band with our local feeder primary schools, so I see Leanne's world and Ian's graduates from the same rehearsal room.", "common": "I think we all agree that every child in a government school should have a real chance to learn music from someone who knows how to teach it.", "frame": "The question, then, is how to make asks that a department can adopt at different levels of cost and then report on without inventing new data. The structure Leanne described, options at three levels of cost, is the right architecture for the whole statement, not only for primary schools.", "proposal": "I would propose that we organise our recommendations as a tiered access framework. Tier one costs nothing: every government secondary school with a specialist music teacher is asked to partner with its feeder primaries in a combined ensemble, which a number of schools already do informally. Tier two is low cost: paid release for primary teachers to co-teach with that secondary specialist, which goes directly to the confidence problem Ian described. Tier three is funded, and it answers Ian's pipeline point: paid practicum placements in specialist music schools, and a recognised pathway for experienced professional musicians to train as teachers.", "close": "That gives us three asks, each with a measure departments already hold: partnerships reported, release days used, placements filled. Ministers have heard why music matters; this tells them what to do on Monday, at whatever price they can afford. I would suggest the Western Australian submission records the tiered access framework as its central recommendation, and I am happy to draft a first version for Ruth by the end of next week."}}, "why": "Dijon adopts Leanne's three-cost idea as the architecture of the whole statement and attaches each tier to someone's point, so his proposal sounds like the room's own conclusion. Pairing each ask with an existing measure answers Ruth's brief exactly, and volunteering the draft secures authorship."}, {"id": "long-b3", "room": "Board of the association that runs the state schools' jazz festival, ordinary meeting, nine directors in person at the festival office", "briefing": "The president, Graham Pellow, tells the board that the festival's long-standing in-kind sponsor, a music retailer that has supplied the backline for every stage for eight years, has entered voluntary administration. The administrators have confirmed that the sponsorship agreement will not be honoured and that the equipment, drum kits, bass and guitar amplifiers, keyboards and two of the three PA systems, will be sold with the business. The festival is four months away, across three stages over four days. Graham observes that the sponsorship never appeared in the cash budget, 'which is precisely why we have never had to think about what it was worth'. He is composed but direct: 'We are not in crisis, but we are one bad decision away from one.' He asks the board for options before the executive meets the venue next week, noting that the cheapest commercial hire quote the office has obtained is well beyond the festival's reserves.", "before": [{"who": "Sophie, vice-president, a marketing consultant", "said": "I would treat this as a sponsorship problem rather than an equipment problem. The retailer's logo has been on every stage for eight years, which tells us the backline has real marketing value to a supplier. There are other retailers and distributors in this city who would want that visibility, and I would want us to approach them with a proper package rather than ask for favours. If we present it well, we may end up with a better arrangement than the one we had."}, {"who": "Andrew, secretary, a retired school principal", "said": "I take Sophie's point, but four months is not long, and suppliers set their marketing budgets a year ahead. My worry is the schools. Many bands do not own a decent bass amplifier or a kit fit for a stage, which is why the festival supplied them in the first place. If we ask schools to bring their own, the bands from schools with nothing will sound worse in front of the adjudicators, and that is exactly the inequity the festival was set up to avoid."}], "notes": ["Split the need: gear that must be uniform on every stage (kit, bass amp, keys) vs gear that can be shared.", "Ask three schools with big programs (incl. ours) to lend one stage's worth each; festival insures and transports; lenders credited on stage.", "Approach distributors, not retailers, for a one-year in-kind stage partner deal (Sophie's package); covers PA.", "From next year, show in-kind sponsorship at cash value in the budget.", "Treasurer: insurance and transport quote for borrowed gear by Tuesday."], "task": "As treasurer, give the board a practical plan for replacing the backline in four months without asking schools to bring their own.", "seconds": 240, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Graham, for putting it so precisely: not a crisis, but one bad decision from one. Thank you, Sophie, for seeing that what we have lost has a market value, and Andrew, for drawing the line I think this board should hold, which is that a band from a school with nothing should not sound worse in front of an adjudicator because of it.", "acknowledge": "Losing a partner of eight years, four months out, is serious, and Graham is right that its value has been invisible to us precisely because it never cost us anything.", "standing": "I speak as treasurer, so I have already been through the hire quotes since Graham's email, and as a bassist who has played festival stages for twenty-six years, I know exactly which pieces of equipment a student band cannot do without.", "common": "We all want the same outcome: every band, from every school, walking onto a stage that is equipped the same way, at a cost the festival can carry.", "frame": "Sophie is right that this is partly a sponsorship question, and Andrew is right that four months is too short to rely on one. So I would separate the two: a bridging plan for this festival that does not depend on a new sponsor, and a partnership approach that can land for the next three.", "proposal": "I would propose a three-point stage plan. First, we divide the need into the equipment that must be uniform on every stage, drum kit, bass amplifier and keyboard, and the equipment that can be shared, and we ask three schools with substantial programs, mine included, to lend one stage's worth each, with the festival insuring and transporting it and the lending schools credited from the stage. Second, we approach distributors rather than retailers for a single-year stage partner arrangement, which becomes the first package in Sophie's prospectus and covers the PA systems we cannot borrow. Third, from next year the budget shows every in-kind sponsorship at its cash value, so we never again lose something we did not know we had.", "close": "So: uniform stages, borrowed from within our own community and properly insured, with a sponsor approach that starts now rather than after we have recovered. The festival's promise is that every band is heard on equal terms, and we can keep it. I would move that the board adopts the stage plan in principle, and I will bring an insurance and transport quote, with a revised budget, to the executive by next Tuesday."}}, "why": "As treasurer he turns a frightening gap into a divided need with a remedy for each part, which calms the room and makes action feel possible. Each point is attached to Sophie or Andrew, so neither feels overruled, and the motion ends with a dated owner."}, {"id": "long-b4", "room": "Festival association board, extraordinary meeting, eight directors in person and two by phone, chaired by the president", "briefing": "The president, Graham Pellow, has convened an extraordinary meeting after the festival's main venue, a university concert hall used for every finals night for more than a decade, advised that it will close for structural refurbishment from February, eight weeks before the festival. The venue manager has apologised and offered a smaller recital hall on the same campus for the same dates. Graham tells the board that the recital hall seats under half the usual finals audience and has no loading access suitable for a big band's equipment. 'The finals have always been the night families remember. I do not want the board's first instinct to be to shrink that night.' He asks for options, noting that the two obvious city venues are already booked on the festival dates, and that the festival's insurance and child safety arrangements were written around the university campus.", "before": [{"who": "Catherine, director, an arts lawyer", "said": "Before we look at alternatives, we should be clear about our position under the hire agreement. If the university has terminated for its own reasons, there may be obligations on its side, at least to assist us in finding an equivalent venue and perhaps to meet additional costs. I would not want us to release it from those by accepting the recital hall too readily. I would suggest we write formally, reserving our position, before we agree to anything at all."}, {"who": "Tom, director, a head of arts at a metropolitan high school", "said": "Practically, the bands do not all need the big room. Most divisions are perfectly well served by a room of two or three hundred. It is the open division finals and the presentation that need the capacity. If we try to keep everything in one place, we will be looking for something that does not exist on those dates. I would rather split the festival across two sites than move all of it somewhere worse."}], "notes": ["Two sites: heats and junior divisions stay on campus in the recital hall plus one adjoining space; finals night only goes elsewhere.", "Finals: a large school theatre; I can ask my principal about ours tomorrow.", "Livestream finals back to the recital hall as overflow for families.", "Catherine's point: ask the university to meet extra hire and transport costs.", "Separate risk and child safety plan for the second site, approved before registrations open."], "task": "Propose a workable venue solution that preserves finals night and protects the association's position with the university.", "seconds": 210, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Graham, for calling this meeting quickly and for refusing to let us shrink the night families remember. Thank you, Catherine, for stopping us from giving away our position before we have understood it, and Tom, for the most liberating observation of the evening, that most of the festival does not need the big room at all.", "acknowledge": "Losing a venue we have used for over a decade, eight weeks out, is a genuine blow, and it touches insurance and child safety as well as logistics.", "standing": "I have brought bands to this festival for many years, I have played most of the concert halls in this city, and as treasurer I have already looked at what a second venue would cost us, so I will be specific.", "common": "We agree, I think, that finals night must remain a finals night, and that nothing we do should leave students with a weaker duty of care than they have now.", "frame": "Tom has shown us the real constraint: we are not looking for one venue, we are looking for one night. And Catherine has shown us who might help pay for it, because if the university has ended the arrangement for its own purposes, it is reasonable to ask it to help make us whole.", "proposal": "I would propose what I will call the two-site plan, in three parts. First, heats and junior divisions stay on campus in the recital hall and one adjoining space, so our insurance and child safety arrangements remain largely as they are. Second, finals night moves to a large school theatre in the metropolitan area, and I can approach my own principal tomorrow about ours, with a livestream back to the recital hall for overflow families. Third, on Catherine's advice, we write to the university reserving our position and asking it to meet the additional hire and transport costs, and the executive approves a separate risk and child safety plan for the second site before registrations open.", "close": "That keeps the festival on campus and the finals in a full house, and it asks the party that changed the arrangement to share its cost. The festival is not the building; it is the night, and we can still give students that night. I would move that the board adopts the two-site plan, that Catherine drafts the letter to the university this week, and that I report back on a finals venue by Friday."}}, "why": "He fuses Tom's practical insight and Catherine's legal caution into one plan, so both directors hear their contribution become the decision. The quotable line restores the president's priority, and the motion assigns two owners and a date."}, {"id": "long-b5", "room": "Festival association board, annual strategy meeting, hybrid: six directors in person and four on Zoom, with the independent governance reviewer presenting", "briefing": "The independent reviewer, Dr Alison Marsh, presents her governance review of the association. She finds a board that is committed and generous with its time but heavily dependent on three long-serving directors; no term limits and no skills matrix; rules that were last substantively revised soon after the Associations Incorporation Act 2015 (WA) came into force; and financial delegations that exist 'more by custom than by resolution'. She recommends a skills-based board, staggered terms, revised rules adopted by special resolution at a general meeting, and a formal register of delegations. She is courteous but firm. 'Nothing I found is improper. Much of it is fragile. The association's reputation is better than its paperwork.' The president thanks her and asks directors for a response and a sequence of implementation, reminding the board that changes to the rules require a special resolution of members, while delegations and the skills matrix are within the board's own power.", "before": [{"who": "Richard, long-serving director and past president, on Zoom", "said": "Thank you, Alison, for a thorough and gracious report. I have served this association for fourteen years, through two near-insolvencies and the festival's move to its current home, and I want to say how much I welcome scrutiny of this kind. Good governance is not a destination but a journey. I would simply caution that what makes this association special is its people, its volunteer spirit, its institutional memory. Let us be careful not to bureaucratise the very thing that has sustained us. I am confident the board will find its way forward together."}, {"who": "Priya, director, a chartered accountant, in the room", "said": "I would support the delegations register immediately. At the moment, if the treasurer or the president were unavailable, I am not confident anyone could say with authority who may approve a payment over a given amount. That is a risk to us individually as well as collectively. I would also do the skills matrix now. Term limits and the rules can follow, but they need members' votes and proper consultation, and I would rather get those right than fast."}], "notes": ["Phase 1, board resolution tonight: delegations register (thresholds, two signatories, treasurer drafts) + skills matrix.", "Phase 2, members: revised rules from the model rules, our objects added, staggered terms.", "Long-serving directors rotate over three years, not all at once; advisory role to keep memory (Richard).", "Members' information evening before the special general meeting."], "task": "Respond to the review with a sequenced implementation plan the board can begin tonight.", "seconds": 240, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Alison, for a review that is exact without being unkind, and for a sentence we will be quoting for some time: that our reputation is better than our paperwork. Thank you, Richard, for reminding us that the people are the asset, and Priya, for putting your finger on the most immediate risk, which is that none of us could say tonight who may approve what.", "acknowledge": "It is not comfortable for a volunteer board to be told it is fragile, especially when so much of what it has built is owed to the directors who have given it the most.", "standing": "As treasurer, I am one of the people the current custom relies on, so I am conscious of my own position, and as chair of a school council I know how governance reform feels from the other end of the table.", "common": "We all want this association to outlast every one of us at this table, and to be able to show members, sponsors and schools that it is run properly.", "frame": "Priya has given us the logic: some recommendations are within the board's power tonight, and some belong to the members. If we separate those cleanly, we can act now without rushing the parts that deserve consultation, and Richard's concern about memory becomes a design question rather than an objection.", "proposal": "I would propose a two-phase implementation plan. Phase one, by board resolution tonight: a formal register of delegations with dollar thresholds and a two-signatory rule, which I will draft as treasurer, and a skills matrix, which tells us what the board needs rather than whom we happen to know. Phase two, for members: revised rules, starting from the model rules and adding our own objects, with staggered terms so that our longest-serving directors rotate over three years rather than all at once, and a recognised advisory role so that the institutional memory Richard rightly values is kept rather than lost. We would hold a members' information evening before the special general meeting, so the special resolution reaches members explained rather than imposed.", "close": "That gives us safety now and legitimacy later, and it honours both Alison's findings and the people they concern. Good governance should make this association easier to give to, not harder. I would ask that the minutes record that the board accepts the review in principle and resolves phase one tonight, and that the secretary and I bring draft rules and a timetable for the special general meeting to the next board meeting."}}, "why": "Dijon uses Priya's distinction between board power and member power to sequence the reforms, and converts Richard's warm generalities into a concrete advisory role, so the most senior voice in the room is honoured rather than opposed. The plan is lawful in its order, owned, and minutable."}, {"id": "long-b6", "room": "School council, ordinary meeting, twelve members in the library; the deputy chair presides for this item after the chair, Dijon, declared an interest", "briefing": "The principal presents next year's proposed schedule of charges and voluntary contributions, which council must approve under the School Education Act 1999 (WA). The contested item is an increase to the music program charge and to the instrument hire fee, the first in four years. The principal explains that the instrument fleet is ageing and that repair costs now exceed hire income. She acknowledges that parent representatives have received emails from families who feel the music program is becoming 'a program for those who can pay'. 'I want council to approve a schedule it can defend to every family, not just one it can balance.' Dijon, as chair, has declared an interest as head of the music department and handed the chair to the deputy for this item. He will not vote, but the deputy chair has invited him to speak to it, given his knowledge of the fleet.", "before": [{"who": "Marcus, parent representative, a senior barrister", "said": "Thank you, Principal, and thank you to the chair for his scrupulous declaration. I want to say at the outset that I regard the music program as one of the jewels of this school; my own daughter has flourished in it. But a council's first duty is to equity. A public school must never permit the size of a family's bank balance to determine the size of a child's opportunity. I would ask council to reflect carefully on the message we send. I am not in a position tonight to support the increase as framed, though I entirely understand the pressures that produced it."}, {"who": "Jenny, parent representative and P&C treasurer", "said": "I have the P&C's figures with me. We already give the music department a grant each year, and we have had families on payment plans for instrument hire who have told us quietly that they are struggling. At the same time, I have seen the instruments. Some of the hire fleet is genuinely past repair. I do not think the answer is no increase. I think it is an increase that does not land on the families who can least absorb it."}], "notes": ["Confidential hardship provision run by the business manager, not music; automatic for families already eligible for department assistance.", "Phase the increase over two years.", "Earmark the P&C grant to fleet renewal with a published replacement schedule.", "Annual report to council: hire income, repair costs, number of families using hardship provision."], "task": "Speaking to the item after declaring your interest, propose a way council can approve the increase while answering the equity objection.", "seconds": 210, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Deputy Chair, and thank you, Principal, for asking council for a schedule we can defend to every family, which is the right standard. Thank you, Marcus, for stating the equity principle so clearly that none of us could forget it, and Jenny, for bringing both the figures and the families behind them.", "acknowledge": "I am conscious that I speak to this as the person who leads the program, which is why I have stepped out of the chair, and I am conscious too that some families have read this increase as a door closing.", "standing": "I have managed instrument fleets in two schools over nearly twenty years, so I can tell council with some confidence what an instrument past repair costs us, and what it costs a student who is handed one.", "common": "No one at this table wants a child's opportunity to depend on a family's income, and no one wants students learning on instruments that are failing them.", "frame": "Marcus has given us the principle and Jenny has given us the shape of the answer: the question is not whether the program costs more, but who carries the cost. At present an unfunded fleet means every family pays in a different currency, which is worse instruments for everyone.", "proposal": "I would suggest council approve the schedule with three conditions, which I would call the access conditions. First, a confidential hardship provision, administered by the business manager rather than the music department, that automatically reduces the charge for families already eligible for the department's secondary assistance arrangements and allows others to apply. Second, the increase is phased over two years; and third, the P&C grant Jenny mentioned is earmarked to fleet renewal under a published replacement schedule, with a short annual report to council on hire income, repair costs and the number of families who used the hardship provision.", "close": "That lets council meet Marcus's test and Jenny's figures at the same time: the program is funded, and no child is priced out of it. Equity is not charging everyone nothing; it is making sure no one is charged what they cannot pay. I would suggest the minute read that council approves the schedule subject to the access conditions, with the principal to report on their operation at the first meeting of Term 3, and I will of course take no part in the vote."}}, "why": "He meets an eloquent objection that offers no alternative by building the alternative from the barrister's own principle and the treasurer's figures. Handling the conflict of interest openly strengthens his authority, and the conditions give the hesitant members a reason to vote yes."}, {"id": "long-b7", "room": "School council, ordinary meeting, eleven members in person, chaired by Dijon", "briefing": "The principal tells council that the department has indicated the school is likely to receive a single capital works allocation in the next budget cycle, and has asked the school to nominate one priority. Three are on the table: refurbishing four science laboratories that no longer meet current standards; replacing six ageing transportable classrooms; and a student wellbeing hub bringing student services, the school psychologist and a quiet space together. Each has an advocate on council, and the staff and student surveys split almost evenly. The principal says: 'Whatever we nominate, two groups will feel unheard. I would like council to give me a priority I can explain, not just one I can announce.' The nomination is due to the regional office in three weeks. Dijon, chairing, has heard from members around the table and now speaks before putting a resolution.", "before": [{"who": "Dr Nguyen, community member, a research scientist", "said": "I will declare my bias: I would put the laboratories first. They are the only option with a compliance dimension. If a laboratory no longer meets standards, it constrains what senior students can do in their practical work, and that affects their results directly. The transportables are unpleasant but functional. The wellbeing hub is valuable, but it could be housed in existing space, at least in part. Of the three, only one carries a risk that grows if we wait."}, {"who": "Kayla, student representative", "said": "The student survey put the wellbeing hub first, by a small margin. I know that might sound soft next to the labs, but students waiting outside the psychologist's office in a corridor where everyone can see them is a real problem. People do not go because of it. I am not saying the labs do not matter. I am saying the hub would change who asks for help. If we fixed that, I think students would notice the difference within a term."}], "notes": ["Labs as the capital nomination: compliance, risk grows with time.", "Hub's urgent need is privacy, not a building: relocate student services this year into existing space with a private entry, school budget.", "Transportables on the next cycle list with a condition report.", "Nomination letter to explain all three, so the region sees a plan.", "Kayla to report back to student council."], "task": "As chair, propose a resolution that ranks the priorities and gives each group a credible answer.", "seconds": 180, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Principal, for asking us for a priority you can explain; that is the right brief for a council. Thank you, Dr Nguyen, for the clearest test anyone has offered tonight, which option carries a risk that grows if we wait, and Kayla, for telling us something no survey percentage could, that the corridor outside the psychologist's office changes who asks for help.", "acknowledge": "This is one of those decisions where every option is good and only one can be first, and council should own that rather than pretend otherwise.", "standing": "As chair I have held back my own view until everyone has spoken, and as a head of department I have taught in more than one of those transportables and sent students down that corridor.", "common": "We all want a school where students can do the science their futures require, and can ask for help without being watched doing it.", "frame": "Dr Nguyen's test settles the capital question, I think: the laboratories carry a compliance risk the other two do not. But Kayla's point shows that the hub's most urgent need is not capital at all; it is privacy, and privacy can be solved sooner and more cheaply than a building.", "proposal": "So I will put a three-part resolution, which we might call the sequenced priorities. First, council nominates the laboratory refurbishment as the school's capital priority, on the grounds of compliance and growing risk. Second, council asks the principal to relocate student services this year into existing space with a private entry, funded from the school's own budget, so Kayla's corridor is gone well before any building could be. Third, the nomination letter sets out the case for all three projects and places the transportables on the next cycle with a condition report, so the region sees a plan rather than a single request.", "close": "That way the labs are funded, the hub's real problem is solved this year, and nobody is simply told to wait. We are not choosing between science and wellbeing; we are choosing the order in which we deliver both. I will put the resolution in those terms, with the principal to report on the relocation of student services at our next meeting and Kayla to take the outcome back to the student council."}}, "why": "Speaking last as chair, he uses the scientist's test to settle the capital question and the student's insight to show that the losing option's real need can be met another way. Nobody leaves unheard, and the resolution is ready to put."}, {"id": "long-b8", "room": "School council, ordinary meeting, ten members in person, chaired by Dijon", "briefing": "Under general business, a parent representative, Simone, tables a letter signed by fourteen families about the late cancellation of the Year 10 outdoor education camp. The letter complains that families were told by text message two days before departure, that deposits have still not been refunded six weeks later, and that emails to the school have gone unanswered. It asks council to 'investigate the decision and the staff responsible'. The principal responds calmly. She accepts that communication fell short and that refunds have taken too long, and explains that the cancellation followed safety advice from the camp provider. 'I understand the frustration, and I accept that we did not handle the aftermath well. But council is not the forum for reviewing individual staff decisions, and I would ask that we are careful about that line.' Several members are visibly uncomfortable. Dijon, as chair, must respond before the meeting moves on.", "before": [{"who": "Simone, parent representative", "said": "I do not want to put anyone on trial. But these families came to me because they felt they had nowhere else to go. They had written to the school and heard nothing. If council's answer is that this is not our business, then I do not know what to tell them. Council represents the school community, and part of that community feels ignored. I would like at least an assurance that someone will look at what happened, and that they will get their money back."}, {"who": "Paul, community member, a former senior public servant", "said": "I would support the principal on the governance point. School councils in this state have defined functions under the Act, and reviewing operational decisions about staff is not one of them. If we start, we will be asked to do it every month, and we will do it badly. But I would distinguish the decision from the system. Council can legitimately ask how the school communicates with families when something goes wrong. That is policy, not personnel."}], "notes": ["Two parts: (a) individual complaint to the principal under the department's complaints process; (b) communication pattern is council's business.", "Ask principal: refunds within a fortnight; written reply to each of the 14 families.", "Protocol for communicating changes or cancellations of camps and excursions, to next meeting.", "Simone needs something concrete to take back."], "task": "As chair, respond to the complaint in a way that respects the council's limits and gives the families a real outcome.", "seconds": 180, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Simone, for bringing this to us with care, and for saying plainly that no one is on trial. Thank you, Principal, for accepting without qualification that the aftermath was not handled well, and Paul, for the distinction I think will let us act tonight: the decision belongs to the school, but the system belongs to all of us.", "acknowledge": "Fourteen families felt they had nowhere to go but this table, and that in itself tells council something it needs to hear.", "standing": "I have chaired this council for some time, and as a head of department I have cancelled an event at short notice myself, so I know both how necessary it can be and how easily the communication afterwards goes wrong.", "common": "Everyone here wants families to be told promptly, to be answered when they write, and to have their money returned without having to chase it.", "frame": "Paul has shown us where the line sits, and the principal has asked us to respect it, which we should. But Simone is right that 'not our business' cannot be the whole answer, because this complaint has two parts: an individual decision, which council does not review, and a pattern of communication, which council is entitled to ask about.", "proposal": "So I would propose we deal with it in two parts, separating the complaint from the system. First, the individual matter goes to the principal under the department's complaints process, with her agreement tonight that the outstanding refunds will be processed within a fortnight and that each of the fourteen families will receive a written reply. Second, council asks the principal to bring to our next meeting a short protocol for communicating with families when a camp or excursion is changed or cancelled: who tells them, how, how quickly, and how refunds are handled.", "close": "That respects the line the principal and Paul have drawn, and it gives Simone something real to take back: an answer, a refund and a better system. Families do not need council to judge a teacher; they need to know they will be heard next time. I would ask the minutes to record the letter as received and referred to the principal, the refund and reply commitment with its date, and the protocol as an item for our next meeting."}}, "why": "As chair he holds the governance line without sounding defensive, by agreeing with the principal and the former public servant and then showing what council can properly do. Simone leaves with three concrete outcomes, which defuses the room."}, {"id": "long-b9", "room": "Principal's leadership meeting, heads of learning area and deputy principals around the conference table, in person", "briefing": "The principal tells heads of learning area that next year's staffing allocation has come in lower than projected, largely because senior enrolments in several elective courses have fallen. She must find the equivalent of a little more than one full-time position across the arts, technologies and languages. She wants proposals rather than defences. 'I have already heard why every program is essential. What I need from this room is how we make the reduction with the least damage, and I would rather it came from you than from me.' The music department has been asked to consider whether its small Year 11 and Year 12 ATAR Music classes could be combined for a year, and whether some ensemble direction could move from timetabled time into co-curricular hours. Decisions on line structures are needed within a fortnight, before subject selection opens.", "before": [{"who": "Deputy principal, curriculum", "said": "To make the principal's request concrete: we have three senior classes in the arts with fewer than ten students each. Composite classes are the obvious lever. Music has a Year 11 and a Year 12 ATAR class that together would be around fourteen students. Visual arts has a similar pattern. The question is not whether composites are ideal, because they are not, but whether they are tolerable for one year while we rebuild numbers."}, {"who": "Anna, head of languages", "said": "I will be honest that languages have been through this twice already. We combined Year 11 and 12 French two years ago, and the result was a teacher running two syllabuses in one room and losing students at the end of Year 11. If composites are the answer, they need to come with something, release time or a different assessment calendar, or they simply move the damage from the budget to the results."}], "notes": ["Accept a Yr 11/12 ATAR Music composite for ONE year; review end of Term 2; sunset unless renewed.", "Align both years' assessment calendars; aural and theory taught together; practical and exam prep separated in final term (Anna's 'something').", "Yr 10 pathway evening + taster unit to rebuild senior numbers.", "Keep ensembles timetabled; don't offer them up as well."], "task": "Offer the music department's share of the reduction in a form the principal can adopt.", "seconds": 180, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Principal, for asking for proposals rather than defences; it is a more respectful request than it sounds, and a harder one. Thank you, Deputy, for putting a number on the table, and Anna, for the warning every composite in this school should now carry: it moves the damage from the budget to the results unless something travels with it.", "acknowledge": "Nobody here wants to be the area that offered up a class, and I recognise that what I am about to say is a loss for my own department.", "standing": "I have taught senior music since 2005 and led the department here since 2021, and I have taught a combined Year 11 and 12 group before, so I can say where it works and where it fails.", "common": "We all want to make this reduction in a way that protects student results and leaves us able to rebuild numbers, rather than in a way that quietly closes a course.", "frame": "Anna's experience is the evidence we should weigh most heavily, because it shows that composites fail at one particular point: the teacher running two syllabuses at the same moment. In music, though, a large part of the course, aural and theory, overlaps between the years, which makes it more combinable than most, provided the assessment calendars are aligned.", "proposal": "So music will offer a composite on what I would call the three safeguards. First, it is for one year only, with a review at the end of Term 2 and a sunset unless the principal decides otherwise. Second, we align the Year 11 and 12 assessment calendars so aural and theory are taught to the whole group together, with practical and examination preparation separated in the final term, which is exactly the something Anna says must travel with a composite. Third, we run a Year 10 pathway evening and a taster unit to rebuild senior numbers, so this becomes a pause rather than a decline.", "close": "That gives the principal music's share of the reduction, given rather than taken, and with the conditions that make it survivable. A composite we design is a strategy; a composite we merely endure is the beginning of a closure. I would suggest the minute record music's offer subject to the three safeguards, and I will give the deputy an aligned assessment calendar by the end of next week."}}, "why": "By offering the class himself, with conditions, he answers the principal's request for proposals and earns the right to set the terms. Building the safeguards on Anna's experience makes them hard to refuse and signals solidarity across learning areas."}, {"id": "long-b10", "room": "Executive meeting to receive an external program review of the music department, hybrid: principal, one associate principal and the business manager in the room, a second associate principal and the review panel chair on Zoom", "briefing": "The review panel chair, Professor Diane Kerr, a retired university dean, presents the findings of an external review of the music department, commissioned by the principal as part of the school's cycle of program reviews. The review praises the quality of the ensembles and the senior results, but finds that the department's growth has outpaced its systems: inconsistent documentation of assessment moderation, heavy reliance on the head of department for both ensemble direction and administration, and unclear lines between the school program and the instrumental service teachers who work within it. 'This is a department running at a very high level on a narrow base. My concern is not what it does, but what happens if one person is unwell for a term.' She has made eleven recommendations. The principal asks the executive and the head of department to agree priorities and a timeline today.", "before": [{"who": "Michael, associate principal, on Zoom", "said": "Thank you, Diane, for a review of real depth and grace. I think it captures beautifully what so many of us have observed: a department of genuine excellence carried on remarkable goodwill. I want to say publicly how much the school owes Dijon and his team. At the same time, the findings on sustainability are important, and I think they speak to a broader cultural question about how we support high-performing programs across the school. I would hope we take these recommendations in a spirit of growth, and see this as the beginning of a conversation."}, {"who": "Sue, business manager, in the room", "said": "From my side, two of the recommendations have cost implications: additional ensemble direction hours and administrative support. I can find a small amount for administration by reallocating part of an existing education assistant's hours, but new teaching hours would have to come out of the staffing allocation, and that is a conversation for the principal. I would suggest we sort the eleven into those that cost nothing, those that cost a little, and those that need a decision."}], "notes": ["Accept the review. Don't defend.", "Moderation: common template and record for every course, by end of term.", "Every ensemble has a named second director (department or instrumental staff) who takes it at least twice a term; short memorandum with the instrumental service on roles.", "Take Sue's EA hours for music admin; new teaching hours left to the principal.", "One-page progress report to executive each term."], "task": "Respond to the review as head of department with agreed priorities and a timeline.", "seconds": 240, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Diane, and the panel, for a review that took the department seriously enough to be exact about it. Thank you, Michael, for your generous words about the team, and Sue, for giving us the sorting tool I would like to use for the rest of this discussion: what costs nothing, what costs a little, and what needs a decision.", "acknowledge": "It is not comfortable to hear that the department depends too heavily on me, but Diane is right, and the proper response from a head of department is to accept that rather than explain it.", "standing": "I have led this department since 2021 and taught for sixteen years before that, and I have watched more than one excellent program fade after one person left, so I would much rather fix this while things are going well.", "common": "I think we all want what Diane wants: a department that would be just as good next year if any one of us were away for a term.", "frame": "Diane's sentence about one person being unwell is the real test, so I would judge each of the eleven recommendations by whether it reduces that risk. Measured that way, three matter most, and two of the three cost nothing, which fits Sue's sorting neatly.", "proposal": "I would propose a resilience plan with three priorities, in this order. First, at no cost, a common moderation template and record for every course, in place by the end of this term, so our assessment practice is documented and not merely remembered. Second, also at no cost, every ensemble has a named second director, drawn from department or instrumental service staff, who rehearses it at least twice a term, and we agree a short memorandum with the instrumental service on roles, which answers the review's point about unclear lines. Third, at small cost, Sue's offer of reallocated education assistant hours for music administration, with the larger question of new teaching hours left, as Sue said, to the principal's staffing decision.", "close": "That deals with the risk Diane named, uses what the school already has, and leaves the hard choices where they belong. A department that depends on one person is fragile; a department that has made sure it does not is excellent. I would ask that the minutes record the executive's acceptance of the review, the three priorities and their timelines, and a one-page progress report from me to the executive each term."}}, "why": "Accepting criticism of his own reliance without defensiveness gives him more authority than any rebuttal could. He sets aside the associate principal's warm abstractions, adopts the business manager's sorting tool, and turns eleven recommendations into three owned, dated priorities."}, {"id": "long-b11", "room": "Principal's leadership meeting, heads of learning area, the timetabler presenting, in person", "briefing": "The timetabler, Nick, presents the proposed timetable structure for next year, endorsed in principle by the executive: a move from six fifty-minute periods to five sixty-minute periods, with a later start on Wednesdays to protect staff professional learning. The rationale has been discussed at length and is broadly supported: fewer transitions, longer lessons for sustained work, and protected collaborative time. But the change removes the before-school period currently used by three ensembles, and the Wednesday late start falls on the morning the symphonic band rehearses. The deputy principal says: 'We have modelled this carefully, and every structure has a casualty. I would ask heads to tell us whether the casualties in their area can be managed, not whether they would prefer the old structure.' The timetable must be locked before subject selection in four weeks, and Nick has said he cannot accommodate individual exceptions without reopening the model.", "before": [{"who": "Rob, head of health and physical education", "said": "We have a similar issue with interschool sport, although Nick's model actually helps us on Wednesday afternoons. I would just say that the arts and sport run co-curricular programs that the timetable has always treated as somebody else's problem. If the school values these programs, the timetable should show it, rather than leaving each head of area to negotiate workarounds year by year. Every year I spend the first weeks of Term 1 rebuilding a sport schedule around a timetable that was never designed with it in mind."}, {"who": "Karen, head of science", "said": "I will be the one to say that I like the five-period day. Sixty minutes makes practical work possible without double periods. I am sympathetic to music, but I would be wary of carve-outs that unravel the structure. Once one area gets an exception, others will want one, and Nick will be back to six periods by stealth. I would much rather we solve music's problem inside the model than beside it."}], "notes": ["Don't ask for an exception; ask for co-curricular time designed into the structure.", "Publish ensembles and interschool sport as a layer on the master timetable.", "Protect two after-school slots a week from staff meetings and PL; the three period-0 ensembles move there.", "Symphonic band: Wednesday to Thursday before school, check instrumental teachers' availability.", "Draft with Nick for next leadership meeting."], "task": "Respond to the new timetable with a solution that protects the ensembles without asking for an exception.", "seconds": 180, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Nick, for modelling this so carefully and for being honest about where it hurts. Thank you, Deputy, for asking the right question, whether the casualties can be managed, and Rob and Karen, for giving me the two constraints I need to respect: that co-curricular programs should not be somebody else's problem, and that a structure riddled with exceptions is not a structure.", "acknowledge": "For music this change is real: three ensembles lose their period, and the symphonic band loses its morning, which together account for a large share of our students' weekly rehearsal time.", "standing": "I have rebuilt ensemble rehearsal schedules around timetable changes in two schools, and I direct several of these groups myself, so I know which times students will actually turn up to.", "common": "We all want the five-period day to work, and we all want the programs that bring families into this school to survive it, without any area having to win an argument at another's expense.", "frame": "Karen is right that I should not ask for an exception, and Rob is right that the problem is a timetable that treats co-curricular time as invisible. Put those together and the answer is not a carve-out from the structure; it is a co-curricular layer designed into it.", "proposal": "So I would propose a co-curricular layer, in three parts. First, ensembles and interschool sport are published as a layer of the master timetable, so they are planned for rather than negotiated around. Second, two after-school slots each week are protected from staff meetings and professional learning, which lets the three ensembles that lose the early period move there without anyone needing an exception. Third, the symphonic band moves from Wednesday to a Thursday before-school rehearsal, subject to the instrumental teachers' availability, which I will confirm this week.", "close": "That leaves Nick's structure intact, answers Karen's concern, and gives Rob's principle a place in the timetable itself. The test of a timetable is not only what happens between the bells, but what it makes possible around them. I would suggest the minute record that the five-period model is supported, with a co-curricular layer to be drafted by Nick and me for the next leadership meeting."}}, "why": "He disarms the likely opposition by agreeing with the head of science first, then uses the PE head's principle to reframe music's problem as a whole-school design question. Supporting the model while changing its edges makes him an ally of the timetabler rather than another petitioner."}, {"id": "long-b12", "room": "National music education conference, closing panel on the future of school ensembles: four panellists on stage, about three hundred delegates, the chair taking questions from the floor", "briefing": "The panel chair, a radio presenter, takes a question from a delegate, a primary music specialist from a regional town. 'Everyone on this panel runs a well-resourced program in a capital city. What do you actually say to someone like me, who has one room, no instrumental service and forty minutes a week with each class?' The question draws sustained applause. The chair turns to the panel with a smile that does not quite let them off. 'That is a fair hit, I think. Let us not answer it with how wonderful music is. Who has something she can use next term?' Two panellists have answered. Dijon is the third to respond, and he knows that the session ends in eight minutes, that the conference report will quote the panel's closing answers, and that the delegate is still standing at the microphone.", "before": [{"who": "Professor Gail Rennie, panellist, a music education academic", "said": "What a powerful question, and thank you for asking it. It goes to the heart of everything this conference has been about. I have spent thirty years researching access, and the evidence is clear that place shapes opportunity. Every state has its own policy settings, some more generous than others, but none has solved the tyranny of distance. What I would say to you is that you are not alone. The work you are doing in that one room matters more than you know, and the sector owes it to you to advocate loudly for change."}, {"who": "Ben, panellist, director of a city youth orchestra", "said": "I would add that technology has changed what is possible. We have had some success with online sectionals for regional players, and there are good free resources now. But I will be honest: an online sectional does not replace a teacher in the room, and I do not want to pretend it does. I suppose my practical answer is to connect with whatever ensembles exist near you. I would rather say that plainly than offer you a link."}], "notes": ["Class ensemble from 40 minutes: one piece per term arranged in layers every child can play (voice, body percussion, untuned, whatever pitched instruments).", "Pair with one secondary school that visits once a term: students play WITH them, not FOR them.", "Offer our feeder band layered arrangements, free.", "Ask the conference body to host a regional pairing register."], "task": "Answer the delegate's question with something she can use next term, in a form the conference report will quote.", "seconds": 150, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you for the question, and thank you, Chair, for refusing to let us answer it with a hymn to music. Gail is right that you are not alone, and Ben was honest enough to say that a screen is not a teacher, which is where I would like to start.", "acknowledge": "You are right that I run a well-resourced program, and it would be unseemly to pretend I know your room better than you do.", "standing": "What I can offer is practical: I have taught since 2005, I have played professionally for twenty-six years, and each year I run a combined band with primary schools that have none of what my school has.", "common": "I think everyone in this hall wants for your students what I want for mine: the experience of making music together, in parts, that sounds like something.", "frame": "Forty minutes and one room are enough for that, if the repertoire is built for them. The bottleneck in your situation is not the students or the time; it is arrangements that assume instruments you do not have.", "proposal": "So here are three things for next term. First, one piece each term arranged in layers every child can play, voice, body percussion, untuned percussion and whatever pitched instruments you have, so every class becomes an ensemble rather than an audience. Second, pair with one secondary school, even a long way off, that visits once a term, so your students play with them, not for them. Third, and this is an offer rather than a gesture: the layered arrangements we use with our feeder primary schools are yours, free, and I would ask the association to host a regional pairing register so that every school like yours can find a partner by the next conference.", "close": "That is repertoire you can teach in forty minutes, a partner you can meet once a term, and a register the sector can build this year. Distance is real, but isolation is a choice we can stop making for one another. If the chair agrees, I will leave my details with the conference office today, and I would ask that the pairing register be noted in the conference report as a commitment."}}, "why": "After an eloquent answer that offered sympathy and a candid one that offered little, Dijon gives the delegate three things she can do, plus a resource and a sector commitment. Conceding his privilege up front earns the room's trust, and the closing line is built to be quoted."}, {"id": "long-b13", "room": "Ministerial roundtable on arts learning in schools, hybrid: fourteen invited participants in a Parliament House meeting room and six on Zoom, chaired by the minister's chief of staff", "briefing": "The minister's chief of staff, Elena, opens the roundtable by explaining that the government intends to announce a creative learning initiative in the next budget, with a modest, fixed envelope and a strong preference for something visible within eighteen months. She is explicit about the constraints: no new permanent staffing, a preference for partnerships with existing arts organisations, and a requirement that the initiative reach regional and low socioeconomic schools. 'The minister does not want another report. She wants something she can stand next to in a school hall before the next election, and something that still exists after it.' Participants include principals, the heads of several arts companies, academics and two departmental officers. Each has been asked for one implementable idea, and Elena has said she will take three of them back to the minister for costing.", "before": [{"who": "James, head of a state arts company, on Zoom", "said": "Thank you, Elena, and please convey our thanks to the minister for this genuine engagement. I have led arts organisations in three states, and I can tell you that this kind of conversation, at this level, is not to be taken for granted. The arts and education sectors have so much to offer each other. Other jurisdictions show what is possible when government, schools and arts companies work as one ecosystem. Whatever we design must be ambitious, it must be equitable, and it must be built to last. I am confident this group has the wisdom to get it right."}, {"who": "Kerry, departmental officer, regional education, in the room", "said": "Practically, the hardest part in regional schools is not goodwill; it is getting the people there. A visiting artist program costs most in travel and accommodation, and schools cannot release staff to travel to the city. And anything that relies on schools applying competitively will favour the schools with the capacity to write applications, which are not the schools the minister wants to reach. If the minister wants regional schools in the photograph, the design has to bring the program to them."}], "notes": ["Residency model: arts company + a government specialist music school do a short residency in a regional or low-SES cluster, ending in a public concert (the school hall moment).", "Allocation not competition: department nominates clusters using an index it already uses (ICSEA).", "Specialist school senior students travel as mentors.", "Lasting part: train two local teachers per residency; leave repertoire and a small instrument set.", "Measures: students participating, teachers trained, return visits."], "task": "Offer the roundtable one implementable initiative that meets the minister's constraints.", "seconds": 210, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Elena, for setting out the constraints so clearly; it is much easier to be useful inside a fence than in an open field. Thank you, James, for reminding us what is at stake in getting this right, and Kerry, for the observation that should shape everything we propose: a competitive round reaches the schools that can write applications, not the schools the minister wants to reach.", "acknowledge": "A fixed envelope and an eighteen-month window are tight, and an initiative that looks good at launch and disappears afterwards would be worse than none.", "standing": "I lead the music department of a government school with a large specialist music program, I have performed professionally in this state for twenty-six years, and I run programs with primary schools that have no music specialist at all.", "common": "We all want what the minister wants: something real in a school hall within eighteen months, and something still there after the cameras have gone.", "frame": "Kerry's point tells us the delivery mechanism has to be allocation rather than competition, and Elena's constraints tell us it has to use people and organisations who already exist. The model that fits both is a residency: concentrated, visible, and designed to leave something behind.", "proposal": "I would propose a regional ensemble residency program, with three design features. First, allocation not application: the department nominates clusters of regional and low socioeconomic schools using an index it already uses, such as ICSEA, so Kerry's schools are reached by design. Second, each residency is delivered by an existing arts company paired with a government specialist music school, whose senior students travel as mentors, and it ends in a public concert, which is the school hall moment the minister wants. Third, the lasting part: every residency trains two local teachers and leaves its repertoire and a small set of instruments with the cluster, so there is something to sustain when the company goes home.", "close": "So the minister has a launch she can attend, schools have a program they do not have to compete for, and the state has teachers trained and instruments left behind. The measure of this initiative should be what is still playing in those schools a year after the residency has gone. I would ask that the regional ensemble residency be recorded as one of the roundtable's options, and I am glad to provide a one-page design to Elena's office within a fortnight."}}, "why": "He answers the chief of staff's political brief precisely, a visible moment and a lasting legacy, while building the mechanism on the departmental officer's hard-won point about competitive rounds. The arts company head's rhetoric is honoured and quietly superseded by a design that can be costed."}, {"id": "long-b14", "room": "Department of Education consultation forum on the instrumental music service delivery model, hybrid: twenty participants in the department's training room and about forty on Teams", "briefing": "The director responsible for the instrumental music service, Mark, presents a consultation paper proposing changes to how instrumental teaching time is allocated to schools. At present allocation largely follows historical patterns and enrolled student numbers; the paper proposes a formula weighted towards schools with no current access, and a cap on any single school's share. Mark acknowledges that some schools with large established programs will see their allocations reduced over three years. 'This is about fairness across the system. I know it will feel like a loss to some schools in this room, and I would rather hear that now, with your ideas, than in the minister's inbox later.' He asks for practical feedback on transition, on protecting continuity for students already learning, and on how the formula should treat specialist music program schools. Written submissions close in a month, and the department intends to model any amendments before then.", "before": [{"who": "Craig, principal of a regional district high school, on Teams", "said": "I want to welcome the direction. My students have never had an instrumental teacher visit. We have families who drive two hours each way for lessons, and most cannot. I understand that schools with large programs will feel this, but from where I sit the current pattern has been a quiet inequity for a long time. My only request is that the new allocation comes with a teacher who can actually get to us, because a number on a spreadsheet does not teach a child."}, {"who": "Fiona, principal of a metropolitan specialist program school, in the room", "said": "I support equity in principle, but I would ask the department to recognise what specialist programs are. Students audition into them, and families choose the school because of them. They are, in effect, a commitment the system has made to those students. If a student who started in Year 8 loses lessons in Year 10 because of a formula, that is a broken commitment. A cap that does not distinguish specialist schools from others will damage programs the department itself created."}], "notes": ["Continuity guarantee: students already learning keep lessons to the end of their program commitment; reductions apply to new intakes.", "Specialist schools as their own category, tied to approved program numbers (not uncapped).", "Specialist schools host outreach: their IMSS teachers deliver some lessons to partner schools with no access; counts towards their allocation.", "Build travel time into regional allocations (Craig's 'teacher who can get to us').", "Publish the formula and every school's figures."], "task": "Give the forum practical feedback that supports equity of access while protecting continuity for students in specialist programs.", "seconds": 240, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Mark, for inviting the loss into the room rather than leaving it for the minister's inbox; that is a generous way to consult. Thank you, Craig, for the sentence I think the department should print on the front of the final paper, that a number on a spreadsheet does not teach a child, and Fiona, for reminding us that a specialist program is a commitment the system has made to a particular child.", "acknowledge": "I should be clear that my school is one of those likely to lose allocation, so I am speaking partly against my own narrow interest and partly for it.", "standing": "I lead the music department of a specialist music school, I have taught since 2005, and our ensembles depend on the instrumental teachers this formula allocates, so I have thought about it one student at a time.", "common": "I do not think anyone here disagrees with Craig that a child in a regional town deserves a teacher, or with Fiona that a child who auditioned in Year 7 deserves the program she was promised.", "frame": "The two principles only collide if the formula changes allocations for students who are already learning. If we separate students in the system now from future allocation, most of the conflict between Craig's equity and Fiona's continuity disappears.", "proposal": "I would suggest three amendments to the paper, which I would call the continuity and reach amendments. First, a continuity guarantee: students already learning keep their lessons until the end of their current program commitment, with reductions applying to new intakes, so no family experiences a broken promise. Second, specialist program schools sit in their own category in the formula, tied to their approved program numbers rather than uncapped, and they host outreach, with their instrumental teachers delivering some lessons to partner schools that have no access, counted towards the specialist school's allocation. Third, travel time is built into regional allocations, so Craig's school receives a teacher who can actually get there, and the formula and every school's figures are published.", "close": "That gives the department its fairness, Fiona's students their promise and Craig's students a teacher. Equity that breaks a commitment to one child in order to keep one to another is not equity; it is a transfer. I will put these three amendments in our school's written submission, and I would ask Mark whether the department could model the continuity guarantee before submissions close."}}, "why": "Declaring his school's interest at the outset makes his support for equity credible, and the continuity guarantee dissolves the apparent conflict between the regional and specialist principals. The outreach amendment turns a loss for his own school into a contribution it can be seen to make."}, {"id": "long-b15", "room": "University music school advisory board, annual meeting, eleven members in the dean's conference room", "briefing": "The head of school, Professor Sarah Lindqvist, tells the board that the faculty has required the school to reduce the cost of its performance degree substantially over two years. Her preferred option is to reduce weekly one-to-one instrumental lessons for first- and second-year students from an hour to forty-five minutes, replacing the difference with a weekly studio class in each instrument area, and to move some first-year ensemble direction from sessional staff to senior students under supervision. 'I would rather change the shape of the teaching than close something outright. But I know one-to-one tuition is the thing students and teachers will defend hardest, and I need this board to tell me whether I am cutting the right thing.' The proposal goes to faculty in eight weeks. Board members include performers, industry figures, an emeritus professor and school music leaders, and Sarah has asked for advice she can quote.", "before": [{"who": "Robert, emeritus professor and former head of school", "said": "Thank you, Sarah, for your characteristic honesty. I was a student in this building when one-to-one lessons were the beating heart of a conservatoire education, and I taught here for thirty years. The relationship between master and apprentice is the oldest pedagogy we have; it is how musicians have been formed for centuries. I understand the financial pressures, of course I do. But I would urge the school to think very carefully before it touches the one thing that makes us what we are. Some things are not merely costs; they are the institution."}, {"who": "Lisa, artistic director of a jazz festival", "said": "I will offer a slightly different view. The young players I book learnt as much from playing with each other as from their teachers. In jazz particularly, the studio class and the combo are where people learn to listen. I am less worried about fifteen minutes of a lesson than about who teaches the ensembles. Senior students directing first-years could be wonderful or disastrous, depending on whether anyone senior is in the room."}], "notes": ["45-minute lessons in years 1 and 2 acceptable IF the studio class is taught by the student's own principal teacher.", "Keep the full hour in third year and honours (recital preparation).", "Senior students direct first-year ensembles only inside a credit-bearing pedagogy unit, staff director present at least fortnightly.", "Review after one year: recital results and student feedback."], "task": "Advise the head of school on whether the proposed reduction cuts the right thing and how to protect quality if it proceeds.", "seconds": 240, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Sarah, for asking whether you are cutting the right thing, rather than asking us to bless a decision. Thank you, Robert, for reminding us that one-to-one teaching is the oldest pedagogy we have, and Lisa, for observing that the players you book learnt to listen in the studio class and the combo, and that the real risk lies in who is in the room.", "acknowledge": "No one who has been formed by a great teacher hears 'forty-five minutes' without a pang, and it would be dishonest to call this anything but a reduction.", "standing": "I have played professionally for twenty-six years, I teach the students who audition for this course, and I studied with teachers whose lessons I can still hear, so I take Robert's point personally.", "common": "We all want a graduate of this school to have been formed by great teachers and to have learnt, as Lisa says, to listen.", "frame": "Robert is right that the relationship is what matters, and Lisa is right that the relationship is not confined to the lesson. So the test I would apply is not the length of the lesson but whether the student's principal teacher still shapes the whole week; if the studio class is taught by the same teacher, the relationship is extended rather than diminished.", "proposal": "So I would advise that the reduction can proceed on four conditions, which I would call the continuity conditions. One: the studio class in each area is taught by the students' own principal teachers, so the forty-five minutes and the studio class are one relationship, not two. Two: the full hour is kept in third year and honours, where recital preparation genuinely needs it. Three: senior students direct first-year ensembles only within a credit-bearing pedagogy unit, with a staff director present at least fortnightly, which answers Lisa's concern about who is in the room; and four: the change is reviewed after one year against recital results and student feedback.", "close": "That way Sarah reduces cost where it does least harm, and the apprenticeship Robert values is preserved where it matters most. Students do not measure a teacher in minutes; they measure them by whether they were heard. I would ask that the board's advice record support for the proposal subject to the four continuity conditions, with a review report to this board after the first year."}}, "why": "He takes the emeritus professor's eloquent but immovable principle and redefines it so that it can survive the cut, which lets the board give the head of school a yes she can use. Lisa's practical worry becomes a condition, and the quotable line reconciles both speakers."}, {"id": "long-b16", "room": "University music school advisory board, special meeting on admissions, hybrid: seven members in person and five on Zoom", "briefing": "The associate dean for admissions, Dr Paul Ferris, presents data showing a steady five-year decline in applications from Western Australian government schools, while applications from non-government schools and from interstate have held steady. He points to the audition requirements, which ask for prepared repertoire, sight-reading, a written theory test and an interview, and suggests that the theory test in particular appears to be filtering out students whose schools do not offer ATAR Music. 'We are not losing talent. We are losing applicants before talent is ever assessed.' He asks the board for advice on whether and how to change the audition, and on outreach that would rebuild applications from government schools. He notes that the theory test is valued by the staff who teach first-year harmony, and that any change must go to the faculty's admissions committee next semester.", "before": [{"who": "Joanne, head of first-year studies, on Zoom", "said": "I would caution against simply removing the theory test. Students who arrive without theory struggle in first-year harmony, and some fail it, which is worse for them than not being admitted at all. I am open to changing when or how we assess it, but I would want any change to come with support. Otherwise we have simply moved the barrier from the door to the first semester. I have seen students admitted in good faith and then lost by week six."}, {"who": "Daniel, a secondary music teacher at a regional school, on Zoom", "said": "From my end, the barrier is partly the theory test and partly just knowing what is expected. My students do not know anyone who has been through an audition like this. They see a list of requirements and assume it is not for them. Even a recorded example of a good audition, or a visit from someone in the school, would change that. Half the battle is persuading a fifteen-year-old that it is meant for someone like them."}], "notes": ["Separate admission from placement: audition = performance, aural, musicianship; theory becomes a diagnostic AFTER admission.", "Diagnostic places students into a bridging theory unit (summer or semester 1): Joanne's support.", "Recorded model audition + plain-English guide (Daniel).", "Regional and outer-metro audition/information days hosted by government specialist music schools.", "Track applications by school sector over next two intakes."], "task": "Advise the board on changing the audition and outreach so that government school applicants are assessed on talent rather than filtered out.", "seconds": 210, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Paul, for a sentence that reframes the whole problem: we are losing applicants before talent is ever assessed. Thank you, Joanne, for insisting that we not move the barrier from the door to the first semester, and Daniel, for reminding us that some students never knock at all, because they have never seen what is behind the door.", "acknowledge": "A five-year decline from government schools matters well beyond this faculty, because those students are a large part of the state's future teachers and performers.", "standing": "I teach in a government school, I have prepared students for this audition for many years, and I have taught harmony to senior students who arrived with none, so I sit on both sides of Joanne's concern.", "common": "We all want the same outcome: the most talented applicants admitted, and every one of them able to succeed once they are here.", "frame": "Joanne and Daniel have described two different barriers, one in the assessment and one in the information. The theory test is doing two jobs at once, admitting and placing, and it is the admitting job that excludes government school students, while the placing job is the one first-year staff actually need.", "proposal": "So I would propose an admissions package in three parts. First, separate admission from placement: the audition assesses performance, aural skills and musicianship, and the theory test becomes a diagnostic after admission that places students into a bridging theory unit over summer or in first semester, which answers Joanne's concern directly. Second, publish a recorded model audition and a plain-English guide, so Daniel's students can see what is expected before they decide it is not for them. Third, hold regional and outer-metropolitan audition and information days hosted by government specialist music schools, and track applications by school sector over the next two intakes to see whether it works.", "close": "That keeps the standard where first year needs it and moves the door to where government school students can reach it. We should not be testing whether a student's school offered theory; we should be testing whether the student can become a musician. I would ask that the board recommend this package to the associate dean for the admissions committee, with a report on applications by sector at this meeting next year."}}, "why": "The pivotal move is the frame: one test doing two jobs, which lets him keep what the first-year head needs while removing what excludes students. The proposal attaches cleanly to both Zoom speakers and ends with a measurable review."}, {"id": "long-b17", "room": "Metropolitan inter-school music network, termly meeting of heads of music from fifteen schools, in person in a host school's library", "briefing": "The network convenor, Rachel, tells the meeting that next year's calendar is already in trouble. Four major events, the schools' jazz festival, the state concert band festival, a choral eisteddfod and a combined schools' orchestral day, fall within three weeks of each other in Term 3, and two schools have already scheduled their own concerts on the same nights. Instrumental teachers who work across several schools have written to say they cannot be in two places at once. 'We all agree that collaboration matters, and every year we collide anyway. I would like us to leave today with something that changes how we plan, not just a nicer conversation about it.' The network has no formal authority over any school, and its members range from large specialist programs to schools with a single music teacher. Rachel has asked for proposals that cost nothing and need no one's permission.", "before": [{"who": "Elizabeth, head of music at an independent school", "said": "Thank you, Rachel, for convening us, and for your tireless work holding this network together. I have been coming to these meetings for fifteen years, and I would say our collegiality is the envy of other states. Calendar clashes are, in a sense, a symptom of our success: there is so much happening because the sector is so vibrant. I think the answer is fundamentally relational. If we keep talking to one another, keep sharing, keep that spirit of generosity, I am confident the clashes will sort themselves out, as they largely have in the past."}, {"who": "Sam, sole music teacher at a small school", "said": "They do not sort themselves out for me. When three events land in three weeks, I have to choose which one my students miss, and I am usually choosing on the basis of which bus I can book. I would love a calendar that I can see in Term 4 for the following year. At the moment I find out about clashes when a parent tells me. By then the bus is booked and the decision has been made for me."}], "notes": ["Shared network calendar, live by end of Term 4, everyone can see and edit.", "Four big events = anchor events, fixed by end of Term 3 for the following year, at least a week apart. I'll take it to the jazz festival board.", "Schools voluntarily avoid anchor weeks for their own concerts.", "Organisers notify network six months out (instrumental teachers)."], "task": "Propose a practical planning mechanism the network can adopt despite having no authority over its members.", "seconds": 180, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Rachel, for asking for something that changes how we plan rather than a nicer conversation about it. Thank you, Elizabeth, for reminding us how much goodwill this network runs on, and Sam, for showing us exactly where that goodwill runs out: at the bus booking, in a school with one music teacher.", "acknowledge": "When clashes fall hardest on the smallest schools and on the instrumental teachers who serve several of us, it is not a minor inconvenience; it is a question of which students get to perform.", "standing": "I sit on the board of one of those four events, the jazz festival, and I run a department that sends ensembles to all of them, so I am both a cause of this problem and in a position to help fix it.", "common": "Everyone here wants every student in every one of our schools to be able to reach the events that matter, and no one here wants to be told what to do by anyone else.", "frame": "Elizabeth is right that the solution has to be relational, because the network cannot direct anyone. But relationships work better with a shared instrument, and what Sam is describing is the absence of one: nobody can see the year ahead until it is too late.", "proposal": "So I would propose what I will call the anchor calendar, in three steps. First, a shared network calendar, live by the end of Term 4, that every member can see and edit. Second, the four major events are fixed as anchor events by the end of Term 3 for the following year, at least a week apart, and I will take that request to the jazz festival board myself; schools then voluntarily keep their own concerts out of anchor weeks. Third, every event organiser notifies the network six months out, so instrumental teachers working across schools can plan their year rather than defend it.", "close": "It costs nothing, needs no authority, and turns Elizabeth's goodwill into something Sam can see in Term 4. We do not need to collaborate more; we need to collaborate earlier. I would suggest the network minutes record the anchor calendar as agreed, with Rachel and me to set it up and report at the first meeting of next year."}}, "why": "He accepts the dominant member's relational premise and then gives it the instrument it lacks, so the most fluent voice in the room cannot easily oppose him. The sole teacher's bus booking becomes the measure of success, and the plan meets Rachel's brief of no cost and no permission."}, {"id": "long-b18", "room": "Inter-school music network, special meeting on specialist instrumental teacher supply, twelve heads of music and two instrumental teachers in person", "briefing": "The convenor, Carol, reports that four schools in the network have been unable to fill instrumental teaching positions in low brass and double reeds this year, and that two community bands have stopped taking junior players for want of tutors. She says the shortage is now audible in the bands. 'We are producing fewer tuba and bassoon players because we have fewer people to teach them, and we will have fewer teachers in ten years because we are producing fewer players. It is a loop, and none of us can break it on our own.' She asks members for practical proposals that schools can contribute to directly, recognising that recruitment and pay are matters for the department and individual employers. Several schools have also reported instruments in storage that sit unused because they have neither students nor teachers for them.", "before": [{"who": "Greg, head of music at a large metropolitan school", "said": "We have three bassoons in a cupboard and no one to teach them. I would happily lend them to a school that has a teacher, if such a school exists. I think a shared register of idle instruments would be a start. It is not glamorous, but it would put the instruments where the teaching is. We also have a euphonium and a contrabassoon that have sat idle for two years, and I suspect every school here could add to the list. If we knew what existed across the network, we might find we are less short of instruments than of a way to share them."}, {"who": "Tim, a low brass instrumental teacher working across three schools", "said": "I am one of the people you are short of. I could teach more students, but not in more places; I already spend much of my week driving between schools for twenty-minute lessons. If students came to one place for a group lesson once a week, I could teach far more of them, and they would have other low brass players to play with, which is half of what keeps a child on tuba."}], "notes": ["Network low-register academy: weekly after-school, low brass + double reeds, one central school; we can host first semester.", "Greg's register becomes the academy instrument bank.", "Supervised senior students and university music students as paid assistant tutors (future teachers).", "Pilot one semester; report enrolment and retention."], "task": "Propose a network-level initiative that breaks the supply loop for low brass and double reeds.", "seconds": 180, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Carol, for naming the loop so clearly; once you have heard it described that way, you cannot unhear it. Thank you, Greg, for the bassoons in the cupboard, and Tim, for the most useful thing anyone has said today: that you could teach far more students if they came to you, and that playing with other low brass players is half of what keeps a child on tuba.", "acknowledge": "This is the kind of problem that quietly empties the bottom of every band in the state over a decade, and by the time it is obvious it is very hard to reverse.", "standing": "I lead a large band program, I am a bass player myself, so the bottom of the ensemble is where I live, and I have spent years trying to staff exactly these instruments.", "common": "We all want our bands to have a bottom end in ten years' time, and we all know none of us can build it alone.", "frame": "Greg has given us the instruments and Tim has given us the method: concentrate the teaching instead of spreading it thin. That changes the question from how to find more teachers to how to let the teachers we have reach more students.", "proposal": "So I would propose a network low-register academy, in three parts. First, a weekly after-school academy for low brass and double reeds at one central school, taught by Tim and colleagues and open to students from every network school, with my school willing to host the first semester. Second, Greg's idea becomes the academy's instrument bank: a shared register of idle instruments, lent to academy students, so no bassoon sits in a cupboard while a student waits for one. Third, supervised senior students and university music students act as paid assistant tutors, which adds teaching capacity now and begins to train the next generation of teachers.", "close": "That gives Tim his students in one room, Greg's bassoons a player, and Carol's loop a break at both ends. If we cannot find more teachers, we can at least stop spending the ones we have on the freeway. I would suggest we pilot the academy for one semester from next term, with Carol, Tim and me to report on enrolment and retention at the network's last meeting of the year."}}, "why": "Each element of the academy is visibly built from someone else's contribution, which makes it the room's proposal with Dijon as its author. Offering to host gives it immediate credibility, and the closing image is concrete enough to be repeated."}, {"id": "long-b19", "room": "Principals' network meeting for government schools with specialist arts programs, hybrid: nine principals and three heads of music in person, five principals on Zoom", "briefing": "The network chair, a senior principal, introduces a departmental proposal to centralise auditions for all government specialist music programs: one application, one audition day for each instrument family, results shared across schools, and families ranking their preferences. She explains the rationale: families currently apply and audition at several schools, children are auditioned repeatedly in the same fortnight, and schools compete for the same applicants. She also names the risks: each program has its own character, and a common panel may not hear what a particular school is looking for. 'Nobody wants a system that serves the department's spreadsheet better than it serves the twelve-year-old with a clarinet. I would like this network to give the department a view, and ideally a design, before it designs one for us.' Dijon has been invited as one of the three heads of music.", "before": [{"who": "Malcolm, principal of a large specialist school, on Zoom", "said": "Thank you, Chair, for framing this so thoughtfully. Having led schools in three regions over a long career, I would say this is precisely the kind of system question that deserves our most careful reflection. There is real merit in reducing the burden on families, and I am deeply sympathetic to that. At the same time, our programs are distinct communities with distinct cultures. I would urge us to approach the department in a spirit of partnership, to be open-minded, and to make sure the voices of our music staff are heard as the model is developed."}, {"who": "Natalie, principal of a smaller specialist school, in the room", "said": "I will be blunt. Under the current system we get the students the larger schools do not take, often after families have already accepted a place somewhere else. A central system with preferences might actually be fairer to us, provided the panels are not dominated by the larger schools' staff. I am in favour, but only if the panel composition is genuinely shared. Smaller programs need to know they will be heard by someone who is listening for them."}], "notes": ["Support one application, one audition per child; results AND brief feedback to every family.", "Panels from at least three schools, rotating, include instrumental service teachers; no school assesses alone (Natalie).", "Each school may add a short second-round element (ensemble workshop or conversation) for shortlisted students who preference it (Malcolm's 'character').", "Pilot with one instrument family (strings?) in year one."], "task": "Give the network a concrete design for a centralised audition model that protects each program's character and is fair to smaller schools.", "seconds": 240, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Chair, for asking us for a design before one is designed for us, and for the twelve-year-old with a clarinet, who I think should be the test of everything we say. Thank you, Malcolm, for reminding us that our programs are communities, and Natalie, for being candid about what the current system costs a smaller school.", "acknowledge": "This would change something every one of our schools has done its own way for a long time, and some of what makes each program distinctive lives in exactly that process.", "standing": "I have run auditions for a large specialist program since 2021, and before that I prepared students for several auditions in the one fortnight, so I have seen what the current system asks of a child.", "common": "We all want each student auditioned once, fairly, by people who can hear what they are capable of, and each program to keep what makes it itself.", "frame": "Malcolm's concern and Natalie's concern point to the same design problem: who sits on the panel, and what the panel can hear. If we get panel composition and a school-specific element right, a central model serves both families and programs better than what we have now.", "proposal": "I would propose a shared audition model with four features. First, one application and one audition per child, as the department suggests, with results and brief feedback given to every family. Second, panels drawn from at least three schools on a rotating basis, including instrumental service teachers, so that no school assesses alone and Natalie's concern about larger schools dominating is answered in the structure. Third, each school may add a short second-round element, an ensemble workshop or a conversation, for shortlisted students who preference it, which protects the character Malcolm rightly values; and fourth, we pilot the model with one instrument family, perhaps strings, in the first year before it applies to all.", "close": "That gives families one audition instead of four, smaller schools a fair hearing, and every program a voice in choosing its own students. The best audition system is one a nervous twelve-year-old only has to survive once. I would ask that the network's response to the department record the shared audition model as our preferred design, and I am glad to draft it with the other two heads of music for the chair by the end of the month."}}, "why": "Malcolm spoke for some time and proposed nothing; Dijon gives his concern a concrete mechanism, the second-round element, and so wins his support without contradicting him. The chair's twelve-year-old becomes the closing image, which ties the design back to the room's own moral test."}, {"id": "long-b20", "room": "State music industry peak body, consultation forum on fees for musicians at publicly funded events, hybrid: twelve participants in person and about thirty on Zoom", "briefing": "The peak body's chief executive, Tanya, presents a draft recommended minimum fee schedule for musicians engaged at events that receive public funding, from festivals and local government concerts to subsidised venue programs. She explains that the schedule would not be legally binding, but that the state's arts funding agency has indicated it may require funded presenters to pay at or above the recommended rates. She names the tension openly: musicians' fees have barely moved in many years, while small presenters, regional festivals and volunteer-run organisations say they are already stretched. 'If we set the rate too low, it is a ceiling dressed as a floor. If we set it too high, we will have fewer gigs paid properly and more paid nothing.' She asks for practical advice on the schedule's structure, on its application to small and volunteer presenters, and on implementation. The draft goes to the board next month.", "before": [{"who": "Marco, venue owner and presenter, on Zoom", "said": "I want musicians paid properly; I am a musician myself. But a hard minimum per player kills the larger ensemble. If the rate is per musician, a presenter will book a trio instead of a septet every time, and the big band and the large ensemble will disappear from small rooms altogether. Whatever we do, it cannot make it rational to hire fewer musicians. I have watched it happen in my own room when budgets tightened."}, {"who": "Jill, director of a volunteer-run regional festival, in the room", "said": "We are volunteer-run, and our budget depends on a council grant and ticket sales. If the funding agency makes the schedule a condition of funding without increasing the grant, we will comply by programming fewer acts, and local musicians will lose the gigs that exist now. I would support the schedule if the funding to meet it travels with it. Otherwise we will have honoured the principle on paper and lost the gigs in practice."}], "notes": ["Tier by event scale and funding level, not one flat rate.", "Ensemble scale: per-player rate tapers above a set size, with a leader's loading (Marco's septet).", "Condition of funding only where the grant has a ring-fenced musicians' fee line (Jill).", "Phase in over two years; annual review; publish compliance by funded events."], "task": "Advise the peak body on a fee schedule structure that raises musicians' pay without reducing the number of paid gigs.", "seconds": 210, "model": {"slots": {"thanks": "Thank you, Tanya, for the line I suspect will outlive the schedule itself: a rate set too low is a ceiling dressed as a floor. Thank you, Marco, for naming the perverse incentive no one else had, that a flat per-player rate makes the septet irrational, and Jill, for insisting that a condition of funding should arrive with the funding to meet it.", "acknowledge": "Musicians in this state have subsidised the arts with their fees for a long time, and a schedule that fixes that for some by removing gigs for others would be a hollow reform.", "standing": "I have earned part of my living from gigs in this city for twenty-six years, often in large ensembles, and as treasurer of a volunteer-run festival association I have also been the person who signs the cheque, so I see both sides of every rate on this page.", "common": "We all want musicians paid properly, and we all want there to be at least as many paid gigs next year as there are now.", "frame": "Marco and Jill have identified the two ways a good schedule could fail: by making large ensembles uneconomic, and by turning an unfunded condition into fewer bookings. So the design question is how to raise the floor without changing what presenters rationally choose to program.", "proposal": "I would propose that the draft be restructured on three principles. First, tiering by event scale and funding level, so that a major funded festival and a volunteer-run regional event are not held to the same rate. Second, an ensemble scale, in which the per-player rate tapers above a certain size with a loading for the leader, which answers Marco directly and keeps the big band in the small room. Third, the funding agency applies the schedule as a condition only where the grant includes a ring-fenced line for musicians' fees, as Jill proposes, phased in over two years, with the schedule reviewed annually and compliance by funded events published.", "close": "That raises the floor, keeps the septet viable, and makes sure the obligation and the money arrive together. The purpose of a fee schedule is not to make each gig dearer; it is to make each gig fair, without making any of them disappear. I would ask that the forum's advice to the board record the three principles, and I am glad to help Tanya's team model the ensemble scale before the board meets."}}, "why": "Speaking as both working musician and festival treasurer gives him standing with every faction on the call. He builds the restructure from the two failure modes others identified, so the room hears its own concerns resolved, and the close commits him to the modelling work."}], "longPhrases": [{"id": "thanks-1", "slot": "thanks", "text": "Thank you, [name], for setting out the Board's reasons so clearly, and in the order they were weighed.", "note": "When the briefing was well structured."}, {"id": "thanks-2", "slot": "thanks", "text": "Can I thank [name] and [name] for the candour of that briefing. It cannot have been easy to deliver."}, {"id": "thanks-3", "slot": "thanks", "text": "My thanks to [name] for taking us through the decision point by point, without softening it."}, {"id": "thanks-4", "slot": "thanks", "text": "I am grateful to [name] for the detail on [the assessment concern]; it gives us something precise to work with.", "note": "Sets up your proposal on that concern."}, {"id": "thanks-5", "slot": "thanks", "text": "Thank you, Chair, for making room for this discussion today rather than deferring it."}, {"id": "thanks-6", "slot": "thanks", "text": "Before anything else, thank you to the three of you for coming to us directly rather than sending the decision in writing."}, {"id": "thanks-7", "slot": "thanks", "text": "I want to thank [name] in particular for the point about [the Board's concern]. It is the one I want to build on.", "note": "Bridges straight into build or proposal."}, {"id": "thanks-8", "slot": "thanks", "text": "Thank you, [name]. The distinction you drew between [the content] and [the assessment design] was especially useful."}, {"id": "thanks-9", "slot": "thanks", "text": "Can I record my appreciation for two years of work by the writing team, and for [name]'s leadership of it."}, {"id": "thanks-10", "slot": "thanks", "text": "Thank you to [name] for the framing. I think it is the right one, and I will try to stay inside it."}, {"id": "thanks-11", "slot": "thanks", "text": "I am grateful, Chair, for the analogy you offered; it put the decision in proportion.", "note": "When the chair used analogy or humour."}, {"id": "thanks-12", "slot": "thanks", "text": "Thank you, [name], for answering our questions so fully. It has saved us a good deal of speculation."}, {"id": "thanks-13", "slot": "thanks", "text": "Let me begin by thanking [name] for the clarity of the timeline. Knowing [the date] changes what is possible."}, {"id": "thanks-14", "slot": "thanks", "text": "Thank you to colleagues online as well; [name]'s point about [topic] has stayed with me.", "note": "Includes the Zoom members explicitly."}, {"id": "thanks-15", "slot": "thanks", "text": "My sincere thanks to [name] for the care with which that news was delivered."}, {"id": "thanks-16", "slot": "thanks", "text": "Thank you, [name], for distinguishing what the Board rejected from what it did not. That matters for what follows."}, {"id": "thanks-17", "slot": "thanks", "text": "I appreciate [name] naming [the Board's concern] so specifically. Specific concerns can be answered."}, {"id": "thanks-18", "slot": "thanks", "text": "Thank you for the opportunity to respond, Chair. I will be brief and practical.", "note": "Signals a short, useful contribution."}, {"id": "thanks-19", "slot": "thanks", "text": "Can I thank [name] for the generosity of that contribution, and for the reference to [jurisdiction]."}, {"id": "thanks-20", "slot": "thanks", "text": "Thank you, all three of you. You have given us the facts; I would like to spend my time on the remedy."}, {"id": "thanks-21", "slot": "thanks", "text": "I am grateful to [name] for keeping students at the centre of that account."}, {"id": "thanks-22", "slot": "thanks", "text": "Thank you, [name], for the papers circulated beforehand; the annotation on [page] has shaped my notes."}, {"id": "thanks-23", "slot": "thanks", "text": "A word of thanks to [name], whose question earlier drew out the detail that I think unlocks this."}, {"id": "thanks-24", "slot": "thanks", "text": "Thank you, [name]. You said [phrase], and I wrote it down because it is exactly right.", "note": "Quoting someone back shows you listened."}, {"id": "thanks-25", "slot": "thanks", "text": "Let me add my thanks to those already expressed, and single out [name]'s work on [section]."}, {"id": "thanks-26", "slot": "thanks", "text": "Thank you, Chair, and thank you to [name] for an account that was both honest and constructive."}, {"id": "thanks-27", "slot": "thanks", "text": "I should thank the secretariat, too, for turning the Board's feedback around so quickly."}, {"id": "thanks-28", "slot": "thanks", "text": "Thank you, [name], for your patience with our questions. They come from how much the committee cares about this course."}, {"id": "thanks-29", "slot": "thanks", "text": "My thanks to [name] for reminding us where the course began; the original rationale is still sound."}, {"id": "thanks-30", "slot": "thanks", "text": "Thank you all. I have listened carefully, and I would like to offer something concrete.", "note": "When speaking late, after many others."}, {"id": "acknowledge-1", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "This is a serious setback, and it deserves to be named as one."}, {"id": "acknowledge-2", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "Two years of work has not landed where we hoped. That is disappointing, and I do not think we should pretend otherwise."}, {"id": "acknowledge-3", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "I imagine everyone in the room, and on the screen, felt the weight of that news.", "note": "Hybrid meetings: includes both audiences."}, {"id": "acknowledge-4", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "It is a hard message to hear, and a harder one to bring."}, {"id": "acknowledge-5", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "We should allow ourselves a moment to register this before we move to solutions."}, {"id": "acknowledge-6", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "This is not the outcome any of us worked toward, and it would be odd to treat it lightly."}, {"id": "acknowledge-7", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "Let me say plainly: this is a significant decision, and it has consequences for teachers and students."}, {"id": "acknowledge-8", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "The disappointment is real, and so is the obligation to respond well."}, {"id": "acknowledge-9", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "I do not want to rush past what this means for the writers who gave their time to it."}, {"id": "acknowledge-10", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "A rejection at Board level is not a minor revision. It asks something more of us."}, {"id": "acknowledge-11", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "This changes our timeline, and probably our approach.", "note": "Brisk; suits a practical speaker."}, {"id": "acknowledge-12", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "I suspect the hardest part is that much of the course was good, and the decision does not say otherwise."}, {"id": "acknowledge-13", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "It is a moment that calls for steadiness rather than haste."}, {"id": "acknowledge-14", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "We are where we are, and the question now is what we do with it.", "note": "Pivot line into common ground."}, {"id": "acknowledge-15", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "There are schools waiting on this course, and they will feel the delay."}, {"id": "acknowledge-16", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "This is the kind of news that tests a committee. I think this one can meet it."}, {"id": "acknowledge-17", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "None of us would have chosen this, but the decision is clear, and clarity is something to work with."}, {"id": "acknowledge-18", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "I share the disappointment around the table, and on the screen."}, {"id": "acknowledge-19", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "It would be easy to be defensive about this. I would rather be useful."}, {"id": "acknowledge-20", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "The decision has been made, and our task is to answer it, not to relitigate it.", "note": "Steers the room away from grievance."}, {"id": "acknowledge-21", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "This is a setback for the course, not a verdict on the people who wrote it."}, {"id": "acknowledge-22", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "I want to acknowledge the position the three of you are in, carrying a decision you did not make.", "note": "Generous to the presenters; earns goodwill."}, {"id": "acknowledge-23", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "This lands at a difficult point in the cycle, with [implementation date] not far off."}, {"id": "acknowledge-24", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "Let us be honest that this is a loss of time, and then see what can be recovered."}, {"id": "acknowledge-25", "slot": "acknowledge", "text": "It is a sobering moment, and I think the tone of the briefing matched it."}, {"id": "standing-1", "slot": "standing", "text": "I come to this as Teacher in Charge of Music at a large specialist music school, where we will be among the first to deliver whatever this course becomes."}, {"id": "standing-2", "slot": "standing", "text": "I have taught senior music since 2005, and I have seen syllabus changes land in classrooms, well and badly.", "note": "Experience without boasting."}, {"id": "standing-3", "slot": "standing", "text": "I lead a specialist music program, so my lens is the practical one: what a teacher does with this on a Monday morning."}, {"id": "standing-4", "slot": "standing", "text": "My perspective is from the delivery end. I run a department that will implement this, and I chair a school council that will ask about it."}, {"id": "standing-5", "slot": "standing", "text": "I have performed professionally for twenty-six years, and I think that bears on how we describe performance assessment.", "note": "When performance or authenticity is in question."}, {"id": "standing-6", "slot": "standing", "text": "As someone who has taught since 2005, I have a fairly clear sense of what teachers will find workable."}, {"id": "standing-7", "slot": "standing", "text": "I sit on this committee as a practitioner, and I will speak as one."}, {"id": "standing-8", "slot": "standing", "text": "I chair a school council, so I hear how parents read decisions like this one.", "note": "When community or parent reaction matters."}, {"id": "standing-9", "slot": "standing", "text": "Through the association that runs the state's schools' jazz festival, I see a great many programs across WA, and the variation is instructive.", "note": "Breadth across sectors and regions."}, {"id": "standing-10", "slot": "standing", "text": "Speaking from the classroom, and from a program that auditions its students, I can tell you how this is likely to be received."}, {"id": "standing-11", "slot": "standing", "text": "My contribution is a practical one. I've implemented curriculum change in a department, and I've learned where it breaks."}, {"id": "standing-12", "slot": "standing", "text": "I should say where I am coming from: a working musician first, a teacher for twenty years, and now leading a department."}, {"id": "standing-13", "slot": "standing", "text": "I bring the view of someone who will write the assessment programs this syllabus generates."}, {"id": "standing-14", "slot": "standing", "text": "I have been on the receiving end of syllabus change as a classroom teacher and as the person leading a department, which may be useful here."}, {"id": "standing-15", "slot": "standing", "text": "In my own school I lead a team of specialist teachers, and they will be asking me what this means within the week."}, {"id": "standing-16", "slot": "standing", "text": "I offer this as a practitioner rather than a policy specialist, and I defer to colleagues on the policy.", "note": "Disarming beside academics; still claims ground."}, {"id": "standing-17", "slot": "standing", "text": "Through the festival I work with directors from government, Catholic and independent schools, and their concerns are fairly consistent."}, {"id": "standing-18", "slot": "standing", "text": "Having taught in both the Catholic and government sectors, I have seen how differently the same syllabus can play out."}, {"id": "standing-19", "slot": "standing", "text": "I have performed in this state for more than twenty-five years, so I am conscious of what the profession expects of graduates."}, {"id": "standing-20", "slot": "standing", "text": "My vantage point is the specialist program, where the course has to stretch the strongest students without losing the rest."}, {"id": "standing-21", "slot": "standing", "text": "I am one of the people who will stand in front of the students, so I will keep this practical.", "note": "Short; use when time is tight."}, {"id": "standing-22", "slot": "standing", "text": "I have been part of the drafting, so I share responsibility for where we are, and for fixing it."}, {"id": "standing-23", "slot": "standing", "text": "From a school council chair's perspective, the governance questions here are as important as the curriculum ones."}, {"id": "standing-24", "slot": "standing", "text": "Coming from a department that runs ensembles at every level, I can speak to the performance component in particular."}, {"id": "standing-25", "slot": "standing", "text": "Briefly, by way of context: I have led a large specialist music department since 2021, and I have some notes on what will work."}, {"id": "common-1", "slot": "common", "text": "I think we all want the same thing here: a course that serves students well and that teachers can deliver with confidence."}, {"id": "common-2", "slot": "common", "text": "Whatever our views on the detail, no one at this table wants students to wait longer than they must."}, {"id": "common-3", "slot": "common", "text": "We share a commitment to a course that is rigorous, fair and recognisably about music."}, {"id": "common-4", "slot": "common", "text": "I take it as common ground that the Board's concerns are legitimate, and that we want to answer them properly.", "note": "Concedes the Board's legitimacy; disarms resistance."}, {"id": "common-5", "slot": "common", "text": "Every one of us wants this course approved, and approved for the right reasons."}, {"id": "common-6", "slot": "common", "text": "The purpose has not changed: the best possible outcomes for students in this subject."}, {"id": "common-7", "slot": "common", "text": "We all want an assessment model that is defensible, reliable and worth teaching to."}, {"id": "common-8", "slot": "common", "text": "I suspect we agree on more than the decision makes it look."}, {"id": "common-9", "slot": "common", "text": "Our shared interest is in a course that holds its standing alongside the other ATAR subjects."}, {"id": "common-10", "slot": "common", "text": "None of us wants to trade the integrity of the discipline for approval, and none of us wants to lose approval for want of flexibility.", "note": "Names both fears at once."}, {"id": "common-11", "slot": "common", "text": "We want a course that works in a metropolitan specialist school and in a regional school with one music teacher."}, {"id": "common-12", "slot": "common", "text": "I think the whole committee would agree that the core of the course is worth protecting."}, {"id": "common-13", "slot": "common", "text": "We are all trying to get to the same place; the question is only the route."}, {"id": "common-14", "slot": "common", "text": "Let me start from what I take to be uncontroversial: teachers need certainty, and students need continuity."}, {"id": "common-15", "slot": "common", "text": "We share the Board's interest in comparability and the profession's interest in authenticity. Those are not in conflict.", "note": "Reconciles two camps before proposing."}, {"id": "common-16", "slot": "common", "text": "Whatever happens next, we want the work of the last two years to count."}, {"id": "common-17", "slot": "common", "text": "I think everyone here would sign up to a course the Board can approve and teachers will be proud of."}, {"id": "common-18", "slot": "common", "text": "Our common purpose is to give students a qualification that means something beyond school."}, {"id": "common-19", "slot": "common", "text": "We agree, I think, that the solution should come from this committee rather than be imposed on it."}, {"id": "common-20", "slot": "common", "text": "The goal we share is simple to state: an approved course, on time, without loss of quality."}, {"id": "common-21", "slot": "common", "text": "Nobody here is arguing for less rigour. We are arguing for the right kind."}, {"id": "common-22", "slot": "common", "text": "Students, teachers and the Authority all benefit from the same outcome here."}, {"id": "common-23", "slot": "common", "text": "I take it we all want to return to the Board with something it can say yes to.", "note": "Leads naturally into the proposal."}, {"id": "common-24", "slot": "common", "text": "We have a shared obligation to the schools that have been preparing for this course."}, {"id": "common-25", "slot": "common", "text": "What unites us is larger than what the Board found wanting."}, {"id": "frame-1", "slot": "frame", "text": "As I understand it, the question is not whether the course is sound, but whether [the assessment model] is defensible at Board level."}, {"id": "frame-2", "slot": "frame", "text": "The way I see it, the Board has raised one substantive concern and two presentational ones, and they need different responses.", "note": "Sorting the feedback makes you the analyst."}, {"id": "frame-3", "slot": "frame", "text": "If I can reframe slightly: this is a question of [comparability], not of [content]."}, {"id": "frame-4", "slot": "frame", "text": "It may help to look at how other jurisdictions have handled this. [State] faced a very similar question with [course].", "note": "Only cite a state you can name accurately."}, {"id": "frame-5", "slot": "frame", "text": "In [jurisdiction], the approach has been to [approach], and it has withstood scrutiny of exactly this kind."}, {"id": "frame-6", "slot": "frame", "text": "The research on [topic] is fairly consistent on one point: [finding]. That seems directly relevant.", "note": "Generic research opener; keep the finding modest."}, {"id": "frame-7", "slot": "frame", "text": "There is a substantial body of work on moderating practical assessment, and it points the same way."}, {"id": "frame-8", "slot": "frame", "text": "Other states have separated [the performance component] from [the written component] for precisely this reason."}, {"id": "frame-9", "slot": "frame", "text": "Looking at comparable ATAR courses here in WA, [subject] resolved a similar tension by [approach]."}, {"id": "frame-10", "slot": "frame", "text": "If we set the Board's concern alongside how [subject] is assessed, the gap is narrower than it first appears."}, {"id": "frame-11", "slot": "frame", "text": "The underlying question, I think, is how much of the course must be externally verifiable."}, {"id": "frame-12", "slot": "frame", "text": "Let me put it as a choice. We can revise the structure, or revise the evidence we present for it. The second is quicker; the first is more durable.", "note": "Frames options so your proposal looks inevitable."}, {"id": "frame-13", "slot": "frame", "text": "I would frame it like this: the Board wants confidence, and confidence comes from [evidence]."}, {"id": "frame-14", "slot": "frame", "text": "It is worth separating what the Board said from what it implied."}, {"id": "frame-15", "slot": "frame", "text": "The issue, as I read the feedback, sits in [section], and it is narrower than the overall decision suggests."}, {"id": "frame-16", "slot": "frame", "text": "Practice in schools suggests [observation], and that cuts in favour of [option]."}, {"id": "frame-17", "slot": "frame", "text": "In my experience of implementing change, the sticking point is rarely the content; it is the workload attached to assessment."}, {"id": "frame-18", "slot": "frame", "text": "There may be precedent within the Authority's own suite: [course] went back once and was approved with [change]."}, {"id": "frame-19", "slot": "frame", "text": "Internationally, [system] handles this through [approach], which may be worth adapting."}, {"id": "frame-20", "slot": "frame", "text": "If we take the Board's concern at its strongest, it is this: [concern]. I think that can be answered.", "note": "Steelmanning first makes the answer credible."}, {"id": "frame-21", "slot": "frame", "text": "The evidence from the pilot schools, as I understand it, points to [finding]."}, {"id": "frame-22", "slot": "frame", "text": "We might think of it the way [analogy].", "note": "Analogy travels; the room remembers it."}, {"id": "frame-23", "slot": "frame", "text": "The real question is sequencing: what must change before resubmission, and what can follow in implementation."}, {"id": "frame-24", "slot": "frame", "text": "Across the national picture, WA is something of an outlier on [feature], and the Board may well have noticed."}, {"id": "frame-25", "slot": "frame", "text": "The literature on teacher-assessed components tends to stress exemplars and moderation rather than format alone."}, {"id": "frame-26", "slot": "frame", "text": "Put simply, the Board has asked a technical question, and it deserves a technical answer."}, {"id": "frame-27", "slot": "frame", "text": "There are, as I see it, three ways to read the decision, and only one of them requires us to start again.", "note": "Reassures, then narrows."}, {"id": "frame-28", "slot": "frame", "text": "Several jurisdictions now use [approach], with [benefit] reported. I would want us to verify that before relying on it.", "note": "Shows rigour about your own evidence."}, {"id": "frame-29", "slot": "frame", "text": "The test I would apply is simple: could a teacher in a regional school deliver this as written?"}, {"id": "frame-30", "slot": "frame", "text": "What the Board approved last cycle in [learning area] tells us something about what it will accept."}, {"id": "proposal-1", "slot": "proposal", "text": "I have three proposals, and I will keep each to a sentence.", "note": "Announcing the count holds the room."}, {"id": "proposal-2", "slot": "proposal", "text": "Let me offer a practical way forward, in two parts."}, {"id": "proposal-3", "slot": "proposal", "text": "First, [action]. Second, [action]. Third, [action]."}, {"id": "proposal-4", "slot": "proposal", "text": "My first proposal I would call the [name] option: [summary].", "note": "A named option gets repeated and minuted."}, {"id": "proposal-5", "slot": "proposal", "text": "Building on what [name] said about [point], I would propose that we [action]."}, {"id": "proposal-6", "slot": "proposal", "text": "Concretely, I suggest a small working group of [number] members, reporting back by [date]."}, {"id": "proposal-7", "slot": "proposal", "text": "I propose we answer [the Board's concern] directly, by [change], and leave the rest of the course intact."}, {"id": "proposal-8", "slot": "proposal", "text": "If it helps the minutes, the wording might be: 'That the Committee [action] and report to the Board by [date].'", "note": "Offer wording; the minutes often keep it."}, {"id": "proposal-9", "slot": "proposal", "text": "May I suggest a form of words for the record: 'That the Committee [action], with [owner] to coordinate.'"}, {"id": "proposal-10", "slot": "proposal", "text": "So the proposal is narrow and specific: revise [section], retain [section], resubmit by [date]."}, {"id": "proposal-11", "slot": "proposal", "text": "This picks up [name]'s point about [jurisdiction]. We could adapt that model rather than invent our own."}, {"id": "proposal-12", "slot": "proposal", "text": "The first step costs very little: [action]. It tells us quickly whether the larger change is needed."}, {"id": "proposal-13", "slot": "proposal", "text": "I would put it as a two-stage response. Before resubmission, [action]. During implementation, [action]."}, {"id": "proposal-14", "slot": "proposal", "text": "I am happy to draft the revised [section] myself and circulate it by [date].", "note": "Owning the work makes it real."}, {"id": "proposal-15", "slot": "proposal", "text": "My suggestion, which I think is consistent with what [name] and [name] have both said, is [action]."}, {"id": "proposal-16", "slot": "proposal", "text": "There is a simple fix to [the concern], and it is this: [fix]."}, {"id": "proposal-17", "slot": "proposal", "text": "Point one is structural, point two concerns evidence, point three is about communication with schools."}, {"id": "proposal-18", "slot": "proposal", "text": "Let me give it a name so we can refer to it: the [term] approach."}, {"id": "proposal-19", "slot": "proposal", "text": "I would propose, as a motion if the Chair is willing, that [motion].", "note": "Formalises the idea; forces a decision."}, {"id": "proposal-20", "slot": "proposal", "text": "In practical terms, that means [action] by [owner] before [date]."}, {"id": "proposal-21", "slot": "proposal", "text": "We could take [name]'s principle and give it a mechanism: [mechanism].", "note": "Lets a dominant speaker's idea carry yours."}, {"id": "proposal-22", "slot": "proposal", "text": "The proposal has one merit above the others: it can be done within the current timeline."}, {"id": "proposal-23", "slot": "proposal", "text": "Rather than revisiting the whole course, I suggest we isolate [component] and rebuild only that."}, {"id": "proposal-24", "slot": "proposal", "text": "To give that some shape: a revised [component], a short evidence paper, and a covering note to the Board."}, {"id": "proposal-25", "slot": "proposal", "text": "I would add one practical element to what [name] has proposed: [element]."}, {"id": "proposal-26", "slot": "proposal", "text": "Can I suggest we ask the secretariat to [action], so the working group starts with the right material?"}, {"id": "proposal-27", "slot": "proposal", "text": "Second, and this is the more important point, [proposal].", "note": "Signals where weight falls."}, {"id": "proposal-28", "slot": "proposal", "text": "I have draft wording for [section] which I am happy to table now, or circulate after the meeting."}, {"id": "proposal-29", "slot": "proposal", "text": "If the committee agrees, I would like the minutes to show that [action] was proposed, and who will carry it."}, {"id": "proposal-30", "slot": "proposal", "text": "My proposal is modest: [action]. I think modest is what the timeline allows."}, {"id": "proposal-31", "slot": "proposal", "text": "So, three things. One, [action]. Two, [action]. Three, [action]. Each has an owner and a date."}, {"id": "proposal-32", "slot": "proposal", "text": "What I am offering is a way to meet the Board's concern without losing what the writers built."}, {"id": "proposal-33", "slot": "proposal", "text": "To connect this with [name]'s comment about [point]: if we do [action], that concern is answered as well."}, {"id": "proposal-34", "slot": "proposal", "text": "I would suggest a test case: apply the revised [component] to one unit and bring it back next meeting.", "note": "Low-risk pilot; easy for a cautious room to accept."}, {"id": "proposal-35", "slot": "proposal", "text": "Let me be specific about who might do what: [name] on [task], the secretariat on [task], and I will take [task]."}, {"id": "close-1", "slot": "close", "text": "So, to draw it together: the Board's concern is specific, it can be met, and we have a way to meet it.", "note": "Three-beat summary, third-speaker style."}, {"id": "close-2", "slot": "close", "text": "In short: keep what works, fix what was questioned, and go back with evidence."}, {"id": "close-3", "slot": "close", "text": "The case, then, is simple. [Point one], [point two], [point three]."}, {"id": "close-4", "slot": "close", "text": "What we need now is not another review but a decision about the next step."}, {"id": "close-5", "slot": "close", "text": "If we leave with one thing, I hope it is [action], with [owner] by [date]."}, {"id": "close-6", "slot": "close", "text": "So I would ask the committee to endorse [proposal] today, so the work can start this week.", "note": "Direct call to action."}, {"id": "close-7", "slot": "close", "text": "The question before us is not whether to respond, but how quickly and how well. I think we can do both."}, {"id": "close-8", "slot": "close", "text": "Two years of work deserves a considered answer, and I believe this is one."}, {"id": "close-9", "slot": "close", "text": "That is the substance of it. The detail I am happy to put in writing."}, {"id": "close-10", "slot": "close", "text": "We were asked a fair question. Let us give a precise answer."}, {"id": "close-11", "slot": "close", "text": "To summarise: three proposals, each modest, each owned, each achievable before [date]."}, {"id": "close-12", "slot": "close", "text": "I would put it to the committee that this is the most efficient route to approval."}, {"id": "close-13", "slot": "close", "text": "The Board has told us what it needs. We are well placed to give it."}, {"id": "close-14", "slot": "close", "text": "Let me end where [name] began: with the students. They are the reason to get this right, and to get it done.", "note": "Callback to an earlier speaker; memorable."}, {"id": "close-15", "slot": "close", "text": "For the minutes, then: [wording].", "note": "Leave the secretary your exact words."}, {"id": "close-16", "slot": "close", "text": "Other speakers have described the problem very well. What I have tried to offer is the remedy.", "note": "Positions you against the eloquent but empty."}, {"id": "close-17", "slot": "close", "text": "If there is support for this, I am happy to take the first action."}, {"id": "close-18", "slot": "close", "text": "So the choice is between starting again and finishing well. I would urge the second."}, {"id": "close-19", "slot": "close", "text": "Nothing in the Board's decision prevents us from succeeding at the next submission. That should give us some confidence."}, {"id": "close-20", "slot": "close", "text": "I will stop there, Chair, and leave the proposal with the committee."}, {"id": "close-21", "slot": "close", "text": "That, I think, answers the Board's concern, honours the work done, and keeps faith with the schools."}, {"id": "close-22", "slot": "close", "text": "My recommendation, in a sentence: [recommendation]."}, {"id": "close-23", "slot": "close", "text": "Could I ask that we return to this item at the next meeting with a draft in hand?"}, {"id": "close-24", "slot": "close", "text": "We have heard the problem from three angles today. I hope we can agree on one solution."}, {"id": "close-25", "slot": "close", "text": "The detail can be refined. What matters today is that we agree the direction."}, {"id": "close-26", "slot": "close", "text": "So: the concern, the evidence, the fix, and the owner. I think that is everything the Board will ask.", "note": "Recaps your own scaffold in one line."}, {"id": "close-27", "slot": "close", "text": "If we do this, the course goes back stronger than it went in."}, {"id": "close-28", "slot": "close", "text": "I would be glad to hear whether colleagues see any obstacle to that, and if not, to proceed."}, {"id": "close-29", "slot": "close", "text": "That is my proposal. Thank you, Chair.", "note": "Clean stop; resist adding more."}, {"id": "close-30", "slot": "close", "text": "In the end, the Board's question was about confidence. This gives it reason to have some."}, {"id": "hold-1", "slot": "hold", "text": "Let me put that carefully."}, {"id": "hold-2", "slot": "hold", "text": "There are two ways to answer that, and I think the second is more useful."}, {"id": "hold-3", "slot": "hold", "text": "That is a fair question, and it deserves a considered answer.", "note": "Buys a few seconds honestly."}, {"id": "hold-4", "slot": "hold", "text": "If I can come at that from a slightly different angle."}, {"id": "hold-5", "slot": "hold", "text": "Let me take a step back for a moment."}, {"id": "hold-6", "slot": "hold", "text": "I want to be precise about this."}, {"id": "hold-7", "slot": "hold", "text": "The short answer is yes. The longer answer is more interesting.", "note": "Commits first, then gives time to build."}, {"id": "hold-8", "slot": "hold", "text": "It depends, I think, on what we mean by [term]."}, {"id": "hold-9", "slot": "hold", "text": "Let me separate two things that are easily run together."}, {"id": "hold-10", "slot": "hold", "text": "That goes to the heart of it."}, {"id": "hold-11", "slot": "hold", "text": "I will come to that, but first one point of context."}, {"id": "hold-12", "slot": "hold", "text": "There is a distinction worth drawing here."}, {"id": "hold-13", "slot": "hold", "text": "To put it another way."}, {"id": "hold-14", "slot": "hold", "text": "I think the honest answer is this."}, {"id": "hold-15", "slot": "hold", "text": "Let me answer the question as asked, and then the one behind it."}, {"id": "hold-16", "slot": "hold", "text": "That is the right question, and I have been turning it over."}, {"id": "hold-17", "slot": "hold", "text": "Let me take those in order.", "note": "When asked several things at once."}, {"id": "hold-18", "slot": "hold", "text": "Before I answer, may I check I have understood: you are asking whether [restatement]?", "note": "Clarifying is also thinking time."}, {"id": "hold-19", "slot": "hold", "text": "I want to resist the obvious answer for a moment."}, {"id": "hold-20", "slot": "hold", "text": "The way I would approach that is from the practical end."}, {"id": "hold-21", "slot": "hold", "text": "That is not a question with a single answer, but there is a best one."}, {"id": "hold-22", "slot": "hold", "text": "Let me give that the weight it deserves."}, {"id": "hold-23", "slot": "hold", "text": "Bear with me; there is a reason I am starting here.", "note": "When a tangent needs to look intended."}, {"id": "hold-24", "slot": "hold", "text": "The question, really, is one of degree."}, {"id": "hold-25", "slot": "hold", "text": "I take the point. Let me test it against one example."}, {"id": "hold-26", "slot": "hold", "text": "That is where I would want to be careful."}, {"id": "hold-27", "slot": "hold", "text": "If I can draw a thread through what has been said."}, {"id": "hold-28", "slot": "hold", "text": "Let me pause on that, because it matters."}, {"id": "hold-29", "slot": "hold", "text": "There is a simple version of this and a complete one. I will give the simple one."}, {"id": "hold-30", "slot": "hold", "text": "Let me return to the question.", "note": "Recovers from a drift without apology."}, {"id": "floor-1", "slot": "floor", "text": "Chair, may I come in on that point?", "note": "In the room, with eye contact to the chair."}, {"id": "floor-2", "slot": "floor", "text": "Chair, I would be grateful for a moment when you have one."}, {"id": "floor-3", "slot": "floor", "text": "Through the Chair, if I may, a brief response to [name]."}, {"id": "floor-4", "slot": "floor", "text": "Chair, could I be added to the speaking list? I have something practical to offer."}, {"id": "floor-5", "slot": "floor", "text": "Forgive me, [name], may I come in on exactly that point?", "note": "Dominant speaker; wait for a full stop."}, {"id": "floor-6", "slot": "floor", "text": "[name], before you move on, may I pick up that last point?"}, {"id": "floor-7", "slot": "floor", "text": "Chair, my hand has been up online for a little while; may I come in after [name]?", "note": "Zoom, when overlooked."}, {"id": "floor-8", "slot": "floor", "text": "I will put a note in the chat as well, Chair, but I would like to speak to this.", "note": "Zoom; the chat is a queue marker."}, {"id": "floor-9", "slot": "floor", "text": "Chair, could we hear from members who have not yet spoken before we close the item?", "note": "Redistributes the floor without naming anyone."}, {"id": "floor-10", "slot": "floor", "text": "Chair, I am conscious of time. May I make one practical proposal before we move on?"}, {"id": "floor-11", "slot": "floor", "text": "If I may, Chair, a point that goes directly to the decision before us."}, {"id": "floor-12", "slot": "floor", "text": "Chair, it may help to hear from someone who will deliver the course. May I?"}, {"id": "floor-13", "slot": "floor", "text": "Can I come in briefly, [name]? I think it will help your point.", "note": "Dominant speaker; framed as support."}, {"id": "floor-14", "slot": "floor", "text": "Chair, I am unmuting with a short contribution, if that suits.", "note": "Zoom, when the chair cannot see hands."}, {"id": "floor-15", "slot": "floor", "text": "Chair, at a convenient moment, I would like to put a proposal to the committee."}, {"id": "floor-16", "slot": "floor", "text": "[name], I think you are about to make my point for me. May I make it?", "note": "Light touch; only with warmth in the room."}, {"id": "floor-17", "slot": "floor", "text": "Chair, could I ask for two minutes on this item? I have something concrete."}, {"id": "floor-18", "slot": "floor", "text": "Thank you, [name]. Chair, if I could follow on directly from that."}, {"id": "floor-19", "slot": "floor", "text": "Chair, may we hear from the room before we go back online?", "note": "Hybrid, when Zoom voices dominate."}, {"id": "floor-20", "slot": "floor", "text": "Chair, could I reserve a moment before the item closes? I have a proposal to table.", "note": "Ask early to secure a later slot."}, {"id": "reentry-1", "slot": "reentry", "text": "I raised something earlier that connects to [name]'s point, and it may be worth returning to."}, {"id": "reentry-2", "slot": "reentry", "text": "I am glad [name] has come to that view. It is close to what I suggested at the start, and together they make a stronger case.", "note": "After someone repeats your idea."}, {"id": "reentry-3", "slot": "reentry", "text": "If I may return briefly to the proposal I put earlier, because I think the discussion has now caught up with it."}, {"id": "reentry-4", "slot": "reentry", "text": "To build on [name]'s point, which echoes what I raised a few minutes ago: [point]."}, {"id": "reentry-5", "slot": "reentry", "text": "I want to come back to [proposal], because I do not think we resolved it."}, {"id": "reentry-6", "slot": "reentry", "text": "[name] has put that very well, and I am pleased we have arrived at the same place. The detail I would add is [detail]."}, {"id": "reentry-7", "slot": "reentry", "text": "Can I make sure the proposal I tabled earlier is captured, since it seems to have some support now?", "note": "Gets it into the minutes under your name."}, {"id": "reentry-8", "slot": "reentry", "text": "Picking up a thread from earlier, the [name] approach I described would meet that concern."}, {"id": "reentry-9", "slot": "reentry", "text": "Chair, to close the loop on my earlier suggestion, could we test whether there is support for it?"}, {"id": "reentry-10", "slot": "reentry", "text": "I think [name] and I are saying the same thing, which is encouraging. Perhaps we could put it to the committee jointly.", "note": "Turns repetition into co-ownership without contest."}, {"id": "reentry-11", "slot": "reentry", "text": "That is consistent with what I proposed earlier, and I would be happy to work with [name] on it."}, {"id": "reentry-12", "slot": "reentry", "text": "I may not have put it clearly enough the first time, so let me try again, more briefly.", "note": "After being ignored; shorter the second time."}, {"id": "reentry-13", "slot": "reentry", "text": "Returning to the point I made about [topic]: it bears directly on what we are now discussing."}, {"id": "reentry-14", "slot": "reentry", "text": "For the minutes, may I note that this follows from the proposal I made under the earlier item?"}, {"id": "reentry-15", "slot": "reentry", "text": "Since the conversation has come back to [topic], may I restate my proposal in a sentence?"}, {"id": "reentry-16", "slot": "reentry", "text": "I am grateful to [name] for reinforcing that. It was the second of my three points, and I think the first and third still stand."}, {"id": "reentry-17", "slot": "reentry", "text": "Before we move on, the earlier suggestion about [topic] has not been picked up, and I think it is the quickest route."}, {"id": "reentry-18", "slot": "reentry", "text": "[name]'s formulation is better than mine was, and I am happy to adopt it. What it needs now is an owner.", "note": "Concede the wording, keep the action."}, {"id": "reentry-19", "slot": "reentry", "text": "Let me tie this back to where I started, because the pieces now fit."}, {"id": "reentry-20", "slot": "reentry", "text": "I wonder if we could look again at the suggestion I made earlier, in light of what [name] has just said."}, {"id": "build-1", "slot": "build", "text": "[name] made the decisive point earlier, and I want to give it a practical form."}, {"id": "build-2", "slot": "build", "text": "If I can take [name]'s argument one step further: [point]."}, {"id": "build-3", "slot": "build", "text": "I agree with [name], and I think the logic of that point leads to [proposal]."}, {"id": "build-4", "slot": "build", "text": "[name]'s reference to [jurisdiction] is the key, and it suggests a way forward.", "note": "When a respected member cited another state."}, {"id": "build-5", "slot": "build", "text": "What [name] described as [phrase] is, in practice, [proposal]."}, {"id": "build-6", "slot": "build", "text": "Building on [name]'s point about [topic], the mechanism would be [mechanism]."}, {"id": "build-7", "slot": "build", "text": "I think [name] has given us the principle. Let me suggest the procedure.", "note": "Ideal against an eloquent, low-output speaker."}, {"id": "build-8", "slot": "build", "text": "As [name] said, [paraphrase]. The practical consequence is [consequence]."}, {"id": "build-9", "slot": "build", "text": "[name]'s point deserves to be acted on, not just agreed with. Here is one way to act on it."}, {"id": "build-10", "slot": "build", "text": "Following [name]'s lead, I would suggest [proposal]."}, {"id": "build-11", "slot": "build", "text": "I want to endorse [name]'s framing, and then add the step I think it implies."}, {"id": "build-12", "slot": "build", "text": "If we accept [name]'s analysis, and I do, then the next step is [step]."}, {"id": "build-13", "slot": "build", "text": "There is a thread running from [name]'s comment to [name]'s, and I think it ends at [proposal].", "note": "Links two speakers; you become the synthesis."}, {"id": "build-14", "slot": "build", "text": "[name] put it better than I could, so let me borrow the phrase: [phrase].", "note": "Use their exact words; it travels."}, {"id": "build-15", "slot": "build", "text": "What [name] and the Chair have both pointed to is [theme]. I would like to give it some structure."}, {"id": "build-16", "slot": "build", "text": "[name]'s experience at [institution] is directly relevant here, and it supports [proposal]."}, {"id": "build-17", "slot": "build", "text": "In the spirit of what [name] said, I would propose [proposal]."}, {"id": "build-18", "slot": "build", "text": "I suspect [name] would agree that the next practical step is [step].", "note": "Only when confident; invites public assent."}, {"id": "build-19", "slot": "build", "text": "To give [name]'s idea a home in the minutes, I would suggest the wording [wording]."}, {"id": "build-20", "slot": "build", "text": "[name] has identified the problem precisely. I have a suggestion that answers it on those terms."}, {"id": "build-21", "slot": "build", "text": "This is really an extension of [name]'s point, applied to [component]."}, {"id": "build-22", "slot": "build", "text": "Taking up [name]'s challenge to us, here is one concrete response."}, {"id": "build-23", "slot": "build", "text": "I think the committee has heard [name]'s case and found it persuasive. The question is how to give it effect."}, {"id": "build-24", "slot": "build", "text": "[name] reminded us of [principle]. The proposal I want to make follows from it directly."}, {"id": "build-25", "slot": "build", "text": "With [name]'s point as the foundation, the structure I would suggest is [structure]."}], "lexicon": [{"field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "intro": "This is the register of the seminar, the appeals panel and the evaluation report, where terms are used in their strict senses and loose usage is noticed. Sound native by naming the exact move in dispute, the premise, the warrant or the inference, and by applying the same tests to your own case first.", "entries": [{"id": "argument-1", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "premise", "gloss": "A proposition offered as a reason for a conclusion; distinct from the conclusion itself and from the evidence that supports the premise.", "say": "Before we argue about the conclusion, I'd like to test the *premise*: that enrolments fall when the audition is harder.", "note": "Lands when you name the specific premise you dispute; backfires if used as a general sneer at someone's 'premises'.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-2", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "inference", "gloss": "The step from premises to a conclusion; a good inference can still yield a false conclusion if a premise is false.", "say": "The data are fine. It's the *inference* from one cohort's results to the whole program that I can't follow.", "note": "Separating the data from the inference lets you disagree without accusing anyone of getting the numbers wrong.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-3", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "warrant", "gloss": "In Toulmin's model, the usually unstated principle that licenses the move from data to claim; challenging the warrant is different from challenging the data.", "say": "I accept the survey results. What's the *warrant* for treating parent satisfaction as a measure of musical standard?", "note": "Precise and respected in academic company; in a parent forum say 'what links those two things' instead.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-4", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "burden of proof", "gloss": "The obligation on a party to establish a claim; it normally rests on whoever proposes to change the status quo or asserts the positive.", "say": "The *burden of proof* sits with the proposal to cut the ensemble, not with the people asking to keep it.", "note": "Strong when you place it calmly and early; it sounds defensive if you invoke it only after losing the exchange.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-5", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "begging the question", "gloss": "Strictly, petitio principii: an argument that assumes in its premises the very conclusion it sets out to prove; it does not mean 'raising the question'.", "say": "Saying the new rubric is valid because it measures what we value *begs the question*: whether it measures that is exactly what's in dispute.", "note": "In a room of academics the strict use marks you as precise; if others use the loose sense, say 'raises the question' yourself and let it pass.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-6", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "non sequitur", "gloss": "A conclusion that does not follow from the premises offered, whatever its truth on other grounds.", "say": "With respect, the budget shortfall is real but the conclusion that we drop instrumental tuition is a *non sequitur*.", "note": "Use 'with respect' or similar before it; on its own it can sound like a put-down.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-7", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "conflation", "gloss": "Treating two distinct things as one, usually so that evidence about one is used for the other.", "say": "I think we're *conflating* participation with achievement; the report has numbers for the first and none for the second.", "note": "More collegial than 'you're confused'; name both things you think are being merged.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-8", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "equivocation", "gloss": "Shifting between two senses of one word within an argument so that the reasoning only works if the senses are run together.", "say": "There's an *equivocation* on 'excellence' here: the first paragraph means selective entry, the recommendation means broad access.", "note": "Point to the exact word and the two senses; an unspecific charge of equivocation sounds like a debating trick.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-9", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "false dichotomy", "gloss": "Presenting two options as exhaustive when others exist, or as exclusive when both are possible.", "say": "It isn't a choice between rigour and inclusion; that's a *false dichotomy*, and our Year 7 intake shows both can coexist.", "note": "Most persuasive when you immediately name the third option; otherwise it reads as evasion.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-10", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "motte and bailey", "gloss": "A tactic, named by the philosopher Nicholas Shackel, of advancing a bold contentious claim (the bailey) and retreating to a modest defensible one (the motte) when challenged, then returning to the bold claim.", "say": "We've moved between 'the arts should be cut' and 'the arts should be reviewed' three times; I'd like to settle which one is on the table, because the *motte and bailey* is making this hard to vote on.", "note": "Explain it in a clause the first time; unexplained, it sounds like insider jargon from online argument.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-11", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "survivorship bias", "gloss": "Drawing conclusions from cases that passed a selection filter while ignoring those that did not, so the filter's effect is mistaken for a cause.", "say": "Our alumni who became professionals all did the full program, but that's *survivorship bias* unless we know about the students who did it and left music.", "note": "Lands well with funding panels; give the missing group explicitly.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-12", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "confounding", "gloss": "When a third variable influences both the supposed cause and the effect, producing an association that is not causal.", "say": "Specialist music students outperform on NAPLAN, but selection and family background are obvious *confounders*.", "note": "'Confounder' is the noun; avoid using it as a general word for 'complication'.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-13", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "base-rate neglect", "gloss": "Judging the probability of something from case-specific information while ignoring how common it is in the population.", "say": "Three complaints sounds alarming, but against six hundred families a term that's below the *base rate* we've had for a decade.", "note": "Useful in risk and complaints discussions; supply the actual base rate or the point evaporates.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-14", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "correlation and causation", "gloss": "Association between two variables does not establish that one causes the other; causation also needs direction, mechanism and the exclusion of confounding.", "say": "I'm not disputing the *correlation*; I'm saying nothing here yet shows *causation*.", "note": "The bare slogan 'correlation isn't causation' is tired in academic rooms; say what further evidence would establish causation.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-15", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "null hypothesis", "gloss": "Informally, the default assumption that there is no effect or difference; failing to reject it is not proof that it is true.", "say": "Before we credit the new timetable, the *null hypothesis* is that results would have moved this much anyway.", "note": "Informal use is fine in committees; with statisticians present, do not claim a null has been 'proved'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-16", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "falsifiable", "gloss": "Capable in principle of being shown false by some possible observation; Popper's criterion for a scientific claim, distinct from being false.", "say": "If no outcome could count against 'the program builds character', the claim isn't *falsifiable*, and it shouldn't anchor a funding case.", "note": "Strong against vague goals in strategic plans; avoid aiming it at personal values, where it sounds pedantic.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-17", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "parsimony", "gloss": "Preferring the explanation that requires fewer assumptions when explanations account equally well for the evidence; Ockham's razor.", "say": "On *parsimony*, a single timetable clash explains the drop in Year 10 better than a decline in student interest.", "note": "Parsimony breaks ties; it is not evidence, and an expert will say so if you lean on it too hard.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-18", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "steel-man", "gloss": "To state an opponent's position in its strongest form before answering it; the opposite of a straw man.", "say": "Let me *steel-man* the case for centralising the instrumental service, because I think it's better than it's been put today.", "note": "Disarming when sincere; announcing a steel-man and then delivering a weak version damages trust.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-19", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "the counterfactual", "gloss": "What would have happened in the absence of the intervention; causal claims are claims about the difference from this.", "say": "The question isn't whether enrolments rose after the festival; it's what *the counterfactual* looks like without it.", "note": "Ask for it as 'compared with what' in less technical rooms.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-20", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "a priori", "gloss": "Knowable or argued independently of experience, from concepts or principles; distinct from a posteriori claims that depend on evidence.", "say": "We can't settle *a priori* whether a combined ensemble suits Year 8s; that's an empirical question we can pilot.", "note": "Commonly misused to mean 'presumed in advance'; use it only for reasoning from principle. Pronounced ah pree-OR-ee or ay pry-OR-eye; both are accepted.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-21", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "a posteriori", "gloss": "Known or justified through experience and evidence rather than by reasoning alone.", "say": "The principle sounds right, but its value here is an *a posteriori* matter: let's see two years of data.", "note": "Best paired with 'a priori' in a single contrast; on its own it can sound like ornament.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-22", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "necessary condition", "gloss": "A condition without which the outcome cannot occur, though its presence does not guarantee the outcome.", "say": "Adequate rehearsal space is a *necessary condition* for a third concert band; it isn't enough on its own.", "note": "Use when someone treats a precondition as if it guaranteed success.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-23", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "sufficient condition", "gloss": "A condition whose presence guarantees the outcome, though the outcome may occur by other routes as well.", "say": "A high ATAR score is *sufficient* for entry but not necessary; there are portfolio pathways.", "note": "The necessary and sufficient pairing clarifies policy wording quickly; keep the two terms distinct under pressure.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-24", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "slippery slope, used fairly", "gloss": "A legitimate argument that one step makes further steps likely, when a causal mechanism and evidence for the progression are supplied; fallacious only when the progression is merely asserted.", "say": "I'm making a *slippery slope* argument and I'll own it: once one ensemble is optional, the precedent and the timetable pressure both point to the next.", "note": "Name the mechanism at each step; owning the form openly pre-empts the 'that's a slippery slope' dismissal.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-25", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "valid versus sound", "gloss": "An argument is valid if its conclusion follows from its premises; sound only if it is valid and its premises are true.", "say": "The argument is *valid*, I'll grant that. It isn't *sound*, because the second premise is wrong about the funding formula.", "note": "Granting validity first signals fairness and makes the attack on the premise land harder.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-26", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "enthymeme", "gloss": "In Aristotle, a rhetorical syllogism drawn from probabilities; in modern use, an argument with a premise left unstated for the audience to supply.", "say": "There's an *enthymeme* in the proposal: it assumes, without saying so, that every student will have a private teacher.", "note": "Rare outside philosophy and rhetoric; in most rooms 'unstated premise' does the same work.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-27", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "load-bearing assumption", "gloss": "The assumption on which an argument or plan depends, such that if it fails the whole case fails.", "say": "The *load-bearing assumption* in this budget is that hire income holds at last year's level.", "note": "A clear, modern phrase that carries well in boards; identify one, not five.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-28", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "weak man", "gloss": "Answering the weakest version of an opposing view that someone actually holds, then implying the view as a whole is refuted; subtler than a straw man because the version is real.", "say": "Some parents did say that, but answering the angriest email is a *weak man*; the stronger version of their concern is about fairness in auditions.", "note": "Not widely known; gloss it on first use or it will be heard as a slip for 'straw man'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-29", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "ad hominem, strictly", "gloss": "Rejecting an argument because of who made it rather than on its merits; legitimately questioning a witness's interest or competence is not the fallacy.", "say": "His interest in the outcome is relevant to how we weigh his testimony, but dismissing the argument on that basis would be *ad hominem*.", "note": "Knowing when a challenge to credibility is legitimate is the insider distinction; do not cry 'ad hominem' at every criticism.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-30", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "tu quoque", "gloss": "Deflecting a criticism by pointing out that the critic is guilty of the same thing; it may be true and still not answer the criticism.", "say": "Other departments overspend too, but that's a *tu quoque*; it doesn't answer the question about ours.", "note": "Pronounced too KWOH-kway. Best used against your own side's arguments, which shows integrity.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-31", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "argument from authority", "gloss": "Relying on an expert's view as evidence; legitimate when the expert speaks within their field and experts broadly agree, fallacious otherwise.", "say": "A conductor's opinion on assessment design is an *argument from authority* outside its field.", "note": "Distinguish appropriate deference from fallacy; academics will expect you to.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-32", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "genetic fallacy", "gloss": "Judging a claim by its origin or history rather than by its present evidence or merit.", "say": "That the idea came from a consultant isn't a reason to reject it; that would be the *genetic fallacy*.", "note": "Useful for defusing tribal reactions to where a proposal originated.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-33", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "fallacy of composition", "gloss": "Inferring that what is true of the parts is true of the whole; division is the converse.", "say": "Each ensemble can justify its rehearsal time, but it's the *fallacy of composition* to assume the timetable can carry all of them.", "note": "Name 'division' as well if the error runs the other way, from institution to individual.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-34", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "ecological fallacy", "gloss": "Inferring characteristics of individuals from aggregate data about the group to which they belong.", "say": "The school's median ATAR tells us nothing about this applicant; reading it that way is the *ecological fallacy*.", "note": "Well known in social science and epidemiology; outside those rooms explain it in a clause.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-35", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "regression to the mean", "gloss": "The tendency of unusually extreme measurements to be followed by measurements closer to the average, which can make interventions applied after an extreme result look effective.", "say": "We introduced the support program after our worst exam year; some of the recovery will be *regression to the mean*.", "note": "Particularly powerful when the intervention was triggered by the bad result; say so explicitly.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-36", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "selection bias", "gloss": "Distortion that arises when the cases studied differ systematically from the population about which conclusions are drawn.", "say": "The feedback came from families who chose to attend the forum, so there's obvious *selection bias*.", "note": "Pair it with what a less biased sample would look like.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-37", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "small n", "gloss": "A sample too small for differences to be distinguished reliably from chance.", "say": "With an *n* of eleven in the ATAR class, a two-point shift in the average isn't telling us much.", "note": "Say 'small n' or 'small sample'; quoting a precise threshold without basis invites correction.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-38", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "effect size", "gloss": "The magnitude of a difference or relationship, as distinct from its statistical significance, which mainly reflects whether it is distinguishable from chance given the sample.", "say": "The result is statistically significant, but with a sample this large the *effect size* is trivial in practical terms.", "note": "Asking for effect size, not just significance, marks a reader of research; avoid quoting conventional cut-offs as if they were rules.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-39", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "confidence interval", "gloss": "A range produced by a method that would capture the true value in a stated proportion of repeated samples; it is not a probability that this particular range contains the value.", "say": "The *confidence interval* on that satisfaction figure overlaps last year's, so I wouldn't call it a decline.", "note": "The common misreading is the probability claim; avoid it in front of statisticians.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-40", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "reverse causation", "gloss": "When the supposed effect is actually the cause, or the influence runs both ways.", "say": "Perhaps motivated students choose more ensembles, rather than ensembles creating motivation; *reverse causation* is at least as plausible.", "note": "Offer it as a live alternative, not a gotcha.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-41", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "operationalise", "gloss": "To define an abstract concept in terms of specific, observable measures so that claims about it can be tested.", "say": "If 'musical independence' is a goal in the plan, we need to *operationalise* it, or we'll never know whether we met it.", "note": "Natural in academic and policy rooms; with parents, say 'how we'd see it in practice'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-42", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "external validity", "gloss": "The extent to which a finding holds beyond the setting, population and conditions of the study; distinct from internal validity, whether the study's own causal conclusion is sound.", "say": "It's a well-run study, but its *external validity* for a public school in Perth is limited: it was a single private conservatorium.", "note": "Pairing it with internal validity shows you know the distinction rather than borrowing the word.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-43", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "hasty generalisation", "gloss": "Drawing a general conclusion from too few or unrepresentative cases.", "say": "Two difficult rehearsals with a relief teacher is a *hasty generalisation* about the whole casual pool.", "note": "Australian spelling 'generalisation'; use it about arguments, not people.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-44", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "cherry-picking", "gloss": "Selecting the evidence that supports a position while omitting comparable evidence that does not.", "say": "I'm not suggesting anyone's *cherry-picked*, but we've seen the three best years; can we see all ten?", "note": "Framing it as a request for the full set avoids an accusation of bad faith.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-45", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "Goodhart's law", "gloss": "The principle, after the economist Charles Goodhart, often put as: when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.", "say": "If we tie funding to the number of performances, *Goodhart's law* says we'll get more performances and not better music.", "note": "Widely recognised in policy rooms; the popular wording is a later paraphrase, so don't present it as Goodhart's own words.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-46", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "Campbell's law", "gloss": "Donald Campbell's observation that the more a quantitative social indicator is used for decision-making, the more it is subject to corruption pressures and the more it distorts the process it monitors.", "say": "*Campbell's law* is the better reference here than Goodhart, because it's specifically about social indicators like school results.", "note": "Rarer than Goodhart and more apt for education; use it with people who know both.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-47", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "inference to the best explanation", "gloss": "Abductive reasoning: accepting the hypothesis that would, if true, best explain the evidence, judged by scope, simplicity and fit.", "say": "No single piece of evidence proves it, but the timetable clash is the *inference to the best explanation* for all four drops at once.", "note": "Respected in academic company; be ready to say what makes your explanation 'best'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-48", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "inductive and deductive", "gloss": "Deductive reasoning guarantees its conclusion if the premises are true; inductive reasoning makes its conclusion probable and remains open to revision.", "say": "That's an *inductive* case, and a good one, but it isn't a proof, so let's build in a review point.", "note": "Labelling your own argument as inductive is a quiet show of rigour.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-49", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "defeasible", "gloss": "Of reasoning or a rule: holding by default but liable to be overturned by further information.", "say": "The three-year commitment is a strong presumption, but it's *defeasible*; documented hardship should defeat it.", "note": "Pronounced dih-FEE-zuh-bul. Common in law and philosophy; elsewhere 'open to exception' is safer.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-50", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "modus tollens", "gloss": "The valid form: if P then Q; not Q; therefore not P.", "say": "If the change were working, enrolments in Year 10 would have held; they didn't; that's *modus tollens*, and the burden shifts back to the proposal.", "note": "Almost always better to run the argument than to name the form; naming it in a parent forum is affected.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-51", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "affirming the consequent", "gloss": "The invalid form: if P then Q; Q; therefore P.", "say": "If the program were failing, we'd lose students; we have lost students; concluding that the program is failing is *affirming the consequent*.", "note": "Show the structure briefly so the room can see the gap for itself.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-52", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "denying the antecedent", "gloss": "The invalid form: if P then Q; not P; therefore not Q.", "say": "If we had a bigger budget we could tour; we don't, so we can't: that's *denying the antecedent*, because sponsorship is another route.", "note": "Rarely named outside logic; give the counterexample that exposes it instead.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-53", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "reductio ad absurdum", "gloss": "Refuting a claim by showing that its consequences, followed through, are absurd or contradictory.", "say": "Taken as a *reductio*: if every elective must have forty students, we'd also lose Physics.", "note": "Lands if the absurd consequence genuinely follows; if it doesn't, it reads as caricature.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-54", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "prima facie", "gloss": "Sufficient on first appearance to establish a point unless rebutted; it describes the stage of the argument, not its final strength.", "say": "There's a *prima facie* case that the policy disadvantages students without private lessons; I'd want the department to answer it.", "note": "Pronounced PRY-muh FAY-shee or FAY-shuh. Useful for asking for a response without claiming you've won.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-55", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "principle of charity", "gloss": "Interpreting another's statements in their most reasonable sense before evaluating them; it governs interpretation, where steel-manning governs reconstruction.", "say": "On the *principle of charity*, I take the email to be about workload, not about the students.", "note": "Invoking it openly can sound patronising toward the person interpreted; often better to just practise it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-56", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "moving the goalposts", "gloss": "Changing the criteria for success after the evidence has met the original ones.", "say": "We were asked for fifteen enrolments to run the class and we have seventeen; asking now for twenty is *moving the goalposts*.", "note": "Keep the original criterion in writing; the phrase lands only if you can quote it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-57", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "no true Scotsman", "gloss": "Protecting a generalisation from counterexamples by redefining the category to exclude them; named by Antony Flew.", "say": "Saying no real jazz program would include a ukulele group is a *no true Scotsman*.", "note": "Light, recognisable and good for defusing purity arguments; don't overuse it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-58", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "loaded question", "gloss": "A question that presupposes a contested claim, so that any direct answer concedes it; also called a complex question.", "say": "That's a *loaded question*; I can't answer when we stopped favouring private-school students because we didn't start.", "note": "Name it and then answer the honest version of the question; refusing outright looks evasive.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-59", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "category error", "gloss": "Ascribing to something a property that belongs only to things of a different logical type; Ryle's 'category mistake'.", "say": "Asking which ensemble 'is' the music program is a *category error*; the program is the relationship between them.", "note": "Powerful but easily overused for ordinary confusion; keep it for genuine type mistakes.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-60", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "the is-ought gap", "gloss": "Hume's point that no conclusion about what ought to be done follows from purely factual premises without an evaluative premise.", "say": "That most schools have dropped the course is a fact; that we should is an ought, and the *is-ought gap* needs a value premise to bridge it.", "note": "Sounds philosophical; use when a descriptive trend is being passed off as a mandate.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-61", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "naturalistic fallacy", "gloss": "Strictly, G. E. Moore's charge against defining 'good' in terms of a natural property; loosely, inferring that something is good because it is natural. Distinct from the is-ought gap, though often conflated with it.", "say": "I'd avoid calling it the *naturalistic fallacy*, which has a narrower meaning; the point is simply that common practice isn't a justification.", "note": "Most people use it loosely; using it correctly in front of a philosopher earns credit, using it loosely costs it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-62", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "persuasive definition", "gloss": "A definition, in Charles Stevenson's term, that keeps a word's favourable or unfavourable emotional force while quietly changing its descriptive content.", "say": "Defining 'equity' as identical outcomes in every elective is a *persuasive definition*; it keeps the word's approval and changes what it means.", "note": "Precise and rarely heard; best in policy drafting where definitions carry votes.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-63", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "standard of proof", "gloss": "The degree of certainty a decision-maker must reach; distinct from the burden of proof, which concerns who must reach it.", "say": "Before we hear the complaint, we should agree the *standard of proof*: are we deciding on the balance of probabilities?", "note": "Separating the burden from the standard is an insider move in disciplinary and appeals settings.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-64", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "the Briginshaw principle", "gloss": "From Briginshaw v Briginshaw (1938) 60 CLR 336: in Australian civil and administrative decisions on the balance of probabilities, the seriousness of an allegation and its consequences affect how readily the decision-maker should be satisfied.", "say": "Given what's alleged, *Briginshaw* applies; we'll need clear evidence, not inference, before the council finds against her.", "note": "Familiar to lawyers and senior public servants in Australia; it does not create a third standard, so don't describe it as one.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-65", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "dispositive", "gloss": "Decisive of the matter; settling the question rather than merely bearing on it.", "say": "The legal advice is relevant, but it isn't *dispositive*; the council still has to decide what's right for the school.", "note": "Legal register; sounds affected outside boards, councils and committees with lawyers present.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-66", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "probative value", "gloss": "The degree to which evidence tends to prove or disprove a fact in issue, weighed against its capacity to mislead or prejudice.", "say": "A single anonymous review has low *probative value* about a teacher's practice.", "note": "Legal in origin; works on panels and in complaints processes, less well in staff meetings.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-67", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "triangulate", "gloss": "To corroborate a finding by drawing on independent sources or methods whose errors are unlikely to coincide.", "say": "Let's *triangulate* the survey against attendance and retention before we report it to council.", "note": "Standard in research and evaluation; the point is independence of the sources, not just their number.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-68", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "disconfirming evidence", "gloss": "Evidence that would count against a hypothesis; seeking it deliberately is the remedy for confirmation bias.", "say": "What would be *disconfirming evidence* here? If we can't name any, we haven't really tested the plan.", "note": "Asking for it about your own proposal first sets a tone that makes others reciprocate.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-69", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "motivated reasoning", "gloss": "Reasoning shaped by the conclusion one wants to reach, through selective scrutiny of evidence and arguments.", "say": "I'm aware of the risk of *motivated reasoning* on my part; I run the program, so test my numbers harder than theirs.", "note": "Strongest when applied to yourself; applied to an opponent it becomes an insinuation.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-70", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "post hoc ergo propter hoc", "gloss": "Inferring that because B followed A, A caused B.", "say": "Retention improved the year we changed uniforms, but I'd resist *post hoc ergo propter hoc*.", "note": "Pronounced post hok ER-goh PROP-ter hok. The full Latin can sound showy; 'post hoc reasoning' is often enough.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-71", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "the comparator", "gloss": "The baseline or alternative against which a result is judged; without one a figure has no meaning.", "say": "Eighty per cent satisfaction sounds fine, but what's *the comparator*: last year, other schools, or other departments?", "note": "'Compared with what?' is the plain version and is often better in the room.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-72", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "Chesterton's fence", "gloss": "The principle, from G. K. Chesterton, that one should not remove a rule or structure until one understands why it was put there.", "say": "Before we abolish the second audition round, *Chesterton's fence*: does anyone know why it was introduced?", "note": "Recognised in policy and governance; it is a call for inquiry, not an argument against change.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-73", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "epistemic", "gloss": "Concerning knowledge and justification, as distinct from what is true or what we value.", "say": "My concern is *epistemic*: I'm not saying the policy is wrong, I'm saying we don't yet know enough to know.", "note": "Useful for separating 'I disagree' from 'we can't tell'; overused, it sounds academic.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-74", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "the sorites", "gloss": "The paradox of the heap: a vague predicate admits no sharp boundary, yet small steps accumulate into a clear difference; the honest answer to line-drawing problems is a principled but conventional line.", "say": "Yes, any cut-off score is arbitrary at the margin; that's *the sorites*, and it argues for a reviewed line, not for no line.", "note": "Pronounced so-RYE-teez. Keep it for real line-drawing arguments; most rooms need the point more than the name.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-75", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "proxy", "gloss": "A measurable stand-in for something not directly observable; its value depends on how closely it tracks the real target.", "say": "Exam scores are a reasonable *proxy* for theory knowledge, and a poor one for musicianship.", "note": "Name both the proxy and the thing it stands for.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-76", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "absence of evidence", "gloss": "The distinction between not having evidence of an effect and having evidence that there is no effect; the first licenses only suspended judgement.", "say": "We have no complaints about the new schedule, but that's *absence of evidence*, not evidence of absence; we haven't asked.", "note": "The full contrast is familiar; it lands when you add what would turn one into the other.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-77", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "ceteris paribus", "gloss": "Other things being equal; the qualification that a claimed relationship holds only if other relevant factors are held constant.", "say": "*Ceteris paribus*, smaller ensembles mean more individual attention, but other things are rarely equal in our timetable.", "note": "Pronounced KAY-ter-is PA-ri-bus or SET-er-is. Economists' register; elsewhere 'all else being equal'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-78", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "priors", "gloss": "In Bayesian terms, the degree of belief held before new evidence, which the evidence should then revise in proportion to its strength.", "say": "My *priors* were against the merger, but the pilot data have moved me some of the way.", "note": "Naturally used by people from quantitative fields; in other rooms it can sound like borrowed jargon, so pair it with plain words.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-79", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "Simpson's paradox", "gloss": "A trend that appears in several groups reverses or disappears when the groups are combined, usually because group sizes differ.", "say": "Pass rates fell overall but rose in every class; that's *Simpson's paradox*, because the intake mix shifted.", "note": "Spectacular when it genuinely applies; check the numbers yourself before invoking it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-80", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "distinction without a difference", "gloss": "A verbal distinction that changes nothing in practice or substance.", "say": "Calling it a 'restructure' instead of a cut is a *distinction without a difference* for the students affected.", "note": "Effective when the practical sameness is plain; if there is a real difference, the phrase rebounds.", "level": 2}]}, {"field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "intro": "Rhetoric is the craft vocabulary of persuasion, and in educated company the classical terms signal that you understand persuasion as a discipline, not a trick. Sound native by using the terms when planning, reviewing or coaching a speech, and by letting the figures do their work unnamed in the room itself.", "entries": [{"id": "rhetoric-1", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "ethos", "gloss": "Persuasion through the speaker's perceived character, competence and goodwill, as established in and by the speech, not merely by reputation.", "say": "Our *ethos* with the panel rests on the fact that we've reported our failures as well as our concerts.", "note": "In Aristotle ethos is built within the speech; using it to mean 'brand' or 'culture' sounds loose to rhetoricians.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-2", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "pathos", "gloss": "Persuasion through putting the audience into an emotional state that disposes them to judge in a certain way.", "say": "The video of the Year 7s does the *pathos* for us; my job is to give them the numbers that make the feeling defensible.", "note": "Do not use it pejoratively; in the classical sense it is a legitimate proof, not manipulation.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-3", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "logos", "gloss": "Persuasion through the argument itself, the reasoning and evidence presented; one of Aristotle's three artistic proofs.", "say": "The *logos* is solid; what the submission lacks is any reason for this panel to care.", "note": "Pronounced LOG-oss or LOH-goss. Avoid treating it as simply 'facts'; it is about demonstration.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-4", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "kairos", "gloss": "The opportune moment and the fitness of an argument to it; what can be said, and how, depends on when.", "say": "The *kairos* is now, a week before the budget, not in February when the allocations are locked.", "note": "Pronounced KYE-ross. Lands with people who know the term; otherwise 'the moment' carries the meaning.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-5", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "the five canons", "gloss": "The classical divisions of rhetoric: invention, arrangement, style, memory and delivery.", "say": "We've done invention and arrangement; what's left in the *canons* is style and delivery, and that's where we'll lose the room if we aren't careful.", "note": "A useful frame for coaching or reviewing a speech; listing them in a meeting is pedantry.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-6", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "inventio", "gloss": "The canon concerned with discovering the available arguments and material for a case.", "say": "Most of the work on this submission is *inventio*: finding the three arguments this panel will actually weigh.", "note": "Pronounced in-VEN-tee-oh. The English 'invention' is usually clearer and less affected.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-7", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "dispositio", "gloss": "The canon of arrangement: ordering the parts of a speech and the sequence of arguments for effect.", "say": "The content is right but the *dispositio* is wrong; lead with the student outcomes, not the budget.", "note": "Latin terms like this suit a rhetoric seminar or a speechwriting conversation; elsewhere say 'structure'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-8", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "actio", "gloss": "The canon of delivery: voice, gesture, pace and bearing; also called pronuntiatio.", "say": "Demosthenes is said to have ranked *actio* first, second and third, and on a night like this I believe him.", "note": "The Demosthenes anecdote is traditional and reported through Cicero and Quintilian; present it as 'is said to'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-9", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "exordium", "gloss": "The opening of a speech, whose classical task is to make the audience well disposed, attentive and receptive.", "say": "Keep the *exordium* short: thank the panel, state the stake, and get to the case.", "note": "Pronounced ex-OR-dee-um. Useful when planning; in the room itself, it is the effect, not the term, that matters.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-10", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "partitio", "gloss": "The part of a speech that announces the divisions of the argument to follow.", "say": "A clear *partitio* at the start, three points and in this order, will keep a committee with us.", "note": "The concept matters more than the word; most people call it 'the roadmap'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-11", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "refutatio", "gloss": "The part of a classical speech that answers opposing arguments, usually placed after one's own proofs.", "say": "Put the *refutatio* after our case, not before; answering objections first makes them the agenda.", "note": "Placement is the insider point; the Latin is for planning conversations.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-12", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "peroration", "gloss": "The conclusion of a speech, which classically recapitulates, amplifies and moves the audience to the judgement sought.", "say": "The *peroration* should come back to the students in the first image, not to the budget line.", "note": "A recognised English word among educated speakers; in a speechwriting context it is not affected.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-13", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "captatio benevolentiae", "gloss": "The capture of goodwill, typically in the opening, through modesty, courtesy or shared interest.", "say": "A line of *captatio benevolentiae* is wise here: acknowledge how much reading the panel has done before we add to it.", "note": "Pronounced kap-TAH-tee-oh ben-eh-vo-LEN-tee-eye. Overdone, it becomes flattery and costs ethos.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-14", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "anaphora", "gloss": "Repetition of a word or phrase at the beginning of successive clauses or sentences.", "say": "The speech turns on *anaphora*: 'Because of you, they rehearsed. Because of you, they performed. Because of you, they stayed.'", "note": "Powerful at ceremonies; in a committee it can sound like an oration where a submission was expected.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-15", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "epistrophe", "gloss": "Repetition of a word or phrase at the end of successive clauses or sentences.", "say": "End each paragraph with the same phrase, a sort of *epistrophe*: 'and that is why the program matters'.", "note": "Pronounced eh-PIS-truh-fee. Rarer and more insistent than anaphora; use once per speech.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-16", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "antithesis", "gloss": "The juxtaposition of contrasting ideas in balanced, parallel phrasing.", "say": "The line works by *antithesis*: 'We are not asking for more money; we are asking for the money to stay.'", "note": "Strong for the sentence you want quoted; a speech made of antitheses sounds glib.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-17", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "chiasmus", "gloss": "A reversal of grammatical structure or ideas in the second half of a parallel construction (ABBA), not necessarily with the same words.", "say": "The close is a *chiasmus*: 'The band was built for the students, and the students made the band.'", "note": "Pronounced kye-AZ-mus. Many use it loosely for any reversal; see antimetabole for the strict word-for-word form.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-18", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "antimetabole", "gloss": "Repetition of the same words in reverse order; a strict form of chiasmus.", "say": "Strictly that's *antimetabole*, not just chiasmus: the same words come back reversed, as in 'we teach music, and music teaches us'.", "note": "Pronounced an-tee-meh-TAB-oh-lee. The distinction impresses rhetoricians and nobody else; don't correct people in public.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-19", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "tricolon", "gloss": "A series of three parallel elements, words, phrases or clauses.", "say": "The ask is a *tricolon*: keep the staffing, keep the room, keep the timetable.", "note": "Use it for the point you most want remembered; three tricolons in a minute is a tic.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-20", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "tricolon crescens", "gloss": "A tricolon whose members grow in length or weight toward the third.", "say": "Build it as *tricolon crescens*: 'They came. They practised every week. And they stayed with the program through three years of change.'", "note": "Pronounced KRESS-enz. The rising third member naturally lands at a pause; let it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-21", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "praeteritio", "gloss": "Drawing attention to something by claiming to pass over it; also called paralipsis or occupatio.", "say": "I won't dwell on the three years of deferred maintenance in that building; I'll simply note that the room floods. That *praeteritio* gets it on the record.", "note": "Pronounced pree-teh-RIT-ee-oh. Transparent and slightly sly; use it for a fact, never for a personal attack.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-22", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "litotes", "gloss": "Understatement by negating the contrary, as in 'not inconsiderable'.", "say": "'It was not a small achievement' is *litotes*, and in an Australian room it's often more persuasive than praise.", "note": "Pronounced LYE-tuh-teez. Suits understated Australian register; strings of it read as evasive.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-23", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "hypophora", "gloss": "Raising a question and answering it oneself.", "say": "Use *hypophora* to frame the objection: 'Why not run the band after school? Because that's when our students work.'", "note": "Distinct from a rhetorical question, which expects no answer; hypophora controls which question is asked.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-24", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "prolepsis", "gloss": "Anticipating an objection and answering it before it is raised; also procatalepsis.", "say": "A bit of *prolepsis* early on helps: 'Some will say this favours students with private lessons. It doesn't, and here's why.'", "note": "Pronounced proh-LEP-sis. Answer only objections the room will actually have, or you seed new ones.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-25", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "aposiopesis", "gloss": "Breaking off a sentence before its end, leaving the audience to complete it.", "say": "If we lose the Year 7 intake this year, then by 2029 there will be no ATAR class to ... well. That's *aposiopesis*, and it lets the room finish the thought.", "note": "Pronounced ap-oh-sye-oh-PEE-sis. Deliberate only; an unplanned trailing off reads as losing the thread.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-26", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "concessio", "gloss": "Conceding a point to an opponent in order to strengthen one's own position or credibility.", "say": "Start with *concessio*: 'The department is right that the costs have risen.' Then show why the case still holds.", "note": "The concession must be real and specific; a token concession is spotted at once.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-27", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "correctio", "gloss": "Correcting or sharpening one's own word in the act of speaking, for emphasis; also epanorthosis.", "say": "'It was a good year; no, a remarkable year' is *correctio*, and it lets you raise the claim without seeming to boast.", "note": "Planned correctio sounds controlled; frequent unplanned self-correction sounds like hedging.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-28", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "periodic sentence", "gloss": "A sentence whose main clause, and so its sense, is withheld until the end, creating suspense.", "say": "Write the closing line as a *periodic sentence*: 'After four years, three principals and one flood, the program is still here.'", "note": "Excellent for a climax; too many in a row tire a listening audience.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-29", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "loose sentence", "gloss": "A cumulative sentence that states its main clause first and adds modifying elements after.", "say": "Most of the speech should be *loose sentences*; the ear needs the main point first.", "note": "The default for speech; its virtue is that the listener is never kept waiting for the sense.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-30", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "decorum", "gloss": "The fitness of style, argument and manner to subject, audience and occasion; Cicero's decorum, the Greek prepon.", "say": "A joke there would breach *decorum*; it's a memorial, not a concert.", "note": "The classical sense is broader than etiquette; use it to discuss fit, not just politeness.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-31", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "enargeia", "gloss": "Vivid description that brings a scene before the audience's eyes; distinct from energeia, vigour or actuality.", "say": "Thirty seconds of *enargeia*, a Year 7 in the front row of her first festival, will do more than a page of retention data.", "note": "Pronounced en-AR-gay-uh. Easily confused with energeia; if unsure, say 'vivid description'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-32", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "rhetorical question", "gloss": "A question asked for effect, with no answer expected; distinct from hypophora, where the speaker answers.", "say": "Don't end on a *rhetorical question*; in a funding panel they'll answer it, and not in our favour.", "note": "In adversarial rooms rhetorical questions invite hostile answers; prefer statements.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-33", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "anadiplosis", "gloss": "Repetition of the last word of one clause at the beginning of the next.", "say": "The line builds through *anadiplosis*: 'Practice builds skill; skill builds confidence; confidence builds musicians.'", "note": "Pronounced an-uh-dih-PLOH-sis. A strong chain for a ceremony; in a meeting it sounds rehearsed.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-34", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "climax", "gloss": "In its rhetorical sense, an arrangement of words, clauses or ideas in ascending order of importance; also gradatio.", "say": "Order the outcomes for *climax*: participation, then achievement, then the students who became teachers.", "note": "In the rhetorical sense it names the ascending arrangement, not just the high point.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-35", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "asyndeton", "gloss": "Omitting conjunctions between coordinate elements for speed and force.", "say": "*Asyndeton* suits the summary: 'Three bands, two orchestras, one festival, no extra staff.'", "note": "Pronounced a-SIN-dih-ton. Sharp and businesslike; overused, it sounds like a slogan.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-36", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "polysyndeton", "gloss": "Using more conjunctions than necessary, to slow the line and accumulate weight.", "say": "In the tribute, *polysyndeton* gives the weight: 'and the rehearsals and the tours and the early mornings and the late returns'.", "note": "Suits ceremony and gravity; in a report it reads as padding.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-37", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "isocolon", "gloss": "A sequence of clauses of equal length and parallel structure.", "say": "The balance is *isocolon*: 'We hear them weekly; we judge them yearly; we remember them always.'", "note": "Pronounced eye-soh-KOH-lon. Its effect is felt more than noticed; naming it in public is unnecessary.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-38", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "parallelism", "gloss": "Using similar grammatical structures for related ideas, so that sense and rhythm reinforce each other.", "say": "The recommendations need *parallelism*; at the moment two are verbs, one is a noun and one is a question.", "note": "The plain workhorse behind most figures; in written submissions it also signals care.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-39", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "epizeuxis", "gloss": "Immediate repetition of a word for emphasis.", "say": "One *epizeuxis* is enough for the close: 'This matters. It matters, it matters for every child in this room.'", "note": "Pronounced ep-ih-ZOOK-sis. Easy to overdo; a single instance at the climax is the limit.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-40", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "metonymy", "gloss": "Referring to something by a closely associated thing, as in 'the Crown' for the state.", "say": "When we say 'the department wants', that's *metonymy*; there's a person with a name making the decision.", "note": "Pronounced meh-TON-ih-mee. Useful for exposing how institutional language hides agency.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-41", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "synecdoche", "gloss": "Using a part for the whole or the whole for a part, as in 'hands' for workers.", "say": "'Every chair in the band room' is *synecdoche* for every student; it lets the image carry the number.", "note": "Pronounced sin-EK-duh-kee. Often confused with metonymy; the part-whole relation is the test.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-42", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "meiosis", "gloss": "Understatement that presents something as smaller or less important than it is; litotes is understatement by negation.", "say": "'A slight inconvenience' for a flooded band room is *meiosis*, and the panel will feel the irony.", "note": "Pronounced mye-OH-sis. Keep the distinction from litotes for precise company; the effect is the same family.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-43", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "amplification", "gloss": "Expanding and dwelling on a point, through detail, repetition or comparison, to heighten its importance; amplificatio.", "say": "The figure needs *amplification*: three hundred students is a number; three hundred students who rehearse before school is a commitment.", "note": "Amplify one point; amplifying everything flattens the speech.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-44", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "the rhetorical situation", "gloss": "Lloyd Bitzer's account of rhetoric as a response to a situation made of an exigence, an audience able to act on it, and constraints.", "say": "Read the *rhetorical situation* first: who in that room can actually change the allocation?", "note": "Well known in rhetoric and communication studies; elsewhere it is just good analysis without the label.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-45", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "exigence", "gloss": "In Bitzer's sense, an imperfection marked by urgency that discourse can help to modify.", "say": "The *exigence* isn't the budget in general; it's the decision on Thursday.", "note": "Pronounced EK-sih-jens. Distinct from 'exigency' in ordinary use; rarely needed outside rhetoric.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-46", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "stasis theory", "gloss": "The classical method, associated with Hermagoras, of locating the point at issue: conjecture (did it happen), definition (what is it), quality (was it justified) and jurisdiction or procedure.", "say": "By *stasis theory* we're arguing about definition, whether this is a cut, when the real disagreement is about quality, whether it's justified.", "note": "Superb for diagnosing why a committee is going round in circles; the plain version is 'what exactly are we disagreeing about?'", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-47", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "topoi", "gloss": "The 'places' of argument: general lines of reasoning or commonplaces from which arguments can be drawn, such as cause and effect, more and less, or precedent.", "say": "The obvious *topoi* here are precedent and consequence; nobody has tried definition.", "note": "Pronounced TOP-oy. Singular topos. Useful in planning; in debate, just use the argument.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-48", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "deliberative rhetoric", "gloss": "Aristotle's genre concerned with future action and what is advantageous or harmful; the rhetoric of councils and committees.", "say": "This is *deliberative*, not forensic: the question isn't who was at fault, it's what we do next year.", "note": "Re-classifying a blame-laden discussion as deliberative is a strong chairing move.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-49", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "forensic rhetoric", "gloss": "Aristotle's judicial genre, concerned with past acts and what is just or unjust; also called judicial.", "say": "The meeting has drifted into *forensic* mode, reconstructing what happened at the festival, when we came to decide policy.", "note": "Not confined to courts; it describes any argument about past conduct.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-50", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "epideictic", "gloss": "Aristotle's ceremonial genre of praise and blame, concerned with present values and the honourable; the rhetoric of tributes, awards and occasions.", "say": "Speech night is *epideictic*: we're affirming what the community values, not arguing for funding.", "note": "Pronounced ep-ih-DYKE-tik. Recognising that ceremonies also reinforce values helps you write them with purpose.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-51", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "framing", "gloss": "Selecting and emphasising aspects of an issue so that a particular interpretation, cause or remedy becomes salient.", "say": "The *framing* matters: 'consolidation' invites agreement, 'closure' invites resistance, and they're the same proposal.", "note": "Naming the other side's frame explicitly often neutralises it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-52", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "the ask", "gloss": "The specific action or decision requested of the audience.", "say": "Put *the ask* in the first paragraph and the last; the panel shouldn't have to find it.", "note": "Plain and current; vagueness about the ask is the commonest failure in submissions.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-53", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "signposting", "gloss": "Verbal markers that tell the audience where they are in an argument and where it is going.", "say": "A bit more *signposting* would help: 'That's the cost. Now the benefit.'", "note": "Indispensable for listeners; overdone, it becomes a table of contents read aloud.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-54", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "through-line", "gloss": "The single idea that runs through a speech or document and organises everything in it.", "say": "The *through-line* of the submission is continuity: every section should show what's lost if it breaks.", "note": "If you can't state the through-line in a sentence, the speech doesn't have one yet.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-55", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "register", "gloss": "The variety of language suited to a context, set by formality, vocabulary and syntax.", "say": "The draft is in the wrong *register*; it reads like a newsletter, and this is going to the Minister's office.", "note": "Shifting register within a speech can be powerful; failing to notice it is the risk.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-56", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "cadence", "gloss": "The rise and fall of a sentence, especially its close, in rhythm and pitch.", "say": "Let the *cadence* fall at the end of the sentence; you're lifting every ending into a question.", "note": "Relevant to anyone who speaks with an upward inflection under pressure.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-57", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "the strategic pause", "gloss": "A deliberate silence before or after a key line, which marks importance and gives the audience time to absorb it.", "say": "Leave a *pause* after the number; let the room do the arithmetic.", "note": "The single most underused tool of authority; a pause feels longer to the speaker than to the audience.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-58", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "identification", "gloss": "Kenneth Burke's term for the process by which a speaker persuades by establishing shared substance, interests or identity with the audience.", "say": "The opening works through *identification* in Burke's sense: we're all people who were once the kid in the back row of a band.", "note": "Rare outside rhetoric; elsewhere say 'common ground', which is narrower but understood.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-59", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "the plain style", "gloss": "The lowest of the three classical styles (plain, middle, grand): clear, unornamented and suited to teaching and explanation.", "say": "For the committee, the *plain style*; save the grand style for the gala.", "note": "In expert rooms the plain style usually signals the most authority.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-60", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "sententia", "gloss": "A pithy maxim or general truth, often placed to close a passage.", "say": "End the section on a *sententia*: 'A program is only as strong as its weakest year.'", "note": "Pronounced sen-TEN-shee-uh. One per speech; more and it starts to sound like a fridge magnet.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-61", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "exemplum", "gloss": "An illustrative example or short story offered as evidence or model for a general point.", "say": "Use one *exemplum*, the student who joined in Year 7 and now teaches at the school, rather than six statistics.", "note": "Choose an exemplum the audience cannot dismiss as an outlier.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-62", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "in utramque partem", "gloss": "Arguing on both sides of a question, the Ciceronian practice that trains judgement and reveals a case's weaknesses.", "say": "Before the meeting, argue it *in utramque partem*; if the other side's case is stronger than ours, better to know now.", "note": "Pronounced in oo-TRAM-kweh PAR-tem. A planning practice; you'll rarely say the phrase aloud in the room.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-63", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "recapitulation", "gloss": "A brief restatement of the main points, usually at the close.", "say": "A one-sentence *recapitulation* before the ask: three points, one decision.", "note": "Short is everything; a recapitulation longer than the points loses the room.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-64", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "audience analysis", "gloss": "Systematic consideration of who the audience is, what they know, value and fear, and what they can decide.", "say": "The *audience analysis* says the Deputy Director General cares about equity data, not concerts.", "note": "Practical and uncontroversial; the insider move is to analyse the decision-maker, not the room.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-65", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "derived ethos", "gloss": "Credibility the speaker earns during the speech itself, as distinct from initial ethos brought in and terminal ethos left behind.", "say": "We have little initial ethos with that panel, so the *derived ethos* has to come from precision in the first minute.", "note": "The three-stage distinction is used in speech communication; it helps plan for a hostile or unfamiliar room.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-66", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "apostrophe", "gloss": "Turning from the audience to address an absent person, an abstraction or the dead directly.", "say": "There's an *apostrophe* in the eulogy, where she speaks to him directly; it's the emotional centre.", "note": "Belongs to ceremony and elegy; in a meeting it would be theatrical.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-67", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "dysphemism", "gloss": "Substituting a harsher or more offensive term for a neutral one; the opposite of euphemism.", "say": "Calling the ensemble 'a babysitting service' is a *dysphemism*, and it tells us more about the argument than about the ensemble.", "note": "Naming the move calmly is often enough to strip it of force.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-68", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "parrhesia", "gloss": "Frank speech; as a figure, claiming licence to speak candidly, often at risk to oneself, before someone with power.", "say": "I'll exercise a little *parrhesia*, Principal: the plan as written will lose us the program.", "note": "Pronounced pa-REE-zee-uh. Saying the word is rarely wise; doing the thing, sparingly and respectfully, is what counts.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-69", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "soundbite", "gloss": "A short, self-contained line designed to be quoted out of context and still carry the argument.", "say": "We need one *soundbite* for the newsletter and the media: 'Every student who starts, finishes.'", "note": "Write it deliberately and check that it survives being quoted alone.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-70", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "bridging", "gloss": "Acknowledging a question briefly and moving to the message one wants to deliver.", "say": "Answer the question about cost directly, then *bridge*: 'And what that cost buys is continuity.'", "note": "Legitimate after a real answer; bridging without answering is visible to an experienced room.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-71", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "bottom line up front", "gloss": "Placing the conclusion or recommendation first, then the reasoning; often abbreviated BLUF.", "say": "In the briefing, *bottom line up front*: we recommend no change to the audition process.", "note": "From military and civil-service writing; it suits busy decision-makers and frustrates audiences who need to be brought along first.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-72", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "controlling metaphor", "gloss": "A single metaphor sustained across a speech or document to organise and unify it.", "say": "The *controlling metaphor* is the ecosystem; don't switch to a pipeline halfway through.", "note": "Mixed metaphors are the risk; check the vocabulary of every section against the controlling one.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-73", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "the turn", "gloss": "The point at which a speech or passage pivots from one movement to another, from problem to solution or concession to argument; the volta in poetics.", "say": "The *turn* comes too late; we're two-thirds through before we say what we want.", "note": "Make the turn audible with a pause or a 'but'; listeners miss quiet turns.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-74", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "sprezzatura", "gloss": "Castiglione's term for studied nonchalance: art that conceals the effort behind it.", "say": "The ideal for the speech is *sprezzatura*: rehearsed to the word and delivered as if it had just occurred to you.", "note": "Pronounced spret-sah-TOO-rah. A musician's natural concept; say it among people who'll enjoy it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-75", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "presence", "gloss": "In Perelman and Olbrechts-Tyteca's New Rhetoric, the effect of selecting and dwelling on certain elements so that they occupy the foreground of the audience's mind.", "say": "We need to give the students *presence* in the submission; at the moment they're a line item.", "note": "A technical term in argumentation theory; outside it, 'keep them in front of the panel' says the same.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-76", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "rhetor", "gloss": "The speaker or author as the agent of persuasion, considered in relation to audience and situation.", "say": "The *rhetor* matters here; a parent saying this carries more weight than I do.", "note": "Pronounced REE-tor. Common in rhetoric scholarship; elsewhere 'the speaker' or 'the messenger'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-77", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "hedge", "gloss": "A qualifier that softens a claim's force, such as 'perhaps' or 'I think'; useful for calibrated claims and corrosive when habitual.", "say": "Cut the *hedges* from the first paragraph; we're certain about the numbers, so sound like it.", "note": "Calibrated hedging signals rigour; reflexive hedging under pressure reads as doubt about your own case.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-78", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "set piece", "gloss": "A rehearsed passage delivered with polish, often the planned high point of a speech.", "say": "The *set piece* is the student's story; everything else should be plain so that it stands out.", "note": "Effective when surrounded by plainer material; a speech of set pieces feels performed.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-79", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "pacing", "gloss": "Managing the speed and variation of delivery and the distribution of material across the time available.", "say": "Watch the *pacing* in the first minute; nerves will push you to rush exactly where the panel decides about you.", "note": "Variation matters more than speed; slow down for numbers and for the ask.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-80", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "callback", "gloss": "Returning near the end to an image, phrase or story from the opening, to close the frame.", "say": "End with a *callback* to the empty band room from the opening, now full.", "note": "Creates a sense of design; it fails if the opening image wasn't memorable enough to recall.", "level": 1}]}, {"field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "intro": "Arts policy speaks two languages at once: the economist's and evaluator's vocabulary of value, outcomes and market failure, and the practitioner's vocabulary of ecology, protocol and practice. Sounding native means moving between them without apology, conceding the limits of your evidence before a panel does, and naming the frameworks you rely on precisely.", "entries": [{"id": "arts-policy-1", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "intrinsic value", "gloss": "The worth of an artistic experience in and for itself, as distinct from what it produces elsewhere; the term names a kind of value, not a claim that it cannot be evidenced.", "say": "Before we reach for attendance and wellbeing data, I want the panel to hear the *intrinsic value* case plainly, because that is what the students actually came for.", "note": "Lands when you state it calmly and then move on; backfires if it becomes a refusal to be accountable, which is how funders hear it when it is repeated.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-2", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "instrumental value", "gloss": "Value the arts generate for ends outside themselves, such as health, attainment, social cohesion or economic return.", "say": "The *instrumental value* argument will get us into the Minister's office, but it should not be the only thing we say once we are there.", "note": "Use it as a neutral descriptor; said with a sneer it signals you have not read the policy literature, which treats the two kinds of value as complementary.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-3", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "institutional value", "gloss": "John Holden's third category (Demos, 2006): the value an organisation creates through how it behaves towards the public, such as trust, fairness and conviviality, beyond its programs.", "say": "The festival's *institutional value* is that every school, however small its band, is treated with the same courtesy at the desk and on the stage.", "note": "Lands with policy people who know Holden's intrinsic, instrumental and institutional triad; elsewhere you will need a sentence of explanation, so give it without the citation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-4", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "cultural value", "gloss": "The umbrella question of how the worth of arts and culture is understood and evidenced, associated in the literature with the AHRC Cultural Value Project (Crossick and Kaszynska, 2016).", "say": "If the committee wants a *cultural value* framework, I would rather we adapt an existing one than invent our own rubric over morning tea.", "note": "It is a field of inquiry, not a single number; treating it as a metric you can report sounds naive to anyone from the research side.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-5", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "arts ecology", "gloss": "The interdependent system of artists, organisations, venues, educators, funders and audiences, where damage to one part is felt elsewhere.", "say": "School music is the root system of the state's *arts ecology*; cut it and the effect on the professional sector arrives a decade later.", "note": "Lands when you name the specific dependencies; used loosely it is a familiar sector cliché and panels have heard it many times.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-6", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "pathway", "gloss": "A designed, sequenced route of progression for a learner or artist, with defined points of entry, transition and exit.", "say": "Our job on this committee is to make the *pathway* from Year 7 to tertiary audition legible to a family who has never met a conservatoire.", "note": "Stronger when you specify the transitions; a pathway that is only a diagram of stages without support at each step will be called out.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-7", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "pipeline", "gloss": "A supply-side metaphor for talent flowing toward elite or professional outcomes; unlike a pathway, it implies a single direction and invites talk of leakage.", "say": "I am wary of *pipeline* language because it treats every student who stops at Year 10 as a leak rather than a musician for life.", "note": "Useful when you want to critique a narrow elite focus; using it uncritically yourself can make a participation program sound like a talent factory.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-8", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "participation versus excellence", "gloss": "The persistent policy tension between broad engagement in the arts and investment in the highest artistic standards, often treated as a false binary.", "say": "I do not accept the *participation versus excellence* framing for this program: the senior big band exists because two hundred juniors played first.", "note": "Name the tension and then dissolve it with evidence from your own program; simply asserting that it is a false binary sounds like a slogan.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-9", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "access", "gloss": "The removal of barriers, whether financial, geographic, physical or cultural, to engaging with the arts; distinct from participation, which is actual engagement.", "say": "We have solved *access* for the audition, since it is free, but participation still skews to families who already own an instrument.", "note": "Precise when you separate it from participation and equity; used as a catch-all it blurs the argument.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-10", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "regional reach", "gloss": "The extent to which a program, organisation or funding stream delivers outside metropolitan centres, a standing criterion in Australian arts funding.", "say": "If we want state support for the festival, our *regional reach* has to be more than one school from Bunbury every second year.", "note": "Lands when quantified honestly; overstated reach is easy for a panel to check against the participant list.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-11", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "peak body", "gloss": "A representative organisation that speaks for a whole sector or profession to government and the public, rather than delivering programs itself.", "say": "This should go to the relevant *peak body* first, so the submission carries the weight of the sector rather than one school.", "note": "Standard Australian usage; overseas colleagues may say umbrella or membership organisation instead.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-12", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "auspicing", "gloss": "An arrangement in which an incorporated organisation receives and administers a grant on behalf of an unincorporated group or individual, and carries legal and financial responsibility for it.", "say": "The parent ensemble is not incorporated, so AJEA could consider *auspicing* the grant, provided the board understands it then answers for the acquittal.", "note": "Pronounced AW-spis-ing. Lands when you make clear that the auspice carries real liability; treating it as a mere signature is a governance error.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-13", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "acquittal", "gloss": "The formal report accounting for how grant funds were spent and what was delivered, measured against the funding agreement.", "say": "Before we apply again, I want last year's *acquittal* closed out, because a late one is the quickest way to lose a funder's confidence.", "note": "Standard Australian funding term; in other jurisdictions the equivalent is often a final or grant report.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-14", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "in-kind support", "gloss": "Non-cash contributions such as venue hire, staff time or equipment, valued and declared as part of a project's budget.", "say": "The school's contribution is substantial *in-kind support*: the hall, the risers, the staff time and the piano tuning.", "note": "Panels expect it to be costed at a realistic rate; inflated in-kind figures are noticed and damage credibility.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-15", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "arm's length principle", "gloss": "The convention that arts funding decisions are made by expert peers or an independent body at a distance from ministers, to protect artistic freedom from political interference.", "say": "The *arm's length principle* protects us as much as it constrains us: no one in government gets to choose which charts we play.", "note": "Lands in governance and funding discussions; invoking it to avoid all accountability to government sounds precious.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-16", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "peer assessment", "gloss": "Evaluation of funding applications by practitioners and experts from the relevant art form rather than by administrators alone.", "say": "I have sat on *peer assessment* panels, and the applications that succeed are the ones a stranger can understand in four minutes.", "note": "Neutral descriptor; useful to signal you know how decisions are actually made inside a funding body.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-17", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "multi-year organisational funding", "gloss": "Recurrent funding committed to an organisation over several years, as distinct from project funding for a single activity.", "say": "AJEA will never be stable on project grants alone; *multi-year organisational funding* is the conversation we should start now, even if it takes three cycles.", "note": "Signals sector literacy; be careful with specific program names, which change between funding rounds.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-18", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "Revive", "gloss": "Australia's National Cultural Policy, released in January 2023 as Revive: a place for every story, a story for every place, organised around five pillars.", "say": "Our submission should map directly to *Revive*, particularly the pillars on First Nations First and engaging the audience.", "note": "Lands when you show you know the pillars rather than just the title; quoting it as though it carried funding of its own overstates what a policy document does.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-19", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "First Nations First", "gloss": "The first pillar of Revive, recognising and respecting the crucial place of First Nations stories at the centre of Australian arts and culture.", "say": "If we claim *First Nations First* in the application, it has to show in who leads the work, not only in the acknowledgement on page one.", "note": "Use it as a commitment you can evidence; used decoratively it reads as tokenism to First Nations panel members.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-20", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "the centrality of the artist", "gloss": "A Revive pillar asserting that artists are workers whose pay, conditions and careers are a policy concern, not simply a means to programs.", "say": "Paying the guest musicians properly is not generosity; it is *the centrality of the artist* in practice.", "note": "Particularly effective at a festival or board table when fees are being trimmed; it reframes the cost as a policy obligation.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-21", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "Creative Australia", "gloss": "The Australian Government's principal arts investment and advisory body, established under the Creative Australia Act 2023 as the successor to the Australia Council for the Arts.", "say": "*Creative Australia* is unlikely to fund a school program directly, but it may well fund the professional artists who come into it.", "note": "Use the current name; saying the Australia Council marks you as out of date, though older colleagues will still use it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-22", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "Music Australia", "gloss": "The dedicated music body established within Creative Australia to support the contemporary music industry and its development.", "say": "*Music Australia* is focused on the contemporary industry, so we should frame the jazz education case in terms of the industry's future workforce.", "note": "Signals current knowledge of the Commonwealth architecture; check its current program priorities before naming them, as they move.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-23", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "cultural infrastructure", "gloss": "The physical and organisational foundations that make cultural activity possible: venues, rehearsal spaces, collections, and the organisations and skills that sustain them.", "say": "A school performing arts centre is *cultural infrastructure* for the suburb, and it is idle most evenings.", "note": "Lands when you include the soft infrastructure of people and organisations; treating it as buildings only invites a capital-works conversation you may not want.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-24", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "creative industries versus cultural industries", "gloss": "Cultural industries descends from Adorno and Horkheimer's critical term 'culture industry' and centres symbolic production; creative industries, popularised by the UK's 1998 mapping document, extends to IP-generating sectors such as software and design.", "say": "Under a *creative industries* lens, our students become a workforce; under a *cultural industries* lens, they are makers of meaning, and the policy follows from which one we choose.", "note": "Lands with academics and policy officers; used with people who treat the terms as synonyms it can sound like hair-splitting unless you show why it changes the funding logic.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-25", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "cultural rights", "gloss": "The human right to take part in cultural life, recognised in Article 27 of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights and Article 15 of the ICESCR.", "say": "Framed as *cultural rights*, music education is not an enrichment extra; it is part of what a public school owes every child.", "note": "Powerful in advocacy when used sparingly; leaning on it in an operational budget meeting can sound grandiose.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-26", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "cultural democracy versus the democratisation of culture", "gloss": "Democratisation of culture extends access to an established canon; cultural democracy supports people to make and value their own culture on their own terms.", "say": "The feeder band is the *democratisation of culture*; the student-led songwriting collective is closer to *cultural democracy*, and we should fund both knowingly.", "note": "A distinction that marks real reading in cultural policy; lands best when you attach a concrete example, otherwise it sounds abstract.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-27", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property (ICIP)", "gloss": "The rights First Nations peoples hold in their cultural heritage, knowledge and expressions, which extend beyond what copyright protects; strongly associated in Australia with the work of Terri Janke.", "say": "Before the ensemble performs that arrangement, we need to be satisfied on *ICIP*: who holds the song, and have they agreed to this use.", "note": "Say I-C-I-P letter by letter. Lands when it leads to an action; raising it without a process for consent can stall projects and frustrate First Nations collaborators.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-28", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "cultural protocols", "gloss": "Agreed principles for respectful engagement with First Nations cultural material and communities, covering consent, attribution, benefit sharing and cultural integrity.", "say": "We follow *cultural protocols* because they are how the relationship works, not because the funding agreement requires a box ticked.", "note": "Lands when framed as relationship and process; framed as compliance it suggests you have missed the point.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-29", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "free, prior and informed consent", "gloss": "Consent given voluntarily, before an activity begins, with full information, by those entitled to give it; a standard articulated in the UN Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples.", "say": "*Free, prior and informed consent* means we ask before the arrangement is written, not after the concert is programmed.", "note": "Each of the three words carries weight; consent sought after decisions are made is not prior, and a panel member who knows the standard will notice.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-30", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "Welcome to Country versus Acknowledgement of Country", "gloss": "A Welcome to Country is given only by a Traditional Owner or Custodian of that land; an Acknowledgement of Country may be given by anyone, including non-Indigenous people.", "say": "We have booked a *Welcome to Country* from a Whadjuk Noongar Elder for the opening night, and I will give an *Acknowledgement of Country* at the awards.", "note": "Confusing the two is a common error that First Nations colleagues notice immediately; never say you will 'do the Welcome' yourself.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-31", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "Traditional Custodians", "gloss": "The First Nations people with continuing cultural connection to and responsibility for a particular area of land and waters.", "say": "The school stands on Whadjuk Noongar boodja, and the *Traditional Custodians* should be part of planning, not only part of the ceremony.", "note": "Use the specific nation where you know it; a generic reference where a specific one is known can sound perfunctory.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-32", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "self-determination", "gloss": "The right of First Nations peoples to make decisions about matters affecting them, including how their culture is represented and taught.", "say": "The principle of *self-determination* means the community decides what this project is, and we decide whether we can support it.", "note": "Lands when you show where decision-making power actually sits; claiming it for a project that is school-led throughout invites a fair challenge.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-33", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "community music", "gloss": "Intentional, participatory music-making facilitated outside or alongside formal institutions, valuing inclusion and the group's own goals over a set curriculum or standard.", "say": "The Saturday ensemble is closer to *community music* than to a school program, so its success measures should be different.", "note": "Lands when you respect it as a distinct practice with its own scholarship; using it to mean 'less serious' offends practitioners.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-34", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "socially engaged practice", "gloss": "Art-making in which collaboration with communities and social process are central to the work, not merely its context.", "say": "The aged-care residency was *socially engaged practice*: the residents shaped the repertoire, and the students learned to listen differently.", "note": "Common in visual arts and contemporary policy; in music circles you may need to give an example to land it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-35", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "conservatoire model", "gloss": "The tradition of specialist, performance-centred training built on audition entry, intensive one-to-one tuition and progression by demonstrated mastery.", "say": "Our selective program borrows the *conservatoire model* for its seniors, but we would be foolish to impose it on twelve-year-olds.", "note": "Lands in tertiary and SCSA committees; criticising it wholesale sounds uninformed, since much of the profession is trained within it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-36", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "master-apprentice tradition", "gloss": "Transmission of musical knowledge through sustained close study with one expert, emphasising embodied, tacit knowledge over codified curriculum.", "say": "Jazz is still largely a *master-apprentice tradition*, so a syllabus that only assesses what can be written down misses most of what is learned.", "note": "Lands well in jazz and tertiary contexts; acknowledge its risks too, such as dependence and abuse of power, or it sounds nostalgic.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-37", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "sequential, developmental music education", "gloss": "Music learning that builds cumulatively over years under specialist teaching, rather than episodic exposure; a staple phrase of music education advocacy since the 2005 National Review of School Music Education.", "say": "What the primary schools lack is not enthusiasm; it is *sequential, developmental music education* with a specialist in the room every week.", "note": "Expected language in advocacy; be ready to say what sequence actually means in your program, or it becomes a slogan.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-38", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "far transfer", "gloss": "The claim that music learning improves unrelated abilities such as mathematics or general intelligence; meta-analytic support for far transfer is weak.", "say": "I would keep *far transfer* claims out of the submission; the evidence is thin and a sceptical reader will discount everything else we say.", "note": "Shows real command of the research; the claim that music makes children better at maths backfires with academics, so leave it to others.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-39", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "advocacy versus lobbying", "gloss": "Advocacy makes a public case for a cause; lobbying is direct representation to decision-makers to influence a specific decision, and may attract regulation when done by third parties for clients.", "say": "As a school we can do *advocacy* in the open; *lobbying* a particular Minister is a job for the association, with the board's authority.", "note": "Lands in board discussions about who may speak for whom; public servants in particular are careful with this line.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-40", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "evidence base", "gloss": "The body of research and data supporting a claim or policy, with attention to its quality and fit to context.", "say": "Our *evidence base* is strong on retention and weak on outcomes after Year 12; let us say so before the panel does.", "note": "Admitting the limits of your own evidence base raises credibility; overstating it lowers it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-41", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "theory of change", "gloss": "An explicit account of the causal logic linking a program's activities to its intended outcomes, including the assumptions in between.", "say": "Our *theory of change* assumes that a free instrument removes the main barrier; the attrition data suggests it is actually transport.", "note": "Lands with evaluators and funders; as decoration on an application without testable assumptions it is easily seen through.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-42", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "outputs, outcomes and impact", "gloss": "Outputs are what a program delivers (concerts, lessons); outcomes are the changes it produces in participants; impact is the longer-term, broader change attributable to it.", "say": "Forty concerts is an *output*; students still playing at twenty-five would be an *outcome*; we should be modest about claiming *impact*.", "note": "Funders expect the distinction; reporting outputs as outcomes is the commonest weakness in arts acquittals.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-43", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "attribution versus contribution", "gloss": "Attribution claims a program caused an outcome; contribution claims a plausible, evidenced share in an outcome with several causes.", "say": "We cannot claim *attribution* for students who later study music at university, but we can make a sound *contribution* case.", "note": "Signals evaluation literacy; it is the honest framing for most arts outcomes and senior public servants respect it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-44", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "deadweight", "gloss": "In evaluation, the share of an outcome that would have occurred without the intervention.", "say": "Some of these students would have taken lessons anyway; the panel will ask about *deadweight*, so we should estimate it ourselves.", "note": "Lands with economists and Treasury-minded officials; in arts company it can sound cold unless you explain it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-45", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "counterfactual", "gloss": "What would have happened in the absence of the program or decision, the comparison against which effect is judged.", "say": "The *counterfactual* here is not silence; it is students learning privately, which only some families can afford.", "note": "Strong in any evaluation argument; avoid using it for simple hypotheticals, where it sounds inflated.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-46", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "public value", "gloss": "Mark Moore's concept (Creating Public Value, 1995) of the value public organisations create for citizens, judged by what the public values and authorises, not by market measures.", "say": "A school concert series creates *public value* that no ticket price captures, and that is precisely why it sits in a public school.", "note": "Lands with senior public servants, many of whom were trained in Moore's framework; in arts rooms it may need unpacking.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-47", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "market failure", "gloss": "A situation in which markets alone under-supply a good relative to its social value, the standard economic justification for public subsidy of the arts.", "say": "If live jazz for under-18s were profitable, someone would already be doing it; that is *market failure*, and it is why the festival needs support.", "note": "Persuasive with Treasury and economic audiences; use precisely, as it does not mean simply that something is underfunded.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-48", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "merit good", "gloss": "A good that society judges people should have more of than they would choose to buy unaided, a concept associated with the economist Richard Musgrave.", "say": "Instrumental tuition is treated as a *merit good* in this state; the question is whether we fund it as one.", "note": "Precise and rare; lands with economists but can sound paternalistic if you do not frame it carefully.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-49", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "Baumol's cost disease", "gloss": "Baumol and Bowen's observation (1966) that labour-intensive performing arts cannot raise productivity as other sectors do, so their relative costs rise over time.", "say": "A big band still needs seventeen players, as it did in 1940; *Baumol's cost disease* is why its cost per student keeps rising faster than the budget.", "note": "An excellent answer to 'why does it keep getting more expensive'; with non-economists, give the string quartet example rather than the label alone.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-50", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "non-use value", "gloss": "Value people place on a cultural asset they do not use themselves: option value (keeping the possibility), existence value (knowing it exists) and bequest value (passing it on).", "say": "Plenty of ratepayers will never attend, but the *non-use value* of a thriving youth jazz scene still shows up when you ask them.", "note": "Lands with economists and evaluators; elsewhere, lead with the plain example before the term.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-51", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "contingent valuation", "gloss": "A survey method estimating what people would be willing to pay for, or accept the loss of, a non-market good such as a cultural service.", "say": "If the council wants a dollar figure, *contingent valuation* is the defensible method, but it is expensive and the panel should know its limits.", "note": "Signals real economic literacy; claiming results without a properly designed study will be picked apart.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-52", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "crowding out", "gloss": "The possibility that public funding displaces private giving or earned income rather than adding to it; the empirical evidence is mixed.", "say": "Some on the board worry a government grant will cause *crowding out* of our sponsors; I think the reverse is likelier, since grants signal credibility.", "note": "Lands in funding strategy discussions; be clear the evidence is contested rather than asserting either direction.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-53", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "instrumentalisation", "gloss": "The critique that justifying the arts primarily by non-artistic outcomes distorts what is funded and how it is judged.", "say": "My worry about this framework is *instrumentalisation*: within three years we would be choosing repertoire for its wellbeing score.", "note": "A familiar critique among cultural policy scholars; lands when you also concede the political need for instrumental arguments.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-54", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "Bildung", "gloss": "The German humanist ideal, associated with Humboldt, of self-formation through education and culture, underpinning the classical university and conservatoire ethos.", "say": "There is still a *Bildung* argument for music in a public school: it forms the person, not only the player.", "note": "Pronounced BIL-doong. Lands with academics; in a parent meeting it sounds affected, so translate it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-55", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "artistic merit", "gloss": "A funding and assessment criterion judging the quality, ambition and integrity of the proposed work itself.", "say": "The application is strong on reach and weak on *artistic merit*; we need the repertoire and the guest artists named.", "note": "Funders weigh it alongside other criteria; check the specific criteria of each round, as wording varies.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-56", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "thin markets", "gloss": "Markets with too few buyers or sellers to sustain commercial supply, common in regional and specialist arts provision.", "say": "Regional touring for youth jazz is a *thin market*; it will not exist unless someone subsidises the first years.", "note": "Term borrowed from economics and service policy; lands with policy officers, can sound technical to artists.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-57", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "agent of change principle", "gloss": "A planning principle placing responsibility for noise mitigation on whoever introduces a change, so a new residential development near an existing venue must manage noise rather than the venue.", "say": "If the Perth venues are to survive infill, the *agent of change principle* has to be in the planning scheme, not left to individual complaints.", "note": "Adopted in Victoria and in the UK planning framework, among others; check the local planning position before claiming it applies in a given jurisdiction.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-58", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "all-ages", "gloss": "Live music events open to under-18s, which in licensed venues require specific approvals and conditions under liquor licensing.", "say": "There is no point training young jazz players if there are no *all-ages* gigs where they can hear and play the music live.", "note": "Lands in live music policy discussions; be specific about the licensing obstacle rather than blaming venues in general.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-59", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "night-time economy", "gloss": "The economic and cultural activity of a city after dark, including live music, hospitality and transport, increasingly treated as a policy domain in its own right.", "say": "Live jazz is a small but telling part of the *night-time economy*, and its health depends on transport after eleven as much as on venues.", "note": "Lands with local government and planning people; artists may find it reduces music to economics, so pair it with cultural argument.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-60", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "presenter versus producer", "gloss": "A presenter programs and hosts work made by others; a producer initiates, finances and assembles the work and carries its risk.", "say": "At the festival we are the *presenter* for school bands but the *producer* of the gala, and the risk sits very differently.", "note": "Signals industry literacy; the distinction matters for contracts, insurance and who owns the work.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-61", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "small-to-medium sector", "gloss": "The Australian term for arts organisations outside the major performing arts companies, typically leaner and more dependent on project and short-term funding.", "say": "AJEA belongs in the *small-to-medium sector*, and our case should be read alongside theirs rather than against the majors.", "note": "Standard Australian funding language; it describes position and scale, not quality.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-62", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "precarity", "gloss": "Structural insecurity of work and income, characteristic of artistic careers, as documented in David Throsby's long-running studies of Australian artists.", "say": "We should be honest with Year 12s about *precarity* without making it the only thing they hear about a life in music.", "note": "Lands in policy and academic company; avoid quoting income figures unless you have the current survey in front of you.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-63", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "Status of the Artist", "gloss": "The UNESCO Recommendation concerning the Status of the Artist (1980), affirming artists' rights as workers, including to fair remuneration and social protection.", "say": "If the festival is going to cite the *Status of the Artist* recommendation, our own guest fees have to meet it.", "note": "Rare and authoritative; lands at policy forums, sounds excessive in a routine fee negotiation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-64", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "soft power", "gloss": "Joseph Nye's term for influence through attraction, culture and values rather than coercion; cultural diplomacy is its deliberate use by governments.", "say": "A student big band touring Asia is *soft power* on a very small scale, which is exactly how DFAT tends to think about it.", "note": "Lands with government and international audiences; overclaiming a school tour's diplomatic effect will be heard as inflation.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-65", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "decolonising the canon", "gloss": "Examining and changing whose music counts as core repertoire and knowledge, and on what authority, rather than only adding diverse works at the margins.", "say": "*Decolonising the canon* is not removing Bach; it is asking why Bach is the default and others are electives.", "note": "Lands when made concrete; used as a slogan it divides a committee before the argument has started.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-66", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "intangible cultural heritage", "gloss": "Living practices, knowledge and expressions, including music and oral traditions, as defined in UNESCO's 2003 Convention for the Safeguarding of the Intangible Cultural Heritage.", "say": "Jazz in this city is *intangible cultural heritage*; it lives in people who are in their seventies, and nobody is recording how they teach.", "note": "Lands at conferences and with heritage bodies; check the Convention's status in any jurisdiction before relying on it formally.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-67", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "open round versus targeted program", "gloss": "An open round accepts applications for any eligible activity; a targeted program funds specific priorities, cohorts or outcomes set by the funder.", "say": "This is a *targeted program* for regional youth, so a metropolitan festival application would need a genuine regional component to be competitive.", "note": "Reading which kind of round you are in is basic grant literacy; applying to a targeted program with a generic application wastes everyone's time.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-68", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "variation request", "gloss": "A formal request to a funder to change the approved activity, timeline or budget of a funded project.", "say": "The venue has changed, so we should lodge a *variation request* now rather than explain it in the acquittal.", "note": "Funders prefer early variations to late surprises; changing scope without one can make funds repayable.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-69", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "milestone payment", "gloss": "Grant funds released in instalments upon meeting specified deliverables or reporting points.", "say": "The second *milestone payment* depends on the interim report, so the treasurer needs the attendance figures by the end of term.", "note": "Matters for cash-flow planning; small organisations often fail to budget for the gap between milestones.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-70", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "talent identification versus talent development", "gloss": "Identification selects students judged to have potential; development builds capability over time. Programs that over-invest in selection risk mistaking early advantage for aptitude.", "say": "Our audition is *talent identification*; whether the program works depends on *talent development*, and that is where the resources should go.", "note": "Lands in selective-program debates; it lets you defend selection while acknowledging its known biases.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-71", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "audience development", "gloss": "Planned activity to broaden, deepen or diversify an organisation's audiences over time, as distinct from marketing a single event.", "say": "Free entry for primary families is *audience development*; it is the parents of next year's Year 7s in those seats.", "note": "Familiar in the sector; 'audience engagement' is now often preferred where the emphasis is on relationship rather than numbers.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-72", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "receptive versus creative participation", "gloss": "Receptive participation is attending or listening; creative participation is making, performing or composing. Policy data often conflate the two.", "say": "The festival numbers count *receptive participation*; what we care about is *creative participation*, and we should report that separately.", "note": "Lands with researchers and statistically minded policy staff; useful when headline attendance figures are being overvalued.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-73", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "amateur", "gloss": "In its strict sense, one who practises an art for love (from Latin amator) rather than payment; the distinction from professional concerns remuneration, not standard.", "say": "Some of the finest players in the community band are *amateurs* in the true sense, and the students should hear that word as a compliment.", "note": "Lands when you make the etymological point lightly; in everyday speech the word implies lower quality, so signal which sense you mean.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-74", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "vernacular music and informal learning", "gloss": "Music made and transmitted outside formal institutions, and the self-directed, peer-based, by-ear learning through which it travels; Lucy Green's work on popular musicians is the standard reference.", "say": "Half our contemporary students arrive through *informal learning*; our job is to build on it, not to correct it.", "note": "Lands with music educators and SCSA committees; acknowledge the formal strengths too, or it sounds anti-institutional.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-75", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "musicking", "gloss": "Christopher Small's term (1998) treating music as an activity, including listening, rehearsing and organising, rather than as a thing or a work.", "say": "Setting up the stage, selling tickets and running the sound desk is *musicking* too, and those students deserve recognition at the awards.", "note": "Lands with academics and reflective educators; to a general board it may sound like jargon, so gloss it once.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-76", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "case for support", "gloss": "The concise, evidence-based statement of why a funder or donor should invest, written for the reader's priorities rather than the applicant's.", "say": "Our *case for support* talks about our needs; it should talk about what the funder's money will change.", "note": "Standard in fundraising; the commonest failure is writing it for the organisation's own board.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-77", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "philanthropy versus sponsorship", "gloss": "Philanthropy is a gift given without commercial return; sponsorship is a commercial exchange in which the sponsor receives benefits such as branding.", "say": "If they want naming rights on the program, it is *sponsorship*, and we need a contract, not a thank-you letter.", "note": "The distinction affects tax treatment, contracts and the board's obligations; blurring it causes trouble later.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-78", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "social licence", "gloss": "The informal acceptance by the community and stakeholders that an organisation's activities, partners or sponsors are legitimate.", "say": "Before we take that sponsor, the board should ask whether it affects our *social licence* with parents and schools.", "note": "Lands in sponsorship and reputational discussions; overused, it can sound like corporate risk-speak.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-79", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "Deductible Gift Recipient status", "gloss": "Endorsement allowing an organisation to receive gifts that are tax-deductible for donors, central to private giving to Australian arts organisations.", "say": "Without *Deductible Gift Recipient status*, our donors are giving after tax, and the larger ones will notice.", "note": "Usually shortened to DGR status, said letter by letter; check current eligibility rules, which have been reformed over time.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-80", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "parachute programming", "gloss": "Delivering a program into a community briefly from outside, without local partnership or continuing presence, in contrast to embedded, community-led work.", "say": "One workshop day in the Wheatbelt is *parachute programming*; a partnership with the local teacher over a year is reach.", "note": "Lands as a self-critique; directed at others' programs it can sound sanctimonious.", "level": 2}]}, {"field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "intro": "This register rewards exactness and restraint: the right term for the right stage of a process, the right standard for the right question, and no more law than the room needs. Sound native by naming the process and the standard plainly, keeping findings and decisions apart, and leaving doctrine you are unsure of to counsel.", "entries": [{"id": "law-ethics-1", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "procedural fairness", "gloss": "The common law obligation on decision-makers to act fairly in making decisions that affect a person's rights, interests or legitimate expectations, chiefly through the hearing rule and the bias rule; its content varies with the context.", "say": "Whatever we decide about the ensemble placement, *procedural fairness* means the family sees the reasons and has a chance to respond first.", "note": "The preferred Australian term since Kioa v West (1985); it is flexible in content, so asserting it requires a full hearing in every case overstates it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-2", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "natural justice", "gloss": "The older, largely synonymous term for procedural fairness, comprising the hearing rule and the rule against bias.", "say": "*Natural justice* is not a formal trial; it is the right to know the case against you and to have it decided by someone without a stake.", "note": "Interchangeable with procedural fairness in most rooms; lawyers in Australia now usually prefer the latter.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-3", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "hearing rule", "gloss": "The limb of procedural fairness requiring that a person be told the substance of the case against them and given a genuine opportunity to respond before an adverse decision.", "say": "The *hearing rule* is satisfied by a letter, time to respond and genuine consideration of the response; it does not require a panel.", "note": "Lands when you match the process to the stakes; insisting on maximal process for minor matters is its own unfairness to others.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-4", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "audi alteram partem", "gloss": "Latin for 'hear the other side', the traditional name of the hearing rule.", "say": "The principle is as old as *audi alteram partem*: before we act on the complaint, we hear from the teacher.", "note": "Pronounced OW-dee AL-ter-am PAR-tem. Lands with lawyers and academics; in a staff meeting it sounds showy, so use the English.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-5", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "nemo iudex in causa sua", "gloss": "Latin for 'no one should be a judge in their own cause', the traditional name of the bias rule.", "say": "I taught the student privately for three years, so *nemo iudex in causa sua*: I will step out of this audition decision.", "note": "Pronounced NAY-mo YOO-dex in COW-sa SOO-a; sometimes written judex. Use once, then use plain English; repeated Latin sounds affected.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-6", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "apprehended bias", "gloss": "Bias established where a fair-minded lay observer might reasonably apprehend that the decision-maker might not bring an impartial mind to the question; actual bias need not be shown.", "say": "No one doubts your integrity; the problem is *apprehended bias*, because your daughter is in the section being reviewed.", "note": "The test is the observer's reasonable apprehension, not the decision-maker's actual state of mind; saying 'but I am not biased' misses the point.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-7", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "prejudgement", "gloss": "A decision-maker having so fixed a view in advance that they are not open to persuasion by the evidence or submissions.", "say": "If the email already says the student will be removed, any later meeting looks like *prejudgement*, not a hearing.", "note": "The Australian spelling keeps the middle e; the risk usually arises from careless correspondence before a decision.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-8", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "an open mind, not a blank mind", "gloss": "The principle that a decision-maker may hold prior views and experience provided they remain genuinely open to persuasion; discussed by the High Court in Minister for Immigration v Jia Legeng (2001).", "say": "Having a view on the band program does not disqualify me; I need *an open mind, not a blank mind*, and I can show I have changed position before.", "note": "Lands when someone argues that any prior opinion is disqualifying; it is a defence of judgement, not of predetermination.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-9", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "legitimate expectation", "gloss": "Historically, an expectation of a benefit or procedure that grounded a right to be heard; in Australia the High Court has moved away from it as the foundation of procedural fairness.", "say": "I would not rest the argument on *legitimate expectation*; the stronger point is that the decision plainly affects the student's interests.", "note": "Use with care in Australia; it remains prominent in English administrative law, and importing it uncritically can mislead a committee.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-10", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "credible, relevant and significant", "gloss": "The formulation from Kioa v West (1985) identifying adverse information that a decision-maker must generally put to the affected person for comment.", "say": "The neighbour's complaint is *credible, relevant and significant* to the decision, so the teacher must see the substance of it before we decide anything.", "note": "A precise test that lands with lawyers and senior public servants; it is a guide to what must be disclosed, not a requirement to disclose everything.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-11", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "duty of care", "gloss": "The legal obligation to take reasonable care to avoid foreseeable harm to those one could reasonably expect to be affected; schools owe it to students in their charge.", "say": "Our *duty of care* on the tour does not end at the concert; it covers the hotel and the travel between.", "note": "The duty is to take reasonable care, not to prevent all harm; framing it as a guarantee drives unnecessary risk aversion.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-12", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "non-delegable duty", "gloss": "A duty that cannot be discharged simply by appointing a competent person; the school authority must ensure reasonable care is taken, as established for schools in Commonwealth v Introvigne (1982).", "say": "Hiring a tour company does not transfer our responsibility, because the school's *non-delegable duty* remains with us.", "note": "Lands when contracts or volunteers are being treated as a transfer of responsibility; it does not make the authority liable for all harm.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-13", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "reasonable foreseeability", "gloss": "The threshold for duty and breach that a risk was not far-fetched or fanciful, as stated in Wyong Shire Council v Shirt (1980); foreseeability alone does not establish breach.", "say": "Heat stress at an outdoor gig in February meets the test of *reasonable foreseeability*; the question is what reasonable precautions look like.", "note": "A low threshold; the real argument is usually about the reasonable response, which civil liability legislation now frames.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-14", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "standard of care", "gloss": "What a reasonable person in the defendant's position would have done in response to a foreseeable risk, considering probability, seriousness, burden of precautions and social utility.", "say": "The *standard of care* for a Year 7 camp is not the same as for a Year 12 tour, because the students' capacity differs.", "note": "Distinguish it from the existence of a duty; most school disputes are about the standard, not whether a duty existed.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-15", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "vicarious liability", "gloss": "Liability of an employer for wrongs committed by an employee in the course of employment, without fault on the employer's part; distinct from the employer's direct liability for its own negligence.", "say": "The Department's exposure here is *vicarious liability* for the teacher's conduct on duty, separate from any failure in its own supervision.", "note": "Whether intentional wrongdoing falls within the course of employment is a difficult area of law; do not offer a view on a live case.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-16", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "reasonable adjustments", "gloss": "Measures taken to enable a person with disability to access and participate in education or employment on the same basis as others, required unless they would impose unjustifiable hardship.", "say": "Extra time and a separate room for the aural exam are *reasonable adjustments*; changing what the exam assesses is a different question.", "note": "The Disability Standards for Education 2005 frame the obligation; adjustments should preserve the inherent requirements of the assessment.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-17", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "unjustifiable hardship", "gloss": "The statutory limit on the obligation to make adjustments under the Disability Discrimination Act 1992 (Cth), assessed against all relevant circumstances including cost and benefit.", "say": "Cost alone rarely establishes *unjustifiable hardship*; we would need to show we weighed the benefit to the student as well.", "note": "A high bar; organisations that assert it casually tend to lose credibility with tribunals and families.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-18", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "Disability Standards for Education 2005", "gloss": "Standards made under the Disability Discrimination Act 1992 (Cth) setting out how education providers must enable students with disability to participate on the same basis as others, including a duty to consult.", "say": "Under the *Disability Standards for Education 2005*, consultation with the student and family comes before the adjustment, not after a complaint.", "note": "'On the same basis' is the key phrase; it does not mean identical treatment.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-19", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "direct versus indirect discrimination", "gloss": "Direct discrimination is less favourable treatment because of a protected attribute; indirect discrimination is a condition applied to all that disadvantages people with an attribute and is not reasonable.", "say": "Requiring every student to own an instrument before audition looks neutral, but it may be *indirect discrimination* if it is not reasonable.", "note": "Indirect discrimination does not require intent; that is the point most often missed in staff discussions.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-20", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "protected attribute", "gloss": "A characteristic on which discrimination is prohibited by statute, such as sex, race, disability or age, with the list varying between Commonwealth and state law.", "say": "Hearing loss is a *protected attribute*, so the audition process has to be reviewed with that in mind.", "note": "In WA, the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) operates alongside the Commonwealth Acts; the lists are not identical.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-21", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "victimisation", "gloss": "In discrimination law, subjecting a person to detriment because they have made, or intend to make, a complaint or have assisted in one.", "say": "Whatever we think of the complaint, the student's seat in the ensemble cannot change because of it, or we risk *victimisation*.", "note": "A separate wrong from the underlying discrimination; it can succeed even if the original complaint fails.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-22", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "positive duty", "gloss": "The obligation, introduced into the Sex Discrimination Act 1984 (Cth) in 2022, for employers to take reasonable and proportionate measures to eliminate sexual harassment and related conduct, enforceable by the Australian Human Rights Commission.", "say": "Under the *positive duty*, waiting for a complaint is no longer enough; the association has to show what it has done to prevent harassment at the festival.", "note": "Lands at boards; it shifts the question from response to prevention, which many organisations have not absorbed.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-23", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "inherent requirements", "gloss": "The essential, non-peripheral demands of a job or course; in disability discrimination law, inability to meet them even with adjustments can be a defence.", "say": "Sight-reading may be an *inherent requirement* of the ATAR performance exam; it is not obviously one for entry into Year 7.", "note": "The defence is narrow and assessed after considering adjustments; using it to protect preferences rather than essentials fails.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-24", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "special measures", "gloss": "Measures taken to achieve substantive equality for a disadvantaged group, which discrimination statutes recognise as not themselves discriminatory.", "say": "Reserving workshop places for First Nations students could be a *special measure*, but we should check its basis before announcing it.", "note": "A technical term with statutory conditions; confirm with legal advice before describing a program this way.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-25", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "conciliation", "gloss": "A confidential, assisted negotiation conducted by a statutory body such as the Equal Opportunity Commission of WA or the Australian Human Rights Commission, usually the stage before a complaint can proceed to a tribunal.", "say": "If the family's complaint goes to *conciliation*, our aim should be a workable arrangement for the student, not a win on the record.", "note": "The conciliator does not decide the complaint; treating it as a hearing to be won tends to harden positions and lose the chance of settlement.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-26", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "confidentiality versus privacy", "gloss": "Confidentiality is an obligation not to disclose information received in confidence; privacy concerns how personal information is collected, used and disclosed under law and policy.", "say": "This is a *confidentiality* issue because the parent told us in confidence, and a *privacy* issue because it is the student's health information.", "note": "Keeping the two apart clarifies what may be shared and with whom; they overlap but are not the same obligation.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-27", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "need to know", "gloss": "The principle that sensitive information is shared only with those who require it to perform their role in the matter.", "say": "The ensemble directors need to know an adjustment exists; they do not *need to know* the diagnosis.", "note": "Lands when you apply it narrowly and explain why; applied too strictly it can leave staff unable to discharge their duty of care.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-28", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "duty of confidence", "gloss": "The equitable obligation arising where information with the necessary quality of confidence is imparted in circumstances importing an obligation of confidence, and unauthorised use is threatened; the elements are classically stated in Coco v A N Clark (Engineers) (1969).", "say": "The draft syllabus was shared under a *duty of confidence*, so it cannot go to the department meeting until SCSA releases it.", "note": "Rare and precise; useful on committees where embargoed material circulates, but most rooms will be served by 'in confidence'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-29", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "without prejudice", "gloss": "A label for communications made in a genuine attempt to settle a dispute, which are generally inadmissible as evidence of admissions; the label alone does not create the protection where there is no dispute.", "say": "Marking a routine email *without prejudice* does nothing if there is no dispute being settled, and it reads as defensive.", "note": "Often misused by non-lawyers to mean confidential; it is about settlement communications in a dispute.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-30", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "without prejudice save as to costs", "gloss": "An offer made on terms that it cannot be shown to the court on the merits but may be relied upon on the question of costs; known as a Calderbank offer after Calderbank v Calderbank (1975).", "say": "If it reaches that stage, the lawyers may make an offer *without prejudice save as to costs*; that is their call, not the board's.", "note": "Litigation language; use only to show you understand what the lawyers are doing, not to deploy it yourself.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-31", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "without admission of liability", "gloss": "Wording that accompanies a payment, apology or concession to make clear it is not an acceptance of legal fault; distinct from without prejudice.", "say": "The festival can refund the entry fee *without admission of liability* while the complaint is being considered.", "note": "Useful in correspondence; it does not guarantee the statement will be inadmissible, so get advice in serious matters.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-32", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "mandatory reporting", "gloss": "A statutory obligation on specified professionals to report certain beliefs about child abuse to the designated authority; in WA, teachers must report a belief on reasonable grounds of child sexual abuse formed in the course of their work.", "say": "*Mandatory reporting* is a personal obligation; telling the principal does not discharge it on my behalf.", "note": "Keep the scope accurate: categories of reportable abuse and the authority receiving reports differ between jurisdictions.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-33", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "belief versus suspicion", "gloss": "Belief is an inclination of the mind toward accepting a proposition; suspicion is a lesser state of apprehension that it may be so. The High Court drew the distinction in George v Rockett (1990).", "say": "The threshold is a *belief* on reasonable grounds, which is more than *suspicion*, but it does not require certainty or investigation.", "note": "Lands in any discussion of reporting thresholds; never use it to argue a teacher should investigate before reporting.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-34", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "actual, potential and perceived conflicts", "gloss": "An actual conflict is a present clash between private interest and public duty; a potential conflict could arise; a perceived conflict is one a reasonable person might believe exists, regardless of fact.", "say": "My son plays in the band applying for the grant, so this is at least a *perceived conflict*, and I will declare it and step out.", "note": "The perceived category matters most in public bodies; dismissing it with 'but I am not influenced' is a common error.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-35", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "recusal", "gloss": "Withdrawal by a decision-maker from participating in a matter because of a conflict or apprehended bias.", "say": "Declaring the interest is not enough on this item; *recusal* means I leave the room for the discussion as well as the vote.", "note": "Pronounced ri-KYOO-zl. Lands when you specify what it involves; a recusal from the vote only, after influencing the discussion, is not one.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-36", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "conflict of duty and duty", "gloss": "A conflict arising where a person owes duties to two bodies whose interests diverge, as distinct from a conflict between personal interest and duty.", "say": "As chair of the council and Teacher in Charge I face a *conflict of duty and duty* on this budget item, and the council should decide it without me.", "note": "Precise and underused; it explains why someone with no personal interest can still be conflicted, which disarms indignant objections.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-37", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "material personal interest", "gloss": "The statutory phrase, used in the Corporations Act and in WA associations legislation, for an interest of a director or committee member in a matter that must be disclosed.", "say": "Before the board approves the venue contract, anyone with a *material personal interest* in the venue needs to say so, and the minutes should record it.", "note": "Use the phrase accurately; materiality is a judgement, so when in doubt, declare.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-38", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "fiduciary duty", "gloss": "An obligation of loyalty owed by a person in a position of trust, such as a committee member or treasurer, to act in the interests of the body and not for their own advantage.", "say": "As treasurer I owe a *fiduciary duty* to the association, which means its money is never a convenience for my own band's cash flow.", "note": "Pronounced fi-DYOO-sha-ree. Lands in governance training; overstating it to cover every disagreement dilutes it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-39", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "no conflict rule and no profit rule", "gloss": "The two core fiduciary prohibitions: a fiduciary must not place themselves where interest and duty conflict, and must not profit from their position without informed consent.", "say": "Booking my own trio for the gala breaches the *no profit rule* unless the board knows and approves in advance.", "note": "Lands with lawyers and experienced directors; frame consent as the remedy, so it does not sound like an accusation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-40", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "probity", "gloss": "Integrity, fairness and transparency in a decision process, particularly procurement and grant allocation, so that it can withstand scrutiny.", "say": "The *probity* of the audition panel matters as much as its expertise; families must be able to see it is fair.", "note": "Lands when attached to concrete controls such as declarations and documented criteria; used vaguely it sounds bureaucratic.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-41", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "gifts and benefits register", "gloss": "A record of gifts, hospitality and benefits offered to or accepted by staff or office-holders, kept to manage conflicts and maintain public confidence.", "say": "The instrument supplier's concert tickets go on the *gifts and benefits register*, or better, back to the supplier.", "note": "Routine in the public sector; the reputational risk lies in small, frequent benefits from suppliers rather than large gifts.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-42", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "misconduct versus performance", "gloss": "Misconduct concerns breaches of conduct standards, typically culpable; performance concerns capability or standard of work. They call for different processes and should not be blended.", "say": "This is a *performance* matter, not *misconduct*; running it as discipline would be unfair and would almost certainly be overturned.", "note": "Choosing the wrong track is one of the commonest errors in workplace management; name the track before acting.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-43", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "minor misconduct versus serious misconduct", "gloss": "In WA, under the Corruption, Crime and Misconduct Act 2003, serious misconduct falls to the Corruption and Crime Commission and minor misconduct to the Public Sector Commissioner.", "say": "If there is any suggestion of *serious misconduct*, it is not ours to investigate; the principal notifies and we preserve the records.", "note": "Accuracy on who receives which matter carries weight with senior public servants; never decide the classification informally yourself.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-44", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "right of reply", "gloss": "The opportunity for a person to respond to adverse allegations or material before a decision or publication.", "say": "Before the report goes to the council, the staff member named in it is entitled to a *right of reply*, and it should be attached.", "note": "Lands when you give a real timeframe and consider the reply genuinely; a reply invited after the decision is not one.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-45", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "particularised allegations", "gloss": "Allegations stated with enough specificity, including what, when, where and who, for the person to understand and answer them.", "say": "'Unprofessional behaviour' is not an allegation; we need *particularised allegations* or there is nothing for the teacher to answer.", "note": "A vague allegation is itself a procedural fairness failure; senior HR staff will expect you to know this.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-46", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "show cause", "gloss": "A notice inviting a person to explain why a proposed adverse action, such as a sanction, should not be taken.", "say": "The *show cause* letter should state the proposed outcome and why, so the response can address it squarely.", "note": "Strictly a step after findings and before a final decision; using it as the opening of an investigation confuses the stages.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-47", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "grievance", "gloss": "A formal concern raised by an employee about a workplace decision, action or behaviour affecting them, dealt with under a defined grievance process.", "say": "If she wants it on the record, she can lodge a *grievance*, and I will make sure it reaches someone who has no part in the decision.", "note": "Distinguish a grievance (about one's own treatment) from a complaint about misconduct, which may trigger a different process.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-48", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "informal resolution", "gloss": "Resolving a concern by discussion, facilitated conversation or agreed actions without a formal finding, where the matter and the parties suit it.", "say": "*Informal resolution* is right for a clash about rehearsal times; it is wrong for anything that touches safety or alleged misconduct.", "note": "Lands when matched to the matter; pressing a reluctant complainant into it can itself become a grievance.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-49", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "workplace investigation", "gloss": "A structured, impartial fact-finding process into allegations, producing findings on the evidence against a stated standard of proof.", "say": "A *workplace investigation* finds facts; it does not decide the sanction, and we should not let it drift into doing both.", "note": "Lands when you keep its scope to the terms of reference; investigations that wander invite procedural challenge.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-50", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "separation of investigator and decision-maker", "gloss": "The practice of having one person establish the facts and another decide the outcome, protecting against prejudgement and apprehended bias.", "say": "Given my involvement with the ensemble, the *separation of investigator and decision-maker* matters: I can give evidence, but I cannot do either job.", "note": "Not always legally required, but expected as good practice in serious matters; small organisations should plan for it in advance.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-51", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "terms of reference", "gloss": "The written scope of an investigation, review or committee, setting out the questions, powers, timeframe and reporting line.", "say": "The *terms of reference* should name the allegations precisely, or we will find ourselves investigating the whole department culture.", "note": "Well-drafted terms prevent scope creep; vague ones are the source of most later disputes.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-52", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "findings of fact versus recommendations", "gloss": "Findings state what the investigator concludes happened on the evidence; recommendations propose what the decision-maker might do. They should be clearly separated in a report.", "say": "The report should give *findings of fact* first and keep any *recommendations* separate, so the decision-maker can reject one without disturbing the other.", "note": "Blurring the two invites challenge to the whole report; senior readers look for the separation.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-53", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "substantiated and not substantiated", "gloss": "Outcomes indicating whether an allegation has been established to the required standard; 'not substantiated' means not proven, not that the allegation was false.", "say": "*Not substantiated* is not the same as exonerated, and we should be careful how that is communicated to the complainant.", "note": "Precision here protects both parties; describing an unsubstantiated complaint as false or vexatious without evidence is a serious error.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-54", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "balance of probabilities", "gloss": "The civil standard of proof: a fact is established if it is more probable than not, as distinct from the criminal standard of beyond reasonable doubt.", "say": "The investigation applies the *balance of probabilities*; nobody needs to be satisfied beyond reasonable doubt, and saying so in the report would be wrong.", "note": "Lands when you correct the common slide into criminal standards in workplace matters.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-55", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "Briginshaw", "gloss": "The principle from Briginshaw v Briginshaw (1938), particularly Dixon J, that the seriousness of an allegation, its inherent unlikelihood and the gravity of the consequences affect whether a tribunal is reasonably satisfied; it is not a third standard of proof.", "say": "Given the gravity of the allegation, *Briginshaw* applies: reasonable satisfaction should not be produced by inexact proofs, indefinite testimony or indirect inferences.", "note": "Pronounced BRIG-in-shaw. Lands strongly with lawyers and investigators; describing it as a higher standard is a well-known error.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-56", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "onus versus standard", "gloss": "The onus (burden) of proof is who must establish a fact; the standard of proof is the degree of satisfaction required.", "say": "The *onus* is on the investigation to establish the allegation, not on the teacher to disprove it, and the *standard* is the balance of probabilities.", "note": "Pronounced OH-nus. Conflating the two is common in staff rooms; separating them cleanly signals training.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-57", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "hearsay", "gloss": "Evidence of a statement made out of court offered as proof of its contents; generally admissible in workplace investigations, but usually given less weight than direct evidence.", "say": "The parent's account of what another student said is *hearsay*; it can be considered, but it cannot carry the finding on its own.", "note": "Lands when you speak of weight rather than exclusion; workplace processes are not bound by the rules of evidence.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-58", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "weight versus admissibility", "gloss": "Admissibility is whether evidence may be considered at all; weight is how much it counts once considered.", "say": "Nothing in the folder is inadmissible in this process; the question is *weight*, and the anonymous note has very little.", "note": "Lands in investigations and appeals; non-lawyers often argue admissibility when the real issue is weight.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-59", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "credibility versus reliability", "gloss": "Credibility concerns whether a witness is honest; reliability concerns whether an honest witness's account is accurate.", "say": "I do not doubt the student's *credibility*; I have some doubt about *reliability*, because she was across the room at the time.", "note": "A valuable distinction for findings and feedback; it lets you discount evidence without accusing anyone of lying.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-60", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "contemporaneous notes", "gloss": "Records made at or very close to the time of the events they describe, generally given greater weight than later recollection.", "say": "Please make *contemporaneous notes* of the conversation tonight, dated and signed, and do not edit them later.", "note": "Notes amended after the event lose most of their value; add a dated addendum instead.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-61", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "Jones v Dunkel inference", "gloss": "The principle from Jones v Dunkel (1959) that an unexplained failure by a party to call a witness or give evidence may permit an inference that it would not have helped that party's case.", "say": "In court a *Jones v Dunkel inference* might be drawn from that silence; in our process we should simply note that he chose not to respond.", "note": "Strictly a litigation principle; invoking it in a workplace or school process sounds like over-lawyering and should be left to counsel.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-62", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "Browne v Dunn", "gloss": "The rule from Browne v Dunn (1893) that a party intending to contradict a witness must put that contrary case to the witness so they can respond; its spirit informs fair investigations.", "say": "In the spirit of *Browne v Dunn*, the teacher should be told of the contrary account before the investigator prefers it.", "note": "Pronounced brown v dun. A litigation rule; cite it only as an analogy in workplace settings, or it will sound like borrowed authority.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-63", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "support person", "gloss": "A person who accompanies a party to an interview or meeting for support, without acting as an advocate or answering on their behalf.", "say": "You are welcome to bring a *support person*; they can take notes and ask for a break, but the answers need to be yours.", "note": "State the role clearly in advance; confusion between a support person and a representative derails interviews.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-64", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "administrative suspension", "gloss": "Removal of a staff member from duty pending investigation, ordinarily on pay, as a protective measure rather than a finding or penalty.", "say": "The *administrative suspension* protects the investigation and the students; it is not a judgement and should never be described to staff as one.", "note": "Lands when you insist on neutral communication; leaked or loaded descriptions of a suspension can create defamation and fairness problems.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-65", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "proportionality", "gloss": "The requirement that a sanction or response be commensurate with the seriousness of the conduct, taking into account mitigating and aggravating circumstances.", "say": "*Proportionality* matters: a first lapse in record-keeping does not warrant removal from the tour.", "note": "Lands in sanction discussions; it is not leniency, and should be argued on facts rather than sympathy.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-66", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "harsh, unjust or unreasonable", "gloss": "The statutory test for unfair dismissal under the Fair Work Act 2009 (Cth); WA public sector teachers generally fall within the state industrial system, where the governing formulation differs.", "say": "Before anyone threatens termination, remember any dismissal will be tested on whether it was *harsh, unjust or unreasonable*, or the state equivalent.", "note": "Know which jurisdiction applies before quoting the test; leave the classification to industrial relations specialists.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-67", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "reasonable management action", "gloss": "Management action carried out in a reasonable manner, which the federal workplace bullying definition expressly excludes from bullying.", "say": "Setting expectations about rehearsal punctuality is *reasonable management action*, provided it is done in a reasonable manner.", "note": "Both limbs matter: the action and the manner. A legitimate direction delivered abusively is not protected.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-68", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "psychosocial hazard", "gloss": "A workplace hazard arising from work design, management or interactions that may cause psychological harm, now expressly within WA work health and safety duties under the Work Health and Safety Act 2020 (WA) and its regulations.", "say": "An unmanaged festival workload for volunteers is a *psychosocial hazard*, and the board has to treat it as a safety issue, not a morale issue.", "note": "Lands at boards and with leadership; it reframes workload and conflict as matters of legal duty.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-69", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "person conducting a business or undertaking", "gloss": "The primary duty holder under harmonised work health and safety law, usually shortened to PCBU, covering organisations and individuals regardless of the employment relationship.", "say": "For the festival, AJEA is the *person conducting a business or undertaking*, and its duty extends to volunteers and visiting school staff.", "note": "Say P-C-B-U letter by letter. Lands in governance and safety discussions; the breadth of the definition surprises many boards.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-70", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "legal professional privilege", "gloss": "Protection from compelled disclosure of confidential communications made for the dominant purpose of giving or obtaining legal advice or for use in litigation; Esso Australia Resources v FCT (1999) settled the dominant purpose test at common law.", "say": "Forwarding the legal advice to the whole committee could waive *legal professional privilege*, so the chair should summarise it instead.", "note": "Privilege can be waived by careless circulation; that practical point is what lands in a board room.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-71", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "public interest disclosure", "gloss": "A protected disclosure of wrongdoing in the public sector made to a proper authority under the Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA), carrying statutory protections for the discloser.", "say": "If she is making a *public interest disclosure*, it must go to the proper authority, and her identity must be protected from that moment.", "note": "Distinguish it from an ordinary complaint; mishandling the discloser's confidentiality is a serious breach.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-72", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "ultra vires", "gloss": "Beyond power: an act or decision made without legal authority under the relevant statute, constitution or delegation.", "say": "The committee cannot approve the fee change itself; under the rules of association it would be *ultra vires* without a general meeting.", "note": "Pronounced UL-tra VEE-reez or VY-reez. Lands in governance; check the constitution before asserting it, as confidence without the text backfires.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-73", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "delegate", "gloss": "A person exercising a power delegated by an authorised body or officer; the decision remains the delegate's own and must be made within the terms of the delegation.", "say": "As the principal's *delegate* for ensemble placements, I have to make the decision myself, within the policy, not simply endorse what a colleague prefers.", "note": "Lands when roles are muddled; a delegate who rubber-stamps another's decision exposes it to challenge.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-74", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "fettering discretion", "gloss": "Applying a policy so rigidly that a decision-maker fails to consider the merits of an individual case where the power calls for discretion.", "say": "The three-year commitment policy is sound, but refusing even to consider this family's circumstances would be *fettering discretion*.", "note": "Lands with administrative lawyers and senior public servants; it allows you to defend a policy while insisting on individual consideration.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-75", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "relevant and irrelevant considerations", "gloss": "Grounds of review where a decision-maker fails to take into account a matter it was bound to consider, or takes into account a matter it was bound to ignore.", "say": "A parent's seniority on the P&C is an *irrelevant consideration* in the audition result, and the record should show it played no part.", "note": "Lands in public decision-making; the relevant matters are usually found in the governing statute or policy, so name them.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-76", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "Wednesbury unreasonableness", "gloss": "From Associated Provincial Picture Houses v Wednesbury Corporation (1948): a decision so unreasonable that no reasonable authority could have made it; Australian law developed the concept further in Minister for Immigration v Li (2013).", "say": "I do not think the decision reaches *Wednesbury unreasonableness*; it is simply one I would not have made.", "note": "Pronounced WENZ-bree. A high threshold; using it for decisions you merely disagree with signals you do not know how high.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-77", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "reasons for decision", "gloss": "A statement of the findings, evidence and reasoning on which a decision rests; at common law there is no general duty to give reasons, as held in Public Service Board of NSW v Osmond (1986), though statute or policy may require them.", "say": "Whether or not we are obliged to, I would give the family written *reasons for decision*; it is the best protection we have.", "note": "Lands when you present reasons as good practice rather than a legal compulsion you cannot point to.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-78", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "qualified privilege", "gloss": "A defence in defamation for communications made in the performance of a duty or interest to someone with a corresponding duty or interest, which can be defeated by malice.", "say": "A frank reference to the principal is likely covered by *qualified privilege*; the same remark in the staff room is not.", "note": "Lands as a reason to confine sensitive information to proper channels; do not offer it as advice on a live dispute.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-79", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "Freedom of Information", "gloss": "The statutory right to access documents held by government agencies, in WA under the Freedom of Information Act 1992, subject to exemptions.", "say": "Write every file note as though it will be released under *Freedom of Information*, because some day one will be.", "note": "Usually shortened to FOI. Lands as a discipline on tone in records; exemptions exist, but do not rely on them when writing.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-80", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "in camera", "gloss": "In private: a meeting or part of a meeting closed to non-members or the public, typically for confidential personnel or commercial matters.", "say": "We will take the staffing item *in camera*, and the minute will record only the resolution.", "note": "Pronounced in KAM-er-a. Standard in board and council practice; overuse for routine business erodes the transparency a council owes.", "level": 3}]}, {"field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "intro": "Finance is spoken in a register of calm exactness: the native speaker names the basis of accounting, the period and the restriction before giving a number, and never lets a surplus, a reserve or a variance stand without saying what kind it is. Authority comes from distinctions (cash against accrual, restricted against designated, timing against permanent) delivered without drama, and from asking the question the auditor would ask before the auditor asks it.", "entries": [{"id": "finance-1", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "accrual basis", "gloss": "Accounting that recognises income when earned and expenses when incurred, regardless of when cash moves, as distinct from the cash basis.", "say": "On an *accrual basis* the Festival made a small loss; the bank balance looks healthy only because next year's sponsorship arrived in June.", "note": "Lands whenever a cash balance is being mistaken for a result. Backfires if used to dismiss a genuine cash problem: accruals do not pay invoices.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-2", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "cash basis", "gloss": "Accounting that records transactions only when money is received or paid, simple and transparent but blind to obligations and entitlements not yet settled.", "say": "The school's tour account is effectively kept on a *cash basis*, so it tells us nothing about the deposits we have already committed to.", "note": "Useful for naming why a small account misleads. Avoid implying it is improper; many small bodies legitimately run on it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-3", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "restricted funds", "gloss": "Money that may be spent only for a purpose specified by the donor or funder, as distinct from funds the board has merely chosen to set aside.", "say": "That grant is *restricted funds* for the regional workshops; we cannot use it to cover the venue shortfall, however tempting.", "note": "Lands as a firm boundary in board discussion. Say who imposed the restriction; otherwise people assume it is a board preference that can be lifted.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-4", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "unrestricted reserves", "gloss": "Accumulated funds the organisation may apply to any purpose within its objects, the true measure of its financial resilience.", "say": "Once you strip out the grants, our *unrestricted reserves* would carry us for about one bad year, not three.", "note": "The phrase that cuts through a flattering balance sheet. Pair it with a period of cover so it is not merely a number.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-5", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "contingency", "gloss": "A budgeted allowance for identifiable but uncertain costs within a specific project or year, as distinct from a reserve, which sits on the balance sheet across years.", "say": "I have built a ten per cent *contingency* into the tour budget, and I would ask that it is not treated as discretionary spending money.", "note": "Lands when you say what it is for and who may release it. Backfires if it quietly becomes padding; reviewers spot an unexplained round number.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-6", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "variance", "gloss": "The difference between budgeted and actual figures for a line or period, which demands an explanation rather than merely a report.", "say": "The *variance* on instrument repairs is mostly the two sousaphones; it is not a trend.", "note": "Always follow the figure with its cause. A variance report without commentary signals a treasurer who reads numbers but not the business.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-7", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "reforecast", "gloss": "A revised projection of the year-end position made part-way through the year, leaving the original approved budget intact as the benchmark.", "say": "Rather than amend the budget, I will bring a *reforecast* to the August meeting so we can see the year-end position honestly.", "note": "Signals discipline: you keep the budget as the yardstick and update the expectation. Do not call a reforecast a new budget, or you lose the comparison.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-8", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "operating surplus", "gloss": "The excess of recurrent income over recurrent expenditure for the period, excluding capital items and one-off gains.", "say": "The headline surplus is flattered by the bequest; the underlying *operating surplus* is close to nil.", "note": "Lands when separating the core business from windfalls. Specify whether depreciation is included, because people will ask.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-9", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "depreciation", "gloss": "The systematic allocation of an asset's cost over its useful life, a non-cash expense that reflects the using-up of the asset.", "say": "We are not losing money on the timpani; the deficit is mostly *depreciation*, which is exactly why we should be saving for their replacement.", "note": "Powerful when explaining an accounting deficit with healthy cash. Backfires if offered as reassurance, since it signals a replacement cost that is coming.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-10", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "recurrent versus capital", "gloss": "The distinction between ongoing operating expenditure consumed within the year and expenditure on assets that deliver benefit over several years.", "say": "This is a *recurrent versus capital* question: the piano is capital, but the tuning contract it brings with it is recurrent forever.", "note": "Lands when a one-off purchase hides a permanent cost. Funders and principals both respect someone who sees the recurrent tail of a capital request.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-12", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "in-kind contribution", "gloss": "A non-cash contribution of goods, services, space or labour, valued at a fair estimate and usually reported to funders separately from cash.", "say": "The school's *in-kind contribution* of the hall, staff time and equipment is worth more than the cash grant, and the application should say so.", "note": "Lands in funding applications that ask for co-contribution. Value it defensibly; inflated in-kind figures are easily recognised by experienced panels.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-13", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "cost recovery", "gloss": "Setting a charge to recover the cost of providing a service, without aiming at surplus, which raises the question of which costs are counted.", "say": "The camp levy is on a *cost recovery* basis, so if the bus quote rises, the levy rises; there is no margin to absorb it.", "note": "Clear and defensible with parents. Be ready to say whether overheads are included, because 'cost' is where the argument lives.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-14", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "audit versus review", "gloss": "An audit gives reasonable assurance and a positive opinion on the financial statements; a review gives limited assurance and a negative-form conclusion that nothing has come to the reviewer's attention.", "say": "Our revenue tier only requires a *review*, but given the grants I would argue for a full *audit*; it is a different level of assurance, not a more expensive version of the same thing.", "note": "Shows you know what the board is actually buying. Under the Associations Incorporation Act 2015 (WA) the association's revenue tier determines which is required, so check the tier before asserting either.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-15", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "going concern", "gloss": "The assumption that an entity will continue operating for the foreseeable future, on which the normal basis of preparing financial statements depends.", "say": "If the major sponsor walks, the question is not whether we have a bad year but whether we are still a *going concern*.", "note": "A serious phrase; use it only when the question is genuinely live, or you will alarm a board unnecessarily.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-16", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "solvency", "gloss": "The ability to pay debts as and when they fall due, the legal and practical test that matters more than a positive balance sheet.", "say": "We are asset-rich and cash-poor in June; *solvency* is the test, and it is the June cash position I am watching.", "note": "Lands precisely because it is about timing of payment, not net worth. Officers who incur debts they cannot pay take on real exposure, so the word carries weight.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-17", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "cash flow forecast", "gloss": "A month-by-month projection of cash receipts and payments that reveals timing gaps a balanced annual budget conceals.", "say": "The budget balances for the year, but the *cash flow forecast* shows we are short in May before ticket income lands.", "note": "The tool that separates treasurers from bookkeepers. Bring one whenever a board assumes a balanced budget means no cash risk.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-18", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "financial delegation", "gloss": "The authority, formally conferred and usually limited by amount and purpose, for a person to commit or approve expenditure on the organisation's behalf.", "say": "I am happy to sign this, but it is above my *financial delegation*, so it needs the principal's approval first.", "note": "Lands as a clean, impersonal reason to decline or escalate. Know your own limit exactly; vagueness about delegation reads as carelessness.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-19", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "segregation of duties", "gloss": "Dividing responsibilities so that no one person can initiate, approve, record and reconcile the same transaction.", "say": "I should not be both approving and paying the invoices; *segregation of duties* protects whoever holds this role, including me.", "note": "Frame it as protection for the person, not suspicion of them; that is how it lands well in small organisations where people feel trusted.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-20", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "bank reconciliation", "gloss": "The regular matching of the accounting records to the bank statement, explaining every difference, as the foundation control over cash.", "say": "Before we discuss the forecast, can I confirm the *bank reconciliation* is complete to the end of last month?", "note": "A quiet question that signals competence. Asking whether it was independently reviewed signals more.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-21", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "audit trail", "gloss": "The documented chain from source document through approval to ledger entry that lets any transaction be traced and verified.", "say": "The money was spent properly, but there is no *audit trail*: no quote, no approval, no receipt matched to the payment.", "note": "Lands when distinguishing proper conduct from provable conduct. Useful in a gentle conversation with a colleague who 'just paid it'.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-22", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "cost centre", "gloss": "A unit of the organisation to which costs are assigned for budgeting and accountability, without necessarily generating its own income.", "say": "Strings, bands and classroom music should be separate *cost centres*; at the moment one line hides three very different programs.", "note": "Useful when arguing for visibility of a program's real cost. Too many cost centres create administrative noise, so justify each one.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-23", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "carry forward", "gloss": "Unspent funds or budget authority approved to move into the next financial year rather than lapsing.", "say": "We need a formal *carry forward* of the uniform money, or it will be swept back into the general pool at year end.", "note": "Lands when you request it before the year end, with a stated purpose. Asked for after the event, it sounds like rescue.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-24", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "value for money", "gloss": "The best combination of cost, quality, fitness for purpose and whole-of-life outcome, which is not the same as the lowest price.", "say": "The cheapest quote is not *value for money* if we are re-hiring the PA every year because it fails.", "note": "Standard public-sector language and widely trusted. Back it with whole-of-life reasoning, or it sounds like a justification for preference.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-25", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "designated reserves", "gloss": "Unrestricted funds the board has chosen to earmark for a purpose, which the board can undesignate, as distinct from restricted funds, which only the funder can release.", "say": "The instrument replacement fund is a *designated reserve*, not a restricted one; this board created it and this board could release it.", "note": "The distinction many boards blur, and naming it shows command. Be careful not to sound as though you are inviting a raid on the reserve.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-26", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "reserves policy", "gloss": "A board-approved statement of the level of unrestricted reserves the organisation aims to hold, why, and what happens if it falls above or below that range.", "say": "Without a *reserves policy*, every surplus becomes an argument about spending it; I would like us to adopt one before we discuss the surplus.", "note": "Positions you as raising the governance level of the discussion. Bring a proposed range and a rationale rather than an abstract principle.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-27", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "months of operating cover", "gloss": "Unrestricted reserves expressed as the number of months of normal expenditure they could fund if income stopped.", "say": "Our reserves look large in dollars, but they are about four *months of operating cover*, which is modest for a body with one dominant sponsor.", "note": "Translates a balance into risk. Define operating expenditure for the calculation, since festival-month costs distort the average.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-28", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "favourable and adverse variance", "gloss": "A favourable variance improves the result against budget and an adverse variance worsens it, regardless of whether the line is income or expenditure.", "say": "The underspend on catering is a *favourable variance*, but it exists because we cancelled an event, so it is not good news.", "note": "Precise and slightly more senior than 'over' and 'under'. Lands best when you add the cause, as here.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-29", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "timing variance versus permanent variance", "gloss": "A timing variance will reverse within the year because money arrives or leaves later than phased; a permanent variance will persist to year end.", "say": "Most of this is a *timing variance*: the grant arrived in July rather than June. The only *permanent variance* is the insurance increase.", "note": "One of the most useful distinctions in a budget meeting; it calms the room about the right things and focuses it on the rest.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-30", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "budget phasing", "gloss": "Spreading an annual budget across months to match the expected pattern of income and spending, so that monthly comparisons are meaningful.", "say": "The report looks alarming because the budget was divided by twelve; with proper *budget phasing* the festival costs sit in August where they belong.", "note": "Lands when explaining a misleading monthly report. Suggesting it signals that you understand how a budget is used, not only set.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-31", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "income in advance", "gloss": "Cash received for goods or services not yet delivered, held as a liability until earned; also called deferred income.", "say": "The 2027 registration fees are *income in advance*; they are in the bank, but we owe the schools a festival for them.", "note": "Essential when a board feels rich in the months before an event. Auditors will expect it to sit on the balance sheet, not in this year's income.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-32", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "accrued expense", "gloss": "A cost incurred in the period but not yet invoiced or paid, recognised so that the period carries its true cost.", "say": "The adjudicators' fees for December need to go in as an *accrued expense*, or this year looks better than it was and next year worse.", "note": "Lands at year end and in handovers between treasurers. Without accruals, two consecutive years are both misstated.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-33", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "prepayment", "gloss": "Cash paid in one period for a benefit received in a later period, held as an asset until the benefit is consumed.", "say": "The venue deposit is a *prepayment* against next year's festival, not a cost of this one.", "note": "Pairs naturally with income in advance; using both correctly signals a clear grasp of period.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-34", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "capitalisation threshold", "gloss": "The minimum cost at which an item is recorded as an asset and depreciated rather than expensed immediately.", "say": "Under our *capitalisation threshold* the music stands are simply expensed; the marimba, though, should be on the asset register.", "note": "Lands when a large purchase distorts a year's result. Check the policy figure before you cite it; organisations set their own.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-35", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "useful life", "gloss": "The period over which an asset is expected to provide benefit, which determines its depreciation and signals when replacement must be funded.", "say": "If the *useful life* of the student instruments is ten years, then a tenth of the fleet should be replaced every year, and we are nowhere near that.", "note": "Converts depreciation into a replacement plan, which principals and councils find persuasive. Do not overstate precision; useful lives are estimates.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-36", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "non-cash expense", "gloss": "An expense recognised in the accounts that involves no cash payment in the period, such as depreciation or a provision.", "say": "The deficit is almost entirely *non-cash expense*; the question for the board is whether we are funding the replacement those numbers imply.", "note": "Lands when reconciling a loss with a healthy bank. The trap is to treat 'non-cash' as 'not real'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-37", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "full cost versus marginal cost", "gloss": "Full cost includes a fair share of overheads and fixed costs; marginal cost is only the extra cost of doing one more unit or activity.", "say": "On *marginal cost* the extra concert is almost free; on *full cost*, including staff time and venue wear, it is not, and we should price it honestly.", "note": "The heart of most arguments about pricing and 'just one more' activity. Name which basis you are using before anyone gives a figure.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-38", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "overheads", "gloss": "Indirect costs that support the organisation as a whole and are not attributable to a single program, such as insurance, audit fees and administration.", "say": "Funders increasingly accept a share of *overheads*; we should stop pretending the festival runs without an accountant and an insurer.", "note": "Lands in grant budgets where people under-claim. Call them indirect costs in funder language if the guidelines do.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-39", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "cross-subsidy", "gloss": "Using surplus from one activity or group to fund another, a legitimate policy when explicit and a governance problem when hidden.", "say": "The senior tour levy is a *cross-subsidy* of the junior ensembles; I am comfortable with that, but parents should know it is happening.", "note": "Lands when you make the subsidy visible and defend it on purpose. Uttered as an accusation, it can sour a parent meeting fast.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-40", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "break-even", "gloss": "The level of activity, such as ticket sales or enrolments, at which total income equals total costs.", "say": "At this ticket price *break-even* is about four hundred seats; the hall holds five hundred, so there is little margin for a wet night.", "note": "Converts budgets into something a committee can test against reality. Always state the assumptions behind the number.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-41", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "fixed versus variable costs", "gloss": "Fixed costs remain constant regardless of activity level within a range; variable costs rise and fall with activity.", "say": "The venue and the sound crew are *fixed costs*; only catering and medals are variable, so cutting numbers saves less than people think.", "note": "Lands when a committee assumes a smaller event is proportionally cheaper. Note that many fixed costs are fixed only within a range.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-42", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "management letter", "gloss": "The auditor's letter to those charged with governance setting out control weaknesses and recommendations found during the audit, separate from the audit opinion.", "say": "The opinion was clean, but the *management letter* raised the petty cash and the dual signatories, and I would like an action against each point.", "note": "Shows you know the opinion is not the whole story. A board that tracks management letter points from year to year is visibly well governed.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-43", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "materiality", "gloss": "The threshold above which a misstatement or omission could reasonably influence the decisions of users of the financial statements, judged by size and nature.", "say": "Forty dollars is not *material* to the accounts, but a forty-dollar payment to a committee member's business is material by its nature.", "note": "Lands when you show that materiality is qualitative as well as quantitative. Avoid using it to wave away small irregularities.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-44", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "unmodified opinion", "gloss": "The auditor's conclusion that the financial statements are presented fairly in all material respects, the technical term for what people call a clean audit.", "say": "We have an *unmodified opinion* again this year, with one emphasis of matter paragraph I will explain.", "note": "The correct term in audit reports; 'clean' is fine in conversation. Using the right word to an accountant on a board earns quiet credit.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-45", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "qualified opinion", "gloss": "An audit opinion that the statements are fairly presented except for a specified matter, either a material misstatement or an inability to obtain sufficient evidence.", "say": "Cash takings at the door are where small associations get a *qualified opinion*; the auditor simply cannot verify them.", "note": "Know that it is 'except for' and not a failure; adverse opinions and disclaimers are the graver forms. Raising it pre-emptively shows foresight.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-46", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "liquidity", "gloss": "The ability to meet short-term obligations from cash or assets readily convertible to cash, a timing measure distinct from overall solvency or net worth.", "say": "Our problem is *liquidity*, not solvency: the money is in a term deposit that matures a month after the invoices fall due.", "note": "Lands when separating a short-term squeeze from a structural problem. Do not use it as a softer synonym for insolvency.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-47", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "working capital", "gloss": "Current assets less current liabilities, the operating cushion available to fund the gap between paying costs and receiving income.", "say": "We need enough *working capital* to pay the venue and the buses six weeks before school registrations clear.", "note": "Precise in a board setting. In a parent forum, 'the cash to bridge the gap' says the same thing without the jargon.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-48", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "cash runway", "gloss": "The length of time an organisation can continue operating at its current rate of spending before cash runs out, absent new income.", "say": "If the sponsorship is not renewed, our *cash runway* is about seven months, which tells us when the board must decide, not merely that it must.", "note": "Borrowed from start-up language but useful because it converts reserves into a decision deadline. Sounds glib if used for a healthy organisation.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-49", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "delegation register", "gloss": "The formal record of who holds which financial and decision-making authorities, to what limit, and from whom they were delegated.", "say": "Could we confirm the *delegation register* reflects the new committee, because the bank mandate still lists last year's officers?", "note": "Lands at handover and after elections. It signals governance housekeeping without implying anyone has done wrong.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-50", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "related party transaction", "gloss": "A transaction between the organisation and a person or entity connected to someone with influence over it, which requires disclosure and arm's-length terms.", "say": "The printing quote from my brother-in-law's firm is a *related party transaction*; I will declare it, leave the room, and the board can decide on the merits.", "note": "Lands when you name your own conflict first; it makes the standard easy to apply to others. Treat it as disclosure, not as an accusation.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-51", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "provision", "gloss": "A liability of uncertain timing or amount recognised because a present obligation exists and the outflow can be reliably estimated, such as employee leave.", "say": "If we employ a festival coordinator, we must carry a *provision* for their leave; it is a real liability from the first month.", "note": "Lands with boards moving from volunteers to staff. Do not use 'provision' loosely for any money put aside; that is a designated reserve.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-52", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "contingent liability", "gloss": "A possible obligation dependent on an uncertain future event, or a present obligation that cannot yet be measured reliably, disclosed rather than recognised.", "say": "The dispute with the hire company is a *contingent liability*; it belongs in the notes until we know whether we owe anything.", "note": "Shows you know the difference between a liability and a risk. Raise it early with the auditor rather than at sign-off.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-53", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "commitments", "gloss": "Amounts the organisation has contractually or formally undertaken to spend, not yet incurred, which reduce the funds genuinely available.", "say": "There is forty thousand in the account, but twenty-eight of it is *commitments*; what is truly available is twelve.", "note": "Lands in every conversation where someone sees a balance and wants to spend it. In school finance the same idea is often called encumbered funds.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-54", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "matched funding", "gloss": "A funding arrangement where the funder contributes only in proportion to money raised or committed by the applicant or other sources.", "say": "The foundation offers *matched funding* dollar for dollar, so our P&C contribution effectively doubles; that is the case to put to them.", "note": "Lands as leverage in fundraising. Check whether in-kind counts towards the match; guidelines differ and panels notice.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-55", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "zero-based budgeting", "gloss": "Building each budget line from nil and justifying every amount afresh, as opposed to incremental budgeting, which adjusts last year's figures.", "say": "I do not want full *zero-based budgeting* every year, but once a cycle it is worth asking what each line would cost if we were starting today.", "note": "Lands as a periodic discipline. Proposed annually it sounds like an exhausting purge and invites resistance.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-56", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "one-line budget", "gloss": "The WA public school funding arrangement under which the school receives its resources as a single allocation, giving the principal discretion over how it is distributed.", "say": "Because the school runs on a *one-line budget*, the specialist program is a choice the school makes each year, which is why we have to argue its value internally.", "note": "Lands with principals and councils because it names where the discretion really sits. Pair it with the Student-Centred Funding Model only when you are sure of the detail.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-57", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "internal controls", "gloss": "The policies and procedures that safeguard assets, ensure reliable records and prevent or detect error and fraud.", "say": "This is not a question of trust; our *internal controls* should be designed so that nobody has to be trusted alone.", "note": "The standard umbrella term. Become specific quickly (approvals, reconciliations, signatories), or it sounds like compliance theatre.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-58", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "structural deficit", "gloss": "A deficit that persists because recurrent costs exceed recurrent income under normal conditions, as distinct from a one-off deficit caused by a particular event.", "say": "Last year's loss was a one-off; what worries me is that, without the bequest, we would be running a *structural deficit* of about eight thousand a year.", "note": "The phrase that moves a board from 'bad luck' to 'we must change something'. Make sure the numbers support it before you say it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-59", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "ring-fenced", "gloss": "Formally protected for a specific purpose and shielded from reallocation, whether by external restriction or by internal decision.", "say": "I would like the instrument fund *ring-fenced* in the minutes, so it survives a change of principal or committee.", "note": "Lands when you say who did the fencing and how. Overused in public-sector speech; one use per meeting is plenty.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-60", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "drawdown on reserves", "gloss": "The deliberate use of accumulated reserves to fund a deficit or project, which should be approved and time-limited.", "say": "I am comfortable with a planned *drawdown on reserves* for the anniversary year, provided we agree the floor we will not go below.", "note": "Lands when framed as a decision with a limit. An unplanned drawdown is simply a loss; calling it a drawdown afterwards fools nobody.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-61", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "virement", "gloss": "The authorised transfer of budget provision from one line or head to another within an approved total.", "say": "Rather than seek new money, I propose a *virement* from the unused travel line to accompanist fees, within the approved total.", "note": "Precise and Commonwealth public-finance in flavour; lands with accountants and senior public servants. Elsewhere 'reallocation' is safer; pronunciation varies between anglicised and French forms, and either passes.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-62", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "hypothecation", "gloss": "Earmarking a particular revenue stream to fund a specific expenditure, so that the spending rises and falls with that source.", "say": "I would resist *hypothecation* of the bar takings to the scholarship; a good scholarship should not depend on how thirsty the audience is.", "note": "High register, from public finance; lands in a sophisticated board. Avoid it in parent forums, where it sounds like obfuscation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-63", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "fungibility", "gloss": "The quality of money being interchangeable, so that restricting one source may simply free other money for the purpose the restriction meant to prevent.", "say": "Because money is *fungible*, a grant for instruments still lets the school move its own instrument money elsewhere; the funder knows this, and so should we.", "note": "Lands when explaining why funders ask for additionality. Use sparingly; it can sound like a lecture in economics.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-64", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "emphasis of matter", "gloss": "A paragraph in an audit report drawing attention to a matter properly disclosed in the statements that is fundamental to users' understanding, without modifying the opinion.", "say": "The *emphasis of matter* about our reliance on one sponsor is not a criticism; it is the auditor making sure no one can say they were not told.", "note": "Lands when calming a board that has misread an audit report. Make clear it is not a qualification.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-65", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "cut-off", "gloss": "The principle and audit test that transactions are recorded in the correct period, especially around the year end.", "say": "The auditor will test *cut-off*, so the December invoices dated in January need to be accrued properly this time.", "note": "An auditor's word; using it shows you know where year-end errors live. Unknown to most lay board members, so explain it once.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-66", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "sinking fund", "gloss": "A fund built by regular contributions to meet a known future expenditure, such as asset replacement, as it falls due.", "say": "Every hire of the school's instruments should contribute to a *sinking fund*; otherwise the replacement bill arrives as a crisis every decade.", "note": "Slightly old-fashioned, which gives it authority with older board members and accountants. 'Replacement fund' works for everyone else.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-67", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "headroom", "gloss": "The margin between a current position and a limit, such as available cash above a minimum reserve or budget left before a cap.", "say": "We have some *headroom* above the reserve floor, but not enough to underwrite a second venue.", "note": "Common in Treasury and banking speech; precise and unshowy. Specify what limit the headroom is measured against.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-68", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "contribution margin", "gloss": "Income from an activity less its variable costs, showing what the activity contributes towards fixed costs and surplus.", "say": "The workshops lose money on full cost, but their *contribution margin* is positive, so cancelling them would make the overall result worse.", "note": "Wins arguments about cutting apparently loss-making activities. Requires a clean fixed and variable split, or the number is easily challenged.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-69", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "substance over form", "gloss": "The principle that transactions are accounted for according to their economic reality rather than their legal or documentary form.", "say": "On paper it is a donation, but in *substance* it is a sponsorship with obligations attached; we should treat it as such.", "note": "Lands with accountants and lawyers, and in disputes over how a deal is described. Can sound pedantic if the distinction changes nothing.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-70", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "prudence", "gloss": "In accounting, the exercise of caution under uncertainty so that assets and income are not overstated and liabilities and expenses are not understated.", "say": "I have budgeted ticket sales at last year's level as a matter of *prudence*; if they grow, that is a pleasant surprise rather than a hole.", "note": "The technical sense of an ordinary word; signals training. Do not let it justify deliberately understated budgets, which is its own distortion.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-71", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "matching principle", "gloss": "The principle that expenses are recognised in the same period as the income they help to generate.", "say": "Under the *matching principle*, the festival's costs and its fees belong in the same year, even though the cash spans two.", "note": "Precise and somewhat textbook; lands with a treasurer handing over to a successor. In the boardroom, 'same year' usually suffices.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-72", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "nugatory expenditure", "gloss": "Spending that yields no benefit because the purpose for which it was incurred has fallen away, such as fees for a cancelled booking.", "say": "The deposit on the cancelled tour is *nugatory expenditure*; we should record why it happened, not just write it off.", "note": "A Whitehall term, still current in UK public finance; sounds distinctly mandarin. Lands with senior public servants; elsewhere say 'wasted' or 'sunk'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-73", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "imprest", "gloss": "A system in which a fixed float is held and periodically replenished to exactly that amount on presentation of receipts.", "say": "Petty cash should run on an *imprest* of two hundred dollars: receipts plus cash should always equal two hundred.", "note": "Old and exact; accountants recognise it instantly. To others explain it once, then use it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-74", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "material uncertainty related to going concern", "gloss": "The auditing-standards language for a significant doubt about an entity's ability to continue, which must be disclosed in the statements and highlighted in the audit report.", "say": "If we sign the venue contract without the sponsorship confirmed, the auditor may well report a *material uncertainty related to going concern*.", "note": "The exact phrase from audit reports; using it shows you know what follows if the board takes a risk. Reserve it for when that consequence is genuinely possible.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-75", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "management override", "gloss": "The ability of those in authority to bypass controls, which auditors treat as a fraud risk present in every entity regardless of other controls.", "say": "Our controls are fine for volunteers; the risk is *management override*, which is why even the president's reimbursements need a second signature.", "note": "Lands as an impersonal reason for applying controls to senior people. Phrase it as design, not suspicion.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-76", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "reasonable assurance versus limited assurance", "gloss": "Reasonable assurance, given in an audit, is a high but not absolute level of confidence; limited assurance, given in a review, is meaningfully lower and expressed negatively.", "say": "A funder asking for *reasonable assurance* is asking for an audit, not a review; we should budget for the difference.", "note": "Precise language that accountants and funders respect. Do not claim an audit guarantees the absence of fraud; 'reasonable' is the point.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-77", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "below the line", "gloss": "Items presented after the operating result, such as one-off, capital or extraordinary items, so that the core result is shown separately.", "say": "I would present the bequest *below the line*, so the board can see the operating result without it.", "note": "Lands in discussing how a report is presented. Usage varies between organisations, so say which line you mean.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-79", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "tone at the top", "gloss": "The ethical climate set by leaders' behaviour towards controls, which auditors and regulators treat as the foundation of the control environment.", "say": "If I bypass the purchase order process, I cannot expect anyone else to follow it; the *tone at the top* is the control.", "note": "Lands when applied to yourself first. Applied to others, it can sound like a pointed rebuke.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-80", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "run-rate", "gloss": "The annualised level of income or expenditure implied by current performance, used to project the year end.", "say": "On the current *run-rate*, accompanist costs will exceed budget by about a third, so this is the moment to adjust, not November.", "note": "Corporate and slightly brisk; precise in a finance committee. Misleads when income or spending is seasonal, so qualify it in a festival body.", "level": 3}]}, {"field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "intro": "Strategy and leadership talk is easily captured by management jargon, so the native register is the opposite: plain nouns, named trade-offs, and concepts used with their source in mind rather than as slogans. The senior speaker sounds native by diagnosing before prescribing, by saying what will not be done, and by knowing where each borrowed idea (Heifetz, Schein, Rumelt, Kotter) stops being useful.", "entries": [{"id": "strategy-1", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "strategic intent", "gloss": "A concise statement of the position an organisation is committed to reaching over the long term, which sets direction without specifying every step.", "say": "Our *strategic intent* is that every student who auditions for the program finishes Year 12 still playing; everything in this plan should be tested against that.", "note": "Lands when it is a single, testable sentence. Backfires when it is a list of values, which is a mission statement wearing a suit.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-3", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "capability", "gloss": "The skills, knowledge and systems an organisation or person possesses to do a kind of work well, as distinct from capacity, which is the volume of work they can absorb.", "say": "We have the *capability* to run a jazz workshop series; what we do not have is the capacity this term.", "note": "Precise when kept apart from capacity. 'Capability uplift' is public-service jargon and grates outside government.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-4", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "capacity", "gloss": "The amount of work, time or resource available to be applied, regardless of how skilled the people are.", "say": "This is not a question of willingness; the department is at *capacity*, and adding a fifth ensemble means removing something else.", "note": "The cleanest way to decline without seeming unwilling. Back it with a concrete account of current load.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-5", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "sequencing", "gloss": "Deciding the order in which changes are introduced so that each builds the conditions the next requires.", "say": "I agree with all three proposals; my concern is *sequencing*. The timetable change has to land before the new ensembles can work.", "note": "Lets you support an idea while slowing it down, which is often the real point. Lands when you can say why the order matters.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-6", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "trade-off", "gloss": "A choice in which gaining more of one desired thing necessarily means accepting less of another.", "say": "The honest *trade-off* is breadth against depth: we can offer more ensembles or rehearse fewer of them properly.", "note": "Naming the trade-off openly is a mark of senior judgement. Backfires when presented as if the room had not already seen it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-7", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "opportunity cost", "gloss": "The value of the best alternative forgone when a resource is committed to one use.", "say": "The *opportunity cost* of my running the tour is a term of curriculum work I will not do.", "note": "Lands when the forgone alternative is named specifically. In the abstract it sounds like an economics tutorial.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-8", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "program sustainability", "gloss": "The capacity of a program to continue at its intended quality without depending on extraordinary effort, a single person or one-off funding.", "say": "The jazz program is thriving, but it is not *sustainable* if it depends on me giving up every Thursday night.", "note": "Lands when it means durability of quality, not just survival. Be ready to show what makes it unsustainable, or it sounds like a complaint.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-9", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "succession planning", "gloss": "Deliberately preparing people and systems so that key roles can be filled without loss of continuity when incumbents leave.", "say": "If I were hit by a bus tomorrow, there is no *succession planning* for the Festival's finances; that is a governance risk, not a personal one.", "note": "Lands when framed around the organisation, not the incumbent. Raised by the incumbent, it signals security and maturity.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-10", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "distributed leadership", "gloss": "Leadership understood as practice spread across many people and their interactions with the situation, not as the actions of one positional leader.", "say": "*Distributed leadership* is not delegation with a nicer name; the string and guitar programs need genuine authority over their own decisions.", "note": "Associated with James Spillane's work in schools. Backfires when used to dress up offloading work without authority.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-11", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "adaptive versus technical problem", "gloss": "Ronald Heifetz's distinction between problems solvable with existing expertise and problems that require people to change their values, habits or loyalties.", "say": "Rehearsal attendance is not a *technical problem* we can fix with a new roll system; it is an *adaptive* one about what students believe the ensemble is for.", "note": "The single most useful diagnostic in the field. It backfires if used to label every disagreement adaptive, which can sound like avoiding a straightforward fix.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-12", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "change fatigue", "gloss": "The exhaustion and cynicism that build when people face continual or poorly integrated change, reducing their willingness to engage with the next one.", "say": "After the syllabus change and the new reporting system, there is real *change fatigue* in this department; I would rather do one thing well this year.", "note": "Lands when used to argue for sequencing, not to resist all change. Leaders who invoke it about others but never about themselves are noticed.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-13", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "burning platform", "gloss": "A crisis framing used to convince people that staying put is more dangerous than changing, after Daryl Conner's image drawn from the Piper Alpha oil platform disaster.", "say": "I do not want to sell this as a *burning platform*; nothing is on fire, and people will feel manipulated if we pretend otherwise.", "note": "Its limit is the point: fear produces compliance and short bursts, not sustained commitment, and a manufactured crisis spends trust you will need later.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-14", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "organisational culture", "gloss": "The shared assumptions, norms and habits that shape how people in an organisation actually behave, especially when no one is watching.", "say": "The policy says students may leave ensembles freely; the *organisational culture* says they will be judged if they do.", "note": "Lands when tied to specific behaviour. The line 'culture eats strategy for breakfast' is widely attributed to Peter Drucker without a clear source, so do not cite him for it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-15", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "stakeholder mapping", "gloss": "Identifying the people and groups affected by or able to affect a decision, and analysing their interest and influence.", "say": "Before the council paper goes up, I have done some *stakeholder mapping*: the feeder primary principals matter more here than the P&C.", "note": "A useful private discipline. Announcing it to stakeholders can make them feel managed rather than consulted.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-16", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "quick win", "gloss": "An early, visible success that builds credibility and momentum for a larger change.", "say": "The new rehearsal schedule is a *quick win*; it costs nothing and parents will notice it in a week.", "note": "Lands when it genuinely serves the larger change. A series of quick wins without a strategy is simply activity.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-17", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "pilot", "gloss": "A limited, bounded trial of a change, designed in advance to answer specific questions before any decision to scale.", "say": "Let us *pilot* the combined ensemble with Year 8 only, and agree now what result would make us extend or stop it.", "note": "Lands when success and failure criteria are set before it starts. A pilot with no criteria is a launch without the courage to say so.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-18", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "scaling", "gloss": "Extending a practice beyond its original setting while preserving what made it work.", "say": "The feeder band works because three principals trust each other; *scaling* it to ten schools is a different program, not a bigger one.", "note": "Lands when you identify what may not survive scaling. Assuming success transfers unchanged is the common error.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-19", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "key-person risk", "gloss": "The exposure created when an organisation's capability or knowledge is concentrated in one individual.", "say": "The festival's sponsor relationships are a *key-person risk*; they live in one person's phone.", "note": "Precise and impersonal; lands well in a board. Applied to a colleague in their presence, frame it as a compliment to their importance.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-20", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "institutional memory", "gloss": "The accumulated knowledge of an organisation's history, decisions and reasons, whether held in records or in long-serving people.", "say": "We tried a Saturday festival in 2014 and it failed for reasons nobody wrote down; our *institutional memory* is a set of anecdotes.", "note": "Lands when arguing for documentation. Leaning on it too heavily ('we tried that') can sound like a reason never to try again.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-21", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "implementation dip", "gloss": "Michael Fullan's term for the temporary decline in performance and confidence that typically follows the introduction of a new practice before benefits appear.", "say": "Expect an *implementation dip* in the first term of the new assessment model; if results fall in Term 1, that is not evidence it has failed.", "note": "Lands when predicted in advance, which protects the change. Invoked only after results fall, it sounds like an excuse.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-22", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "lead and lag indicators", "gloss": "Lag indicators measure outcomes after the fact; lead indicators measure earlier behaviours that predict those outcomes and can still be influenced.", "say": "ATAR results are a *lag indicator*; Year 10 retention into the elective is the *lead indicator* we can actually act on.", "note": "Lands in planning conversations obsessed with outcome data. Make sure the lead indicator genuinely predicts the outcome.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-23", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "logic model", "gloss": "A structured chain from inputs and activities to outputs, outcomes and impact, used to plan and evaluate a program.", "say": "The funder wants a *logic model*, so let us be clear that the number of workshops is an output, not an outcome.", "note": "Expected in funding applications. The common error, confusing outputs with outcomes, is exactly what panels look for.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-24", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "buy-in versus ownership", "gloss": "Buy-in is agreement to someone else's plan; ownership is the commitment that comes from having shaped it.", "say": "I do not want *buy-in* from the instrumental staff; I want *ownership*, which means they help write the plan rather than receive it.", "note": "Lands as a stance on consultation. Promising ownership and then delivering a finished plan does lasting damage.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-25", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "deliberate versus emergent strategy", "gloss": "Henry Mintzberg's distinction between strategy realised as intended and strategy that forms from patterns of action in the absence of, or despite, intentions.", "say": "The jazz program was never in a plan; it is an *emergent strategy* that the school should now decide whether to make *deliberate*.", "note": "Lands when legitimising what has grown organically, and when asking a board to own it. Avoid implying that planning is pointless.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-26", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "the kernel of strategy", "gloss": "Richard Rumelt's three-part core of good strategy: a diagnosis of the challenge, a guiding policy for dealing with it, and coherent actions that carry it out.", "say": "Our plan has goals but no *kernel*: no diagnosis of why students leave after Year 9, and no guiding policy for responding.", "note": "Devastatingly effective in reviewing a plan that is all aspiration. Rumelt also names 'mistaking goals for strategy' as bad strategy, which is often the exact problem.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-27", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "guiding policy", "gloss": "In Rumelt's terms, the overall approach chosen to deal with the diagnosed challenge, which rules some actions in and others out.", "say": "The *guiding policy* is depth before breadth: no new ensembles until existing ones are properly staffed.", "note": "Lands when it excludes something; a guiding policy that rules out nothing is not one.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-28", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "initiative overload", "gloss": "The condition in which too many concurrent initiatives fragment attention and resources so that none is properly implemented; colloquially, initiativitis.", "say": "Our problem is *initiative overload*: seven priorities, each reasonable, together impossible.", "note": "'Initiativitis' raises a smile and lands well in a staff meeting; in a formal paper, 'initiative overload' reads better.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-29", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "absorptive capacity", "gloss": "An organisation's ability to recognise, assimilate and apply new knowledge, which depends on what it already knows; from Wesley Cohen and Daniel Levinthal.", "say": "The new syllabus resources are excellent, but small schools lack the *absorptive capacity* to use them without professional learning.", "note": "Lands in SCSA and system-level discussions about rollout. In a department meeting, 'room to take it in' says the same.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-30", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "critical mass", "gloss": "The minimum scale at which an activity becomes self-sustaining or viable in quality.", "say": "Two bassoons is not *critical mass*; we either recruit a section or arrange the parts elsewhere.", "note": "Lands when the threshold is defined. Used vaguely, it becomes a reason for growth for its own sake.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-31", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "bench strength", "gloss": "The depth of capable people ready to step into key roles, an indicator of succession readiness.", "say": "We have strong leaders in the Festival committee, but very little *bench strength*; most of us will retire from it together.", "note": "Sporting metaphor used in corporate and board settings. Lands well; slightly brisk for parent bodies.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-32", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "single point of failure", "gloss": "A component, role or person whose failure alone would halt the whole system.", "say": "The booking system is a *single point of failure*: one login, one person, no backup.", "note": "Borrowed from engineering; precise and unemotional. More neutral than 'key-person risk' when the issue is a system rather than a person.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-33", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "sunk cost", "gloss": "Cost already incurred and unrecoverable, which rationally should not influence decisions about the future.", "say": "The two years we spent on the old model are a *sunk cost*; the question is only which option is better from here.", "note": "Lands as a release for people anchored to past effort. Be gentle; the people who did the work may hear it as dismissal.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-34", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "path dependence", "gloss": "The way earlier decisions constrain later options, so that history, not current merit, explains the present arrangement.", "say": "Our ensemble structure is *path dependent*: it reflects the staff we had in 2010, not the students we have now.", "note": "Lands when explaining why an odd arrangement persists without blaming anyone. Do not use it to imply that change is impossible.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-35", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "mission drift", "gloss": "The gradual movement of an organisation away from its founding purpose, usually through accumulated pragmatic decisions or funding pressures.", "say": "Every sponsorship ask pushes us towards gala events; I worry about *mission drift* away from the student festival we exist to run.", "note": "Lands in boards of purpose-driven bodies. Be specific about the drift, or it sounds nostalgic.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-36", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "strategic drift", "gloss": "Gerry Johnson's term for the gradual divergence between an organisation's strategy and its changing environment, often unnoticed because change is incremental.", "say": "We have not done anything wrong; we have experienced *strategic drift*. The schools changed and we kept running the 2015 festival.", "note": "Lands as a non-blaming account of decline. Distinguish it from mission drift: one is losing the environment, the other losing the purpose.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-37", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "loose-tight", "gloss": "Holding a few things non-negotiable while leaving everything else to local discretion, after Tom Peters and Robert Waterman's 'simultaneous loose-tight properties'.", "say": "We should be *loose-tight*: tight on the assessment standards and the three-year commitment, loose on how each teacher runs their ensemble.", "note": "Lands as a principled answer to autonomy debates. Only works if the tight items are genuinely few.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-38", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "subsidiarity", "gloss": "The principle that decisions should be taken at the lowest level competent to make them, with higher levels acting only where they add value.", "say": "On *subsidiarity* grounds, ensemble repertoire belongs with the director, not with me or with council.", "note": "Originally from Catholic social teaching and later European Union law; lands with policy and governance people. Sounds grand in a department meeting.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-39", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "guiding coalition", "gloss": "John Kotter's term for a group with enough credibility, expertise and authority to lead a change, assembled before the change is announced.", "say": "Before we take the proposal to the whole staff, I want a *guiding coalition*: the HOLA, one senior instrumental teacher and a parent with standing.", "note": "Lands when it is genuinely a coalition and not a group of allies. Kotter's eight-step model is often treated as a recipe, which he did not intend everyone to follow mechanically.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-40", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "sense of urgency", "gloss": "In Kotter's model, the shared recognition that change is needed now, as the first condition for any significant change.", "say": "There is no *sense of urgency* because enrolments are fine this year; the risk is three years out.", "note": "Lands as a diagnosis of why nothing moves. Manufacturing urgency is the burning platform's problem in milder form.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-41", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "getting on the balcony", "gloss": "Heifetz and Linsky's image for stepping back from the action to observe patterns, including one's own part in them, before re-entering.", "say": "Let me *get on the balcony* for a moment: we have had this same argument about rehearsal times three meetings in a row.", "note": "Lands when you use it to reframe a stuck discussion. Saying it too often makes it a mannerism.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-42", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "critical path", "gloss": "The sequence of dependent tasks that determines the earliest possible completion date, where any delay delays the whole.", "say": "The venue contract is on the *critical path*; nothing else in the festival plan matters until that is signed.", "note": "From project management; precise and useful for committees that treat all tasks as equally urgent.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-43", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "change readiness", "gloss": "The degree to which people and systems are prepared, in belief, capacity and capability, to adopt a particular change.", "say": "The idea is sound but the *change readiness* is not there; half the staff do not yet see the problem it solves.", "note": "Lands as a reason to prepare rather than abandon. Can sound like a polite way of blaming staff for reluctance.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-44", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "institutionalise", "gloss": "To embed a practice in structures, roles, budgets and routines so that it persists beyond the people who introduced it.", "say": "The feeder band only works because I run it; until we *institutionalise* it in the budget and a role description, it is a personal project.", "note": "Lands when connected to succession and sustainability. Avoid in emotional settings; it sounds bureaucratic.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-45", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "load-bearing", "gloss": "Describes a program, role or practice that other parts of the organisation depend on, so that removing it causes unexpected collapse.", "say": "The Year 7 band camp looks like a luxury, but it is *load-bearing*: it is where the three-year commitment is formed.", "note": "A vivid architectural metaphor that lands with principals deciding what to cut. Use once; it loses force with repetition.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-46", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "early adopters", "gloss": "In Everett Rogers's diffusion of innovations, the respected minority who take up a change early and influence the majority who follow.", "say": "I would start with the *early adopters* in the instrumental team and let the rest see it working before we ask them.", "note": "Lands as a practical rollout strategy. Avoid casting the remainder as 'laggards', Rogers's term, in their hearing.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-47", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "espoused values versus basic assumptions", "gloss": "Edgar Schein's distinction between the values an organisation professes and the unspoken assumptions that actually drive behaviour, with visible artefacts as the third, surface level.", "say": "Our *espoused value* is inclusion; our *basic assumption*, judging by who gets solos, is that talent is fixed by Year 7.", "note": "The sharpest tool in Schein's model. Lands best as self-diagnosis; directed at another department it is an accusation.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-48", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "espoused theory versus theory-in-use", "gloss": "Chris Argyris and Donald Schön's distinction between how people say they act and the theory that can be inferred from how they actually act.", "say": "Our *espoused theory* is that ensembles are for everyone; our *theory-in-use* is that the strongest players get the best rehearsal time.", "note": "Lands in reflective leadership settings and with academics. Like Schein's version, it is safest applied to your own group first.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-49", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "double-loop learning", "gloss": "Argyris's term for learning that questions the underlying goals and assumptions behind an action, rather than only adjusting the action (single-loop learning).", "say": "Changing the audition date is single-loop; asking whether we should audition at all is *double-loop learning*.", "note": "Lands in reviews where adjustments keep failing. With the uninitiated, explain single-loop in the same breath.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-50", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "psychological safety", "gloss": "Amy Edmondson's term for a shared belief that a team is safe for interpersonal risk-taking, such as admitting mistakes or raising concerns.", "say": "If no one in this department ever disagrees with me, that is not harmony; it may be a lack of *psychological safety*.", "note": "Lands when the leader turns it on themselves. Widely misused to mean comfort or the absence of challenge; Edmondson's sense includes high standards.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-51", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "small wins", "gloss": "Karl Weick's argument that large problems are better tackled as a series of concrete, completed outcomes of modest importance that change perceptions and build momentum.", "say": "This is a *small wins* strategy: fix the storeroom, then the loan system, then the repair budget, and the bigger argument makes itself.", "note": "Lands with people overwhelmed by a large problem. It is a considered strategy, not the same as a quick win.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-52", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "premortem", "gloss": "Gary Klein's technique of imagining that a plan has already failed and asking the team to explain why, surfacing risks that optimism suppresses.", "say": "Before council approves the tour, can we do a *premortem*: it is next December and the tour was a disaster; what went wrong?", "note": "Lands as a constructive way to voice doubts without seeming obstructive. Run it briskly; a long premortem becomes a wake.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-53", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "wicked problem", "gloss": "Horst Rittel and Melvin Webber's term for a problem with no definitive formulation, no stopping rule and no right answer, only better or worse responses.", "say": "Equity of access to instrumental music is a *wicked problem*; any solution changes the problem, so we should judge options, not seek the answer.", "note": "Lands in policy and academic rooms. Overused for problems that are merely hard; reserve it for genuinely contested ones.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-54", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "transition versus change", "gloss": "William Bridges's distinction between the external event of change and the internal psychological process of transition people go through to accept it.", "say": "The timetable change happens on day one; the *transition* for the staff who lose their Friday rehearsal will take a year.", "note": "Lands when leaders confuse announcing a change with completing it. Bridges's point is that transition starts with an ending, not a beginning.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-55", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "neutral zone", "gloss": "Bridges's term for the uncomfortable in-between phase after the old way has ended and before the new way is established.", "say": "We are in the *neutral zone*: the old ensemble structure is gone and the new one does not yet feel like ours.", "note": "Lands by normalising discomfort rather than treating it as failure. Saying it too early can seem to excuse poor planning.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-56", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "fidelity versus adaptation", "gloss": "The tension in implementation between delivering a program exactly as designed and adapting it to local context.", "say": "With the new syllabus resources, the question is *fidelity versus adaptation*: which elements are core, and which can schools reshape?", "note": "Lands in SCSA and system discussions. The useful move is to name the core components that require fidelity.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-57", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "stop-doing list", "gloss": "A deliberate list of activities the organisation will cease, popularised by Jim Collins, so that new priorities are resourced from existing capacity.", "say": "If this plan adds three things, I would like a *stop-doing list* with three things on it as well.", "note": "Lands well because it is concrete and fair. The difficulty is political, so propose your own items first.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-58", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "decision rights", "gloss": "The explicit allocation of who has the authority to make which decisions, as distinct from who is consulted or informed.", "say": "Most of our conflict is about *decision rights*: nobody is sure whether repertoire is my call, the director's or the principal's.", "note": "Lands as a structural diagnosis that depersonalises conflict. Particularly useful in role-definition discussions.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-59", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "change saturation", "gloss": "The point at which the total volume of concurrent change exceeds people's capacity to absorb it, regardless of the merit of any single change.", "say": "Each change is reasonable; together they exceed the department's capacity. We have reached *change saturation*, not resistance.", "note": "More precise than change fatigue because it is about volume, not mood. Lands when you can list the concurrent changes.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-60", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "holding environment", "gloss": "Heifetz's term, borrowed from the psychoanalyst Donald Winnicott, for the bonds, structures and trust that contain the stress of adaptive work so people can stay engaged.", "say": "The department meeting has to be a *holding environment* for this argument, not a place where it is settled by whoever speaks loudest.", "note": "Lands with people who know the literature. To others, explain it as keeping people in the room while it is hard.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-61", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "productive zone of disequilibrium", "gloss": "Heifetz's band of stress high enough to motivate adaptive work but not so high that people become overwhelmed or retreat.", "say": "Our job as leaders is to keep the staff in the *productive zone of disequilibrium*: uncomfortable enough to change, not so stressed they shut down.", "note": "High register and recognisable to leadership scholars. 'Regulating the heat' is Heifetz's plainer phrase and often lands better.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-62", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "work avoidance", "gloss": "Heifetz's term for the ways groups evade adaptive challenges, such as scapegoating, denial, a premature technical fix or diverting attention.", "say": "Commissioning another review is *work avoidance*; we already know the diagnosis.", "note": "Sharp and accurate; lands in a confident group. Directed at a superior's proposal, it can sound insolent.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-63", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "giving the work back", "gloss": "Heifetz's principle that leaders should return adaptive problems to the people who must change, rather than solving them on their behalf.", "say": "I could write the ensemble policy myself, but this is a case for *giving the work back* to the directors; they are the ones who have to live with it.", "note": "Lands as a deliberate leadership choice. If the group lacks the capacity, it looks like abdication.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-64", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "isomorphism", "gloss": "Paul DiMaggio and Walter Powell's term for the tendency of organisations in a field to become alike through coercive, mimetic and normative pressures.", "say": "Every specialist program in the state now looks the same; that is *isomorphism*, and it is not the same thing as best practice.", "note": "Lands in academic and policy rooms; signals sociology training. Explain the three pressures if you use it with a lay board.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-65", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "ambidexterity", "gloss": "An organisation's ability to exploit existing strengths efficiently while also exploring new possibilities, after James March's distinction between exploitation and exploration.", "say": "The Festival needs *ambidexterity*: run the core event flawlessly and reserve some capacity to experiment with the regional format.", "note": "Lands in strategy discussions with educated boards. Ambitious organisations often stop exploring once exploitation works.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-66", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "muddling through", "gloss": "Charles Lindblom's account of policy-making as successive limited comparisons, incremental steps adjusted by feedback, rather than comprehensive rational planning.", "say": "In defence of *muddling through*: we do not know enough to design the perfect intake model, so let us change one variable a year and watch.", "note": "A respectable position when cited knowingly; Lindblom's 1959 paper is a classic. Said without the reference, it sounds like an admission of drift.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-67", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "bounded rationality", "gloss": "Herbert Simon's concept that decision-makers are limited by information, time and cognitive capacity, so they cannot optimise and instead seek acceptable solutions.", "say": "Given *bounded rationality*, the committee will decide on what is in the paper; so the paper must carry the real trade-offs, not an appendix.", "note": "Lands when designing decision processes. As an excuse for a poor decision, it sounds evasive.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-68", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "satisficing", "gloss": "Simon's term for choosing the first option that meets an acceptable threshold rather than searching for the optimal one.", "say": "For the bus contract, *satisficing* is the right approach; three acceptable quotes, pick the best value, move on.", "note": "Lands with precision when deployed as a deliberate method. Without context, people hear a typo for 'satisfying'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-69", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "requisite variety", "gloss": "W. Ross Ashby's law that a system can control its environment only if it has at least as much variety in its responses as the environment presents.", "say": "A single audition format lacks *requisite variety*; the students arriving are too different for one test to read them all fairly.", "note": "Cybernetic and genuinely rare; lands with systems thinkers and some academics. Elsewhere it sounds abstruse.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-70", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "loosely coupled system", "gloss": "Karl Weick's description of educational organisations as made of units that are responsive to each other yet retain their own identity and separateness, so change at one point travels weakly.", "say": "Schools are *loosely coupled systems*; a directive from the head of department does not reach the rehearsal room unchanged.", "note": "Lands with educational researchers and senior system leaders. Weick saw loose coupling as having advantages too, so do not present it only as a fault.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-71", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "decoupling", "gloss": "John Meyer and Brian Rowan's observation that organisations often separate formal structures, adopted for legitimacy, from the actual work, which proceeds differently.", "say": "The policy satisfies the review panel; what happens in the practice room is *decoupled* from it entirely.", "note": "Precise in institutional theory and with academic committees. Can sound like an accusation of hypocrisy if not framed as a structural observation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-72", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "garbage can model", "gloss": "Michael Cohen, James March and Johan Olsen's account of decision-making in 'organised anarchies' as the chance collision of problems, solutions, participants and choice opportunities.", "say": "This committee follows the *garbage can model*: the solution walked in looking for a problem, and the agenda supplied one.", "note": "Witty and accurate with academics, who often know it; the name alone can offend a lay committee. Use with a light touch.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-73", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "second-order change", "gloss": "From Paul Watzlawick and colleagues: change that alters the system's rules or assumptions, as opposed to first-order change, which adjusts behaviour within the existing system.", "say": "Moving rehearsals is first-order; asking whether the program is ensemble-led or curriculum-led is *second-order change*.", "note": "Lands in reflective leadership settings; overlaps with double-loop learning. Do not claim second-order change for a reorganisation of the same assumptions.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-74", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "no-regret moves", "gloss": "Actions worth taking under any plausible future scenario, so that they can proceed before strategic uncertainty is resolved.", "say": "Whatever council decides about the specialist program, documenting the instrument inventory is a *no-regret move*.", "note": "From scenario planning; lands when a group is paralysed by uncertainty. Make sure the move is genuinely good in every scenario.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-75", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "optionality", "gloss": "The value of keeping future choices open, especially when a decision is costly or impossible to reverse.", "say": "Signing a three-year venue contract destroys *optionality*; a one-year contract with a right of renewal keeps it.", "note": "Lands when distinguishing reversible from irreversible decisions. Overused in finance-adjacent speech; avoid using it for trivial choices.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-76", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "policy window", "gloss": "John Kingdon's term for the brief moment when problem, policy and political streams converge, making change possible that was impossible before or after.", "say": "The syllabus review is a *policy window*; if we do not put the ensemble assessment case now, it will not reopen for years.", "note": "Lands with policy people and senior public servants, who know the multiple streams framework. Name what has converged, or it sounds like opportunism.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-77", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "theory of action", "gloss": "The working logic connecting a leader's actions to intended results, often stated as 'if we do this, then that will follow', and testable in practice.", "say": "My *theory of action* is simple: if Year 7s have a good first term in ensembles, retention into Year 10 rises.", "note": "Associated with Argyris and Schön and taken into school leadership by Richard Elmore and colleagues. It is closer to the leader's own reasoning than a program's theory of change.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-78", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "exit, voice and loyalty", "gloss": "Albert Hirschman's framework for how members respond to decline: leaving, speaking up, or staying out of loyalty, with loyalty affecting the balance between the other two.", "say": "When families withdraw silently, that is *exit*; I would rather build channels for *voice* before Year 9, when loyalty is still strong.", "note": "Lands in governance and parent-body discussions with an educated audience. The insight is that suppressing voice increases exit.", "level": 3}]}, {"field": "Education policy and research", "intro": "The register is that of the research seminar and the cabinet submission at once: cautious about causal claims, exact about what evidence can and cannot bear, and quick to ask for the mechanism. Native speakers concede limits early, name designs and effects precisely, and treat measures as instruments with side effects rather than as facts.", "entries": [{"id": "education-policy-and-research-1", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "evidence hierarchy", "gloss": "A ranking of research designs by their capacity to support causal claims, usually with systematic reviews and randomised trials at the top; it ranks designs for one question type, not the worth of all knowledge.", "say": "Before we rank these submissions, can we agree which *evidence hierarchy* we are applying, because a case study and a trial are answering different questions.", "note": "Lands when it disciplines a loose discussion of what counts. Backfires if wielded to dismiss practitioner or qualitative evidence outright, which policy people now regard as naive.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-2", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "evidence-informed versus evidence-based", "gloss": "Evidence-based implies practice derived from and licensed by research; evidence-informed concedes that research is one input weighed alongside context, professional judgement and values.", "say": "I'd prefer the policy say *evidence-informed*, because the research on ensemble pedagogy is thin and we should not claim more than it can carry.", "note": "Choosing the weaker phrase deliberately signals that you know the difference. Used by habit with no reason given, it reads as hedging.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-4", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "standardised mean difference", "gloss": "The difference between two group means divided by a pooled or control standard deviation, of which Cohen's d and Hedges' g are the common forms, g correcting for small-sample bias.", "say": "Is that a *standardised mean difference* against a business-as-usual control, or against no instruction at all? The number means something quite different in each case.", "note": "Precise and senior in a research committee. In a parent forum it is jargon; say how big the difference was instead.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-5", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "the hinge point", "gloss": "Hattie's benchmark of d = 0.40 in Visible Learning, offered as the average effect of a year of schooling across his syntheses, above which an influence is said to be worth attention.", "say": "I'd be cautious about the *hinge point*; it averages across outcome measures and age groups that aren't comparable, so it isn't a threshold any single program should be held to.", "note": "Knowing its origin and its critiques signals real reading. Treating 0.40 as a pass mark sounds like a professional learning slide.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-6", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "systematic review versus meta-analysis", "gloss": "A systematic review is a protocol-driven search and appraisal of all relevant studies; a meta-analysis is the statistical pooling of their effects, which a systematic review may or may not include.", "say": "It's a *systematic review*, not a meta-analysis; they found the studies too heterogeneous to pool, which is itself a finding.", "note": "The distinction is routinely blurred, so holding it lands well with academics. Easy to over-labour; one clause is enough.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-7", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "synthesis of meta-analyses", "gloss": "A second-order aggregation that combines the results of many meta-analyses, as in Visible Learning, compounding any differences in the populations, designs and outcome measures beneath them.", "say": "A *synthesis of meta-analyses* is a very long way from any classroom; I'd want to see the primary studies on the specific practice before we fund it.", "note": "Lands as a quiet challenge to league-table use of evidence. Sounds pedantic if no decision actually turns on it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-8", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "outcome measure sensitivity", "gloss": "The tendency for researcher-designed, narrowly aligned tests to produce much larger effect sizes than broad standardised assessments, so effects measured on different instruments are not directly comparable.", "say": "Half of those large effects come from tests the researchers wrote themselves; that's *outcome measure sensitivity*, not a better intervention.", "note": "A high-register point that unsettles a weak evidence claim. Make it once and briefly, or it sounds like you are dismissing research wholesale.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-9", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "internal versus external validity", "gloss": "Internal validity is whether a study's design supports its causal claim within the sample; external validity is whether that claim generalises to other people, settings and times.", "say": "The trial has strong *internal validity*; my question is external validity, because nothing in that sample looks like a selective music program.", "note": "Standard in any research committee and safe to use. Keep the two halves distinct; collapsing them into 'is it valid' loses the point.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-11", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "randomised controlled trial", "gloss": "A design in which participants or units are randomly allocated to conditions so that, in expectation, the only systematic difference between groups is the intervention.", "say": "A *randomised controlled trial* would tell us whether the model works on average; it won't tell us why, or under what staffing conditions.", "note": "Credit its strength before naming its limits. Calling it 'the gold standard' unqualified marks you as either naive or provocative.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-12", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "quasi-experimental design", "gloss": "A study comparing treated and untreated groups without random allocation, relying on matching, statistical controls or natural variation to approximate a counterfactual.", "say": "Short of randomising schools, a *quasi-experimental design* with matched comparison sites is the most defensible thing we could do.", "note": "Signals methodological literacy in a university or departmental setting. Pronounce quasi as KWAY-zye or KWAH-zee; both are accepted.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-13", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "regression discontinuity", "gloss": "A quasi-experimental method that compares units just either side of an eligibility cut-off, treating the cut-off as locally random to estimate a causal effect.", "say": "The audition cut score would give us a natural *regression discontinuity*: students who just made it against those who just missed out.", "note": "Impressive and exactly right where a sharp threshold exists. Out of place anywhere else; reserve it for a research audience.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-14", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "intention to treat", "gloss": "Analysing participants in the groups to which they were assigned, regardless of whether they completed the intervention, to avoid inflating effects by excluding drop-outs.", "say": "If we only count the students who stayed for three years, we flatter the program; an *intention to treat* view counts everyone who started.", "note": "Very precise and persuasive with academics and senior public servants. Explain it in a clause for anyone else.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-15", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "publication bias", "gloss": "The systematic over-representation of positive or significant findings in the published literature, which inflates pooled effect estimates.", "say": "Allowing for *publication bias*, the true effect is probably smaller than that review suggests.", "note": "Standard in research settings. Used reflexively against any finding you dislike, it becomes a cheap shot.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-16", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "file-drawer problem", "gloss": "Rosenthal's name for null results that remain unpublished in researchers' drawers, the mechanism behind publication bias.", "say": "Every school that tried this and saw nothing simply didn't write it up; it's the *file-drawer problem* at the scale of a sector.", "note": "Vivid and well understood by academics. A touch informal for a written submission, where publication bias is cleaner.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-17", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "heterogeneity", "gloss": "Variation in effect sizes across studies beyond what sampling error would predict, signalling that the effect depends on context, population or implementation.", "say": "The average looks healthy, but the *heterogeneity* is enormous; it works brilliantly somewhere and not at all elsewhere.", "note": "A senior point that moves the conversation from whether to where and how. Pronounce het-er-oh-jeh-NEE-ih-tee.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-18", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "moderator versus mediator", "gloss": "A moderator changes the strength or direction of an effect (for whom, when); a mediator is the mechanism through which the effect operates (how, why).", "say": "Prior instrumental experience looks like a *moderator* here, while practice time is the likely mediator.", "note": "The distinction is frequently confused even by researchers, so holding it lands. Labour it and you sound like a methods lecture.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-19", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "implementation fidelity", "gloss": "The degree to which a program is delivered as designed, covering adherence, dosage, quality of delivery and participant responsiveness.", "say": "Before we conclude the model failed, I'd want to know about *implementation fidelity*; two of the four sites never ran the sectionals.", "note": "Essential in any evaluation discussion. Treated as rigid compliance, it alienates teachers who adapt for good reasons.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-20", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "lethal mutation", "gloss": "An adaptation of a research-based practice that preserves its surface form while destroying the mechanism that made it work, a term from design research popularised in schools by Dylan Wiliam.", "say": "Peer feedback without criteria is a *lethal mutation*; it keeps the ritual and loses the learning.", "note": "Memorable and precise in a curriculum or professional learning setting. Sounds theatrical if used about minor variations.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-21", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "dosage", "gloss": "The amount, frequency and duration of an intervention actually received, as distinct from what was planned.", "say": "The planned *dosage* was two sessions a week; with excursions and assemblies most students got closer to one.", "note": "Clean and borrowed from health research, which gives it weight. Avoid it with parents, where it sounds clinical.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-22", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "active ingredients", "gloss": "The components of an intervention that carry its effect and must be preserved under adaptation, as distinct from its incidental features.", "say": "Sites can change the timetable and the repertoire; the *active ingredients* are weekly small-group playing and specific feedback.", "note": "The best way to reconcile fidelity with local adaptation. Ask what the evidence says the ingredients are before asserting them.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-24", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "depth of scale", "gloss": "Coburn's argument that genuine scale requires depth of change in practice, sustainability and a shift in ownership, not merely spread to more sites.", "say": "Twenty schools have the badge, but there's no *depth of scale*; in most of them nothing in the rehearsal room has changed.", "note": "A precise rebuttal to scaling by numbers. Name Coburn only to a research audience; elsewhere the idea stands without the citation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-25", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "Hawthorne effect", "gloss": "Improvement in performance caused by participants' awareness of being studied or given attention rather than by the intervention itself.", "say": "Any new program gets a lift from novelty and attention; the *Hawthorne effect* is why I'd want three years of data.", "note": "Widely known, so safe. Some scholars dispute the original studies, so use it as a label for the phenomenon, not as settled history.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-29", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "backward mapping", "gloss": "Elmore's approach to implementation that begins with the behaviour required at the point of delivery and works back up through the system to determine what policy must enable.", "say": "Let's do some *backward mapping*: start with what a Year 11 teacher needs in the room in week three, then ask what the syllabus document has to say.", "note": "A senior move on a curriculum committee. Not to be confused with backward design in curriculum planning, though the logic is cousin to it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-30", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "unintended consequences", "gloss": "Effects of a policy that were not part of its purpose, which may be beneficial, harmful or perverse, and which a mature policy process tries to anticipate.", "say": "What are the likely *unintended consequences* if the ensemble requirement becomes a graduation condition?", "note": "Expected in any serious deliberation. Name a specific one; the generic phrase alone sounds like obstruction.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-31", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "perverse incentive", "gloss": "An incentive that rewards behaviour contrary to the policy's own purpose.", "say": "Funding by enrolment numbers creates a *perverse incentive* to keep students in a course that no longer suits them.", "note": "Sharp and widely understood. Accusatory if it implies colleagues are responding cynically, so frame it as structural.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-34", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "gaming", "gloss": "Strategic behaviour that improves measured performance without improving the underlying outcome, such as reclassifying students or timing entries.", "say": "I'm not suggesting anyone is cheating; I'm saying the metric invites *gaming*, and good people respond to incentives.", "note": "The neutral technical term. Always detach it from individual motive or it lands as an allegation.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-35", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "curriculum narrowing", "gloss": "The reduction of taught content and time to what is assessed under high-stakes regimes, typically at the expense of the arts, humanities and unassessed capabilities.", "say": "If the reporting regime only sees literacy and numeracy, music will be the first casualty of *curriculum narrowing*.", "note": "Well understood across the sector. Avoid presenting it as a uniquely Australian problem; it is documented internationally.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-36", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "educational triage", "gloss": "Booher-Jennings' term for concentrating resources on students near a proficiency threshold, the so-called bubble students, at the expense of those well above or below it.", "say": "A rule that only counts students reaching a grade cut-off encourages *educational triage*; the strongest and the weakest stop being anyone's priority.", "note": "Precise and sobering in an accountability debate. Use the term, not 'bubble kids', in formal settings.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-37", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "high-stakes accountability", "gloss": "Accountability arrangements in which measured results carry significant consequences for schools, staff or students, such as funding, reputation, sanction or selection.", "say": "My School made NAPLAN a form of *high-stakes accountability* for schools even though it was designed as a diagnostic.", "note": "Standard term. Stakes are relative and perceived; acknowledge that before treating a test as inherently high-stakes.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-38", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "intelligent accountability", "gloss": "Onora O'Neill's term, from her 2002 Reith Lectures, for accountability that relies on informed professional judgement and trust rather than proliferating performance indicators.", "say": "What I'm proposing is *intelligent accountability*: fewer measures, external moderation and honest reporting of what we can't yet quantify.", "note": "Carries weight with principals and public servants. Can sound like a plea to escape scrutiny unless paired with a concrete alternative.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-39", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "lateral accountability", "gloss": "Accountability exercised among peers and professional communities, such as moderation and collegial review, as distinct from vertical accountability upward to authorities.", "say": "Consensus moderation across the four schools is a form of *lateral accountability*, and it changes practice more than any audit does.", "note": "A precise senior term. Make sure the peer process is real before claiming it; reviewers know the difference.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-40", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "performativity", "gloss": "Stephen Ball's term for a regime in which teachers and institutions are governed through judgements, comparisons and displays of performance that come to reshape professional identity.", "say": "Some of what we call quality assurance is really *performativity*: evidence produced to be seen, not to improve anything.", "note": "High register and sociological; lands in a university or conference setting. In a school leadership meeting it can sound like theory used to avoid work.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-41", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "value-added measure", "gloss": "An estimate of a school's or teacher's contribution to student progress after adjusting for prior attainment and sometimes background characteristics.", "say": "Our ATAR results look modest in raw terms, but on any *value-added measure* the music cohort is doing well above expectation.", "note": "Persuasive when you have the data. Value-added models are statistically noisy at small cohort sizes, so do not over-claim for a single class.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-43", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "selection effect", "gloss": "A difference in outcomes attributable to who enters a group rather than to what the group experiences, the central problem in evaluating selective programs.", "say": "Our students outperform, but that's partly a *selection effect*; they were auditioned in. The honest claim is about growth, not raw results.", "note": "Conceding it first, especially about your own selective program, earns enormous credibility. Avoid the cruder synonym 'creaming' in formal settings.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-44", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "compositional effect", "gloss": "The effect of the social or academic composition of a school or class on an individual's outcomes, over and above that individual's own characteristics.", "say": "Some of the advantage of a specialist program is *compositional*: students learn from the standard set by the people around them.", "note": "Precise and senior. Distinguish it from peer effects narrowly defined if speaking to economists.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-45", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "residualisation", "gloss": "The process by which public schools come to serve disproportionately disadvantaged students as more advantaged families exit to selective or non-government schools.", "say": "Selective programs can slow the *residualisation* of a public school, but they can also accelerate it in the schools nearby.", "note": "A recognised term in Australian education debate. Use it with care in public forums, where it can sound like a judgement on a school's community.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-46", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "vertical versus horizontal equity", "gloss": "Horizontal equity treats like cases alike; vertical equity treats unlike cases differently in proportion to their need.", "say": "Equal instrumental hours per student is horizontal equity; *vertical equity* would weight the allocation towards schools with no private tuition base.", "note": "Clarifies a muddled fairness argument instantly. Lands best when you then give the concrete allocation.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-47", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "proportionate universalism", "gloss": "Marmot's principle that actions should be universal but delivered with a scale and intensity proportionate to the level of disadvantage.", "say": "I'd argue for *proportionate universalism*: every Year 7 gets an instrument, and the schools with the steepest gradient get extra tuition time.", "note": "Respected among policy people, from public health. Can sound borrowed if you cannot say how the proportion would be set.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-48", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "needs-based funding", "gloss": "Funding that allocates resources according to measured student need, in the Australian context through the Schooling Resource Standard and its loadings following the Gonski review.", "say": "The instrumental program sits outside *needs-based funding* entirely, which is why access tracks postcode so closely.", "note": "Standard national vocabulary. Be accurate about which loadings apply before arguing from them.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-49", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "excellence and equity", "gloss": "The paired goal at the head of the Alice Springs (Mparntwe) Education Declaration, treated in policy as complementary rather than as a trade-off.", "say": "The Declaration puts *excellence and equity* side by side; a specialist program has to show it serves both, not one at the expense of the other.", "note": "Expected in any national policy discussion. Treating the pair as a slogan rather than a tension to be managed sounds hollow.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-50", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "social gradient", "gloss": "The stepwise relationship between socio-economic position and outcomes across the whole distribution, not only at the extremes.", "say": "Participation in instrumental music follows a steep *social gradient*, and it's visible at every step, not just at the bottom.", "note": "More precise than 'disadvantage', which implies a line between groups. Commonly used in public health and in Australian education research.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-51", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "policy borrowing", "gloss": "The adoption of a policy or practice from another system, typically justified by that system's apparent success, often without regard to the conditions that made it work there.", "say": "Finland's results are a weak basis for *policy borrowing* unless we are also borrowing its teacher education, salaries and social policy.", "note": "A core term in comparative education. The Finland example is now a cliche; choose a less familiar case if you can.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-52", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "travelling versus embedded policy", "gloss": "The distinction between globally circulating policy ideas and the local histories, institutions and politics into which they land and by which they are transformed.", "say": "That model is *travelling policy*; what we need to understand is how it becomes embedded in a WA public school with our industrial agreement.", "note": "High register, from comparative education sociology. Lands at a conference; overly theoretical in a principal's office.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-53", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "PISA shock", "gloss": "A sudden national policy reaction to disappointing international assessment results, the archetype being Germany after the first PISA results in 2001.", "say": "I'm wary of a *PISA shock* response that cuts the arts to fund remedial literacy; the evidence for that trade is poor.", "note": "Widely recognised among policy people. Avoid implying every reform after a PISA release is a panic.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-54", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "Global Education Reform Movement", "gloss": "Pasi Sahlberg's label, abbreviated GERM, for a cluster of market-oriented reforms: standardisation, core-subject focus, test-based accountability, competition and choice.", "say": "Much of what's being proposed sits squarely in the *Global Education Reform Movement* playbook, and the arts tend to lose in that playbook.", "note": "Recognised but partisan; it signals a position. Use it when you are prepared to own that position.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-55", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "institutional isomorphism", "gloss": "DiMaggio and Powell's account of why organisations in a field grow alike through coercive, mimetic and normative pressures, independently of efficiency.", "say": "Every school now has a music academy brand; that's *institutional isomorphism*, not evidence that the model works.", "note": "Senior and sociological; impressive at a university committee. Pronounce eye-so-MOR-fizm.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-56", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "loose coupling", "gloss": "Weick's description of educational organisations as systems whose parts are connected weakly, so that decisions at one level only loosely determine practice at another.", "say": "Schools are *loosely coupled*; the policy can say whatever it likes, and the rehearsal room on Tuesday afternoon will decide.", "note": "An explanatory concept senior people respect. Can sound like an excuse for non-implementation if not followed by a remedy.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-57", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "street-level bureaucracy", "gloss": "Lipsky's account of front-line public servants, teachers among them, whose discretion under resource pressure effectively makes policy in its delivery.", "say": "Instrumental teachers are *street-level bureaucrats* in Lipsky's sense; their discretion about who gets the extra lesson is the real allocation policy.", "note": "Powerful in a policy or implementation discussion. 'Bureaucrat' can grate with teachers; explain that it is a compliment to their discretion.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-58", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "implementation gap", "gloss": "The distance between a policy's stated intent and what is actually delivered at the point of practice.", "say": "The syllabus is sound; the *implementation gap* is in resourcing, because half the schools don't have a specialist teacher.", "note": "Common and useful. Locate the gap precisely or it becomes a vague complaint.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-59", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "the policy cycle", "gloss": "A stylised sequence of identifying issues, analysis, instruments, consultation, coordination, decision, implementation and evaluation, given its standard Australian form by Bridgman and Davis.", "say": "We've gone straight from issue to decision; on the *policy cycle* we've skipped analysis and consultation, and that's where this will come undone.", "note": "A shared reference for public servants. Acknowledge that real policy rarely follows the cycle; insiders treat it as a heuristic, not a description.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-61", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "incrementalism", "gloss": "Lindblom's account of policy as 'muddling through': successive limited comparisons and small adjustments rather than comprehensive rational planning.", "say": "I'd defend some *incrementalism* here; we don't know enough to redesign the whole program in one stroke.", "note": "Respectable when chosen deliberately. Can be heard as timidity, so give the reason you are choosing small steps.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-65", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "consultation versus co-design", "gloss": "Consultation seeks views on a proposal largely formed by the decision-maker; co-design shares the framing and development of the proposal itself with those affected.", "say": "Let's be honest with parents about whether this is *consultation* or co-design; calling it co-design when the decision is made will cost us trust.", "note": "The honest labelling is what lands. Promising co-design you cannot deliver is a common, costly error.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-66", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "the IAP2 spectrum", "gloss": "The International Association for Public Participation's scale of public participation: inform, consult, involve, collaborate and empower, each carrying a different promise to the public.", "say": "On the *IAP2 spectrum* this is 'consult', not 'involve'; our communication should make that promise and no more.", "note": "Widely used in Australian government engagement. Senior public servants recognise it instantly; teachers may not.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-67", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "tokenism", "gloss": "Participation that gives people a voice without influence, a rung on Arnstein's ladder of citizen participation below genuine partnership and delegated power.", "say": "A single student on the committee with no vote is *tokenism*; either give them a real role or don't pretend.", "note": "Sharp but legitimate. Directed at a colleague's initiative, it stings; frame it as a design problem.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-68", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "consultation fatigue", "gloss": "The disengagement of stakeholders who have been consulted repeatedly without visible effect on decisions.", "say": "The parents have *consultation fatigue*; three surveys in two years and none of them changed anything they could see.", "note": "Common in government. Lands when you then propose closing the loop on earlier consultations.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-69", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "policy entrepreneur", "gloss": "Kingdon's term for an actor who invests time and reputation in coupling a problem to their preferred solution when a policy window opens.", "say": "Every reform like this needs a *policy entrepreneur* inside the Department; without one it will sit in a drawer.", "note": "Strategic and precise. Calling yourself one sounds self-aggrandising; use it of others or of the role.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-70", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "unfunded mandate", "gloss": "A requirement imposed by a higher authority without the resources needed to meet it.", "say": "Requiring every school to run a performance showcase without staffing it is an *unfunded mandate*.", "note": "Instantly understood and often decisive. Use it sparingly or you will be heard as the person who always asks for money.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-72", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "cost-effectiveness", "gloss": "The ratio of outcome achieved to cost incurred, allowing comparison of interventions that pursue the same outcome, as distinct from cost-benefit analysis, which monetises the outcome.", "say": "On *cost-effectiveness*, small-group tuition beats a new auditorium by a wide margin.", "note": "Persuasive with funders and executives. Don't call it cost-benefit unless you are actually monetising benefits.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-73", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "sunset clause", "gloss": "A provision that a policy or arrangement expires on a set date unless actively renewed after review.", "say": "Can we put a *sunset clause* on the new structure? Two years, then it lapses unless the evaluation supports it.", "note": "A constructive way to win support for a trial. Signals governance maturity on a board or council.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-75", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "the instructional core", "gloss": "The relationship between teacher, student and content in the presence of a task, which on Elmore's argument is the only place improvement in learning actually occurs.", "say": "None of these structural changes touches the *instructional core*; unless the rehearsal itself changes, results won't.", "note": "A powerful test for any reform proposal. Lands with principals familiar with instructional rounds.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-76", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "compliance versus commitment", "gloss": "Compliance is behaviour secured by rules and monitoring; commitment is behaviour arising from staff conviction, and reform that relies only on the former tends to decay when monitoring stops.", "say": "We can get *compliance* with the new reporting template by Friday; commitment will take a term of showing staff why it helps.", "note": "A useful leadership distinction. Avoid implying staff who comply are uncommitted; often they are simply overloaded.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-77", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "adaptive versus technical challenge", "gloss": "Heifetz's distinction between problems solvable with existing expertise and authority, and problems requiring people to change values, habits or beliefs.", "say": "Booking the venue is technical; getting staff to share ensemble students across programs is an *adaptive challenge*.", "note": "Diagnoses why a good plan stalls. Sounds like leadership-course language if used too often.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-78", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "realist evaluation", "gloss": "Pawson and Tilley's approach that asks what works, for whom, in what circumstances and how, by identifying context, mechanism and outcome configurations.", "say": "A *realist evaluation* would be more useful to us than a single effect size; we need to know which students it works for and why.", "note": "Highly respected in evaluation circles. Requires you to be able to name a plausible mechanism, or the term is decoration.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-80", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "system coherence", "gloss": "The alignment of goals, curriculum, assessment, resourcing and accountability so that they pull in the same direction rather than sending competing signals.", "say": "The syllabus rewards improvisation and the exam doesn't assess it; that's a *system coherence* problem, not a teacher problem.", "note": "Moves a debate from blame to design. Lands strongly on SCSA committees.", "level": 2}]}, {"field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "intro": "The register is cognitive science spoken with a practitioner's authority: named effects, their boundary conditions, and the difference between performance today and learning that lasts. Native speakers cite findings by their proper names, state the conditions under which they hold, and translate them into the rehearsal room without overclaiming.", "entries": [{"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-1", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "cognitive load theory", "gloss": "Sweller's instructional theory that learning is constrained by limited working memory and facilitated by schemas in long-term memory, so instruction should manage the load it imposes.", "say": "*Cognitive load theory* would say the problem isn't the piece, it's that we've asked them to read, count and blend in the same rehearsal.", "note": "Widely invoked in schools, often loosely. Precision about its limits and its evidence base is what separates the informed from the converted.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-2", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "intrinsic versus extraneous load", "gloss": "Intrinsic load arises from the inherent complexity of the material relative to the learner's expertise; extraneous load arises from the way it is presented and can be reduced by design.", "say": "The chart's harmony is *intrinsic load*, fine at Year 11; the badly copied parts are extraneous load, and that we can fix.", "note": "The distinction gives teachers something to act on. Remember intrinsic load is relative to the learner, not fixed in the material.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-3", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "germane load", "gloss": "Originally the load devoted to schema construction, later reconceptualised by Sweller as the working memory resources allocated to intrinsic load rather than a separate category.", "say": "I'd avoid building the argument on *germane load*; even Sweller has largely folded it back into intrinsic load.", "note": "Knowing its revised status marks real currency with the literature. Using it as a fresh third category sounds dated.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-4", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "element interactivity", "gloss": "The degree to which elements of material must be processed simultaneously because they interact, the central determinant of intrinsic load.", "say": "Swing phrasing has high *element interactivity*; articulation, placement and subdivision can't be learnt one at a time in any meaningful sense.", "note": "The technically exact concept beneath intrinsic load. Lands with researchers; needs a gloss for most staff.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-5", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "limited-capacity working memory", "gloss": "The constraint that working memory can hold and manipulate only a few novel elements at once, while long-term memory has no known practical limit.", "say": "Sight-reading a new chart taxes *limited-capacity working memory*; that's why patterns they've already automated free up the attention.", "note": "Foundational. Avoid quoting 'seven plus or minus two' as settled; current estimates for novel material are lower and the figure is contested.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-6", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "schema", "gloss": "An organised structure in long-term memory that integrates many elements into a single unit, allowing experts to treat complex material as one chunk; plural schemas or schemata.", "say": "A rhythm section player with a strong blues *schema* hears twelve bars as one object, not forty-eight beats.", "note": "Standard in learning science. 'Schemata' is correct but can sound affected; 'schemas' is accepted in cognitive load literature.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-7", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "automaticity", "gloss": "The capacity to perform a skill with minimal conscious attention, achieved through extensive practice, freeing working memory for higher-order processing.", "say": "Until the scale fingerings have *automaticity*, there's no attention left over for line or time feel.", "note": "Precise and safe. Distinguish it from mastery; something can be automatic and still wrong.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-8", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "chunking", "gloss": "The grouping of individual elements into larger meaningful units in memory, the mechanism by which expertise expands effective working memory capacity.", "say": "Good readers aren't reading notes, they're *chunking*: a ii V I in a bar, a turnaround in a glance.", "note": "Long established, from the chess-memory research. Easy to overextend into a general theory of expertise.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-9", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "worked example effect", "gloss": "The finding that novices learn more from studying worked solutions than from solving equivalent problems unaided, because problem solving imposes heavy search load.", "say": "For beginners, a transcribed solo with the analysis annotated exploits the *worked example effect*; asking them to improvise cold doesn't.", "note": "Robust for novices in well-structured domains. Pair it with expertise reversal, or you will be heard as anti-inquiry across the board.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-10", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "split-attention effect", "gloss": "Reduced learning when learners must mentally integrate physically or temporally separated sources of information that are unintelligible in isolation.", "say": "Putting the chord symbols on a separate sheet from the melody creates a *split-attention effect*; integrate them on the part.", "note": "Practical and well supported. Applies only when sources must be integrated to be understood.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-11", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "redundancy effect", "gloss": "Reduced learning when the same information is presented in more than one form, so learners must process both unnecessarily.", "say": "Reading out every word on the slide is the *redundancy effect* in action; the panel can either read or listen.", "note": "Useful about presentations as well as teaching. Not to be confused with the benefits of dual coding, which combine different, complementary information.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-12", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "expertise reversal effect", "gloss": "Kalyuga's finding that instructional techniques effective for novices, such as worked examples, become ineffective or harmful as expertise grows, because the guidance becomes redundant.", "say": "The step-by-step guide suits Year 7; for the ATAR group it triggers the *expertise reversal effect*, and they'll learn more from open problems.", "note": "Resolves the false choice between explicit instruction and inquiry. Lands with anyone who has heard the debate simplified.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-13", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "guidance fading", "gloss": "The progressive withdrawal of instructional support, often through completion problems, as the learner's schemas develop.", "say": "We use *guidance fading* across the voicing unit: fully written, then guide tones only, then just the symbols.", "note": "Precise term for good practice many teachers do intuitively. Name the steps or it sounds like a label without substance.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-14", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "productive failure", "gloss": "Kapur's design in which students attempt complex problems before instruction, generating solutions that fail but prepare them to understand the canonical solution more deeply.", "say": "There's a case for *productive failure* with the senior arrangers; let them voice the chorale badly before we show the rules.", "note": "Supported under specific conditions, notably subsequent explicit instruction. Not a licence for unstructured discovery.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-15", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "desirable difficulty", "gloss": "Bjork's term for conditions that slow apparent progress during learning but enhance long-term retention and transfer, such as spacing, interleaving and testing.", "say": "Rotating keys every few minutes feels worse to the students, but it's a *desirable difficulty*; the gains show up a week later.", "note": "Precise when the difficulty is the right kind. Not every difficulty is desirable; if the learner cannot respond successfully, it is simply difficulty.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-16", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "storage strength versus retrieval strength", "gloss": "In Bjork's new theory of disuse, storage strength is how well learned something is; retrieval strength is how accessible it is now, and low retrieval strength at practice increases gains in storage strength.", "say": "They nailed it in the lesson, so retrieval strength was high, but *storage strength* was low; that's why it was gone by Thursday.", "note": "Explains the forgetting that frustrates teachers and students. Senior and exact; few people in the room will know it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-17", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "retrieval practice", "gloss": "Learning activity that requires recalling information from memory, which strengthens and reorganises the memory more than restudying it.", "say": "Playing the head from memory at the start of rehearsal is *retrieval practice*; reading it off the part again isn't.", "note": "Widely used and well supported. Do not reduce it to low-stakes quizzing; the principle is effortful recall in any form.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-18", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "testing effect", "gloss": "The finding that taking a test on material produces better long-term retention than an equivalent period of restudy, independently of feedback.", "say": "The *testing effect* is about learning, not measurement; the mock is worth doing even if we never mark it.", "note": "Distinguishing assessment as learning event from assessment as measurement lands well. The name misleads parents; explain it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-19", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "spacing effect", "gloss": "The finding that practice distributed over time produces better long-term retention than the same amount massed into one session.", "say": "Twenty minutes a day beats two hours on Sunday; the *spacing effect* is among the most replicated findings we have.", "note": "Safe and robust. Its strength makes it a good anchor for practice advice to parents.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-20", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "optimal lag", "gloss": "The interval between study episodes that maximises retention, which research indicates scales with how long the material must be retained.", "say": "If they need the audition excerpt in three months, the *optimal lag* between reviews is weeks, not days.", "note": "Precise and rarely heard outside research. Avoid quoting specific ratios unless you have the study in hand.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-21", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "interleaving", "gloss": "Mixing different types of problems or skills within a practice session, which improves discrimination between categories and long-term retention compared with blocked practice.", "say": "*Interleaving* the three modes forces them to decide which sound is which, and that decision is what they need in the exam.", "note": "Well supported for discrimination tasks. Its benefit depends on the categories being confusable; interleaving unrelated material does less.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-22", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "contextual interference", "gloss": "The motor learning finding, from Shea and Morgan, that high-interference practice schedules impair performance during practice but improve retention and transfer.", "say": "Changing the excerpt every few repetitions increases *contextual interference*; it'll look messier now and hold up better in the audition.", "note": "The motor-learning ancestor of interleaving research; exact in a tertiary performance setting. Rare elsewhere.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-23", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "blocked versus random practice", "gloss": "Blocked practice repeats one task many times before moving on; random practice mixes tasks in unpredictable order, generally yielding weaker practice performance but stronger retention.", "say": "Ten times in a row is *blocked practice*; it gives a feeling of mastery the concert will expose.", "note": "Directly useful in instrumental teaching. Novices may need some blocked practice first; don't overstate the case.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-24", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "fluency illusion", "gloss": "The mistaken judgement that material is learned because it feels familiar or easy to process, typically produced by rereading or massed repetition.", "say": "Listening to the recording ten times creates a *fluency illusion*; they recognise it, which isn't the same as being able to play it.", "note": "Lands with students and parents as well as staff. Also called the illusion of competence.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-25", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "learning versus performance", "gloss": "The distinction between relatively permanent changes in capability, which must be inferred, and momentary performance during acquisition, which can be a poor index of learning.", "say": "What we see in the rehearsal is *performance*; whether they've learnt it, we'll only know after the holidays.", "note": "The foundational distinction behind desirable difficulties. Precise wording matters; the casual use of the words blurs it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-26", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "generation effect", "gloss": "Better retention of information that learners produce themselves than of the same information presented to them.", "say": "Getting them to work out the voicing themselves, even slowly, uses the *generation effect*; handing it out doesn't.", "note": "Robust in laboratory settings. Generation must succeed often enough, so pair it with support.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-27", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "elaborative interrogation", "gloss": "A learning strategy of prompting learners to explain why a fact or relationship is true, connecting it to prior knowledge.", "say": "Ask why the bridge modulates to the fourth: that's *elaborative interrogation*, and it fixes the form far better than a diagram.", "note": "Exact term from the Dunlosky review of learning techniques. Sounds clinical; usually better to describe the move.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-28", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "dual coding", "gloss": "Paivio's theory that verbal and non-verbal representations are processed in partly separate systems, so complementary words and images can strengthen encoding.", "say": "A form diagram alongside the recording gives them *dual coding*; the same words on the slide as I'm saying doesn't.", "note": "Often misused to justify decorative images. The images must carry complementary information.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-29", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "encoding specificity", "gloss": "Tulving's principle that retrieval is most effective when cues present at retrieval match those present at encoding.", "say": "They can play it in the band room and not on stage; *encoding specificity* says practise in conditions closer to the performance.", "note": "High precision; explains context-dependent forgetting. Rare outside cognitive psychology.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-30", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "near versus far transfer", "gloss": "Near transfer applies learning to closely similar contexts; far transfer to contexts that differ substantially, which is much harder to achieve and to demonstrate.", "say": "Improvising over rhythm changes in B-flat to F is near transfer; the claim that music improves maths would be *far transfer*, and the evidence there is weak.", "note": "Using it to temper advocacy claims for music earns trust with researchers. Overstating far transfer loses it quickly.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-31", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "low-road versus high-road transfer", "gloss": "Salomon and Perkins' distinction between transfer through extensive, varied practice to automaticity and transfer through deliberate, mindful abstraction of a principle.", "say": "Learning tunes in all keys builds *low-road transfer*; analysing why the voice leading works builds high-road.", "note": "Elegant and exact in a tertiary pedagogy setting. Unfamiliar to most; give the gloss.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-32", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "inert knowledge", "gloss": "Whitehead's term for knowledge that can be recalled when asked but is not used spontaneously in situations where it is relevant.", "say": "They can name every mode and can't hear one in a solo; that's *inert knowledge*.", "note": "Sharp diagnosis of rote theory teaching. Lands as a challenge to curriculum design, not to students.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-33", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "adaptive versus routine expertise", "gloss": "Hatano and Inagaki's distinction between experts who perform familiar tasks efficiently and experts who can also invent new procedures and understand why procedures work.", "say": "The audition panel rewards routine expertise; jazz needs *adaptive expertise*, and our assessment should look for it.", "note": "Highly relevant to performance and improvisation. Rare register; best in a committee designing assessment.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-34", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "deliberate practice", "gloss": "Ericsson's term for effortful, teacher-designed practice targeting specific weaknesses with immediate feedback, in a domain with established training methods, as distinct from mere repetition or play.", "say": "Running the tune top to bottom isn't *deliberate practice*; isolating the bridge at a tempo you can't yet manage, with recorded playback, is.", "note": "Its strict definition is often lost in popular use. Its role in explaining expertise is contested; avoid claiming it accounts for all variance.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-35", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "purposeful versus naive practice", "gloss": "Ericsson's later distinction: naive practice is repetition, purposeful practice has specific goals and feedback, and deliberate practice adds expert-designed methods and teaching.", "say": "Most home practice is *naive practice*; getting them to purposeful practice is the realistic goal before Year 10.", "note": "Shows you know the definitions are graded. Calling a student's practice naive in a parent meeting will be heard as an insult.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-36", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "mental representation", "gloss": "In Ericsson's account, the domain-specific internal model an expert uses to perceive, plan and monitor performance, refined through practice.", "say": "What distinguishes the strongest students is the *mental representation*: they hear what the phrase should be before they play it.", "note": "Close to audiation in music, and the comparison is worth making. Vague if not tied to observable behaviour.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-37", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "long-term working memory", "gloss": "Ericsson and Kintsch's proposal that experts use retrieval structures in long-term memory to extend effective working memory within their domain.", "say": "Professional readers aren't superhuman at holding notes; they're using *long-term working memory* built over years of the idiom.", "note": "Exact and rare; persuasive with cognitive scientists. Not the same as simply having a large working memory.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-38", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "biologically primary versus secondary knowledge", "gloss": "Geary's distinction between knowledge humans evolved to acquire readily without instruction, such as speech, and culturally recent knowledge, such as notation, that typically requires explicit teaching.", "say": "Singing a tune back may be close to *biologically primary*; reading notation is secondary, and it needs explicit teaching.", "note": "Influential in cognitive load circles. Contested by some evolutionary psychologists, so present it as a framework rather than settled fact.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-39", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "formative assessment", "gloss": "Assessment whose evidence is used to adapt teaching and learning to meet learners' needs, defined by the use made of the information rather than by the instrument.", "say": "The same practice exam is *formative* if we change next week's teaching because of it and summative if we just record the mark.", "note": "Defining it by use, not instrument, is the insider move from Black and Wiliam. Calling every quiz formative sounds uninformed.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-40", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "assessment as learning", "gloss": "The use of assessment by students themselves to monitor and direct their own learning, distinct from assessment for learning, which informs the teacher's decisions.", "say": "Self-recording and comparing against the reference recording is *assessment as learning*; the student is both assessor and learner.", "note": "Common in Australian curriculum documents. Some researchers regard the three-way split as more rhetorical than conceptual.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-41", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "hinge question", "gloss": "Wiliam's term for a diagnostic question at a pivotal point in a lesson, designed so each wrong answer reveals a specific misconception and the class's responses can be read in seconds.", "say": "Before moving to modulation, a *hinge question* on the relative minor tells me whether half the room is lost.", "note": "Exact and well regarded. Its value lies in the diagnostic design of the distractors, not in pausing to ask any question.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-42", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "responsive teaching", "gloss": "Teaching that elicits evidence of learning during instruction and adjusts in the moment, the practical core of formative assessment.", "say": "*Responsive teaching* in a big band rehearsal means stopping for the section that actually needs it, not the one that's loudest.", "note": "Wiliam now prefers it to 'assessment for learning', which many heard as more testing. A nice signal of current reading.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-43", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "feed up, feed back, feed forward", "gloss": "Hattie and Timperley's three questions effective feedback answers: where am I going, how am I going, and where to next.", "say": "The comments were all *feed back* with no feed forward; the student knew what went wrong and had no idea what to do next.", "note": "Widely known in Australian schools. Fluent use means applying all three, not reciting them.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-44", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "the four levels of feedback", "gloss": "Hattie and Timperley's levels at which feedback operates: the task, the processing of the task, self-regulation, and the self as a person, with effectiveness varying by level.", "say": "Most of our written feedback sits at the task level; the *four levels of feedback* would push us towards process and self-regulation.", "note": "Senior and exact. The levels are not a strict hierarchy; task feedback is often what novices need.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-45", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "feedback about the self", "gloss": "Praise or criticism directed at the person rather than the task or process, which Hattie and Timperley identify as least effective for learning.", "say": "'You're so talented' is *feedback about the self*; it feels kind and teaches nothing.", "note": "Lands with staff and parents alike. Not a ban on warmth; the point is that warmth isn't the feedback.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-46", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "Sadler's three conditions", "gloss": "Royce Sadler's argument that for feedback to improve performance the learner must hold a concept of the standard, compare their current performance with it, and act to close the gap.", "say": "We give marks, but on *Sadler's three conditions* the students don't have the standard; they've never heard an exemplary submission.", "note": "Foundational in assessment scholarship; impresses at SCSA and university level. Name it only if you can state all three.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-47", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "Kluger and DeNisi", "gloss": "Shorthand for Kluger and DeNisi's 1996 meta-analysis showing that feedback interventions on average improved performance but reduced it in a substantial share of cases, especially when attention shifted to the self.", "say": "Feedback isn't automatically good; *Kluger and DeNisi* found a sizeable fraction of feedback interventions made performance worse.", "note": "The authoritative counterweight to 'feedback is the biggest effect'. Do not quote a precise fraction unless you have checked it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-48", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "metacognition", "gloss": "Knowledge about and regulation of one's own cognition: planning, monitoring and evaluating one's learning, distinct from cognition itself.", "say": "Teaching *metacognition* here means getting them to plan a practice session and judge afterwards whether it worked.", "note": "Very common and often used vaguely. Specify which component, knowledge or regulation, you mean.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-49", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "self-regulated learning", "gloss": "The cyclical process, in Zimmerman's model, by which learners set goals, select strategies, monitor performance and reflect, through forethought, performance and self-reflection phases.", "say": "The jump from Year 10 to ATAR is really a jump in *self-regulated learning*; the content is the easier part.", "note": "Precise and current. Distinguish it from independence; self-regulated learners still seek help strategically.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-50", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "calibration", "gloss": "The accuracy of learners' judgements of their own performance or knowledge relative to actual performance.", "say": "The issue isn't confidence, it's *calibration*; she rates every performance a nine, whatever happened.", "note": "Neutral and exact; better than 'overconfident'. Lands in reporting and feedback discussions.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-51", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "judgement of learning", "gloss": "A learner's prediction of how well they will later remember or perform something, studied as a measure of metacognitive monitoring.", "say": "Their *judgement of learning* is inflated straight after rehearsal; asked a day later, it's far more accurate.", "note": "Research vocabulary, rare in schools. Useful when explaining why delayed self-assessment is more honest.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-52", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "self-efficacy", "gloss": "Bandura's term for belief in one's capacity to succeed at a specific task, distinct from self-esteem, which is a global evaluation of self-worth.", "say": "His self-esteem is fine; his *self-efficacy* for sight-reading is close to nil, and that's what's driving the avoidance.", "note": "The distinction from self-esteem is what lands. Self-efficacy is task-specific; using it globally blurs the concept.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-53", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "self-determination theory", "gloss": "Deci and Ryan's theory that motivation is sustained when three basic psychological needs are met: autonomy, competence and relatedness, along a continuum from controlled to autonomous motivation.", "say": "*Self-determination theory* would explain the retention problem: we've stripped out autonomy in Year 9 just when they most need it.", "note": "Robust and widely respected. Autonomy here means volition, not absence of structure.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-54", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "expectancy-value theory", "gloss": "Eccles' model in which achievement choices depend on expectations of success and the subjective value of the task, including intrinsic, attainment and utility value and perceived cost.", "say": "On *expectancy-value theory*, the ATAR course loses students on cost: the time it takes against everything else in Year 11.", "note": "Particularly useful about subject choice. 'Cost' as a value component is the point most people miss.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-55", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "mastery versus performance goals", "gloss": "In achievement goal theory, mastery goals aim at developing competence, performance goals at demonstrating it relative to others, with performance-avoidance goals the most consistently harmful.", "say": "A ranked seating audition every term pushes students towards *performance goals*, and the anxious ones towards avoidance.", "note": "Senior and relevant to ensemble culture. Note the approach and avoidance split; performance-approach goals are not uniformly harmful.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-56", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "attribution theory", "gloss": "Weiner's account of how people explain success and failure along dimensions of locus, stability and controllability, and how those explanations shape subsequent motivation.", "say": "If they put a bad audition down to talent, that's a stable, uncontrollable cause in *attribution theory* terms, and they'll stop practising.", "note": "Precise and older than mindset research, which draws on it. Rare enough to need a gloss.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-57", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "overjustification effect", "gloss": "The decline in intrinsic motivation that can follow the introduction of expected, tangible rewards for an activity people already enjoy.", "say": "Paying practice points for minutes logged risks the *overjustification effect*; we could turn a love of playing into a chore.", "note": "Lands in a debate about incentive schemes. The effect depends on reward type and contingency; don't claim all rewards harm motivation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-58", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "growth mindset", "gloss": "Dweck's construct that beliefs about the malleability of ability affect learning behaviour; large-scale replications show small average effects concentrated in specific groups.", "say": "I'd keep *growth mindset* posters off the wall; the replication effects are modest and it's better embedded in how we give feedback.", "note": "Everyone uses it; knowing its evidence status is the distinguishing move. Don't dismiss it outright either; the effects are small, not nil.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-59", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "the meshing hypothesis", "gloss": "The claim, tested and unsupported in Pashler and colleagues' 2008 review, that matching instruction to a learner's preferred style improves learning.", "say": "The issue with learning styles is the *meshing hypothesis*; preferences exist, but matching teaching to them doesn't improve outcomes.", "note": "The precise way to dismiss learning styles without overclaiming. Concede that people do have preferences.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-60", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "scaffolding", "gloss": "Temporary, responsive support enabling a learner to accomplish what they cannot yet do alone, introduced by Wood, Bruner and Ross and often linked to, though distinct from, Vygotsky's zone of proximal development.", "say": "*Scaffolding* means it comes down; if the bass line is still written out in Term 4, it's a crutch.", "note": "Widely used and widely diluted. Emphasising contingency and withdrawal restores its meaning.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-61", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "cognitive apprenticeship", "gloss": "Collins, Brown and Newman's model that makes expert thinking visible through modelling, coaching, scaffolding, articulation, reflection and exploration.", "say": "Thinking aloud while I transcribe is *cognitive apprenticeship*; the craft is in the reasoning they normally can't see.", "note": "Elegant bridge between the studio tradition and learning science. Sounds academic in a parent forum.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-62", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "constructive alignment", "gloss": "Biggs' principle that intended learning outcomes, teaching activities and assessment tasks should be aligned, with students constructing meaning through relevant activity.", "say": "If the outcome is improvisation and the assessment is a written test, there's no *constructive alignment*.", "note": "Standard vocabulary in Australian universities; strong at an academic committee. Less known in schools.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-63", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "threshold concept", "gloss": "Meyer and Land's term for a concept that is transformative, irreversible, integrative and often troublesome, opening a previously inaccessible way of thinking in a discipline.", "say": "Hearing harmony functionally is a *threshold concept*; before it chords are labels, after it they're directions.", "note": "Popular in higher education. Not every hard idea is a threshold concept; argue the criteria.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-64", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "pedagogical content knowledge", "gloss": "Shulman's term for the specialised knowledge of how to teach particular content: the representations, examples and anticipated misconceptions specific to a subject.", "say": "Being a fine player isn't enough; what we need in the tutor pool is *pedagogical content knowledge* about teaching beginners to swing.", "note": "Highly respected and directly relevant to hiring and tutor training. Abbreviated PCK in research circles.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-65", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "signature pedagogy", "gloss": "Shulman's term for the characteristic forms of teaching through which a profession prepares its practitioners, such as the clinical round or, in music, the one-to-one studio lesson.", "say": "The one-to-one lesson is the *signature pedagogy* of the conservatoire; that's why it's so hard to question.", "note": "Lands at a university committee. Using it critically, not just descriptively, shows real command.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-66", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "legitimate peripheral participation", "gloss": "Lave and Wenger's account of learning as movement from peripheral to full participation in a community of practice.", "say": "Year 7s sitting in on the big band is *legitimate peripheral participation*; they learn the culture before they can play the charts.", "note": "Exact and high-register. The whole phrase is cumbersome; use it once and then say peripheral participation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-67", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "audiation", "gloss": "Edwin Gordon's term for hearing and comprehending music in the mind when the sound is not physically present, distinct from mere aural imagery in that it involves understanding.", "say": "The students who can *audiate* the line before they play it are the ones whose intonation sorts itself out.", "note": "Specific to music learning theory; signals tertiary music pedagogy literacy. Pronounce aw-dee-AY-shun.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-68", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "sound before sign", "gloss": "The principle, central to Kodaly-inspired and Gordon traditions, that musical concepts should be experienced aurally before they are named or notated.", "say": "Teaching the dotted rhythm from the page first ignores *sound before sign*; they should sing it a hundred times before they read it.", "note": "A well-established maxim. Its strength is as a sequencing principle, not as a reason to avoid notation.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-69", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "enculturation", "gloss": "The largely implicit acquisition of a musical culture's norms through immersion, as distinct from formal training.", "say": "Swing feel is mostly *enculturation*; the students who've listened for years have it before we teach a thing.", "note": "Precise in music cognition and ethnomusicology. Careful with assumptions about which students lack it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-70", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "informal learning", "gloss": "In Lucy Green's research, the practices by which popular musicians learn: choosing their own music, learning by ear from recordings, learning with peers, and integrating listening, playing and composing.", "say": "The Cont ensembles already run on *informal learning* principles in Green's sense; we should evaluate them on those terms.", "note": "Grounded in her How Popular Musicians Learn. Distinguish informal learning from unstructured teaching.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-71", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "master-apprentice model", "gloss": "The traditional one-to-one model of instrumental teaching in which a recognised performer transmits craft, repertoire and values to a student, largely through modelling and correction.", "say": "The *master-apprentice model* is powerful and largely unexamined; we should keep the strengths and question the dependence.", "note": "Common in tertiary music research. Critique it carefully; panel members may be its products and practitioners.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-72", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "tacit knowledge", "gloss": "Polanyi's term for knowledge that can be enacted but not fully articulated: 'we can know more than we can tell'.", "say": "Time feel is *tacit knowledge*; I can model it and correct it, but I can't write it down in a rubric.", "note": "Exact and respected. Using it as a reason to resist any explicit criteria sounds defensive.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-73", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "external focus of attention", "gloss": "Wulf's finding that directing attention to the effect of a movement rather than the body's movements generally improves motor performance and learning.", "say": "Telling her to relax her wrist is an internal focus; '*external focus*' would be 'let the bow ride the string'.", "note": "Strongly supported in motor learning and directly useful in instrumental teaching. Few music educators know the term.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-74", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "explicit monitoring", "gloss": "The account of choking under pressure in which anxiety prompts skilled performers to attend consciously to automated components, disrupting their execution.", "say": "In the audition she started thinking about her fingers; that's *explicit monitoring*, and it's why we rehearse under pressure.", "note": "Precise and rare; credible with performance science researchers. Distinguish it from distraction accounts of choking.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-75", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "embodied cognition", "gloss": "The view that cognitive processes are grounded in bodily states, action and perception, not only in abstract representation.", "say": "Dalcroze was doing *embodied cognition* a century before it had a name; moving the phrase is understanding the phrase.", "note": "Fashionable, so use it with substance. Lands in tertiary music education, where Dalcroze provides the obvious bridge.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-76", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "reflection-in-action", "gloss": "Schon's term for thinking about practice while engaged in it, distinct from reflection-on-action, which happens afterwards.", "say": "Good improvisers are constantly doing *reflection-in-action*; the debrief after the gig is reflection-on-action.", "note": "From The Reflective Practitioner; well known in tertiary teacher education. The distinction is the point.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-77", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "artistic research", "gloss": "Research in which artistic practice is itself the method and a primary output, distinct from research about art and from practice merely accompanied by an exegesis.", "say": "What I'm proposing is *artistic research*; the performances are the inquiry, not an illustration of it.", "note": "Established in European and Australian conservatoire research. Contested in its boundaries; know the debate before claiming it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-78", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "overlearning", "gloss": "Continued practice beyond the point of first correct performance, which improves short-term retention and robustness under pressure, though its long-term benefit is smaller than spacing.", "say": "For the audition excerpt, some *overlearning* is right; for general repertoire, spacing will serve them better.", "note": "Precise when you also state its limits. Unqualified, it sounds like an argument for drilling.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-79", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "mastery learning", "gloss": "Bloom's model in which learners must demonstrate mastery of a unit before proceeding, supported by formative assessment and corrective instruction, associated with his 'two sigma problem'.", "say": "Grade exams are a crude form of *mastery learning*; nobody moves to Grade 5 without passing Grade 4.", "note": "Respected and current again. Bloom's two sigma tutoring result is often quoted loosely; avoid precise claims about it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-80", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "SOLO taxonomy", "gloss": "Biggs and Collis' Structure of the Observed Learning Outcome: prestructural, unistructural, multistructural, relational and extended abstract levels of understanding.", "say": "The analysis answers are mostly multistructural on the *SOLO taxonomy*; they list features without relating them.", "note": "Useful for writing marking keys and criteria. Less known than Bloom's taxonomy; give the level names.", "level": 2}]}, {"field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "intro": "Australian meeting practice speaks in the compact, slightly formal idiom of Westminster: motions are moved, seconded, carried or lost, and the chair rules rather than opines. Sounding native means using the exact form of words once, without commentary, and keeping the procedure invisible until it is needed.", "entries": [{"id": "procedure-1", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "standing orders", "gloss": "The written rules that govern how a body conducts its meetings and debates, subordinate to its constitution and unable to override it.", "say": "Before we admit a late item, can I check what our *standing orders* say about notice?", "note": "Lands in any formally constituted body; in a small committee with no written rules, say 'our usual procedure' or it sounds borrowed from Parliament House.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-2", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "substantive motion", "gloss": "The motion that states the actual proposal before the meeting, as distinct from procedural motions about how the meeting will deal with it.", "say": "Let's dispose of the amendment first and then come back to the *substantive motion*.", "note": "Signals that you can see the structure of the debate; use it when the room has lost track of what is actually being decided.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-3", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "procedural motion", "gloss": "A motion about the conduct of the meeting itself, such as to adjourn, defer or put the question, which usually needs no notice and takes precedence over the business in hand.", "say": "That's a *procedural motion*, so I'll deal with it before we go back to the budget.", "note": "Useful for explaining why a motion jumps the queue; do not use the label to brush off a member whose point is really substantive.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-4", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "the question be now put", "gloss": "The closure motion: if carried, debate ends and the immediate question goes straight to a vote, though the chair may decline to accept it where debate has been inadequate.", "say": "Chair, we have heard every view twice; I move that *the question be now put*.", "note": "This is the Westminster form; 'I call the question' is American and reads oddly in an Australian room. Moving it early looks like a gag and costs goodwill.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-5", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "point of order", "gloss": "An interruption alleging a breach of the rules, which the chair must hear and rule on at once; it is not a vehicle for disagreeing with the substance.", "say": "*Point of order*, Chair: the amendment is a direct negative of the motion.", "note": "Name the rule breached in your first sentence. Using it to make a debating point is the fastest way to lose a room's patience.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-6", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "through the chair", "gloss": "The convention that all remarks are addressed to the chair rather than across the table, which depersonalises debate and keeps the chair in control.", "say": "*Through the chair*, could I ask Professor Lee whether the moderation data supports that?", "note": "Most useful when a two-person exchange is heating up; insisting on it in a relaxed working group can sound stiff.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-7", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "second a motion", "gloss": "To indicate that at least one other member wishes the motion to be debated; seconding does not commit the seconder to vote for it.", "say": "I'll *second* it so we can have the discussion, though I may well vote against.", "note": "Saying explicitly that you second without endorsing is a gracious way to let a colleague's idea be heard.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-8", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "lapses for want of a seconder", "gloss": "The fate of a motion that no other member is willing to second: it is not debated, not put and not recorded as lost.", "say": "With no seconder, the motion *lapses for want of a seconder*, and we move to item six.", "note": "Say it flatly and move on; any commentary on the motion at that point looks like the chair taking sides.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-9", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "amendment", "gloss": "A proposal to alter the words of a motion by omitting, inserting or substituting words, which must be relevant to the motion and must not simply negate it.", "say": "I'd like to move an *amendment*: omit 'from Term 1' and insert 'from Term 3, subject to staffing'.", "note": "Always state the exact words to omit and insert. A vague 'can we amend it to be softer' leaves the chair and secretary to draft it for you.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-10", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "direct negative", "gloss": "An amendment whose only effect is to reverse the motion, ruled out of order because members can achieve the same result by voting the motion down.", "say": "Inserting 'not' is a *direct negative*; if you oppose the motion, vote against it.", "note": "A clean, unanswerable ruling; explain the reason in one clause so the mover does not feel stonewalled.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-11", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "foreshadowed amendment", "gloss": "An amendment announced during debate, to be moved once the amendment currently before the meeting is disposed of, reflecting the Australian practice of taking one amendment at a time.", "say": "I'll *foreshadow an amendment* on the reporting date, once we have dealt with this one.", "note": "Lets members vote on the current amendment knowing the alternative; foreshadowing several at once can feel like holding the meeting hostage.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-12", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "the motion as amended", "gloss": "The text that results once an amendment is carried, which becomes the substantive motion, open to further amendment, and is then put in its new form.", "say": "The amendment is carried; I'll now read *the motion as amended* before we vote.", "note": "Always read the amended text aloud before putting it. Votes taken on wording nobody has heard are the commonest source of disputed minutes.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-14", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "notice of motion", "gloss": "Written advance notice that a member intends to move a specified motion, so that others can prepare, required by most rules for significant or constitutional business.", "say": "This changes the ensemble fee structure, so I'll give *notice of motion* for the March meeting.", "note": "Giving notice for contentious business is both correct and disarming; springing it without notice invites a procedural fight you may lose.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-15", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "take on notice", "gloss": "To accept a question that cannot be answered accurately on the spot and commit to a considered answer later, usually in writing or at the next meeting.", "say": "I'll *take that on notice* and circulate the enrolment figures before the next meeting.", "note": "Senate Estimates usage, now standard in Australian committees; it signals precision, but used repeatedly it reads as evasion.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-16", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "rescission motion", "gloss": "A motion to revoke a resolution previously carried, which usually requires notice and sometimes a higher majority to protect decisions from relitigation.", "say": "If members want to reverse last month's decision, the proper course is a *rescission motion* on notice.", "note": "Pointing to it calmly is the best answer to someone reopening a settled decision under 'business arising'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-17", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "by leave", "gloss": "With the unanimous consent of the meeting, signified by the absence of objection, permitting a departure from the rules; a single objection denies leave.", "say": "*By leave*, I'll withdraw the motion and bring a revised version next month.", "note": "Precise and economical; remember that leave is not a vote, so the chair asks 'is leave granted?' and one 'no' ends it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-18", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "friendly amendment", "gloss": "An American parliamentary notion with no standing in Westminster practice, where a mover may alter their motion only by leave of the meeting.", "say": "There's no such thing as a *friendly amendment* under our rules, but if the mover agrees we can alter the wording by leave.", "note": "Correcting it is accurate but can sound pedantic; do it once, lightly, and offer the proper mechanism in the same breath.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-19", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "suspension of standing orders", "gloss": "A resolution, often requiring a special majority, that sets aside particular rules for a defined purpose or period so that business can proceed differently.", "say": "I'd move a *suspension of standing orders* so the guest presenters can speak before the formal business.", "note": "Specify which rule and for how long; a blanket suspension invites disorder.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-20", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "quorum", "gloss": "The minimum number of members, fixed by the constitution or rules, that must be present for business to be validly transacted.", "say": "We have eight apologies, so I'll confirm we have a *quorum* before we begin.", "note": "Pronounced KWOR-um. Check it at the start and whenever someone leaves on a decisive item.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-21", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "inquorate", "gloss": "Lacking a quorum, so that the meeting cannot validly transact business and any decisions purportedly made are invalid.", "say": "Two members have left, which makes us *inquorate*; I'll have to adjourn the meeting.", "note": "Saying it before someone else notices protects the decision; carrying on regardless is a classic ground for later challenge.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-22", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "minutes", "gloss": "The formal record of a meeting: attendance, apologies, declarations, the motions put and their outcomes, and actions, as distinct from a transcript of discussion.", "say": "The *minutes* should record the resolution and who is responsible, not a précis of everyone's views.", "note": "The insider point is brevity: minutes that narrate debate create disputes and discoverable material without adding authority.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-23", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "confirmation of the minutes", "gloss": "The motion that the minutes of the previous meeting be confirmed as a true and accurate record; the meeting confirms the accuracy of the record, not the merit of the decisions.", "say": "On *confirmation of the minutes*, the only question is whether they accurately record what we decided.", "note": "Use it to stop debate being reopened under the guise of correcting the record.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-24", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "business arising", "gloss": "The agenda item that follows up matters recorded in the previous minutes, for reporting on actions rather than reopening decisions.", "say": "Under *business arising*, can the treasurer confirm the grant acquittal was lodged?", "note": "A disciplined chair keeps it to progress reports; anything substantive should be its own agenda item.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-25", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "taken as read", "gloss": "The convention that papers circulated before the meeting are deemed to have been read, so they are not presented in full.", "say": "The reports are *taken as read*; I'll invite questions on each in turn.", "note": "Works only if papers went out in time; declaring a paper tabled that morning 'taken as read' breeds resentment.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-26", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "for noting", "gloss": "The classification of an agenda item presented for information only, on which no decision is sought, as distinct from items for decision or for discussion.", "say": "That report is *for noting*; if anyone wants a decision on it, we need a paper next month.", "note": "Insist the classification is accurate; decisions smuggled in under 'for noting' are a known governance failure.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-27", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "en bloc", "gloss": "Taking several items or recommendations together in a single resolution, rather than one by one.", "say": "Unless anyone wants one starred, I'll take items 4.1 to 4.6 *en bloc*.", "note": "Pronounced 'on BLOCK'. Always offer members the chance to pull an item out first.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-28", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "consent agenda", "gloss": "A block of routine items that are adopted together without discussion unless a member asks for an item to be removed for separate consideration.", "say": "We'll put the routine reports on a *consent agenda* and spend the time on the strategic plan.", "note": "Frees serious time for serious business; its integrity depends on any single member being able to release an item without justification.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-29", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "starred item", "gloss": "An item a member has asked to be removed from the consent agenda or block resolution so that it can be discussed and resolved separately.", "say": "I'd like the facilities report to be a *starred item*; I have a question about the risk rating.", "note": "Common in university and public-sector committees; asking to star an item needs no explanation, which is the point.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-31", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "Chatham House Rule", "gloss": "The rule that participants may use the information received but may not reveal the identity or affiliation of the speaker or of any other participant.", "say": "This session is under the *Chatham House Rule*, so the ideas can travel but not the names.", "note": "It is singular, 'the Rule', not 'Rules', and it does not mean total confidentiality; misstating it is a tell.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-32", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "ruling", "gloss": "The chair's binding determination of a question of procedure or order, which stands unless the meeting carries a motion of dissent.", "say": "My *ruling* is that the amendment is in order, and we'll debate it now.", "note": "Give the ruling and a brief reason, then stop; a chair who argues for a ruling invites dissent.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-33", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "dissent from the chair's ruling", "gloss": "A motion, moved immediately after the ruling, that the meeting disagrees with it; if carried, the ruling is overturned, though the motion is about the ruling and not confidence in the chair.", "say": "With respect, Chair, I move *dissent from your ruling*; the item was listed on the agenda.", "note": "A legitimate tool rather than a personal attack; a chair who receives it graciously gains authority.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-34", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "casting vote", "gloss": "An additional vote used to resolve a tie, held by the chair only where the constitution or rules expressly confer it; at common law the chair has no casting vote.", "say": "Our rules give the chair a *casting vote*, but I'd rather we tried to reach agreement first.", "note": "Check the rules before assuming you have one; exercising a casting vote you do not hold invalidates the decision.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-35", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "deliberative vote", "gloss": "The chair's ordinary vote as a member of the body, distinct from any casting vote; some rules give the chair both, some only one.", "say": "I'll exercise my *deliberative vote* like any other member, and it will be recorded with the rest.", "note": "Knowing the difference matters when the chair has both and a tie results.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-36", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "Denison's rule", "gloss": "The convention, associated with Speaker Denison of the House of Commons, that a casting vote is used to preserve the status quo or allow further discussion rather than to decide on the chair's personal view.", "say": "I'll follow *Denison's rule* and vote against change, so the proposal can come back with better data.", "note": "Impressive in a room that knows it; elsewhere say 'the status quo convention' and explain it in a clause.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-37", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "carried", "gloss": "The formal declaration that a motion has received the required majority, its counterpart being 'lost'.", "say": "The ayes have it; the motion is *carried*.", "note": "'Carried' and 'lost' are the correct pair in Australian practice; 'passed' and 'failed' sound imported.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-38", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "nem con", "gloss": "Short for nemine contradicente, meaning that no one voted against, though some may have abstained; distinct from unanimous, which means everyone voted in favour.", "say": "Carried *nem con*, with two abstentions recorded.", "note": "Pronounced NEM-KON. Only use it when it is precisely true; in a casual room 'carried, no votes against' does the same work.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-39", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "majority of votes cast", "gloss": "The standard threshold under most rules, counting only those who vote for or against, so that abstentions do not count against the motion.", "say": "It's a *majority of votes cast*, so abstaining doesn't help the opponents.", "note": "The distinction from 'majority of those present' decides close votes; know which your rules require.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-40", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "absolute majority", "gloss": "A majority of the total membership of the body, regardless of how many are present or vote, used for decisions that should not turn on attendance.", "say": "Changing the fee policy needs an *absolute majority*, so we need seven votes in favour whoever turns up.", "note": "Often confused with a simple majority; getting it wrong is a reliable way to invalidate a decision.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-41", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "special resolution", "gloss": "A resolution requiring notice and a supermajority, commonly three-quarters of votes cast, reserved by legislation or the constitution for fundamental matters such as amending the rules.", "say": "Amending the objects requires a *special resolution* at a general meeting, with proper notice to members.", "note": "Check the exact threshold and notice period in the governing rules and Act rather than relying on memory.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-42", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "division", "gloss": "A formal counted vote in which members' votes are recorded individually, called for when a voice vote or show of hands is doubted.", "say": "I'd ask for a *division*, Chair; it was too close to call on the voices.", "note": "Parliamentary in flavour; in a school council 'can we have a count, and record it?' achieves the same without the theatre.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-43", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "have one's dissent recorded", "gloss": "To ask that the minutes show one voted against a resolution, which preserves one's position without breaching the body's collective decision.", "say": "I accept the decision, but I'd like *my dissent recorded* in the minutes.", "note": "Dignified and final; it matters most where liability or professional judgement is at stake. Overuse makes it look like posturing.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-44", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "adjourn", "gloss": "To suspend a meeting or a debate to a later time, with business resuming where it left off; adjourning the debate is different from adjourning the meeting.", "say": "I'll *adjourn* the debate on this item until we have the legal advice, and we'll continue with the agenda.", "note": "Be explicit about which you mean and when it resumes; an open-ended adjournment is often a quiet burial.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-45", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "sine die", "gloss": "Adjourned without a fixed date for resumption.", "say": "The working party has finished its task, so I'll adjourn it *sine die*.", "note": "Pronounced SY-nee DY-ee or SEE-nay DEE-ay. Fine in a formal minute; spoken in a small meeting it can sound grand.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-46", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "table a paper", "gloss": "In Australian and British usage, to present a document formally to the meeting; in American usage 'to table' means to postpone, the opposite sense.", "say": "I'll *table* the auditor's report now so it is part of the record.", "note": "With international or American colleagues, say 'present' or 'set aside' to avoid a genuine misunderstanding.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-47", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "proceed to the next business", "gloss": "A procedural motion that, if carried, sets aside the question before the meeting without a vote on its merits.", "say": "Rather than defeat it outright, I move that we *proceed to the next business*.", "note": "A courteous way to let an untimely motion fall without a recorded defeat; using it to dodge a vote that should be taken looks evasive.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-48", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "dilatory motion", "gloss": "A motion whose purpose or effect is to delay business, such as repeated motions to adjourn, which the chair may rule out of order when it is an abuse.", "say": "That's the third adjournment motion tonight; I'm ruling it a *dilatory motion* and out of order.", "note": "Use only when the pattern is clear; ruling a genuine motion dilatory looks like suppression.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-49", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "out of order", "gloss": "Contrary to the rules of debate or outside the meeting's competence, as determined by a ruling of the chair.", "say": "That motion is *out of order*, because it deals with a staffing matter outside this council's functions.", "note": "Rule the motion or the conduct out of order, never the person.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-50", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "impute improper motives", "gloss": "To attribute bad faith or dishonourable intent to another member, which standing orders typically prohibit.", "say": "I'll ask you not to *impute improper motives*; argue the proposal, not the proposer.", "note": "A clean way for the chair to cool a personal attack; members can use it on a point of order.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-51", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "withdraw the remark", "gloss": "The chair's direction that a member retract an offensive or unparliamentary remark, in Westminster practice unconditionally.", "say": "I'll ask you to *withdraw the remark*, without qualification, and then we'll continue.", "note": "The withdrawal should be unconditional; a 'withdrawal, but' is a repetition.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-52", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "order of business", "gloss": "The sequence in which the agenda items are taken, set by the rules or the agenda and varied only by the chair with the meeting's agreement.", "say": "With the meeting's agreement, I'll vary the *order of business* so our guests can present first.", "note": "Seek agreement explicitly; a chair who reorders silently can appear to be steering outcomes.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-53", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "general business", "gloss": "Items raised by members that are not otherwise on the agenda, ideally notified to the chair in advance so they can be dealt with properly.", "say": "Please send *general business* items to the secretary by Friday, so nothing arrives as a surprise.", "note": "Prefer it to 'any other business', which invites ambush; matters needing decisions belong on the agenda with papers.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-54", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "late item", "gloss": "An agenda item not circulated with the agenda papers, admitted at the chair's discretion or by leave, usually only for urgency.", "say": "I'll accept it as a *late item* given the grant deadline, but it can only be for noting tonight.", "note": "Admit late items sparingly; a habit of them signals weak secretariat or worse.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-55", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "flying minute", "gloss": "An Australian term for a resolution made out of session by written circulation and signature, valid only where the constitution expressly permits it and on the required level of agreement.", "say": "Given the timing, we'll deal with the venue contract by *flying minute* and record it at the next meeting.", "note": "Check the rules on whether all members must sign; a flying minute that falls short of the required threshold is no decision at all.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-56", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "out of session", "gloss": "Taking place between formal meetings, by circulation or delegated decision, rather than at a properly constituted meeting.", "say": "That was agreed *out of session*, so I'll ask the meeting to note it formally for the record.", "note": "Out-of-session decisions should always be reported to the next meeting; otherwise they become invisible.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-57", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "ratify", "gloss": "To adopt and confirm, after the event, an action taken without prior authority, giving it the body's endorsement where the law and rules permit.", "say": "I'd ask the board to *ratify* the chair's decision to accept the late entry fee waiver.", "note": "Ratification cures want of authority, not a decision the body could never have made in the first place.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-58", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "chair's action", "gloss": "A decision taken by the chair alone between meetings, under an express delegation or for genuine urgency, and reported to the next meeting.", "say": "That was approved by *chair's action* under the delegation, and it's reported for noting at item seven.", "note": "Standard in British-tradition university committees; used beyond its delegation it erodes trust quickly.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-60", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "ex officio", "gloss": "Holding membership by virtue of an office, such as a principal on a school council; ex officio members usually have full rights, including voting, unless the rules say otherwise.", "say": "The principal is a member *ex officio*, and under our rules that includes a vote.", "note": "Pronounced ex o-FISH-ee-oh. The common misconception that ex officio means non-voting is worth correcting gently.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-62", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "committee of the whole", "gloss": "The full body sitting as a committee, with relaxed rules on speaking, to work through detail before resuming formal session to resolve.", "say": "Let's resolve into *committee of the whole* to go through the draft syllabus, and then resume to adopt it.", "note": "Parliamentary; in most associations an informal workshop session achieves the same thing with less ceremony.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-63", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "seriatim", "gloss": "Taking a document clause by clause or point by point, rather than as a whole.", "say": "Given it's a syllabus revision, I suggest we take the assessment outline *seriatim*.", "note": "Pronounced seer-ee-AY-tim. Elegant in a drafting committee; in a general meeting 'clause by clause' is plainer and better.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-64", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "motion versus resolution", "gloss": "A motion is a proposal before the meeting; once carried it becomes a resolution, the body's decision.", "say": "It's no longer a motion; it's a *resolution* of council, and it binds us until rescinded.", "note": "A precise distinction that quietly signals command of procedure, especially in minutes and correspondence.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-66", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "the floor", "gloss": "The right to speak at a given moment, given by the chair; to 'have the floor' is to be the recognised speaker.", "say": "Professor Lee has *the floor*; I'll come to you next.", "note": "A light, effective way to protect a speaker from interruption without scolding the interrupter.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-67", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "secret ballot", "gloss": "A vote in which each member's choice is not disclosed, used for elections and sensitive matters where the rules require or allow it.", "say": "For the office-bearer elections we'll use a *secret ballot*, with the returning officer counting.", "note": "Agree the method before nominations close; changing it mid-process looks like manipulation.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-68", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "exhaustive ballot", "gloss": "An election method in which repeated rounds of voting are held, eliminating the lowest candidate each time, until one candidate has an absolute majority.", "say": "With four candidates, I suggest an *exhaustive ballot* so the winner has majority support.", "note": "Distinct from preferential voting, which achieves a similar result in a single round of ranked ballots.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-69", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "returning officer", "gloss": "The neutral person appointed to conduct an election, receive nominations, count votes and declare the result.", "say": "The secretary will act as *returning officer*, since she isn't standing.", "note": "Choose someone visibly neutral; an interested returning officer taints even a clean result.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-70", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "chair pro tem", "gloss": "A person presiding temporarily, pro tempore, in the absence or withdrawal of the chair.", "say": "The deputy will be *chair pro tem* while I step out for the AJEA item.", "note": "Pronounced pro TEM. Fine in a minute; spoken, 'acting chair' is warmer.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-71", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "vacate the chair", "gloss": "For the chair to step aside from presiding, for example because of a conflict of interest or to take part in debate, with a deputy or elected member presiding.", "say": "Because I have an interest in this item, I'll *vacate the chair* and the deputy will preside.", "note": "Doing it promptly and visibly is one of the clearest signals of integrity a chair can give.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-72", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "guillotine", "gloss": "A timetable fixing the time allowed for an item or stage of debate, after which the question is put automatically.", "say": "I'll put a *guillotine* on this item at eight o'clock and put the question then.", "note": "Parliamentary jargon with a forceful image; in most rooms 'we'll vote at eight' is the same rule without the threat.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-73", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "refer to committee", "gloss": "A motion sending a matter to a committee or working party to investigate and report back, rather than deciding it now.", "say": "I move that we *refer* the proposal to the finance committee to report at the next meeting.", "note": "Specify the question and the report date; referral without either is a polite burial.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-74", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "frame the question", "gloss": "To state precisely, before debate, the decision the meeting is being asked to make and the options available.", "say": "Let me *frame the question*: we are deciding whether to pilot this in 2027, not whether to adopt it.", "note": "The chair's single most powerful act; a well-framed question shortens debate more than any time limit.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-75", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "put the question", "gloss": "To state the motion formally and take the vote on it.", "say": "If there's no further debate, I'll *put the question*: those in favour?", "note": "Read the exact words first; the vote is on the text, not on a paraphrase of it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-76", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "sense of the meeting", "gloss": "The chair's articulation of where the meeting has arrived, without a formal vote, used in consensus processes and to guide drafting.", "say": "The *sense of the meeting* is that we support the principle but want the costings; is that fair?", "note": "Always test it with the room; a sense of the meeting the chair has invented is resented once noticed.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-77", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "straw poll", "gloss": "An informal, non-binding show of hands to gauge opinion before formal debate or drafting.", "say": "Before we draft a motion, a quick *straw poll*: who could support a two-year pilot?", "note": "Make clear that it binds no one; a straw poll later cited as a decision damages trust.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-78", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "consensus versus unanimity", "gloss": "Consensus means every participant can accept and will not obstruct the decision; unanimity means every participant positively votes for it.", "say": "I'm not asking for *unanimity*; I'm asking whether anyone can't live with this.", "note": "Naming the distinction frees reluctant members to agree without pretending to enthusiasm.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-79", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "stand aside", "gloss": "In formal consensus decision-making, a participant's recorded disagreement that does not prevent the decision, as distinct from a block, which does.", "say": "I'll *stand aside* on this one; I disagree, but I won't block it.", "note": "Natural in community and co-operative settings; in a Westminster-style board it may need a sentence of explanation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-80", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "the chair is the servant of the meeting", "gloss": "The principle that the chair's authority derives from the meeting and is exercised for it, which is why the meeting may overrule a ruling by dissent.", "say": "*The chair is the servant of the meeting*, so if you'd rather take the items in a different order, say so.", "note": "Disarming when said sincerely and early; said in the middle of a contested ruling it can sound defensive.", "level": 3}]}, {"field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "intro": "Governance talk is precise about roles, duties and evidence: who decides, on what information, with what interest, and how the board knows. The native register is calm and exact, naming the duty or distinction plainly and asking for evidence rather than reassurance.", "entries": [{"id": "board-1", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "governance versus management", "gloss": "Governance is the board's work of setting direction, approving strategy and policy, overseeing performance and holding management to account; management is the executive work of running the organisation within that frame.", "say": "That's a question of *management*, not governance; our role is to ask whether the principal has the resources and the plan.", "note": "The distinction is about the nature of the role, not the topic: a board may discuss rosters if a roster creates a strategic risk.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-2", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "noses in, fingers out", "gloss": "The shorthand that directors should probe and understand operations closely but leave execution to management.", "say": "*Noses in, fingers out*: we can ask how the tour is staffed, but we don't pick the chaperones.", "note": "Useful once, said lightly; repeated it becomes a cliché and can sound like a rebuke to the person asking.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-3", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "duty of care and diligence", "gloss": "The duty to exercise the degree of care and diligence a reasonable person would exercise in the director's position and circumstances, an objective standard set for companies by s180(1) of the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth).", "say": "Signing off accounts we haven't read wouldn't meet our *duty of care and diligence*.", "note": "The standard is objective and rises with the director's role and expertise; a treasurer is held to more on the financials than a lay member.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-4", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "business judgment rule", "gloss": "The safe harbour in s180(2) of the Corporations Act deeming the care-and-diligence duty met for a business judgment made in good faith, for a proper purpose, without material personal interest, on adequate information and in the rational belief it is in the company's best interests.", "say": "The *business judgment rule* protects a considered decision, not a decision we failed to inform ourselves about.", "note": "The statute spells it 'judgment'. It protects only the duty of care, not good faith, conflicts or insolvent trading; overclaiming its reach is a common error.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-5", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "good faith and for a proper purpose", "gloss": "The duty, for companies in s181 of the Corporations Act, to exercise powers in good faith in the best interests of the organisation and for the purpose for which the power was conferred.", "say": "Using the fee waiver to reward a donor's child would fail the *proper purpose* test, however generous it looks.", "note": "Proper purpose asks why a power was used, not whether the outcome was sensible; a well-meant use for the wrong purpose still breaches it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-6", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "improper use of position", "gloss": "The prohibition, for companies in ss182 and 183 of the Corporations Act, on directors and officers using their position or information gained through it to gain an advantage or cause detriment.", "say": "Using the board's festival data to pitch a private workshop would be an *improper use of position*.", "note": "Applies even when no loss results; the wrong is in the use, not the harm.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-7", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "fiduciary", "gloss": "Describing a relationship of trust in which one party must act in the interests of another; in Australian law fiduciary duties are proscriptive, forbidding conflict and unauthorised profit, rather than imposing positive obligations.", "say": "As directors we're *fiduciaries*, so the question isn't whether it's a good deal for us but whether we're conflicted at all.", "note": "Pronounced fi-DYOO-shee-uh-ree. The proscriptive point is the mark of real understanding and distinguishes it from the statutory duty of care.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-8", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "no-conflict and no-profit rules", "gloss": "The two strands of fiduciary obligation: not to place oneself where interest and duty conflict, and not to profit from the position without informed consent.", "say": "Even if the price is fair, the *no-conflict rule* bites the moment a director stands on both sides.", "note": "The insider point is that fairness of the transaction is not a defence; only proper disclosure and authorisation are.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-12", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "register of interests", "gloss": "The standing record of each director's relevant interests, offices and relationships, reviewed regularly and tabled with each meeting's conflict declarations.", "say": "Could we have the *register of interests* in each pack, so declarations aren't left to memory?", "note": "Its value is currency; an annual form nobody revisits gives false comfort.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-13", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "standing notice", "gloss": "A general disclosure of an ongoing interest that remains effective for future matters, so the director need not re-declare it each time, though it must be updated if the interest changes.", "say": "I've given *standing notice* of my role with the association, and it's in the register.", "note": "Does not excuse a director from withdrawing when the interest becomes live on a specific item.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-14", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "recuse", "gloss": "To withdraw from deliberation and voting on a matter because of a conflict, which is more than abstaining, since an abstainer may still shape the discussion.", "say": "I'll *recuse* myself from this item and leave the room, rather than just abstain.", "note": "Pronounced ri-KYOOZ. Leaving the room is cleaner than staying silent at the table; minute both the departure and the return.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-15", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "nominee director", "gloss": "A director appointed by, or to represent, a stakeholder group, who nonetheless owes duties to the organisation as a whole rather than to the appointer.", "say": "As a parent representative you're effectively a *nominee director*: you bring the parent view, but your duty is to the school.", "note": "One of the most important things to explain to new council and board members, kindly and early.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-18", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "materiality threshold", "gloss": "A defined level of financial or other impact above which matters must be escalated to the board or reported specifically.", "say": "We should set a *materiality threshold* so the treasurer knows what comes to us and what doesn't.", "note": "Thresholds focus board attention but never displace judgement; sub-threshold matters can still be material.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-19", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "risk appetite", "gloss": "The amount and type of risk the board is willing to pursue or accept in pursuit of its objectives, distinct from risk tolerance, the acceptable variation around particular measures, and risk capacity, the maximum the organisation can bear.", "say": "Our *risk appetite* for reputational risk with students is low; for artistic risk in programming it should be much higher.", "note": "Most persuasive when expressed by category; a single appetite statement for everything says nothing.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-20", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "risk capacity", "gloss": "The maximum level of risk an organisation can absorb before its objectives or survival are threatened, which sets the outer limit for appetite.", "say": "A second festival venue might fit our appetite, but one bad year would exceed our *risk capacity*.", "note": "Distinguishes the willing from the able; useful in small not-for-profits where a single loss can be existential.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-21", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "inherent and residual risk", "gloss": "Inherent risk is the level before controls; residual risk is the level remaining after current controls, which is what the board must judge against appetite.", "say": "The *inherent risk* of an overseas tour is high; the question is whether the *residual risk* is within appetite.", "note": "Boards that read only residual ratings never see what happens if a control fails.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-22", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "risk register", "gloss": "The structured record of identified risks, their ratings, controls, owners and treatments, reviewed by the board or its committee.", "say": "Child safeguarding should sit at the top of the *risk register*, with named controls and a named owner.", "note": "A register is a tool for discussion, not evidence that risk is managed.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-23", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "risk owner", "gloss": "The person accountable for managing a particular risk and reporting on its controls and treatments.", "say": "Who is the *risk owner* for volunteer screening?", "note": "A risk owned by 'the committee' is owned by nobody.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-24", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "key risk indicator", "gloss": "A metric that signals a change in the level of a risk, as distinct from a key performance indicator, which measures progress toward objectives.", "say": "Volunteer numbers are a *key risk indicator* for the festival, not just a performance measure.", "note": "Pairing KPIs with KRIs is a mark of sophisticated board reporting.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-25", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "Three Lines Model", "gloss": "The Institute of Internal Auditors' framework, revised in 2020 from the 'three lines of defence', distinguishing management's first-line ownership of risk, second-line risk and compliance functions, and third-line independent assurance from internal audit.", "say": "In *Three Lines* terms, the department head's checks are first line; we need something independent at the third.", "note": "Use the current name; 'three lines of defence' marks you as working from the older version.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-26", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "assurance", "gloss": "Objective, evidence-based confidence that controls and processes are working, ideally from a source independent of those who run them, as distinct from reassurance, which is simply being told all is well.", "say": "That's *reassurance*, not assurance; what independent evidence do we have that the controls work?", "note": "One of the most incisive questions a director can ask, provided it is asked courteously.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-27", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "assurance map", "gloss": "A matrix showing, for each key risk, which sources of assurance cover it and how independent and recent they are, revealing gaps and duplication.", "say": "An *assurance map* would show that nobody independent has looked at the ticketing system.", "note": "Powerful in audit and risk committees; in a small association a short table does the job without the jargon.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-28", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "delegations of authority", "gloss": "The documented framework specifying who may make which decisions and commit which resources, within what limits; the board delegates authority but retains accountability.", "say": "Under our *delegations*, the treasurer can approve up to two thousand dollars; above that it comes to the board.", "note": "The insider point is that delegation does not discharge the board's responsibility to oversee the delegate.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-29", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "instrument of delegation", "gloss": "The formal document by which authority is delegated, specifying the delegate, the scope, any limits and conditions, and how the delegation can be revoked.", "say": "Before the coordinator signs venue contracts, we need an *instrument of delegation* that says so.", "note": "Public-sector language; in a small association 'written delegation' is enough, so long as it exists.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-30", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "delegatus non potest delegare", "gloss": "The principle that one to whom power is delegated may not delegate it further unless the delegation expressly permits it.", "say": "The committee can't hand that decision on to a subcommittee: *delegatus non potest delegare*, unless the rules say otherwise.", "note": "Pronounced del-eh-GAH-tus non POH-test del-eh-GAH-reh. Say 'a delegate can't sub-delegate' in most rooms; the Latin suits a legal audience.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-31", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "reserved powers", "gloss": "The matters a board keeps for its own decision and does not delegate, typically including strategy, budget approval, the chief executive's appointment and major commitments.", "say": "Signing a three-year venue lease is one of our *reserved powers*, not something the coordinator can do.", "note": "A clear schedule of reserved matters prevents most turf disputes before they start.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-32", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "board charter", "gloss": "The document setting out the board's role, composition, responsibilities, relationship with management and ways of working.", "say": "The *board charter* says the chair speaks for the association publicly, so I'll handle the media inquiry.", "note": "Useful precisely because it is agreed before a dispute; citing it mid-conflict lowers the temperature.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-33", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "board calendar", "gloss": "The annual work plan scheduling when the board deals with recurring business such as strategy, budget, audit, risk review, evaluation and the AGM.", "say": "If we put the risk review on the *board calendar* for May, it won't keep slipping behind festival logistics.", "note": "A well-designed calendar is the cheapest governance improvement a small board can make.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-34", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "company secretary", "gloss": "The officer responsible for the board's governance processes, papers, minutes and statutory compliance, and in listed-company practice accountable directly to the board, through the chair, on the board's functioning.", "say": "The *company secretary* should own the minutes and the register, not whoever happens to volunteer.", "note": "In an incorporated association the equivalent is usually the secretary under the rules; the function matters more than the title.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-35", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "officer", "gloss": "In the Corporations Act sense, a person who makes or participates in decisions affecting the whole or a substantial part of the business, which extends statutory duties beyond formally appointed directors.", "say": "As festival director she may be an *officer* in the legal sense, with duties of her own.", "note": "A reminder that senior executives carry personal duties, not just their boards.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-36", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "de facto and shadow directors", "gloss": "A de facto director acts as a director without valid appointment; a shadow director is one whose instructions the appointed directors are accustomed to follow; both can bear directors' duties.", "say": "If the founder still directs every decision from outside, he risks being a *shadow director*.", "note": "A delicate point to raise; make it about protecting the person, not accusing them.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-37", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "accountability versus responsibility", "gloss": "Accountability is answerability for outcomes, which rests with one party and cannot be delegated; responsibility is the obligation to carry out tasks, which can be shared or delegated.", "say": "The coordinator is *responsible* for the bookings, but the board remains *accountable* for the festival.", "note": "Use the pair precisely and the room starts to use it with you.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-38", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "independence", "gloss": "Freedom from any interest, position or relationship that might influence, or reasonably be perceived to influence, a director's capacity to bring independent judgement.", "say": "For audit and risk, we want at least one member whose *independence* is beyond question.", "note": "Independence is a judgement made by the board about each director, not a status claimed by the director.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-39", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "independent non-executive director", "gloss": "A director who is not part of management and is free of relationships that could compromise independent judgement.", "say": "An *independent non-executive director* with finance expertise would strengthen the board considerably.", "note": "Corporate language; for a school council or association, 'an independent member' carries the same idea.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-40", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "independence of mind and in appearance", "gloss": "The auditing distinction between actually exercising objective judgement and being seen by a reasonable observer to be free of influence; both are required.", "say": "I'm confident of my *independence of mind*, but *in appearance* I'm too close to the applicant to sit on this panel.", "note": "Particularly useful on arts funding panels; it lets you withdraw without conceding any actual bias.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-41", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "information asymmetry", "gloss": "The structural gap between what management knows and what the board knows, which the board must manage through the design of its information flows.", "say": "There's an *information asymmetry* here; we only hear about the program when something has gone wrong.", "note": "Framing it structurally avoids implying anyone is hiding anything.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-42", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "the resolution sought", "gloss": "The exact words a board paper asks the board to adopt, stated at the outset so that directors read the paper against a decision.", "say": "Could papers open with *the resolution sought*? Then we'd know what we're deciding before page six.", "note": "One of the most effective reforms a chair can make to board papers.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-43", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "exception reporting", "gloss": "Reporting that highlights only departures from plan, policy or tolerance, so board attention goes to what needs it, a form of management by exception.", "say": "Rather than twelve pages of updates, let's move to *exception reporting* against the plan.", "note": "It works only if the thresholds for an exception are agreed and trusted.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-44", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "deep dive", "gloss": "A scheduled, focused examination of a single risk, program or function in more depth than routine reporting allows.", "say": "Could we schedule a *deep dive* into volunteer safeguarding for the August meeting?", "note": "Widely used and slightly worn; 'a focused session on' sounds less like consultancy.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-45", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "constructive challenge", "gloss": "Rigorous, respectful questioning of management proposals that tests assumptions and evidence while supporting the executive's capacity to deliver.", "say": "I mean this as *constructive challenge*: what would have to be true for these ticket numbers to hold?", "note": "The adjective matters; challenge without visible support reads as distrust.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-46", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "stewardship", "gloss": "The board's role as custodian of the organisation's purpose, assets and reputation for those who come after, beyond any current interests.", "say": "Our *stewardship* question is whether the festival will still exist for the students of 2040.", "note": "Lands in mission-driven bodies; in a purely commercial room it can sound sentimental.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-48", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "Carver's Policy Governance", "gloss": "John Carver's governance model in which the board defines ends, the outcomes for whom and at what cost, and sets executive limitations, leaving management free to choose means within them.", "say": "In *Carver* terms, we'd define the ends and set executive limitations, and stop approving individual purchases.", "note": "Influential and sometimes applied dogmatically; cite it as a lens, not a creed.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-49", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "executive limitations", "gloss": "In Policy Governance, the board's written statements of what management may not do, within which the executive is free to act.", "say": "Rather than approving every contract, we could set *executive limitations* and monitor against them.", "note": "The concept transfers well even to boards that do not adopt the full model.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-50", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "the CEO is the board's only employee", "gloss": "The principle that the board directs and holds to account the chief executive alone, who in turn directs staff, so the board does not instruct staff directly.", "say": "*The CEO is the board's only employee*, so concerns about staff go through the director, not to the staff member.", "note": "Clarifying in associations where volunteers and directors overlap; in schools the principal's statutory role adds complications.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-51", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "separation of chair and CEO roles", "gloss": "The principle that the person who leads the board should not be the person the board oversees, to preserve the board's independence from management.", "say": "The *separation of chair and CEO roles* matters even in a small association; the person running the festival shouldn't chair its board.", "note": "Uncontroversial in principle, sensitive in practice where a founder holds both roles.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-52", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "directors-only session", "gloss": "A scheduled part of a board meeting held without management present, so directors can discuss performance and concerns freely.", "say": "Let's make a *directors-only session* a standing item, so holding one never signals a problem.", "note": "Making it routine is the whole point; called ad hoc it alarms the executive.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-53", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "insolvent trading", "gloss": "Incurring debts when an organisation is insolvent or would thereby become so, which directors of companies have a statutory duty to prevent under s588G of the Corporations Act.", "say": "Before we commit to the venue deposit, I want to be sure we're nowhere near *insolvent trading*.", "note": "Solvency is assessed when each debt is incurred; 'we'll sort it out after the festival' is not a defence.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-55", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "true and fair view", "gloss": "The standard that financial statements present the organisation's financial position and performance faithfully, beyond technical compliance with accounting standards.", "say": "These may comply with the standards, but do they give a *true and fair view* of the festival's position?", "note": "The phrase carries legal weight; use it when you mean the substance, not as a flourish.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-56", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "ASIC v Healey", "gloss": "The 2011 Federal Court decision in the Centro case holding that each director must read, understand and apply their own mind to the financial statements and cannot rely wholly on management and auditors.", "say": "Since *Healey*, no director can say 'the auditors signed off, so I didn't look'.", "note": "Cite it to a board of directors or lawyers; in a school council, state the principle and leave the case name out.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-57", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "reliance on others", "gloss": "The statutory position, for companies in s189 of the Corporations Act, that a director may rely on information or advice from competent others if the reliance is in good faith and after an independent assessment.", "say": "We can rely on the auditor's advice, but *reliance* is reasonable only if we've assessed it ourselves.", "note": "The independent-assessment condition is the part most often forgotten.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-58", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "independent professional advice", "gloss": "Expert advice obtained by the board, or a director, separately from management, often provided for in the board charter at the organisation's expense.", "say": "Given the stakes, I'd like *independent professional advice* on the sponsorship contract before we sign.", "note": "Asking for it signals diligence, not distrust, if framed as the board's normal practice.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-59", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "collective responsibility", "gloss": "The convention that once the board has decided, all directors support and implement the decision publicly, whatever their view in the room.", "say": "I argued against it, but under *collective responsibility* I'll defend the decision to the members.", "note": "Dissent may be minuted, but it stays inside the boardroom; a director who campaigns against a board decision should resign.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-60", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "confidentiality of board deliberations", "gloss": "The obligation not to disclose board papers, discussions or individual positions without authority, which protects candour and the board's collective voice.", "say": "Given the *confidentiality of board deliberations*, I can tell staff the decision but not who argued what.", "note": "Applies to parent and staff representatives as much as anyone, and is worth restating at the first meeting each year.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-61", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "board evaluation", "gloss": "A structured periodic review of the board's performance, processes and composition, and often of individual directors and the chair.", "say": "A short *board evaluation* each year would tell us whether these meetings are actually adding value.", "note": "An external facilitator every few years makes it honest; self-assessment alone tends toward comfort.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-62", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "skills matrix", "gloss": "A table mapping the skills, experience and attributes the board needs against those its current directors bring, used to guide recruitment.", "say": "Our *skills matrix* shows we have plenty of musicians and no one with legal or audit experience.", "note": "Most useful when it drives a specific recruitment decision rather than sitting in the annual report.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-63", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "board renewal", "gloss": "The deliberate refreshing of board membership over time through tenure limits, succession planning and recruitment against identified needs.", "say": "*Board renewal* means planning now for the treasurer's role, before I step down.", "note": "Raising your own succession first gives you standing to raise others'.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-64", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "audit and risk committee", "gloss": "The board committee that oversees financial reporting, internal control, risk management and the relationship with the external auditor, reporting to the full board.", "say": "The *audit and risk committee* should meet the auditor without management at least once a year.", "note": "The committee advises; the full board remains accountable for the financial statements.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-66", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "control environment", "gloss": "The overall attitude, structures and practices governing internal control, including segregation of duties, authorisation and oversight.", "say": "With one person banking and reconciling the takings, our *control environment* is weaker than we'd like.", "note": "In small organisations segregation of duties is the issue that matters most and is most often missing.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-68", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "objects clause", "gloss": "The provision of a constitution or set of rules stating the purposes for which the organisation exists, against which its activities and decisions are tested.", "say": "A commercial recording label might be worthwhile, but is it within our *objects clause*?", "note": "Particularly important for charities, whose registration depends on acting within charitable purposes.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-69", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "rules of association", "gloss": "The constitution of an incorporated association, which under the Associations Incorporation Act 2015 (WA) is called its rules and binds the association and its members.", "say": "Under our *rules of association*, the treasurer has to present the financial report at the AGM.", "note": "In a WA association, 'rules' is the correct term; 'constitution' is understood but less precise.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-70", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "members in general meeting", "gloss": "The members of an organisation acting collectively at a properly convened general meeting, holding the powers the rules reserve to them, such as amending the rules and electing the committee.", "say": "Changing the membership categories is for *members in general meeting*, not for this committee.", "note": "Respecting the line between board powers and member powers avoids most AGM revolts.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-71", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "responsible persons", "gloss": "The ACNC term for the directors or committee members who govern a registered charity, whose duties are set out in ACNC Governance Standard 5.", "say": "As *responsible persons* under the ACNC standards, we have duties regardless of our being volunteers.", "note": "A sobering point for volunteer boards; deliver it as protection, not threat.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-72", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "deed of access, indemnity and insurance", "gloss": "An agreement giving a director access to board records after leaving office, an indemnity against certain liabilities, and directors and officers insurance cover.", "say": "Before new members join, we should check our D and O cover and whether we offer a *deed of access*.", "note": "Standard in companies, rarely considered in associations; raising it signals experience on other boards.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-73", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "escalation protocol", "gloss": "The agreed rules for when and how matters move from management to the chair or full board, including timing and thresholds.", "say": "We need an *escalation protocol*, so a safeguarding incident reaches the chair within hours, not at the next meeting.", "note": "Good protocols are specific about who, how quickly and by what channel.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-76", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "put on inquiry", "gloss": "Placed, by information that raises a concern, under an obligation to investigate further rather than accept assurances at face value.", "say": "Once the auditor flagged the cash discrepancy, we were *put on inquiry*; we can't simply wait for next year.", "note": "A legally flavoured phrase that sharpens a board's sense of obligation; use it when the concern is concrete.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-77", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "agency problem", "gloss": "The risk that those managing an organisation pursue interests that differ from those of the principals they serve, which governance mechanisms exist to contain.", "say": "Every *agency problem* we have comes back to the same thing: the people running the festival also set their own fees.", "note": "Economic language that suits a university or policy audience; elsewhere name the specific misalignment.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-78", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "capture", "gloss": "The condition in which a board ceases to exercise independent judgement because it has come to identify with management or a dominant stakeholder.", "say": "After ten years with the same executive, I worry about board *capture*.", "note": "A serious charge; raise it as a structural risk for the board to manage, never as an accusation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-79", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "groupthink", "gloss": "The tendency of a cohesive group to converge on a view without critical evaluation, in Irving Janis's account, suppressing dissent in favour of consensus.", "say": "We all agreed very quickly; can someone argue the other side so we're sure this isn't *groupthink*?", "note": "Worn from overuse; it lands better as a request for a devil's advocate than as a diagnosis.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-80", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "conformance and performance", "gloss": "The two dimensions of board work: conformance, meaning compliance and accountability, and performance, meaning strategy and value creation, which a board must hold in balance.", "say": "We spend nearly every meeting on *conformance*; when do we talk about *performance*?", "note": "A useful frame for rebalancing agendas, especially for compliance-heavy school and charity boards.", "level": 3}]}, {"field": "High register: the precise word", "intro": "This is the register of the senior common room, the bench and the drafting committee, where one exact word replaces a vague phrase and is noticed for its exactness. Native speakers use these words sparingly and correctly, deploy them on documents and arguments rather than on people, and know which of them must be translated back into plain English for a lay room.", "entries": [{"id": "high-register-1", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "adumbrate", "gloss": "To outline in a sketchy, provisional way or to foreshadow, as distinct from 'set out', which implies completeness.", "say": "The discussion paper *adumbrates* a senior pathway in contemporary music but stops well short of a course outline.", "note": "Lands in writing or in a committee of academics when you mean precisely 'sketched but not specified'; spoken in a school council it sounds like showing off, so use 'foreshadows' there. Stress on the first syllable: AD-um-brate.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-2", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "gainsay", "gloss": "To deny or contradict a claim, usually used in the negative ('no one can gainsay').", "say": "No one at this table would *gainsay* the quality of the ensemble program; the question is what it costs per student.", "note": "Almost always works best in the negative construction; as a positive verb ('I gainsay that') it sounds archaic. Pronounced gain-SAY.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-3", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "vitiate", "gloss": "To impair the quality or validity of something, often fatally, as in a flaw that vitiates a finding or a contract.", "say": "A sample that small *vitiates* the comparison with the state mean, however striking the numbers look.", "note": "Exact and powerful in an assessment or research committee; overkill for a minor weakness, where 'weakens' is truer. Pronounced VISH-ee-ate.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-4", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "obviate", "gloss": "To remove the need for something by anticipating it, as distinct from 'avoid' or 'prevent' in general.", "say": "If we publish the audition criteria in advance we *obviate* most of the appeals we had last year.", "note": "Means 'make unnecessary', not 'make obvious'; misusing it is a giveaway. Fine in any formal room.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-5", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "elide", "gloss": "To omit or pass over something, often so that a distinction is quietly suppressed, as distinct from simply 'leaving out'.", "say": "The draft *elides* the difference between participation and achievement, and the two need separate measures.", "note": "Carries a faint charge that the omission was convenient, so it doubles as a polite criticism; in a parent meeting, say 'skips over'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-6", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "foreground", "gloss": "To give something prominence in an argument or document, deliberately placing it ahead of other considerations.", "say": "I would *foreground* student retention in the submission and leave the facilities argument for the appendix.", "note": "Standard in academic and policy talk and safe as a verb; overuse in one meeting makes it a tic.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-7", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "problematise", "gloss": "To treat something taken for granted as open to question and examine its assumptions, as distinct from simply calling it a problem.", "say": "I want to *problematise* the idea that the audition measures potential rather than prior tuition.", "note": "Native among education academics; among principals, board members or public servants it can sound like jargon for 'question', so translate it there.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-8", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "conflate", "gloss": "To merge two distinct ideas so they are treated as one, usually in error.", "say": "We are *conflating* the instrumental program's budget with the classroom music budget, and they come from different lines.", "note": "Precise and widely understood; use it on the idea, not the person ('the paper conflates', not 'you conflate'), to keep it collegial.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-9", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "reify", "gloss": "To treat an abstraction as if it were a concrete thing with independent existence.", "say": "We should be careful not to *reify* the achievement band; it is a description of a performance, not a property of the child.", "note": "Exact in a philosophy-of-assessment discussion; elsewhere it puzzles people. Pronounced REE-uh-fy or RAY-uh-fy; both are accepted.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-10", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "traduce", "gloss": "To speak badly of someone or misrepresent them in a way that damages their reputation.", "say": "I would not want the minutes to *traduce* the previous coordinator, who built the program from nothing.", "note": "A strong, formal word for defamation-lite; useful precisely because it is sharper than 'criticise'. Too heavy for anything short of real unfairness.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-11", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "impugn", "gloss": "To call into question the honesty, motives or validity of someone or something.", "say": "I am not *impugning* anyone's motives; I am questioning whether the data supports the conclusion.", "note": "Most useful in the disclaimer form above, which separates the argument from the person; you impugn integrity or a claim, not a person directly.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-12", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "cavil", "gloss": "To raise trivial or petty objections; as a noun, such an objection.", "say": "My only *cavil* is with the timeline, which assumes the venue is free in Week 3.", "note": "Elegant when applied to yourself as a modest reservation; applied to someone else's objection it is a put-down. Pronounced KAV-il.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-13", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "inveigh against", "gloss": "To protest or speak out strongly and at length against something.", "say": "I could *inveigh against* the scheduling of exams over the festival, but it is more useful to propose an alternative date.", "note": "Always takes 'against'; used self-deprecatingly it signals you know you are on a hobby horse. Pronounced in-VAY.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-14", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "militate against", "gloss": "Of a fact or circumstance, to be a powerful factor working against an outcome; distinct from 'mitigate', which means to lessen severity.", "say": "The timing *militates against* a Term 4 launch; the staffing is not in place until January.", "note": "The subject is a circumstance, not a person. Confusing it with 'mitigate' ('mitigate against') is a common and noticed error.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-15", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "mitigate", "gloss": "To make something less severe, serious or painful; it takes a direct object and never 'against'.", "say": "We can *mitigate* the risk by having two staff on every bus, but we cannot remove it.", "note": "Say 'mitigate the risk', never 'mitigate against the risk'; the latter is a conflation with 'militate' that careful listeners notice.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-16", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "refute", "gloss": "To prove a claim false with evidence or argument; distinct from 'rebut' or 'deny', which only contest it.", "say": "The enrolment figures *refute* the claim that the specialist program draws students away from the local intake.", "note": "Only say 'refute' if you have actually disproved the point; 'I refute that' meaning 'I deny that' is a common misuse that undercuts you in front of academics.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-17", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "rebut", "gloss": "To contest or argue against a claim, offering a counter-case, without implying you have disproved it.", "say": "Let me *rebut* that briefly, and the committee can weigh the two positions.", "note": "The modest, accurate word when you are arguing rather than demonstrating; in law a rebuttal answers a case, which is the sense it carries.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-18", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "subsume", "gloss": "To include or absorb something within a larger category or structure, so that it loses its separate status.", "say": "If music technology is *subsumed* under Media, it will lose its specialist staffing within two years.", "note": "Precise for organisational and conceptual absorption; not a synonym for 'consume' or 'replace'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-19", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "countenance", "gloss": "As a verb, to tolerate, approve or permit, usually in the negative.", "say": "The council would not *countenance* a fee that excluded students on the basis of family income.", "note": "Strong and formal, best in the negative; signals a firm line without raising the voice.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-20", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "equivocate", "gloss": "To use ambiguous language to avoid committing oneself; distinct from 'hedge', which signals uncertainty rather than evasion.", "say": "I will not *equivocate*: the program cannot run at current staffing.", "note": "Powerful when used of yourself to announce directness; used of someone else it is an accusation of evasion.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-21", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "temporise", "gloss": "To delay a decision or avoid committing in order to gain time, often while appearing to engage.", "say": "We can *temporise* until the budget is released, but parents will need an answer before re-enrolment.", "note": "Accurate and slightly critical; acknowledging that you are temporising, and why, makes it honest rather than evasive.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-22", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "dissemble", "gloss": "To conceal one's real motives or feelings under a false appearance; distinct from 'disassemble'.", "say": "There is no point *dissembling* about the reason for the change: it is the timetable, not the pedagogy.", "note": "A moral word; aimed at a colleague it is an accusation of deceit, so keep it for yourself or for institutions.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-23", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "valorise", "gloss": "To assign high value to something, often implicitly and in a way that privileges it over alternatives.", "say": "The current criteria *valorise* notated performance and give almost nothing for improvisation.", "note": "Common in critical theory and arts education writing; outside academic rooms it can sound like jargon, so 'privilege' or 'rewards' may travel better.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-24", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "privilege", "gloss": "As a verb, to treat one thing as more important than another, especially in a framework or criteria.", "say": "Any rubric *privileges* something; the question is whether it privileges what we actually teach.", "note": "Native in academic and policy talk; distinct from its legal and social senses, so keep the context clear.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-25", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "belie", "gloss": "To give a false impression of something, or to show something to be false; distinct from 'betray', which reveals the truth.", "say": "The modest budget line *belies* how much of the festival depends on volunteer hours.", "note": "Often misused to mean 'reveal'; the core sense is contradiction between appearance and reality.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-26", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "arrogate", "gloss": "To claim or take a power, right or responsibility without justification.", "say": "The subcommittee should not *arrogate* to itself a decision the constitution reserves to the full board.", "note": "Exact for overreach of authority; often confused with 'abrogate', which means to repeal or abolish.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-27", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "abrogate", "gloss": "To repeal or formally abolish a law, agreement or right; in common use, to fail to discharge a responsibility.", "say": "Delegating it entirely would *abrogate* the board's responsibility for financial oversight.", "note": "The 'abdicate responsibility' sense is widespread but some purists hold to the formal 'repeal' sense; either way, do not confuse it with 'arrogate'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-28", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "interrogate", "gloss": "In academic usage, to examine an idea or assumption critically and systematically.", "say": "We need to *interrogate* what 'excellence' means before it goes into the mission statement.", "note": "Fluent in a university or curriculum room; among parents and principals it sounds like seminar jargon, and overuse anywhere becomes a tell.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-29", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "elucidate", "gloss": "To make something clear by explanation, especially something complex or obscure.", "say": "Could the writer *elucidate* how the moderation sample will be drawn?", "note": "Polite and formal as a request; asking a colleague to 'elucidate' in a casual meeting can sound arch.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-30", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "prevaricate", "gloss": "To speak or act evasively so as to avoid the truth; distinct from 'procrastinate', which is to delay.", "say": "The department is not refusing; it is *prevaricating*, which is worse because we cannot plan around it.", "note": "Frequently confused with procrastinate; using it precisely signals care. It is an accusation of evasion, so use it of institutions more readily than of people present.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-31", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "tendentious", "gloss": "Promoting a particular cause or point of view, especially a controversial one, under the appearance of neutrality.", "say": "The framing of the survey question is *tendentious*; it assumes the program is a burden on the school.", "note": "A measured but pointed criticism of a document or framing; aimed at a person it reads as an accusation of bad faith.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-32", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "specious", "gloss": "Superficially plausible but actually wrong; distinct from 'spurious', which means false or not genuine.", "say": "The comparison with interstate programs is *specious*; they are funded on a completely different basis.", "note": "The key is 'looks right but isn't'; using it for something simply wrong loses the nuance. Pronounced SPEE-shus.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-33", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "sanguine", "gloss": "Optimistic, especially in a difficult situation; often used in the negative to signal measured doubt.", "say": "I am less *sanguine* than the report about recruitment in regional areas.", "note": "'I am not sanguine' is an elegant way to express worry without alarm; pronounced SANG-gwin.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-34", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "perfunctory", "gloss": "Carried out with minimum effort or reflection, as a routine duty.", "say": "A *perfunctory* consultation will not satisfy the parent body; they need to see their submissions answered.", "note": "Precise and widely understood; it is a criticism of manner, not of motive.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-35", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "salutary", "gloss": "Producing a beneficial effect, especially through an unwelcome or unpleasant experience; distinct from 'salubrious', which means healthy.", "say": "Losing the grant was a *salutary* lesson in not relying on a single funding source.", "note": "Best for a useful lesson learnt the hard way; not a synonym for 'good'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-36", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "fulsome", "gloss": "Traditionally, excessive or insincere to the point of being distasteful; in modern usage increasingly 'abundant'.", "say": "I would avoid anything too *fulsome* in the vote of thanks; a short, specific acknowledgement will land better.", "note": "'Fulsome praise' was once a criticism; many careful speakers still hear it that way, so 'generous' is safer if you mean praise you approve of.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-37", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "enormity", "gloss": "Traditionally, great wickedness or a moral outrage; in common use it also means immense size.", "say": "Only later did the board grasp the *enormity* of the misappropriation.", "note": "Careful listeners still reserve it for wrongdoing; for scale say 'magnitude' or 'scale', or you risk sounding as if a workload were a crime.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-38", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "disinterested", "gloss": "Impartial, having no stake in the outcome; distinct from 'uninterested', which means not interested.", "say": "We need a *disinterested* panel member, someone with no student in the ensemble.", "note": "Using it for 'bored' is a common error that a precise room notices; in governance it is exactly the word for an unconflicted person.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-39", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "meretricious", "gloss": "Apparently attractive but having no real value; showily superficial.", "say": "The marketing video is *meretricious*: lovely footage, no evidence of learning.", "note": "Strong and literary; it has nothing to do with merit, and its root refers to prostitution, so it is a sharp insult. Use sparingly.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-40", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "otiose", "gloss": "Serving no practical purpose; redundant.", "say": "With the new clause in place, the second paragraph is *otiose* and can be struck.", "note": "Excellent in drafting committees; elsewhere 'redundant' is clearer. Pronounced OH-shee-ohss or OH-tee-ohss.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-41", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "anodyne", "gloss": "Not likely to cause offence or disagreement and, as a result, somewhat bland.", "say": "The draft mission statement is so *anodyne* that it could belong to any school in the state.", "note": "A neat critique of safe drafting; it can also be a virtue in a press line, so say which you mean.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-42", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "apposite", "gloss": "Apt in the circumstances, well suited; distinct from 'opposite'.", "say": "That example is *apposite*: it is exactly the kind of task the new syllabus is designed to reward.", "note": "A gracious way to credit a colleague's contribution; pronounced AP-uh-zit.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-43", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "germane", "gloss": "Relevant to the subject under consideration.", "say": "The history of the dispute is *germane* only insofar as it explains the current constitution.", "note": "Useful for steering a discussion politely; 'not germane' is a courteous way to rule a tangent out.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-44", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "cogent", "gloss": "Clear, logical and convincing.", "say": "The submission is *cogent*, but it argues for a resource we do not control.", "note": "A respectful acknowledgement before a 'but'; it praises the argument, not necessarily its conclusion.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-45", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "moot", "gloss": "In Australian and British usage, open to debate; in American usage, of no practical significance.", "say": "Whether the festival moves to August is a *moot* point; the board has not yet considered it.", "note": "The two senses point in opposite directions, so make the meaning clear from context; in an Australian room the debatable sense is native.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-46", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "egregious", "gloss": "Outstandingly bad; shocking.", "say": "The most *egregious* error is the one in the published timetable, because parents have already acted on it.", "note": "Strong but well understood; save it for real failures so it keeps its force.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-47", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "invidious", "gloss": "Likely to arouse resentment or unfairness, especially by making unfair distinctions.", "say": "It would be *invidious* to single out one ensemble for praise in the newsletter.", "note": "Good for declining to rank people or groups; distinct from 'insidious', which means harmful in a gradual, hidden way.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-48", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "putative", "gloss": "Generally considered or reputed to be; supposed.", "say": "The *putative* savings from merging the ensembles disappear once you count the extra transport.", "note": "Precise for something assumed but not yet established; avoids saying 'alleged', which implies wrongdoing.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-49", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "inchoate", "gloss": "Just begun and not fully formed or developed; distinct from 'incoherent'.", "say": "The idea of a regional outreach program is still *inchoate*; I would not put it in the plan yet.", "note": "Often misused to mean 'confused'; the correct sense is 'undeveloped'. Pronounced in-KOH-ate.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-50", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "sui generis", "gloss": "Unique; of its own kind and not comparable to others.", "say": "The jazz festival is *sui generis* in the state calendar; there is no comparable event to benchmark against.", "note": "Latin that lands in a legal or academic room; in a parent body say 'unique'. Pronounced SOO-ee JEN-er-is.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-51", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "parlous", "gloss": "Full of danger or uncertainty; precarious.", "say": "The association's reserves are in a *parlous* state after two years of reduced sponsorship.", "note": "A dignified old word that suits a treasurer's report; it signals alarm without hysteria.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-52", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "peremptory", "gloss": "Insisting on immediate attention or obedience, brusquely; in law, not open to appeal or challenge.", "say": "The email was rather *peremptory*, but the deadline itself is reasonable.", "note": "Useful for naming tone without overreacting; stress on the second syllable: puh-REMP-tuh-ree.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-53", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "ineluctable", "gloss": "Unable to be resisted or avoided; inescapable.", "say": "The *ineluctable* conclusion is that the program needs a second full-time position.", "note": "Grand; it lands once in a speech or report and sounds overwrought if repeated.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-54", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "facile", "gloss": "Superficially easy and lacking depth, ignoring the true complexities.", "say": "It would be *facile* to blame the decline on the timetable alone.", "note": "A cutting word for an argument; applied to a person it is a serious insult.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-55", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "desideratum", "gloss": "Something needed or wanted; a thing regarded as essential (plural desiderata).", "say": "A dedicated rehearsal space is a *desideratum*, not a luxury, if we want the ensembles to grow.", "note": "Lands in a university or funding submission; spoken in a department meeting it can sound mannered.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-56", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "sine qua non", "gloss": "An essential condition; something without which the thing cannot exist.", "say": "Instrumental tuition is the *sine qua non* of a specialist music program.", "note": "Latin that most educated listeners know; overusing Latin tags in one meeting looks performative. Pronounced SIN-ay kwah NON or SY-nee kway NON.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-57", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "lacuna", "gloss": "A gap or missing part, especially in a text, argument or body of knowledge (plural lacunae).", "say": "The literature review has an obvious *lacuna*: nothing on instrumental programs in public schools.", "note": "Native in academic and research rooms; in plain talk 'gap' is clearer. Pronounced luh-KYOO-nuh.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-58", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "aporia", "gloss": "An irresolvable internal contradiction or logical impasse in a text or argument.", "say": "The policy reaches an *aporia*: it requires selection by merit and open access in the same paragraph.", "note": "A term from philosophy and literary theory; very precise in an academic room, affected anywhere else.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-59", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "corollary", "gloss": "A proposition that follows directly from one already established.", "say": "The *corollary* of open enrolment is that we must resource beginners as well as advanced students.", "note": "Precise and widely understood; British and Australian stress on the second syllable: kuh-ROL-uh-ree.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-60", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "shibboleth", "gloss": "A custom, phrase or belief that distinguishes a group, often one that is outdated or unexamined.", "say": "The idea that only auditioned students benefit from ensembles is a *shibboleth* worth testing.", "note": "Captures 'an unquestioned insider belief'; strongest when you add that it should be examined, not mocked.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-61", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "canard", "gloss": "An unfounded rumour or story, often one repeated so often it is believed.", "say": "The claim that music students underperform in other subjects is a *canard*; the school's own data shows the opposite.", "note": "A strong word for a false claim; be sure of your evidence before using it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-62", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "sophistry", "gloss": "The use of clever but fallacious arguments, especially with the intention of deceiving.", "say": "Calling the cut a 'reallocation' is *sophistry*; the program will have less money.", "note": "Implies bad faith; aimed at a person present it ends the conversation, so apply it to the argument.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-64", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "imprimatur", "gloss": "Formal or official approval or sanction.", "say": "The proposal will move faster if it carries the principal's *imprimatur* before it reaches council.", "note": "A crisp, weighty word in governance; pronounced im-pri-MAH-tur.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-65", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "gravamen", "gloss": "The essence or most serious part of a complaint or accusation.", "say": "The *gravamen* of the parents' complaint is not the result but the lack of written feedback.", "note": "A legal term that is very exact for complaints handling; to a lay room say 'the heart of the complaint'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-66", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "caveat", "gloss": "A warning or proviso of specific stipulations, conditions or limitations.", "say": "I support the motion, with one *caveat*: the trial needs a review date.", "note": "Standard and safe; one caveat sounds judicious, five sound like hedging.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-68", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "purview", "gloss": "The scope of the influence or concerns of a person, body or document.", "say": "Uniform policy is outside the *purview* of this committee.", "note": "A clean way to mark boundaries of authority; more formal than 'remit' and less legal than 'jurisdiction'.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-69", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "decimate", "gloss": "Strictly, to kill one in ten; in modern usage, to destroy a large proportion of something.", "say": "Losing the Year 10 elective would *decimate* the senior pipeline within three years.", "note": "Some purists object to the modern sense, but it is standard; the real error is using it for total destruction ('annihilate').", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-70", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "nonplussed", "gloss": "Surprised and confused so much that one is unsure how to react; not 'unbothered' or 'calm'.", "say": "The panel was *nonplussed* by the request; it had not been raised in any earlier correspondence.", "note": "The 'unfazed' sense is a common error, especially in North America; in a precise room the traditional sense is expected.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-71", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "comprise", "gloss": "To consist of or be made up of; the whole comprises the parts.", "say": "The board *comprises* seven elected members and two appointees.", "note": "'Is comprised of' is widely used but criticised by careful writers; 'comprises' or 'is composed of' is safe.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-72", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "reticent", "gloss": "Not revealing one's thoughts or feelings readily; reserved. Not 'reluctant'.", "say": "The previous treasurer was *reticent* about the details, so the audit took longer than expected.", "note": "Using it to mean 'unwilling' ('reticent to commit') is a common slip; say 'reluctant' for that.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-73", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "bemused", "gloss": "Puzzled or bewildered; not 'mildly amused'.", "say": "I am a little *bemused* that the proposal reached the agenda without coming through the department.", "note": "The 'amused' sense is spreading but is still read as an error by careful listeners; the puzzled sense is a polite way to signal concern.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-74", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "fortuitous", "gloss": "Happening by chance, rather than by design; not necessarily 'fortunate'.", "say": "The overlap of the two festivals was *fortuitous*, not planned, but we should plan for it next year.", "note": "Traditionally means 'by chance', whether good or bad; using it for 'lucky' is common but not universally accepted.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-75", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "restive", "gloss": "Unable to keep still or silent, especially due to boredom or dissatisfaction; restless in a way that resists control.", "say": "The parent body is growing *restive* about the delay in the instrument hire scheme.", "note": "Not a synonym for 'restful'; it signals mounting dissatisfaction, which makes it valuable in governance.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-76", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "proscribe", "gloss": "To forbid, especially by law; distinct from 'prescribe', which means to direct or require.", "say": "The policy *proscribes* the use of school instruments for private engagements.", "note": "Confusing the two inverts the meaning; this is one of the errors precise drafters catch immediately.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-77", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "tortuous", "gloss": "Full of twists and turns; excessively complex. Distinct from 'torturous', which means causing torture.", "say": "The approval process for the tour was *tortuous*: six forms and three sign-offs.", "note": "Mixing the two is a common error; 'tortuous' is the correct word for a convoluted process.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-78", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "credulous", "gloss": "Having too great a readiness to believe things; distinct from 'credible', which means believable.", "say": "We should not be *credulous* about the vendor's claims on durability.", "note": "Using 'credible' for 'credulous' or vice versa is a familiar slip; get them right and you will be heard as precise.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-79", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "flout", "gloss": "To openly disregard a rule or convention; distinct from 'flaunt', which means to display ostentatiously.", "say": "The booking agent *flouted* the agreed timetable and moved our slot without notice.", "note": "'Flaunting the rules' is a well-known error; precise speakers notice it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-80", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "simplistic", "gloss": "Treating complex issues as if they were much simpler than they are; not a synonym for 'simple'.", "say": "A single participation figure is *simplistic*; it hides the drop-off between Years 9 and 10.", "note": "It is always a criticism; using it as a fancier 'simple' is a giveaway.", "level": 1}]}, {"field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "intro": "This is the register of people who move decisions through institutions: understated, procedural and precise about who decides, who must be told and what has actually been agreed. Fluency shows less in vocabulary than in distinctions, such as the office versus the department, off the record versus not for attribution, and a position versus the interest behind it.", "entries": [{"id": "politics-1", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "stakeholder management", "gloss": "The deliberate identification, prioritisation and engagement of those affected by or able to influence a decision; distinct from consultation, which is one tool within it.", "say": "The proposal is sound; what it lacks is *stakeholder management*, especially with the feeder primaries.", "note": "Standard in public sector rooms; among academics it can sound managerial, so 'who needs to be brought along' may travel better.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-2", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "coalition", "gloss": "A temporary alliance of parties with distinct interests who combine to achieve a shared aim.", "say": "We need a *coalition* with the Arts and Languages areas if the timetable change is to get through.", "note": "The core idea is that members keep their own interests; you build a coalition around a specific ask, not a friendship.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-4", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "BATNA", "gloss": "Best Alternative To a Negotiated Agreement: what you will do if the negotiation fails, which sets your real leverage (from Fisher and Ury, Getting to Yes).", "say": "Our *BATNA* is to run the festival at the university venue; it costs more, but it means we do not have to accept their dates.", "note": "Lands with anyone trained in negotiation; with others, describe it ('our fallback') rather than using the acronym. Pronounced BAT-nuh.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-5", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "anchoring", "gloss": "Setting an initial figure or position that frames all subsequent discussion and pulls the outcome towards it.", "say": "They opened at forty per cent of the hall fee, which was *anchoring*; we need to put our own number on the table before we respond.", "note": "Naming the tactic aloud can neutralise it, but usually it is better to re-anchor quietly with your own credible figure.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-6", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the walk-away point", "gloss": "The least favourable outcome you will accept before ending the negotiation; also called the reservation point.", "say": "Our *walk-away point* is a two-year agreement; anything shorter and we lose the sponsorship cycle.", "note": "Decide it before the meeting and keep it private; revealing it invites the other side to settle at it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-7", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "ZOPA", "gloss": "Zone of possible agreement: the range between the parties' walk-away points within which a deal is possible.", "say": "If their maximum is below our minimum, there is no *ZOPA* and we should stop spending time on this.", "note": "A negotiation-course term; useful with trained negotiators, jargon to everyone else. Pronounced ZOH-puh.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-8", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "interests versus positions", "gloss": "The distinction between what a party says it wants (its position) and why it wants it (its interest); central to principled negotiation.", "say": "It is a case of *interests versus positions*: their position is that rehearsals finish at five, their interest is the cleaners' roster, and that we can solve.", "note": "The single most useful analytic move in a stalemate; saying 'what is the interest behind that?' signals sophistication without jargon.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-9", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "principled negotiation", "gloss": "Negotiation on the merits, focused on interests and objective criteria rather than positional bargaining (Fisher and Ury's method).", "say": "I would rather approach the venue on the basis of *principled negotiation*: show them the attendance data and agree a fee formula.", "note": "Credible with people who know the literature; elsewhere just do it, without naming it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-10", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "integrative bargaining", "gloss": "Negotiation that looks for trade-offs creating joint gains ('expanding the pie'), distinct from distributive bargaining over a fixed sum.", "say": "This is *integrative bargaining*: they want weekday use of our hall, we want weekend use of their stage.", "note": "A technical term; with a lay board, 'a trade that suits both of us' is the same idea.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-11", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "distributive bargaining", "gloss": "Negotiation over a fixed resource in which one party's gain is the other's loss.", "say": "The hall fee is pure *distributive bargaining*; there is nothing to trade except the number.", "note": "Useful for diagnosing when to stop looking for creative solutions and simply haggle.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-12", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "quid pro quo", "gloss": "An exchange of one thing for another, a favour or advantage given in return for something.", "say": "There is a reasonable *quid pro quo* available: our students perform at their open day, they waive the venue fee.", "note": "Neutral in negotiation but can imply impropriety in public office; avoid it where it could be read as an inducement.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-13", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "horse-trading", "gloss": "Hard, shrewd bargaining in which each side concedes something to get something, often informally.", "say": "There will be some *horse-trading* over the timetable, and I am prepared to give up the Friday slot.", "note": "Candid and a little cynical; fine among peers, unwise in a minute or in front of parents.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-14", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "logrolling", "gloss": "The trading of support between parties so each gets something passed: I back your measure, you back mine.", "say": "I suspect some *logrolling* between the two faculties; they have voted together on every item today.", "note": "An Americanism from legislative politics; in an Australian room it may need a gloss, and it implies the trade is self-serving.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-15", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "sotto voce", "gloss": "Literally 'under the voice': said quietly or privately, often aside, so that it is not part of the formal record.", "say": "The deputy mentioned *sotto voce* that the budget might be revisited in Term 2.", "note": "A musician will pronounce it correctly (SOT-oh VOH-chay); used once it is elegant, but it can sound precious in an email.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-16", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "off the record", "gloss": "Information given on the understanding that it will not be quoted or attributed, and often not used at all.", "say": "*Off the record*, the panel was split; on the record, the decision is unanimous.", "note": "Its meaning varies between people, so agree the terms explicitly before saying anything sensitive; never assume it binds a journalist.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-17", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "not for attribution", "gloss": "Information that may be used but not attributed to the source by name; distinct from 'off the record', which means not used at all.", "say": "You can use the figure, but it is *not for attribution*; say it came from the sector.", "note": "The distinction from 'off the record' matters and is often missed; spell the terms out.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-19", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the back channel", "gloss": "An informal, unofficial line of communication used alongside formal ones, often to test positions or resolve deadlock.", "say": "The formal letter will go to the Authority, but I would use *the back channel* through the reference group chair first.", "note": "Valuable and normal; overt reliance on it can look like going around formal process, so use it to prepare, not replace, the official channel.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-20", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "in principle", "gloss": "Accepted as to the general idea, but not committed as to the detail, resources or timing.", "say": "The principal supports the tour *in principle*, which means we still need to find the money.", "note": "Often a polite delay; always ask what would make it 'in practice'.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-21", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "a holding position", "gloss": "A provisional public stance adopted to buy time while a fuller response is prepared, committing to nothing substantive.", "say": "Our *holding position* is that the matter is under review and families will hear by Friday.", "note": "A legitimate tool in a crisis; held too long, it reads as evasion.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-22", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "political capital", "gloss": "The trust, goodwill and influence a person has accumulated, which can be spent to win support for a contested decision.", "say": "I am not going to spend *political capital* on the uniform debate; I need it for the staffing case.", "note": "Thinking in these terms is a sign of seniority; saying it too often can sound calculating.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-23", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "optics", "gloss": "How an action or decision will appear to the public or an audience, as distinct from its substance.", "say": "The decision is defensible, but the *optics* of cutting the band in the same week as the new sports oval are poor.", "note": "Ubiquitous in politics and increasingly mocked as a buzzword; 'how it will look' is plainer and sometimes stronger.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-24", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the minister's office", "gloss": "The minister's personal political staff, distinct from the department, whose officers are public servants.", "say": "The department supports the idea, but *the minister's office* will decide the timing.", "note": "Knowing the difference between the office and the department is a basic marker of fluency in government; mixing them up reads as naive.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-25", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "briefing up", "gloss": "Informing a superior or more senior body of an issue, its implications and a recommended action, so they are not surprised.", "say": "I will be *briefing up* to the principal before the parents' letter goes out.", "note": "The cardinal rule in public institutions is 'no surprises'; briefing up before an issue breaks is the expected practice.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-26", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "no surprises", "gloss": "The convention that seniors and ministers must be told of significant issues before they become public or reach them from elsewhere.", "say": "In the spirit of *no surprises*, I wanted you to know the festival will run at a loss this year.", "note": "A deeply held norm in Westminster systems; breaching it costs more trust than the bad news itself.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-27", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "a briefing note", "gloss": "A short formal document setting out an issue, background, options and a recommendation for a decision-maker.", "say": "I will put a one-page *briefing note* to the principal with three options and a recommendation.", "note": "Senior readers want the recommendation and the decision required on the first page; background goes last.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-28", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "noting brief versus decision brief", "gloss": "The distinction between a brief that informs a decision-maker and one that asks them to make or approve a decision.", "say": "Let us be clear on *noting brief versus decision brief*: this one informs the board, it does not ask it to decide.", "note": "Labelling it explicitly saves meetings and avoids accidental decisions; mislabelling invites scope creep.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-29", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the long game", "gloss": "A strategy focused on long-term outcomes, accepting short-term losses or delays.", "say": "Losing this vote is fine; we are playing *the long game* on the senior syllabus.", "note": "Persuasive when backed by a visible plan; without one, it sounds like a rationalisation.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-30", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "face-saving", "gloss": "Allowing a party to concede without humiliation, often by framing or a token concession.", "say": "We should give them a *face-saving* way out: let them announce the change as part of their review.", "note": "Often the cheapest concession in any negotiation; ignoring face is a common cause of deadlock.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-31", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "a golden bridge", "gloss": "An easy, dignified route for an opponent to retreat or concede, offered deliberately.", "say": "Let us build them *a golden bridge*: if they accept the August dates, we will credit them in the program.", "note": "An old military-strategy image; it signals magnanimity, so use it without triumph.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-32", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "constructive ambiguity", "gloss": "Deliberately ambiguous wording that lets parties with differing positions each accept a text and move forward.", "say": "The phrase 'where practicable' is *constructive ambiguity*; it lets both the Authority and the schools sign off.", "note": "Powerful for unlocking a stalemate; leave too much ambiguity and the dispute returns at implementation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-33", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "nothing is agreed until everything is agreed", "gloss": "The principle that concessions on individual points are provisional until the whole package is settled.", "say": "We can discuss the dates, but *nothing is agreed until everything is agreed*, including the fee.", "note": "A standard protection against having concessions banked one by one; state it at the outset, not mid-way.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-35", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "red lines", "gloss": "Non-negotiable positions that a party will not cross.", "say": "Our *red lines* are student safety and the audition criteria; everything else is negotiable.", "note": "Declare few; too many red lines and none of them are credible.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-36", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "linkage", "gloss": "Making progress on one issue conditional on progress on another.", "say": "I would avoid *linkage* between the hall fee and the sponsorship; it gives them leverage over both.", "note": "A diplomatic term of art; useful for analysing why talks stall when unrelated issues are tied together.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-37", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the side payment", "gloss": "A concession or benefit given to a party outside the main deal to secure its agreement.", "say": "The *side payment* to the sports department is the use of the bus on Saturdays.", "note": "Analytic language from negotiation theory; in public it can sound like an improper inducement, so keep it to private planning.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-38", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the nibble", "gloss": "A small additional demand made late in a negotiation, after the main deal seems settled.", "say": "Watch for *the nibble*: they will ask for free tickets once the contract is otherwise agreed.", "note": "Recognising it allows you to trade rather than concede ('we can, if you…'); naming it out loud can feel combative.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-39", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "kite-flying", "gloss": "Floating an idea publicly or informally to gauge reaction before committing to it; also called a trial balloon.", "say": "The principal's comment at assembly was *kite-flying*; she wants to see how parents react before proposing it formally.", "note": "Doing it yourself is legitimate; being seen to do it too often looks unprincipled.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-40", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "socialising the idea", "gloss": "Introducing a proposal informally to key people over time so it is familiar and less threatening when formally put.", "say": "I have been *socialising the idea* of a jazz elective with heads of department since Term 2.", "note": "Effective and normal; done visibly in some rooms and invisibly in others, so judge the culture.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-41", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "taking soundings", "gloss": "Informally consulting people to discover their views before a decision or formal proposal.", "say": "Before I put this to council, I will *take soundings* from the parent representatives.", "note": "A courteous, very Westminster phrase; it suggests openness without committing to follow what you hear.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-42", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "counting the numbers", "gloss": "Establishing in advance how many votes a proposal will receive.", "say": "Before we move the motion, let us *count the numbers*; I am not confident of the staff representatives.", "note": "A core Australian political idiom; it implies discipline, not manipulation, but never move without doing it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-43", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the corridor conversation", "gloss": "An informal discussion outside the formal meeting in which positions are tested and deals often made.", "say": "Most of the real progress was in *the corridor conversation* after the committee broke.", "note": "Normal in every institution; be careful not to let it hollow out formal process or exclude people.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-44", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the gatekeeper", "gloss": "A person who controls access to a decision-maker or to resources, formally or informally.", "say": "The executive assistant is the real *gatekeeper*; if she knows the briefing is urgent, it gets read.", "note": "Respect gatekeepers and they become allies; going around them usually costs more than it saves.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-45", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "managing up", "gloss": "Actively shaping the relationship with superiors to secure support, resources and good decisions.", "say": "Part of the role is *managing up*: making sure the principal has the information she needs to back us.", "note": "Widely used; it implies competence rather than manipulation, provided it is done transparently.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-46", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the authorising environment", "gloss": "The set of people and bodies whose support confers legitimacy and resources on a public initiative.", "say": "The idea is sound, but the *authorising environment* is not there yet: the Director General's office has not signalled any interest.", "note": "A public-value term familiar to senior public servants; in a school setting, 'who needs to say yes' is plainer.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-47", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "machinery of government", "gloss": "The structures, allocation of responsibilities and administrative arrangements of government departments and agencies.", "say": "After the *machinery of government* changes, arts education may sit with a different department.", "note": "Standard in the public service; using it correctly signals you understand how responsibilities can move.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-48", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "caretaker conventions", "gloss": "In Westminster systems, conventions limiting major decisions, appointments and contracts by a government in the period before an election.", "say": "Under the *caretaker conventions* the grant cannot be announced until after the election.", "note": "A well-established Australian convention; misjudging the timing can stall a project for months.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-49", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "Cabinet-in-confidence", "gloss": "A security classification for documents relating to Cabinet deliberations, which must not be disclosed.", "say": "I cannot share the paper; it is *Cabinet-in-confidence* until the decision is announced.", "note": "Signals respect for process; never treat it lightly, as breach is a serious matter for public officers.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-50", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "frank and fearless advice", "gloss": "The public service ideal of giving honest, impartial advice to ministers, regardless of how welcome it is.", "say": "My job on the reference group is to give *frank and fearless advice*, not to tell the Authority what it wants to hear.", "note": "A recognised Westminster phrase; it carries weight because it invokes a professional duty rather than personal opinion.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-51", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the talking points", "gloss": "A set of agreed key messages for use in conversations, interviews or meetings.", "say": "Let us agree *the talking points* before the parent forum so we are all saying the same thing.", "note": "Useful for consistency; reciting them verbatim sounds scripted and undermines trust.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-52", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the wedge", "gloss": "A tactic of raising an issue that divides an opponent's supporters or allies.", "say": "The question about fees is a *wedge*; it splits the parent body from the staff.", "note": "Australian political usage is common ('to wedge'); naming it analytically is fine, doing it to colleagues erodes trust.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-53", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "deniability", "gloss": "The ability to deny knowledge of or responsibility for something, often because one was deliberately not told.", "say": "The principal does not want details, for *deniability*; she wants to know it is handled.", "note": "Analytically accurate; in an accountable public institution, cultivating it deliberately can be seen as poor governance.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-54", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "political cover", "gloss": "Protection from blame or criticism provided by a supporting decision, endorsement or process.", "say": "A council resolution gives the principal *political cover* to make the change.", "note": "Legitimate and valuable; seeking it is good practice, demanding it from others can look like blame-shifting.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-55", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the quiet word", "gloss": "A discreet private conversation to raise a concern or seek a change, avoiding formal confrontation.", "say": "A *quiet word* with the head of sport will sort this out faster than an email.", "note": "Very Australian and very effective; it works because it gives the other person room to fix the problem without loss of face.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-56", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "going over someone's head", "gloss": "Appealing to a person's superior without their involvement or consent.", "say": "I would rather not *go over his head*, but if the booking is not confirmed by Friday I will speak to the deputy.", "note": "Sometimes necessary, always costly; saying in advance that you will do it is more honourable than doing it quietly.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-57", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "being across the detail", "gloss": "Having a thorough working knowledge of the specifics of an issue.", "say": "The business manager is *across the detail* of the levy; I would defer to her on the figures.", "note": "An Australian idiom; it is high praise in public service and a reliable way to signal confidence in a colleague.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-58", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the circuit breaker", "gloss": "An action or proposal designed to break a stalemate or change the dynamic of a dispute.", "say": "A joint meeting with both ensembles might be the *circuit breaker* we need.", "note": "Common in Australian politics; it promises momentum, so follow through quickly.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-59", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the carve-out", "gloss": "An exception excluding certain cases from a general rule or agreement.", "say": "We can accept the uniform policy with a *carve-out* for concert dress.", "note": "A precise drafting tool; too many carve-outs hollow out the rule.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-60", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the sunset clause", "gloss": "A provision that causes a rule or arrangement to expire on a set date unless renewed.", "say": "If we include a *sunset clause*, the board can try the new fee without being locked in.", "note": "A reassuring way to secure agreement for an experiment; it forces a later review.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-61", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the stalking horse", "gloss": "A proposal or candidate put forward to test the waters or disguise someone's real intentions.", "say": "The motion to review all electives was a *stalking horse* for cutting music.", "note": "An accusation of covert motive; be sure of your evidence before alleging it publicly.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-62", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "a straw man proposal", "gloss": "In drafting and negotiation, a rough first proposal put forward for criticism and improvement; distinct from the 'straw man' fallacy.", "say": "Let me put up a *straw man proposal* for the rehearsal schedule so we have something to shoot at.", "note": "Make the sense clear; in an academic room 'straw man' usually means misrepresenting an opponent's argument.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-63", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "holding the pen", "gloss": "Being responsible for drafting the text that others will negotiate or approve.", "say": "If we *hold the pen* on the draft guidelines, our framing will carry through to the final version.", "note": "In diplomacy and committees, the drafter often shapes the outcome; volunteering to hold the pen is a quiet power move.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-64", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "square brackets", "gloss": "In negotiated drafts, the marking of text not yet agreed, signalling disputed language.", "say": "The wording on assessment weighting is still in *square brackets*; we need to resolve it before the final meeting.", "note": "A drafting convention in diplomacy and policy; using it signals that you know how texts are actually negotiated.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-65", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "ad referendum", "gloss": "Agreed by a negotiator subject to confirmation by their principal or governing body.", "say": "I can agree to the terms *ad referendum*; the board will confirm at its next meeting.", "note": "A diplomatic term of art; useful to accept provisionally without overstepping authority, but obscure outside formal settings. Pronounced ad ref-er-EN-dum.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-66", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "ripeness", "gloss": "In conflict resolution, the moment when parties perceive a stalemate as costly enough to make settlement attractive (associated with I. William Zartman).", "say": "I do not think the dispute has reached *ripeness* yet; neither side is feeling the cost of the stalemate.", "note": "Analytic language from conflict studies; useful for advising patience, though it can sound detached to those in the dispute.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-67", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "good offices", "gloss": "The services of a neutral party in facilitating communication between disputing parties, short of mediation.", "say": "Perhaps the principal could offer her *good offices* to bring the two committees together.", "note": "A dignified diplomatic phrase; it implies neutrality, so do not use it for someone with a stake.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-68", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "shuttle diplomacy", "gloss": "Mediation conducted by an intermediary moving between parties who do not meet directly.", "say": "It turned into *shuttle diplomacy*: I spent the afternoon between the venue manager and the festival committee.", "note": "Wry in a school or association context; it signals the parties are not yet ready to meet.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-69", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "track two", "gloss": "Informal, unofficial dialogue between non-governmental or semi-official actors, alongside official negotiations.", "say": "The teacher networks are a kind of *track two* process; they can test ideas the Authority cannot discuss formally.", "note": "Very specialised diplomatic language; apt with policy people, obscure elsewhere.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-70", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the démarche", "gloss": "A formal diplomatic representation or protest made to another government, or more loosely a formal approach.", "say": "The association will make a formal *démarche* to the department about the festival dates.", "note": "Precise in diplomacy; outside it, in a school or board, it sounds grandiose unless used with a smile. Pronounced DAY-marsh.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-71", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the non-paper", "gloss": "An informal document presented without attribution or official status, to test ideas without commitment.", "say": "I have circulated a *non-paper* to the committee: options for discussion, not a proposal.", "note": "A diplomatic tool that translates well to committees; make sure everyone understands it has no formal standing.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-72", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the aide-mémoire", "gloss": "A written summary of points made in a meeting or discussion, given to the other party as a record.", "say": "I will send an *aide-mémoire* after the meeting so we have a shared record of what was agreed.", "note": "Useful and courteous; too formal for everyday matters. Pronounced ayd-mem-WAH.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-73", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the communiqué", "gloss": "An official statement issued at the end of a meeting or negotiation, setting out agreed outcomes.", "say": "The joint *communiqué* from the festival committee should name both schools as co-hosts.", "note": "Its wording is often negotiated as hard as the substance; what is omitted can matter as much as what is included.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-74", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "rapprochement", "gloss": "The re-establishment of cordial relations, especially after estrangement.", "say": "There has been a real *rapprochement* between the two ensembles' parent groups this term.", "note": "Elegant and diplomatic; avoid it for minor disagreements, where it sounds inflated. Pronounced ra-PROSH-mon.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-75", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the modus vivendi", "gloss": "A practical arrangement allowing parties in dispute to coexist, without resolving underlying differences.", "say": "We have reached a *modus vivendi* with the drama department over the hall: we alternate weeks.", "note": "Exact for a truce rather than a resolution; it signals realism about lasting differences.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-76", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the fait accompli", "gloss": "Something already decided or done before those affected are consulted, leaving them no option but to accept it.", "say": "The new timetable was presented as a *fait accompli*; there was no real consultation.", "note": "A pointed criticism of process; using it signals you notice when consultation is hollow. Pronounced fet a-KOM-plee.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-77", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "salami tactics", "gloss": "Achieving a large change through a series of small steps, each too minor to provoke resistance.", "say": "The reduction in rehearsal time has been *salami tactics*: ten minutes each year.", "note": "A vivid, slightly cynical term; useful for naming a pattern, but it implies deliberate intent.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-78", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the usual channels", "gloss": "In Westminster parliaments, the informal arrangements between party managers or whips; more loosely, established informal routes of communication.", "say": "I will raise it through *the usual channels* before the next council meeting.", "note": "Very British in flavour; in an Australian school context it sounds dry and knowing, which can be useful.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-80", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "primus inter pares", "gloss": "First among equals: a leader with formal equality but greater standing or influence.", "say": "As chair I am *primus inter pares*; the decision belongs to the council, not to me.", "note": "Classic description of a chair or prime minister; it sounds modest when applied to yourself, pompous when used of others. Pronounced PREE-mus IN-ter PAR-ays.", "level": 3}]}, {"field": "Academic discourse and stance", "intro": "This is the register of people who argue for a living: claims are calibrated to the evidence, concession is offered before it is extracted, and disagreement is signalled by form rather than heat. Sounding native means choosing the epistemic verb that fits exactly, stating the limit of your claim before anyone else does, and using Latin only where it does work plain English cannot.", "entries": [{"id": "stance-1", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "on the evidence", "gloss": "Ties a claim to what has actually been shown, conceding in advance that different evidence would change the view; narrower than 'in my opinion' and stronger than 'I think'.", "say": "*On the evidence* we have from the pilot, the listening task discriminates well at the top band and poorly in the middle.", "note": "Lands when you can name the evidence if asked; backfires if the next question is 'which evidence?' and you have none to hand.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-2", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "the literature", "gloss": "The body of published scholarship on a question, treated as a collective position rather than a list of authors.", "say": "*The literature* on ensemble participation and retention is larger than the policy paper suggests, and less unanimous.", "note": "Safe in academic rooms; in a council or parent body it can sound like gatekeeping, so say 'the research' there.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-3", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "contested", "gloss": "Describes a claim or concept on which informed people genuinely disagree, as distinct from one that is merely unpopular or new.", "say": "The notion of 'musical ability' as a fixed trait is *contested*, and the selection criteria should not rest on it.", "note": "Use it of concepts and claims, not of people; calling everything contested sounds evasive.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-4", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "suggests", "gloss": "The weakest of the epistemic verbs: the evidence points in a direction without ruling out alternatives.", "say": "The retention data *suggests* that Year 9 is where we lose students, though the sample is one cohort.", "note": "Precise when the evidence really is partial; using it of something well proven reads as timidity rather than rigour.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-5", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "indicates", "gloss": "Stronger than 'suggests': the evidence points reasonably clearly, though it stops short of proof or a demonstrated mechanism.", "say": "Three years of moderation data *indicates* that the performance marks drift upward in the final term.", "note": "Sits well in a committee paper; pair it with the source so it does not float.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-6", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "I take the point, but", "gloss": "A concession that registers the force of an objection before qualifying or limiting it, rather than rejecting it outright.", "say": "*I take the point, but* the cost of the extra adjudicator is small against the cost of a challenged result.", "note": "Only works if you then show you did take the point; said reflexively it signals that you did not listen.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-7", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "granted", "gloss": "A one-word concession that accepts a premise for the sake of the argument while moving on to what follows from it.", "say": "*Granted*, the timetable is tight. That is an argument for scheduling the sectionals earlier, not for cutting them.", "note": "Elegant at the head of a sentence; repeated, it starts to sound like a debating trick.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-8", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "with respect", "gloss": "In Westminster and legal usage a formal marker that disagreement follows, not a softener; listeners hear it as 'I disagree'.", "say": "*With respect*, Chair, the motion as worded commits the association to a deficit budget.", "note": "Everyone in the room knows what it means, so do not use it unless you are about to disagree; 'with great respect' is stronger still.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-9", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "I'm not persuaded that", "gloss": "Disagreement framed as the current state of the speaker's judgement, which leaves room for the other side to supply more argument.", "say": "*I'm not persuaded that* a single external examiner improves consistency more than a second internal marker would.", "note": "Cooler and more senior than 'I disagree'; it puts the onus back on the proposer without heat.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-10", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "necessary but not sufficient", "gloss": "A condition that must be present for an outcome but does not on its own produce it.", "say": "Instrument hire is *necessary but not sufficient* for access; the families who drop out cite the timetable, not the cost.", "note": "Exact and widely understood; avoid stacking it with other logical terms in one sentence.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-13", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "provisional", "gloss": "Held as the best current view and explicitly open to revision when further evidence arrives.", "say": "Treat this as a *provisional* recommendation until we have the second round of audition data.", "note": "Sounds rigorous rather than weak because it names the condition for revision.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-14", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "the evidence base is thin", "gloss": "A measured way of saying a claim rests on little or weak evidence, without saying it is false.", "say": "*The evidence base is thin* for that claim; it comes from one small study of private-studio students.", "note": "Disarming in a policy room because it criticises the support, not the person advancing the view.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-15", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "it doesn't follow that", "gloss": "Identifies a gap between a premise and a conclusion while accepting the premise itself.", "say": "The ensemble is oversubscribed. *It doesn't follow that* we should audition harder; we might simply run a second ensemble.", "note": "Clean and unanswerable when the gap is real; then stop, and let the room see it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-16", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "an empirical question", "gloss": "A question that can only be settled by looking at data, as opposed to one settled by argument, definition or values.", "say": "Whether the change reduces drop-out is *an empirical question*; let's agree what we'd measure before we argue about it.", "note": "Useful for ending a circular debate; misused when the real disagreement is about values.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-17", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "in principle versus in practice", "gloss": "Separates whether something is coherent or permissible from whether it is workable with the actual resources and people.", "say": "I support the model *in principle*; *in practice* it needs a second teacher on Wednesdays we do not have.", "note": "Lets you agree and object in one breath; keep the 'in practice' half concrete.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-18", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "a straw man", "gloss": "A weaker version of an opponent's position, set up to be knocked down in place of the real one.", "say": "That is a bit of *a straw man*: nobody is proposing to abolish the audition, only to change its weighting.", "note": "Soften with 'a bit of'; said flatly it can sound like an accusation of dishonesty.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-19", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "I wonder whether", "gloss": "Frames a contribution as a question, which invites the room to test it rather than defend against it.", "say": "*I wonder whether* the problem is the rubric or the training of the people applying it.", "note": "Senior and disarming when the point is sharp; on a trivial point it can sound coy.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-20", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "a working hypothesis", "gloss": "A claim adopted to guide action or inquiry while it is being tested, not asserted as true.", "say": "My *working hypothesis* is that the Year 8 drop is about the timetable clash with sport.", "note": "Signals rigour and openness together; follow it with what would test it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-21", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "the evidence is mixed", "gloss": "Studies or data point in different directions, so no confident general claim is yet warranted.", "say": "*The evidence is mixed* on whether early specialisation helps; it seems to depend heavily on the instrument.", "note": "Honest and respected; avoid using it to dodge a view you actually hold.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-22", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "arguably", "gloss": "Marks a claim as defensible but open to dispute; the speaker is prepared to argue for it.", "say": "The festival is *arguably* the most important professional-development event for jazz teachers in the state.", "note": "One per paragraph at most; stacked, it becomes the hedging tic you are trying to drop.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-23", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "on my reading", "gloss": "Offers an interpretation of a text, policy or syllabus as one reading among possible others, owned by the speaker.", "say": "*On my reading* of the syllabus, improvisation is assessable in Unit 3 but not required.", "note": "Better than 'I think it says' because it implies you have read it closely; be ready to cite the clause.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-24", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "implies versus infers", "gloss": "The speaker or text implies; the listener infers. Swapping them is a marker educated listeners notice.", "say": "The report doesn't say the program is failing; I *infer* that from the numbers, and I accept the report *implies* the opposite.", "note": "Get this right reliably and nobody comments; get it wrong once in a university committee and someone will.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-27", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "under-theorised", "gloss": "Lacking an adequate conceptual account, so practice or policy rests on assumptions nobody has examined.", "say": "Ensemble pedagogy in the senior syllabus is badly *under-theorised*; we assess the product and say almost nothing about the learning.", "note": "A legitimate academic criticism; use it of a field or document, not of a colleague's work in front of them.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-28", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "a category error", "gloss": "Treating something as belonging to a kind it does not belong to, so a question asked of it makes no sense.", "say": "Asking for a single 'correct' interpretation of the solo is *a category error*; improvisation is not marked against an answer key.", "note": "Devastating when exact; used loosely for any mistake, it sounds pretentious.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-29", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "establishes", "gloss": "Shows a claim to be settled to an accepted standard; stronger than 'indicates' and weaker in implication than 'proves'.", "say": "The audit *establishes* that the hire fleet is under-insured; it doesn't establish why.", "note": "Reserve it for findings that would survive scrutiny; overclaiming here costs credibility for the rest of the meeting.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-30", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "demonstrates", "gloss": "Shows by evidence or argument that something is the case, typically with the mechanism visible; the strongest of the common epistemic verbs.", "say": "The recording *demonstrates* the problem better than the mark sheet does: the time collapses at bar 33.", "note": "Use it when you can point to the demonstration itself; if you can't, step down to 'indicates'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-31", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "I'd want to resist", "gloss": "A firm but courteous signal of disagreement with a framing, conclusion or move, short of outright rejection.", "say": "*I'd want to resist* the idea that the elective is a feeder for ATAR; it has its own value.", "note": "Senior and measured; the conditional 'want to' keeps it collegial without weakening it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-32", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "the burden of proof", "gloss": "Which party must make the case: usually the one proposing change or asserting the positive claim.", "say": "*The burden of proof* sits with the proposal; the current structure is working, so it is for the change to show it improves on it.", "note": "Use it to reallocate the argument, not to avoid making one yourself.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-33", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "a non sequitur", "gloss": "A conclusion that does not follow from what preceded it; a gap in logic, not merely a change of subject.", "say": "With respect, that's *a non sequitur*: the festival's growth doesn't tell us anything about the judging standard.", "note": "Pronounced non SEK-wit-er. Name it once; repeated, it sounds like point-scoring.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-34", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "the strong version and the weak version", "gloss": "Distinguishes the ambitious, harder-to-defend form of a claim from a modest, more defensible form of it.", "say": "*The weak version* of the claim, that ensembles aid retention, I accept. *The strong version*, that they cause higher ATAR results, I don't.", "note": "One of the best tools for agreeing and disagreeing with precision; it also shows you are being fair to the proposer.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-35", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "generalisable", "gloss": "Holds beyond the particular sample or setting in which it was found.", "say": "It's a good result, but I'm not sure it's *generalisable* from a selective school to a comprehensive one.", "note": "Applies to findings, not people; pair it with the specific difference that limits it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-36", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "a distinction without a difference", "gloss": "A verbal distinction that changes nothing in substance or practice.", "say": "Calling it a 'review' rather than an 'audit' is *a distinction without a difference* if the same people see the same files.", "note": "Crisp in governance rooms; it can sound dismissive if the other side has a real reason for the distinction.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-37", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "begs the question", "gloss": "In the strict sense: assumes the conclusion in the premises. It does not mean 'raises the question'.", "say": "Defining a strong program as one with high ATAR enrolment *begs the question* we're trying to answer.", "note": "Many educated listeners now hear the loose sense; if precision matters, say 'that assumes what it sets out to prove'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-38", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "stipulate", "gloss": "To fix the meaning of a term for the purpose of a discussion, openly, rather than claim it is the ordinary meaning.", "say": "Let me *stipulate* that by 'specialist' I mean the selective program, not the general elective.", "note": "Clears confusion early; it signals you know definitions can be argued over.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-39", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "normative versus descriptive", "gloss": "A descriptive claim says what is the case; a normative claim says what ought to be. Arguments often slide between them.", "say": "That's a *descriptive* point about current enrolments; the paper's real claim is *normative*, about what the program should be for.", "note": "Very useful for exposing a hidden shift in an argument; explain it in plainer terms in non-academic rooms.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-40", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "an unstated premise", "gloss": "An assumption an argument needs but does not declare, which is where it is often weakest.", "say": "The proposal rests on *an unstated premise*, that every student in the program intends to study music after school.", "note": "Naming it calmly is often enough; the room does the rest.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-41", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "confounded", "gloss": "Of a comparison: another variable varies with the one being studied, so its effect cannot be separated out.", "say": "The comparison is *confounded* by socio-economic status; the program students come disproportionately from higher-fee instrumental studios.", "note": "A research term of art; use it only where you can name the confounding variable.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-42", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "a selection effect", "gloss": "An apparent effect produced by who ends up in the sample rather than by the thing being studied.", "say": "Some of that is *a selection effect*: the students who stay to Year 12 were always the most committed.", "note": "Lands with academics and principals alike; it is one of the most useful corrections in school data.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-43", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "won't bear the weight", "gloss": "The evidence or argument is too slight to support the conclusion being placed on it.", "say": "One cohort of results *won't bear the weight* of a restructure.", "note": "Measured and visual; implies the conclusion might be right while denying it is shown.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-44", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "reductive", "gloss": "Explaining something complex by one of its factors in a way that loses what matters.", "say": "Treating the audition as a test of pitch discrimination alone is *reductive*.", "note": "Accurate criticism in arts rooms; it can sound like a reflex if you don't say what is lost.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-45", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "the received view", "gloss": "The position generally accepted in a field or institution, usually named in order to question it.", "say": "*The received view* is that big band is the entry point to jazz; I'm less sure it should be.", "note": "Elegant way to set up a challenge without attacking anyone in the room who holds it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-47", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "bracket", "gloss": "To set an issue aside deliberately for the moment, without dismissing it, so that another can be discussed cleanly.", "say": "If we can *bracket* the budget for ten minutes, I'd like to settle what we actually want the program to do.", "note": "A good chair's move; promise to return to it and then do.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-49", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "the question I'd put is", "gloss": "Frames a contribution as a question for the room, which positions the speaker as setting the inquiry rather than defending a view.", "say": "*The question I'd put is* what a pass in this unit should guarantee a student can do.", "note": "A chair-like move that shifts the meeting; use it once, for the question that matters.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-50", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "what would count as evidence that", "gloss": "Asks the other side to specify what would confirm or refute their claim, which tests whether it can be tested.", "say": "*What would count as evidence that* the new structure is working, in two years' time?", "note": "Constructive rather than hostile; it often reveals that a claim has no agreed measure.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-51", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "a proxy", "gloss": "A measurable stand-in for something harder to measure, which can drift away from the thing it stands for.", "say": "Attendance is *a proxy* for engagement, and a poor one in Year 11, when everyone attends.", "note": "Strong in data discussions; follow with why this proxy fails.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-52", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "there is a body of work on", "gloss": "Cites a research area without naming individuals, signalling familiarity without name-dropping.", "say": "*There is a body of work on* informal learning in popular music education that speaks directly to this.", "note": "Lands well if you can name a source when asked; if you can't, don't use it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-53", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "the standard objection", "gloss": "The objection anyone familiar with the debate would raise, named in order to answer it pre-emptively.", "say": "*The standard objection* is that a portfolio is harder to moderate. That's true, and here's how other jurisdictions handle it.", "note": "Shows command of the debate and takes the wind out of the reply.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-55", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "entails", "gloss": "Strictly: if the first is true, the second must be. Stronger than 'implies' in its loose sense.", "say": "Accepting the new rubric *entails* re-marking last year's sample, or the comparison is meaningless.", "note": "Use it only when the necessity is real; for 'probably leads to', use 'suggests' or 'would likely mean'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-56", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "the charitable reading", "gloss": "The interpretation that makes an opponent's argument as strong as it can reasonably be, before responding to it.", "say": "*The charitable reading* of the proposal is that it's about workload, not about the program's standing.", "note": "Disarming and fair; avoid saying it with an edge, or it sounds like condescension.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-57", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "I'd stop short of saying", "gloss": "A calibrated hedge that marks the exact limit of the speaker's claim, which sounds rigorous because the limit is stated.", "say": "The data shows a real decline. *I'd stop short of saying* it's caused by the timetable change.", "note": "One of the best replacements for vague hedging: it says how far you go and no further.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-58", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "so far as it goes", "gloss": "Concedes that an argument is valid within its scope while signalling that its scope is too narrow.", "say": "The financial case is sound *so far as it goes*; it doesn't account for the students we'd lose.", "note": "Quietly cutting; the pause before 'so far as it goes' does the work.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-59", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "agnostic", "gloss": "Holding no view either way on a specific question, often deliberately, while taking a view on others.", "say": "I'm *agnostic* on whether the band rehearses before or after school; I care that it rehearses twice.", "note": "Signals focus rather than indecision; useful for narrowing a debate to what matters.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-61", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "mutatis mutandis", "gloss": "With the necessary changes made: an argument or rule applies to a second case once the relevant differences are adjusted for.", "say": "The same moderation process should apply to the composition portfolio, *mutatis mutandis*.", "note": "Pronounced moo-TAH-tis moo-TAN-dis. Natural in legal and governance drafting; in conversation, once per meeting at most.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-62", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "pace X", "gloss": "Placed before a name: 'with due respect to X', acknowledging that the person named holds a contrary view.", "say": "*Pace* the working party, I don't think a second audition round would change the outcome.", "note": "Pronounced PAY-see or PAH-chay, never like 'pace' the walk. Rare in speech and easily misheard; safer in writing.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-64", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "underdetermined", "gloss": "Of a conclusion: the available evidence is consistent with more than one explanation, so it does not settle which is true.", "say": "The decline is *underdetermined* by the data; the timetable, the staffing change and the pandemic cohort all fit.", "note": "A philosophy-of-science term; very precise with academics, opaque to most others.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-65", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "a fortiori", "gloss": "With stronger reason: if a claim holds in a weaker case, it holds all the more in a stronger one.", "say": "If we require a second marker for the elective, then *a fortiori* we should require one for ATAR.", "note": "Pronounced ay for-tee-OR-eye or ah for-tee-OR-ee. Elegant in argument; 'all the more so' does the same work plainly.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-66", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "inter alia", "gloss": "Among other things: signals that the items listed are a selection, not exhaustive.", "say": "The review considered, *inter alia*, the staffing ratio and the instrument fleet.", "note": "Natural in minutes and reports; in speech it can sound like reading aloud.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-68", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "ex ante and ex post", "gloss": "Before the event and after it: distinguishes judgements made with the information then available from those made with hindsight.", "say": "*Ex ante* the decision was reasonable; *ex post* it looks costly, but that isn't the test for the board.", "note": "Native in finance and governance; strong for defending a past decision fairly.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-69", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "heterodox", "gloss": "Departing from the accepted view in a field, usually in a considered and defensible way.", "say": "My view on the audition weighting is somewhat *heterodox*, so let me make the case properly.", "note": "Self-applied, it pre-empts the charge of eccentricity; applied to others, it can sound like a slight.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-71", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "teleological", "gloss": "Explaining or justifying something by its end or purpose, or assuming that development is heading towards a fixed goal.", "say": "The syllabus has a *teleological* view of jazz history, as if every style were heading towards bebop.", "note": "A precise critique in arts and humanities rooms; check the listener knows it before building on it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-72", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "parsimonious", "gloss": "Of an explanation: the simplest one that accounts for the data, with the fewest assumptions.", "say": "The more *parsimonious* explanation is the timetable clash; we don't need a theory about motivation.", "note": "Native in research methods talk; in ordinary use it means stingy, so context matters.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-74", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "pro tanto", "gloss": "To that extent: a reason that genuinely counts in favour of something, though it may be outweighed by others.", "say": "Parental demand is a *pro tanto* reason to add the ensemble; it isn't a conclusive one.", "note": "Philosophers' Latin; superb in an ethics or academic committee, obscure almost everywhere else.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-75", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "scope conditions", "gloss": "The circumstances under which a finding or theory is claimed to hold, beyond which it makes no claim.", "say": "Before we adopt the model, what are its *scope conditions*? It was developed for private studios, not classes of thirty.", "note": "Precise in research and policy rooms; it sounds rigorous because it limits rather than inflates.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-76", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "obiter", "gloss": "Short for obiter dictum: a remark made in passing that is not part of the reasoning a decision rests on.", "say": "The comment about outsourcing was *obiter*; the report's recommendation doesn't depend on it.", "note": "Pronounced OB-it-er. Lawyers will hear it exactly; others may need the plain gloss.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-77", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "tout court", "gloss": "Without qualification; simply, and in its entirety.", "say": "I'm not arguing against assessment *tout court*, only against this rubric.", "note": "Pronounced too KOOR. Elegant when it sharpens a distinction; affected if dropped in casually.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-78", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "a desideratum", "gloss": "Something desirable or needed, listed among the criteria a solution should meet.", "say": "Portability between schools is a *desideratum*, not a requirement.", "note": "Pronounced dez-id-er-AH-tum; plural desiderata. Useful in design and policy rooms for ranking criteria.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-79", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "a lacuna", "gloss": "A gap, especially in an argument, a text, a policy or a body of knowledge.", "say": "There's a *lacuna* in the policy: it says nothing about students who join mid-year.", "note": "Pronounced la-KOO-na. Precise and quietly scholarly; 'gap' is fine in most rooms.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-80", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "ipso facto", "gloss": "By that very fact: the conclusion follows directly from the fact stated, with no further step.", "say": "Being an accredited adjudicator doesn't *ipso facto* qualify someone to judge improvisation.", "note": "Most often used in the negative, as here, which is where it lands best.", "level": 3}]}, {"field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "intro": "Speaking about music to academics, panels and adjudicators means translating what the bandstand knows into terms that hold up under scrutiny, without losing the players' precision. Sounding native means using the working musician's word for the thing, then the scholar's word for the concept, and knowing which room needs which.", "entries": [{"id": "music-1", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "time feel", "gloss": "The way a player or ensemble inhabits the pulse: placement, density and weight of the beat, as distinct from mere tempo accuracy.", "say": "The tempo was steady, but the *time feel* was rigid; nothing breathed between the beats.", "note": "The word adjudicators reach for when 'good time' is too vague; be ready to say what the feel was.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-2", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "pocket", "gloss": "The shared rhythmic placement in which a rhythm section locks, so that the groove feels settled and inevitable.", "say": "Once the bass and drums found the *pocket* in the second chorus, the horns relaxed into it.", "note": "Native among players; to an academic panel, gloss it once as 'a shared rhythmic placement'.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-3", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "beat placement", "gloss": "Where a player sits relative to the nominal beat: behind, in the middle or on top, as a stylistic and expressive choice.", "say": "His *beat placement* is slightly behind, in the manner of the Basie rhythm section, and the drummer has to accommodate it.", "note": "Shows you understand time as a choice rather than a correctness; precise in adjudication feedback.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-4", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "phrasing", "gloss": "The shaping of a musical line over time, through breath, accent, dynamics, articulation and placement, into units of sense.", "say": "The *phrasing* was idiomatic in the head but became square in the solo, two-bar units with no larger shape.", "note": "Universal, so it needs a specific attachment to sound professional.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-5", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "idiomatic articulation", "gloss": "Articulation that belongs to a style, such as the tonguing, ghosting and accent patterns of swing, rather than generic accuracy.", "say": "The section is accurate, but the *articulation* isn't idiomatic yet: the off-beats need weight and the ghosted notes need to disappear.", "note": "The distinction between correct and idiomatic is one panels often lack the language for; give it to them.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-6", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "voicing", "gloss": "The vertical arrangement of a chord's notes: which tones, in what register, in which instruments, with what spacing.", "say": "The *voicing* in the trombones sits too low for that tempo; it muddies the bass line.", "note": "Standard among musicians; with non-musicians, say 'how the chord is spread across the band'.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-7", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "voice leading", "gloss": "The way individual notes move from one chord to the next, typically by step and with minimal motion, so that harmony is heard as lines.", "say": "His comping is all about *voice leading*: the top note moves by step through the bridge and you hear a melody.", "note": "The term most likely to signal training in both jazz and classical theory; precise with academics.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-8", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "reharmonisation", "gloss": "Supplying new chords beneath an existing melody, changing its harmonic colour and implication while keeping the tune.", "say": "The arrangement's *reharmonisation* of the bridge is ambitious, but it obscures the melody the students should be learning.", "note": "Distinguish it from substitution of single chords; reharmonisation is a broader design decision.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-9", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the form", "gloss": "The structural layout of a piece, such as AABA, the twelve-bar blues or through-composed, which every player must hold in mind while improvising.", "say": "The soloist lost *the form* at the bridge, and the rhythm section, to its credit, didn't follow.", "note": "Losing the form is a serious fault in jazz adjudication; say so plainly.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-10", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "idiom", "gloss": "The stylistic language of a genre or tradition: its characteristic gestures, rhythms, harmonies and sound.", "say": "It's a fine performance, but not in the *idiom*; it sounds like a concert band playing a jazz chart.", "note": "Works with every audience; 'idiomatic' is the adjective panels quote back.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-11", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "stylistic authenticity", "gloss": "Fidelity to the practices, sound and conventions of the style as its practitioners understand them, not merely to the notes.", "say": "The criterion should be *stylistic authenticity*, not accuracy to the page, because in this music the page is a sketch.", "note": "Useful in criteria debates; note that 'authenticity' is itself contested in the scholarship, so expect pushback from musicologists.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-12", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the tradition", "gloss": "In jazz, the living lineage of practice, repertoire and values handed down through players, recordings and the bandstand.", "say": "The students know the chart; what they don't yet know is *the tradition* the chart is drawing on.", "note": "Lands with musicians as a term of seriousness; with academics, be ready for 'whose tradition?'", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-13", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "repertoire", "gloss": "The body of works a performer, ensemble or program draws on, chosen with intent rather than accumulated.", "say": "The *repertoire* across the four bands needs to be planned as a sequence, not chosen chart by chart.", "note": "Pronounced REP-er-twah. Sounds professional when tied to curation and sequence.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-14", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "aural tradition", "gloss": "Transmission of music by listening, imitation and memory, in which the sound is the primary text.", "say": "Jazz is fundamentally an *aural tradition*; the notated chart is a convenience, not the work.", "note": "Distinct from 'oral tradition', which some use for the same idea; be consistent once you choose.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-15", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "notated tradition", "gloss": "Transmission and authority grounded in the written score, in which the performer interprets a fixed text.", "say": "Our assessment model was built for the *notated tradition*, and it shows when we try to apply it to improvisation.", "note": "Paired with 'aural tradition', it frames the most important assessment debate in music education.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-16", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "improvisation as composition", "gloss": "Treats improvisation as real-time composition, with structure, development and design, rather than as spontaneous decoration.", "say": "If we accept *improvisation as composition*, then motivic development and architecture are legitimate criteria.", "note": "The framing that wins over composers and classical academics; it gives them a familiar standard.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-17", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "adjudication criteria", "gloss": "The stated standards against which performances are judged, ideally published, weighted and applied consistently.", "say": "The *adjudication criteria* weight technique at forty per cent and style at ten, which tells bands what we value.", "note": "Point to the criteria rather than to taste when a result is challenged.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-18", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "balance and blend", "gloss": "Balance is the relative volume of parts; blend is how well timbres fuse into one sound. They are separate faults.", "say": "The *balance* is right, the melody comes through, but the *blend* isn't there: the altos are brighter than the tenors.", "note": "Separating the two is a mark of a trained ensemble director.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-19", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "comping", "gloss": "Accompanying in jazz, chiefly by piano or guitar, with rhythmically placed chords that support and converse with the soloist.", "say": "The *comping* was harmonically correct but rhythmically static; it never answered the soloist.", "note": "Short for accompanying or complementing; both explanations are heard.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-20", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "head arrangement", "gloss": "An arrangement worked out by ear and memory in rehearsal, not written down, in the manner of the Kansas City bands.", "say": "The riffs behind the soloist are a *head arrangement*; the students built them in the room.", "note": "Useful for showing panels that aural creation is part of the tradition, not a shortcut.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-21", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "interpretive latitude", "gloss": "The degree of freedom a style or score allows the performer in tempo, phrasing, ornamentation and sound.", "say": "The *interpretive latitude* in a Basie chart is much wider than in a band-festival test piece, and the criteria should allow for it.", "note": "Lets you argue that 'deviation' is sometimes the point.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-22", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "historically informed performance", "gloss": "Performance that draws on period evidence about instruments, technique and conventions to inform interpretation.", "say": "The jazz equivalent of *historically informed performance* is listening to the original recordings, not just reading the chart.", "note": "Often shortened to HIP; the analogy to jazz lands well with classical panellists.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-23", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "vocabulary", "gloss": "In jazz, the stock of phrases, gestures and devices a player has absorbed from the tradition and can deploy fluently.", "say": "She has the *vocabulary*; what she's developing now is the ability to shape it into a solo.", "note": "Pairs with 'language'; useful to distinguish knowledge of the idiom from creative use of it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-24", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the Great American Songbook", "gloss": "The core repertoire of popular songs from roughly the 1920s to the 1950s that provides much of jazz's standard repertoire.", "say": "If the students don't know *the Great American Songbook* as songs, with lyrics, they phrase the melodies like exercises.", "note": "Widely recognised; a good bridge to academics outside music.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-25", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "rubato", "gloss": "Expressive flexibility of tempo within a phrase, traditionally 'stolen' time that is given back, as distinct from simply slowing down.", "say": "The ballad needed *rubato* in the opening, and then strict time once the rhythm section enters.", "note": "Distinguish true rubato from unsteady time when giving feedback; students hear the difference only when you name it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-26", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "swing ratio", "gloss": "The proportion between the long and short notes of a swung pair, which varies with tempo and player rather than sitting at a fixed triplet.", "say": "At that tempo the *swing ratio* should flatten toward even; playing hard triplets makes it sound dated.", "note": "Shows real understanding of swing; it also corrects the widespread classroom simplification.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-27", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "hemiola", "gloss": "A rhythmic shift in which groups of three are heard against a duple frame, or vice versa, typically across a cadence.", "say": "The *hemiola* in the Brahms is the same device as the three-over-four in the shout chorus; the students should hear both.", "note": "Pronounced hem-ee-OH-la. A bridge term between classical and jazz rhythm.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-28", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "metric modulation", "gloss": "A change of tempo or metre in which a note value in the old tempo becomes a different note value in the new.", "say": "The arrangement uses a *metric modulation* into the double-time section, and the drummer needs to own it.", "note": "Associated with Elliott Carter in art music and common in contemporary jazz; precise with composers.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-29", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the hookup", "gloss": "The rhythmic relationship between bass and drums, especially the lock between walking bass and ride cymbal.", "say": "The *hookup* between bass and drums is the first thing I listen for; if it isn't there, nothing else settles.", "note": "Players' vernacular; gloss it for academic panels.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-30", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "tritone substitution", "gloss": "Replacing a dominant chord with the dominant a tritone away, which shares its guide tones and creates chromatic bass motion.", "say": "The *tritone substitution* in bar 8 is fine, but the bass player has to play it or the horns' voicing clashes.", "note": "A standard term; to an academic panel, frame it as an example of functional equivalence.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-31", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "modal interchange", "gloss": "Borrowing chords from a parallel mode or key, such as the minor iv in a major key, for colour without changing the tonic.", "say": "The *modal interchange* in the turnaround gives the ending its melancholy.", "note": "Also called modal mixture in classical theory; knowing both names helps in mixed rooms.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-32", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "guide tones", "gloss": "The thirds and sevenths of chords, which define their quality and form smooth lines through a progression.", "say": "If the soloist targets the *guide tones*, you hear the changes even with the rhythm section out.", "note": "Practical and precise; it shows pedagogy rooted in the harmony, not in scales.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-33", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "contrafact", "gloss": "A new melody written over the chord progression of an existing tune, a central compositional practice of bebop.", "say": "The set list has three *contrafacts* of 'I Got Rhythm'; I'd like more variety in the form.", "note": "Pronounced KON-tra-fakt. A term that signals real jazz-history literacy to academics.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-34", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "motivic development", "gloss": "Building a solo or composition from the transformation of a small motif, through repetition, sequence, inversion and variation.", "say": "The second solo had genuine *motivic development*; the first was a chain of licks.", "note": "A criterion that classical adjudicators recognise instantly; it makes improvisation legible to them.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-35", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the architecture of a solo", "gloss": "The large-scale shape of an improvisation: its arc of density, register, intensity and climax across choruses.", "say": "Technically he's ready; what's missing is *the architecture of a solo*, a sense of where it's going.", "note": "Pitches feedback above note-level; panels hear it as compositional thinking.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-36", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "tessitura", "gloss": "The range in which a part predominantly lies, as distinct from its full range; it governs comfort, colour and fatigue.", "say": "The lead trumpet part's *tessitura* is too high for Year 9 for a whole chart, even if the top note is within range.", "note": "Pronounced tess-i-TOO-ra. Distinguishing it from range is a mark of experience.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-37", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "sound concept", "gloss": "A player's internalised ideal of their own tone, built from listening, which drives technical choices.", "say": "Before we fix the embouchure, he needs a *sound concept*; he hasn't heard enough great tenor players.", "note": "Common among jazz educators; it frames tone as aural and aesthetic, not purely mechanical.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-38", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "performance practice", "gloss": "The conventions of execution in a style or period that the notation does not specify but practitioners understand.", "say": "The swing-era *performance practice* includes falls, scoops and section vibrato that the chart never marks.", "note": "Lets you argue that unnotated conventions are part of the work, which helps in criteria debates.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-39", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the canon", "gloss": "The works treated as central and exemplary in a tradition, whose formation is itself a historical and contestable process.", "say": "Whatever we think of *the canon*, students need to know it before they can argue with it.", "note": "Expect academics to question it; acknowledging canon formation pre-empts that.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-40", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "transcription as apprenticeship", "gloss": "Learning solos by ear and reproducing them, understood as the traditional apprenticeship in jazz, not a mere technical exercise.", "say": "*Transcription as apprenticeship* is how the tradition has always taught itself; it belongs in the senior course.", "note": "Strong in curriculum debate; it reframes copying as a historically grounded pedagogy.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-41", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "lineage", "gloss": "The line of influence from teacher or model to player, through which style and values are transmitted.", "say": "You can hear the *lineage* in her playing, from Ray Brown through Christian McBride.", "note": "A respectful, precise term; lands with musicians and historians alike.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-42", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "microtiming", "gloss": "Small, systematic deviations from metronomic time, often in milliseconds, that produce groove and feel.", "say": "The *microtiming* between the ride cymbal and the bass is what makes that recording swing.", "note": "The empirical research term for what players call feel; it gives academics something to hold on to.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-43", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "inside and outside", "gloss": "Playing within the prevailing harmony versus deliberately departing from it, with the departure resolved or controlled.", "say": "Going *outside* is legitimate, but only if you can find your way back *inside*; here it sounded like losing the changes.", "note": "The distinction is about control, not quantity; make that explicit when adjudicating.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-44", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "enclosure", "gloss": "Approaching a target note from above and below, chromatically or diatonically, a signature bebop device.", "say": "The line is full of *enclosures* around the thirds; it's very good Charlie Parker vocabulary.", "note": "A precise technical term; useful to show the analytic depth of jazz pedagogy.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-45", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "harmonic rhythm", "gloss": "The rate at which chords change, which shapes momentum and phrasing independently of tempo.", "say": "The *harmonic rhythm* doubles in the bridge, which is why the soloists are rushing.", "note": "A classical term that maps perfectly onto jazz; it opens doors with theorists.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-46", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "planing", "gloss": "Moving a chord shape in parallel motion, so that its quality stays constant as it moves, rather than functionally.", "say": "The *planing* in the saxophone soli is pure Ellington, and very hard to tune.", "note": "Associated with Debussy in art music; the cross-reference often impresses classical panels.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-47", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "arrangement versus orchestration", "gloss": "Arrangement shapes the material: form, harmony, sequence. Orchestration assigns it to instruments and colours.", "say": "The *arrangement* is strong; the *orchestration* is the issue, with too much doubling in the middle register.", "note": "Separating the two marks a professional ear; many panels treat them as one.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-48", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "inner-voice movement", "gloss": "The motion of the middle parts of a texture, which gives harmony life even when the outer voices are static.", "say": "The *inner-voice movement* in the trombones is what makes this chart sound expensive.", "note": "Useful praise and diagnosis; it directs attention to parts students treat as filler.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-49", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "shout chorus", "gloss": "In big-band writing, the climactic ensemble chorus, usually after the solos, in which the full band plays a newly composed tutti line.", "say": "The *shout chorus* needs the lead trumpet to commit to the phrasing, or the section has nothing to follow.", "note": "Native to jazz arranging; to academics, call it the structural climax.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-50", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the lead-player concept", "gloss": "In big-band practice, the lead player of each section sets phrasing, articulation and dynamics, and the section follows.", "say": "We teach the *lead-player concept* from Year 7; the conductor is not the source of phrasing in this music.", "note": "Shows how jazz ensemble pedagogy differs from wind-band pedagogy; strong in curriculum discussions.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-51", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "chamber listening", "gloss": "Players listening to each other across the ensemble, as in chamber music, rather than relying on the conductor.", "say": "The band played better with my hands down; that's *chamber listening*, and it's the goal.", "note": "A useful bridge to classical colleagues; it frames jazz ensemble skill in their terms.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-52", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "diagnostic rehearsal", "gloss": "Rehearsing by identifying the cause of a problem, such as time, tuning or balance, rather than repeating the passage.", "say": "Running it again won't fix it. *Diagnostic rehearsal* says it's the third trombone's placement, not the ensemble.", "note": "Signals rehearsal craft to panels and colleagues; use it to argue for skilled ensemble staffing.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-53", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "sound before symbol", "gloss": "The principle that musical concepts should be experienced aurally before they are named or notated.", "say": "Our junior program follows *sound before symbol*: they play the blues before they see it on paper.", "note": "A long-standing principle in music education; it connects jazz practice to classroom pedagogy.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-54", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "criterion-referenced adjudication", "gloss": "Judging each performance against published standards, not ranking performances against one another.", "say": "The festival is moving to *criterion-referenced adjudication*; bands are judged against the descriptors, not the band before them.", "note": "Distinct from norm-referencing; essential when a festival awards ratings rather than places.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-55", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "holistic versus analytic judgement", "gloss": "Holistic judgement weighs the performance as a whole; analytic marking scores separate criteria and sums them.", "say": "The panel split on *holistic versus analytic judgement*: the marks summed to silver, but every adjudicator heard a gold performance.", "note": "A central assessment distinction; make the trade-off explicit rather than picking a side.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-56", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "moderation", "gloss": "The process of comparing and adjusting judgements across assessors so that standards are applied consistently.", "say": "Before the festival, the adjudicators should do *moderation* on three recordings, so we agree what a gold rating sounds like.", "note": "The Australian term; in other systems it may be called standardisation or calibration.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-57", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "technical demand versus musical realisation", "gloss": "Distinguishes how difficult a piece is from how well it is actually performed.", "say": "A hard chart is not a merit in itself; the criteria should reward *musical realisation*, not *technical demand*.", "note": "The single most useful distinction in festival debates; it stops bands choosing music beyond them.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-58", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "Urtext", "gloss": "An edition that aims to present the composer's text as faithfully as possible, without later editorial additions.", "say": "The jazz equivalent of an *Urtext* is the original recording, not the published chart.", "note": "Pronounced OOR-text. It shows classical literacy and makes the analogy with jazz sharp.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-59", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the long line", "gloss": "The sense of continuity across a whole phrase or movement, so that local details serve a larger shape.", "say": "The detail is beautiful, but the *long line* is missing; each phrase stops instead of leading on.", "note": "A classical teacher's phrase; using it in jazz feedback shows breadth.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-60", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "linear versus vertical thinking", "gloss": "Linear improvisation thinks in melodic lines through the changes; vertical improvisation thinks chord by chord.", "say": "He's still thinking *vertically*, chord by chord; the next step is *linear*, so the line connects the changes.", "note": "A practical diagnostic distinction; it frames the developmental step clearly.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-61", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "upper-structure triads", "gloss": "Triads placed above a chord's basic tones to produce the upper extensions and alterations, a common voicing technique in modern jazz.", "say": "The pianist is using *upper-structure triads* over the dominants; the guitar needs to stay out of that register.", "note": "Precise and technical; use it with players, not general panels.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-62", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the score as script, not text", "gloss": "The idea that a score is a set of instructions for social performance, not a fixed text whose correct reading is the goal.", "say": "If we treat *the score as script, not text*, the criteria have to reward what the performers make of it.", "note": "From performance studies; lands with musicologists and can clarify assessment debates. Attribute only if you know the source.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-63", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "Werktreue", "gloss": "Fidelity to the musical work as conceived by its composer, an ideal of nineteenth-century and later concert culture.", "say": "Our criteria carry an unexamined *Werktreue* ideal; it doesn't fit music where the performance is the work.", "note": "Pronounced VAIRK-troy-uh. Very strong with musicologists; opaque elsewhere.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-64", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the work-concept", "gloss": "The historically situated idea of a musical work as a fixed, composed object, which shapes how we value performance.", "say": "Jazz sits awkwardly with *the work-concept*; a standard is a vehicle, not a work in that sense.", "note": "Associated with Lydia Goehr's account of the work-concept; use it only if you can sustain the discussion.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-65", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "signifyin(g)", "gloss": "An African American rhetorical practice of repetition with critical difference, applied in scholarship to jazz quotation and revision.", "say": "Coltrane's 'My Favorite Things' is the classic case of *signifyin(g)*: the tune is honoured and transformed at once.", "note": "From African American literary theory, then jazz scholarship; use it with care and with knowledge of its lineage.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-66", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "participatory discrepancies", "gloss": "Small asynchronies and timbral discrepancies between players that generate groove and draw listeners in.", "say": "The slight push of the ride against the bass is the kind of *participatory discrepancy* that makes the groove.", "note": "An ethnomusicology term from Charles Keil; it gives academic weight to what players call feel.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-67", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "entrainment", "gloss": "The process by which players' and listeners' internal rhythms synchronise to an external pulse and to each other.", "say": "Ensemble time isn't imposed by the conductor; it's *entrainment*, and it can be trained.", "note": "A cognitive-science term; lands with researchers and makes time feel a teachable construct.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-69", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "tempo giusto", "gloss": "The right tempo, or a strict tempo, implied by the character and style of the music rather than given by a marking.", "say": "The chart says 'medium swing', but the *tempo giusto* is the Basie tempo, and the students need to hear it.", "note": "Pronounced TEM-po JOO-sto. Elegant in classical rooms; affected if used with jazz players.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-71", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "heterophony", "gloss": "Simultaneous variation of a single melody by several performers, each ornamenting it differently.", "say": "The front line in early New Orleans style works close to *heterophony*, three versions of one tune at once.", "note": "Pronounced het-er-OFF-o-nee. An ethnomusicological term that shows breadth.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-72", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "Klangfarbenmelodie", "gloss": "Melody of tone colours: a line shaped by changing timbres, from Schoenberg's Harmonielehre.", "say": "Ellington's mutes and plungers create a kind of *Klangfarbenmelodie* long before the classical avant-garde made it a doctrine.", "note": "Pronounced KLAHNG-far-ben-meh-lo-DEE. Strong in a classical room, but the comparison needs care.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-73", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "agogic accent", "gloss": "Emphasis created by lengthening a note in time rather than by playing it louder.", "say": "The lead alto shapes the phrase with *agogic accent*, a little more time on the top note, not more volume.", "note": "Pronounced a-GOJ-ik. Precise in classical feedback, rarely heard in jazz rooms.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-74", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "orality", "gloss": "The character of a culture or tradition grounded in oral transmission, including its modes of memory, variation and authority.", "say": "Jazz has a primary *orality* with a literate overlay; our assessment model has it backwards.", "note": "Borrowed from oral-literary theory; use it in academic rooms where the framing will be followed.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-75", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the recording as primary text", "gloss": "In jazz, the canonical recording rather than the score functions as the authoritative text of a piece.", "say": "In this music we should treat *the recording as primary text*; a band that only reads the chart is reading a summary.", "note": "Clarifies a key difference from the classical tradition; useful when panels ask why scores are insufficient.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-76", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "polymeter versus polyrhythm", "gloss": "Polyrhythm sets conflicting rhythmic divisions within a shared metre; polymeter sets different metres with different bar lengths simultaneously.", "say": "Strictly it's *polyrhythm*, three against four within the bar, not *polymeter*; the bar lines still coincide.", "note": "Often confused even by trained musicians; get it exactly right or leave it alone.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-77", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the changing same", "gloss": "Amiri Baraka's phrase for the continuity of Black musical tradition through constant stylistic change.", "say": "The tradition is what Baraka called *the changing same*; teaching it as a museum misses the point.", "note": "Attribute it when you use it; it lands in jazz scholarship and cultural-policy rooms.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-78", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "notes inégales", "gloss": "The French Baroque convention of playing written equal notes unequally, an often-cited analogy for swing.", "say": "Swing isn't exotic to the classical tradition; *notes inégales* is a written convention played otherwise, just as in a swing chart.", "note": "Pronounced nawt een-ay-GAHL. Use the analogy loosely; the two practices are not identical.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-79", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "intertextuality", "gloss": "The way a work draws meaning from its relationships to other works, through quotation, allusion and revision.", "say": "Quotation in a solo is *intertextuality*, a dialogue with the tradition, not merely a joke.", "note": "A literary-theory term; it can sound inflated unless the example is concrete.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-80", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "Klangideal", "gloss": "The ideal sound of an ensemble, instrument or tradition, held as an aesthetic norm by its practitioners.", "say": "Every great band has a *Klangideal*; ours is closer to the Thad Jones and Mel Lewis Orchestra than to the festival sound.", "note": "Pronounced KLAHNG-ee-day-AHL. Rare; use it with classical and organological specialists.", "level": 3}]}, {"field": "University governance and academic life", "intro": "The register is procedural, understated and exact: senior academics win by routing a matter to the right body, naming the test that applies and asking the one question the paper avoids. Sound native by speaking in dispositions, delegations and evidence rather than opinions, and by letting the terms of reference do the arguing.", "entries": [{"id": "university-governance-1", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "academic governance", "gloss": "The oversight of academic quality and standards (courses, assessment, research integrity) by academic bodies, as distinct from corporate governance of finance, risk and strategy by the governing body.", "say": "That is a question of *academic governance*, so it belongs at Academic Board before it goes anywhere near Council.", "note": "Lands when you are routing an item to the right body. Backfires if used to wall off a resourcing question that is really corporate.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-2", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "self-accrediting authority", "gloss": "The power of a registered provider, which every Australian university holds, to accredit its own courses rather than having each one accredited by TEQSA.", "say": "Because the university has *self-accrediting authority*, the burden of rigour sits with this committee, not with Canberra.", "note": "Use it to remind a committee that it is the last line of quality assurance. Do not imply TEQSA approves individual university courses.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-3", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "the Threshold Standards", "gloss": "The Higher Education Standards Framework (Threshold Standards), the minimum standards every provider must meet to be registered and to offer accredited courses.", "say": "I would want to be confident this proposal meets the *Threshold Standards* on staffing before we worry about whether it is ambitious.", "note": "Insiders say 'the Threshold Standards' or 'the HESF'. 'Threshold' signals a floor, so do not cite it as a mark of excellence.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-4", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "risk-based and proportionate regulation", "gloss": "TEQSA's statutory approach of regulating according to a provider's risk profile and in proportion to that risk, rather than applying identical scrutiny to all.", "say": "TEQSA's approach is *risk-based and proportionate*, so a long record of compliance buys us latitude, not exemption.", "note": "Accurate and quietly authoritative. Avoid quoting section numbers unless you have them in front of you.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-5", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "remit", "gloss": "The scope of matters a body is empowered to deal with, as set by its terms of reference.", "say": "I am not sure workload falls within this committee's *remit*; I would rather we referred it than resolved it.", "note": "A cleaner word than 'jurisdiction' for committees. Pronounced REE-mit as a noun.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-6", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "delegated authority", "gloss": "Power formally passed from a governing body to a committee or officer, exercisable only within the terms and limits of the instrument of delegation.", "say": "The Dean can approve the change under *delegated authority*, but only if it does not alter the learning outcomes.", "note": "Lands when you can name the instrument. Backfires if asserted vaguely: someone will ask to see the delegations schedule.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-8", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "standing committee versus working party", "gloss": "A standing committee is a permanent body with continuing terms of reference; a working party is time-limited, set up to report on a defined question and then dissolve.", "say": "This needs a *working party* with a reporting date, not another *standing committee* that meets forever.", "note": "Shows you think about the life cycle of a body. Useful for killing scope creep.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-9", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "quorate", "gloss": "Having the minimum number of members present for a meeting's decisions to be valid; its opposite is inquorate.", "say": "Before we vote, Chair, could we confirm we are still *quorate*? Two members left at the break.", "note": "Precise and courteous. Raise it before the vote, not after, or it looks tactical.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-10", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "for noting, for discussion, for decision", "gloss": "The three standard dispositions of an agenda item: information received, matters canvassed without resolution, and matters requiring a resolution.", "say": "Can we be clear whether this paper is *for noting* or *for decision*? The cover sheet says one and the recommendation says the other.", "note": "Asking this early is the mark of an experienced committee member. Overused, it sounds procedural for its own sake.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-11", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "starred items", "gloss": "Agenda items reserved for discussion at a meeting; unstarred items are taken together and adopted without debate.", "say": "I would like to *star* item seven; the recommendation has implications for the conservatorium that the paper does not mention.", "note": "Practice varies by institution, so learn the local convention first. Starring sparingly preserves credibility.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-13", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "to have one's dissent recorded", "gloss": "To ask that the minutes note one's opposition to a resolution by name, which matters where members may later be held responsible for a decision.", "say": "I accept the decision of the committee, but I would ask that my *dissent be recorded*.", "note": "Powerful precisely because it is rare. Use it once a year at most, and say it without heat.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-16", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "actual, perceived and potential conflicts", "gloss": "The standard taxonomy of conflicts of interest: one that exists, one a reasonable observer might think exists, and one that could arise.", "say": "It may only be a *perceived conflict*, but perception is exactly what the minutes need to address.", "note": "The 'perceived' limb is the one novices dismiss and seniors take seriously.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-19", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "the hearing rule and the bias rule", "gloss": "The two limbs of natural justice: the right to be heard before an adverse decision, and the right to an impartial decision-maker.", "say": "We have satisfied the *hearing rule*; my worry is the *bias rule*, given who chairs the panel.", "note": "Splitting the doctrine like this signals legal literacy. Keep it for appeal and grievance settings.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-20", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "academic judgement", "gloss": "An evaluation of the quality of academic work that review bodies generally decline to second-guess, confining appeals to process, procedure and evidence of bias.", "say": "The appeal cannot reopen the *academic judgement* of the examiners; it can only ask whether the process was followed.", "note": "Clarifies the scope of any appeal instantly. Do not hide a procedural defect behind it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-21", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "collegiality", "gloss": "Governance and decision-making by peers through deliberation and consent, as distinct from line management; also the expectation of professional good faith among colleagues.", "say": "I value *collegiality*, but collegial does not mean unanimous, and it does not mean nobody decides.", "note": "Lands when defending deliberation. Backfires if it sounds like a demand that colleagues stop disagreeing with you.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-26", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "the three lines model", "gloss": "A risk governance framework distinguishing operational management (first line), risk and compliance functions (second) and independent internal audit (third).", "say": "Our moderation process is a *first line* control; nobody in the *second line* has looked at it.", "note": "Lands with audit and risk committees. Means nothing to a faculty board, so translate it there.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-28", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "trustee, not delegate", "gloss": "The Burkean distinction between a representative who exercises independent judgement for the whole and one who carries instructions from those who elected them.", "say": "On Council I sit as a *trustee, not a delegate*, which is why I may vote against what the music staff would prefer.", "note": "Burke's distinction is well known to well-read colleagues. Name him only if the room is that kind of room.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-31", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "teach-out", "gloss": "The arrangement by which students already enrolled in a discontinued course are enabled to complete it, often with protections set out in policy.", "say": "Discontinuing the degree is defensible; the *teach-out* plan for current students is what Academic Board will scrutinise.", "note": "Shows you think about obligations to existing students, which regulators take seriously.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-32", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "disestablishment", "gloss": "The formal closure of a course, unit, position or organisational unit through governance processes.", "say": "This is a *disestablishment* in substance, whatever the paper calls it, and it should come to us as one.", "note": "Sharper than 'closure' and harder to euphemise. Use it when a cut is being disguised as a restructure.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-33", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "AQF level", "gloss": "The level of a qualification within the Australian Qualifications Framework, from Certificate I at level 1 to doctoral degrees at level 10, with bachelor degrees at level 7.", "say": "The learning outcomes as drafted read closer to *AQF level* 6 than 7; the verbs are about knowing, not judging.", "note": "Lands in course accreditation. Know the numbers cold: bachelor 7, honours and graduate diploma 8, masters 9, doctorate 10.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-34", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "volume of learning", "gloss": "The AQF's measure of the duration, in equivalent full-time years, typically required for a learner to achieve a qualification's outcomes.", "say": "A one-year masters for graduates without a cognate degree sits awkwardly with the AQF *volume of learning*.", "note": "A term insiders use in accreditation papers. Do not confuse it with contact hours.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-35", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "articulation", "gloss": "A formal arrangement giving students guaranteed admission and specified credit from one qualification into another.", "say": "An *articulation* agreement with the school program would give our strongest players a known pathway.", "note": "Precise in tertiary admissions. In secondary rooms it may be mistaken for the musical sense, so frame it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-36", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "course learning outcomes versus unit learning outcomes", "gloss": "Course outcomes describe what a graduate of the whole program can do; unit outcomes are the narrower, assessable outcomes of a single unit, mapped to the course outcomes.", "say": "The *unit learning outcomes* are fine; the problem is that nothing maps to the *course learning outcome* on collaboration.", "note": "Insiders talk in mappings. Lands when you have actually read the matrix.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-37", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "external referencing", "gloss": "Comparing a course's outcomes, standards or student achievement against those of other institutions or external benchmarks, as the Threshold Standards expect.", "say": "Has anyone done *external referencing* of the recital standard against another conservatorium?", "note": "A quality term that regulators recognise. Asking for it is a legitimate and non-hostile challenge.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-38", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "external examiner", "gloss": "An academic from outside the institution who examines a thesis or reviews assessment standards, providing independent judgement.", "say": "For a creative doctorate the choice of *external examiner* matters as much as the work.", "note": "Standard in HDR settings. In Australia doctoral theses are normally examined by external examiners without a viva, though practice is changing.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-39", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "higher degree by research", "gloss": "A research masters or doctorate, abbreviated HDR, examined primarily on a thesis or creative work with exegesis rather than on coursework.", "say": "Our *HDR* candidates in performance need supervisors who are active practitioners, not just published ones.", "note": "Say 'HDR' in university rooms; spell it out once elsewhere.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-40", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "confirmation of candidature", "gloss": "The formal early milestone at which a research candidate's proposal is reviewed and their continued enrolment confirmed.", "say": "The *confirmation of candidature* panel should include someone who understands practice-led methods.", "note": "The key gate in Australian doctoral programs. Terminology varies slightly by university.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-41", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "exegesis", "gloss": "The written component accompanying a creative work in a practice-based research degree, situating and theorising the work as research.", "say": "The recordings are outstanding; the *exegesis* has to show what they contribute to knowledge.", "note": "Pronounced ek-se-JEE-sis. Do not use it for any old program note.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-42", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "practice-led versus practice-based research", "gloss": "Commonly distinguished as research leading to new understanding about practice (practice-led) versus research where the creative artefact is itself the basis of the contribution (practice-based).", "say": "Is this project *practice-led* or *practice-based*? It changes what the examiners should be reading for.", "note": "The distinction is drawn differently by different scholars, so state the definition you are using.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-43", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "non-traditional research outputs", "gloss": "Research outputs other than publications, such as original creative works, live performances, recorded works and curated exhibitions; abbreviated NTROs.", "say": "A festival premiere is a legitimate *non-traditional research output* if it comes with a research statement.", "note": "Essential vocabulary for arts academics. Pronounced as letters or 'en-trows'; follow local habit.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-44", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "research statement", "gloss": "A short statement accompanying an NTRO that sets out its research background, contribution and significance.", "say": "Without a *research statement*, the album is a career achievement, not a research output.", "note": "Lands with research committees. Sounds bureaucratic to artists, so explain why it matters.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-45", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "research impact versus research engagement", "gloss": "Impact is the contribution research makes beyond academia, to the economy, society, culture or policy; engagement is the interaction with end users through which impact may occur.", "say": "Workshops with schools are *engagement*; a changed syllabus would be *impact*.", "note": "A distinction research offices care about. Conflating them weakens an impact case.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-46", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "pathway to impact", "gloss": "The described route by which research outcomes are expected to reach and benefit users beyond academia.", "say": "The application has excellent science and no *pathway to impact*; who is actually going to use this?", "note": "Standard in grant language. Ask it of others' proposals gently; it is a sore point.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-48", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "Category 1 income", "gloss": "Research income from Australian competitive grants, such as ARC and NHMRC schemes, under the national research income categories.", "say": "Arts faculties rarely carry much *Category 1 income*, which is exactly why our NTROs need to be documented.", "note": "Insider shorthand from research finance. Leave it out of rooms without university research staff.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-50", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "audit culture", "gloss": "The spread of auditing logics and performance indicators into professional and academic life, often displacing professional judgement with verifiable compliance.", "say": "I worry we are building an *audit culture* in which documenting the rehearsal matters more than the rehearsal.", "note": "Strathern's edited volume made the term current. Persuasive in faculty critique, but sounds sour if used against every reporting request.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-51", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "managerialism", "gloss": "The application of corporate management techniques and hierarchies to public and academic institutions, often at the expense of collegial authority.", "say": "The concern is not management, which we need, but *managerialism*, which treats academic judgement as a cost centre.", "note": "Distinguishing it from management is what keeps it from sounding like a complaint.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-52", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "workload model", "gloss": "The formal scheme by which an institution allocates and counts academic work across teaching, research and service, usually in hours or points.", "say": "One-to-one studio teaching is badly undercounted in the *workload model*; it is treated as a tutorial.", "note": "The single most consequential document in many academics' lives. Know your institution's units.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-53", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "the 40/40/20 split", "gloss": "The conventional nominal allocation for teaching-and-research academics of 40 per cent teaching, 40 per cent research and 20 per cent service.", "say": "On paper she is *40/40/20*; in practice the studio teaching swallows the research forty.", "note": "Widely understood shorthand. Do not present it as a regulatory requirement; it is a convention.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-54", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "teaching and research versus education-focused roles", "gloss": "Academic roles with research expectations versus roles weighted to teaching and educational scholarship with little or no research allocation.", "say": "Converting performers to *education-focused* roles saves money, but it removes them from the research case.", "note": "Current Australian terminology varies (teaching-focused, teaching-specialist). Use the local term.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-55", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "service", "gloss": "The component of academic work comprising committee work, leadership, professional and community contribution.", "say": "Running the festival is real *service* to the discipline, and the workload model should say so.", "note": "In academic usage it is a category of work, not a favour. Do not let it be treated as invisible.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-56", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "sessional staff", "gloss": "Academic staff employed casually or on short contracts, typically paid by the hour for teaching.", "say": "Most of the instrumental teaching is done by *sessional staff*, and their continuity is the quality issue nobody minutes.", "note": "'Sessional' is the respectful term; 'casuals' is common but blunter.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-57", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "Commonwealth supported place", "gloss": "A university place for which the Australian Government pays part of the cost and the student pays a student contribution, abbreviated CSP.", "say": "If the new major is not funded as a *Commonwealth supported place*, it will be full-fee and the enrolments will tell us so.", "note": "Basic sector literacy. Funding cluster details change, so do not quote rates from memory.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-58", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "EFTSL", "gloss": "Equivalent full-time student load: the standard measure of a student's study load, used for funding and planning.", "say": "The proposal adds headcount but almost no *EFTSL*; the money follows load, not bodies.", "note": "Pronounced EFF-tsl by some and spelt out by others. Lands with planning and finance people.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-60", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "the studio model", "gloss": "Conservatorium teaching built on one-to-one tuition between a principal study teacher and student, in the master and apprentice tradition.", "say": "The *studio model* is expensive, but it is also the pedagogy the profession recognises.", "note": "Defend it with outcomes, not nostalgia. 'Master and apprentice' can sound gendered; 'principal teacher' avoids it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-61", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "principal study", "gloss": "The student's major instrument, voice or creative discipline in a conservatorium degree, carrying the largest weighting.", "say": "Ensemble credit should not be cannibalised to give more hours to *principal study*.", "note": "Native terminology in Australian conservatoria. 'Major study' is used in some institutions.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-62", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "conservatorium versus conservatoire", "gloss": "Australian institutions generally style themselves conservatorium; conservatoire is the British and French form; both denote a specialist music school.", "say": "The *conservatorium* is embedded in a university, which changes its governance entirely from a stand-alone *conservatoire*.", "note": "Use the institution's own name. Conservatoire is pronounced con-SER-va-twah.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-63", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "embedded institution", "gloss": "A specialist school, such as a conservatorium or academy, that sits within a larger university and is subject to its governance and budget rules.", "say": "Because we are an *embedded institution*, our case has to be made in the university's language, not the profession's.", "note": "Useful framing for strategy discussions; avoid it if it sounds like a complaint about the host.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-64", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "panel examination", "gloss": "Assessment of a performance by a panel of examiners rather than by the student's own teacher, intended to secure independence and comparability.", "say": "A *panel examination* protects the student as much as the standard; nobody marks their own studio.", "note": "In US usage this is a jury. Say 'panel' in Australia.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-65", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "Chancellor versus Vice-Chancellor", "gloss": "The Chancellor chairs the governing body and presides at ceremonies; the Vice-Chancellor is the chief executive and senior academic officer.", "say": "That decision is the *Vice-Chancellor's*; the *Chancellor* chairs Council and should not be lobbied on it.", "note": "Outsiders confuse them constantly. Getting it right is a small, reliable signal.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-66", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "Senate, Council and Academic Board", "gloss": "The governing body of an Australian university is usually its Council (at UWA, its Senate); the Academic Board, or Academic Senate in some universities, is the peak academic body reporting to it.", "say": "At UWA the *Senate* is the governing body, so do not assume it is the academic forum it would be elsewhere.", "note": "Nomenclature varies by statute; check each institution's Act before using it in writing.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-67", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "emeritus", "gloss": "An honorary title retained by a retired professor in recognition of distinguished service.", "say": "Professor *Emeritus*, or *Emeritus* Professor, depending on the university's style.", "note": "Pronounced e-MER-i-tus. It is honorific, not a rank of current employment.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-68", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "honoris causa", "gloss": "For the sake of honour: designating a degree conferred as an honour rather than earned by study.", "say": "She holds a doctorate *honoris causa*, so 'Dr' is a courtesy many recipients choose not to use.", "note": "Ceremonial register. Pronounced on-OR-is COW-za. Overused in conversation it sounds pompous.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-69", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "the academy", "gloss": "The community of scholars and the institutions of higher learning considered collectively.", "say": "The *academy* tends to underrate the knowledge held by practising musicians who never wrote it down.", "note": "Lands in reflective and strategic talk. In a funding meeting it can sound aloof.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-70", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "academic freedom", "gloss": "The freedom of academic staff to teach, research and publish according to scholarly judgement, and to express views about their institution, without institutional penalty.", "say": "*Academic freedom* protects the scholarly judgement; it does not protect a refusal to teach the curriculum.", "note": "Draw its boundaries yourself before someone else does. Distinct from general freedom of speech.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-71", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "the French Model Code", "gloss": "The Model Code for the Protection of Freedom of Speech and Academic Freedom, recommended in Robert French's 2019 review and adopted in some form by Australian universities.", "say": "Our draft policy should be read against the *French Model Code*; it is the benchmark the sector adopted.", "note": "Accurate and current. Lands in policy committees; overkill in a staff meeting.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-72", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "institutional autonomy", "gloss": "The freedom of a university as an institution to govern its own academic affairs, admissions, appointments and curriculum without external direction.", "say": "Accepting the tied funding is legal, but it chips at *institutional autonomy*, and we should say that we know it.", "note": "Distinguish it from academic freedom, which belongs to individuals; confusing the two is a common slip.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-73", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "peer review", "gloss": "Evaluation of scholarly or creative work by experts in the same field before publication, funding or acceptance.", "say": "The festival commission went through *peer review*, which is what lets us count it as research.", "note": "Name the type where it matters: double-blind, single-blind, open.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-74", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "double-blind review", "gloss": "Peer review in which neither the author nor the reviewers know each other's identity; single-blind conceals only the reviewers.", "say": "In a field this small, *double-blind review* is more aspiration than reality; everyone recognises the playing.", "note": "Shows you understand the limits as well as the ideal. 'Anonymised' is the increasingly preferred term.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-75", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "research integrity versus academic integrity", "gloss": "Research integrity concerns the responsible conduct of research by researchers; academic integrity concerns honesty in students' and staff learning and assessment work.", "say": "That is an *academic integrity* matter for the unit coordinator, not a *research integrity* matter for the Research Office.", "note": "Routing the matter correctly is half the competence. Both have separate policies in every university.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-76", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "the Australian Code", "gloss": "The Australian Code for the Responsible Conduct of Research (2018), the national principles-based framework for research integrity.", "say": "Under the *Australian Code*, authorship follows contribution; rank does not earn a byline.", "note": "Insiders call it 'the Code'. Supported by guides on specific topics such as authorship and breaches.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-77", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "low and negligible risk", "gloss": "A category of human research under the National Statement on Ethical Conduct in Human Research where the foreseeable risk is no more than discomfort, allowing lighter ethics review.", "say": "Surveying our own ensemble students is probably *low and negligible risk*, but the power relationship still has to be addressed.", "note": "Abbreviated LNR. The National Statement is the governing document; the HREC decides, not the researcher.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-78", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "epistemic community", "gloss": "A network of professionals with recognised expertise and shared standards of validity in a domain, whose authority rests on that shared judgement.", "say": "Jazz performers are an *epistemic community* with rigorous standards; they simply do not write them in journal articles.", "note": "Precise and useful in arguing for practitioner knowledge. Affected if used outside scholarly settings.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-79", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "the Universities Accord", "gloss": "The Australian Universities Accord, the national review of higher education whose final report was released in February 2024.", "say": "The *Accord* talks about equity targets; a selective music pathway is one place we could actually show what that means.", "note": "Recommendations have been adopted unevenly, so speak of its recommendations rather than its outcomes.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-80", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "constituency", "gloss": "The group a member is understood to represent or draw legitimacy from, whose interests are distinct from the institution's as a whole.", "say": "I hear the music staff as a *constituency*, but Council has to weigh them against every other one.", "note": "Lands when you want to acknowledge a group without being captured by it.", "level": 3}]}, {"field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "intro": "This is a field where precise distinctions carry the argument: validity against reliability, standard against criterion, comparable against identical. Sound native by treating every result as an inference, every cut score as a judgement and every design choice as a trade-off that can be named.", "entries": [{"id": "curriculum-assessment-1", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "standards-referenced assessment", "gloss": "Judging student work against described standards of quality, such as grade descriptions, so that a grade reports what was achieved regardless of how others performed.", "say": "A *standards-referenced* system means a whole cohort can earn an A, and we should not apologise for that.", "note": "The SCSA frame for WACE courses. Distinguish it from criterion-referenced, which it is often loosely called.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-2", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "norm-referenced assessment", "gloss": "Interpreting a result by its position relative to other candidates, so that grades are distributed by rank rather than against a fixed standard.", "say": "Allocating a fixed number of A grades per ensemble is *norm-referencing* by stealth.", "note": "Lands when exposing a quota masquerading as a standard. Do not confuse it with scaling, which is a different operation.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-3", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "criterion-referenced versus standards-referenced", "gloss": "Criterion-referenced assessment checks whether specified criteria are met, often as pass or fail; standards-referenced assessment judges the quality with which they are met against described levels.", "say": "A checklist tells you whether the scale was played; it is *criterion-referenced*. The standard is about how well.", "note": "Sadler's work underpins much of the Australian usage. Getting this distinction right marks you as an assessment person.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-4", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "construct", "gloss": "The underlying attribute an assessment is designed to measure, such as aural discrimination or stylistic understanding, inferred rather than directly observed.", "say": "What is the *construct* here? If it is improvisation, a transcription test is measuring something else.", "note": "The first question in any validity conversation. Asking it calmly usually reorganises the discussion.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-5", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "validity", "gloss": "The degree to which evidence and theory support the interpretations and uses of assessment results; a property of inferences, not of instruments.", "say": "The test is not *valid* or invalid in itself; the question is whether this inference from these scores is justified.", "note": "The modern unitary view, associated with Messick. Saying 'a valid test' marks an outsider in expert rooms.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-6", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "reliability", "gloss": "The consistency of assessment results across occasions, tasks, markers or forms; a necessary but not sufficient condition for valid interpretation.", "say": "The marking is highly *reliable*, which tells us markers agree; it does not tell us they are rewarding the right thing.", "note": "The validity and reliability distinction is the core insider marker. Do not use reliability to mean trustworthiness in general.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-7", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "the trade-off between reliability and validity", "gloss": "The tension whereby tightening task conditions and criteria to increase marking consistency can narrow what is assessed and weaken the construct.", "say": "Every step that made the composition task more marker-proof made it less like composition; that is the *trade-off between reliability and validity*.", "note": "A senior argument for authentic tasks. Concede that some reliability is non-negotiable in high-stakes settings.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-8", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "construct-irrelevant variance", "gloss": "Variation in results caused by factors other than the target construct, such as reading load, test format or performance anxiety.", "say": "The written aural paper carries a lot of *construct-irrelevant variance*; weaker readers score lower on listening.", "note": "Messick's term, and a precise weapon in syllabus review. Heavy for a parent audience.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-9", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "construct under-representation", "gloss": "The failure of an assessment to sample important aspects of the construct, so that results reflect only part of what is claimed.", "say": "A twelve-bar solo on one standard is *construct under-representation* if we are claiming to assess improvisation.", "note": "Pairs naturally with construct-irrelevant variance; use the pair to frame validity threats.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-10", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "consequential validity", "gloss": "The aspect of validity concerned with the intended and unintended consequences of assessment use, including effects on teaching and learning.", "say": "The *consequential validity* of dropping the practical exam is the decline in instrumental enrolments we would see within two years.", "note": "Messick placed consequences within validity; some theorists dispute that. Acknowledge the debate if challenged.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-11", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "face validity", "gloss": "Whether an assessment appears, to non-specialists, to measure what it claims; not a technical form of validity.", "say": "The task has strong *face validity* with parents, which is useful, but it is not evidence that it measures the construct.", "note": "Use it to separate optics from evidence. Claiming face validity as a strength in an expert room backfires.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-12", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "content validity", "gloss": "The extent to which an assessment samples the content and skills of the domain in appropriate proportion; now usually treated as content-related evidence for validity.", "say": "Two set works out of eight is thin *content-related evidence*; students can bet on which ones appear.", "note": "The phrasing 'content-related evidence' signals current thinking.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-13", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "argument-based validation", "gloss": "The approach, associated with Michael Kane, of stating the chain of inferences from performance to score to interpretation to decision, and testing each link.", "say": "Using *argument-based validation*, the weak link is from the performance to the score, not from the score to the grade.", "note": "Lands with measurement specialists at SCSA level. Too technical for most school rooms.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-14", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "evidence-centred design", "gloss": "A framework, associated with Robert Mislevy and colleagues, that builds assessments from the claims to be made, through the evidence needed, to the tasks that elicit it.", "say": "If we used *evidence-centred design*, we would start with the claim about the student and only then write the task.", "note": "Accurate and current. Name it once; then talk in its logic of claim, evidence and task.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-16", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "standard error of measurement", "gloss": "The estimated spread of a candidate's observed scores around their true score, quantifying the imprecision of an individual result.", "say": "A two-mark difference is inside the *standard error of measurement*; we cannot rank those students meaningfully.", "note": "Decisive in arguments about fine-grained ranking. Use only if you can explain it in one sentence.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-17", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "generalisability", "gloss": "The extent to which a result on a particular set of tasks, markers and occasions would hold across the wider universe of possible ones; formalised in generalisability theory.", "say": "One recital, one panel, one day: the *generalisability* of that mark is lower than its precision suggests.", "note": "Technical register. Spelling: Australian English uses 's'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-18", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "inter-rater reliability", "gloss": "The consistency of judgements between different markers assessing the same work.", "say": "Before we argue about the rubric, let us check *inter-rater reliability* on five recordings.", "note": "Proposing an empirical check usually ends unproductive debate.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-19", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "intra-rater reliability", "gloss": "The consistency of a single marker's judgements across time, order and fatigue.", "say": "Marks drop after the fortieth script; that is an *intra-rater reliability* problem, not a cohort problem.", "note": "Less commonly named than inter-rater, so it signals depth.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-20", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "rater severity and leniency", "gloss": "Systematic tendencies of individual markers to award lower or higher marks than others for equivalent work.", "say": "We know which examiner is *severe*; the question is whether the process corrects for it.", "note": "Frame as systematic, not personal, or it reads as an attack on a colleague.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-21", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "halo effect", "gloss": "The tendency for a positive impression on one feature, or prior knowledge of the student, to raise judgements on unrelated criteria.", "say": "Blind marking of the written component would reduce the *halo effect* from their reputation as players.", "note": "Well understood; name the specific mechanism to keep it from sounding like a generic bias claim.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-23", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "consensus moderation", "gloss": "Moderation in which teachers compare and discuss samples of work against the standard to reach agreed judgements.", "say": "*Consensus moderation* across schools would do more for our practical marks than another rubric revision.", "note": "Builds assessment literacy as a by-product, which is its strongest argument.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-24", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "statistical moderation", "gloss": "Adjusting school-based marks using an external reference such as examination results, preserving the school's rank order while aligning its scale.", "say": "*Statistical moderation* keeps our rank order but moves our marks; so the rank order is what we have to get right.", "note": "This is how school marks in WACE ATAR courses are adjusted against the examination. The insight about rank order is what insiders understand.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-25", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "comparability", "gloss": "The degree to which results from different schools, markers, tasks or years can be treated as reporting the same standard.", "say": "Without some cross-school sample, we have no evidence of *comparability*, only confidence.", "note": "Comparability is a claim that needs evidence. Saying so is fair and usually unanswerable.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-26", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "comparable, not identical", "gloss": "The principle that comparability requires equivalence of demand and standard, not identical tasks or conditions.", "say": "The recital programs need to be *comparable, not identical*; a bassist and a flautist should not play the same piece.", "note": "Defuses the naive demand for uniformity. Be ready to explain how equivalence of demand will be judged.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-28", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "exemplars", "gloss": "Selected pieces of student work that illustrate particular standards, used to communicate and stabilise judgement.", "say": "Our *exemplars* are all from strong students; we have nothing that shows what a C actually sounds like.", "note": "Exemplars should span the range, especially the borderlines. Say it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-29", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "grade descriptions", "gloss": "Statements describing the typical characteristics of achievement at each grade in a course, used to make and report on-balance judgements.", "say": "The *grade descriptions* talk about stylistic awareness; our tasks barely ask for it.", "note": "The SCSA instrument for standards-referenced grading. Treat them as descriptive of typical performance, not as checklists.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-30", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "achievement standards", "gloss": "Descriptions in the Australian Curriculum of the expected quality of learning at each year level or band.", "say": "The *achievement standard* for Years 9 and 10 asks for evaluation; most of our assessment asks for description.", "note": "Use the exact phrase; 'outcomes' belongs to older frameworks.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-31", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "marking key versus rubric", "gloss": "A marking key specifies how marks are awarded for a particular task or question; a rubric sets out criteria against levels of quality, often applicable across tasks.", "say": "The examination has a *marking key*; the practical needs a *rubric*, because the evidence is not item by item.", "note": "SCSA uses 'marking key' for examinations and tasks. Using the right term in the right setting is noticed.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-32", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "analytic versus holistic marking", "gloss": "Analytic marking scores separate criteria and sums them; holistic marking makes a single overall judgement of the work.", "say": "*Analytic marking* is more reliable at the criterion level, but for performance a *holistic* judgement may be more valid.", "note": "Both have defenders. A senior position acknowledges what each trades away.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-33", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "on-balance judgement", "gloss": "An overall judgement of which standard best describes the work as a whole, weighing strengths and weaknesses rather than adding parts.", "say": "Taken *on balance*, this is A-grade work; the weak sight-reading does not outweigh the rest.", "note": "The proper mode for using grade descriptions. Also called 'best fit'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-34", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "atomistic criteria", "gloss": "Criteria broken into many small, separately scored components, which can lose the qualities that only appear in the work as a whole.", "say": "The rubric has become *atomistic*; it can award high marks to a performance no musician would call good.", "note": "Sadler's critique of analytic decomposition gives this weight. It reads as critique, so offer an alternative.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-35", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "criteria compliance", "gloss": "Harry Torrance's term for the tendency of explicit criteria and feedback to shift learning toward meeting the criteria rather than learning the domain.", "say": "We have taught them *criteria compliance*: they now write program notes that tick every descriptor and say nothing.", "note": "Accurate and pointed. Use it to argue against over-specification, not against criteria themselves.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-36", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "connoisseurship", "gloss": "Elliot Eisner's notion of the expert's refined capacity to perceive and appreciate qualities in a domain, as the basis of educational judgement and criticism.", "say": "Judgement-based assessment rests on *connoisseurship*, which is why we cannot replace the panel with a checklist.", "note": "Lands with arts educators and assessment scholars. Elsewhere it can sound like special pleading for experts.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-37", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "judgement-based assessment", "gloss": "Assessment in which qualified assessors make holistic or qualitative judgements of complex performances against standards, rather than scoring discrete items.", "say": "Music is a *judgement-based assessment* discipline; the job is to make the judgement defensible, not to remove it.", "note": "Positions expert judgement as rigorous rather than subjective. Pair it with moderation to be credible.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-38", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "tacit knowledge of standards", "gloss": "Knowledge of quality that experienced assessors hold and use but cannot fully articulate in written criteria.", "say": "Much of the standard is *tacit*; exemplars and moderation are how we make it shareable.", "note": "Draws on Polanyi and on Sadler's work on standards. Do not use it to exempt judgements from scrutiny.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-39", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "authentic assessment", "gloss": "Assessment tasks that replicate the demands, conditions and standards of real practice in the discipline.", "say": "The most *authentic assessment* we run is the jazz festival set, and it counts for nothing in the school marks.", "note": "Authenticity is a matter of degree. Saying a task is 'more authentic' is more defensible than calling it authentic.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-40", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "performance assessment", "gloss": "Assessment requiring students to demonstrate a skill directly, through doing, rather than by selecting or describing.", "say": "The *performance assessment* carries half the weighting, so its reliability matters as much as the exam's.", "note": "Familiar to everyone; precision lies in what you claim about it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-41", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "washback", "gloss": "The effect of an assessment, especially a high-stakes one, on teaching and learning before it; also called backwash.", "say": "The *washback* from a listening exam built on set works is that schools stop teaching unfamiliar listening.", "note": "Neutral in itself: washback can be positive. Saying so shows command of the concept.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-44", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "high-stakes assessment", "gloss": "Assessment whose results carry significant consequences for students, teachers or schools, such as tertiary entrance or accountability.", "say": "Because the exam is *high-stakes*, small reliability problems become large fairness problems.", "note": "Frames why rigour matters. Overused, it becomes a way of shutting down innovation.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-45", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "cut score", "gloss": "The point on a score scale that separates one performance category from the next, set through a standard-setting process.", "say": "Where we put the *cut score* between B and A is a policy decision dressed as a measurement.", "note": "Insiders stress that cut scores are judgements. Saying so earns respect in standards committees.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-46", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "standard setting", "gloss": "Structured methods by which panels of experts determine cut scores, typically by judging what borderline candidates can do.", "say": "We need a proper *standard setting* exercise for the new syllabus, not last year's boundaries carried over.", "note": "A distinct technical field. Asking for it shows you know cut scores do not set themselves.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-47", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "the Angoff method", "gloss": "A standard-setting method in which judges estimate, item by item, the probability that a minimally competent candidate would answer correctly.", "say": "An *Angoff* exercise works for the theory paper; it does not translate to a recital.", "note": "Pronounced ANG-off. Name it only in a room that knows standard setting.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-48", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "the bookmark method", "gloss": "A standard-setting method in which judges place a bookmark in a booklet of items ordered by difficulty at the point where a borderline candidate would stop succeeding.", "say": "With item response data, a *bookmark* procedure would make the boundary less arbitrary.", "note": "Technical, used by testing agencies. Overreach in most school rooms.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-49", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "the borderline candidate", "gloss": "The hypothetical candidate just at the boundary of a performance category, whose likely performance anchors standard-setting judgements.", "say": "Before we argue about the A band, can we agree what the *borderline* A candidate sounds like?", "note": "Moves a panel from abstraction to a concrete reference point. Often a turning point in discussion.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-51", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "the TEA and the ATAR", "gloss": "In WA, the Tertiary Entrance Aggregate is computed from a student's best scaled scores; the ATAR is a percentile rank derived from the TEA.", "say": "The *ATAR* is a rank, not a mark; a student cannot 'get 95 per cent' in it.", "note": "Lands with parents and principals alike. Avoid quoting bonus rules or exact formulas from memory.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-52", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "ATAR course versus General course", "gloss": "In the WACE, ATAR courses are examinable and contribute to tertiary entrance; General courses are school-assessed and do not.", "say": "Moving a strong student from the *ATAR course* to the *General course* changes her entrance pathway, not her musicianship.", "note": "Basic WA sector literacy. Capitalise as SCSA does.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-53", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "externally set task", "gloss": "In WACE General courses, a task set by SCSA and completed in Year 12 under school supervision, used to support comparability; abbreviated EST.", "say": "The *externally set task* gives us the one comparison point we have across General course schools.", "note": "WA-specific. Check current weighting and timing before citing them.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-54", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "assessment outline", "gloss": "The school's document setting out, for a course, the assessment tasks, types, weightings and timing, as SCSA requires.", "say": "If the *assessment outline* says 30 per cent, the task cannot quietly become 20 per cent in Term 3.", "note": "A compliance document with real consequences in appeals. Treat amendments formally.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-55", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "syllabus", "gloss": "The authority-issued document prescribing the content and, often, the assessment requirements of a course.", "say": "The *syllabus* prescribes the content; it is the school's program that decides sequence and emphasis.", "note": "In SCSA usage the syllabus is mandated. Distinguish it from the school's program and teaching outline.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-56", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "scope and sequence", "gloss": "A document setting out what is taught (scope) and in what order and progression (sequence) across a year or stage.", "say": "Our *scope and sequence* introduces modes in Year 7 and never returns to them; that is a sequence problem.", "note": "Talk in both halves: scope and sequence fail in different ways.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-57", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "the intended, enacted and attained curriculum", "gloss": "The distinction between what the syllabus specifies, what is actually taught, and what students actually learn.", "say": "The *intended curriculum* includes improvisation; the *enacted curriculum* in most schools does not.", "note": "From comparative studies such as TIMSS. A precise way to locate a problem without blaming the syllabus writers.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-59", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "content descriptions versus elaborations", "gloss": "In the Australian Curriculum, content descriptions specify what is to be taught and are mandatory; elaborations are illustrative examples and are not.", "say": "That example is an *elaboration*, not a *content description*; we are not obliged to teach it.", "note": "A distinction that frequently settles curriculum arguments. Check whether a state has adapted the structure.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-60", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "degree of prescription", "gloss": "How tightly a syllabus specifies content, works, methods or assessment, as against leaving them to schools.", "say": "A higher *degree of prescription* in set works would help comparability and hurt programs like ours.", "note": "Frames the policy trade-off neutrally. Good opening for a syllabus committee position.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-61", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "powerful knowledge", "gloss": "Michael Young's term for specialised, disciplinary knowledge that allows students to think beyond their own experience, as an entitlement of schooling.", "say": "Harmony and voice-leading are *powerful knowledge*; leaving them out is not student-centred, it is a loss.", "note": "Persuasive in curriculum debates; contested by some as elitist. Be ready to defend breadth of repertoire too.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-62", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "substantive versus disciplinary knowledge", "gloss": "Substantive knowledge is the content established in a discipline; disciplinary knowledge is how the discipline makes, tests and revises its claims.", "say": "Knowing the chord symbols is *substantive*; knowing how a musician decides a reharmonisation works is *disciplinary*.", "note": "Current in curriculum design discourse. Precise and non-partisan.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-63", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "threshold concepts", "gloss": "Meyer and Land's idea of transformative, often troublesome concepts that, once grasped, reorganise a learner's understanding of a discipline.", "say": "Hearing function rather than chord quality is a *threshold concept*; students are different musicians on the other side.", "note": "Lands with university colleagues. Overclaiming every concept as a threshold one empties the term.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-64", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "the SOLO taxonomy", "gloss": "Biggs and Collis's hierarchy of increasing structural complexity in responses, from prestructural through unistructural, multistructural and relational to extended abstract.", "say": "Most of the analysis answers are *multistructural*: lots of correct observations with nothing connecting them.", "note": "More useful than Bloom for marking extended responses. Know the five levels if you name it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-65", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "command terms", "gloss": "The verbs in a task or question, such as describe, analyse, evaluate, that specify the kind of thinking required.", "say": "The *command term* is 'evaluate', but the marking key rewards description; one of them has to change.", "note": "Lands in examination and marking key review. Precise and hard to argue with.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-66", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "learning progression", "gloss": "An evidence-based description of the typical pathway by which learners develop increasing sophistication in a domain.", "say": "Without a *learning progression* for aural skills, our year-level standards are guesses.", "note": "Distinguish from a sequence of content. Good in scope and sequence work.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-67", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "item facility and discrimination", "gloss": "Facility is the proportion of candidates answering an item correctly; discrimination is how well the item separates stronger from weaker candidates.", "say": "That question has high *facility* and almost no *discrimination*; everyone got it, so it told us nothing.", "note": "Item analysis language from examining. Use it in exam review panels, not with parents.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-68", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "ceiling effect", "gloss": "When a task is too easy for a cohort, so that many score at or near the maximum and differences among them cannot be detected; the floor effect is its converse.", "say": "The sight-reading test has a *ceiling effect* for the specialist students; it cannot tell a good reader from an exceptional one.", "note": "Clear and concrete. Ask for the distribution rather than asserting it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-69", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "differential item functioning", "gloss": "When candidates of equal ability from different groups have different probabilities of success on an item, indicating possible bias.", "say": "Before we keep the jazz listening item, we should check it for *differential item functioning* across school types.", "note": "Technical; abbreviated DIF. Use it in examining contexts where data exist.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-70", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "reasonable adjustment", "gloss": "A change to assessment conditions that enables a student with disability to demonstrate learning on the same basis as others, without changing the standard.", "say": "Extra time is a *reasonable adjustment*; lowering the standard of the recital would not be.", "note": "Grounded in the Disability Standards for Education 2005. The distinction between conditions and standard is the essential point.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-71", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "fitness for purpose", "gloss": "The principle that an assessment's design should be judged against the specific decision or use it serves.", "say": "A task can be excellent for feedback and unfit for ranking; *fitness for purpose* is the question.", "note": "Good for resolving arguments where people are defending different purposes without realising it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-72", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "conjunctive versus compensatory scoring", "gloss": "Compensatory scoring lets strength in one component offset weakness in another; conjunctive scoring requires a minimum on each.", "say": "Under *compensatory scoring* a student can fail the performance and still pass the course; is that the standard we want?", "note": "Exposes a design choice most rooms have never noticed they made. Senior and precise.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-73", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "ipsative assessment", "gloss": "Assessment against the learner's own previous performance rather than against others or a fixed standard.", "say": "*Ipsative* feedback suits the practice journal; it cannot carry a reported grade.", "note": "Pronounced IP-sa-tiv. Rare and useful; explain it briefly.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-74", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "standards drift", "gloss": "The gradual change over time in the level of achievement associated with a grade or mark, without any deliberate decision.", "say": "Comparing this year's A recordings with 2019's would tell us whether we have *standards drift*.", "note": "Asking for evidence is better than asserting drift. Applies upward as well as downward.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-75", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "assessment literacy", "gloss": "The knowledge and skill needed to design, use and interpret assessment soundly, including its limits.", "say": "The fastest way to raise *assessment literacy* in the department is to moderate together, not to run a workshop.", "note": "Can sound patronising if aimed at colleagues; frame it as collective.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-76", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "ecological validity", "gloss": "The extent to which assessment conditions resemble the real settings in which the capability will be used.", "say": "Playing to an empty room for three examiners has limited *ecological validity* for a performer.", "note": "Borrowed from psychology. Distinguish it from authenticity, which concerns the task rather than the setting.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-77", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "sufficiency of evidence", "gloss": "Whether enough evidence has been gathered, across tasks and occasions, to support the judgement being made.", "say": "One composition is not *sufficient evidence* to report a standard in composing for the year.", "note": "A quiet but decisive challenge to thin assessment programs.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-78", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "triangulation", "gloss": "Drawing on several independent sources or types of evidence to support a judgement.", "say": "We have the exam, the recital and the portfolio; *triangulating* those is the real basis for the grade.", "note": "Useful with parents and principals. Keep the methodological sense; it is not just 'checking'.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-79", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "assessment as accountability versus improvement", "gloss": "The tension between assessment used to judge schools and teachers and assessment used to improve learning, which often pull design in different directions.", "say": "This data was collected for *improvement* and is now being used for *accountability*; that changes how people will behave.", "note": "Precise framing for policy discussions. Avoid sounding as if accountability is illegitimate.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-80", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "consultation draft", "gloss": "A version of a syllabus or policy released for formal comment before finalisation.", "say": "Our response to the *consultation draft* should propose wording, not just objections.", "note": "Practical process vocabulary. Proposing text is what moves authorities.", "level": 1}]}], "moves": [{"id": "the-reframe", "name": "The reframe", "family": "Framing", "what": "Changes the question the room believes it is answering, so that the options already on the table are judged against a different and better test.", "when": "When the discussion has settled into a binary that neither side can win, or when the room is about to produce a sound answer to the wrong question.", "teach": "A committee rarely disagrees about answers as much as it assumes it shares a question. Once a question has been posed and two or three people have spoken to it, its terms harden, and every later contribution is heard as for or against the matter as framed. The reframe works upstream of that. It does not argue for an option; it changes the test by which options are judged, so that the arguments already in the room rearrange themselves. Done well, people find they agree with you without having conceded anything, because you have not attacked their position. You have relocated it.\n\nWith highly educated audiences this is among the most efficient moves available. Academics and senior officials are trained to evaluate arguments within a frame and are often less vigilant about the frame itself. They also respect a frame shift as an intellectual contribution in its own right: it signals that you have been thinking about the problem rather than waiting for your turn.\n\nSenior practitioners execute it in three beats. First, state the current question accurately, in a form its proponents would accept: 'We have been treating this as a question of whether to keep the elective.' Second, offer the alternative as an observation, not a correction: 'I wonder whether it is really a question about what the course is for.' Third, show briefly what follows, one consequence rather than five. Then stop. The silence after a good reframe is the room recalculating, and filling it with elaboration lets people retreat to the familiar frame.\n\nTiming matters more than wording. Reframe too early and you seem to be dodging a question nobody yet finds difficult. Reframe too late, once a vote is foreshadowed, and it reads as obstruction. The ideal moment is when the second or third speaker repeats an argument already made, because repetition signals that the existing frame is exhausted.\n\nKeep it under forty-five seconds. Speak slightly slower than usual and a little lower, and address the chair or the whole table rather than the last speaker, so the move does not land as a rebuttal of one person. Stillness helps; a reframe delivered while leaning forward looks like a pitch.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways. The clever reframe that is merely different: if the new question does not make the decision easier or better, it is a tangent with good posture. The hostile reframe, which implies the room has been foolish ('the real issue, which nobody has mentioned'). And the reframe that multiplies: a new question, then a qualification, then a second new question, until the room has nothing to hold.", "phrasings": ["We have been treating this as a question of cost. I wonder whether it is more usefully a question of purpose.", "Could I suggest a slightly different way of putting the question? If we ask what the course is for, rather than how it is weighted, several of the points already made fall into place.", "What strikes me is that both positions share an assumption: that the format has to stay as it is. If we set that aside, the choice looks rather different.", "Suppose we approached this not as a timetabling matter but as a decision about the status of the program. What would we then need to know?", "The arguments on both sides are good ones. I am not sure they are arguments about the same thing.", "Perhaps the question is less whether we should do this than what we would be committing ourselves to if we did.", "Seen from the point of view of the student who sits this course in three years' time, the question is not how much the section is worth but what it is evidence of.", "I would put the question this way, and I offer it tentatively: not whether this is risky, but whether it is the kind of risk we exist to take."], "avoid": ["With respect, I think everyone has missed the real issue here : it tells the room it has been foolish and turns the frame into a contest of status rather than ideas.", "I just want to throw something out there, and it might be completely off track, but : the hedge invites the room to dismiss the reframe before it has been heard.", "If I could step back and look at the meta-question, the paradigm we are operating within : abstraction and jargon signal performance and give the room no new question to hold.", "So it is not really about the elective at all, it is about who controls the timetable : a reframe that imputes motive converts a policy discussion into an accusation."], "scenarios": [{"id": "the-reframe-s1", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "A SCSA committee for Music is reviewing the written examination for the ATAR course. A university assessment specialist has tabled marker-consistency data showing that the aural section produces the widest spread between markers, and proposes reducing its weighting. Two members have already spoken for and against, largely on whether the data are robust. The decision will shape what teachers across the state prioritise for years.", "said": "I have no quarrel with aural skills as such. My concern is narrower and, I think, hard to answer: if a section of the paper cannot be marked consistently, it should not carry the weight it does. We owe candidates a result they can trust. Either we fix the marking or we reduce the exposure.", "task": "Move the question from marker reliability to what the examination is meant to be evidence of, without dismissing the reliability concern.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I agree we owe candidates a result they can trust, and I don't want to wave the data away. But I think we've been treating this as a question about reliability, when underneath it is a question about validity. If aural perception is central to what the course claims to certify, then reducing its weight makes the result more consistent and less true. So perhaps the question for us is not how much weight the section deserves, but whether it is the right instrument for something we agree matters.", "why": "It grants the speaker's premise in full, then names the criterion the current frame has left out. It ends on a question both sides can work on together."}, {"text": "Could I put the question slightly differently? Suppose we asked what a teacher in Kalgoorlie will do in 2028 if we halve this weighting. My guess is that aural work quietly disappears from Year 11. If that's right, we're not really deciding a marking question; we're deciding what the subject is. That doesn't settle it, but it does suggest we should be looking at the design of the items before the weighting.", "why": "It reframes through consequence, using the effect of assessment on teaching, which makes an abstract point concrete and statewide. It proposes a next step rather than a verdict."}], "checks": ["I stated the reliability concern in a form its proponent would accept before shifting.", "I named the new question in a single sentence.", "I offered one consequence, not a list.", "I stopped talking once the new question was on the table."]}, {"id": "the-reframe-s2", "room": "school council", "setup": "At a school council meeting, the principal presents a proposal to increase the charge for the optional interstate tour attached to the specialist music program. A parent representative has argued, with figures, that the increase will price out families on lower incomes; the business manager has replied that the tour cannot be subsidised further without cutting elsewhere. The discussion has become a contest between affordability and solvency. You are a member of the council and the program is your responsibility.", "said": "I understand the budget is tight, and I'm not suggesting anyone is being careless. But every time we raise this charge we're told it's modest, and every time a few more families quietly withdraw. At some point we have to decide whether this tour is for the program or for the families who can afford it.", "task": "Shift the question from the size of the charge to what the tour is for, so the council can decide its design rather than its price.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think both points are right, and that's why we keep circling. We're arguing about price, but the price follows from a prior decision we've never made explicitly: is this tour a core part of the program's education, or an enrichment option for those who choose it? If it's core, we have to find a way for every student to go, and the design changes. If it's enrichment, a higher charge is defensible, but we should say so honestly to families. I'd suggest we settle that first.", "why": "It honours both speakers and exposes the unmade decision that sits beneath their disagreement. The two branches show the council exactly what each answer would commit it to."}, {"text": "Could I suggest we stop asking what the tour should cost and ask what it is for? If the answer is performing interstate, there may be a cheaper way to do that. If the answer is the cohort travelling and performing together, then the withdrawals the parent representative describes are the very thing the tour is meant to prevent. Either way, the charge becomes a consequence of the purpose rather than the subject of the argument.", "why": "It turns the parent's evidence into an argument about purpose, which takes it more seriously than the price frame did. The closing sentence names the shift explicitly so the room can follow it."}], "checks": ["I acknowledged both the affordability and the solvency case.", "I named a question that sits prior to price.", "I showed what each answer to that question would mean in practice.", "I did not propose a figure."]}, {"id": "the-reframe-s3", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The board of the association that runs the state's schools' jazz festival is discussing a proposal from the festival director to cap entries at two bands per school, because last year's sessions ran up to ninety minutes late. A board member who is a long-serving principal opposes the cap as penalising schools that have built large programs. The director has replied that the venue and the adjudicators cannot stretch further. You are the treasurer.", "said": "I accept the schedule has become unmanageable. But the schools that would be hit by this cap are precisely the ones that have done what we've spent twenty years asking them to do: build depth. We'd be punishing success. If the problem is time, let's find more time, not fewer bands.", "task": "Move the board from a capacity argument to a question about what the festival exists to do.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think we've framed this as a capacity question, and on that framing there's no good answer: either the schedule breaks or the large programs lose out. But the cap is really a proxy for something we haven't discussed. Is the festival primarily a showcase of the state's strongest school bands, or a developmental event where every band receives expert feedback? Those purposes want different formats. If we can agree which comes first, the number of bands per school more or less decides itself.", "why": "It shows why the current frame cannot produce a good answer before offering a new one. The question it poses is one only the board can answer, which is where governance belongs."}, {"text": "Can I come at this from the treasury side, though not about money? Every option on the table assumes the festival has to look the way it does now: every band, one adjudication model, the same session structure. Perhaps the question isn't how many bands fit, but whether one format still serves both the schools starting out and the schools with three senior bands. If it doesn't, the cap is solving the wrong problem.", "why": "It surfaces the shared assumption beneath both positions and questions that instead. Opening from his office as treasurer gives the move authority while its content stays educational."}], "checks": ["I named why the capacity frame leaves no good answer.", "I posed a question about purpose or format, not numbers.", "I did not take sides between the principal and the director.", "I finished in under a minute."]}, {"id": "the-reframe-s4", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "A university academic board is considering a proposal to introduce a commercial music major within the Bachelor of Music. A senior professor of musicology has argued that the major's admission and assessment standards are lower than those of the existing classical and jazz majors. The proposing school has defended it on enrolment demand and industry relevance. You sit on the board as an external member from the schools sector.", "said": "No one doubts there's demand. My objection is that we would be awarding the same degree for demonstrably different levels of achievement. Relevance is not a standard. If we want this major, its entry and exit requirements should be equivalent to those of the majors we already offer, and on the paper in front of us, they are not.", "task": "Shift the argument from 'standards versus relevance' to what any holder of the degree must be able to do.", "seconds": 70, "models": [{"text": "I think the professor is right that relevance is not a standard, and I'd resist any argument for this major that rested on demand alone. But I'm not sure the question is whether the new major resembles the existing ones. The question is what anyone holding a Bachelor of Music from this university can be relied upon to do as a musician. If the commercial major delivers that, its difference is a strength. If it doesn't, no level of demand should carry it, and the paper should be sent back.", "why": "It concedes the professor's sharpest line and then relocates the test from resemblance to capability, which is a standard both sides can accept. It also states plainly what failure would mean."}, {"text": "We seem to be choosing between standards and relevance, which is a choice no academic board should want to make. Could we instead ask what the degree promises? If it promises a common core of musicianship, aural, theoretical, historical, at a stated level, then every major should be judged against that core, and this proposal can be tested on evidence rather than on genre. That's a question the school could answer before our next meeting.", "why": "It refuses the false binary and replaces it with a test that is neutral between genres. Ending with a concrete request gives the board something to resolve rather than a debate to continue."}], "checks": ["I agreed explicitly with the strongest part of the professor's case.", "I replaced the binary with a single test.", "I did not defend commercial music on taste or popularity.", "I gave the board something it could ask for or decide."]}, {"id": "the-reframe-s5", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "A state arts funding panel is assessing an application from a small community organisation that runs a free youth jazz workshop series in the outer suburbs. The artistic case is strong, but the organisation has a thin financial history and one previous grant acquitted late. A panel member with a finance background has recommended against on risk grounds, and two others have agreed reluctantly.", "said": "I'm sympathetic to the project; the support letters are excellent. But we're stewards of public money, and the track record here is one late acquittal and no reserves. If this organisation folds halfway through, the young people are let down and we are the panel that funded it against the evidence. I don't see how we recommend it.", "task": "Move the panel from 'is this risky?' to what kind of risk this funding program exists to take.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I agree it's a risk, and I don't think we should pretend otherwise. But I wonder whether the question is whether it's risky, or whether it's the kind of risk this program was set up to carry. The guidelines speak of emerging organisations and under-served communities, and almost by definition those applicants won't have reserves. If we fund only applicants with clean histories, we're running a different program from the one we've been asked to run. That suggests managing the risk, through staged payments or a milestone, rather than declining it.", "why": "It grounds the reframe in the panel's own guidelines, so the new question has authority beyond his opinion. It converts the finance member's concern into a design condition rather than a veto."}, {"text": "Can I ask the question from the other side? If we decline, what's the most likely outcome for these young people? Probably not a better-run workshop; probably no workshop. So the comparison isn't this organisation against a safe one. It's this organisation against nothing. That doesn't remove the stewardship concern, but it changes what we're weighing it against.", "why": "It reframes by changing the counterfactual, which is a fair and rigorous move that a finance-minded member will recognise. Its brevity lets the point land without argument."}], "checks": ["I accepted that the risk is real.", "I changed the comparison or the test, not the facts.", "I referred to the program's purpose or guidelines.", "I offered a way to manage the concern rather than dismiss it."]}, {"id": "the-reframe-s6", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "You are meeting the principal and an associate principal about next year's timetable. To reduce supervision costs and early starts, they propose moving all ensemble rehearsals from before school into a single after-school block. The associate principal has the timetable model on the table and has clearly done careful work. They are presenting it as a straightforward operational change.", "said": "We've modelled this carefully. Moving everything to one afternoon block saves us roughly forty hours of supervision a term and means no one is in the building at seven. I appreciate it's a change for the ensembles, but it's a timetabling matter, and frankly it's the kind of efficiency the system expects us to find.", "task": "Reframe the conversation from a timetabling efficiency to a decision about the status of the ensemble program in the school's curriculum.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I can see the work in this, and the saving is real. I'd just like us to be clear about what we'd be deciding. If ensembles move into a single afternoon block, they'll compete with sport, part-time jobs and the bus timetable, and in practice they'll become optional. That might be the right call, but it's a decision about whether ensemble playing is part of the specialist program or an extra to it. I'd rather we made that decision deliberately than by timetable.", "why": "It credits the associate principal's work and does not contest the figures. It names the decision hidden inside the operational change, which a principal will recognise as a matter for leadership rather than scheduling."}, {"text": "Could I ask what the before-school rehearsals are, in the school's view? If they're co-curricular, this is a sensible efficiency and I'd want to talk about detail. If they're the practical core of a selective program we audition children into, then supervision isn't an overhead; it's the cost of delivering what we promised families at enrolment. I suspect the answer is the second, and if so, we're looking at a different question.", "why": "It reframes through a genuine question, which lets the principal arrive at the new frame rather than be pushed into it. The reference to what families were promised raises the stakes without raising the voice."}], "checks": ["I acknowledged the modelling before questioning its premise.", "I did not dispute the supervision figure.", "I named the decision about the program's status explicitly.", "My tone stayed collegial rather than defensive."]}]}, {"id": "naming-the-real-question", "name": "Naming the real question", "family": "Framing", "what": "Fixes the boundaries of the discussion by stating plainly what decision is actually before the room, what it commits the body to and what it leaves open.", "when": "When a capable room is holding a good discussion about a question that is not on the agenda, or when adjacent decisions have begun to run together and the one with a deadline is being lost.", "teach": "Committees of clever people are prone to a particular failure: they hold an excellent discussion about a question that is not before them. The paper asks for approval to consult; the room debates whether the policy is wise. The resolution is to receive a report; members argue its ninth recommendation. Nothing is decided, or something is decided by drift that nobody would have approved if asked.\n\nNaming the real question is a scoping move. It states what the body is actually being asked to do, what that commits it to and what it does not. It differs from the reframe: the reframe changes the test, this move fixes the boundaries. It draws on distinctions every experienced member knows but which slip under argument: to note, receive, endorse, approve or adopt; in principle or final; this cycle or the next review; the body's own decision or advice to another body.\n\nHighly educated people tend to welcome it, because it offers relief without condescension. Most sense the drift; few want to say so, because it can sound like impatience. Done carefully, the move does not shut down the wider conversation. It places it: 'That is the question for the review; today's question is narrower.' The aim is not to win but to make the decision legible, so that people can agree or disagree with something definite.\n\nSenior practitioners usually put it as a question to the chair or to the paper's author rather than as an assertion: 'Could I check what we are being asked to decide?' This has two advantages. It invites the person with authority to confirm the scope, which gives the move legitimacy, and if you have misread the paper you learn so cheaply. Where the scope is genuinely unclear, offer a formulation the room can accept or amend: 'I take it we are deciding X, and that Y and Z remain open.' Then propose where the displaced concern should go, so that the person who raised it is not simply silenced.\n\nTiming: after the drift has become visible but before anyone has invested in a position on the wider question. Length: two or three sentences. Speak to the chair, evenly, with your papers in front of you; a glance at the recommendation as you speak quietly anchors the room to the document.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways. It can be used to suppress a legitimate concern ('that is out of scope') that does bear on today's decision. It can be delivered as a procedural rebuke. Or you can name the question wrongly, in which case you look as though you had not read the paper. Read the recommendation literally before you speak.", "phrasings": ["Could I check what we are being asked to decide today?", "As I read the recommendation, we are approving this for consultation, not adopting it. If that is right, several of the concerns raised belong to the next stage.", "I think there are three decisions in play, and only one of them is ours today.", "Before we go further, it might help to separate what this meeting has to settle from what it would be good to discuss.", "The question in front of us is narrower than our discussion: it is simply whether the outlines go forward for 2028.", "Is the board being asked for a decision or for advice? The answer changes how I would speak to it.", "If we resolve only one thing before we leave, I think it has to be this.", "Whatever view we take of the larger question, the decision needed by the end of the month is whether to hold the booking."], "avoid": ["This is all very interesting, but it is not actually on the agenda : it dismisses colleagues' contributions and sounds like impatience rather than clarity.", "Point of order, Chair: we are out of scope : reaching for procedure to solve a scoping problem escalates it into a dispute about the rules.", "I think what we are really deciding, or maybe what we should be deciding, or at least one way of looking at it, is : hedging defeats a move whose entire value is definiteness.", "Can we just make a decision? We have been going round in circles : it voices frustration but names no decision, so the room has nothing to agree to."], "scenarios": [{"id": "naming-the-real-question-s1", "room": "faculty curriculum committee", "setup": "A university education faculty's curriculum committee is considering a proposal to restructure the secondary music method units in the Master of Teaching. The paper asks the committee to approve the revised unit outlines for submission to the academic board for 2028 commencement. For twenty minutes, discussion has ranged over the philosophy of teacher education, popular music pedagogy and the accreditation environment. You are on the committee as a practitioner member.", "said": "I think this goes to the heart of what kind of teachers we're producing. If we're serious about culturally responsive pedagogy, we can't keep treating the Western art canon as the default and everything else as an elective. I'd want to see that principle stated explicitly in the unit outcomes, and frankly I'd want to see it across the whole program, not just in music.", "task": "Bring the committee back to the decision it must make today, while giving the wider concern a proper place.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "That's a principle I'd support, and I'd like to see it taken further. Can I check the scope, though? As I read the recommendation, we're deciding whether these outlines go to academic board for 2028. The program-wide question is larger and probably belongs with the course review. So perhaps the useful question today is narrower: do these outlines, as drafted, make that principle possible, or do they block it? If they make it possible, we can approve and refer the wider point.", "why": "It supports the speaker before scoping, and it converts her principle into a test the outlines can be measured against today. The wider point is referred, not discarded."}, {"text": "Chair, I think we have two decisions running together. One is whether these four outlines are fit to go to academic board. The other is what the whole program should say about repertoire and pedagogy. Only the first is before us, and it has a deadline. Could we resolve that one, with a note to the board that this committee wants the second taken up in the review?", "why": "It is brief and addressed to the chair, which gives the scoping procedural legitimacy. The proposed note to the board gives the concern a destination and a record."}], "checks": ["I said what the recommendation actually asks for.", "I named where the wider concern should go.", "I did not use the words 'out of scope'.", "I spoke to the chair rather than to the last speaker."]}, {"id": "naming-the-real-question-s2", "room": "SCSA reference group", "setup": "A SCSA reference group is discussing whether to permit recorded performance submissions as an alternative to live practical examinations for candidates in regional and remote schools. The paper proposes a trial for 2027 under defined conditions. Members have begun debating the entire practical examination model: panel composition, moderation and whether live examination is still defensible at all.", "said": "If we accept recordings for regional candidates, we've conceded the principle. Within three years every school will want the option, and we'll be assessing production quality as much as playing. I'd rather we had the honest conversation now about whether the live practical has a future than slide into a two-tier system by stealth.", "task": "Establish that the decision before the group is a bounded trial, and locate the larger question without dismissing it.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think that's the right conversation to have, and I'd want to be part of it. But it isn't the decision on this paper. What we're asked is whether to trial recorded submissions in 2027, for a defined group, under stated conditions. A trial is how we'd get evidence for exactly the debate you're describing. So could we ask a narrower question: are the conditions tight enough that the trial doesn't concede the principle? If they're not, let's tighten them.", "why": "It treats the trial as the means of answering the larger question, which turns a scoping move into an ally of the objector. It ends with a task the group can complete today."}, {"text": "Can I ask the Authority's officer to confirm what our recommendation would commit us to? My reading is a one-year trial, with an evaluation, and no change to the examination model. If that's right, the risk of drift is real but manageable, and it might best be addressed by writing the evaluation criteria into today's recommendation. The future of the live practical deserves a paper of its own.", "why": "Asking the officer to confirm scope draws on the person with authority and protects him if his reading is wrong. It offers a concrete safeguard against the drift the objector fears."}], "checks": ["I stated the decision as a one-year trial with conditions.", "I gave the larger question a proper future home.", "I offered a safeguard that addresses the objector's fear.", "I did not argue the merits of recordings themselves."]}, {"id": "naming-the-real-question-s3", "room": "music department meeting", "setup": "You are chairing a music department meeting of nine staff, including visiting instrumental teachers. The item is next year's concert calendar. Within minutes it has become a discussion about workload, the failure to replace a retired colleague, the auditorium's lighting rig and last year's late reports. Each grievance is legitimate; together they are swamping the item. The calendar must go to the principal on Friday.", "said": "With respect, I don't think we can talk about the calendar without talking about the fact that we're one person down and nobody has told us whether the position will be filled. We ran four concerts last year on goodwill. Before we commit to anything, I'd want some assurance we're not doing that again.", "task": "As chair, name the narrow decision the meeting must reach on the calendar while dealing honestly with the staffing concern.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "That's fair, and it needs an answer I don't have yet. So let me separate them. The decision we need today is only this: how many full-department concerts go in the calendar, and in which terms. The staffing question I'll take to the principal this week, in writing, with your concern attached. And if the answer is that we stay one person down, the calendar we agree today should be one we could still deliver. Can we work on that basis?", "why": "It gives the staffing concern a specific action and owner before narrowing the item. The closing condition ties the two together, so the colleague's point shapes the decision rather than being parked."}, {"text": "I think the staffing point actually tells us what today's question is. We're not deciding the shape of the program for next year; we're deciding what goes to the principal on Friday. So I'd suggest we agree a calendar that's deliverable at current staffing, and note that anything more depends on the position being filled. That way the calendar makes the argument for us.", "why": "It absorbs the grievance into the scoping, so the colleague hears his point adopted rather than deferred. The strategic note at the end gives staff a reason to accept a narrow decision."}], "checks": ["I acknowledged the staffing concern as legitimate.", "I stated the decision in one sentence with the Friday deadline.", "I committed to a specific action on the wider grievance.", "I asked the room to agree to the narrowed question."]}, {"id": "naming-the-real-question-s4", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The festival association's board has received an offer from a regional bank to sponsor next year's festival in exchange for naming rights and branding on adjudication sheets. Discussion has moved into a broad debate about the commercialisation of school arts events and whether the association should have a sponsorship policy. The bank wants an answer within a fortnight. As treasurer, you know the festival needs the money.", "said": "My worry isn't this bank; it's the precedent. Once our name has a sponsor in front of it, every future negotiation starts from there, and I've seen festivals lose their identity that way. I think we need a sponsorship policy, discussed properly, before we sign anything with anyone.", "task": "Clarify what the board must decide within the fortnight, separating it from the policy question.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I agree we need a policy, and I'd be glad to help draft one. But the bank isn't asking us to sign; it's asking whether we'll negotiate. So perhaps the decision tonight is simply whether to authorise the President and me to open discussions, with two conditions we set now: no naming rights over the festival title, and nothing on the adjudication sheets. That protects the precedent you're worried about and keeps the offer alive while the policy is written.", "why": "It identifies the true decision, authority to negotiate, which is smaller than the one the room was fearing. The two conditions convert the objector's concern into binding terms."}, {"text": "Could I try to pin down what we actually have to decide? There are three questions on the table: whether we want sponsorship at all, on what terms we'd accept this offer, and what our general policy should be. Only the second has a deadline, and answering it doesn't commit us on the other two. If we set the terms tonight, the policy can come to the next meeting, informed by what we learn from the negotiation.", "why": "It lays out the three questions in sequence and shows that only one is pressing. Stating that the narrow decision does not prejudge the others is what makes the objector able to agree."}], "checks": ["I named the decision with the fortnight deadline precisely.", "I gave the policy concern a timeline.", "I did not argue that the festival needs the money.", "I proposed terms or conditions the board could minute."]}, {"id": "naming-the-real-question-s5", "room": "music parents' association", "setup": "At a meeting of the music parents' association, the committee is discussing a proposed interstate tour for the senior ensembles. Parents have raised cost, dates that may clash with examinations, supervision ratios and whether younger students should be included. The tour company needs a commitment by the end of the month to hold flights and accommodation. You attend as Teacher in Charge.", "said": "I don't think we can commit to anything until we know exactly what each family will pay, what the itinerary looks like and whether the exam clash is real. Some of us have two children in the program. We've been burned before by tours where the final cost came in a third higher than the estimate.", "task": "Separate the decision due this month from the questions that can properly be settled later.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "Those are exactly the questions families should ask, and you'll have answers to all of them before anyone commits money of their own. What the committee needs to decide tonight is narrower: whether to hold a provisional booking by the end of the month, so the tour stays possible. The final cost, the itinerary and the exam dates come back to this committee in March, and that's the point at which families decide. If we don't hold the booking, those questions become academic.", "why": "It validates the parent's caution and promises a specific forum and date for every concern. The final sentence shows why the narrow decision is needed now without pressuring anyone."}, {"text": "Can I suggest we name tonight's decision precisely? It isn't whether the tour goes ahead; it's whether we keep the option open. If the answer is yes, I'll bring a costed itinerary and a checked exam calendar to the March meeting, and the decision to proceed is made then, against a cost ceiling we agree tonight. If the final price comes in above the ceiling, the tour doesn't go.", "why": "It distinguishes keeping an option open from committing to the tour. The agreed ceiling answers the history of cost blowouts directly, which a parent who has been burned will value."}], "checks": ["I distinguished holding an option from committing to the tour.", "I gave a date when the parents' questions will be answered.", "I addressed the history of cost increases directly.", "I avoided sounding as though I was selling the tour."]}, {"id": "naming-the-real-question-s6", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "A university academic board is considering the report of a working party on assessment in the creative and performing arts. The report contains fourteen recommendations. The resolution before the board is to receive the report and refer the recommendations to faculties for response. Two members have spent fifteen minutes debating recommendation nine, on panel grading in studio assessment, which one of them strongly opposes.", "said": "I'm sorry to labour this, but recommendation nine would be a serious mistake. Panel grading in the studio dilutes the judgement of the principal teacher, who knows the student's development better than anyone. If the board endorses this, we'll be imposing a model the discipline has rejected for good reasons.", "task": "Clarify that the board is receiving and referring, not endorsing, and direct the objection to where it will be effective.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "I think that objection deserves to be heard properly, and it will be. Could I just check the resolution with the Chair? As I read it, we're receiving the report and referring all fourteen recommendations to faculties for response. Receiving isn't endorsing. So the place for this argument is the faculty response on recommendation nine, and if it's as strong as I suspect, it should shape what comes back to this board.", "why": "It checks the scope through the chair and states the distinction in three words. It tells the objector where his argument will actually have force, which is better than telling him to stop."}, {"text": "Chair, it might help to be clear about the verb. We're asked to receive, not to adopt. If the board receives the report, nothing in recommendation nine binds anyone; it goes to faculties, who can reject it. If members are uneasy even about receiving, the remedy might be a sentence in the resolution noting that the board has formed no view on any recommendation. That would protect the concern without holding up the other thirteen.", "why": "It locates the issue precisely in the verb of the resolution. The proposed amendment offers the objector a formal protection, which is more persuasive than reassurance."}], "checks": ["I named the exact verb in the resolution.", "I said where the objection would be effective.", "I did not express a view on panel grading.", "I offered a remedy if the objector remained uneasy."]}]}, {"id": "drawing-a-distinction", "name": "Drawing a distinction", "family": "Framing", "what": "Separates two things the room has been treating as one, so that people discover where they actually agree and where the real disagreement lies.", "when": "When two capable speakers are using the same word in different senses and each hears the other as wrong, or when an argument is escalating while remaining unresolvable.", "teach": "A great deal of unproductive disagreement in committees comes from a single word doing two jobs. 'Standards' means both entry requirements and exit outcomes; 'access' means both who may enrol and who can afford to; 'fairness' means both equal treatment and equal effect. When two speakers use the word in different senses, each hears the other as wrong, and the argument escalates while remaining unresolvable. Drawing a distinction separates the two things, names them, and usually shows that the room agrees about one and disagrees about the other.\n\nThis is the native move of the academy, which is why it works so well there. Philosophers, lawyers and historians are trained to respect a well-drawn distinction and to recognise one quickly; a crisp distinction is among the few things that will make a room of experts stop and write something down. It also does valuable social work. It allows both earlier speakers to be right, each about a different thing, which lowers the temperature without anyone having to retreat.\n\nThe craft is economy. Name the two things in parallel form, give each a short label, and attach one consequence: 'There are two questions here. One is who we admit; the other is what we require of them by the end. On the first, I think we are close. The second is where the real difference lies.' Parallel syntax does much of the work, because the ear registers the structure before the mind weighs the content. Labels matter too: a good label becomes the room's vocabulary for the rest of the meeting, and whoever supplied it has quietly shaped the discussion.\n\nDeliver it with slight deliberateness: a small pause between the two halves, perhaps a hand marking each, but no more. It should sound like clarification, not cross-examination. Address the room rather than the two speakers, and credit both: 'I think Helen and Mark are each right about a different thing.'\n\nThere are three common failures. The first is the distinction without a difference: separating two things that do not need separating, which reads as pedantry and slows the room for nothing. The second is the proliferating taxonomy: three, four, five distinctions, at which point you are lecturing. The third is using the distinction to smuggle in a conclusion, defining the terms so that your preferred option wins by definition. A sophisticated room notices this quickly, and the credibility you lose is hard to recover. The test of a good distinction is that both sides would accept it as fair, even if it then happens to work in your favour.", "phrasings": ["I think there are two things here that we have been treating as one.", "It might help to distinguish coverage from depth. On the first, I suspect we largely agree; on the second, we genuinely differ.", "When we say 'standards', we may mean the bar for entry or the bar for graduation. Those pull in different directions.", "There is a difference between whether the policy is right and whether it was applied well in this case.", "I wonder whether we are running together the question of who decides with the question of what they should decide.", "Both of those points seem to me right, because they are about different things.", "Can I separate the principle from its timing? I support the first; I am less sure about the second.", "Let me call one of these 'access' and the other 'affordability', because the remedies are quite different."], "avoid": ["Well, technically, if we are being precise about our terms : it signals point-scoring rather than clarification and invites the room to resent the precision.", "There are actually five different things being confused here. First : a taxonomy turns a clarification into a lecture and loses the room by the third item.", "You are conflating two completely different things : addressed to a person, a distinction becomes an accusation of muddled thinking.", "If we define quality properly, it is obvious the proposal fails : a distinction drawn to deliver a conclusion by definition will be detected and discounted."], "scenarios": [{"id": "drawing-a-distinction-s1", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "A SCSA syllabus committee is reviewing a draft revision of the Year 11 and 12 Music syllabus. The draft reduces the number of prescribed works in the context component. A university member has argued that this lowers rigour; a teacher member has replied that the current load forces superficial coverage. The discussion is becoming a proxy war over whether the course is getting easier.", "said": "I'm not against the teaching being more manageable. But we have to be honest that fewer works means less. When I look at what first-year students arrive knowing, the breadth has already thinned. Reducing the list further sends a signal to schools that depth of knowledge matters less than it did.", "task": "Separate rigour from volume so the committee can argue about the one that matters.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think we're using 'rigour' to mean two different things. One is coverage: how many works a student encounters. The other is depth: how demanding the analysis of any one work is. The draft reduces coverage; whether it reduces depth depends entirely on what the assessment then asks for. If the examination requires sustained analysis of fewer works, I'd call that more rigorous, not less. So perhaps the test of this draft lies in the assessment design rather than the list.", "why": "It splits the contested word cleanly and shows that the draft touches only one half. The proposed test moves the committee to the place where its decision actually has effect."}, {"text": "It might help to separate what students know from what they can do with it. Breadth of repertoire is the first; the capacity to analyse an unfamiliar score is the second, and it's the one universities most often say they want. Both matter, but they're different levers. A shorter list could weaken the first and strengthen the second. I'd like us to decide which we're prioritising, and then judge the list against that.", "why": "It uses a knowledge and capability distinction that the university member will recognise from his own discipline. It treats both speakers as right and asks the committee for a priority rather than a verdict."}], "checks": ["I named both halves of the distinction in parallel form.", "I credited the university member's concern as real.", "I stated what follows from the distinction for this draft.", "I drew one distinction, not several."]}, {"id": "drawing-a-distinction-s2", "room": "school council", "setup": "At a school council meeting, a parent member raises a complaint that her child was penalised for a late practical assessment despite a medical certificate. She asks the council to review the decision and to consider whether the assessment policy is too rigid. Other members are unsure whether this is their business. The principal is present.", "said": "I'm not asking for special treatment. But if the policy allows a teacher to disregard a medical certificate, then the policy is wrong, and this council has a say in the school's policies. I think we have a responsibility to look at what happened here and make sure it doesn't happen to another family.", "task": "Distinguish the individual case, which is the principal's to resolve, from the policy question, which the council may properly consider.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think there are two matters here, and the council has a proper role in only one of them. Your child's case is a question of how the policy was applied, and that sits with the principal, who I know will look at it. Whether the policy handles medical evidence well is a fair question for this council, as part of its interest in the school's general directions. Could we ask the principal to resolve the individual case through the usual process, and bring the assessment policy to a future meeting for review?", "why": "It tells the parent that her concern is legitimate and gives each half of it the right forum. It keeps the council within its role without lecturing anyone on governance."}, {"text": "There's a difference between whether the policy is right and whether it was applied correctly. If it was misapplied, the remedy lies with the principal, and quickly. If it was applied as written and still produced this result, the policy needs looking at, and that's a conversation the council can have without discussing any individual student. I'd suggest we keep the two separate, partly to protect your child's privacy.", "why": "The two conditional branches show the parent that both possible outcomes have a remedy. Grounding the separation in her child's privacy makes it a protection rather than a deflection."}], "checks": ["I did not discuss the details of the individual case.", "I named the principal as responsible for the case itself.", "I affirmed the council's legitimate interest in the policy.", "I proposed a concrete next step for each half."]}, {"id": "drawing-a-distinction-s3", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "An arts funding panel is assessing a commission application from an established jazz composer for a large-scale work with a youth orchestra. Two assessors rate it highly on artistic merit. A third rates it low, citing an unconfirmed venue, an unsigned partnership with the orchestra and a timeline that depends on another grant. Scores are averaging to a borderline result.", "said": "I'm not questioning the composer. Her work is outstanding and the concept is exciting. But the application as submitted isn't ready. There's no confirmed partner, no venue, and the budget assumes money from a source that hasn't decided yet. Merit can't compensate for that.", "task": "Separate artistic merit from project readiness so that the panel can reach a better recommendation than a borderline average.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "I think we agree on more than the scores suggest. On artistic merit, all three of us rate it highly. On readiness, I think the concerns are well founded. Those are separate criteria, and averaging them hides the fact that this is an excellent project that isn't yet ready. That points to a different outcome from a borderline score: perhaps funding conditional on the partnership being confirmed, or a clear invitation to reapply next round.", "why": "It shows the panel that its apparent disagreement is really agreement on two separate axes. Naming what the average conceals leads directly to a better form of recommendation."}, {"text": "Could we score merit and readiness separately for a moment? I suspect we'd find the panel unanimous on the first and broadly agreed on the second. That's a much clearer message to the applicant than a mark in the middle, and it's fairer to the composer, whose work isn't borderline at all. The question for us then becomes whether our guidelines allow conditional funding.", "why": "It turns the distinction into a procedure the panel can perform at once. The point about fairness to the composer gives the separation an ethical as well as a technical justification."}], "checks": ["I credited the third assessor's concerns as valid.", "I named merit and readiness as distinct criteria.", "I showed what the averaged score conceals.", "I pointed towards a concrete form of recommendation."]}, {"id": "drawing-a-distinction-s4", "room": "university school of music admissions committee", "setup": "A university school of music's admissions committee is reviewing a proposal for an alternative entry pathway for applicants from schools without specialist music programs. These applicants would be admitted on portfolio and interview with a lower audition threshold, supported by a bridging year. A senior performance lecturer opposes it; the school's equity lead supports it. You are an external member from the schools sector.", "said": "I'm all for widening participation in principle. But an audition threshold exists for a reason. If we admit students below it, we either fail them in second year or we pass them and the degree means less. I don't see a third option, and I don't think it's kind to pretend there is one.", "task": "Separate the standard for entry from the standard for graduation, and show that the proposal touches only the first.", "seconds": 65, "models": [{"text": "I think there is a third option, and it turns on a distinction. The audition measures where a student is at entry. The degree standard is where they must be at the end. The proposal lowers the first and leaves the second untouched; the bridging year is the mechanism meant to close the gap. So the question isn't whether we're lowering standards. It's whether the bridging year is good enough to do that work, and that's something we can test.", "why": "It answers the lecturer's 'no third option' directly and precisely. Relocating the question to the bridging year gives the sceptic something legitimate to scrutinise."}, {"text": "Can I separate two things? One is the bar students must clear to graduate, and I'd defend that as strongly as anyone here. The other is the evidence we use to predict who will clear it. An audition is one predictor; for a student who has never had a teacher, it may be a poor one. The proposal changes the predictor, not the bar. If we hold the exit standard fixed and track progression, we'll know within two intakes whether it works.", "why": "Framing the audition as a predictor rather than a standard is the sharper version of the distinction, and the lecturer will recognise it as a fair point about evidence. The monitoring proposal answers his concern about kindness."}], "checks": ["I stated my own commitment to the graduating standard.", "I named entry and exit as separate things.", "I identified what the proposal changes and what it leaves fixed.", "I gave the sceptic a legitimate thing to test."]}, {"id": "drawing-a-distinction-s5", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The festival association's board is considering whether to stop announcing first, second and third places in each division, after several schools complained that placings demoralise developing bands. Every band also receives a gold, silver or bronze rating against published criteria. A long-serving adjudicator on the board argues that without placings the festival loses its rigour and its draw. A newer board member supports the change.", "said": "Schools come to this festival because the result means something. Take away the placings and you've got a series of concerts with comment sheets. Directors use those results to argue for their programs with principals. If we remove them, we'll be making the festival nicer and less useful.", "task": "Distinguish assessment against a standard from ranking against other bands, so that the board can keep what serves schools.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "I think the ratings and the placings do different jobs. A rating tells a school how its band measured against a published standard; that's what a director can take to a principal, and it's where the festival's rigour lives. A placing tells a school how it measured against the bands that happened to be in the same session. The complaints, as I read them, are almost all about the second. We could keep the standard and drop the league table.", "why": "It shows that the adjudicator's strongest argument actually belongs to the ratings, which nobody proposes to remove. The closing phrase gives the board a memorable label for the decision."}, {"text": "There's a difference between being assessed and being ranked. Assessment against criteria is the educational heart of what we do, and I'd resist anything that softened it. Ranking is a choice about presentation, and it has costs for exactly the developing bands we most want to keep coming. If we separate the two, I suspect the board can agree to keep one and review the other.", "why": "It states his own commitment to rigour first, which disarms the charge that the change is soft. Calling ranking 'a choice about presentation' makes it easier for the adjudicator to let go of."}], "checks": ["I distinguished rating from placing in one or two sentences.", "I linked the adjudicator's concern to the part being kept.", "I did not dismiss the value of results to schools.", "I gave the board a decision it could take."]}, {"id": "drawing-a-distinction-s6", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "In a budget meeting, the principal explains that every learning area will take a uniform eight per cent reduction in its operating budget next year. Most of the music program's operating budget is spent maintaining a large fleet of school-owned instruments. The principal is keen to be seen as even-handed across departments.", "said": "I know no one likes this, and I'm genuinely sorry. But the only way I can do it fairly is to do it evenly. If I start making exceptions, every head of learning area will have a case, and most of them will be good cases. Eight per cent across the board is the least bad option.", "task": "Distinguish equal treatment from equal effect without asking for special treatment.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I understand why an even cut is the fairest way to present it, and I'm not asking for an exception. I'd only point out that an even cut can have very uneven effects. For most areas, eight per cent means fewer consumables. For us, most of the operating budget maintains instruments the school owns, so eight per cent means instruments out of service and students without one. Could we look at the effect rather than the rate, perhaps with the business manager, so the cut lands as evenly as the percentage suggests?", "why": "It renounces an exception at the outset, which removes the principal's main fear. The concrete contrast between consumables and assets makes the distinction undeniable."}, {"text": "I think there are two kinds of fairness here. One is treating every area the same; the other is asking every area to bear the same burden. Usually they coincide. Where a budget is mostly the upkeep of fixed assets, they don't, because the cut falls on the assets rather than the discretionary spending. I'm happy to carry the same burden as everyone else. I'd just like us to measure it the same way.", "why": "It names the distinction in its general form, which lets the principal apply it consistently to other areas. The final line turns his request into a demand for greater fairness, not less."}], "checks": ["I said explicitly that I was not asking for an exception.", "I named equal treatment and equal effect as distinct.", "I gave one concrete example of the uneven effect.", "I proposed a way of measuring the burden rather than a different number."]}]}, {"id": "raising-the-altitude", "name": "Raising the altitude", "family": "Framing", "what": "Moves the discussion from the particular to the principle that governs it, or from management to governance, so that the body decides what only it can decide.", "when": "When a board or committee has descended into operational detail that belongs to staff or delegates, or when an individual case is being argued at length while the policy question it exposes goes unaddressed.", "teach": "Boards and councils drift downward. Detail is concrete, easy to discuss and satisfying to resolve, so members with expertise in an operational area gravitate to it, and an hour can pass on the wording of a letter or the choice of a caterer while the body's actual responsibilities go unattended. Raising the altitude moves the discussion from the particular to the principle that governs it, or from management to governance: from what should be done in this instance to what the body should decide, set or delegate so that such instances are handled well without it.\n\nThe move rests on a distinction every competent board understands in theory. The body sets direction, policy, risk appetite and accountability; staff or delegates execute. The committee of an incorporated association is responsible for managing its affairs, but a well-run committee does that by setting frameworks and holding people to them, not by doing their jobs. A school council's functions in Western Australia concern the school's objectives, priorities and general policy directions, not the day-to-day running of the school, which remains the principal's. You rarely need to say any of this. The room knows; it has simply slipped.\n\nThe difficulty is tone. Raising the altitude implies that the room has been at the wrong level, and highly educated people dislike being told so. Senior practitioners manage this in three ways. They honour the detail first, briefly and genuinely, often by saying that it has revealed something: 'This case is useful, because it shows we have no policy for it.' They frame the move as a service to those who will execute, not a criticism of those who have been discussing: 'Could we give Sarah a principle to work from, so this does not need to come back to us?' And they offer the principle concretely, in a sentence that could be minuted, rather than gesturing at 'the bigger picture'.\n\nTiming: once the detail has been explored enough to expose the principle at stake, but before anyone has committed to a position on the instance. Length: twenty to forty seconds. A slightly slower delivery and an open posture, sitting back rather than forward, signal perspective rather than impatience.\n\nIt fails in three ways. The first is condescension: any phrasing that implies others cannot see what you see. The second is abstraction, rising so high that the principle is a platitude ('we should act in the students' best interests') that decides nothing. The third is abandoning a detail that genuinely matters. Sometimes the instance is itself the governance question, a material risk, a legal exposure or a conflict of interest, and the body must deal with it directly. Altitude is a judgement, not a reflex.", "phrasings": ["This case is useful, because it shows us something we do not yet have a policy for.", "Could we give the director a principle to work from, so that decisions like this need not come back to the board?", "I wonder whether our role here is to settle the detail or to settle the criteria by which it will be settled.", "Taking this one step up: what would we want to be true in every case like this one?", "I am conscious that we may be doing the executive's job rather than our own. What is it this board needs to decide?", "If we agree the principle, I would be content to leave the particulars to those closest to them.", "There is a governance question underneath this, and I think it is the one we are best placed to answer.", "Let me try to put the principle in a sentence that could be minuted."], "avoid": ["Can we lift our heads out of the weeds for a moment? : the metaphor tells colleagues they have been in the weeds, which is condescension however lightly meant.", "I think we need to think about the bigger picture here : it gestures at altitude without offering a principle anyone could decide.", "This is really an operational matter and should not be at board level : true or not, it rebukes whoever raised it and gives the room nothing to do instead.", "Ultimately, what matters is the students : a principle so high it decides nothing, and it implies others had forgotten them."], "scenarios": [{"id": "raising-the-altitude-s1", "room": "school council", "setup": "You chair a school council. Under general business, a member has raised the arrangements for the school's community fair: stall allocations, food handling, whether a particular vendor should be invited. Members have spent twenty minutes on catering, and the business manager, who is running the event, is trying to take notes on preferences that contradict one another. Two strategic items remain on the agenda.", "said": "I just think if we're going to have food trucks, we need at least one that does proper vegetarian food, and frankly the coffee last year was a disgrace. These things matter to families. It's the one day a year the community really sees the school, and the details are what people remember.", "task": "As chair, lift the discussion from the event's details to what the council should properly decide, without dismissing the member.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think that's right: the details are what people remember. And that's exactly why I'd like to leave them with Anna, who's running the day and is far better placed than we are to get them right. What council can usefully do is say what the fair is for and what we'd want to be true of it: that it's inclusive, that it's safe and that it's affordable for families. If we're agreed on that, Anna can work within it and report back. Can I take that as the council's view?", "why": "It agrees with the member's final point and uses it as the reason to delegate. The three principles are concrete enough to minute and to guide the business manager."}, {"text": "Can I suggest we give Anna a brief rather than a list? From what's been said, I'm hearing three principles: food that caters for the whole community, a standard of quality that reflects well on the school, and costs that keep it accessible. If council endorses those, the specific choices are hers. I'd then like to move to the two strategic items, which only this council can decide.", "why": "It distils the room's own preferences into principles, so members hear their contributions honoured. The final sentence names the governance work waiting without criticising the time spent."}], "checks": ["I agreed with something the member said before moving up.", "I named the person who will execute and expressed confidence in them.", "I stated principles concrete enough to minute.", "I moved the meeting on to the strategic items."]}, {"id": "raising-the-altitude-s2", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The festival board is discussing the adjudication panel for next year. One board member has proposed a specific interstate adjudicator; another has concerns about his manner with young bands; a third suggests a local alternative. The festival director, who normally appoints adjudicators, is at the table and has said little. There is no written policy on adjudicator appointment.", "said": "I've heard him at three festivals and he's brilliant with senior bands, but I watched him reduce a Year 8 trumpet section to tears in Melbourne. I'm not comfortable with him in the junior divisions. If we're going to bring him over, it should be for the top division only.", "task": "Raise the board from choosing a particular adjudicator to setting the criteria and the delegation for appointing adjudicators.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "That's useful, and I suspect it's exactly the kind of judgement our director makes every year without the board's help. What strikes me is that we have nothing in writing about what we expect of adjudicators: how they work with developing bands, the balance of local and interstate, and who appoints. Could we ask the director to draft a short set of criteria for the next meeting, and agree that within those the appointments are hers? Then this conversation needn't happen at board level again.", "why": "It credits the speaker's observation and returns authority to the director who has been sidelined. Naming the missing policy turns an awkward personnel discussion into a governance task."}, {"text": "I wonder whether the board's job is to choose the adjudicator or to say what a good one looks like. The concern about the junior divisions is a principle in disguise: adjudicators must be able to give developmental feedback to beginning bands. If we put that, and two or three similar standards, in writing, the director can make the appointment, and we've protected every junior band, not just next year's.", "why": "It extracts the general principle from the anecdote, so the speaker's concern becomes policy rather than a comment on one person. It also avoids discussing a named individual's conduct at length."}], "checks": ["I did not express a view on the individual adjudicator.", "I turned the specific concern into a general standard.", "I returned the appointment to the director explicitly.", "I proposed a written outcome the board could ask for."]}, {"id": "raising-the-altitude-s3", "room": "SCSA assessment committee", "setup": "A SCSA committee is reviewing sample examination questions to be published as exemplars for the revised ATAR Music course. Members have spent some time debating whether one aural question should specify 'jazz' or 'contemporary' as its stylistic context. Both positions have merit. The broader purpose of the exemplar set has not been discussed.", "said": "I'd strongly prefer 'jazz'. 'Contemporary' is so broad it's almost meaningless, and if we're vague in an exemplar, teachers will be vague in their programs. A specific label tells schools exactly what to prepare.", "task": "Lift the committee from one question's wording to the principle governing what the exemplars should signal.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "That last point seems to me the important one: teachers read exemplars as instructions. So before we settle this question, perhaps we should agree what the set as a whole is meant to tell schools. If the exemplars are meant to indicate the range of contexts the examination might draw on, a specific label here needs balancing elsewhere. If they're meant to model the type of question rather than its content, the label matters less. Once we've decided that, I suspect this question will be easy.", "why": "It builds on the speaker's own reasoning, which makes the move feel like agreement taken further. The two options for the set's purpose give the committee a clear decision at the right level."}, {"text": "I'm persuaded that exemplars shape programs, and I think that applies well beyond this question. Could we agree a principle for the whole set: that the stylistic contexts across the exemplars should reflect the breadth of the syllabus, and that no single exemplar should be read as a prediction? Then 'jazz' here might be entirely right, provided the others range more widely.", "why": "It proposes the principle in a minutable sentence and then shows that it can accommodate the speaker's preference. The committee gets both a policy and a resolution of the detail."}], "checks": ["I took up the speaker's reasoning rather than opposing it.", "I stated a principle for the exemplar set as a whole.", "I showed how the detail would be resolved once the principle was agreed.", "I did not use the phrase 'bigger picture'."]}, {"id": "raising-the-altitude-s4", "room": "university academic board assessment subcommittee", "setup": "A university academic board's assessment subcommittee is considering a report showing a sharp rise in special consideration applications in performance units, concentrated around end-of-semester recitals. Members have been discussing particular cases from the de-identified appendix and debating whether specific applications should have been granted. The subcommittee's terms of reference are to advise the board on assessment policy.", "said": "Look at case fourteen. Performance anxiety, a GP's letter, granted a deferral. And case twenty-two, almost identical, refused. Either the criteria are being applied inconsistently or we have no criteria. I'd want to know who made these decisions.", "task": "Raise the discussion from individual case outcomes to what the pattern says about policy and assessment design, consistent with the subcommittee's advisory role.", "seconds": 65, "models": [{"text": "The inconsistency is real and should be looked at, through the faculty, as a matter of process. But I'd suggest our role is a level above the cases. What the pattern tells me is that a single high-stakes recital at the end of semester generates a predictable demand for special consideration. That's an assessment design question. Perhaps our advice to the board should be whether performance units need more than one assessment point, which would lower the stakes for everyone, including the students in cases fourteen and twenty-two.", "why": "It directs the inconsistency to the proper process and then reads the data as evidence about design. The advice it proposes is squarely within the subcommittee's terms of reference."}, {"text": "I think these two cases are telling us something general, which is what we're here to advise on. Either the policy doesn't say how performance anxiety is to be treated, or it does and isn't being followed. Both are policy questions, and neither requires us to revisit individual decisions already made by those responsible for them. Could we ask for the relevant policy clause and frame our recommendation around that?", "why": "It turns the speaker's either-or into two policy questions and keeps the subcommittee out of individual adjudication. The request for the clause gives the discussion a precise object."}], "checks": ["I accepted that the inconsistency matters.", "I referred to the subcommittee's advisory role.", "I named a policy or design question the pattern raises.", "I did not discuss the merits of either individual case."]}, {"id": "raising-the-altitude-s5", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "An arts funding panel is assessing a mid-sized application from a regional music festival. One panel member, an accountant, has been going through the budget line by line, querying catering, artist accommodation rates and a marketing figure. The panel has thirty applications to assess today and has spent fifteen minutes on this budget. The guidelines ask panels to assess whether a budget is realistic and represents value for money.", "said": "And I'd query the accommodation line too. Four hundred a night is a lot for a regional town. If they used the motel instead of the hotel, they'd save nearly two thousand. I don't think we should be funding a budget that hasn't been looked at this carefully.", "task": "Raise the panel from re-budgeting the application to the assessment the guidelines actually ask for.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Those are fair queries, and it's the pattern I'd take from them. Our guidelines ask us to judge whether the budget is realistic and good value overall, not to rebuild it. Taken together, these lines suggest a budget that's somewhat generous but not unrealistic. If that's our view, we can say so in the feedback, with the accommodation line as the example, and score it accordingly. Otherwise I worry we'll give this applicant far more scrutiny than the other twenty-nine.", "why": "It converts the accountant's line items into a single overall judgement, which is what the guidelines require. The point about consistency across applicants is a fairness argument he will respect."}, {"text": "Could I ask the panel a slightly different question? Leaving the individual lines aside, do we think this organisation can deliver what it proposes for the money it's asking? If yes, a few generous items are a matter for the feedback. If no, it isn't the catering that's the problem. I suspect we're broadly at yes.", "why": "It poses the governing question in a single sentence and shows how both answers dispose of the detail. Its brevity models the pace the panel needs."}], "checks": ["I acknowledged the queries as legitimate.", "I named the test the guidelines actually set.", "I proposed where the detailed points should go.", "I kept my contribution under fifty seconds."]}, {"id": "raising-the-altitude-s6", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "You are meeting the principal and the business manager about an invoice for an expensive overhaul of a school-owned baritone saxophone. The business manager has questioned whether an instrument of its age is worth repairing. The conversation has narrowed to whether this one repair should be approved. The school has a large instrument fleet and no replacement schedule.", "said": "I'm not saying no. But this is the third major repair on that instrument in four years, and the quote is getting close to what a replacement would cost. At some point we're throwing good money after bad. I need a reason to approve it beyond 'we always have'.", "task": "Raise the conversation from a single invoice to an asset management principle the school can apply to the whole fleet.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "That's a fair test, and honestly I'd apply it more widely. This instrument is a symptom: we have a fleet worth a great deal of money and no schedule for replacing any of it, so every repair becomes a one-off decision like this. Could I bring you a simple fleet register next term, with each instrument's age, condition and repair history, and a replacement threshold we agree? Then decisions like this make themselves, and you're not dependent on my say-so.", "why": "It accepts the business manager's test and generalises it, which makes him an ally. Offering to remove the dependence on the TIC's judgement is a mark of good governance that a business manager values."}, {"text": "I'd suggest we approve this one, because the student who plays it has no alternative this year, and use it as the occasion to set a rule. Something like: once cumulative repairs reach half the replacement cost, the instrument goes on the replacement list. That gives you a defensible principle, it gives me a planning horizon, and it means the next one of these doesn't need a meeting.", "why": "It resolves the instance and the principle in one move, which is often what a busy executive wants. The proposed threshold is specific enough to adopt on the spot."}], "checks": ["I accepted the business manager's concern as a fair test.", "I proposed a principle that applies to the whole fleet.", "I made the principle specific enough to adopt.", "I dealt with or deliberately parked the immediate invoice."]}]}, {"id": "opening-a-discussion-well", "name": "Opening a discussion well", "family": "Framing", "what": "Uses the first contribution on an item to set the vocabulary, the scale and the question to which everyone else will respond, without closing the discussion down.", "when": "When the chair invites the first comment on an item, when you are lead assessor or designated opener, or when you chair and must introduce the item yourself.", "teach": "The first contribution on an agenda item has disproportionate influence. Whoever speaks first sets the vocabulary, the scale and the implied question, and later speakers tend to respond to that opening rather than to the paper itself. Any experienced chair knows this: the first speaker hands the room its terms. Opening well means using that influence responsibly, so that the discussion begins at the right level and on the right question, without your foreclosing it.\n\nA strong opening has three parts and rarely runs past sixty seconds. It orients: a sentence on what the item is and what kind of decision it asks for. It evaluates: a short, honest view of the paper's strength and its central difficulty, named precisely. And it invites: it hands the room a question that is genuinely open and that others are better placed than you to answer. The invitation is the part most people omit, and it is what distinguishes an opening from a speech. It is also a courtesy to the expertise around the table.\n\nWith a highly educated room the risk is not that you will say too little but that you will say everything. The opener who covers every concern has in effect closed the item, and the room will either defer, which wastes its intelligence, or react against the completeness, which makes you the topic. Choose the one or two points that matter most and leave the rest for others; if nobody raises them, you can do so later, now as a response rather than an opening.\n\nSenior practitioners often begin by acknowledging the author's work in one specific clause, which is far more credible than general praise: 'The modelling in section three is what I found most useful.' They then state a position provisionally, as a view open to revision: 'My instinct is to support it, though I can see the case against.' When opening as chair, the balance shifts further. The chair's opening should frame the decision and the criteria and withhold the chair's own view, or give it last.\n\nVoice: settled and unhurried, as though you had expected to be asked, which you should have. Prepare the opening of every item where you might plausibly be called first. Do not begin with apology or throat-clearing ('I suppose I can start'). Make eye contact around the table as you reach the invitation, not only with the chair, because the invitation is to everyone.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways: the comprehensive opening that exhausts the item; the opening that announces a conclusion so firmly that disagreement becomes personal; and the merely procedural opening ('I'm happy to go first') that wastes the anchor altogether.", "phrasings": ["Thank you for the paper. The section I found most useful was the modelling of enrolment trends, and it is also where my main question lies.", "This seems to me to ask us for a decision in principle, with the detail to follow. On that basis my instinct is to support it, with one reservation.", "Let me offer a starting view, which I hold provisionally.", "I think the paper is strongest on governance and least developed on resourcing, and I would be interested to hear whether others read it the same way.", "There are, as I see it, two questions here, and it may help to take them in turn.", "Before I give a view, could I ask the author one question of fact? The answer will shape what I think.", "As chair, I will hold my own view for now. What I would like us to settle is whether the proposal meets the criteria in section two, and I suggest we take them in order.", "I will be brief, because others will know more than I do about the accreditation implications, but from the schools' side the central issue is this."], "avoid": ["I suppose I can kick things off, though I have not had a chance to look at it properly : it apologises for the anchor and discounts your view before you have given it.", "I have about eight points, so bear with me : the comprehensive opening exhausts the item and makes others respond to you rather than to the paper.", "This is clearly unworkable and I cannot see how anyone could support it : an opening this categorical makes disagreement personal and closes the discussion before it starts.", "Great paper, really great work, well done to everyone involved : generic praise is noise to an expert room and delays the substance."], "scenarios": [{"id": "opening-a-discussion-well-s1", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "A SCSA syllabus committee is considering the summary of statewide consultation on a draft revised Music syllabus. The summary is long and reports mixed responses: support for a new contemporary context, concern about workload in the composition component and polarised views on aural assessment. The chair has introduced the item and invited comment. No one has yet spoken.", "said": "Thank you, everyone. You'll have seen the consultation report; there's a great deal in it, and some of the responses pull in opposite directions. I don't want us to try to resolve everything today. Who would like to start us off?", "task": "Open the item by setting the terms of the discussion: what the committee should focus on and in what order.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I'm happy to start. My reading of the report is that most of it confirms the direction we've already agreed, and that the real work lies in two places: workload in composition, and aural assessment. On composition, the concern looks practical and fixable. On aural, the responses suggest a genuine disagreement about the subject, and I don't think we'll settle it by counting submissions. I'd suggest we spend most of our time there, and I'd be interested in how colleagues from the universities read those responses.", "why": "It orients, evaluates and invites in under a minute, and it ranks the issues so the committee knows where its time should go. The closing invitation is directed at members with relevant expertise."}, {"text": "Thank you, Chair. Could I suggest a way in? The responses seem to sort into three kinds: those that support the draft, those that want it adjusted, and those that question its premise. The third group is small, but it includes some careful submissions on aural assessment. If we deal quickly with the first two, we'll have time to take the third seriously, which I think it deserves.", "why": "It offers a structure for the whole item rather than a view on any part of it, which is a generous use of the anchor. Singling out the minority submissions protects them from being outvoted by volume."}], "checks": ["I did not apologise or clear my throat before starting.", "I identified no more than two priority issues.", "I suggested an order or allocation of time.", "I ended with an invitation to others."]}, {"id": "opening-a-discussion-well-s2", "room": "school council", "setup": "You chair a school council. The next item is the school's draft business plan priorities for the next three years, presented by the principal. The council has a formal role in endorsing the plan. Several members are new and unsure how much they are expected to contribute; one experienced member has strong views about the absence of a priority on student wellbeing. You must open the discussion as chair.", "said": "That's the draft, and I'd welcome the council's views. I've tried to keep it to five priorities so that it's achievable, but I'm very open to hearing where you think we have the emphasis wrong.", "task": "As chair, open the discussion by framing the decision and the criteria, without giving your own view first.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Thank you, Principal. Just to be clear about our role: the council is asked to endorse the priorities, not to write the operational plan, so the useful questions for us are whether these five are the right ones and whether anything important is missing. I'd suggest we take those in order. I'll hold my own view until others have spoken, and I'd particularly like to hear from our newer members, who may see the draft with fresher eyes than the rest of us.", "why": "It states the council's role without lecturing, sets a sequence and explicitly makes room for new members. Withholding the chair's view protects the quality of the discussion."}, {"text": "Thank you. I'd suggest three questions to guide us. Do these priorities reflect what this community most needs from the school over the next three years? Is anything significant missing? And are they framed so that the council can tell, in three years, whether they've been achieved? Perhaps we could start with the second, since I suspect it's where members have most to say.", "why": "The three questions give the council criteria rather than opinions, including an accountability test that only a governing body would ask. Starting with omissions creates a natural place for the wellbeing concern."}], "checks": ["I did not state my own view on the priorities.", "I named what the council is being asked to do.", "I gave the room criteria or questions to work from.", "I made explicit space for members who might otherwise stay silent."]}, {"id": "opening-a-discussion-well-s3", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The festival board is considering a proposal to move the festival from its long-standing university venue to a new performing arts centre in the city. The new venue offers better acoustics and more stages, but at a significantly higher hire cost. As treasurer you have prepared the financial comparison, and the President has asked you to open the item.", "said": "We've all had a chance to read the proposal, and I know there are strong feelings about leaving the university after so many years. Before we get into that, I've asked our treasurer to open, since the numbers will frame a lot of what follows.", "task": "Open with the financial picture in a way that informs the decision without letting finance become the only frame.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "Thank you. The short version is that the move is affordable, but only just, and only if entries hold at this year's level. The new venue costs substantially more, and we'd need either a modest rise in entry fees or a sponsor to cover the gap. So money doesn't rule the move out, but it does make it a decision with a real cost to schools. The question I'd put to the board is whether what the new venue offers educationally is worth that cost, and on that I'm one voice among many.", "why": "It gives the financial verdict in one sentence and then hands the decision back to the board on educational grounds. The final clause deliberately reduces the weight of the treasurer's voice on the question that follows."}, {"text": "I'll keep the numbers brief, because I don't think they should decide this on their own. Both options are viable. The new venue is more expensive and carries more risk if entries fall; the current one is cheaper, but we're already turning bands away. So the financial question is really a question about growth: if we believe the festival will keep growing, the move pays for itself within a few years. I'd be interested in whether the board thinks that's realistic.", "why": "It translates the numbers into the strategic assumption on which they depend, which is the question the board should debate. It neither advocates nor obstructs the move."}], "checks": ["I gave the financial position in one or two sentences.", "I named the non-financial question the board must answer.", "I did not use the numbers to decide the matter.", "I ended by inviting the board's view."]}, {"id": "opening-a-discussion-well-s4", "room": "university course advisory committee", "setup": "A university school of music's course advisory committee, which includes external members from the profession and the schools sector, is considering a proposal for a new jazz performance pathway within the Bachelor of Music. The proposal is detailed and well argued and has internal support, but its assumptions about the preparedness of incoming students are optimistic. You are the first external member invited to speak.", "said": "The proposal has been through the school's internal processes, so I'm particularly keen to hear from our external members today. Dijon, you see these students before we do. Would you like to begin?", "task": "Open the external members' contribution by affirming what is strong and naming the one assumption that most needs testing.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "Thank you. I think this is a strong proposal, and the sequencing of the improvisation units in particular is better than anything I've seen in a comparable course. My one substantial question is about who arrives. The proposal assumes entrants with secure jazz language, and from the schools' side, outside a handful of programs, that isn't who's coming through. That doesn't undermine the pathway, but it may mean the first year needs a different shape. I'd be interested in whether colleagues in the profession see the same gap.", "why": "The specific praise is credible and earns the right to the critical point that follows. It names one assumption from evidence only he holds and hands the next question to the other external members."}, {"text": "I'd like to start with a question rather than a view, if I may. What does the school expect a typical entrant to be able to do on the first day? I ask because the answer shapes almost everything else. If the expectation is fluent jazz language, the proposal is excellent but will serve a small cohort. If it's strong general musicianship, the first-year units may need rethinking. Either is defensible; I think the committee needs to know which it's endorsing.", "why": "Opening with a question of fact is a disciplined way to surface the weak assumption without asserting it. The two branches make clear that he is testing the proposal, not opposing it."}], "checks": ["I praised one specific feature, not the proposal in general.", "I named a single central assumption.", "I drew on what I see from the schools' side.", "I left room for the other external members."]}, {"id": "opening-a-discussion-well-s5", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "An arts funding panel follows the convention that a designated lead assessor opens discussion of each application. You are lead assessor for an application from a community choir seeking funds to commission and premiere a new work by a Noongar composer, in partnership with an Aboriginal community organisation. The application is artistically strong and its budget is sound, but the partnership documentation is thin.", "said": "Next is the choral commission. Dijon, you're lead on this one. We're running about ten minutes behind, so if you can give us your recommendation and the key issues, that would help.", "task": "Open with a clear provisional recommendation, the application's strength and its key issue, in a way that structures the panel's discussion.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Thank you. My provisional recommendation is to fund, with one condition. Artistically this is among the strongest applications in the round, and the budget is realistic. The issue is the partnership. For a work of this kind, the relationship with the community organisation is not an administrative detail; it's central to whether the project is done properly. At present the evidence is one brief letter. I'd suggest funding conditional on a fuller partnership agreement, including cultural protocols and the composer's rights. I'd welcome views on whether that condition is enough.", "why": "It leads with the recommendation, as the chair asked, and explains why the gap matters substantively rather than procedurally. The proposed condition is specific and the invitation is precise."}, {"text": "Briefly: strong, sound, with one gap that matters. The artistic case and the budget are both clear. The partnership is described rather than evidenced, and for a commission involving a Noongar composer and community, that's the part I'd most want to be confident about. So my question for the panel is whether we treat it as a condition to attach or a reason to defer. I lean towards a condition, but I'd value others' views.", "why": "Its compressed opening respects the time pressure and gives the panel the shape of the issue at once. Posing condition versus deferral structures the discussion around the one real choice."}], "checks": ["I gave a recommendation in my first or second sentence.", "I named the partnership issue as substantive, not administrative.", "I offered a specific condition or choice for the panel.", "I kept within the time the chair asked for."]}, {"id": "opening-a-discussion-well-s6", "room": "conference panel", "setup": "You are on a panel at a national music education conference titled 'The future of specialist music programs in public schools'. The other panellists are a professor of music education, a senior education department official and a well-known performer. The moderator, an academic, has introduced the panel and turns to you first. The audience is several hundred teachers and administrators.", "said": "We've framed this session around the future, but I suspect many people here are anxious about the present: staffing, funding, the pressure on the timetable. So let me start with someone who runs one of these programs day to day. What do you think the real question is for specialist programs over the next decade?", "task": "Open the panel with a clear, arguable starting position that sets useful terms for the other panellists.", "seconds": 75, "models": [{"text": "Thank you. I'd put the question this way: not whether specialist programs survive, but whether they can justify themselves as public goods rather than private advantages. A selective program in a public school has to answer to every family whose child didn't get in. Programs that can show what they give back, to the whole school, to feeder primaries, to the wider community, will thrive. Those that can't will be the first to go when budgets tighten, and some of that scrutiny will be deserved. I'll be interested to hear whether the Department sees it the same way.", "why": "It takes a position sharp enough to argue with, which is what a panel opening needs. Ending on the official gives the moderator an obvious next move and puts policy in the conversation early."}, {"text": "I think the real question is about the pipeline. Specialist programs at secondary level depend on children arriving who have already started an instrument, and in much of the public system the primary years are where that is eroding. If that's right, the future of these programs is being decided in schools that don't have them. I offer that as a hypothesis, and I suspect the professor has better evidence than I do on whether it holds.", "why": "It relocates the question from the anxious present to its structural cause, which reorients the whole session. Offering it as a hypothesis and naming the professor turns the opening into a genuine invitation."}], "checks": ["I stated one clear position in my first two sentences.", "My position was arguable rather than a platitude.", "I handed the next question to a named fellow panellist.", "I spoke to the audience as well as to the moderator."]}]}, {"id": "testing-an-assumption", "name": "Testing an assumption", "family": "Testing", "what": "It moves the discussion from a proposal's conclusion to the unstated premise it depends on, and puts that premise to the room as an open question.", "when": "When a proposal is gathering momentum, its reasoning is sound, and everything turns on a premise nobody has stated, or one that was true once and may no longer be.", "teach": "Arguments in committee are rarely invalid. Where they fail is in their premises, and clever people defend conclusions far better than they inspect premises, because the premises arrive already agreed in the framing of the paper. Testing an assumption relocates the debate to the ground where the decision is actually being made. It works with highly educated rooms because it is the form of their own disciplines: the referee's report that says the analysis is careful but rests on a condition that has not been shown. It is also generous. You are not saying the proposer is wrong; you are asking whether a condition holds, and you are giving them the first chance to check it.\n\nThe craft is in choosing the premise. Not the weakest premise, the load-bearing one: the one which, if it fell, would take the recommendation with it. A weak but decorative premise wastes the move and spends your credit. Before you speak, run the test silently: if this were false, would we still be doing this? If the answer is yes, find another premise or say nothing.\n\nState the premise neutrally and in the proposer's own terms, ideally more clearly than they put it, so they recognise it as theirs. Acknowledge the reasoning in a clause, not a paragraph. Then ask a genuine question, one whose answer you do not presume. The whole contribution should take under thirty seconds.\n\nTiming matters more than wording. The move belongs after the proposal has been fully put and before the chair turns to implementation. Once the room is discussing dates and budgets, the premise has been silently adopted, and raising it then sounds like relitigating.\n\nThe voice is level and slightly slower on the premise itself, which is the sentence you want remembered. Look at the proposer as you state it, then open your gaze to the room as you ask. After the question, stop. Let the silence do the work; filling it with your own answer converts an inquiry into an argument.\n\nIt goes wrong in three familiar ways. Listing several assumptions reads as general scepticism and lets the room answer the easiest one. Asking a question to which you plainly know the answer is a trap, and a room of lawyers and academics will see it as one. And misreading the premise: if you are corrected, accept the correction at once and with grace. The room credits a member who clarifies more than one who wins.", "phrasings": ["The reasoning follows if one thing holds, and I would like us to check it: are we confident that the drop in numbers is about demand?", "Can I surface something the paper takes as given?", "Before we turn to the detail, it might help to name what this depends on. As I read it, everything turns on the venue being available on weekdays.", "I think the proposal is sound on its own premises. My question is about one of them.", "If it turned out that the cohort had not changed, would we still recommend this?", "What would have to be true for this to work? I count one thing, and I am not sure we have tested it.", "That was certainly the case when the policy was written. Is it still the case?", "I may be reading too much into the paper, but it seems to assume that schools will release students in term time. Do we know that?"], "avoid": ["I just think there are a lot of assumptions in here. : vague; it invites reassurance rather than inspection, and nobody can answer it.", "With respect, the whole thing rests on a false premise. : it delivers the verdict before the question, so the proposer has to defend rather than examine.", "Surely nobody seriously believes students will pay that. : sarcasm; it insults whoever does believe it, possibly the chair.", "Have we stopped to ask whether any of our assumptions are right? : a seminar gesture with no purchase; it names no premise and tests nothing."], "scenarios": [{"id": "testing-an-assumption-s1", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The Music ATAR syllabus committee is reviewing the weighting of the aural and analysis examination. An Authority officer has tabled four years of cohort data and a recommendation to reduce the aural component's weighting. Several members teach in non-specialist schools and are sympathetic. The recommendation will go to the Board of the Authority if the committee endorses it today.", "said": "The data are fairly unambiguous. Aural scores have drifted down for four successive cohorts, and the drift is steepest in schools without a specialist program. I think we have to accept that the student coming through now has a different musical formation, and the weighting ought to reflect what we can reasonably expect of them.", "task": "Surface the premise that the decline reflects students rather than the paper or the provision, and ask whether it has been tested.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "The trend is clear, and I do not doubt it. What I would like to check is the causal step. The recommendation assumes the drift is in the students, rather than in the paper or in the provision. Over those four years, did the item types or the marking guide change? And did specialist-program students drift as well? If they held steady, we may be looking at a provision problem, and reweighting would treat the symptom.", "why": "It concedes the data in one sentence, isolates the causal premise precisely, and proposes two checks that could actually separate the explanations."}, {"text": "Can I ask about what the recommendation takes as given? It reads the decline as a change in students' musical formation. That may well be right. But if the cause were reduced timetabled aural teaching in non-specialist schools, the remedy would be support for teachers, not a change to the weighting. Do we have anything that distinguishes those two?", "why": "It frames the premise as a reading of the paper, grants that it may be right, and shows the room why the answer changes the remedy."}], "checks": ["I named one premise, not several.", "I acknowledged the data before questioning the inference.", "I asked a question whose answer I did not already claim to know.", "I stopped speaking after the question."]}, {"id": "testing-an-assumption-s2", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "The academic board of a Perth university is considering a proposal from the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Education) to move first-year lectures across several schools, including the conservatorium's history and analysis units, to recorded-only delivery. Attendance has fallen sharply since 2020. The proposal frees lecture theatres and staff time for small-group teaching. It has the support of most deans.", "said": "Attendance at first-year lectures is now below a third in most units. Students are voting with their feet; they are telling us, quite clearly, that the live lecture is not where they learn. I would rather redeploy that effort into tutorials, where the evidence for active learning is strong, than keep performing to empty rooms.", "task": "Name the premise that low attendance means students do not value live teaching, and ask whether another explanation has been ruled out.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "I am sympathetic to the redeployment. The step I would want to test is the reading of the attendance figure. The proposal treats it as a judgement on the lecture. It could equally be a judgement on the timetable: students working twenty hours a week will choose the recording whatever its merits. Do we know which it is? Because if it is the timetable, we may be removing the thing they would attend if they could.", "why": "It accepts the destination, isolates the inference, and offers a concrete rival explanation without claiming it is the right one."}, {"text": "One question before we vote. The case rests on attendance being a signal of value. Has anyone asked the students who stopped coming why they stopped? If the answer is paid work, we have a scheduling problem; if the answer is that the lectures add nothing, the proposal is right. The survey would take a fortnight.", "why": "It reduces the premise to a single testable question and attaches a cheap, quick way to answer it."}], "checks": ["I stated the premise in the proposer's terms.", "I offered one rival explanation, not a list.", "I did not argue against the redeployment itself.", "I kept to under a minute."]}, {"id": "testing-an-assumption-s3", "room": "school council", "setup": "The school council is endorsing the next year's contributions and charges schedule. The business manager proposes raising the specialist music program's instrumental levy by a substantial amount to cover rising repair and hire costs. The council includes parent representatives, two staff members and a community member. Approval is expected to be a formality.", "said": "We benchmarked against comparable programs and we are still below most of them. More to the point, we raised it modestly last year and received no complaints at all, not one. I think that tells us the families in this catchment can absorb it.", "task": "Test the premise that the absence of complaints indicates capacity to pay.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "The benchmarking is useful. I would be cautious about the second point, though. The proposal reads silence as capacity. In my experience, the families who struggle with a charge rarely complain; they quietly do not enrol, or they withdraw at the end of Year 9. Do we know whether last year's rise changed our uptake or our withdrawals? If it did not, I am comfortable. If it did, the silence was telling us something else.", "why": "It brings practitioner knowledge to bear on the premise without accusation, and makes approval conditional on a fact the business manager can check."}, {"text": "Can I ask about what we are inferring from no complaints? Families do not usually complain to a school council about a voluntary charge; they make a private decision. Before we read silence as consent, could we look at how many families applied for hardship assistance after last year's increase?", "why": "It explains briefly why the inference is weak and redirects to an existing, more reliable measure."}], "checks": ["I credited the benchmarking before testing the inference.", "I identified the specific inference: silence as capacity.", "I proposed a measure that already exists.", "I did not imply the business manager was indifferent to families."]}, {"id": "testing-an-assumption-s4", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The board of the association that runs the state schools' jazz festival is reviewing the next festival's dates. Venue hire on weekends has risen sharply, and the president proposes moving the festival to a Thursday and Friday during term. The treasurer has confirmed the saving would be real. Registrations open in six weeks.", "said": "The saving is significant, roughly a fifth of our venue budget. And frankly, a weekday festival is more educational: it treats jazz as part of the school week, not an extracurricular hobby. Schools release students for sport carnivals all the time; I cannot see why music would be different.", "task": "Surface the assumption that principals will release ensembles in term time on the same basis as sport, and ask how it has been tested.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "I like the argument about jazz belonging in the school week. The whole saving depends on one thing, though: that principals release twenty-piece ensembles for two days in Term 3 the way they release teams for a carnival. Sport carnivals are usually whole-school events with relief already budgeted. Have we asked any principals? If three or four of our large schools say no, the saving disappears with the registrations.", "why": "It honours the educational argument, names the premise exactly, and shows its connection to the financial case the board cares about."}, {"text": "Could we test the comparison with sport before we commit? It assumes the release decision is the same. For a festival it often falls to the music teacher to argue for relief cover across several year groups. I would be happy to ring five principals this week and report back before registrations open.", "why": "It questions the analogy precisely and volunteers the test, which turns a doubt into a contribution."}], "checks": ["I named the premise in one sentence.", "I connected the premise to the money, not just to principle.", "I suggested a way to test it within the timeline.", "I avoided predicting that schools would refuse."]}, {"id": "testing-an-assumption-s5", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "The principal and an associate principal have asked to meet with you about restructuring performing arts leadership. Their proposal is to create a single Head of Performing Arts across music, drama and dance, with the current Teacher in Charge roles reduced. You have a stake in the outcome, and they know it. The discussion is informal but the direction is largely set.", "said": "We think a single line of leadership is cleaner. At the moment, when something goes wrong between music and drama, there's no one person who owns it, and it ends up on my desk. One head, one budget, one voice at executive. It's the model most large schools are moving to.", "task": "Test the premise that the problem is the number of leaders rather than the clarity of their authority, without sounding self-interested.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I can see why one line of accountability is attractive, and I would rather say this now than after the structure is set. The proposal assumes the difficulty is that there are two leaders. My reading is that the difficulty is that neither has defined authority over shared resources: the theatre, the calendar, the budget lines. If that is right, one head would solve it, but so would a clear protocol, at lower cost. Which problem are we solving?", "why": "It names the premise plainly, offers a rival diagnosis with concrete examples, and ends on a question that keeps the principal's options open."}, {"text": "Can I test one thing, knowing I am not a neutral party? The case assumes the conflicts come from structure. When you think of the last three that reached your desk, were they about who was in charge, or about the calendar and the theatre? If they were about who was in charge, I take the point. If they were about shared resources, I wonder whether we are fixing the right thing.", "why": "Declaring the interest disarms the obvious objection, and asking the principal to test the premise against her own cases lets her reach the conclusion herself."}], "checks": ["I acknowledged the appeal of the proposal first.", "I distinguished the number of leaders from the clarity of authority.", "I did not argue for my own role.", "I ended with a question the principal could answer from her own experience."]}, {"id": "testing-an-assumption-s6", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "You are on a state arts funding panel assessing youth music applications. A regional youth orchestra has applied for a two-year program. Another panel member, an experienced program manager, recommends against, citing previous acquittals. The panel is ranking applications against a limited pool and has three more to discuss before lunch.", "said": "I've seen a number of these regional applications over the years and the pattern is consistent: they project ambitious participant numbers and they underdeliver at acquittal, sometimes by forty per cent. I don't doubt the intent, but past performance is the best predictor we have, and the guidelines ask us to weigh deliverability.", "task": "Test whether past underdelivery reflects the applicant's capacity or a condition of the funding itself.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "Deliverability is the right criterion. I would want to know what the pattern is a pattern of. If regional underdelivery tracks the timing of our own grant releases, so that funds arrive after the school term has planned around them, then the acquittals measure our process as much as their capacity. Do we know when those previous grants were paid relative to the project start?", "why": "It accepts the criterion, questions what the evidence measures, and points to a fact the panel's own records can supply."}, {"text": "Can I ask whether those were acquittals from this organisation or from regional applicants generally? The recommendation seems to assume that the general pattern predicts this applicant. Their own last acquittal is in the attachments, and as I read it they delivered close to target.", "why": "It distinguishes a base rate from the specific case, and brings the room back to the applicant's own record without making it a contest."}], "checks": ["I accepted the guideline the colleague cited.", "I identified what the evidence was actually measuring.", "I pointed to information available in the room.", "I did not accuse the colleague of bias against regional applicants."]}]}, {"id": "the-evidence-question", "name": "The evidence question", "family": "Testing", "what": "It asks what a claim rests on in a way that invites the speaker to show their working, and lets the room set the weight of the claim to match its support.", "when": "When an assertion is carrying more of the decision than its stated basis can bear, or when 'the research shows' is being used to close a discussion rather than inform it.", "teach": "In committee, claims gather authority through confidence, seniority and repetition. By the third time a claim has been cited, it has become the room's premise, whatever its origin. The evidence question restores proportion. It works with highly educated audiences because asking for the basis of a claim is a norm of their own professions; nobody in an academic board or an Authority committee can object to the question in principle. The whole risk is in tone. Asked as curiosity, it is collegial. Asked as challenge, it is cross-examination, and the speaker's first task becomes defending face rather than producing evidence.\n\nAsk for the kind of evidence, not for proof. Is this from our own data or the wider literature? How many schools does it reflect? Was that a review or a single study? These questions are specific, answerable and neutral. Where it is plausible, give the speaker a graceful route: it may be in an appendix I have not absorbed. That small courtesy changes the temperature of the exchange.\n\nThe senior part of the move is what you do when the evidence turns out to be weaker than presented: anecdotal, a single site, a different jurisdiction, a different population. Do not pounce. Resize the claim instead of refuting it. Restate it at the strength the evidence supports, and propose that the decision be scaled to match: a strong signal from one school is enough to pilot on and not yet enough to mandate. This leaves the speaker's judgement intact, gives the room a better decision, and makes you the member who improved the proposal rather than the one who embarrassed its author.\n\nTiming is early. The question belongs in the first few minutes after the claim is made, before others build on it. The voice is interested and a little lighter than your ordinary register; the body is open, hands visible, attention on the speaker as someone whose answer you want.\n\nIt goes wrong in four ways. Asking for evidence only of claims you dislike: the room notices the asymmetry quickly, and your standing as an honest broker goes with it. Demanding a standard the domain cannot supply, such as randomised trials in arts education, which looks like obstruction. Following an honest 'I do not have that to hand' with a second, harder question, which turns inquiry into humiliation. And failing to apply the same question to your own claims, which is the surest way to lose the right to ask it.", "phrasings": ["That is an important claim for this decision. What is it based on?", "Is that from our own data or from the wider literature? Both matter, but in different ways.", "I would find it helpful to know how strong that finding is before we lean on it.", "When we say the research is clear, which research are we thinking of?", "How many schools does that reflect?", "So, if I have understood, it is a strong signal from one site. That seems enough to pilot on, and not yet enough to mandate.", "Would it be possible to have that tabled before we finalise? I suspect it supports the recommendation, and I would like the minutes to show it.", "Do we know that, or is it what we would expect to find?"], "avoid": ["Where is your evidence for that? : bare and prosecutorial; the speaker must defend their standing before they can address the substance.", "I have read the literature and it says the opposite. : trumps rather than inquires, and turns the meeting into a citation contest.", "That is just anecdotal. : dismissive, and many committee decisions properly rest on practitioner judgement.", "I am not sure that would survive peer review. : condescending in a room that is not a journal, and it implies the speaker is not a scholar."], "scenarios": [{"id": "the-evidence-question-s1", "room": "SCSA assessment committee", "setup": "A Curriculum and Assessment committee is considering whether to move the Music ATAR practical examination from school-based recording to externally set, live examination at central venues. The change would be expensive and logistically demanding for regional schools. An Authority officer is presenting the case. Members are inclined to agree because the language of equity is persuasive.", "said": "There is good research showing that externally conducted practical examinations reduce inequity between schools. When the conditions are standardised, the advantage of well-resourced schools with recording studios largely disappears. That is the core of the case.", "task": "Ask what the research is, and if it proves narrower than described, scale the claim to what it supports.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "That would be a strong argument, and I would like us to have it in front of us. Which studies are we drawing on? I ask because the equity effects can run both ways: standardised venues remove the studio advantage, but they add travel and accommodation costs for regional students. If the research is from a jurisdiction with comparable distances, it carries real weight here. If not, we might want to model the regional cost before deciding.", "why": "It asks for the source warmly, explains exactly why the context of the research matters, and proposes a proportionate next step."}, {"text": "Thank you. Can I ask a follow-up, since I take the point seriously? Is that research a review of several systems, or one interstate evaluation? If it is one evaluation, I would still find it persuasive as a direction. I would want to pilot central examination in one region and compare before committing every school to it.", "why": "It anticipates the likely answer and resizes the claim in advance, offering a pilot rather than a rejection."}], "checks": ["I asked for the kind of evidence, not for proof.", "I explained why the source mattered to this decision.", "I proposed a decision scaled to the evidence.", "My tone was curious rather than adversarial."]}, {"id": "the-evidence-question-s2", "room": "faculty curriculum committee", "setup": "A university faculty curriculum committee is reviewing the Bachelor of Music structure. The Head of School proposes cutting a core theory unit to make room for a compulsory ensemble-practice unit across all majors. The proposal has been framed around graduate employability. You are a practitioner member of the committee.", "said": "Employers are telling us, very consistently, that our graduates lack ensemble skills. They can play, but they cannot rehearse, lead a section or adapt in real time. That is the feedback we keep hearing, and I think we have an obligation to respond to it.", "task": "Find out what the employer feedback consists of, without diminishing it, and calibrate the decision accordingly.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "That rings true to me, and I want to make sure we can defend it. When we say employers, who are we hearing from, and how? If it is a structured survey of the people who hire graduates, it is a strong basis for restructuring the core. If it is conversations at industry evenings, it is still valuable, but I would want to test it before we remove a unit everyone else relies on.", "why": "It affirms the claim's plausibility, distinguishes types of evidence clearly, and connects the strength of evidence to the size of the change."}, {"text": "Could we say a bit more about the feedback? I ask partly because I would expect bandleaders and orchestral managers to describe the problem differently, and the unit we design would differ depending on which we are hearing. Is there anything written down we could circulate?", "why": "It turns the evidence question into a design question, which makes it clearly constructive."}], "checks": ["I said the claim sounded plausible.", "I asked who the employers were and how they were consulted.", "I linked the evidence to the scale of the change.", "I did not dismiss informal feedback as worthless."]}, {"id": "the-evidence-question-s3", "room": "school council", "setup": "The school council is discussing a parent submission proposing that the school day start forty minutes later for wellbeing reasons. The proposal would affect bus contracts, instrumental tuition schedules and before-school ensemble rehearsals. A parent representative is presenting it with conviction. The council can recommend the proposal for consultation or decline it.", "said": "Most families want this, and the adolescent sleep research is overwhelming. We ran a poll and the response was clear. I really don't think this is controversial among parents.", "task": "Separate the sleep research from the claim about parent opinion, and ask what the poll can tell the council.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "The sleep research is well established, and I think it deserves a proper look. The claim about families is a separate question. How was the poll run, and how many responded? If it was a social media poll, it tells us there is real interest, which is enough to justify a formal survey. I would be glad to support consultation on that basis. I would be cautious about telling the community that most families already want it.", "why": "It grants the strong part of the claim, inquires precisely about the weaker part, and supports the next step the evidence warrants."}, {"text": "Can I check the poll? I am not doubting the interest; I would like to know whose views it captures. Families with students in before-school ensembles would be affected most, and they may not have seen it. If we take this to consultation, could we make sure the survey reaches them directly?", "why": "It identifies a likely gap in the sample and converts it into a practical improvement to the process."}], "checks": ["I distinguished the research from the poll.", "I asked how the poll was conducted.", "I supported the step the evidence justified.", "I did not suggest the parent representative had misled the council."]}, {"id": "the-evidence-question-s4", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "You are in a planning meeting with the principal, a deputy and the head of learning data. The deputy is reviewing the school's ATAR results and has suggested that the music courses are lowering the school's median. You have an obvious interest in the answer, and everyone in the room knows it. How you ask matters as much as what you ask.", "said": "I've looked at the subject-level results and music is sitting below the school median. When you consider how many of our strongest students are in the specialist program, I would have expected the reverse. I think we need to ask whether the course is serving them.", "task": "Ask what the analysis is based on, in a way that is clearly not defensive, and propose how to read it properly.", "seconds": 65, "models": [{"text": "That is a fair question to ask of any course, and I would rather we look at it together than have it sit unresolved. Can I ask which figure we are using? Raw course marks, scaled scores and ATAR contributions tell different stories in music, because scaling depends on the whole state cohort. If we can see our students' scaled results against their results in their other subjects, we will know whether the course is serving them. I am happy to be held to that.", "why": "It welcomes scrutiny, asks precisely which measure is in use, names the technical distinction that matters, and proposes the right comparison."}, {"text": "I want to take this seriously, so may I ask how the comparison was made? With a cohort of our size, two or three results can move the median noticeably. If the head of data could show us the last three years rather than this one, and compare each student's music result with their other subjects, I think we would have a fair test.", "why": "It identifies the small-number problem without sounding defensive, and offers a test the data team can run, with the speaker accepting its outcome in advance."}], "checks": ["I made clear I welcomed the scrutiny.", "I asked which measure was being used.", "I proposed a fairer comparison rather than rejecting the finding.", "I avoided sounding defensive about my own program."]}, {"id": "the-evidence-question-s5", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The festival association board is discussing a drop in school registrations for the competitive sections of the jazz festival. A board member proposes abolishing adjudicated placings and moving to a non-competitive, feedback-only format. The board has a long tradition of awards and sponsors attached to them. A decision affects next year's sponsorship agreements.", "said": "It's clear that the competitive format is putting schools off. That's why registrations have dropped two years running. I've heard it directly from teachers; one told me in writing that her principal won't let the band enter if they might come last.", "task": "Treat the teacher's account seriously while establishing how widely it applies before restructuring the festival.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "That is important, and I am glad we have it in writing. Before we change the format, could we find out how representative it is? Registrations can fall for several reasons: term dates, staffing, the cost of buses. If we sent a short survey to the schools that did not return, we would know within a month whether placings are the cause. If they are, I would support the change.", "why": "It honours the evidence offered, lists alternative causes briefly, proposes a quick test, and commits in advance to the outcome."}, {"text": "So we have one clear account and a sense from conversations. That is enough, I think, to try a non-competitive stream alongside the existing sections next year and see who registers for it. It is not yet enough, for me, to remove placings our sponsors have funded for years.", "why": "It resizes the claim exactly to its support and translates that into a proportionate decision."}], "checks": ["I treated the teacher's account as real evidence.", "I named other plausible causes.", "I proposed a decision scaled to the evidence.", "I committed to acting if the evidence confirmed the claim."]}, {"id": "the-evidence-question-s6", "room": "conference panel", "setup": "You are on a panel at a national music education conference on senior secondary assessment. A fellow panellist, a respected assessment researcher, has argued that improvisation should not be assessed for university entrance purposes. The audience includes teachers and Authority staff from several states. The exchange is public and being recorded.", "said": "I'd love it to be otherwise, but the reliability evidence simply isn't there. Inter-rater agreement on improvisation is consistently weaker than on prepared repertoire, and for a high-stakes measure that's disqualifying. We can value improvisation enormously and still decline to put an ATAR on it.", "task": "Ask what the reliability claim rests on, in public and with collegial grace, and keep the question open rather than scoring a point.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "That is an argument I take seriously, so may I ask about the studies? My understanding is that agreement rises considerably when markers are trained on exemplars and the criteria are specific to the style. Were the studies you have in mind using general performance criteria, or style-specific ones? If it is the former, I wonder whether the problem lies in the rubric rather than the activity.", "why": "It acknowledges the colleague's expertise, asks a precise methodological question, and raises an alternative explanation as a question, not a verdict."}, {"text": "I would be interested to know the comparison point. Is improvisation less reliable than prepared performance, or less reliable than a threshold we have set for high-stakes use? Prepared repertoire is not perfectly reliable either. If we could see where both sit against that threshold, the audience could judge the gap for themselves.", "why": "It reframes the claim as a comparison and invites the evidence into the open, without contesting the researcher's data."}], "checks": ["I acknowledged the researcher's expertise.", "I asked about method, not about competence.", "I offered an alternative explanation as a question.", "I left the panellist room to answer well in public."]}]}, {"id": "the-precise-question", "name": "The precise question", "family": "Testing", "what": "It replaces a general debate with one exact question whose answer decides the matter, so the room stops trading positions and starts answering something.", "when": "When discussion has gone round twice, when speakers are agreeing or disagreeing past each other, or when a single unknown fact or commitment is the real hinge of the decision.", "teach": "Committee discussion tends towards breadth. Each speaker adds a consideration, and within twenty minutes the room holds a dozen of them and no way of choosing between them. The precise question collapses that space. A question that can be answered with a number, a yes or no, a name or a date moves the conversation onto ground where it can resolve. Clever rooms respect it because it does their work for them: it shows you have listened to everything and found the hinge.\n\nThe question must pass two tests. It must be answerable, by someone present or by someone who can be asked before the next meeting. And it must be decisive: whatever the answer, the room knows what to do next. Run the test silently before you speak. If the answer were yes, what follows? If no? If both answers lead to the same place, it is not the question, however sharp it sounds.\n\nAddress it to the person who can answer it, by name or role. Ask it once. Do not preface it with three sentences explaining why you are asking. Ask first; then, if the room needs it, add one sentence after the question on why its answer decides the matter. Do not attach a second question, because the double question lets the respondent choose the easier half. The question itself should ideally be under fifteen words. Its brevity is its authority.\n\nTiming is after the second circuit of discussion, when the room has felt that it is not converging, or just before the chair begins a summary that would paper over the hinge. Asked too early, the precise question can seem to cut short people who have not yet been heard.\n\nThe voice should fall at the end of the question rather than rise. A falling contour marks a question that expects an answer; a rising one sounds like wondering aloud, and invites more wondering. Then stop and hold the gaze of the person you asked. The silence that follows is part of the move, and it is uncomfortable only for the person who must answer.\n\nIt goes wrong in four ways. The question is precise but not decisive: trivia, which costs credibility. The question exposes someone publicly when it could have been asked in the corridor; a precise question about a colleague's missed deadline is a reprimand. The overloaded question, with nested clauses, which nobody can answer cleanly. And the leading question, which clever rooms detect at once and resist on principle.", "phrasings": ["Can I ask one question? Who owns this after July?", "What would it cost, in dollars, to run it for one more year?", "Is this a decision for us, or a recommendation to the principal?", "If we do nothing, what happens on the first of February?", "Does the Authority require this, or permit it?", "Has anyone asked the students?", "If we could fund only one of these, which would it be?", "Do we need to decide this today?"], "avoid": ["I suppose my question, and it is more of a comment really, is... : the preface dissolves the question before it arrives.", "Can I ask what we think about the broader implications for the program and, relatedly, whether the timeline works? : two questions, neither exact; the room will answer the vaguer one.", "Is it not the case that we would obviously lose money? : leading; it invites resistance rather than an answer.", "Just so I am really clear, and correct me if I am wrong, because I may have missed something... : the hedging buries the question and signals that it need not be answered."], "scenarios": [{"id": "the-precise-question-s1", "room": "school council", "setup": "The school council has spent forty minutes on the uniform supply arrangement. Parents have raised quality and price; staff have raised the administrative burden of changing supplier; the chair is trying to find a way to close the item. Everyone has spoken, and the positions have not moved. The business manager is present.", "said": "I think we're all agreed the current arrangement isn't ideal. The question is whether the disruption of changing is worth it. My instinct is that we go to market but take our time over it, perhaps eighteen months, so families aren't caught mid-cycle.", "task": "Ask the one factual question that determines whether the council can act at all.", "seconds": 30, "models": [{"text": "We have heard every side of this, and I think one fact governs it. Can I ask the business manager: does the current contract allow us to exit before 2028 without penalty? If it does, we can go to market on the chair's timeline. If it does not, the question answers itself for now.", "why": "It is a single, answerable question directed at the right person, and the answer determines whether the rest of the discussion is relevant."}, {"text": "One question that might settle the timing. What is the earliest date we can give notice under the existing contract? If it is next year, the eighteen-month plan works neatly. If it is later, we should be planning around that date rather than debating the principle.", "why": "It narrows the chair's own proposal to the fact that governs it, and gives the room a date to plan around."}], "checks": ["I asked one question.", "I addressed it to the person who could answer it.", "Both possible answers lead to a clear next step.", "I did not preface it with an explanation."]}, {"id": "the-precise-question-s2", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The Music syllabus committee is debating whether the revised ATAR course should include a prescribed list of set works for each context, or only list the musical elements and concepts to be studied. Teachers on the committee are split: some want certainty, some want freedom to choose repertoire. The debate has gone round twice. An Authority officer with responsibility for examination design is present.", "said": "I understand the appeal of a list, but every list dates, and it narrows what teachers can do with their particular students. If the syllabus specifies concepts clearly enough, any well-chosen work will do. I'd rather trust teachers than prescribe.", "task": "Ask the question about the examination that will determine whether teachers actually have the freedom being described.", "seconds": 35, "models": [{"text": "Could I ask the examination officer one thing? Would a question in the written paper ever require knowledge of a specific work that is not named in the syllabus? If the answer is no, teachers genuinely have the freedom we are describing. If it is sometimes, they will build their own list anyway, and we would do better to write it.", "why": "It moves the debate from philosophy to the examination, which is what teachers actually respond to, and the answer resolves whether freedom of choice is real."}, {"text": "Can I put one question to the officer? Under the concepts-only model, how would the examiners write the unseen analysis questions so that no school's choice of repertoire becomes an advantage? I ask because that is where teachers will feel the difference between the two models, far more than in the syllabus document.", "why": "It asks how the preferred model would actually work in the paper, which tests it constructively rather than opposing it."}], "checks": ["I asked about the examination, not about principle.", "I directed the question to the officer responsible.", "The answer would change what the committee decides.", "I asked it in one sentence."]}, {"id": "the-precise-question-s3", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The festival association board is choosing between two venues for next year's festival. One is cheaper but further from public transport; the other is central but more expensive. Board members have spent half an hour on acoustics, parking, staging and atmosphere. The president is ready to call for a show of hands. The events coordinator has been negotiating with both venues.", "said": "On balance I lean towards the central venue. It's more expensive, but it's a better experience for the students and it lifts the profile of the festival. I think sponsors will notice the difference.", "task": "Ask the question about the central venue that determines whether choosing it is safe.", "seconds": 30, "models": [{"text": "Before we vote, one question for the coordinator. Has the central venue confirmed our dates in writing, with a refundable deposit? If it has, I am happy to support it. If it has not, we would be choosing a venue we may not actually have, and the cheaper option becomes the safer one.", "why": "It identifies the practical condition that would make the board's preference meaningless if unmet, and says in one sentence what each answer means."}, {"text": "One question that might decide it. If we choose the central venue, what is the total cost difference once the sponsor contribution we have already been offered is taken into account? If the gap is small, the case for the central venue is strong. If it is large, the board should see the number before it votes.", "why": "It reduces the debate about atmosphere and profile to a single figure that the board can weigh honestly."}], "checks": ["I asked before the vote, not after.", "The question concerned a fact, not an opinion.", "I directed it to the coordinator.", "I did not restate the arguments already made."]}, {"id": "the-precise-question-s4", "room": "university academic committee", "setup": "A university academic committee is considering a new double degree combining music and education. The proposal has strong support from both deans. Discussion has focused on curriculum design, accreditation and student demand. Nobody has addressed what happens if enrolments are modest. A finance representative is present.", "said": "The market research is encouraging and the accreditation pathway is clear. I think the design is elegant, and it answers a real need in the state for specialist music teachers. My only concern is that we launch it well.", "task": "Ask the question about risk that the enthusiasm in the room has bypassed.", "seconds": 35, "models": [{"text": "I share the enthusiasm, and I would like the degree to survive its first few years. So, one question: which faculty carries the cost if first-year enrolments fall below the threshold for viability? If that is agreed now, a slow start is manageable. If it is not, it becomes a dispute between two deans in the second year.", "why": "It names the risk that both deans have an interest in not raising, in a single answerable question."}, {"text": "One question for finance. What is the minimum intake at which the degree covers its own teaching costs, and how does that compare with the market research? If the research comfortably clears it, we can approve with confidence. If it barely clears it, we should build in a review point.", "why": "It turns optimism into a number, which allows the committee to support the degree with its eyes open."}], "checks": ["I asked about the risk the room had avoided.", "The question had a factual answer.", "I did not oppose the degree.", "I asked it of the person who could answer it."]}, {"id": "the-precise-question-s5", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "You are meeting with the principal and the business manager about next year's staffing. The business manager has proposed reducing contemporary ensemble provision by one group. The discussion has moved between budget, timetabling and student numbers without settling. You suspect the real constraint has not been named.", "said": "I appreciate the ensembles matter to students, but we have to find savings somewhere, and the contemporary groups are the most expensive per student. I'm not trying to target music; I'm trying to balance a budget.", "task": "Ask the question that identifies whether the constraint is money or staffing allocation, because the remedies differ.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "Can I ask one thing? Is the constraint the dollar budget, or the staffing allocation? If it is the dollars, there may be other places to find them, and I would be glad to look at the music budget with you. If it is the teacher hours, that is a different conversation, and I would rather we had that one directly.", "why": "It asks a single decisive question and explains in two short sentences why the answer matters, which keeps it collaborative."}, {"text": "Is the saving you need in dollars, or in teacher hours? I ask because the two lead to quite different answers. One might be solved through charges or sponsorship; the other only through the timetable. Once I know which, I can come back to you with options rather than objections.", "why": "In the starkest form, it gives the business manager a question that can be answered directly and that frames everything after."}], "checks": ["I asked one question.", "The answer would change the solution.", "I did not argue for the ensemble before the question was answered.", "My tone was collaborative."]}, {"id": "the-precise-question-s6", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "The arts funding panel is discussing a new chamber music project from a small organisation. Members have debated whether the organisation is 'ready' for the grant size it has requested. The conversation has been about reputation, past work and the artistic director's experience. There is not enough in the pool to fund everything at the requested level.", "said": "I like the artistic vision very much, but I'm not sure they're ready for a grant of this size. It's a big step up for a small organisation, and I'd hate to see them overextend themselves.", "task": "Ask the question that turns 'readiness' into something the panel can decide.", "seconds": 35, "models": [{"text": "Could we ask a more specific question than readiness? If we funded at sixty per cent of the request, would the project still happen? If it would, we can support a strong vision at a scale that limits the risk. If it would not, we are deciding whether to back them in full, and we should say so.", "why": "It makes 'readiness' concrete and gives the panel a decision it can make within its limited pool."}, {"text": "May I ask the program officer one thing? Does the budget show a version of the project at a lower scale, or is it all or nothing? The application should tell us, and the answer would let us move from whether they are ready to what we can actually fund from this pool.", "why": "It asks a factual question about the application itself, which the panel can answer from the papers in front of them."}], "checks": ["I turned an abstract concern into a concrete question.", "The question could be answered from the application or by the officer.", "I did not dismiss the colleague's concern.", "The answer leads directly to a funding decision."]}]}, {"id": "stress-testing-with-a-scenario", "name": "Stress-testing with a scenario", "family": "Testing", "what": "It moves a proposal from the abstract into a concrete case, a counterfactual or an edge case, so the room can see how it behaves under pressure before it is adopted.", "when": "When a policy is being agreed in principle and nobody has walked it through a real instance, especially a rule about eligibility, exceptions, appeals, money or safety.", "teach": "Abstract policies are agreed easily because each member imagines the typical case, and each imagines a slightly different one. A scenario makes everyone imagine the same case, and usually an untypical one. It is how law, medicine and engineering test their rules: the hard case, the hypothetical, the load at the limit. Highly educated audiences recognise the method and respect it. It is also face-saving. You are not attacking the proposal; you are offering it a trial run, and if it passes, you have strengthened it.\n\nChoose a case that is plausible, specific and brief. Use roles, not people; a date; a number. 'Say a Year 11 student transfers in Term 2 with Grade 7 performance and no portfolio.' Then ask the room, not yourself, what the policy does with that student, and let them run it. If the proposal handles the case, say so plainly. If it does not, offer a drafting fix or ask the proposer for one.\n\nThe best scenario is the most likely difficult case, not the most extreme. The extreme case lets everyone say that will never happen, and the move is lost. Two kinds of case are especially productive. The boundary case: the student who scores exactly the threshold, or one mark either side. And the conflict case: two rules that point in different directions, so the room must decide which wins. A third is the backward test: if this rule had been in place last year, what would we have done in the situation we all remember?\n\nTiming is once in-principle support is forming and before the wording is finalised. Earlier, the scenario seems premature; later, it seems obstructive. Put the scenario in two sentences and the question in one.\n\nThis is the one Testing move that permits narrative pace. Slow slightly, as you would when telling a story, so the room can picture it. Lean in a little; the hands can sketch the case. Then hand it over with the question and wait.\n\nIt goes wrong in four ways. The parade of hypotheticals: one, perhaps two, and more makes you look as though you are obstructing. The lurid edge case, the litigation or the injury, which raises anxiety without informing the drafting. The scenario that is really a disguised anecdote about an identifiable student or colleague, which breaches confidentiality and makes the debate personal. And answering the scenario yourself before the room has tried, which turns a test into a speech.", "phrasings": ["Could we walk it through one case?", "Say it is the night before the festival and the adjudicator withdraws. Under this policy, who decides?", "What happens to the student who scores exactly sixty-five?", "Imagine it is 2029 and enrolments have halved. Does this still work?", "Let me give it the hardest likely case rather than the hardest possible one.", "Suppose both conditions apply at once. Which one prevails?", "If this had been in place last year, what would we have done differently in Term 3?", "I think it passes that test, which is reassuring. May I try one more?"], "avoid": ["What if a meteor hits the concert hall? : an absurd case trivialises the method and the proposer will rightly ignore it.", "Well, what if this, and what if that, and also what if... : a parade of cases reads as obstruction, and none gets examined properly.", "What about the girl in 9B whose father wrote to us last week? : identifies an individual, breaches confidentiality, and makes the policy debate personal.", "I can tell you exactly what will happen: chaos. : predicts instead of testing, and asks the room to trust your pessimism over their own reasoning."], "scenarios": [{"id": "stress-testing-with-a-scenario-s1", "room": "school council", "setup": "The school council is reviewing a draft policy on academic standing for students in the selective specialist music program. The draft says that any program student who receives a grade below C in two or more subjects in a semester will be placed on review, with possible exit from the program. The intention is to protect students' broader achievement. In-principle support is forming.", "said": "I think this strikes the right balance. It's clear, it's fair, it applies to everyone equally, and it sends the message that the specialist program isn't an excuse to neglect other subjects. I'd be happy to endorse it.", "task": "Test the policy against a plausible difficult case, such as a student with a documented illness, before it is endorsed.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "I support the intent. Can I walk it through one case? Say a Year 8 student has six weeks off in Term 2 with a documented illness, and comes back with two D grades because of missed assessments, while still achieving an A in Music. Under the draft, she goes on review. Is that what we want? If not, we may need a clause about documented absence.", "why": "It offers a specific, plausible case, lets the council see the consequence, and suggests the drafting fix."}, {"text": "One test, if I may. If this had been in place last year, how many students would have gone on review, and would we have wanted to review all of them? If the business manager or the deputy could check the numbers, we would know whether the threshold is in the right place.", "why": "It uses the backward test with real data, which grounds the policy without any hypothetical at all."}], "checks": ["I gave one scenario, not several.", "The case was plausible, not extreme.", "I asked the council what the policy would do.", "I suggested a fix rather than opposing the policy."]}, {"id": "stress-testing-with-a-scenario-s2", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The festival association board is considering a new eligibility rule: every ensemble must consist of at least eighty per cent students enrolled at the entering school. The aim is to stop schools bringing in outside players to strengthen their bands. Several board members have experienced the problem and the rule is popular. It would take effect for next year's festival.", "said": "This is overdue. We've all seen bands turn up with three ring-ins from the local university, and it's unfair on schools that play by the rules. Eighty per cent is generous. I'd like to move that we adopt it.", "task": "Test the rule against a legitimate arrangement it might accidentally exclude, such as a combined small-school ensemble.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "I support the principle. Let me give it the hardest likely case. Two small regional schools combine to make one viable big band, because neither has enough players alone. Each contributes about half the band. Under this rule, neither school can enter. Is that the outcome we intend? If not, a clause recognising registered combined ensembles would solve it.", "why": "It identifies a real, sympathetic case the rule would catch, and offers a precise drafting solution."}, {"text": "One case before we vote. A student leaves the school in Term 2 but has rehearsed with the band all year, and the festival is in August. Does she count towards the eighty per cent? I suspect the answer should be yes, but the draft does not say.", "why": "It tests a boundary of the rule's definition and shows that the wording needs one more line."}], "checks": ["I supported the principle first.", "The scenario was a legitimate arrangement, not an abuse.", "I proposed a fix to the wording.", "I kept to one case."]}, {"id": "stress-testing-with-a-scenario-s3", "room": "SCSA assessment committee", "setup": "An assessment committee is considering allowing ATAR practical examination candidates to submit a video recording in place of a live examination, to reduce travel and improve access. The proposal includes broad guidelines on recording conditions. Members are broadly in favour, especially those representing regional schools. The recommendation will go forward if the committee endorses it today.", "said": "This is a clear gain for access and I think the guidelines are adequate. Candidates record in a supervised session, the school verifies it, and the examiners mark the recording. It is simpler for everyone.", "task": "Test the proposal against a case that exposes an equity or integrity problem the guidelines have not addressed.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I can see the access benefit. Can we test it against one case? One candidate records in a single supervised take in a school hall. Another records in a studio, with a teacher present, and selects the best of fifteen takes. Both are verified by their schools. Would the guidelines treat those as equivalent? If not, we may need to specify the number of takes and the conditions.", "why": "It pairs two plausible cases to show the gap in the guidelines, and the solution follows naturally."}, {"text": "Suppose the recording fails, or the file is corrupted, after the supervised session. Under the guidelines, does the candidate re-record, sit a live examination, or receive a mark based on the school assessment? We should decide that now rather than in November, when a candidate and their family are waiting for an answer.", "why": "It tests the failure case, which is likely and which nobody had considered, and gives the reason for deciding now."}], "checks": ["I acknowledged the access benefit.", "The scenario was plausible.", "I asked what the guidelines would do.", "I indicated where the guidelines needed strengthening."]}, {"id": "stress-testing-with-a-scenario-s4", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "The academic board is considering a new quality rule: any unit with a failure rate above twenty-five per cent will automatically trigger a formal review. The rule is designed to identify units where teaching or assessment design is failing students. It applies to all faculties, including the conservatorium. The board is close to endorsing it.", "said": "It's a simple, transparent trigger. It doesn't presume fault; it just ensures that we look. Twenty-five per cent is a reasonable line, and it gives us a consistent approach across the institution.", "task": "Test the rule against a small-enrolment unit to show how the threshold behaves.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "Can I try it on a small unit? Take an advanced composition seminar with eight students. Two fail, perhaps for reasons unrelated to the teaching. That is exactly twenty-five per cent, and a third failure would trigger a formal review. Does the rule need a minimum enrolment, or a requirement that the rate be sustained over two offerings?", "why": "It shows how a sensible threshold misbehaves at small numbers, and suggests two possible fixes."}, {"text": "Imagine the rule applied to last year's results. Do we know how many units would have triggered review, and how many of those were under fifteen students? If the small units dominate, the rule will spend its effort in the wrong place.", "why": "It applies the backward test, asking for data that would tell the board whether the threshold targets what it intends."}], "checks": ["I tested the rule rather than opposing it.", "The case was specific and realistic.", "I proposed a fix.", "I spoke for under a minute."]}, {"id": "stress-testing-with-a-scenario-s5", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "The panel is reviewing new guidelines for a multi-year organisational funding round. One proposed condition is that at least half of each funded organisation's activity must take place in regional Western Australia. The policy intent is to address the concentration of funded activity in Perth. Panel members broadly support it.", "said": "Regional access has been a priority for years and nothing has changed. A clear percentage is the only way to make organisations take it seriously. I'd keep it simple: fifty per cent, measured by activity.", "task": "Test what 'activity' means against a case where an organisation reaches regional audiences differently.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "I support the intent. Can we test it on one case? A chamber ensemble streams its full season live to regional schools and runs workshops online, reaching more regional students than it could by touring. Is that regional activity under the guideline? If yes, we should say so. If no, we may be rewarding travel rather than reach.", "why": "It forces the panel to define the key term by putting a plausible case to it, and states both consequences."}, {"text": "Suppose an organisation tours regionally twice a year but every concert is in a regional city of more than thirty thousand people. Is that the regional access we are trying to fund? If not, we may need to distinguish regional centres from remote communities.", "why": "It tests a boundary within the definition of 'regional' and shows the guideline needs one more distinction."}], "checks": ["I supported the policy intent.", "My scenario tested a term in the guideline.", "I stated what follows from each answer.", "I chose a likely case, not an extreme one."]}, {"id": "stress-testing-with-a-scenario-s6", "room": "department meeting", "setup": "You are chairing a music department meeting about the instrumental tuition timetable. A rotating schedule is proposed so that no student misses the same academic class more than twice a term for lessons. Instrumental teachers favour it; classroom teachers have long asked for it. The deputy principal is attending.", "said": "This is the fairest system we've looked at. It spreads the disruption evenly, classroom teachers can plan around it, and parents will understand it. I think we can adopt it from Term 1.", "task": "Test the schedule against a case where two needs collide, such as a Year 12 student with an assessment in a lesson slot.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "I think it is the right model. Let me test it against one collision. A Year 12 student's tuition rotates into a period where she has an ATAR chemistry test. Does the lesson move, or does she miss it? If the lesson moves, who arranges it, and does that count as one of her two? Settling that now will save the instrumental staff a great deal of negotiation in Term 3.", "why": "It finds the conflict case the system has not addressed and frames the solution as a benefit to the staff who favour it."}, {"text": "Can we run it backwards against last year? If the instrumental staff take one week of last year's timetable and apply the rotation, we would see quickly where it breaks, particularly for Year 12. I would rather discover that in a spreadsheet than in March.", "why": "It proposes a practical test using real data, which tests the scheme without arguing about it."}], "checks": ["I supported the model before testing it.", "My scenario involved a genuine collision of rules.", "I asked who would decide.", "I proposed a practical way to test it."]}]}, {"id": "exposing-a-conflation-or-false-choice", "name": "Conflations and false choices", "family": "Testing", "what": "It separates two ideas the room is treating as one, or opens a further option in a choice presented as binary, so the decision can be made on its actual terms.", "when": "When a debate has hardened into two camps and each seems right about something different, or when one word is carrying two meanings and the disagreement depends on it.", "teach": "Much committee deadlock is not disagreement but equivocation. 'Standards' means rigour to one speaker and standardisation to another. 'Access' means entry to one and affordability to another. 'Quality' means outcome to one and reputation to another. Or the deadlock comes from an artificial binary: keep it or cut it, when the real options include keeping it in a different form, deferring, piloting or sharing it. Separating the terms often dissolves half the disagreement and reveals the real one, which is usually smaller and easier to decide.\n\nThe move lands strongly with highly educated audiences because conceptual clarity is their trade. Done well, it reads as intelligence rather than opposition, and it gives both camps something: each turns out to be right about one of the separated things. That is why it is one of the most effective ways for a member to change a room's direction without appearing to take a side.\n\nExecution has three parts. Name the two meanings or the two questions, anchoring each in a concrete example rather than a label. Give each camp its term fairly, so neither feels caricatured. Then ask which one the decision actually turns on, or propose the specific further option. With a false choice, do not merely announce that there is a middle way; specify it, or ask what prevents it. 'What would stop us doing both, in sequence?' is a better contribution than 'surely there is a compromise'.\n\nTiming is after both positions have been clearly stated and the room has felt the deadlock. Too early, and the separation seems pedantic, because nobody yet feels the confusion it resolves. The contribution should take under a minute.\n\nThe voice is clarifying and even-handed. Look at each camp in turn as you name its term. A slight warmth helps; this is the move that lets people climb down without losing face, and the tone should make that easy. Use 'we': we seem to be using this word in two ways. Never 'you'.\n\nIt goes wrong in four ways. Pedantry: separating terms the decision does not depend on, which wastes the room's patience. The further option that is merely compromise without principle, splitting the difference so that nobody gets what they need. Implying that people have been confused, which insults them; the conflation should be presented as natural and shared. And the false false choice: sometimes the binary is real, a budget either is or is not approved, and insisting otherwise looks evasive.", "phrasings": ["I wonder whether we are using 'standards' in two senses.", "I think both sides are right, about different things.", "Is the choice really between cutting it and keeping it as it is? There seems to be a third shape it could take.", "We seem to be treating access and affordability as the same question. They have different remedies.", "Could we separate whether we should do this from whether we can afford it this year?", "Two questions are travelling together here. Could we take them one at a time?", "What would stop us doing both, in sequence?", "If we split the motion, I suspect we could agree on the first half today."], "avoid": ["You are conflating two completely different things. : it accuses, and 'you' places a person in the dock instead of the idea.", "There is always a middle ground. : a platitude that offers no specific option and suggests the speaker has not thought one through.", "It depends what you mean by 'quality', does it not? : a seminar-room reflex that gestures at the distinction without stating it.", "Well, technically, semantically speaking... : a pedantic register that signals point-scoring rather than clarification."], "scenarios": [{"id": "exposing-a-conflation-or-false-choice-s1", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The committee is reviewing the General Music course. One group argues the course has become too demanding for the students who take it; another argues that lowering demands would erode its standing. The debate has been framed as rigour against accessibility and has gone round several times. The Authority officer wants a recommendation today.", "said": "I'm not prepared to dilute the course. If we make it accessible by making it easier, we confirm every prejudice about General courses being second-class. Rigour is the whole point. Students rise to what we expect of them.", "task": "Separate the rigour of the content from the difficulty of the assessment format, so the committee can agree on both.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I wonder whether we are using 'rigour' in two senses. One is the depth of the musical thinking we expect, which I think everyone here wants to keep. The other is the format of assessment: extended written analysis under timed conditions, which is where many General students actually struggle. We could keep the musical demand and change how students show it, through annotated recordings or practical demonstrations. Would that satisfy both concerns?", "why": "It separates the two meanings concretely, gives each side what it needs, and proposes a specific option rather than a compromise."}, {"text": "I think both sides are right, about different things. Those defending rigour are talking about what students understand. Those raising accessibility are talking about the barrier of written expression. Could we take those as separate questions: first, what musical understanding the course requires; then, which assessment forms best reveal it?", "why": "It reframes the deadlock as two sequential questions, giving the committee a process it can follow today."}], "checks": ["I named both meanings concretely.", "I used 'we', not 'you'.", "Each side could recognise its own concern.", "I proposed a specific option or a sequence."]}, {"id": "exposing-a-conflation-or-false-choice-s2", "room": "school council", "setup": "The school council is discussing priorities for the school's business plan. A parent member argues that the proposed refurbishment of the music performance space should be deferred in favour of additional wellbeing staffing. Staff members disagree. The discussion has become a contest between the arts and student wellbeing.", "said": "I'm sorry, but I can't support a performance space when we have students waiting weeks to see a psychologist. It's a question of priorities. Facilities can wait; children's mental health can't.", "task": "Show that the choice depends on two different funding sources, without diminishing the wellbeing concern.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "The wellbeing concern is serious, and I would support pressing for more staffing. Can I check whether these are actually competing? The refurbishment is drawing on capital funding, which as I understand it cannot be spent on salaries. If that is right, deferring the refurbishment would not fund a single extra hour of psychology. Could the business manager confirm? If so, we can pursue both.", "why": "It affirms the concern, identifies the factual basis of the false choice, and asks for confirmation rather than asserting it."}, {"text": "I think two questions are travelling together. One is whether the school should put more into wellbeing, and I suspect we all agree it should. The other is whether this particular refurbishment should go ahead. If the funding sources are separate, could we take those as two items, and make a clear recommendation on wellbeing staffing today?", "why": "It separates the issues and gives the parent member an immediate outcome on the matter they care about most."}], "checks": ["I took the wellbeing concern seriously.", "I identified the factual basis for separating the issues.", "I asked for the fact to be confirmed.", "I avoided setting music against wellbeing."]}, {"id": "exposing-a-conflation-or-false-choice-s3", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The festival association board is debating the future of its competitive format. One faction wants to keep placings and awards; another wants a purely educational, non-competitive festival. Sponsors, tradition and educational philosophy are all in play. The board needs a direction before planning begins.", "said": "We have to decide what this festival is for. Either it's a competition, with all the drive and excellence that brings, or it's an educational event, where every band gets support. I don't think we can be both, and I think the fudge is what's been hurting us.", "task": "Separate adjudication from ranking, so the board can see that feedback and placings are distinct choices.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "I agree we should be clear about what the festival is for. I wonder whether we are bundling two things: adjudication, meaning expert feedback on every band, and ranking, meaning placings. Most teachers value the first enormously. The disagreement is really about the second. We could keep rigorous adjudication for everyone and offer placings only in sections where schools opt in. That is not a fudge; it is two distinct choices.", "why": "It accepts the speaker's call for clarity, separates the two elements precisely, and proposes a principled structure."}, {"text": "Is the choice really between competition and education? Many of the most educational festivals I know use adjudication against a published standard, gold, silver, bronze, rather than ranking bands against each other. That keeps the drive for excellence without anyone coming last. Could we look at that model?", "why": "It names a specific alternative that preserves what each faction values, and asks the board to consider it."}], "checks": ["I agreed with the call for clarity.", "I separated adjudication from ranking.", "I offered a principled alternative, not a compromise.", "I made it easy for both factions to agree."]}, {"id": "exposing-a-conflation-or-false-choice-s4", "room": "faculty curriculum committee", "setup": "A university faculty curriculum committee is revising its jazz studies major. A senior academic argues the program should be anchored in the historical canon; a younger lecturer argues it should focus on contemporary practice. The debate has become generational and slightly heated. You are a practitioner member.", "said": "If we abandon the canon, we produce graduates who can't swing, can't play a standard and don't know where the music came from. Contemporary practice is built on that foundation. You can't skip to the end.", "task": "Separate the canon as repertoire from the canon as a method of transmission, opening an option both sides could accept.", "seconds": 65, "models": [{"text": "I think 'the canon' is doing two jobs in this conversation. One is a body of repertoire: the standards, the recordings. The other is a way of learning: transcription, imitation, playing in the tradition until you have a voice. Contemporary players learn that way too. We could keep the method as non-negotiable and broaden the repertoire it is applied to. Would that address the concern about foundations?", "why": "It draws a distinction a practitioner would recognise, gives the senior academic his core concern, and lets the younger lecturer broaden the material."}, {"text": "Is it really a choice between the canon and contemporary practice? Every contemporary player I respect learned the language first and then left it deliberately. Could we sequence it: the tradition as the foundation in first and second year, contemporary practice as the focus of third year? That seems to me what the music itself did.", "why": "It turns the binary into a sequence, supported by a practitioner's observation, and offers a concrete structure."}], "checks": ["I identified two senses of 'canon'.", "Both colleagues could see their concern addressed.", "I offered a specific structure.", "I lowered the temperature of the exchange."]}, {"id": "exposing-a-conflation-or-false-choice-s5", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "The principal has asked for a conversation about the selective audition for the specialist music program. A board member has raised concerns that selective entry is elitist. The principal is weighing whether to recommend removing the audition. You run the audition process and believe it can be defended, but you also take the equity concern seriously.", "said": "I'm caught between two things. If we keep the audition, we're open to the charge that we're selecting children whose parents can afford private lessons. If we drop it, we probably lose what makes the program distinctive. I don't see a way through.", "task": "Separate selection for aptitude from selection for prior tuition, and show what that makes possible.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think there is a way through, because those are two different things. Selecting for prior tuition does favour families who can pay. Selecting for aptitude, such as pitch, rhythm and musical memory, does not, because those can be tested without an instrument. We could weight the audition more heavily towards aptitude, so that a child who has never had a lesson can still gain a place. That answers the equity concern and keeps the program selective.", "why": "It separates the two meanings of 'selection' and turns the principal's dilemma into a specific reform she could take to the board."}, {"text": "Can I suggest that the question is not whether we select, but what we select for? If the concern is that we reward prior tuition, we could check that directly: how many of our students last year had no private lessons before entry? If the number is reasonable, we have an answer for the board. If it is low, we know what to change.", "why": "It reframes the binary as a question about criteria and proposes a factual test the principal can use."}], "checks": ["I distinguished aptitude from prior tuition.", "I took the equity concern seriously.", "I offered a specific option or test.", "I did not become defensive about the program."]}, {"id": "exposing-a-conflation-or-false-choice-s6", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "The arts funding panel is assessing an application from a community choir that wants to commission and perform new Western Australian works. One panel member argues it belongs in the community category; another argues it meets the excellence criterion. The category determines which pool the application draws on. The panel is unable to agree.", "said": "This is lovely work, but it's community work. These are amateur singers. The excellence stream is for professional artists working at a professional standard. If we blur that line, every community group will apply for it.", "task": "Separate a professional standard of work from the professional status of the participants, and ask which the guideline requires.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "I think the guideline may be ambiguous here. It refers to a professional standard of work, which is about the outcome. The concern raised is about professional status, which is about who is performing. Those are not the same. The composers, the conductor and the commissioning are all professional. Could we ask the program officer which reading the guideline intends? That would settle it for this and future applications.", "why": "It separates two meanings in the guideline itself and asks for an authoritative reading, which resolves the deadlock beyond this case."}, {"text": "I wonder whether we are asking one question when there are two. Is the work excellent? And are the performers professional? If the guideline only requires the first, the application belongs in excellence. If it requires both, we should say so explicitly in the next round, because I suspect other panels have read it differently.", "why": "It names the two questions plainly and makes a constructive recommendation about the guideline for future rounds."}], "checks": ["I located the conflation in the guideline itself.", "I separated outcome from status.", "I asked for an authoritative reading.", "I did not imply the colleague had misread the guideline."]}]}, {"id": "building-on-a-colleague", "name": "Building on a colleague", "family": "Building", "what": "It credits a colleague's point exactly enough that they recognise it as theirs, then carries it one step further than they took it, so the idea gains weight and you gain an ally.", "when": "When someone has said something right but unfinished, and the room is about to move past it; or when your own point will travel further attached to a respected colleague's than standing alone.", "teach": "The move has two halves, and most people only perform the first. The credit must be precise: not 'as Margaret said', but the specific claim, in a phrase close to her own words, so that she nods and the room hears the idea a second time. Precision is what separates a genuine build from a social courtesy. Clever people notice when they have been paraphrased into something softer, and they notice equally when someone has actually listened. The extension is the second half, and it must be a real step: a consequence she did not draw, a case it explains, a mechanism it implies, or a decision it makes possible. If you only restate, you have spent the room's time agreeing.\n\nWith highly educated audiences this works because it honours the norms of scholarship. Attribution is the currency of the academy; a contribution that cites before it extends reads as rigorous rather than competitive. It also changes the arithmetic of the meeting. A point made by one person is an opinion; a point made by one and developed by another is a position, and the chair starts to treat it as such.\n\nSenior practitioners keep the credit to one sentence and spend the rest on the extension. They name the colleague once, look at her briefly as they do it, then turn to the chair or the room for the new step, which signals that the idea now belongs to the meeting. The verb matters: 'Margaret's point about moderation load implies...' is stronger than 'I agree with Margaret, and...'. Aim for thirty to sixty seconds. The voice should drop slightly and slow on the extension, because that is the part you want minuted.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways. First, the credit becomes a hijack: you name her and then make an unrelated point of your own, and she feels used. Second, the extension overreaches, taking her somewhere she would not go, so she has to correct you publicly; if in doubt, offer the step as a question to her ('Would it follow that...?'). Third, inflation: 'Margaret's brilliant point' sounds like flattery and makes the room discount both of you. Plain, exact attribution is the compliment.", "phrasings": ["Professor Hale put her finger on something a moment ago: that the problem is not the rubric but who moderates it. If that's right, it changes where the money should go.", "I want to stay with Ian's point about the timing of the second sitting, because I think it has a consequence we haven't drawn yet.", "If I've understood Sarah correctly, the risk is less the cost than the precedent. Taking that one step further:", "Building on what Dr Ng said about load, there's a second implication for the smaller schools.", "David's distinction between access and attainment seems to me the most useful thing said this morning. What it lets us do is separate the two recommendations.", "Can I test whether Anna's argument extends to the regional cohort? Because if it does, it answers the question we parked earlier.", "That's exactly the right test, and I think it cuts further than Peter suggested.", "I'd like to put Helen's observation to work rather than just agree with it."], "avoid": ["As everyone has said, I totally agree with that : it credits no one in particular and adds nothing, so it reads as filler.", "To echo Margaret's absolutely brilliant point : the inflation sounds like flattery and makes the room discount the point and you.", "Margaret mentioned assessment, which reminds me of something quite different : it uses her as a doorway to a tangent, and she will feel hijacked.", "What Margaret is really trying to say is : it implies she said it badly and invites her to correct you in public."], "scenarios": [{"id": "building-on-a-colleague-s1", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The committee is reviewing the draft Music ATAR course, specifically the weighting of the aural and analysis examination against the practical. A university musicologist has raised a point about construct validity that the chair acknowledged and then moved past. You think her point is the key to the weighting question and do not want it lost.", "said": "\"I'd only observe that the aural paper as currently designed tests recognition of conventions far more than it tests listening. Those are related, but they aren't the same construct, and the weighting assumes they are. Anyway, I'll leave that with the committee.\"", "task": "Pick up her construct point precisely and show what it implies for the weighting decision now on the table.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Before we move on, I'd like to stay with Dr Albrecht's distinction between recognising conventions and actually listening, because I think it settles the weighting question. If the paper mostly tests recognition, then raising its weight rewards students who have memorised style markers, which is not what the rationale says the course values. The consequence would be to fix the paper's design first and set the weighting after, rather than the other way round.", "why": "It restates her distinction in her terms, then draws a procedural consequence she did not draw, which converts a passing remark into a sequencing decision."}, {"text": "Chair, may I take Dr Albrecht's point one step further? She's said the paper conflates two constructs. In my experience marking it, the practical has the mirror problem: it claims to assess interpretation, but students lose most marks on technical accuracy. So both components are drifting from what they claim to measure. I'd suggest we ask the writers to state the construct for each component before we argue about the split between them.", "why": "It extends her point by symmetry into the component she did not address, bringing his practitioner knowledge in under her academic authority."}], "checks": ["Did I restate her claim in words close enough to her own that she would recognise it?", "Is my extension a genuine consequence, not a restatement?", "Did I keep the credit to one sentence?", "Did I turn to the chair, not to her, for the new step?"]}, {"id": "building-on-a-colleague-s2", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The association board is discussing whether to move the schools' jazz festival from its traditional venue to a larger one. The secretary, a retired accountant who rarely speaks on artistic matters, has made a remark about volunteer numbers. The president is keen on the bigger venue. You think the secretary's point is the real constraint.", "said": "\"I'm not across the artistic side, so take this for what it's worth. But on the volunteer roster, we have barely managed the current venue the last two years. A second stage would need perhaps another fifteen people we don't have.\"", "task": "Give the secretary's point the weight it deserves and extend it into a condition the board can actually use.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "I don't think Robyn should discount that at all; I think it's the decisive point. If we need fifteen more volunteers to run a second stage, the bigger venue isn't an artistic decision, it's a workforce one. So perhaps the motion should be conditional: we move only if we can name those fifteen people by the end of Term 1, and we ask each member school to commit two.", "why": "It refuses her self-deprecation on her behalf, reframes the decision through her point, and turns it into a conditional motion with a deadline."}, {"text": "Robyn's figure deserves a second look, because it cuts deeper than the roster. Fifteen more volunteers is also fifteen more Working with Children checks, more inductions, more people to supervise on the day. That's a governance load on this board, not just a staffing gap. I'd like us to cost that properly before we vote on the venue.", "why": "It extends the point into a consequence (compliance and supervision) she did not raise but that follows directly from it."}], "checks": ["Did I treat her contribution as serious rather than kind?", "Did I use her figure, not a vaguer version of it?", "Did I offer the board something it can decide?", "Did I avoid making it a contest with the president?"]}, {"id": "building-on-a-colleague-s3", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "You sit on a university academic committee as an external member from the schools sector. The committee is debating whether to keep an audition requirement for entry to the Bachelor of Music. A deputy dean has argued that the audition is a barrier for students from schools without specialist programs. A colleague from education has made a brief point that the deputy dean has not picked up.", "said": "\"The audition doesn't so much measure talent as measure access to tuition. Students who've had eight years of private lessons look talented at eighteen. That's not a moral point, it's a measurement point.\"", "task": "Credit the measurement framing precisely and extend it into a design option rather than a yes or no.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "Professor Okafor's phrase, that it's a measurement point rather than a moral one, is what moves this forward. If the audition measures accumulated tuition, then the question isn't whether to keep it but what else it should measure. From the schools side, rate of learning is visible in a workshop format: give an applicant a short unfamiliar passage, teach it for ten minutes, see what they do with it. That measures potential rather than history.", "why": "It quotes her framing and converts it into a concrete alternative design that only a practitioner would know is workable."}, {"text": "I'd like to follow Professor Okafor's logic through, because it has an awkward implication for us. If the audition rewards access to tuition, then removing it doesn't remove the advantage, it just moves it to first-year attrition, where students without that history fall behind. So the honest choice is between selecting on history at entry or supporting the gap after it. Either is defensible, but we should choose knowingly.", "why": "It extends her argument to a consequence that complicates the deputy dean's position, keeping the credit with her while sharpening the real decision."}], "checks": ["Did I quote or closely echo her phrasing?", "Did my extension go somewhere she would accept?", "Did I offer an option or a sharper choice, not just agreement?", "Was it under a minute?"]}, {"id": "building-on-a-colleague-s4", "room": "school council", "setup": "You chair the school council. A parent representative with a background in public health has made a point about student wellbeing during the camp program. A staff member has responded defensively. As chair you want to give the parent's point its due without taking sides against the staff member.", "said": "\"I'm not criticising the camp. My concern is narrower: we evaluate it on whether the activities ran, not on whether the students who were most anxious beforehand came back better or worse. We don't know, because we don't ask.\"", "task": "As chair, credit the precise concern and extend it into something the council can request without implying blame.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "Thank you, Fiona. I think the distinction you're drawing is between evaluating delivery and evaluating effect, and that's a fair one for a council to raise. It also gives us a way forward that doesn't question anyone's work: we could ask the school to add a short pre and post check for the next camp and report back. Mr Davies, would that be feasible?", "why": "It names her distinction cleanly, separates it from criticism, and hands the staff member a role in the solution."}, {"text": "Fiona's point is about what we measure, not how well the camp was run, and I think Mr Davies and Fiona actually agree on the second. If I take Fiona's point one step further, it applies to most of our programs: we report activity, not effect. Perhaps the council could ask for that across the board next year, so camp isn't singled out.", "why": "It generalises her point to the whole program, which protects the staff member and raises the council's oversight to a policy level."}], "checks": ["Did I state her concern without amplifying it into criticism?", "Did I keep the chair's neutrality?", "Is the extension something the council has authority to ask for?", "Did I bring the staff member back in?"]}, {"id": "building-on-a-colleague-s5", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "You are on a state arts funding panel assessing applications for youth music programs. Another assessor, a composer, has made a sharp observation about one application's budget. The panel convenor is inclined to rank it highly on artistic merit. You think the composer has spotted something important but stated it too briefly to register.", "said": "\"The artistic case is strong, I don't dispute that. But look at the budget: almost nothing goes to the participating musicians. The artists are paid, the venue is paid, the young people are the product.\"", "task": "Make the composer's point land with the convenor by giving it a precise extension tied to the assessment criteria.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "I think Tom's last line, that the young people are the product, is worth the panel pausing on. Our third criterion is benefit to participants, and on that criterion the budget is the evidence. It shows no tuition, no travel support, no recording for the students to keep. So I'd separate the scores: high on artistic merit, as Tom says, but materially lower on participant benefit. That's where the ranking should move.", "why": "It anchors his remark to the panel's own criteria and translates it into a specific scoring adjustment."}, {"text": "Can I extend Tom's point with a question to the applicant rather than a lower score? If the budget really sends almost nothing to the participants, the program could be strong and still exploit them. We could fund conditionally, asking them to show how ten per cent of the grant reaches the young musicians directly. That keeps the artistic strength Tom acknowledged and fixes what he identified.", "why": "It credits both halves of his comment and extends it into a funding condition, which gives the convenor a way to agree without reversing."}], "checks": ["Did I keep his sharpest phrase?", "Did I tie the extension to the panel's criteria or powers?", "Did I propose a specific consequence for the ranking or the grant?", "Did I avoid turning it into a disagreement with the convenor?"]}, {"id": "building-on-a-colleague-s6", "room": "department meeting", "setup": "You run the music department meeting. A younger instrumental teacher has, somewhat tentatively, suggested that sectional rehearsals be scheduled by repertoire need rather than by fixed weekly slot. Two senior staff have reacted coolly. You think the idea is right and want it to survive.", "said": "\"It's probably not practical, but I just wonder if we're holding sectionals because it's Thursday, not because the brass actually need them that week. Some weeks they're fine and the woodwinds are drowning.\"", "task": "Give her idea authorship and standing, and extend it into something trialable that addresses the senior staff's likely objection.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "I don't think it's impractical, Emma, I think it's the right question: scheduling by need rather than by habit. What would make it workable is a simple rule. Each conductor flags the sections they need by Monday, and Thursday's rooms are allocated from that. Let's trial it for the five weeks before the concert and see whether the rehearsals are better.", "why": "It restates her principle in a crisp phrase, removes her hedge, and adds the mechanism that answers the practicality concern."}, {"text": "Emma's put her finger on something: the slot is fixed but the need moves. I'd add that it moves predictably, because we know the concert program weeks in advance. So we could plan sectionals from the repertoire backwards at the start of term, rather than week by week. Would that address your concern, Greg, about tutors needing notice?", "why": "It extends her insight with a planning mechanism that also meets the senior teacher's objection directly, inviting him into it."}], "checks": ["Did I remove her hedge without patronising her?", "Did I keep her as the author of the idea?", "Does the extension answer the objection likely in the room?", "Is there a concrete next step?"]}]}, {"id": "the-synthesis", "name": "The synthesis", "family": "Building", "what": "It gathers the threads of a sprawling discussion into a single position that most people can recognise their own contribution in, and offers it to the room as something to adopt.", "when": "When the discussion has produced several good points that are being treated as competing, energy is dropping, and nobody has yet said what the room actually thinks.", "teach": "A synthesis is not a summary. A summary lists what was said; a synthesis shows how the points fit together and what follows from them. The test is whether it contains a claim that no single speaker made, but that every speaker can accept. That is why it carries authority: the person who synthesises is seen to have understood the whole discussion, and in committees that is very close to being seen as its leader, whatever the formal chair.\n\nThe mechanism works with clever audiences because they rarely disagree as much as their language suggests. Academics and senior officials argue in distinctions, and two people defending different distinctions are often defending compatible halves of one position. The synthesis names the underlying agreement and puts the real disagreement, if any remains, into a much smaller box. It also relieves the room, because intelligent people tire of circling and are grateful to whoever lets them land.\n\nSenior practitioners build it in three beats. First, a signpost that claims the floor for a different kind of contribution: 'May I try to pull this together?'. Second, the threads, named briefly with their owners, usually three, never more than four. Third, the position, stated as a single sentence that could be minuted, followed by an explicit offer: 'Is that where we are?'. The offer is essential, because it converts your synthesis into the room's. Keep it under seventy-five seconds; a long synthesis becomes another speech. Speak a little more slowly than in debate, sit back slightly rather than forward, and look around the table as you name each thread so that each owner sees themselves included.\n\nTiming is the craft. Too early and you flatten a discussion that still needs to happen; people will resent being summarised before they have spoken. Too late and the chair has already done it, or the room has fractured. The right moment is usually the second time a point is repeated: repetition means the room has run out of new material.\n\nIt fails in three ways. The false synthesis smuggles your own view in as the consensus, and sharp people notice at once. The lowest common denominator produces something everyone can accept because it commits nobody to anything. And the synthesis that leaves someone out, particularly a dissenter, which reads as an attempt to bury them. Name the remaining disagreement honestly; it makes the rest more credible.", "phrasings": ["May I try to pull this together, because I think we're closer than it sounds.", "I've heard three things this morning, and I don't think they're in tension.", "Let me see whether this captures where the room is, and people can tell me where I've got it wrong.", "It seems to me the disagreement has narrowed to one question, and everything else we broadly share.", "If I put Professor Hale's concern and Ian's together, what we have is not two positions but one condition.", "Could I offer a form of words that I think most of us could live with?", "Underneath the different vocabularies, I think we agree on the principle and differ only on the timing.", "So the position might be this: we support the change, on two conditions, and we review it in a year. Is that where we are?"], "avoid": ["So basically everyone agrees with me, then : it disguises your own view as the consensus and will be resisted.", "To summarise what everyone has said in order : that is minutes, not synthesis, and it adds no claim.", "I think we all just want what's best for the students : the lowest common denominator commits nobody and ends nothing.", "Leaving aside David's objection for a moment : burying the dissenter makes the whole synthesis suspect."], "scenarios": [{"id": "the-synthesis-s1", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The committee has spent forty minutes on whether the revised Music General course should include a compulsory composition component. A university lecturer wants it compulsory; a regional principal worries about teacher capacity; an assessment officer is concerned about moderation; a teacher member wants flexibility. Positions are being restated. The chair looks at the clock.", "said": "\"I keep coming back to the same place. If composition isn't compulsory, schools without specialist staff will simply never teach it, and those students will be the ones who most need it. I don't see how optional serves equity.\"", "task": "Draw the four threads into a single position the committee could minute.", "seconds": 70, "models": [{"text": "May I try to pull this together? I think we agree on more than it sounds. Dr Reid says composition matters for equity; Mr Hart says teachers outside the metro area can't yet deliver it; Ms Lim says it must be moderated reliably; Kate wants room for local context. Those fit together as a sequence: make it compulsory, but from a later start date, with a supported task bank that solves both capacity and moderation. Is that where we are?", "why": "It names each owner, shows the points are compatible rather than competing, and offers a single minutable position with an explicit check."}, {"text": "I think the disagreement has narrowed to one question: whether compulsion comes before support or after it. Nobody has argued composition is unimportant, and nobody has argued teachers are ready now. So perhaps the recommendation is that composition be compulsory from 2029, conditional on professional learning and an exemplar bank in place by 2028. If the conditions aren't met, it comes back to us.", "why": "It reduces a four-way debate to a single question of sequence and builds the safeguard in, which addresses the sceptics without diluting the principle."}], "checks": ["Did my synthesis contain a claim no single speaker made?", "Did I name no more than four threads?", "Did I end with an explicit offer to the room?", "Did I avoid smuggling my own preference in as the consensus?"]}, {"id": "the-synthesis-s2", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The board has been discussing complaints after this year's festival: adjudication felt inconsistent, the schedule overran, and a smaller school felt sidelined in the program. Different members have taken each complaint in turn and the discussion has become a list of grievances. As treasurer you can see a pattern.", "said": "\"And the overrun isn't only a scheduling problem. When we ran forty minutes late, the adjudicators had less time per band, and the last few schools got rushed comments. I'd be upset too.\"", "task": "Show the board that the three complaints share one cause and propose a position on it.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "I think Jo has just connected the three complaints. The overrun squeezed the adjudicators, which made their comments uneven, and the schools that suffered were the ones scheduled last, which this year happened to be the smaller ones. So we don't have three problems, we have one: a schedule with no buffer. If we build in fifteen minutes every two hours, I think most of the rest takes care of itself. Can we agree that as the first fix?", "why": "It credits the member who saw the link, turns a list into a causal chain, and offers one action the board can agree before anything else."}, {"text": "Can I suggest a way of seeing all of this? Every complaint we've heard is about fairness of experience between schools. Inconsistent comments, uneven time, a small school feeling marginal. So perhaps the principle for next year is that every band gets the same conditions, and we test the schedule, the adjudication brief and the program order against that one principle before we publish.", "why": "It synthesises at the level of principle rather than cause, which gives the board a test it can apply to future decisions too."}], "checks": ["Did I turn a list of complaints into one claim?", "Did I credit whoever started the connection?", "Is the proposed position specific enough to act on?", "Did I avoid defending last year's decisions?"]}, {"id": "the-synthesis-s3", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "You are in a meeting with the principal, the associate principal and the business manager about the music program budget for next year. The business manager has argued for cutting instrumental tuition hours; the associate principal has talked about timetabling pressure; the principal has spoken about the program's reputation. Each has a point and the meeting is drifting.", "said": "\"I'm not against music, Dijon, I want to be clear about that. But I've got a deficit to close, and tuition is the biggest variable line in the faculty. If not there, where?\"", "task": "Bring the three perspectives together into a proposition the principal can decide on, without merely defending your budget.", "seconds": 65, "models": [{"text": "Can I try to put the three concerns together? Mark needs a saving that's real; Steve needs fewer timetable clashes; Lesley needs the program's reputation intact. I think one change serves all three: consolidate tuition into larger group lessons in Years 7 and 8, where the pedagogy actually supports it, and protect individual lessons from Year 9 up. That saves hours, reduces pull-outs, and protects the senior results that carry the reputation.", "why": "It names each person's real interest and shows a single proposal satisfies all three, which moves him from defender of a budget to author of the solution."}, {"text": "I hear three questions, and I think they have the same answer. Where can we save, what can the timetable bear, and what can't we lose? The answer to all three is the junior years. That's where tuition is least efficient, where pull-outs hurt most, and where reputation is least at stake. So I'd propose we find Mark's figure there, and hold the senior program steady. Is that a basis to work from?", "why": "It reframes the drifting discussion as three questions with one answer, then hands the decision back with an explicit check."}], "checks": ["Did I state each person's interest in terms they would accept?", "Did I offer a proposition, not a defence?", "Did I concede the need for a saving?", "Did I end by handing the decision back?"]}, {"id": "the-synthesis-s4", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "A university curriculum committee is debating whether a new interdisciplinary unit on music and technology should sit in the music school or the engineering faculty. Academics from both sides have made territorial but intelligent arguments. A student representative has asked who students will actually go to for help.", "said": "\"With respect, it's not a question of ownership. The pedagogy of the unit is critical listening; that's our discipline. The tools may be engineering tools, but a microscope doesn't make biology a branch of optics.\"", "task": "Synthesise the two disciplinary claims and the student's question into a governance position.", "seconds": 70, "models": [{"text": "If I may try to draw this together: Professor Marsh is right that the pedagogy is critical listening, and Dr Patel is right that students will need the engineering infrastructure. The student representative's question tells us what matters to the people taking it: one place to go. So perhaps the unit is owned by music for curriculum and assessment, co-taught with engineering, with a single named coordinator. Ownership and resourcing don't have to sit in the same place.", "why": "It grants each side its strongest claim, uses the student's question as the tiebreaker, and separates ownership from resourcing."}, {"text": "I think we've been arguing about the wrong noun. Both faculties have made a case about who owns the unit; the student representative asked about who supports the student. If we organise around the second question, the answer is joint: academic ownership with music, as Professor Marsh says, technical support from engineering, and one coordinator for students. Could we minute that as the principle and leave the workload split to the deans?", "why": "It shifts the room's question rather than splitting the difference, and delegates the contested detail to the right level."}], "checks": ["Did I credit both disciplinary claims fairly?", "Did I use the student's question rather than ignore it?", "Is there a minutable principle?", "Did I keep the detail out of the room?"]}, {"id": "the-synthesis-s5", "room": "school council", "setup": "You chair the school council. The agenda item is the school's draft mobile phone policy. A parent member wants a total ban; a student representative argues phones are used for learning; a community member raises safety on the way home; a staff member worries about enforcement. Twenty minutes have passed and positions have hardened.", "said": "\"I just don't think a policy we can't enforce is worth having. If staff are spending half of every period confiscating phones, we've made things worse, not better.\"", "task": "As chair, synthesise the four positions into a draft position the council can endorse or amend.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "Let me see if I can capture where we are, and people can correct me. Everyone here wants phones out of lessons. Jess has a point that some learning uses them; Paul wants them available for the trip home; Mr Kaur needs something staff can enforce. So the shape might be: phones off and away during the school day, teacher-directed use as the only exception, and phones available at the gate. Is that something the council could endorse?", "why": "It opens with the shared ground, then fits each concern in as a feature of the policy, and ends with a clear question to the council."}, {"text": "I think the disagreement is smaller than it sounds. Nobody wants phones out in lessons and nobody wants students without them on the way home. The real question is enforcement, as Mr Kaur says. So perhaps the council's position is the principle, phones away during the school day, and we ask the school to bring back an enforcement model it is confident in. That respects the council's role and the school's.", "why": "It narrows the dispute to the one live issue and allocates it correctly between council and school, which is good governance as well as good synthesis."}], "checks": ["Did I start from what everyone shares?", "Did each member hear their concern reflected?", "Did I respect the line between council and school operations?", "Did I end with a decision the council can make?"]}, {"id": "the-synthesis-s6", "room": "conference panel", "setup": "You are on a panel at a state music education conference on the future of specialist programs. Two co-panellists, a university researcher and a department official, have disagreed about whether selective programs entrench advantage. The moderator turns to you with five minutes left. The audience is largely teachers.", "said": "\"I accept the research on selection effects. My point is that the alternative isn't a level playing field, it's private tuition for those who can afford it. Public specialist programs are the only route some of these students have.\"", "task": "Draw the two panellists' positions into a synthesis that gives the audience something to take away.", "seconds": 75, "models": [{"text": "I'd like to try to put Dr Moore and Ms Fischer together, because I don't think they're as far apart as the last ten minutes suggest. Dr Moore shows that selection by audition favours students with prior tuition. Ms Fischer argues that public programs are the counterweight to private tuition. Both are true, and together they tell us where the work is: not abolishing selection, but making the path to audition public. Primary school programs, feeder bands, free tuition before Year 7. That's the agenda I'd leave this room with.", "why": "It holds both claims as true, derives a third claim from them, and gives a teacher audience something actionable."}, {"text": "What I hear is agreement on the problem and disagreement only on the remedy. Both panellists accept that talent at twelve largely reflects opportunity before twelve. Dr Moore would change the selection; Ms Fischer would protect the programs. The synthesis, I'd suggest, is to select on potential rather than attainment, and to widen the pool well before anyone is selected. Both are things the people in this room can actually do on Monday.", "why": "It locates the precise point of difference, resolves it with a design principle, and finishes on the audience's agency."}], "checks": ["Did I treat both panellists' claims as true rather than splitting the difference?", "Did the synthesis produce a new claim?", "Did I land it for this audience?", "Did I stay inside the time?"]}]}, {"id": "the-amendment", "name": "The amendment", "family": "Building", "what": "It accepts the purpose of a proposal and changes one feature of it, so that the room keeps its momentum and the proposal is improved rather than defeated.", "when": "When a proposal is broadly right but has a flaw that will cause harm if adopted as it stands, and opposing it outright would either lose or cost more goodwill than the flaw is worth.", "teach": "The amendment is the most efficient form of disagreement available in a committee. Opposition asks the proposer to lose; amendment asks them to win slightly differently. People who argue for a living understand this immediately, and most will accept a well-made amendment because it lets them keep authorship of the idea. The result is that you often achieve more of what you want by amending than you would have by defeating the motion and starting again.\n\nThe mechanism has three parts. First, an explicit acceptance of purpose, stated in the proposer's terms, so that the room knows you are not an opponent in disguise: 'I support what this is trying to do.' Second, the specific change, as narrow as possible: one word, one condition, one date, one exception. The narrower the amendment, the harder it is to resist. Third, the reason, given as a benefit to the proposal itself rather than as a criticism of it: 'This makes it more likely to survive implementation' rather than 'This fixes the problem with it.' In formal meetings, Australian practice treats an amendment that simply negates the motion as out of order; the discipline is worth keeping even informally, because an amendment that guts the proposal will be recognised as opposition and resisted as such.\n\nSenior practitioners usually speak to the proposer before the meeting, if they can, and offer the change privately. A proposer who accepts it as a friendly amendment will often move it themselves, which is the best outcome. In the room, aim for forty to sixty seconds, and have the exact wording ready, ideally written, because chairs and secretaries will ask for it. Deliver the acceptance warmly and the change plainly; do not apologise for the change, and do not pause between purpose and change long enough for anyone to hear a 'but'. Use 'and' or 'so that' rather than 'but'.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways. The wrecking amendment changes so much that the proposal no longer does what it was for; sharp people will name it, and you will lose credibility. The cascade happens when one amendment invites several others and the proposal is redesigned on the floor; if that begins, suggest it go back to its author with the points noted. And the vague amendment ('perhaps we could soften it a little') hands the chair nothing to put and is simply discarded. An amendment is only as strong as its wording.", "phrasings": ["I support the proposal, and I'd like to suggest one change that I think makes it more durable.", "Could I offer a friendly amendment? Replace 'all students' with 'all students in Years 7 to 10'.", "I agree with the direction entirely. My amendment is about the date, not the substance.", "Would the mover accept adding a review clause, so that the board revisits this after the first festival?", "I think this is right, and I'd like it to survive contact with the timetable. One condition would do that.", "Rather than oppose the motion, may I propose that it read 'up to' rather than 'not less than'?", "I'd move that we adopt the recommendation with one exception, which I'll put in writing for the secretary.", "If Professor Hale is willing, I'd add a single clause so that the smaller schools aren't caught by this unintentionally."], "avoid": ["I support this in principle, but : 'in principle, but' signals opposition and undoes the acceptance before the change is heard.", "Maybe we could just water it down a bit : vague, gives the chair nothing to put, and insults the proposal.", "I'd like to amend it so that it doesn't actually apply this year : a wrecking amendment, which will be recognised as opposition.", "With all due respect, the proposal is flawed, so I'd suggest the following changes : leads with criticism and invites the proposer to defend instead of accept."], "scenarios": [{"id": "the-amendment-s1", "room": "school council", "setup": "A parent member has moved that the council endorse a proposal that every Year 7 student take part in a compulsory term of instrumental music. You admire the intent, but the school has nowhere near the instruments or tutors to deliver it next year, and the specialist program would be stretched. The motion has general warmth around the table.", "said": "\"I'll keep this brief. Every child deserves the chance to learn an instrument, not just the ones who auditioned. I'm moving that council endorse a compulsory term of instrumental music for all Year 7s from next year.\"", "task": "Support the purpose and amend the motion so it can actually be delivered.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "I'd like to support this, and to help it happen. I'd ask whether the mover would accept one amendment: replacing 'from next year' with 'from 2028, subject to the school reporting to council on instrument and staffing requirements by Term 2'. That keeps the principle exactly as moved, and gives us a date we can honestly meet.", "why": "It accepts the principle in the mover's words and narrows the change to a date and a reporting condition, worded ready for the minutes."}, {"text": "I support the principle without reservation, and I'd like to propose a small amendment so that it lands well. Council endorses the aim of a term of instrumental music for every Year 7 student, and asks the school to bring back a staged plan, beginning with a pilot class next year. Would the mover accept that as friendly?", "why": "It reframes endorsement of an action as endorsement of an aim plus a staged plan, which respects the line between council and school operations."}], "checks": ["Did I accept the purpose in the mover's terms?", "Is my change narrow and worded?", "Did I avoid the word 'but'?", "Did I ask whether the mover would accept it?"]}, {"id": "the-amendment-s2", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The committee is considering a recommendation from the writing group that all Music ATAR candidates submit a recorded performance rather than performing live for examiners. The rationale is equity for regional students and reduced logistics. You support the direction but believe recordings without controls will advantage students with access to studios and editing.", "said": "\"The writing group's view is that a recorded submission is fairer to regional candidates, reduces examiner travel, and reflects how musicians actually work now. We'd ask the committee to endorse the recommendation as drafted.\"", "task": "Accept the move to recorded submissions and amend it to close the production-advantage problem.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think the writing group is right, and the equity case for regional students is strong. I'd like to propose one condition so the recommendation achieves what it intends: that the recording be a single unedited take, made on school premises, with a teacher declaration. Otherwise we replace one advantage, proximity to Perth, with another, access to a studio. With that clause, I'd support it fully.", "why": "It endorses the rationale, then frames the amendment as protecting that rationale, and names the specific condition."}, {"text": "I'd support the recommendation with a single amendment: 'recorded submissions must be continuous and unedited, with audio and video from a fixed position'. It costs regional schools nothing extra, and it means the recording measures the performance rather than the production. The writing group's aim stays intact, and arguably becomes easier to defend.", "why": "It offers exact wording the secretary can minute, shows the amendment does not erode the equity rationale, and keeps authorship with the writing group."}], "checks": ["Did I endorse the equity rationale before changing anything?", "Is my amendment specific enough to draft?", "Did I frame it as serving the proposal's purpose?", "Did I keep the writing group as author?"]}, {"id": "the-amendment-s3", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The president has proposed that the festival introduce a ranked awards ceremony with first, second and third places in each division, arguing it will raise standards and attract sponsors. Several directors like it. You believe outright ranking will discourage smaller and newer school bands from entering, but the sponsor argument is real.", "said": "\"I'll be honest, the sponsors have asked for it. They want winners they can put on a banner. And I think a bit of competition would lift the standard across the board. I'd like us to approve ranked placings from next year.\"", "task": "Keep the sponsor-friendly recognition and amend the format so it does not drive small schools away.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "I'm with you on recognition, and the sponsor point is one we have to take seriously. Could I suggest an amendment that gives sponsors their winners without losing the new bands? Rather than first, second, third, we award gold, silver and bronze standards against criteria, with one sponsor's award for the outstanding band in each division. Sponsors get a banner, and every band has something to aim for.", "why": "It accepts both of the president's aims, recognition and sponsorship, and changes only the mechanism to a criterion-referenced model."}, {"text": "I'd support ranked placings with one amendment: that they apply only to the open and advanced divisions, and the developing divisions stay non-competitive with adjudicator commendations. That gives the sponsors their winners where the standard is highest, and protects the bands we most need to keep entering. Could that be put as a friendly amendment?", "why": "It narrows the scope of the proposal rather than changing its nature, which is easier for the president to accept."}], "checks": ["Did I take the sponsor point seriously?", "Did I change the mechanism rather than reject the idea?", "Is the amendment clear enough to minute?", "Did I avoid lecturing about competition?"]}, {"id": "the-amendment-s4", "room": "faculty curriculum committee", "setup": "A university faculty committee is considering a proposal from the dean's office to reduce first-year contact hours in the Bachelor of Music by replacing some aural tutorials with online modules. You are an external member. You think online modules suit theory content but not the sung and dictated parts of aural training, which depend on live correction.", "said": "\"The proposal is cost neutral in year one and saves about fifteen per cent of tutorial load by year two. The modules are well designed and have strong completion data from other institutions. I'd suggest we can adopt it without further delay.\"", "task": "Accept the efficiency case and amend the proposal to protect live aural work.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I'm persuaded on the theory content, and I don't doubt the modules. Could I propose that the change apply to the written and analytical components only, and that sight-singing and dictation remain in live tutorials? Those depend on someone hearing the student and correcting in the moment. That would still give the faculty most of the saving, perhaps ten of the fifteen per cent, while keeping the part that doesn't translate online.", "why": "It concedes the efficiency case, draws a principled line, and quantifies the trade so the dean's office can see the cost of accepting."}, {"text": "I'd support this with a review amendment: adopt the modules for one year, with a comparison of first-year aural results against the previous cohort, reported to this committee before the change is made permanent. If the results hold, we've confirmed the saving; if they don't, we've caught it in one cohort rather than four.", "why": "It does not contest the proposal's design at all, it adds an evidence condition, which is very hard for a data-led proposer to refuse."}], "checks": ["Did I accept the efficiency argument openly?", "Is the line I've drawn principled, not just preferential?", "Did I give the proposer something they can say yes to?", "Was the wording exact?"]}, {"id": "the-amendment-s5", "room": "department meeting", "setup": "In the Performing Arts learning area meeting, the head of learning area has proposed a single common assessment calendar across drama, dance and music, with all major assessments in the same two weeks of each term. You see the coordination benefit, but music's ensemble performances depend on concert dates that are set by external venues. The proposal has broad support, and you do not want music to look like the exception everyone has to work around.", "said": "\"I want us to stop treading on each other's toes. Students tell me they have three practical assessments in one week and a production on top. One calendar, two assessment windows a term, and everyone works to it.\"", "task": "Back the shared calendar and amend it to accommodate fixed external performance dates.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "I think one calendar is the right fix, and music will work to it. I'd propose one exception: assessments tied to an external performance date, a concert at a booked venue for instance, can sit outside the window, provided they're entered on the shared calendar at the start of the term. That keeps the coordination you want and means we're not assessing ensembles at an artificial date.", "why": "It commits music to the plan first, then asks for a defined, bounded exception with a transparency condition."}, {"text": "Can I support this and add a clause? Each learning area nominates its fixed external dates in Week 1, and the windows are set around them. That way the calendar is built once, with everything visible, and no one has to negotiate exceptions halfway through the term.", "why": "It amends the process rather than asking for an exception, which turns music's constraint into a design feature for everyone."}], "checks": ["Did I commit to the plan before amending it?", "Is my exception bounded and transparent?", "Did I avoid making music sound special?", "Is it short?"]}, {"id": "the-amendment-s6", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "On a state arts funding panel, the convenor proposes a new rule for the youth music round: no organisation may receive funding in two consecutive years. The aim is to spread funding to new applicants. You agree with the aim, but several of the strongest ongoing programs for disadvantaged young people would lose continuity.", "said": "\"We keep funding the same dozen organisations. They're good, but the round is meant to grow the sector, not sustain incumbents. A one-year stand-down gives new applicants a real chance.\"", "task": "Support the aim of spreading funding and amend the rule so it does not break ongoing programs that depend on continuity.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "I share the aim, and the pattern you describe is real. I'd propose an amendment: instead of a stand-down, reserve a fixed share of the round, say forty per cent, for first-time applicants. That guarantees new entrants their chance without forcing an established program for at-risk young people to stop for a year and lose its students.", "why": "It achieves the convenor's goal by a different mechanism and makes the cost of the original rule concrete."}, {"text": "I'd support the stand-down with one exception: programs that have been funded for three or more consecutive years may apply for multi-year funding through the separate stream, and are exempt from the stand-down if successful. That moves incumbents out of this round, which is what you want, without cutting them off.", "why": "It keeps the rule intact and redirects the strongest incumbents elsewhere, which serves the convenor's purpose more fully than opposing it would."}], "checks": ["Did I state agreement with the aim before the change?", "Does my amendment achieve the convenor's goal, not dodge it?", "Did I name the cost of the unamended rule specifically?", "Is the amendment precise enough to adopt?"]}]}, {"id": "principled-dissent", "name": "Principled dissent", "family": "Dissenting", "what": "It puts a clear disagreement on the record, on the substance and with evident respect, so that the room has to weigh it even if it decides the other way.", "when": "When a senior person or a majority is moving towards a decision you believe is wrong on the merits, and silence would be read as assent.", "teach": "Principled dissent is not the same as opposition. Its first purpose is not to win the vote but to make sure the decision is taken with the objection in view. That changes how it is delivered. You are not trying to defeat the senior person; you are giving the room the information it needs to decide well, and putting your own judgement on the record. Clever rooms respect this, often more than they respect agreement, because most of them have watched institutions make avoidable mistakes in silence.\n\nThe structure is simple and must be audible. State your position in the first sentence, without preamble: 'I disagree with the recommendation.' Then give the single strongest reason, not three. Then state what would change your mind, or what you would support instead. Finish by acknowledging the room's right to decide: 'If the committee goes the other way, I'd ask that my view be noted.' That last line matters. It shows you understand the difference between dissent and obstruction, and in formal settings it is the conventional way of recording a minority view.\n\nWith a senior person, the respect must be in the form, not in qualifiers. Use their title or name once, address the argument rather than the person, and avoid every softener that sounds like apology: 'I may be wrong', 'just', 'I wonder if'. Those make the dissent easy to dismiss and, paradoxically, sound less respectful, because they imply the senior person cannot take a direct disagreement. Senior practitioners speak more slowly and slightly more quietly when dissenting, keep their hands still, and hold eye contact with the person whose view they are opposing for the first sentence before turning to the chair. Forty to seventy seconds is ample. A long dissent reads as grievance.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways. The first is the hedge: the disagreement is wrapped in so many concessions that nobody is sure it happened, and the minutes record consent. The second is the personal turn, where the dissent drifts from the proposal to the proposer's motives or competence; that converts a disagreement on substance into a conflict, and the room will side with the senior person. The third is the repeat: having been heard and outvoted, the dissenter raises it again under the next item. Dissent once, clearly, and then let the decision stand unless new facts emerge. That restraint is what earns the right to be listened to next time.", "phrasings": ["I disagree with the recommendation, and I'd like to say why briefly.", "Principal, I have a different view on this, and I think it should be in front of the meeting before we decide.", "I can't support this as it stands. My reason is a single one, and it's about the students in the smaller schools.", "I'm conscious I'm in the minority here, so I'll be brief, but I think we're about to make a mistake.", "My objection isn't to the aim, which I share. It's to the evidence we're relying on.", "I'd change my view if we had data on attrition. Without it, I think the risk is ours, not the applicants'.", "If the board decides otherwise, I'll accept that, and I'd ask that my dissent be minuted.", "With respect, Professor, I read the same report and came to the opposite conclusion."], "avoid": ["I might be totally wrong here, and I don't want to rock the boat, but : the hedge makes the dissent disappear and the minutes record consent.", "I think we all know why this is really being pushed : imputes motive and turns a disagreement on substance into a personal conflict.", "I just want to say again, as I said last time : repetition after being outvoted reads as obstruction and costs credibility.", "Frankly this is a terrible idea : contempt for the proposal makes the room defend it, and you lose the substance."], "scenarios": [{"id": "principled-dissent-s1", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "You are meeting the principal and associate principal about next year's timetable. They have decided, subject to your view, to move senior ensemble rehearsals from timetabled lessons to before-school sessions to free up teaching periods. The principal is a personal friend and a capable leader. You believe the change will reduce participation among students who rely on public transport.", "said": "\"I know it isn't ideal, Dijon, but it's the cleanest way to get the periods back. Plenty of schools run ensembles before school. I'd rather we just make the call and move on. Can you live with it?\"", "task": "Disagree clearly with the decision on its merits, without straining the relationship or making it about her judgement.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Honestly, Lesley, I can't support it as it stands, and I'd rather say so now than later. My reason is one: a large share of our senior players come in by train and bus from outside the area, and a 7.15 start isn't reachable for many of them. We'd lose those students first, and they're the ones the program exists for. If we need the periods, I'd rather find them elsewhere in the music allocation, and I'll bring you an option by Friday.", "why": "It answers the direct question directly, gives one concrete reason, and offers an alternative with a date, which keeps the dissent constructive."}, {"text": "I'll give you a straight answer: no, I don't think I can. I accept that other schools run ensembles before school. Ours is different because it's a selective program drawing from a wide catchment, and the students who'd drop out are the ones furthest away. I may be outvoted on this, and that's your call. I'd just ask that we look at the travel data first.", "why": "It concedes the factual point, identifies why it doesn't transfer, acknowledges her authority, and asks for one specific thing."}], "checks": ["Did I say 'no' or 'I disagree' in the first sentence?", "Did I give one reason rather than several?", "Did I acknowledge her right to decide?", "Did I avoid apologising for disagreeing?"]}, {"id": "principled-dissent-s2", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "A senior academic who chairs a subcommittee has recommended removing the jazz and contemporary context from the Music ATAR course to make room for an expanded Western art music analysis component. Several members are nodding. You believe the change narrows the course's cultural range and will disadvantage students in contemporary-focused programs.", "said": "\"The analytical demands of the Western art tradition give students a rigour that the contemporary contexts, frankly, have struggled to provide. I think we've been spreading the course too thinly, and this is the moment to consolidate around depth.\"", "task": "Dissent from a senior academic's recommendation on substance, with respect, and make the objection impossible to overlook.", "seconds": 65, "models": [{"text": "Professor Lang, I disagree with the recommendation, and I'd like to say why. The rigour of a context depends on how it is examined, not on the repertoire itself. Jazz has an analytical tradition as demanding as any, harmonic, formal and historical. If the contemporary papers have lacked rigour, that is a design problem we can fix, not a reason to remove the context. I'd support strengthening the analytical demand across all contexts. If the committee proceeds, I'd ask that my view be minuted.", "why": "It addresses the stated premise directly, offers an alternative that shares his goal, and formally records the dissent."}, {"text": "I'd like to put a contrary view on the record. I agree with the aim of depth, but removing the jazz and contemporary context would make this a narrower course than the one students and schools actually take. Many of the schools that offer this course are built around contemporary programs. We'd be asking their candidates to study a tradition their schools barely teach, which isn't depth, it's attrition. I'd rather we deepen within each context.", "why": "It grants the aim, then argues from the consequence for candidates, which shifts the ground from taste to impact."}], "checks": ["Did I name the disagreement before the reasons?", "Did I contest the premise, not the person?", "Did I offer something I would support?", "Did I avoid a claim or figure I can't back up?"]}, {"id": "principled-dissent-s3", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The board is voting on a proposal to accept naming sponsorship from a gambling-adjacent company for the schools' jazz festival. The offer would cover a major shortfall. The majority favour accepting. As treasurer you know the money matters, but you believe the association's reputation with schools and parents is at risk.", "said": "\"Look, I share people's discomfort, but this is a lot of money and we're not in a position to be precious. The sponsor isn't asking for anything other than signage. I think we accept, with gratitude, and move on.\"", "task": "Dissent from the majority on the merits, as treasurer, without suggesting your colleagues lack principles.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "As treasurer, I'd like to vote against this, and I'll explain from the treasurer's side. Our income is mostly school entry fees, and principals sign those off. If even a handful of schools withdraw because of the sponsor, we lose more than the sponsorship brings in, and we lose it every year after. I'd support a motion to find the shortfall through a revised fee structure instead, and I'll prepare one. If the board goes the other way, I'll accept that, and I'd ask that my dissent be noted.", "why": "It frames the dissent in financial terms that respect the majority's reasoning, offers an alternative, and records the view formally."}, {"text": "I disagree, and not because I think anyone here is being careless. We exist to serve schools, and schools have obligations to parents about what they promote to children. A sponsor like this puts every member school in an awkward position. I'd rather run a leaner festival this year than ask them to defend our signage. I'll accept the board's decision, but I want to be clear I'm voting no.", "why": "It disowns any accusation of carelessness at the start, grounds the objection in the association's purpose, and states the vote unambiguously."}], "checks": ["Was my vote unambiguous?", "Did I frame the objection in terms the majority accepts?", "Did I avoid implying anyone lacks integrity?", "Did I ask for my dissent to be recorded?"]}, {"id": "principled-dissent-s4", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "A university academic board is about to approve a proposal to replace the final-year recital in the performance degree with a portfolio of recorded work. The proposal comes from the head of school, and there is a strong consensus. You, as an external member, believe the live recital is the professional threshold students most need to cross.", "said": "\"I think we can take this as broadly agreed. The portfolio better reflects the profession as it is now, students produce more and better work, and the anxiety associated with a single recital has been a recurring concern. Unless there's anything further, I'll put it.\"", "task": "Stop the item being put without objection, and dissent clearly and briefly on substance.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "Before it's put, Chair, I'd like to record a different view. I agree the portfolio reflects much of the profession. But performers are still hired on what they do live, under pressure, with no second take, and the recital is the only point in the degree where that's assessed. Removing it trades a real professional threshold for a more comfortable one. I'd support keeping a shorter recital alongside the portfolio. If the board disagrees, I'd ask that my view be minuted.", "why": "It intervenes before the item is closed, concedes the true part of the case, and identifies the specific thing that would be lost."}, {"text": "Chair, I'm not able to take it as agreed. I've performed professionally for twenty-six years, and the skill that most separates working musicians from others is recovery under live conditions. A portfolio can't assess that. I take the point about anxiety seriously, which is why I'd rather we teach to it than remove it. I'll accept the board's decision, but I don't want silence to be read as consent.", "why": "It uses his professional standing economically, addresses the strongest argument for the proposal, and makes clear why he is speaking."}], "checks": ["Did I speak before the item was closed?", "Did I concede what is true in the proposal?", "Did I name the specific loss?", "Did I make clear silence would have been read as consent?"]}, {"id": "principled-dissent-s5", "room": "school council", "setup": "You chair the school council. The council is about to endorse the school's annual report. The principal's draft describes the music program's results in terms you know overstate the outcomes: participation figures include students who withdrew mid-year. Every other member is ready to endorse. As chair you must be careful, but you cannot let the inaccuracy pass.", "said": "\"It's a strong report, and I think it tells our story well. I'd move that council endorse it as presented so it can go to the community next week.\"", "task": "As chair, dissent from endorsing the report as presented, on accuracy, without discrediting the principal or the report.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Before I put that, I need to raise one point, and I raise it because the report is strong. The music participation figure includes students who withdrew during the year. I'd be uncomfortable with council endorsing a figure we know to be overstated, because it's our endorsement on the document. I'd suggest we endorse the report subject to that figure being corrected, which I'm sure the principal would prefer too.", "why": "It frames the dissent as protecting the report and the council, offers a conditional endorsement, and gives the principal a dignified way to agree."}, {"text": "I'm not able to put the motion as moved, and I want to be clear the issue is narrow. The participation figure on page six counts enrolments at the start of the year, not completions. When council endorses the report, we're vouching for it. A small correction resolves it. Could we endorse it with that figure revised?", "why": "It names the specific figure and why it matters, states plainly what the council's endorsement means, and keeps the remedy small."}], "checks": ["Did I make the dissent narrow and specific?", "Did I protect the principal's standing?", "Did I explain why the council's endorsement matters?", "Did I offer a way to endorse?"]}, {"id": "principled-dissent-s6", "room": "conference panel", "setup": "At a national music education conference, you are on a panel with a high-profile academic who argues that ensemble programs in schools are a nineteenth-century model and should give way to individual, technology-based music making. The audience is receptive. The moderator invites you to respond.", "said": "\"The large ensemble is a relic of military bands and the factory model of schooling. Young people today make music alone, on laptops, and share it globally. We should meet them there rather than drill them in rows.\"", "task": "Disagree publicly, on substance, with a senior and popular speaker, in a way the audience will respect.", "seconds": 70, "models": [{"text": "I disagree, and I'll say why with real respect for the argument. The history is right: school bands came partly from military models. But the case for ensembles isn't historical, it's musical. Playing in a group trains things a laptop can't: listening while playing, adjusting time and intonation to other people in real time, subordinating yourself to a shared result. Those are the skills professional musicians actually trade on. I'd keep both, and I'd resist the idea that one has to replace the other.", "why": "It grants the historical claim, contests the inference, and grounds the dissent in observable musical skills rather than nostalgia."}, {"text": "I'm going to take a different view, Professor Ellis. Making music alone on a laptop is real and valuable, and I teach it. But what I see in students is that it rarely teaches them to listen to anyone else. The ensemble is one of the few places a young person learns that their part only makes sense in relation to others. That's not drill, it's an education in attention. I'd argue we need it more now, not less.", "why": "It names the speaker respectfully, reframes 'drill' as attention, and argues that the speaker's own premise strengthens the case for ensembles."}], "checks": ["Did I state the disagreement clearly at the start?", "Did I concede what was accurate?", "Did I contest the inference rather than mock the view?", "Did I avoid sentimentality?"]}]}, {"id": "concede-to-gain", "name": "Concede to gain", "family": "Dissenting", "what": "It states the opposing case at its strongest, concedes the part of it that is true, and then places the real objection exactly where it cannot be dismissed as a misunderstanding.", "when": "When the other side has a genuinely strong point that you cannot credibly deny, and your objection will only be heard once the room sees you have understood and accepted it.", "teach": "Clever people discount any objection that seems not to have grasped their argument. The concession removes that defence. By putting their case better than they did, and granting what is true in it, you show that your disagreement survives full understanding, which is the only kind of disagreement a serious room takes seriously. The technique is old: it is the discipline of the steel-man, and in advocacy the practice of conceding the bad facts before your opponent can use them. It works because it changes your position in the room from adversary to judge. Having conceded, you are visibly fair, and the objection that follows inherits that fairness.\n\nThe structure has three beats and the proportions matter. First, the steel-man: their argument in its strongest form, in one or two sentences, ideally stronger than they put it. Second, the concession, explicit and unhedged: 'That's right, and I accept it.' Third, the pivot to the remaining objection, which must be narrow, precise and genuinely untouched by the concession. The pivot word should be quiet; 'and yet', 'what that doesn't settle is', or simply a new sentence. 'However' is acceptable; 'but' after a concession often sounds as if the concession was insincere.\n\nSenior practitioners make the concession real. They concede something that costs them, not a trivial point; a concession of a minor matter while the major one goes uncontested reads as a debating trick, and a room of academics will name it. They also pause after the concession, a full beat, so that the room registers it before the objection arrives. The voice on the steel-man should be warm and unhurried, as though the argument were their own; on the objection, it should become a little lower and more exact. Keep the whole thing to under a minute; the concession earns attention, and the objection must use it before it expires.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways. The token concession concedes nothing of weight, which signals manipulation. The over-concession grants so much that nothing remains to object to, and the room rightly asks why you are still speaking. And the straw steel-man restates the opposing case in a slightly weakened form; the person whose argument it is will correct you, and you will have handed them the floor. If you are not sure you can state their case well, ask them to confirm your version before you object to it.", "phrasings": ["Let me put the case for this as strongly as I can, because I think it is strong.", "I accept the point about cost entirely; I don't think it's contestable. What it doesn't settle is the question of who bears it.", "You're right that the current model favours students with prior tuition. I'd go further: it was designed by people who'd had it. And yet abolishing the audition doesn't fix that.", "If I've understood the argument at its best, it's this. I agree with all of it except the final step.", "That's a fair hit, and I'd rather concede it than argue around it. The objection I still have is narrower.", "Professor Hale's evidence on attrition is better than anything I can offer, and I accept it. My concern is about what it measures.", "Grant everything you've said about efficiency. The question remaining is whether we can reverse it.", "I'll concede the principle. Where I part company is the timing."], "avoid": ["I see where you're coming from, but : a vague, non-committal acknowledgement that concedes nothing and signals the 'but' is the real point.", "Obviously you have a point, though it's not really relevant : the concession is withdrawn as it is offered, which reads as contempt.", "So what you're saying is that you don't care about the regional students : a straw steel-man that misstates their view and invites correction.", "I completely agree with everything you've said, and I just have a tiny concern : over-concession that leaves nothing standing for the objection."], "scenarios": [{"id": "concede-to-gain-s1", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "The principal and business manager are proposing to reduce the music program's instrumental tutor budget by moving to group lessons in all year levels. The business manager has strong data showing group tuition is more cost effective and that other schools have done it without obvious harm. You think group lessons are fine in junior years but will damage senior performance standards.", "said": "\"I've looked at four other specialist programs. Three have moved to group tuition in every year, and their ATAR music results haven't dropped. It's simply more efficient. I'd want a compelling reason not to follow them.\"", "task": "Concede the strength of the efficiency evidence, then land the specific objection about senior students.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "That's good evidence, Mark, and I won't argue with it. Group tuition is more efficient, and for most students in Years 7 to 10 I think it's as good or better. What the ATAR results don't show is the top end: the students who go on to audition for WAAPA or interstate conservatoriums. They need individual work on repertoire that a group can't share. So I'd accept group lessons through Year 10, and ask that we protect individual lessons for the Year 11 and 12 performance students.", "why": "It concedes the evidence fully and precisely, then identifies the specific population the evidence does not capture."}, {"text": "I'll put your case at its strongest: group tuition costs less, the results elsewhere haven't fallen, and nobody can show harm. I accept all three. My objection is that ATAR averages wouldn't show harm to the five or six students a year whose next step is a tertiary audition. That's where individual lessons earn their cost. Could we keep them for that group alone?", "why": "It restates the opposing argument better than it was put, concedes each limb, and confines the objection to a small, costable group."}], "checks": ["Did I concede something that costs me?", "Was my steel-man at least as strong as theirs?", "Is my remaining objection untouched by what I conceded?", "Did I pause after the concession?"]}, {"id": "concede-to-gain-s2", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The committee is debating whether to retain a compulsory set work in the Music ATAR course. A respected assessment expert argues that set works narrow teaching, encourage rote preparation, and disadvantage schools with fewer resources. Her argument is strong and the room is persuaded. You believe a set work gives every student a shared analytical anchor that open choice will not.", "said": "\"Set works produce exactly what you'd predict: teaching to the work, memorised analyses, and a generation of students who can talk about one symphony and nothing else. Open choice of repertoire would let teachers build genuine analytical skill.\"", "task": "Concede the strongest parts of her case and then show what open choice would lose.", "seconds": 65, "models": [{"text": "Dr Chen is right about the rote preparation, and I'd add that the published study guides make it worse: some students learn an analysis rather than learn to analyse. I accept that. What open choice would lose is comparability. If every candidate brings different repertoire, the examiner can't set a question that tests the same thing for everyone, and the paper drifts toward generic questions that reward fluent writing. I'd keep a set work, and change how it's examined.", "why": "It concedes more than she claimed, then names a precise assessment consequence of her alternative."}, {"text": "I'd like to put Dr Chen's case in its strongest form. Set works reward preparation over thinking, they favour well-resourced schools, and they bore students. All of that is true. And yet the schools least able to build their own repertoire program are the ones who benefit most from a shared work with shared resources. Open choice frees the strong schools and leaves the others on their own. That's the part I can't accept.", "why": "It steel-mans her case including the equity argument, then turns equity against the remedy, which is the strongest available objection."}], "checks": ["Did I concede her points in words she'd accept?", "Did my objection follow from a different consideration, not a denial?", "Did I avoid conceding only the trivial point?", "Did I keep the objection to one idea?"]}, {"id": "concede-to-gain-s3", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "A new board member, a marketing professional, argues that the festival should move from a weekday format to a weekend to attract larger audiences and better media coverage. The case is well prepared and several directors are impressed. You know weekend scheduling will lose many teachers, who volunteer on school time.", "said": "\"Weekday events get no audience apart from the bands themselves. Parents can't come, the media won't cover it, and sponsors notice. A Saturday festival would double the audience, and that's what keeps us viable.\"", "task": "Concede the audience and sponsorship argument, then land the workforce objection.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "You're right, and I'd go further: a weekday festival is invisible to most families, and that's been a problem for years. I don't dispute any of it. The difficulty is who runs it. Almost every band director and volunteer adjudicator gives us their time on school days because their employers release them. On a Saturday they're unpaid and have families. We'd gain an audience and lose the people who make the festival.", "why": "It concedes beyond what was argued, then introduces a constraint the proposal had not considered."}, {"text": "I think the audience case is unanswerable, and I accept it. What I'd want to test is whether a Saturday is the only way to get it. A weekday festival with a Saturday showcase for the award-winning bands might give us the audience and the media you want, without asking forty teachers for an unpaid weekend. Would you be willing to look at that?", "why": "It concedes the goal entirely and redirects the objection into a hybrid that preserves the proposer's aims."}], "checks": ["Did I concede the core of his argument?", "Did I introduce a consideration he hadn't addressed?", "Did I keep the tone collegial towards a new member?", "Did I avoid a long list of practical problems?"]}, {"id": "concede-to-gain-s4", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "On a university academic committee, a dean argues for consolidating three small specialist music electives, including jazz improvisation, into one general musicianship unit. The financial and pedagogical case is well made. You believe jazz improvisation is a distinct discipline that will disappear inside a general unit.", "said": "\"Each of these electives runs with eight or nine students. A combined unit would run at thirty, with a stronger peer cohort, and students would get exposure to three traditions instead of one. I don't think that's a loss; I think it's a gain.\"", "task": "Concede the strength of the dean's case, then make the objection about improvisation land.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think that's largely right, Dean. Classes of eight are hard to justify, and exposure to three traditions is a real gain for most students. I accept both. What a combined unit can't do is teach improvisation, because improvisation isn't content you can cover in four weeks; it's a practice built over hundreds of hours of playing. A general unit would introduce it and stop. I'd support consolidating the other two, and keep improvisation as a stand-alone elective.", "why": "It concedes the class-size and breadth arguments fully, then distinguishes improvisation on the nature of the learning, not its prestige."}, {"text": "Let me grant the case completely: the numbers are unsustainable, and the combined unit would be a good unit. My concern is narrower. The students who take jazz improvisation do so because they intend to work in it, and the skill takes sustained practice that a survey unit can't give. If it's absorbed, those students will simply go elsewhere. Could we consolidate, but run improvisation as a strand with its own assessed component?", "why": "It concedes everything, then makes the objection about who is lost and offers a design fix the dean can accept."}], "checks": ["Did I accept the financial case without qualification?", "Did my objection rest on the nature of the discipline, not its status?", "Did I offer something the dean can say yes to?", "Was it under a minute?"]}, {"id": "concede-to-gain-s5", "room": "parent body", "setup": "At a meeting of the music program's parent support group, a parent who is a senior lawyer argues that the program's commitment rule, requiring students to stay in the program through Year 9, is unfair and should be dropped. She is articulate and has the sympathy of the room. You enforce the rule and believe it protects ensembles and the students themselves.", "said": "\"A twelve-year-old can't meaningfully commit to three years of anything. Holding a child to an enrolment decision made in Year 6 is, frankly, not something we'd accept in any other area of school. If they've lost interest, why keep them?\"", "task": "Concede the strength of her argument, then land the reason for the rule.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think that's a fair argument, and I'll accept the premise: a twelve-year-old can't fully understand a three-year commitment. That's precisely why the rule exists. In my experience, the point at which students most want to stop is the middle of Year 8, when the instrument gets harder before it gets easier. If they stop there, many of them later regret it. The rule isn't there to hold children to a decision; it's to carry them through the plateau. And we do release students where there's genuine reason.", "why": "It accepts her premise outright and turns it into the rationale for the rule, which is the strongest form of concede-to-gain."}, {"text": "Let me put your case fairly: children change, interests change, and an enrolment decision made at eleven shouldn't bind a child at fourteen. I agree with that as a general principle. What's different here is that each student holds a place in an ensemble someone else auditioned for and missed. When one leaves, the ensemble loses a part and another child lost the chance. That's the obligation the rule protects.", "why": "It steel-mans the principle and then introduces the fact that changes its application: places are scarce and shared."}], "checks": ["Did I accept her premise rather than argue around it?", "Did my objection rest on a fact she hadn't addressed?", "Did I avoid sounding defensive about the rule?", "Did I mention that exceptions exist?"]}, {"id": "concede-to-gain-s6", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "On an arts funding panel, a fellow assessor argues that an application from a long-standing youth jazz orchestra should be ranked below a newer, community-based program because the orchestra serves students who are already advantaged. The assessor is persuasive and the equity criterion is real. You think the orchestra's application is strong on its own merits: its outreach section, on page eight, proposes free workshops in eleven public schools, three of them schools the community program also serves.", "said": "\"The orchestra is excellent, nobody disputes that. But its players come overwhelmingly from selective programs and private tuition. The community program reaches kids who've never touched an instrument. On our equity criterion, it isn't close.\"", "task": "Concede the equity argument as stated, then make the case for the orchestra's application on the evidence in front of the panel.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "On the criterion as you've framed it, I agree, it isn't close: the community program reaches students the orchestra doesn't. I accept that. What I'd ask the panel to look at is the orchestra's outreach section on page eight: they're proposing free workshops for eleven public schools, and three of those schools are on the community program's list. If we fund both, the community program's students have somewhere to go next. That's a pathway, not a competition.", "why": "It concedes the equity judgement fully, then uses the application's own evidence to reframe the two bids as complementary."}, {"text": "I'd concede the point entirely on current membership. The orchestra's players are mostly advantaged, and that should count. Where I'd differ is that our criterion asks about the program's reach, not just its membership, and this application is largely about extending that reach. I'd score it lower on current equity, as you would, and higher on the outreach, and I think that puts it in the fundable band.", "why": "It concedes the factual point and the scoring consequence, then separates two elements of the criterion so the objection lands on the scoring itself."}], "checks": ["Did I concede the equity point without qualification?", "Did I point to specific evidence in the application?", "Is my objection about the criterion, not about the other assessor?", "Did I avoid arguing that excellence should outweigh equity?"]}]}, {"id": "naming-what-the-room-is-avoiding", "name": "Naming what the room is avoiding", "family": "Dissenting", "what": "It says aloud the issue that everyone in the room is aware of and nobody has raised, so that the discussion can deal with the real question instead of circling a proxy for it.", "when": "When the discussion keeps returning to safe surface issues, people are visibly talking around something, and the decision will be poor or unworkable unless the unspoken matter is put on the table.", "teach": "Every committee has topics it prefers not to touch: a colleague's performance, a funding source, a decision already made elsewhere, a conflict of interest, the fact that the proposal cannot actually be delivered. Highly educated people are very good at avoiding these elegantly. They discuss the proxy instead, at length and with real intelligence, and the meeting ends with a decision that does not address what everybody knew. Naming the avoided issue is one of the most valuable contributions a member can make, and one of the rarest, because it costs the speaker something: they become the person who said it.\n\nThe mechanism is relief. Most rooms are not avoiding the issue because they disagree about it, but because nobody wants to be first. Once it is named in a measured way, the room usually follows quickly, and the speaker is often privately thanked. What makes the difference between relief and embarrassment is the manner of the naming. It must be impersonal, descriptive and brief, and it must be framed as being in the room's interest: 'I think we may be circling something, and it would help to say it.' The issue is then stated factually, without accusation, and followed at once by a question that gives the room a next step.\n\nSenior practitioners name the avoided issue as an observation about the discussion, not about any person. They describe what is happening ('We've spent twenty minutes on the timetable, and I suspect the real question is staffing') rather than what anyone is doing. They speak calmly and a little more slowly than usual, and they sit still; any visible tension in the body reads as confrontation. They name it once, then stop and let the silence do its work. A gap of several seconds after the naming is normal and should not be filled. Thirty to fifty seconds is right; the shorter the better.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways. The first is naming a person rather than an issue, which turns the observation into an attack and makes the room close ranks. The second is mistaking your own private grievance for the room's avoidance; if only you are thinking it, you have not named the room's issue, you have raised your own, and you should do so in the ordinary way. The third is the wrong forum: some issues, such as an individual's conduct or health, should be named to the chair privately, not to the meeting. Part of the judgement is knowing which room the avoided issue belongs in.", "phrasings": ["I wonder if we're circling something, and it might help to name it.", "Can I say what I think is underneath this, so we can deal with it directly?", "We've spent half an hour on the timetable, and I suspect the real question is whether we have the staff.", "I may be the only one thinking this, but I don't think I am: is this decision already made elsewhere?", "It seems to me the difficulty isn't the policy, it's that we don't have the money to implement it. Should we say so in the recommendation?", "I'd rather we discussed the thing we're all aware of than vote on something that doesn't address it.", "If it would help, I'll put it plainly: the question is whether the current arrangement is working.", "I think we're being very careful around one issue, and I'd suggest we'll make a better decision if we aren't."], "avoid": ["Let's be honest, the real problem here is Greg : names a person rather than the issue, turning observation into accusation.", "Am I the only one who sees the elephant in the room? : cliché and slightly theatrical, it performs courage rather than doing the work.", "I don't want to say anything controversial, but there might possibly be an issue nobody's mentioned : so hedged the room cannot tell what the issue is.", "Nobody here has the courage to say it, so I will : implies the room is cowardly and makes everyone defensive."], "scenarios": [{"id": "naming-what-the-room-is-avoiding-s1", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The board is discussing the decline in entries for the open division of the festival over three years. Members have proposed changes to the entry fee, the date and the adjudication format. Everyone knows, but no one has said, that the decline began when a long-serving adjudicator with a reputation for harsh public comments was appointed head adjudicator. He is a respected figure and a friend of several board members.", "said": "\"Perhaps it's the timing in the school year. Term 3 is very full for schools. If we moved to Term 2, we might pick up some of the bands that have dropped away.\"", "task": "Name the issue the board is avoiding, impersonally and in the board's interest, without attacking the adjudicator.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "Can I raise something that I think may be underneath this? We've looked at fees, date and format, and the decline began the year we changed the adjudication panel. I'm not suggesting anyone is at fault, but I've heard from several directors that the public comments have discouraged students. Should we ask schools directly before we change anything else?", "why": "It ties the issue to observable timing, attributes the concern to others' reports rather than to his view, and ends with an evidence-gathering step."}, {"text": "I think we may be circling one thing. Every director I've spoken to about not entering has mentioned the tone of adjudication, not the date or the fee. I'd suggest we survey last year's non-entering schools before we vote on anything, and include a question about adjudication. If I'm wrong, the survey will show it.", "why": "It names the issue as data rather than opinion and proposes a method that lets the board confirm or dismiss it without anyone losing face."}], "checks": ["Did I describe a pattern rather than name a person?", "Did I frame it as in the board's interest?", "Did I give the board a next step?", "Did I stop and let the silence sit?"]}, {"id": "naming-what-the-room-is-avoiding-s2", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The committee has spent an hour refining the wording of a new practical assessment for the Music ATAR course. Everyone knows that the Authority has already indicated it will not fund the additional examiners the new assessment would require, but the committee is carrying on as though it might. The chair has not raised it.", "said": "\"I think the descriptors for the improvisation criterion are much stronger now. If we tighten the language around stylistic awareness, I think this is ready to go forward.\"", "task": "Name the resourcing issue the committee is avoiding, so the recommendation is realistic.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "Before we finalise, could I name something I think we're all aware of? The Authority indicated last term that additional examiners aren't funded. If that's still the position, this assessment can't be delivered as written, however good the descriptors are. I'd suggest we either recommend it with an explicit resourcing condition, or design a version that works within existing examiner numbers. I'd rather we chose that knowingly.", "why": "It names the fact plainly, separates it from the quality of the work, and offers two realistic paths."}, {"text": "I think the descriptors are excellent, and I'm reluctant to say this. But we're refining an assessment that, on what we've been told, won't be resourced. It would help me to know from the chair whether that's changed. If not, I'd want our recommendation to say so explicitly, so the decision about resourcing is made by the people who hold it.", "why": "It credits the work, names the issue as a question to the chair, and puts responsibility where it belongs."}], "checks": ["Did I separate the issue from the quality of the work?", "Did I state the fact, not speculate?", "Did I offer a way to proceed?", "Did I avoid implying the chair was concealing it?"]}, {"id": "naming-what-the-room-is-avoiding-s3", "room": "school council", "setup": "You chair the school council. A proposal to introduce a new enrichment program is on the table. The school's facilities budget is overcommitted, and the council has been told at a previous meeting that the music building's refurbishment is deferred. Members keep discussing the enrichment program's merits. Nobody has connected it to the deferred refurbishment, which they all know about.", "said": "\"I think it's a wonderful idea. Our families have been asking for exactly this kind of program, and it would put us on a level with the private schools in the area.\"", "task": "As chair, name the budget trade-off the council is avoiding, neutrally.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "I'd like to name something that I think belongs in this discussion. We were told in Term 2 that the music building refurbishment is deferred for budget reasons. This program would draw on the same facilities budget. So the real question for council may be whether this is a higher priority than the refurbishment. I'd rather we answered that directly than endorse both.", "why": "It cites what the council was formally told, names the trade-off as the real question, and declines a false consensus."}, {"text": "Before we go further, I think it would help to say aloud what's implied. Supporting this program means choosing it over something else in a constrained budget, and the obvious candidate is the deferred refurbishment. I've an interest in that, so I'll declare it. Could the principal tell us what the program would cost, so council can weigh it fairly?", "why": "It names the trade-off and, crucially, declares his own interest as head of music, which keeps the naming clean."}], "checks": ["Did I declare my interest if I have one?", "Did I name a trade-off, not a preference?", "Did I cite something the council already knows?", "Did I stay neutral as chair?"]}, {"id": "naming-what-the-room-is-avoiding-s4", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "A university committee is reviewing a performance program whose enrolments have halved. The discussion has focused on marketing, scholarships and competitor programs. Everyone in the room knows that two senior staff in the program have been in open conflict for some time and that students have spoken about it, but no one has mentioned it.", "said": "\"I suspect the marketing has simply not kept pace. Our competitors are on every platform students use. We need a proper digital strategy and probably some targeted scholarships.\"", "task": "Name the issue being avoided in a way that is appropriate to the forum and does not single out individuals in the meeting.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Those are reasonable measures. Could I add one thing that I think we may be leaving aside? The exit surveys mention the culture of the program more often than marketing or cost. I'm not in a position to judge what's behind that, and this may not be the forum to explore it. But I'd suggest the review's terms include the student experience, so that it can be looked at properly.", "why": "It names the issue at the level of evidence and culture, not individuals, and recognises this is not the forum for the detail."}, {"text": "I'd like to suggest the review look beyond recruitment. Enrolment is one measure; whether students recommend the program to others is another, and that's partly about how the program feels from the inside. I'll leave the detail to the dean, who is better placed than this committee. But I don't think we'll fix the numbers by marketing alone.", "why": "It names the area without naming the conflict, hands it to the right person, and stops the room from settling on an incomplete answer."}], "checks": ["Did I avoid naming individuals?", "Did I use evidence rather than rumour?", "Did I respect the limits of this forum?", "Did I prevent the room settling on a proxy?"]}, {"id": "naming-what-the-room-is-avoiding-s5", "room": "department meeting", "setup": "In a music department meeting, the team is discussing why the junior concert band's results have slipped. Suggestions include rehearsal times and repertoire choice. Everyone knows the junior band has had four different conductors in eighteen months because of staffing changes, and that this is the most likely cause. Nobody wants to say it, partly because one of those conductors is in the room.", "said": "\"I wonder if the repertoire's been pitched too high. Some of those charts were ambitious for Year 7 and 8 players.\"", "task": "Name the continuity issue without implying any conductor is at fault.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "Repertoire may be part of it. I think the larger factor is one we all know: that band has had four conductors in eighteen months. None of that is anyone's fault; it's staffing. But young players need one consistent voice, and they haven't had it. I'd suggest we commit one conductor to them for the next two years, and judge the results after that.", "why": "It names the cause plainly, explicitly removes individual fault, and turns it into a structural commitment."}, {"text": "Can I put something on the table that I think is under this? Every conductor that band has had has been good. The problem is that there have been four of them. I'd rather we fixed continuity first, and then see whether the repertoire is still an issue.", "why": "It credits every conductor, including the one in the room, before naming the issue, and offers a sequence for testing it."}], "checks": ["Did I remove fault from any individual?", "Did I name the structural cause?", "Did I propose a concrete fix?", "Was it brief?"]}, {"id": "naming-what-the-room-is-avoiding-s6", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "You are meeting the principal and associate principal about a proposed restructure of performing arts leadership. They are discussing reporting lines and meeting schedules. You know, and they know you know, that the restructure has been shaped to resolve a long-running tension between two senior colleagues, and that the reporting lines being discussed reflect that rather than the needs of the program.", "said": "\"I think if we have music and drama both reporting through the performing arts head, and a monthly coordination meeting, that should make communication much clearer for everyone.\"", "task": "Name the purpose the structure is really serving, respectfully, so the decision can be made on its actual merits.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "Can I be candid, because I think it will help us get this right? I understand the structure is partly meant to settle some of the tension we've had in the area. That's a legitimate aim. But if that's the real purpose, I'd rather we designed for it directly, because reporting lines won't fix a working relationship. They'll just formalise it. Could we separate the two questions?", "why": "It names the purpose respectfully, grants that it is legitimate, and shows why conflating it with structure will fail."}, {"text": "I'd like to ask a direct question, because I think it's underneath this. Is the structure being designed around the program's needs, or around a difficulty between people? I can work with either, but they lead to different structures, and if it's the second, I'd want to be part of solving that properly, rather than having the reporting lines carry it.", "why": "It frames the naming as a question, keeps it to the issue rather than the individuals, and offers himself as part of the remedy."}], "checks": ["Did I name the purpose rather than the people?", "Did I grant the legitimacy of the aim?", "Did I explain why the avoided issue matters to the decision?", "Did I stay calm and brief?"]}]}, {"id": "procedural-intervention", "name": "The procedural intervention", "family": "Procedure", "what": "It steps outside the substance to correct the frame the substance is travelling in: what the item is, what the body may decide, what process was agreed, or whether the rules are being kept.", "when": "When the room is about to decide something it has no remit to decide, by a path it has not agreed, or on a proposal nobody has actually stated. It is strongest early, before positions harden, or at a seam where a motion is being framed.", "teach": "A procedural intervention addresses the frame rather than the picture. Its force lies in the fact that it is not an argument. In a room of people who argue for a living, a substantive objection invites a substantive rebuttal; a procedural one changes the terms of the exchange. Clever people concede a question of jurisdiction or sequence far more readily than a counter-argument, because it appeals to a standard everyone has already accepted and asks no one to admit they were wrong.\n\nSenior practitioners keep four species distinct. Scope asks whether the matter falls within the item, or within the body's remit at all. Process asks whether the agreed path (a paper, a consultation, a referral, a first reading) has been followed. Clarification asks what is actually being proposed before anyone debates it. A point of order proper is narrower: it alleges a breach of the rules or standing orders, it is addressed to the chair, and the chair rules on it before debate resumes, which is how Joske and Renton both treat it. Calling something a point of order when it is really a disagreement is the quickest way to spend credibility you will need later.\n\nExecution is short and addressed to the chair, not to the speaker. Name the category in the first clause ('A question of scope, Chair'), state the problem in one sentence, and offer a remedy: defer, refer, separate the motion, or note the concern and proceed. Then stop. The chair now owns the problem, and a good chair will be grateful to have been handed a solution with it. Twenty seconds is usually enough; forty is the outer edge.\n\nThe register is administrative, almost unexcited, which signals housekeeping rather than combat. Drop the voice a little and slow it. A light raised hand or a hand flat on the table catches the chair's eye; look at the chair while you speak and at nobody else, so that no colleague feels personally corrected.\n\nTiming carries much of the meaning. At a seam, the intervention reads as stewardship. In the middle of a colleague's best paragraph it reads as a tactic, however correct it is.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways. Used often, it marks you as someone who hides behind process, and the room starts discounting you. Used as disguised substance, a 'point of order' that is really 'I disagree', it invites a ruling against you in front of everyone. And used without a remedy, it merely stalls the room and leaves the chair irritated rather than helped.", "phrasings": ["A question of scope, Chair, before we go further: is this item about the assessment weighting or the content itself? The paper only authorises us to consider the first.", "Chair, may I raise a point of order? The motion now before us differs materially from the one circulated, and our rules require notice of that change.", "Purely on process: has the reference group seen this draft? If not, I wonder whether we are in a position to endorse it today rather than note it.", "Before we debate it, could we be clear about what is being proposed? I think I have heard two versions in the last ten minutes, and they are not the same.", "I raise this only as a matter of sequence. We are settling the budget line before we have agreed the program it is meant to fund.", "Chair, under our terms of reference I think this sits with the finance subcommittee. Might we refer it there and take a recommendation at the next meeting?", "Might we take the two parts of the motion separately? I suspect we agree on the first and not on the second, and I would rather not lose the first.", "A procedural observation, nothing more: we are past time on this item, and the next one carries a deadline. Do you want to extend, or carry it over?"], "avoid": ["Point of order! That's not what we're here to discuss. : Exclamatory and accusatory, and almost certainly not a point of order; the chair will rule against you and the room will remember the tone.", "With the greatest respect, I really must insist that we follow proper procedure here. : Pompous and vague; it names no rule, offers no remedy, and implies colleagues are behaving improperly.", "I'm not sure if this is a procedural thing, but I just wonder whether maybe we should possibly be talking about this at all? : Hedged into nothing; the move lives on its precision, and this has none.", "Just procedurally, I think this whole proposal is misconceived. : Substance in a procedural costume; it forfeits the neutrality that gives the intervention its force."], "scenarios": [{"id": "procedural-intervention-s1", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The committee is reviewing the content section of the revised Year 11 Music syllabus. The agenda item is content only; assessment is scheduled for a separate meeting with the assessment officers present. A senior moderator has begun arguing that the practical component's weighting should move, and two members are warming to the idea. If the committee drifts into an informal view on weighting, it will be cited later as the committee's position.", "said": "I take the point that this is a content item, but honestly the content and the weighting are inseparable. If we are expanding the composition strand, then forty per cent on performance is simply indefensible. I would like the committee to signal, today, that we expect that figure to come down.", "task": "Hold the item to its scope without dismissing the substance of her point.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "A question of scope, Chair. I suspect Margaret is right that the two are linked, and that is exactly why I would be cautious about signalling anything today. Weighting is listed for the assessment meeting, when the officers who model the consequences will be at the table. Could we minute that the committee considers the composition change has implications for weighting, and refer that explicitly to the assessment meeting? That preserves the point without pre-empting it.", "why": "It concedes the link, names the scope problem, and turns her concern into a minuted referral, so she loses nothing and the committee commits to nothing."}, {"text": "Chair, may I ask what we are being invited to do? If it is to note that composition and weighting interact, I think we all agree. If it is to form a view on the forty per cent, I do not think this item, or this group without the assessment officers, is the place for it. I would be grateful for a ruling before we go further.", "why": "It separates the harmless version of the proposal from the improper one and puts the decision squarely with the chair."}], "checks": ["Did I name the category (scope) in the first sentence?", "Did I address the chair rather than the moderator?", "Did I offer a remedy, such as a minuted referral, rather than just a refusal?", "Did I stay under forty seconds and stop?"]}, {"id": "procedural-intervention-s2", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "He is treasurer of the association that runs the state schools' jazz festival. Late in general business, a board member moves that school entry fees rise by fifteen per cent for next year. The association's rules require fee changes to be on notice in the agenda. The proposal may well be sound, but a fee rise passed without notice would be open to challenge by member schools.", "said": "I know this is general business, but we are all here and the numbers are obvious. Costs are up, the venue has gone up, and we keep subsidising entries. I move that entry fees rise by fifteen per cent from next year. I would rather we just decide it than lose another two months.", "task": "Raise a genuine point of order without opposing the fee rise itself.", "seconds": 35, "models": [{"text": "Point of order, Chair. Under our rules a change to fees has to be on notice, and this was not on the agenda. As treasurer I am sympathetic to the substance, and I would be happy to bring a costed paper to the next meeting so the motion can be properly moved. If we pass it tonight, any member school could fairly challenge it.", "why": "It is a true point of order, cites the rule, separates procedure from substance, and offers the treasurer's own work as the remedy."}, {"text": "Chair, before this is seconded: I think fee changes require notice under the rules, and I would ask you to rule on that. If that is right, might the board instead resolve tonight that the treasurer bring a fee proposal on notice next meeting? That gets us most of the way without leaving the decision exposed.", "why": "It asks for a ruling rather than asserting one and offers a resolution the board can pass tonight, so the mover's urgency is honoured."}], "checks": ["Did I cite a rule rather than a preference?", "Did I make clear I was not opposing the fee rise?", "Did I propose a way to proceed tonight?", "Did I leave the ruling to the chair?"]}, {"id": "procedural-intervention-s3", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "A new Bachelor of Music major is listed for final approval. He sits on the board as an external member from the school sector. The version before the board is the third draft; the faculty curriculum committee approved the second, and the third changes the prerequisite structure substantially. The Deputy Vice-Chancellor is keen to approve it so that it can be marketed for next year's intake.", "said": "I appreciate there have been late refinements, but these are improvements and the faculty has been through this in great detail. We are under real time pressure with the prospectus. Unless anyone has a substantive objection, I would propose we approve the major as presented.", "task": "Raise the process gap without appearing to obstruct a proposal the DVC wants.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "I have no substantive objection, Deputy Vice-Chancellor, and the changes look sensible. My concern is narrower. The faculty committee approved the second draft, and the prerequisite changes in the third are the kind it would normally see. Is there a way to approve today subject to the faculty chair confirming the revised prerequisites out of session? That protects the timetable and the process at once.", "why": "It removes any suggestion of obstruction, names the specific gap, and proposes a conditional approval that keeps the DVC's deadline intact."}, {"text": "Purely on process: could the secretary confirm whether the faculty committee has seen the current prerequisites? If it has, I am content. If it has not, I would prefer we approve in principle and delegate final sign-off to the chair once the faculty has confirmed, rather than have the board approve something its own committee has not.", "why": "It frames the point as a question of fact first, so it can dissolve cleanly, and offers a delegated remedy if it does not."}], "checks": ["Did I say early that I had no objection to the substance?", "Did I identify the exact step that was skipped?", "Did my remedy keep the deadline intact?"]}, {"id": "procedural-intervention-s4", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "He sits on a state arts funding panel assessing project grants against four published criteria. Discussion of a contemporary jazz collective's application has turned to the leader's public comments about the funding body itself. Two panellists are using those comments to mark the application down. Assessment outside the published criteria would expose the decision to complaint and is unfair to the applicant.", "said": "I just find it hard to separate the application from the person. He has been openly contemptuous of this agency in the press. Surely capacity to deliver includes some judgement about whether this is someone we can work with. I would mark it down on the third criterion.", "task": "Return the panel to the published criteria without defending the applicant's comments.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "Could I bring us back to the criteria for a moment? Capacity to deliver, as published, is about track record, resources and the project plan. His public comments may well be unwise, but they are not evidence on any of those. If we let them shape the score, we are assessing something applicants were not told we would assess, and the decision becomes hard to defend.", "why": "It concedes nothing about the comments themselves, reads the criterion back to the room, and names the institutional risk calmly."}, {"text": "A question of scope, Chair. I think the panel's remit is the four published criteria and nothing else. If there is a genuine concern about the working relationship, that seems a matter for the agency's officers, not for scoring. Might we score on the published criteria and note the concern separately for staff?", "why": "It gives the concern a legitimate destination so the panellists are not simply told to drop it."}], "checks": ["Did I avoid defending or criticising the applicant personally?", "Did I state the criterion's actual content?", "Did I offer a proper place for the concern?"]}, {"id": "procedural-intervention-s5", "room": "faculty curriculum committee", "setup": "A university faculty curriculum committee is considering a motion from the head of performance. The motion bundles two things: replacing the first-year aural unit with a broader musicianship unit, and cutting contact hours for that unit from three to two. He supports the first and has serious doubts about the second. If the motion is voted as one, he will have to oppose a change he wants.", "said": "These two things really go together. The new unit is designed to be leaner, and the hours are part of the design. I would rather we take the motion as it stands than unpick it. We have discussed this at length and I think the committee is ready.", "task": "Ask for the motion to be divided, with a reason that does not reopen the whole debate.", "seconds": 35, "models": [{"text": "Chair, might we take the motion in two parts? I strongly support the new musicianship unit and would like to vote for it. I am less persuaded about the reduction in hours, and I suspect I am not alone. Dividing it lets the committee show its real support for the first part without forcing anyone into a single vote on both.", "why": "It leads with support, gives a reason that serves the committee rather than himself, and asks the chair for a standard procedural remedy."}, {"text": "Could I ask that the question be divided? The unit and the contact hours are separable on the face of the motion, and I think the minutes would more accurately reflect the committee's view if each were put separately. If the hours are as integral as Paul says, the committee will carry both.", "why": "It justifies the division on accuracy and quietly invites the mover to trust that his argument will survive a separate vote."}], "checks": ["Did I state which part I support?", "Did I avoid rearguing the hours?", "Did I ask the chair rather than the mover?"]}, {"id": "procedural-intervention-s6", "room": "school council", "setup": "He chairs the school council. During general business, a parent member, an experienced barrister, raises complaints about a particular teacher's marking and asks the council to request a report on that teacher. Staff management is outside the council's functions and belongs to the principal. The principal is present. The parent's underlying concern about consistency of marking is legitimate.", "said": "I think the council has a responsibility here. Several families have raised this with me, and the pattern is consistent. I am not asking for anything disciplinary, simply for the principal to report back to council on this teacher's marking practices so we can be satisfied.", "task": "As chair, rule on scope firmly but courteously, and redirect the legitimate concern.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Thank you, Catherine. I am going to rule that a report on an individual teacher is outside the council's functions; staff matters sit with the principal, and I think council needs to be scrupulous about that line. What is within our remit is the school's approach to marking consistency in general. If you would like, we could ask the principal for a short paper on moderation practice across the school for the next meeting.", "why": "It rules clearly as chair, explains the boundary in one sentence, and converts the complaint into an agenda item the council can properly hold."}, {"text": "Catherine, I think we need to separate two things. Concerns about an individual teacher are for the principal, and I would encourage the families to raise them directly, perhaps with your help. The broader question of how marking is moderated is properly ours. I will put that on the next agenda. Lesley, would that be workable from your side?", "why": "It draws the line without the word 'ruling', gives the families a route, and brings the principal in as a partner rather than a defendant."}], "checks": ["Did I decide clearly as chair rather than inviting debate on the boundary?", "Did I avoid any comment on the individual teacher?", "Did I give the legitimate concern a proper destination?", "Did I bring the principal in?"]}]}, {"id": "taking-it-on-notice", "name": "Taking it on notice", "family": "Procedure", "what": "It converts a question you cannot answer well in the moment into a precise commitment, so that the gap in your knowledge becomes evidence of rigour rather than of weakness.", "when": "When a question turns on a fact, figure or source you do not have to hand, or on advice you have no standing to give, and an improvised answer would be wrong, partial or later quoted against you.", "teach": "Learned rooms punish bluffing and reward exactness about the limits of what one knows. Taking a question on notice uses that norm. Australians in public life will recognise the convention from Senate estimates, where officials routinely take questions on notice and the committee expects written answers. The practice works because it has a shape: a clear statement of what will be provided, by when, and through which channel. Without that shape, it is simply evasion with better manners.\n\nThe move has four parts. First, show you have understood the question, ideally by restating its core in a clause: 'the number of schools whose practical marks would move'. This proves the deferral is not a failure of comprehension. Second, give whatever you can responsibly say now, briefly: the principle, the direction, the order of magnitude, or what the answer depends on. Third, make the commitment specific: the deliverable, the date, and the route (to the secretary for circulation, in the next treasurer's report, by email to the questioner). Fourth, stop, and return the floor to the chair.\n\nThe partial answer is where most people undo themselves. Anxious speakers, and fast associative thinkers in particular, begin with a sentence of context and find themselves improvising the full answer they have just said they cannot give. Two sentences at most. If you notice yourself saying 'and also', you have left the move.\n\nThe voice should be level and unhurried, with no apologetic laugh. Look at the questioner while restating their question, then at the chair when committing, so the commitment is made to the meeting and will be minuted. If the chair does not minute it, ask: 'Could the minutes record that?' Then deliver, on time, and refer to it at the next meeting. That last step is what makes the move credible the next time you use it.\n\nDistinguish fact questions from judgement questions. A figure can go on notice; your view usually cannot. If someone asks whether you support a change, taking it on notice looks like ducking. Answer the principle now and take the supporting data on notice.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways. As a reflex for every hard question, it becomes a reputation for never knowing. Vaguely ('I'll get back to you on that'), it commits to nothing and everyone hears it. And undelivered, it converts one small gap into a lasting doubt about your reliability.", "phrasings": ["That is a fair question, and I would rather give you the right number than a near one. I will take it on notice and circulate it through the secretary by Friday.", "I can tell you the direction now: the figure has risen. The exact variance I would like to confirm against the ledger, and I will include it in the next treasurer's report.", "Let me take the detail on notice. The principle I can answer now: I would not support any change that disadvantages regional candidates.", "I do not want to paraphrase the study loosely in this company. May I send you the reference and the relevant page this afternoon?", "That turns on legal advice we have not sought. I would propose the council take it on notice and that I write to the department this week.", "If I understand you, the question is how many schools would see their practical marks move. I do not have that modelling, but I can ask the assessment officers for it before the next meeting.", "I will take that on notice. Could the minutes record it against my name, with a date of the twelfth?"], "avoid": ["Um, I'm not sure off the top of my head, I'd have to check, I think it might be around... : Thinking aloud in public; it signals unpreparedness and produces a half-number that will be quoted back to you.", "I'll get back to you on that. : No deliverable, no date, no channel; educated listeners hear it as polite evasion.", "I'm terribly sorry, I really should know this, I apologise, I'll find out. : Over-apology turns a routine deferral into a confession and lowers your standing for the rest of the meeting.", "I'd rather not answer that now. : Sounds like concealment; a deferral needs a reason and a commitment, not a refusal."], "scenarios": [{"id": "taking-it-on-notice-s1", "room": "SCSA assessment committee", "setup": "The committee is weighing a proposal to change how the ATAR Music practical examination is scaled against the written component. An assessment officer from a university asks him a precise question about how many schools would be materially affected. He knows the general pattern from his own and neighbouring schools but has no system data. The proposal will go to consultation in a month.", "said": "Dijon, you have been the most supportive of this change, so let me ask you directly. How many schools would actually see a material shift in their candidates' combined marks? If it is a handful, this is a technical adjustment. If it is half the cohort, we are talking about something else entirely.", "task": "Take the figure on notice while keeping his position credible.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "That is exactly the right question, and I do not have the system figure. From my own school and the three I moderate with, the shift would be small and in both directions, but four schools are not a cohort. I would suggest we ask the officers to model it on last year's data before the consultation draft goes out, and I am happy to frame the request with them this week.", "why": "It admits the gap plainly, offers a properly qualified partial answer, and turns the deferral into a concrete request with an owner."}, {"text": "Let me take that on notice rather than guess. What I can say is that my support rests on the principle, that the practical should not be scaled down for being practical, and I would want to see the numbers before I claimed it was merely technical. Could we minute a request to the officers for that modelling by the next meeting?", "why": "It separates his judgement, which he answers now, from the fact, which he defers, and asks for the commitment to be minuted."}], "checks": ["Did I restate or acknowledge the exact question?", "Did I limit the partial answer to two sentences?", "Did I name who will provide what, by when?", "Did I avoid apologising?"]}, {"id": "taking-it-on-notice-s2", "room": "festival association annual general meeting", "setup": "He is presenting the treasurer's report to member schools at the festival association's AGM. A deputy principal from a member school, a former accountant, notices that sponsorship income is down by a significant amount on last year and asks why. He knows one sponsor did not renew but has not reconciled whether the rest is timing or a real loss. Members are paying close attention to fees.", "said": "Thank you for the report. Can I ask about sponsorship? It is down quite markedly on last year. Is that a lost sponsor, a timing issue, or something more structural? Because if it is structural, it rather changes how we should think about the fee discussion later.", "task": "Answer what he can, take the rest on notice, and protect confidence in the accounts.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Part of it I can explain now: one major sponsor did not renew this year, and that accounts for a good share of the gap. Whether the remainder is timing or a genuine fall I want to confirm against the agreements rather than guess. I will circulate a short note to members within a fortnight, and I would suggest the fee discussion proceeds on the assumption that it is structural until I show otherwise.", "why": "It gives the known fact, defers the unknown, sets a date, and handles the link to fees conservatively, which a former accountant will respect."}, {"text": "That is the right distinction to draw. I can confirm one non-renewal; the rest I have not yet reconciled, and I would rather not characterise it until I have. I will take it on notice and report to members by the end of the month. If that leaves the fee item uncertain, the board could reasonably defer the fee decision to its next meeting.", "why": "It credits the questioner's framing, commits to a date, and offers the room a procedural consequence instead of leaving the uncertainty hanging."}], "checks": ["Did I give the known fact before the deferral?", "Did I commit to a date and channel for members?", "Did I address the implication for the fee discussion?"]}, {"id": "taking-it-on-notice-s3", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "He is meeting the principal and the associate principal about next year's staffing and resourcing for the specialist music program. The associate principal, who is rigorous with numbers, asks for the per-student cost of the program compared with the school's other specialist programs. He knows his own program's costs closely but not how the other programs' figures are calculated. A loose comparison could damage his program.", "said": "Before we go further, it would help me to know what the program costs per student, set against the other specialist programs. I am not suggesting it is out of line. But if we are going to argue for more instrumental time, I need to be able to defend the number to the business manager.", "task": "Defer the comparison without seeming to avoid scrutiny.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "I can give you our own figure with confidence. What I would be wary of is putting it beside the other programs before we know they are counted the same way, because instrumental staffing is often booked differently. Could I work with the business manager on a like-for-like comparison and bring it to you by the end of next week?", "why": "It volunteers his own number, names the methodological risk without sounding defensive, and offers a joint process with a date."}, {"text": "Let me take the comparison on notice. I would rather it be right than quick, and I would rather the business manager agree the method before either of us quotes it. I will have something to you by Friday week, and in the meantime I can send our own costing today.", "why": "It frames the deferral as protecting the associate principal's credibility as much as his own, and gives him something immediately."}], "checks": ["Did I offer what I did know straight away?", "Did I explain the reason for deferring in one sentence?", "Did I name a date?"]}, {"id": "taking-it-on-notice-s4", "room": "university music school advisory committee", "setup": "He sits on a university music school's advisory committee as a school-sector representative. The committee is discussing a proposed first-year theory unit. A professor asks whether the unit's assumed entry knowledge matches what students actually bring from the ATAR Music course. He knows the syllabus intimately but not the latest examiners' report on theory performance.", "said": "You would know better than anyone here. Are we pitching this at what students genuinely arrive with? My impression is that harmonic analysis has been weakening for some years, but impressions are cheap. I would rather design the unit from evidence than from anecdote.", "task": "Give what the syllabus establishes and take the performance evidence on notice.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "On what the syllabus requires, I can answer now: the harmonic content examinable in each unit is set out explicitly, and it is more demanding than people often assume. On how well students actually perform on it, I would rather not trade impressions either. The examiners' reports comment on that each year. I will pull the relevant sections from the last three and send them to you before the next meeting.", "why": "It separates the documentary fact from the performance evidence and commits to exactly the evidence the professor asked for."}, {"text": "Let me take the performance question on notice. I can say what is assessed; whether students are meeting it is a separate matter, and the examiners' reports are the proper source. I will circulate the relevant extracts through the secretary within a fortnight, and if useful I can ask two colleagues who mark the theory paper for their reading.", "why": "It honours his empirical stance and adds a second, credible source without overpromising."}], "checks": ["Did I separate what I know from what I do not?", "Did I name the source I will use?", "Did I avoid offering my own impression as evidence?"]}, {"id": "taking-it-on-notice-s5", "room": "school council", "setup": "He chairs the school council. A parent member asks whether the school can lawfully require families to pay instrumental hire charges for students in the specialist program, or whether these must be voluntary. He has views but no legal advice, and the question has implications beyond music. The principal is present but has not been asked.", "said": "I am asking this as a genuine question, not a complaint. Several families have asked me whether the instrument hire charge is compulsory or voluntary. I am not sure the school's documents are clear, and I think council should know where we stand before families start refusing to pay.", "task": "As chair, take the legal question on notice properly and avoid giving an informal answer.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "Thank you, that is a proper question for council, and I do not think any of us should answer it informally. I would propose that we take it on notice, that the principal seek the department's position on the charge as it is currently framed, and that we receive a short written answer at the next meeting. Lesley, is that workable? I will ask the minutes to record it.", "why": "It refuses to improvise a legal view, routes the question to the proper authority, and secures the principal's agreement and a minute."}, {"text": "I am going to take that on notice for council. It turns on the department's rules about charges, and I would rather we have the actual position in writing than our impressions of it. Could I ask the principal to obtain it, and in the meantime could the families be told the question is being considered? We will return to it next meeting.", "why": "It defers the substance but responds to the immediate concern about families, so the deferral does not leave the parent empty-handed."}], "checks": ["Did I avoid stating a legal position myself?", "Did I name who will obtain the answer?", "Did I ensure it was minuted with a timeframe?"]}, {"id": "taking-it-on-notice-s6", "room": "conference panel", "setup": "He is speaking on a panel at a national music education conference about sustaining specialist programs in public schools. In passing he referred to research on the link between ensemble participation and retention. A university researcher in the audience asks for the specific study. He remembers the finding but not the authors or year with certainty.", "said": "Thank you, that was really interesting. You mentioned research linking ensemble participation to retention in the later years. Could you tell us which study that is? I ask because the literature I know is rather more mixed than that, and I would like to look at the design.", "task": "Decline to cite from uncertain memory and commit to providing the source.", "seconds": 35, "models": [{"text": "A fair challenge, and I would rather not cite it from memory in a room that will check. Let me send you the reference this afternoon. You may well be right that the wider literature is mixed; my point was narrower, about our own program, and I should have framed it that way.", "why": "It refuses to bluff, makes a quick commitment, and gracefully narrows the claim without collapsing it."}, {"text": "I will take that on notice: I want to give you the exact paper, not my recollection of it. If you find me afterwards I will take your email. And I take the point about the mixed evidence. I would be interested in what you are reading, because the design question matters.", "why": "It converts a public challenge into a professional exchange and signals respect for the questioner's expertise."}], "checks": ["Did I avoid naming an author or year I was not sure of?", "Did I make a specific commitment?", "Did I adjust the claim honestly rather than defending it?"]}]}, {"id": "declaring-an-interest", "name": "Declaring an interest", "family": "Procedure", "what": "It puts a personal, financial or role-based interest on the record before the item is discussed and proposes how you will participate, so the decision stays clean and your standing on everything else rises.", "when": "At the adoption of the agenda or the opening of the item, whenever a reasonable observer might think your judgement could be affected: money, a close relationship, a second role, or an outcome that benefits you or an organisation you represent.", "teach": "In rooms of senior people, the legitimacy of a decision usually matters more than any single member's view on it. A clean declaration protects the decision, and it also does something for the declarer: it signals that you understand the institution better than your own interest. A declaration made early and economically raises your credibility on every other item. One that is extracted by someone else, or made after you have already spoken, does lasting damage even where the interest was trivial.\n\nSenior practitioners distinguish three kinds. A material personal interest is the familiar one: money, property, employment, a family member's benefit. For incorporated associations in Western Australia, the Associations Incorporation Act 2015 (WA) requires a committee member with a material personal interest in a matter being considered to disclose it, and the association's rules will usually say what follows. A conflict of duty arises when you wear two hats, for example as an association officer and as the head of a department whose students compete under that association. A perceived interest is one a fair-minded observer might suspect even where none exists in fact. All three are declared; the remedy differs.\n\nThe declaration itself has three parts: the interest, stated precisely and without decoration ('I have performed regularly with one of the nominated adjudicators'); a proposal for how you will participate (remain and not vote, leave for deliberation, or remain at the chair's discretion); and a handing back to the chair, who decides. Ask for it to be minuted. Do not add an assurance that it will not affect you. The disclosure exists precisely because no one, including you, can be the judge of that.\n\nStepping back gracefully is physical. If you are leaving, gather your papers and go without commentary. If you are staying but not voting, sit back, put the pen down, and keep silent unless the chair asks you a question of fact. If you are asked for information, give it in the form of facts, then say, 'I will leave the deliberation to colleagues.' If you chair, hand the chair to the deputy for the item, and move seats if the room allows it.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways. Over-declaring every acquaintance clutters the minutes, reads as anxiety, and can be used to exclude you from items where your expertise is needed. Declaring and then advocating, from the floor or by facial expression, makes the declaration worse than useless. And a speech about your integrity invites exactly the scepticism it is meant to forestall.", "phrasings": ["Before we take this item, Chair, I should declare an interest: one of the nominated adjudicators is a regular colleague of mine in performance. I would propose to leave the room for the discussion.", "I need to declare a conflict of duty on this item. My own school is a likely applicant, so I will not take part in the deliberation or the vote. Could that be minuted?", "For the record, I co-wrote one of the resources on the shortlist. I am happy to answer factual questions about it if that helps, and then to step out.", "I should note that I know this applicant well; she was a student of mine some years ago. I leave it to you, Chair, whether I should remain for this one.", "I will hand the chair to Rachel for this item, since it concerns my own department's program, and I will speak, if at all, only to matters of fact.", "I have a perceived interest rather than an actual one, but I would rather it be on the record. I propose to remain, take no part, and abstain.", "Thank you. I will leave the deliberation to colleagues."], "avoid": ["I suppose I should probably mention that I sort of know one of these people, but it honestly won't affect me at all. : Hedged and self-exonerating; the assurance is exactly what a declaration cannot give.", "In the interests of complete transparency, and because integrity matters a great deal to me, I want to make a full and frank disclosure... : A speech about one's own virtue invites suspicion and wastes the room's time.", "I'll declare an interest, but I do want to say a few words in support before I step out. : Declaring and then advocating is worse than not declaring; it shows you knew and spoke anyway.", "I should declare that I have met everyone on this list at some conference or other. : Over-declaring trivialises the mechanism and can be used to sideline you from items where your expertise is needed."], "scenarios": [{"id": "declaring-an-interest-s1", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The festival association board is appointing adjudicators for next year's festival. He is treasurer. One of the three recommended jazz adjudicators is a pianist he performs with regularly and has done for years. He believes she is the best candidate. His own school's ensembles will be adjudicated.", "said": "The subcommittee has recommended three names for the jazz panel, and I think they are strong. Unless there is discussion, I would like to move that we appoint all three. Dijon, you know the scene better than any of us. Do you have a view on the pianist?", "task": "Declare the interest before giving any view, and step back from the decision.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "Before I say anything, I should declare two interests. I perform regularly with her, and my own school's bands will be adjudicated at the festival. I do not think I should offer a view on her appointment, so I propose to leave the room for this item and take no part in the vote. Could that be minuted, please?", "why": "It declares both the relationship and the conflict of duty, refuses the invitation to comment, and secures the minute."}, {"text": "Thank you, but I need to declare an interest first. She and I play together regularly, and my school competes. I can confirm factual matters, such as her adjudicating experience elsewhere, if the board wants them, and then I will step out. I would rather the board's view not be coloured by mine.", "why": "It offers usefulness strictly confined to facts, makes the exit clear, and frames it as protecting the board."}], "checks": ["Did I declare before saying anything about her merits?", "Did I name both the relationship and my school's involvement?", "Did I propose a specific form of withdrawal?", "Did I ask for the minute?"]}, {"id": "declaring-an-interest-s2", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The committee is selecting recommended listening and analysis resources to accompany the revised syllabus. One of the five shortlisted packages is a resource set he co-authored with colleagues, which has been used widely across the state. The other committee members know he is a co-author but nobody has raised it. The selection will be published under SCSA's name.", "said": "We have five packages. Frankly I think two of them are clearly stronger than the others, and one of those is the set many of us already use in schools. I would welcome views. It would be good to get this to two names before the break.", "task": "Declare his authorship and decide how far to participate.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "Chair, I should declare a direct interest: I am a co-author of one of the shortlisted packages. I think that means I should take no part in comparing them. I am happy to answer factual questions about how the set was built, if the committee wants that, but I propose to step out for the deliberation itself.", "why": "It names the interest plainly, accepts the consequence, and offers factual help in a bounded way."}, {"text": "Before views are taken, I need to put on the record that I co-wrote one of these. Given it will be published under the Authority's name, I think the cleanest course is for me to leave the room for this item. I will not comment on the others either, because any comparison from me would be compromised.", "why": "It reasons from the publication stakes and recognises that commenting on rival packages is just as compromised as praising his own."}], "checks": ["Did I declare even though everyone already knew?", "Did I refrain from commenting on any of the five?", "Did I state the remedy rather than leave it hanging?"]}, {"id": "declaring-an-interest-s3", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "He sits on a state arts funding panel for music projects. The next application is from a young bandleader he taught at school eight years ago and has since hired twice for gigs. He has no financial stake. The panel is small and his jazz expertise is the main reason he was appointed.", "said": "Next is the quintet recording project. I will be honest, I am not sure how to assess the jazz side of this, so I was hoping Dijon would lead us through it. It looks ambitious, and the budget is tight.", "task": "Declare a moderate interest proportionately, and let the chair decide on participation.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "I should declare an interest before I lead anything. I taught the bandleader at school and have hired him twice since. There is no financial connection, but a reasonable observer might see a relationship. I am in your hands, Chair. I can offer general comments on what a project like this usually costs and requires, without scoring it, or I can step out entirely.", "why": "It discloses proportionately, gives the chair two real options, and preserves the panel's access to his general expertise."}, {"text": "Before I do, I have a relationship to declare: he was my student and I have since employed him on gigs. I think I should not score this one. If it would help, I can describe the general standards the panel might apply to a jazz recording project, and leave the assessment of this application to colleagues.", "why": "It proposes its own remedy and offers help at the level of criteria rather than the applicant."}], "checks": ["Did I state the relationship precisely without minimising it?", "Did I separate general expertise from this application?", "Did I leave the final call to the chair?"]}, {"id": "declaring-an-interest-s4", "room": "school council", "setup": "He chairs the school council. The next item is the principal's proposal to reallocate a portion of the specialist program budget across the arts programs, including the music program he leads. Council's role is to endorse the budget framework. Several members will look to him for a view.", "said": "That brings us to the arts program allocation. Lesley's paper is in the pack. I imagine you will want to lead this one, Dijon, given it is your area. Should we just go straight to questions?", "task": "Hand the chair to the deputy and limit his own role to facts.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "Thank you, but I think I should step out of the chair for this item. It directly concerns the program I lead, and I have an obvious interest in its outcome. Rachel, would you take the chair? I will remain only to answer questions of fact if members have them, and I will abstain on any endorsement. Could the minutes record that?", "why": "It hands the chair over cleanly, defines exactly how far he will participate, and asks for the minute."}, {"text": "I need to declare an interest here, as head of one of the affected programs, so I will hand over to the deputy chair for this item. If council wants to know how the music figures were built, I can explain the arithmetic. I will not offer a view on the allocation itself.", "why": "It draws a clear line between arithmetic and advocacy, which is the line that matters."}], "checks": ["Did I hand over the chair rather than chairing my own item?", "Did I define the limit of my participation?", "Did I abstain from any endorsement?"]}, {"id": "declaring-an-interest-s5", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "He sits on a university academic board as an external member. A proposal is tabled for an early-entry pathway that would give guaranteed interviews to students from a small number of schools with specialist music programs, his own school among them. The pathway would help his students. Other schools in the state are not included.", "said": "This pathway is modest in scale and the faculty has worked hard on it. It recognises the quality of the specialist programs. Dijon, you are closer to those programs than anyone here. I would be glad to hear whether it would work on the ground.", "task": "Declare his school's benefit and contribute only in a way that does not compromise the board's decision.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "I should declare an interest first: my school is one of the named schools, and my students would benefit directly. So I do not think I should comment on whether it is a good idea. I can say, as a matter of fact, how specialist programs in the state are structured, if that helps the board judge the selection of schools. Beyond that I will abstain.", "why": "It declares the benefit, refuses the evaluative question, and offers only the factual context that bears on fairness."}, {"text": "Thank you, but I need to declare that my own school would benefit, so my view on whether it works would not be disinterested. If the board wants information, I would suggest the faculty consult principals from schools both inside and outside the proposed list. I will leave the decision to colleagues and abstain.", "why": "It goes further than self-exclusion and suggests a fairer evidential process, which shows institutional judgement."}], "checks": ["Did I declare before answering the question put to me?", "Did I avoid evaluating the proposal?", "Did I abstain explicitly?"]}, {"id": "declaring-an-interest-s6", "room": "school selection panel", "setup": "He is on a three-person panel to appoint a new instrumental teacher, with a deputy principal and a human resources officer. Reading the shortlist, he sees that one candidate is a bassist he has performed with for years and considers a friend. Interviews start tomorrow. Panel procedures require disclosure of any close relationship with a candidate.", "said": "So we have five on the shortlist. I suggest we interview all five and score independently before we compare. Before we lock the schedule, is there anything anyone needs to raise about any of the candidates?", "task": "Disclose the friendship and accept the procedural consequence without fuss.", "seconds": 35, "models": [{"text": "Yes, I need to declare a close relationship. One of the candidates is a long-standing performing colleague and a friend. I think the procedure requires that to be noted, and I suspect it means I should not sit on that interview, or perhaps on the panel at all. I would rather you and HR decide which, and I will accept either.", "why": "It discloses fully, names the consequence honestly, and leaves the decision with the people properly placed to make it."}, {"text": "I do. One of the five is a close friend I have performed with for many years. I would ask that it be recorded, and I would suggest a replacement panellist for the whole process, so that no candidate can argue the scoring was uneven. I can recommend someone with the right instrumental background if that helps.", "why": "It takes the more protective option and offers to solve the problem it has created."}], "checks": ["Did I describe the relationship as 'close' rather than minimise it?", "Did I propose a remedy?", "Did I avoid any comment on the candidate's suitability?"]}]}, {"id": "handling-the-dominant-member", "name": "Handling the dominant member", "family": "Procedure", "what": "It redistributes airtime away from a member who is monopolising the discussion, without contesting their status or turning the room's attention into a contest.", "when": "When one person has spoken repeatedly, others have visibly withdrawn, and the discussion has narrowed to that person's frame; or when quieter members with relevant expertise have not yet been heard.", "teach": "Most dominant members are not trying to silence anyone. They repeat because they do not feel registered, or because nobody has given the room a structure that makes them stop. The move therefore has two components: an accurate acknowledgement that removes the motive for repetition, and a structural change that redistributes the floor. Educated rooms close ranks quickly against public correction of a colleague, so status must be preserved; the aim is to make the dominant member's point part of the record and to make it natural for others to speak.\n\nAs chair, you have structural tools. Summarise the member's point, accurately and generously, in one sentence, then say that you will now hear from others. Go round the table. Name a specific person whose expertise is relevant: 'Anna, you have run this assessment in a regional school; how does it look from there?' Set a rule that applies to everyone ('Let us hear from those who have not yet spoken'). If the member interjects, a flat palm and 'I will come back to you, Robert' is usually enough. The first intervention should be light; the second, if needed, is firmer and shorter.\n\nAs a member, you have fewer tools and must not usurp the chair. The usual approach is a bridge: take the dominant point in a clause, then turn it into a question addressed to someone else. 'Robert's point about workload seems central. I would be interested to know whether the regional members see it the same way.' Alternatively, address the chair: 'Chair, I would value hearing from colleagues who have not spoken yet.' If the dominant member is the chair, the bridge is the only safe tool, and it should be framed as a request for information, never as a complaint about process.\n\nWait for a breath or the end of a sentence before you come in. Interrupting mid-clause turns the move into a contest. The voice is warm and decisive, slightly quicker than usual, and uses the member's name. An open hand turned towards the next speaker does a great deal of the work.\n\nIt goes wrong in four ways. Visible irritation or a sardonic summary shames the member and the room sympathises with them. Summaries that are too long become condescending. Fighting fire with fire, talking over the member, makes you the second dominant voice. And waiting too long means frustration leaks into your tone, which is far harder to recover from than an early, light redirection.", "phrasings": ["Thank you, Robert; I think the point is clear, and it is on the record. I would like to hear now from those who have not yet spoken.", "Let me go round the table on this, starting with Anna, and come back to you at the end, Robert.", "Professor Hale's concern is that the change compromises rigour. I would be interested to know whether the teachers in the room see the same risk in their classrooms.", "Chair, I would value hearing from the regional members before we go further; they are the ones who will carry this.", "I want to make sure I have your point exactly, so that we do not lose it: you think the timeline is unrealistic. Now, Sam, you have done this before. Is it?", "I am going to hold you there, if I may. I will come back to you.", "Let us hear three views we have not had yet, briefly, and then I will bring us back to the motion."], "avoid": ["Can someone else please get a word in? : Shames the member publicly and expresses frustration rather than offering a structure; the room will side with the person rebuked.", "With respect, Robert, you've made that point several times now. : Accurate but hostile; it forces a status contest and will often prompt a defensive repetition.", "Sorry, sorry, I just, can I just jump in here quickly? : Apologetic and scrambling; it sounds like you are competing for airtime rather than restructuring the conversation.", "I'm sure we'd all agree that some voices have been rather dominant this morning. : Passive aggressive and vague; everyone knows who you mean and nobody respects the indirection."], "scenarios": [{"id": "handling-the-dominant-member-s1", "room": "school council", "setup": "He chairs the school council. The item is a proposed revision to the uniform policy. A parent member, a senior lawyer, has spoken five times, each time returning to her concern about enforcement and student rights. The two student representatives and a staff member have not spoken. The item has twenty minutes left.", "said": "I am sorry to keep returning to this, but I really do think we are underestimating the enforcement problem. If the policy says students must wear the blazer in Terms 2 and 3, then what happens when a student refuses? Detention? Exclusion from class? We need to know, because otherwise the policy is unenforceable.", "task": "As chair, register her point accurately and open the floor to the student representatives and staff.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "Thank you, Helen. I think the point is now well established: the policy needs a clear and proportionate consequence, or it will not hold. I will make sure it is minuted as a matter for the drafting. I would like to hear now from the student representatives, because they are the ones who will live with it. Jess, Sam, how does it look from where you sit?", "why": "It summarises generously, assures her the point will survive in the record, and opens the floor to specific named members."}, {"text": "Helen, I want to capture that properly, so let me put it as a question for the drafters: what is the consequence of non-compliance, and is it proportionate? I will hold you there for now and go to people who have not spoken. Mark, from a staff point of view, what is enforcement like at the moment?", "why": "It turns her repetition into a concrete drafting question and uses a light, firm hold before moving to a staff voice."}], "checks": ["Did my summary of her point satisfy her accuracy test?", "Did I name specific people to speak next?", "Did I avoid any reference to how often she had spoken?"]}, {"id": "handling-the-dominant-member-s2", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "He is a member, not the chair, of an SCSA syllabus committee reviewing the composition component. A professor of composition has dominated the meeting with a strong argument for raising technical requirements. Two teachers from regional schools have said almost nothing, though their schools would be most affected. The chair is reluctant to intervene with a senior academic.", "said": "I have heard the concern about accessibility, but frankly we cannot design a syllabus around the weakest provision. If the composition component does not demand serious technical command of harmony and orchestration, we will produce students who cannot do the first year of a degree. I would want any revision to raise the bar, not lower it.", "task": "As a member, bridge from the professor's point to the regional teachers without challenging the chair.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "Professor Hale's point about first-year readiness seems central to me, and I would not want to lose it. But I am conscious that the regional schools will feel any rise in the bar most sharply. Chair, would it help to hear from Anna and Tom on what their students would need in order to meet it?", "why": "It honours the professor's argument, then addresses the chair rather than taking over the floor, and names specific colleagues."}, {"text": "Could I put a question to the regional members, building on that? If the requirement rose as the professor suggests, what support would your students need to meet it, and is that support realistic in a school without a specialist teacher? I think the answer may tell us how to raise the bar rather than whether to.", "why": "It converts the professor's position into a design question that only the quieter members can answer."}], "checks": ["Did I affirm the professor's point before turning away from it?", "Did I avoid presuming the chair's role?", "Did I name the people I wanted to hear from?"]}, {"id": "handling-the-dominant-member-s3", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "He is treasurer on the festival association board. The president is a charismatic, long-standing figure who has spoken over each attempt to discuss the budget for next year's festival, steering repeatedly back to his preferred plan for an expanded gala concert. Two newer board members have tried to raise venue costs and been talked over. He needs the board to look at the numbers.", "said": "Look, the gala is what people remember. Every year we argue about the money, and every year it works out. If we cut the gala we lose the profile, and if we lose the profile we lose the sponsors. I really think we should lock in the expanded format tonight and sort out the detail later.", "task": "Redirect the president to the numbers and bring the newer members back in.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "I agree the gala carries the profile, Tony, and I do not want to lose it either. Before we lock anything in, though, I think the board should see what the expanded format does to the bottom line. Priya and James raised venue costs earlier, and I do not think we heard them out. Could we hear that now, and then I can put the gala figures alongside it?", "why": "It concedes the president's value point, uses the treasurer's natural authority over numbers, and returns the floor to the members who were talked over."}, {"text": "Tony, could I suggest an order? Let us take ten minutes on the costs, starting with Priya's question about the venue, and then come back to the gala with the figures in front of us. I suspect the expanded format may well survive the scrutiny, and if so we will lock it in with confidence.", "why": "It proposes a sequence rather than opposing the plan, and gives the president a reason to agree."}], "checks": ["Did I affirm what the president values?", "Did I bring the newer members back by name?", "Did I propose a sequence rather than a confrontation?"]}, {"id": "handling-the-dominant-member-s4", "room": "department meeting", "setup": "He leads the music department meeting as Teacher in Charge. A senior colleague with many years in the school has spoken repeatedly about the proposed change to the concert calendar, each time with growing frustration. Three early-career teachers have said nothing. The change affects all of them.", "said": "I just think we are making a mistake moving the winter concert. We did this in 2019 and it was a disaster. Nobody listened then either. I have said it, I will keep saying it, and I would like it noted that I think this is the wrong decision.", "task": "Note the colleague's objection fully and draw out the early-career teachers.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "It is noted, Graham, and I mean that: I will put it in the minutes as your view that the move repeats the 2019 problem. I would like to hear from everyone before we settle anything. Hannah, Leo, Priya, you will each be running ensembles on that night. What does the change mean for you?", "why": "It gives him the formal registration he is asking for, which removes the motive to keep repeating, and then names each quiet colleague."}, {"text": "Thank you, Graham. Could you tell us in a sentence what went wrong in 2019, so the others have it? Then I would like to go round the table. I want to make sure the people who were not here in 2019 have a chance to weigh in.", "why": "It converts the colleague's grievance into useful institutional memory and then makes the round-table explicit."}], "checks": ["Did I give the colleague a formal record of his objection?", "Did I name each quiet colleague?", "Did I keep my tone warm despite his frustration?"]}, {"id": "handling-the-dominant-member-s5", "room": "faculty curriculum committee", "setup": "He sits as an external member on a university faculty curriculum committee. The chair is the dominant member: she has spoken at length on each item, framed every question, and moved through items with little discussion. A lecturer who designed the unit under discussion has not been asked to speak. He cannot use chairing tools.", "said": "So I think we are broadly agreed. The unit is sound, though I would want the assessment pattern tightened, and I think the reading list is too long. I will ask the school to revise along those lines. Unless anyone objects strongly, we can move to item six.", "task": "As a member, open the floor to the unit's designer without challenging the chair's authority.", "seconds": 35, "models": [{"text": "Before we move on, Chair, could we hear briefly from Dr Okafor? It would help me to know how she sees the assessment pattern and the reading list before the revisions are framed, since she will be making them, and I suspect the committee would value her rationale on the record.", "why": "It is framed as a request for information, not a complaint about process, and gives the chair a reason to agree."}, {"text": "I agree with tightening the assessment, Chair. I would be grateful to hear what Dr Okafor intended with the current pattern, though; there may be a reason it looks the way it does, and it would help the revision to have that on record.", "why": "It agrees with the chair first, then makes the designer's contribution useful to the chair's own outcome."}], "checks": ["Did I frame it as a request, not a criticism?", "Did I agree with something the chair said?", "Did I name the person I wanted to hear?"]}, {"id": "handling-the-dominant-member-s6", "room": "conference panel", "setup": "He is one of four panellists at a national music education conference on equity in instrumental programs. One panellist, a well-known commentator, has taken most of the time, answering questions directed to others. The moderator has not intervened. Twelve minutes remain, and a question from the floor has just been addressed to the youngest panellist.", "said": "If I could just come in on that, because I think it goes to the heart of what I was saying earlier. The real issue is not access, it is aspiration. Until we change the culture around what music education is for, we are just tinkering with logistics. And I would say that to any minister.", "task": "As a fellow panellist, return the question to the panellist it was addressed to without rebuking the commentator.", "seconds": 30, "models": [{"text": "That is a provocative distinction, and I suspect it deserves its own session. But the question was for Mei, and I would love to hear her answer, because she is the one running a program in a school where access is the daily problem.", "why": "It compliments the idea, returns the question to its addressee, and gives the room a reason to want to hear her."}, {"text": "I would like to come back to the aspiration point, because I am not sure I agree with it. But first, Mei, the question was yours, and you are the one running a program where access is the daily reality. I suspect your answer will test that distinction rather well.", "why": "It is short, honours the commentator's frame, and turns Mei's answer into the test of it, which raises her status."}], "checks": ["Did I return the question to its addressee by name?", "Did I avoid any criticism of the commentator?", "Did I keep it under thirty seconds?"]}]}, {"id": "the-chairs-summation", "name": "The chair's summation", "family": "Closing", "what": "It states the sense of the meeting, the residual disagreement and the decision in a form the room can correct, accept and minute.", "when": "When the arguments have been made and are beginning to repeat, when agreement exists but nobody has said so, or when the chair is about to put a question and the room needs to know exactly what it is agreeing to.", "teach": "Articulate rooms produce a great deal of qualified agreement. Each speaker adds a nuance, a reservation, a condition, and nobody wants to be the one who closes, because closing feels like claiming authority over clever colleagues. The summation does that work on the room's behalf. It turns a conversation into a position, and by offering the room a chance to correct it, it secures consent without a vote. Taking the sense of the meeting in this way is ordinary Australian practice for matters that do not require a formal resolution; where a resolution is required, the summation tells the room exactly what the motion should say.\n\nThe structure has three parts. First, the agreement: what the room has converged on, stated more cleanly than anyone stated it. Second, the live disagreement, stated fairly and in its strongest form, attributed to positions rather than people, or to people generously. Third, the decision or next step, with whatever conditions the room attached. Signpost it ('Let me try to state where we are'), deliver it in under a minute, and then ask a tight checking question: 'Is that a fair account?' or 'Is there anything I have misstated?' Pause for a full three seconds. Then direct the minute-taker.\n\nThe minority position is the key to a durable summation. If you leave it out, those who held it will reopen the matter, often under general business or at the next meeting. If you state it well, they can accept the decision because their view is on the record. The experienced chair states the minority view so well that its holders nod.\n\nSlow down and lower the voice. Let the intonation fall at the end of each sentence; rising intonation turns the summation into a series of questions. Sit back slightly and look around the table, not at the paper. A short pause between the three parts helps the room hear the structure.\n\nA member can offer a summation when the chair is struggling: 'Chair, may I try a summary? Correct me if I have it wrong.' This is only safe when you are seen as fair and when you hand the result straight back to the chair.\n\nIt goes wrong in four ways. Summarising your own view as the sense of the meeting is capture, and clever people spot it at once. A long summation re-argues the case. Omitting the minority invites reopening. And closing with 'Is everyone happy?' invites every residual reservation to resurface; ask whether the account is accurate, not whether people are happy.", "phrasings": ["Let me try to state where we are. There is agreement that the aural component should stay. There is a real difference about its format. And I think the decision is to ask the working group for two options by the next meeting. Is that a fair account?", "I think the sense of the meeting is clear, and I will put it in a sentence for the minutes. Correct me if I misstate it.", "We are agreed on the principle and not yet on the timing. I would put the timing view of the minority this way: that a year's delay costs less than an unprepared start.", "Three things, I think. We support the proposal. We want the costings tested. And we want it back in March. Have I missed anything?", "I do not think we need a vote; the room is clearly with the recommendation. Rachel's reservation about workload should be minuted, and the officers asked to address it.", "Chair, may I try a summary? Correct me if I have it wrong, and then I will hand it back to you.", "If that is a fair account, I will ask the minutes to record the decision in those terms and we will move on."], "avoid": ["So basically I think we're all agreeing with what I said at the start. : Capture disguised as summary; it claims the room's view as your own and invites immediate correction.", "Is everyone happy with that? : Invites every residual reservation to resurface; ask whether the account is accurate, not whether people are happy.", "Right, well, I think there's sort of a range of views, and some people think one thing and some think another, so maybe we'll come back to it. : A summation without a decision; it records nothing and guarantees the debate will return.", "In summary, and I'll be brief, the first point was, and this is important, that... : Signposting brevity and then not delivering it; the room hears the gap and loses patience."], "scenarios": [{"id": "the-chairs-summation-s1", "room": "school council", "setup": "He chairs the school council. For forty minutes the council has discussed the principal's proposal to bring forward the specialist program's audition timeline by a term. Members agree the earlier timeline would help families, but two members worry it disadvantages students from primary schools with less music provision. The principal needs a council view to proceed.", "said": "I think I have said what I wanted to. I do support the earlier date. I simply want us to be sure that the primary schools with no music specialist are not left behind. If that can be addressed, I have no objection.", "task": "As chair, state the sense of the meeting including the condition, and land a minutable decision.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "Let me try to state where we are. Council supports bringing the audition timeline forward a term. There is a clear and shared concern, which Kate has just put well, that students from primary schools without a music specialist must not be disadvantaged. So I would propose council endorse the change, on the condition that the principal reports next meeting on how those students will be reached. Is that a fair account?", "why": "It states the agreement, credits the reservation, converts it into a condition, and closes on a checking question about accuracy."}, {"text": "I think the sense of the meeting is this. We support the earlier date in principle. We want it to come with a deliberate outreach to schools with limited music provision. And we want to see that outreach before the date is finalised. If no one thinks I have misstated it, I will ask the minutes to record council's endorsement on that condition.", "why": "It uses a three-part structure and makes the condition a precondition, which may better reassure the members who raised it."}], "checks": ["Did I name the minority concern fairly?", "Did I convert it into a specific condition?", "Did I ask whether the account was accurate rather than whether people were happy?", "Did I direct the minute-taker?"]}, {"id": "the-chairs-summation-s2", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "He is treasurer, not chair, of the festival association board. The board has discussed next year's budget for an hour. Most agree to hold fees and draw on reserves for one year; two members want a modest fee rise instead. The president, who chairs, turns to him to sum up as treasurer.", "said": "Dijon, you have been following the numbers more closely than any of us. Can you give us a summary of where we have got to, and what you think the board should resolve? I would like to get this done before nine.", "task": "Summarise as a member at the chair's invitation and hand the decision back to the chair.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "Happy to. The board's clear majority is to hold fees for next year and meet the shortfall from reserves, on the understanding that this is a one-year measure. The alternative view, that a modest rise now is kinder than a larger one later, has real force and should be minuted. On the numbers, reserves can absorb one year comfortably, not two. So I think the resolution is to hold fees, draw on reserves, and review fees by June. Over to you, Tony.", "why": "It states both views fairly, adds the treasurer's one essential fact, frames the resolution, and returns the decision to the chair."}, {"text": "I think we are agreed on the objective, which is affordability for member schools, and divided on the method. Most of us favour holding fees this year and using reserves; Priya and James favour a small rise now. Either is defensible. What reserves will not bear is holding fees two years running. If the chair agrees, I would suggest a resolution to hold fees, with a fee review on the June agenda.", "why": "It frames the disagreement as one of method rather than values, which lowers the temperature, and builds the minority's concern into the resolution."}], "checks": ["Did I represent the minority without diminishing it?", "Did I add only the fact the board needed from me?", "Did I hand the decision back to the chair?"]}, {"id": "the-chairs-summation-s3", "room": "SCSA working group", "setup": "He chairs an SCSA working group reviewing aural assessment in senior music. After two hours, the group agrees aural skills must remain examined. It is divided between those who want an extended, analysis-based aural paper and those who want shorter, skills-based items. The group must report to the full committee in a month.", "said": "I think we have probably exhausted this. I remain of the view that the shorter items are fairer to students from schools with less aural teaching. But I can see the force of the analytical approach. I just do not want our report to pretend we agreed when we did not.", "task": "State the agreement, represent the division honestly, and decide how it goes to the committee.", "seconds": 70, "models": [{"text": "Let me try to state where we are, and Ben, I will take your point about honesty seriously. We agree that aural skills must remain examined. We are divided on format: one view favours extended analytical questions as a better test of musicianship; the other favours shorter skills items as fairer to students with less aural teaching. Rather than force a choice, I propose we report both options to the committee with their trade-offs, and say plainly that the group was divided. Is that a fair account?", "why": "It honours the member's integrity point by building it into the decision, and turns an unresolved split into a usable report."}, {"text": "I think the sense of the group is this. Agreement on retention, real division on format, and good reasons on both sides. I would rather report that division accurately than paper over it. So the decision is to put two options to the committee, each with its rationale and its risks, and to record that we did not reach a single view. I will draft it and circulate it to you all before it goes.", "why": "It is shorter, states the division as a strength of the report, and assigns the drafting to himself with a circulation step."}], "checks": ["Did I state the division without favouring my own view?", "Did I make the honest reporting part of the decision?", "Did I state the next step and who owns it?"]}, {"id": "the-chairs-summation-s4", "room": "department meeting", "setup": "He leads a music department meeting about a clash between the Term 3 jazz night and a year-group camp that removes thirty of his students. Staff have proposed three solutions: moving the jazz night, running it with reduced ensembles, or asking the year coordinator to move the camp. Two staff strongly prefer moving the night. Time is short.", "said": "I just want to say I really do not think we can ask the year coordinator to move the camp. It has been booked for months and they will say no. I would rather we take control of our own date. But I will go with whatever we decide.", "task": "Summarise the three options, register the strong preference, and state the decision.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Let me pull this together. We have three options and a clear preference in the room for moving our own date rather than relying on the camp moving. I agree that is the safer course. So the decision is that we move the jazz night to the first Thursday of Term 4, subject to the hall being free. I will check the hall tomorrow and confirm by email. Does that capture it?", "why": "It is brisk, names the preference and his own agreement openly, and closes with a specific action and owner."}, {"text": "Here is where I think we are. Nobody wants to lose thirty players. Asking the camp to move is unlikely to work, as Ellie says, and reduced ensembles would short-change the students. That leaves moving the night, which most of us favour. Unless anyone thinks I have that wrong, that is the decision, and I will confirm a new date by Friday.", "why": "It eliminates options in sequence so the decision feels reasoned rather than imposed, then closes with a date."}], "checks": ["Did I deal with all three options?", "Did I state a single decision?", "Did I attach an owner and a date?"]}, {"id": "the-chairs-summation-s5", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "He convenes a state arts funding panel ranking twelve music applications. After the morning, the panel has converged on rankings for eleven. One application, an ambitious multi-venue tour, has split the panel: two think it outstanding, two think it overreached on budget. The panel must submit a ranked list by the end of the day.", "said": "I do not think we are going to persuade each other on the tour. I still think it is the most exciting thing we have seen. But I can see that if the budget is wrong, it fails badly. I would rather we put something forward than argue for another hour.", "task": "As convenor, state the consensus, frame the split fairly, and propose a way to finalise the ranking.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "Let me state where we are. We have agreed rankings for eleven applications, and I will read them back in a moment. On the tour, the panel is evenly divided: two of us see an outstanding project; two of us see a budget that may not hold. Both views are well grounded. I propose we rank it seventh, within the fundable range, and recommend funding conditional on a revised budget. Is that a fair account?", "why": "It records the consensus, states the split evenly, and offers a ranking that reflects the division and protects the agency."}, {"text": "I think we agree on eleven, and on the tour we agree on the ambition and disagree on the risk. Rather than argue further, I suggest our report says exactly that: a project of high artistic merit with a budget the panel was not confident in. We can rank it at the margin and leave the conditional decision to the agency. Have I put both views fairly?", "why": "It uses the report itself to carry the disagreement, which lets both camps sign off honestly."}], "checks": ["Did I record the eleven agreed rankings before the split?", "Did I state both sides of the split evenly?", "Did I propose a concrete way to finalise?"]}, {"id": "the-chairs-summation-s6", "room": "university academic board subcommittee", "setup": "He is an external member of a university academic board subcommittee on admissions. The chair has lost the thread after a long debate about portfolio-based entry for music. Several members agreed on a pilot but differed on the number of places and whether ATAR should still be required. The chair looks at the clock and hesitates.", "said": "Well, I think we have had a very rich discussion. I am not entirely sure where that leaves us. Perhaps we should take it offline and come back to it next time?", "task": "As a member, offer a summation that rescues the decision, then hand it straight back to the chair.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Chair, may I try a summary before we defer? Correct me if I have it wrong. I think we agree on a pilot of portfolio entry. We differ on two details: the number of places and whether an ATAR threshold remains. Those could be settled by the faculty proposing options for next meeting. So the decision today might be to approve the pilot in principle and refer the two details. I will leave it with you.", "why": "It asks permission, compresses the debate into one agreement and two details, proposes a decision, and returns authority to the chair."}, {"text": "Before we take it offline, Chair, I wonder whether we have more agreement than it feels. I heard support for a pilot from almost everyone. The open questions are the size and the ATAR requirement. If the subcommittee approved the pilot in principle today and asked the faculty to bring options on those two, we would not lose the ground we have made.", "why": "It reframes the chair's sense of confusion as hidden progress and offers a way to bank it."}], "checks": ["Did I ask the chair's permission before summarising?", "Did I separate the agreement from the details?", "Did I hand the decision back explicitly?"]}]}, {"id": "landing-the-decision", "name": "Landing the decision", "family": "Closing", "what": "It moves a drifting room from exploration to a resolution with a specific action, a named owner and a date.", "when": "When the arguments have begun to repeat, when the item has used most of its time, or when the room is circling a decision it is capable of making but nobody is making it.", "teach": "Educated rooms drift for good reasons. Exploration is pleasurable, every speaker can see one more consideration, and deciding creates exposure: a decision can be wrong and attributed. Deferral can pass for rigour. The move names the drift without blame and offers the room the smallest decision that will let it move. Its force comes from concreteness. In a room of abstractions, a draft resolution with a verb, a person and a date has a gravitational pull, because it gives everyone something to amend rather than something more to say.\n\nThe practitioner's sequence runs as follows. First, mark the moment: 'We have been round this twice; can I suggest a way to land it?' Second, propose a specific form of words, ideally one that can be minuted unchanged: 'That the board approve venue A for 2027, subject to the treasurer confirming the hire cost by 15 March.' Third, attach an owner, asked rather than assumed, and a date anchored to a calendar fact such as the next meeting or a consultation deadline. Fourth, test it: put the question, or ask the chair whether there is support.\n\nWhere the room is not ready to decide the substance, land a smaller decision. A pilot, an interim decision with a review date, a decision about who will bring options, or a decision about how the decision will be made all count. Distinguish clearly between a decision and a decision to decide, and say which one you are proposing; both are legitimate, but blurring them lets the room think it has done more than it has.\n\nThe voice is brisk and practical; use concrete nouns, numbers and dates. It helps to have a pen in hand, as though you are already writing the minute. Lean slightly forward. If you are not the chair, address the proposal to the chair so that you are offering a resolution rather than seizing the meeting.\n\nVolunteering yourself as owner is often the quickest way to land a decision, but use it with care. Each volunteered action is a commitment you must deliver, and a pattern of volunteering can leave you with work that belongs to others.\n\nIt goes wrong in four ways. Forcing closure on a question that is genuinely not ready creates resentment and guarantees relitigation. A vague action ('someone will look into it') lands nothing. A decision that happens to be your preferred outcome, framed as procedural tidying, will be recognised and resisted. And naming an owner who was not asked produces an action that will quietly fail.", "phrasings": ["We have been round this twice. Can I suggest a way to land it?", "Could I propose a form of words? That the board approve the Perth venue for next year, subject to the treasurer confirming the hire cost by the March meeting.", "I do not think we are ready to decide the substance today, but we could decide the process: that the officers bring two costed options to the next meeting.", "Would a pilot answer most of the concerns? One cohort, one year, reviewed in November.", "Who would be best placed to own that? I am happy to, if that helps, but I do not want to take it from someone better placed.", "So, for the minutes: action, a revised survey draft; owner, Kate; date, circulated by the twentieth. Is that right?", "Chair, is there support for that, or would you like to put it formally?", "Can we be clear whether this is a decision or a decision to decide? I think it is the second, and that is fine, but the minutes should say so."], "avoid": ["OK, we really need to make a decision now, guys. : Impatient and informal; it expresses frustration without offering anything to decide.", "I think someone should probably look into this and report back at some stage. : No owner, no date, no deliverable; it lands nothing and everyone knows it.", "Well, the obvious decision, surely, is to go with option A, so can we just agree that and move on? : Smuggles a preference in as procedural tidying; those who favour option B will rightly resist.", "Let's say Mark will draft that by Friday. : Assigns an owner without asking; the action is likely to fail, and Mark may resent it."], "scenarios": [{"id": "landing-the-decision-s1", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The festival association board has spent forty minutes on next year's venue. Two venues are under consideration: the current concert hall, which is expensive but loved, and a university venue, which is cheaper but less central. Every argument has been made twice. The concert hall needs confirmation within three weeks or the dates will be released.", "said": "I keep coming back to the atmosphere. The concert hall is the festival for a lot of families. But I take the point about cost, and I take the point about access for the northern schools. I honestly do not know. Perhaps we need more information before we commit either way.", "task": "Land a decision with an owner and a date despite the remaining uncertainty.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "Could I suggest a way to land this? I think we agree the concert hall is preferred if we can afford it. So I would propose that the board resolve to book the concert hall for next year, subject to my confirming as treasurer, by the fifteenth, that it can be funded without a fee rise above five per cent. If I cannot, the university venue is booked instead. That way we decide tonight and the hall's deadline is met.", "why": "It converts the uncertainty into a single condition, takes ownership as treasurer, and gives the board a decision that holds either way."}, {"text": "I wonder whether the information we need is actually one number: what the hall costs per entry against the university. I can have that to everyone by Friday. If the board agreed tonight that the president and I may confirm the hall should the difference be under a set figure, we would not need another meeting before the deadline.", "why": "It narrows the missing information to one figure and proposes a delegated decision that avoids another meeting."}], "checks": ["Did I name a specific form of decision?", "Did I attach an owner and a date tied to the deadline?", "Did I make the decision hold whichever way the uncertainty resolved?"]}, {"id": "landing-the-decision-s2", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The SCSA committee has spent an hour on the wording of a single content descriptor about improvisation in the revised syllabus. Three drafts are in circulation. The consultation draft must go to the Authority within a fortnight, and two other sections are still unreviewed.", "said": "I like elements of all three drafts. The second is the most precise, but I think the first captures the creative dimension better. I wonder whether we might ask for a fourth that brings the strengths together. I would be reluctant to settle on wording we are only half happy with.", "task": "Move the committee from crafting wording to a decision that protects the deadline.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "I think we are close, and we have also run out of time for drafting as a committee. Could I suggest we adopt the second draft as the base, add the clause from the first about creative intent, and authorise the chair and the officer to finalise wording by Friday? The descriptor goes to consultation, where it will be tested anyway. Then we can turn to the two sections still outstanding.", "why": "It proposes a concrete composite, delegates final wording, reminds the room that consultation follows, and names the opportunity cost."}, {"text": "Chair, may I propose a resolution? That the committee approve draft two for consultation, with an amendment to include 'with creative intent'. I suspect a fourth draft will raise as many questions as it solves, and the consultation is precisely where we learn whether teachers read it as we intend.", "why": "It frames a minutable resolution with a one-line amendment and uses the consultation process to lower the stakes of imperfection."}], "checks": ["Did I propose a specific draft as the base?", "Did I delegate the final wording to named people?", "Did I connect the decision to the deadline?"]}, {"id": "landing-the-decision-s3", "room": "school council", "setup": "He chairs the school council. A proposed parent survey on the specialist programs has been discussed across two meetings. Each discussion adds new questions; the draft now has forty items. The principal needs the survey in the field before the end of term to inform next year's planning.", "said": "I just think, if we are going to the trouble of surveying families, we should ask about homework and wellbeing as well. We will not get another chance this year. And I am still not sure about question twelve, which reads a little leading to me.", "task": "As chair, stop the survey expanding and land an action, owner and date.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think we have a strong survey and a risk of a long one. Let me propose a way to land it. The survey stays focused on the specialist programs; homework and wellbeing go on a list for a separate survey next year. Kate and the principal will cut it to no more than twenty items, fixing question twelve, and circulate the final draft by the twentieth. If there are no objections by the twenty-fifth, it goes out. Is there support for that?", "why": "It contains the expansion without dismissing it, names owners and dates, and builds in a silent-approval mechanism that prevents a third discussion."}, {"text": "Can we be clear what we are deciding today? I would like council to decide the survey's scope, which I suggest is the specialist programs only, and to delegate the wording to the principal and Kate, to be circulated by the twentieth. Homework and wellbeing are good questions; they deserve their own survey rather than being squeezed into this one. Can I take that as agreed?", "why": "It separates scope, which council decides, from wording, which it delegates, and gives the new topics a dignified future."}], "checks": ["Did I stop the expansion without rejecting the new topics?", "Did I name owners and a date?", "Did I build in a mechanism that prevents further rounds?", "Did I test for support?"]}, {"id": "landing-the-decision-s4", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "He meets the principal and the business manager about replacing ageing instruments. The conversation has wandered through leasing, grants, a staged replacement plan and the possibility of a parent appeal. The business manager has the budget lines open. The principal has fifteen minutes before another meeting.", "said": "These are all reasonable ideas. I would be happy to look at the leasing model, and a grant could be worth pursuing. I am conscious we have not got a lot of time today. Perhaps you could put something in writing and we can come back to it in a few weeks?", "task": "Secure a decision now rather than a vague deferral.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "I will certainly put it in writing. Could we decide one thing today, though, so the paper has a frame? If you were comfortable approving the first stage, the two tubas and the double basses, from this year's capital line, I would bring a costed plan for stages two and three, including the leasing comparison, to you by the end of the month.", "why": "It accepts the deferral for the larger plan but lands the urgent part now, with a specific scope and date for the rest."}, {"text": "Can I suggest a decision about the process rather than the whole plan? That the business manager and I meet next week to compare leasing against purchase, and that I bring you a single recommendation by the twenty-eighth. It would help if we agreed today that urgent replacements can proceed under a set figure in the meantime.", "why": "It lands a process decision with owners and a date, and secures a bounded interim authority for urgent needs."}], "checks": ["Did I land at least one concrete decision today?", "Did I propose owners and a date for the rest?", "Did I respect the principal's time?"]}, {"id": "landing-the-decision-s5", "room": "faculty curriculum committee", "setup": "He is an external member of a university faculty curriculum committee. A proposed jazz improvisation elective has been discussed for half an hour. Members support it but keep raising staffing and enrolment risks. Nobody has proposed a resolution, and the chair seems content to let it run.", "said": "I am very sympathetic to the elective. My worry is that we do not know whether enough students will take it, and we may be committing staff we cannot spare. It might be wiser to wait a year and see how the existing electives settle.", "task": "Propose a smaller, landable decision that addresses the risk.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "Would a pilot answer the worry? The committee could approve the elective for one year, run if it reaches a minimum enrolment of twelve, staffed by sessional appointment rather than a continuing load, and reviewed after the first semester. That tests demand without committing staff. Chair, could that be put as a resolution?", "why": "It answers each stated risk with a specific mechanism and asks the chair to formalise the proposal."}, {"text": "I think we have agreed the elective is worth having and are unsure whether it will run. Those are different decisions. Could the committee approve it in principle today, and ask the school to report by the end of next term on expected enrolment and staffing? Then the decision to offer it can be made on evidence rather than deferred indefinitely.", "why": "It separates the merit decision from the operational one and lands the first while setting a date for the second."}], "checks": ["Did I address each risk raised?", "Did I propose something smaller than full approval?", "Did I put a review date in it?"]}, {"id": "landing-the-decision-s6", "room": "feeder primary principals' meeting", "setup": "He is meeting the principals of five local primary schools that feed the combined primary concert band he runs. The group has discussed rehearsal times for next year for half an hour: some prefer before school, some after school, and transport complicates both. Letters to families need to go out in two weeks.", "said": "I think every option has a cost for one of us. Before school suits most of our parents, but our bus does not arrive until eight-fifteen. After school is easier for us but harder for two of the others. I am not sure there is a perfect answer.", "task": "Land a rehearsal decision acceptable to all five schools with owners and a date.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I do not think there is a perfect answer either, so may I propose a workable one? Rehearsals stay before school, starting at eight-twenty rather than seven-forty-five, so Alison's bus arrives in time. I will check that with the hall and confirm by Wednesday. If any school cannot make that work, tell me by Friday; otherwise I will draft the family letter for you all by the following Monday.", "why": "It adjusts the majority option to solve the specific objection, assigns himself the actions, and sets dates working back from the letter deadline."}, {"text": "Could we decide by elimination? After school is harder for two schools; before school is harder for one, and that can be fixed by starting twenty minutes later. So I would suggest before school at eight-twenty. If everyone is content, I will confirm the hall today and send a draft letter to all five of you by the end of next week.", "why": "It makes the reasoning transparent, which helps a group of principals accept the outcome, and closes with clear actions and dates."}], "checks": ["Did I name the specific time?", "Did I solve the stated objection rather than override it?", "Did I set dates that work back from the letter deadline?"]}]}], "craft": [{"id": "craft-1", "title": "The controlling idea", "teach": "Every speech that matters exists to deliver one sentence. Aristotle put it bluntly in the third book of the Rhetoric: a speech has only two necessary parts, the statement of the case and its proof. Everything else is arrangement in service of those two. The Roman rhetoricians called the statement the propositio, and the discipline of finding it belongs to invention, not to style. If you cannot write the proposition before you write the speech, you do not yet have a speech; you have a subject.\n\nThe difference between a subject and a proposition is the difference between \"performance assessment in the Music ATAR course\" and \"the performance examination should be marked against a published repertoire standard, not against the examiner's sense of what a Year 12 should sound like.\" The first is a topic for a committee agenda. The second is a claim that someone in the room could disagree with, which is precisely what makes it worth saying. A useful test: if no intelligent person present could object to your sentence, it is not yet a controlling idea. It is a heading.\n\nThe classical tool for sharpening it is stasis theory, associated with Hermagoras of Temnos. Before speaking, ask where the real disagreement sits. Is it conjectural (did something happen, is something true), definitional (what kind of thing is it), qualitative (is it good, just, expedient), or translative (is this the right body, the right time, the right procedure)? A great many senior meetings go wrong because two people argue at different stases: one says the ensemble program is underfunded, the other says the council is not the body that sets the budget. Locate the stasis and your sentence will locate itself.\n\nEpideictic occasions need a controlling idea as much as deliberative ones. A valedictory for a retiring colleague is not a chronology of her career; it is a claim about her, such as \"she made rigour feel like generosity.\" An award acceptance is not a list of thanks; it is a statement of what the award honours beyond you. A eulogy without a controlling idea becomes an obituary read aloud. With one, every anecdote becomes evidence.\n\nWrite the sentence in no more than about twenty-five words, with a finite verb that commits. Then test it three ways. Can the room repeat it afterwards to someone who was absent? Is it said in your own register rather than in the language of the policy document? Does it tell the room what you want them to do, believe or feel? Qualifications belong in the proof, where they can be handled precisely; they do not belong in the proposition, where they read as hedging.\n\nFor a fast, associative mind the controlling idea is also a governor. Write it at the top of every page of your notes. Every example, aside and digression must answer to it: if a passage does not advance or prove that sentence, it is cut, however good it is. The best material you remove is often the best material you have, and that is the price of a speech that lands.\n\nFinally, decide where the sentence will be heard. In a sympathetic room, state it early and plainly, then prove it. In a sceptical one, you may hold it back and let the narration and proof arrive at it, so that the room reaches your conclusion a moment before you say it. Either way, it should be said aloud, in nearly the same words, at least twice.", "model": "Chair, I want to put one proposition to the committee, | and I will spend my five minutes defending it. || The performance examination should be marked against a *published* repertoire standard, | not against the examiner's private sense of what Year 12 sounds like. || That is the whole of my case. | Everything I say next is evidence for it, | and if the evidence does not persuade you, | the proposition should fall with it. || But I would ask the committee to hold that sentence in mind, | because I will come back to it, | in *exactly* those words, | before I sit down.", "exercise": {"kind": "write", "task": "Take an occasion in your calendar for the next month. Write its controlling idea in no more than twenty-five words, name the stasis at which it sits, and write beneath it the sentence the room would most like you to say instead."}, "checks": ["The sentence has a finite verb and makes a claim an intelligent person present could contest", "It contains no qualifiers such as perhaps, arguably or to some extent", "You can name the stasis (conjecture, definition, quality or procedure) at which it sits", "A colleague who was absent could repeat it after hearing it once"]}, {"id": "craft-2", "title": "Audience and occasion", "teach": "Cicero says in the Orator that nothing is harder in speaking, as in life, than to see what is fitting. The Greeks called it to prepon, the Romans decorum, and it is less a matter of manners than of judgement: the fit between what you say, who says it, to whom, on what occasion, and for what end. Most failures among clever speakers are failures of decorum rather than of argument. The case is sound; it is simply pitched at the wrong room.\n\nBegin with Aristotle's three kinds of oratory, because they tell you what the room is for. Deliberative speech concerns the future and asks what is expedient: a submission to a board, a proposal to the principal, an argument to a minister's adviser. Forensic speech concerns the past and asks what is just: a review of an incident, a response to a complaint, a moderation dispute. Epideictic speech concerns the present and assigns praise or blame: the valedictory, the festival opening, the eulogy, the award. Mixing the kinds without meaning to is a common error. A festival opening that turns into a funding plea has misjudged its occasion; a council report that turns into a celebration has avoided its accountability.\n\nNext, ask what the room must do when you stop. Decide, approve, note, fund, feel, remember. Cicero's three duties of the orator, to teach, to delight and to move (docere, delectare, movere), are weighted differently by that answer. A curriculum committee needs mostly docere, with enough delectare to keep attention in the fortieth minute. A eulogy needs movere, held in check by docere so that grief has something true to rest on. A parent body needs all three, in that order.\n\nAt senior level, audience analysis is political rather than demographic. Who holds the decision? Who holds the veto? Who will be asked about this afterwards, and by whom? There is nearly always a second audience: the minutes, the adviser who briefs the minister, the deputy who reports to the principal, the academic who repeats your sentence in another faculty meeting. Write at least one sentence that is designed to be quoted accurately by someone who was not convinced.\n\nDecorum also governs you. The same proposal sounds different from the Teacher in Charge addressing his principal, the chair addressing his own council, and the treasurer addressing a board on which he is a peer. Your ethos is partly brought into the room (role, reputation, record) and partly made in it. Aristotle is clear that the persuasive character must be produced by the speech itself, not merely assumed. In a room of academics, the speech produces ethos by precision, fair statement of opposing views and economy. In a room of parents, it produces ethos by specific knowledge of their children and plain speaking.\n\nRegister follows. Academic committees reward the exact term and the explicit concession. Funding panels reward their own criteria, named and addressed in order. Australian rooms of almost every kind punish pomposity faster than error, and reward understatement. A senior speaker sounds as if he has said less than he could.\n\nFinally, respect the clock as an element of decorum. Kairos, the right moment, includes the right length. To finish a minute early in a crowded agenda is itself an argument about your judgement.", "model": "Good evening, | and welcome to the opening night of the festival. || Some of you are here because your child plays second trumpet in a band you had not heard of until March. | Some of you are here because you played in this festival yourselves, | in a hall with worse acoustics and better carpet. || And some of you are here because you fund it, | for which, | *thank* you. || Tonight is not a night for argument. | It is a night to listen to young musicians do something difficult, | in public, | *well*. || So I will be brief, | and they will not.", "exercise": {"kind": "write", "task": "For a single upcoming occasion, write four lines: the kind of oratory (deliberative, forensic or epideictic), what the room must do when you stop, who holds the veto, and the one sentence you want quoted accurately in the minutes or afterwards."}, "checks": ["You named the genre and the speech does not drift into another without intending to", "You can state in five words what the room must do when you finish", "At least one sentence is designed to be repeated accurately by someone who disagrees", "The planned length is shorter than the time you have been given"]}, {"id": "craft-3", "title": "Arrangement", "teach": "The canon of arrangement (dispositio) gives you a sequence of parts that has survived two thousand years because it follows the order in which a listener needs things. Cicero's De Inventione names six: exordium, narratio, partitio, confirmatio, reprehensio and conclusio. The Rhetorica ad Herennium gives the same six under slightly different names, with divisio for partition, confutatio for refutation. Modern speechwriters rarely use the Latin, but they nearly all use the sequence.\n\nIn outline: the exordium makes the audience well disposed, attentive and ready to learn. The narratio sets out the situation. The partitio states what is agreed, what is at issue, and the heads you will cover. The confirmatio proves your case. The refutatio deals with the case against you. The peroratio sums up, amplifies, and asks. Aristotle, characteristically, thought most of this was optional and only statement and proof were necessary. Both views are right: the six parts are a checklist of jobs, not a set of compartments, and the jobs can be compressed, merged or reordered.\n\nCompression is the usual modern case. A three-minute report to a school council can perform all six jobs in six sentences: one to frame, one or two of situation, one that names the two matters requiring a decision, two of reasons, one meeting the obvious objection, one stating the motion. Expansion is the other case. A forty-minute keynote can give each part its own movement, and the narration may itself contain a narrative arc with its own small exordium.\n\nReordering is where judgement shows. Aristotle advises that a speaker who follows an opponent should deal with the opponent's arguments first, because a mind already occupied by an objection will not receive your proof. In practice: if the festival board has arrived convinced the new venue is unaffordable, the refutation of cost comes before the case for the venue, not after. Where the room is neutral, proof first and refutation second is stronger, because you define the ground before the objection appears.\n\nWithin the confirmation, the order of proofs matters. The old advice, which the rhetoricians called Nestorian order after Nestor's placing of his weakest troops in the middle in the Iliad, is to put your strongest argument last, your second strongest first, and the others between. Listeners remember beginnings and endings; the middle is where you may safely put the argument that is necessary but unexciting.\n\nDifferent occasions have their own conventional shapes, and decorum requires you to know them. A eulogy tends to move from loss, to the life, to what continues. A toast moves from the occasion, to the person, to the raised glass. An award acceptance moves from gratitude, to what the work was for, to the people who will continue it. A submission to a funding panel follows the panel's criteria in their own order, because a panel scoring against a rubric will not reconstruct your arrangement for you.\n\nThe practical method: before drafting any sentences, write the six job names down the margin and one line against each. If a line is empty, decide deliberately that the job is unnecessary. If two lines say the same thing, you have found redundancy before writing it.", "model": "Chair, this is the music report, | and it needs one decision tonight. || Since the last meeting, | the department has lost access to the hall on Thursday evenings | for the rest of the year. || That leaves two matters: | where the senior bands rehearse, | and who meets the cost. || The library is free, | close, | and large enough. | It is also, | I concede, | not acoustically ideal. || But the alternative is cancelling rehearsals in the term before the festival. || I therefore move that council approve the library as the interim venue, | at no cost, | until the hall is *returned*.", "exercise": {"kind": "write", "task": "Plan your next substantial speech by writing the six job names down the margin and one line against each. Then decide whether refutation should come before or after proof for this particular room, and state why in one sentence."}, "checks": ["Every one of the six jobs has either a line or a deliberate decision to omit it", "The order of refutation and proof is justified by the room's starting position", "Your strongest argument is placed last in the confirmation", "No two lines in the plan say the same thing"]}, {"id": "craft-4", "title": "The exordium", "teach": "The Roman handbooks give the exordium three tasks: to make the hearer well disposed (benevolum), attentive (attentum) and receptive (docilem). They also distinguish two kinds. The direct opening, principium, suits a case that is honourable and an audience that is willing. The subtle approach, insinuatio, suits a case the audience is already set against, or an audience that is tired, and works by indirection: it begins somewhere the room can agree before arriving at the ground it resists. Knowing which you need is the first decision of any opening.\n\nA sophisticated audience grants you perhaps thirty seconds of provisional attention, and spends them deciding whether you are worth more. They are listening less for content than for signals of control: whether you know what you are going to say, whether you will respect their time, whether you think as well as they do. Everything in the first minute should send those signals.\n\nSome openings reliably fail in senior rooms. The protocol list that runs past four names. The dictionary definition. The rhetorical question with an obvious answer. The joke imported from elsewhere. Above all, the apology: \"I'm not much of a speaker\", \"I'll try to be quick\", \"I wasn't sure what to say.\" The classical captatio benevolentiae, the seeking of goodwill, was meant to be graceful, not servile. An apology does not win goodwill from people who argue for a living; it tells them where to press.\n\nOpenings that work tend to do one of five things. They begin with a specific instance: a student, a rehearsal, a document, a date. They state the stakes plainly: what the room will lose or gain. They pose a precise question that the speech will answer. They concede something the audience expects you to deny, which buys trust at once. Or they begin in medias res, in the middle of the matter, and supply context afterwards. Each of these makes the listener do a small amount of work, and attention follows work.\n\nFormalities are part of the exordium but not its substance. In Australia an Acknowledgement of Country is customary at most public occasions; give it properly, in your own sentences, without hurry, and then pause before you begin. The same applies to the protocol of naming dignitaries: do it once, accurately, and then mark the shift with a held silence, so the room hears where the speech itself begins. The worst openings let the formalities blur into the first idea.\n\nFor insinuatio, the classic move is to begin with a value the hostile room holds. Addressing an academic committee sceptical of performance assessment, you might begin with their commitment to validity, not your commitment to music. You arrive at your proposal as an application of their principle. This is not manipulation; it is courtesy to a reasonable opponent, and it is how most minds are actually changed.\n\nWrite the first sentence last, after the speech exists, and then memorise it word for word. Nerves are highest in the first ten seconds, and a memorised first sentence lets you look at the room while you say it. It should be short. It should be yours. And it should be something only this speaker could say on this occasion.", "model": "In February, | a Year 7 trombonist asked me why we play scales in a key nobody writes in. || I gave her the answer I was given at her age, | and I watched her decide, | politely, | that I did not know. || She was *right*. || I want to use the next forty minutes to answer her properly, | because her question is the one this profession has been avoiding for thirty years: | what, | exactly, | is technical work *for*? || I will not be offering you a method. | I will be offering you a reason.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Write and then deliver the first forty-five seconds of an upcoming speech, using one of the five openings that work. Deliver the first sentence from memory while looking at an imagined room, and record it.", "seconds": 45}, "checks": ["The opening contains no apology and no reference to your own nerves or preparation", "The first sentence was spoken from memory with eyes up", "There is an audible pause between the formalities and the first real sentence", "The room knows by the end of the opening what question or stakes the speech will address"]}, {"id": "craft-5", "title": "Narration", "teach": "The narratio tells the room what the situation is. Quintilian and the Rhetorica ad Herennium agree on its three virtues: it should be brief, clear and plausible (brevis, aperta, probabilis). Brief does not mean short; it means containing nothing the case does not need. Clear means the listener never has to reconstruct who did what, when. Plausible means that the account fits what the room already knows of the world, so that nothing in it provokes the thought \"that cannot be the whole story.\"\n\nThe art of narration is that it does not argue. It appears to be a neutral account, and the argument is carried entirely by selection and order. If the narration is well made, the conclusion seems inevitable before you state it, and the room experiences your proposition as the obvious next sentence rather than as a demand. This is why skilled advocates spend more time on the facts than on the submissions: a case that is won in the narration does not need to be won again.\n\nSelection is therefore the whole craft, and it carries an ethical and a tactical obligation at once. Leave out the inconvenient fact and a sophisticated audience will find it, usually in questions, and then distrust everything else you said. Put it in, early and in your own words, and you control its framing. \"The ensemble was over budget last year, by a little under a fifth, and I will come to why\" is far stronger than waiting for the business manager to say it for you. The classical name for this is concessio built into the facts: the narration that concedes what is true earns the right to have the rest believed.\n\nOrder the narration for understanding, not necessarily for chronology. A strict timeline suits a forensic matter, where sequence is the issue. For a deliberative matter, it is often better to begin with the present state, then the cause, then the trend: here is where we are, here is how we got here, here is where it is going. Each sentence should leave the listener knowing one more thing than before, and needing the next.\n\nWrite the narration with agents and verbs. \"The Authority changed the weighting in 2024\" is narration. \"There was a change to the weighting\" is evasion, and a careful listener hears it as such. Passive constructions and nominalisations hide who acted, and in a room of policy people, hidden agency invites the question \"by whom?\"\n\nVividness has a place. The rhetoricians called it enargeia, or in Latin evidentia: the quality that sets the matter before the eyes. One concrete image in a narration, such as the twenty-year-old tuba with a soldered bell that a Year 9 student carries home on the bus, does more than a paragraph of description about the condition of the instrument fleet. Use it once. A second image is decoration; a third is a performance.\n\nThe test of a narration is simple and severe. Read it to someone who knows nothing of the matter and ask them what should happen next. If they give your proposition, the narration has done its work. If they give a different one, either your facts do not support your case or you have chosen the wrong facts. Both are better discovered at your desk than in the room.", "model": "Here is where we are. || The department has forty-one instruments on loan to students. | Eleven are more than twenty years old, | and four of those cannot now be repaired. || One of them is a tuba with a soldered bell | that a Year 9 student carries home on the bus | twice a week. || The replacement budget has not changed since 2019. | The cost of a student tuba has. || So each year, | we replace fewer instruments than we retire, | and the gap is now *wider* than the budget can close. || I will come to what I am asking for. | But I suspect the committee can already see it.", "exercise": {"kind": "write", "task": "Write a narration of no more than 150 words for a matter you are bringing to a decision-maker, including the one fact that most weakens your case. Read it to someone unfamiliar with the matter and ask what they think should happen next."}, "checks": ["The narration contains no evaluative words such as unfortunately, clearly or unacceptable", "The most inconvenient fact appears, in your own words, before anyone else can raise it", "Every action has a named agent and an active verb", "An uninformed listener, asked what should happen next, gave your proposition"]}, {"id": "craft-6", "title": "Partition and signposting", "teach": "A reader can turn back a page; a listener cannot. The partitio exists because the ear needs a map. In Cicero's account it does one of two things: it sets out what is agreed and what remains in dispute, or it lists, briefly, the heads under which you will argue. Both are acts of courtesy. The first tells a sophisticated audience that you know where the real disagreement lies; the second tells them how long they will be asked to hold their attention, and on what.\n\nQuintilian warns against partitions that are too long or too fine. Three heads are easily held; four can be; seven cannot. If your matter genuinely has seven parts, group them under three and partition the groups. Name the heads with nouns that carry content: not \"my first point, my second point\" but \"the case rests on validity, workload and equity.\" Each of those words then becomes a hook to hang everything under it.\n\nOnce you have numbered, you are bound by the number. A speaker who promises three reasons and delivers four sounds disorganised; one who promises three and delivers two sounds as if he has lost his place, which he usually has. Keep the order you announced. When you reach each head, name it again in the same word you used in the partition, so that the listener hears the click of the map aligning with the territory.\n\nBetween the heads, use transitions that do real work. The weakest transitions are purely mechanical: \"moving on\", \"next\", \"secondly.\" The strongest are hinges that close one section and open the next with a question the room now wants answered: \"That is the case for validity. Does it cost more teacher time?\" This is hypophora, the figure of raising a question and answering it, and it is the most efficient signpost in spoken English because it creates the need for the next section before supplying it.\n\nInternal summaries matter more in speech than writers expect. At the end of a long section, one sentence that restates the point made lets late arrivals and wandering minds re-enter. In a forty-minute keynote, two or three such summaries are not redundancy; they are load-bearing.\n\nCalibrate the quantity of signposting to the room. Academics tolerate less metadiscourse than school audiences; too much reads as condescension, as if you think they cannot follow. A crisp partition, heads repeated at arrival, and hinge questions between sections is usually enough. In a short committee contribution, a single sentence of partition (\"two points, one on timing and one on cost\") replaces all of it.\n\nFor a speaker who thinks associatively, the partition is also a discipline on yourself. A digression is permissible if it is flagged and returned from: \"A word on the history, because it bears on the third reason,\" then, after it, \"Which brings us back to equity.\" Without the flag, the room cannot tell digression from structure. Without the return, neither can you. Speakers who are known for their tangents are usually forgiven them when the return is audible and exact.", "model": "The proposal before the board rests on three things: | *validity*, | *workload*, | and *equity*. | I will take them in that order, | and I will be brief on the second. || First, | validity. || [section] || That is the case for validity. | The board's next question will be a fair one: | does it cost more teacher time? || The answer is, | modestly, | yes, | and here is how much. || [section] || Which leaves equity, | and this is the reason I would ask the board to weigh most *heavily*.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Deliver a partition of three named heads for an upcoming contribution, then deliver the hinge sentence between the first and second heads, using a question the room would genuinely ask.", "seconds": 40}, "checks": ["The partition names each head with a content noun rather than an ordinal", "Each head is named again, in the same word, when you arrive at it", "The hinge between sections is a question the room would plausibly ask", "The number of heads promised equals the number delivered"]}, {"id": "craft-7", "title": "Proof", "teach": "Aristotle divides proofs into two kinds. Inartificial proofs (atechnoi) are found rather than made: documents, minutes, contracts, data, the syllabus itself. Artificial proofs (entechnoi) are made by the speaker, and come in three modes: the character of the speaker (ethos), the disposition of the audience (pathos), and the argument itself (logos). In a senior room all three operate at once, but they operate differently in speech than on the page.\n\nThe characteristic form of spoken argument is what Aristotle called the enthymeme: a syllogism with a premise suppressed, which the audience supplies. \"The examination rewards students who can afford private tuition, so it is not measuring what the syllabus says it measures\" leaves unstated the premise that the syllabus intends to measure something other than access to tuition. The listener who supplies that premise has participated in the argument, and owns its conclusion a little. The skill lies in suppressing only premises the room already holds. Suppress one they reject, and the argument falls silently, which is worse than falling loudly.\n\nThe second form is example (paradeigma), the rhetorical equivalent of induction. A single, well-chosen example, fully developed, persuades a listening audience more than five examples listed. The page can carry an enumeration; the ear tends to retain the first and last items and lose the rest. Choose the example that the most sceptical person in the room would find hardest to dismiss, and spend your time on it.\n\nFor structure, Stephen Toulmin's model in The Uses of Argument is useful precisely because it names what speakers leave out: the claim, the grounds that support it, the warrant that connects them, the backing for the warrant, the qualifier, and the rebuttal. Most weak spoken arguments state a claim and grounds and assume the warrant. In an academic room, the warrant is often exactly what is contested. Say it.\n\nEvidence in speech needs a different grammar. Name the source before the claim, not after it, because the listener weighs a statement by its source as it arrives: \"The Authority's own moderation report found\" prepares the ear in a way that a footnote cannot. Quote briefly and exactly. If you have a document, hold it, refer to its page, and do not read long passages: the room will start reading ahead of you and stop listening.\n\nQualifiers belong once, precisely, where they are true. \"In the three schools we examined\" is a qualifier; \"it could perhaps be argued that\" is a hedge. A sophisticated audience respects the first and discounts everything said under the second. If you are anxious, the hedges multiply; strike them from the script and keep only the qualifications you could defend under questioning.\n\nEthos and pathos are proofs, not decorations. Ethos is made by the visible fairness of the argument, by concessions offered before they are demanded, and by evident command of detail. Pathos in a committee rarely means emotion; it means making the room feel the weight of a consequence, usually through one concrete case. After each proof, restate the claim it proves. On the page this would be repetition; aloud it is the only way to bind evidence to conclusion.", "model": "The question is whether the current task measures performance, | or measures access to private tuition. || The Authority's own guidelines say the task assesses *interpretation*. | But the repertoire list rewards pieces that, | in practice, | are only taught in private studios. || Take two students of equal ability. | One has had a private teacher since Year 3; | the other learnt in a school band. | The list allows the first to choose a piece the second has never been *shown*. || If the task measures interpretation, | that difference should not matter. | It plainly does. || So the task, | as written, | measures something the syllabus did not *intend*.", "exercise": {"kind": "write", "task": "Take one claim you need to make in an upcoming committee and write it out in Toulmin's terms: claim, grounds, warrant, backing, qualifier, rebuttal. Then rewrite it as a spoken paragraph of under 120 words built on one fully developed example."}, "checks": ["The warrant connecting grounds to claim is stated aloud, not assumed", "The source of each piece of evidence is named before the evidence itself", "There is at most one qualifier, and it describes a real limit of the evidence", "The claim is restated in the final sentence of the paragraph"]}, {"id": "craft-8", "title": "Refutation and prolepsis", "teach": "Refutation (refutatio, or reprehensio in De Inventione) answers the case against you. Its anticipatory form, raising an objection before anyone has voiced it, is prolepsis, also called procatalepsis. In rooms full of people who argue for a living, prolepsis is often more powerful than proof, because it demonstrates the one thing a sceptical expert most wants to see: that you have thought about this as hard as they have.\n\nBegin by stating the objection at its strongest. Philosophers now call this steelmanning; the older courtesy was simply to state your opponent's case so fairly that he would accept your version of it. \"The strongest objection to this proposal is cost, and it is a serious one\" earns more credit than any rebuttal that follows, because it shows the room that you are not afraid of the objection. Weakening the objection before answering it is visible to everyone, and turns a refutation into a confession.\n\nThen decide which part of the objection you are attacking. Most objections can be met at one of four points. You can deny the premise: the cost is not what it is said to be. You can deny the inference: the cost is real, but it does not follow that the proposal is unaffordable. You can deny the relevance: cost is not the criterion this body is meant to apply. Or you can deny the proportion: the cost is real and relevant, but it is outweighed. Speakers who attack all four at once sound defensive. Choose the strongest ground, say which one it is, and hold it.\n\nConcessio is the companion figure: grant part of the objection in order to win the larger point. \"Yes, the new venue costs more; it also seats twice as many and pays for itself by the second year\" concedes a fact and turns it. A further move is to turn the objection into an argument for your side, showing that the very consideration raised against you, properly understood, supports you. This is the most satisfying refutation to deliver and the most dangerous; if it does not hold completely, it looks like a trick.\n\nNot every objection deserves a reply. Answer the objection the most influential sceptic in the room holds, and perhaps the one the minutes will record. Raising objections nobody holds, in order to defeat them, is the straw man, and academics detect it at once. Answering too many objections suggests that the proposal has too many weaknesses. One or two, handled well, are the norm.\n\nPlacement matters, as noted under arrangement. If the room has arrived with the objection already formed, meet it early, before your proof, or your proof will be heard through it. If the objection is latent, let your proof establish the ground first and then raise it yourself near the end, so that you choose the words in which it enters the discussion.\n\nTone is the final element. Refutation should sound cooler than proof. Lower the pitch slightly, slow down, and avoid all intensifiers. Concession is not the same as hedging: a hedge withdraws your claim a little everywhere; a concession surrenders one precise point completely and holds everything else. The room hears the difference instantly.", "model": "The strongest objection to the new venue is cost, | and the board is *right* to raise it. || The hire fee is a little under double what we pay now. | I will not pretend otherwise. || But the objection assumes that the fee is the whole of the cost, | and it is not. || The present hall cannot seat the finals, | so each year we hire a second room, | and pay twice for staffing. || The new venue removes the second room *altogether*. || So the question for the board is not whether it costs more to hire. | It does. || The question is whether it costs more to *run*.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "State aloud the strongest objection to a proposal of yours, in words its most capable opponent would accept, then answer it at one of the four points (premise, inference, relevance, proportion), naming which.", "seconds": 75}, "checks": ["The objection was stated without weakening adjectives or a dismissive tone", "You attacked one point of the objection and named it", "At least one fact was conceded completely and plainly", "Your pitch and pace were cooler in the refutation than in the proof"]}, {"id": "craft-9", "title": "The peroration", "teach": "The peroration is the part of a speech most often remembered and most often wasted. De Inventione and the Rhetorica ad Herennium give it three possible components: the summing up (enumeratio), the amplification that heightens feeling against an opponent or for a cause (indignatio or amplificatio), and the appeal for sympathy (conquestio). Translated into modern practice: recapitulate, elevate, ask. Not every ending needs all three, but every ending needs to do at least one of them on purpose.\n\nRecapitulation is not repetition. It returns to the controlling idea in nearly the words you gave it at the start, and binds the proofs to it in a single sentence: \"Validity, workload and equity all point the same way.\" A sophisticated audience will accept one sentence of summary gladly. Three sentences, and you are re-delivering the speech.\n\nElevation moves the matter from the particular to the principle. The council report about rehearsal space ends on what the space is for; the festival budget ends on what the festival has made possible for a generation of players; the eulogy ends on what the person's life asks of those who remain. It is the one place in a deliberative speech where a little grandeur is decorous, because the case has already been made and the room has earned the lift. Elevation without a case beneath it is merely loud.\n\nThe ask is the deliberative ending. Name precisely what you want the room to do: the motion, the vote, the funding, the change of wording in clause four. Many capable speakers build to an excellent close and then fail to say what they want. In a committee, the chair will then summarise your contribution in his own words, and you will have lost control of it at the moment of greatest leverage.\n\nRing composition, returning at the end to an image or phrase from the exordium, is the oldest closing device in literature and still among the most effective aloud. The Year 7 trombonist who asked why she plays scales comes back in the last minute, now answered. The listener feels the speech close like a phrase resolving to the tonic, and you will recognise the effect from music more readily than most speakers.\n\nSeveral things undo endings. New material introduced at the close tells the room you have not finished thinking. False endings, \"finally\" followed by another point, followed by \"in closing\", spend the room's goodwill in seconds. Trailing off, letting the voice fall into \"so, yes, that's really all I wanted to say\", erases the work of the previous sentence. And the reflexive \"thank you\" spoken as the last words, in the same breath as the final sentence, flattens it. If you wish to thank the room, deliver the final sentence, hold the silence, and then thank them as a separate act.\n\nThe final sentence should be written and memorised, and it should end on its most important word. English places stress naturally at the end of the clause, so build the sentence so that the weight falls there. Shorter is usually stronger. Then stop, hold eye contact for a breath, and do not move until the silence has done its work.", "model": "When she retired, | she left us a timetable, | a cupboard of scores annotated in three colours of pencil, | and a department that assumes, | without ever saying so, | that every child can learn to read music. || That assumption is her legacy. | It was not common when she arrived. | It is not common now. || We will not keep it by remembering her. | We will keep it by *teaching* as if she were still down the corridor, | listening. || So I ask you to raise your glasses, | not to her career, | but to the assumption. || To *Margaret*.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Deliver the last sixty seconds of an upcoming speech: one sentence of recapitulation, one of elevation, and an ask or a closing image. End on the stressed word, hold the silence for three seconds, and record it.", "seconds": 60}, "checks": ["The recapitulation is a single sentence that echoes the controlling idea", "No new argument or fact appears in the final minute", "The last sentence ends on its most important word", "You held three seconds of silence after the last word before moving or thanking"]}, {"id": "craft-10", "title": "Writing for the ear", "teach": "Prose written to be read and prose written to be heard obey different laws, and most educated speakers write the first when they need the second. The reader controls pace, can re-read, and sees the shape of a sentence before finishing it. The listener receives one word at a time, at your pace, once. Writing for the ear means writing for working memory.\n\nAristotle distinguished two styles of sentence in the third book of the Rhetoric: the strung-on style (lexis eiromene), in which clauses follow one another with no end in sight until the matter ends, and the periodic style (lexis katestrammene), in which the sentence is turned back on itself and its sense is complete only at its close. English rhetoric inherited the distinction as the loose and the periodic sentence.\n\nThe loose sentence states its main clause first and then adds: \"The festival has grown every year since 2015, in entries, in audiences, and in the standard of the bands.\" It is easy to follow and has a cumulative, conversational feel. Its risk is sprawl. The periodic sentence suspends its main clause until the end: \"In entries, in audiences, and above all in the standard of the bands, the festival has grown every year since 2015.\" It builds tension and gives the close real weight. Its risk is that the listener forgets what the suspended elements were for. Use periodic sentences sparingly and at moments of emphasis, keep the suspended elements short and parallel, and let loose sentences carry the narrative.\n\nEnd focus is the principle, described in the standard grammars of English, that new and important information falls most naturally at the end of the clause, where the intonation nucleus usually lands. Build sentences so that the given comes first and the new comes last. \"The committee asked for three options, and we have one\" puts the weight on the one. \"We have one option, which is fewer than the committee asked for\" buries it. Many weak spoken sentences end on a qualifier, a date or a reference: \"as I mentioned earlier\", \"in that regard\", \"going forward.\" Move them to the front or cut them.\n\nSentence length should vary, but the average must be shorter than in your written prose. A spoken sentence of more than about twenty-five words needs a structure the ear can track, usually a list or a parallel. A sequence of short sentences followed by one long one, or the reverse, creates rhythm. A paragraph of uniform medium-length sentences creates drone.\n\nCadence is the rhythmic close of a phrase. Cicero was famously attached to particular rhythmic endings, and the Latin tradition of clausulae shows how seriously the ancients took the sound of a sentence's last syllables. In English the practical rule is simpler: a stressed syllable at the end of a sentence sounds final; an unstressed one sounds unfinished. \"That is the decision before us\" closes on a stress. \"That is the decision that is before us here\" dribbles away.\n\nKeep subject and verb close. Prefer verbs to nominalisations: \"we decided\" over \"a decision was made\", \"students improved\" over \"there was improvement.\" Prefer concrete nouns to abstractions. And always read the draft aloud, standing, at speaking pace. Every place you stumble is a sentence written for the eye.", "model": "The committee asked us for three options. | We have *one*. || I should explain why. || We looked at moving rehearsals to the weekend, | and lost half the players. | We looked at sharing the hall with drama, | and lost half the hall. || What remains is the library, | which is free, | which is close, | and which is large enough for every ensemble we run. || It is not beautiful. | It does not need to be. || It needs to be *available*, | and it is.", "exercise": {"kind": "write", "task": "Take a paragraph you have recently written for a report or paper and rewrite it for the ear: shorten the average sentence, put new information at the end of each clause, replace nominalisations with verbs, and make every sentence end on a stressed syllable. Read both versions aloud and note where you stumbled in the first."}, "checks": ["No sentence ends on a qualifier, a date or a phrase such as going forward or in that regard", "Sentences vary in length, with at least one under eight words", "Any periodic sentence has short, parallel suspended elements", "The rewritten version can be read aloud at speaking pace without stumbling"]}, {"id": "craft-11", "title": "Figures that work aloud", "teach": "The figures of speech were catalogued by rhetoricians for the voice before they were studied on the page, and some of them only make full sense heard. Others, admired in print, collapse when spoken. A senior speaker chooses figures by what the ear can register at speaking pace, and by what a sophisticated audience will accept without noticing the machinery.\n\nThe figures of repetition work best aloud because the ear learns the pattern and anticipates its return. Anaphora repeats the opening of successive clauses: \"It is a program for the gifted. It is a program for the persistent. It is a program for the child who has never touched an instrument.\" Epistrophe repeats the ending. Anadiplosis takes the end of one clause and begins the next with it: \"We need a standard, and a standard needs a list.\" Each builds structure that a listener can hold without effort.\n\nAntithesis, the balanced contrast, and isocolon, clauses of matching length, are the backbone of memorable spoken lines. Max Atkinson's study of political oratory, Our Masters' Voices, showed how reliably contrasts and lists of three draw applause, because they let the audience predict the moment of completion. The tricolon is useful in the same way, but a sophisticated audience hears the mechanism of an obvious three. The remedy is to make the third member longer or different in kind, which the older rhetoric would call a rising or climactic arrangement, so that the pattern is fulfilled and exceeded at once.\n\nThe figures of reticence suit Australian rooms and academic ones especially. Litotes, affirming by denying the opposite (\"not a small achievement\"), is native understatement and almost impossible to overuse in Perth. Praeteritio, or paralipsis, mentions a thing by saying you will not mention it: \"I will not dwell on the fact that the hall has flooded twice.\" Used once, it is elegant; used twice, it is sarcasm. Hypophora, raising a question and answering it, gives a speech the rhythm of dialogue and serves as the best of signposts.\n\nRhetorical questions with obvious answers fail in senior rooms because they invite the audience to answer differently in their heads. A genuine question, left open for one held breath, works far better.\n\nSome figures do not survive the voice. Chiasmus (\"ask not what your country can do for you\") works only when short, concrete and rehearsed; an abstract chiasmus with long members is lost before it inverts. Parenthesis, the clause inserted mid-sentence, is clear on the page and confusing aloud unless marked by a clear drop in pitch and kept very short. Puns that depend on spelling, allusions the room does not share, zeugma, sustained alliteration and any acronym that must be decoded all cost more attention than they repay. Extended metaphors are dangerous aloud because the ear cannot check whether they remain consistent; a mixed metaphor, heard, sounds like a joke you did not intend.\n\nThe governing rule is the classical one: figures should arise from the matter and from feeling, not be applied to them. Quintilian's view was that ornament which draws attention to itself defeats its purpose. A single anaphora at the emotional peak of a festival speech is powerful. Six figures in a council report are a performance, and a council is not an audience that wants one.", "model": "This festival is not a competition for the gifted. | It is not a showcase for the well-resourced. | It is a weekend in which a band from a school with no music teacher | plays the same stage, | to the same adjudicators, | as a band that rehearses five days a week. || That is *not* a small thing. || I will not dwell on the fact that we have done it, | for twenty years, | on a budget most schools would spend on a piano. || I will only say that it is worth *doing*, | and that it is worth doing *well*.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Write and deliver a 60-second passage for a ceremonial occasion that uses one anaphora with a lengthened third member and one litotes, and no other figure. Record it and listen for whether the pattern is audible without sounding mechanical.", "seconds": 60}, "checks": ["The anaphora is audible on first hearing and its third member is longer or different in kind", "The litotes sounds natural rather than arch", "No chiasmus, parenthesis or acronym required the listener to decode it", "No rhetorical question with an obvious answer was used"]}, {"id": "craft-12", "title": "The anecdote and the specific detail", "teach": "In a room of experts, the detail that proves you were there outweighs any amount of argument that could have been written by anyone. The classical exemplum, the illustrative case, was a staple of proof; the modern anecdote is its descendant, and its power lies in its particularity. A general claim about student engagement is ignored. The student who practised the bass line of a chart for six weeks on an instrument with a cracked rib, because it was the only one left, is remembered for years.\n\nChoose the detail before the story. The best details are concrete, sensory and slightly unexpected: the rosin dust on the music stand, the borrowed tie at the concert, the parent who stood at the back of the hall because she had come straight from a night shift. One such detail authenticates a whole anecdote. Five turn it into description, and description in a speech is the point at which attention leaves.\n\nAn anecdote has a shape, and it is short. A sentence of setting: who, where, when. A complication: what went wrong or what was asked. A turn: what happened that you did not expect. And a link: the single sentence that binds the anecdote to your controlling idea. Without the link the room enjoys the story and forgets why you told it. In a fifteen-minute speech, an anecdote should rarely run past a minute. In a committee contribution, it should be three sentences.\n\nTense and voice matter. Telling an anecdote in the present tense, the historic present, brings it closer: \"It is the night before the festival. The bus has not arrived.\" Use it for the narrative core, then return to the past tense for the reflection. Direct speech, a short line actually said, is more vivid than reported speech, provided it is brief and you are certain of the words.\n\nAnecdotes involving students carry obligations that are not merely ethical but rhetorical. A child used as a prop, named, identifiable, or made to seem pitiable, costs a speaker the room's trust, especially in a parent body. Anonymise, change identifying particulars, seek permission where the story could be recognised, and never tell a story a student would be embarrassed to hear. The audience knows instinctively whether the speaker respects the person in the story.\n\nStories about yourself need care in the other direction. An anecdote in which you are the hero reads as self-promotion; one in which you were wrong, or learnt something, builds ethos. The best award acceptances and keynotes often turn on the moment the speaker misjudged something and a student or colleague corrected him. That is not false modesty; it is evidence that you are still paying attention.\n\nAvoid stock anecdotes. The starfish on the beach, the teacher and the jar of rocks, the misattributed quotation: a sophisticated audience has heard them, recognises them in the first sentence, and discounts both the story and the teller. A real anecdote from your own work, however small, is always stronger than a borrowed parable, however polished.\n\nFinally, test each anecdote against the controlling idea. If it is a good story that proves nothing, keep it for the dinner afterwards.", "model": "It is the night before the State finals. | The double bass we hired has not arrived, | and our bassist, | who is fifteen, | has been practising for six weeks on a school instrument with a cracked rib. || I offer to find another. | She says, | \"No. I know where *this* one is out of tune.\" || She played it the next day, | and played it *well*. || I tell you this because it is the best definition of musicianship I have heard from anyone, | and it came from a student who knew her instrument better than I did.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Tell one anecdote from your own teaching or playing in under sixty seconds, using the historic present, a single concrete detail, and a final sentence that links it explicitly to a claim. Record it and time it.", "seconds": 60}, "checks": ["The anecdote contains exactly one memorable concrete detail", "The final sentence explicitly links the story to a claim", "No student is identifiable and none is made to seem pitiable", "It ran under sixty seconds"]}, {"id": "craft-13", "title": "Numbers and data", "teach": "A table can hold forty figures; a listener can hold about two. Numbers in a speech are therefore not data in the analytical sense but evidence chosen for its effect on judgement, and every number you speak should have earned its place by changing what the room believes. The discipline is to decide, before writing, which one or two numbers carry the case, and to let the rest live in the written paper that accompanies you.\n\nGive one number per sentence. Two numbers in a sentence invite comparison the ear cannot perform at speaking pace; three produce a blur. If a comparison is the point, make it for the listener: not \"enrolments were 212 in 2019 and 287 this year\" but \"enrolments have risen by about a third since 2019.\" The arithmetic is your job, not theirs.\n\nRound, and say that you are rounding. \"About one in three\" is heard and kept; \"32.7 per cent\" is heard and lost, and in an academic room it can sound like false precision. Keep the exact figure for the moment when exactness is the argument, such as a funding shortfall that the panel will check, and then say it slowly, once, and repeat it.\n\nEvery number needs a comparison, because a number without one has no meaning. Forty-one instruments is a fact; forty-one instruments for a hundred and sixty players is an argument. The comparison can be with the past (a trend), with a peer (a benchmark), with a standard (a requirement), or with something human-scaled. Translating a total into a unit the listener can picture is often the decisive move: a budget figure becomes the cost per student per year, a deficit becomes the price of one tuba.\n\nUse end focus. The number usually belongs early, as given information, and the meaning belongs at the end, where the stress falls: \"We replace four instruments a year, and we retire seven.\" Or, if the number itself is the surprise, hold it to the end: \"The replacement budget, adjusted for prices, has fallen by nearly half.\"\n\nName the source in the sentence, before the figure, and choose the source the room trusts most. In a school council, the business manager's report. In a curriculum committee, the Authority's own published data. In a funding panel, the panel's own guidelines. Never cite a figure you could not reproduce on request; in rooms of policy people, someone will ask.\n\nWatch the familiar distortions, because a sophisticated audience certainly will. A percentage change on a small base (\"a hundred per cent increase\" from two students to four) invites ridicule. Mixing counts and rates confuses. Selecting a start year that flatters the trend will be noticed by anyone who knows the history. The honest framing is also the persuasive one, because it survives questions.\n\nIf you use slides, show the one number large and say what it means; do not show a table and read from it. Slides of dense data divide the room into those reading and those listening, and you lose both.\n\nFinally, repeat the decisive number at the close, in exactly the same form. Repetition is what turns a figure into the room's shared fact.", "model": "I want to give the panel one number, | and then explain what it means. || The department replaces four loan instruments a year. | It retires *seven*. || That gap has held, | on the business manager's figures, | for five years. || Put another way: | each year, | three more students begin a specialist program | without an instrument the school can lend them. || The grant we are seeking would close that gap for three years. | It would cost, | per student, | about what a family pays for one term of private lessons. || Four replaced. | Seven retired. || That is the *case*.", "exercise": {"kind": "write", "task": "Take a report or submission containing at least ten figures. Choose the one or two that carry the case, and write a spoken paragraph of under 120 words that gives each a named source, a comparison and a human-scale translation, and repeats the decisive figure at the end."}, "checks": ["No sentence contains more than one number", "Every number is paired with a comparison or translation", "The source of each figure is named before the figure", "The decisive number is repeated in identical form at the end"]}, {"id": "craft-14", "title": "Humour and gravity", "teach": "The serious treatment of laughter in rhetoric is ancient. In the second book of Cicero's De Oratore, Julius Caesar Strabo gives a long account of wit and distinguishes humour arising from the matter (res) from humour arising from the words (dicta). Quintilian devotes a chapter of the Institutio Oratoria to laughter and admits, candidly, that it is difficult to teach. Both treat humour as a tool of persuasion with real risks, not as decoration.\n\nIn senior rooms, humour almost always means wit rather than jokes. A prepared joke, imported from elsewhere, announces itself and puts the room under an obligation to laugh. Wit arises from the situation: the observed absurdity of the agenda, the gap between the policy and the practice, the thing everyone was thinking and no one had said. Humour of the matter is safer than humour of the words, because it shows you see clearly; wordplay shows only that you prepared.\n\nSelf-deprecation is the Australian default, and it works within limits. Deprecate your circumstances, your taste, your past self, your timekeeping; never your competence on the matter at hand. A speaker who jokes that he does not really understand the budget has invited the room to believe him. Self-deprecation by a senior figure also lowers the temperature for others: it signals that the room is safe for honesty.\n\nThe lines that must not be crossed are the lines of power. Humour at the expense of someone in the room with less standing than you, a junior colleague, a student, a parent, is not wit but a demonstration of rank, and the room recognises it. Humour at the expense of a body that is absent can also return to you in the minutes. The safest target is the shared situation, and the next safest is yourself.\n\nTiming is mechanical and can be learnt. The word that carries the joke goes last in its sentence, where end focus places the stress. A small pause before that word sets it up; a pause after it allows the laugh. If the laugh does not come, do not wait for it. Continue as if the line were simply a good sentence, which, if it was well made, it still is. Never explain a line that missed.\n\nGravity is the other skill, and its signals are physical. When the matter turns serious, the voice drops in pitch, slows, and loses ornament. Figures become fewer; sentences become plainer and often shorter. The transition into gravity should be marked by a held silence, so that the room hears the change of key before the first grave sentence arrives. Moving from a laugh straight into grief, without that rest, jars.\n\nThe two can coexist, and the eulogy shows how. Humour that is true to the person, the story everyone recognises, gives the room permission to grieve by first letting it remember the living person. It must come early or in the middle, never at the close, and it must be warm rather than clever. A good eulogy usually earns one real laugh and spends it on making the loss specific.\n\nA last principle: Australian rooms dislike pomposity more than they dislike error. A speaker who can be grave without sounding solemn, and light without sounding flippant, is understood to be in command of both.", "model": "When the committee asked me to accept this on behalf of the department, | I was told I would have *three* minutes. || As a bass player, | I have never been given three minutes for anything, | so I intend to use every one of them. || [pause] || This award is not for me. | It is for eleven years of rehearsals on Tuesday nights, | in winter, | in a hall with no heating, | by teachers who could have gone home. || [held] || Two of them are not here to see it. | I would like this to be *theirs*.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Deliver a 90-second award acceptance or toast that contains one line of situational wit with its key word last, followed by a held silence and a deliberate shift into one grave sentence. Record it and check that the pitch and pace audibly change at the shift.", "seconds": 90}, "checks": ["The humour is drawn from the situation or yourself, not from anyone with less standing", "The word that carries the line comes last in its sentence", "A held silence marks the shift into gravity", "Pitch drops and pace slows audibly in the grave passage"]}, {"id": "craft-15", "title": "Memory: notes, anchors and the full text", "teach": "Memoria was the fourth canon of classical rhetoric, and the ancients took it seriously because they spoke without paper. The Rhetorica ad Herennium sets out the artificial memory: a series of familiar places (loci) in which the speaker stores vivid images (imagines), walking through them in order as he speaks. Cicero, in De Oratore, attributes the method's invention to Simonides of Ceos, who identified the dead at a collapsed banquet hall by remembering where each had sat. The method of loci still works, and it remains the best technique for memorising a structure, as opposed to a text.\n\nThe modern speaker chooses among four modes, and the choice is a judgement of occasion. The fully scripted text, read or near-memorised. The extemporaneous speech, prepared thoroughly and delivered from notes in words found in the moment. The memorised speech, rare now and risky. And the impromptu remark, which in committees is most of what you do and which is improved by having a controlling idea and a partition ready before you raise your hand.\n\nScript the text when the words themselves carry consequence. A eulogy, because emotion will take your memory and the family deserves exact words. A formal statement of policy, a motion, anything that will be quoted or minuted, because a paraphrase can commit you to something you did not intend. Ceremonial addresses with strict time limits. Any occasion broadcast or recorded. In these cases the craft is to write for the ear and then rehearse until the script is a score you interpret, not a page you read.\n\nSpeak from notes when the room needs responsiveness more than exactness: a council report, a committee contribution, a panel, a department meeting, a talk to parents. Notes allow you to look at people, to adjust to a question in the faces before anyone asks it, and to sound like yourself. Good notes are not sentences. They are keywords in large type, one card or one half-page per section, with the controlling idea written at the top of every card.\n\nAnchors are the hybrid that most senior speakers actually use. Memorise, word for word, the first sentence, the partition, the transitions between sections, any precise formulation (a figure, a motion, a definition), and the final sentence. Everything between the anchors is spoken from keywords. The anchors give the speech its exact architecture; the spaces between give it life. If you lose your place, you need only find the next anchor.\n\nFor a mind that moves associatively, anchors are also the safety line. A digression is permissible as long as you can see the next anchor and know how to return to it. When you notice yourself three tangents deep, stop, look at the card, say the anchor, and the room will hear only a speaker moving to his next point. The danger is not the tangent; it is not knowing the way back.\n\nWhatever the mode, memorise the structure first, using a sequence of places if it helps: the door of the hall for the exordium, the stage for the narration, and so on. Then memorise the anchors. Only then, and only if the occasion requires it, memorise the text. A speaker who knows his structure cold can lose any sentence and keep the speech.", "model": "[Anchor, memorised] | The panel asked us three questions, | and I will answer them in order: | who the festival serves, | what it costs, | and what changes if it *stops*. || [Keywords: regional schools, travel, first stage] | Most of the bands come from schools with no specialist teacher. | For many players, | this is the first time they have played on a stage at all. || [Anchor, memorised] | That is who it serves. | What does it cost? || [Keywords: per player, per day] | ... || [Anchor, memorised] | If it stops, | those players do not find another festival. | They stop *playing*.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "For an upcoming contribution, write a single note card with the controlling idea at the top, three keywords per section, and the first sentence, transitions and final sentence written out in full. Memorise only the anchors and deliver the whole contribution from the card.", "seconds": 120}, "checks": ["The first sentence, each transition and the final sentence were delivered verbatim from memory", "Everything between the anchors was spoken from keywords, not read", "You looked at the card only at section boundaries", "If you digressed, you returned through the next anchor"]}, {"id": "craft-16", "title": "Rehearsal and delivery", "teach": "Delivery (actio, or pronuntiatio) was the fifth canon, and Cicero reports the story that Demosthenes, asked what mattered most in oratory, answered delivery, and delivery, and delivery. A written text is a score; delivery is its performance. You already know this from the bandstand better than most speakers do: the notes are necessary and nowhere near sufficient, and the difference between a reading and a performance is time, dynamics and the relationship with the room.\n\nPrepare the script physically. Large type, at least sixteen point, with generous line spacing. Never break a sentence across a page. Number the pages, do not staple them, and slide each finished page aside rather than turning it. Leave a wide right margin for marks. Then mark it as a score: a single slash for a breath, a double slash for a held full stop, an underline for the stressed word in each sentence that carries the meaning, and a marginal note where pace or pitch changes. The markup in the models in this module is a version of exactly that.\n\nPace for a written text should be slower than you think. Nervous speakers accelerate, and a scripted text, being denser than conversation, needs more time per word than talk does. As a planning figure, allow around 130 words a minute for a formal scripted address and cut the script to fit. Vary pace deliberately: faster through narrative and context, slower through the controlling idea, the decisive number and the close.\n\nPause is the most powerful instrument a speaker has and the one inexperienced speakers fear most. A pause before a key word makes the room lean towards it; a pause after a key sentence lets it land. A held silence of two or three seconds, which feels interminable to the speaker, reads to the audience as authority. Your sense of time as a bassist is an asset here: the pause is a rest with a value, not a gap to be filled, and the next phrase should arrive in time, not early.\n\nFor eye contact while reading, the technique is to look down, take the phrase in, look up, and deliver it to one person. Then look down for the next phrase. The end of every sentence, and especially its stressed final word, should be delivered to the room, not to the page. Hold each person for a complete thought rather than sweeping. In a committee, direct the key sentence to the person whose view will decide the outcome, and the refutation to the person who holds the objection.\n\nOwning the room begins before you speak. Arrive early and stand where you will speak. Walk to the lectern without hurry, arrange your pages, look up, and wait for silence before the first word, however long that takes. Plant your feet. Hands rest on the lectern or at your sides; gestures, if any, arise from the meaning. Do not apologise for technology, for time or for yourself. Stillness reads as confidence; movement without purpose reads as nerves.\n\nRehearse aloud, standing, at full voice, and at least once in the room or one like it. Time every run. Record one and listen for the places where you speed up, drop the ends of sentences or add fillers. Rehearse the first minute and the last minute most, because they carry the most nerves and the most weight. A speech rehearsed five times aloud sounds spontaneous; one rehearsed only in your head sounds read.", "model": "She joined the department in 1998, | when the music block was two portable classrooms | and a piano no one would claim. || [slow] | She leaves it with five ensembles, | a waiting list, | and a generation of players who think rehearsing on a Tuesday night in July is *normal*. || [look up] || It is not normal. || It is what she made *possible*. || [held, three seconds] || So tonight, | before the speeches and the cake, | I would ask you to stand, | and to thank her, | *properly*.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Print a two-minute passage from a speech you are giving, mark it as a score (breaths, held stops, one stressed word per sentence, pace notes), and deliver it standing at full voice, looking up for the end of every sentence. Record and time it, and repeat once with every pause doubled.", "seconds": 120}, "checks": ["The end of every sentence was delivered looking at the room, not the page", "Each held stop lasted at least two seconds", "The timed run was within ten seconds of the target length", "You waited for silence before the first word and held still after the last"]}], "types": [{"id": "type-keynote", "name": "Conference paper or keynote", "occasion": "An invited address to music educators at a state or national conference, setting the intellectual tone for the day.", "minutes": [15, 45], "purpose": "Leave a room of expert peers holding one idea they did not walk in with, argued well enough that they can repeat it, test it and act on it on Monday.", "parts": [{"part": "Opening image", "what": "Puts a single concrete scene in front of the room before any claim is made.", "prompt": "What one moment from a rehearsal room contains your whole argument in miniature?", "share": 8}, {"part": "The problem stated", "what": "Names the tension the profession lives with, in terms the room recognises as its own.", "prompt": "What do these people already half-know but have not said aloud?", "share": 12}, {"part": "Thesis", "what": "States the claim once, plainly, in a sentence that could be quoted.", "prompt": "If they remember one sentence, which is it?", "share": 8}, {"part": "Development in three movements", "what": "Builds the argument through practice, evidence and structure, each movement doing a different job.", "prompt": "What three steps take a sceptic from the problem to your thesis?", "share": 42}, {"part": "Concession and limit", "what": "Grants the strongest objection at full strength and draws the boundary of the claim.", "prompt": "What would the cleverest person in the room say against you, and what do you concede?", "share": 12}, {"part": "Return and charge", "what": "Returns to the opening image, transformed, and gives the room something specific to do.", "prompt": "What should they do differently on Monday, and how does the opening scene look now?", "share": 18}], "pitfalls": ["Mistaking coverage for argument: a keynote that surveys the field leaves nothing behind, while one claim developed properly does.", "Spending the opening on thanks and credentials after the chair has already introduced you; acknowledge in a sentence, then begin.", "Citing research you could not defend in questions; one source you know thoroughly outweighs five you have skimmed.", "Ending on a summary of the three points rather than on the image and the charge; an expert room supplies its own summary.", "Writing for the page: nested clauses and long lists that read well and cannot be heard."], "model": {"title": "The Bar Before the Entry", "setting": "A fifteen-minute opening keynote at a state conference of school music educators, delivered from the lectern after a formal introduction by the conference chair.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "Thank you. I want to begin in a rehearsal room, at about twenty past three on a Thursday. A Year 9 bass player has forty-three bars rest. The band is loud, the trumpets are enjoying themselves, and she is counting. Not tapping, not mouthing the numbers: *counting*, | with her whole body still. At bar forty-one she lifts the instrument. At bar forty-three she comes in, | exactly, || and nobody in the room notices. || That is my subject this morning: the musicianship nobody notices, and why our assessment cannot see it.", "note": "Begins with one scene rather than a greeting or a thesis, so the room is listening before it is asked to agree. The held stop after 'nobody in the room notices' lets the paradox land before it is named."}, {"text": "Everyone here knows the shape of the problem. We assess what can be marked: the performance on the day, the aural paper, the analysis essay. Those are real things, and I have marked thousands of them. But the competence that made her entry possible, | sustained attention inside a texture, an internal pulse that survives forty bars of other people's noise, a sense of her own place in a whole, | appears nowhere in a criterion. We teach it every afternoon. || We report it never.", "note": "States the problem in the room's own vocabulary and claims experience in passing rather than as a credential. The list is held between two breaths so it is heard as one long object, and the closing antithesis is set apart by a stop."}, {"text": "So here is the claim, as plainly as I can put it. *The ensemble is not an adjunct to the music curriculum. It is the place where the curriculum's deepest outcomes are actually achieved,* | and until our assessment reflects that, we will keep rewarding the visible and starving the essential.", "note": "The thesis arrives once, in a quotable sentence stressed as a whole. The second clause supplies the stakes so the claim does not sit inert."}, {"text": "Let me make the case in three steps. The first is about what musicians actually do. Ask any working professional what separates a player you want to hire from one you do not, and you will not hear about scales. You will hear about listening, about time, and about whether the person makes the band sound better. I have spent twenty-six years on bandstands in this city, and I have never once been booked because of a theory result. | I have been booked, repeatedly, because I count rests.", "note": "The first movement argues from professional practice and uses his own authority once, briefly. The dry final line earns credibility through understatement rather than claiming it."}, {"text": "The second step is about what students remember. When former students come back, and they do, they do not talk about the composition task in Year 11. They talk about the night the band locked in at the festival, or the rehearsal where the conductor stopped, said nothing for ten seconds, and they finally heard the inner parts. That is not nostalgia. | It is a report from the learner on where the learning happened.", "note": "The second movement shifts from the professional to the learner, so the argument widens rather than repeats. The final sentence converts anecdote into evidence by naming what kind of evidence it is."}, {"text": "The third step is the uncomfortable one. Our timetables and our staffing follow our assessment. What is examined gets classroom hours; what is not gets lunchtimes, early mornings and goodwill. Every one of us has watched an ensemble program survive on the private generosity of a few teachers. | That is not a model of a curriculum. || It is a model of a hobby that happens to be essential.", "note": "The third movement moves from pedagogy to structure, which is where the room's frustration actually lives. The held stop sets up the closing antithesis, 'hobby' against 'essential', which should be delivered slower than anything before it."}, {"text": "Now the obvious objection, and it is a good one. Ensemble achievement is collective. How do you assess an individual inside a group without rewarding passengers, or penalising the student who is carried by a weak section? That is not solved by a rubric written over a weekend. But we already accept considered judgement in other places: the moderated portfolio, the observed practical, the teacher's evidence gathered over time. The question is not whether it can be done. | It is whether we think it is worth the trouble.", "note": "Grants the strongest objection at full strength before answering it, which is what a sceptical expert audience needs to see. The answer does not overclaim; it reframes a technical objection as a question of will."}, {"text": "Let me also be clear about what I am not saying. I am not saying the performance examination should go, or that theory and analysis matter less than we think. They matter a great deal; the best ensemble players I know are formidable analysts. Nor am I saying that every school can staff a full ensemble program tomorrow. I am making a narrower claim, | and I think a harder one to dismiss: that the competences we most value in a musician are being developed in a place our documents treat as optional, || and that this is a design fault, not an accident of history.", "note": "Draws the boundary of the thesis explicitly, which protects it from being caricatured in questions. Narrowing a claim in front of experts makes it stronger, and the final clause restates it in sharper terms."}, {"text": "So I will ask three modest things of you. This term, write down what you actually observe in rehearsal that tells you a student has grown, in words precise enough to put in front of a moderator. Share those words with each other, because that is how criteria begin. And when you sit on a committee, as many of you do, ask where the ensemble lives in the document. | If the answer is 'nowhere', || ask why.", "note": "The charge is concrete, small and achievable, which makes it more likely to happen than a grand appeal. The three requests escalate from private practice to public influence, ending on the one that matters most."}, {"text": "Back to Thursday afternoon. Bar forty-three. The entry nobody noticed. | I would like us to build a curriculum that notices. || Thank you.", "note": "Returns to the opening scene in a handful of short sentences, so the image now carries the whole argument. The last line restates the thesis as an aspiration and stops; there is no summary."}]}}, {"id": "type-council-report", "name": "Report to a school council or board", "occasion": "A formal spoken report to a governing body that must understand performance, risk and the decisions in front of it, within a fixed agenda item.", "minutes": [4, 10], "purpose": "Give members an accurate, proportionate account of performance against what they agreed, surface anything that needs their judgement, and leave a clear resolution or noting on the record.", "parts": [{"part": "Headline", "what": "Gives the bottom line in two sentences so members hear everything after it correctly.", "prompt": "If the council heard only your first thirty seconds, what must they know?", "share": 10}, {"part": "Against the plan", "what": "Reports performance against what the body previously agreed, not against what is convenient to report.", "prompt": "What did the council approve last time, and how did each item actually go?", "share": 30}, {"part": "What did not go well", "what": "States shortfalls and risks plainly, with cause and remedy.", "prompt": "What would a member be annoyed to discover later that you knew tonight?", "share": 20}, {"part": "Matters for judgement", "what": "Separates what the council must decide from what it is simply being told, with a recommendation on each.", "prompt": "What exactly do you need them to resolve, note or endorse, and what do you recommend?", "share": 25}, {"part": "Close", "what": "States the resolution sought in its exact words and invites questions on the paper.", "prompt": "What single sentence should the minutes record?", "share": 15}], "pitfalls": ["Narrating activity instead of reporting outcomes; a governing body needs results against agreed aims, not a diary of the term.", "Placing the risk mid-report so it can later be said to have been mentioned; members notice, and trust in every later report erodes.", "Blurring governance and management: drawing the council into operational detail it should not decide, or withholding something it must.", "Reading the circulated paper aloud; the spoken report should assume the paper has been read and add judgement to it.", "Leaving the decision sought implicit, so the minutes record a discussion rather than a resolution."], "model": {"title": "Chair's Report to Council, Term 4", "setting": "Agenda item 4 at the final school council meeting of the year; the written report was circulated a week earlier and members have read it.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "Thank you. Members have the written report, so I will not read it to you. I will give you the headline, two matters that need your judgement, and one resolution I will ask you to pass tonight. || The headline is this: council did three of the four things it set itself in February, | and the fourth, community consultation, fell short, for reasons I will be candid about.", "note": "Announces the structure and the ask in the first breath, which is a courtesy to members and a discipline for the speaker. The shortfall is admitted in the headline itself, so nothing later can read as a surprise."}, {"text": "Against the plan. We undertook to review progress on the school's business plan priorities at every meeting rather than once a year, and we did, | at all four meetings. We undertook to receive a report from the student council each term; we have received three, and the fourth is on tonight's agenda. And we undertook to review our own procedures. The revised standing orders were adopted in August, and I think members have found our meetings shorter, | and better, for it.", "note": "Reports each commitment in the same form, undertaking then result, so members can check them against February's minutes. The one evaluative remark is brief and is one members can verify from their own experience."}, {"text": "The shortfall. We said we would consult the school community on the draft uniform guidelines, and we received forty-one responses from a community of more than two thousand families. That is not consultation. It is a sample of the already engaged. The cause, I think, was mine: we relied on one newsletter item and one email, in the busiest fortnight of Term 2. || I do not propose that we treat those responses as a mandate.", "note": "States the number, judges it honestly and takes ownership without self-reproach. The final sentence draws the governance consequence, which is what members need from the chair."}, {"text": "That brings me to the two matters for your judgement. The first is whether to reopen the uniform consultation in Term 1, using the student council and the parent association as channels as well as the newsletter. I recommend that we do. The second is a question of scope. A member has asked whether council should receive the detailed budget by cost centre. My view is that council's role is to approve the budget and monitor it against the plan, | not to manage it, || and I would suggest we ask the principal for a one-page summary each term instead. I would welcome other views on both.", "note": "Separates the two matters cleanly and offers a recommendation on each, so discussion has something definite to respond to. The second point marks the line between governance and management without lecturing the member who raised it."}, {"text": "Finally, the resolution. I would ask a member to move that council adopt the 2027 work plan as circulated, including a reopened uniform consultation in Term 1. | If that is agreed, the minutes will record it in those words. I am happy to take questions on anything in the written report.", "note": "Ends with the exact wording sought, so the minute-taker and members know precisely what is being decided. Questions are invited on the paper, which anchors discussion rather than reopening the whole year."}]}}, {"id": "type-submission", "name": "Submission to a committee or panel", "occasion": "An oral submission to a funding panel, review committee or decision-making body, advocating a position or a resource within a strict time limit.", "minutes": [5, 12], "purpose": "Make it easy for the panel to say yes: state the ask, prove the need, meet the panel's own criteria, answer the strongest objection before it is raised, and show exactly what the decision will buy.", "parts": [{"part": "The ask", "what": "States precisely what is sought, for how long and to produce what.", "prompt": "What do you want this panel to decide, in one sentence with a number in it?", "share": 10}, {"part": "The need", "what": "Establishes the problem the ask solves, with evidence the panel could verify.", "prompt": "What is the gap, and how do you know it exists?", "share": 20}, {"part": "The proposal", "what": "Shows what will be done and maps it onto the panel's own criteria, in their language.", "prompt": "Which of the panel's stated criteria does this meet, and where exactly?", "share": 25}, {"part": "The objection answered", "what": "Raises the panel's likely reservation in fair terms and resolves it.", "prompt": "What is the reason they would say no, and what is your answer?", "share": 20}, {"part": "Accountability", "what": "Shows how success will be measured, reported and, if necessary, judged a failure.", "prompt": "How will they know in a year that their decision was right?", "share": 15}, {"part": "The ask restated", "what": "Closes on the ask, in identical terms.", "prompt": "What words do you want them to carry into deliberation?", "share": 10}], "pitfalls": ["Advocating the cause rather than the proposal; the panel is already sympathetic to music and needs reasons to fund this, not reasons to care.", "Ignoring the panel's criteria and vocabulary, which forces members to translate your case into their framework themselves, usually unfavourably.", "Inflating the need with figures you cannot source; one challenged number taints every other one.", "Leaving the obvious objection for questions, where you will answer it defensively instead of on your own terms.", "Softening or changing the ask at the close in response to resistance you have imagined."], "model": {"title": "Regional Ensemble Residencies", "setting": "A seven-minute oral submission to a state arts funding panel on behalf of the schools' jazz festival association, followed by questions from the panel.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "Thank you, Chair. The association is asking the panel for forty-eight thousand dollars over two years to fund eight three-day ensemble residencies in regional schools, | four each year, led by professional jazz musicians, and culminating in performance at the state festival. || That is the whole of the request, and I will spend the next six minutes showing why it deserves funding.", "note": "Opens with the complete ask, amount, period and output, so the panel hears everything after it as justification. The held stop separates the ask from the case."}, {"text": "The need. Last year the festival received entries from one hundred and twelve ensembles. Nine came from schools outside the metropolitan area. Those nine schools told us the same thing in different words: they have students who want to play this music, and no one within three hundred kilometres who can teach the style. The regional music teacher is often the only one in the school, and frequently a generalist from a brass or choral background. | The gap is not enthusiasm. || It is access to people who do this for a living.", "note": "Gives two figures the association holds and can verify, and lets the ratio argue without commentary. The closing antithesis names the precise gap, which forestalls the response that sheet music and a video would do."}, {"text": "The proposal. Each residency places two professional musicians in a regional school for three days. They rehearse the ensemble, take sectionals, and spend one session with the music teacher alone, on repertoire, rhythm section set-up and rehearsal technique. That last session is the one I would ask you to notice, because it is what remains when the musicians leave. Your guidelines ask for benefit sustained beyond the funded period. | *This is where it lives.*", "note": "Describes the activity concretely, then maps it directly onto the panel's own criterion in the panel's own words. The short stressed sentence makes the match impossible to miss."}, {"text": "I expect the panel will ask, reasonably, why three days in a school should change anything. It is a fair question to put to any short intervention. Our answer is the structure around the three days. Each school commits in writing to enter the festival that year; the festival waives the entry fee, and the association contributes to travel. So the residency is not an event. || It is the first rehearsal of a season that ends on a stage in Perth, in front of their peers.", "note": "Raises the panel's likeliest reservation in its own terms and calls it reasonable, which disarms it. The answer is structural rather than rhetorical, and the closing image gives the panel something to remember in deliberation."}, {"text": "Accountability. We will report to the panel on four measures: the number of students reached; the number of residency schools that enter the festival; the number that enter again the following year without support; and the teachers' own account of what they now do differently, gathered through a short structured interview. The third measure is the honest one. If schools do not come back without us, | we will tell you, || and we will not ask for a third year.", "note": "Offers measures the panel can hold the association to, and singles out the hardest. Volunteering a failure condition is the strongest signal of seriousness a submitter can give."}, {"text": "The costs are set out on page four of the written application: musicians' fees at award rates, travel and accommodation, and a small amount for parts. There is no administration charge; the association's volunteers carry that. | So the request, again: forty-eight thousand dollars, over two years, for eight residencies and the festival season each one begins. Thank you. I am happy to take the panel's questions.", "note": "Refers to the written detail rather than reciting it, and makes the one cost point panels always look for. The ask is restated in identical terms so the panel deliberates on exactly what was proposed."}]}}, {"id": "type-festival-opening", "name": "Opening a festival", "occasion": "A short formal address that declares a festival open, usually to a large mixed audience of students, families, adjudicators, sponsors and officials.", "minutes": [3, 6], "purpose": "Mark the threshold: give the event its meaning and its scale, honour the people who made it possible, set the spirit in which it should be played, and get out of the way of the music.", "parts": [{"part": "Acknowledgement", "what": "Acknowledges Country and welcomes those who must be welcomed, efficiently and sincerely.", "prompt": "Who must be acknowledged, in what order, and who can be welcomed collectively?", "share": 12}, {"part": "What this is", "what": "Gives the occasion its meaning in one idea rather than a history.", "prompt": "What is this festival for, in a sentence a fourteen-year-old would remember?", "share": 20}, {"part": "The image of scale", "what": "Makes the size and effort of the event vivid through one or two concrete details.", "prompt": "What single fact shows what it took to get everyone here?", "share": 15}, {"part": "Thanks", "what": "Thanks those whose work is invisible, collectively and with one specific detail.", "prompt": "Whose work would otherwise go unseen, and what did they actually do?", "share": 13}, {"part": "To the performers", "what": "Speaks directly to the students, setting the spirit in which they should play and listen.", "prompt": "What do you want the youngest player here to carry onto the stage?", "share": 30}, {"part": "Declaration", "what": "Opens the festival in a clear ceremonial sentence and hands over at once.", "prompt": "What is the exact sentence of declaration, and what immediately follows it?", "share": 10}], "pitfalls": ["Turning the opening into a roll call of thanks the audience endures rather than hears; name few, thank many collectively.", "Recounting the festival's history at length; one sentence of lineage is enough for an audience that came for music.", "Addressing the officials and sponsors in the front row while eight hundred students sit behind them.", "A declaration that trails off or is buried mid-sentence, so the applause does not know where to land.", "Running over time on a day where every minute is taken from a performing ensemble's schedule."], "model": {"title": "Opening the Schools' Jazz Festival", "setting": "The formal opening of a three-day state schools' jazz festival, delivered from the stage of a concert hall to students, families, adjudicators and guests immediately before the first ensemble plays.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "Good morning. I would like to begin by acknowledging the Whadjuk Noongar people, on whose land we gather, and to pay my respects to their elders, past and present. This country carried music long before any of it was written down, and it is right that a music festival begins by saying so. | I also welcome our adjudicators, our guests, and the teachers and families who got everyone here before nine o'clock on a Friday.", "note": "Gives the acknowledgement a sentence of its own meaning so it is not heard as formula, then welcomes groups rather than individuals. The light final clause warms the room without a joke being announced."}, {"text": "This festival exists for one reason. It is the place where young musicians in this state play this music for each other. Not for a mark, though there will be comments. Not for a prize, though there will be awards. | *For each other.* || Over three days you will hear more than a hundred ensembles, and the best thing that can happen to you this weekend is to hear a band from a school you have never heard of play something that sends you home to practise.", "note": "States the purpose in one idea, set up by two concessions and resolved by repetition. The second half turns the purpose into a concrete hope the students will recognise."}, {"text": "To give you a sense of scale: by Sunday evening more than two thousand students will have walked across this stage. Somewhere in this building there is a spreadsheet with every one of those names and every changeover timed to the minute, and a volunteer who has been looking at it since July. | The adjudicators have between them heard more school bands than anyone in the country should, and they will write something useful about every one. The association's committee, our volunteers, the venue crew and the stage managers are the reason this works, || and I would ask you to thank them now.", "note": "Conveys scale with one figure and one human image rather than a catalogue. The thanks are collective and invite applause, which also gives the speaker a natural breath before turning to the students."}, {"text": "And to the players. Some of you are about to perform in public for the first time; some of you have done this every year since Year 7. Here is what I would say to all of you, as someone who has been walking onto stages in this city for twenty-six years. *Listen* more than you play. Count the rests. When your solo comes, play one idea and mean it. If something goes wrong, keep going; nobody in the audience will remember the mistake, but they will remember how you recovered. And when the band before you sounds good, || let them hear you cheer.", "note": "Speaks directly to the students and invokes his own standing once, briefly, to earn the advice. Four short imperatives build to the last, which is set off by a held stop because it is the real point of the festival."}, {"text": "It is my great pleasure to declare this year's festival open. || Please welcome our first ensemble.", "note": "The declaration stands as a sentence of its own, followed by a held stop so the applause has a place to land. The immediate handover keeps the day moving and removes any temptation to keep talking."}]}}, {"id": "type-concert-address", "name": "Concert address (opening and closing)", "occasion": "The two short spoken frames of a school concert: a welcome before the music and a close that thanks, names what happened and sends the audience home.", "minutes": [2, 5], "purpose": "Open: settle the hall, give the evening one frame and start the music cleanly. Close: honour the people the audience cannot see, name what the evening meant, and send everyone home on the students rather than on administration.", "parts": [{"part": "Opening welcome", "what": "Settles the hall, acknowledges Country and welcomes guests.", "prompt": "What do people need to hear in the first twenty seconds to stop talking and settle?", "share": 15}, {"part": "The evening's frame", "what": "Gives the audience one thing to listen for.", "prompt": "What links tonight's program, or what one thing should they listen for?", "share": 20}, {"part": "Practicalities and handover", "what": "Covers phones and exits in plain sentences and hands over to the first ensemble.", "prompt": "What must be said, and what is the shortest way to say it?", "share": 10}, {"part": "Closing thanks", "what": "Thanks specific unseen groups with one concrete detail each.", "prompt": "Whose work would the audience never see unless you named it?", "share": 25}, {"part": "What tonight meant", "what": "Names, briefly, what the audience has just witnessed.", "prompt": "What happened tonight that was not printed in the program?", "share": 20}, {"part": "Send-off", "what": "Ends cleanly and gives the last moment to the musicians.", "prompt": "What is your last sentence, and what happens immediately after it?", "share": 10}], "pitfalls": ["Front-loading the thanks at the opening when the audience wants music, leaving nothing fresh for the close.", "Explaining the music so thoroughly that the audience listens for the commentary instead of the piece.", "Closing with a thank-you list read from a page, so the evening ends on administration.", "Letting the address drift towards the director's own work rather than the students and their families.", "Improvising the closing words; they are what the audience walks out with, and should be chosen in advance."], "model": {"title": "Winter Concert: Welcome and Close", "setting": "The opening and closing words at a school's winter concert of concert bands, string orchestras and jazz ensembles, spoken from the side of the stage; the first three paragraphs open the concert and the last four close it.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "Good evening, and welcome. I acknowledge the Whadjuk Noongar people, the traditional custodians of this land, and pay my respects to their elders. A particular welcome to our principal, to members of the school council, and to the grandparents I can see who have driven a very long way. | Tonight you will hear three hundred and forty students in eleven ensembles, || and they are rather keen to start.", "note": "Settles the hall with the essentials and one warm, specific welcome. The final figure and the dry understatement tell the audience the music is close."}, {"text": "If I could offer one thing to listen for, it is the passing of a melody. In almost every piece tonight a tune begins in one section and is handed to another: from the clarinets to the violas, from the trumpets to the saxophones. Watch for the moment it changes hands, and for the players who have just let it go and are now listening, very carefully, to someone else. || That is what rehearsal is for.", "note": "Gives one listening idea instead of program notes, which deepens attention without lecturing. The final short line, after a held stop, quietly tells families what their children's term has been about."}, {"text": "Phones on silent, please, and photographs without flash. Exits are at the back and on either side. To begin, Concert Band 3, | with their conductor.", "note": "Dispatches the practicalities in plain sentences, without apology. The breath before the conductor gives the applause its cue."}, {"text": "Thank you. Before you go, a few people who will not take a bow. Our instrumental teachers, who have been hearing these pieces one bar at a time since February. The parent volunteers who ran the interval kiosk and will be stacking chairs at ten o'clock. The students on the lighting desk, who learned the cue sheet in an afternoon. And our families, who drive to rehearsals at seven in the morning, || in winter.", "note": "Thanks four groups, each with one concrete detail instead of an adjective. The held stop before 'in winter' lets the audience's recognition do the work."}, {"text": "What you heard tonight was not only a series of performances. It was three hundred and forty young people agreeing, for two hours, to listen to each other with complete attention. That is rarer than it sounds, | at any age, || and they did it beautifully.", "note": "Names what the evening meant in one idea families can carry home. The breath and the stop slow the delivery so the praise is heard as a considered judgement rather than routine."}, {"text": "For our Year 12 musicians, this was their last winter concert. Some of them joined the program as Year 7s who could barely hold their instruments; tonight several of them led their sections, and two of them conducted. We will miss them, | and we will be listening for them, || wherever they play next. Would the Year 12s please stand.", "note": "Marks a rite of passage briefly and concretely, with two facts that show growth rather than an adjective that claims it. Asking them to stand gives families a moment of recognition without a further speech."}, {"text": "Please join me in one last round of applause for every musician on this stage. | Safe home, and goodnight.", "note": "Ends on the students and a short farewell, with nothing administrative after it. The audience leaves on applause rather than on a reminder about next term."}]}}, {"id": "type-award-acceptance", "name": "Award acceptance", "occasion": "Receiving an honour from a professional body, institution or community, usually immediately after a citation has been read.", "minutes": [1, 4], "purpose": "Receive the honour with grace, share credit truthfully without false modesty, say one thing worth hearing, and sit down.", "parts": [{"part": "Thanks to the giver", "what": "Thanks the body making the award, briefly and specifically.", "prompt": "Who gave this, and what about their purpose or process can you honour?", "share": 15}, {"part": "Lineage", "what": "Places the honour among those who received it before, which is humility without self-deprecation.", "prompt": "Who has held this before, and what do you owe them?", "share": 15}, {"part": "The credit shared", "what": "Names the groups and conditions that made the work possible.", "prompt": "Without whom would this genuinely not have happened?", "share": 25}, {"part": "One idea", "what": "Offers a single thought that gives the moment meaning beyond the speaker.", "prompt": "What is the one thing you believe about this work that this room should hear?", "share": 35}, {"part": "Close", "what": "Ends with gratitude and a clean exit line.", "prompt": "What is your last sentence?", "share": 10}], "pitfalls": ["False modesty that protests so much it draws attention back to the speaker.", "An unedited list of names, which honours no one in particular and loses the room by the fifth.", "Using the platform for a grievance or a policy pitch the occasion did not invite.", "Running long because the moment has made time disappear; write it to two minutes and keep it there."], "model": {"title": "On Receiving an Award for Service to Music Education", "setting": "Acceptance of a state professional association's award for service to music education at its annual dinner, immediately after a citation read by the president.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "Thank you, President, and thank you to the association. I know how these awards are decided: by colleagues, after argument, | which is exactly why this one matters to me.", "note": "Thanks the giver specifically and compliments the process rather than the recipient. The dry final clause sets the register for the whole speech."}, {"text": "I also know some of the names on the list of previous recipients. Several of them taught the teachers who taught my students, and one or two of them, in my early years, | gently told me I was wrong. They were right. To be added to that list is an honour I did not expect.", "note": "Places the speaker within a lineage, the most graceful form of humility because it honours others rather than diminishing oneself. The self-deprecation is concrete and brief."}, {"text": "Nothing in that citation was done alone. It was done with instrumental teachers who say yes to an extra rehearsal they are not paid for; with a principal who backed a program when the numbers were against it; and with families who have trusted us with their children's evenings and weekends for years. And it was done, mostly, by students, who did the practice. I accept this on their behalf, || and I will tell them on Monday.", "note": "Shares credit among three groups and then the students, avoiding a list of individuals. The final line adds a specific, human promise that makes the deflection believable."}, {"text": "I have spent most of my working life moving between two rooms: the classroom in the afternoon and the bandstand at night. For a long time I thought of them as two jobs. I no longer do. What the bandstand teaches, that you are responsible for the time and for the people around you, is exactly what I try to teach in the classroom, | and what the classroom teaches, patience and the long view, || has made me a better player.", "note": "Offers a brief personal reflection that earns the idea to come, without autobiography. The parallel structure of the final sentence makes the two rooms mirror each other audibly."}, {"text": "If I may say one thing. Music in schools is still sometimes described as an enrichment, something added to the real work. Everyone in this room knows otherwise. A rehearsal is one of the few places in a school where a young person learns that their attention has consequences for other people, | in real time, with no second take. It is where they learn to listen to someone they did not choose, and to make something with others that none of them could make alone. *That* is not enrichment. || It is an education.", "note": "Offers a single idea framed as something the room already knows, which includes the audience rather than instructing it. The stressed 'That' and the short final sentence carry the point."}, {"text": "Thank you again, to the association and to the colleagues who put my name forward without telling me. I am very grateful, | and I will try to deserve it.", "note": "Closes in two plain sentences with no reprise. Ending on obligation rather than triumph is the classic note for an acceptance."}]}}, {"id": "type-award-citation", "name": "Presenting an award or citation", "occasion": "Conferring an award, life membership or other honour on someone, by reading a citation before the presentation.", "minutes": [2, 5], "purpose": "Make the room understand why this person, specifically, deserves this honour, so that the applause is for the person and not for the ritual.", "parts": [{"part": "The honour", "what": "States what the award is and what it recognises.", "prompt": "What does this honour stand for, and what is it not given for?", "share": 12}, {"part": "The portrait", "what": "Shows the recipient through checkable facts and one characteristic habit.", "prompt": "What did they actually do, and what habit shows who they are?", "share": 30}, {"part": "The scene", "what": "Gives one concrete moment that reveals character better than any adjective.", "prompt": "What is the one moment that, told plainly, shows this person entirely?", "share": 20}, {"part": "The significance", "what": "Names what the work has meant to other people.", "prompt": "Who is different because of this person, and how?", "share": 23}, {"part": "Conferral", "what": "Builds to the name and formally confers the honour, then hands over the stage.", "prompt": "What is the exact sentence of conferral, and where does the name fall in it?", "share": 15}], "pitfalls": ["A curriculum vitae read aloud: dates and positions without a single scene that shows the person.", "Superlatives in place of evidence; 'tireless' and 'passionate' tell the room nothing it believes.", "Making the citation about the presenter's friendship with the recipient rather than the recipient's work.", "Airing private material the recipient would not want made public, however affectionately meant.", "Losing the moment of conferral in shuffled papers or a swallowed name."], "model": {"title": "Life Membership Citation", "setting": "Conferring life membership of the schools' jazz festival association on a long-serving adjudicator and teacher at the association's annual general meeting; her name is withheld until the conferral.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "Life membership is the highest honour this association can give. It is not awarded for long service alone, though there is plenty of that in this room. It is awarded to someone without whom the association would be recognisably a different thing. | This year the committee was unanimous that such a person is here tonight.", "note": "Defines the honour by what it is not, which raises the bar and the room's attention together. The unanimous committee gives the citation institutional weight before anything about the person is said."}, {"text": "She began teaching in a country high school with a band of eleven students and one working saxophone. She came to the city, built two school jazz programs from nothing, and has adjudicated at this festival for twenty-two consecutive years. By our estimate she has written something over four thousand adjudication sheets. I have read a good number of them. | Every one begins with something the band did well, || and every one ends with a single thing to practise.", "note": "Builds the portrait from specific facts in ascending scale, then turns from quantity to quality. The closing sentence describes a habit, which reveals character more convincingly than praise."}, {"text": "One scene, if I may. Some years ago, at the end of a long Saturday, a junior band from a small school played a ballad very badly and very bravely. Their teacher was close to tears. She came down from the adjudicators' table, which we do not normally do, and spent ten minutes with the rhythm section showing them how to play softly. | That school has entered every year since.", "note": "One concrete scene does the work of a paragraph of tributes. The consequence, stated flatly at the end, turns anecdote into significance."}, {"text": "She is not, it should be said, a soft marker. Directors who have received her sheets will know the phrase 'the time was negotiable', and some of us have had it written about our own bands. But her severity has always been in the service of the students. She has never written a comment a fourteen-year-old could not act on the following week, | and she has never written one to be clever. || In twenty-two years, that is a remarkable discipline.", "note": "Adds a true and slightly sharp edge to the portrait, so the warmth is credible. The quoted phrase lets the room recognise her voice, and the final sentences turn severity into a virtue."}, {"text": "There are teachers across this state, some of them in this room, who learned how to run a rhythm section from her written comments, and former students, now professionals, who still quote them. That is what life membership is meant to recognise: | a contribution that has gone into other people, || and stayed there.", "note": "Moves from the person to her effect on others, which is the true ground of the honour. The definition of the award returns, now filled with evidence."}, {"text": "It is my privilege, on behalf of the committee and the members, to confer life membership of the association on || Ann. | Ann, would you please come up?", "note": "The held stop before the name gives the room its moment of recognition and the applause its cue. Inviting her up by name hands over the stage cleanly."}]}}, {"id": "type-valedictory", "name": "Valedictory or farewell to a colleague", "occasion": "A farewell at a staff function or assembly for a colleague who is retiring or leaving after long service.", "minutes": [4, 8], "purpose": "Honour a career truthfully and warmly, let colleagues recognise the person they worked with, and let the person leaving feel seen rather than eulogised.", "parts": [{"part": "Opening", "what": "Sets a warm, light register through an image everyone shares.", "prompt": "What is the first thing everyone in the room associates with this person?", "share": 12}, {"part": "The career in brief", "what": "Gives the shape of the career in a few accurate facts.", "prompt": "What three or four facts show the arc of their work?", "share": 18}, {"part": "The method", "what": "Shows how they worked, through a habit or principle colleagues will recognise.", "prompt": "What did they do, every week, that nobody else did quite the same way?", "share": 25}, {"part": "A moment of friction", "what": "Acknowledges, with good humour, a time they disagreed with you or the institution and were right.", "prompt": "When did they tell you something you did not want to hear?", "share": 15}, {"part": "The legacy", "what": "Names what will remain after they leave, in terms colleagues can verify.", "prompt": "What will still be true in the department in five years because of them?", "share": 15}, {"part": "The farewell", "what": "Speaks to the person directly and leads the room in a shared action.", "prompt": "What do you want to say to them, directly, as the last thing?", "share": 15}], "pitfalls": ["Writing a eulogy for a living person: solemnity and summary where warmth and specificity are wanted.", "In-jokes that delight six people and exclude sixty, including the family.", "Ignoring a difficult truth everyone knows, such as a hard final year or a reluctant departure; acknowledge it with tact or leave it entirely alone, never half-mention it.", "Drifting into the department's future needs or the replacement, which turns a farewell into a staffing announcement.", "Forgetting to speak to the person directly before the end."], "model": {"title": "Farewell to a Woodwind Teacher", "setting": "A farewell at the end-of-year staff function for an instrumental woodwind teacher retiring after thirty-one years, delivered with members of her family present.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "If you have walked past the woodwind room after four o'clock at any point in the last thirty-one years, you will have heard a reed being scraped, a clarinet student being told to breathe lower, and Ruth laughing. Those three sounds have been the background of this department for longer than some of our staff have been alive. || From next year, | we are going to have to get used to the quiet.", "note": "Opens with a sound image every colleague recognises, which makes the speech about her from the first sentence. The stop and the final line move gently from humour to feeling."}, {"text": "The facts first, because she would want them accurate. Ruth joined the instrumental music service in the mid-nineties, after ten years as a freelance clarinettist. She has taught here longer than anywhere else and, by her own count, more than six hundred students. She has prepared the woodwinds for every major concert we have given, played second clarinet in the staff band whenever we were short, and never once, | that I know of, | let a student leave with a split reed.", "note": "Gives the career in a handful of facts, with a nod to her own precision. The last fact becomes a characteristic habit, softened by the qualifying breaths, and draws a smile."}, {"text": "What the facts do not show is how she taught. Ruth believed a student should leave every lesson able to do one thing they could not do when they walked in. Not five things: one. She would find it in the first two minutes, spend the rest of the lesson on it, and the student always knew what it was. I have watched her do that with a nervous Year 7 and with a student preparing for a conservatorium audition, || and it was exactly the same lesson.", "note": "Moves from facts to method, which is where a teacher's character really lives. The observation that both lessons were the same is the speech's central compliment, delivered as observation rather than praise."}, {"text": "She was also, it should be said, entirely unafraid of me. In my first year in charge of the department I proposed a change that would have moved every woodwind sectional to lunchtime. Ruth came to my office, closed the door, and explained, | kindly and in some detail, | why I was wrong. I changed it. She was right, and I have been grateful for that conversation ever since.", "note": "Admits a moment of friction with good humour and generosity, which makes the warmth elsewhere credible. A senior colleague's self-deprecation honours the person leaving."}, {"text": "What remains when she goes is the method. At least four of our current instrumental teachers learned it from her, and you can hear it in their lessons. The woodwind section of our top concert band plays with a blend that adjudicators keep remarking on. That blend is not an accident. | It is thirty-one years of one thing at a time.", "note": "Names the legacy in terms colleagues can hear and verify for themselves. The closing phrase echoes her method and compresses the whole career into a line."}, {"text": "Ruth, on behalf of everyone in this room and the six hundred who are not: thank you. Enjoy the quiet. | We will try to keep the reeds in order. || Would you all please raise a glass to Ruth.", "note": "Speaks to her directly and calls back to the opening image, closing the circle. The toast gives the room a collective action, so the speech ends in shared feeling rather than on the speaker."}]}}, {"id": "type-eulogy", "name": "Eulogy", "occasion": "A tribute at a funeral or memorial service, usually at the family's request and alongside other speakers.", "minutes": [5, 9], "purpose": "Honour a life truthfully, give the mourners a portrait they recognise, speak to grief without managing it, and leave them with something of the person to carry.", "parts": [{"part": "Opening", "what": "Establishes who is speaking and in what relationship, and steadies the room.", "prompt": "In what capacity are you speaking, and what is the first true thing you can say?", "share": 10}, {"part": "The life", "what": "Gives the shape of the life in a few facts, chosen for meaning rather than completeness.", "prompt": "Which facts of the life would they themselves have chosen?", "share": 20}, {"part": "The person", "what": "Shows character through scenes, habits and, where possible, their own words.", "prompt": "What did they do or say that nobody else would have?", "share": 25}, {"part": "The true flaw", "what": "Admits, affectionately, something less than perfect, so the portrait is recognisable.", "prompt": "What would the family laugh at in recognition?", "share": 12}, {"part": "The loss", "what": "Names the grief honestly through one precise image, without hollow consolation.", "prompt": "What exactly is missing now?", "share": 13}, {"part": "What remains", "what": "Locates what continues in the people present.", "prompt": "What of them is still in this room?", "share": 13}, {"part": "Farewell", "what": "Says goodbye directly, in very few words.", "prompt": "What is the last thing you want to say to them?", "share": 7}], "pitfalls": ["Making the eulogy about the speaker's grief or relationship rather than the person who has died.", "Sanitising the person into a saint the family does not recognise; a true flaw lovingly told honours them more.", "Offering consolations of belief the family may not share; speak to what everyone present can hold.", "Losing composure at an unrehearsed passage; rehearse aloud until the hardest sentence can be spoken.", "Running long when other speakers follow; agree the time with the family and keep to it."], "model": {"title": "Eulogy for a Pianist", "setting": "A eulogy at the funeral of a jazz pianist and longtime bandmate, given at the family's request as the second of three speakers.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "My name is Dijon. I played bass with Peter for twenty-two years, in more rooms in this city than either of us could remember, and I am here because his family asked me to speak for the musicians. It is a privilege I would very much rather not have. || So let me try to do it properly.", "note": "Identifies the speaker's role and relationship at once, as mourners need. The plain admission of reluctance is truthful and steadies both speaker and room before the portrait begins."}, {"text": "Peter was born in Kalgoorlie, the youngest of four, and learned the piano in the back room of his grandmother's house, on an instrument that had, according to him, eleven working notes in the bottom octave. He studied in Perth, played on cruise ships for two years, which he called the best and worst education available, and came home to be, for thirty years, one of the pianists everyone in this town wanted on the gig. He taught hundreds of students. He was married to Helen for thirty-four years. | He was very proud of his daughters, || and slightly embarrassed by how proud.", "note": "Selects facts that carry character, in his own phrasing where possible. The last line moves from biography to affection without announcing the shift."}, {"text": "What was he like to play with? He listened more closely than anyone I have known. He would hear a note I was not sure of, | and on the next chorus he would build a chord underneath it so that it sounded as if I had meant it. He did that for everyone. Nervous singers, young players sitting in for the first time: Peter made them sound like the musicians they were about to become. || I don't think most of them ever knew.", "note": "Shows his character through a musical habit every musician present will recognise and every non-musician can follow. The short final line carries the quiet generosity of it."}, {"text": "He was not a saint. He was late, reliably, for everything except the downbeat. He held opinions about drummers that I will not repeat in a chapel. And he once stopped a tune in the middle of a wedding reception to tell the room that the piano was a crime, | which it was. Helen tells me he was exactly the same at home, and that she would not have changed any of it.", "note": "Includes affectionate, true flaws, which makes the portrait recognisable and gives the room permission to laugh. Anchoring the humour in his widow's own words keeps it safe for the family."}, {"text": "There is a particular silence on a bandstand when a tune ends and nobody has yet decided what to play next. For twenty-two years I could look across that silence, and Peter would already know. || I don't know what to do with that silence now. | I don't think I am meant to know yet.", "note": "Names the loss through one precise image from their shared life rather than general statements about grief. Refusing false consolation is heard by mourners as honesty."}, {"text": "But I do know this. Every musician here who learned to comp from him, every student who learned that you play *for* the soloist and not at them, is carrying a piece of how he played. You will hear it on bandstands in this city for a long time. And when you do, | that will be Peter.", "note": "Locates what remains in the people present, a consolation everyone can hold regardless of belief. The stress on 'for' carries his whole musical ethic in one word."}, {"text": "Thank you, Peter, for the chords under my wrong notes. || Goodnight, old friend.", "note": "Speaks to him directly in very few words, recalling the central image of the speech. Short sentences are easier to speak through emotion, and stronger for it."}]}}, {"id": "type-toast", "name": "Toast", "occasion": "A short raised-glass tribute at a dinner, celebration or anniversary.", "minutes": [1, 3], "purpose": "Lift the room, honour the subject in a way everyone recognises, turn once to sincerity, and bring everyone to their feet with a clear call.", "parts": [{"part": "Opening hook", "what": "Catches the room with something specific and light.", "prompt": "What one detail will make everyone smile in recognition?", "share": 20}, {"part": "The tribute", "what": "Says why this person, group or occasion deserves the glass.", "prompt": "What is the single thing you most want to honour?", "share": 40}, {"part": "The turn", "what": "Moves from wit to feeling in one or two plain sentences.", "prompt": "What is the sincere sentence underneath the humour?", "share": 20}, {"part": "The call", "what": "Asks the room to stand and names exactly what they drink to.", "prompt": "What are the exact words of the toast?", "share": 20}], "pitfalls": ["Running past three minutes; a toast that needs notes has become a speech.", "Humour at the subject's expense that works for the speaker and costs the person honoured.", "No turn to sincerity, so the toast entertains and leaves nothing behind.", "An unclear call, so half the room raises a glass while the other half is still listening."], "model": {"title": "To the Founders", "setting": "A toast at the dinner marking the thirtieth anniversary of the schools' jazz festival association, with several founding committee members present.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "Thirty years ago, a group of teachers met in a staffroom after school and decided, with what I can only call magnificent optimism, that this state needed a jazz festival for students. The minutes of that meeting survive. They record the decision, a budget of four hundred dollars, and a note that refreshments were not provided. || Tonight, I am pleased to report, | refreshments have been provided.", "note": "Opens with a specific detail the founders will recognise and the room will enjoy. The held stop gives the punchline its timing."}, {"text": "In those thirty years the festival has grown from eleven bands in a borrowed school hall to more than a hundred ensembles across three days in a concert venue, and the budget has grown rather faster, | as your treasurer can confirm with some feeling. What has not changed is the idea in that first meeting: that young people should play this music for each other, and that teachers would give up their weekends to make it happen. Several of the people who had that idea are here tonight. || Some of them are still giving up their weekends.", "note": "Delivers the tribute in one movement, growth then continuity, with a self-deprecating aside about his own office. The last line lands lightly because the room knows it is true."}, {"text": "I will not embarrass anyone by name, but I am told that for the first four festivals the entire sound system travelled in the boot of one founder's station wagon, that the program was photocopied in another founder's school office after hours, | and that the first adjudicators were paid in sandwiches. || I am also told the sandwiches were very good.", "note": "One accumulating sentence of specific, affectionate detail honours the founders' labour without naming anyone. The second punchline, held back by a stop, keeps the register light just before the turn."}, {"text": "Every student who has walked onto that stage, and there are tens of thousands of them now, owes something to a decision made over cold tea in a staffroom. Some of them are teachers themselves now, standing in front of their own bands on festival weekend. Somewhere tonight, in a practice room or a bedroom in this state, a student is working on a solo for next year's festival, without the faintest idea that it exists because a handful of teachers once decided it should. Most of them will never know your names. | I suspect you would prefer it that way. || But this room knows them, and this room is grateful.", "note": "The turn to sincerity, stated plainly. The final line honours the founders' modesty rather than embarrassing them with praise."}, {"text": "So would you please stand, and raise your glasses. || To the founders, | and to the next thirty years.", "note": "A clear instruction to stand, a held stop while the room rises, then a two-part toast with a breath between. It is short enough for everyone to say back together."}]}}, {"id": "type-panel-remarks", "name": "Panel remarks (four-minute position statement)", "occasion": "An opening statement on a conference or public panel, where each panellist has a fixed short time before moderated discussion.", "minutes": [3, 5], "purpose": "Stake out a clear, defensible position distinct from the other panellists, give the moderator something to work with, and set up the discussion you want to have.", "parts": [{"part": "Position", "what": "States the answer to the panel's question, with its condition, in the first thirty seconds.", "prompt": "What is your answer to the question, in one sentence?", "share": 15}, {"part": "Ground", "what": "Gives the strongest reason, preferably as a counterfactual the room can test.", "prompt": "What is the strongest reason, and what happens if you are ignored?", "share": 20}, {"part": "Distinction", "what": "Separates your view from the predictable answer on your own side.", "prompt": "How does your view differ from the comfortable version of your own position?", "share": 20}, {"part": "Concession", "what": "Grants what is true in the opposing view, specifically.", "prompt": "What does the other side get right?", "share": 20}, {"part": "Proposal and handover", "what": "Ends with a concrete proposal and a question the discussion can take up.", "prompt": "What should actually change, and what do you want the panel to argue about next?", "share": 25}], "pitfalls": ["Using the four minutes to survey the issue, so the moderator cannot tell what you think.", "Over-running into colleagues' time; a panellist who goes over loses the room's goodwill before discussion begins.", "Repeating the previous speaker because the prepared text cannot adapt; keep one paragraph you can cut and one sentence you can add.", "Taking an extreme position for effect and then retreating under the first hard question.", "Ending on a summary instead of a provocation the discussion can pick up."], "model": {"title": "Selection Is a Debt", "setting": "A four-minute opening statement on a conference panel asked whether selective specialist music programs in public schools are defensible, alongside a policy academic, a principal and a parent advocate.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "My answer is yes, | but only on a condition that most selective programs, including at times my own, do not fully meet. A public school that selects students for a specialist program is taking something from the public: a place, a resource, a teacher's time, that could have gone elsewhere. *Selection is a debt.* || The program is defensible to exactly the extent that it repays it.", "note": "States the position at once, with its condition, and includes his own program in the critique, which buys credibility with a sceptical panel. The stressed four-word thesis gives the moderator and the room a handle."}, {"text": "Why yes at all? Because the alternative is not equity. Without selective public programs, serious ensemble training does not disappear; it goes to the families who can buy it privately. For a good number of the students I have taught, the specialist program was the only route into a full ensemble that their families could ever have reached. Take away selection and those students do not get a fairer system. || They get no system.", "note": "Grounds the position in a counterfactual, which persuades more than an abstract defence. The closing antithesis is short enough to be quoted back during discussion."}, {"text": "But I want to distinguish this from the comfortable version of the argument, which says selective programs are good because excellence is good. That is true, and it is not enough. A program that selects the strongest players, gives them the best rehearsal slots and sends them to festivals, and does nothing else, | is running a private music school on the public payroll.", "note": "Separates his view from the predictable answer on his own side, so his position is distinct on the panel. The breath sets up a sharp final phrase."}, {"text": "The critics are right about several things. Auditions favour children who have already had lessons, and those children are not evenly distributed across postcodes. Programs do concentrate staffing. And it is fair to ask why a twelve-year-old's musical potential should be judged on a single afternoon. I do not think any of that is answered by abolishing selection. | I think it is answered by changing what selection obliges.", "note": "Concedes three specific criticisms, which shows he has heard the other side at its strongest. The last two sentences turn concession into a pivot to his proposal."}, {"text": "Let me give you a picture of what that looks like when it works. Each week, a group of senior students from a selective program takes a bus to a primary school with no music specialist and runs a lunchtime band of thirty children. The seniors arrange the parts themselves, and they are, frankly, better teachers by the end of the year than some graduates. Three of those primary children auditioned for the program the following year. Two were offered places. Neither had ever had a private lesson. | That is the debt being repaid, || and it costs very little.", "note": "Gives one concrete, generic example of repayment, so the proposal that follows is heard as practical rather than aspirational. The small numbers are deliberately modest, which makes them more believable than a sweeping claim."}, {"text": "So, concretely: a selective program should have to show, every year, what it gives back. Its senior students teaching in feeder primary schools. Its ensembles playing in communities that have none. Auditions that test aptitude rather than prior tuition. And places held for students who have never had a lesson. Those things are measurable, | and they should be reported. || I would be interested to hear whether my fellow panellists see that as a burden, | or as a licence.", "note": "Ends with a concrete, checkable proposal and a question that sets up the discussion he wants. 'Burden or licence' frames the debate on his terms and hands it to the moderator."}]}}, {"id": "type-parent-address", "name": "Address to a parent body on a contested change", "occasion": "A meeting of parents called, or used, to explain a decision or proposal that some of them oppose.", "minutes": [8, 15], "purpose": "Explain the change and the reasoning honestly, show that objections have genuinely been heard, separate what is decided from what remains open, and leave parents trusting the process even if they still disagree.", "parts": [{"part": "Opening and frame", "what": "Names the change and the purpose of the meeting, and draws the line between decided and open.", "prompt": "What exactly is changing, and is this meeting to inform, to consult, or both?", "share": 12}, {"part": "The concerns acknowledged", "what": "States the parents' concerns accurately, in their terms, before answering any of them.", "prompt": "What are parents actually worried about, put as they would put it?", "share": 15}, {"part": "The reasons", "what": "Explains why the change is being made, with evidence the school holds.", "prompt": "What problem does this solve, and how do you know it exists?", "share": 18}, {"part": "The hardest objection", "what": "Takes the most pointed concern, often about motive, head on, conceding what is true in it.", "prompt": "What is the accusation behind the questions, and what part of it is fair?", "share": 12}, {"part": "What it means in practice", "what": "Shows concretely what will happen to their children.", "prompt": "What will a student's week and year actually look like?", "share": 15}, {"part": "What is open", "what": "Specifies what parents can still shape, how, and by when.", "prompt": "What can parents still change, through what mechanism, and when will they hear back?", "share": 15}, {"part": "Close", "what": "Restates commitment with candour and opens the floor.", "prompt": "What do you want them to believe about you and the program when they leave?", "share": 13}], "pitfalls": ["Defending the decision before acknowledging the concern, so parents hear that they have not been listened to.", "Pretending to consult on something already decided, which destroys more trust than the decision itself.", "Arguing the program's interests in the abstract when every parent is thinking about one child.", "Answering the angriest parent at the expense of the room; take the concern seriously, but speak to everyone.", "Over-explaining when anxious, so the key commitment is buried under reassurance."], "model": {"title": "Ensemble Placement by Audition", "setting": "A meeting of the music parents' association called to discuss the program's decision to place students in ensembles by audition rather than by year level from next year, with around one hundred and twenty parents present.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "Good evening, and thank you for coming out on a weeknight. I know some of you are here because you are worried, some because you are cross, and a few because the email mentioned supper. All three are reasonable. || Let me say at the start what tonight is. From next year, students in the program will be placed in ensembles by audition rather than by year level. That decision has been made. | What has not been decided, and what I want your help with tonight, is how the audition works and how we support students through it.", "note": "Names the room's feelings with a light touch, then states the change and draws the line between decided and open within the first minute. Parents can only engage constructively once they know which conversation they are in."}, {"text": "I want to start with your concerns, because I have read every email and I think I can state them fairly. You are worried that younger students will sit in lower ensembles for years and lose heart. You are worried that friendship groups will be split. You are worried that one stressful afternoon will decide a whole year. And some of you are worried that this is about the program's results at festivals rather than about your children. | If I have missed something, I would like to hear it tonight.", "note": "States the concerns in the parents' own terms before any defence, including the most pointed one, about motive. Inviting additions signals that the summary is a starting point, not a dismissal."}, {"text": "Here is why we are doing it. At present, a Year 8 clarinettist playing at the level of a Year 11 sits in a Year 8 band and is bored. A Year 10 student who started late sits in the Year 10 band and is frightened. We have both of those students in the program now, and some like them have left. I have the withdrawal records for the last three years, and the reason most often given is not workload. | It is 'the band is the wrong level for me'.", "note": "Explains the reasoning through two students every parent can picture, and points to evidence the program holds. The quoted reason, delivered after a breath, makes the case in the students' own words."}, {"text": "And to the hardest concern, about motive. Yes, our ensembles will probably sound better. I will not pretend otherwise, and I do not think that is bad for the students in them. But if this were about festival results, the simplest thing would be to put the strongest players in the top band and leave everyone else where they are. That is not the proposal. *Every* ensemble will be placed by level, | so that every student is in a band where they are stretched and not swamped.", "note": "Takes the most pointed objection head on and concedes what is true in it, which is the only credible way to answer a question about motive. The stress on 'Every' carries the difference between this change and its cynical version."}, {"text": "What will this mean for your child? Every student will still be in an ensemble every year; no one is dropped. Placement will be reviewed each semester, not once a year, so a student who improves moves up within months, not years. Section leaders will be drawn from every year level. And students will still work together by year level in their classroom music lessons, so the friendships formed there do not depend on the ensemble. || Nothing about the three-year commitment changes.", "note": "Translates the policy into concrete guarantees that answer each concern in turn, without labelling them as answers. The last line closes a likely question before it is asked."}, {"text": "Now, what is open. We have not settled the audition format. I would like it to be a short, relaxed play-through with the student's own instrumental teacher present, not a panel, and I would like students to be able to submit a recording if they are unwell or anxious on the day. We have not settled how students are told their placement, and I would value your views on that, particularly from those of you whose children have found transitions hard. There is a short form on your chairs. | I will read every one, and a working group that includes two parents will report back to this association before the end of Term 1.", "note": "Makes the open questions genuinely open, with proposals parents can react to and a concrete mechanism for their input. Parent membership of the working group and a reporting date give the consultation substance."}, {"text": "I would rather you hear this from me than from a newsletter, and I would rather you disagree with me in this room than in the car park. I believe this change is right for your children. I may well be wrong about some of the details, | which is exactly why the details are still open. || Thank you. I will take questions for as long as you have them.", "note": "Closes with candour about his fallibility on the details, which strengthens rather than weakens his authority on the decision. An open-ended offer of questions signals confidence and respect."}]}}], "great": [{"id": "great-lincoln-gettysburg", "speaker": "Abraham Lincoln", "occasion": "Dedication of the Soldiers' National Cemetery, Gettysburg, Pennsylvania, 19 November 1863", "year": "1863", "context": "Four and a half months after the battle, the cemetery for the Union dead was dedicated with a two-hour oration by Edward Everett, the principal speaker. Lincoln had been invited to offer a few closing remarks, and spoke for roughly two minutes, in about 270 words.", "excerpt": "Having been asked to dedicate the ground, Lincoln declares that the dead have already consecrated it beyond anything words can add, and transfers the act of dedication from the ground to the living, who must finish the work the dead began.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Temporal arc (past, present, future)", "how": "The speech moves in three short panels: the founding of the nation four score and seven years earlier, the present war and this field, and the future the living must secure. The whole argument is carried by that movement in time, so the audience feels the occasion as one point on a long line."}, {"name": "Correctio (self-correction)", "how": "He sets out the formal purpose of the day, then corrects it: in a larger sense the living cannot dedicate or hallow this ground, because the soldiers already have. The ceremony is redefined in the act of being performed."}, {"name": "Tricolon with climax", "how": "The refusal is built as three near-synonymous verbs of consecration, each heavier than the last, and the closing sentence ends on a three-part phrase describing government by, of and for the people. Triads give both the middle and the end their cadence."}, {"name": "Antithesis", "how": "He contrasts what is said here, which the world will soon forget, with what was done here, which it never can. The modesty about words is itself a rhetorical claim about the weight of deeds."}, {"name": "Abstraction from particulars", "how": "He names no general, regiment, state or side, and does not mention slavery or the Confederacy by name. The omission lets the speech speak for every grave and for the nation as a proposition, rather than for one battle."}, {"name": "Transfer of obligation", "how": "The final, longest sentence turns honour for the dead into a task for the hearers. The audience leaves charged rather than consoled."}], "borrow": "At a ceremony or a valedictory he can do what Lincoln did: name the formal purpose, quietly correct it, and hand the obligation to the people in the room. Following a long principal address, the short speech that refuses to compete and instead reframes the occasion is often the one remembered."}, {"id": "great-pericles-funeral", "speaker": "Pericles, as reported by Thucydides", "occasion": "Public funeral for the Athenian dead of the first year of the Peloponnesian War, Athens", "year": "431 BC", "context": "Athenian custom required a chosen citizen to deliver an oration over the war dead each year; Pericles was chosen after the first campaigning season of the war with Sparta. The text survives in Book 2 of Thucydides' History, which is the historian's composition of what was fitting to the occasion, not a transcript.", "excerpt": "Instead of praising the fallen as individuals, Pericles praises the city and its way of life, so that the dead are honoured as people who judged Athens worth dying for, and the living are invited to judge the same.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Exordium on the difficulty of the task", "how": "He opens by doubting the custom itself: deeds are better honoured by deeds, and a speaker risks either disappointing those who knew the dead or straining the belief of those who did not. This disarms the audience and establishes his judgement before he praises anything."}, {"name": "Praeteritio", "how": "He passes briefly over the ancestors and the military campaigns, saying his hearers know them already, and moves to the institutions and character of the city. Declining the expected material frees the speech for its real subject."}, {"name": "Encomium of the polity", "how": "The core is a portrait of Athenian government, law, openness, leisure and the habit of deliberating before acting. The dead are praised by being shown to be the expression of that order."}, {"name": "Synkrisis (implicit comparison)", "how": "Athenian openness and self-directed courage are set against a disciplined, closed rival without Sparta needing to be dwelt on. The contrast sharpens the portrait and flatters the audience's identity."}, {"name": "Consolatio addressed by audience segment", "how": "Near the end he turns to parents, sons and brothers, and widows in turn, giving each a distinct and brief word. The consolation is short and unsentimental, in keeping with the opening claim that words should not outrun deeds."}], "borrow": "For a valedictory, an anniversary or a memorial within a school community he can praise the institution and its practices as the thing the honoured person embodied, which avoids the inflation of personal eulogy. Opening by acknowledging the limits of what any speech can do earns the right to say something substantial."}, {"id": "great-churchill-june-1940", "speaker": "Winston Churchill", "occasion": "Statement to the House of Commons on the war situation after the Dunkirk evacuation, London, 4 June 1940", "year": "1940", "context": "Churchill had been Prime Minister for less than a month. The evacuation of the British Expeditionary Force and allied troops from Dunkirk had just ended, with more than 330,000 men brought off but almost all their equipment lost, and invasion of Britain was a real prospect.", "excerpt": "He gives Parliament an unsparing account of a military disaster, refuses to let the rescue be mistaken for a victory, and only then builds to a resolve to fight on, so that the defiance carries the authority of the candour that preceded it.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Narratio of hard facts", "how": "Most of the speech is a sober, detailed military report: the collapse in Belgium and France, the losses of men and matériel, the work of the navy and air force. The peroration is persuasive because the House has first been treated as adults."}, {"name": "Correctio against false comfort", "how": "At the moment of relief he warns that wars are not won by evacuations and that the episode must not be read as a victory. Puncturing premature celebration is what gives his later confidence its credibility."}, {"name": "Anaphora in the peroration", "how": "The close repeats a single short clause of resolve across a sequence of places, from the seas to the beaches, fields, streets and hills. The repetition is rhythmic and cumulative, and the vocabulary is almost entirely plain, short, old English words."}, {"name": "Climax by widening scope", "how": "The peroration moves outward from the island to the empire and finally to the New World coming to the rescue. The last movement is aimed past the chamber, at the United States, without naming it as a request."}, {"name": "Ethos of shared ownership", "how": "He speaks of what the nation and House will do, not what he will do, and folds himself into the collective. The resolve is presented as the country's, which Parliament is invited to ratify."}], "borrow": "In a report to a council or board on a setback, he can give the full, unvarnished account first, explicitly refuse the comforting reading, and only then state the resolve. The order matters: candour first buys the authority for confidence second."}, {"id": "great-keating-redfern", "speaker": "Paul Keating", "occasion": "Launch of the Australian celebration of the International Year for the World's Indigenous People, Redfern Park, Sydney, 10 December 1992", "year": "1992", "context": "The High Court's Mabo decision of June 1992 had overturned the doctrine that Australia was land belonging to no one at settlement, and the Commonwealth was preparing its legislative response. Keating, Prime Minister, spoke to a largely Aboriginal audience in Redfern; the speech was drafted with his speechwriter Don Watson.", "excerpt": "Keating has non-Indigenous Australia accept that it was the agent of dispossession, naming the acts plainly in the first person plural, and frames that acknowledgement not as guilt but as the precondition for a test the nation can pass.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Confessio in the active voice", "how": "He lists the wrongs as things we did: taking the land, destroying ways of life, bringing disease and alcohol, committing killings, removing children, practising discrimination. The grammar refuses the passive constructions that usually let a speaker avoid the agent."}, {"name": "Anaphora", "how": "The acknowledgement is a run of parallel clauses with the same opening pattern. The repetition gives an official, almost liturgical weight to what had rarely been said by a head of government."}, {"name": "Invitation to imaginative reversal", "how": "He asks the non-Indigenous listener to imagine those things having been done to them. This is a direct appeal to empathy that does the work of argument without any moralising tone."}, {"name": "Framing as a test", "how": "The issue is placed as a test of the nation's maturity and decency, a measure of what kind of country Australia is. This lets him address the whole nation, not only the audience in the park."}, {"name": "Turn to opportunity", "how": "After the acknowledgement he moves to Mabo, to practical reconciliation and to what can now be built. The speech ends forward-facing, so the confession reads as a beginning rather than a verdict."}], "borrow": "In an acknowledgement at a ceremony, or a submission about past inequity in access to a program, he can name what the institution itself did, in the active voice, and then frame the remedy as a test the institution can choose to pass. Owning agency plainly is more disarming to a sceptical room than any amount of qualification."}, {"id": "great-keating-unknown-soldier", "speaker": "Paul Keating", "occasion": "Funeral service for the Unknown Australian Soldier, Australian War Memorial, Canberra, 11 November 1993", "year": "1993", "context": "On the 75th anniversary of the Armistice, the remains of an unidentified Australian soldier of the First World War, exhumed from Adelaide Cemetery near Villers-Bretonneux in France, were interred in the Hall of Memory. Keating's eulogy, written with Don Watson, is a few minutes long.", "excerpt": "Keating builds the eulogy on everything that cannot be known about the man, and converts that ignorance into the reason he can stand for every Australian who served, and into a redefinition of the Anzac legend as something ordinary and democratic.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Litany of the unknown", "how": "The opening is a list of what we do not know: his name, his rank, his age, where he came from, what he did, whether he was loved. Each absence is stated flatly, and together they make the anonymity itself the subject."}, {"name": "Synecdoche", "how": "Because he is no one in particular, he becomes all of them: the one stands for the sixty thousand Australians who died in that war and for all who served since. The logic of the speech depends on the part standing for the whole."}, {"name": "Correctio of the myth", "how": "He corrects the heroic legend: the soldiers were not supermen or born warriors but ordinary people, and the legend's real meaning lies in courage, ingenuity and mateship shown by ordinary people. The ceremony is used to say what the tradition should mean."}, {"name": "Restraint of register", "how": "The sentences are short, concrete and largely unadorned, with no martial grandeur. The plainness suits a grave for a man without a name and makes the few heightened sentences carry."}, {"name": "Closing affirmation", "how": "It ends by stating simply that he is one of us. The final move returns from abstraction to belonging."}], "borrow": "For a eulogy where he knew the person only in part, or a memorial for an anonymous or collective subject, he can build on what is not known and let that make the subject representative. At a ceremony steeped in tradition, the occasion can be used to say quietly what the tradition ought to mean."}, {"id": "great-rfk-indianapolis", "speaker": "Robert F. Kennedy", "occasion": "Campaign rally, Indianapolis, Indiana, on the evening Martin Luther King Jr was assassinated, 4 April 1968", "year": "1968", "context": "Kennedy, campaigning for the Democratic presidential nomination, learned on arrival that King had been shot dead in Memphis. Against the advice of police, he went to a largely Black neighbourhood rally and, standing on a flatbed truck, broke the news to a crowd most of whom had not heard it, speaking for a few minutes from brief notes.", "excerpt": "He tells the crowd the worst news at once, names the choice it faces between bitterness and understanding, and earns the right to ask for the second by speaking, for the only time in public, of his own brother's murder.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Immediate disclosure", "how": "He delivers the news in the first sentences, without preamble. The audible shock of the crowd is allowed to happen, and he speaks into it rather than around it."}, {"name": "Dilemma posed as a choice", "how": "He sets out the two paths open to the listeners, hatred and revenge or an effort to understand and to replace violence with compassion. Putting the choice to them treats the crowd as moral agents, not a threat to be managed."}, {"name": "Ethos from shared grief", "how": "He says that he too had a member of his family killed, by a white man. The disclosure is brief and unelaborated; its power lies in being something he never otherwise said in public."}, {"name": "Citation of authority", "how": "He recites from memory a passage from Aeschylus, in Edith Hamilton's translation, about wisdom that comes through suffering. The unexpected classical voice lifts the moment out of politics into something older."}, {"name": "Brevity and close in a shared task", "how": "He ends by asking the crowd to dedicate themselves to an ancient aim of taming savagery and making life gentle, and to pray for King's family and the country. The speech stops as soon as the request is made."}], "borrow": "When he must announce bad news to a community, whether a death, a closure or a failed bid, he can say it first and plainly, then name the choice of response, and only then offer a word of his own experience. The authority comes from the brevity and the refusal to manage the room."}, {"id": "great-king-mountaintop", "speaker": "Martin Luther King Jr", "occasion": "Address at Mason Temple, Memphis, Tennessee, in support of the striking sanitation workers, 3 April 1968", "year": "1968", "context": "King had come to Memphis to support Black sanitation workers striking for recognition and safe conditions after two workers were crushed in a truck. He had not planned to speak that stormy night but was called to the temple; he was assassinated the next evening.", "excerpt": "King joins a practical program for the strike to a sweeping view of history and his own mortality, so that a local labour dispute becomes a moment in a long journey whose end he has seen but may not reach.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Imagined survey of history", "how": "He opens by imagining being offered a vantage on any age of human history, from Egypt to Greece, the Reformation and the Emancipation, and choosing to live in the second half of the twentieth century. The device places the Memphis struggle in the long story and makes the present feel chosen."}, {"name": "Practical program", "how": "In the middle he gives concrete instructions: unity, economic withdrawal from named companies, moving deposits to Black-owned banks. The visionary frame is anchored by something the audience can do on Monday."}, {"name": "Parable reinterpreted", "how": "He retells the Good Samaritan and reverses the question that passers-by asked themselves: not what will happen to me if I stop, but what will happen to him if I do not. The scriptural story becomes an argument for supporting the strikers."}, {"name": "Personal narrative", "how": "He recounts being stabbed in New York years earlier and a letter from a schoolgirl glad he had not sneezed, because the blade was close to his aorta. The anecdote of near death prepares the close."}, {"name": "Peroration on the Moses figure", "how": "He ends by likening himself to Moses shown the promised land from the mountain, saying he may not get there with them but that they will. The audience hears both triumph and farewell, delivered with mounting cadence."}], "borrow": "In a keynote to music educators he can place a local, practical demand inside a long historical view, then give the room something concrete to do, then close on what the work is for beyond himself. Reversing the question a familiar story asks is a strong way to reframe a duty."}, {"id": "great-mandela-rivonia", "speaker": "Nelson Mandela", "occasion": "Statement from the dock at the opening of the defence case, Rivonia Trial, Supreme Court, Pretoria, 20 April 1964", "year": "1964", "context": "Mandela and his co-accused were charged with sabotage and conspiracy to overthrow the state, offences that carried the death penalty. Rather than give evidence and face cross-examination, he made an unsworn statement from the dock, reading for about three hours; he was sentenced to life imprisonment in June 1964.", "excerpt": "Mandela admits the core facts, that he helped found Umkhonto we Sizwe and planned sabotage, and turns the admission into a reasoned justification, so that the court becomes the setting for a statement of principle addressed to the world.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Concessio and confession and avoidance", "how": "He freely admits the acts the state can prove, then explains why they were undertaken and denies what is false, such as being directed by communists or foreign powers. Conceding the provable strengthens everything he denies."}, {"name": "Narratio from personal history", "how": "He sets his choices in his biography and in the history of the African National Congress, showing decades of lawful protest met with repression. Violence is presented as a last resort, reached deliberately and with limits against loss of life."}, {"name": "Distinctio", "how": "He carefully distinguishes the ANC from the Communist Party, sabotage from terrorism, and his admiration for parliamentary institutions from the state that excluded him. Each distinction narrows the charge to what he can defend."}, {"name": "Appeal to the grievances as evidence", "how": "He describes in concrete terms poverty, pass laws, broken families and lack of education. The material shifts the question from what he did to what made it necessary."}, {"name": "Peroration at maximum stakes", "how": "He closes on the ideal of a democratic and free society for which he has lived and, if necessary, is prepared to die. Spoken by a man facing a possible death sentence, the conditional carries enormous weight; his counsel had urged him to temper the ending."}], "borrow": "Before a board or an inquiry where a decision of his is under challenge, he can concede the facts outright, explain the reasoning in historical order, draw the exact distinctions that matter, and close on the principle. Admitting what is provable is often the strongest defensive position."}, {"id": "great-elizabeth-tilbury", "speaker": "Elizabeth I", "occasion": "Address to the land forces assembled at Tilbury, Essex, against the threatened Spanish invasion, August 1588", "year": "1588", "context": "The Spanish Armada was in the Channel and an invasion force under the Duke of Parma was expected from the Netherlands; Elizabeth reviewed the army camped at Tilbury under the Earl of Leicester. The best-known text derives from a later letter by Leonel Sharp, a chaplain, printed in the seventeenth century, so the exact wording is uncertain.", "excerpt": "The queen turns the presumed weakness of a woman ruler into a pledge: she has come among her soldiers in person, in defiance of advice about her safety, and stakes her own life and honour alongside theirs.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Ethos by presence", "how": "She begins by noting that she has been warned against trusting herself to armed multitudes for fear of treachery, and has come anyway. The act of being there becomes the first argument."}, {"name": "Concessio turned to strength", "how": "She concedes that she has the body of a weak and feeble woman and then claims the heart and stomach of a king, and a king of England. The concession is the setup for the claim, and the gendered objection is answered before anyone raises it."}, {"name": "Personal stake", "how": "She resolves to live or die among them and to lay down her honour and blood in the field. The ruler accepts the same risk she asks of the ranks."}, {"name": "Promise of reward", "how": "She tells them they have deserved rewards and crowns and that they will be paid. The heroic register is grounded in a practical assurance."}, {"name": "Delegation of authority", "how": "She names her lieutenant general and asks obedience to him as to herself. The speech ends by settling the chain of command."}], "borrow": "When he takes on a hard task or a contested room, he can name the objection others are thinking, concede it, and turn it into the basis of his commitment. Appearing in person where he was expected to delegate, and saying so, is itself persuasive."}, {"id": "great-cicero-catiline", "speaker": "Marcus Tullius Cicero", "occasion": "First Oration against Catiline, delivered to the Senate in the Temple of Jupiter Stator, Rome, 8 November 63 BC", "year": "63 BC", "context": "Cicero, consul, had evidence that Lucius Sergius Catilina was organising a conspiracy, including an attempt on Cicero's life the previous morning. Catiline himself attended the Senate meeting; Cicero's published version was revised for circulation some years later.", "excerpt": "Rather than prove the conspiracy to the Senate, Cicero addresses Catiline directly, shows that every detail of his plot is known, and so makes his continued presence in Rome impossible, driving him to leave the city that night.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Apostrophe and interrogatio", "how": "He opens with a volley of rhetorical questions aimed straight at Catiline, asking how long his effrontery will go on and whether he thinks his plans are hidden. The Senate is made an audience to a confrontation, not a jury hearing argument."}, {"name": "Exclamatio", "how": "He breaks off to lament the times and their moral condition, that the Senate knows and the consul sees, yet the man lives. The exclamation turns individual outrage into a collective indictment of complacency."}, {"name": "Demonstration of knowledge", "how": "He recounts the meeting at the house of Laeca, the night, the participants, the allocation of tasks. Each detail tells Catiline that concealment has failed, which is more destabilising than a formal proof."}, {"name": "Prosopopoeia", "how": "He gives the fatherland a voice, first addressing Catiline and later addressing Cicero himself over why he does not act more severely. The personification lets him put arguments in a mouth more authoritative than his own and answer the charge of weakness."}, {"name": "Dilemma", "how": "He offers Catiline the choice to go into exile and take his followers, presenting departure as both a mercy and a confession. Either outcome serves Cicero's purpose."}], "borrow": "In a meeting where someone is acting in bad faith, he can, sparingly, set out precisely what is already known rather than argue a case, and offer a clean exit. Giving the institution itself a voice, such as what the program or the students would ask, lets him make the strongest point without appearing personal."}, {"id": "great-feynman-cargo-cult", "speaker": "Richard Feynman", "occasion": "Commencement address, California Institute of Technology, Pasadena", "year": "1974", "context": "Feynman, a Nobel laureate in physics on the Caltech faculty, was asked to give the address to graduating students. It was later published as the closing chapter of his memoir collection Surely You're Joking, Mr. Feynman!", "excerpt": "Using a comic image of people who imitate the outward forms of a practice without its substance, Feynman isolates the one quality that makes science science: an active, uncomfortable integrity about everything that might show you wrong, starting with yourself.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Controlling analogy", "how": "He describes South Sea islanders after the war who built imitation runways and control huts in the hope that planes would land again. Pseudoscience is defined by that image: perfect form, missing substance."}, {"name": "Exempla from the laboratory", "how": "He tells stories of a psychologist who traced how rats were really navigating a maze, and of how later measurements of the electron's charge crept only gradually away from Millikan's value. Each case shows a specific failure or success of integrity, not an abstraction."}, {"name": "Distinctio", "how": "He distinguishes not lying from scientific integrity, which requires reporting everything that might undermine your result, including what you did not test. The distinction lifts the standard above ordinary honesty."}, {"name": "Maxim placed at the centre", "how": "He states as a first principle that one must not fool oneself, and that oneself is the easiest person to fool. Placing the maxim mid-speech, after the stories, lets it land as a conclusion rather than a sermon."}, {"name": "Application to the audience's future", "how": "He turns to what graduates will face: advertising, funding pressures, the temptation to omit inconvenient results. The close is a hope that they find a place where they are free to keep this kind of integrity."}], "borrow": "Addressing an assessment committee or a conference on evidence, he can open with one vivid image of form without substance, give two concrete cases, then state the principle as the conclusion. Distinguishing the ordinary standard from the higher one his field demands is a strong way to raise a room's expectations without lecturing it."}, {"id": "great-gehrig-farewell", "speaker": "Lou Gehrig", "occasion": "Lou Gehrig Appreciation Day, between games of a doubleheader, Yankee Stadium, New York, 4 July 1939", "year": "1939", "context": "Gehrig, the New York Yankees first baseman who had played 2,130 consecutive games, had been diagnosed weeks earlier with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis and had retired. Before a large crowd and his past and present teammates, he spoke briefly and without notes.", "excerpt": "Facing a fatal illness, Gehrig declares himself fortunate and spends almost the whole speech counting the people who made him so, so that a farewell becomes an act of gratitude rather than lament.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Paradox in the exordium", "how": "He opens by saying people have read about his bad break and then claims to be the luckiest man alive. The gap between the circumstances and the claim seizes the audience at once."}, {"name": "Enumeratio of gratitude", "how": "He works through owners, managers, teammates, opponents, groundsmen, his parents, his mother-in-law and his wife. The catalogue is the argument: the evidence for his good fortune is the people in front of him."}, {"name": "Parallel conditionals", "how": "Several sentences share a shape: when such and such a person or group has done this for you, that is something. The repeated structure gives a plain speaker a rhythm and lets each item build on the last."}, {"name": "Litotes and understatement", "how": "His illness is mentioned only as a bad break, and its prognosis not at all. The restraint makes the gratitude more moving, because the audience supplies what he leaves unsaid."}, {"name": "Ring composition", "how": "He ends by returning to the opening idea, acknowledging that he may have had a tough break but has an awful lot to live for. The close completes the frame without adding new material."}], "borrow": "For an award acceptance or a valedictory he can open with one honest paradox and then let a specific catalogue of people carry the speech. Saying little about himself and much about others, in a repeated simple structure, suits a plain delivery and avoids self-regard."}, {"id": "great-heaney-nobel", "speaker": "Seamus Heaney", "occasion": "Nobel Lecture, Crediting Poetry, Swedish Academy, Stockholm, 7 December 1995", "year": "1995", "context": "Heaney received the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1995. His lecture was delivered while the Northern Ireland peace process was fragile, the 1994 ceasefires recent, and he had spent his life as a poet from a Catholic farming background in County Derry writing through the Troubles.", "excerpt": "Heaney moves from childhood memories of the radio in a rural farmhouse to the violence of Northern Ireland, and argues that poetry earns its credit by being both true to that violence and capable of a balancing affirmation that does not betray it.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Autobiographical narratio", "how": "He begins with the family kitchen, the wireless, the names of foreign stations on the dial and the sense of a wider world reaching an enclosed childhood. The personal story becomes the frame for a theory of poetry."}, {"name": "Paronomasia on the title", "how": "The lecture plays on credit as belief, as acknowledgement and as something owed. The argument is gathered under that double sense: crediting poetry means both trusting it and giving it its due."}, {"name": "Testimony from the Troubles", "how": "He recounts the 1976 massacre of workmen at Kingsmill, in which a Protestant worker squeezed the hand of the one Catholic in the van as a sign of solidarity, moments before gunmen let the Catholic go and shot the rest. The anecdote is told with restraint and refuses to resolve into easy hope."}, {"name": "Exemplum from legend", "how": "He tells the story of St Kevin, who stayed motionless with his hand outstretched until a blackbird that had nested in it had hatched its young. The legend models a patient endurance he connects to the poet's task."}, {"name": "Literary authority", "how": "He reads closely from Yeats's poems written during the Irish Civil War, showing how a poet can register violence and still find an image of something living. The argument is made by demonstration through another poet's work."}], "borrow": "For a conference keynote on why music matters, he can begin with a concrete personal memory, choose a word with a double meaning as the spine, and test his claim against the hardest case he knows rather than an easy one. Arguing through close attention to one piece of art is more persuasive to scholars than generalisation."}, {"id": "great-obama-2004-keynote", "speaker": "Barack Obama", "occasion": "Keynote address, Democratic National Convention, Fleet Center, Boston, 27 July 2004", "year": "2004", "context": "Obama was an Illinois state senator and the Democratic candidate for the United States Senate, little known nationally. The keynote supported the nomination of John Kerry and made Obama a national figure.", "excerpt": "Obama offers his own improbable family history as proof of a national story, then uses it to argue against the habit of dividing the country into opposed camps, and ends by defining hope as a demanding stance rather than wishful thinking.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Biography as argument", "how": "He recounts his father's origins in Kenya and his mother's family in Kansas, his grandfather's war service and the opportunities each generation found. His life is presented as evidence for a claim about the country, which is ethos and logos at once."}, {"name": "Enumeratio of ordinary citizens", "how": "He describes people he met in the campaign: workers losing jobs to offshoring, a father unable to afford medicine, a young man enlisting for Iraq. The particulars make policy concrete and set up the case for the nominee."}, {"name": "Anaphora and antithesis", "how": "The central passage repeats a pattern rejecting divisions: there is not a liberal or a conservative country but one country, not red states and blue states but one nation. The repetition builds toward applause while carrying the argument."}, {"name": "Distinctio", "how": "He separates hope from blind optimism or wilful ignorance, defining it as the conviction that drove slaves, immigrants and soldiers. Defining the key term protects it from the obvious objection."}, {"name": "Self-deprecating peroration", "how": "He ends with a skinny kid with a funny name who believes he has a place in the American story. The humour lightens the close and returns the abstract theme to his own figure."}], "borrow": "Introducing himself to a new body, such as a university committee or a funding panel, he can use one concise piece of his own history as evidence for the claim he wants to make. Defining his central word against its weaker cousin is an efficient way to anticipate the room's scepticism."}, {"id": "great-gillard-misogyny", "speaker": "Julia Gillard", "occasion": "Reply to the Leader of the Opposition's motion to remove the Speaker, House of Representatives, Canberra, 9 October 2012", "year": "2012", "context": "Tony Abbott moved to remove Speaker Peter Slipper over crude and sexist text messages that had become public, and argued that the government's defence of Slipper showed it tolerated sexism. Gillard, Prime Minister, replied for about fifteen minutes, largely from notes; the motion failed and Slipper resigned that evening.", "excerpt": "Gillard turns a motion accusing her government of tolerating sexism into a detailed prosecution of the accuser, using his own documented words and conduct as the evidence.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Retorsio (turning the argument back)", "how": "The motion's premise, that sexism disqualifies someone from office, is accepted and applied to the mover. She does not defend the Speaker's messages; she changes the defendant."}, {"name": "Enumeratio of evidence", "how": "She lists specific remarks Abbott had made over years, about women in power, abortion, women and housework, and his conduct in the chamber, with dates and contexts. The particularity makes the charge hard to dismiss as rhetoric."}, {"name": "Anaphora", "how": "She repeats that she will not be lectured on sexism and misogyny by this man. The refrain gives the extemporised speech its structure and its quotable unit."}, {"name": "Direct address and personal stake", "how": "She speaks of being personally offended and refers to her father, whom Abbott's side had invoked. The personal register is controlled and bounded, used to give the evidence moral force."}, {"name": "Kairos", "how": "The speech seized a narrow procedural moment and reached an audience far beyond the chamber, particularly overseas. Domestically, much commentary at the time focused on the defence of Slipper, which shows the gap between the room and the wider audience."}], "borrow": "When a motion or complaint in a meeting rests on a principle, he can accept the principle and apply it with documented particulars, rather than defending the narrow point. A single refrain gives an extemporised reply a spine, and he should judge in advance which audience, the room or the wider community, the reply is really for."}, {"id": "great-burke-bristol", "speaker": "Edmund Burke", "occasion": "Speech to the electors of Bristol at the conclusion of the poll, Bristol, 3 November 1774", "year": "1774", "context": "Burke had just been elected one of Bristol's two members of Parliament. His fellow member had promised to follow the instructions of his constituents, and Burke used his thanks to the electors to set out a different view of a representative's duty.", "excerpt": "Burke tells the people who have just elected him that he owes them not obedience but his judgement, and that Parliament is a deliberative assembly of one nation, not a meeting of delegates from rival interests.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Captatio benevolentiae", "how": "He opens with gratitude and warm acknowledgement of the electors and of his colleague. The goodwill is secured before he says something the audience may not wish to hear."}, {"name": "Concessio", "how": "He grants that a representative should live in close communication with constituents, give great weight to their wishes and attend to their business. The concession makes the disagreement narrow and precise."}, {"name": "Distinctio", "how": "He distinguishes opinion, which a representative should weigh, from authoritative instructions, which would bind him against his judgement and conscience. The line between them is the whole argument."}, {"name": "Definition by contrast", "how": "He defines Parliament by what it is not: not a congress of ambassadors from hostile interests each advocating for its own, but a deliberative assembly of one nation with one interest. The negative definition makes the positive clearer."}, {"name": "Ethos through candour at the moment of victory", "how": "He makes the argument at the moment he has least need to persuade and most to lose by offending. Saying it at once establishes the terms of the relationship from the start."}], "borrow": "As chair of a council or a member of a committee nominated by a constituency, he can say early, with warmth, what he owes the people who put him there and what he does not. Conceding their claim on his attention while reserving his judgement is a defensible position he can state once and then rely on."}], "openings": [{"id": "open-1", "device": "the scene", "occasion": "conference", "text": "At a quarter past eight on a Tuesday, | a Year 7 trombonist is holding a note she cannot yet hear in tune. || Everything I want to say this morning concerns the next ninety seconds of her life.", "note": "A single concrete moment makes the abstract policy argument that follows feel owed to a real person."}, {"id": "open-2", "device": "the paradox", "occasion": "conference", "text": "The most rigorous thing we ask of music students | is the thing we are least able to *measure*. || I want to keep that a problem a little longer than is comfortable.", "note": "A contradiction stated plainly creates tension the audience wants resolved."}, {"id": "open-3", "device": "the concession", "occasion": "council report", "text": "Let me begin with what has not gone well. || Enrolments held, but retention into Year 10 did *not*, | and the council is entitled to hear that first.", "note": "Leading with bad news buys credibility for every good figure that follows."}, {"id": "open-4", "device": "the question", "occasion": "panel", "text": "What would we lose | if music disappeared from the senior certificate tomorrow? || Less than we fear in the first year, I suspect, | and far more than we could recover in the tenth.", "note": "A question answered unexpectedly signals that the speaker has already thought past the obvious reply."}, {"id": "open-5", "device": "the stake", "occasion": "board submission", "text": "The decision before the board is small on paper | and large in consequence. || It concerns one budget line | and several hundred students who will never know it was made.", "note": "Naming the gap between scale and consequence makes a routine item feel weighty."}, {"id": "open-6", "device": "the plain ask", "occasion": "board submission", "text": "I will be brief, and I will ask for one thing. || The association needs the board to approve the venue contract *tonight*.", "note": "Stating the request first respects a board's time and frames everything after as support."}, {"id": "open-7", "device": "the welcome", "occasion": "festival opening", "text": "Welcome. || For the next three days this building belongs to young musicians, | and the rest of us are guests in it.", "note": "A gracious transfer of ownership sets the tone of the whole event in one sentence."}, {"id": "open-8", "device": "the image", "occasion": "concert", "text": "Before the first note, | look for a moment at the empty chairs on stage. || Each one has been fought for, | by a student, a parent, or a teacher who would not let go.", "note": "Directing the audience's eyes turns a familiar sight into a symbol."}, {"id": "open-9", "device": "self-deprecation", "occasion": "award", "text": "I have spent twenty-six years at the back of bandstands, | which is the proper place for a bass player. || Standing at the front is a new skill, | and I am still learning the changes.", "note": "Modesty grounded in a true detail disarms the room without false humility."}, {"id": "open-10", "device": "the portrait", "occasion": "valedictory", "text": "When she arrived, the department had one room, | a piano with opinions, | and a timetable written in pencil. || She leaves it with a building, a reputation, and a timetable written, regrettably, in pencil.", "note": "A before-and-after sketch with a comic repetition honours achievement without solemnity."}, {"id": "open-11", "device": "the particular detail", "occasion": "eulogy", "text": "He tuned every instrument in the room before anyone arrived, | and he never once mentioned it. || That is how most of us knew him: | by the things that were already done.", "note": "One exact habit conveys character more truthfully than any list of virtues."}, {"id": "open-12", "device": "the wry preamble", "occasion": "toast", "text": "I have been asked to keep this short, | which, as anyone who sits on a committee with me knows, | is an act of considerable faith.", "note": "Gentle self-mockery relaxes a social room and promises brevity."}, {"id": "open-13", "device": "the definition", "occasion": "conference", "text": "By *excellence* I do not mean the best performance in the room. || I mean the distance a student travels between February and November, | and whether anyone noticed.", "note": "Redefining a contested term at the outset controls the terms of the argument."}, {"id": "open-14", "device": "the turn", "occasion": "panel", "text": "We have been asked whether specialist programs are elitist. || I would like to turn the question around: | when did *access* to expertise come to be called elite?", "note": "Reframing the prompt shifts the burden of proof without refusing the question."}, {"id": "open-15", "device": "the shared ground", "occasion": "parent body", "text": "Every person in this room wants the same thing for these children. || We may disagree about the route, | so let us begin from the destination.", "note": "Establishing common purpose first lowers defences before any contested point."}, {"id": "open-16", "device": "the long view", "occasion": "conference", "text": "Music was taught in Australian schools long before many of the subjects that now outrank it on the timetable. || I want to ask how that ranking happened, | and whether anyone ever actually decided it.", "note": "Historical framing exposes an assumption as a choice that can be revisited."}, {"id": "open-17", "device": "the narrowing", "occasion": "council report", "text": "There is much I could report tonight. || I will report three things, | because three is what the council can act on before the end of term.", "note": "Announcing selection signals discipline and makes the agenda feel manageable."}, {"id": "open-18", "device": "the admission of difficulty", "occasion": "board submission", "text": "This is not a comfortable submission to make. || It asks the board to fund something whose benefits will arrive | long after most of us have left the table.", "note": "Candour about the hard part pre-empts the objection and invites trust."}, {"id": "open-19", "device": "the held silence", "occasion": "concert", "text": "Good evening. || That silence, just now, | is the last you will hear for an hour, | and the students have earned the right to fill it.", "note": "Using an actual pause as the subject turns stillness into anticipation."}, {"id": "open-20", "device": "the anecdote", "occasion": "conference", "text": "Last year a student asked why we still learn to read notation | when she could hear the whole score on her phone. || I gave her a poor answer that afternoon, | and this paper is my second attempt.", "note": "An honest failure makes the speaker a fellow inquirer rather than a lecturer."}, {"id": "open-21", "device": "the contrast", "occasion": "festival opening", "text": "In the rehearsal room, a mistake costs nothing. || On this stage it costs a little more, | and that small price is exactly why we built a festival.", "note": "Setting two spaces side by side states the event's purpose without abstraction."}, {"id": "open-22", "device": "the expectation reversed", "occasion": "award", "text": "I am told this award recognises a contribution to music education. || It does, | but it really recognises the colleagues who covered my classes while I was making it.", "note": "Redirecting credit mid-sentence is gracious and slightly surprising."}, {"id": "open-23", "device": "the apostrophe", "occasion": "valedictory", "text": "You asked us not to make a fuss. || The council has considered your request carefully, | and has decided, with respect, to ignore it.", "note": "Addressing the honoured person directly draws the room into a shared affection."}, {"id": "open-24", "device": "the stated purpose", "occasion": "council report", "text": "The purpose of this report is narrow: | to tell the council where the music budget went, | and why next year's must go somewhere else.", "note": "A tight remit tells governors precisely what they are being asked to judge."}, {"id": "open-25", "device": "the hypothetical", "occasion": "board submission", "text": "Imagine the festival without its junior divisions. || It would be shorter, cheaper and easier to run, | and within a few years it would have no senior divisions either.", "note": "A projected consequence makes an invisible dependency visible."}, {"id": "open-26", "device": "the precedent", "occasion": "panel", "text": "The last time this course was revised, | it followed years of complaint. || I would like us, this time, to revise it *before* the complaint.", "note": "Recalling how things were done invites the room to do better without accusing anyone."}, {"id": "open-27", "device": "the object", "occasion": "eulogy", "text": "On the music stand in his studio | there is still a chart with his pencil marks. || Bar thirty-two says, in his hand, *breathe here*.", "note": "A small surviving artefact carries grief more quietly and powerfully than description."}, {"id": "open-28", "device": "the thanks that moves on", "occasion": "award", "text": "Thank you. || I will keep the thanks brief, | because the people who deserve them most are in the second row | and would prefer I got on with it.", "note": "Acknowledging gratitude economically shows respect for both the honoured and the audience."}, {"id": "open-29", "device": "situational humour", "occasion": "toast", "text": "Twenty years ago this band could not agree on a key signature. || Tonight, I am told, it has agreed on a restaurant, | which is the greater achievement.", "note": "Affectionate exaggeration from shared history unites a celebratory room."}, {"id": "open-30", "device": "the show of hands", "occasion": "parent body", "text": "How many of you learned an instrument as a child | and stopped? || Keep your hand up a moment, | because that is the conversation I want to have.", "note": "Physical participation makes the audience's own experience the evidence."}, {"id": "open-31", "device": "the thesis first", "occasion": "conference", "text": "My argument is simple, | and I will spend twenty minutes making it difficult: | we assess *performance*, | and we have forgotten we meant to assess understanding.", "note": "Declaring the claim at once lets an expert audience follow the reasoning rather than hunt for it."}, {"id": "open-32", "device": "the wide shot", "occasion": "festival opening", "text": "Somewhere in this building, | a drummer is checking the same snare for the fourth time | and a parent is pretending not to be nervous. || Both are exactly where they should be.", "note": "Panning across unseen scenes captures the scale and feeling of an event at once."}, {"id": "open-33", "device": "the steelman", "occasion": "panel", "text": "The case against specialist selection is a serious one, | and it deserves to be put at its strongest | before I disagree with it.", "note": "Granting the opposing view its best form signals confidence and fairness to a sceptical panel."}, {"id": "open-34", "device": "the occasion named", "occasion": "concert", "text": "We gather at the end of a long year, | in a hall that has heard a great many endings. || Tonight it hears a few beginnings as well.", "note": "Marking the moment in time gives the evening a frame larger than the program."}, {"id": "open-35", "device": "the understatement", "occasion": "board submission", "text": "The financial position of the association is, | to put it with some restraint, | *interesting*. || I will explain why, | and then what we propose.", "note": "Dry restraint signals seriousness more effectively than alarm."}, {"id": "open-36", "device": "the tricolon", "occasion": "eulogy", "text": "She taught for most of her life, | played for all of it, | and listened, I think, | longer than either.", "note": "A rising three-part line compresses a life and lands on its quietest virtue."}, {"id": "open-37", "device": "the shared memory", "occasion": "valedictory", "text": "Most of us remember the first staff meeting he chaired. || It ran forty minutes over, | and nobody left, | which tells you most of what you need to know.", "note": "A remembered moment the room shares makes the tribute communal."}, {"id": "open-38", "device": "the correction of record", "occasion": "council report", "text": "Before the report proper, one correction. || The figure circulated last month was wrong, | the error was mine, | and the corrected figure is in front of you.", "note": "Owning an error immediately and briefly strengthens authority for everything after."}, {"id": "open-39", "device": "the invitation", "occasion": "parent body", "text": "I am not here to persuade you. || I am here to show you what your children do between three and five on a Thursday, | and to let you judge what it is worth.", "note": "Disclaiming persuasion lowers resistance and positions the evidence as decisive."}, {"id": "open-40", "device": "the sound foretold", "occasion": "concert", "text": "In a few minutes you will hear a chord | that took this ensemble six weeks to tune. || When it arrives, | I hope you will hear the six weeks as well.", "note": "Preparing the ear for one moment teaches the audience how to listen."}], "closings": [{"id": "close-1", "device": "the return to the opening image", "occasion": "conference", "text": "I began with a trombonist holding a note she could not yet hear. || By November she will hear it, | and the question is whether our systems will notice when she does.", "note": "Returning to the opening scene closes the frame and shows how far the argument has travelled."}, {"id": "close-2", "device": "the charge", "occasion": "conference", "text": "My request of this conference is modest and specific. || Go home, | find one assessment task you have set for years, | and ask whether it measures what you *believe*.", "note": "A concrete, doable instruction turns agreement into action."}, {"id": "close-3", "device": "the quiet ending", "occasion": "eulogy", "text": "He would not have wanted a long speech. || He would have wanted the room tuned, | and then he would have wanted us to play. || So let us.", "note": "A diminishing cadence lets grief resolve into action without rhetoric."}, {"id": "close-4", "device": "the tricolon", "occasion": "festival opening", "text": "Play *boldly*, | listen *generously*, | and leave this building better musicians than you entered it.", "note": "Three parallel imperatives give a youthful audience a memorable send-off."}, {"id": "close-5", "device": "the toast", "occasion": "toast", "text": "Please stand. || To the band that could not agree on a key, | and has agreed, at last, on almost everything else.", "note": "Calling back to the opening joke turns humour into warmth at the moment of the raised glass."}, {"id": "close-6", "device": "the ask restated", "occasion": "board submission", "text": "The board has the figures and the risks. || What it needs now is a decision, | and I would ask that it be made *tonight*.", "note": "Repeating the request in its simplest form leaves the board nothing to wonder about."}, {"id": "close-7", "device": "the summary in a line", "occasion": "council report", "text": "In short: | the program is healthy, | the budget is tight, | and the next twelve months will decide which of those matters more.", "note": "A compressed recapitulation gives governors the sentence they will repeat afterwards."}, {"id": "close-8", "device": "the turn", "occasion": "panel", "text": "We were asked whether specialist programs are elitist. || The better question is why we have let *excellence* become a word we apologise for.", "note": "Ending on a reframed question leaves the panel arguing on the speaker's ground."}, {"id": "close-9", "device": "the deflection of credit", "occasion": "award", "text": "I accept this on behalf of the people who never stand at the front. || Thank you.", "note": "Brevity and generosity at the close are what an award audience remembers."}, {"id": "close-10", "device": "the image left behind", "occasion": "concert", "text": "When the last chord fades, | sit with it for a moment before you applaud. || That silence belongs to the students too.", "note": "Giving the audience something to do with the final moment shapes how the night ends."}, {"id": "close-11", "device": "the open door", "occasion": "parent body", "text": "The rehearsal room is open on Thursday afternoons. || Come and listen. || Then tell me what it is worth.", "note": "An invitation in place of a demand returns judgement to the parents and builds trust."}, {"id": "close-12", "device": "the farewell", "occasion": "valedictory", "text": "You leave us a better department than you found, | and a harder act to follow than you know. || Go well.", "note": "A balanced pair followed by a plain blessing is affectionate without sentimentality."}, {"id": "close-13", "device": "the stake restated", "occasion": "board submission", "text": "If the board declines, | nothing will happen this year. || That is precisely the danger.", "note": "Making inaction the risk reverses the usual safety of saying no."}, {"id": "close-14", "device": "the concession", "occasion": "panel", "text": "I may be wrong about the mechanism. || I am not wrong about the students, | and I would rather we argue about the first than forget the second.", "note": "Yielding the minor point makes the major one harder to dislodge."}, {"id": "close-15", "device": "the question left open", "occasion": "conference", "text": "I do not have a tidy answer to the question I began with. || I have a better question, | and that may be the more useful thing to leave in the room.", "note": "Intellectual honesty at the close earns the respect of an expert audience."}, {"id": "close-16", "device": "the forward look", "occasion": "council report", "text": "Next term I will bring the council a proposal, | not a report. || Tonight was the diagnosis; | the treatment comes in March.", "note": "Signposting the next step converts information into a timeline the council can hold you to."}, {"id": "close-17", "device": "the understatement", "occasion": "award", "text": "It has been, | on balance, | a reasonable way to spend twenty-six years.", "note": "Dry restraint about a long career moves an audience more than any declaration."}, {"id": "close-18", "device": "the personal turn", "occasion": "eulogy", "text": "I learned more about time from him than from any metronome. || I am still keeping it, | badly, | and I think of him every time I rush.", "note": "A self-implicating confession makes the loss present and continuing."}, {"id": "close-19", "device": "the applause cue", "occasion": "concert", "text": "Please join me in thanking the musicians, | who have given you an evening, | and their families, | who have given them years.", "note": "A parallel structure scales gratitude up to its true size and cues the room."}, {"id": "close-20", "device": "the anaphora", "occasion": "festival opening", "text": "This festival is for the student who is ready. || It is for the student who is not. || It is, above all, for the student who comes back next year.", "note": "Repetition with variation builds to the value the event actually serves."}, {"id": "close-21", "device": "the plain close", "occasion": "council report", "text": "That concludes the report. || I am grateful to the council for its patience, | and happy to take questions.", "note": "A clean procedural ending signals command of the meeting and invites scrutiny."}, {"id": "close-22", "device": "the reversal", "occasion": "toast", "text": "We came to celebrate her retirement. || Knowing her, | I suspect we have actually come to her next rehearsal.", "note": "A gentle reversal honours a person's restlessness with affection."}, {"id": "close-23", "device": "the callback", "occasion": "eulogy", "text": "On the stand in his studio, | bar thirty-two still says *breathe here*. || So, for a moment, | let us.", "note": "Returning to the remembered object turns his instruction into the room's shared act."}, {"id": "close-24", "device": "the charge to the young", "occasion": "valedictory", "text": "You will not remember most of what we taught you. || Remember how it felt to play in tune with forty other people, | and go and find that again, wherever you end up.", "note": "A modest admission followed by one lasting instruction feels truthful to students leaving."}, {"id": "close-25", "device": "the principle named", "occasion": "board submission", "text": "This is, finally, a question of stewardship. || We hold this festival on trust | for students who have not yet enrolled anywhere.", "note": "Elevating a budget item to a governance principle reminds a board of its duty."}, {"id": "close-26", "device": "the full stop", "occasion": "panel", "text": "That is my position. || I am happy to be argued out of it, | but not tonight.", "note": "A short, firm close with a touch of wit projects settled confidence."}, {"id": "close-27", "device": "the shared resolve", "occasion": "parent body", "text": "We will not agree on everything. || We agree on the children, | and that is enough to begin with.", "note": "Ending on common ground leaves a divided room with somewhere to stand together."}, {"id": "close-28", "device": "the analogy from the art", "occasion": "conference", "text": "In a good rhythm section, | nobody leads and everybody listens. || I would settle for a sector that managed half of that.", "note": "An image from the speaker's own craft lands the argument with wit and authority."}, {"id": "close-29", "device": "the gentle humour", "occasion": "award", "text": "I am told the trophy is heavier than it looks. || So, I suspect, was the work, | and I share both with my colleagues.", "note": "A light physical joke opens into sincere acknowledgement."}, {"id": "close-30", "device": "the ascending tricolon", "occasion": "conference", "text": "We can measure it badly, | we can measure it well, | or we can stop pretending that what matters most in music | is the part we find *easiest* to count.", "note": "The lengthening third limb carries the thesis to its strongest form."}, {"id": "close-31", "device": "the dedication", "occasion": "concert", "text": "We dedicate the final piece | to the teachers who started these students, | in rooms none of us will ever see.", "note": "Honouring unseen contributors widens the evening's gratitude beyond the stage."}, {"id": "close-32", "device": "the release", "occasion": "festival opening", "text": "The stage is yours. || Begin.", "note": "Two short sentences hand over the event with ceremony and economy."}, {"id": "close-33", "device": "the cumulative toast", "occasion": "toast", "text": "So raise a glass | to the music, | to the people who made it, | and to the ones who carried the gear.", "note": "A comic final item in the list keeps the toast warm rather than solemn."}, {"id": "close-34", "device": "the undertaking", "occasion": "council report", "text": "I give the council this undertaking: | the next report will be shorter, | and the news, I hope, | better.", "note": "A personal commitment with a light touch leaves governors with goodwill."}, {"id": "close-35", "device": "the long view", "occasion": "eulogy", "text": "Years from now, | a student of a student of his | will tune a room before anyone arrives. || That is the legacy he would have preferred.", "note": "Projecting influence forward offers consolation without cliché."}, {"id": "close-36", "device": "the restatement of thesis", "occasion": "panel", "text": "Access and excellence are not opposites. || They are two halves of one promise, | and we break it whenever we choose only one.", "note": "A crisp final formulation gives the panel the line it will quote."}, {"id": "close-37", "device": "the hinge", "occasion": "board submission", "text": "The board can treat this as a cost. || I would ask it to treat it as a *decision*, | because that is how the students will experience it.", "note": "Shifting the category of the question changes what the board feels responsible for."}, {"id": "close-38", "device": "the confession", "occasion": "conference", "text": "I came to this work for the music. || I have stayed for the students, | and I suspect most of this room could say the same.", "note": "A candid personal note at the end binds speaker and audience in a shared vocation."}, {"id": "close-39", "device": "the final image", "occasion": "parent body", "text": "In five years your children will be somewhere else. || What they take with them | is being built on Thursday afternoons, | one rehearsal at a time.", "note": "A forward image makes present routine feel consequential to parents."}, {"id": "close-40", "device": "the last word", "occasion": "concert", "text": "Thank you for listening. || That, in the end, | is what music asks of all of us.", "note": "Turning a conventional thanks into the evening's meaning closes on an unexpected depth."}], "formulas": [{"id": "gf-temporal-arc", "name": "The temporal arc", "who": "Abraham Lincoln", "source": "Gettysburg Address, 19 November 1863", "family": "speech", "gist": "Moves the room from what was founded, through what is being tested now, to an unfinished obligation that the listeners themselves must carry.", "steps": [{"slot": "Founding past", "job": "Name the origin and the promise it made, in one plain sentence.", "cue": "Twenty years ago this program began with a simple promise."}, {"slot": "Testing present", "job": "Say what is now testing whether that promise holds.", "cue": "What we are facing now is a test of whether that promise can last."}, {"slot": "The turn", "job": "Concede that words or ceremonies cannot do the work; the work was done by others.", "cue": "Nothing we say tonight adds to what they have already done."}, {"slot": "Transfer", "job": "Hand the unfinished task to the people in the room as their obligation.", "cue": "What is left unfinished is ours, and it is specific."}, {"slot": "Renewed future", "job": "State the future the obligation secures, compactly.", "cue": "If we do this, the promise does not just survive; it is renewed."}], "teach": "Lincoln was asked for a few appropriate remarks after Edward Everett's two-hour oration, and he spoke for about two minutes. The architecture is a single sweep of time. He opens with the founding ('Four score and seven years ago') and the proposition it was dedicated to; moves to the present war as a test of whether any nation so conceived 'can long endure'; then turns, saying the living cannot consecrate the ground because the dead already have. The last and longest sentence transfers the 'unfinished work' to the listeners and names the future it secures.\n\nIt works on an educated room because the structure is visible without being announced. Past, present and future give the listener a map, and the turn in the middle (we cannot do this with words) disarms the suspicion that the speaker is about to perform. The final move converts admiration into duty. Nobody leaves merely moved; they leave owing something.\n\nJason's scaffold builds the speaker's credibility before the proposal: thanks, acknowledgement, standing. The temporal arc does almost none of that. Its authority comes from humility at the turn and from the size of the time frame. It suits ceremonies, anniversaries, and any moment where the room needs to see the present as one chapter in a longer story.\n\nFor a practical problem-solver the transfer is where the proposal lives. Lincoln's obligation is abstract; yours can be concrete. Name the unfinished work as two or three tasks, with owners if the setting permits, and let the renewed future be the outcome those tasks produce. The arc carries the substance; the substance gives the arc its weight.", "when": "Anniversaries, program milestones, farewells, festival openings, and committee moments where a long-running project has just been tested or set back.", "risk": "Borrowed grandeur: a sweep of history for a small matter sounds inflated. Fit the time frame to the stakes, keep the whole thing short, and make the transfer concrete.", "model": {"room": "Jazz festival gala, twentieth year", "prompt": "As treasurer you are asked to say a few words at the festival's twentieth-year dinner, months after a funding cut nearly cancelled it.", "slots": ["Twenty years ago a handful of teachers decided that every school band in this state deserved a *real* stage, with real adjudicators and a real audience. | That was the whole idea.", "This year we found out whether that idea could survive without a safety net. || When the grant went, we had eleven weeks and a budget with a hole in the middle of it.", "I could thank everyone who got us through, and I will, privately and at length. But nothing I say tonight adds anything to what the volunteers, the directors and the students already did. They *kept* the festival. We are only here to notice.", "What is left unfinished is ours, and it is specific. We need a reserve that covers one full festival, so no single decision in a government office can close us again. We need three-year sponsorship, not annual favours. And we need every school here to bring one new school next year, a school that has never entered.", "If we do those three things, the idea from twenty years ago does not just survive. || It becomes *permanent*."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "School council, final meeting of the year", "prompt": "You are chairing the last council meeting before the long-serving business manager retires. Give a short tribute that ends by handing the council the work she leaves unfinished: the new instrument storage build.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "Music department end-of-year staff function", "prompt": "The specialist music program has just survived a review that recommended cutting Year 7 intake. Speak to the staff, placing this year in the program's history and naming what they must finish next year.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee, first meeting after the rejection", "prompt": "The committee reconvenes after the Board rejected its course. You are asked to open discussion. Use the arc: why the course was begun, what the rejection tests, and the specific unfinished work.", "seconds": 180}, {"room": "Awards night, Year 12 music graduates", "prompt": "Speak for the department to the graduating music cohort and their families, placing their six years in the history of the program and giving them one obligation to carry out.", "seconds": 90}]}, {"id": "gf-praise-standard", "name": "Praise the standard, then charge", "who": "Pericles, as reported by Thucydides", "source": "Funeral Oration, History of the Peloponnesian War, Book 2, 431 BC", "family": "speech", "gist": "Celebrates the honourees by describing the standard of the community they belonged to, then charges the living to be worthy of that standard.", "steps": [{"slot": "Doubt the custom", "job": "Acknowledge briefly that speeches cannot match deeds.", "cue": "I am conscious that no speech matches what these people did."}, {"slot": "The standard", "job": "Describe what kind of community this is, its habits and values, not its achievements.", "cue": "Let me say what kind of place produces work like this."}, {"slot": "They met it", "job": "Show that the honourees embodied that standard, with one or two telling particulars.", "cue": "And this year's group did not just meet that standard; they set it."}, {"slot": "The charge", "job": "Tell the living what being worthy of the standard now requires.", "cue": "Which leaves the rest of us with a question we have to answer."}, {"slot": "Brief consolation", "job": "Close warmly and briefly, releasing the room.", "cue": "So, to the families: be proud, and then let them go and do it again."}], "teach": "Thucydides gives Pericles a funeral oration that barely mentions the dead by name. Pericles opens by doubting the custom itself, since words risk falling short of deeds. He spends a sentence on the ancestors and then devotes most of the speech to Athens: its constitution, its openness, its way of combining ease with readiness, the city as 'the school of Hellas'. Only then does he say the fallen were worthy of such a city, and he turns to the living with a charge to be equal to it. The consolation to families is short, almost brisk.\n\nThe insight is that you honour people most by describing what they were part of. A list of achievements invites comparison and fatigue; a portrait of a standard invites belonging. The educated listener recognises this as praise with an argument inside it: this is who we are, therefore this is what we owe.\n\nJason's scaffold praises individuals for specific things, then builds the speaker's own standing. Pericles' architecture places the speaker almost nowhere, and the praise is collective rather than personal. The authority comes from the accuracy of the portrait. If the room hears its own best self described, the charge lands as recognition, not instruction.\n\nThe proposal sits in the charge. Once the standard is described, a practical step can be framed as what the standard demands: a mentoring scheme, a new auditions protocol, a reserve fund. It no longer sounds like the TIC's wish list. It sounds like the community keeping faith with itself.", "when": "Awards nights, retirements, end-of-year ceremonies, conference welcomes, and any time a group must be reminded of its identity before being asked to do something hard.", "risk": "Self-congratulation: a portrait of 'our' excellence can sound smug or exclusive to visitors. Describe habits and values rather than rankings, and make the charge genuinely demanding.", "model": {"room": "Music department awards night", "prompt": "You present the department's major awards and want to use the moment to ask families and staff to back next year's mentoring program for incoming Year 7s.", "slots": ["I am conscious that nothing I say in the next two minutes will match what you have heard on this stage tonight. | So I will be brief.", "Let me say instead what kind of place produces music like that. It is a place where a Year 12 bassist stays back to show a Year 8 how to set up a bow. Where the senior band rehearses at seven in the morning without being asked. Where nobody is too good to stack *chairs*.", "Tonight's award winners did not just meet that standard. They set it. The concertmaster ran sectionals all of Term 3 while carrying a full ATAR load. The jazz combo arranged its own set, and arranged it well.", "Which leaves the rest of us with a question. || Next year a hundred and twenty new Year 7s walk in. If the standard is to survive, every one of them needs an older student who knows their name. We are starting a mentoring program in Week 2, one senior to every four juniors, and we need families to make the time for it.", "So, to the families: be *proud*. And then let them go and do it again."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "Retirement function for a long-serving instrumental teacher", "prompt": "Honour a woodwind teacher retiring after 30 years by describing the culture she helped build, then charge the department to keep it.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "State schools jazz festival closing ceremony", "prompt": "As festival treasurer, close the festival by describing what the festival community values and charging directors to bring a new school next year.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "Conference welcome, music educators' association", "prompt": "Welcome 200 music teachers to a state conference. Describe what the profession at its best looks like and charge them with one thing to take back to their schools.", "seconds": 180}, {"room": "School council, recognising student volunteers", "prompt": "The council formally thanks the student leaders who ran the community concert series. Speak to the standard they upheld, then ask the council to fund next year's series.", "seconds": 90}]}, {"id": "gf-six-part-oration", "name": "The six-part oration", "who": "Marcus Tullius Cicero", "source": "De Inventione, c. 84 BC, and his forensic speeches", "family": "speech", "gist": "Walks a sceptical room through a case in the classical order: win goodwill, tell the facts, map the argument, prove it, refute the objection, and close with feeling.", "steps": [{"slot": "Exordium", "job": "Win attention and goodwill, briefly and without flattery.", "cue": "I want to take five minutes, and I will keep it to five."}, {"slot": "Narratio", "job": "State the facts in order, plainly, as if neutral.", "cue": "Here is what happened, in order."}, {"slot": "Partitio", "job": "Say what is agreed, what is disputed, and the points you will make.", "cue": "We agree on the goal; we differ on two points, and I will take them in turn."}, {"slot": "Confirmatio", "job": "Prove your case point by point.", "cue": "First, the evidence that the change works."}, {"slot": "Refutatio", "job": "Take the strongest objection and answer it directly.", "cue": "The best argument against this is cost, so let me deal with it head on."}, {"slot": "Peroratio", "job": "Summarise, amplify, and appeal to what the room cares about.", "cue": "So, in short, and this is what matters most:"}], "teach": "De Inventione, Cicero's youthful treatise, sets out the parts of a speech as exordium, narratio, partitio, confirmatio, reprehensio (often called refutatio) and conclusio or peroratio; the Rhetorica ad Herennium, long attributed to him, gives a near-identical order. In the courts he used the order flexibly. The First Catilinarian famously skips the gentle exordium altogether and opens with 'Quo usque tandem', because the situation was an emergency and the order was a tool, not a ritual.\n\nThe order works because it matches how a sceptical listener processes a case. Goodwill first, so they listen; facts next, so they share a picture; then a map, so they know where you are; then proof, then the answer to the objection already forming in their minds; then a close that tells them why it matters. The partitio is the step most speakers skip, and it is the one an academic room most appreciates, because it separates agreement from dispute.\n\nJason's scaffold is relational: thanks, acknowledgement, standing, common ground. Cicero's is forensic. It assumes the room must be persuaded on the merits, and it puts the opposing argument inside your own speech. Where Jason summarises, Cicero refutes. That is the gap a practical strategist can fill: the committee already has eloquence; what it lacks is someone who has dealt with the Board's objection before the Board raises it again.\n\nCarry the proposal in the confirmatio and make the refutatio your strongest moment. Keep each part short. In a five-minute committee turn the whole six-part structure can run at under a minute per part, and the partitio can be a single sentence.", "when": "Making a formal case to a committee, board or panel, especially when there is a known objection; presentations to funding panels; defending a proposal against a prior rejection.", "risk": "It can sound like a lecture if every part is signposted in Latin or spoken at equal length. Signpost only the partitio, compress the narration, and let the refutation do the work.", "model": {"room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee, hybrid", "prompt": "The Board rejected the course partly because the practical assessment was judged unreliable across schools. You propose a moderation model.", "slots": ["Thank you, Chair. I will take four minutes, and I will keep it to four.", "The Board's note says the practical component could not be marked consistently between schools. That is the objection, as written, in paragraph three.", "We agree that reliability matters. We differ on one point: whether the fix is to *shrink* the practical component or to moderate it properly. I will argue the second, and then deal with cost.", "Three things. A shared recorded exemplar set, released each February. Two moderation days a year, clustered regionally, so country schools are not disadvantaged. And a sample of five recordings per school, remarked centrally. That gives the Board the evidence it asked for without gutting what makes the course worth teaching.", "The strongest objection is cost. But the recordings already exist; teachers make them now for their own marking. What is new is two days of relief per cluster, and that is a smaller sum than redesigning the course again.", "So: the Board asked for reliability. We can give it reliability *and* keep the practical heart of the course. || I would like the minutes to record that the working group will cost the moderation model by the next meeting."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "Arts funding panel interview", "prompt": "You are defending the festival's application for a three-year operating grant. The panel's known concern is that attendance is concentrated in metropolitan schools.", "seconds": 180}, {"room": "Principal's office, budget meeting", "prompt": "Make the case for replacing the department's twenty-year-old timpani. The principal's known objection is that the money was promised to the science labs.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "University academic board subcommittee", "prompt": "Argue that a first-year music performance unit should keep its live jury assessment. The objection on the table is that recorded submissions are cheaper and fairer.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "School council", "prompt": "Propose that the council support a change to the music program's audition timing from Term 3 to Term 2. The likely objection is that primary schools will not cope with the earlier date.", "seconds": 150}]}, {"id": "gf-hard-truth-resolve", "name": "Hard truth, then resolve", "who": "Winston Churchill", "source": "First speech as Prime Minister, House of Commons, 13 May 1940", "family": "speech", "gist": "States the worst plainly and in short words, offers nothing false, then names a single policy and a single aim with total commitment.", "steps": [{"slot": "What I offer", "job": "Say plainly what you can and cannot give.", "cue": "I have no good news to soften this, so I will not try."}, {"slot": "The ordeal", "job": "Name the difficulty in its real size, without hedging.", "cue": "This will be hard, and it will take longer than any of us wants."}, {"slot": "The policy", "job": "State what we will do, in one short sentence.", "cue": "So here is what we will do."}, {"slot": "The aim", "job": "State the single goal, in one word or phrase if possible.", "cue": "And the aim is simple."}, {"slot": "Resolve", "job": "Close with confidence that does not depend on luck.", "cue": "I am confident we can do it, and I am asking you to do it with me."}], "teach": "Three days into the premiership, with the German offensive under way in the West, Churchill told the Commons that he had nothing to offer but 'blood, toil, tears and sweat'. He then asked his own rhetorical questions, what is our policy and what is our aim, and answered each in a few blunt words. The speech is very short. Its force comes from the refusal to console, followed immediately by total clarity about what will be done.\n\nIt is also a lesson in diction. Churchill drew heavily on short, old English words for the moments that mattered and kept his Latinate vocabulary for the connective tissue. Under pressure an educated speaker tends to reach for abstractions; this formula insists that the hard truth and the resolve be said in words a child could follow, which is exactly why they sound adult.\n\nJason's scaffold softens the room before the content. This does the reverse: the bad news comes first and is not cushioned. It suits a room that already suspects the truth and is waiting to see whether anyone will say it. Credibility is earned by not managing them.\n\nThe proposal is the policy step. Name it in one sentence, then let the aim say what success looks like. For someone inclined to over-explain, the discipline of the formula is the point: a sentence of truth, a sentence of policy, a word of aim, and stop.", "when": "Delivering bad news to staff, students, parents or a board; the first meeting after a setback; any moment where people need clarity more than comfort.", "risk": "Theatrical gloom, or borrowed wartime grandeur for a timetable clash. Keep it proportionate, use your own short words rather than echoes of his, and make sure the resolve is backed by a real plan.", "model": {"room": "Music department staff meeting", "prompt": "The department has just lost two instrumental teaching positions for next year. You tell the staff first, before the news spreads.", "slots": ["I have no good news to soften this, so I will not try. || We lose two instrumental positions next year: one brass, one strings.", "It will be hard. Some students will have bigger groups. Some of you will teach outside your *main* instrument. And it will not be fixed in one budget round; I expect two years, possibly three.", "So here is what we will do. We keep every ensemble running. We combine Years 8 and 9 brass lessons, we move strings sectionals to a rotating roster, and I take on one strings group myself. Nobody's ensemble disappears, nobody's concert date moves, and the Year 12 recital program stays exactly as it is.", "The aim is simple. || No student leaves the program because of this.", "I am confident we can do it, because this team has done harder things with less notice. I will meet each of you this week to go through your load, and I will tell the principal on Friday exactly what this costs us in hours, not in feelings. And I am asking you to do it *with* me."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "Parent information evening", "prompt": "Tell parents of the senior concert band that the interstate tour is cancelled because the venue fell through and the insurance does not cover a rebooking. Say what you will do instead.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "As treasurer, tell the board that the festival will run a deficit this year unless two events are cut. Give the policy and the aim.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "Year 12 ATAR music class", "prompt": "Tell the class that the external practical exam date has moved forward by two weeks and their preparation must compress. Be plain, then resolute.", "seconds": 90}, {"room": "School council", "prompt": "As chair, open a council meeting by telling members that the school's enrolment cap means the music program intake will be reduced next year, and set out the council's response.", "seconds": 180}]}, {"id": "gf-hard-choice-reframe", "name": "The hard-choice reframe", "who": "John F. Kennedy", "source": "Address at Rice University, Houston, 12 September 1962; Inaugural Address, 1961", "family": "speech", "gist": "Turns the difficulty of a goal into the reason for choosing it, sets a deadline, and closes on a balanced antithesis or chiasmus.", "steps": [{"slot": "Compress time", "job": "Make the scale of change vivid with a compressed comparison.", "cue": "Put it this way: in the time it takes a student to go from Year 7 to graduation,"}, {"slot": "Name the choice", "job": "State the goal as a deliberate choice, not a necessity.", "cue": "We are choosing to do this."}, {"slot": "Hard is the reason", "job": "Say the difficulty is the reason, because it will test and organise our best.", "cue": "Not because it is easy, but because it will ask the most of us."}, {"slot": "Deadline and cost", "job": "Give a date and admit the cost honestly.", "cue": "By the end of next year, and it will cost us this."}, {"slot": "Balanced close", "job": "Close on an antithesis or chiasmus that reverses the room's question.", "cue": "The question is not what this costs us, but what it costs us not to."}], "teach": "At Rice, Kennedy compressed fifty thousand years of human history into half a century to make the pace of change felt, then announced that the nation would go to the Moon within the decade, framing the goal as chosen 'because they are hard'. The difficulty is not hidden; it becomes the justification, because a hard goal organises and measures the best of our energies. He was candid about cost and about the scale of the engineering still unsolved. His inaugural address the year before had given him the chiastic close he is most remembered for, the reversal of what the country could do for the citizen.\n\nThe reframe works because a sceptical room's natural objection to any ambitious proposal is that it is too hard. Kennedy pre-empts the objection by agreeing with it and then reversing its meaning. Antithesis and chiasmus then give the close a balanced, memorable shape that people repeat to each other afterwards, which is precisely what Dijon's practical contributions have lacked.\n\nJason's scaffold seeks consensus by finding common ground. This formula seeks commitment by naming a hard choice. It is better suited to a moment of ambition than to a moment of negotiation, and it gives a practical proposal a horizon and a deadline rather than a list of steps.\n\nCarry the proposal in the choice and the deadline. The compression step is optional but powerful in an educated room; it should be accurate and specific to the domain. The chiasmus must be built from the room's own words, ideally the objection itself, reversed.", "when": "Launching an ambitious goal at a conference, board or staff meeting; answering 'it is too hard' with a commitment rather than a rebuttal; the close of a strategic plan presentation.", "risk": "Sounding like a campaign speech, or a chiasmus so neat it feels manufactured. Use one balanced line only, make the deadline real, and state the cost in actual terms.", "model": {"room": "Music department planning day", "prompt": "You propose that the department aims to have every Year 10 student able to improvise over a blues by the end of next year, across classical and contemporary streams.", "slots": ["Put it this way. In the time it takes a student to go from Year 7 to Year 12, much of the music they love outside this building will have been *made* by someone improvising, sampling or arranging on the fly.", "So I am proposing that we choose to do something about it. By the end of next year, every Year 10 student in the program, string players and percussionists included, can improvise a convincing chorus over a twelve-bar blues.", "Not because it is easy. It is not easy, for them or for us. But because it will ask the most of every teacher in this room, and it will show us exactly where our teaching is *strong* and where it is thin.", "The cost is real: two staff PD days, a shared set of backing tracks, and about ten minutes of every ensemble rehearsal in Term 2. I will write the sequence over the holidays, and I will teach the first lesson in every stream myself.", "We keep asking whether our students are ready for improvisation. || I think the better question is whether our *program* is."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "State music educators' conference, plenary", "prompt": "Propose that the state's specialist music schools commit to a shared, publicly available repertoire bank for regional schools within two years.", "seconds": 180}, {"room": "Festival association AGM", "prompt": "Propose that the festival aims to include at least one entry from every education region in the state within three years, despite the travel costs.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee", "prompt": "Propose that the committee resubmits a revised course to the Board within twelve months rather than waiting for the next review cycle.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "Principal's office", "prompt": "Ask the principal to back a goal of a full symphony orchestra at the school within three years, starting from a strings program of twenty students.", "seconds": 90}]}, {"id": "gf-anaphora-vision", "name": "The anaphora build to vision", "who": "Martin Luther King Jr.", "source": "Address at the March on Washington, Lincoln Memorial, 28 August 1963", "family": "speech", "gist": "Grounds the speech in a concrete metaphor of what is owed, insists on the urgency of now, then builds a vision through repeated openings that rise to a close.", "steps": [{"slot": "The debt", "job": "Frame the injustice or gap as a promise unpaid, with a concrete metaphor.", "cue": "Every student who auditions is handed a promise."}, {"slot": "Urgency", "job": "Say why this cannot wait for the gradual.", "cue": "And it cannot wait for the next review cycle."}, {"slot": "Anaphora vision", "job": "Paint the future in three or four sentences that begin the same way, rising in specificity.", "cue": "I can see a festival where... I can see a festival where..."}, {"slot": "Widening cascade", "job": "Spread the vision across places or groups by name.", "cue": "In Kalgoorlie, in Albany, in Karratha..."}, {"slot": "Arrival", "job": "Land on a single line that unites the room.", "cue": "And when that happens, it will be all of ours."}], "teach": "King's speech has two halves that are often remembered as one. The first is argument by metaphor: the founding documents as a promissory note, the cheque that came back marked insufficient funds, and a refusal to believe the bank of justice is bankrupt. He then insists on the urgency of the moment against the tranquilising counsel of gradualism. Only after that foundation does he move to the visionary anaphora, the repeated 'I have a dream' sentences, each more concrete than the last, and the cascade of named places where freedom must ring.\n\nAnaphora works because repetition lowers the cognitive load of each new sentence. The listener knows the frame, so attention goes entirely to what changes. As the content becomes more specific, the room feels momentum. The named places then turn a general vision into a map, and every listener finds themselves in it.\n\nJason's scaffold is a conversational architecture for a committee. This is a platform architecture for a hall or a conference. It is not about establishing standing; it is about moving a room from grievance to shared picture. The debt metaphor is the logos, the urgency is the ethic, and the anaphora is the pathos.\n\nThe practical problem-solver's opening is the vision itself: each repeated sentence can carry one concrete element of a plan. Instead of 'a world where all is well', say 'a festival where a band from Kununurra gets its bus paid'. The rhythm makes it memorable; the specifics make it a proposal.", "when": "Conference keynotes, festival openings, advocacy for equity of access, any address to a hall where the goal is shared commitment to a future picture.", "risk": "Imitation of King's cadence by someone in a staff room is ruinous. Use your own repeated opening, keep it to three or four repetitions, and make each one concrete.", "model": {"room": "State schools jazz festival opening night", "prompt": "As treasurer, you open the festival and want to launch the regional travel fund.", "slots": ["Every student who walks onto a festival stage has been handed a *promise*: that if you practise, you get to play. || For students in our regions, that promise has been coming back stamped insufficient funds.", "And it cannot wait for the next strategic plan. A Year 9 trumpet player in Esperance gets one Year 9. That is all. A fund that arrives in three years arrives for someone else's child.", "So I can see a festival where every regional band has its bus *paid* before it is asked. I can see a festival where a country director gets the same adjudicator feedback, in the same week, as a school in Mount Lawley. I can see a festival where the first question about entering is about the music, and never about the fuel.", "In Esperance, in Broome, in Geraldton, in Kalgoorlie, and in the hills and suburbs we already know.", "Tonight we launch the regional travel fund, and every dollar from tonight's raffle goes to it. || When that promise is kept, it will be kept by *all* of us."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "Conference keynote, music education", "prompt": "Give a short keynote arguing that every public secondary school in WA should have access to a qualified instrumental teacher. Use a debt metaphor and a three-sentence vision.", "seconds": 180}, {"room": "School council", "prompt": "Advocate for a fee-relief fund so that no family is priced out of the specialist music program. Build to a concrete vision of the program in five years.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "Year 7 orientation, families", "prompt": "Welcome incoming specialist music families with a vision of what their children's six years will look like, using a repeated opening.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "Arts funding panel", "prompt": "Argue for a grant to fund a mentoring partnership between professional jazz musicians and low-SES schools, closing with a cascade of named suburbs.", "seconds": 90}]}, {"id": "gf-self-us-now", "name": "Story of self, us, now", "who": "Marshall Ganz; Barack Obama", "source": "Ganz's public narrative framework (Harvard Kennedy School); Obama's Democratic National Convention keynote, 27 July 2004", "family": "speech", "gist": "Earns the right to lead by telling the moment that made you care, widens it into the shared values of the room, then turns those values into an urgent, specific choice.", "steps": [{"slot": "Story of self", "job": "Tell one specific choice point from your own life that explains why you care.", "cue": "I want to tell you about the moment I decided this mattered."}, {"slot": "The value", "job": "Name the value that moment taught you, in a sentence.", "cue": "What I learned then is simple."}, {"slot": "Story of us", "job": "Show that the room shares that value, with an example from its own history.", "cue": "And I think everyone here has a version of that story."}, {"slot": "Story of now", "job": "Name the challenge that threatens that shared value now.", "cue": "Which is why what is happening now matters."}, {"slot": "The choice", "job": "Give a specific action, with hope that it can work.", "cue": "So here is what I am asking us to do, starting this week."}], "teach": "Marshall Ganz, an organiser who worked with the farm workers' movement and later taught at Harvard, describes public narrative as three linked stories. The story of self tells a choice point in the speaker's own life that shows why they are called to this work. The story of us shows the values the listeners share. The story of now names the urgent challenge to those values and the choice it demands, with hope. Ganz often points to Obama's 2004 convention keynote as an example: Obama told his parents' and grandparents' story, widened it into an American story, and turned it toward the election as a choice.\n\nThe architecture works because it moves from the particular to the shared to the actionable. A personal story creates trust quickly, but on its own it is self-regard; the move to 'us' converts it into belonging, and the move to 'now' converts belonging into action. An educated room that is weary of policy talk will often listen to a story, and then find itself agreeing with the policy inside it.\n\nJason's scaffold establishes standing by stating qualifications. This formula establishes standing by revealing motive. It is a better fit than Jason's when the room doubts not your expertise but your reasons, or when the subject has become a bureaucratic abstraction that needs to be made human.\n\nFor a practical strategist the story of now is where the plan goes. The story of self must be brief and specific, a moment with a place and a time, not a career summary. Ganz is clear that a story of now without a concrete action is only anxiety.", "when": "Speaking for a cause rather than a technical motion; recruiting volunteers or support; first address to a new group you will lead; when the room doubts your motives.", "risk": "Self-indulgence, or an emotional story out of scale with the decision. Keep the self story under a minute, make it a single moment, and spend the most time on now.", "model": {"room": "Parent association general meeting", "prompt": "You need parent volunteers to run the instrument loan scheme, which is about to collapse because the coordinator has left.", "slots": ["When I was fourteen, in Brisbane, my school lent me a double bass with a cracked rib and one good string. Nobody at home could have bought one. || I have played that instrument's *grandchildren* for twenty-six years.", "What I learned then is simple. Talent is everywhere; instruments are not. Somebody has to decide to close that gap.", "And I think a lot of you have a version of that story. Some of your children are playing loan instruments tonight. The scheme here started because a group of parents, fifteen years ago, refused to let price decide who got to play.", "Which is why this matters now. Our coordinator has moved interstate, and without someone to run it, the loan scheme stops at the end of term. Forty-two students are on loan instruments right *now*, and eleven more are waiting for one.", "So here is what I am asking. Three volunteers, two hours a fortnight each, and I will train you myself on Thursday the 14th, in M1.3, straight after school. If three hands go up tonight, the scheme survives. || I would like to see them."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee", "prompt": "The committee is debating whether to keep fighting for the rejected course. Tell a brief story of why you teach music, widen it to the committee's shared purpose, and propose the next step.", "seconds": 180}, {"room": "Festival association, volunteer recruitment night", "prompt": "Recruit stage managers and runners for the festival by telling your own story of a first festival, then the association's story, then the shortfall now.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "New staff induction, Performing Arts", "prompt": "Introduce yourself to new Performing Arts staff as TIC Music in a way that tells them why you lead the way you do, what the department values, and what you need from them this term.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "School council, election of new chair", "prompt": "You are standing for re-election as council chair. Give a short statement using self, us and now.", "seconds": 90}]}, {"id": "gf-account-principle-cost", "name": "Account, principle, accepted cost", "who": "Nelson Mandela", "source": "Statement from the dock, Rivonia Trial, Pretoria, 20 April 1964", "family": "speech", "gist": "Admits what is true without evasion, explains how you came to act through your own history, states the principle beneath it, and openly accepts the cost of holding it.", "steps": [{"slot": "Admit the facts", "job": "State plainly what you did or hold, without minimising.", "cue": "Let me be clear about what I did, because it is not in dispute."}, {"slot": "The account", "job": "Explain the history that led you there, calmly and in order.", "cue": "I want to explain how I came to that position."}, {"slot": "The alternatives", "job": "Show the other options were tried or weighed and why they failed.", "cue": "I did not start there. I tried the other routes first."}, {"slot": "The principle", "job": "State the principle you are standing on, in its simplest form.", "cue": "The principle underneath it is this."}, {"slot": "Accept the cost", "job": "Say what you are willing to bear for it, without drama.", "cue": "I understand what this may cost, and I accept that."}], "teach": "Mandela chose to make a statement from the dock rather than give evidence under cross-examination, and he spoke for hours. He did not deny what was true: he admitted that he had helped plan sabotage. He then gave a long, orderly account of his own history, of the ANC's decades of non-violent methods, and of why, after those avenues were closed, the turn was made. He laid out the principle of a democratic and free society, and closed by saying it was an ideal for which 'I am prepared to die'.\n\nThe power lies in the absence of evasion. An admission at the start removes the adversary's best weapon, because nothing can be exposed. The account then makes the decision intelligible rather than impulsive, and the principle lifts it from personal to general. The accepted cost is what gives the principle credibility: a principle you will pay nothing for is a preference.\n\nJason's scaffold seeks favour. This seeks respect, and it is for moments when favour is not available: when you have dissented, broken with consensus, or done something that will be criticised. It is also the architecture for a principled minority report or a resignation statement.\n\nFor a strategist, the alternatives step is where you show your practical record: the routes you tried, the meetings, the proposals. The principle then explains why you are not yet willing to accept the outcome, and the cost tells the room you are serious. The scale must always be honest: in a staff room the cost is a reputation or a role, and should be named as such.", "when": "Explaining a dissent or a minority position; owning a controversial decision; a resignation or withdrawal from a body; answering criticism of something you did on principle.", "risk": "Martyrdom: claiming a moral stature the stakes do not support. Keep the cost real and modest, speak calmly, and make the account factual rather than aggrieved.", "model": {"room": "School council", "prompt": "Against advice from the deputy principal, you refused to let a student leave the specialist program mid-year, and the parent has complained to council. You explain your decision.", "slots": ["Let me be clear about what I did, because it is not in dispute. I declined the request to withdraw the student from the specialist program in Term 2, and I did so against the deputy's advice.", "I want to explain how I came to that. The family enrolled under the three-year commitment, which every family signs at audition. The student's results in music are strong; the issue raised was timetable pressure in Year 9.", "I did not start with refusal. I offered a reduced ensemble load, a lesson rescheduled out of maths, and a meeting with the year coordinator. The family declined all three. I put each offer in writing, and those letters are in the council papers.", "The principle underneath it is this. A selective place is *held* on behalf of another child who missed out. If commitments lapse whenever they become inconvenient, we are not running a selective program; we are running a waiting list.", "I understand council may take a different view, and if it does I will apply its decision without complaint. || But I would rather be overruled on a principle than *quietly* abandon one."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee", "prompt": "You are the only member who voted against accepting the Board's revisions. Explain your position to the committee before the minutes are finalised.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "As treasurer you refused to sign off a payment to a sponsor's preferred supplier without quotes. Explain this to the board, which includes the sponsor's representative.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "Principal's office", "prompt": "You declined to run the senior band at a corporate event the principal agreed to, because it fell in exam week. Explain the decision and what you accept as a consequence.", "seconds": 90}, {"room": "Music department meeting", "prompt": "You are stepping down as convenor of the department's assessment working party because you disagree with its direction. Give a statement that keeps relationships intact.", "seconds": 180}]}, {"id": "gf-we-acknowledgement", "name": "The 'we' acknowledgement", "who": "Paul Keating", "source": "Redfern Park Speech, Sydney, 10 December 1992", "family": "speech", "gist": "Names a hard test for the community, owns past failure in the first person plural, asks the room to imagine it from the other side, then turns to a practical, unashamed way forward.", "steps": [{"slot": "Name the test", "job": "Say that how we respond to this will reveal who we are.", "cue": "I think this is a test of what kind of department we are."}, {"slot": "Own it as 'we'", "job": "Acknowledge what was done, in repeated first-person plural sentences, without blaming others.", "cue": "It was we who... It was we who..."}, {"slot": "Imagine if", "job": "Ask the room to imagine the experience from the other side.", "cue": "Imagine if it had been your child, your school, your instrument."}, {"slot": "Not guilt", "job": "Say the point is not guilt but recognition and opening a way forward.", "cue": "This is not about guilt. It is about seeing it clearly enough to fix."}, {"slot": "The way forward", "job": "Name practical steps and close with confidence.", "cue": "So here is where we start, and I think we can do it well."}], "teach": "At Redfern Park, launching the International Year for the World's Indigenous People, Keating said the starting point was an act of recognition. He then used a run of sentences beginning 'It was we who' to acknowledge dispossession, the taking of children and the exclusion, owning those acts in the first person plural rather than attributing them to distant others. He asked non-Indigenous Australians to imagine if it had been done to them, and he framed the task as one that need not be approached in guilt but in a spirit of shared opportunity. Don Watson drafted the speech.\n\nThe pronoun is the technique. 'They' allows the listener to stand aside; 'we' makes the listener part of the acknowledgement, which is uncomfortable and therefore memorable. The 'imagine if' step then converts acknowledgement from an abstraction to an experience. And the explicit refusal of guilt keeps the room from defensiveness, so it can move to action.\n\nJason's scaffold acknowledges the situation in a sentence and moves on. This formula makes acknowledgement the centre of the speech. It suits moments when an institution has failed some group and the room needs to hear the failure owned before any plan can be credible.\n\nThe plan arrives last and should be practical: a policy change, a resourcing shift, a named review. Because the acknowledgement has been real, the practical step lands as repair rather than public relations. Use the 'we' repetitions sparingly, three at most, and only where the institution genuinely did the thing.", "when": "Institutional apologies or recognition; when the department, school or association has excluded or failed a group; reconciliation and inclusion addresses.", "risk": "Hollow apology, or a 'we' that drags in people who had no part. Own only what the institution actually did, keep it specific, and attach a real remedy.", "model": {"room": "Music department staff meeting", "prompt": "An internal review found the specialist program's auditions had for years disadvantaged students from primary schools without instrumental programs. You address the staff.", "slots": ["I think how we respond to this review is a test of what kind of *department* we are.", "It was we who set an audition that rewarded two years of private lessons. It was we who scheduled it in a week when many primary schools had camp. It was we who kept the sight-reading test after we knew it measured privilege as much as potential.", "Imagine if your child had been the one with a good ear, a good rhythm, and no instrument at home, sitting in front of a panel asked to read a line she had never been *taught* to read.", "This is not about guilt. Every person here wanted the best students. It is about seeing the process clearly enough to fix it.", "So here is where we start. From next year, the sight-reading test goes. We add an aptitude task that needs no prior training. We run a second audition date in Term 3. And I will report the intake data to council each year, by feeder school. || I think we can do this *well*."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "Festival association AGM", "prompt": "The festival's adjudication guidelines have historically favoured large metropolitan bands. Address members, owning this and proposing a revised adjudication framework.", "seconds": 180}, {"room": "School council", "prompt": "The music program's concerts have repeatedly been scheduled on dates that conflict with major cultural and religious observances. Acknowledge this to council and propose a calendar protocol.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "Conference panel on inclusion in music education", "prompt": "Speak about how specialist music programs in general have excluded students with disability, using 'we', and propose two practical changes.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "NAIDOC Week assembly, music performance", "prompt": "Introduce a performance by First Nations students and a guest musician with a brief acknowledgement of how school music has historically overlooked First Nations music, and say what the department is doing.", "seconds": 90}]}, {"id": "gf-concession-reversal", "name": "Concession, then reversal", "who": "Elizabeth I", "source": "Speech to the troops at Tilbury, August 1588", "family": "speech", "gist": "Arrives among the people at risk, concedes the weakness others see in you, then reverses it into a stronger claim and commits yourself personally.", "steps": [{"slot": "Among you", "job": "Say you have come to stand with them rather than speak from safety.", "cue": "I wanted to say this in person, not in an email."}, {"slot": "Concede", "job": "Admit, briefly and without apology, the weakness others perceive.", "cue": "I know what people think: that a music department is the first thing to go."}, {"slot": "Reverse", "job": "Turn the concession into a stronger counter-claim.", "cue": "But what we have is something no budget line can measure."}, {"slot": "Commit self", "job": "Pledge your own effort or exposure alongside theirs.", "cue": "And I will be in the room for every one of these conversations."}, {"slot": "Promise", "job": "Promise the outcome or recognition they will share.", "cue": "And when this is done, everyone here will know what you did."}], "teach": "With the Armada in the Channel, Elizabeth went to the camp at Tilbury and, by the account later printed from Leonel Sharp's letter, told the troops she had come among them 'not for my recreation and disport' but to live or die with them. Her famous move is the concession and reversal: 'I know I have the body of a weak and feeble woman; but I have the heart and stomach of a king, and of a king of England too.' She then committed herself to take up arms and promised rewards. The surviving text was published decades later and its exact wording is debated, but the architecture has been taught as hers for centuries.\n\nThe concession disarms. By saying aloud what the listener privately thinks, the speaker shows she is not deceived about her position, and the reversal then gains force from the honesty that preceded it. It is an especially strong move for someone who is underestimated, because it names the underestimation and refuses it in one breath.\n\nJason's scaffold establishes standing by listing credentials. This formula establishes standing by first conceding a weakness. It suits moments where the room's doubt is about whether you, or your area, can carry the weight: the arts in a budget meeting, a teacher among academics, a small association among big institutions.\n\nThe practical proposal sits after the reversal and the personal commitment: once the room has accepted the stronger claim, the plan is what follows from it. Concede one thing only, and make the reversal concrete rather than rhetorical.", "when": "When your area or role is underestimated; rallying a small team before a contest; addressing a room that assumes the arts, or teachers, are soft.", "risk": "False modesty or a reversal that sounds like bragging. Concede something genuinely perceived, keep the reversal factual, and pair it with personal commitment rather than boasting.", "model": {"room": "School council, budget session", "prompt": "The council is weighing cuts across the school, and music is widely seen as an expensive luxury. You speak for the program.", "slots": ["I wanted to say this in the room, not in a submission, because the people affected are in the room.", "I know what many people think, and some have said it kindly: that a music program is a beautiful thing and the *first* thing to go. It is expensive, it is visible, and it does not appear in the NAPLAN tables.", "But here is what that view misses. This program is why many of our Year 7 families chose this school over their local one. It is what families name, unprompted, at enrolment interviews. It is not an ornament on the school; it is part of its *foundation*.", "And I will put my own time where my argument is. I will cost three alternative savings within the department by the next meeting, and I will sit with the business manager to test each one against next year's enrolments.", "If council holds the line on music this year, the families who chose us will know why, and so will the students who stay. || I am asking you to hold it."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "University academic committee", "prompt": "You are the only school teacher on a university committee that has been treating teacher input as anecdotal. Concede the point about research methods, then reverse it.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "Festival association, pitch to a corporate sponsor", "prompt": "Concede that the festival is small compared to the sponsor's other partnerships, then reverse that into the reason to fund it.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "Senior band before a national competition", "prompt": "The band is the smallest in its division. Speak to them backstage, concede it, reverse it, and commit yourself.", "seconds": 90}, {"room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee", "prompt": "Board members see a music course as a niche concern compared to English or Mathematics. Concede the enrolment figures and reverse them into the case for the course.", "seconds": 180}]}, {"id": "gf-turn-the-occasion", "name": "Turning the occasion against itself", "who": "Frederick Douglass", "source": "'What to the Slave is the Fourth of July?', Corinthian Hall, Rochester, 5 July 1852", "family": "speech", "gist": "Honours the occasion and its ideals sincerely, then turns those same ideals into an indictment of the present, and closes in hope that the ideals can still be met.", "steps": [{"slot": "Modest opening", "job": "Acknowledge the honour of being asked and the gap between speaker and occasion.", "cue": "I am grateful to be asked, and a little surprised."}, {"slot": "Honour the ideal", "job": "Praise the founders or the founding ideal genuinely and at some length.", "cue": "What this event stands for is admirable, and I mean that."}, {"slot": "The pivot", "job": "Turn with a pronoun shift: this is your celebration, and here is who it leaves out.", "cue": "But I have to ask who this celebration is for."}, {"slot": "The indictment", "job": "Measure the present against the ideal just praised, with specifics.", "cue": "By the standard we have just applauded, here is where we fall short."}, {"slot": "Hope", "job": "Close by affirming that the ideal is still within reach.", "cue": "I say this because I believe we can still live up to it."}], "teach": "Invited by the Rochester Ladies' Anti-Slavery Society to speak on the Fourth of July, Douglass began modestly and then praised the signers of the Declaration of Independence as brave and great men. Having established that sincerity, he pivoted with a pronoun: 'This Fourth of July is yours, not mine. You may rejoice, I must mourn.' He then used the occasion's own principles to indict the nation, with withering irony, and closed by saying he did not despair of the country, drawing hope from the Declaration and the tendencies of the age.\n\nThe structure is a trap built from the audience's own values. Because the praise is genuine and extended, the listener cannot dismiss the indictment as hostility to the ideal; the speaker is holding them to their own standard. The pivot on a single pronoun is an unforgettable moment of reframing, and the closing hope prevents the room from simply feeling attacked.\n\nJason's scaffold finds common ground and stays there. This formula finds common ground and then uses it as a lever. It is the formula for being invited to celebrate something whose celebration conceals a problem.\n\nThe practical step can sit at the end of the indictment or inside the hope: the ideal is reachable, and here is the specific change that would reach it. The praise must be sincere and well informed, or the turn will look like an ambush.", "when": "Invited speeches at celebrations, anniversaries or launches where the celebration leaves someone out; awards nights where the system that produced the winners needs scrutiny.", "risk": "It can read as ingratitude or ambush. Make the praise long and genuine, keep the indictment specific rather than sweeping, and finish in real hope with a real proposal.", "model": {"room": "Launch of a state arts education strategy", "prompt": "You are invited, as a teacher, to respond at the launch of a new state arts education strategy that focuses on flagship programs and says little about schools without music teachers.", "slots": ["I am grateful to be asked to respond tonight, and a little surprised that the person asked is a classroom teacher.", "Let me say first that this strategy is admirable. Its aim, that every young person in this state should *experience* the arts as a maker and not only as an audience, is the right aim, and the people who wrote it fought for it.", "But I have to ask who tonight's celebration is for. || For the flagship programs, it is a good night. For a student in a school with no music teacher, it is a launch they will never hear about.", "By the standard we have just applauded, every young person as a *maker*, we fall short in every public secondary school that has no qualified instrumental teacher at all, and there are too many of them. The strategy funds showcases; it does not fund teachers.", "I say this because I think we can still meet the standard we have set. One shared instrumental position per regional cluster, in the strategy's first year, would make tonight's aim true for students who are not in this room. || I would be glad to help cost it."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "Festival association, twentieth anniversary dinner", "prompt": "Asked to toast the festival, honour its founding ideal of open access and then point out that entry fees have quietly excluded many low-fee schools.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "School music awards night", "prompt": "Asked to give the closing address, celebrate the program's excellence and then point to the students who never get to audition, and what the school could do.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "University graduation, guest address", "prompt": "Invited to address music education graduates, honour the profession's ideal and then name the gap in regional teacher supply, closing with hope.", "seconds": 180}, {"room": "SCSA function marking a syllabus anniversary", "prompt": "Asked to say a few words at a function celebrating ten years of the current music syllabus, honour it, then turn to what it fails to recognise in contemporary and non-Western musics.", "seconds": 90}]}, {"id": "gf-motivated-sequence", "name": "The motivated sequence", "who": "Alan H. Monroe", "source": "Principles and Types of Speech, 1935", "family": "speech", "gist": "Moves a listener from attention through felt need to a solution, then makes them see the future with and without it, and asks for one specific action.", "steps": [{"slot": "Attention", "job": "Open with something concrete that makes the room look up.", "cue": "Last Tuesday, at 3.40 pm, a Year 8 cellist sat alone in the foyer."}, {"slot": "Need", "job": "Show the problem is real, widespread and affects them.", "cue": "She is not the only one, and here is why it matters to us."}, {"slot": "Satisfaction", "job": "Present the solution and show how it meets the need.", "cue": "What fixes this is simple, and it is already working elsewhere."}, {"slot": "Visualisation", "job": "Picture the future with the solution, and briefly without it.", "cue": "Picture next year with this in place, and then without it."}, {"slot": "Action", "job": "Ask for one specific, easy action now.", "cue": "So what I need tonight is one thing."}], "teach": "Alan Monroe, who taught speech at Purdue, set out the motivated sequence in Principles and Types of Speech in 1935, drawing on John Dewey's account of reflective thinking and on the psychology of persuasion of his day. The five steps are attention, need, satisfaction, visualisation and action. It became a staple of speech teaching in the United States and has been used for decades in advertising, public health campaigns and sales.\n\nIt is included here not because it is grand but because it is the cleanest proposal architecture in the set. Each step answers the question the listener is silently asking: why should I listen, why should I care, what would fix it, what would that look like, what do you want from me. The visualisation step is its distinctive contribution: most proposals jump from solution to request, and miss the moment where the listener sees the future and wants it.\n\nJason's scaffold is a committee contribution designed to build the speaker's credit. The motivated sequence is designed to produce a decision. It is less suited to a formal committee where proposals are debated, and more suited to a pitch, a parent meeting, a funding panel or a short address that ends in a request.\n\nFor a practical problem-solver the risk is to compress need and visualisation and spend the time on the solution's details. Resist that. The solution can be one sentence if the need is felt and the future is seen.", "when": "Pitches, parent meetings, sponsorship requests, short addresses that end in an ask; any time you need a decision or a yes rather than discussion.", "risk": "It can sound like a sales pitch, especially the visualisation. Keep the attention step factual, the picture modest and specific, and the ask small and clear.", "model": {"room": "Parent association meeting", "prompt": "You want the parent association to fund a supervised after-school practice room, three afternoons a week.", "slots": ["Last Tuesday, at twenty to four, I found a Year 8 cellist sitting in the foyer with her case, waiting an hour and a half for a lift. She had nowhere to *play*.", "She is not the only one. On any afternoon about thirty music students wait on site for parents or buses. Most of them live in flats or share rooms, and school is the only place they can practise without disturbing anyone. That time is going to waste, every day.", "What fixes this is simple. Open M1.3 from 3.15 to 5.00, three afternoons a week, with a paid supervisor. It costs about four thousand dollars a year. Two other specialist schools already do it.", "Picture next year with this in place: thirty students getting four extra hours of practice a week, and an hour and a half of foyer time turned into the hour that makes the *difference* at audition. || Without it, nothing changes, and the foyer stays full.", "So what I need tonight is one thing. A vote to fund a two-term trial from the association's equipment budget. I will report the attendance figures at the end of Term 2."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "Corporate sponsorship meeting", "prompt": "Pitch to a local business for sponsorship of the festival's regional travel fund, using all five steps and ending with a specific dollar request.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "Year 7 parent evening", "prompt": "Persuade parents to sign their children up for the optional Saturday ensemble program, which has low uptake.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "Principal's office", "prompt": "Ask the principal to approve a staff member's release to attend a national music education conference, using the motivated sequence.", "seconds": 90}, {"room": "School council", "prompt": "Ask the council to endorse a submission to the Department for an acoustic upgrade of the music rehearsal space, which currently exceeds safe noise levels.", "seconds": 180}]}, {"id": "gf-principled", "name": "Principled negotiation", "who": "Roger Fisher and William Ury (Harvard Negotiation Project)", "source": "Getting to Yes, Fisher and Ury, 1981", "family": "meeting", "gist": "Moves a stuck dispute off positions and onto interests, options and fair standards, so agreement becomes a shared piece of work rather than a concession.", "steps": [{"slot": "People apart", "job": "Separate the relationship from the problem so nobody has to lose face to agree.", "cue": "Can I take the personalities out of this for a moment, because I think we all want the same thing here."}, {"slot": "Interests", "job": "Name what each party actually needs beneath the position they have stated.", "cue": "What I hear underneath the two positions is this: one side needs certainty, the other needs room."}, {"slot": "Options", "job": "Generate more than one way through before anyone has to choose.", "cue": "Before we decide, can we put three options on the table rather than two?"}, {"slot": "Criteria", "job": "Propose an objective standard the decision can be measured against.", "cue": "Could we agree the test first, and then see which option meets it?"}], "teach": "Fisher and Ury built the method out of the Harvard Negotiation Project's study of what worked in labour, diplomatic and commercial disputes. Their claim was that positional bargaining, where each side states a demand and then trades away from it, produces poor agreements and damaged relationships. They replaced it with four moves: separate the people from the problem, focus on interests rather than positions, invent options for mutual gain, and insist on objective criteria.\n\nIt works on an educated room because it gives everyone a dignified exit. Academics and senior officers will rarely abandon a stated position in public, but they will happily agree that their underlying interest has been met by a better option. The criteria step is the quiet power move: once the room has agreed what a good decision looks like, the decision tends to make itself, and the person who proposed the test has shaped the outcome.\n\nJason's scaffold is a speech; this is a mediation. Thanks, acknowledgement and standing establish you as worth hearing. Principled negotiation establishes you as the person who can get two camps out of a ditch, which in a deadlocked committee is more valuable than eloquence. It suits Dijon's temperament, because it is analytic and practical rather than performative.\n\nThe proposal travels inside the options step. Do not lead with your fix. Name the interests first so both camps feel understood, offer your solution as one of three options, then propose a criterion that your option happens to meet best. The room chooses it, and it is now theirs.", "when": "A two-camp deadlock: a timetable clash between ensembles, a funding split on a festival board, a committee divided between a teacher-workload position and an assessment-rigour position.", "risk": "It can sound like a management seminar if the four labels are announced. Never name the method; just do the moves, in plain language, and keep the options genuinely open.", "model": {"room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "The board is split: two members want to cut the junior division to save venue costs, two want to raise entry fees instead. It has gone back and forth for twenty minutes.", "slots": ["Can I step back from the two positions for a moment? | Nobody at this table wants a smaller festival or a festival that poorer schools can't afford. We're arguing about *means*, not about each other.", "As I hear it, the Treasurer's side needs the venue line to come down by roughly a quarter, and needs that certain before we sign the contract in March. The other side needs the junior division protected, because that's our pipeline for the next decade. || Those two interests aren't actually in conflict. Only the two proposals are.", "So let me put three options on the table rather than two. One, cut the junior division. Two, raise fees across the board. Three, move the junior division to a single school-hosted day at no venue cost, with a modest fee rise only for the senior division, where schools have the budgets.", "Before we pick, could we agree the *test*? I'd suggest two criteria: the venue saving has to be confirmed before March, and no school's total cost rises by more than the CPI. If we agree that test, I think the choice becomes fairly clear, and I'm happy to cost all three against it for the next meeting."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "School council", "prompt": "Parent representatives want homework capped school-wide; the teaching staff representatives want each learning area to set its own expectations. The Principal has asked you, as chair, to find a way through before the item is deferred again.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "Principal's office", "prompt": "The Principal wants Music rehearsals moved out of period 1 to free a teaching space; you need the space for the senior wind ensemble. Negotiate an outcome without either of you giving ground on principle.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "prompt": "Half the committee wants an externally set performance exam; the other half wants school-based assessment with moderation. The executive officer wants a recommendation today. Move the room off positions.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "Department meeting", "prompt": "Two instrumental staff both want the Friday afternoon slot for their ensemble. Both have reasonable cases and it is becoming personal.", "seconds": 90}]}, {"id": "gf-batna", "name": "The walk-away in hand", "who": "Roger Fisher and William Ury", "source": "Getting to Yes, Fisher and Ury, 1981 (the BATNA)", "family": "meeting", "gist": "You walk into the room knowing your best alternative to a negotiated agreement, and you let it set your floor calmly rather than as a threat.", "steps": [{"slot": "List alternatives", "job": "Before the meeting, write down what you will do if no agreement is reached.", "cue": "(Before the meeting) If this room says no, what exactly will I do on Monday?"}, {"slot": "Develop the best", "job": "Turn the most promising alternative into something real and costed.", "cue": "(Before the meeting) What would make that alternative genuinely workable rather than a bluff?"}, {"slot": "Read theirs", "job": "Estimate the other side's alternative, so you know how much they need you.", "cue": "It might help to think about what happens on their side if we don't agree today."}, {"slot": "State the floor", "job": "In the room, make your floor known plainly, as information rather than pressure.", "cue": "I should be transparent about where I sit: below this point, I'd have to pursue another path."}, {"slot": "Invite better", "job": "Return the room to finding an agreement that beats both alternatives.", "cue": "I'd much rather we found something here that's better for both of us than that."}], "teach": "Fisher and Ury introduced the BATNA, the Best Alternative To a Negotiated Agreement, as the answer to a negotiator's oldest weakness: not knowing when to walk away. Their argument was that a fixed bottom line is brittle, and that the real measure of any offer is what you would do without it. They advised inventing a list of alternatives, developing the most promising into something practical, and choosing the best as your standard. They also stressed that the stronger your alternative, the more power you have in the room, whatever the formal hierarchy.\n\nMost of this formula happens before the meeting, which is why it suits an associative thinker who prepares well and then struggles to hold a line live. If Dijon has already costed his alternative, he does not need to argue hard in the room. He can be gracious, because he is not desperate. Educated rooms read that composure instantly, and it changes how seriously they take his proposal.\n\nJason's scaffold earns attention through speech craft. The walk-away earns it through leverage. It is not a speaking structure so much as a posture that shapes every sentence: fewer hedges, fewer qualifications, no pleading. The spoken part is short: the floor, stated once, without heat, followed immediately by an invitation to do better.\n\nThe practical problem-solver carries his proposal by making his alternative concrete enough that the room can see it would happen. A proposal backed by a credible, unshowy alternative is far more likely to be adopted than one backed by passion.", "when": "A negotiation with someone who holds formal power over you: a role, workload or resourcing conversation with a principal, a venue or sponsor negotiation for the festival, a contract with an external provider.", "risk": "Stated too early or too forcefully it sounds like an ultimatum and hardens the room. State it once, late, as information, and never bluff: if you would not really do it, it is not your BATNA.", "model": {"room": "Principal's office", "prompt": "The Principal proposes cutting the Music department's administrative allocation next year. You have already costed a fallback: handing the festival entries and the instrumental hire scheme back to central administration.", "slots": ["(Prepared beforehand, not spoken) If the allocation goes, I stop running instrument hire and festival entries from Music and hand them to the front office from Term 1.", "(Prepared beforehand, not spoken) I've checked with the business manager what that would take: roughly a day a week of an administrative officer's time, plus the hire database.", "I understand the pressure on the staffing budget, and I don't want to make it harder. | It might help to look at what happens on the school's side if the allocation simply goes. The work doesn't disappear. Instrument hire still has to be invoiced, festival entries still have to go in by the deadline.", "So I should be transparent about where I sit. || If the allocation is cut, I'd have to hand both of those back to administration from Term 1. I've spoken to the business manager, and it's about a day a week of an AO's time. That isn't a threat; it's simply where the *work* would land.", "I'd much rather we found something better for both of us. | One option: keep half the allocation, and I move the hire scheme onto the existing online payments system, which saves the front office time as well. Could we look at that before the staffing plan is locked in?"]}, "prompts": [{"room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "The usual venue has raised its hire fee by a third for next year and says the price is final. You have identified a university concert hall as an alternative. Speak to the board about how to approach the venue manager.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "Principal's office", "prompt": "You are asking for your TIC role to be reclassified at Level 3 with clear authority. The Principal is sympathetic but says the timing is wrong. You have a realistic alternative if it is declined. Make your case.", "seconds": 180}, {"room": "Arts funding panel", "prompt": "A funder offers half the grant you applied for, on condition you drop the regional workshop component. You can run a reduced program without them. Respond in the negotiation call.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "School council", "prompt": "A private tutoring company wants to run paid lessons on site and offers the school a small fee. The council is tempted. As chair, set out the alternatives so the council negotiates rather than just accepts.", "seconds": 90}]}, {"id": "gf-pyramid", "name": "The answer-first pyramid", "who": "Barbara Minto", "source": "The Pyramid Principle, Barbara Minto, developed at McKinsey and Company", "family": "meeting", "gist": "Sets up the shared situation, names what has changed and the question it raises, gives the answer first, then supports it with three grouped reasons.", "steps": [{"slot": "Situation", "job": "State the stable facts everyone already accepts.", "cue": "We all know where we stand on this."}, {"slot": "Complication", "job": "Name what has changed or gone wrong.", "cue": "What's changed is this."}, {"slot": "Question", "job": "Say aloud the question the complication raises.", "cue": "So the question for us is simply this."}, {"slot": "Answer", "job": "Give your recommendation in one sentence, before any reasoning.", "cue": "My answer is that we should..."}, {"slot": "Three supports", "job": "Give no more than three grouped reasons, in a stated order.", "cue": "Three reasons, briefly."}], "teach": "Barbara Minto, one of the first women hired as a consultant by McKinsey, developed the Pyramid Principle to fix the way clever people write and speak: they recount their thinking in the order they did it, so the conclusion comes last, when the reader is tired. She inverted it. The governing idea sits at the top, supported by a small number of grouped arguments, each of which can be supported in turn. Her introduction, situation, complication, question, exists to bring the listener to the exact point where they want the answer.\n\nFor Dijon, this is the cure for the tangent. An associative mind has ten good points and delivers them in the order they arrived. The pyramid forces a single governing sentence, said early, which the room can hold onto while the reasons arrive. Senior officers and academics, who sit through hours of meandering contribution, will listen to the person who answers first and stops after three.\n\nJason's scaffold front-loads relationship: thanks, acknowledgement, standing. The pyramid front-loads the answer. The two can be combined, a single line of thanks then straight into situation, but the pyramid is the formula for when the room is tired, time is short, or the chair has asked a direct question. It reads as executive, not ceremonial.\n\nThe practical proposal is the answer at the apex. That is the whole point: the solution is not buried in slot six, it is the fourth sentence he says. The three supports should be grouped and parallel, for instance one on students, one on workload, one on cost, rather than a list of whatever came to mind.", "when": "When the chair asks for his view directly, when time is short, or when the room has drifted and needs one clear recommendation to react to.", "risk": "It can sound clipped or presumptuous if the situation is skipped. Take one honest sentence for the situation so the room recognises its own understanding before you give the answer.", "model": {"room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee", "prompt": "The Board has rejected the committee's draft course, citing concerns about the reliability of the performance assessment. The chair asks what the committee should do next.", "slots": ["We've spent two years building a course that this committee, and the teachers we consulted across the state, believe in. The syllabus has been through two rounds of consultation and the feedback on content was strong.", "What's changed is that the Board has told us the performance assessment isn't *reliable* enough to stand alongside the written exam.", "So the question for us is narrow. | Not whether the course is right, but how we make the performance component defensible to the Board.", "My answer is that we resubmit with a moderated performance component, set against published marking keys, rather than redesigning the course. ||", "Three reasons, briefly. First, reliability is the Board's stated objection, and moderation answers reliability directly. Second, it protects the two years of syllabus work, which nobody has criticised. Third, it's achievable: other learning areas already moderate practical work at scale, so the model exists and the cost is known. || I'd suggest a small working group of three, with one member from each sector, draft the marking keys and a moderation timeline for the next meeting, so that we go back to the Board with an answer rather than an apology."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "School council", "prompt": "The chair asks your view on whether the school should apply for a new specialist program in another learning area, which would compete with Music for rooms and enrolments. Give your answer first.", "seconds": 90}, {"room": "University academic committee", "prompt": "The committee is reviewing entry auditions for a performance degree. The convenor turns to you and asks what the schools' perspective is on moving to recorded auditions only.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "Entries are down this year and the board has spent half an hour on theories. The president asks what you, as Treasurer, would recommend.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "Department meeting", "prompt": "Staff have drifted into a long discussion about whether to keep the end-of-year concert format. You need a decision. Put a clear recommendation on the table.", "seconds": 90}]}, {"id": "gf-rapoport", "name": "Rapoport's rules", "who": "Anatol Rapoport, as popularised by Daniel Dennett", "source": "Intuition Pumps and Other Tools for Thinking, Daniel Dennett, 2013", "family": "meeting", "gist": "Before criticising a view, restate it better than its owner did, name what you agree with, say what you learned, and only then disagree.", "steps": [{"slot": "Restate", "job": "Re-express the other person's position so fairly they would wish they had put it that way.", "cue": "Let me check I have this right, because I think it's a strong argument."}, {"slot": "Agree", "job": "Name the specific points where you genuinely agree.", "cue": "I agree with two things in that completely."}, {"slot": "Learned", "job": "Say what you have learned from their view.", "cue": "And I hadn't considered something until just now."}, {"slot": "Rebut", "job": "Only then make your criticism, narrowly and precisely.", "cue": "Where I part company is one specific point."}], "teach": "Daniel Dennett set out these rules in Intuition Pumps and Other Tools for Thinking, crediting the game theorist Anatol Rapoport. The sequence is: re-express your target's position so clearly and fairly that they would thank you for it; list any points of agreement, especially if they are not widely held; mention anything you have learned from them; and only then say a word of rebuttal or criticism. Dennett's point was practical: your opponent is now a receptive audience, because you have shown that you understand them and are not simply attacking.\n\nIn a room of people who argue for a living, the restatement is the move that wins respect. Most contributors respond to a caricature of the previous speaker. When someone restates a colleague's view better than the colleague did, the room recognises intellectual seriousness immediately, and the colleague is disarmed. For a contributor who is not listened to, it is also a way of borrowing attention: the room is already invested in the previous speaker's argument, and you are speaking about it.\n\nJason's scaffold thanks people for what they did. Rapoport's rules thank them, implicitly, for what they think, by taking it seriously. The two are related but different: Jason's thanks are social; this restatement is intellectual. It is the formula for disagreeing with someone more senior or more fluent without appearing to compete with them.\n\nThe proposal arrives inside the rebuttal, which should be narrow. Do not disagree with everything. Isolate the one point your practical solution addresses, and present the solution as the fix for that point, so it reads as a refinement of their argument rather than a rival to it.", "when": "When you need to disagree with a fluent or senior speaker, especially one the room admires, without making it a contest.", "risk": "If the restatement is long, it becomes a second copy of their speech and the room loses patience. Keep it to two or three sentences, and make sure the agreement is genuine, or it reads as flattery.", "model": {"room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee (hybrid)", "prompt": "A university academic online has argued, at length and well, that the committee should abandon the rejected course and adopt another state's existing syllabus. You think that would waste the work and miss the Board's actual objection.", "slots": ["Let me check I have the Professor's argument right, because I think it's a strong one. | The Board has said no once; another state's syllabus has already survived scrutiny; adopting it gives the Board a *precedent* it can rely on, and saves us another two years.", "I agree with two things in that completely. Speed matters, because every year we delay is a cohort of students on the old course. And the Board does respond to precedent.", "And I hadn't considered until just now how much the Board might value a model it has already seen working elsewhere. That's a real insight.", "Where I part company is one specific point. || The Board's objection was to the reliability of our performance assessment, not to our syllabus content. Adopting another state's syllabus replaces the part nobody criticised and may not fix the part they did. So my suggestion is to take the Professor's insight about precedent and apply it narrowly: we borrow that state's moderation model for performance, and keep our own content. We get the precedent and keep the two years of work."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "University academic committee", "prompt": "A senior academic has argued persuasively that ensemble credit should be removed from the first-year degree to make room for theory. You think ensemble is essential. Respond to them.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "School council", "prompt": "A parent representative, articulate and well respected, argues that the specialist music program is elitist and should be opened to all students without audition. Respond as a council member, not the chair.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "A long-serving board member argues the festival should drop adjudicated awards because they discourage weaker schools. You think the awards drive standards.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "Principal's office", "prompt": "A deputy principal argues that Music should share its rehearsal rooms with Drama in exchange for access to the theatre. You see the logic but the acoustics would wreck rehearsals.", "seconds": 90}]}, {"id": "gf-lead-back", "name": "Leading from the back", "who": "Nelson Mandela, describing the regent Jongintaba", "source": "Long Walk to Freedom, Nelson Mandela, 1994", "family": "meeting", "gist": "The leader opens the floor, listens without signalling a view, speaks last, and draws the room's contributions into a consensus that everyone recognises as theirs.", "steps": [{"slot": "Open the floor", "job": "Invite everyone to speak, including those who usually don't.", "cue": "Before I say anything, I'd like to hear from everyone, and especially from those closest to it."}, {"slot": "Listen", "job": "Hear each view without signalling agreement or disagreement.", "cue": "Thank you. | Who haven't we heard from yet?"}, {"slot": "Speak last", "job": "Mark the shift from listening to synthesis.", "cue": "Let me try to draw together what I've heard."}, {"slot": "Synthesise", "job": "Name the contributions, attributed, and find the line that runs through them.", "cue": "What runs through everything that's been said is this."}, {"slot": "Test consensus", "job": "Propose the consensus and check that the room owns it.", "cue": "Is that a fair reading of where the room is?"}], "teach": "In Long Walk to Freedom, Mandela describes the tribal meetings he watched as a young man at the Great Place, where the regent Jongintaba presided. Anyone could speak, at length, and the regent listened without comment. Only at the end did he speak, summarising what had been said and seeking a consensus among the views. Mandela draws from this his image of the leader as a shepherd who stays behind the flock and lets the most nimble go ahead, so that the others follow without realising they are being directed. He says he applied the lesson throughout his life.\n\nThis works on an expert room because experts dislike being told and like being heard. The person who speaks last has heard everything, can attribute each good idea to its owner, and can build the conclusion out of other people's material. The room then experiences the decision as its own. It is also a remedy for being unheard: whoever speaks last, well, is the one whose words are carried into the minutes.\n\nJason's scaffold is a contribution among contributions. This is the opposite: Dijon does not compete for airtime at all. He waits, collects, and closes. For a chair, it is the classic posture. For a member, it can be used by holding back until the discussion has run, then offering the synthesis, which the chair will often adopt.\n\nThe proposal is carried inside the synthesis. If Dijon's practical solution is sound, others will usually have raised parts of it. He names them, attributes them, joins them, and adds the missing piece. The proposal arrives as the room's conclusion, with his own contribution as the binding element.", "when": "When chairing a contested item, or as a member when the discussion has many voices and no shape, and you want your solution to be the one the room adopts.", "risk": "It fails if the synthesis is really your own view dressed up, because the room will notice its contributions were bent. Attribute accurately, and include at least one point you did not agree with.", "model": {"room": "School council (Dijon in the chair)", "prompt": "The council is discussing whether to introduce a voluntary contribution for co-curricular music. Views are divided and several members have not spoken.", "slots": ["Before I say anything, I'd like to hear from everyone on this, and particularly from the parent members, because it lands on families first.", "(After each speaker) Thank you. | (Later) Who haven't we heard from? I'd value the community member's view in particular.", "Let me try to draw together what I've heard. ||", "What runs through everything is *fairness*. The P&C representative is worried that a contribution excludes families who can't pay. The staff member pointed out that the program already depends on a few families who quietly pay more than their share. And our community member made the point that a contribution only works if it's genuinely voluntary and no child ever knows who paid. Those three points aren't in conflict. | They describe a design: a voluntary contribution, invoiced discreetly, with a hardship fund that covers anyone who asks, no questions, and no difference in access.", "Is that a fair reading of where the council is? | If so, I'd propose we ask the business manager to model it on those three conditions and bring it back next meeting, with the hardship fund costed separately."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "Department meeting (Dijon in the chair)", "prompt": "Staff disagree about whether to reduce the number of concerts next year. Run the item so that every teacher speaks, then close it with a synthesis.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee", "prompt": "After the Board's rejection, twelve members have given their reactions, in no particular order. The chair looks around for someone to pull it together. Speak last, and synthesise.", "seconds": 180}, {"room": "Festival association board (member)", "prompt": "The board has discussed a rebrand of the festival for forty minutes. Six people have spoken; nothing has been decided. Offer the synthesis.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "Parent body", "prompt": "At a parent music support group meeting, parents have raised many concerns about the tour to the eastern states: cost, supervision, timing. Draw them together and propose a way forward.", "seconds": 120}]}, {"id": "gf-third-speaker", "name": "The thematic rebuttal", "who": "The third speaker in competitive debating", "source": "Australian schools and Australs debating; World Schools Debating Championships format", "family": "meeting", "gist": "Instead of answering the previous speakers point by point, organises the whole discussion into two or three central clashes and shows, clash by clash, why one approach wins.", "steps": [{"slot": "Name the clashes", "job": "Tell the room the discussion comes down to two or three questions.", "cue": "I think this whole discussion comes down to three questions."}, {"slot": "Clash one", "job": "Set out what each side has said on the first question, and why one answer is stronger.", "cue": "The first question is... On one side we've heard... but..."}, {"slot": "Clash two", "job": "Do the same for the second question, building on the first.", "cue": "The second question is..."}, {"slot": "Clash three", "job": "Close the hardest question last.", "cue": "And the third, which I think is the real one..."}, {"slot": "Summary", "job": "Restate the result of each clash and what follows from them together.", "cue": "So, on all three questions, the case points the same way."}], "teach": "In Australian schools debating and in the Australs and World Schools formats, the third speaker's job is not to add new arguments but to rebut and summarise. Weak third speakers go through the opposition's points one at a time. Strong ones identify the two or three central clashes of the debate, the questions on which the outcome really turns, and organise their whole speech around them, showing for each what both sides said and why their side wins it. Adjudicators reward it because it shows command of the whole debate, not just a reply to the last speaker.\n\nThis is the structure Jason uses when he 'concludes like a third debater', and it is why he is remembered. Thematic rebuttal makes the speaker sound like the person who understands the discussion best, because they have organised everyone else's contributions into a shape. An educated room, which has itself been struggling to see the shape, is grateful.\n\nJason's scaffold contains a third-speaker close as its final slot. This formula is the third speaker's whole speech: the entire contribution is structure, with the clashes as the spine. It is best used late, after many people have spoken, and it is more combative than leading from the back, because it adjudicates rather than synthesises.\n\nThe practical proposal sits at the end of the last clash. Choose the clashes so that the third one is the question your solution answers. The first two clear the ground; the third makes the solution feel like the inevitable conclusion of the debate.", "when": "Late in a contested discussion, when several people have spoken and the room needs someone to say what it is really arguing about.", "risk": "It sounds combative or like a schoolroom exercise if the language of 'sides' is overplayed, or if three clashes become five. Keep to three, call them 'questions', and be scrupulously fair in stating the view you are answering.", "model": {"room": "University academic committee", "prompt": "The committee has debated for an hour whether to accept a new pathway that admits school leavers to the music degree on portfolio rather than ATAR. Opinion is split.", "slots": ["I think this whole discussion comes down to three *questions*. | Whether the pathway lowers standards, whether it's fair to students who sat ATAR, and whether we can run it well.", "The first question is standards. We've heard the concern that portfolio entry admits weaker students. But every candidate on this pathway still passes the audition, which is the standard that actually predicts success in a performance degree. So on standards, the concern is real, but it's answered.", "The second question is fairness. It's been said that ATAR students might feel disadvantaged. But the pathway doesn't take places from them; it widens the pool for a degree that's under-subscribed. Fairness cuts the other way: the students it reaches are often the ones the ATAR system already misses.", "And the third, which I think is the real one, is *capacity*. Can we assess portfolios consistently? || This is where I'd make a proposal. Two assessors per portfolio, a published rubric, and a review after the first intake, reported back to this committee.", "So on all three questions, the case points the same way. Standards are protected by the audition, fairness favours the pathway, and capacity can be built. I'd move that we approve it for one intake, subject to that review."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "prompt": "After an hour of discussion on whether to include popular music as a compulsory context in the Year 11 course, the chair asks for final contributions. Organise the debate into its central questions.", "seconds": 180}, {"room": "School council", "prompt": "The council has debated a new mobile phone policy at length. Parents, staff and student representatives have all spoken. Offer a thematic summary that resolves it.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "The board has argued about whether to move the festival from August to May. Several views, all reasonable. Give the third speaker's summary and land on a recommendation.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "Department meeting", "prompt": "Staff have argued about whether to stream Year 8 classes by ability. Draw the debate into its clashes and land it.", "seconds": 120}]}, {"id": "gf-socratic", "name": "The Socratic sequence", "who": "Socrates, as portrayed by Plato", "source": "Plato's dialogues, for example the Euthyphro and the Meno, early fourth century BC", "family": "meeting", "gist": "Moves a committee by questions rather than assertions: ask for the definition, test it against a case, expose the tension, and invite the room to revise its own position.", "steps": [{"slot": "Define", "job": "Ask the room what it means by the key term.", "cue": "Can I ask a genuine question: what do we mean by that word here?"}, {"slot": "Instance", "job": "Bring a concrete case and ask whether the definition fits it.", "cue": "So how would that apply to a case like this one?"}, {"slot": "Tension", "job": "Show, gently, that the definition and the case do not fit together.", "cue": "Then I'm a little stuck, because those two things don't sit together."}, {"slot": "Revise", "job": "Invite the room to revise its own definition, rather than offering yours.", "cue": "How would we need to put it so that it covers both?"}], "teach": "In Plato's early dialogues Socrates professes not to know, and asks his interlocutor to define a term they use confidently: piety in the Euthyphro, virtue in the Meno, courage in the Laches. He then tests the definition against cases until it collapses, leaving the interlocutor in aporia, the productive puzzlement that comes before better thinking. The method is the elenchus, examination by question. It is pre-modern in origin and was not always comfortable for its targets, but it has been the core of tutorial and seminar teaching for centuries.\n\nIn a committee, assertions invite counter-assertions, but questions have to be answered. A well-placed question forces the room to examine a word it has been using loosely, such as 'rigour', 'equity' or 'excellence'. Academics respect a precise question more than any speech, because it is their own method. And it is the safest way to move a room when you are not the most senior person in it: you are not contradicting anyone, you are asking for help.\n\nJason's scaffold is declarative. The Socratic sequence is interrogative: Dijon makes almost no claims of his own until the end. It is the formula for an unheard contributor, because a question addressed to the room demands a response, and the room's own revised answer then carries his point.\n\nThe practical proposal emerges as the revision. Choose the key term on which the problem turns, ask the questions that reveal the gap, and when the room reaches for a better definition, offer your solution as the practical form of the definition the room has just agreed.", "when": "When a committee is using a key term loosely and heading for a poor decision, and you are not senior enough to simply contradict it.", "risk": "It becomes patronising or a cross-examination if pressed too hard on an individual. Address the questions to the room, not one person, and genuinely want the answer.", "model": {"room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "prompt": "The committee is about to recommend that the performance component be reduced 'to improve rigour', because written exams are seen as more rigorous.", "slots": ["Can I ask a genuine question before we vote? | What do we mean by *rigour* here? I think we've been using it to mean 'harder to fake', and I want to check that's right.", "Because if that's what we mean, how would it apply to a student who scores well on the written paper by memorising set analyses, and then can't play the opening of the set work at the concert? On paper, that's rigorous. In practice, I'm not sure it is.", "So I'm a little stuck. || If rigour means 'harder to fake', a live performance in front of two examiners is arguably the *least* fakeable thing we assess. Yet we're about to cut it in the name of rigour.", "How would we need to put it so that it covers both cases? | Perhaps rigour means valid, reliable and hard to game. If the committee agrees with that, then the fix isn't to cut performance; it's to make it reliable, with two examiners, published criteria and recorded moderation samples. I'd be glad to draft that as our recommendation instead."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "School council", "prompt": "The council is about to approve a new policy on 'excellence' that would restrict ensemble entry to students above a certain grade. Ask the questions that expose the problem.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "University academic committee", "prompt": "The committee wants to cut contact hours in first year 'for efficiency'. You suspect efficiency is being confused with cost. Move the committee by questions.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "The board is about to adopt a new 'inclusion' policy that would remove the top division. Ask what inclusion means and test it.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "Department meeting", "prompt": "A colleague proposes cutting the theory component of Year 9 because students find it 'not engaging'. Use questions to examine what engagement means.", "seconds": 90}]}, {"id": "gf-burke", "name": "Judgement, not mandate", "who": "Edmund Burke", "source": "Speech to the Electors of Bristol, 3 November 1774", "family": "meeting", "gist": "When a body expects you to rubber-stamp a position, you honour its wishes, then explain that you owe it your judgement rather than your obedience, and give that judgement openly.", "steps": [{"slot": "Honour them", "job": "Acknowledge, sincerely, the weight of the wishes of those you represent.", "cue": "I take the view of the people I represent very seriously, and I want to say that first."}, {"slot": "The duty", "job": "Distinguish your role: you are there to deliberate, not to carry instructions.", "cue": "But I don't think I'm here to carry a vote; I think I'm here to bring my judgement."}, {"slot": "The whole", "job": "Point to the interest of the whole body over any part of it.", "cue": "This body serves the whole, not any one part of it, including mine."}, {"slot": "My judgement", "job": "Give your actual view, with reasons, openly.", "cue": "So here is my honest judgement, and why."}, {"slot": "Accountability", "job": "Accept that you answer for it.", "cue": "And if I've got it wrong, I'll answer for that."}], "teach": "Edmund Burke, just elected as one of Bristol's Members of Parliament, addressed his electors on 3 November 1774. His fellow Member had promised to follow the voters' instructions. Burke declined to. He said a representative owed his constituents 'not his industry only, but his judgment', and that he betrayed them if he sacrificed it to their opinion. Parliament, he argued, was not a congress of ambassadors from hostile interests but 'a deliberative assembly of one nation', with one interest, that of the whole. He paid for this: in 1780, having taken positions on Irish trade and Catholic relief that many Bristol electors opposed, he withdrew from the poll.\n\nThe formula is for a specific and common moment: a body expects you to carry its view into a committee, or a committee expects you to approve something without deliberating. Burke's structure lets you decline without contempt. It begins by honouring the people whose view you are departing from, then makes an argument about the nature of the role, not about them, and only then gives your view.\n\nJason's scaffold seeks agreement. This seeks respect for disagreement. It is the formula for the representative's dilemma: a stakeholder representative on an SCSA committee, a staff member on a council, a treasurer on a board. In each, the room is helped by someone who will say what they actually think, and Burke shows how to do it with dignity.\n\nThe practical proposal sits in 'my judgement'. Burke's architecture earns the right to make it, because by then the room has heard you acknowledge their position and explain why you owe them your thinking. A practical solution offered in this frame reads as principled, not oppositional.", "when": "When a group you represent, or the room itself, expects you to rubber-stamp: approving a budget without scrutiny, endorsing a policy because the Principal wants it, voting as a sector bloc.", "risk": "It can sound pompous or self-regarding if the duty is overstated. Keep the honouring sincere and the principle brief; the weight should fall on the reasoning, not on your conscience.", "model": {"room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "The board has been presented with next year's budget and asked to approve it as a formality. As Treasurer, you believe the projected sponsorship income is unrealistic.", "slots": ["I want to say first that I know a great deal of work has gone into this budget, and that the president and the events team want it settled tonight so they can book the venue. | That matters, and I respect it.", "But I don't think I'm on this board to carry a vote. I think the members elected me as Treasurer to bring my *judgement* to exactly this kind of decision.", "And this board serves the whole festival, every school that enters and every student who plays, not any one committee within it, including mine.", "So here is my honest judgement. || The sponsorship line assumes a twenty per cent increase, and none of our current sponsors has committed to that. If it falls short, we'll be running a deficit by Term 3. My proposal: we approve the budget tonight, so the venue is secured, but with the sponsorship line at last year's actual figure, and a contingency we release only when new sponsorship is confirmed in writing.", "If I've got that wrong and the sponsorship arrives, I'll be the first to say so at the next meeting. But I'd rather answer for caution than for a deficit."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee", "prompt": "Colleagues from the public school sector have asked you to vote against a change because it adds to teacher workload. You think the change is educationally right. Speak to the committee.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "School council (Dijon in the chair)", "prompt": "The Principal has brought a new uniform policy to council and expects it to be endorsed without discussion. You think council has a duty to consider the parent survey first.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "University academic committee", "prompt": "You were nominated to the committee to represent schools, and schools want lower audition requirements. You believe the current standard is right. Explain your position.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "Department meeting", "prompt": "Your instrumental staff want you, as TIC, to oppose the Principal's plan for a combined Performing Arts concert. You think it is a good plan. Speak to them.", "seconds": 90}]}, {"id": "gf-appellate", "name": "The appellate submission", "who": "The appellate advocate, common-law Bar tradition", "source": "Common-law appellate advocacy, as practised at the Australian and English Bars", "family": "meeting", "gist": "States the order sought at the outset, concedes what must be conceded, distinguishes the adverse point, gives the ground, and returns to the precise order.", "steps": [{"slot": "The order", "job": "Say at the outset exactly what you are asking the body to decide.", "cue": "What I'm asking the committee to do is one specific thing."}, {"slot": "Concede", "job": "Concede, cleanly, the strongest point against you.", "cue": "I accept, without reservation, that..."}, {"slot": "Distinguish", "job": "Show why that point does not govern this case.", "cue": "But this case is different in one material respect."}, {"slot": "The ground", "job": "Give the single strongest reason for the order.", "cue": "And the reason the committee should make this decision is this."}, {"slot": "Return", "job": "Restate the precise order, in words that could be minuted.", "cue": "So the order I'm asking for is..."}], "teach": "Experienced appellate counsel open by telling the court what orders they seek, so the bench knows from the first minute what the argument is for. They concede early and cleanly what cannot be won, because a concession wins credibility for everything else and saves the court's time. They meet the strongest adverse authority directly, distinguishing it on its facts or reasoning, rather than hoping it will not be raised. And they close by returning to the orders sought, so the bench can write them down. Australian appellate courts, which work from written outlines of submissions, reward this discipline.\n\nIt works on an educated room because it treats the room as a decision-maker. Academics and senior officers are used to contributions that are interesting but ask for nothing. A contribution that opens with what it asks for, and closes with the words for the minutes, is unusual and authoritative. The concession is the most powerful moment: a speaker who concedes cleanly is believed when he says the rest.\n\nJason's scaffold builds warmth before it asks. The appellate submission asks first, then argues. It is colder and sharper, suited to a room that has heard enough speeches. It is also the natural home for a practical mind, because the order is a solution and the whole structure exists to get it made.\n\nThe proposal is the order, stated twice: once at the outset and once at the close, ideally in identical words. The concession and distinction clear the obstacle; the ground gives the room a reason to act.", "when": "When you need a body to make a specific decision, and you know the strongest objection: a resubmission to the SCSA Board, an appeal against a council or principal's decision, a motion at a board.", "risk": "It can sound legalistic or cold. Use plain language for the order and the concession, never 'submit' or 'with respect', and keep it brief.", "model": {"room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee", "prompt": "The committee is deciding whether to resubmit the rejected course this year or wait two years. The executive officer has advised waiting, citing the Board's concern about assessment reliability.", "slots": ["What I'm asking the committee to do is one specific thing: to recommend resubmission in *this* cycle, with a revised assessment component.", "I accept, without reservation, that the Board's concern about reliability was legitimate. Our performance assessment, as drafted, did leave too much to individual judgement. That's a fair criticism.", "But this case is different from a course that needs redesigning. || The Board didn't question the syllabus, the rationale or the content. The objection was confined to one component, and one component can be fixed in a cycle.", "And the reason to act now is the students. Waiting two years means two more cohorts on a course that teachers and the Board both regard as outdated, to fix a problem we can solve in six months. Other learning areas already moderate practical work; we can adopt their model rather than invent one, and the teachers who would run it have told us they are ready.", "So the order I'm asking for is this: that the committee recommends resubmission in the current cycle, with a moderated performance component drafted by a working group and reported to the next meeting."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "Principal's office", "prompt": "The Principal has declined your request for a second specialist music teacher on cost grounds. You are asking her to reconsider for one specific reason: enrolment growth. Make your case like an appellant.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "School council", "prompt": "A parent has appealed to council against a decision that excludes their child from the specialist program after Year 9. As TIC, you speak to support a narrow exception, conceding the policy is sound.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "Arts funding panel", "prompt": "Your festival's grant was declined on the ground that it served too few regional students. You are reappearing before the panel to ask for reconsideration.", "seconds": 180}, {"room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "A school has been disqualified for a late entry. You are asking the board to readmit them, conceding that the rule is clear.", "seconds": 90}]}, {"id": "gf-count", "name": "Counting before the meeting", "who": "Lyndon B. Johnson, as Senate Majority Leader", "source": "Master of the Senate, Robert A. Caro, 2002 (Johnson's leadership, 1955 to 1961)", "family": "meeting", "gist": "Before the meeting, you speak to each member individually, learn what each needs, adjust the proposal, count the room, and only bring it to a decision when you know the result.", "steps": [{"slot": "Map the room", "job": "List every member and where you think they stand.", "cue": "(Before) Who are the votes, and where does each one sit today?"}, {"slot": "One by one", "job": "Speak privately to each member to learn what they need.", "cue": "(Before) I'd value your view before Thursday. What would you need to see in this?"}, {"slot": "Adjust", "job": "Change the proposal to meet the needs you have heard, where you can.", "cue": "(Before) If I built that in, would it work for you?"}, {"slot": "Count", "job": "Tally firm, leaning and opposed, and decide whether to proceed.", "cue": "(Before) Do I have the numbers, or do I hold it?"}, {"slot": "In the room", "job": "Let others carry the argument, and keep your own contribution short.", "cue": "I think the Deputy put it well; I'd simply add one thing."}], "teach": "Robert Caro's Master of the Senate describes Lyndon Johnson as Senate Majority Leader in the late 1950s, and in particular his gift for counting votes. Johnson knew each senator's needs, pressures and ambitions; he worked them individually, in person, in what became known as the Johnson treatment; and he did not call a vote until he knew he would win it. Caro credits this method with legislation that had long seemed impossible, including the Civil Rights Act of 1957. The meeting itself was often the least important part: the decision had been made in the corridors.\n\nStripped of its rougher methods, the discipline is entirely legitimate in an educational body: it is consultation, done thoroughly and early. Most proposals fail in meetings not because they are wrong but because members first hear them in the room, feel ambushed, and defer. A proposal that members have already shaped, and whose objections have already been met, passes quietly. It suits Dijon, because one-to-one conversations play to his strengths more than live performance before fourteen people.\n\nJason's scaffold is about winning the room in the moment. This formula is about not needing to. The live contribution can be short, because the persuasion has already happened, and it is often better if someone else proposes it.\n\nThe practical proposal is improved by the process. Each conversation tells Dijon what the objections will be, and he can build the answers in. By the time the proposal reaches the table it is no longer his alone, and the count tells him whether to put it, amend it, or hold it.", "when": "Before any meeting where a proposal of yours must pass: a resubmission strategy at SCSA, a structural change at the department, a budget change at the festival board, a council motion.", "risk": "It can look factional or like a stitch-up if the room learns of conversations it was not part of. Be open that you have consulted, never misrepresent one member's view to another, and let the meeting genuinely decide.", "model": {"room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "You want the board to move the festival's adjudication from a single adjudicator to a panel of three. You have spoken to all seven members over the past fortnight.", "slots": ["(Before) Seven votes. The president is leaning yes; two members are firmly for; the secretary is worried about cost; two long-serving members are opposed on tradition; one is unknown.", "(Before) I had coffee with each of them. The secretary needed the cost to be neutral. The long-serving members needed the head adjudicator to keep the final word. The unknown member simply wanted to be asked.", "(Before) So I changed the proposal. Panels of three, but the head adjudicator retains the final decision, and the extra fees are funded by a small rise in senior entries only. The secretary is now a yes; one long-serving member has moved to neutral.", "(Before) That's five likely in favour, one neutral, one opposed. I'll put it.", "(In the room) I've spoken to most of you about this, so I'll be brief. | I think the secretary's point about cost has made this a *better* proposal, and the panel model now keeps the head adjudicator's final say, which several of you rightly wanted. || I'd move that we trial it this year and review it at the August meeting."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "Department meeting (before)", "prompt": "You want to restructure the contemporary ensembles next year. Talk through, aloud, how you would map and count your six staff, and what you would change in response, then deliver your short contribution in the meeting.", "seconds": 180}, {"room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee (before)", "prompt": "You want the committee to adopt your moderation model for the resubmission. Plan aloud who you would speak to among the fourteen members, in which order and why, then give the brief contribution you would make on the day.", "seconds": 180}, {"room": "School council (before)", "prompt": "You want the council to support a submission for a new rehearsal building. Plan the conversations, then give the short in-meeting contribution.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "University academic committee (before)", "prompt": "You want a schools representative added permanently to the committee. Count the room aloud and give your short contribution.", "seconds": 120}]}, {"id": "gf-question-put", "name": "Summary and question put", "who": "The Westminster chair", "source": "Westminster parliamentary practice (Erskine May); in Australia, Joske's Law and Procedure at Meetings in Australia", "family": "meeting", "gist": "The chair states the precise question, takes debate through the chair, summarises the debate fairly, puts the question in exact words, and declares and records the result.", "steps": [{"slot": "State it", "job": "Say exactly what the meeting is deciding, in the words of the motion.", "cue": "The question before the meeting is that..."}, {"slot": "Debate", "job": "Take contributions in turn, through the chair, alternating for and against where possible.", "cue": "I'll take speakers in turn. Is there a speaker against?"}, {"slot": "Summarise", "job": "Summarise both sides fairly, without advocating.", "cue": "Before I put the question, let me summarise where the debate has landed."}, {"slot": "Put it", "job": "Read the exact question and take the vote.", "cue": "The question is that... All those in favour? Against? Abstentions?"}, {"slot": "Declare", "job": "Declare the result and confirm what will be minuted.", "cue": "The motion is carried. The minutes will record..."}], "teach": "In Westminster practice, the Speaker proposes the question from the chair, takes debate, and then puts it in fixed words, traditionally 'As many as are of that opinion say Aye; of the contrary, No', before declaring which side has it. Australian meeting practice, codified in Joske's Law and Procedure at Meetings in Australia, follows the same logic for councils, associations and boards: a motion is moved and seconded, debated through the chair, and put in its exact terms so there is no doubt about what has been decided. Under an incorporated association's rules in Western Australia, that precision is what makes a decision valid and minutable.\n\nIt works because educated rooms are full of discussion and short of decisions. A chair who states the question precisely, keeps debate to it, and then puts it, gives the meeting something rare: certainty. The summary before the question is put is the chair's moment of authority; done fairly, it shows command and impartiality at once.\n\nJason's scaffold is a member's contribution. This is the chair's architecture, and it carries authority that no contribution can. For Dijon as council chair and festival board officer, it is the most important formula in the set. It is also how a member can help a chair: by proposing the precise wording of the question.\n\nThe practical proposal travels inside the wording of the question. Whoever drafts the motion shapes the decision. Dijon's practical solution, framed as a precise motion with owner and date, can be put by the chair, and once carried, it is the decision of the body.", "when": "Whenever he is in the chair and the discussion has run its course, or as a member when the discussion needs converting into a decision and the chair would welcome a draft motion.", "risk": "It can feel abrupt or procedural if the debate has not finished, or if the summary leans. Ask whether anyone else wishes to speak before summarising, and summarise the side you disagree with first.", "model": {"room": "School council (Dijon in the chair)", "prompt": "The council has discussed a motion to support a submission to the Department for a new performing arts building. Debate has been lively; it is time to decide.", "slots": ["The question before the council is that the council supports the Principal's submission to the Department for a performing arts building, as set out in the paper circulated.", "I'll take speakers in turn. | We've heard from the staff representative in favour. Is there a speaker against? (Later) Does anyone else wish to speak before I put the question?", "Let me summarise where the debate has landed. || Against, we've heard a concern that the submission could crowd out the science laboratory upgrade, which is also overdue. In favour, we've heard that the current spaces fail acoustic standards and that the building would serve the whole school community, not just the specialist program. Both points are *reasonable*, and the motion doesn't preclude the laboratory bid.", "The question is that the council supports the Principal's submission to the Department for a performing arts building, as set out in the paper. All those in favour? | Against? | Abstentions?", "The motion is carried, with one against and one abstention. The minutes will record the council's support, and the Principal will report on the submission's progress at the next meeting."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "Festival association board (Dijon chairing in the president's absence)", "prompt": "A motion to increase entry fees by ten per cent has been debated for half an hour. Close the debate, summarise, put the question and declare the result.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "School council (Dijon in the chair)", "prompt": "A motion to adopt a revised dress code is being discussed, and a member proposes an amendment. Handle the amendment, then put the substantive question.", "seconds": 180}, {"room": "Department meeting (chair)", "prompt": "After discussion on whether the department should adopt a common assessment policy for Year 10, convert the discussion into a precise question and take a decision.", "seconds": 120}, {"room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee (member)", "prompt": "The discussion of next steps after the Board's rejection is circling. Help the chair by proposing the exact wording of a recommendation the committee could decide on.", "seconds": 90}]}, {"id": "gf-quaker", "name": "The minute of the meeting", "who": "The Religious Society of Friends (Quakers)", "source": "Quaker business method, as described in Quaker Faith and Practice (Britain Yearly Meeting, 1995)", "family": "meeting", "gist": "The meeting does not vote; contributions are heard without debate, and the clerk drafts a written minute of the sense of the meeting in the room, reads it aloud, and the meeting accepts or refines it.", "steps": [{"slot": "Settle", "job": "Slow the room before the item, so contributions are considered.", "cue": "Before we begin, can we take a moment, because this matters."}, {"slot": "Hear once", "job": "Invite each person to contribute once, to the question rather than to each other.", "cue": "I'd ask everyone to speak once, to the question, rather than reply to each other."}, {"slot": "Draft", "job": "Draft, in the room, a written minute capturing where the meeting has arrived.", "cue": "Let me try to put the sense of the meeting into words for the minutes."}, {"slot": "Read", "job": "Read the draft minute aloud, word for word.", "cue": "Here is what I've written."}, {"slot": "Accept", "job": "Invite refinement until the meeting accepts the wording.", "cue": "Does that capture where we are, or does it need changing?"}], "teach": "Quaker meetings for worship for business, described in Britain Yearly Meeting's Quaker Faith and Practice, take no votes. Contributions are offered once, to the matter at hand rather than in reply to other speakers, often with silence between them. The clerk listens for the sense of the meeting and drafts a minute in the meeting itself, which is read aloud and accepted, amended or redrafted by those present. The minute is the decision. The method aims for unity rather than majority, and it treats the wording of the record as the place where agreement is actually reached.\n\nIt works on an educated room because it shifts the contest from speeches to words on a page, and educated people are much better at refining a sentence than at abandoning a position. The pause before contributions lowers the temperature, and the single-contribution rule stops the most fluent members from dominating. The minute read aloud gives the room a concrete object to agree to, and once agreed, it is very hard to relitigate.\n\nJason's scaffold is oral and individual. This is written and collective. It takes the fluent speaker's advantage away almost entirely, which is why it suits Dijon: the person who drafts the minute holds the pen, and the person who holds the pen shapes the record that everyone later remembers.\n\nThe practical proposal travels inside the draft minute. Dijon need not advocate it; he writes it into the minute as the agreed next step, precisely, with owner and date, and the meeting refines it into its own decision.", "when": "When chairing a discussion that is too sensitive for a vote, such as a values question, a response to bad news or a matter of conscience, or as a member when the room has reached agreement but nobody has written it down.", "risk": "It can seem quaint or religious if named. Do not name it; just slow the room, ask for single contributions and offer a draft minute. Draft honestly: if the minute bends the sense of the room, it will be rejected and trust with it.", "model": {"room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee", "prompt": "The committee has just heard that its course has been rejected. The mood is raw, and the chair asks Dijon to help draft the committee's response for the minutes.", "slots": ["Before we try to respond, can we take a moment? | This is two years of work, and I think we owe it a considered response rather than a quick one.", "Could I suggest that each of us speaks once, to the question of what we want to say to the Board, rather than replying to each other? That way we hear everyone, including those online.", "(After contributions) Let me try to put the sense of the committee into words for the minutes. ||", "Here is what I've written. 'The committee notes the Board's decision with disappointment and respects it. The committee remains confident in the syllabus content, which the Board did not criticise. The committee recognises the Board's concern about the *reliability* of performance assessment, and resolves to establish a working group to draft a moderated assessment model for consideration at its next meeting, with a view to resubmission in the current cycle.'", "Does that capture where we are, or does it need changing? | I'm very happy to change any word of it. It's the committee's minute, not mine."]}, "prompts": [{"room": "School council (Dijon in the chair)", "prompt": "A staff member has died suddenly, and the council must decide how the school community will mark it. A vote would be inappropriate. Run the item to an agreed minute.", "seconds": 180}, {"room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "The board has received a serious complaint about an adjudicator's conduct. Members are upset and divided. Help the board reach an agreed minute without a vote.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "Department meeting (chair)", "prompt": "Staff are divided on whether to keep the department's long-standing tour to the eastern states after a difficult trip. Run the discussion to a written minute.", "seconds": 150}, {"room": "University academic committee (member)", "prompt": "The committee has, in effect, agreed after a long discussion to review the audition panel's composition, but no one has framed it. Offer a draft minute.", "seconds": 90}]}], "notesLessons": [{"id": "nt-1", "title": "Notes for the room, not the record", "teach": "Minutes exist so that someone who was absent can reconstruct what was decided. Speaking notes exist for a different reader: you, three seconds before you open your mouth. A minute-taker's page is chronological, complete and neutral. A speaker's page is selective, attributed and pointed at the future. The test is not whether the page would let an absent member follow the meeting, but whether a single glance tells you whom to credit, what the room actually has to decide, and what you are about to add.\n\nThe contributors who seem to recall everything are not recalling more than you; they are recalling differently. They hold three things: names fastened to phrases, the shape of the argument so far, and the one move they intend to make. Everything else is allowed to fall away. Full sentences capture content at the expense of structure, so you arrive at your turn with a transcript and no launch pad. Transcribing also takes your attention off the speaker's emphasis, and emphasis is exactly where the creditable phrase sits.\n\nThe discipline is therefore subtraction. Write roughly one line per minute of talk, and only when something changes: a new position, a figure, a phrase you could quote back, a shift in the question, or a point you want to answer. If a line would not survive into your contribution, it does not need to exist. The executive officer is taking the minutes; you are taking aim.\n\nCompare two versions of the same moment at an SCSA committee. Record note: \"Dr Lim said the Board's concerns related mainly to assessment load in Unit 3 and that Victoria had dealt with this differently.\" Speaking note: \"SL: Bd = assess load U3 'weight, not width' ≈ Vic\". The second is a third of the length, carries the phrase you will credit, and the ≈ already tells you there is a comparison you can build on.", "model": "RECORD (what not to write):\nDr Lim said the Board's concerns related mainly to assessment load in Unit 3 and that Victoria had dealt with this differently.\n\nSPEAKING (what to write):\nROOM: SL  Bd = assess load U3  'weight, not width'  ≈ Vic\nME: ✓ + our U3 task audit #  ★", "exercise": {"kind": "write", "task": "Take one page of notes from a recent meeting, or reconstruct the last agenda item you sat through. Rewrite it as no more than six speaking lines: initials, keyword, symbol, and one quoted phrase per speaker. Then add one ME line saying what you would have said.", "seconds": 90}, "checks": ["I can tell the difference between a line that records and a line that launches.", "I write when something changes, not continuously.", "Every speaker on my page has initials and, where possible, a phrase I could quote.", "I can glance at my page for three seconds and know what I will add.", "I leave the complete record to the minutes."]}, {"id": "nt-2", "title": "The two-column page and its two lines", "teach": "Rule the page before the meeting starts. A vertical line about two thirds across gives you a wide left column headed THE ROOM and a narrow right column headed ME. Across the top, a line marked Q for the question the room actually has to decide. Across the bottom, a line marked GAP for what nobody has yet said. That frame is the method; everything else is technique within it.\n\nThe left column is reportage: initials, keyword, symbol, exact phrase in quote marks. Keep it disciplined and impersonal. The right column is yours: a reaction, a hook to another speaker, a piece of evidence you hold, a fragment of a proposal. Keeping the two apart matters more than it looks. When your reactions are mixed into the record, you cannot see at a glance which ideas belong to whom, and you lose the ability to credit cleanly. When they are separated, your right column becomes the draft of your contribution while the meeting is still running.\n\nWrite the Q line in pencil, or leave room to rewrite it. The question on the agenda paper and the question the room is really deciding are often different, and the difference usually becomes visible twenty minutes in. The GAP line stays blank for most of the meeting. You fill it only when you notice an absence: no one has costed it, no one has named who does it, no one has asked what students experience, no one has offered a timeline. That absence is where your practical contribution lives.\n\nA worked fragment from a school council item on instrumental fees shows the shape. The left column carries four speakers in four lines. The right column carries two short reactions and a star. The bottom line has three words. That is enough to speak for two minutes with authority.", "model": "Q: Do we set the 2027 instrument hire fee tonight, or refer it?\nROOM: KP (P&C)  fees ↑ 2nd yr  'priced out by stealth'  !\nME: ✓ KP; I hold hire numbers #\nROOM: Principal  budget fixed  'no new money, only new choices'\nROOM: AT (staff)  ? hardship fund unused\nME: ≈ AT: fund exists, nobody knows ★\nROOM: Chair  → refer to finance cttee?\nGAP: who tells families?", "exercise": {"kind": "write", "task": "Rule a page now with Q, THE ROOM, ME and GAP. Recall the last council, board or committee item you attended and fill it: one Q line, four ROOM lines, two ME lines, one GAP line. Keep the columns strictly separate.", "seconds": 90}, "checks": ["I rule the page before the meeting begins.", "My left column reports; my right column reacts.", "I treat the Q line as provisional and rewrite it when the room's question changes.", "I leave the GAP line blank until I genuinely notice an absence.", "I can see at a glance which ideas belong to whom."]}, {"id": "nt-3", "title": "Symbols and keywords, never sentences", "teach": "A fixed symbol set does two jobs. It saves time, and it pre-sorts the material by function, so that when you look down you see not just what was said but what it is for. Use the set below until it is automatic; do not improvise new symbols mid-meeting, because a symbol you have to decode later is worse than a word.\n\nThe set: → leads to, so; ✓ agree; ✗ object; ? question to raise; ★ my moment, speak here; ≈ same as, builds on; Δ change proposed; ∴ therefore; ! important; \"…\" exact words to credit; @ action and owner; # number or figure; ⟲ came back to an earlier point. Three of these carry most of the weight in a contribution. Quote marks give you the credit. ≈ gives you the bridge between two speakers, which is the material of a good summary. ★ tells you where you intend to enter, and therefore stops you entering too early or not at all.\n\nKeywords should be the speaker's nouns, not your paraphrase. If a deputy director says \"moderation burden\", write \"moderation burden\", not \"marking workload\". Using the room's own vocabulary when you speak signals that you were listening, and it lets the minute-taker attach your proposal to the existing thread. Abbreviate consistently: Bd for Board, U3 for Unit 3, PL for professional learning, Yr 11 for Year 11. Initials for people; role for anyone you do not yet know by name, and correct it the moment you hear the name.\n\nA three-minute contribution from a contrarian at a festival association board can be held in two lines. \"MR ✗ new venue: 'we'd be renting prestige' # $14k ↑\" and, beneath it in your column, \"ME: ? cost per student vs 2026 ≈ Treasurer report\". You have his objection, his phrase, his figure, your question and your source. Nothing more is needed.", "model": "ROOM: MR  ✗ new venue  'we'd be renting prestige'  # $14k ↑\nME: ? cost per student vs 2026  ≈ treasurer report\nROOM: JD  ≈ MR, but Δ split venue across 2 days\nROOM: Chair  ∴ decide by Nov mtg  !\nME: ★ split + per-student figure → motion", "exercise": {"kind": "write", "task": "Write out the full symbol set from memory with meanings. Then convert this spoken sentence into a single note line: \"I'd strongly resist moving the moderation meeting online; it was the only thing last year teachers said actually changed their marking, and we had forty-two schools attend.\" Speaker: Ms Alvarez.", "seconds": 60}, "checks": ["I can write the whole symbol set from memory.", "I use the speaker's own nouns rather than my paraphrase.", "I mark exact phrases with quote marks every time.", "I use ≈ to link speakers, which gives me my summary.", "I place ★ where I intend to speak, before I speak."]}, {"id": "nt-4", "title": "Catching the phrase you will credit", "teach": "Crediting by name with someone's exact words is the single most visible sign of a skilled contributor. It flatters the speaker, proves you were listening, and borrows their authority for whatever you say next. It works only if the words are exact. A paraphrase credited by name sounds like a summary; a verbatim phrase credited by name sounds like respect.\n\nMost articulate speakers deliver one phrase per contribution that they have half prepared: a compression of their argument into four to eight words, often with a slight change in pace or a pause before it. Listen for the shift in delivery, the phrase that sounds quotable, the metaphor, or the moment they repeat themselves for emphasis. Write it in quote marks the instant you hear it, before you write anything else about their point. The keyword can be reconstructed afterwards; the exact phrase cannot.\n\nKeep phrases short. Six words are retrievable; fifteen are a paraphrase in disguise. If you miss part, write what you have and mark the gap with an ellipsis rather than filling it from imagination: crediting someone with words they did not say is worse than not crediting them. Put the initials immediately before the quote, so the phrase is never orphaned.\n\nIn use, the phrase becomes the hinge of your opening. The note \"HO 'a syllabus is a promise, not a menu'\" turns into: \"Dr Okafor said a syllabus is *a promise, not a menu*. I want to hold us to that promise in one practical respect.\" Notice that the credit is followed at once by your move. Credit without a move is applause; credit with a move is leadership.", "model": "NOTE:\nROOM: HO  'a syllabus is a promise, not a menu'\nME: ≈ HO → assessment must match the promise ★\n\nSPOKEN:\nHelen Okafor said a syllabus is *a promise, not a menu*. | I'd like to hold us to that promise in one practical respect, which is assessment.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Recall a phrase someone used in a recent meeting, conversation or broadcast that stuck with you. Credit them by name with the exact words, then make one move of your own that builds on it. Keep the credit to one sentence and the move to two.", "seconds": 45}, "checks": ["I write the exact phrase first, before the keyword.", "I keep captured phrases to about eight words or fewer.", "I mark gaps with an ellipsis rather than inventing words.", "I attach initials to every quotation.", "When I credit a phrase I follow it immediately with my own move."]}, {"id": "nt-5", "title": "Tracking the real question, and the drift", "teach": "Every agenda item has a nominal question and a real one. The nominal question is on the paper: \"Response to Board feedback on the draft course.\" The real one emerges from the talk: \"Do we revise and resubmit for 2028, or reframe the course as an elective endorsed program?\" The contributor who names the real question first gains control of the discussion, because everyone after them has to answer it.\n\nWrite the nominal question on the Q line before the item opens. Then listen for three signals that the real question is surfacing: a senior person reframes the item (\"what we really need to decide is\"); two speakers disagree about something the paper did not mention; or the chair's summary uses words the agenda did not. When you hear one, rewrite the Q line. Keep the old version crossed through rather than erased, because the distance between the two is useful material for your contribution.\n\nDrift is different. Drift is when the room moves to a question that does not need deciding today: a grievance about process, a broader policy debate, an anecdote that becomes a tangent. Mark the moment drift begins with a short line and a dash in the margin, and mark the return with ⟲. If drift runs for more than a few minutes, you have an opening: a contributor who gently restores the question is doing the chair's work, and rooms remember who did that.\n\nThe restoring move is short and generous: \"These are important points about consultation, and I'd like them minuted. On the question in front of us, though, which I take to be whether we resubmit for 2028...\" Your notes make that move possible, because you can see on the page exactly when the room left the question and who took it there.", "model": "Q (was): Response to Bd feedback\nQ: Resubmit for 2028, or reframe as endorsed program?\nROOM: DP  'what we really need to decide is the vehicle'  !\nROOM: KW  consultation was rushed  (drift)\nROOM: RT  ≈ KW, 2019 process  (drift)\nROOM: Chair  ⟲ vehicle\nME: ★ restore Q, credit DP, then proposal", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Using the model notes, give the restoring move: acknowledge the drift without dismissing it, credit DP's reframing with the exact phrase, and restate the real question in one sentence.", "seconds": 45}, "checks": ["I write the nominal question before the item opens.", "I rewrite the Q line when the real question surfaces, and keep the old version visible.", "I mark where drift begins and where the room comes back.", "I can restore the question without making anyone wrong.", "I name the real question before I make my proposal."]}, {"id": "nt-6", "title": "Finding the gap where your proposal lives", "teach": "Articulate rooms are often rich in analysis and poor in mechanism. Speakers diagnose, compare, contextualise and agree on values, and then the item closes with a referral and nothing changes. The gap is what nobody has said, and in committees it is very often the practical layer: who does it, by when, at what cost, using which existing structure, measured how. That layer is your strength, so the GAP line is where you should expect to find your contribution.\n\nListen with a short silent checklist running in the right column. Has anyone said who? When? With what resource? Through what existing vehicle? What does the student, teacher or parent actually experience? What would make the Board, the principal or the funder say yes? The first of these that remains unanswered after three or four speakers is usually your gap. Write it in one plain line at the foot of the page.\n\nA gap is not the same as an objection. If you find yourself writing ✗ against everyone, you are holding a counterargument, which positions you against the room. A gap positions you as the person who completes the room's thinking. The language follows: not \"nobody has considered\", which accuses, but \"what we haven't yet settled is\", which includes.\n\nIn a university partnership committee discussing pathways from school specialist programs into a conservatorium, four speakers might agree that \"the pipeline is leaking\" and cite interstate models. If no one has named who will run the bridging workshops, how many students, and from which term, your GAP line reads \"who runs it, how many, from when\". Your proposal is then three numbered answers to that line, attached by name to the people who diagnosed the problem.", "model": "Q: How do we strengthen pathways into the BMus?\nROOM: PN (Uni)  'the pipeline is leaking'  ≈ Qld model\nROOM: GS (DoE)  ✓ PN, equity !\nROOM: LF  ? audition prep access\nME: who? when? how many? cost?\nME: ★ 1 pilot 3 schools  2 Term 2 2027  3 uni staff + TICs\nGAP: who runs it, how many, from when", "exercise": {"kind": "write", "task": "Take a current issue in one of your own committees or your department. Write four ROOM lines summarising what has been said about it, then run the silent checklist in the ME column and write one GAP line. Finish with a three-part numbered proposal that answers the gap.", "seconds": 90}, "checks": ["I listen for the missing mechanism, not just for points to rebut.", "I run the who, when, resource, vehicle and experience checklist silently.", "I write my gap as one plain line at the foot of the page.", "I frame the gap as completing the room's thinking, not correcting it.", "My proposal answers the gap in numbered parts."]}, {"id": "nt-7", "title": "The pre-meeting page", "teach": "The best impromptu contributions are rarely impromptu. The speakers you admire have walked in with two or three settled points, the evidence for each, and a view about where they are likely to land. In the meeting they are not inventing; they are attaching prepared material to what others say. A pre-meeting page gives you the same advantage without making you rigid.\n\nUse a separate half page, written the evening before or in the car park. At the top, your prediction of the real question, which may differ from the agenda. Then your three points, each in no more than six words. Under each, one piece of evidence you can state with confidence: a figure, a precedent, a comparison with another jurisdiction you know accurately, or a specific experience from your own program. Then a single line of standing, chosen for this room: the credential that makes your practical point credible here, not your whole biography. Finally, a draft of the wording you would like in the minutes.\n\nBe strict about evidence. Write only what you can defend if questioned by someone who knows the field. A precise local fact (\"forty-one Year 11 students across our program sat the practical exam this year\") carries more weight in a hybrid committee than a vague reference to research. If you are relying on interstate policy, note the jurisdiction and the document so you can name it exactly, or leave it out.\n\nDuring the meeting, keep the pre-meeting page beside your note page. When a speaker touches one of your points, write ≈ and their initials next to it. By the time you speak, your prepared points have been attached to other people's names, and your contribution sounds responsive rather than rehearsed.", "model": "PRE-MEETING  CAC, Board feedback item\nLikely Q: revise and resubmit, or abandon?\n1 Assessment load fixable  # tasks 9 → 6   ≈ JS\n2 Pilot schools ready  # 4 schools named\n3 Timeline: resubmit by Term 2 2027\nStanding: TIC, specialist program, teaching since 2005\nMinutes: 'That the committee revise assessment and resubmit by ...'", "exercise": {"kind": "write", "task": "Write a pre-meeting page for the next real meeting in your diary: likely real question, three points of six words or fewer, one defensible piece of evidence under each, one line of standing chosen for that room, and draft wording for the minutes.", "seconds": 90}, "checks": ["I arrive with my likely question, three points and evidence already written.", "My standing line is chosen for this room, not recited in full.", "Every piece of evidence on my page is something I can defend under questioning.", "I mark ≈ with initials when a speaker touches one of my prepared points.", "I have draft wording for the minutes before I speak."]}, {"id": "nt-8", "title": "The twenty-second conversion", "teach": "The moment between \"I'll come to you next\" and your first word is where most contributions are lost. You look down at a page of good notes and see everything at once. The fix is a fixed routine, done the same way every time, that turns the page into an ordered contribution in about twenty seconds: circle, number, star.\n\nCircle two phrases in the ROOM column, the two you will credit. Choose them for usefulness, not prominence: ideally one from a senior or dominant speaker and one from a quieter voice whose point carries the crucial fact. Crediting the quiet expert is generous and strategically sound, because it makes their fact part of your case. Then number the margin to match the seven slots of the long contribution: 1 thanks, 2 acknowledge, 3 standing, 4 common ground, 5 frame and evidence, 6 proposal, 7 close. Not every line gets a number; most get none. You need only one line per slot, and some slots, such as standing, may come from the pre-meeting page.\n\nThen star. The ★ goes on the GAP line and on the first part of your proposal. That is your destination, and knowing it lets you move through slots one to five briskly, without over-explaining, because you know where you are going. If you have time for only one action, make it the star: a contribution with a clear destination survives a weak opening, but a polished opening without a destination becomes the kind of speech people admire and do not act on.\n\nPractise the routine until the order is reflexive. Under pressure you will not remember a method; you will only remember a habit. A useful rehearsal is to set a timer for twenty seconds against an old page of notes, mark it, then speak for ninety seconds following only the numbers.", "model": "Q: Resubmit for 2028?\nROOM: (JS 'the Board has given us a map, not a verdict')  →5\nROOM: AM  Bd ✗ assess load  →2\nROOM: (TN 'nine tasks is a year of rehearsal for nothing')  →5\nME: TIC, teaching since 2005  →3\nME: best for students  →4\nGAP: who redrafts, by when  ★ →6\nME: @ working group, Term 1 2027  →7", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Using the model notes, run the routine silently in twenty seconds, then speak for sixty seconds following the numbers. Credit both circled phrases by name with the exact words, and land on the starred proposal.", "seconds": 60}, "checks": ["I circle exactly two phrases to credit.", "I include at least one quieter speaker among my credits when their point matters.", "I number only one line per slot.", "I place my star on the gap and my proposal before I speak.", "I can complete the routine in about twenty seconds."]}, {"id": "nt-9", "title": "Notes in hybrid meetings", "teach": "A hybrid meeting is two rooms pretending to be one. The physical room has eye contact, side remarks and the chair's body language; the online room has the chat, the hands queue, cameras on or off, and a delay of a second or two that makes interjection hazardous. Your notes must track both, because the people online are easy to overlook and therefore especially grateful to be credited.\n\nBefore the item starts, write a short roster across the top of the page: who is in the room, who is online, with initials. It takes thirty seconds and pays for itself, because a contribution that credits someone online by name (\"Jason made the point on screen that...\") signals unusual attentiveness. Mark online speakers' lines with a small (Z) after the initials, and treat the chat as a second speaker: prefix those lines with \"chat:\". Points made only in chat are often lost from the minutes, and a contributor who surfaces them earns goodwill from the people who typed them.\n\nWatch the hands queue. When a polished online speaker raises a hand repeatedly, note it, because the chair will be balancing turns and you can predict when you will be called. If the chair keeps a queue, write your position in the corner and update it; if not, raise your hand early, in the physical room as well as on screen, and note that you have done so. Lag also changes timing: online members often respond to a point two speakers late. Mark this with ⟲ so you do not mistake a late reply for a new argument.\n\nFinally, because online listeners lose prosody and sometimes audio, a contribution in a hybrid room needs clearer signposting than one in person. Your numbered slots help here: \"Two things, briefly. First... Second...\" carries over a weak connection in a way that elegant subordinate clauses do not.", "model": "ROOM: SL, AM, TN, KW, DS (me)   Z: JS, HO, PR, MW\nROOM: JS(Z)  'a map, not a verdict'  hand ×3\nchat: PR  # NSW cut tasks to 5 in 2024?  ? verify\nROOM: AM  Bd ✗ assess load\nROOM: HO(Z)  ⟲ JS (lag)  ✓\nME: credit PR chat point; queue #3\nGAP: who redrafts, by when ★", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Speak a forty-five-second opening for a hybrid meeting that credits one in-room speaker and one online speaker by name, surfaces a point raised only in the chat, and signposts that you have two things to propose.", "seconds": 45}, "checks": ["I write a roster of in-room and online members before the item opens.", "I treat the chat as a speaker and note it with a chat: prefix.", "I track the hands queue and my place in it.", "I mark lagged replies with ⟲ rather than treating them as new points.", "I signpost more explicitly when people are listening online."]}, {"id": "nt-10", "title": "Notes as a chair", "teach": "When you chair, your notes change purpose. You are no longer collecting material for your own contribution; you are holding the meeting's memory so that it can reach a decision. A chair's page has four elements: the speaker queue, the positions being taken, decisions as they are made, and actions with owners and dates. The two-column layout still works, but the right column becomes CHAIR rather than ME.\n\nKeep the queue in the top corner, in order, crossing names off as they speak and noting online members separately so that the room is not privileged by proximity. Next to each speaker, one keyword and a ✓, ✗ or Δ to show where they stand. This lets you see the balance of the room at a glance and decide whether to call for more views, test a compromise or move to a resolution. Mark every action with @, an owner by name and a date; an action without an owner and a date is an aspiration, and the minutes will show it.\n\nThe most valuable thing a chair does with notes is the summary before a decision. It should be short, fair and attributed: the question restated, the main positions credited by name, any change proposed, and the form of words to be put. A summary that is vague or tilted invites reopening; one that is precise allows the room to vote with confidence. Under an incorporated association's rules or a council's standing orders the formal requirements may differ, so follow the governing document, but the practice of a clear attributed summary is good form everywhere.\n\nOn a school council discussing a uniform policy review, your summary might draw on five lines: \"We've heard three positions. Karen proposes a full consultation; Ahmed favours a limited trial of the new jacket; and Ms Tran has reminded us that families need notice before the January order. The motion before us is a trial in Term 1, with a survey in Term 2. Ahmed to draft, Ms Tran to brief families by 5 December.\"", "model": "QUEUE: KP ✓  AH  MT  (Z) RB\nQ: Uniform review: full consult or trial?\nROOM: KP  Δ full consultation  'ask before we buy'\nROOM: AH  Δ trial jacket T1\nROOM: MT  ! families need notice pre-Jan order\nCHAIR: ≈ AH + MT → trial T1, survey T2\n@ AH draft trial  by 28 Nov\n@ MT family notice  by 5 Dec\nDECISION: motion carried / referred", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Using the model notes, give the chair's summary before the vote in no more than forty-five seconds: restate the question, credit each position by name, state the motion, and name the owners and dates for actions.", "seconds": 45}, "checks": ["I keep a visible queue, with online members included.", "I mark each speaker's position so I can see the balance of the room.", "Every action on my page has an owner and a date.", "My summary before a decision is short, fair and attributed.", "I state the exact form of words before the room decides."]}], "notesSims": [{"id": "ns-a1", "room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee, hybrid, 8 in the room and 6 on Zoom", "item": "Response to the Board decision on the two-year course", "context": "At the last meeting three senior SCSA officers reported that the Board had not approved the two-year course this committee spent two years developing. Today the committee decides how to respond.", "speakers": [{"who": "Ms Claire Hendry, chair, in the room", "voice": "f", "said": "Thank you all for coming back so soon. I'll be honest, this feels a little like the second rehearsal after a bad concert: everyone knows what went wrong and nobody wants to say it first. So let me say it. The Board's letter names three concerns: overlap with the existing ATAR course, the comparability of the school-assessed portfolio, and whether schools outside the metropolitan area could staff it. None of those is a rejection of the idea. They are objections to the build. What I want from today is a decision on whether we revise and resubmit, and in what order we fix things. Please keep us on the build, not the grief."}, {"who": "Dr Adrian Vesey, university academic, on Zoom", "voice": "m", "said": "Thank you, Chair, and thank you to the three officers who briefed us last time with such candour; that was not an easy message to carry, and it was carried with real care. I've served on accreditation panels interstate for a long time, and a first submission declined on comparability is far more common than this room may feel today. The second submission is almost always the stronger one. The Board is not saying no to students; it is saying show us the evidence. So I'd urge us to treat this as a draft that met its first examiner. We share one purpose, the best outcomes for young musicians in this state, and that purpose survives a second draft."}, {"who": "Mr Rajiv Menon, SCSA principal consultant, in the room", "voice": "m", "said": "Just a procedural note, because it shapes everything else. If the committee wants the course considered at the Board's March meeting, the revised documentation has to reach the secretariat by the last week of January. Realistically that means the substantive drafting happens before schools close in December, because nobody does good work in the first fortnight of January. So the calendar is the real constraint, more than the content. I'd also say the Board will read a resubmission against its own letter, line by line, so whatever we send should answer each concern in the order the Board raised it."}, {"who": "Mrs Lorraine Tuckey, regional teacher, on Zoom with a slight delay", "voice": "f", "said": "Sorry, can you hear me? Good. I'll be brief. On the staffing concern, I think the Board is right, and I say that as someone who wants this course. Most schools in my network have one music teacher, often part-time, and that person is already running the ensembles, the instrumental timetable and the concert. The portfolio as drafted assumes a second teacher to cross-mark. In a regional school you have one teacher doing the work of a department. If the moderation could happen online, across schools, rather than inside each school, that concern largely disappears. We already moderate that way informally."}, {"who": "Mr Graham Pell, metropolitan principal, in the room", "voice": "m", "said": "Let me be the unpopular voice. Before we pour another year into this, should we resubmit at all? The Board's first concern is overlap, and frankly I share it. If two thirds of what we've written could sit inside an amended ATAR course, then we may be building a second house on the same block. Schools have finite lines on the timetable. I'm not against the ambition, but I'd want someone to show me, concretely, what a student gets from this course that they could not get from the existing one with some sensible amendments."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Hendry", "point": "Board objected to design, not the idea; decide resubmission", "phrase": "objections to the build"}, {"who": "Vesey", "point": "First rejections on comparability are common; resubmit with evidence", "phrase": "a draft that met its first examiner"}, {"who": "Menon", "point": "January deadline drives everything; answer the letter in order", "phrase": "the calendar is the real constraint"}, {"who": "Tuckey", "point": "Single regional teachers cannot cross-mark; moderate online", "phrase": "one teacher doing the work of a department"}, {"who": "Pell", "point": "Overlap may mean amending ATAR instead of a new course", "phrase": "building a second house on the same block"}], "question": "Does the committee revise and resubmit by January, and how will it answer each of the Board's three concerns?", "gap": "No one has matched each Board concern to a specific fix with an owner, and no one has answered Graham's overlap challenge with evidence. A working group per concern, reporting before December, would turn the mood into a plan.", "modelNotes": "Q: resubmit by Jan? how answer 3 concerns?\nROOM: CH \"objections to the build\" ! 3 = overlap / comparability / regional staffing\nME: ✓ frame it as 3 fixes, 1 per concern\nROOM: AV thanks officers, interstate panels, \"a draft that met its first examiner\"\nME: ≈ AV, but needs owners not optimism\nROOM: RM # March Board → docs by last wk Jan, drafting before Dec, \"the calendar is the real constraint\"\nME: @ groups report 4 Dec, full draft 11 Dec\nROOM: LT (lag) \"one teacher doing the work of a department\" → online moderation ★\nME: ∴ LT answers concern 2 AND 3\nROOM: GP ✗ resubmit? \"building a second house on the same block\"\nME: ? answer GP with side-by-side map vs ATAR syllabus\nME: Δ portfolio → common task + online consensus moderation\nME: ★ I convene comparability group\nGAP: no fix per concern, no owner, no reply to GP on overlap", "task": "The chair turns to you. Credit at least two speakers, name the decision, and propose how the committee answers the Board.", "seconds": 150, "model": "Thank you, Chair, and thank you for calling these *objections to the build*, because that gives us something we can fix. Adrian called this a draft that met its first examiner, and Rajiv reminded us that the calendar is the real constraint. Lorraine said the thing I think matters most: in a regional school there is one teacher doing the work of a department. That sits underneath two of the Board's three concerns, not one. I say this as someone who runs a specialist music program and has worked on this course since its first draft. We all want a course a student in the Great Southern can take as confidently as a student in the city. | Graham's question deserves an answer in evidence, not in feeling. So the question for today is not whether we believe in the course; it is whether we can answer three concerns by January. I propose three working groups, one per concern, in the Board's own order. One: overlap. A side-by-side map against the ATAR syllabus, showing exactly what is distinct; that answers Graham directly. Two: comparability. Replace the open portfolio with one common task and online consensus moderation across schools. Three: regional delivery. Build Lorraine's model in, with exemplars and moderation online so no school needs a second teacher. Each group reports by 4 December, the full draft comes to us on 11 December, and Rajiv's secretariat has it in January. || For the minutes: the committee resolved to revise and resubmit, with three working groups addressing overlap, comparability and regional delivery, each reporting by 4 December. I'm happy to convene the comparability group."}, {"id": "ns-a2", "room": "SCSA Music syllabus review panel, hybrid, 9 members, 3 online", "item": "Proposal to allow recorded submissions in the ATAR practical examination", "context": "A teacher submission asked SCSA to allow recorded performances instead of the live practical examination. The panel must advise whether to explore it.", "speakers": [{"who": "Professor Simon Ashdown, conservatorium, in the room", "voice": "m", "said": "I want to put the case for the live examination plainly, because I think it is the heart of the course. A live performance tests something a recording cannot: the capacity to recover, to manage nerves, to communicate in real time to a listener who is actually there. Every audition this student will ever face is live. If we allow recordings we will, within three years, be marking the quality of a school's microphones and the patience of its sound engineer. A recording is a product; a performance is an event. I would be very reluctant to see the examination drift from the event to the product."}, {"who": "Ms Fiona Lacey, instrumental teacher, online", "voice": "f", "said": "I agree with a lot of that, and it reminds me of the accompanist question, which I know isn't on the agenda, but it's related. Some families pay a fortune for accompanists for the rehearsal and the exam, and some students simply can't get one, particularly in voice. I had a student last year whose accompanist cancelled the night before. It was awful. So whatever we decide, I think we have to look at the accompanist problem, because that's where the real inequity sits, honestly, more than in recordings."}, {"who": "Mr Peter Nguyen, practical examination coordinator, in the room", "voice": "m", "said": "Can I add something factual? The submission talks about recordings as though they're simple. In our trial marking of recorded material for a different course, the variable that most affected marks was not the playing; it was the room. Markers consistently scored resonant rooms higher, without realising it. That's a validity problem, not just a fairness problem. If the panel goes anywhere near recordings, it needs specified conditions: single take, unedited, a fixed microphone position, a witnessed declaration. Without those, we would be marking acoustics."}, {"who": "Dr Amira Haddad, education researcher, online", "voice": "f", "said": "I'd push back on the professor, respectfully. The live examination is not a neutral event either. Students from regional schools travel, sometimes hundreds of kilometres, and perform on an unfamiliar instrument or in an unfamiliar hall after a long drive. We already accept that the live event carries noise. The question is which noise we are willing to tolerate. A recorded option under strict conditions might reduce more inequity than it creates. I'm not saying abolish the live exam; I'm saying the status quo has costs we've stopped noticing."}, {"who": "Ms Deborah Fitch, chair, in the room", "voice": "f", "said": "Thank you, all. So, if I can summarise, I think we're broadly agreed that recordings are a fairness issue, and that there are concerns about accompanists, and some people are for and some against. Perhaps the way forward is to note the submission and come back to it next year when we have more time. Does anyone want to add anything before we move on? I'm conscious we have three more items, and I don't want us to rush a decision of this size."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Ashdown", "point": "Live exam tests recovery and communication; keep it", "phrase": "A recording is a product; a performance is an event"}, {"who": "Lacey", "point": "Accompanist access is the bigger inequity", "phrase": "that's where the real inequity sits"}, {"who": "Nguyen", "point": "Room acoustics skewed marks; strict conditions needed", "phrase": "we would be marking acoustics"}, {"who": "Haddad", "point": "Live exam also carries inequity for regional students", "phrase": "the status quo has costs we've stopped noticing"}, {"who": "Fitch", "point": "Note submission and defer to next year", "phrase": "note the submission and come back to it next year"}], "question": "Should the panel advise SCSA to investigate a conditional recorded pathway, or keep the live examination unchanged?", "gap": "The chair's summary loses Peter's evidence and defers without a plan. No one has proposed a limited, evidence-gathering trial that keeps the live exam as the default while testing a conditional recorded pathway.", "modelNotes": "Q: investigate recorded option or keep live only?\nROOM: SA ✗ recordings \"a recording is a product; a performance is an event\"\nME: ✓ live stays default\nROOM: FL drift → accompanists \"that's where the real inequity sits\"\nME: ! separate item, @ next agenda\nROOM: PN ! trial: room skewed marks, \"we would be marking acoustics\"\nME: ★ PN = crucial fact, chair dropped it\nROOM: PN conditions: 1 take, unedited, fixed mic, declaration\nROOM: AH ≈ inequity both ways, \"the status quo has costs we've stopped noticing\"\nROOM: DF summary misses PN → \"note... come back next year\"\nME: ✗ defer = no data next year either\nME: Δ pathway on application only, PN conditions\nME: Δ 2027 pilot: same students live + recorded, compare marks\nME: @ PN + SCSA, design by Term 1\nGAP: no trial, no evidence plan, accompanists homeless", "task": "Before the chair closes the item, you raise your hand. Correct the summary gently, credit two speakers, and propose a way forward.", "seconds": 120, "model": "Thank you, Chair. Before we move on, could I add one thing to the summary, because I think the most important fact in this discussion came from Peter. In trial marking, the room skewed the marks, and as he put it, without conditions *we would be marking acoustics*. That changes the question. Professor Ashdown is right that a recording is a product and a performance is an event, and I would keep the live examination as the default. But Amira is also right that the status quo has costs we've stopped noticing, especially for students who drive half a day to perform. So the question is not live or recorded. It is whether we gather evidence now, or arrive next year with exactly what we have today. I'd propose three things. One: the panel advises SCSA that the live examination remains the default. Two: we recommend a small 2027 pilot in which a sample of candidates is examined live and also submits a recording under Peter's conditions: single take, unedited, fixed microphone, witnessed declaration. Markers score both blind, and we compare. Three: Fiona's point about accompanists goes on the next agenda as its own item, because that's where the real inequity sits and it deserves more than a footnote. | For the minutes: the panel recommends the live examination be retained, and that SCSA scope a 2027 pilot comparing live and recorded marking, with a design brought to the first panel meeting of Term 1. I'm glad to work with Peter on the brief."}, {"id": "ns-a3", "room": "SCSA Arts Learning Area committee, hybrid, 11 members, 5 on Zoom", "item": "Implementation support for the revised Years 7 to 10 Music syllabus", "context": "The revised syllabus is due for implementation and SCSA is deciding what support materials to produce first. Budget and officer time allow for roughly one major resource set this year.", "speakers": [{"who": "Mr Owen Castellano, SCSA Arts curriculum officer, in the room", "voice": "m", "said": "Our resourcing is honest about itself: we can produce one substantial set of materials this year, perhaps two smaller ones. Teachers have asked for three things: sample assessment tasks, annotated work samples at each grade, and a scope and sequence. My instinct is that annotated work samples do the most, because they show the standard rather than describe it. A description of an A grade is a sentence; a sample is a conversation starter in every staffroom. But I'm genuinely open to the committee's view, because whatever we choose, we'll be living with it for several years."}, {"who": "Ms Harriet Doyle, Arts head of learning area, online", "voice": "f", "said": "I'd go further than Owen. Work samples are what moderation runs on. Without them every school invents its own standard, and then we wonder why the grades don't line up across the system. When my team moderates internally, the argument is always about what a B looks like in composition. A sample would settle that in five minutes. If SCSA produces one thing, let it be samples that make the standard visible."}, {"who": "Mr Tom Brierley, principal, online with lag", "voice": "m", "said": "Sorry, I came in late, the link dropped. On the support materials, I'd just say from a principal's perspective that the people teaching Year 7 and 8 music in many schools are not music teachers. They're generalists filling a line, or a drama teacher picking up a class. Samples are lovely for specialists, but a non-specialist doesn't know what to do on Monday. For them, the first need is a unit they can actually teach, with the lessons in it. Otherwise the syllabus is a document nobody opens."}, {"who": "Dr Grace Whitford, music education lecturer, in the room", "voice": "f", "said": "I want to support both views and add a caution. Research on curriculum implementation is fairly consistent that resources alone change little unless teachers use them together. A beautiful folder sent to schools tends to sit on a shared drive. The materials that shift practice are the ones teachers meet in a professional setting, discussed, tried and brought back. So whichever resource we choose, the delivery matters as much as the content. A resource without a meeting is a resource without a reader."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Castellano", "point": "Annotated samples show the standard better than descriptors", "phrase": "a sample is a conversation starter in every staffroom"}, {"who": "Doyle", "point": "Samples underpin moderation and consistent grades", "phrase": "samples that make the standard visible"}, {"who": "Brierley", "point": "Non-specialists need a teachable unit first", "phrase": "a non-specialist doesn't know what to do on Monday"}, {"who": "Whitford", "point": "Resources change practice only when used together", "phrase": "A resource without a meeting is a resource without a reader"}], "question": "Which single resource set should SCSA produce first for the revised syllabus, and how will it reach teachers?", "gap": "The room treats samples and teachable units as rivals. No one has proposed a combined design: one exemplar unit per year level whose assessment produces the annotated samples, launched through network moderation meetings.", "modelNotes": "Q: which ONE resource first? how does it reach teachers?\nROOM: OC # 1 big set or 2 small, prefers samples, \"a sample is a conversation starter in every staffroom\"\nROOM: HD ≈ OC, moderation, \"samples that make the standard visible\"\nROOM: TB (late, lag) ✗ generalists \"a non-specialist doesn't know what to do on Monday\"\nME: ! TB = who actually teaches Yr 7-8\nROOM: GW research: use together, \"a resource without a meeting is a resource without a reader\"\nME: ✓ GW, delivery = half the job\nME: ∴ samples vs units = false choice\nME: Δ 1 unit per yr level, its task → annotated samples\nME: Δ launch via network moderation days, Term 2\nME: # 4 units = 1 set, fits OC budget\nME: @ OC scope; @ HD + me draft Yr 7 unit by Term 1\nGAP: no one joined samples + units; no delivery plan", "task": "The chair asks for any final view before the committee advises Owen. Credit two speakers and propose the resource.", "seconds": 120, "model": "Thank you, Chair. I think we are closer to agreement than it sounds. Owen and Harriet made the case that a sample is a conversation starter in every staffroom, and that samples make the standard visible. Tom gave us the reality check: in many schools, a non-specialist doesn't know what to do on Monday. And Grace reminded us that a resource without a meeting is a resource without a reader. Those are not competing views. They are three parts of one design. So the question is not samples or units. It is which single set meets all three needs within Owen's budget. My proposal has three parts. One: SCSA produces one exemplar unit for each of Years 7 to 10, written so a generalist can teach it, with lessons sequenced and recordings included. Two: each unit ends in an assessment task, and the student work from that task becomes the *annotated samples*. One build, two products, which keeps us inside one substantial set. Three: the units are launched through network moderation days in Term 2, so teachers meet the samples in a room, not on a shared drive. | That gives Tom his Monday, Harriet her standard, and Grace her reader. For the minutes: the committee advises that the first resource set be exemplar units for Years 7 to 10, with assessment tasks producing annotated work samples, launched through network moderation in Term 2. Harriet and I could draft the Year 7 unit as a prototype for Owen by the end of Term 1."}, {"id": "ns-a4", "room": "SCSA Music General course assessment review group, hybrid, 7 members, 4 online", "item": "Review of this year's externally set task and the design brief for next year", "context": "Teachers have reported that this year's externally set task was harder than intended. The group must decide what goes into next year's design brief.", "speakers": [{"who": "Ms Rebecca Sloane, SCSA assessment consultant, in the room", "voice": "f", "said": "Thank you for coming. To set the scene: we received more teacher feedback on this year's task than on any task in recent memory, and almost all of it said the same thing. The listening section was pitched at an ATAR standard rather than a General one. I want to be careful, though. Feedback tells us how teachers felt; it does not yet tell us how students performed. The results data won't be fully available until after the marking period. So the brief we write today should be provisional, and written so that the data can still change it."}, {"who": "Mr Callum Reid, music teacher, online", "voice": "m", "said": "With respect, Rebecca, the students told us how they performed. I had kids walk out of that task in tears. These are students who chose the General course because they love music and want to make it, not analyse it. The task asked them to identify a modulation from an unfamiliar score excerpt. That's not General. Honestly, we need to stop designing these tasks for the students we wish we had and design them for the students we've got."}, {"who": "Associate Professor Neil Tan, university, online", "voice": "m", "said": "I'd agree with the direction but be precise about the problem. The difficulty may not have been the concept; it may have been the format. Identifying a modulation aurally, with a guided question, is achievable at this level. Identifying it from notation without scaffolding is a different skill entirely. In my experience, students fail at the interface more often than at the idea. So before we lower the demand, we should check whether the question design, rather than the content, did the damage."}, {"who": "Mrs Joanne Pirie, head of learning area, in the room", "voice": "f", "said": "I just want to say we've been here before. Three or four years ago we had a very similar conversation about the composition task, and we fixed it by adding a scaffolded template. It worked, and teachers loved it. I wonder if the answer here is the same kind of fix. We came back to that one in the end because the template was published early, and teachers could prepare students for the format."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Sloane", "point": "Feedback isn't performance data; keep the brief provisional", "phrase": "Feedback tells us how teachers felt"}, {"who": "Reid", "point": "Task was pitched above General students", "phrase": "design them for the students we've got"}, {"who": "Tan", "point": "Question format, not content, may have caused failure", "phrase": "students fail at the interface more often than at the idea"}, {"who": "Pirie", "point": "Earlier scaffold fix worked because published early", "phrase": "the template was published early"}], "question": "What should next year's design brief require, given strong teacher feedback but incomplete results data?", "gap": "No one has proposed how to separate content difficulty from format difficulty in the data. An item-level analysis, plus an early published sample of question formats, would answer Neil and Callum together.", "modelNotes": "Q: what goes in next yr brief, w/o full data?\nROOM: RS ! record feedback volume, \"feedback tells us how teachers felt\", data after marking\nME: ✓ provisional brief\nROOM: CR ✗ RS, kids in tears, modulation from score, \"design them for the students we've got\"\nROOM: NT Δ format not concept, \"students fail at the interface more often than at the idea\"\nME: ★ NT = testable\nROOM: JP ⟲ composition task, scaffold, \"the template was published early\"\nME: ≈ JP + NT → publish formats early\nME: Δ 1 item analysis by question type, aural vs notation\nME: Δ 2 brief: listening Qs guided + aural first\nME: Δ 3 sample paper of formats out in Term 1\nME: @ RS data to group by Feb mtg\nGAP: no method to split content from format", "task": "Rebecca asks if anyone can help shape the brief. Credit two speakers and propose what the brief should say.", "seconds": 120, "model": "Thank you, Rebecca. I'd like to try to hold Callum's urgency and Neil's precision together, because I think they lead to the same place. Callum asked us to design them for the students we've got, and he's right that a General student should not be decoding a modulation from an unfamiliar score. Neil then gave us the sharper diagnosis: students fail at the interface more often than at the idea. If he's right, we can keep the musical demand and fix the question. And Joanne reminded us that the last fix worked because the template was published early. So the question for the brief is how we act now without guessing at the data. I'd suggest three things. One: Rebecca's team runs an *item analysis* by question type, comparing aural items with notation items, so we can see whether format or content did the damage. Two: the provisional brief requires listening questions at this level to be guided and aural first, with notation used only in support. That responds to Callum now, and the data can confirm it later. Three: following Joanne's precedent, a short sample of question formats goes to schools in Term 1, so students meet the interface before the exam rather than in it. | For the minutes: the group endorses a provisional design brief requiring guided, aural-first listening questions, subject to item analysis reported at the February meeting, with sample formats released to schools in Term 1."}, {"id": "ns-a5", "room": "University conservatorium and schools partnership committee, hybrid, 10 members, 4 on Zoom", "item": "Proposed early-offer pathway for Year 12 music applicants", "context": "The conservatorium wants to offer conditional early places to strong Year 12 musicians, based on an audition and a school recommendation. Schools have been asked for their view before the scheme is approved.", "speakers": [{"who": "Professor Lydia Marchetti, head of conservatorium, in the room", "voice": "f", "said": "Thank you for engaging with this. The rationale is simple. We are losing our strongest applicants to interstate institutions that make offers earlier than we do. A student offered a place in August by another conservatorium is unlikely to wait until December for us. An early pathway lets us say to a gifted young musician, we want you, before someone else does. The offer would remain conditional on completing the WACE, so academic standards are protected. What we need from schools is a recommendation and some help with timing."}, {"who": "Dr Martin Gale, associate dean, online", "voice": "m", "said": "Building on Lydia's point, there's a broader principle here about recognising capability rather than only a rank. Performance at this level is evidence of sustained discipline over many years. Several institutions nationally now weigh portfolios and auditions alongside or ahead of the rank for creative disciplines, and I think we should see this as part of that movement. The early offer is a statement of values as much as a recruitment tool. It says we see the musician, not just the number."}, {"who": "Ms Priya Raman, school careers coordinator, online", "voice": "f", "said": "Can I ask a practical question? When are the auditions? Because if they fall in August, they sit right on top of the ATAR practical examinations, and our music students are preparing two different programs at once. I've had students sit an interstate audition and a practical exam within a fortnight and both suffered. Whatever the timing, it needs to be designed around the school year, not just the university's recruitment calendar."}, {"who": "Mr Declan Ross, independent school director of music, in the room", "voice": "m", "said": "I'll be the contrarian. An early offer can quietly tell a student that Year 12 no longer matters. I've seen students coast after an early place, and some of them then underperform badly enough that the condition becomes a problem. I'm not against the scheme, but if we sign up, schools carry the risk of the disengaged student for four months, and the university gets the credit for the offer. There has to be something that keeps the student playing and learning between offer and enrolment."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Marchetti", "point": "Strong applicants lost to earlier interstate offers", "phrase": "we want you, before someone else does"}, {"who": "Gale", "point": "Early offers recognise capability beyond the rank", "phrase": "It says we see the musician, not just the number"}, {"who": "Raman", "point": "Audition timing must avoid ATAR practical exams", "phrase": "designed around the school year"}, {"who": "Ross", "point": "Early offers risk disengagement; schools carry that risk", "phrase": "schools carry the risk of the disengaged student"}], "question": "Should schools support the early-offer pathway, and on what conditions about timing, repertoire and engagement?", "gap": "No one has proposed concrete conditions that would make schools willing partners: aligned audition dates and repertoire, a standard school reference, and a structured link between offer and enrolment.", "modelNotes": "Q: support early offers? on what conditions?\nROOM: LM losing students interstate, \"we want you, before someone else does\"\nME: ✓ intent, conditional on WACE\nROOM: MG values, \"it says we see the musician, not just the number\"\nME: ≈ LM, little new\nROOM: PR ? timing → Aug = ATAR practical, \"designed around the school year\" ★\nROOM: DR ✗ coasting, \"schools carry the risk of the disengaged student\"\nME: ✓ DR fair, needs a mechanism\nME: Δ 1 auditions before end Term 2 OR accept ATAR exam program\nME: Δ 2 one-page school reference template\nME: Δ 3 offer holders join a uni ensemble, Term 4 + offer conditional on WACE\nME: @ LM + 2 school reps draft by Feb\nGAP: no conditions that make schools partners", "task": "The chair invites a school perspective. Credit two speakers, state the question, and set out conditions.", "seconds": 150, "model": "Thank you, Chair, and thank you, Lydia, for putting the case so plainly: we want you, before someone else does. Every school director in this room has watched a student leave the state for exactly that reason, so I support the intent. I lead a specialist music program, and I've prepared students for both conservatorium auditions and the ATAR practical exam in the same term, so I want to speak to how this works on the ground. Priya named the first risk: the scheme has to be designed around the school year. Declan named the second: schools carry the risk of the disengaged student. Both are fixable. So the question is not whether schools support early offers. It is what conditions turn schools from bystanders into *partners*. I'd propose three. One: timing. Auditions are held before the end of Term 2, or the conservatorium accepts the student's ATAR practical program as the audition program, so no student prepares twice. Two: a one-page standard school reference, agreed with schools, so recommendations are comparable and quick to write. Three: engagement. Offer holders join a university ensemble or masterclass series in Term 4, and the offer stays conditional on completing the WACE, which keeps the student playing and gives Declan's concern a mechanism rather than a hope. | For the minutes: the committee supports the pathway in principle, subject to conditions on timing, a standard reference and a Term 4 engagement program, to be drafted by Lydia's office with two school representatives and brought back in February. I'm happy to be one of them."}, {"id": "ns-a6", "room": "University initial teacher education partnership committee, in person, 9 members", "item": "Professional experience placements for secondary music pre-service teachers", "context": "The university is struggling to find placement schools for secondary music students. Partner schools have been asked why they decline and what would change their minds.", "speakers": [{"who": "Dr Elaine Foster, professional experience director, chairing", "voice": "f", "said": "Our position is difficult. This year we placed fewer than half of our music students in a music classroom; the rest went to general placements and taught other subjects. That isn't good enough for them or for the profession. What we hear from schools is that mentoring is a burden. I'd like to understand what sits behind that, so we can design something schools actually want to say yes to."}, {"who": "Mr Hamish O'Rourke, metropolitan deputy principal", "voice": "m", "said": "I'll speak frankly. A placement is ten weeks of a senior teacher's attention, on top of a full load, for a payment that barely covers a coffee a week. And in music, the mentor isn't only supervising lessons. They're supervising rehearsals, instrumental programs, concerts, excursions. The pre-service teacher wants to see all of that, and they should. But we are asking our best people to do two jobs for the price of none. That's why they say no."}, {"who": "Ms Kiri Walton, music teacher and experienced mentor", "voice": "f", "said": "I love mentoring, and I still said no this year. It wasn't the hours. It was the timing. The university placement fell in the term we have our major concert season, and I couldn't give a student the attention they deserve while running four ensembles at the same time. If placements came in a quieter term, I'd say yes tomorrow. The calendar decides whether I can mentor well, not my goodwill."}, {"who": "Professor Andrew Bell, head of school, online", "voice": "m", "said": "I'd add the national picture. Every state is facing the same pressures on placements, and the response elsewhere has often been structural: paired placements, where two pre-service teachers share one mentor, and hub schools that take a larger number and receive more support. I'd caution against seeing this as a music problem. It's a mentoring problem that music shows us most clearly, because music teaching extends well beyond the timetable."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Foster", "point": "Fewer than half placed in music classrooms", "phrase": "something schools actually want to say yes to"}, {"who": "O'Rourke", "point": "Mentors carry double load for negligible recognition", "phrase": "two jobs for the price of none"}, {"who": "Walton", "point": "Placement timing clashes with concert season", "phrase": "The calendar decides whether I can mentor well"}, {"who": "Bell", "point": "Structural models elsewhere: paired placements, hub schools", "phrase": "music shows us most clearly"}], "question": "What placement model would lead partner schools to accept music pre-service teachers next year?", "gap": "No one has turned the extra-curricular load into part of the placement design. A music-specific model that times placements away from concert seasons, pairs students, and makes ensemble work an assessed component would answer all three concerns.", "modelNotes": "Q: what model makes schools say yes in 2027?\nROOM: EF # < half placed in music, \"something schools actually want to say yes to\"\nROOM: HO'R ✗ burden, ensembles + concerts, \"two jobs for the price of none\"\nROOM: KW ! not hours, timing, \"the calendar decides whether I can mentor well\" ★\nME: ≈ KW, my school: Term 3 concert season too\nROOM: AB national: paired + hub schools, \"music shows us most clearly\"\nME: ✓ AB, ∴ build music model not generic\nME: Δ 1 music placements Term 1 / early Term 2, not concert terms\nME: Δ 2 pairs: 2 PSTs, 1 mentor, one leads an ensemble\nME: Δ 3 ensemble work assessed → PST does real work, mentor gets help\nME: Δ 4 hub status + PL hours for mentors\nME: @ EF draft model, pilot 3 schools 2027\nGAP: no one made ensemble load part of the design", "task": "Elaine asks the school representatives what they would accept. Credit two speakers and propose a model.", "seconds": 150, "model": "Thank you, Elaine, for asking the question that way, because I think the answer is a design question rather than a goodwill question. Hamish put it bluntly: two jobs for the price of none. Kiri then told us something I think is decisive: the calendar decides whether I can mentor well. That matches my own school, where Term 3 is the concert season and I would not offer a placement then either. And Andrew pointed out that music shows us most clearly a problem every subject has. So the question is what model makes schools say yes next year. I'd propose four things. One: *timing*. Music placements are scheduled in Term 1 or early Term 2, away from concert seasons. Two: pairs. Two pre-service teachers share one mentor, as Andrew described, so the mentor's attention is shared but the pre-service teachers support each other. Three: the ensemble work becomes an assessed component of the placement. Each pre-service teacher leads a sectional or a junior ensemble under supervision. That turns Hamish's second job into real help for the mentor, and it gives the student exactly the experience they most need. Four: schools taking pairs receive hub status and recognised professional learning hours for their mentors. | For the minutes: the committee asks the professional experience office to draft a music placement model incorporating timing, pairing, an assessed ensemble component and mentor recognition, for a pilot in three partner schools in 2027. My school would be willing to be one of the three."}, {"id": "ns-a7", "room": "Conservatorium schools outreach steering group, hybrid, 8 members, 3 on Zoom", "item": "Renewal of the schools outreach agreement for the next three years", "context": "The conservatorium's undergraduate ensembles have visited partner schools under an informal agreement. The agreement is up for renewal and the university wants evidence that it is worth continuing.", "speakers": [{"who": "Ms Naomi Kerr, conservatorium engagement manager, chairing", "voice": "f", "said": "The good news is that everyone wants to renew. The harder news is that the university has asked us to show impact, and at the moment our evidence is a folder of thank-you emails and some lovely photos. They're genuine, but they won't persuade a budget committee. So today I'd like us to agree what the next agreement should look like and how we'll know whether it's working. I'd also like us to be realistic about staff time on both sides."}, {"who": "Associate Professor Julian Price, ensembles, online", "voice": "m", "said": "I'd resist a purely metric approach. The value of a student orchestra arriving at a school is partly unmeasurable. A twelve-year-old sitting a metre from a cellist experiences something no survey captures. I've seen students decide on a career in a single concert. If we reduce outreach to counting, we may end up optimising for what is easy to count. The magic is the point, and the magic doesn't fit in a spreadsheet."}, {"who": "Mr Ben Achebe, school music teacher, in the room", "voice": "m", "said": "I agree it's magic, but I'll tell you what happens in practice. The ensemble visits, the kids love it, and then nothing follows. There's no workshop, no follow-up, no link to what we're teaching that term. Most of my students couldn't tell you a week later what they heard. The visits are wonderful one-offs. What would change things is continuity: the same undergraduates coming back, working with our ensembles, so the students form a relationship. Right now it's a visit, not a partnership."}, {"who": "Dr Sophie Lindqvist, music education researcher, online, with delay", "voice": "f", "said": "Sorry, there's a delay on my end. I'd just note that impact doesn't have to mean large surveys. Small, consistent measures work well: the number of repeat visits per school, the number of school students who later enrol in instrumental tuition, and a short teacher reflection after each term. Three measures, collected the same way every year, will tell a budget committee more than a hundred photos. Consistency is more persuasive than volume."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Kerr", "point": "University wants impact evidence beyond thank-you emails", "phrase": "a folder of thank-you emails"}, {"who": "Price", "point": "Outreach value partly unmeasurable; beware counting", "phrase": "the magic doesn't fit in a spreadsheet"}, {"who": "Achebe", "point": "One-off visits lack continuity and follow-up", "phrase": "it's a visit, not a partnership"}, {"who": "Lindqvist", "point": "Three consistent small measures persuade budget committees", "phrase": "Consistency is more persuasive than volume"}], "question": "What should the renewed agreement commit both sides to, and how will its impact be shown?", "gap": "No one has linked the design of the program to the evidence. A model built on repeat visits to fewer schools produces Sophie's measures naturally, addresses Ben's continuity point, and protects Julian's magic.", "modelNotes": "Q: what does new agreement commit to + how show impact?\nROOM: NK need evidence, now \"a folder of thank-you emails\"\nROOM: JP ✗ metrics, \"the magic doesn't fit in a spreadsheet\"\nME: ✓ JP, but magic needs a second visit\nROOM: BA ! no follow-up, \"it's a visit, not a partnership\" ★\nROOM: SL (delay) # 3 measures, \"consistency is more persuasive than volume\"\nME: ≈ SL + BA → design produces evidence\nME: Δ 1 fewer schools, 3 visits each per yr, same undergrads\nME: Δ 2 each visit = concert + workshop w school ensemble\nME: Δ 3 SL measures: repeat visits, tuition enrolments, teacher reflection\nME: @ named liaison each school; calendar locked by wk 3 Term 1\nME: @ NK report to uni Dec 2027\nGAP: program design and evidence treated separately", "task": "Naomi asks the group for a shape for the agreement. Credit two speakers and propose it.", "seconds": 120, "model": "Thank you, Naomi. I think the most useful thing said today was Ben's line: it's a visit, not a partnership. And Sophie gave us the answer to your budget committee: consistency is more persuasive than volume. I'd also hold on to Julian's point, because he's right that the magic doesn't fit in a spreadsheet. But in my experience as a school director, the magic only lasts if there's a second visit. A student who meets the same cellist three times is the one who asks about lessons. So the question is what the agreement commits both sides to, and whether the design itself can generate the evidence. I'd propose three things. One: fewer schools, deeper. Each partner school receives three visits a year from the *same* group of undergraduates. Two: every visit pairs a short concert with a workshop alongside the school's own ensemble, tied to what that school is teaching that term. Three: we adopt Sophie's three measures exactly: repeat visits per school, school students later enrolling in instrumental tuition, and a short teacher reflection each term. Two practical conditions make it work: a named liaison at each school, and the visit calendar locked by week 3 of Term 1. | That gives the budget committee numbers, gives Ben continuity, and gives Julian's magic the chance to land. For the minutes: the group recommends a renewed three-year agreement based on repeat visits, paired concerts and workshops, and three agreed impact measures, with Naomi reporting to the university in December 2027."}, {"id": "ns-a8", "room": "State arts funding panel, youth and education stream, online, 6 assessors", "item": "Final ranking of the youth music applications for this round", "context": "The round is heavily oversubscribed: the strong applications together request well over the available pool. The panel must decide whether to fund fewer projects fully or more projects partially.", "speakers": [{"who": "Ms Margaret Ellison, panel chair and senior program officer", "voice": "f", "said": "We have eleven applications rated competitive, and funds for perhaps six of them at the requested amounts. The guidelines allow partial funding, but they also say that partial funding should not compromise a project's viability. So our decision is really a philosophical one: do we fund six projects properly or nine projects thinly? I'd like the panel's view before we finalise the ranking. We also need to finish today, because applicants were promised outcomes by the end of the month, and several of them are planning school-term activities around our answer."}, {"who": "Mr Victor Lemaire, arts consultant", "voice": "m", "said": "I've sat on many of these panels and I'm firmly of the view that thin funding is false generosity. You fund nine projects at seventy per cent, and three of them quietly collapse, or deliver a diminished version that disappoints everybody, including the young people. Funders feel good, applicants feel grateful, and the outcome is worse. Spreading the money is a kindness to the panel, not to the sector. Fund six properly and write constructive feedback to the rest."}, {"who": "Ms Tayla Morgan, regional arts officer", "voice": "f", "said": "The trouble with that is who ends up in the six. The highest-scoring applications are written by organisations that have grant writers. Three of the five regional applications sit just below the line, and they're the ones with the least access to other money. If we fund six fully, the outcome is metropolitan by default. I'm not saying lower the bar; I'm saying be aware of who wrote the bar."}, {"who": "Dr Ian Pascoe, academic assessor", "voice": "m", "said": "Can I add a procedural point? We're debating partial funding in the abstract. The guidelines say partial funding must not compromise viability, but none of us actually knows what a viable reduced version of each project looks like. Only the applicants know that. Some of these budgets clearly have flexibility; others are already lean. Without that information, we're guessing on their behalf. I'd rather ask them than assume, and the guidelines do permit us to seek clarification from applicants before a final decision."}, {"who": "Ms Grace Abernethy, community music director", "voice": "f", "said": "Just to add, the youth orchestra application, I know that organisation well, and they've had a difficult few years with their venue, and I think their new conductor is doing remarkable work, though I'm not sure that's in the application. They also had a lovely tour a few years ago. Anyway, I'd hate to see them miss out. Their kids are wonderful, and I've seen them play many times myself."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Ellison", "point": "Choose six funded fully or nine partially", "phrase": "six projects properly or nine projects thinly"}, {"who": "Lemaire", "point": "Partial funding causes failures and diminished outcomes", "phrase": "thin funding is false generosity"}, {"who": "Morgan", "point": "Full funding favours metropolitan grant writers", "phrase": "be aware of who wrote the bar"}, {"who": "Pascoe", "point": "Only applicants know viable reduced budgets", "phrase": "we're guessing on their behalf"}, {"who": "Abernethy", "point": "Personal knowledge of one applicant, beyond the application", "phrase": "I'm not sure that's in the application"}], "question": "Does the panel fund fewer projects fully or more projects partially, and how does it test viability?", "gap": "No one has proposed asking the borderline applicants for scaled budgets before ranking, and Grace's comment raises a conflict-of-interest and evidence question no one has named.", "modelNotes": "Q: 6 full or 9 partial? how test viability?\nROOM: MEll # 11 competitive, funds ≈ 6, \"six projects properly or nine projects thinly\"\nROOM: VL ✗ partial, \"thin funding is false generosity\"\nROOM: TM ! 3 of 5 regional just below line, \"be aware of who wrote the bar\"\nME: ✓ TM, equity real\nROOM: IP ★ \"we're guessing on their behalf\"\nME: ∴ IP resolves VL vs TM\nROOM: GA drift, personal knowledge, \"I'm not sure that's in the application\"\nME: ! assess on application only, ? GA declare\nME: Δ 1 fund top 5 fully now\nME: Δ 2 next 6 asked for scaled budget + viability statement, 5 working days\nME: Δ 3 rank those on the same criteria, check regional spread\nME: @ ME emails today, panel reconvenes in 2 wks\nGAP: no one asked applicants; conflict unnamed", "task": "Margaret asks for a recommendation. Credit two speakers, deal with the evidence issue tactfully, and propose a method.", "seconds": 150, "model": "Thank you, Margaret. I think Ian has given us the way through. Victor's warning that thin funding is false generosity is right, and so is Tayla's reminder to be aware of who wrote the bar. Those two positions only conflict because, as Ian said, without real budgets we're guessing on their behalf. So the question is not six or nine. It is how we find out which reductions are genuinely viable before we rank. Before I propose a method, one gentle procedural point. Grace, I know your knowledge of the youth orchestra comes from real care, but we need to assess on what's in the application, and it may be worth recording whether you have any association with them. That protects you and the panel. My proposal has three steps. One: fund the top five applications fully now, since their merit is clear and the pool allows it. Two: the next six applicants are asked for a *scaled budget* and a short viability statement, with five working days to respond. Three: we rank those six on the published criteria, using their own scaled figures, and we check the final list for regional spread before confirming. That answers Victor, because nothing is funded below what the applicant says is viable. It answers Tayla, because the regional projects get to tell us what they can do. | For the record: the panel recommends full funding for the top five and a scaled-budget request to the next six, with Margaret's office sending requests today and the panel reconvening in two weeks to finalise."}, {"id": "ns-a9", "room": "Philanthropic foundation grants advisory panel, hybrid, 7 members, 2 online", "item": "Guidelines for next year's regional instrument access round", "context": "The foundation has funded instruments for regional schools for several years. A review found many instruments unused or in disrepair, and the panel is redrafting the guidelines.", "speakers": [{"who": "Mr Richard Halloran, foundation chair, in the room", "voice": "m", "said": "I'll start with the uncomfortable finding. When our staff visited past recipients, a worrying number of the instruments we funded were in cupboards, some broken, some never used. Our donors bought those instruments because they imagined children playing them. I don't blame the schools; I suspect we designed the program badly. An instrument in a cupboard is a gift that hasn't arrived yet. I'd like the new guidelines to fix that."}, {"who": "Ms Eleanor Watts, retired music educator, in the room", "voice": "f", "said": "I can tell you exactly why it happens, because I taught in the country for twenty years. The instrument arrives, the one teacher who applied for it moves to Perth after two years, and nobody else can teach it. Or a clarinet needs a pad, and the nearest repairer is five hours away. The instrument outlives the teacher. If the guidelines don't fund the teaching and the maintenance, you are just buying future cupboard stock."}, {"who": "Mr Lachlan Fry, donor representative, online", "voice": "m", "said": "From the donors' side, I'd be cautious about drifting into funding salaries. Our donors give to tangible things. They like to know their money bought a cello. If we turn this into a tuition program, I think we lose some of our donors, and then there's less money for everyone. A cello is a story people can tell. A contribution to staffing is harder to love. Our donors are generous, but they're also proud, and they want to point to something."}, {"who": "Ms Anika Rao, foundation program manager, in the room", "voice": "f", "said": "One fact that might help. Our grant agreements currently have no reporting requirement after the first year. So the school receives the instruments, sends one report, and we never hear from them again. We genuinely don't know what's happened in most cases; we only found the cupboards because we visited. Whatever we change, we have to fix the silence after year one. Most of our recipients would happily report if we simply asked."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Halloran", "point": "Funded instruments found unused or broken", "phrase": "a gift that hasn't arrived yet"}, {"who": "Watts", "point": "Teachers leave and repairs are distant", "phrase": "The instrument outlives the teacher"}, {"who": "Fry", "point": "Donors prefer tangible items over salaries", "phrase": "A cello is a story people can tell"}, {"who": "Rao", "point": "No reporting after year one hides outcomes", "phrase": "the silence after year one"}], "question": "How should the guidelines change so that funded instruments are played for their whole life, while keeping donors engaged?", "gap": "The room frames tuition against tangible gifts. No one has proposed that sustainability be a condition of the grant rather than a separate cost: a teaching plan, a maintenance allocation inside the instrument budget, and a multi-year check-in.", "modelNotes": "Q: guidelines so instruments get played, donors still on board?\nROOM: RH ! instruments in cupboards, \"a gift that hasn't arrived yet\"\nROOM: EW ★ teacher leaves, repairer 5 hrs, \"the instrument outlives the teacher\"\nROOM: LF ✗ salaries, \"a cello is a story people can tell\"\nME: ✓ LF, keep the cello as the gift\nROOM: RA ! no reports after yr 1, \"the silence after year one\"\nME: ∴ conditions, not salaries\nME: Δ 1 application includes a 3-yr teaching plan (who teaches, what if they leave)\nME: Δ 2 % of grant ring-fenced for maintenance + 1 repair trip/yr\nME: Δ 3 short report each yr for 3 yrs, photo of instrument in use\nME: ≈ LF: reports give donors the story\nME: @ AR redraft guidelines by Feb panel\nGAP: sustainability as grant condition, not extra cost", "task": "Richard asks for practical changes to the guidelines. Credit two speakers and propose them.", "seconds": 120, "model": "Thank you, Richard, for opening with the hard finding, and for the line that will stay with me: a gift that hasn't arrived yet. Eleanor told us why: the instrument outlives the teacher. And Anika told us why we didn't know: the silence after year one. I think Lachlan's point matters too. A cello is a story people can tell, and we shouldn't lose that. So the question is how we make sure the instruments are played for their whole life without turning this into a staffing program. My answer is that sustainability becomes a *condition* of the grant, not an extra cost. Three changes. One: every application includes a three-year teaching plan. Who will teach the instruments, and what happens if that person leaves. That's Eleanor's problem, answered at the point of application. Two: a fixed share of each grant is ring-fenced for maintenance, including one repairer visit a year, so a missing pad doesn't retire a clarinet. The donor still buys the cello; the grant simply keeps it playable. Three: a short annual report for three years, with a photograph of the instruments in use. That ends the silence, and it gives Lachlan's donors exactly the story they want: not the cello arriving, but a child still playing it in year three. | For the minutes: the panel asks Anika to redraft the guidelines to include a three-year teaching plan, a ring-fenced maintenance allocation and annual reporting for three years, for consideration at the February meeting."}, {"id": "ns-a10", "room": "Principal's leadership meeting, in person, 11 members", "item": "2027 timetable: protecting specialist music program lines", "context": "The timetabler has flagged that next year's lines cannot hold every elective, and the specialist music classes have been proposed for a shared line. Dijon attends as Teacher in Charge of Music.", "speakers": [{"who": "Ms Patricia Langford, principal, chairing", "voice": "f", "said": "We have to make some hard calls on the timetable, and I want us to make them as a team, not by attrition in the timetabler's office. The constraint is real. We have more electives than lines, and something has to share. I'm also aware that the specialist program is one of the reasons families choose this school. So let's hear the options and keep the whole school in view, not just our own area."}, {"who": "Mr Darren Kowalski, deputy principal and timetabler", "voice": "m", "said": "Here's the arithmetic. If specialist music keeps its own dedicated line in every year group, three electives in Years 9 and 10 have to merge or run off-line. If we put specialist music on a shared line with two other subjects, everything fits. I'm not choosing a winner. I'm just telling you the timetable is a zero-sum game, and someone always loses a line. I can model either option for you by next week, but I need a decision of principle first."}, {"who": "Ms Fiona Grady, head of mathematics", "voice": "f", "said": "I'll say what others are thinking. Specialist music has had a protected line for years, and it's a small number of students relative to the cohort. Mathematics has had to compromise on extension classes every year. I'm not against music, but fairness means everyone shares the pain. A protected line for a few dozen students is hard to justify to the rest of the staff. I'd want any exception to be argued on evidence, not history."}, {"who": "Mr Kevin Ashby, business manager", "voice": "m", "said": "Just to add the financial side. Specialist program students bring additional funding tied to the program, and enrolments from out of area depend on it. If the program weakens, we'd likely see it in enrolments within two years, and that affects every budget at this table. So the timetable decision has a budget tail. I'd urge us not to treat this as a matter of numbers alone. Out-of-area families tell us the program is the reason they apply."}, {"who": "Ms Leanne Okoye, student services manager", "voice": "f", "said": "From my side, the students in the specialist program are a cohort with strong attendance and wellbeing, and they're also the students most at risk of overload. If music is on a shared line, some of them will have to choose between music and another subject they need. We've had students in tears over that choice before. Whatever we do, we shouldn't create a choice they can't win. Some of these students are in Year 10, choosing pathways that matter for their ATAR years."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Langford", "point": "Decide together; keep the whole school in view", "phrase": "not by attrition in the timetabler's office"}, {"who": "Kowalski", "point": "Dedicated music line forces three electives to merge", "phrase": "the timetable is a zero-sum game"}, {"who": "Grady", "point": "Protected line for few students seems unfair", "phrase": "fairness means everyone shares the pain"}, {"who": "Ashby", "point": "Program funding and enrolments depend on it", "phrase": "the timetable decision has a budget tail"}, {"who": "Okoye", "point": "Shared lines force impossible choices on students", "phrase": "a choice they can't win"}], "question": "Does specialist music keep a dedicated line in 2027, and if not, what protects the program and its students?", "gap": "The room frames it as protected versus shared. No one has proposed protecting the program's selected cohort in Years 7 and 8 while testing a shared line in Years 9 and 10 against actual student choice data, nor asked for clash data before deciding.", "modelNotes": "Q: dedicated music line 2027? if not, what protects program + students?\nROOM: PL \"not by attrition in the timetabler's office\", whole school\nROOM: DK # dedicated → 3 electives merge Yr 9-10, \"the timetable is a zero-sum game\"\nROOM: FG ✗ \"fairness means everyone shares the pain\"\nME: ✓ FG on fairness, ✗ numbers framing\nROOM: KA ! funding + out-of-area enrolments, \"the timetable decision has a budget tail\"\nROOM: LO \"a choice they can't win\" ★\nME: ≈ LO + KA = case for Yr 7-8\nME: Δ 1 dedicated line Yr 7-8 (selected entry, contract)\nME: Δ 2 Yr 9-10 shared line IF clash data shows < handful of clashes\nME: Δ 3 DK runs clash report on 2027 selections before decision\nME: @ DK + me, report to next leadership mtg\nGAP: no data on actual clashes; protected vs shared = false binary", "task": "Patricia turns to you as Teacher in Charge of Music. Acknowledge the fairness concern, credit two speakers, and propose a solution.", "seconds": 150, "model": "Thank you, Patricia, and thank you for saying this should be decided as a team, not by attrition in the timetabler's office. I want to start by acknowledging Fiona's point, because it's fair. Fairness means everyone shares the pain, and music shouldn't be exempt just because it always has been. Darren's right that the timetable is a zero-sum game. But Kevin and Leanne have given us two facts we can't set aside. The timetable decision has a budget tail, because out-of-area enrolments depend on the program. And Leanne warned us against creating a choice they can't win. So the question is not protected or shared. It is where a dedicated line is genuinely necessary, and where it isn't. I'd propose three things. One: Years 7 and 8 keep a dedicated line. That's the selected-entry cohort families enrol for, and it's where Kevin's enrolment risk sits. Two: Years 9 and 10 move to a shared line, *if* the data shows it causes no more than a handful of clashes. That's music sharing the pain, as Fiona asked. Three: before we decide, Darren runs a clash report on the actual 2027 subject selections, so we're deciding on evidence about real students rather than on principle. | I'm happy to sit with Darren on the report. For the minutes: leadership agrees in principle to a dedicated specialist music line in Years 7 and 8, with Years 9 and 10 to be decided on a clash report from Darren and me at the next leadership meeting."}, {"id": "ns-a11", "room": "Principal's leadership meeting, in person with one member on Teams, 10 members", "item": "Student absence from classes for rehearsals, tours and festivals", "context": "Several heads of learning area have raised concerns about students missing lessons for performing arts commitments, particularly in Terms 2 and 3. The principal wants a whole-school position.", "speakers": [{"who": "Mr Stephen Vale, associate principal, chairing", "voice": "m", "said": "The concern isn't that performing arts activities exist; everyone at this table values them. The concern is the cumulative effect. One head of learning area tracked a Year 10 student who missed eleven lessons of a single subject in Term 3 through approved activities. Each absence was reasonable. Together they weren't. I'd like us to leave with a position we can explain to staff and parents. It needs to be fair to every program, and clear enough that a teacher can apply it on a Tuesday."}, {"who": "Ms Helena Burke, head of science, in the room", "voice": "f", "said": "I'll be specific. Science has practical assessments that can't be easily rescheduled. When a student is away for a festival on the day of a practical, my teachers have to set up the whole experiment again for one student. That's an hour of a technician's time and a teacher's goodwill. It's not the absence, it's the invisibility. We often find out the day before. With a fortnight's notice, we could plan around almost anything."}, {"who": "Mr Adam Pryce, head of humanities, on Teams with lag", "voice": "m", "said": "Sorry, I froze briefly. Just to add, I think there's a culture issue too. Some students treat an approved absence as a free pass on the work. They come back and say, I was at band. And because the activity is school-sanctioned, teachers feel awkward chasing them. We need it understood that approved absence is not exemption from learning. I'd like whatever we decide to make that explicit to students and to parents, in writing, so that teachers aren't left negotiating it individually every time."}, {"who": "Ms Rosa Delgado, head of the arts, in the room", "voice": "f", "said": "I understand all of this, but I'd say gently that the arts are being singled out for something sport and other programs also do. Interschool sport, the debating finals, the leadership camps, they all pull students out. If we want a policy, it should be a whole-school policy on approved absences, not an arts policy. Otherwise the message to the music students is that their commitment is a nuisance. These students give up a great deal of their own time."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Vale", "point": "Cumulative absences matter even when each is reasonable", "phrase": "Each absence was reasonable. Together they weren't"}, {"who": "Burke", "point": "Late notice makes practicals hard to reschedule", "phrase": "It's not the absence, it's the invisibility"}, {"who": "Pryce", "point": "Students treat approved absence as exemption", "phrase": "approved absence is not exemption from learning"}, {"who": "Delgado", "point": "Policy should cover all programs, not only arts", "phrase": "a whole-school policy on approved absences"}], "question": "What whole-school position on approved absences will protect learning without penalising students' performing commitments?", "gap": "No one has proposed the practical tools: a published calendar of all approved activities set in advance, a cap trigger that prompts a conversation rather than a ban, and a clear catch-up protocol owned by the student.", "modelNotes": "Q: whole-school position on approved absences, protect learning + commitments?\nROOM: SV # Yr 10 missed 11 lessons in 1 subject T3, \"each absence was reasonable. Together they weren't\"\nME: ✓ SV, cumulative is real\nROOM: HB practicals + technician, \"it's not the absence, it's the invisibility\" ★\nROOM: AP (Teams, froze) \"approved absence is not exemption from learning\"\nME: ✓ AP, music students too\nROOM: RD ✗ arts singled out, \"a whole-school policy on approved absences\"\nME: ≈ RD, all programs\nME: Δ 1 all approved activities on one calendar, published by wk 4 of term before\nME: Δ 2 trigger: 4 absences in one subject → HoLA + coordinator conversation\nME: Δ 3 catch-up protocol: student owns it, form signed before leaving\nME: @ SV + RD + me draft for Term 1 staff mtg\nGAP: no tools: calendar, trigger, catch-up", "task": "Stephen asks the Teacher in Charge of Music to respond. Credit two speakers and propose a position.", "seconds": 120, "model": "Thank you, Stephen. I want to say first that the concern is fair, and I share it. Your example said it exactly: each absence was reasonable; together they weren't. And Helena gave us the heart of the problem: it's not the absence, it's the invisibility. That's something my area can fix immediately. I'd also support Rosa's point that this should be a whole-school policy on approved absences, because sport, debating and camps create the same pattern, and a policy that names only one area will be resented and won't work. So the question is how we protect learning while keeping the commitments that make students want to be here. I'd propose three tools. One: a single calendar. Every approved activity, across all programs, is published by week 4 of the term before. Helena's teachers then know about practicals clashing with festivals weeks ahead, not the day before. Two: a *trigger*, not a ban. When a student reaches four approved absences in one subject in a term, the head of learning area and the program coordinator have a conversation with the student about the load. Three: a catch-up protocol owned by the student. Before leaving, they collect the work, and they return it within an agreed time. That puts Adam's principle into practice: approved absence is not exemption from learning. | For the minutes: leadership supports a whole-school approved absence protocol with a published calendar, a four-absence trigger and student-owned catch-up, drafted by Stephen, Rosa and me for the first staff meeting of Term 1."}, {"id": "ns-a12", "room": "Principal's leadership meeting, budget session, in person, 9 members", "item": "2027 budget: instrument replacement and repair", "context": "The school's instrument fleet is ageing and the music department's repair costs have risen sharply. The business manager has asked whether the department's allocation can absorb it or whether a separate line is needed.", "speakers": [{"who": "Ms Patricia Langford, principal, chairing", "voice": "f", "said": "We have a tight budget and every area has a case. I'd ask us to be disciplined: what's the need, what's the evidence, and what's the option that serves students best over several years rather than this one. I'd rather fund one good plan than three good intentions. I want to see the need, the evidence and the cost over at least three years. A request that only solves next year will come back to this table again, and I'd rather not have that conversation twice."}, {"who": "Mr Kevin Ashby, business manager", "voice": "m", "said": "Here's the picture. Music's repair spending has roughly doubled over three years, and it's coming out of the department's operating allocation, which means it's crowding out everything else. Nothing on the asset register tells us when instruments are due for replacement; we only find out when something fails. So we're running the fleet by breakdown. That's the most expensive way to manage any asset. Every year we patch, and every year the bill gets bigger."}, {"who": "Ms Fiona Grady, head of mathematics", "voice": "f", "said": "I'd ask a fair question: why should instruments get a dedicated line when other areas buy equipment from their own allocation? Science replaces its equipment from its budget. Technologies does too. If music needs more, perhaps the program fees should rise. It's a choice families have made to join a specialist program. I'm happy to be persuaded, but I'd need to see why music is different, and what the families are already paying."}, {"who": "Mr Raymond Choi, deputy principal, finance portfolio", "voice": "m", "said": "Just a thought, and it may be a digression, but have we considered leasing? I read something about schools leasing laptops and it seemed to work well. I'm not sure if instrument leasing exists here, but it might be worth looking at, and it would be off the capital side, I think. Anyway, it's just an idea. Someone could ring around a few suppliers and find out what the terms look like."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Langford", "point": "Fund a multi-year plan that serves students", "phrase": "one good plan than three good intentions"}, {"who": "Ashby", "point": "Repairs doubled; no replacement schedule exists", "phrase": "running the fleet by breakdown"}, {"who": "Grady", "point": "Other areas self-fund equipment; consider higher fees", "phrase": "a choice families have made"}, {"who": "Choi", "point": "Possibly explore instrument leasing", "phrase": "have we considered leasing"}], "question": "Should instrument replacement and repair be funded through a separate multi-year plan, and how is it paid for fairly?", "gap": "No one has proposed the plan Kevin's evidence demands: an asset register with replacement cycles, a rolling multi-year schedule, and a transparent funding split between school, program contributions and hire fees.", "modelNotes": "Q: separate multi-yr line for instruments? who pays, fairly?\nROOM: PL \"one good plan than three good intentions\"\nME: ✓ PL → bring a plan, not a plea\nROOM: KA # repairs ≈ x2 in 3 yrs, no register, \"running the fleet by breakdown\" ★\nME: ≈ KA, he has made my case\nROOM: FG ✗ dedicated line, raise fees, \"a choice families have made\"\nME: ✓ FG partly: contributions can share, not carry\nROOM: RC drift, leasing? \"have we considered leasing\"\nME: ? leasing → test for high-cost items only\nME: Δ 1 register: every instrument, age, condition, cycle, by end Term 1\nME: Δ 2 rolling 5-yr replacement schedule from register\nME: Δ 3 split: school + program contribution + hire fees, published to families\nME: @ KA + me, 2027 interim repairs capped\nGAP: no register, no schedule, no fair split", "task": "Patricia asks the Teacher in Charge of Music to respond. Credit two speakers and propose a plan.", "seconds": 150, "model": "Thank you, Patricia. You asked for one good plan rather than three good intentions, so let me offer a plan. Kevin has given us the diagnosis better than I could: we are running the fleet by breakdown, and that's the most expensive way to manage any asset. Fiona's question is also fair. Music shouldn't ask for something other areas don't get, and families who chose the program can reasonably contribute. So the question is not whether music gets special treatment. It's whether we replace a breakdown model with a planned one, and how we share the cost honestly. I'd propose three steps. One: an *asset register*. By the end of Term 1, every instrument is listed with its age, condition and expected replacement cycle. Two: from that register, a rolling five-year replacement schedule, so each year's cost is known in advance and smoothed, rather than arriving as a crisis. Three: a transparent funding split between the school, the program contribution and instrument hire fees, published to families so they can see what they pay for. That answers Fiona, because families share the cost openly, but they don't carry the whole fleet. And Raymond's idea about leasing deserves a look for the few high-cost items, like tubas and double basses, where it may be cheaper than buying. | For 2027, I'd ask that repairs be funded at an interim cap while the register is built. For the minutes: leadership asks Kevin and me to present an instrument asset register and a five-year replacement and funding plan at the first budget meeting of Term 2."}, {"id": "ns-b1", "room": "School council, public senior high school, Perth, hybrid, 11 members: 8 in the library, 3 on Teams", "item": "2027 schedule of contributions and charges, including a proposed increase to the specialist music program charge", "context": "You chair the council. The principal's draft schedule lifts the specialist music program charge by $40; council must approve the schedule tonight so it can be published to families before enrolment and the Year 6 audition round.", "speakers": [{"who": "Ms Priya Raman, Principal, in the room", "voice": "f", "said": "Thank you, Chair. The schedule in front of you holds the general contribution where it is and lifts the specialist music program charge by forty dollars. I want to be clear that this is not a grab. Instrument repairs, strings and reeds, and the hire of the concert hall for our two major concerts have all risen, and for three years we have quietly absorbed that from the general budget. What I am asking council for is a charge that tracks the real cost, published early, with the hardship process unchanged. Families in financial difficulty will still come to me or to the manager corporate services, confidentially, and no student is turned away from the program."}, {"who": "Mr Gareth Llewellyn, parent representative, in the room", "voice": "m", "said": "I don't doubt a single one of those costs, Principal, and I thank you for the transparency. My concern is the optics. We are a public school with a selective program, and we already field the perception, fair or not, that music is the private school inside the public one. Every dollar we add is a dollar a family has to justify at the kitchen table. I would like council to be able to say, hand on heart, that we tested the alternatives: sponsorship, the P&C, a staged increase over two years. If we approve forty dollars in one step tonight, we own that perception, and I'm not sure we've earned the right to."}, {"who": "Mrs Ana Kovac, staff representative, on Teams", "voice": "f", "said": "Just a fact that may help. Last year eleven families in the program asked for hardship support on the music charge, and every one of those requests was granted. The collection rate on the music charge is also higher than on the general contribution. So the families who are in the program are paying it; collection is not the risk. The risk, if there is one, is the families who never apply. They see the figure on the audition information, decide the program is not for them, and we never hear from them at all. We have no data on that group, and I think we should."}, {"who": "Mr Tom Ferris, community member, in the room", "voice": "m", "said": "I suppose I keep coming back to how all of this gets communicated. When my kids were here the newsletter came home on paper, and now it's all on the app and half the parents never open it. The canteen put its prices up last year and nobody noticed until the sausage rolls. Look, I'm not against the increase as such. I just think whatever we decide tonight, nobody reads the schedule. They read the invoice. So if there's something families need to understand, it has to be on the thing they actually look at, not in a policy document on the website."}, {"who": "Ms Leah Dunstan, Year 11 student representative, in the room", "voice": "f", "said": "From the student side, most of us in the program honestly don't know what the charge pays for. If people knew it covered the hall for the concerts and the repairs on the school instruments, I think it would feel different, more like paying for something real. The other thing I'd say is about timing. The audition flyer is where Year 6 families first see the number, before they've seen a concert or met anyone. That's the moment that matters most, and right now the flyer just has a figure and nothing else."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Raman", "point": "Lift the charge to match costs; hardship process unchanged", "phrase": "a charge that tracks the real cost"}, {"who": "Llewellyn", "point": "Optics of a selective program; test alternatives first", "phrase": "the private school inside the public one"}, {"who": "Kovac", "point": "Hardship requests all granted; unseen risk is non-applicants", "phrase": "the families who never apply"}, {"who": "Ferris", "point": "Families read invoices, not policy schedules", "phrase": "nobody reads the schedule. They read the invoice."}, {"who": "Dunstan", "point": "The audition flyer is the first and decisive impression", "phrase": "where Year 6 families first see the number"}], "question": "Whether council approves the 2027 schedule with the $40 music charge increase as drafted, or defers or stages it.", "gap": "Nobody has joined the cost case to the communication case: the risk Kovac names is created at the flyer and the invoice, so council can approve the cost-based charge and fix what families see, then measure whether applications fall.", "modelNotes": "Q: approve 2027 schedule incl. +$40 music charge? tonight\nROOM: PR  # +$40 music, gen. contrib held  \"a charge that tracks the real cost\"  hardship unchanged !\nME: ✓ cost case sound; 3 yrs absorbed\nROOM: GL  ✗ optics  \"the private school inside the public one\"  → test alternatives / stage it\nME: ? does staging change the non-applicant risk? no\nROOM: AK (Teams)  # 11 hardship, all granted; collection higher than gen.\nROOM: AK  ! \"the families who never apply\"  no data\nME: ★ this is the real risk ≈ GL's optics\nROOM: TF  drift: newsletter, canteen ⟲ \"nobody reads the schedule. They read the invoice.\"\nROOM: LD  \"where Year 6 families first see the number\"\nME: ≈ TF + LD: risk lives on flyer + invoice, not in the figure\nME: Δ 1 approve as drafted  2 itemise + hardship line on flyer + invoice  3 report applicants + hardship Term 2\nME: @ Principal, flyer before audition round; report Term 2 mtg\nGAP: no one fixed what families see, or how we'd know if fewer apply", "task": "As chair, give your summary before council votes. Credit at least two members, state the question, and put a resolution that answers the concern about optics.", "seconds": 150, "model": "Thank you all. Let me try to draw this together before we vote. | The question for council is narrow: do we approve the 2027 schedule tonight with the music charge at forty dollars more, or not. The Principal has made the case for *a charge that tracks the real cost*, after three years of absorbing it, and nobody has disputed those costs. Gareth has put the counterweight plainly: that we risk confirming the view of music as the private school inside the public one. || Ana then gave us the fact that I think resolves the tension. The families in the program pay, and every hardship request was granted. The risk is the families who never apply. And Tom and Leah have told us exactly where that risk is created: not in the schedule, which nobody reads, but on the invoice and on the flyer, which is where Year 6 families first see the number. So I'd suggest the optics are a communication problem we can fix, not a reason to hold the charge below cost. I'll put a resolution in three parts. One: that council approves the 2027 schedule of contributions and charges as tabled. Two: that the Principal arranges for the audition information and the invoice to carry a short itemised line on what the music charge funds, together with a plain statement of the confidential hardship process, before the audition round opens. Three: that the Principal reports to council at our first Term 2 meeting on audition applications and hardship requests, compared with this year, so that if Ana's unseen group exists, we will see it. Gareth, does that meet your concern? Then may I have a mover."}, {"id": "ns-b2", "room": "School council, public senior high school, Perth, hybrid, 12 members: 9 in the staffroom, 3 on Teams", "item": "Revised student dress code for 2027: optional uniform items and the formal blazer", "context": "You chair the council. After a student and parent survey, the deputy principal has brought a revised dress code that adds optional items and makes the Year 12 blazer optional except at formal events; the council's approval is required before the school can adopt it.", "speakers": [{"who": "Mr Julian Aspinall, Deputy Principal, in the room", "voice": "m", "said": "Thank you, Chair. The draft responds directly to the survey: more than six hundred responses, and the clearest message was comfort and cost. We have added tailored shorts and a plain navy option that any student may wear, and the blazer becomes optional for Year 12 except at assemblies, ceremonies and concerts. Nothing here lowers the standard of presentation. What it does is give families a dress code that fits the students we actually have, in the climate we actually live in. We have consulted, the wording is ready, and I'd ask council to approve it tonight so that families can plan their purchases over summer."}, {"who": "Mrs Deborah Quinlan, parent representative, in the room", "voice": "f", "said": "I'll be the one to say it. Every time we add an option, we add an argument at the front gate. Staff already tell us they spend the first ten minutes of the day on socks and jumpers. If the blazer is optional most of the time, then by Term 3 it's optional all of the time, because nobody wants that fight. A dress code nobody enforces is a suggestion. So I'd want to see, before we vote, exactly how this is enforced and by whom, not just what's permitted."}, {"who": "Mr Kai Watanabe, Year 12 student representative, in the room", "voice": "m", "said": "Students really do support this, but not because we want to dress down. Most of us like the blazer at formal things; it makes us look like one school. The complaint is wearing it at forty degrees between Period 2 and Period 3. What we'd ask is that whatever comes in, everyone can actually buy it. If the new shorts are only in some sizes, or cost twice the old ones, then we'll look like one school with two wardrobes, and that's worse than where we started."}, {"who": "Mrs Lorraine Pike, P&C uniform shop convenor, on Teams", "voice": "f", "said": "Sorry, can you hear me? Good. A practical point from the shop. Our supplier needs final orders by the first week of December to deliver for Term 1, and new items have a minimum order. If council approves tonight, we can make that date for the shorts and the navy option. If approval slides into next year, the earliest delivery is late Term 1, and families who buy over summer will buy the old items. So the decision date matters as much as the decision."}, {"who": "Dr Martin Oyelaran, community member, on Teams with lag", "voice": "m", "said": "Just coming back to the enforcement point, sorry, my connection dropped for a moment. I think Deborah is right that permission without enforcement drifts. But I'd frame it slightly differently. It's not really about more rules at the gate, it's about clarity. If the document says exactly when the blazer is required, staff can point to a line rather than make a judgement, and the argument goes away. Clear occasions, not more policing."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Aspinall", "point": "Survey-led changes; approve tonight so families can plan", "phrase": "in the climate we actually live in"}, {"who": "Quinlan", "point": "Optional items erode standards unless enforcement is defined", "phrase": "A dress code nobody enforces is a suggestion"}, {"who": "Watanabe", "point": "Students value the blazer formally; new items must be affordable", "phrase": "one school with two wardrobes"}, {"who": "Pike", "point": "Supplier deadline early December; delay pushes delivery to late Term 1", "phrase": "the decision date matters as much as the decision"}, {"who": "Oyelaran", "point": "Name the occasions precisely so staff need no judgement", "phrase": "Clear occasions, not more policing"}], "question": "Whether council approves the revised dress code tonight, and on what conditions about enforcement and supply.", "gap": "No one has set out the transition: what happens to students in existing items, who checks price and size range before orders close, and when council sees how enforcement is working.", "modelNotes": "Q: approve revised dress code tonight? conditions?\nROOM: JA  # 600+ survey; shorts, navy option, blazer optional Yr 12 except formal  \"in the climate we actually live in\"\nROOM: DQ  ✗  \"A dress code nobody enforces is a suggestion\"  ? who enforces\nROOM: KW  ✓ blazer at formal  \"one school with two wardrobes\"  ! price + sizes\nROOM: LP (Teams)  # orders 1st wk Dec; else late T1  \"the decision date matters as much as the decision\"\nME: ★ LP = the crux; deferral = families buy old stock\nROOM: MO (lag) ⟲ DQ  \"Clear occasions, not more policing\"\nME: ≈ MO answers DQ: list occasions in the text\nME: Δ 1 approve tonight  2 schedule of blazer occasions in doc  3 price/size check before Dec order  4 transition: both permitted to end T2\nME: @ DP: occasions + transition wording to members in 7 days; LP: price/size to DP before order\nME: @ review enforcement at first T3 mtg\nGAP: transition + price check + review date", "task": "As chair, summarise before the vote. Credit at least two speakers by name, state what council is deciding, and put a resolution with the conditions.", "seconds": 150, "model": "Thank you. I think we're close, so let me summarise and then put something to you. || The question is whether we approve the revised dress code tonight, and if we do, on what conditions. Julian has made the case for a code that fits our students in the climate we actually live in. Deborah has given us the necessary warning: a dress code nobody enforces is a suggestion. And Martin, coming back to that point, offered what I think is the answer to it: clear occasions, not more policing. If the document names the occasions, staff point to a line instead of making a call at the gate. || Lorraine then told us something that changes the shape of tonight. The supplier needs orders in the first week of December. If we defer, families buy old stock over summer. As she put it, the decision date matters as much as the decision. And Kai has reminded us that if the new items are dearer or come in fewer sizes, we will be *one school with two wardrobes*. So I'd propose this. One: that council approves the revised dress code, subject to the following. Two: that the Deputy Principal adds a schedule naming every occasion on which the blazer is required, and circulates that wording to members within seven days. Three: that before the December order, the uniform shop confirms to the Deputy that the new items are available across the full size range at a price comparable to the items they replace. Four: that existing items remain acceptable until the end of Term 2, 2027. And five: a short report on enforcement at our first Term 3 meeting. Deborah, does that give you what you asked for? Then I'll take a mover."}, {"id": "ns-b3", "room": "School council, public senior high school, Perth, hybrid, 10 members: 7 in the room, 3 on Teams", "item": "Proposed community-use agreement for the school's performing arts centre with an external theatre company", "context": "You chair the council. A community theatre company has asked to hire the performing arts centre on weekends for a year; the principal seeks council's endorsement of the agreement before signing.", "speakers": [{"who": "Mr Sam Petrakis, P&C President, in the room", "voice": "m", "said": "Chair, I'll put my cards on the table. I think this is exactly what a public school should do. That centre was built with public money, and for most weekends of the year it sits dark. A theatre company that brings families and young people onto our site, that sees our facilities and our students' work, is good for the school's standing in this suburb. A building is only a community asset if the community can actually use it. I'd endorse this tonight and see it as the start of something, not a risk to be managed."}, {"who": "Ms Fiona Grieve, manager corporate services, in the room", "voice": "f", "said": "I support the principle, but council should know what the draft fee covers, because it doesn't cover everything. It covers the venue and the utilities. It does not cover the duty officer we must have on site whenever an external hirer is in the building, and it doesn't cover the extra cleaning after a show with an audience. On the current draft, every weekend booking runs at a small loss. That's fixable, but as it stands, we would be subsidising them out of the school's budget."}, {"who": "Mrs Rosa Delacroix, community member, in the room", "voice": "f", "said": "My worry is simpler. I've seen this happen at another school. An external group books a run of weekends, the dates get locked in early, and then the school's own concert or the Year 12 drama production has nowhere to go. The students end up performing in the gym. I'd want it written into any agreement that our students come first in their own building, without exception, and that the school's calendar is fixed before the hirer chooses dates."}, {"who": "Mr Hamish Boyd, parent representative, on Teams with lag", "voice": "m", "said": "Sorry, I think I missed the last part, but on the first point Sam made: I agree it's good for the school's profile. I'd only add that we should have a way out. A year is a long time. If there's damage, or if bookings clash, the school needs to be able to end it without a legal fight. Even a simple review halfway through would do it. Something like a six-month checkpoint, so we're not locked in."}, {"who": "Ms Priya Raman, Principal, in the room", "voice": "f", "said": "I'll be brief. The company is reputable, they hold their own public liability insurance, and I'd be comfortable signing. I bring it to council because it affects how the community sees the school, and I want the council's view on the terms, not only the principle. I'd welcome conditions. The company has been running for over twenty years, they have hired school venues before without incident, and their youth program already includes some of our own students. My concern is less the company than the precedent. Whatever terms we set here become the template for the next hirer, so I'd rather we set them carefully once."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Petrakis", "point": "Public building should serve the community; endorse tonight", "phrase": "only a community asset if the community can actually use it"}, {"who": "Grieve", "point": "Draft fee omits duty officer and cleaning; runs at loss", "phrase": "we would be subsidising them out of the school's budget"}, {"who": "Delacroix", "point": "Lock the school calendar first; students first", "phrase": "our students come first in their own building"}, {"who": "Boyd", "point": "Build in a review point and a way out", "phrase": "a six-month checkpoint"}, {"who": "Raman", "point": "Comfortable signing; wants council's view on terms", "phrase": "the terms, not only the principle"}], "question": "Whether council endorses the community-use agreement, and on what terms.", "gap": "Everyone has supplied one condition but nobody has put them together as terms: full cost recovery, school dates fixed first with a named date, and a review with an exit.", "modelNotes": "Q: endorse PAC agreement? on what terms\nROOM: SP  ✓✓  \"only a community asset if the community can actually use it\"\nROOM: FG  ! fee excl. duty officer + cleaning → loss each wkend  \"we would be subsidising them out of the school's budget\"\nME: ★ must fix: full cost recovery\nROOM: RD  ✗ clash risk; other school → gym  \"our students come first in their own building\"\nME: Δ school calendar locked first, by a date\nROOM: HB (lag) ⟲ SP ✓  exit  \"a six-month checkpoint\"\nROOM: PR  insured, reputable; wants \"the terms, not only the principle\"\nME: ∴ all agree principle; each gave one term\nME: Δ 1 endorse 12 mths  2 fee = full cost incl. duty officer + clean (FG to set)  3 school dates fixed end T4, hirer books after  4 review mid-yr, exit on notice\nME: @ FG revised fee to Principal before signing; @ Principal report T2 mtg\nGAP: nobody has assembled the terms", "task": "As chair, give the summary before council decides. Credit at least two members by name, state the question, and put the terms as a resolution.", "seconds": 150, "model": "Thank you all. I'll summarise, because I think the room agrees more than it might seem. || The question is whether we endorse this agreement, and on what terms. On the principle there is no dissent. Sam put it well: a building is *only a community asset if the community can actually use it*. The Principal has asked us for the terms, not only the principle, and in fact each of you has given us one. Fiona told us the draft fee leaves out the duty officer and the cleaning, so that as it stands we would be subsidising them out of the school's budget. Rosa gave us the condition I'd call non-negotiable: our students come first in their own building. And Hamish, through the lag, asked for a six-month checkpoint and a way out. || So let me assemble those into a resolution. That council endorses the community-use agreement for twelve months, on four terms. One: the hire fee is set at full cost recovery, including the duty officer and post-performance cleaning, with Fiona providing the revised figure to the Principal before signing. Two: the school's own performance calendar for 2027 is fixed by the end of this term, and the company books only from the dates remaining. Three: a review at six months, reported to council, with the school able to end the agreement on reasonable written notice. Four: the Principal reports to council on the first bookings at our first Term 2 meeting. Principal, are those terms you can take to the company? Then I'll ask for a mover and a seconder."}, {"id": "ns-b4", "room": "Schools' jazz festival association board, hybrid, 9 members: 6 in a school staffroom, 3 on Zoom", "item": "2027 festival entry fees", "context": "You are treasurer. Venue hire and adjudicator costs have risen, the association holds a modest reserve, and the board must set 2027 entry fees before registrations open in Term 1.", "speakers": [{"who": "Ms Catherine Marsh, President, in the room", "voice": "f", "said": "Colleagues, I'll frame this, and then I'd like the treasurer's view. The festival has grown every year for five years, and that's a credit to everyone at this table. But growth costs money: more venue hours, more adjudicators, more stage crew. I think we owe the schools a fee that is honest about that. I'd propose a flat increase per ensemble, modest, explained clearly, so that nobody can say we have been anything other than straight with them. We are a festival run by teachers for teachers, and that has always meant fairness first."}, {"who": "Mr Darren Whitlock, board member, in the room", "voice": "m", "said": "I'd go the other way. Freeze the fees. We have a reserve, and in my view the reserve exists for exactly this kind of year. Schools are being squeezed from every direction, and a jazz festival fee is one of the first things a principal looks at when the budget is tight. Let the reserve do its job for one year, and revisit when we know what the venue does in 2028. Raising fees in a tight year is how a festival shrinks."}, {"who": "Mrs Joanne Tully, regional schools representative, on Zoom with lag", "voice": "f", "said": "Sorry, coming in a bit late on Catherine's point. For a country school, the entry fee is honestly the smallest cheque we write. It's the bus, the accommodation, the relief teacher. The fee is not what keeps us away. What would keep us away is if the timetable put our bands on at nine in the morning after a five-hour drive. So whatever you do with fees, please don't treat it as the thing that decides whether regional schools come."}, {"who": "Mr Nathan Cho, operations coordinator, on Zoom", "voice": "m", "said": "One fact for the board. The rise in our costs isn't evenly spread. Most of it comes from the extra venue hours on the Saturday, and those hours are driven almost entirely by schools entering three or four ensembles. A school with one band costs us about the same as it did three years ago. The schools with many bands are the ones extending the day. So when the board talks about a fee rise, it's worth asking who is actually driving the cost. On the current numbers, the extra Saturday hours are almost entirely a multi-ensemble effect."}, {"who": "Mr Leo Fairbairn, festival director, in the room", "voice": "m", "said": "Can I just say, whatever we do, I hope we protect the open section and the combo program, because that's where the real jazz is happening. Some of those combos last year were extraordinary; one of the Year 11 bass players played a solo on a Mingus tune that I'm still thinking about. If we lose that energy to save a few dollars we've lost the point of the festival, honestly."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Marsh", "point": "Flat modest increase, explained honestly to schools", "phrase": "run by teachers for teachers"}, {"who": "Whitlock", "point": "Freeze fees and draw on reserves this year", "phrase": "Let the reserve do its job"}, {"who": "Tully", "point": "Fee is minor for regional schools; timetable matters more", "phrase": "the smallest cheque we write"}, {"who": "Cho", "point": "Cost rise driven by multi-ensemble schools' Saturday hours", "phrase": "A school with one band costs us about the same"}, {"who": "Fairbairn", "point": "Protect the open section and combo program", "phrase": "where the real jazz is happening"}], "question": "What structure and level of entry fee the board sets for 2027, before registrations open.", "gap": "Nobody has matched the fee to Cho's fact about where the cost falls: a tiered fee holds the single-ensemble and regional schools, recovers cost where it is created, and leaves the reserve for its proper purpose.", "modelNotes": "Q: 2027 fee: level + structure, before T1 registrations\nROOM: CM (Pres)  Δ flat modest rise, explained  \"run by teachers for teachers\"\nROOM: DW  ✗ freeze  \"Let the reserve do its job\"\nME: ✗ reserve = shock buffer, not recurring cost; ! # reserve covers ~1 bad yr\nROOM: JT (Zoom, lag) ⟲ CM  \"the smallest cheque we write\"  ! timetable > fee\nROOM: NC  ! cost from Sat hours, 3-4 ensemble schools  \"A school with one band costs us about the same\"\nME: ★ NC = the answer; fee should follow cost\nROOM: LF  drift: combos, Mingus solo  \"where the real jazz is happening\"\nME: ✓ protect combos: no rise on combo entries\nME: Δ 1 hold first ensemble fee  2 rise on 3rd+ ensemble  3 combos + regional held  4 no 9am slots for regional (JT)\nME: @ me: model tiers + reserve position to board by 30 Nov; @ NC timetable rule\nGAP: fee not linked to where cost falls; reserve purpose unstated", "task": "The president turns to you as treasurer. Give the board your view, credit at least two speakers, and propose the fee structure with a next step.", "seconds": 150, "model": "Thank you, Catherine. As treasurer, let me give the board the financial view and then a proposal. || The question tonight is not only how much, but how we structure the fee before registrations open. Darren's case is a serious one: let the reserve do its job. I'd only say that our reserve is a buffer against a bad year, a lost venue or a cancelled festival. Using it for a cost that will recur every year simply moves the problem to 2028 with less in the bank. || Nathan has given us the fact that I think settles the structure. The rise is not evenly spread: a school with one band costs us about the same as it did three years ago. The extra Saturday hours come from schools entering three or four ensembles. And Joanne, from the regions, has told us the fee is the smallest cheque a country school writes; the timetable matters more. So a flat increase, Catherine, would ask the schools that cost us least to pay for the schools that cost us most. I'd propose a *tiered* fee. One: hold the fee for a school's first and second ensembles. Two: apply the increase from the third ensemble onward, where the cost arises. Three: hold combo entries, which protects what Leo rightly called the place where the real jazz is happening. Four: ask Nathan to build a timetable rule that no regional ensemble plays before ten. It is fair, it is explicable, and it is honest about cost, which is what being run by teachers for teachers means. If the board agrees in principle, I'll circulate the modelled tiers and our reserve position to members by the thirtieth of November, for decision by email."}, {"id": "ns-b5", "room": "Schools' jazz festival association board, hybrid, 8 members: 5 in the room, 3 on Zoom", "item": "Sponsorship offer from a local brewery for naming rights to the festival's evening concert", "context": "You are treasurer. A local craft brewery has offered a significant sum for naming rights to the Saturday evening concert, which features school big bands and an open-section adult band; the association has no written sponsorship policy.", "speakers": [{"who": "Mr Bruce Halvorsen, sponsorship officer, in the room", "voice": "m", "said": "I've been chasing a sponsor of this size for three years, so I'll declare my enthusiasm. The brewery has supported local music for a decade, they want nothing on stage, no product, no sampling. Their name on the program and a banner in the foyer. For that, they'd fund the adjudicators and the sound hire for the whole weekend. I'd ask the board not to let the perfect be the enemy of the funded. This is a good local business that wants to back young musicians."}, {"who": "Ms Anjali Mehta, board member and deputy principal, on Zoom", "voice": "f", "said": "I don't doubt the brewery's good intentions, and I hate turning money away. But I'd ask every principal at this table to imagine the consent note going home. A Year 8 student performing at an event branded with an alcohol company. Our schools have their own obligations about the advertising students are exposed to, and the first time a parent rings a principal about it, the festival becomes the problem rather than the solution. The question is not whether they're good people. It's whose name sits above our students."}, {"who": "Mr Owen Bright, board member, in the room", "voice": "m", "said": "With respect, money is money. The arts in this state run on sponsorship, and half the venues our students play in have a bar. Students see a brewery's name every time they watch the football. If we hold ourselves to a standard no other arts organisation in Perth holds, we will be a very principled, very small festival. I'd take it and stop apologising. And frankly, the brewery is more local and more community-minded than some of the national brands we've happily accepted in the past."}, {"who": "Mrs Clare Donnelly, secretary, on Zoom", "voice": "f", "said": "Can I just add, quietly, that whatever we decide, we have nothing written down. I looked back through the minutes to 2015. Every sponsorship decision has been made case by case, by whoever was in the room. So we're not only deciding about this brewery. We're setting a precedent we haven't named, and the next offer could be a betting app. I'm not arguing for or against this offer. I'm saying the board should know it is writing policy tonight, whether it means to or not."}, {"who": "Ms Catherine Marsh, President, in the room", "voice": "f", "said": "Thank you all. I'm hearing strong views in both directions. Treasurer, before we go any further, I'd like your view, both on what the money would mean for us and on how the board should handle the decision. I don't want us to decide this on the strength of feeling in the room tonight. The board has to be able to explain its decision to schools, to the brewery and to our members at the AGM, and I'd like that explanation to rest on something more than who spoke most persuasively."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Halvorsen", "point": "Generous, low-profile sponsor funding adjudicators and sound", "phrase": "the perfect be the enemy of the funded"}, {"who": "Mehta", "point": "Alcohol branding above students creates school risk", "phrase": "whose name sits above our students"}, {"who": "Bright", "point": "Arts run on sponsorship; don't hold an unusual standard", "phrase": "a very principled, very small festival"}, {"who": "Donnelly", "point": "No written policy; each case sets unnamed precedent", "phrase": "a precedent we haven't named"}, {"who": "Marsh", "point": "Asks treasurer for financial view and process", "phrase": "how the board should handle the decision"}], "question": "Whether the association accepts alcohol-industry naming rights for a concert in which school students perform, and how it decides such offers.", "gap": "Nobody has separated the decision about this offer from the policy that should govern all offers, or noticed that the money could be accepted for an event without students under a different name.", "modelNotes": "Q: accept brewery naming rights for Sat evening concert? + how we decide offers\nROOM: BH  ✓ funds adjudicators + sound  \"the perfect be the enemy of the funded\"\nROOM: AM (Zoom)  ✗ consent note home  \"whose name sits above our students\"\nME: ✓ AM; principals bear the risk, not us\nROOM: OB  ✗ AM  \"a very principled, very small festival\"\nROOM: CD  ! no policy since 2015  \"a precedent we haven't named\"  next = betting app?\nME: ★ CD = the real issue; separate offer from policy\nROOM: CM  asks treasurer: money + \"how the board should handle the decision\"\nME: # sponsorship = adjudicators + sound; budget can carry 1 yr without, tight\nME: Δ 1 decline naming of student concert  2 offer open-section night / bar event only  3 policy: no alcohol, gambling, tobacco on student events\nME: @ me + CD draft policy by Feb mtg; @ BH reply to brewery within 2 wks\nGAP: offer vs policy not separated; alternative placement unspoken", "task": "The president turns to you. Give the financial position, credit at least two speakers, and propose how the board should decide, with an action.", "seconds": 150, "model": "Thank you, Catherine. Two things: the money, and the method. || On the money, Bruce has done the festival a real service, and I don't want that lost. The offer would fund the adjudicators and the sound hire. We can carry one more year without it, but only just, so this is not money to wave away lightly. || On the method, I think Clare has put her finger on the real issue: we are setting a precedent we haven't named. So the question is really two questions. Should student performances sit under alcohol branding, and how do we decide offers like this in future. On the first, I'm with Anjali. It is the principals who will take the call from a parent, and the right test is whose name sits above our students. Owen is right that we must not become a very principled, very small festival, but there is a way to keep the money and the principle. || I'd propose three things. One: that the board declines naming rights for any event in which school students perform. Two: that Bruce goes back to the brewery within a fortnight with an alternative, sponsorship of the open-section adult night and the musicians' function, where no students perform. That keeps *the relationship* and much of the funding. Three: that Clare and I draft a sponsorship policy for the February meeting, excluding alcohol, gambling and tobacco sponsors from student events and setting who decides and how. For the minutes: the board declines the offer as made, authorises the sponsorship officer to propose an alternative, and requests a draft sponsorship policy from the secretary and treasurer for February."}, {"id": "ns-b6", "room": "Schools' jazz festival association board, online, 9 members on Zoom", "item": "Proposal to engage a paid part-time festival coordinator", "context": "You are treasurer. The festival has run on volunteer labour for twenty years; after the long-serving secretary signalled she cannot continue at the same load, a member has proposed a paid coordinator role.", "speakers": [{"who": "Ms Catherine Marsh, President", "voice": "f", "said": "I'll be candid. This festival exists because a handful of people have given far more than anyone should be asked to give. Clare has carried a load that, in any other organisation, would be a paid job. If we don't act, we won't lose the festival in one dramatic moment; we'll lose it one tired volunteer at a time. I'd like the board to agree in principle tonight that we will move to a paid coordinator, and then work out the detail."}, {"who": "Mrs Clare Donnelly, secretary", "voice": "f", "said": "Thank you, Catherine. Just so the board has the facts. I kept a log this year. It came to a little over three hundred hours, most of it between July and the festival weekend. Registrations, the adjudicator contracts, the timetable, the volunteer roster, the emails from schools. Some of it a paid person could do; some of it really needs a teacher who knows the schools. I'd say the timetable and the school liaison are the parts that need judgement. The rest is administration."}, {"who": "Mr Darren Whitlock, board member", "voice": "m", "said": "I support the sentiment, but I want the board to see what it's walking into. The moment we pay someone, we become an employer. Superannuation, workers' compensation insurance, payroll, leave, a contract, possibly performance management. That's a different organisation from the one we've been. I'm not saying no. I'm saying a festival is easy to love and an employer is hard to run, and we shouldn't vote on a principle without knowing its price."}, {"who": "Mr Leo Fairbairn, festival director", "voice": "m", "said": "Whatever we do, the person has to understand jazz. I mean that. The last time we had a hired-in events company for the gala they put the big band's rhythm section at the back of the stage behind the trombones. You can't teach someone that in a handover document. It has to be someone who's been to the festival, who knows the schools. They were lovely people and very professional, but they had never stood in front of a big band. I'd rather have someone part-time who knows the music than someone full-time who knows events."}, {"who": "Ms Grace Lindqvist, board member, with lag", "voice": "f", "said": "Sorry, I froze for a second. On Catherine's point about doing it in principle, I agree, but could we look at grant funding before we commit our own money? The state arts funding programs sometimes support organisational capacity, and a festival with twenty years of history is a strong case. It might pay for a first year while we see whether it works. I'd be happy to look into what's open in the first half of next year, if that would help."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Marsh", "point": "Agree in principle tonight to a paid coordinator", "phrase": "one tired volunteer at a time"}, {"who": "Donnelly", "point": "Over 300 hours logged; split judgement from administration", "phrase": "The rest is administration"}, {"who": "Whitlock", "point": "Employing someone carries obligations; know the price first", "phrase": "an employer is hard to run"}, {"who": "Fairbairn", "point": "The coordinator must know jazz and the schools", "phrase": "You can't teach someone that in a handover document"}, {"who": "Lindqvist", "point": "Seek grant funding for a first year", "phrase": "pay for a first year"}], "question": "Whether the board commits in principle to a paid coordinator tonight, and what it needs to know before it commits money.", "gap": "Nobody has used Donnelly's split: the role can be scoped to the administrative hours, costed with on-costs, and the judgement work kept with a volunteer teacher, giving Whitlock his price and Marsh her principle.", "modelNotes": "Q: paid coordinator: in principle tonight? what before money?\nROOM: CM  Δ in principle now  \"one tired volunteer at a time\"\nROOM: CD  # 300+ hrs, Jul to festival  judgement = timetable + liaison  \"The rest is administration\"\nME: ★ CD's split = the role design\nROOM: DW  ✗ not yet  super, workers comp, payroll  \"an employer is hard to run\"\nME: ✓ DW: need price; ∴ cost on-costs\nROOM: LF  drift: rhythm section behind trombones  \"You can't teach someone that in a handover document\"\nME: ≈ LF supports split: judgement stays with teacher\nROOM: GL (lag) ⟲ CM  grants  \"pay for a first year\"\nME: Δ 1 in principle ✓  2 scope = admin hrs only  3 me: cost 2 options incl. on-costs  4 GL: grant scan  5 advice on engagement model\nME: @ CD task log split by 15 Dec; @ me costing Feb mtg; @ GL grant rounds Feb\nGAP: role not scoped; price unknown; judgement work unassigned", "task": "The president asks the treasurer for a way forward. Credit at least two members by name, state the question, and propose numbered next steps with owners and dates.", "seconds": 180, "model": "Thank you, Catherine, and thank you, Clare, for something most volunteers never do: you counted. || The question, I think, is whether we commit in principle tonight, and what we need to know before we commit money. Catherine has told us what happens if we don't act: we lose the festival one tired volunteer at a time. Darren has told us what happens if we act carelessly: a festival is easy to love and an employer is hard to run. Both are right, and I don't think we need to choose between them. || Clare's log gives us the design. Of those three hundred hours, the timetable and the school liaison need judgement; as she said, the rest is administration. And Leo has told us, with the rhythm section behind the trombones, why the judgement work cannot be handed to an outsider. So the role we are talking about is *smaller* and cheaper than a festival director. It is an administrator. || I'd propose five things. One: the board agrees in principle to engage paid administrative support from the 2027 festival cycle. Two: Clare splits her task log into judgement and administration by the fifteenth of December. Three: I prepare a costing for the February meeting, with two options, a casual employee and an engaged contractor, including superannuation, workers' compensation and payroll, so Darren has his price. Four: Grace scans the state arts funding rounds that open before mid-year, as she suggested, to pay for a first year. Five: we seek advice on the right engagement model before anything is signed. For the minutes: agreed in principle; costing and grant scan to the February meeting; no commitment of funds until then."}, {"id": "ns-b7", "room": "Music department meeting, in person, 9 staff: classroom and instrumental teachers, music room", "item": "2027 ensemble rehearsal schedule: before-school versus lunchtime slots", "context": "You lead the department and chair the meeting. The new bell times move the start of Period 1 earlier, and the 7.30 am rehearsal slot for two ensembles now collides with bus arrivals; the schedule must go to families before the end of term.", "speakers": [{"who": "Ms Harriet Vance, strings teacher", "voice": "f", "said": "I'll say it plainly: before school is where ensembles are built. The students are fresh, nobody is rushing off to eat, and you get a full fifty minutes. At lunch you get thirty minutes, minus the five it takes to set up, minus the five it takes to pack down, minus whoever is in a detention. I'd rather keep the 7.30 start for the senior orchestra and find a solution for the buses than give up the best rehearsal time in the week."}, {"who": "Mr Callum Reid, instrumental brass teacher, visiting two days a week", "voice": "m", "said": "From my side, I'm only on site Tuesday and Thursday, so whatever happens I'd ask that the brass-heavy ensembles stay on my days. I also want to flag that if we move bands to lunch, the brass students lose the only time I can run sectionals with them. Lunch is already where I do catch-up lessons. I'm not precious about the slot, I just can't be in two places at once."}, {"who": "Mrs Nadia Russo, classroom music teacher", "voice": "f", "said": "Can I raise something slightly different? I've been thinking we don't really talk about what the ensembles are for, as a department. Is it performance, is it retention, is it just keeping the parents happy? When I was at my last school we had a big conversation about that and it changed everything. I'd love us to have that conversation next year, because otherwise we're just shuffling rooms. Anyway, I'm happy with whatever we decide on the times."}, {"who": "Mr Peter Lam, music assistant and ensemble coordinator", "voice": "m", "said": "I checked the bus data with the front office. Of the forty-two students in the two early ensembles, nineteen come on the special school bus services, and under the new times those buses arrive at 7.50. So a 7.30 start loses nearly half of each ensemble for the first twenty minutes. If we started at 7.45 instead, the buses still miss it by five minutes, but every one of them makes an 8.00 start. Nineteen students is the whole problem."}, {"who": "Ms Grace Okeke, classroom music teacher", "voice": "f", "said": "I don't love any of this, honestly. Every year we rebuild the schedule around the timetable and every year someone loses. I'd rather we keep it as it is and let families sort out transport. If they want to be in a selective program, getting here at 7.30 is part of the commitment. Other programs manage it. I know families who drive across the city for ensembles at other schools. I just worry that if we keep bending the program around the buses, we're telling everyone the commitment is negotiable."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Vance", "point": "Before school gives the best full rehearsal; keep early slot", "phrase": "before school is where ensembles are built"}, {"who": "Reid", "point": "Brass ensembles must stay on Tuesday and Thursday", "phrase": "can't be in two places at once"}, {"who": "Russo", "point": "Department should discuss what ensembles are for", "phrase": "otherwise we're just shuffling rooms"}, {"who": "Lam", "point": "Nineteen bus students arrive 7.50; an 8.00 start fits", "phrase": "Nineteen students is the whole problem"}, {"who": "Okeke", "point": "Keep current times; transport is the families' commitment", "phrase": "part of the commitment"}], "question": "What start time the two early ensembles rehearse at in 2027, and on which days, so the schedule can go to families this term.", "gap": "Nobody has done the arithmetic with Lam's fact: an 8.00 start keeps the before-school rehearsal Vance wants, keeps all nineteen bus students, and the shortened slot can be offset by Reid's days.", "modelNotes": "Q: 2027 start time + days for 2 early ensembles; to families this term\nROOM: HV  ✓ early  \"before school is where ensembles are built\"  # lunch = 30 less 10\nROOM: CR  Tue/Thu only  \"I can't be in two places at once\"  lunch = his catch-ups\nROOM: NR  drift: purpose of ensembles  \"otherwise we're just shuffling rooms\"\nME: ✓ NR → park for T1 PD, not tonight\nROOM: PL  # 42 students, 19 on bus, arrive 7.50  \"Nineteen students is the whole problem\"\nME: ★ PL = solution; 8.00 start, all 19 in\nROOM: GO  ✗ change  \"part of the commitment\"\nME: ? bus timetable is not a family choice; equity\nME: Δ 1 both early ensembles 8.00 start  2 brass-heavy on Tue/Thu (CR)  3 HV: senior orch adds 1 Thu lunch sectional if needed\nME: @ PL confirm room + bus times by Fri; @ me letter to families wk 8\nME: @ NR lead ensemble-purpose session, T1 PD day\nGAP: arithmetic not done; 8.00 serves everyone", "task": "As chair, sum up before the department settles the schedule. Credit at least two colleagues by name, state the decision, and set actions with owners and dates.", "seconds": 120, "model": "Thanks, everyone. Let me pull this together, because I think Peter's numbers have given us the answer. || The decision is the start time and the days for our two early ensembles, so the schedule can go home before the end of term. Harriet has made the case that before school is where ensembles are built, and I agree; I don't want to lose that to a thirty-minute lunch slot. Grace, I hear you that getting here early is part of the commitment, but a bus timetable isn't something a family chooses. And Peter has told us the scale of it: nineteen of forty-two students arrive at 7.50. *Nineteen students is the whole problem.* || So here's what I'd propose. One: both early ensembles move to an 8.00 start. We keep the before-school rehearsal and every bus student is in the room. Two: the brass-heavy ensemble sits on Tuesday and Thursday, so Callum is there, because as he says he can't be in two places at once. Three: Harriet, if the senior orchestra needs back the time we've lost, I'll support an additional Thursday lunch sectional. || Nadia, your question about what ensembles are for is the right one, and it deserves more than the last ten minutes of a meeting. Would you lead that session on our Term 1 professional learning day? || Actions, then. Peter confirms the rooms and the bus times with the front office by Friday. I'll send the letter to families in Week 8. Nadia scopes the Term 1 session. Is anyone unable to live with that? Good. Agreed."}, {"id": "ns-b8", "room": "Music department meeting, in person, 8 staff, music staffroom", "item": "Year 11 2027: whether to propose a combined ATAR and General Music class", "context": "You lead the department. Nine students have selected Music ATAR and seven Music General for 2027; the timetabler has asked whether the department would accept one combined class, and you must take a department position to the principal next week.", "speakers": [{"who": "Mr Adrian Keogh, senior classroom teacher", "voice": "m", "said": "I've taught combined classes, and I'll say it can be done well. The practical work overlaps, the ensemble work overlaps, the aural overlaps more than people think. If we fight for two classes with nine and seven, we may win, but we spend capital we'll want later for something bigger. A combined class with a clear plan is a reasonable ask, and saying yes gracefully puts us in credit with the timetabler and the principal."}, {"who": "Ms Imogen Hart, classroom music teacher, Year 12 ATAR teacher", "voice": "f", "said": "The practical overlaps, yes. But I've marked enough ATAR written papers to know where combined classes lose students marks. It's the analysis and the set works. ATAR students need sustained, exam-focused contact on the written component, and in a combined class that time is the first to go, because the General students need something different in the same minutes. The written exam is where combined classes quietly fail. I'm not saying never. I'm saying not without protected time."}, {"who": "Mrs Wendy Ashdown, classroom music teacher", "voice": "f", "said": "I just keep thinking about how it looks to the Year 10s. If we combine, the message is that ATAR Music is a small, struggling subject, and next year it's seven, and then five. Whereas if we hold two classes, even small ones, we're saying this school takes music seriously. It's about the signal, really, more than the timetable. Students at that age are very alert to what the school values, and a combined class reads as a downgrade, whatever we intend by it."}, {"who": "Mr Callum Reid, instrumental brass teacher", "voice": "m", "said": "I'm not across the classroom side, but I'd note that most of the nine ATAR students are also in the senior ensembles, so whatever is decided, please protect their ensemble rehearsals. If their Year 11 class collides with orchestra on a Thursday, you lose them from the band, not from the subject. That's happened before, and it took us a whole term to get those students back into rehearsals. Please check the lines."}, {"who": "Ms Siobhan Carr, learning support teacher", "voice": "f", "said": "One small thing. Two of the General students have individual plans that need a quieter, more structured room. In a combined class of sixteen with two very different programs running at once, those two will struggle. I'm not against combining, but I'd want it resourced so that I or an education assistant can be in there for some of it. They're both capable musicians, and I'd like them to succeed in the subject they chose."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Keogh", "point": "Combined classes can work; saying yes builds credit", "phrase": "puts us in credit with the timetabler"}, {"who": "Hart", "point": "ATAR written component suffers in combined classes", "phrase": "The written exam is where combined classes quietly fail"}, {"who": "Ashdown", "point": "Combining signals decline to Year 10 students", "phrase": "It's about the signal"}, {"who": "Reid", "point": "Protect ensemble rehearsals for ATAR students", "phrase": "you lose them from the band, not from the subject"}, {"who": "Carr", "point": "Two General students need support in a combined room", "phrase": "I'd want it resourced"}], "question": "What position the department takes to the principal on a combined Year 11 ATAR and General Music class for 2027.", "gap": "Nobody has turned the conditions into a counter-offer: accept a combined class, but with separately timetabled ATAR written contact, support time, and a protected ensemble line, which addresses Hart and Carr while keeping Keogh's credit.", "modelNotes": "Q: dept position to Principal: combined Yr 11 ATAR + General? next wk\nROOM: AK  ✓ combine  practical/aural overlap  \"puts us in credit with the timetabler\"\nROOM: IH  ✗ written/analysis/set works  \"The written exam is where combined classes quietly fail\"\nME: ★ IH = the condition, not a veto\nROOM: WA  \"It's about the signal\"  ≈ retention fear\nME: ? signal to Yr 10 is set by results + ensembles, not class lists\nROOM: CR  \"you lose them from the band, not from the subject\"  ! Thu orch\nROOM: SC  2 IEPs; quiet room  \"I'd want it resourced\"\nME: ∴ yes, with conditions\nME: Δ 1 accept combined for practical + aural  2 ATAR written: 1 separate period/cycle  3 EA or SC in 2 periods  4 class not on Thu orch line\nME: # if no separate period: ask for 2 classes\nME: @ me: written position to Principal by Tue; @ IH outline written-component plan\nGAP: no counter-offer with conditions", "task": "As head of department, give the position you will take to the principal. Credit at least two colleagues by name and set out the conditions as numbered points.", "seconds": 120, "model": "Thank you, all of you. I'll say where I think we've landed, and you can tell me if I've got it wrong. || The question is what position we take to the principal next week. Adrian is right that a combined class can be done well, and that saying yes puts us in credit with the timetabler, which I'd like to bank. Imogen has told us exactly where it goes wrong: the written exam is where combined classes quietly fail. So I don't think our answer is yes or no. I think it's *yes, with conditions*. || Wendy, I take the point that it's about the signal. I'd suggest the signal Year 10s actually read is our results and our ensembles, so protecting those is how we protect the subject. That's also Callum's point: if the class collides with Thursday orchestra, you lose them from the band, not from the subject. || So the position I'll take is this. One: we accept a combined Year 11 class for the practical, ensemble and aural work. Two: ATAR students receive one separately timetabled period each cycle for the written component, analysis and set works. Three: the class is resourced, as Siobhan said, with her or an education assistant present for at least two periods each cycle. Four: the class is not timetabled against the senior orchestra line. And if the separate ATAR period can't be found, our position reverts to two classes. || I'll put that in writing to the principal by Tuesday, and Imogen, could you give me a half-page outline of the written-component plan by Monday so it goes in with the request? Agreed?"}, {"id": "ns-b9", "room": "Music parents' support group meeting, school music room, 14 parents in person, 2 on a phone call", "item": "2027 fundraising target and event: replacing ageing large instruments", "context": "You attend as Teacher in Charge of Music. The parent group wants to fund instrument replacement in 2027 and is choosing between a gala concert dinner and a raffle; they want to hear from you on what the department actually needs.", "speakers": [{"who": "Mrs Belinda Shore, convenor", "voice": "f", "said": "Thank you all for coming, and on a Tuesday. I'll be honest that I'd love us to be ambitious this year. A gala dinner with the senior ensembles performing, a silent auction, a proper night. The last one raised a remarkable amount and people still talk about it. It brings the community together and it shows off the students. I think the music program deserves something with a bit of sparkle, not another raffle ticket in the school bag."}, {"who": "Mr Raj Iyer, parent group treasurer", "voice": "m", "said": "Just the numbers, briefly. The last gala grossed well, but once you take out the venue, the catering and the hire, the net was less than half of what people remember. It also took about four months of volunteer time from six families. The raffle nets less, but it costs almost nothing and three people ran it. I'm not against the gala. I'd just like us to remember the net, not the gross."}, {"who": "Ms Tessa Lombardi, parent", "voice": "f", "said": "I'll be the slightly unpopular voice. A lot of families in this program already pay the music charge, buy their own instrument, pay for private lessons and drive to rehearsals at seven in the morning. A gala dinner at a hundred dollars a head is, frankly, for the families who can afford it. I'd rather we did something where every family can take part at some level, even if it raises less."}, {"who": "Mr Geoff Pardoe, parent, on the phone", "voice": "m", "said": "Sorry, the line's a bit patchy. Following on from Belinda, I think the gala's great, and actually my company might be interested in sponsoring a table. But can I ask, what are we actually raising for? I hear tubas, I hear percussion, I hear a new piano. We'll raise more if we can tell people exactly what their money buys. A tuba is a lot easier to sell to a sponsor than a general fund. People like to know their name is on something."}, {"who": "Mrs Belinda Shore, convenor", "voice": "f", "said": "That's a good question, Geoff. Perhaps our Teacher in Charge can tell us what the priorities are, and whether the department has a view on the event. I'll admit I'm keen on the gala, but I'm not wedded to it if the department has a different view. Above all, I'd like us to raise money for the things that will actually make a difference to the students, and not just for whatever sounds best at a meeting. So please be frank with us."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Shore", "point": "Ambitious gala dinner to showcase students", "phrase": "something with a bit of sparkle"}, {"who": "Iyer", "point": "Gala net far below remembered; raffle cheap to run", "phrase": "remember the net, not the gross"}, {"who": "Lombardi", "point": "Gala excludes families already paying a lot", "phrase": "every family can take part at some level"}, {"who": "Pardoe", "point": "Name exactly what the money buys", "phrase": "exactly what their money buys"}, {"who": "Shore (convenor)", "point": "Asks TIC for priorities and a view", "phrase": "what the priorities are"}], "question": "What the parent group sets as its 2027 target and which fundraising event it runs.", "gap": "Nobody has a named, costed target, and nobody has noticed the event can be tiered: a concert open to every family with a separate sponsored-table component, aimed at one specific instrument purchase.", "modelNotes": "Q: 2027 target + event: gala or raffle?\nROOM: BS  gala, auction  \"something with a bit of sparkle\"\nROOM: RI  # net < half of gross; 6 families x 4 mths  \"remember the net, not the gross\"\nME: ✓ RI: volunteer hours are a cost\nROOM: TL  ✗ $100 a head  \"every family can take part at some level\"\nROOM: GP (phone)  ? what for  \"exactly what their money buys\"  + sponsor a table\nME: ★ GP = my opening: one named target\nME: # priority: 2 tubas, then timpani; piano later (not this yr)\nME: Δ 1 one target: 2 tubas  2 concert, tickets low, all families  3 sponsored tables (GP) for the dinner part  4 RI to set break-even\nME: @ me: quotes for tubas to RI by end T4; @ RI budget for Feb mtg\nGAP: no named costed target; event not tiered", "task": "The convenor turns to you. Thank the group, credit at least two parents by name, give the department's priority, and propose an approach with actions.", "seconds": 120, "model": "Thank you, Belinda, and thank you all, on a Tuesday, as you say. I'm conscious that everything this group raises goes straight into the students' hands, so I'll be practical. || Geoff asked the question I'd most like to answer: what exactly does the money buy. The department's priority for 2027 is clear. *Two tubas.* Our current instruments are past economical repair, and without them the concert bands have no foundation. Timpani come next; the piano is a longer project, not this year. So I'd ask the group to set one named target, two tubas, and tell every family exactly that. || On the event, I think Raj and Tessa have, between them, given us the design. Raj has reminded us to remember the net, not the gross, and that volunteer time is a cost too. Tessa has asked for something where every family can take part at some level. And Belinda, I'd hate to lose the sparkle, because the students love that night. So I'd propose a combined approach. One: a concert evening with tickets priced so every family can come. Two: a sponsored dinner component before it, for the families and businesses who want it, which is where Geoff's table fits. Three: one target, two tubas, with a display at the concert so people can see progress. Four: Raj sets a break-even figure before anything is booked. || For actions: I'll get quotes for the two tubas to Raj by the end of this term, and Raj, could you bring a simple budget to the February meeting so the group can decide then? If we can show families a photograph of the tubas at the concert, I think the rest takes care of itself."}, {"id": "ns-b10", "room": "P&C Association general meeting, school library, hybrid, 22 members: 17 in the room, 5 on Teams", "item": "Parent proposal for a 2028 music tour to Japan", "context": "You attend as Teacher in Charge of Music. A parent has brought a proposal for the P&C to support a senior ensemble tour to Japan in 2028; the P&C is being asked to endorse it and commit seed funding.", "speakers": [{"who": "Mrs Yvette Sandoval, parent and proposer", "voice": "f", "said": "Thank you, Madam President. I've done a lot of homework on this. A ten-day tour in 2028, the senior wind ensemble and the jazz band, a school exchange concert in Osaka, a public performance in Kyoto. I've spoken to two tour companies and to a school that went last year. Their students called it the defining experience of their schooling. I'm asking the P&C to endorse it tonight and put in seed funding for the deposit, so the families can start planning now. Our students deserve a world stage, not just a school hall."}, {"who": "Mr Kieran Moss, P&C treasurer", "voice": "m", "said": "I'll speak to the money. The P&C's funds are raised from all families, not just music families. If we commit seed funding to a tour that perhaps forty students go on, we'd want a very clear case for why that's a fair use of everyone's money. I'm not against it. But this association has a duty to the whole school, and I'd need to see who pays, and when, before I could recommend anything."}, {"who": "Ms Danielle Okafor, parent, on Teams", "voice": "f", "said": "I'll be blunt because someone should be. A tour to Japan is going to cost each family thousands of dollars. My son is in the jazz band and we will not be able to afford it. So the 'defining experience' will be defined by who can pay. If the school runs it, there has to be a real plan for the students who can't go, and I don't mean a fundraising sausage sizzle."}, {"who": "Mrs Helen Fraser, parent, previously organised a school tour at another school", "voice": "f", "said": "Can I offer one thing from experience? When we did a tour at my daughter's previous school, families paid in instalments over eighteen months, starting well before the deposit was due. That's what made it possible for families on tighter budgets. Starting late is what makes tours exclusive. If 2028 is the year, the payment plan has to start in early 2027. We also set the deposit dates around school holidays, so families weren't hit with a payment in the same month as fees."}, {"who": "Mr Stuart Linden, P&C President, chairing", "voice": "m", "said": "Thank you. So, if I've got this right, we have broad enthusiasm, some concern about money, and some concern about fairness. I'm not quite sure where that leaves us. Perhaps the Teacher in Charge can tell us whether the school is even in a position to run this. I don't want to say no to something that clearly matters to a lot of families, and I don't want us to commit money we can't stand behind. And I'm conscious we have a full agenda after this."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Sandoval", "point": "Endorse a 2028 Japan tour and fund the deposit", "phrase": "a world stage, not just a school hall"}, {"who": "Moss", "point": "P&C money belongs to all families; needs clear case", "phrase": "a duty to the whole school"}, {"who": "Okafor", "point": "Cost will exclude students; need real equity plan", "phrase": "defined by who can pay"}, {"who": "Fraser", "point": "Long instalment plan from early 2027 enables access", "phrase": "Starting late is what makes tours exclusive"}, {"who": "Linden (President)", "point": "Summarises vaguely; asks whether school can run it", "phrase": "I'm not quite sure where that leaves us"}], "question": "Whether the P&C endorses and seeds a 2028 tour now, or first asks for a feasibility proposal that addresses cost and access.", "gap": "Nobody has said the school, not the P&C, must first decide whether it will run the tour through its own approval processes, or that Fraser's timeline makes a feasibility decision by early 2027 the real deadline.", "modelNotes": "Q: P&C endorse + seed 2028 Japan tour now? or feasibility first\nROOM: YS  Osaka, Kyoto, 10 days  \"a world stage, not just a school hall\"\nROOM: KM  ✗ seed now  \"a duty to the whole school\"\nROOM: DO (Teams)  ✗ cost  \"defined by who can pay\"  ! plan for those who can't\nROOM: HF  # instalments 18 mths  \"Starting late is what makes tours exclusive\"\nME: ★ HF sets the deadline: payment plan early 2027\nROOM: SL (Pres)  vague  \"I'm not quite sure where that leaves us\"  → me\nME: ! school decides + approves first, not P&C; principal + Dept processes\nME: Δ 1 no seed funding tonight  2 I take feasibility to Principal  3 costed proposal: per-family cost, 18-mth plan, equity fund  4 back to P&C March\nME: @ me + YS feasibility by end Feb; @ KM advise on equity fund rules\nGAP: school approval not mentioned; timeline is the real deadline", "task": "The president turns to you. Clarify what the meeting is really deciding, credit at least two speakers by name, and propose a way forward with owners and dates.", "seconds": 150, "model": "Thank you, Mr President, and thank you, Yvette, for the work you've already put into this. Two tour companies and a school that's done it is a serious start. || Let me try to clarify what tonight can decide. A tour like this is a school activity before it is a P&C project. The principal has to approve it, and it has to go through the school's planning and approval processes for travel. So I don't think the P&C should be asked to fund a deposit tonight. I think the real question is whether the P&C supports the school preparing a feasibility proposal. || And that proposal has to answer the two things this room has raised. Kieran has reminded us the P&C has a duty to the whole school. Danielle has put the harder truth: without a plan, the experience will be *defined by who can pay*. Helen has given us the practical answer, and also the deadline. Families paid over eighteen months, and starting late is what makes tours exclusive. If 2028 is the year, we need to know by early 2027. || So I'd propose this. One: no funding decision tonight. Two: I take the proposal to the principal next week to ask whether the school will consider a 2028 tour in principle. Three: if she agrees, Yvette and I prepare a feasibility proposal by the end of February: the cost per family, an eighteen-month instalment plan, and a funded plan for students who could not otherwise go. Four: Kieran advises us on how any P&C contribution could go to that access fund rather than to the deposit, which I think meets his concern. For the minutes: the P&C supports the preparation of a feasibility proposal, to return to the March meeting."}, {"id": "ns-b11", "room": "North metropolitan secondary music heads network, hybrid, 12 members: 7 at a host school, 5 on Teams", "item": "Cross-school moderation of Year 10 performance assessment", "context": "The network meets each term. A member school has proposed that heads moderate a sample of each other's Year 10 performance recordings, as preparation for senior school standards; the convenor needs agreement on whether and how to run it in 2027.", "speakers": [{"who": "Dr Philippa Grant, head of music at a large independent school, in the room", "voice": "f", "said": "I'll make the case. Our Year 10 marks are, frankly, each school's private currency. A B at one school and a B at another may mean quite different things, and our students find that out in Year 11 when they meet the senior standards. If we moderate a sample of each other's recordings, we develop a shared eye. It costs us a couple of hours a term, and we all become better markers. I'd suggest three recordings per school, at the A, C and borderline levels, exchanged in Term 2."}, {"who": "Mr Tony Brennan, head of music, public school, in the room", "voice": "m", "said": "I like it in principle, but I'll be the realist. I'm a department of two. Term 2 is concerts, reports and the instrumental audition round. If this becomes another obligation, it won't be the large schools that drop out, it'll be the small ones, and then it's moderation among the schools that need it least. Whatever we design has to be light enough that a department of two can do it in an afternoon."}, {"who": "Ms Lena Petrovic, head of music, public school, on Teams with lag", "voice": "f", "said": "Sorry, I'm a bit behind, the video froze. On Philippa's point, I agree we need a shared standard. One thing nobody's mentioned: the consent and privacy side. These are recordings of students, and they'll be going to staff at other schools. We'd need to be clear how they're shared and stored, and families would need to know. It's not a reason not to, but it's the thing that would stop it at my school."}, {"who": "Mr Owen Tait, head of music, Catholic school, in the room", "voice": "m", "said": "Just a thought. We already moderate informally, in a way, through the festival adjudications and the eisteddfod. Maybe we're overcomplicating it. We all know roughly what a good Year 10 sounds like. I'd hate to build a whole new system when the festival and the eisteddfod already put our students in front of the same adjudicators every year. Perhaps we could just share our adjudication reports instead, and save ourselves the paperwork."}, {"who": "Mrs Joan Whitfield, network convenor, in the room", "voice": "f", "said": "Thank you. So, to sum up, I think we all like moderation, but there are some concerns about time, and maybe privacy, and Owen thinks we might already do it. Perhaps we should form a working group and come back next term? Dijon, you haven't said anything yet. It sounds like there's plenty to work through, and I don't want us to rush into something half-formed, so a small group could take it away and report back."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Grant", "point": "Moderate three recordings per school to build shared standards", "phrase": "each school's private currency"}, {"who": "Brennan", "point": "Must be light enough for small departments", "phrase": "a department of two can do it in an afternoon"}, {"who": "Petrovic", "point": "Consent and privacy of recordings must be settled", "phrase": "the thing that would stop it at my school"}, {"who": "Tait", "point": "Festivals already moderate informally", "phrase": "We all know roughly what a good Year 10 sounds like"}, {"who": "Whitfield (convenor)", "point": "Vague summary; proposes working group", "phrase": "form a working group and come back next term"}], "question": "Whether the network runs cross-school Year 10 performance moderation in 2027, and in what form.", "gap": "A working group would lose a year. The design questions are answerable now: a lighter sample in a quieter term, privacy handled by each school's existing consent, and a pilot with volunteers.", "modelNotes": "Q: run Yr 10 perf. moderation 2027? what form\nROOM: PG  Δ 3 recordings A/C/borderline, T2  \"each school's private currency\"\nME: ✓ PG; Yr 11 standard shock real\nROOM: TB  ! dept of 2; T2 worst  \"a department of two can do it in an afternoon\"\nROOM: LP (Teams, lag) ⟲ PG  consent/privacy  \"the thing that would stop it at my school\"\nROOM: OT  ≈ festival adjudication  \"We all know roughly what a good Year 10 sounds like\"\nME: ? adjudication ≠ assessment against criteria\nROOM: JW  vague; \"form a working group and come back next term\"\nME: ★ working group = lost year; decide design now\nME: Δ 1 pilot T3, volunteers  2 two recordings (C + borderline)  3 shared via school-managed drive, each school's consent  4 one 90-min online session\nME: @ me: draft consent wording + protocol by T1 wk 5; @ PG host session T3\nGAP: design answerable now; pilot not working group", "task": "The convenor turns to you. Gently correct the summary, credit at least two colleagues by name, and propose a concrete design so the network can decide today.", "seconds": 120, "model": "Thanks, Joan. If I may, I'd like to suggest we can go a step further today than a working group, because I think the room has already done the design work. || The question is whether we run moderation in 2027, and in what form. Philippa has given us the reason: our Year 10 marks are each school's private currency, and students discover that in Year 11. Tony has given us the test any design has to pass: a department of two can do it in an afternoon. And Lena has named the thing that would stop it at her school, which is consent and privacy. Owen, I take the point about festivals, though an adjudication isn't quite marking against our own criteria, which is what we're trying to align. || So here's a design that I think meets all three tests. One: a *pilot* in Term 3, not Term 2, for schools that volunteer, so no one is obliged. Two: two recordings per school, a C and a borderline, which is where the disagreement actually lives. Three: recordings shared through each school's own managed storage, under the consent each school already uses for performance recordings, with a short shared protocol. Four: one ninety-minute online session to moderate, so no one travels. If the pilot works, we extend it in 2028. || For actions: I'll draft the protocol and a consent paragraph and circulate them by Week 5 of Term 1. Philippa, would you be willing to host the Term 3 session? And Joan, could we record in the notes that the network agrees to a voluntary Term 3 pilot, with the protocol to be confirmed at our Term 1 meeting?"}, {"id": "ns-b12", "room": "Regional and metropolitan school music network, online, 10 members on Zoom, several from regional WA", "item": "Supporting regional students to take part in jazz ensembles and the schools' jazz festival", "context": "You attend as a metropolitan head of music and as treasurer of the festival association. Regional members report that their students rarely reach the festival, and the network wants practical ways to change that in 2027.", "speakers": [{"who": "Mr Dale Hutchins, head of music, regional high school in the Goldfields, with lag", "voice": "m", "said": "Thanks, sorry about the delay on my end, the connection out here isn't great. I'll be frank. I have four kids who could hold their own in any Perth big band. But we have no jazz specialist, no rhythm section to speak of, and Perth is a long drive away. The talent isn't the problem. Distance is a tax on talent. Every year I tell those kids there'll be something next year, and every year there isn't."}, {"who": "Ms Ruth Kerslake, head of music, regional school in the Great Southern", "voice": "f", "said": "Same story here, though we're closer. I'd add that it isn't only distance, it's isolation. A student who is the only jazz player in a town has nobody to play with. What would make the biggest difference isn't a trip once a year, it's regular contact. Something that happens every fortnight, even online, so they feel part of a scene rather than visitors to one. Online can do that in a way a bus trip can't."}, {"who": "Dr Adam Rees, university jazz lecturer, network guest", "voice": "m", "said": "I'd offer the university's side. We have tertiary students who need teaching and mentoring experience as part of their study, and some would jump at structured online lessons with regional school students. The constraint is supervision and child safety. Any arrangement would need the schools to own the safeguarding and the university to own the mentor's training. Get that structure right and you have a pipeline, not a favour. We've had informal arrangements before, and they fade when the keen individual moves on."}, {"who": "Mrs Kim Ng, head of music, metropolitan school", "voice": "f", "said": "Can I bring us back to the festival itself, because I think that's where students actually see what's possible. When my school went for the first time, the students came back different. It's the hearing other bands that does it. I'm just not sure how we fix the cost of getting there. Accommodation in Perth on a festival weekend is very expensive. Billeting worked once at my old school, but it was a lot of work."}, {"who": "Ms Theresa Gallo, network convenor", "voice": "f", "said": "These are all great ideas. Dijon, you wear two hats here, school and festival. What could actually happen in 2027? We've heard about distance, about isolation, about mentors and about the cost of the festival weekend. I don't want this to become another meeting where we all agree it matters and then nothing changes. I'd like us to leave today with something specific that schools can plan around this summer."}], "keyPoints": [{"who": "Hutchins", "point": "Talented students; no specialist; distance blocks participation", "phrase": "Distance is a tax on talent"}, {"who": "Kerslake", "point": "Regular fortnightly contact matters more than one trip", "phrase": "part of a scene rather than visitors to one"}, {"who": "Rees", "point": "University mentors online if safeguarding is structured", "phrase": "a pipeline, not a favour"}, {"who": "Ng", "point": "Festival attendance transforms students; travel cost is barrier", "phrase": "It's the hearing other bands that does it"}, {"who": "Gallo (convenor)", "point": "Asks what could actually happen in 2027", "phrase": "What could actually happen in 2027?"}], "question": "What concrete arrangements the network commits to in 2027 so regional jazz students get regular contact and reach the festival.", "gap": "Nobody has connected the pieces: Rees's mentors give Kerslake's fortnightly contact, and the festival could pair regional students with a metro host school, joining that band for the weekend, which cuts accommodation and gives them the hearing Ng describes.", "modelNotes": "Q: what will the network commit to in 2027 for regional jazz students?\nROOM: DH (lag)  4 students, no specialist  \"Distance is a tax on talent\"\nROOM: RK  ≈ DH + isolation  \"part of a scene rather than visitors to one\"  fortnightly\nROOM: AR  uni mentors online; safeguarding split  \"a pipeline, not a favour\"\nME: ★ AR → RK: fortnightly online mentoring = the scene\nROOM: KN  festival transforms  \"It's the hearing other bands that does it\"  ✗ accomm. cost\nROOM: TG  → me: 2 hats  \"What could actually happen in 2027?\"\nME: Δ 1 pilot: 6 regional students, fortnightly online with uni mentors T2-T3  2 festival: regional student joins metro host band (billet / host school)  3 festival regional bursary: travel\nME: # I'll take bursary to festival board Feb\nME: @ AR + DH + RK: safeguarding + mentor framework by end Feb; @ me festival board; @ KN host school list\nGAP: pieces not linked; host-band model unspoken", "task": "The convenor turns to you. Credit at least two colleagues by name, name the question, and propose a numbered plan for 2027 with owners and dates.", "seconds": 150, "model": "Thank you, Theresa. I'll try to answer with both hats on, and make it concrete. || Dale has given us the phrase I'll be repeating to my festival board: distance is a tax on talent. And Ruth has told us what the answer actually is. It isn't one trip a year. It's being part of a scene rather than visitors to one. So the question for us is what we commit to in 2027 that gives regional students regular contact and a real path to the festival. || I think the pieces are already in this meeting. Adam's university mentors are exactly the fortnightly contact Ruth is asking for, and, as he says, if we get the safeguarding right it becomes a pipeline, not a favour. Kim has reminded us why the festival matters: it's the hearing other bands that does it. || So I'd propose three things. One: a pilot from Term 2, for up to six regional students across Dale's and Ruth's schools, with fortnightly online lessons from university jazz students, the schools owning safeguarding and the university owning mentor training. Two: for the festival, a *host band* model. A regional student joins a metropolitan school's band for the weekend, rehearses with them online beforehand, and plays with them on the day. It gives them a band to play in, which their own town can't, and it means hosting rather than hotels. Three: I'll take a proposal to the festival association board in February for a small regional travel bursary. || For actions: Adam, Dale and Ruth draft the mentoring and safeguarding framework by the end of February. Kim, could you canvass which metropolitan schools would host? And I'll report back on the bursary at our Term 1 meeting."}], "packs": [{"id": "gf-temporal-arc", "examples": [{"title": "Ten years of the mentoring partnership", "room": "University conservatorium partnership dinner", "prompt": "The university has folded its outreach office into a central unit, and the partnership that sends conservatorium students to mentor school ensembles now has no named owner. You speak at the partnership's tenth-year dinner, with the Dean present.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Ten years ago, a lecturer from this conservatorium and a head of music from a public school agreed on a simple idea: that a fifteen-year-old cellist should hear, every week, what *twenty* sounds like. | That was the whole agreement. It fitted on one page.", "note": "The founding is a concrete image rather than a mission statement; the age contrast does the work an abstraction such as 'aspirational pathways' could only gesture at, and 'one page' plants the modesty the turn will need."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "This year we found out whether an idea on one page can outlive the people who signed it. || When the outreach office was folded into the central unit in March, the partnership did not lose its funding. It lost its *owner*, which is worse.", "note": "The test is framed as a question of endurance, Lincoln's 'can long endure', and the distinction between funding and ownership is an exact diagnosis a Dean respects because it is not a complaint about money."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "I could list the people who kept it going anyway, and they know who they are. But nothing said at a dinner adds anything to what thirty-odd students did by catching two buses to a public school on a wet Tuesday in July.", "note": "The turn disclaims the speech's own power; placing the credit on a humble physical particular (two buses, a wet Tuesday) keeps the humility from sounding like false modesty."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "What is left unfinished is ours, and it is specific. The partnership needs a named owner inside the university, someone whose position description includes it. It needs a three-year memorandum rather than a handshake renewed each year. And it needs one unit of credit for the mentors, so the students doing the work are not doing it as a favour.", "note": "The transfer converts goodwill into three tasks of rising specificity; the triad gives the ear a shape, and placing the credit point last lands the request the Dean actually controls when attention is highest."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "Do those three things, and the idea from ten years ago stops depending on luck. || It becomes part of what this conservatorium *is*.", "note": "The future is stated as a single contrast, luck against identity, and offered to the institution as self-description, which a senior listener accepts more readily than a demand."}], "weak": {"text": "Thanks so much for having me tonight, it's really lovely to see so many familiar faces. The partnership has been fantastic for our students over the years and I think everyone would agree it's been a real success story. Obviously things have been a bit uncertain this year with the restructure, and I'm not entirely sure where it all sits now, but I'm sure it'll get sorted out. We'd love it if the partnership could keep going, maybe in a more formal way at some point, if that's possible. Anyway, I just wanted to say thank you to everyone involved, the mentors have been amazing, and here's to the next ten years.", "why": ["Opens with pleasantries; the strong version opens on the founding image and the promise it made.", "Calls the restructure 'a bit uncertain'; the strong version names the real test, that the partnership lost its owner.", "Buries the request in 'maybe in a more formal way at some point'; the strong version transfers three specific tasks.", "Ends on a toast to time passing; the strong version ends on the future the tasks secure."]}}, {"title": "Last concert in the old hall", "room": "Concert speech, final performance before demolition", "prompt": "The school's ageing performing arts hall is to be demolished for a new building, which is funded but whose acoustic treatment was cut in value management. You speak from the stage at the final concert, with the principal and a regional executive in the audience.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "When this hall opened, nearly sixty years ago, it made a promise that was unusual for a public school: that music would have a room *built* for it, not borrowed from assembly.", "note": "The founding is cast as a promise rather than a date; 'built for it, not borrowed' is a compact antithesis that tells the room what was rare about the hall without a word of nostalgia."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "Tonight we find out whether that promise can move house. || The new building is funded, and it is welcome. But in the last round of value management, the acoustic treatment was the line that went.", "note": "The test is posed with a homely metaphor ('move house'), and the hard fact comes after a genuine concession, so the principal and the executive hear information rather than grievance."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "I will not pretend a speech can do anything about acoustics. Nothing I say from this stage adds a single decibel to what these students just did, in a room with a leaking roof and a piano that would not hold its tuning past interval.", "note": "The turn uses a deliberately technical noun ('decibel') to deflate the speaker's own medium, then hands all the authority to the students; the self-deprecation earns the right to ask."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "What is unfinished is ours. Before the design is locked in February, the acoustic specification needs to go back in, in writing. Families can help by writing one letter each to the school council. And I will be asking the regional office for a meeting before Christmas.", "note": "The transfer names a deadline, a channel and a single action for each group, so the audience leaves with a job rather than a mood; announcing the meeting request in public raises the cost of declining without naming anyone."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "If we do that, the promise this hall made does not end with it. || It is *kept*, in a better room.", "note": "The passive 'it is kept' makes the promise the grammatical subject, and the last four words supply the hope without overstatement."}], "weak": {"text": "Well, this is a bit of a sad night really, the last concert in the old hall, and a lot of us have so many memories here. I think we all know the hall has its problems, the roof, the piano, all of that, so the new building will be great in a lot of ways. I understand there have been some changes to the design around the acoustics, which is a bit disappointing, and it would be good if people could perhaps keep that in mind going forward. Anyway, I'd just like to thank the students for a wonderful concert and everyone for coming, and let's enjoy the rest of the evening.", "why": ["Opens on sentiment and memories; the strong version opens on what the hall promised.", "Calls the acoustic cut 'a bit disappointing'; the strong version names it as the test of whether the promise survives.", "'Keep that in mind going forward' asks for nothing; the strong version gives a deadline, a letter and a meeting.", "Ends on thanks; the strong version ends on the promise kept."]}}, {"title": "Thirty years of the parents' association", "room": "Music parents' association AGM", "prompt": "The association that funds tour bursaries and runs the concert canteen holds its thirtieth AGM with four nominations for eleven committee roles. You speak before the elections.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Thirty years ago a group of parents sat in a staffroom and decided that no child would miss a tour because their family could not pay for it. | They had a cash tin and a raffle book.", "note": "The founding promise is moral and concrete at once; the closing detail scales the origin down so the present difficulty does not seem beneath it."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "Tonight is the test of whether that decision outlives the people who made it. || We have eleven positions to fill and *four* nominations.", "note": "Two numbers and no adjective: the plain shortfall carries more weight than 'concerning', and the pause lets the room arrive at the problem a beat before it is named."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "I am not going to make a speech about volunteering. Nobody here needs one, and nothing I could say adds to the hours the outgoing committee has already given, most of them at night, most of them unseen.", "note": "Refusing the expected speech is the turn; the paired 'most of them' gives the tribute rhythm without a roll call of names, which would turn honour into administration."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "What is unfinished is ours, and it can be made smaller. I propose we split the treasurer's role in two, so nobody carries the books alone. That the canteen roster moves to a term-by-term sign-up rather than a year's commitment. And that every ensemble nominates one parent, just one, before we close tonight.", "note": "'It can be made smaller' reframes the obligation as manageable, the honest persuasion a volunteer body needs; each proposal lowers the cost of saying yes, and the last is actionable before anyone leaves the room."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "Do that, and the promise made with a cash tin thirty years ago is still being *kept* when today's Year 7s are the parents in these chairs.", "note": "A ring composition: the close returns to the opening image, giving closure, and projects the promise a generation forward so the duty feels inherited rather than imposed."}], "weak": {"text": "Hi everyone, thanks for coming tonight. As you probably know, we're a bit short on nominations this year, which is a shame, because the association does such great work for the kids. I know everyone's really busy, and I totally understand that, but it would be great if a few more people could put their hands up. It doesn't have to be a huge commitment. We've been going for thirty years now, which is amazing, and it would be a pity to see things fall over. So if anyone's interested in any of the roles, have a chat to one of us afterwards. Thanks again for everything you all do.", "why": ["Mentions the thirty years in passing; the strong version opens on the founding promise.", "'A bit short' blurs the problem; the strong version gives the two numbers.", "Apologises for asking; the strong version turns instead to the outgoing committee's unseen hours.", "'Have a chat afterwards' defers the decision; the strong version makes three structural changes and asks for one parent per ensemble tonight."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["[Number] years ago, [who] decided that [the promise].", "This began with a simple promise: [promise].", "When [program] started, the whole idea fitted on one page.", "The founding idea was modest: [idea]. | That was all.", "It started in [place], with [humble detail] and a conviction that [promise].", "Before there was a [program], there was a question: why should [group] not have [thing]?", "The people who began this asked for very little and promised a great deal.", "In [year], this [body] committed itself to one proposition: [proposition].", "The original promise was never about [surface thing]; it was about [real thing].", "Everything we do tonight goes back to one decision, made by people who could not have known it would last."]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["This year we found out whether that promise could survive [the test].", "What is being tested now is not our goodwill but our [structure / funding / ownership].", "The question this year put to us is whether an idea can outlive the people who started it.", "When [event] happened in [month], the promise met its first real test.", "We are now finding out whether [promise] holds when [condition].", "The test is plain: [fact, stated as a number].", "For the first time, [program] has had to ask whether it can last without [support].", "What [event] tests is whether this belongs to a person or to an institution.", "This is the year the promise stopped being assumed and started being examined.", "[Event] did not end the idea; it asked whether the idea was strong enough to continue."]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["Nothing I say tonight adds to what [they] have already done.", "I could thank everyone by name, and I will, privately. But a speech is not the work.", "I will not pretend a speech can do anything about [problem].", "The real tribute was paid already, by [people], on [humble particular].", "Words are the cheap part of this evening.", "We are not here to make this matter. [They] already did that.", "I am not going to make a speech about [virtue]; nobody here needs one.", "Whatever I add tonight is commentary on work that is already finished.", "If anything was made sacred this year, it was done by [people] at [time], not by anyone at a lectern.", "The honour belongs to the people who [did the work], and they did not wait to be asked."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["What is left unfinished is ours, and it is specific.", "What remains is not a feeling; it is [number] tasks.", "The unfinished work is this: [task one], [task two], and [task three].", "What is left can be made smaller, and here is how.", "Before [deadline], [the specific thing] needs to happen, and [owner] is best placed to do it.", "I am asking each of us for one thing: [single action].", "The task they leave us is concrete: [task].", "If we owe them anything, we owe them [concrete outcome] by [date].", "Here is what I would ask this room to own: [task], [task], and [task].", "That work cannot be handed back to the people who have already done it."]}, {"tag": 4, "lines": ["If we do those things, the promise does not just survive. || It becomes permanent.", "Do that, and [program] stops depending on luck.", "Then the idea from [year] is still being kept when [future group] are sitting where we are.", "If we finish this, [program] becomes part of what this [institution] is.", "Then the promise is kept, in a better [room / structure / form].", "Then the next [anniversary] will be a celebration, not a rescue.", "That is how a good idea becomes an institution.", "Do this, and [the founders'] decision outlives all of us, as it should.", "If we do our part, the next generation inherits a [program], not a problem.", "Finish this, and the promise renews itself with every [intake / season / cohort]."]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Music department morning tea", "prompt": "A colleague marks ten years running the string orchestra, which began with eleven students and now has three ensembles. Place her decade in a short arc, then hand the department one unfinished task: next year's first regional tour needs two staff to share the logistics.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 1, "room": "Feeder primary combined band, end-of-year concert", "prompt": "The combined Year 5 and 6 band from local primary schools is in its fifth year. Arc from the founding promise (no child in these suburbs without access to a band) through the test (two primary schools lost their music specialists this year) to the transfer: parents to run the Thursday transport roster and one primary to host next year's concert.", "seconds": 90}, {"level": 2, "room": "University music education partnership board", "prompt": "The faculty has reduced professional experience placements in specialist music programs. Open the agenda item with the arc: why the placements were begun, what the reduction tests, and three tasks (a shared placement calendar, co-supervision with school mentors, a review in twelve months). Some academics present supported the reduction.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 2, "room": "School council, twentieth year of the specialist program", "prompt": "Council marks the specialist music program's twentieth year in a term when the enrolment cap has squeezed intake. Speak to the arc and hand council the unfinished work: a written submission to the regional office on intake numbers, and an instrument scholarship fund with a target and a deadline.", "seconds": 120}, {"level": 3, "room": "Festival association board, after a sponsor's withdrawal", "prompt": "The festival's naming sponsor of twelve years has withdrawn, and one board member has blamed the treasurer's reporting in an email to all directors. Open the strategy item with the arc and transfer three tasks: a reserve policy covering one full festival, a sponsorship subcommittee, and a revised budget by the next meeting. Expect an interruption about the reporting within the first minute.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "SCSA sector forum on practical assessment", "prompt": "You speak at a forum where senior SCSA officers will respond, on the future of practical assessment in music after the Board rejected the committee's course. Use the arc without sounding aggrieved: why practical assessment entered the syllabus, what the rejection tests, and the unfinished work (a moderation pilot, a recorded exemplar bank, a resubmission timeline). Expect a senior officer to interrupt on reliability.", "seconds": 210}]}, {"id": "gf-praise-standard", "examples": [{"title": "Life membership for an adjudicator", "room": "Festival association AGM", "prompt": "The association confers life membership on an adjudicator who has judged the festival for twenty-two years. You want to use the moment to ask members to approve a paid training scheme for new adjudicators.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "I am conscious that a certificate is a modest thing to hand someone who has given us twenty-two years of Saturdays. | It will not match them, and I will not pretend it does.", "note": "Doubting the custom at once lowers the ceremonial temperature; 'Saturdays' turns years into a unit of personal cost the room can feel."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "So let me say instead what kind of festival this is. It is one where a band from a school with no music room gets the same written feedback as a band from a school with three. Where an adjudicator's comments are read aloud in rehearsals for months afterwards. Where the standard is *candour*, delivered kindly.", "note": "Pericles' move: the praise is a portrait of the community's habits, built as an anaphoric series ('Where...'), ending on a paradoxical pairing that an educated room recognises as a real value rather than a slogan."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "Our adjudicator did not just meet that standard; for many of us she *defined* it. Directors still quote her line that tuning is an act of listening, not of tuners. And she once wrote a full page for a band that came last.", "note": "Two particulars, one quoted and one surprising, carry the tribute; the page for the band that came last proves the standard better than any adjective could."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "Which leaves the rest of us with a question. || When she steps back, who writes that page? I am asking members tonight to approve a paid training scheme for new adjudicators: four places a year, each mentored through two festivals by an experienced panel member.", "note": "The charge is posed as the standard's own question, not the treasurer's request, so the motion arrives as continuity; the specifics make it costable on the spot."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "So, to our life member: thank you, and please keep sending us notes. | To everyone else, the motion is on page six.", "note": "The consolation is brisk and warm, then a dry practical pointer releases the room with a smile and a task; brevity here is Periclean, not curt."}], "weak": {"text": "It's a real honour tonight to present life membership to someone who's been such a huge part of the festival for so many years. I don't think there's anyone in this room who hasn't benefited from her feedback at some point. She's always been incredibly generous with her time and expertise, and she's just been amazing. We're going to miss her a lot. On a related note, we've been talking at committee level about maybe looking at some kind of training for new adjudicators, so that's something we'll probably bring to members at some stage. But tonight is about celebrating her, so please join me in congratulating her.", "why": ["Opens with 'a real honour'; the strong version doubts the custom, which makes the tribute more credible.", "Praises with adjectives; the strong version portrays the festival's standard and shows her defining it with two particulars.", "Buries the training scheme as 'on a related note' for 'some stage'; the strong version makes it the charge, with numbers, tonight.", "Lets the applause end the item; the strong version releases the room with a page number."]}}, {"title": "Farewell to four council members", "room": "School council he chairs, final meeting of the year", "prompt": "Four council members finish their terms, including the deputy chair. As chair you farewell them and ask council to adopt a formal induction and mentoring process for incoming members.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Council minutes are not the place for eloquence, and a farewell at item eleven cannot do justice to six years of service. I will keep it short.", "note": "Doubting the custom through the meeting's own furniture (minutes, item eleven) is a light, self-aware touch that suits a governance setting and signals the chair will not indulge himself."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "But let me say what kind of council this has become. One where members read the papers before they arrive. Where disagreement is put in the meeting and not in the car park. Where we ask what a decision does for the student who is *not* in the room.", "note": "The standard is three governance habits, each concrete enough to check against conduct; the third lifts the portrait from procedure to purpose."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "Our four departing members did not just keep that standard; they built it. Our deputy chair rewrote the conflict of interest register when nobody asked her to. Two of you sat through every enrolment-cap hearing. And one of you asked, at my first meeting as chair, the question I still use: *who* is this for?", "note": "The particulars are distributed so every member is recognised by deed rather than by name; ending on the quoted question returns the credit to them inside the chair's own practice."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "Which leaves us with a question. A standard that lives in four people walks out the door with them. I move that council adopt an induction process: a briefing pack, a mentor from the continuing members, and a review conversation after the first two meetings.", "note": "The charge states the risk as an aphorism before the motion, so the proposal sounds like the obvious consequence of the praise; moving it formally keeps it from dissolving into sentiment."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "To the four of you: thank you, sincerely. You leave this council better than you found it, | which is the only measure that matters.", "note": "One sentence of thanks and one of judgement; the closing clause gives the departing members a standard to be proud of rather than a list."}], "weak": {"text": "Before we close, I just want to say a big thank you to our four members who are finishing up. You've all been fantastic and it's been great working with you. You've put in so many hours and we really appreciate everything. I think the council's in a much better place now, and a lot of that is down to you. Going forward we might need to think about how we bring new people up to speed, because there's a lot to learn, so maybe we can put that on the agenda for next time. Anyway, thank you all again, and please stay in touch.", "why": ["Skips the doubt and goes straight to generic thanks; the strong version frames the farewell honestly as inadequate to the service.", "'In a much better place' is a conclusion without a picture; the strong version describes three habits that make the council good.", "No member is recognised for a particular act; the strong version gives each a deed.", "The induction idea is deferred to 'next time'; the strong version moves it, now, as what the standard requires."]}}, {"title": "Prize for music education graduates", "room": "Conservatorium graduation prize-giving", "prompt": "As an invited speaker from the profession you present the prize for music education graduates. You want the graduates to register as mentors for the school partnership program before they leave.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "I am conscious that I am the last speaker before you are allowed to celebrate, and that nothing said from a lectern adds much to four years of practice rooms. | So I will be quick.", "note": "The doubt is delivered with a smile at the speaker's own position, which buys goodwill from a young audience and patience from the faculty."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "Let me tell you instead what kind of profession you are joining. It is one where the best musician in the building is often teaching the weakest ensemble. Where a Tuesday afternoon with twelve beginners is taken as seriously as a recital. Where we judge ourselves by what our students can do when we are *not* there.", "note": "The portrait is of the profession, so graduates hear an invitation into something; each clause inverts a status assumption, and the last defines teaching by its absence, a quiet paradox that lands with an educated room."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "This year's prizewinner already works like that. She ran a lunchtime band at her placement school because nobody else would, and when her placement ended, the students *kept* rehearsing without her.", "note": "One particular, proving the last clause of the standard exactly; the tribute is made by structure, because she has met the definition just given."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "Which leaves the rest of you with a question. || Who taught you to work like that, and who will you teach? The partnership program needs twenty mentors next year, one afternoon a fortnight in a public school ensemble. The forms are at the back of the hall, and I would like most of them gone by nine o'clock.", "note": "The charge asks a question that implicates every graduate, then names a small, precise commitment; the wry deadline turns an abstract duty into a physical action before they leave."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "To the families: be proud, they earned it. | To the graduates: go and be the reason someone stays in music.", "note": "Brief consolation in two balanced sentences; the final phrase states the stakes of the profession in plain words and lets the room go."}], "weak": {"text": "Congratulations to all of you, what an amazing achievement. Teaching is such an important job and I really believe music education changes lives. The prizewinner tonight has been outstanding throughout her degree and has shown real commitment, so huge congratulations to her. As you go out into your careers, please remember how much schools need good music teachers, and if any of you are interested, there's a mentoring program you might want to look into, I think there's some information somewhere. It's a great way to give back. Anyway, I won't keep you from the celebrations. Well done, everyone.", "why": ["'Music education changes lives' is a slogan; the strong version describes the profession's habits so the claim is shown, not asserted.", "'Outstanding' and 'real commitment' are generic; the strong version gives one act that fits the standard exactly.", "The mentoring program is 'somewhere'; the strong version names the number, the commitment and where the forms are.", "Never doubts the custom, so the speech sounds like every other one; the strong version earns brevity by naming it."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["I am conscious that no speech matches what [they] did.", "Nothing I say in the next [two] minutes will match what you have seen tonight, so I will be brief.", "A certificate is a modest thing to hand someone who has given [years / Saturdays / nights].", "Speeches at these occasions tend to fall short of the people they honour, and I expect this one will too.", "[Occasion] is not the place for eloquence, and I will not attempt it.", "I am the last thing standing between you and [supper / the celebration], so I will be quick.", "Words are a poor return on [years] of work, but they are what the custom asks for.", "I hesitate to add anything to what the music has already said.", "Whatever I say tonight will be smaller than what happened here this year.", "I will try not to spoil a good evening with a long speech."]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["Let me say instead what kind of place produces work like this.", "It is a place where [habit], where [habit], and where [habit].", "This is a [community] where nobody is too good to [humble task].", "What marks this [program / festival / council] is not its results but its habits.", "Here the standard is [value], delivered [manner].", "It is one where [the strongest] spend their time on [the least experienced].", "We are a [body] that [habit], even when nobody is watching.", "The thing visitors notice first is not the [trophies]; it is [habit].", "Let me tell you what kind of profession you are joining.", "If you want to know what this [community] values, watch what happens at [ordinary moment]."]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["And this year's group did not just meet that standard; they set it.", "[Name or role] did not just keep that standard; for many of us, they defined it.", "One particular will do: [telling act].", "I could list the results, but this says more: [small act].", "[They] ran [task] all of [term] without being asked.", "Directors still quote [their] line: [quotation].", "When [their] [placement / term / role] ended, [the work] carried on without them, which is the test.", "[They] once [unexpected act], and that is the standard in one gesture.", "Every one of [them] can be recognised by a deed, not a title.", "If you want the standard in a single person, it is [them]."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["Which leaves the rest of us with a question we have to answer.", "A standard that lives in [a few people] walks out the door with them.", "When [they] step back, who does [their work]?", "If the standard is to survive, [specific requirement].", "Being worthy of that standard now requires [concrete step].", "So I am asking [the room] to [action], starting [date].", "Here is what keeping faith with that looks like: [proposal].", "I move that [body] [proposal], because that is what the standard demands.", "Who taught you to work like that, and who will you teach?", "The standard does not maintain itself; it needs [resource] by [date]."]}, {"tag": 4, "lines": ["So, to the families: be proud, and then let them go and do it again.", "Thank you, sincerely. You leave this [body] better than you found it.", "To [them]: thank you. To everyone else: [practical pointer].", "Be proud tonight; the work can wait until [Monday].", "Go and be the reason someone stays in music.", "Thank you for [years / the year]. We will try to deserve it.", "That is all I wanted to say. || Enjoy your evening.", "To the [honourees]: well done, and please keep [habit].", "Celebrate tonight; you have earned it.", "Thank you. | Now let them have the stage back."]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Junior big band, end-of-year rehearsal", "prompt": "Speak to the junior big band and the parents waiting at the door after the last rehearsal of the year. Describe the band's habits (setting up without being asked, older students helping beginners), name one or two moments that showed them, and charge the Year 8s to mentor next year's newcomers in Week 1.", "seconds": 60}, {"level": 1, "room": "Music department staff meeting", "prompt": "Thank the instrumental staff who marked and moderated the semester practical exams across two weekends. Describe the department's standard for fair assessment, give one particular, and charge the department to write next year's shared exemplar recordings in Term 1.", "seconds": 90}, {"level": 2, "room": "Feeder primary combined band ceremony", "prompt": "Five primary principals attend the combined band's end-of-year presentation. Describe the standard the program stands for (every child plays, nobody is turned away for an instrument), show one or two students who met it, and charge each primary to release a staff member for two Thursday afternoons next year.", "seconds": 120}, {"level": 2, "room": "Music parents' association volunteers' thank-you", "prompt": "Thank the parents who ran the canteen, uniforms and tour fundraising this year. Some feel taken for granted. Describe the community's standard, give particulars, and charge the association to fund three tour bursaries next year from a new monthly giving scheme.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 3, "room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee, farewell to the outgoing chair", "prompt": "The committee farewells its chair after the Board rejected the course; several members believe the committee failed and one has said so on the record. Praise the standard the committee kept (evidence, consultation, candour), show the chair meeting it, and charge members to commit to the resubmission timetable. Expect a member to interject that praise is premature.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "Regional principals' network meeting", "prompt": "You are given five minutes at a meeting of secondary principals, several of whom regard specialist music programs as expensive and exclusive. Describe the standard of public school music at its best (open access, older students teaching younger, public performance), give particulars from more than one school, and charge them to establish a shared instrument bank. Expect a principal to interrupt about cost per student.", "seconds": 210}]}, {"id": "gf-six-part-oration", "examples": [{"title": "Raising the entry fee", "room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "As treasurer you propose raising the festival entry fee by forty dollars per band, with a hardship waiver for any school that asks. The objection you expect is that small and regional schools will withdraw.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Thank you, Chair. This is the least glamorous item on the agenda, so I will be brief and I will be exact.", "note": "Goodwill through a promise of economy and precision, the only flattery a board accepts; naming the item's dullness disarms the groan before it forms."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "The entry fee has not changed in six years. Over the same period venue hire has risen every year, and adjudicator fees rose last year by resolution of this board. This year the gap was met from reserves.", "note": "Facts in chronological order, without adjectives or blame; the last sentence lands the consequence flatly, so the board reaches the problem from the evidence rather than from the treasurer's alarm."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "We agree the festival must stay open to every school. We differ, I suspect, on one point: whether a fee rise threatens that. I will make the case for the rise, then deal with that concern *directly*.", "note": "The map separates shared value from disputed means, which lowers defences, and announces the refutation so the sceptics wait for it rather than interrupting."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "First, forty dollars a band closes most of the gap without touching reserves. Second, a hardship waiver, granted on request and without documentation, protects any school for which the fee is a barrier. Third, a fee that reflects cost gives us a stronger position in next month's sponsorship conversation.", "note": "Three proofs, enumerated and escalating from arithmetic to equity to strategy; the third widens the frame so the rise reads as policy, not a patch."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "The strongest objection is that small and regional schools will withdraw. But the waiver is designed for exactly them, and it asks nothing but an email. If the fee is the barrier, the waiver removes it; | if it is not, the fee was never the problem.", "note": "The best objection is stated in its own terms and answered with the mechanism already proposed; the closing dilemma, in balanced antithesis, leaves the objector no third position."}, {"tag": 5, "text": "So: we keep every school, and we stop spending the reserve that *protects* them. || I move the fee schedule as circulated, with the waiver attached.", "note": "Summary in one clause, amplification through the irony that the reserve exists for the very schools in question, then a formal motion so the feeling becomes a vote."}], "weak": {"text": "So, just quickly on the fees. Costs have gone up a fair bit, as everyone knows, venues and adjudicators and so on, and we've had to dip into reserves a little this year. I was wondering whether we might think about a small increase, maybe forty dollars or so, although I know some people are worried about the smaller schools, and that's a really valid concern, so maybe we could have some kind of waiver if people needed it. I don't want to push it if people aren't comfortable. Maybe we could just discuss it and see where everyone lands?", "why": ["Opens with 'just quickly', which shrinks the item; the strong version opens with a promise of brevity and exactness.", "Blurs the facts ('a fair bit', 'a little'); the strong version gives the sequence and the consequence plainly.", "Raises the objection before making the case and concedes it as 'really valid'; the strong version proves first, then refutes with the waiver's design.", "Ends with 'see where everyone lands'; the strong version ends with a motion."]}}, {"title": "A shared reporting rubric", "room": "Music department meeting", "prompt": "You propose a common rubric for all instrumental teachers' semester reports. The objection you expect is that it erodes each teacher's professional judgement about their own instrument.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "I know this is the last item before lunch, and I know 'rubric' is nobody's favourite word. Give me four minutes.", "note": "Exordium by honest acknowledgement of the room's mood; naming resistance to the topic itself earns attention more reliably than enthusiasm."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "At the moment each of us writes semester comments in our own format. Last semester two families compared reports and asked why one child was 'excellent' at a level another child was 'developing'. The deputy asked me for an answer, and I did not have a good one.", "note": "The narration is a short story with a specific trigger, told without blaming a colleague; admitting he had no answer puts the speaker inside the problem rather than above it."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "We all agree reports should be fair to students. Where we may differ is whether a shared rubric helps that or *flattens* what is distinctive about each instrument. I will argue that it helps, then take the flattening objection seriously.", "note": "The partitio isolates one disputed question and names the objection in the colleagues' own likely word, a sign of respect that lowers the temperature before the argument begins."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "Three points. Four common criteria, tone, technique, ensemble skills and practice habits, give families one consistent language. Each teacher keeps a fifth criterion specific to their instrument, written by them. And the comment box stays exactly as it is, so the professional voice is not lost.", "note": "Proof by design: the confirmatio presents a mechanism with its concessions already built in, which prepares the refutation before the objection is named."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "The real objection is that ensemble skills on brass are not ensemble skills on percussion. Agreed. That is why the descriptors will be written together, in one session, by *us*, not by me. If they do not work after a semester, we revise them.", "note": "Concession first ('Agreed'), then a remedy that hands ownership to the objectors, then a review clause; the refutation succeeds by sharing control rather than by winning the point."}, {"tag": 5, "text": "So: one language for families, your judgement intact, and an answer I can actually give the deputy. || Can we book the writing session for Week 3?", "note": "The peroration compresses the three proofs into a tricolon and ends on a small, concrete request, which is easier to agree to than 'the rubric' in the abstract."}], "weak": {"text": "I've been thinking it might be good if we all used a similar format for reports. There was a bit of an issue last semester with a couple of families, nothing major, but it got me thinking. I know everyone does things differently for their instrument and that's totally fine, I'm not trying to tell anyone how to do their job. It's just an idea really. Maybe we could have some common things and then people could add their own bits. I don't know, what does everyone think? We could maybe look at it next term if people are interested.", "why": ["Has no exordium, so the room hears 'I've been thinking' and relaxes; the strong version asks for four minutes and gets them.", "'A bit of an issue, nothing major' hides the facts; the strong version narrates the two reports and the deputy's question.", "Apologises for the objection instead of answering it; the strong version concedes, then hands the descriptors to the staff.", "'Maybe next term' closes nothing; the strong version asks for a session in Week 3."]}}, {"title": "An in-house instrument technician", "room": "Principal's office", "prompt": "You ask the principal to fund an instrument technician one day a week instead of sending repairs out piece by piece. Her known objection is that the school cannot add staffing lines mid-year.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Thank you for the time. I have one request, and I have tried to bring you the numbers rather than the frustration.", "note": "The exordium honours only the principal's time and signals discipline; contrasting numbers with frustration tells a busy senior listener what kind of meeting this will be."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "This year we sent out forty-one instruments for repair. The average turnaround was five weeks, and in that time those students either borrowed or sat out. The external repair bill is already above what we budgeted for the year.", "note": "The narration runs in causal order: volume, delay, effect on students, then cost; leaving money last ensures the human consequence is heard first."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "We agree the money has to be spent well. The questions are whether one day a week in-house is better value than piecemeal repairs, and whether it can be done *without* a new staffing line. I will take each.", "note": "Partitio by two questions, the second of which is the principal's own objection, so she hears it adopted into the case rather than opposed."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "On value: one day a week costs less than this year's external bill, and most of our repairs are pads, corks and valves, which take an hour, not five weeks. Students are back playing within days.", "note": "The confirmatio is short because the narration has done the work; the concrete nouns make the case tangible and imply the speaker knows the trade."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "On staffing: it does not need to be a line. It can be a contract for services for the rest of this year, paid from the repair budget it replaces, and reviewed in Term 4 before anything permanent is decided.", "note": "The refutation dissolves the objection by changing the mechanism rather than disputing the rule, which leaves her constraint intact and her authority respected."}, {"tag": 5, "text": "So: cheaper, faster, and no new line. I am asking for approval to seek two quotes this week. || The students sitting out today would be *playing* by the end of the month.", "note": "Summary as a tricolon, then a minimal request, then an appeal through a single image; the closing sentence gives the decision a face."}], "weak": {"text": "I wanted to chat about repairs, because it's been a real headache this year. We've had so many instruments out and it takes forever to get them back, and the kids are missing out, and honestly the costs are getting a bit out of hand. I was thinking it would be amazing if we could get someone in-house, even just a day a week. I know staffing is tricky at the moment and I completely understand if it's not possible. I just thought I'd raise it. Is there any way we could look at something like that?", "why": ["Opens with 'a real headache', leading with frustration; the strong version promises numbers instead.", "'So many' and 'forever' replace the facts; the strong version gives forty-one instruments and five weeks.", "Surrenders to the staffing objection; the strong version answers it with a contract for services paid from the budget it replaces.", "Ends with a vague question; the strong version asks for approval to seek two quotes this week."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["Thank you, Chair. I will take [four] minutes, and I will keep it to [four].", "This is the least glamorous item on the agenda, so I will be brief and exact.", "I know this is the last item before [lunch / the break]. Give me [four] minutes.", "Thank you for the time. I have one request, and I have brought the numbers.", "I will not repeat what is in the paper; I want to add one thing to it.", "I am grateful for the chance to put this properly, rather than in passing.", "I know this has been discussed before, so I will try to say something new.", "Let me be useful rather than long.", "I raise this with some care, because I know [the room] has views on it.", "I will keep this to the point, and the point is [subject]."]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["Here is what happened, in order.", "The facts are these: [fact], then [fact], then [consequence].", "[Thing] has not changed in [years]. Over the same period, [change].", "The Board's note says [objection], in paragraph [number].", "This year we [did X]. The result was [result].", "In [month], [event]. Since then, [development].", "At the moment, [current practice]. Last [term], [triggering incident].", "Put simply: [number] [items], [duration] each, at [cost].", "The record shows [fact], and nobody has disputed that.", "That is the position as it stands; I will not add colour to it."]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["We agree on the goal; we differ on [number] points, and I will take them in turn.", "We agree that [shared value]. We differ on one point: whether [disputed question].", "I will make the case for [proposal], then deal with [objection] directly.", "There are two questions here: [first], and [second]. I will take each.", "What is not in dispute is [X]. What is in dispute is [Y].", "Let me separate what we agree on from what we do not.", "I will argue [position] and then answer the strongest objection to it.", "The disagreement is narrower than it looks: it is about [means], not [ends].", "I have three points, and the third is the objection.", "We are not arguing about whether, only about how."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["First, the evidence that [proposal] works.", "Three points. [First], [second], and [third].", "On value: [proof].", "The mechanism is simple: [step], [step], [step].", "That gives [the Board / the panel] what it asked for without [cost].", "The evidence is already in hand: [evidence].", "Second, and more important, [stronger point].", "[Proposal] costs less than [current practice], and it does more.", "What this protects is [the thing the room values].", "Each part answers a concern already on the record."]}, {"tag": 4, "lines": ["The best argument against this is [cost], so let me deal with it head on.", "The strongest objection is [objection]. But [answer].", "The real objection is that [objection]. Agreed. That is why [remedy].", "On [objection]: it does not need to be [X]. It can be [Y].", "If [objection] is the barrier, [mechanism] removes it; if it is not, [it] was never the problem.", "I take that concern seriously, and the proposal is built around it.", "It is fair to ask [question]. The answer is [answer].", "The objection assumes [premise]; the [figures / record] show otherwise.", "If after [period] it does not work, we [revise / withdraw] it.", "That objection was right about the old model; it does not apply to this one."]}, {"tag": 5, "lines": ["So, in short, and this is what matters most: [point].", "So: [benefit], [benefit], and [benefit].", "I move [proposal] as circulated.", "I would like the minutes to record that [action] by [date].", "What I am asking for today is only [small step].", "[The room] asked for [X]. We can give it [X] and keep [Y].", "Can we [book / agree / approve] [action] for [date]?", "If we do this, [the image of the outcome].", "The choice is between [option A] and [option B], and I think it is clear.", "That is the case. || I am happy to take questions."]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Junior big band parents' meeting", "prompt": "Propose moving the junior big band's rehearsal from before school to Wednesday after school. Facts: attendance before school has fallen across the term and several students catch early buses. Expected objection: after-school clashes with sport.", "seconds": 90}, {"level": 1, "room": "Music department meeting", "prompt": "Propose a shared, catalogued sheet music library in place of each teacher's own cupboard. Facts: parts have been lost twice this year before concerts. Expected objection: nobody has time to catalogue it.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 2, "room": "School council he chairs", "prompt": "Propose that council endorse a confidential hardship policy for the voluntary music contribution, administered by the business manager. Expected objection from a parent member: that it forces families to disclose their finances to the school.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 2, "room": "Feeder primary principals' meeting", "prompt": "Propose moving the combined primary band from Thursday to Tuesday afternoons so it no longer clashes with interschool sport. Expected objection: specialist teachers' timetables are already set for next year.", "seconds": 120}, {"level": 3, "room": "Arts funding panel interview", "prompt": "Defend the festival's application for project funding to take adjudicator clinics to regional schools. A panel member has signalled that school music is the education department's responsibility, not an arts funder's. Expect to be interrupted during the narratio.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "Principal and associate principal, staffing review", "prompt": "Argue against a proposal to merge the music and drama ensemble allocations into one performing arts pool. The associate principal's objection is efficiency and flexibility. Both have read the department's figures, and the associate principal will challenge your numbers mid-argument.", "seconds": 210}]}, {"id": "gf-hard-truth-resolve", "examples": [{"title": "The course will slip a year", "room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee", "prompt": "As convenor of the working group, you must tell the committee that the revised course cannot be ready for the next approval round, so implementation moves back a year.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "I have no way of making this sound better than it is, so I will give it to you straight. || The revised course will not be ready for the next approval round.", "note": "The offer of nothing but the truth is followed at once by the truth, in plain short words; the held pause separates the promise of candour from its proof."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "That means implementation moves back a full year. Schools that planned staffing around it will have to replan. And some of the goodwill we built with teachers during consultation will be *spent*; we will have to earn it again.", "note": "The ordeal is set out as three descending consequences in short declaratives; 'spent' is a monetary metaphor that names the reputational cost exactly without dramatising it."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "So here is what we will do. We use the extra year to pilot the practical assessment in eight schools, so the Board's reliability question is answered with evidence rather than argument.", "note": "The policy is one sentence with its rationale attached, converting the delay into an instrument; 'evidence rather than argument' speaks the Board's own language back to it."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "The aim is one thing. || A course the Board cannot reject for the same reason *twice*.", "note": "The aim is compressed into a noun phrase, Churchillian in shape; defining success by what the Board cannot do gives the committee a measurable and slightly defiant target."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "I am confident we can, because the course was never wrong in its intent; it was short of proof, and proof can be gathered. I will circulate the pilot design within a fortnight, and I am asking each of you to name one school by then.", "note": "Resolve grounded in a diagnosis rather than in hope, then a dated action and a request with a deadline; confidence resting on a plan sounds adult to a senior room."}], "weak": {"text": "So, I've got a bit of an update from the working group, and unfortunately it's not quite the news we were hoping for. It's looking like it might be quite difficult to get everything ready in time for the next round, so there may be some delay, possibly. We're still working through it, and there are a few options on the table. One thing we could potentially do is some kind of pilot, which might help with the Board's concerns. I think it's still a really good course and I'm hopeful we'll get there in the end. I'll keep everyone posted as things develop.", "why": ["Wraps the news in 'not quite' and 'might' and 'possibly'; the strong version states it in one plain sentence.", "Never says what the delay costs; the strong version names the year, the replanning and the spent goodwill.", "'Some kind of pilot' among 'a few options' is no policy; the strong version names eight schools and the purpose.", "Ends on hope and 'keep everyone posted'; the strong version ends on a dated plan and a request to each member."]}}, {"title": "The tour is shortened", "room": "Parent body, tour families' briefing", "prompt": "Airfares and accommodation for the senior ensembles' overseas tour have risen well beyond the budget. You must tell families the tour will be six days, not nine, before rumours reach them.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "I would rather you heard this from me tonight than from a group chat tomorrow, so I will not dress it up. || The tour will be six days, not nine.", "note": "Naming the alternative channel justifies the bluntness and signals respect; the news follows immediately in short words, so no parent sits through a preamble dreading it."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "Airfares and accommodation rose well beyond what we budgeted, and the choice was to raise the cost to families by a large amount or to shorten the trip. Some of you have already booked leave around the original dates. Some students will lose the workshop they were most looking forward to. I know that, and I am *sorry* for it.", "note": "The ordeal includes the decision logic and the human cost in concrete particulars; a single plain apology, unhedged, is stronger than several softened ones."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "So here is what we will do. The cost to families stays exactly as quoted. The three concerts stay; the sightseeing days go. And anyone who needs to change leave will have a letter from the school by Friday.", "note": "The policy is three short guarantees, ordered by what parents weigh first: money, then purpose, then their own logistics."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "The aim is simple. || Every student plays every concert they rehearsed for.", "note": "The aim is a student-centred guarantee in plain, mostly single-syllable words, so parents can repeat it at home in exactly the same terms."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "I am confident in this, because the music was always the point of the tour and the music is *intact*. I will be here after the meeting for anyone who wants to talk it through, and I ask you to bring your questions to me rather than to the chat.", "note": "Resolve anchored in purpose rather than optimism, then a practical offer and one firm request that keeps the information where the speaker can answer it."}], "weak": {"text": "Thanks for coming, everyone. So, there's been a bit of a development with the tour that we need to talk about. As you know, costs everywhere have been going up, flights especially, and it's been really challenging to make the numbers work. We've looked at lots of different options, and after a lot of discussion, we think the best thing might be to make the tour a little bit shorter, probably around six days. I know that's disappointing, and I'm really sorry, I know some of you have made plans. We're still finalising the details, but we'll try to keep the most important parts.", "why": ["Delays the news behind 'a bit of a development'; the strong version gives it in the second sentence.", "'A little bit shorter, probably around six days' sounds negotiable; the strong version says six days, not nine.", "'The most important parts' is undefined; the strong version guarantees the cost and the three concerts.", "No aim and no resolve; the strong version states the aim in one line and ends with an offer and a request."]}}, {"title": "The intake we cannot staff", "room": "Principal's office", "prompt": "Next year's Year 7 music intake is larger than the department can staff without closing an ensemble. You must tell the principal before the offer letters go out.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "I need to give you a problem I cannot solve inside the department, and I would rather you heard it now than in February.", "note": "Upward bad news opens by marking the boundary of the speaker's own authority, which is honest and frames the principal's role without asking for sympathy."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "With the intake as offered, we are about six teaching hours a week short. If nothing changes, one ensemble closes, and the likeliest is intermediate strings, which is where we keep the Year 8s not yet ready for the senior orchestra. *Losing* it would cost us students by Year 9.", "note": "The ordeal is quantified, located and traced to its consequence; the chain of reasoning shows the principal the downstream cost she would otherwise discover too late."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "So here is what I propose. We hold the intake as offered, and the school funds six hours of instrumental teaching for one year, reviewed at the end of Term 2 against retention.", "note": "One sentence of policy with a limit and a review built in, so it becomes a decision the principal can defend rather than an open-ended commitment."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "The aim is plain. || No ensemble closes because we said yes to too many children.", "note": "The aim reframes the problem as the cost of success, which persuades a principal, and the plain diction keeps it from sounding like a pitch."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "I am confident it works, because the retention figures will either justify it or they will not, and you will see them before anything is permanent. I will have a costed one-page summary on your desk by Thursday.", "note": "Resolve rests on a test the principal controls and ends with a dated deliverable; the confidence is procedural, which is the kind a senior leader trusts."}], "weak": {"text": "I just wanted to flag something about next year, it's probably nothing you haven't heard before, but staffing is looking really tight with the bigger intake. We'll do our best, obviously, but there's a chance we might have to cut something, maybe one of the string groups, which would be a real shame. I'm not sure what the answer is. I suppose if there was any extra money floating around for a few hours that would help, but I know the budget is tight for everyone. Anyway, I thought you should know. We'll try to make it work.", "why": ["'Just wanted to flag' and 'probably nothing new' shrink the problem; the strong version names it as one the department cannot solve alone.", "'Really tight' and 'might have to cut something' replace the figures; the strong version gives six hours, the ensemble and the Year 9 consequence.", "'Any extra money floating around' is a hint, not a proposal; the strong version asks for six hours for one year with a review.", "'We'll try to make it work' undercuts the case; the strong version ends with a test and a summary by Thursday."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["I have no good news to soften this, so I will not try.", "I would rather you heard this from me than from [rumour / the newsletter], so I will not dress it up.", "I need to give you a problem I cannot solve on my own.", "There is no way of making this sound better than it is.", "I will give you the news first and the plan second.", "What I can offer you is the truth and a plan; I cannot offer you [what they hoped for].", "I am not going to manage you on this. Here it is.", "You deserve this plainly, so I will say it once and clearly.", "I would be doing you no favours by softening this.", "I have one hard thing to tell you, and then what we will do about it."]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["This will be hard, and it will take longer than any of us wants.", "It means [consequence]. It also means [consequence].", "Some of you will [cost]. Some students will [cost].", "I expect it to take [two] years, possibly [three].", "If nothing changes, [specific loss].", "We are [number] [hours / dollars / places] short.", "It will not be fixed in one [budget round / term / meeting].", "Some of the goodwill we built will be spent, and we will have to earn it again.", "I know that, and I am sorry for it.", "That is the full size of it; I have not left anything out."]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["So here is what we will do.", "So here is what I propose: [one sentence].", "We keep [what matters]. We change [what can change].", "We use [the delay / the loss] to [purpose].", "The cost to [families / students] stays exactly as [quoted / planned].", "[What matters] stays; [what can go] goes.", "We hold [commitment], and [resource] covers the gap for [period].", "I will take on [task] myself.", "Nobody's [ensemble / concert / place] disappears.", "The plan is one line: [policy]."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["And the aim is simple.", "The aim is one thing. || [Aim].", "No student leaves the program because of this.", "Every student plays every concert they rehearsed for.", "A [course / proposal] that cannot be rejected for the same reason twice.", "No [ensemble / program] closes because we said yes to too many children.", "The aim is that by [date], [measurable outcome].", "What success looks like is [one plain sentence].", "Nothing the students see changes.", "The aim is plain: [aim]."]}, {"tag": 4, "lines": ["I am confident we can do it, and I am asking you to do it with me.", "I am confident, because [reason that does not depend on luck].", "This team has done harder things with less notice.", "I will [meet each of you / circulate the plan] by [date].", "I will have a costed summary on your desk by [day].", "I am asking each of you for [one thing] by [date].", "Bring your questions to me, not to [the chat / the car park].", "You will see the figures before anything is permanent.", "The [music / purpose] is intact, and that is what we came for.", "We will get through this, and we will do it properly."]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Music department staff meeting", "prompt": "Tell staff that the practice room air-conditioning will not be repaired until the end of next term because the contractor's quote exceeds the minor works limit. Give the policy (a rotating timetable using the two cooler rooms, lessons moved earlier in the day) and the aim.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 1, "room": "Junior big band rehearsal", "prompt": "Tell the junior big band that the festival entry was lodged late and they will perform as a non-competitive band this year. Own the error plainly, then give the policy (full adjudicator feedback still arranged, a showcase concert for families) and the aim.", "seconds": 60}, {"level": 2, "room": "Feeder primary principals' meeting", "prompt": "Tell the primary principals that the combined band cannot take Year 4 students next year because two of its tutors have left and replacements are not funded. Give the policy (Year 4 taster workshops each term, priority entry in Year 5) and the aim. One principal had promised Year 4 places to parents.", "seconds": 120}, {"level": 2, "room": "University conservatorium partnership steering group", "prompt": "Tell the steering group that the school cannot host music education placements in Semester 1 because of staff leave. Give the policy (doubled placements in Semester 2, a co-supervision arrangement with a neighbouring school) and the aim. The university's placement officer is under pressure to find sites.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 3, "room": "Festival association general meeting", "prompt": "A scoring error placed one band third when it should have been first. Directors are angry, and two have threatened to withdraw their schools. As a board member, tell the meeting what happened in plain terms, then give the policy (corrected results published, an independent check of tabulation from next year, a written apology to the school) and the aim. Expect interruptions demanding to know who was responsible.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "School council he chairs", "prompt": "Open a council meeting by telling members that the voluntary music contribution must rise next year to keep all ensembles running, with a P&C delegate already circulating objections. Give the ordeal in figures, the policy (the rise, a confidential hardship waiver, a published breakdown of where the money goes) and the aim. Expect the delegate to interrupt during the ordeal.", "seconds": 210}]}, {"id": "gf-hard-choice-reframe", "examples": [{"title": "Written feedback for every band", "room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "Directors have complained for years that the festival returns a score and a sentence. You propose that every band receives written feedback against a published rubric from next year's festival.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Consider the arithmetic of a festival. A band rehearses for six months, plays for twelve minutes, and goes home with a number on a sheet. || Half a year of work, answered in a *single* digit.", "note": "Compression by descending quantities: six months, twelve minutes, one digit. The scale shrinks in front of the room so the imbalance is felt before it is argued, and the held pause lets the members do the sum themselves."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "So I am proposing that this board *chooses* to change that. Every band entering next year's festival receives written feedback from every adjudicator, against a rubric we publish in advance, within a fortnight of playing.", "note": "The verb 'chooses' carries the formula: the reform becomes an act of will by this board rather than a concession to complaints, which gives members ownership instead of defensiveness. Three hard specifics (every, published, a fortnight) turn a value into something auditable."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "Not because it is easy. Adjudicators will have to write while they listen, and some will resist a rubric they did not design. But that is exactly why it is worth doing: it makes us decide, *in public*, what we actually reward.", "note": "Concession, then inversion. He names the two real objections before anyone else can, then turns difficulty into the mechanism of value. 'In public' signals accountability, which an educated board respects more than enthusiasm."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "The cost is a rubric workshop in March, about forty dollars more per adjudicator session, and my time collating the sheets. I have costed it, and the figures are on page four of the treasurer's report.", "note": "Cost stated in actual units and delivered flat, with no apology. Pointing to a page already tabled moves the claim from rhetoric to record and pre-empts 'has this been costed?'"}, {"tag": 4, "text": "For thirty years we have judged the bands. || It is time we let the bands judge *our judging*.", "note": "Chiasmus on the festival's own core verb: subject and object swap, so the board hears itself as the thing being assessed. Built from the room's vocabulary, it sounds inevitable rather than manufactured."}], "weak": {"text": "I just wanted to raise something a few directors have mentioned to me about the feedback side of things. I think, and I might be wrong, that some of them feel the scores don't really tell them much. It's obviously a big ask for the adjudicators, and a lot of them are volunteers, so I don't want to put extra pressure on anyone. But maybe we could look at some kind of written component down the track, possibly with a rubric, if people think that's worthwhile. There'd be some costs, I imagine, but we could work that out later. Anyway, I think it's something to think about, because feedback is really important for the kids.", "why": ["Opens on hearsay ('a few directors') instead of a compressed image that makes the problem felt.", "Treats difficulty as a reason to hesitate; the strong version names it and makes it the reason.", "'Down the track' and 'some costs' replace a deadline and a figure on a tabled page.", "Ends on a generality about feedback instead of a balanced line that turns the room's question on itself."]}}, {"title": "From goodwill to credit", "room": "University school of music, joint partnership committee", "prompt": "The school's partnership with a university school of music has run for seven years on masterclasses and concert invitations. You propose that within two years Year 12 specialist students can take a first-year improvisation unit for credit.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "When this partnership was signed, most of our current Year 12s had not yet started primary school. In that time we have exchanged concert invitations, one masterclass a year, and a great deal of goodwill. || Goodwill is not a *pathway*.", "note": "The compression measures the partnership in a student's lifetime, the unit an academic committee cares about. The short final sentence is a definitional antithesis that names the gap without blaming anyone at the table."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "So I would like us to choose one. Within two years, a Year 12 student in our specialist program can enrol in your first-year improvisation unit, sit its assessment, and carry the credit into a degree.", "note": "'Choose one' picks up 'pathway' from the previous line, a quiet anadiplosis that makes the proposal feel like the logical next word. The tricolon of verbs (enrol, sit, carry) describes the whole administrative chain in one breath."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "Not because it is simple. It touches your admissions rules, our timetable, supervision on campus, and the question of who marks what. But those are exactly the questions a real partnership has to answer, and a ceremonial one *never* does.", "note": "He lists the obstacles in the committee's own bureaucratic terms, which proves he has thought like them. The closing contrast of real against ceremonial reframes difficulty as evidence of seriousness."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "On our side the cost is a Wednesday afternoon timetable block and a teacher released to co-teach. On yours it is a few places in a unit you already run, and one academic on a joint moderation panel. I will bring a draft agreement to your October meeting.", "note": "Parallel 'on our side, on yours' distributes the cost evenly, so neither party feels it is the one being asked. Offering to draft the agreement converts a deadline into a personal undertaking."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "For seven years we have asked what the university can offer our students. || I think the better question now is what our students could offer the *university*.", "note": "A Kennedy-shaped reversal built from the partnership's own habit of mind. It flatters the academics by implying the school brings something they need, which is the only basis on which a university changes its rules."}], "weak": {"text": "Thanks for having us, and I think the partnership has been really valuable for our students, especially the masterclasses. One thing we've wondered about is whether there might be scope, at some point, for students to do something a bit more formal, maybe a credit arrangement. I realise that's probably complicated with admissions and so on, and I don't really know how your rules work. It might be worth having a conversation about whether it's feasible at all. We'd certainly be keen to explore it if there's interest on your side. Obviously no pressure, and I know everyone is stretched at the moment.", "why": ["Thanks the partnership without saying what it has and has not achieved; the strong version compresses seven years into one sentence.", "'Explore' and 'at some point' replace a named goal and a two-year deadline.", "Admits ignorance of the rules instead of naming the obstacles in the committee's own terms.", "Closes by releasing the room from obligation ('no pressure') rather than reversing its question."]}}, {"title": "No instrument over twenty", "room": "Music parents' association AGM", "prompt": "The program's instrument fleet is ageing, and fundraising has always gone to tours. You ask the association to adopt a rolling replacement plan so that no instrument is more than twenty years old by the end of 2029.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "The sousaphone a Year 8 student marched with last week was bought when her parents were in Year 8 themselves. || In one generation, that instrument has gone from new to *heroic*.", "note": "Compression through a single object and a family's lifetime: the parents in the room become the measure of the instrument's age. The wry final word gets a laugh, and a room that has laughed is a room that is listening."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "So I am asking this association to choose something it has never quite chosen before: a rolling replacement plan, so that by the end of 2029 no instrument in our fleet is more than twenty years old.", "note": "'Never quite chosen before' frames the plan as a departure the members can own, without criticising past committees. The target is a single, checkable number, which gives the AGM something to vote for rather than a mood to agree with."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "Not because it is easy. Brass and low reeds are expensive, and replacement is less exciting to fund than a tour. But a tour lasts a week; a fleet is the program's *spine*. Choosing it will make us disciplined about everything else.", "note": "The antithesis of week against spine contrasts the glamorous with the structural in five words. He concedes the real emotional objection, that tours are more fun, and turns it into the case for discipline."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "The cost is roughly eighteen thousand dollars a year for three years, from fundraising and two grant applications I will write by March. The audit of every instrument we own is attached, oldest first.", "note": "An annual figure and a term are easier for parents to picture than a total. 'Oldest first' is a small, vivid detail that tells the room the work is done and the evidence is ordered for them."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "For twenty years we have asked our students to sound better than their instruments. || I would like to give them instruments as good as they *sound*.", "note": "A true chiasmus: instruments and sound trade places across the two sentences. It recasts the plan as a debt of fairness to the students rather than an equipment budget, and it is short enough to be repeated in the car park."}], "weak": {"text": "I know everyone is excited about the tour, and I am too. I just wanted to flag that some of our instruments are getting quite old, and at some point we'll probably need to think about replacing a few of them. It's not urgent exactly, and I know it's not the most glamorous thing to fundraise for. Maybe we could set aside a bit each year, or look at some grants. I haven't worked out the exact numbers yet, but I can do that if people are interested. I just think it's important that the kids have decent instruments to play on.", "why": ["'Quite old' and 'a few of them' replace the single object that made the age vivid.", "Calls the plan 'not urgent' and 'not glamorous', conceding the objection without reversing it.", "No figure, no year and no audit; the strong version had all three before anyone asked.", "Ends on a platitude about 'decent instruments' instead of a chiasmus the room could repeat."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["Put it this way: in the time it takes a student to go from Year 7 to graduation, [the change].", "When [this policy] was written, [comparison that shows its age].", "Six years ago, [state then]. Today, [state now].", "A student who starts in [our program] this year will have [left school] before [this body's next review].", "Consider the arithmetic: [long effort], answered by [tiny outcome].", "In the life of a single cohort, [the field] has [changed beyond recognition].", "The [instrument / document / rule] we still rely on was [made] when [comparison].", "Between this meeting and the next review, [a whole cohort] will pass through without [the thing].", "Compress the last [decade] into one school year, and [vivid image].", "By the time this [committee] next returns to the subject, [this cohort] will already have [graduated]."]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["So I am proposing that we choose to [goal].", "This is not something we have to do. It is something I think we should *choose* to do.", "I would like this [board] to decide, today, that [goal].", "Nobody is forcing our hand, which is exactly why the choice is ours.", "The proposal is simple to state: by [date], [measurable outcome].", "We could wait to be told. I would rather we chose.", "Let me put the goal plainly: [goal], for [whom], by [when].", "I am asking us to commit to [goal], not to explore it.", "We can keep managing [the problem], or we can end it. I am proposing we end it.", "The question in front of us is not whether [goal] is possible. It is whether we choose it."]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["Not because it is easy, but because it will ask the most of us.", "It is hard. That is the argument for it, not against it.", "It will be difficult, and the difficulty is exactly what will show us where we are [strong] and where we are [thin].", "If it were easy, someone would have done it already, and it would matter less.", "The hard part is [specific obstacle]. That is also the part worth solving.", "I am not going to pretend this is simple. [Obstacle one] and [obstacle two] are real.", "A goal we can reach without effort will not organise anyone's effort.", "It will test [our assessment / our timetable / our nerve], and I would rather be tested than comfortable.", "Yes, it is ambitious. Only ambition will make us decide [what we actually value].", "The difficulty is the discipline: it forces us to agree on [what matters most]."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["By the end of [Term / year], and here is what it will cost.", "The cost is real: [amount], [time], and [whose effort].", "I have costed it at [figure], and the working is on [page] of [the papers].", "The deadline is [date]. Not a review point: a delivery date.", "On our side it costs [X]. On yours, [Y].", "I will [write the sequence / draft the agreement] by [date] myself.", "It will cost us [a term of rehearsal time / a tour / an opportunity], and I think that should be named.", "The first milestone is [milestone] by [date]; the finish line is [date].", "If we start in [month], we finish in [month]. If we wait, [consequence].", "Let me be exact about the cost, because vagueness is how good ideas die in committee."]}, {"tag": 4, "lines": ["The question is not what this costs us, but what it costs us not to.", "We keep asking whether [they] are ready for [X]. The better question is whether [we] are.", "For years we have asked what [A] can do for [B]. It is time to ask what [B] can do for [A].", "We do not do this because we are ready. We will be ready because we did it.", "We have judged [them] for years. It is time we let [them] judge us.", "The risk is not that we aim high and miss. It is that we aim low and hit.", "We can let [the constraint] shape [the program], or let [the program] reshape [the constraint].", "We have asked [students] to [rise to X]. Now [X] should rise to them.", "Not [the comfortable version], but [the hard one].", "If we wait until it is easy, we will be doing it for someone else's students."]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Music department meeting", "prompt": "Propose that by the end of Term 3 every ensemble has a trained student section leader able to run a sectional unsupervised. You will design a two-session leadership course, and the cost is two lunchtimes per term from each director.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 1, "room": "Junior big band parent evening", "prompt": "Propose that the junior big band plays its end-of-year set entirely from memory. The cost is a recorded practice track for every part, which you will make, and ten minutes of every rehearsal from Week 3.", "seconds": 60}, {"level": 2, "room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee", "prompt": "Propose that the committee produces annotated exemplar student responses for every assessment type before the course reaches schools, within nine months, drawing on teachers from government, Catholic and independent schools. Some members think exemplars are the Authority's job, not the committee's.", "seconds": 135}, {"level": 2, "room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "Propose that registration, payment and scheduling move fully online within one year. Several long-serving volunteers prefer the paper system; the cost is a platform subscription and two training evenings you will run.", "seconds": 120}, {"level": 3, "room": "Principal's office, associate principal present", "prompt": "Propose that the school bids to host the state schools' jazz festival in two years' time, which needs a venue upgrade and a weekend of staff time. Expect the associate principal to interrupt on risk and workload, and the principal to ask what else gives.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "Arts funding panel, final interview", "prompt": "Argue for a three-year residency that sends professional jazz musicians into regional secondary schools each term. The panel chair interrupts to ask about sustainability after the grant ends and whether it duplicates existing touring programs.", "seconds": 200}]}, {"id": "gf-anaphora-vision", "examples": [{"title": "The ticket to the bandstand", "room": "State music educators' conference, jazz education session", "prompt": "You speak in a conference session on why girls leave school jazz programs, and want directors to change who they bring in as guest artists.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Every girl who picks up a trumpet in Year 7 is handed a *ticket* to the bandstand. || Too many discover, somewhere around Year 10, that the ticket was printed for the audience.", "note": "The debt metaphor turns a problem usually argued with figures into an object the room can hold: a ticket with the wrong destination. Because it is not a number it cannot be met with a counter-number, and the reversal lands in the second sentence where the stress falls."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "And it cannot wait for the profession to sort itself out. A student is fifteen once. If her section is all boys, and her soloists are all boys, and her teachers are all men, she draws a conclusion, and she draws it *this year*.", "note": "Polysyndeton (and, and, and) piles the evidence up at the pace a student experiences it. Setting the clock to her year rather than the profession's decade is King's answer to gradualism in a single clause."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "I can see a jazz program where a girl's first improvised chorus is *heard*, not tolerated. I can see a jazz program where the guest artist who walks into the band room is as likely to be a woman as a man. I can see a jazz program where the question in Year 10 is which instrument she takes further, and never whether she belongs.", "note": "Three repetitions of the same opening, each more concrete: a chorus, a guest, a Year 10 decision. The repeated frame lowers the listener's effort so attention goes entirely to what changes, and the third sentence ends on its own antithesis."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "In our big bands and our combos, in the regional festivals and the city ones, in the audition room at the conservatorium, and in the club on a Tuesday night.", "note": "The cascade traces a career rather than a map: school, festival, tertiary, profession. Every director in the room finds the point on that line where their own students stand."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "So I am asking every director here to book one woman as a guest artist before the end of Term 2, and to send me the name so we can share the list. || When that happens, the ticket will finally mean what it *says*.", "note": "The arrival carries the practical request first, then returns to the opening metaphor so the speech closes its own circle. The concrete ask keeps the vision from evaporating as the room stands up."}], "weak": {"text": "I think we all know there's an issue with gender balance in jazz education, and it's been talked about for a long time. I've certainly noticed it in my own program. Girls tend to drop out of the jazz bands at a certain age, and I think it's partly role models and partly culture and partly lots of things, really. It would be great if we could all try to do a bit more. Maybe more female guest artists, or thinking about how we run rehearsals. I don't have all the answers, and it's a complex issue. But I do think the future could look really different if we all made an effort.", "why": ["Opens on 'we all know' instead of a concrete metaphor of what is owed.", "No urgency; the strong version sets the clock to a single student's year.", "The vision is a vague 'could look really different'; the strong version builds three parallel sentences, each more specific.", "The one practical action is buried as a 'maybe'; the strong version makes it a dated request in the arrival."]}}, {"title": "A key cut somewhere else", "room": "University school of music, access and pathways committee", "prompt": "Students from schools without specialist music rarely succeed at tertiary jazz auditions. You propose a funded Saturday preparation program co-taught by university and school staff.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "An audition is meant to be a door that opens to *merit*. || But for a student from a school with no jazz band, the key to that door was cut years ago, somewhere she has never been.", "note": "The metaphor accepts the committee's own value, merit, and then locates the injustice upstream of it. Academics will defend the audition; this lets them keep defending it while conceding the problem."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "And it cannot be left to the next access review. Preparation missed at sixteen is not recovered at nineteen. She auditions against people who had it, she loses, and we record the result as merit.", "note": "A short aphorism, then three clipped clauses that end on the committee's own word turned against the status quo. The repetition of 'merit' from the opening gives the urgency a logical edge rather than an emotional one."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "I can see a pathway where a bassist from a school with no ensemble gets twenty Saturday mornings with your staff before she ever plays for a panel. I can see a pathway where our senior students sit beside her as section partners, not rivals. I can see a pathway where the panel hears what she can *do*, because someone first gave her the chance to learn it.", "note": "Each repeated sentence carries one element of the actual proposal: duration, peer model, outcome. The rhythm makes it memorable; the specifics make it a program the committee could cost."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "From Armadale and Kwinana, from Mirrabooka and Midland, from Rockingham and Ellenbrook, and from the country schools whose names this committee rarely *hears*.", "note": "Named suburbs paired in twos give the cascade a walking rhythm, and the final clause widens to the places that are absent from the room. Naming them makes the abstraction of 'access' a map."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "I propose a two-year pilot: one Saturday program, thirty places, co-taught by your staff and ours, reporting to this committee after the first year. || Then the merit we measure will be merit we can all be *proud* of.", "note": "The arrival is the motion itself, stated in the committee's register. The last line returns to 'merit' a third time and unites the room around the value it already holds."}], "weak": {"text": "I wanted to raise the issue of access, which I know the committee cares about. We see a lot of talented kids from schools that don't have strong music programs, and they often struggle in auditions because they haven't had the same preparation. It doesn't seem quite fair, although I understand the audition has to be rigorous. I wonder if there's some way the university and schools could work together to give those students more support, perhaps some kind of preparation course. I'm not sure how it would be funded or exactly how it would work, but it might be worth looking into. Even a small pilot could make a difference.", "why": ["States 'access' as a topic instead of a metaphor that places the problem before the audition.", "'Doesn't seem quite fair' apologises for the point; the strong version uses the committee's own word, merit, to make it.", "No vision, only 'some way'; the strong version builds three sentences that each carry a part of the program.", "The pilot appears as an afterthought, with no size, staffing or reporting line."]}}, {"title": "Music that is theirs", "room": "End-of-year concert, families in the hall", "prompt": "At the end-of-year concert you want to launch student composition commissions, so that every major ensemble premieres a student work next year.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "For as long as music has been taught in schools, we have paid our students in other people's music. It is a generous currency. || But it is a *borrowed* one.", "note": "The debt metaphor is gentle because the room is friendly: it honours the repertoire while naming what it cannot give. Three short sentences, the last one isolated by a pause, make the turn audible."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "And I do not think it can wait. A student has six years in this program. If in all that time she never hears an ensemble play a note she wrote, we have taught her that music is something that happens *to* her.", "note": "Urgency measured in a student's six years, the span every family in the hall is living through. The stress on a preposition, 'to her', is the whole argument in one syllable."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "I can see a concert where the first piece on the program was written by someone in Year 9. I can see a concert where the composer stands at the side of the stage and hears her own harmony come back from forty players. I can see a concert where the parents in this hall applaud a premiere, and the composer's name is one they *know*.", "note": "The vision rises from program note, to the composer's experience, to the audience's, so each sentence widens the circle of people who feel it. The final image puts the listeners themselves into the picture."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "In the concert bands and the string orchestras, in the guitar ensembles and the big bands, in the choir, and in the lunchtime combo that rehearses because it cannot stay away.", "note": "A cascade by ensemble rather than by place, so every family hears its own child's group named. The last item breaks the pattern with affection, which keeps the list human."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "So from next year, every major ensemble will premiere one student work at this concert, and the commissions open in February. || When that happens, the music on this stage will be *theirs* as well as ours.", "note": "The commitment is stated as fact, with a month attached, before the uniting line. 'Theirs as well as ours' resolves the borrowed-currency metaphor by sharing ownership rather than transferring it."}], "weak": {"text": "Thanks everyone for coming tonight, it's been a wonderful evening and the students have worked so hard. I just wanted to mention something we're thinking about for next year. We'd like to do more with composition, because it's important for students to be creative and not just play other people's music. There are some challenges with time, obviously, but we're hoping to have some student pieces performed at some point, possibly at this concert, if it all comes together. It would be lovely to see that. So keep an eye out for more information. Thanks again, and enjoy the rest of the night.", "why": ["Thanks without content where the strong version opens on a metaphor of what the program owes.", "Gives no reason the change cannot wait; the strong version measures it against six years.", "'Some student pieces at some point' has no picture; the strong version builds three rising images.", "The commitment is hedged ('if it all comes together') instead of announced with a month."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["Every student who [auditions / enrols] is handed a promise: [the promise].", "[The policy] was a promissory note, and for [group] it has come back marked insufficient funds.", "We hand every [student] a ticket to [destination]. Too many find it was printed for [somewhere else].", "The [course rationale / charter] makes a promise in its first paragraph. I want to talk about who it is kept for.", "We have been paying [group] in [currency]. It is a generous currency, but a borrowed one.", "An audition is meant to be a door that opens to merit. For some students, the key was cut somewhere they have never been.", "The word on the front of our [program / constitution] is [word]. That word is a debt.", "We told [these families] that [promise]. They believed us, and that belief is an account still open.", "Think of [the program] as a lease we signed on behalf of every student who comes after.", "I do not believe the account is empty. I believe it has not yet been drawn on."]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["And it cannot wait for the next review cycle.", "A student is fifteen once. [The consequence] happens this year, not in the plan's third phase.", "A [Year 9 trumpet player] gets one [Year 9].", "Gradual sounds prudent. To the student in the room now, gradual means never.", "A fund that arrives in three years arrives for someone else's child.", "If we wait for [the profession / the Board] to sort this out, [this cohort] will have left before it does.", "Every term we defer is a term a real student does without.", "The cost of patience is paid by people who are not in this room.", "There is no neutral delay. Waiting is also a decision, and it has a cohort attached.", "We are not early. We are, if anything, [six years] late."]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["I can see a [festival] where [first concrete change].", "I can see a [festival] where [a more specific change], in the same week as [a comparison].", "I can see a [festival] where the first question is about [the music], and never about [the fuel].", "Picture a program where [X]. Picture a program where [Y]. Picture a program where [Z].", "Imagine a Year 7 who [first image]. Imagine a Year 10 who [second]. Imagine a Year 12 who [third].", "A school where [X] is ordinary. A school where [Y] is expected. A school where [Z] is simply how we work.", "I want a committee room where [X]. I want a committee room where [Y]. I want a committee room where [Z].", "There will be a day when [first]. There will be a day when [second]. And there will be a day when [third].", "Where a student's first [improvised chorus] is heard, not tolerated.", "Where the [guest artist / adjudicator] who walks in is as likely to be [X] as [Y]."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["In Kalgoorlie, in Albany, in Karratha, and in the suburbs we already know.", "From Armadale and Kwinana, from Mirrabooka and Midland.", "In the concert bands and the string orchestras, in the guitar ensembles and the big bands.", "In the big bands and the combos, in the festival and in the club on a Tuesday night.", "In Year 7 orientation, in the Year 10 subject interview, in the Year 12 audition room.", "In the government schools and the Catholic ones and the independent ones.", "For the ones who read and the ones who play by ear, for the [first-chair players] and the [back desks].", "In the metropolitan schools, the regional ones, and the remote ones whose names [this committee] rarely hears.", "In every rehearsal room, every staffroom, every [council] meeting where this gets decided.", "From the first lesson to the last concert, and beyond it, into [the profession / the next generation's classrooms]."]}, {"tag": 4, "lines": ["And when that happens, it will be all of ours.", "When that promise is kept, it will be kept by all of us.", "Then the ticket will finally mean what it says.", "Tonight we [launch / commit to] [X], and [the first concrete action].", "That is the [festival / program] I want to hand to whoever stands here next.", "When that day comes, nobody in this room will need to ask whose [program] it is.", "I am asking every [director] here to [one action] by [date]. Then it begins.", "And then the music on this stage will be theirs as well as ours.", "That is not a dream. It is [a budget line / a timetable / a decision] we can make tonight.", "We can start that tomorrow. I would like us to."]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Year 12 music valedictory dinner", "prompt": "Speak to graduating students and their families. Frame what the program still owes them once they leave, build a short vision of them returning as mentors, and launch an alumni mentoring network with a first session in March.", "seconds": 90}, {"level": 1, "room": "Combined feeder primary concert band, final concert", "prompt": "Speak to Year 5 and 6 families about what their children's music could look like through secondary school, using a repeated opening, and invite them to an open rehearsal day in Term 1.", "seconds": 60}, {"level": 2, "room": "Parents and citizens' association general meeting", "prompt": "Advocate for an accessible rehearsal space and adaptive instruments so students with disability can join every ensemble. Ask the association to commit eight thousand dollars to be matched by a grant you will write. Some members think the money belongs to the library refurbishment.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 2, "room": "Cross-sector instrumental teachers' forum", "prompt": "Teachers from government, Catholic and independent schools are in the room, some wary of each other. Propose a shared regional mentoring network for isolated instrumental teachers, with a cascade of named regions and a first meeting date.", "seconds": 120}, {"level": 3, "room": "SCSA stakeholder consultation forum", "prompt": "After the Board's rejection of the committee's course, argue at a consultation forum that the next course must recognise jazz and contemporary practice as fully as the classical tradition. Senior leaders who think the current course is adequate will interrupt to ask for evidence and to question the cost to schools.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "Arts funding panel, final round", "prompt": "Seek three-year funding for a statewide youth jazz orchestra with regional audition centres. The panel chair interrupts twice, once for measurable outcomes and once for cost per participant, and you must answer without losing the build of the vision.", "seconds": 200}]}, {"id": "gf-self-us-now", "examples": [{"title": "The five minutes after", "room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "The draft budget cuts the festival's non-competitive development division to save money. You argue to keep it for one more year while a sponsor is found.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "When I was sixteen, at a school band festival in Brisbane, our band played so badly that the drummer and I lost each other for sixteen bars. The adjudicator came backstage anyway, found me, and showed me on my own bass *exactly* where I had dropped the form.", "note": "A single choice point with a place, an age and a physical detail, told in under forty words. The self-deprecating specifics earn trust quickly, and the board meets the speaker as a beneficiary of what it is about to cut."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "What I learned then is simple. The point of a festival is not the ranking. || It is the *five minutes after*.", "note": "The value is stated as an antithesis and then compressed into a phrase the board can carry out of the room. Naming it plainly stops the story from reading as nostalgia."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "And I think everyone at this table has a version of that. The development division exists because a board before us decided that a band's first festival should not be its last. Most of us have adjudicated in it, and some of us started our own bands there.", "note": "The move from 'I' to 'everyone at this table' converts a personal anecdote into a shared institutional memory. Citing the board's own earlier decision makes keeping the division an act of continuity rather than sentiment."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "Which is why this budget line matters now. Cutting the division saves about nine thousand dollars. Last year fourteen bands entered it, most of them from schools without a specialist program, and for several it was the only feedback they had all year.", "note": "The story of now arrives as a line item, in the treasurer's own register, so the board hears that the value is being tested by an ordinary budget decision. The figures are his own festival's, not borrowed research."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "So here is what I am asking. Keep the division for next year, and give two of us until the March meeting to find a sponsor to carry it. I will be one of them. || If we cannot, I will bring the cut back myself.", "note": "A bounded request with a deadline and a named volunteer, followed by a concession that makes it easy to say yes. Offering to reintroduce the cut himself shows the ask is about the value, not about winning."}], "weak": {"text": "I just wanted to say I'm a bit concerned about cutting the development division. I know money is tight, and I understand why it's been put forward. But I think the division does a lot of good, especially for schools that don't have big music programs. I've seen it make a real difference over the years. It would be a shame to lose it. I wonder if there's any other way we could find the savings, or maybe look for a sponsor at some stage. I'm happy to help if people want. I just think it's an important part of what we do.", "why": ["'I've seen it make a real difference' replaces a single remembered moment with a place and an age.", "No value is named; the strong version compresses it into 'the five minutes after'.", "Never shows the board its own history with the division, so the case stays personal.", "'A sponsor at some stage' and 'happy to help' replace a deadline and a named volunteer."]}}, {"title": "Two cups of tea", "room": "State music educators' conference, early-career session", "prompt": "Many first-year music teachers start as the only music teacher in their school. You recruit experienced teachers in the room to a fortnightly mentoring pair for Term 1.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "In my first year of teaching, in 2005, I had a Year 8 class that had seen off two teachers before me. On the third Monday, an older brass teacher from down the corridor walked in at recess with two cups of tea and a *seating plan*.", "note": "The year, the class's reputation and two objects make the moment concrete. The seating plan is the punchline: practical help rather than sympathy, which is the exact thing he is about to ask the room to give."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "What I learned then is simple. Nobody becomes good at this *alone*.", "note": "A seven-word value, with the stress placed on the final word so it is the one the room remembers. Brevity here signals that the story was evidence, not performance."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "And I suspect most of you were rescued by somebody too. A head of department who covered a lesson. A colleague who lent you their marking. Somebody who answered the phone on a Sunday night before your first concert.", "note": "Three sentence fragments in a list invite each listener to supply their own rescuer, which is how the story of us is built: by recognition, not assertion. The specificity of 'Sunday night before your first concert' lands with teachers precisely because it is ordinary."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "Which is why this matters now. Many of the people starting this year will be the *only* music teacher in their school. There is no corridor to walk down. If nobody calls them, they will work it out alone, or they will leave.", "note": "The now is told as the absence of the very thing in his story: no corridor. The stark binary at the end ('or they will leave') is the urgency, stated without a statistic."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "So here is what I am asking. One phone call a fortnight, for Term 1, with one new teacher. The sign-up sheet is at the back table, and I will match every pair by Friday. || Two cups of tea, by phone.", "note": "The request is small, timed and bounded, so the cost of a yes is visible. The closing callback to the opening image ties self, us and now into one portable phrase."}], "weak": {"text": "I think mentoring is really important for new teachers, especially in music where people are often on their own. I was lucky to have good mentors when I started, and it made a big difference. I know everyone is very busy, and I don't want to add to anyone's workload. But if anyone is interested in mentoring an early-career teacher, even informally, that would be great. We're thinking of putting together some kind of list. There's no set commitment really, it would just depend on what works for people. Anyway, have a think about it, and let me know if you're interested.", "why": ["'I was lucky to have good mentors' is a career summary, not a moment; the strong version gives a year, a class and two cups of tea.", "Never invites the room to recall its own rescuers, so there is no story of us.", "No account of why it matters now; the strong version names the missing corridor.", "'No set commitment' and 'have a think' replace a fortnightly call, a sheet and a Friday deadline."]}}, {"title": "Keeping the Year 7 camp", "room": "Principal's office", "prompt": "The draft budget replaces the specialist program's Year 7 camp with a single in-school day. You ask the principal to keep the camp for 2027.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "At the first camp I ran here, there was a Year 7 clarinettist who had not said a word in rehearsal all term. On the Saturday night, in front of a hundred and twenty students, she stood up and played the solo nobody had asked her to *learn*.", "note": "A single student, unnamed, and one night: enough to be specific without trading on a child's privacy. In a one-to-one room the story is short, because the principal's time is the scarcest resource present."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "What that taught me is that belonging comes before excellence. || It is what makes the excellence *possible*.", "note": "The value is stated, then sharpened in a second sentence that turns it from a soft claim into a causal one. A principal judges programs by outcomes, so the value is framed as a condition of outcomes."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "And that is not my value. It is the first line of our own program handbook: that the specialist program is a community before it is a stream. Every leader of this program before me has protected the camp for that reason.", "note": "He moves to 'us' by citing the school's own document and its own history, which makes the request one of institutional consistency. With a principal, quoting the school back to itself is more persuasive than quoting oneself."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "Which is why the draft budget matters now. Replacing the camp with an in-school day saves about six thousand dollars. It also means next year's Year 7s would be the first cohort in a decade to *start* without it.", "note": "The saving is acknowledged in the principal's terms before the cost is named in the program's. 'The first cohort in a decade' makes the decision historic without exaggeration."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "So I am asking you to keep the camp for 2027. I will lift the family contribution by forty dollars and ask the parent association for the rest, so the school's share is unchanged. I need your answer by the end of the month to hold the booking.", "note": "The request removes the principal's main objection by solving the funding in the same breath. A dated answer, tied to an external booking, gives the decision a real deadline without pressure."}], "weak": {"text": "I wanted to talk about the camp, if that's okay. I know the budget's under a lot of pressure and I'm not trying to make things difficult. It's just that the camp has always been such a big part of the program, and the kids really get a lot out of it socially. I've seen some amazing things happen there over the years. I'm not sure the in-school day will quite do the same job. Is there any chance we could keep it, even in a reduced form? I could maybe look at other funding options if that would help.", "why": ["'Amazing things over the years' replaces a single student and a single night.", "Never states the value or connects it to the school's own handbook, so it sounds like personal attachment.", "Omits what the change means now: the saving, and the first cohort to start without camp.", "The ask is a question with an offer to 'maybe' find funding; the strong version solves the money and sets a date."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["I want to tell you about the moment I decided this mattered.", "When I was [age], in [place], [one specific event].", "In my first year of teaching, [one specific moment].", "There was a night at [venue / festival] when [moment].", "I can tell you exactly where I was when this stopped being abstract for me.", "The first time I [conducted / adjudicated / sat on a panel], [moment].", "One student taught me this. In [year], [what she did].", "I nearly [said no / walked out] once. What stopped me was [moment].", "Before I say anything about the [budget / policy], let me tell you about one [rehearsal / afternoon].", "Twenty-odd years ago, on a stage in [place], [moment]."]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["What I learned then is simple.", "Talent is everywhere; [opportunity] is not.", "The point of [a festival] is not [the ranking]. It is [the five minutes after].", "Nobody becomes good at this alone.", "Belonging comes before excellence. It is what makes the excellence possible.", "Somebody has to decide to close that gap.", "Students remember who [stayed / noticed / made room] long after they forget the [result].", "What that taught me is that [value], and I have not found a reason to unlearn it.", "The lesson was not really about music. It was about [who gets heard].", "I came away believing one thing: [value in a sentence]."]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["And I think everyone here has a version of that story.", "I suspect most of us in this room were [rescued / taught / noticed] by somebody.", "This [association / council] exists because [people before us] decided [that value].", "It is written into [our handbook / our constitution / the course rationale]: [phrase].", "Some of your children are [playing loan instruments] tonight because [parents before you] refused to let [price] decide.", "We did not all come here by the same road, but we came for the same reason.", "When this [committee] wrote [document], it argued that [value]. That was us at our best.", "Every one of you has [stayed late / written the reference / driven the bus] for exactly that reason.", "That is not my value. It is the one this [school] was built on.", "Look around the table: [the shared history or credential]."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["Which is why what is happening now matters.", "[The draft budget / the Board's decision / the restructure] puts that at risk, and here is how.", "Right now, [number] students are [situation], and [number] more are waiting.", "Next year's [cohort] would be the first in [a decade] to [start without X].", "Our [coordinator] has [left], and without someone to run it, [the scheme] stops at [date].", "The proposal saves [amount]. Here is what it costs.", "This is where the value gets tested: not in a mission statement, but in [this line item].", "If nothing changes by [date], [concrete consequence].", "Many of the people starting this year will be [the only music teacher in their school].", "It is not a crisis. It is a choice point, and it is ours."]}, {"tag": 4, "lines": ["So here is what I am asking us to do, starting this week.", "I am asking for [number] volunteers, [time] each, and I will train you myself on [date].", "Keep [X] for [year], and give [two of us] until [meeting] to [find the money]. I will be one of them.", "The sign-up sheet is at [place], and I will match everyone by [day].", "I need your answer by [date] so I can [hold the booking].", "If [three hands] go up tonight, [the scheme] survives. I would like to see them.", "The first step is small: [action], before [date].", "I am not asking you to agree with everything. I am asking for [one decision].", "Move that [the council] [action], and I will report back at [the next meeting].", "It has worked before, in this room. I am asking us to do it again."]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Music department meeting", "prompt": "At the start of term, ask each colleague to run one lunchtime ensemble for a semester. Tell the story of the lunchtime combo that kept you at school, name what the department already believes about student-led music, and set a sign-up deadline of Friday.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 1, "room": "Combined feeder primary concert band, first rehearsal", "prompt": "Welcome Year 5 and 6 families and partner primary teachers. Explain briefly why you run the band, what the partner schools share, and ask for parent drivers for a transport roster for the six Saturday rehearsals.", "seconds": 60}, {"level": 2, "room": "University school of music, partnership meeting", "prompt": "The academics treat the school as a feeder of talent. You want them to co-design professional learning for secondary music teachers. Tell why teacher learning changed your own practice, name what both institutions say they value, and propose a first jointly run day next term.", "seconds": 135}, {"level": 2, "room": "Community arts funding panel", "prompt": "Seek funding to rebuild an instrument loan scheme for families who cannot afford hire. The panel knows little about school music and has questions about why a public school needs community money.", "seconds": 120}, {"level": 3, "room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee, Authority officer present", "prompt": "After the Board's rejection, several members want to accept the outcome and disband the working group. Tell briefly why you joined the committee, recall what it set out to do, and propose a small working group to prepare a revised submission. An Authority officer will interrupt to clarify what the Board will and will not consider.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "Parents and citizens' association, contested motion", "prompt": "A motion proposes redirecting music fundraising to general school use. Speak against it using self, us and now, and offer a compromise in which a fixed share goes to general use. Members will interrupt to say music families already receive more than others.", "seconds": 165}]}, {"id": "gf-account-principle-cost", "examples": [{"title": "Sending the score sheets", "room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "After a disputed Division One result, you sent every director the full adjudicator score sheets before the board had discussed it. The president is unhappy and the board has asked you to explain.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Let me be clear about what I did, because it is not in dispute. After the Division One results were queried, I sent every director the complete adjudicator sheets, all three panels, unedited. And I did it *before* this board had discussed it.", "note": "The admission includes its most damaging detail, the timing, in a separate sentence of its own. Volunteering the worst fact first leaves the critics nothing to reveal, which is Mandela's opening move scaled to a board table."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "Here is how I came to that. Two directors wrote to me as treasurer, because their entry fees pay for adjudication, asking to see what they had paid for. The spread at the top was three points, and one panel's sheets did not add up to the published totals.", "note": "The account is chronological and in the treasurer's own frame: fees paid, a service owed. A single precise discrepancy does more work than any adjective about fairness."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "I did not start there. I raised it with the president that evening and suggested a short statement. I asked the chief adjudicator to recheck the sums. The answer both times was that it would be dealt with after the festival, by which point the directors' reports to their principals would be *written*.", "note": "Three tried routes in three short sentences, each with its result, show that disclosure was the last resort. Ending on the directors' deadline explains why waiting was not neutral."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "The principle underneath it is this. A festival that charges schools for judgement owes them the *reasons* for it. Scores without working are not feedback. They are verdicts.", "note": "The principle is stated generally, so it is no longer about this result or this president. The closing antithesis of feedback against verdicts uses an educator's distinction the whole board already accepts."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "I understand some of you think I should have waited, and that it may have cost me the president's confidence. If the board wishes to record its disagreement, that is entirely proper, and I will draft a disclosure protocol so this never falls to one officer again. || I would not *unsend* it.", "note": "The cost is real and modest: confidence and a recorded rebuke, not martyrdom. Offering the protocol turns the dispute into governance, and the four-word close holds the principle without raising the temperature."}], "weak": {"text": "I'm sorry if sending the sheets caused any problems, that really wasn't my intention. A couple of directors had asked about the results and I thought it would be helpful to share them, since there seemed to be some confusion about the scores. I probably should have checked with everyone first, and I did mention it to the president, although maybe not as clearly as I could have. I just felt that transparency is important. I'm happy to talk about whether we need a policy around this. I hope it hasn't damaged any relationships, because that's the last thing I'd want.", "why": ["Apologises for consequences instead of stating plainly what was done and when.", "Gives 'some confusion' where the strong version names the three-point spread and the totals that did not add up.", "Blurs the alternatives ('maybe not as clearly') instead of listing each route and its answer.", "'Transparency is important' is a preference; the strong version states a principle and accepts a named cost."]}}, {"title": "Withdrawing from the youth ensemble", "room": "University school of music, joint steering group", "prompt": "You withdrew the school's twenty students from a university youth ensemble mid-semester over supervision arrangements, without waiting for the steering group to meet.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "I want to be plain about what happened. On the Thursday before the Saturday rehearsal, I withdrew our twenty students from the youth ensemble for the rest of the semester. I did it *unilaterally*, and I told you by email rather than in person.", "note": "Two admissions, the decision and the manner of it, both owned before anyone at the table can raise them. Naming the discourtesy of the email signals respect for the partnership while holding the decision."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "This is how I got there. Our students are minors, and the school's duty of care goes with them off site. In the Term 2 schedule, rehearsals moved to an evening building with no staffed entry, and two sectionals were listed with a single adult in a closed room.", "note": "The account sets out the obligation, then the change in circumstances, in neutral, checkable language. Academics will accept a factual description of a schedule far more readily than a characterisation of their staff."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "I tried the other routes first. I raised it at our March meeting. In April I sent a proposed supervision roster and offered a staff member of ours. In May I asked for a written variation to the agreement. I had no reply to any of them before that rehearsal.", "note": "Dated, escalating steps with no adjectives: the record does the arguing. The final sentence is not an accusation, only a fact, which leaves the steering group to draw the conclusion itself."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "The principle is simple, and I suspect we share it. A partnership can ask a great deal of our students. It cannot ask the school to *lend* out its duty of care.", "note": "'I suspect we share it' invites the university onto the principle rather than placing it in the dock. The verb 'lend' makes the abstraction of duty of care concrete and slightly absurd, which is what makes it persuasive."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "I know this has cost the ensemble a section, and it may cost us this partnership, which I value more than this statement probably suggests. I accept that. If supervision is fixed, our students are back the following week. || I would rather lose a semester than have to *explain* one.", "note": "The cost is stated at its true size, including the possible end of the partnership. The conditional return gives the university an easy path back, and the closing antithesis says, without drama, what the alternative risk was."}], "weak": {"text": "I'm really sorry about how this happened, and I know it's caused a lot of disruption for the ensemble. We just had some concerns about the supervision arrangements in the new venue, and with child safety being such a big focus at the moment, I didn't feel I could let it go ahead. I did try to raise it a few times, but I don't think it got picked up. I probably should have called rather than emailed. I really value the partnership and I'd hate for this to affect it. Hopefully we can sort something out and get the students back soon.", "why": ["Opens with an apology for disruption rather than a plain account of the decision and its timing.", "'Some concerns' and 'a big focus at the moment' replace the specific obligation and the specific schedule change.", "'Tried to raise it a few times' hides three dated steps that would have carried the case.", "Ends hoping the cost will not arrive instead of naming it, accepting it, and stating the condition for return."]}}, {"title": "Cancelling the Melbourne tour", "room": "Music parents' association, special meeting", "prompt": "You cancelled the senior ensembles' interstate tour after deposits had been paid and a term of fundraising done, because costs rose beyond what many families could pay. The association has called a special meeting.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Let me start with the fact, because I know it is why the room is full. Last Monday I cancelled the senior ensembles' Melbourne tour. I did it after families had paid deposits, and after this association had spent a *whole term* fundraising.", "note": "He names the decision and both of the grievances in the room before anyone stands. Acknowledging the parents' work as a fact, not a regret, shows he understands what they lost."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "Here is how it happened. When we launched the tour in February, the per-student cost sat within the range families told us they could manage. In August the airfare and accommodation quotes came back, and the gap had grown to several hundred dollars a student.", "note": "A two-point chronology, February and August, makes the decision intelligible rather than impulsive. Citing the families' own stated range ties the account to the room's earlier word."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "I did not go straight to cancellation. I asked the agent to re-quote on other dates. I costed a coach option and a shorter itinerary. I asked the school whether equity funds could close the gap. Each option either broke the program or still left some families *unable* to send their child.", "note": "Four alternatives in four short sentences show the work, and the summary sentence names the single test every option failed. That test becomes the bridge to the principle."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "The principle underneath it is this. A school tour that only some families can afford is not a *school* tour. It is a private trip with our name on it.", "note": "A definitional principle: by saying what a school tour is, he makes the cancellation follow rather than appear chosen. The blunt second sentence is the one parents will repeat to each other, which is the aim."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "I know what this costs: deposits the agent will only partly refund, a term of your work, and for some of you, your confidence in me. I accept all of that, and I will speak to every family individually this week. || I would rather disappoint *everyone* than leave out a few.", "note": "The cost is itemised, including the personal one, and accepted without self-pity. The closing antithesis states the principle as a choice between harms, which is how a room of parents can respect a decision it dislikes."}], "weak": {"text": "I know a lot of people are upset about the tour, and I completely understand that. It was a really hard decision and not one I made lightly. The costs went up quite a bit, and we looked at a few different options, but nothing really worked. I didn't want some kids to miss out because of money, which I think is fair. I'm really sorry for all the work that went into fundraising, and I hope we can use the money for something else. If anyone has questions, please come and see me. I know it's disappointing.", "why": ["Starts with the room's feelings instead of the plain fact of what he did and when.", "'Went up quite a bit' replaces the February range and the August quotes.", "'A few different options' hides the four alternatives that showed the decision was the last resort.", "Never states the principle in a sentence or names the costs he accepts, so the decision sounds like regret rather than judgement."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["Let me be clear about what I did, because it is not in dispute.", "I want to be plain about what happened. On [date], I [action].", "The facts are simple, and I will not dress them up: I [action].", "I [did X], I did it against [advice], and I did it [before the board had discussed it].", "Let me start with the fact, because I know it is why [the room is full].", "Nobody needs to draw this out of me. I [action].", "Yes, I voted against it. I was the only one, and the minutes should say so.", "I declined [the request], and I would rather own that decision than have it described for me.", "I made the call unilaterally, and I told you by [email] rather than in person. Both of those are true.", "Before anyone asks: yes, [the uncomfortable detail] is also correct."]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["I want to explain how I came to that position.", "Here is how I got there, in order.", "It began on [date], when [first fact].", "The background is this: [the agreement / the policy] requires [X].", "When we [launched / agreed to] [X] in [month], [condition]. By [month], [the condition had changed].", "[Two directors / the family] wrote to me asking [X]. That is where it started.", "Let me give you the sequence, because the order matters.", "None of this was sudden. It developed over [a term], and the record is in [the papers].", "The relevant facts are three: [one], [two] and [three].", "I am not asking you to agree yet. I am asking you to follow the steps."]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["I did not start there. I tried the other routes first.", "I raised it at [meeting]. I proposed [X] in [month]. I asked for [Y] in writing.", "I offered [three alternatives]. Each was declined, and the letters are in [the papers].", "I looked at [option one], [option two] and [option three]. Each either [broke X] or [left Y].", "Refusal was the last option on my list, not the first.", "I asked [the president / the deputy] to [act]. The answer was that it would be dealt with [later].", "I had no reply to any of them before [the deadline].", "If there had been a quieter route, I would have taken it. I looked for one for [a month].", "Every step before this one was meant to make this one unnecessary.", "I exhausted the courteous options. What remained is the one you are now discussing."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["The principle underneath it is this.", "A [selective place] is held on behalf of [another child who missed out].", "A [festival] that charges schools for judgement owes them the reasons for it.", "A partnership can ask a great deal of our students. It cannot ask the school to lend out its duty of care.", "A [school tour] that only some families can afford is not a [school] tour.", "Put simply: [an obligation] does not lapse because it has become inconvenient.", "This is not about [the person / the sponsor]. It is about [the rule that protects everyone].", "Advice is only worth asking for if it is free to be unwelcome.", "If [the rule] bends for [the powerful party], it is not a rule. It is a courtesy.", "I hold one view here, and it is not complicated: [principle in a sentence]."]}, {"tag": 4, "lines": ["I understand what this may cost, and I accept that.", "If [council] takes a different view, I will apply its decision without complaint.", "I know this may cost me [the role / the president's confidence / the partnership]. I accept that.", "I would rather be overruled on a principle than quietly abandon one.", "If the board wishes to record its disagreement, that is entirely proper.", "I will speak to every [family / director] individually this week.", "If [the condition] is met, [the decision] reverses the following week.", "I would rather lose [a semester] than have to explain one.", "I am not asking for approval. I am asking for the record to be accurate.", "Whatever the [board] decides, I will carry it out, and I will not relitigate it."]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Music department meeting", "prompt": "Over the holidays you moved two ensemble rehearsals to new days without consulting the directors, because of a room clash with exams. Explain the decision, the alternatives you checked, and what you accept about the way you did it.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 1, "room": "Feeder primary concert band, partner teachers' meeting", "prompt": "Two partner schools asked for extra places and you capped the band at sixty. Explain the cap, the options you considered, the principle of supervision and rehearsal quality behind it, and what you will do for the students who missed out.", "seconds": 90}, {"level": 2, "room": "Festival association AGM", "prompt": "As treasurer you told members that the accounts had not yet been independently reviewed, rather than presenting draft figures as final as the president had suggested. Explain this to members, some of whom feel embarrassed in front of sponsors.", "seconds": 135}, {"level": 2, "room": "Arts funding panel, chair's debrief", "prompt": "As a panel member you declared a conflict and left the room on a regional application, which left the panel short and delayed the decision a month. The chair thinks the connection was too remote to matter. Explain your decision.", "seconds": 120}, {"level": 3, "room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee, chair and Authority officer present", "prompt": "After the Board's rejection, you circulated a written minority view to committee members without the chair's approval. The chair and an Authority officer are present and will interrupt on confidentiality and process. Explain what you did and why.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "Principal's office, associate principal present", "prompt": "You refused to audition a student outside the published audition window, although the family is well connected and has raised it with the associate principal. Explain the refusal, what you offered instead, and what you accept if the principal overrules you. Expect pointed interruptions.", "seconds": 165}]}, {"id": "gf-we-acknowledgement", "examples": [{"title": "Regional bands in the early slot", "room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "A letter from several regional schools says the festival has quietly become a Perth event. Entries from outside the metropolitan area have fallen for several years. You speak to the board.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "I think how this board answers the regional schools' letter is a test of whether we run a *state* festival or a Perth one.", "note": "The test is framed as a choice between two identities, and the board will want to be the first. Placing 'state' and 'Perth' at the end of their clauses lets the contrast do the arguing."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "It was we who put the regional bands in the eight o'clock Saturday slot, year after year, because it suited the metropolitan schedule. It was we who sent the feedback survey only to schools already on our list. It was we who read the falling entries as a lack of *interest*.", "note": "Anaphora on 'It was we who', three times and no more, with each clause a specific act and the last one an act of interpretation. The third is the sharpest because it admits the board misread the evidence, not just mishandled the logistics."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "Imagine if your band had left Kalgoorlie at four in the morning, played to a half-empty hall and three tired adjudicators, and then driven home to be told they *sounded* under-rehearsed.", "note": "The imagined experience is built as a journey in sequence, so the listener lives it in time. The final clause turns the board's own adjudication language back on itself, which is where the discomfort lands."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "This is not about guilt. Every person at this table has given years to this festival, unpaid. It is about seeing the schedule clearly enough to *change* it.", "note": "The refusal of guilt is paired with a genuine credit to the volunteers, which lowers defensiveness before anyone has to voice it. Reducing the problem to 'the schedule' makes it fixable rather than moral."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "So here is where we start. From next year, no band travelling more than three hours plays before ten. We set aside a travel subsidy from the reserve, and I will bring a costed figure to the March meeting. And the survey goes to every school that has entered in the last five years. || I think we can win those schools *back*.", "note": "Three concrete remedies, each tied to one of the three admissions, so the plan reads as repair rather than a new initiative. The treasurer names the money and the date himself, and the held pause before the closing line gives the confidence room to sound earned."}], "weak": {"text": "Thanks everyone. So, the letter from the regional schools. Look, I think there are probably some fair points in it, although obviously scheduling is incredibly complex and we're all volunteers here. Some of the time slots maybe haven't been ideal for them, and the survey possibly didn't reach everyone, which is a shame. I suppose we could look at whether there's a way to be a bit more flexible going forward, and maybe a subcommittee could explore options around timing and possibly some funding, if the budget allows. I just think it's important that we're seen to be responsive. Happy to take it offline if people want to discuss further.", "why": ["The failures are passive and vague ('haven't been ideal'); the strong version owns three specific acts with 'It was we who'.", "The defence (complexity, volunteers) comes before any acknowledgement; the strong version credits the volunteers only after the failure is named.", "There is no 'imagine if', so the board never feels the drive from Kalgoorlie.", "The remedy is handed to a subcommittee 'if the budget allows'; the strong version names three changes, a sum to be costed and a meeting date."]}}, {"title": "Consulting in one language", "room": "School council (he chairs)", "prompt": "Council is about to vote on a revised schedule of charges for the music program. The consultation drew almost no responses from families who are new to Australia or who speak languages other than English at home. As chair, you speak before the vote.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Before we vote, I think we should be honest that this decision is a test of whose voice this council actually *hears*.", "note": "The chair names the test before the vote rather than after it, which is the only point at which acknowledgement can still change the outcome. 'Actually' quietly concedes the gap between principle and practice."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "It was we who ran the consultation as an online survey, in English only, across the school holidays. It was we who treated a hundred and forty responses as the view of the parent body. And it was we who did not ask why almost none came from our newest *families*.", "note": "Each 'we' sentence is narrower and more uncomfortable than the last, moving from method to inference to an omission. The 'And' before the third signals the end of the run, so the repetition never becomes ritual."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "Imagine if you had arrived here eighteen months ago, your daughter had just been offered a place in the program, and the first you heard of the new instrument hire charge was the *invoice*.", "note": "The scenario uses 'you' and a daughter, so every parent at the table supplies their own child. The single-word ending is the device: the invoice is the whole failure of consultation in one object."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "I am not raising this to assign blame. The survey was well meant, and I signed off on it as chair. The point is to see the gap clearly enough that we do not decide on *half* the evidence.", "note": "The chair includes himself in the failure, which licenses the earlier 'we' and removes any suggestion that he is scoring off the executive. Reframing the issue as evidence rather than fairness speaks to a council's sense of due process."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "So I propose we defer the vote by one meeting. In that time we hold two face-to-face sessions with interpreters, one in the evening and one at school pick-up, and we translate the summary into the four main community languages. I will report what we hear, and then we decide. || We will make a *better* decision for it.", "note": "A deferral is the smallest real remedy available to a chair, and it is specified down to times and languages so it cannot be read as delay. The close asserts quality, not virtue, which is the register a council respects."}], "weak": {"text": "Just before we vote, I wanted to flag that the survey response rate was perhaps not as representative as we might have liked, particularly from some of our newer communities, which is something to be mindful of. That said, I think the process was reasonable and we did put it out there for everyone. Going forward we might think about more inclusive ways of consulting, perhaps translations, if resources allow. I don't want to hold things up, so I'm comfortable proceeding with the vote tonight, with a note in the minutes that we'll aim to be more inclusive next time.", "why": ["'Not as representative as we might have liked' describes a result; the strong version names what council did to produce it.", "The speaker defends the process in the next breath, which cancels the acknowledgement; the strong version takes personal responsibility as chair.", "Nothing makes the room feel the family's experience; the strong version ends on the invoice.", "The remedy applies 'next time', after the decision; the strong version defers the vote and specifies the sessions."]}}, {"title": "What placement students get from us", "room": "University partnership committee", "prompt": "The university's professional experience office has raised concerns about how pre-service music teachers are treated on placement in schools, including yours. You speak to the partnership committee.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "I think how a school receives its student teachers is a test of whether we believe in the profession we are asking them to *join*.", "note": "The test is moved from compliance to belief, which raises the stakes without accusing anyone. Ending on 'join' makes the student teacher the subject of the sentence before they appear in the speech."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "It was we, in my department, who used placement students to cover ensembles when we were short. It was we who gave them a timetable and called it mentoring. It was we who returned final reports a month late, after the university had *chased* us twice.", "note": "'In my department' limits the 'we' to what his institution actually did, which keeps the acknowledgement honest in a room of other schools. The middle sentence uses a deflating parallel, 'gave them a timetable and called it mentoring', that an academic room will remember."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "Imagine if your first sustained experience of a school had been a semester of being useful and *unseen*, and then a report that arrived after you had applied for jobs.", "note": "The pairing 'useful and unseen' compresses the whole complaint into two words with matching sounds. The consequence clause makes the harm practical, which a professional experience office cannot dismiss as feeling."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "This is not about guilt; my colleagues are stretched, and they are generous. It is about seeing the arrangement clearly enough to make it worth the student's *time*, and ours.", "note": "He protects his colleagues in the same breath as he refuses guilt, which keeps faith with the people not in the room. The trailing 'and ours' recasts the fix as mutual benefit rather than penance."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "So here is what I will do from Semester 1. Every placement student gets a named mentor and a protected hour with them each week. No student covers a class alone. Reports go back within ten working days. And I would like this committee to review it with us at the end of the year, with the students in the room. || I think we can become a school students *ask* for.", "note": "Short declaratives, one commitment per sentence, each measurable. Inviting the committee to review the result, with students present, converts the apology into accountability, and the close turns a failure into an ambition."}], "weak": {"text": "I think it's fair to say placements across the sector have had some challenges, and schools are under a lot of pressure at the moment with staffing, so things don't always go as planned. In our case there may have been one or two occasions where students helped out with classes, which can actually be good experience for them. Reports have sometimes been a little delayed. We're certainly open to feedback and keen to keep improving the partnership, and I'd be happy for us to discuss some ideas offline about how we might support students better going forward.", "why": ["'Across the sector' spreads the failure so thinly that no one owns it; the strong version says 'in my department'.", "The speaker reframes covering classes as 'good experience', which reads as justification; the strong version calls it what it was.", "There is no step that puts the committee in the student's position.", "'Discuss some ideas offline' commits to nothing; the strong version gives a mentor, a protected hour, a ten-day deadline and a review."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["I think how we respond to [the finding] is a test of what kind of [department] we are.", "This is one of those moments that shows an institution what it actually believes.", "Whatever we decide tonight, it will say more about us than anything in our [mission statement].", "I think this is a test, and it is not a test of [the policy]. It is a test of us.", "The question in front of us is simple to state: are we the [festival] we say we are?", "Every organisation is measured by how it answers the people it has let down. This is our turn.", "I want to put this plainly: [the letter] is asking us who we are.", "How we handle this will be remembered long after the detail of [the review] is forgotten.", "There are decisions that are about money, and decisions that are about character. This is the second kind.", "I think [the families] are watching to see whether we mean what we print."]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["It was we who [set the audition date in camp week].", "It was we who [kept the test after we knew what it measured].", "It was we, in [my department], who [used placement students to cover classes].", "It was we who read [the falling entries] as [a lack of interest].", "It was we who [consulted in English only], and we who called that consultation.", "And it was we who did not ask why [almost no one from those schools replied].", "We designed [the process]. We ran it. And we did not check who it left out.", "No one else made [this schedule]. We made it, every year, for [our convenience].", "I signed off on [the survey] myself, so I include myself in every one of these.", "We knew about [the clash] by [2023], and we did not change it."]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["Imagine if it had been your child [sitting in front of that panel].", "Imagine if you had [driven six hours] to be told [you sounded under-rehearsed].", "Imagine if the first you heard of [the new charge] was [the invoice].", "Picture being [fourteen], [new to the school], and [the only student who had never had a lesson].", "Put yourself, for a moment, on the other side of [the audition table].", "Imagine if your [first semester in a school] had been [useful and unseen].", "Think of the [student] who [had the ear and the rhythm], and [no instrument at home].", "Imagine if [our concert] had been scheduled, every year, on [your family's most important night].", "Imagine opening [the festival program] and finding [no school like yours] in it.", "If it had been done to us, we would not have called it [an oversight]."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["This is not about guilt. It is about seeing [the process] clearly enough to fix it.", "I am not raising this to assign blame. [Everyone here] wanted [the best students].", "No one in this room set out to [exclude anyone]. That is exactly why it went unnoticed.", "Guilt would be easy, and useless. What [these schools] need is a change.", "This is not an indictment of [the volunteers]; it is a description of [the schedule].", "My colleagues are [stretched], and they are [generous]. The arrangement is what failed, not the people.", "The point is not to feel badly. The point is to decide [on all the evidence].", "We can acknowledge this without beating ourselves up about it, and then we can act.", "I would rather we were uncomfortable for ten minutes than [unfair] for another decade.", "Recognition is not the same as remorse. Recognition is the first step of [a plan]."]}, {"tag": 4, "lines": ["So here is where we start. [From next year, the sight-reading test goes.]", "So I propose we [defer the vote by one meeting] and [hold two face-to-face sessions].", "From [Semester 1], every [placement student] will have [a named mentor].", "I will bring [a costed figure] to [the March meeting].", "I will report [the intake data] to [council] every year, [by feeder school].", "Three changes, one for each thing we got wrong: [first], [second] and [third].", "I would like [this committee] to review it with us at [the end of the year].", "None of this is expensive. What it costs is [attention], and we can give that.", "I think we can do this well.", "I think we can become [a school students ask for]."]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Music department meeting", "prompt": "For two years the Year 7 percussion students have spent most ensemble rehearsals waiting, because the parts written for them are thin and they rotate through one kit. Several have asked to leave. Acknowledge it to staff and propose a rotation through tuned percussion, a percussion-only sectional each fortnight and a check of every new arrangement for real percussion parts.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 1, "room": "End-of-year concert, school hall", "prompt": "The old hall has no ramp to the stage, and for years students using wheelchairs have performed from the floor while their ensembles sat above them. Before the final item, acknowledge this to families and announce that from next year the senior concerts move to the accessible performing arts centre and the department will fund a portable ramp for the hall.", "seconds": 90}, {"level": 2, "room": "Parents and Citizens' Association", "prompt": "The music camp fee has risen each year, and it is clear from enrolment patterns that some students stop attending in Year 9 because families cannot pay. Some parents on the committee organised the camp and are proud of it. Own the department's part, and propose a hardship fund drawn from concert takings, a confidential application through the student services team and a capped fee for the second sibling.", "seconds": 135}, {"level": 2, "room": "State music teachers' conference", "prompt": "Speak to colleagues about how school music assessment, including your own, has treated students who learn by ear in contemporary, church or community traditions as if they were unskilled. Use 'we' for the profession, and propose an aural performance option in internal assessment and a shared bank of tasks that recognise playing by ear.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 3, "room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee, senior officers present", "prompt": "The committee's consultation on a draft course drew on metropolitan teachers almost exclusively, and regional and remote teachers have written to say they were never asked. Senior officers regard the consultation as complete and may interrupt to say so. Acknowledge the committee's part without blaming the officers, and propose a targeted round of online consultation with regional teachers before the draft goes further, with a summary tabled at the next meeting.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "Festival association AGM, members on the floor", "prompt": "A school's formal complaint about an adjudicator's comments to students went unanswered by the board for eight months. The school has raised it from the floor, and some long-standing members are hostile to any suggestion of fault. Own the delay as the board's, and propose a written complaints procedure with acknowledgement within five working days, a decision within six weeks and an annual report of complaints to members.", "seconds": 200}]}, {"id": "gf-concession-reversal", "examples": [{"title": "Before the staffing review", "room": "Music department meeting", "prompt": "The school executive has announced a review of staffing costs across learning areas, and music, with its small senior classes, is widely expected to be a target. Your staff are anxious. You speak to them before the review begins.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "I wanted to say this to you together, in this room, before anyone reads a line of it in the *minutes*.", "note": "Choosing the room over the record is the Tilbury move: the leader comes to the people at risk rather than speaking from a document. Naming the minutes as the alternative tells staff they are hearing it first, which is a form of respect."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "I know how the review will see us. We are a small department, our senior classes are small, and on a spreadsheet of cost per student we look *expensive*.", "note": "The concession is stated in the reviewers' own terms and in a single sentence, without apology or qualification. Saying it first means no one in the room has to carry the fear privately."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "But a spreadsheet cannot see what we carry. Our ensembles are the school's public face at every open night and every ceremony. Our students sit the ATAR course and rehearse before school three mornings a week. Small senior classes are not our weakness; they are the *price* of that standard.", "note": "The reversal is factual, not rhetorical: it lists what the department does, then reframes the conceded fact itself. 'Not our weakness; they are the price' uses antithesis so the same number now argues for them."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "So I will take the exposure for this. I will write our submission myself, I will present it to the executive in person, and if a cut has to be argued, it will be argued with me, not with any one of you in a *corridor*.", "note": "The commitment is a tricolon that rises from writing to presenting to absorbing the argument personally. The corridor is a precise image of how cuts actually happen in schools, and promising to stand in front of it is what makes the reversal believable."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "What I need from you is the evidence: your rehearsal hours, your performance dates, your results, by Friday week. || And when this review is done, the executive will know exactly what this department does, and *who* does it.", "note": "The practical request comes inside the promise, so the work feels like contribution to a shared outcome. The final clause promises recognition by name, which is what Elizabeth promised and what staff under review most want."}], "weak": {"text": "So, as you've probably heard, there's going to be a staffing review, and I won't lie, music might be looked at fairly closely, given our class sizes. I don't think we should panic, though. We do a lot of great things and hopefully that will be recognised. I'm going to try to put something together for the executive, and it would be really helpful if people could send me any information they think is relevant, whenever you get a chance. We'll just have to wait and see, really. I'll keep everyone posted as much as I can.", "why": ["The opening is hearsay ('as you've probably heard'); the strong version chooses to say it in person, before the minutes.", "'We do a lot of great things' is a generality; the strong version reverses the concession with specific facts and reframes the class sizes themselves.", "'I'm going to try' offers no exposure; the strong version commits to writing, presenting and taking the argument personally.", "The request is vague and undated, and there is no promise of recognition; the strong version asks for named evidence by Friday week."]}}, {"title": "A volunteer association before a panel", "room": "Arts funding panel interview", "prompt": "The festival association has been shortlisted for a regional access grant and is interviewed by a panel that also funds professional companies. You attend as treasurer. Panel members have noted that the application is less polished than others.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "I asked to come in person, rather than rest on the application, because the people who run this festival are volunteers, and someone should be able to answer for them *directly*.", "note": "Attending is presented as a duty to the volunteers, not a sales opportunity. That establishes him as standing among the people at risk, and it frames everything that follows as accountability."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "I will concede the obvious point before you raise it. We have no paid staff, no development officer, and our application is less *polished* than the ones you will read from the major companies.", "note": "'Before you raise it' signals that he knows what the panel is thinking, which earns credit for candour. The concession is a plain list of three absences, with the panel's own criticism, 'polished', placed last."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "But consider what that means in practice. Every dollar you give us reaches a student, because there is no salary for it to pass through. The festival has run every year for a generation on the weekends of working teachers. That is not fragility. It is *continuity*, and it is cheap to fund.", "note": "The reversal takes each conceded absence and shows its consequence for the funder. 'That is not fragility. It is continuity' is a correction in two short sentences, and the tag 'cheap to fund' brings it back to the panel's actual decision."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "As treasurer, I will sign the acquittal myself, and I will send the panel a short report at the midpoint of the grant, not only at the end, so you can see where the money has gone while it is still being *spent*.", "note": "The personal commitment answers the unspoken risk of a volunteer body, which is weak reporting. Offering more accountability than the panel requires is the practical equivalent of taking up arms alongside the troops."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "If you fund the regional travel line, forty more students from outside the metropolitan area will play on a festival stage next year, and their schools will know who made it *possible*.", "note": "The promise names a number of students and shares the credit with the panel, which is what funders are accountable for. Ending on 'possible' leaves the panel as the agent of the outcome."}], "weak": {"text": "Thanks for having us. I know our application probably isn't as professional as some of the others you'll see, and I apologise for that, we're all volunteers and it's hard to find the time. But we really do think the festival is important, and the students absolutely love it. Any funding you could give us would make a huge difference, especially for the regional schools. We'd obviously do all the reporting you need. I'm happy to answer any questions, and thanks again for considering us.", "why": ["The concession becomes an apology, which concedes the panel's point and stops there; the strong version concedes without apology, then reverses.", "'The students absolutely love it' is sentiment; the strong version shows that having no staff means every dollar reaches a student.", "'All the reporting you need' is the minimum; the strong version offers a personal signature and a midpoint report.", "'Any funding' has no target or outcome; the strong version names the travel line and forty students."]}}, {"title": "The teacher on the research panel", "room": "Music education research conference", "prompt": "You are the only practising teacher on a panel of researchers at a national music education conference. Earlier sessions have treated classroom knowledge as anecdotal. You speak in your turn.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "I have been sitting in the audience for two days, and I wanted to come up here and speak *with* the researchers in this room, not about them.", "note": "The preposition is the technique: 'with', not 'about', places him among the researchers rather than opposite them. It defuses any expectation that the teacher on the panel has come to complain."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "I am the only person on this panel without a doctorate, and I have published nothing. By the usual measure, what I say is *anecdote*.", "note": "He concedes in the room's own vocabulary and uses the very word that has been used against practitioners. Two short declaratives make the concession sound settled, not wounded."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "But I have taught adolescent musicians every week for twenty years. I see in a fortnight what a study sees in a semester, in students who were never anybody's sample. Practitioner knowledge is not weaker evidence. It is *earlier* evidence: it is where your next good question comes from.", "note": "The reversal concedes the hierarchy of evidence and then relocates practitioner knowledge within it, before the study rather than beneath it. 'Not weaker, earlier' is a single-word substitution that researchers will recognise as a real epistemic claim."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "So I will make an offer rather than a complaint. My department will host a research partnership next year: open rehearsals, our assessment data with consent, and my own time as a co-investigator, *unpaid* if need be.", "note": "'An offer rather than a complaint' names and refuses the role the room expected him to play. The offer is specific enough to be accepted on the spot, and the personal cost is stated plainly."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "If someone here takes that up, the paper that results will carry a teacher's name alongside yours, and the profession will see what this kind of partnership can *produce*.", "note": "The promise is shared recognition, a joint authorship, which reverses the original concession about publishing nothing. The circle closes on the room's own currency."}], "weak": {"text": "I guess I'm a bit of the odd one out up here, not being an academic, so I hope you'll bear with me. I do think there's sometimes a bit of a gap between research and what actually happens in classrooms, and teachers have a lot of knowledge that maybe doesn't always get valued as much as it could. It would be great if there were more opportunities for teachers and researchers to work together. If anyone's interested in that kind of thing, feel free to come and chat to me afterwards.", "why": ["'Bear with me' turns the concession into a request for indulgence; the strong version concedes and moves straight to the reversal.", "'A lot of knowledge that maybe doesn't get valued' is a complaint; the strong version reframes practitioner knowledge as earlier evidence.", "'More opportunities' commits him to nothing; the strong version offers his department, data with consent and his own time.", "There is no promise of shared outcome; the strong version ends on a paper with a teacher's name on it."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["I wanted to say this in person, not in an email.", "I wanted to say this in the room, because the people affected are in the room.", "I asked to come in person, rather than rest on [the application].", "I wanted you to hear this from me, before you read it in [the minutes].", "I have come to speak with [the researchers in this room], not about them.", "I did not want this to arrive as [a memo], so I am here.", "This is not something I am prepared to say from behind [a submission].", "I could have sent [the figures]. I would rather stand next to them.", "You are the people [this decision] falls on, so I have come to you first.", "Someone should be able to answer for [the volunteers] directly, and that is my job."]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["I know what people think: that [music] is the first thing to go.", "I will concede the obvious point before you raise it: [we have no paid staff].", "By the usual measure, [what I say is anecdote].", "On a spreadsheet of [cost per student], we look expensive.", "I am the only person here without [a doctorate], and I will not pretend otherwise.", "It is true that [our enrolments are small]. I will not dress that up.", "We are [the smallest band in the division], and every other band knows it.", "Some have said it kindly, and some less kindly: [we are a luxury].", "Our application is less polished than [the ones from the major companies].", "I accept that [the figures], taken alone, do not make our case."]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["But here is what that view misses: [the families named us at enrolment].", "But a spreadsheet cannot see what we carry.", "But consider what that means in practice: [every dollar reaches a student].", "[Small senior classes] are not our weakness; they are the price of [that standard].", "That is not fragility. It is [continuity].", "It is not [weaker] evidence. It is [earlier] evidence.", "What we have is something [no budget line] can measure, and I can show you what it is.", "Precisely because [we are small], [every student is heard].", "It is not an ornament on [the school]; it is part of its foundation.", "The figure is accurate. The conclusion drawn from it is not."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["And I will be in the room for every one of these conversations.", "I will write [our submission] myself, and I will present it in person.", "If a cut has to be argued, it will be argued with me, not with any one of you.", "As [treasurer], I will sign [the acquittal] myself.", "I will put my own time where my argument is: [three costed alternatives by the next meeting].", "So I will make an offer rather than a complaint: [my department will host the partnership].", "I will take the exposure for this, not [the junior staff].", "My name will be on [the report], and so will the responsibility for it.", "I will [sit with the business manager] and test every figure myself.", "I am not asking you to do anything I will not be doing [on Saturday morning]."]}, {"tag": 4, "lines": ["And when this is done, everyone here will know what you did.", "When this review is finished, [the executive] will know exactly who does this work.", "If you [hold the line], the families who chose us will know why.", "[Forty more students] will play on [a festival stage] next year, and their schools will know who made it possible.", "The paper that results will carry [a teacher's] name alongside yours.", "Whatever [the adjudicators] decide, you will walk off that stage having earned it.", "When [the course] is running, [the teachers who wrote it] will be named in it.", "I will make sure [council] hears your names, not just [the department's].", "This will be the year people remember [the small band].", "If we get this right, no one will ask again whether [music] belongs here."]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Backstage, Junior Big Band", "prompt": "Your Junior Big Band is about to play its first festival set, and it is the youngest band of the day, several players only a term into jazz. Speak to them for a minute before they go on: concede that they are the youngest, reverse it into what they can do that older bands cannot, commit yourself to the stand beside them, and promise what they will take away.", "seconds": 60}, {"level": 1, "room": "Year 7 parent information evening", "prompt": "Parents have heard the specialist program is demanding: before-school rehearsals, three years of commitment, practice every night. Concede that it is a heavy load, reverse it into why that load is the point, commit to a named contact and a mid-term check-in for every Year 7 family, and promise what their children will be able to do by Year 9.", "seconds": 90}, {"level": 2, "room": "Principal's office", "prompt": "The principal is considering not renewing a part-time instrumental teacher's contract, and has said, fairly, that music costs more per student than most areas. Concede the cost, reverse it with what that teacher's students achieve and what would be lost, offer to find part of the saving yourself within the department's budget, and promise a clear report on outcomes at the end of the year.", "seconds": 120}, {"level": 2, "room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "A commercial company has launched a glossy competition with large cash prizes on the same weekend as the festival, and some board members fear schools will switch. Concede that the association cannot match its marketing or prizes, reverse it into what a teacher-run, educational festival offers that a competition cannot, commit to personally calling every director who entered last year, and promise the board a picture of entries by the end of Term 1.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 3, "room": "SCSA, meeting with senior officers", "prompt": "Senior officers have suggested the committee's work on a music course amounts to teacher advocacy rather than curriculum design, and they are likely to interrupt with enrolment figures. Concede that the course would be small and that teachers have an interest, reverse it into why that is exactly where curriculum expertise lies, commit to a written response to each of the Board's stated concerns within a month, and promise a revised proposal the officers can take forward under their own names.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "University school of music review panel", "prompt": "As the external school representative on a review panel considering closing a university's music education pathway, you face panel members who interrupt with cost figures and argue the pathway is too small to sustain. Concede the numbers, reverse them with what the state's schools will lose in teacher supply, commit your school to guaranteed placements and a co-taught unit, and promise the panel public credit for keeping the pathway open.", "seconds": 200}]}, {"id": "gf-turn-the-occasion", "examples": [{"title": "Who is on the stand", "room": "Jazz festival opening night", "prompt": "You are asked, as treasurer of the association, to open the state schools' jazz festival. Across the weekend's bands, very few of the young women who start jazz in Year 7 are still playing by Year 11.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "I am honoured to open the festival, and a little conscious that the association usually keeps its treasurer well away from a *microphone*.", "note": "Self-deprecation about role rather than ability lowers the stakes without undercutting his authority. A light laugh at the start buys licence for the seriousness later."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "Jazz is a remarkable inheritance. It was made by people shut out of almost everything else, and they answered with a music in which every player is heard, in which the youngest musician on the stand can take a chorus and be listened to as an equal. The teachers who founded this festival believed every young player in this state deserved that *conversation*.", "note": "The praise is sincere and extended, and it defines the ideal precisely: every player heard. That definition is the lever the whole speech will pull, so it has to be stated in words the room will remember."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "But tonight I want to ask who is on the stand. || Look at the saxophone sections this weekend, the trumpets, the rhythm sections. For many of the young women who start jazz in Year 7, this festival is a place they have *stopped* coming by Year 11.", "note": "The pivot is a question and an instruction to look, delivered after a held pause, so the room checks the evidence with its own eyes. The verb 'stopped' implies a process, not an absence, which points to something the festival can change."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "By the standard we have just honoured, every player heard, we fall short in the most *audible* way: in who gets to play. We adjudicate the music carefully. We have not yet counted who is making it.", "note": "The indictment quotes the ideal back in apposition, which makes the shortfall impossible to argue with. 'Not yet counted' is measured and specific; it accuses the association of an omission it can correct, not of bad faith."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "I say this because I think this festival can meet its own ideal. Next year the association will publish participation by section and gender, and fund a mentoring program pairing young players with professional women from this city's scene. I ask every director here to nominate one student. || The music has always made room. So can *we*.", "note": "The hope is made concrete with two measures and a single request of the audience. The close uses a two-sentence parallel, music then us, so the final word returns the ideal to the people in the hall."}], "weak": {"text": "Welcome, everyone, to this year's festival. It's wonderful to see so many bands here and so much talent. Jazz is such a fantastic music for young people. I did just want to mention, though, that we could probably do better with getting girls involved in jazz, because the numbers do drop off as they get older, which is something the association is aware of and keen to look at. Anyway, I won't keep you, I know everyone's keen to hear the bands. Have a great weekend, and good luck to all the bands.", "why": ["The praise is generic ('fantastic music'); the strong version defines the ideal, every player heard, so it can be used later.", "The concern arrives as an aside with 'I did just want to mention'; the strong version makes the pivot a held, deliberate moment.", "Nothing measures the present against the ideal; the strong version quotes the standard back and names the omission.", "'Keen to look at' is not hope; the strong version commits to publishing figures, funding mentoring and asking for nominations."]}}, {"title": "The teachers the award cannot see", "room": "State teaching awards ceremony", "prompt": "You have been named a finalist and are asked to speak briefly on behalf of the finalists. The instrumental teachers in your department, who travel between schools on annual contracts, are not eligible to be nominated under the current criteria.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Thank you. I am grateful, and genuinely surprised, because several of the people who taught me most about teaching are not on any shortlist *tonight*.", "note": "The modest opening already contains the seed of the turn, which is the Douglass method: the gap between speaker and occasion foreshadows the gap the speech will name. Said warmly, it reads as humility, and only later as a thesis."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "These awards honour something we do not honour often enough: the idea that teaching is a craft, practised daily, that deserves the same public regard as any other profession. The people who established them wanted the best teaching in this state to be *seen*, and for one evening it is.", "note": "He praises the purpose of the awards, not just the night, and names it exactly: teaching as a craft deserving public regard. Ending on 'seen' sets up the speech's central metaphor of visibility."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "But I have to ask whose teaching this evening can see. || In my department, instrumental teachers give our students much of what they learn. They travel between schools, they are on contracts renewed each year, and under the current criteria not one of them can be *nominated*.", "note": "The pivot reuses the word 'see' from the praise, so the turn feels inevitable rather than hostile. He speaks only of his own department, which keeps the claim factual and modest in a room full of officials."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "By the standard we have just applauded, we have built a system that cannot see some of its most skilled practitioners, because they move between sites and their contracts *end* each December.", "note": "The indictment is aimed at a system, not at the organisers, and gives the precise mechanism of exclusion. Placing 'end each December' last gives a concrete, seasonal image a ceremony audience can carry home."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "I say this because these awards can still reach them. Open the criteria to teachers on contract and to those working across more than one school, and add a category for specialist instrumental teaching. I would gladly help draft it. || Then next year the shortlist will look more like the *profession*.", "note": "The proposal is two specific changes and a personal offer, delivered as imperatives because a ceremony leaves no room for a long case. The close projects the ideal onto next year's shortlist, which the organisers can picture and want."}], "weak": {"text": "Thank you so much, it's a real honour to be here with so many amazing teachers. I'd like to thank my colleagues, my family and my students. I also want to give a shout-out to all the instrumental teachers out there, who I think sometimes get a bit forgotten, because they're not always eligible for things like this, which is a shame. Hopefully that might change one day. Anyway, congratulations to all the finalists, and thanks again to the organisers for a lovely evening.", "why": ["Thanks without content replaces the praise of the ideal; the strong version names what the awards are for.", "'A shout-out' and 'a bit forgotten' trivialise the issue; the strong version pivots on the word 'see' and states the eligibility rule.", "'Which is a shame' is regret, not indictment; the strong version names the mechanism, annual contracts across sites.", "'Hopefully that might change one day' is not a proposal; the strong version gives two criteria changes and offers to draft them."]}}, {"title": "Whose talent the audition finds", "room": "University school of music anniversary symposium", "prompt": "A university school of music is marking a significant anniversary, and you are invited to give a response from the schools sector. Its entry auditions require a level of repertoire that most students who start in public school programs in Year 7 cannot reach by Year 12.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "I am grateful to be asked, and a little aware that most people who stand at this lectern have *taught* here, rather than sent students here.", "note": "The modest opening locates him precisely: a schools voice in a university room. It flatters the institution while quietly marking the angle from which he will look at it."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "This school was founded on an ideal I admire without reservation: that musical excellence should be open to talent wherever it is found, and judged by what a young musician can do, not by who they know. Its graduates teach, perform and compose across this country, and many of the best musicians I have worked with trained in these *rooms*.", "note": "'Without reservation' tells the room the praise is not a set-up, which is exactly what makes the turn work. The specific tribute to graduates shows he knows the institution well, so his later criticism will carry the weight of an insider's."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "But I have to ask whose talent the audition is able to *find*. || For a student who began lessons at seven, with a private teacher and a family who could pay, it finds them very well.", "note": "The pivot borrows the ideal's own verb, 'found', and turns it into a question. The second sentence describes who the audition works for, leaving the room to infer who it misses, which is more persuasive than saying it."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "By the standard this school set itself, talent wherever it is found, we fall short for the student who picked up a trumpet in Year 7 at a public school and has five years, not ten. The repertoire list does not measure her talent. It measures her *start*.", "note": "The indictment quotes the founding ideal back in apposition and then gives one representative student. The closing antithesis, talent against start, makes the critique precise enough that faculty cannot hear it as sentiment."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "I say this because I think the ideal is still within reach. A preparatory program in Year 11, run with partner public schools, and an audition pathway that weighs aural and improvisational ability alongside repertoire, would find the students this school was founded to find. || My school would be glad to be the *first* partner.", "note": "The hope is a two-part proposal described as fulfilling the founders' ideal, not correcting it, which lets the institution adopt it with pride. Offering his own school as first partner turns criticism into an invitation."}], "weak": {"text": "Congratulations on this wonderful milestone. The school has done fantastic work over the years and produced so many great musicians. From a schools perspective, I suppose one thing we do notice is that it can be quite hard for our students to get in, because the audition requirements are pretty high and not everyone has had private lessons. I'm not sure what the answer is, but it might be worth thinking about whether there's a way to make it a bit more accessible. Anyway, congratulations again, and here's to the next fifty years.", "why": ["The praise is a list of compliments, not an ideal; the strong version names the founding principle, talent wherever it is found.", "There is no pivot, only 'one thing we do notice'; the strong version turns on the ideal's own verb.", "'Quite hard to get in' is a complaint without a standard; the strong version measures the audition against the founders' words.", "'I'm not sure what the answer is' abandons the hope step; the strong version proposes a preparatory program and a revised pathway, and offers a partner."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["I am grateful to be asked, and a little surprised.", "I am honoured to [open the festival], and a little conscious that [treasurers are rarely given a microphone].", "Thank you. I am grateful, and genuinely surprised, because [the people who taught me most] are not here tonight.", "Most people who stand here have [taught in this building]; I have only [sent students to it].", "I suspect I was asked because [I am the only classroom teacher on the committee], and I am glad of it.", "It is a privilege to speak on [the tenth anniversary], and a slightly daunting one.", "I am not the obvious choice to [respond tonight], which may be why I was asked.", "Thank you for the invitation. I will try to repay it with honesty rather than length.", "I am conscious that [the founders] are in this room, and I am grateful to be in it with them.", "I accepted this invitation gladly, and I want to honour it properly."]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["What this [strategy] stands for is admirable, and I mean that.", "This [festival] was founded on an ideal I admire without reservation: [every young player heard].", "Its aim, that [every young person should be a maker], is the right aim.", "The people who [wrote it] fought for it, and they deserve our thanks tonight.", "[These awards] honour something we do not honour often enough: [teaching as a craft].", "For [ten years] this [syllabus] has asked students to [compose as well as perform], and that was a brave decision.", "The founders believed [excellence should be open to talent wherever it is found].", "Many of the best [musicians] I have worked with [trained in these rooms].", "There is a reason [this association] has lasted, and it is [the principle it was built on].", "I want to be clear: I think [this ideal] is right, and worth defending."]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["But I have to ask who this celebration is for.", "But tonight I want to ask [who is on the stand].", "But I have to ask whose [teaching] this evening can see.", "For [the flagship programs], it is a good night. For [a student in a school with no music teacher], it is a launch they will never hear about.", "But I have to ask whose [talent] the [audition] is able to find.", "This is [your] anniversary. I want to speak for [the schools that are not here].", "Look around [this room], and then think about [who is not in it].", "And yet [the ideal] has a question attached to it, and tonight is the right night to ask it.", "For some of [our students], this is a celebration of something they were never offered.", "I would be failing the invitation if I did not ask [who this leaves out]."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["By the standard we have just applauded, here is where we fall short: [the schools with no instrumental teacher].", "By the standard we have just honoured, [every player heard], we fall short in [who gets to play].", "[The strategy] funds [showcases]; it does not fund [teachers].", "[The repertoire list] does not measure [her talent]. It measures [her start].", "We [adjudicate the music carefully]. We have not yet counted [who is making it].", "We have built a system that cannot see [some of its most skilled practitioners].", "In [ten years], the [syllabus] has recognised [Western art music] in detail and [contemporary and non-Western musics] in a sentence.", "Measured by [our own founding words], [the entry fee] is a door we have quietly closed.", "That is not [an oversight in the margins]. It is [the main way students are excluded].", "The gap is not between us and [our ideal]. The gap is between [our ideal] and [our timetable]."]}, {"tag": 4, "lines": ["I say this because I believe we can still live up to it.", "I say this because [this festival] can meet its own ideal.", "[One shared instrumental position per regional cluster] would make tonight's aim true for [students who are not in this room].", "Open [the criteria] to [teachers on contract], and add [a category for instrumental teaching].", "Next year [the association] will [publish participation by section and gender].", "I would be glad to help [cost it].", "[My school] would be glad to be the first partner.", "Then next year [the shortlist] will look more like [the profession].", "The music has always made room. So can we.", "If we do this, [the next anniversary] will be one [every school] can celebrate."]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Music department end-of-year breakfast", "prompt": "Colleagues are celebrating the department's best ATAR results in years, and you are asked to say a few words. Honour the result and the teaching behind it genuinely, then turn to the students who left the program in Year 10 and never reached Year 12, and propose an exit conversation with every student who withdraws and a review of the Year 10 load.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 1, "room": "Junior Big Band concert", "prompt": "The after-school jazz program is celebrating ten years. Honour its founding idea that any student could join without an audition, then point out that students who rely on buses cannot stay until five, and propose moving one rehearsal to lunchtime and asking the P&C to fund a late bus once a week.", "seconds": 90}, {"level": 2, "room": "Parents and Citizens' Association anniversary meeting", "prompt": "The P&C marks a long anniversary and asks you to speak as a staff member. Honour its ideal of every family having a voice in the school, then turn to the fact that meetings are at seven in the evening, in English, in the library, which excludes shift workers and newer families. Propose rotating meeting times, an online option and translated summaries.", "seconds": 135}, {"level": 2, "room": "State music teachers' conference opening", "prompt": "The conference theme is 'Music for All', and you are asked to open it. Honour the theme sincerely, then turn to the fact that in many public secondary schools there is no qualified music teacher at all, and propose that the association lobby for a minimum music staffing entitlement and publish a register of schools without one.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 3, "room": "Department of Education program launch, senior executives present", "prompt": "You are asked, as a teacher, to respond at the launch of a new excellence program for high-achieving students in the arts, with the Director General and senior executives in the front row. Honour the ambition, then turn to the teacher shortage that means many students will never be identified, and propose that a fixed share of the program's funding go to instrumental staffing in schools without music. Expect visible discomfort and possibly a pointed question afterwards.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "Festival association members' forum, anniversary year", "prompt": "In the association's anniversary year, you are asked to speak at a members' forum. Honour the founders' ideal of open access to performance, then show that rising entry fees have quietly reduced entries from low-fee public schools. Life members who set the fees are present and likely to interrupt. Propose a sliding fee scale based on the school's socio-economic index and a fee waiver for first-time entrants.", "seconds": 200}]}, {"id": "gf-motivated-sequence", "examples": [{"title": "An accompanist for the practical exam", "room": "Principal's office", "prompt": "Year 12 ATAR music students must arrange and pay for their own accompanists for the practical examination, and some rehearse with one for only minutes. You ask the principal to fund a school accompanist.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Last October, a Year 12 flautist walked into her practical exam having rehearsed with her accompanist for *twenty minutes*. It was all her family could afford.", "note": "The attention step is a single, dated, verifiable incident with a precise number, which suits a principal who distrusts rhetoric. The second sentence supplies the cause without comment, so the principal draws the conclusion himself."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "She was not unusual. Every year about a third of our ATAR students go into that exam under-rehearsed with their pianist, and it is not the weaker students; it is the ones whose families cannot pay for extra sessions. The practical is a large part of the course mark, so this lands directly on our *results*.", "note": "The need moves from one student to a pattern, then to the principal's own interest, results, which is Monroe's 'show it affects them'. The correction 'it is not the weaker students' pre-empts the most likely objection."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "What fixes this is a school accompanist for Terms 2 and 3, a small fraction of a position, booked through the department so every student gets the same rehearsal time. Several schools with specialist programs already *do* it.", "note": "The solution is stated in one sentence with its scope and its fairness mechanism, followed by a precedent. Brevity here is deliberate: the need has done the work, so the solution only has to fit."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "Picture next October: every candidate walking in having rehearsed properly with the same pianist, and a set of practical results that reflects their *playing*, not their parents' budget. || Without it, we will have the same conversation about the same students.", "note": "The visualisation is concrete and modest, a scene rather than a vision, and it ends on the antithesis of playing against budget. The brief 'without it' gives the cost of inaction in one flat sentence, which is more persuasive than an elaborated threat."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "So what I am asking for is one thing: approval for a one-year trial from next year's budget. I will bring you the costing by Friday and the results comparison at the end of the year.", "note": "One request, sized for a yes, with the speaker carrying the follow-up work. Offering a results comparison frames the trial as evidence the principal can use, which makes approval easy to defend."}], "weak": {"text": "I wanted to raise the issue of accompanists for the ATAR practical. It's something that's been bothering me for a while. The families have to organise and pay for them, and it's a bit of an equity issue, because some students don't get much rehearsal time. There are various ways we could approach it. We could look at a school accompanist, or maybe a subsidy, or some kind of roster with the instrumental staff, although they're pretty stretched. I'm not sure what the budget looks like. Maybe it's something we could think about for next year?", "why": ["The opening is an agenda item, not an attention step; the strong version opens with the flautist and twenty minutes.", "'A bit of an equity issue' is abstract; the strong version shows the pattern and connects it to the school's results.", "Three half-options replace one solution; the strong version proposes a single accompanist with a precedent.", "There is no picture of the future and the request is a question; the strong version asks for one trial and offers the costing by Friday."]}}, {"title": "An instrument bank for regional players", "room": "Arts funding panel", "prompt": "The festival association is applying for funding to create a bank of maintained instruments that regional schools can borrow for the year. You present to the panel.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "At last year's festival, a Year 9 trumpeter from a Wheatbelt school played her whole set on an instrument held together with *gaffer tape*. She played well. She should not have had to.", "note": "The image is specific and slightly comic, which makes a panel look up without feeling manipulated. The two short sentences that follow pivot from admiration to need in the space of six words."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "Her school is not unusual. Many regional schools have a cupboard of instruments that were donated decades ago and have never been serviced, and there is no repairer within hours of them. Students in those schools start on instruments that *fight* them, and many give up before they have a chance to find out what they could do.", "note": "The need is widened from one student to a condition, with the cause, distance from repairers, explained rather than asserted. The verb 'fight' makes the problem physical, which a panel of arts people will feel immediately."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "What fixes this is an instrument bank: forty maintained instruments, loaned to regional schools for the year, serviced by the association between festivals and sent back in good order. We have the volunteers and the storage. What we lack is the *instruments*.", "note": "The solution is a single mechanism with a number and a cycle. The final two sentences isolate exactly what the grant would buy, which makes the request feel precise and therefore safe."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "Picture next year's festival with forty regional students playing instruments that respond to them, and teachers in those schools able to start a beginner band without waiting for a donation. || Without it, the tape stays on, and the cupboard stays *shut*.", "note": "The picture has two layers, the student on stage and the teacher at school, so the panel sees both the event and the lasting effect. The negative image recalls the opening tape, a callback that ties the speech together."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "So what I am asking the panel for is the instrument line in our application, in full. I will sign the acquittal myself, and you will have an inventory and a loan register at the end of the first year.", "note": "The request is precise, one line in full, and the accountability offer is concrete and personal. Ending on documents rather than feeling matches the panel's actual duties."}], "weak": {"text": "Thank you for the opportunity to present. The association is seeking funding for an instrument library for regional schools. As outlined in our application, many regional schools have limited access to quality instruments, which impacts on student engagement and outcomes. The library would help address this need by providing instruments on loan. We believe this project aligns strongly with the panel's priorities around access and equity. We would be very grateful for any support the panel is able to provide, and we're happy to answer any questions.", "why": ["It opens with the request and a reference to the application; the strong version opens with the trumpeter and the tape.", "'Impacts on student engagement and outcomes' is grant jargon; the strong version explains the cause and how instruments fight beginners.", "There is no picture of the future at all; the strong version shows the festival stage and the beginner band.", "'Any support' is not an action; the strong version asks for one line in full and offers an inventory and register."]}}, {"title": "One shared ensemble roll", "room": "Music department meeting", "prompt": "Ensemble attendance is recorded on separate paper rolls by each director, and patterns of absence are noticed only when a family asks to withdraw. You want colleagues to trial a single shared online roll.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Three weeks ago, a Year 10 clarinettist missed her fifth rehearsal in a row, and the first anyone outside her ensemble knew was when her mother rang to *withdraw* her.", "note": "The incident is internal, recent and familiar to every colleague, so it gains attention without dramatics. Placing 'withdraw' last names the outcome the whole department fears."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "That is not a failure by her director. It is what happens when attendance lives on six different paper rolls. A student can drift out of two ensembles and a sectional before anyone sees the *pattern*, and by then the conversation with the family is about leaving, not about what went wrong.", "note": "He removes blame from the colleague first, which is essential in a staffroom, then locates the problem in the system. The final contrast, leaving against what went wrong, shows the cost in terms colleagues care about."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "What fixes this is one shared roll, online, that takes a director two minutes at the start of rehearsal. After two absences, it flags the student to me, and I make the *call*.", "note": "The solution is sized in minutes, which is the currency of a department meeting. The last clause shifts the extra work onto the speaker, which removes the most likely objection before it is raised."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "Picture Term 1 with this in place: a student who misses two rehearsals gets a phone call that week, and we find out it is a timetable clash or a bad month at home while there is still time to *help*. || Without it, we keep finding out from the withdrawal form.", "note": "The visualisation is small and human, a phone call in the same week, which suits colleagues who distrust grand plans. The negative image returns to the withdrawal from the opening, closing the frame."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "So I am asking for one thing today: that we trial the shared roll for Term 1. I will set it up, show each of you in five minutes, and report back at the first meeting of Term 2.", "note": "A one-term trial is the smallest yes a department can give. The speaker carries setup, training and evaluation himself, so agreement costs colleagues almost nothing."}], "weak": {"text": "Just quickly, I've been thinking about attendance across the ensembles. At the moment everyone does their own rolls, which is fine, but it does mean things can slip through the cracks sometimes. I'm wondering whether it might be worth moving to some kind of shared system, maybe online, though I know everyone's busy and people have their own ways of doing things. I don't want to create extra work. Maybe people could have a think about it and we could come back to it at a later meeting?", "why": ["'Just quickly' signals it does not matter; the strong version opens with the clarinettist's withdrawal.", "'Slip through the cracks' is a cliché; the strong version shows how a student drifts out of three groups unseen.", "The solution is hedged into 'some kind of shared system'; the strong version specifies one roll, two minutes and a flag after two absences.", "There is no picture and no ask; the strong version requests a Term 1 trial and takes on the setup and follow-up."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["Last [Tuesday], at [twenty to four], I found [a Year 8 cellist] [sitting alone in the foyer].", "Last [October], [a Year 12 flautist] walked into her exam having rehearsed for [twenty minutes].", "At last year's festival, [a Year 9 trumpeter] played on an instrument held together with [gaffer tape].", "[Three weeks ago], [a Year 10 clarinettist] missed her [fifth rehearsal in a row].", "I want to start with one student, because she is the reason I am here.", "On [Monday morning], the sound meter in [M1.3] read [a level I would not let a builder work in].", "[Last term], I had the same phone call [three times] in [one week].", "The first thing [a new Year 7 parent] asked me this year was [whether we lend instruments].", "Here is a number I did not expect: [thirty students waiting on site every afternoon].", "Something happened at [the audition] that I have not been able to stop thinking about."]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["She is not the only one, and here is why it matters to us.", "She was not unusual. [Every year], [a third of our candidates] [go in under-rehearsed].", "That is not a failure by [her director]. It is what happens when [attendance lives on six paper rolls].", "It is not [the weaker students]; it is the ones whose families [cannot pay].", "This lands directly on [our results].", "Her school is not unusual. [Many regional schools] have [instruments that have never been serviced].", "By the time anyone sees [the pattern], the conversation is about [leaving].", "Every afternoon that time is going to waste, and it is the time [that makes the difference at audition].", "This is not a rare case; it is the ordinary condition of [schools without a music teacher].", "If it were your child, it would already matter to you. It is [thirty] people's children."]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["What fixes this is simple, and it is already working elsewhere.", "What fixes this is [one shared roll], that takes [two minutes] at the start of [rehearsal].", "Open [M1.3] from [3.15 to 5.00], [three afternoons a week], with [a paid supervisor].", "It costs about [four thousand dollars] a year.", "[Two other specialist schools] already do it.", "We have [the volunteers and the storage]. What we lack is [the instruments].", "After [two absences], it flags the student to me, and I make the call.", "The whole proposal fits in one sentence: [a school accompanist for Terms 2 and 3].", "It meets the need directly: [every student gets the same rehearsal time].", "It does not need [new staff or new rooms]; it needs [one decision]."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["Picture next year with this in place, and then without it.", "Picture next [October]: [every candidate walking in having rehearsed properly].", "Picture [thirty students] getting [four extra hours of practice] a week.", "Imagine [the festival stage] with [forty regional students] playing instruments that respond to them.", "A student who [misses two rehearsals] gets [a phone call that week].", "Without it, nothing changes, and [the foyer stays full].", "Without it, [the tape stays on], and [the cupboard stays shut].", "Without it, we keep finding out from [the withdrawal form].", "In a year's time, [the results] will reflect [their playing], not [their parents' budget].", "Think of the first [concert] after this, and who will be on the stage who would not have been."]}, {"tag": 4, "lines": ["So what I need tonight is one thing.", "So what I am asking for is [approval for a one-year trial] from [next year's budget].", "A vote to fund [a two-term trial] from [the association's equipment budget].", "So I am asking for one thing today: that we [trial the shared roll for Term 1].", "I will bring you [the costing] by [Friday].", "I will report [the attendance figures] at [the end of Term 2].", "What I am asking the panel for is [the instrument line in our application], in full.", "If you can say yes today, [the trial can start in Week 1].", "One signature, and I will do the rest.", "That is the whole request. I am happy to take questions on [the costing]."]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Year 7 parent evening", "prompt": "Uptake of the school's instrument hire scheme is low, and some new students are starting on cheap online instruments that are hard to play. Ask parents to join the hire scheme this term, explaining that the school's instruments are maintained, the cost is spread across the year, and the first term is half price.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 1, "room": "Music department meeting", "prompt": "Ask colleagues to each give one lunchtime per term to a feeder primary school workshop, since several primary schools have no music specialist and their students arrive at audition without ever having held an instrument. You will organise transport, the roster and the instruments.", "seconds": 90}, {"level": 2, "room": "Principal's office", "prompt": "Ask the principal to approve and fund a bus for the senior jazz ensemble's regional tour to three schools in the South West, which would also serve as recruitment for the specialist program. The deputy principal is present and has concerns about duty of care and cost. You have a draft itinerary, a risk assessment and a quote.", "seconds": 120}, {"level": 2, "room": "Parents and Citizens' Association", "prompt": "Ask the P&C to fund hearing protection for every student in the band program, after a staff member measured sustained sound levels in rehearsal that concerned you. Some committee members think it is unnecessary. You have a quote for musicians' earplugs and a plan to fit them in Term 1.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 3, "room": "Corporate sponsorship meeting", "prompt": "Pitch to the community investment team of a large company for three years of sponsorship of the festival's regional program. The team is sceptical, asks about measurable outcomes, and is likely to interrupt with questions about brand exposure and reach. You have figures on regional entries, a proposed recognition package and a reporting schedule.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "Education Department regional office", "prompt": "Ask a senior Department director to approve an additional staffing allocation for the school's string program, which is turning away qualified students. The director has said publicly that specialist programs are already well resourced and may challenge your figures. You have waiting-list data, a timetable model and a proposal for a two-year trial with an evaluation.", "seconds": 200}]}, {"id": "gf-principled", "examples": [{"title": "Saturday or Wednesday", "room": "University and conservatorium partnership steering group", "prompt": "The university wants its pre-tertiary program held on campus on Saturdays and open by audition across the metropolitan area; the school wants it on site on a weekday afternoon for its own specialist students. Both sides have restated their positions for two meetings.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Can I set aside the history of the last two meetings for a moment? | Both sides of this table have put real goodwill into the partnership, and I don't think either of us is defending territory. We're designing one program under two sets of *constraints*.", "note": "Recasting the parties as 'constraints' is the depersonalising move: people can be wrong, constraints simply exist. Naming the goodwill first gives each side an identity worth keeping, so nobody has to lose face later."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "As I understand it, the university needs the program to recruit beyond one school, because its case for funding rests on reach. We need our specialist students in it without taking a Saturday they already give to ensembles and part-time work. || Neither of those needs requires the program to sit only on campus or only here.", "note": "The two interests are cast in matching 'needs' clauses, so neither sounds more legitimate. The held pause before the last sentence lets the room discover for itself that the incompatibility lay in the positions, not the interests, which is the core Fisher and Ury distinction."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "So rather than choose between Saturday on campus and Wednesday at the school, could we look at three shapes? One, a fortnightly Saturday on campus. Two, a weekday afternoon here, open to students from other schools by audition. Three, a split: a term of weekday sessions at the school, then a block of Saturday masterclasses on campus where the wider cohort joins.", "note": "Three options break a binary that has hardened into a contest, and placing his preferred option last gives it the advantage of recency without a word of advocacy. Each option is named by its concrete shape, so the room pictures timetables rather than defends principles."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "Before we pick, could we agree what a good answer has to do? I'd suggest three tests: it recruits from at least five schools, no specialist student loses every Saturday, and the university can report it as metropolitan reach. I'm happy to draft a timetable for each option against those tests by the next meeting.", "note": "Every criterion is lifted from an interest already conceded, so both parties have pre-approved the test. Volunteering the drafting makes him the holder of the pen, which is where outcomes are quietly shaped."}], "weak": {"text": "I think we've been going around in circles a bit, and look, I don't want to step on anyone's toes. I suppose from our side we really do feel quite strongly that it needs to be here at the school, because that's what works for our kids, and Saturdays are just really hard for them. But I do understand the university has its own pressures, obviously. Maybe there's some kind of compromise we could find? I'm not sure exactly what that would look like. Perhaps we could take it offline, have a think and come back to it. I just think it's really important we get this right for the students.", "why": ["It restates its own position instead of naming both sides' interests beneath the positions.", "'Some kind of compromise' gestures at a solution where the strong version put three concrete shapes on the table.", "It proposes no test, so the decision remains a contest of preferences.", "It defers vaguely, where the strong version volunteered to draft the options against agreed criteria."]}}, {"title": "Timpani or the tour", "room": "Music parents' association", "prompt": "The music parents' association has raised a substantial sum. One group wants it spent on a set of timpani; the other wants it to subsidise the interstate tour for families who cannot afford it. The discussion is becoming heated.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Can I take the heat out of this for a minute? | Everyone in this room has given evenings and weekends to raise that money, and everyone wants it to do the most good. *That* isn't in dispute.", "note": "Crediting the shared labour reframes both factions as co-owners of one fund rather than rivals for it. 'That isn't in dispute' fences the relationship off from the problem with a single short sentence, landing on the stressed word."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "What I hear is that one group needs the money to leave something lasting, an asset that serves students for twenty years. The other needs no student to miss the tour because of what it costs their family. || Those are both good uses of a community's money.", "note": "He translates two objects, timpani and tour, into the values beneath them: permanence and access. Hearing its own value stated with respect lowers each group's cost of moving, and the closing sentence blesses both before any choice is made."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "So let me offer three ways through rather than two. We fund the tour subsidy in full this year and the timpani next. We split the money and put the balance of the timpani on the school's capital works list. Or we hire timpani for this year's concerts and run a dedicated appeal for the purchase, since a named instrument is often easier to raise for than a general fund.", "note": "Each option honours both interests in a different proportion, so nobody is asked to lose outright. Concrete mechanisms, a capital works list, a hire arrangement, a named appeal, show that the options are workable and not diplomatic decoration."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "Could we agree the test before we vote? I'd propose two: no student misses the tour for financial reasons, and the school owns its own timpani within two years. Whichever option meets both, I'd move that we adopt it tonight.", "note": "One criterion per interest makes the vote nearly procedural. Moving to adopt 'whichever option meets both' commits the room to the standard before the answer, which is exactly what insisting on objective criteria means in practice."}], "weak": {"text": "Look, I can see both sides, I really can. The timpani are obviously important, we've needed them for ages, and the tour's important too, nobody wants kids left behind. I guess I'd lean a little towards the tour, but honestly I could go either way. Maybe we could just split it down the middle? That seems fairest. Or we could put it to a vote and let the numbers decide. I just don't want this turning into a big thing between families, because we all have to work together on the next fundraiser.", "why": ["It names the objects, not the interests, so neither group feels its real concern was understood.", "Splitting down the middle is one blunt compromise, where the strong version offered three options that could meet both needs.", "A vote without a test turns it into a contest of numbers; the strong version agreed criteria first.", "The concern for relationships arrives last as an aside, instead of opening the contribution and setting its tone."]}}, {"title": "Who the theatre is for", "room": "School council (as chair)", "prompt": "The council is divided over hiring the school's performing arts centre. Two members want it let at commercial rates to cover maintenance; two want community groups to use it at no charge. As chair, you need to move the item forward.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Before we go round again, can I say something as chair? | Nobody here is arguing for an empty building, or against the community. Both views have the school's standing at heart; they simply *measure* it differently.", "note": "Speaking explicitly 'as chair' lifts him above the factions. 'Measure it differently' moves the disagreement from character to metric, which lets members agree later without conceding that they were wrong."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "One view needs the centre to earn enough to cover its own maintenance, so it never draws on the teaching budget. The other needs the community to see the centre as partly theirs, because that goodwill brings enrolments and volunteers. || I don't think either need is negotiable, and I don't think they're incompatible.", "note": "Calling both interests non-negotiable sounds like escalation but works as reassurance: each side hears its core need protected. The paired 'negotiable' and 'incompatible' give the room a clean rhythmic hinge to remember."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "So, three options. Commercial hire at full rates, with two community nights a term at no charge. A sliding scale, where not-for-profit groups pay cost recovery only. Or full commercial hire in the holidays, when the school needs no access, and community use at cost during term.", "note": "The options vary along a single axis, price against timing, which makes them comparable at a glance. Short, verbless list items are easy to hold in working memory, and that sets up the test that follows."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "Can we agree the test first? I'd suggest two lines: across the year the centre covers its maintenance, and no local community group is priced out. I'll ask the business manager to model all three against those lines, and we decide at the next meeting with *figures* in front of us.", "note": "Criteria, a named officer and a deadline convert a deadlocked item into an action. Deferring with figures rather than deferring indefinitely is how a chair uses objective standards to end positional bargaining."}], "weak": {"text": "Okay, so we've heard both views, and they're both valid. I think we probably need more information before we can decide this one. It's a tricky balance, isn't it, between making money and being part of the community. I'm conscious of time, so perhaps we could defer this to the next meeting and people can have a think about it in the meantime. If anyone wants to email me their thoughts, that would be great, and I'll try to pull something together. Does that sound alright to everyone?", "why": ["'Both valid' acknowledges views without naming the needs beneath them, so nobody feels understood.", "It frames the issue as a 'tricky balance' instead of offering options that could meet both needs.", "It defers without a test, an officer or figures, so the next meeting will repeat this one.", "It ends on a question that invites more opinion, where the strong version ended on an action."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["Can I step back from the two positions for a moment?", "I don't think anyone at this table is arguing against [the students]; we're arguing about means.", "Before we go round again, can I say that both views here come from the same place?", "This has started to feel like a contest, and I don't think it is one.", "Everyone in this room has put real work into [the program], and that's not in question.", "Let me take the history out of this for a minute and look only at the problem in front of us.", "We're not disagreeing about [the goal]; we're disagreeing about [the route].", "I'd like us to treat this as one problem with two sets of constraints, rather than two sides.", "Nobody needs to give ground on principle for us to find a way through.", "Can I put the problem on the table, rather than either proposal?"]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["As I hear it, [one side] needs [certainty about X], and [the other] needs [room to do Y].", "Underneath the two positions, I hear two needs.", "The concern behind [the proposal] seems to be [the interest]; is that fair?", "What [the Treasurer] actually needs is [the venue line down before March], not necessarily [this cut].", "Those two interests aren't in conflict. Only the two proposals are.", "Can I ask what would have to be true for [you] to be comfortable with any outcome?", "If I've understood, the worry isn't [the position] itself but [what it protects].", "Both of those needs strike me as legitimate, and neither has to lose.", "Let me name what I think each side is protecting, and you tell me if I've got it wrong.", "The position is [X]; the interest, I think, is [Y], and [Y] can be met more than one way."]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["Before we decide, can we put three options on the table rather than two?", "Let me sketch three shapes this could take.", "One, [option]. Two, [option]. Three, [option].", "There's a third way that hasn't been raised yet: [option].", "Could we spend five minutes inventing options before anyone has to choose?", "What if we divided it [by term / by year level / by venue]?", "I'm not attached to any of these; I just want the choice wider than two.", "A version that might serve both needs is [option].", "Can we park judgement for a moment and simply list what's possible?", "We could also stage it: [first step] this year, [second step] next."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["Could we agree the test first, and then see which option meets it?", "I'd suggest two criteria: [interest A, made measurable] and [interest B, made measurable].", "Whatever we choose has to [meet the deadline] and [keep cost within X].", "What would a good decision look like to someone outside this room, say [an auditor or a parent]?", "If we agree the test now, I think the choice will become fairly clear.", "Is there an existing standard we can borrow, such as [the Department's policy] or [last year's figures]?", "I'm happy to cost all three against those criteria for the next meeting.", "I'd move that we adopt whichever option meets both tests.", "Can we take [the CPI / the published guideline] as the measure, rather than anyone's preference?", "Let's settle the test tonight and the option once the figures are in."]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Department meeting", "prompt": "Two colleagues have both booked the school theatre for the same Thursday in Term 3, one for the strings showcase and one for the guitar ensembles' concert. Each says theirs was first. You could propose a shared double bill, an alternative date in the following week, or a split between the theatre and the drama studio, and a rule for future bookings.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 1, "room": "Feeder primary school concert band planning meeting", "prompt": "Two primary school music teachers disagree about the combined band's rehearsal day: one needs Tuesdays because of her own school's assemblies, the other can only release students on Thursdays. You could propose alternating weeks, a single extended rehearsal each fortnight, or a Term 4 intensive, judged against attendance and travel time.", "seconds": 90}, {"level": 2, "room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "Adjudicators want to give written feedback only, to save time; school directors want live verbal comments after each performance. The program is already running late each day. You could propose short recorded verbal comments released after the session, a two-minute verbal limit, or written feedback with a verbal session for directors at the end of the day.", "seconds": 120}, {"level": 2, "room": "Music parents' association", "prompt": "Half the committee wants a cheaper five-day interstate tour; the other half wants eight days to include a workshop at a university and a public concert. Families are watching costs. You could propose a six-day tour with the workshop, a shorter tour with optional extension, or eight days with a subsidy, measured against a per-family cost ceiling and the educational content.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 3, "room": "SCSA syllabus committee (hybrid)", "prompt": "The committee is split between weighting the performance component at half the course and keeping it at a third. A senior academic online keeps interrupting to say a third is the only defensible figure, and the executive officer wants a recommendation before the meeting closes. Separate the people from the problem, name the interests (rigour and workload against the integrity of the discipline), offer options such as a staged weighting or a moderated portfolio, and propose criteria the Board itself has used.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "Principal's office", "prompt": "The Principal and an Associate Principal both want the music block's largest room: one for a senior school study centre, the other is defending your ensembles' use of it. The Principal is impatient and wants it settled today. Find the interests beneath both positions, propose at least three arrangements (timetabled sharing, a different room for study after school, a trial for a term), and a test the decision should meet.", "seconds": 165}]}, {"id": "gf-batna", "examples": [{"title": "Naming rights", "room": "Festival association, sponsor meeting", "prompt": "The festival's major sponsor, a music retailer, will renew at the same amount only if it holds naming rights to every stage, including the stages named for host schools. You meet its marketing manager on behalf of the board.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "(Prepared beforehand, not spoken) If they walk away: two smaller sponsors, a modest rise in entry fees, or a leaner guest artist program.", "note": "The list is written before the meeting, when he is calm, so that in the room he never has to invent a fallback under pressure. Writing every alternative, including the uncomfortable ones, is what stops a bottom line being a guess."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "(Prepared beforehand, not spoken) Another retailer has offered in writing to sponsor one stage for a little under half the current amount. With a small fee rise, the budget balances.", "note": "A written offer converts a hope into a BATNA. As Treasurer he has done the arithmetic, so the alternative can be stated in one sentence with a fact behind it."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "I appreciate the offer to renew, and the company has been a generous partner. | It's worth saying what a no would mean on your side too. The festival puts your name in front of every school music department in the state over three days, and I don't know of another event with that audience.", "note": "Reading their alternative aloud, framed as an observation about audience, invites the manager to do the arithmetic of their own BATNA. Opening with appreciation keeps the observation from sounding like a warning."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "So I should be candid about where the board sits. || Naming rights to every stage would put a retailer's name over the schools' own stages, and the board won't agree to that. If it's the condition, we'll go ahead with two smaller sponsors; we already have a firm offer for one stage.", "note": "The floor is stated as information, 'the board won't', rather than as defiance, and backed with a fact so it cannot be read as bluff. The held pause signals weight without any rise in volume, which an educated room reads as composure."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "I'd much rather we found something that suits us both. | What if the company held naming rights to the main stage and the awards night, with its logo on every program and on the website? That's the most *visible* part of the festival, and the schools keep their stages. I can take that to the board on Thursday with a recommendation.", "note": "The invitation follows the floor immediately, so the conversation never settles into stand-off. Offering the most valuable thing he can give shows good faith while protecting the interest behind the floor."}], "weak": {"text": "We really do value the partnership, and I want to say that up front. Look, the naming rights thing is a bit tricky for us, I'm not going to lie. Some of the board are quite uncomfortable with it. I mean, we'd hate to lose you, obviously, you've been with us a long time. Is there any flexibility at all on your side? Maybe we could meet you halfway on some of the stages? I don't really know what the board would say, to be honest; I'd have to take it back to them. But we'd really love to keep you on board.", "why": ["'We'd hate to lose you' reveals need without any alternative, which hands the sponsor the leverage.", "It never mentions what the sponsor would lose, so their alternative goes unread.", "The floor is vague ('a bit tricky') where the strong version stated it once, plainly, with a fact behind it.", "'Meet you halfway' invites haggling on positions; the strong version offered a specific package that protected the interest."]}}, {"title": "The masterclass fee", "room": "University partnership meeting", "prompt": "The university has announced a new per-student fee for school students attending its weekend masterclass program. Your specialist students have attended free for years. You meet the head of the performance department.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "(Prepared beforehand, not spoken) If the fee stands: leave the program and run our own masterclasses, combine with another school, or apply for a small community grant.", "note": "Three alternatives of different kinds, in-house, partnership and external money, so that if one falls over the list still holds. Breadth here is what gives the next step something real to choose from."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "(Prepared beforehand, not spoken) Four professional players I work with have agreed to take a masterclass each next year at sessional rates. The total is well below the proposed fee.", "note": "He develops the alternative he alone can make credible: his own network as a working musician. Confirmed names and a costed total are what let him speak calmly in the room."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "I understand the department has to recover costs, and I'm not here to argue with that. | It may help to look at what the program becomes without us. Our students make up a good share of each session, and several audition for your degree every year. That's a pipeline the department would need to *rebuild*.", "note": "Conceding their interest first makes the reading of their alternative sound analytical rather than combative. Naming recruitment, a priority any university department holds, shows them that their BATNA is weaker than the fee assumes."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "So I'll be plain about where we sit. || At the proposed fee, we'd run our own masterclasses next year with working professionals. I've already confirmed four, and it would cost us less. I say that not as leverage, but so you know the decision is real for us.", "note": "'Not as leverage' is a deliberate disclaimer: it lowers defensiveness while delivering exactly that leverage. Specificity, four players already confirmed, is what makes an alternative credible to a numerate, sceptical listener."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "I'd genuinely prefer to stay in, because the link to the department matters to our students. | Could we look at a reduced fee in exchange for our senior ensemble performing at one of your open days? You gain a recruitment event; we keep the access. I'd be glad to put that in writing for your head of school.", "note": "Restating why he prefers agreement keeps the relationship warm after the floor. The proposal trades in something the department values, so it beats both sides' alternatives, which is Fisher and Ury's test of a good agreement."}], "weak": {"text": "Thanks for meeting with me. I have to say the fee came as a bit of a shock, and a lot of our families just won't be able to afford it. Our kids have been coming for years and they absolutely love it, so it would be a real shame to lose that. Is there any way the fee could be waived for us, given the history? I know you've got budget pressures, everyone does. I suppose we might have to look at other options if it goes ahead, but I'd really rather not. I'd love to find some way to keep it going.", "why": ["It leads with shock and hardship, which signals dependence instead of a prepared alternative.", "'Other options' is a vague hint that sounds like a bluff; the strong version named confirmed players and a cost.", "It never reads the department's own alternative, so the department's need for recruitment goes unspoken.", "Asking for a waiver on the grounds of history offers nothing in return; the strong version proposed a trade the department values."]}}, {"title": "The funder's dates", "room": "Arts funding body, negotiation call", "prompt": "A state arts funder will fund the festival's education program only if the festival moves to dates that fall in the senior school exam period. As Treasurer, you have already modelled running a year on the association's reserve.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "(Prepared beforehand, not spoken) Without the grant: draw on the association's reserve for one year, trim the guest artist program, or move to a two-day festival.", "note": "Listing alternatives before the call means the condition can be weighed against something concrete rather than against the fear of losing the money."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "(Prepared beforehand, not spoken) The reserve carries one year at a reduced program without touching the sinking fund. The committee has endorsed that as the fallback.", "note": "Committee endorsement is the decisive detail: it makes the alternative an approved plan, not one officer's preference, so he can state it on the association's behalf."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "Thank you for the offer, and for the panel's confidence in the festival. | It's worth both of us being clear about the alternative on your side. The program's purpose is participation by school students, and those dates fall when the schools you most want to reach will simply not *enter*.", "note": "He ties their alternative to the funder's own stated objective, which shows that the condition defeats the grant's purpose without ever calling the condition foolish."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "So I'll be transparent about where the association sits. || We can't move to those dates. If the grant depends on it, we'll run next year on our reserve at a slightly reduced program; I've modelled that, and the committee has endorsed it. We'd be disappointed, but we'd be fine.", "note": "'Disappointed, but fine' is the tonal signature of a real BATNA: it removes all desperation without any edge. Short declaratives after the pause carry the weight; there is no qualifier to argue with."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "I'd much rather we found a version that works for both of us. | If the panel's interest in those dates is about spreading funded events across the year, could we keep our dates and add a regional workshop day the following term, which your program could badge as its own? I can send a costed outline by the end of the week.", "note": "He probes the interest behind their condition and builds an option on it, returning the call from walk-away to joint design. The offer of badging gives the funder something to report, which is often what a program officer actually needs."}], "weak": {"text": "Thanks so much for the offer, we're really grateful. The dates are a bit of a problem for us, though, because of the exams. I'm not sure we could get the schools to come. Is there any chance at all of some flexibility there? We'd really love to have your support, it would make such a difference to the program. If not, I suppose we'd have to see what we can do, maybe scale things back somehow. I'd need to talk to the committee about that. Would you be able to go back to the panel and ask?", "why": ["It shows how much the money matters without showing any prepared alternative, so the funder has no reason to move.", "It mentions the exam clash as the festival's problem rather than as a threat to the funder's own purpose.", "'See what we can do' is an unformed fallback; the strong version stated a modelled, endorsed plan.", "It asks the funder to do the work, where the strong version proposed an option built on the funder's interest."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["If this room says no, what exactly will I do on Monday?", "Alternatives if there's no agreement: [one], [two], [three].", "Who else could provide [the venue / the funding / the service]?", "What could I do entirely within my own authority, without their agreement?", "If [the allocation] goes, which tasks stop, and who picks them up?", "Could we [run it in-house / partner with another school / defer a year]?", "What would a peer in another school or association do if they faced this?", "Which of these alternatives would I actually be willing to carry out?", "List everything, including the uncomfortable options: [option].", "What happens if we do nothing and the current arrangement simply lapses?"]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["What would make [the best alternative] genuinely workable rather than a bluff?", "Get it in writing: [a quote / a confirmed offer / an email from X].", "Cost it in [hours, dollars and dates], so I can say it in one sentence.", "Check with [the business manager / the committee] that the fallback is approved.", "Who needs to say yes for this alternative to happen, and have they?", "Name the start date: from [Term 1], we would [action].", "What is the one fact that makes this credible to a sceptical room?", "If I had to begin it tomorrow, what's still missing?", "Write the floor as a single sentence: below [X], we [do Y].", "Compare honestly: is my alternative better than their likely best offer?"]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["It might help to think about what happens on your side if we don't agree today.", "If we step away, the work doesn't disappear; it lands with [them].", "I imagine [the department] would then need to [rebuild its recruitment pipeline].", "From where you sit, the alternative to us is presumably [X], and that has its own costs.", "I'm conscious that [the event] puts you in front of [an audience] you don't easily reach elsewhere.", "What would you do instead, if this didn't go ahead?", "I'd guess a no from us would mean [a new search / a gap in the calendar] on your side.", "It's worth both of us being clear about the alternatives.", "Your program's stated aim is [participation]; without us, how is that met?", "I don't want to overstate it, but [our students] are a real part of [your intake]."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["I should be transparent about where I sit.", "Below [this point], I'd have to pursue another path.", "If that's the condition, we would [alternative] from [date].", "I say that not as leverage, but so you know the decision is real for us.", "That isn't a threat; it's simply where the work would land.", "We'd be disappointed, but we'd be fine.", "I've already [confirmed X], so it's a genuine option for us.", "The board won't agree to [X], and I'd rather say so now than at the end.", "To be plain: at [the proposed figure], we'd go a different way.", "I'd rather you heard our limit from me directly than had to guess at it."]}, {"tag": 4, "lines": ["I'd much rather we found something here that's better for both of us than that.", "So let's see if we can beat both alternatives.", "What if [you held X] and [we kept Y]?", "If your interest in [the condition] is really about [Z], could we meet that another way?", "My strong preference is to stay in, because [the link matters to our students].", "Could we look at [a reduced figure] in exchange for [something you value]?", "Let's put the alternatives aside and design the arrangement we'd both prefer.", "Is there a version of this you'd be pleased to take back to [your committee]?", "I'm keen to find a way; could we test [option] before anything is locked in?", "What would you need from us to make [option] work on your side?"]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "The adjudicator panel has asked for a substantial rise in fees. You have identified two interstate adjudicators who would assess recorded performances remotely at the current rate. Brief the board on how you will approach the panel convenor: your alternative, what the panel loses without the festival, your floor and an offer.", "seconds": 90}, {"level": 1, "room": "Department meeting", "prompt": "The company that services the department's brass and woodwind instruments has raised its prices sharply. You have a written quote from an independent technician at the old rate. Tell colleagues how you plan to negotiate, including what you would offer the company to stay, such as a longer contract or bundled holiday servicing.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 2, "room": "University partnership meeting", "prompt": "The university proposes halving the number of pre-service music teachers it places in your department, which you rely on for sectional tutoring. You have an offer from another university to send placement students instead. Negotiate with the placement coordinator, reading what the university loses (a strong placement school) and proposing a better arrangement.", "seconds": 120}, {"level": 2, "room": "School council", "prompt": "The company that runs the school's instrument hire scheme wants to raise family charges and says no other provider covers the full range. You have a quote from a music retailer for the core instruments, and the school owns the larger ones. As chair, set out the alternatives so the council negotiates rather than simply accepts, then propose a floor and a counter-offer.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 3, "room": "Principal's office", "prompt": "The Principal and an Associate Principal propose folding the TIC Music role into a single Performing Arts leadership position without additional time. You have prepared an alternative: handing back specific administrative tasks you currently absorb, listed and costed. They are firm and the Associate Principal interrupts often. State your floor once, as information, and turn the meeting toward a structure with clear authority.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "Commercial venue negotiation", "prompt": "The venue manager for the festival's main concert hall says the new hire fee is final and other events are waiting for the dates. You hold a provisional booking at a university concert hall with a smaller foyer. The manager is brisk and talks over you. Read the venue's alternative (a gap in a slow week), state the festival's floor, and offer a multi-year booking at a lower rate.", "seconds": 200}]}, {"id": "gf-pyramid", "examples": [{"title": "Protect the line", "room": "Principal's office", "prompt": "The draft 2027 timetable places a new STEM elective on the same line as the specialist music classes for Year 10. The Principal asks for your view in a short meeting before the timetable goes to staff.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "The specialist program has had its own timetable line since it began, and that's what lets students take Music alongside a full academic load. It's an arrangement nobody has questioned.", "note": "One honest sentence of shared ground lets the Principal recognise their own understanding before anything is asked of them. Ending on 'nobody has questioned' establishes the status quo as the default that a change must justify."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "What's changed is that the draft 2027 timetable puts the new STEM elective on the same line, so a Year 10 student would have to *choose* between them.", "note": "The complication is a single concrete fact, not a grievance. Stressing 'choose' names the cost in the student's terms, which is the frame a principal is obliged to care about."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "So the question is simply this: | do we accept the clash, or protect the line?", "note": "Asking the question aloud brings the listener to the exact point where they want an answer, which is Minto's reason for the introduction. A binary question is deliberate here: it makes the answer that follows feel like a decision, not an opinion."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "My answer is that we protect the line, and move the STEM elective to line four, where it meets only smaller cohorts. ||", "note": "The recommendation comes fourth, before any reasoning, so the Principal holds it while the reasons arrive. It includes the mechanism, line four, so the answer is a proposal and not merely a preference."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "Three reasons. First, students: we auditioned these students into a three-year commitment, and a forced choice breaks a promise we made to their families. Second, enrolments: in our school the strongest musicians and the strongest STEM candidates overlap a good deal, so the clash would weaken both electives. Third, cost: line four needs no extra staffing. || I'm happy to sit down with the timetabler this week and test it against the full line structure.", "note": "The supports are grouped and parallel, students, enrolments, cost, each introduced by a one-word label that lets the listener file it. The offer to test it with the timetabler closes the pyramid with an action, so the meeting ends on a next step."}], "weak": {"text": "So I've had a look at the draft timetable, and there are a few things I wanted to raise. The music kids are pretty worried, and some of the parents have already been asking me about it. And then there's the whole question of the specialist program and what we've told families at audition. I also think, and I don't want to step on anyone's toes here, that STEM might actually do better on a different line anyway. I'm not sure exactly which one. Anyway, I just think it would be a real shame if we lost students because of a timetable clash.", "why": ["It recounts concerns in the order they occurred to him, so the recommendation never arrives.", "It skips the shared situation, so the Principal has no stable ground before the worry begins.", "The only proposal, a 'different line', is buried late and left unspecified; the strong version named line four as its answer.", "Its reasons are an unordered list, where the strong version grouped three parallel supports and stopped."]}}, {"title": "No registrar next year", "room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "The board learns that the long-serving volunteer registrar is stepping down after this festival. Discussion wanders into the history of the entry process. The president asks you, as Treasurer, what the board should do.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "For many years entries have come in on paper or emailed forms from each school, and our registrar has keyed them into the database. It works, and the schools know it.", "note": "He credits the current system before replacing it, which honours the departing volunteer and keeps the room on side. 'It works' is a concession that makes the complication sharper."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "What's changed is that our registrar is stepping down after this festival, and nobody has offered to take the keying on.", "note": "A plain statement of fact, unadorned. Naming the gap, 'nobody has offered', closes the tempting exit of finding another volunteer without having to argue against it."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "So the question for the board is narrow. | How do we take entries next year without a registrar?", "note": "Calling the question 'narrow' quietly pulls a wandering discussion back to a single decision. The breath before the question gives it a line of its own."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "My answer is that we move to online entry, with payment at the point of entry, and we do it for next year, not the year after.", "note": "The answer includes its timing, which pre-empts the board's instinct to defer. The closing antithesis, 'next year, not the year after', is short enough to be quoted back in the minutes."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "Three reasons, briefly. First, workload: it replaces the registrar's role rather than asking another volunteer to inherit it. Second, cash flow: as Treasurer, I'd far rather the payment arrive with the entry than chase invoices for a term. Third, *risk*: we keep a paper form as a fallback for one year, so no school is shut out while it adjusts. || I'd suggest we get two quotes for an entry platform before the next meeting and decide then.", "note": "Workload, money and risk are the three lenses a board actually uses, so the grouping feels complete. The third reason anticipates the strongest objection and answers it inside the case, which is economical and disarming."}], "weak": {"text": "Well, it's a big loss, obviously, and I think we all owe her an enormous amount. I suppose we could look for another volunteer, although that's been hard in the past. A few other festivals have gone online, I think, and it seems to work for them, though I'm not sure of the details. There'd be a cost, of course, and some schools might struggle with it. It would certainly help with the money side, from my end. Maybe it's something we could look into over the next year or so and see where we land?", "why": ["It never states a recommendation, so the board has nothing to react to.", "It thinks aloud through options in the order they occur, where the strong version gave the answer fourth.", "Objections and benefits arrive mixed together, rather than as three grouped supports.", "It ends by deferring for 'a year or so', where the strong version fixed the decision for next year and named a next step."]}}, {"title": "One program, not three", "room": "Arts funding panel interview", "prompt": "At interview for a youth jazz mentoring grant, a panel member asks bluntly why the panel should fund one program for school-age players rather than spread the money across several community groups.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Western Australian schools produce strong young jazz players, and the festival hears them every year. That part of the pipeline is working.", "note": "Beginning on a strength concedes nothing to the challenge and quietly establishes his authority: he is the person who hears these players every year. Short sentences signal composure under a blunt question."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "What's changed is that the informal paths into the profession, the jam sessions and the rooms that used to take young players, have thinned out. Students leave school with *nowhere* to play beside professionals, at exactly the age when that matters most.", "note": "The complication is drawn from his own working life as a performer rather than from statistics he cannot cite, which keeps it accurate and lets his experience carry the weight."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "So the question is whether that gap is best closed with one targeted program, | or with the money spread thinly.", "note": "He restates the panel member's challenge as the question, which shows he has heard it and accepts its terms. The breath before the second option lets 'spread thinly' land as the weaker choice without a word of criticism."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "My answer is that one targeted program does more, because the gap is specific and so is the fix.", "note": "The answer is one sentence with its own reason folded in, a compact form that survives being repeated by the panel member afterwards."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "Three reasons. First, depth: each student plays alongside working musicians for a full year, which no single workshop can do. Second, multiplication: those students go back to their school bands and lead the next cohort. Third, accountability: one program, one set of outcomes, reported to the panel each term. || I'd welcome a condition that the second year of funding depends on the first year's report.", "note": "The supports answer the panel's real criteria, impact, reach and accountability, in parallel one-word heads. Volunteering a condition on future funding is a confident close: it shows he expects the program to meet its outcomes."}], "weak": {"text": "That's a really good question, and I can certainly see why you'd ask it. I suppose I'd say that jazz is a bit different from other art forms, in that you really have to learn it by playing with people who are better than you. And there's a real gap at the moment, I think most people in the scene would agree. Community groups do wonderful work, of course, and I'm not saying they don't deserve funding. But I do think there's something special about a longer program. And the students we'd select are very talented.", "why": ["It opens by praising the question instead of establishing the shared situation.", "It never gives a one-sentence answer, so the panel is left to infer the case.", "The reasons are general ('something special', 'very talented') rather than three grouped supports tied to the panel's criteria.", "It defends rather than proposes; the strong version closed by offering a condition on future funding."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["We all know where we stand on this: [the accepted fact].", "For [many years], [the arrangement] has worked, and nobody has questioned it.", "The starting point is uncontroversial: [fact].", "We've spent [two years] building [X], and the feedback on [content] has been strong.", "As things stand, [state of affairs].", "Everyone here agrees that [shared goal].", "The [program] currently [does X], and that's what allows [Y].", "Let me start with what isn't in dispute.", "Our position has been stable for some time: [fact].", "The schools know [the process], and it works."]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["What's changed is this: [event].", "The difficulty is that [new fact].", "But [the registrar is stepping down / the Board has said no].", "That changed when [the draft timetable / the funder's condition] arrived.", "The new element is [X], and it puts [Y] at risk.", "What we didn't have last year is [constraint].", "Since the last meeting, [development].", "The problem is narrower than it looks: [specific].", "That arrangement now runs into [obstacle].", "What's shifted is not [the goal], but [the condition]."]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["So the question for us is simply this: [question]?", "Which leaves one question: [question]?", "The question isn't whether [X], but how [Y].", "So the decision in front of us is narrow.", "Put plainly: do we [option A] or [option B]?", "The question that raises is [question].", "So what we have to decide today is [question].", "The real question, I think, is [question].", "That brings us to the one thing we need to settle: [question].", "So: how do we [achieve the goal] without [the cost]?"]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["My answer is that we should [action].", "I'd recommend we [action], and do it [this year].", "In one sentence: [recommendation].", "My view is that we [action], rather than [alternative].", "The short answer is [answer].", "I'd suggest we [action], and I'll give you three reasons.", "We should [action].", "My recommendation is [action], starting [date].", "I think the answer is [answer], and it's simpler than it looks.", "So I'd propose [action] at this meeting."]}, {"tag": 4, "lines": ["Three reasons, briefly.", "First, [students]. Second, [workload]. Third, [cost].", "The first reason is [the Board's stated objection].", "Second, it protects [the work nobody has criticised].", "Third, it's achievable: [the model exists / the cost is known].", "One on [students], one on [staff], one on [money].", "The last reason is the practical one: [reason].", "That's the case. I'd suggest [next step] for the next meeting.", "Those three reasons point the same way.", "I can take any of those further, but that's the core of it."]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Department meeting", "prompt": "Colleagues are weighing two dates for the winter concert: the last Thursday of Term 2, which clashes with Year 12 mock exams for some students, or the second Tuesday of Term 3, which leaves less rehearsal time after the holidays. Give your recommendation first, then three grouped reasons.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 1, "room": "Music parents' association", "prompt": "A parent asks whether Year 7 students should join next year's interstate tour, which has been for Years 9 to 12. You could recommend a separate Year 7 and 8 day trip instead, or a supervised place for the junior concert band. Answer first, with three supports on students, supervision and cost.", "seconds": 90}, {"level": 2, "room": "University academic committee", "prompt": "The convenor turns to you and asks whether schools would support a common audition repertoire list for entry to the performance degree. You think a short list of set pieces plus one free choice would serve both schools and the university. Give the situation, the complication, the question and your answer in under thirty seconds, then three reasons.", "seconds": 120}, {"level": 2, "room": "School council", "prompt": "The council is considering whether the school should apply for a new specialist program in another learning area, which would compete with Music for rooms and enrolments. The chair asks your view. You could recommend supporting the application on condition that a facilities plan protects existing specialist spaces. Answer first.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 3, "room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee (hybrid)", "prompt": "After the Board's rejection of the committee's course, discussion has run for an hour. A senior public servant cuts in and says, 'Give us one line: what do we do?' Two others talk over each other online. Deliver an answer-first recommendation (for example, a targeted resubmission addressing the Board's stated objections, with a working group and a timeline) and hold the structure if interrupted.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "Principal's office", "prompt": "The Principal and the business manager propose cutting one of the four concert bands next year to fund staffing elsewhere. They have figures and are sceptical. Situate the program, name the complication, ask the question aloud and give your answer (for example, merging two bands for one year with a review, or recovering the cost through ensemble levies), with three supports, while they press you on cost.", "seconds": 200}]}, {"id": "gf-rapoport", "examples": [{"title": "The six-week rule", "room": "School council (as a member)", "prompt": "A respected council member, a lawyer, argues persuasively that every excursion should require six weeks' notice to the council, for reasons of risk and parent consent. You know it would end short-notice performance invitations, which are often the best opportunities students get.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Let me check I have the argument right, because I think it's a strong one. | At present, notice for excursions varies by department; that inconsistency is a risk the school carries; a single rule of six weeks gives parents and the council time to consent *properly*.", "note": "Three short clauses separated by semicolons restate the case as a chain of reasoning, which is fairer and tighter than the original. A restatement that sharp tells the room he is engaging with the argument, not with the speaker."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "I agree with two things in that completely. Inconsistency is a real risk, and consent given in a rush isn't really informed consent.", "note": "The agreement is specific, two named points, so it reads as considered rather than polite. 'Informed consent' is the speaker's own professional vocabulary, which signals that he has met them on their ground."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "And I hadn't thought until now about how exposed the council itself is when departments set their own timelines. That's a useful point, and I'll take it back to Music.", "note": "Saying what he learned, and what he will do with it, proves the learning is real. It also quietly concedes that his own department is part of the problem, which buys credibility for the rebuttal that follows."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "Where I part company is one narrow point. || Performance invitations, a civic ceremony or a festival opening, often come with three or four weeks' notice, and a flat rule would mean declining them. So I'd suggest we adopt the six-week rule as the standard, with a short-notice pathway for performances: a risk assessment approved by the Principal and parent consent within a week. We keep the consistency, and the students keep the opportunities.", "note": "The rebuttal is confined to a single category of excursion, which makes it look like a refinement of the colleague's policy rather than an attack on it. The closing parallel, 'we keep... the students keep', gives the council a balanced sentence to adopt."}], "weak": {"text": "I can see where you're coming from, but I have to say, from a music point of view, this would be a disaster. We get invitations all the time at short notice, and they're some of the best experiences the kids have. If we have to give six weeks' notice for everything, we'll just have to say no. I'm not saying risk isn't important, obviously it is. But I think we need to be careful not to wrap everything in cotton wool. Maybe music could be exempt, or something along those lines?", "why": ["'I can see where you're coming from' gestures at understanding without restating the argument, so the colleague feels unheard.", "It names no specific point of agreement, which makes 'risk is important' sound grudging.", "It learns nothing aloud, so the rebuttal reads as departmental self-interest.", "'Wrap everything in cotton wool' caricatures the position; the strong version disagreed with one narrow point and proposed a pathway."]}}, {"title": "Selective programs and advantage", "room": "Education conference panel", "prompt": "On a conference panel, a well-known education academic argues that selective specialist programs entrench advantage and should be phased out in favour of universal provision. The audience is sympathetic. You run such a program and believe the problem lies upstream.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Let me make sure I'm answering the argument that was made, because it's a serious one. | Selection by audition rewards students who have already had lessons, and lessons cost money. So a selective program, however well meant, can end up *concentrating* opportunity among families who already have it.", "note": "He restates the argument in its strongest causal form, two premises and a conclusion, so that the audience recognises it as fair. Prefacing with 'answering the argument that was made' signals that he will not attack a straw man, which a sympathetic audience is watching for."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "I agree with the premise entirely. Prior tuition does shape audition results, and any program that ignores that is measuring opportunity rather than potential.", "note": "Accepting the premise outright, and sharpening it with the opportunity and potential distinction, is the most disarming move available to someone defending the program under attack."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "And I learned something from that framing just now: the question isn't only who gets in, but at what age the gap opens. That's a better question than the one I usually ask.", "note": "Credit given in public, with the precise insight named, is what Dennett means by saying you have learned something. It also redefines the problem in a way that makes his rebuttal possible."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "Where I'd part company is the remedy. || If the gap opens before Year 7, abolishing the program closes it for no one; it removes an option and leaves the gap where it was. So I'd suggest we keep the program and move the investment upstream: a combined primary concert band with local feeder schools, free instrument loans in Years 5 and 6, and audition tasks that test aural and rhythmic aptitude rather than prior repertoire.", "note": "He grants the diagnosis and disputes only the remedy, the narrowest possible ground. Three concrete upstream measures turn the disagreement into a proposal the academic could endorse, which is the point of the rules."}], "weak": {"text": "With respect, I think that's a bit unfair on programs like ours. We work really hard to be inclusive, and we have students from all sorts of backgrounds. I'm not saying there's no issue at all, but I don't think the answer is to get rid of specialist programs altogether. That would hurt the very students we're trying to help. Talented kids deserve to be challenged too. I think the research on this is quite mixed, actually, and it really depends on how the program is run.", "why": ["'With respect, that's unfair' responds to tone rather than to the argument, which was never restated.", "It concedes 'some issue' vaguely instead of naming the precise premise it accepts.", "Appealing to 'mixed research' without a source is a weak move in front of an expert; the strong version argued from the mechanism instead.", "It defends the program rather than proposing a remedy; the strong version moved the investment upstream with three specific measures."]}}, {"title": "Saturday rehearsals", "room": "Department meeting", "prompt": "A senior, well-liked colleague argues that the department should stop all Saturday rehearsals because staff are exhausted and it is not sustainable. You agree about workload, but the senior big band needs some weekend time before the festival.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Can I check I've got this right? | The argument is that Saturday rehearsals have quietly become an expectation rather than a choice, and that an expectation nobody is paid for isn't *sustainable*, however much we love the work.", "note": "The restatement improves on the original by naming the mechanism, an expectation nobody chose, which is the strongest version of the colleague's case. Including 'however much we love the work' shows he has heard the emotional content as well as the argument."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "I agree with that completely. Nobody should be running a rehearsal on their seventh straight working day, and I've done it myself more often than I'd like. It also models something we'd never accept for our students.", "note": "Admitting his own part makes the agreement personal and therefore believable. A colleague who hears their point endorsed from experience has no reason to defend it further."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "And I hadn't really seen until now that the problem is the open-endedness, not the Saturdays themselves. That's helpful.", "note": "Naming the precise insight, open-endedness rather than Saturdays, shows that the learning is real, and it relocates the problem to the place his proposal will address."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "So where I'd differ is narrow. || The senior big band does need some weekend time before the festival, because it's the one ensemble that can't get its full rhythm section together in the week. I'd suggest four Saturdays, all in Term 2, published in Term 1, with time in lieu for whoever runs them. Every other ensemble stops weekend rehearsal entirely, and we review the arrangement after the festival.", "note": "The exception is bounded by number, term, notice and compensation, so it answers the colleague's real objection, open-ended demand, rather than overriding it. Ending on a total stop for every other ensemble, with a review, gives the colleague the bigger win, which is how a narrow rebuttal secures agreement."}], "weak": {"text": "Yeah, look, I get it, everyone's tired, me included. But I think if we stop Saturdays altogether, the big band just won't be ready for the festival, and that reflects on all of us. Maybe we just need to be a bit smarter about how we use them? I don't know, maybe fewer of them, or shorter ones. I'm happy to do more of them myself if that helps. I just don't want us to throw the baby out with the bathwater.", "why": ["'I get it, everyone's tired' compresses the colleague's argument into a mood rather than restating its logic.", "It agrees in passing instead of naming the specific point it shares.", "It learns nothing aloud, so the colleague has no sign their view changed anything.", "The counter-proposal is vague and self-sacrificing; the strong version bounded the exception by number, term, notice and time in lieu."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["Let me check I have this right, because I think it's a strong argument.", "If I've understood [the Professor], the case is this: [restatement].", "Put at its strongest, the argument is that [X], and therefore [Y].", "As I hear it, the point is not [the easy version] but [the actual claim].", "Tell me if I'm doing your argument justice: [restatement]?", "The heart of what [name] said, I think, is [core claim].", "There are really two steps in that argument: [step one], and so [step two].", "Let me put that back in a sentence, because it deserves a careful answer.", "So the claim is that [X] matters more than [Y], because [reason].", "I want to make sure I'm answering what was said, not a version of it."]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["I agree with two things in that completely.", "On [X], I don't think there's any daylight between us.", "[Name] is right that [point], and I'd go further.", "That's a point that isn't made often enough in this room: [point].", "I share the concern about [issue] entirely.", "I accept the premise: [premise].", "[Speed / Equity / Rigour] matters, and [name] is right to press it.", "The diagnosis is right, I think.", "Nobody should dismiss [point]; it's real.", "I've seen that myself, in [our own program], more often than I'd like."]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["And I hadn't considered something until just now: [insight].", "That's changed how I think about [X].", "What I take from that is [insight], and I'll be using it.", "I'd been looking at this from [our side]; [name] has shown me [their side].", "That's a genuinely useful point about [X], and it sharpens my own thinking.", "I came in thinking [X]; I now see [Y] more clearly.", "I hadn't appreciated how much [the Board] values [precedent].", "That reframing is helpful, and I'd like to build on it.", "The question isn't only [A], it's [B], and that's a better question than mine.", "That's an insight I'd like to keep, whatever we decide."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["Where I part company is one specific point.", "My difficulty is narrower than it might sound: [point].", "The one step I'm not sure follows is [step].", "So my suggestion is to take [name]'s insight and apply it to [the actual problem].", "I'd keep [their principle] and change only [the mechanism].", "Where I'd differ is the remedy, not the diagnosis.", "That fixes [the part nobody criticised] and may not fix [the part they did].", "If we [refinement], we get [their benefit] and keep [ours].", "I'd suggest a refinement rather than an alternative: [proposal].", "So I'd propose [action], which I think meets [name]'s concern directly."]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Department meeting", "prompt": "A colleague argues well that the end-of-year concert should move from the theatre to the courtyard, as an informal evening, to draw more families. You like the idea but worry about sound and weather for the strings. Restate, agree, say what you learned, and propose a narrow refinement such as a courtyard first half and a theatre second half, with a wet-weather plan.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 1, "room": "Music parents' association", "prompt": "An articulate parent argues that the interstate tour should be optional for Year 12 students because of exam pressure. You agree in principle, but the senior ensembles cannot perform without their Year 12 leads. Respond, then propose a refinement such as a shorter tour for Year 12s or scheduling it before the exam block.", "seconds": 90}, {"level": 2, "room": "University academic committee", "prompt": "A senior academic argues persuasively that ensemble credit should be removed from the first-year degree to make room for theory and analysis. You think ensemble is essential. Restate the case at its strongest, agree with the point about theoretical depth, and propose a narrow alternative, such as integrating analysis into ensemble repertoire assessment.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 2, "room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "A long-serving board member argues that the festival should drop adjudicated awards because they discourage weaker schools from entering. You think awards drive standards. Respond using all four steps and propose a refinement, for example tiered divisions or awards for improvement alongside the existing ones.", "seconds": 120}, {"level": 3, "room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee (hybrid)", "prompt": "A senior public servant argues, fluently and with the Board's evident sympathy, that the committee's course should be shelved for three years until the broader review of senior secondary pathways is complete. The executive officer is nodding and another member interjects in support. Restate the case better than it was put, agree genuinely, name what you learned, and isolate the one point you dispute, proposing a version of the course that could run as a pilot inside the review.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "Conference keynote Q&A", "prompt": "From the floor, a prominent professor of music education argues that schools should replace jazz ensembles with broader popular music programs, because jazz has become a niche, exclusive tradition. The hall applauds. You are on stage. Respond with all four steps and a narrow rebuttal, proposing a program that keeps jazz improvisation as the method while widening the repertoire.", "seconds": 200}]}, {"id": "gf-lead-back", "examples": [{"title": "The lesson rotation", "room": "Department meeting (Dijon in the chair)", "prompt": "Classroom teachers across the school are complaining that instrumental students keep missing the same subject. The instrumental staff want fixed lesson slots for continuity. The item has been deferred twice.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Before I say where I sit on this, I'd like to hear from everyone, and particularly from the instrumental staff, because the rotation is *your* working week, not mine.", "note": "He withholds his view and names who is closest to the problem. The closing clause, 'not mine', tells the room that ownership of the answer will sit with them, which is what makes the later synthesis credible."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "Thank you. | That's clear. || We haven't heard from strings yet, and I'd value it, because you carry the largest roster.", "note": "A neutral receipt: 'Thank you' and 'That's clear' acknowledge without evaluating, so no one learns which way the chair leans. The silent group is named by function and given a reason, which invites without putting an individual on the spot."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "Let me try to draw together what I've heard. ||", "note": "The formal hinge from listening to synthesis. Said once, slowly, and followed by a held pause, it signals that the floor is closing and earns the room's attention for what comes next."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "What runs through all of it is *predictability*. Brass want continuity, so a lesson builds week on week. The classroom staff want no student missing the same Maths lesson more than twice a term. And strings made the point that the real disruption isn't the rotation at all; it's the late notice when it changes.", "note": "One abstract noun names the common thread, then each contribution is attributed in its owner's terms. Placing strings' point last lets the most reframing observation do the joining work."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "Those aren't competing demands. | They describe a rotation published a full term ahead, on a six-week cycle, with a cap of two absences per subject per term.", "note": "The device is 'they describe a design': the proposal is presented as the sum of the room's points rather than the chair's preference, and every element of it can be traced to someone who spoke."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "Is that a fair reading of the room? | If it is, I'll draft the Term 1 cycle and circulate it by Friday, and we review it at week five. Strings, if the cap doesn't work for ensembles, tell me before it goes out.", "note": "The question invites correction rather than assent. A dated next step owned by the chair converts consensus into action, and the final sentence gives the most likely dissenter a defined channel."}], "weak": {"text": "Okay, so, look, I think we all know there are some issues with the rotation. I've had a few emails about it. Personally I've always thought fixed slots make more sense, but I'm open to whatever people think. I suppose the classroom teachers have a point too; it is hard for them. Maybe we could look at something a bit more flexible, or some kind of hybrid, I don't know. Does anyone have strong feelings about it? Otherwise we could keep going as we are for now and revisit it later in the year. Thanks everyone for your input, it's been really valuable.", "why": ["He states his own preference first, so the room shapes its contributions around the chair; the strong version withholds it until the end.", "No one is named or attributed, so no one sees their point carried forward.", "'Some kind of hybrid' replaces the specific design: a term's notice, a six-week cycle and a two-absence cap.", "It ends in deferral and generic thanks instead of a tested consensus with a date and an owner."]}}, {"title": "Rankings or ratings", "room": "Festival association board (Dijon as member)", "prompt": "The board has spent half an hour on whether to replace ranked placings in each division with ratings bands. Five people have spoken, nothing has been decided, and the president looks for a way out.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "President, before I add anything, could we hear from the two directors who adjudicated last year? They've sat in the chair we're arguing about.", "note": "As a member he cannot open the floor himself, so he asks the chair to open it further. Directing attention to those with first-hand experience positions him as the person interested in evidence, not airtime."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "Thank you, that's very helpful. | Could we also hear from the schools' representative? We haven't yet had the view from the bandroom.", "note": "Brief, non-committal acknowledgement, then a second invitation. 'The view from the bandroom' is a concrete, slightly warm phrase that makes the missing perspective feel necessary rather than courteous."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "If it helps the president, let me try to pull the threads together. ||", "note": "Offering the synthesis as a service to the chair, not a bid for control, makes it easy for the president to accept and adopt."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "What I've heard is one concern expressed three ways: that the results should tell a school something *true*. The adjudicators said rankings separate bands by half a mark that no panel could defend. Our schools' representative said principals read placings and fund accordingly, so a band that comes fifth by half a mark can lose its bus money. And the secretary said sponsors want a winner to announce.", "note": "'One concern expressed three ways' is a unifying frame that converts disagreement into agreement about a value. He includes the sponsor point, which he does not share, so the synthesis is visibly fair."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "Put together, that isn't an argument for or against rankings. It's a case for ratings bands in every division, with one sponsor's award per division judged on a separate published criterion.", "note": "The 'not X, but Y' turn dissolves the binary the board has been stuck in, and the sponsor's award is the binding element that answers the point he disagreed with."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "Is that a fair reading of where the board is? | If so, I'd suggest we trial it this year, brief the adjudicators in May, and test the award with our two major sponsors before anything is announced.", "note": "He hands the question of consensus back to the chair's room, then supplies a trial, a date and a check with the stakeholders who were the source of the objection."}], "weak": {"text": "I've been listening to all this and I have to say I'm not a fan of rankings. I've adjudicated, and honestly half a mark doesn't mean anything. But I take the point about sponsors, I suppose. And schools, obviously. There are lots of considerations. I wonder whether we could look at some kind of ratings system, maybe like the eastern states do, although I'm not across all the details. It might be worth a subcommittee looking at it. I don't want to hold things up. Anyway, that's my two cents.", "why": ["He leads with his own view and credentials instead of inviting the adjudicators and the schools' representative in.", "Points are gestured at ('schools, obviously') rather than attributed and joined.", "The proposal is vague and handed to a subcommittee; the strong version names bands, a sponsor's award and a trial.", "'That's my two cents' closes by discounting the contribution instead of testing a consensus."]}}, {"title": "Twelve Saturday places", "room": "University conservatorium partnership steering group (Dijon chairing the item)", "prompt": "A steering group of the conservatorium's head of strings, two school heads of music and a faculty administrator must decide how to allocate twelve places in a Saturday pre-tertiary program. Dijon has been asked to chair the item.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Before I put anything forward, I'd like to hear from each of you, and I'd like to start with our administrator, because you're the one who'll have to *run* whatever we decide.", "note": "Starting with the lowest-status voice in an academic room is a deliberate inversion. It signals that operational reality will count, and it prevents the senior academic from fixing the frame first."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "Thank you. | And from the schools? || Head of strings, we haven't had your view yet, and you'll be teaching these students.", "note": "Short, open invitations keep the order moving without commentary. Holding the most senior voice until last, with a reason attached, honours it while stopping it from anchoring the discussion."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "Let me draw together what I've heard. ||", "note": "A plain hinge sentence. In a room of academics the absence of flourish reads as confidence."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "Three things have been said, and they fit together better than it sounded. The head of strings wants places to go on demonstrated potential, heard in a live audition, not on prior grades. Both heads of music worry that audition-only selection favours students whose families pay for private lessons. And our administrator needs a process one person can run in four weeks.", "note": "'Fit together better than it sounded' reframes apparent conflict as complementarity before the evidence is laid out. Each point is restated in its owner's strongest form, which is the test of a fair synthesis."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "That points to an audition, yes, but with four of the twelve places held for students nominated by their school music teacher who have had no private tuition, heard for *potential* rather than polish.", "note": "'Yes, but' concedes the senior academic's principle and then adds the binding element. The alliteration of potential and polish makes the criterion memorable enough to be minuted."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "Is that a fair account of where we've landed? | If it is, I'll write it as a one-page selection policy by Friday, and we confirm by email. Head of strings, I know you'd prefer pure audition, so if four is too many, say so now.", "note": "A one-page policy and an email confirmation respect the administrator's time constraint. Naming the likely dissent openly is what makes the consensus genuine rather than presumed."}], "weak": {"text": "Thanks for coming, everyone. I think we all want the best students, obviously. My feeling is that audition is the fairest way, but there's an equity issue as well, which I'm very conscious of, coming from a public school. I'm not sure how we'd handle that. Perhaps we could have some places for disadvantaged students, or perhaps that's too complicated. What does everyone think? We need to make a decision fairly soon, I gather, because of the timeline. Maybe we could all think about it and come back next week with some ideas.", "why": ["He opens with his own feeling, so the head of strings and the administrator respond to him rather than to the problem.", "No contribution is heard first and then attributed; the strong version names three positions and their owners.", "'Some places for disadvantaged students' is undefined; the strong version specifies four places, a nomination route and a criterion.", "It ends by deferring the decision a week, ignoring the administrator's four-week constraint."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["Before I say anything, I'd like to hear from everyone, and especially from [the people closest to it].", "I'm going to hold my own view back for now. Could we go round the table?", "I'd like to start with [the group this lands on first], because it lands on them first.", "Let's hear every view before anyone, including me, gets attached to an answer.", "I'd particularly value hearing from those who haven't spoken on this before.", "[Role], would you be willing to open for us?", "I'd rather the room shaped this than I did. Who'd like to begin?", "Chair, before I come in, could we hear from [the people with first-hand experience]?", "This affects [the staff / families / schools] directly, so I'd like their voices in first.", "Take whatever time you need. I'll speak at the end."]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["Thank you.", "Thank you. | Who haven't we heard from yet?", "That's clear. Does anyone see it differently?", "Thank you. [Role], we haven't had your view, and I'd value it.", "Let me make sure I have that: [their point in their words]. Is that right?", "Thank you. Is there anything that hasn't been said yet?", "I'm noting that. Who'd like to come next?", "We've heard a good deal from [one side]. Is there a view from [the other]?", "Before we move on, is anyone waiting to come in?", "Thank you. I'd rather have the full view than a quick one, so take your time."]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["Let me try to draw together what I've heard.", "If it helps, let me attempt a summary.", "I've been listening rather than speaking, so let me offer what I've heard.", "Chair, would it help if I tried to pull the threads together?", "We've had [six] contributions. Let me see whether they have a shape.", "I don't want to add another view. I'd like to join the ones we have.", "Let me play back what I think the room has said. ||", "Before we decide, let me try a synthesis, and please correct me.", "I think we're closer than it sounds. Let me show why.", "I'll be brief, because the work has already been done around the table."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["What runs through everything that's been said is [the shared concern].", "[Role] said [point]. [Role] said [point]. Those aren't in conflict.", "Three points have come up, and they fit together better than it sounded.", "Put together, those describe a design: [the proposal].", "The concern about [X] and the concern about [Y] are the same concern from two sides.", "[Role]'s point is the one I'd build on, because it answers [the other worry].", "The one piece no one has quite said is [the missing element], and it joins the rest.", "I'd include [the point you disagreed with], because the answer has to survive it.", "So the shape is: [condition one], [condition two] and [condition three].", "That isn't a compromise between the views. It's what they add up to."]}, {"tag": 4, "lines": ["Is that a fair reading of where the room is?", "Have I missed anything, or bent anyone's point?", "If that's right, I'd propose we [concrete next step] by [date].", "Does anyone feel their view has been left out of that?", "[Role], I know you'd put it differently. Can you live with it?", "If there's no objection, I'll take that as the sense of the meeting.", "Shall we record that as our position and bring the detail back next meeting?", "If anyone couldn't support that, I'd rather hear it now than afterwards.", "If that holds, I'll draft it and circulate it for comment by [day].", "Could we confirm that with a show of hands, so the minutes are clear?"]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Department meeting (Dijon in the chair)", "prompt": "Your team must choose a date for the end-of-year awards evening. One candidate date clashes with Year 12 exams, another with a staff professional learning day, and a third is late enough that some families will have left on holiday. Run the item so every teacher speaks, then propose the date and name who owns the running order.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 1, "room": "Parent music support group", "prompt": "The parent group is deciding how to spend a modest surplus from the concert canteen: new music stands for the junior bands, a subsidy for the instrumental camp, or a contribution towards a new tuba. Hear every parent, then draw it together, and propose splitting the money between the camp subsidy and stands, with the tuba carried into next year's fundraising.", "seconds": 90}, {"level": 2, "room": "School council (Dijon in the chair)", "prompt": "Council is discussing a proposal to move the school start time to 8.30 am. Staff, parents and the student representative disagree, and the bus contractor's timetable cannot change this year. Run the item, draw out the quieter members, and close with a consensus for a one-term trial with an agreed review date and an attendance measure.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 2, "room": "University music education partnership", "prompt": "A steering group with a university music education department is deciding how preservice teachers on placement in your department should be supervised. The lecturers want weekly written reports, the two mentor teachers want fewer forms, and the placement officer needs consistency across schools. Speak last and synthesise into a supervision model of one structured observation per week and a fortnightly ten-minute debrief.", "seconds": 120}, {"level": 3, "room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee (Dijon as member)", "prompt": "The committee has spent an hour on a revised assessment table. Two academics are openly at odds over the weighting of composition, a senior officer keeps pointing to the timeline, and the chair has twice tried to close. Ask the chair to let you speak last, then offer the synthesis: a staged weighting that rises over two years, with a review after the first cohort. Expect to be interrupted mid-synthesis.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "Festival association board (Dijon as member)", "prompt": "The board is arguing about whether schools may field ensembles with paid professional players in the rhythm section. Two directors are angry, the president wants a vote tonight, and one director interrupts every attempt at a summary. Hold your view, let the room run, then offer a synthesis proposing a student-players-only rule with a published exemption process for genuine instrument shortages.", "seconds": 180}]}, {"id": "gf-third-speaker", "examples": [{"title": "Tour or commissions", "room": "Arts funding panel", "prompt": "A grants panel has argued for forty minutes over whether to fund a youth jazz orchestra's regional tour or a new composition commission series. There is only enough for one. Dijon is an assessor and speaks late.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "I think this comes down to three *questions*: | which application reaches people who would otherwise go without, which one leaves something behind once the money is spent, and which one can actually be delivered.", "note": "He names all three questions in a single tricolon before arguing any of them. The panel can see the map at once, and the order, reach, legacy, delivery, quietly saves the decisive question for last."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "The first question is reach. We've heard that the tour takes live jazz to six regional towns that rarely see it, and that's true. But the commission series is streamed and goes into school libraries across the state. On reach, I think it's closer than it looked, and if anything the tour edges it on depth of contact.", "note": "He concedes the first clash to the option he will not recommend. Giving ground early is what makes the later verdicts credible, and the qualifier 'depth of contact' shows he has weighed it, not dismissed it."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "The second question is legacy. The case for the commissions is strong here: four new works, published, playable by school ensembles for years. The tour leaves memories and perhaps a few students who take up an instrument. So on legacy, the commissions win, and I'd say so *plainly*.", "note": "The concrete inventory, 'four new works, published, playable', does the arguing. 'I'd say so plainly' marks a clear verdict without raising the temperature."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "And the third, which I think is the real one, is *delivery*. || The tour budget has no contingency for travel in the Wheatbelt in winter, and the applicant hasn't toured before. The commission applicant has delivered three comparable projects.", "note": "The formulaic phrase 'which I think is the real one' tells the panel the debate's weight sits here. The held pause before the evidence lets the reframe register."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "My proposal: fund the commissions in full, and invite the tour applicant to resubmit next round with a costed contingency and an experienced touring partner.", "note": "The proposal sits at the end of the decisive clash, as the brief prescribes. Pairing the decision with a constructive path for the unsuccessful applicant protects the panel's reputation for fairness."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "So on the three questions: reach is roughly level, legacy favours the commissions, and delivery decides it. I'd move that we fund the series and give the tour written feedback to strengthen its next application.", "note": "A one-line verdict per clash, in the same order, then a formal motion. The repetition of structure is what an adjudicating room remembers."}], "weak": {"text": "I've been going back and forth on this. Both applications are really strong and I'd love to fund both. The tour is fantastic for regional kids, and I know how rare that is. Then again the commissions are a lasting thing. I did notice the tour budget looked a bit thin in places, but I don't want to be unfair, it's a first-time applicant. And someone said streaming reaches more people, which is true. I suppose I'd lean towards the commissions, maybe, unless others feel strongly. Could we split it somehow?", "why": ["It replies to points as they come to mind instead of organising them into three named questions.", "The decisive issue, delivery risk, is mentioned in passing and then withdrawn rather than made the final clash.", "'I'd lean towards, maybe' replaces a verdict on each question and a formal motion.", "Proposing to 'split it somehow' ignores the constraint that only one can be funded."]}}, {"title": "Semester or full year", "room": "Principal's office (leadership meeting)", "prompt": "The Principal, two deputies and three heads of learning area have argued about whether to move Year 10 electives from semester lines to full-year lines. Dijon is the last to speak.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "If it helps, I think we've really been arguing about three *questions*: | what students lose, what staffing can bear, and what the change is actually for.", "note": "'If it helps' softens the move in a hierarchical room without weakening it. The third question is phrased as purpose, which signals that the earlier two are secondary."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "First, what students lose. The deputy's point was that a full-year line halves the number of electives a student can try. That's right. But Technologies made the point that a semester is too short to finish anything substantial. Breadth is real, but depth is what the senior courses demand, so on this question the full year wins, *narrowly*.", "note": "He states the strongest objection in its owner's terms and agrees with it before answering. 'Narrowly' is a precise concession that keeps the verdict honest."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "Second, staffing. The concern was that full-year lines lock us in if a teacher leaves mid-year. That's a fair risk, but it applies equally to semester lines in Term 3, and we've managed those. So staffing doesn't decide it either way.", "note": "A clash can end in a draw. Declaring it neutral removes an argument from the table without defeating anyone, which narrows the debate to the question that matters."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "And the third, which I think is the real one: what is the change *for*? || If it's to lift senior course enrolments, full-year lines in the pathway subjects do that, and semester lines elsewhere don't harm it.", "note": "Recasting the debate as a question of purpose is the third speaker's characteristic move: it reveals that the earlier disagreement was about means."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "So my proposal is a hybrid by design, not by compromise: full-year lines for subjects with a direct senior pathway, semester lines for exploratory ones, and the list set by heads of learning area by the end of Term 2.", "note": "The antithesis 'by design, not by compromise' pre-empts the charge of splitting the difference. Assigning the list to heads of learning area gives authority to the people who argued."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "So on all three: students gain depth where it counts, staffing risk is unchanged, and the purpose points to the pathway subjects. That's the shape I'd recommend we take to staff.", "note": "Each clash is restated as a result in a single clause. 'Take to staff' is pitched at the Principal's next step rather than at winning the room."}], "weak": {"text": "I can see both sides, honestly. In music we'd probably prefer full-year, because you can't build an ensemble in a semester. But I understand the deputy's point about choice, and kids do like to try things. And staffing is always a nightmare, as we know. I guess it depends what we're trying to achieve. Maybe some subjects could be full-year and some could stay as semesters? I'm not sure how that would work with the timetable. It's probably something for the heads of learning area to sort out between them.", "why": ["It argues from his own subject's interest instead of naming the three questions the whole room is divided on.", "'It depends what we're trying to achieve' is the decisive question, raised and then abandoned instead of answered.", "The hybrid sounds like a compromise because it lacks the criterion: a direct senior pathway.", "There is no summary of results and no recommendation, only a hand-off."]}}, {"title": "AI in the composition task", "room": "SCSA assessment working party", "prompt": "A working party has debated whether to permit AI-assisted tools in the externally assessed composition task. Teachers, an academic and an assessment officer are divided, and the chair asks for final contributions.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "I think this has come down to three *questions*: | whether AI undermines authorship, whether banning it is fair, and whether we can verify anything at all.", "note": "Three short noun phrases in rising difficulty. The last, 'whether we can verify anything at all', is deliberately blunt, so the room already senses where the weight will fall."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "The first is authorship. We've heard that a composition generated with AI isn't the student's work. Agreed, if the tool writes the piece. But students already use notation software that voices chords and plays them back, and nobody calls that ghost-writing. So the question isn't whether a tool was used; it's where the *decisions* were made.", "note": "He agrees with the objection on its own terms, then uses an analogy the room already accepts to relocate the issue. Redefining the question is often more persuasive in an expert room than rebutting the answer."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "The second is fairness. The case for a ban is that it keeps a level field. But a ban we can't detect rewards the students willing to ignore it, and penalises the ones who comply. On fairness, a ban is the *weaker* answer.", "note": "The argument turns the opponent's value against their remedy: fairness is the reason for the ban and also the reason it fails. The balanced 'rewards... penalises' antithesis carries it."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "And the third, which I think is the real one, is *verification*. || If we can't verify authorship from a submitted file, we have to verify it from the process.", "note": "The pause isolates the decisive word. The sentence that follows is a simple inference, which makes the proposal feel like a consequence rather than an opinion."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "So my proposal: permit specified tools, require a dated process portfolio with drafts, and add a short supervised component in which the student revises a passage of their own work under exam conditions.", "note": "Three concrete mechanisms, each answerable to one of the earlier clashes. The supervised revision is the binding element: it tests decisions, which was the definition of authorship established in the first clash."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "So on all three questions, the answer points the same way: authorship is about decisions, a ban fails on fairness, and process evidence solves verification. I'd suggest we put that to the committee as a draft for consultation with schools.", "note": "The summary uses the same key words as the clashes, decisions, fairness, verification, so the room hears the structure close. 'Draft for consultation' fits SCSA's process and lowers the cost of agreeing."}], "weak": {"text": "This is such a difficult area and it's moving so fast. I don't think any of us really know where it's going. Personally I'm worried about authenticity, but I also think banning it is unrealistic, because kids will use it anyway. Maybe we need some kind of declaration where students say what they used. Or a viva? I know some universities do that, although it's resource-intensive. I'd hate us to fall behind. Perhaps we could get some expert advice before we decide anything, because I'm not sure we're equipped to make this call today.", "why": ["It opens with general anxiety instead of naming the three questions the working party is divided on.", "Authenticity and enforceability are mentioned but neither clash is resolved with a verdict.", "The mechanisms are floated as alternatives rather than combined into one proposal tied to verification.", "It closes by doubting the room's competence to decide, which hands the decision away."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["I think this whole discussion comes down to three questions.", "If it helps, we've really been arguing about two things.", "Underneath the detail, there are three questions here: [A], [B] and [C].", "Let me try to organise what we've heard into the questions it turns on.", "I'd put the debate in three questions, and I'll take them in order.", "There are a lot of points on the table, but only [two] real disagreements.", "We've heard a dozen points. I think they reduce to three questions.", "Rather than respond to each speaker, I'd like to take the questions we're actually divided on.", "The outcome turns on [three] questions, and only the last is hard.", "Let me name the questions first, so we can see where we already agree."]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["The first question is [X]. On one side we've heard [view]. But [answer].", "Take [X] first. The concern is real: [their point, fairly put].", "On [X], [role]'s point is the strongest version of the case, so let me answer that.", "The first question is the easiest, and I think we largely agree on it.", "On [X], the worry is that [risk]. That's answered by [fact already on the table].", "First, [X]. I'd concede part of this: [concession]. But [what remains].", "So on [X], the concern is real, but it's met.", "The first clash is between [value] and [value], and here [one] has to give way.", "On [X], I think the other view edges it, and I'd rather say so.", "That clears the first question. It doesn't settle the debate, but it narrows it."]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["The second question is [Y].", "Second, [Y]. It's been said that [view]. But that cuts the other way, because [reason].", "Which brings us to [Y], and this follows from the first.", "On [Y], I don't think this question decides it either way, and I'd say so plainly.", "The second question is harder: [Y]. Both views have something here.", "On [Y], the strongest argument we've heard is [role]'s, and it favours [option].", "If we accept the answer to the first question, then on [Y] we have to accept [consequence].", "Second, [Y]. The risk is real, but it isn't unique to this proposal: [comparison].", "So on [Y], [option] is the stronger answer, narrowly.", "Two questions down. The third is the one that actually decides this."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["And the third, which I think is the real one, is [Z].", "The real question, the one we keep circling, is [Z]. ||", "And finally [Z], which is where I'd make a proposal.", "The hardest question is [Z], and it's the one our answer has to survive.", "On [Z], I don't think either view has the answer yet, so here's one: [proposal].", "If [Z] is the real question, the practical answer is [proposal].", "Third, [Z]. This is where the matter is won or lost.", "[Z] matters most, because if we get it wrong the other two don't count.", "On [Z], let me be concrete: [step one], [step two], and a review after [period].", "What answers [Z] isn't [the old frame]; it's [the new mechanism]."]}, {"tag": 4, "lines": ["So, on all three questions, the case points the same way.", "To put the three together: [result one], [result two] and [result three].", "Each question, taken on its own, points to [option].", "So [X] is answered, [Y] favours us, and [Z] is solved by [proposal].", "That's why I'd move that we [motion], subject to [condition].", "The first two questions clear the ground. The third decides it.", "On that basis I'd recommend [proposal], with a review after [period].", "None of that dismisses the concerns raised. It answers them.", "So the discussion, taken as a whole, lands in one place: [conclusion].", "If the room accepts those three answers, the decision follows."]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Department meeting", "prompt": "Staff have discussed whether the junior concert should move from the school hall to a hired community venue. Cost, parking and atmosphere have all come up, in no particular order. Organise the discussion into its questions and land on a recommendation: stay in the hall this year, with improved staging and lighting, and cost the venue properly for next year.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 1, "room": "Parent music support group", "prompt": "The parent group has debated whether to replace the cake stall at concerts with a coffee van on commission. Volunteer load, income and tradition keep coming up. Name the questions, take them in order, and recommend a coffee van at the two largest concerts with the cake stall kept for the rest.", "seconds": 60}, {"level": 2, "room": "School council", "prompt": "Council has debated a proposal to let a private provider run fee-paying before-school instrumental tuition on site. Equity, supervision and the effect on the school's own program are all in play, and members have repeated themselves. Give the thematic summary and recommend a twelve-month licence with conditions on fees, child safety checks and no overlap with the school's own instruments.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 2, "room": "University course advisory committee", "prompt": "A joint committee is deciding whether first-year music education students should complete fifteen hours of observation in schools. Lecturers worry about timetable load; school representatives worry about supervision and disruption. Organise the debate into three questions and land on a one-semester pilot in four partner schools.", "seconds": 120}, {"level": 3, "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "prompt": "After a long discussion about replacing the written aural paper with a computer-based test, a senior officer is pressing for a decision, an academic interrupts to say the evidence is thin, and two teacher representatives disagree with each other. Give the third speaker's summary and propose a staged trial with paper and digital versions run in parallel for one year. Expect the officer to cut in at clash two.", "seconds": 200}, {"level": 3, "room": "State arts funding panel", "prompt": "The panel is split on renewing a long-standing youth orchestra's operational grant after a weak acquittal. The chair is impatient, one assessor declares a past board connection with the applicant but keeps speaking, and another interjects. Organise the debate into its questions and recommend renewal for one year, with quarterly reporting and an independent financial review as conditions.", "seconds": 180}]}, {"id": "gf-socratic", "examples": [{"title": "Flexible on the way out", "room": "Principal's office", "prompt": "The Principal proposes making the specialist music program 'more flexible' by letting students drop ensemble in Year 9 if they choose another elective. The draft policy is on the table and she wants his support.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Can I ask a genuine question before we go further? | When we say *flexible*, what do we mean? I think we mean that a student can change direction without being penalised, and I want to check that's what you have in mind.", "note": "He supplies a charitable definition and asks her to confirm it. Offering the best version of the term, rather than demanding one, keeps the exchange collaborative with a senior colleague."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "Because if that's the meaning, how would it apply to a Year 9 cellist who drops ensemble in Term 1 to try Media? In Year 10 she wants to come back. The orchestra has moved on a year of repertoire without her, and there's no lesson slot left for her.", "note": "A single, specific, plausible student. The concrete detail does the work that an argument would otherwise have to do, and it is posed as a question, so she must reason through it herself."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "So I'm a little stuck. || If flexible means she can change direction without penalty, the policy gives her one change, and the penalty arrives the moment she wants to change back. It's flexible on the way *out*, and rigid on the way *in*.", "note": "'I'm a little stuck' places the difficulty in himself rather than in her policy. The closing antithesis, out and in, compresses the tension into a line she will remember."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "How would we need to put it so it covers both directions? | Perhaps flexibility means a student can leave and return without losing her place. If that's the definition, the practical form isn't open exit; it's a one-semester leave option, with her lesson slot held and her re-entry guaranteed. I'd be glad to draft that for you.", "note": "He asks for the revision first, then offers one tentatively with 'perhaps'. The proposal arrives as the practical form of a definition she has just accepted, and the offer to draft keeps her in authority."}], "weak": {"text": "I'm not sure about this, to be honest. I think it could really damage the program. Once kids drop out of ensemble in Year 9 they never come back, in my experience, and then we lose numbers in senior school, and the whole thing starts to unravel. I understand you want to give students choice, and that's fair enough, but the specialist program is supposed to be a commitment. Families sign up for three years. Could we maybe leave it as it is for now and look at it again next year?", "why": ["He asserts a conclusion at once, which invites her to defend the policy rather than examine it.", "No key term is defined; the strong version makes 'flexible' the hinge of the whole exchange.", "'Kids never come back' is a generalisation where the strong version used one specific cellist.", "It asks for delay instead of letting a revised definition produce a better policy."]}}, {"title": "What talent measures", "room": "Conservatorium scholarship committee", "prompt": "A conservatorium and school partnership committee is about to rank scholarship applicants by 'talent', measured by the AMEB grade attained by age fourteen. Dijon sits on the committee with faculty staff.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Can I ask something I'm genuinely unsure about? | What do we mean by *talent* in this criterion? I think we're using it to mean the capacity to become an outstanding musician, rather than what someone has achieved so far. Is that fair?", "note": "The distinction between capacity and attainment is planted inside the question. Faculty staff will accept it readily, because it is how they speak about talent themselves."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "Then how would it apply to two students I could describe? One has Grade 8 at fourteen after nine years of private lessons. The other has Grade 5 after two years, started at twelve, and is the only student in her school who can play by ear in three keys. Which of them has more of what we've just defined?", "note": "A paired case is the sharpest Socratic instrument: it forces a comparison the measure cannot make. Ending on 'what we've just defined' binds the room to its own words."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "Because I find I can't answer it with the measure we've chosen. || If talent is capacity, the grade at fourteen measures *opportunity* as much as capacity, and on our own definition the second student may have more of it. Yet our rubric would rank her well below.", "note": "He states the incoherence as his own difficulty, not the committee's error. Naming the hidden variable, opportunity, is the moment of aporia the method aims for."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "How would we need to word the criterion so it measures what we mean? | Perhaps something like rate of progress relative to time and access, heard in a short lesson rather than read off a certificate. If the committee agrees, we keep the grade as context and add a twenty-minute trial lesson with a faculty member, scored for responsiveness. I'd be happy to draft the rubric.", "note": "The revision is offered as a wording question first, which is an academic room's natural territory. The practical mechanism, a trial lesson, uses the faculty's own expertise, so it reads as their solution."}], "weak": {"text": "I just want to raise a concern about using grades. I think it really disadvantages kids from public schools who can't afford private lessons. In my school I see lots of very talented students who don't have high grades because they started late. It's an equity issue. I know grades are an easy measure, and I understand why we use them, but they don't really show potential. Could we maybe think about adding something else? I'm not sure what exactly. Maybe an interview, or teacher references?", "why": ["It opens with a concern and a charge of inequity, which puts faculty on the defensive.", "'Talent' is never defined, so there is no shared standard against which the measure can fail.", "General claims about 'lots of students' replace a single paired case the room must answer.", "The alternative is a list of guesses; the strong version derives a mechanism from the agreed definition."]}}, {"title": "Who counts as community", "room": "Arts funding panel", "prompt": "A panel's guidelines prioritise 'community benefit'. The panel is about to downgrade a free school-based jazz workshop series in four outer-suburban public schools because it serves 'only students'.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Can I ask a genuine question about the criterion? | What do we mean by *community benefit*? I think we've been reading it as benefit to the general public, people beyond the applicant's own organisation. Is that the panel's reading?", "note": "He states the panel's implicit reading accurately and neutrally. A definition the room recognises as its own cannot later be dismissed as a straw man."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "If so, how would it apply to this application? The workshops run in four public schools, open to any student, free, and each series ends with a performance that families and neighbours attend. Are those students and families inside the community, or outside it?", "note": "The facts of the application are laid out flatly, then a binary question is posed. Either answer leads to the tension, which is the design of a good elenctic question."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "Because I'm a little stuck. || If they're outside it, then almost no youth project could ever meet the criterion. And if they're inside it, we're about to downgrade this application for the very thing the guideline *asks* for.", "note": "A dilemma with two horns, both uncomfortable. He does not say the panel is wrong; he shows that its reading cannot survive either answer."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "How would we need to put it so the criterion does what it was written to do? | Perhaps community benefit means public access and public outcome, not adult audience. If that's the reading, I'd suggest we score this application on access and outcome, and note the ambiguity for the program officer so the guidelines are clarified next round.", "note": "Appealing to the criterion's purpose, 'what it was written to do', invites a revision without accusing anyone. Referring the wording to the program officer gives the panel a dignified way to change its mind."}], "weak": {"text": "I have to disagree with downgrading this one. Students are part of the community. I think it's a bit unfair to say it only serves students, because the families come to the concerts too, and these are public schools in areas that don't get much. I'd argue this is exactly what community benefit should mean. I know the guidelines are a bit vague. Maybe we could give it the benefit of the doubt? I'd really hate to see it miss out over a technicality.", "why": ["Opening with 'I have to disagree' sets up a contest; the strong version asks the panel to define its own term.", "It asserts that students are community instead of asking the panel to place them.", "The dilemma, which would show the panel's reading cannot hold, is never constructed.", "'Benefit of the doubt' asks for leniency where the strong version produces a principled revised reading."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["Can I ask a genuine question: what do we mean by [term] here?", "Before we vote, could we pin down what [term] means in this context?", "I think we've been using [term] to mean [reading]. Is that right?", "I'm genuinely unsure, so let me ask: when we say [term], do we mean [A] or [B]?", "Could someone help me with the word [term]? I want to be sure we all mean the same thing.", "What would count as [term], if we saw it?", "If we had to define [term] in one sentence for the minutes, what would it be?", "I've heard [term] used in three ways this morning. Which one is the policy relying on?", "Is [term] here a description of the outcome, or of the process?", "Before I say anything else, can I check what we mean by [term]?"]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["So how would that apply to a case like this one: [case]?", "Let me test it on a student I can picture: [case].", "If that's the definition, where would [specific case] sit?", "Take [case]. Does it meet the definition we've just given?", "How would that work for [the person at the edge of the policy]?", "Could we try it on an awkward case? [case].", "What would that definition say about [case from last year]?", "Suppose [scenario]. Is that [term], on our reading?", "Which of these two would count as more [term]: [A] or [B]?", "I'm thinking of [a real situation, anonymised]. How would we classify it?"]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["Then I'm a little stuck, because those two things don't sit together.", "So on our own definition [case] qualifies, and yet the policy would exclude it.", "I can't make those fit. || Can anyone else?", "If [definition] is right, then [proposal] seems to work against it.", "That's the part I can't reconcile: we want [A], and the measure gives us [B].", "It seems we're about to [action] in the name of [term], and [term] points the other way.", "So either the definition is wrong, or the policy is. I'm not sure which.", "I may be missing something, but on that reading [case] is the strongest example, not the weakest.", "That leaves us with a puzzle, and I think it's a useful one.", "The difficulty is that the policy is [term] in one direction and the opposite in the other."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["How would we need to put it so that it covers both?", "What wording would let the definition include [case] as well?", "Perhaps [term] means [revised definition]. Would the committee accept that?", "If we agree with that, then the practical form is [proposal].", "So the fix isn't to [old action]; it's to [new action].", "Could we adopt that as our working definition and test the policy against it?", "If that's where we've landed, I'd be glad to draft it.", "Would anyone put it better? I'd rather the wording were the room's.", "On that definition, I think the recommendation becomes [proposal].", "Shall we note the revised meaning in the minutes, so the policy is read against it?"]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Department meeting", "prompt": "A colleague wants to scrap practice diaries because they 'don't show real practice'. Ask what real practice means, test the definition against a student who logs forty minutes of playing through pieces, and land on a better evidence method: a short weekly recording of one targeted passage.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 1, "room": "Parent music support group", "prompt": "The parent group wants to restrict the camp subsidy to 'committed' families, meaning those who volunteer at events. Use questions to examine what commitment means, test it against a single parent who works weekend shifts, and propose that the subsidy be needs-based with volunteering encouraged separately.", "seconds": 90}, {"level": 2, "room": "Principal's office", "prompt": "The Principal wants all department communications to families to be 'consistent', which in the draft means one template and approval through the front office, adding a week to every notice. Use questions to establish what consistency is for, test it against an urgent rehearsal change, and propose a shared template with approval required only for new events.", "seconds": 120}, {"level": 2, "room": "Conservatorium partnership committee", "prompt": "A partnership committee wants to choose the school ensembles invited to perform at the conservatorium by 'standard', measured by festival placings. Ask what standard means, test it against a strong ensemble from a school that cannot afford to enter festivals, and propose invitation by recorded submission against published criteria.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 3, "room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee", "prompt": "A senior officer says the course must be 'evidence-based' before it goes back to the Board, and two members use the phrase to dismiss practitioner experience. The officer cuts in quickly and dislikes being questioned. Use the Socratic sequence to establish what counts as evidence for a course of this kind, and propose a submission that pairs published research with documented classroom outcomes from pilot schools.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "School council", "prompt": "A council member with a legal background argues that the music program's audition entry is 'discriminatory' and wants it scrapped. The room is uneasy, the member interrupts, and the Principal is watching. Use questions to separate selection on a relevant criterion from unfair exclusion, without making legal claims yourself, and propose an independent review of the audition's accessibility for students without private tuition.", "seconds": 200}]}, {"id": "gf-burke", "examples": [{"title": "Carrying the department's view", "room": "Principal's office", "prompt": "The music staff have asked Dijon, as TIC, to tell the Principal they reject moving Year 11 and 12 ensembles to after school. He shares some of their concerns but thinks the principle is right.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Before anything else, I want you to have the department's view clearly, because they asked me to bring it and they're entitled to have it heard in full. | They're opposed to moving senior ensembles after school, and their reasons are serious: travel, part-time jobs, and staff who already give two evenings a week.", "note": "He reports the staff position completely, with its reasons, before any view of his own. Fair representation is what earns the right to depart from it."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "But I don't think my job as TIC is only to carry their vote into this room. You need my *judgement* as well, and I owe them honesty about that too.", "note": "The pivot word is 'judgement', placed late in the sentence for weight. 'I owe them honesty' turns apparent disloyalty into a form of respect for his staff."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "And the program serves every student in it, including the Year 7s who can't get a rehearsal room before school because the seniors have it, not only the staff or the seniors.", "note": "Burke's appeal to the whole made concrete: a specific group with no voice in the room. It reframes the issue from staff convenience to the program's interest."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "So here's my honest view. || The staff are right about the evenings, and I think wrong about the principle. Moving one senior ensemble frees the morning slot for two junior groups. My proposal: move Senior Orchestra only, to Tuesday until five, cap each teacher at one evening a week, and review it at the end of Term 2 with attendance data.", "note": "He divides the staff position precisely, conceding the part that is right. The proposal is narrow, one ensemble, with a cap and a dated review, which makes it hard to refuse."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "I'll tell the department exactly what I've said here, including where I disagreed with them. If the review shows it isn't working, that's on *me*, and I'll bring it back.", "note": "Accountability runs in both directions: to the staff he represents and to the Principal. Promising to report the disagreement openly is what distinguishes judgement from manoeuvre."}], "weak": {"text": "So I've spoken to the team and they're pretty unhappy about the after-school idea. There's a lot of concern about workload and about the kids with jobs. To be honest I can see some merit in it myself, because the mornings are a squeeze, but I don't really want to be seen to go against them. Maybe we could look at it again later in the year, or try a smaller version? I'm not sure. I just think if we push it now there'll be a lot of resistance.", "why": ["The staff view is summarised loosely, without the specific reasons that would honour it.", "He admits his own view and then withdraws it to avoid being 'seen to go against them'; the strong version claims a duty of judgement.", "No appeal is made to the students the current arrangement disadvantages.", "'A smaller version' replaces a defined proposal, and no one takes responsibility for the outcome."]}}, {"title": "Not the tour they asked for", "room": "Parent music support group (Dijon as school representative)", "prompt": "Parents have fundraised for three years and expect Dijon to endorse an international tour. He believes the cost would quietly exclude families and that a domestic tour is the right choice for next year.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "I know how much this group has done for the tour fund, and how many of you have been asking for an international trip for three years. | That isn't a small thing, and I don't take it lightly.", "note": "Specific acknowledgement, three years and the fund itself, rather than general thanks. The plain negative 'I don't take it lightly' is more convincing to a parent room than an effusive positive."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "But you've asked me here as the school's representative, and I don't think I'm here to report back what you'd like to hear. I think you'd want my *judgement*, even where it differs from yours.", "note": "He attributes the desire for honest judgement to the parents themselves. This lets him decline instruction while flattering their good sense rather than lecturing them."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "And the tour has to serve every family in the program, including the ones who would never say out loud that a trip at that price is out of reach.", "note": "The whole includes the silent. Naming families who cannot speak up shifts the discussion from preference to fairness without pointing at anyone in the room."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "So my honest judgement is this. || An international tour at that price would exclude some of our players, and we would never know exactly who, because they'd simply not put their hand up. My proposal: a domestic tour next year, to Melbourne, with a festival performance and a masterclass, at a fraction of the cost, and the international trip planned for two years' time, with a payment plan opened well ahead.", "note": "The reason is psychological and precise: exclusion that is invisible. The proposal does not cancel the parents' goal; it defers and funds it, which honours their three years of work."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "If I've misjudged it, and families tell us cost isn't the barrier, I'll come back to this group and say so. I'd rather be wrong in *that* direction.", "note": "A specific condition under which he will reverse, plus a statement of which error he prefers. It shows the judgement is held honestly, not stubbornly."}], "weak": {"text": "Thanks so much, everyone, for all the fundraising, it's been amazing. I know a lot of you are really keen on the overseas trip. I guess I just have a few reservations. It's quite expensive, and I worry some families might struggle, although obviously I don't know everyone's situation. I'm not saying no. Maybe we could look at somewhere closer as well, as an option, and see what people prefer? We could do a survey. Whatever the group decides, the school will support it.", "why": ["The thanks are generic; the strong version names the three years and the fund.", "'A few reservations' replaces an open statement of judgement and the reason for it.", "Promising to support 'whatever the group decides' surrenders the judgement he was there to give.", "There is no specific alternative, no timeline for the international trip, and no condition under which he would change his view."]}}, {"title": "Nominated, not instructed", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "prompt": "Dijon was nominated to the committee by the subject association, whose members want the prescribed list of set works kept unchanged. He believes a broader list with school choice is better for students across the state.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "I was nominated to this committee by the subject association, and I want to say plainly that its members hold a clear view: they want the set-works list kept as it is. | Many of them have built years of teaching around those works, and that deserves respect.", "note": "He declares his constituency and its view before anything else, which is both honest and procedurally sound in a committee that values transparency about representation."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "But I don't believe I'm here as a delegate carrying a vote. I think the association put me here to bring my *judgement* to the committee, and they'd expect nothing less.", "note": "The delegate and representative distinction is Burke's own, stated in modern terms. 'They'd expect nothing less' frames the association as principled, so he departs from its view without disparaging it."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "And this committee serves every student who takes the course in Western Australia, in every kind of school, not any single group within it, including the one that nominated me.", "note": "The phrase 'including the one that nominated me' is the critical admission. Naming his own constituency as a part, not the whole, is what makes the principle credible."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "So here is my judgement. || A short fixed list favours schools that have taught those works for a decade and have the resources to match. My proposal is a prescribed list of twelve, from which schools choose four, with SCSA publishing core analytical notes on each, so that no school starts from nothing.", "note": "The reason is stated as a structural effect, not an accusation. The published notes answer the association's likely workload objection inside the proposal itself."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "I'll report that back to the association exactly as I've said it here. If members think I've got it wrong, they're entitled to tell me, and to *nominate* someone else.", "note": "The strongest form of accountability is to name the sanction. It echoes Burke's own acceptance of the cost, and it reassures the committee that his view is not a private agenda."}], "weak": {"text": "I should say the association is very keen to keep the list as it is, and I've been asked to pass that on. A lot of teachers have resources built up around those works. Personally, and this isn't the association's view, I do wonder whether a bit more choice might be good, especially for schools that haven't taught them before. But I don't want to go against the members. Maybe it's something we could look at in the next review cycle, once people have had more time to get used to the idea.", "why": ["He reports the association's view but never explains why he owes the committee more than that.", "His own view is marked off as 'personal' and then abandoned rather than given as judgement.", "There is no appeal to the interest of every student in the state.", "The proposal is deferred to a future cycle, and he accepts no accountability for any position."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["I take the view of the people I represent very seriously, and I want to say that first.", "I know a great deal of work has gone into this, and I respect it.", "The [staff / parents / members] asked me to bring their view, and I want it heard in full.", "Their reasons are serious: [reason one] and [reason two].", "I don't take it lightly that [group] feels strongly about this.", "Let me state their position as fairly as I can, before I say anything of my own.", "I understand why [the president / the Principal] wants this settled tonight.", "That's a legitimate view, held by people whose judgement I trust.", "I've heard this from many colleagues, and I want the room to know it's widely held.", "Nothing I'm about to say is a criticism of the people who hold that view."]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["But I don't think I'm here to carry a vote; I think I'm here to bring my judgement.", "I was asked to sit here to think, not only to report.", "I owe [them] my honest view, even where it differs from theirs.", "If I simply passed on instructions, I don't think I'd be doing the job I was given.", "A representative who only relays a position isn't much use to a committee.", "My role is to deliberate with you, and I'd be letting [group] down if I didn't.", "I think this body is entitled to my judgement as well as my report.", "I'd rather be useful to this board than comfortable.", "Approving this as a formality would be easy, and I don't think it would be right.", "I'm here as [Treasurer / a member], and that comes with a duty to scrutinise."]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["This body serves the whole, not any one part of it, including mine.", "We're here for every [student / school / member], not only the ones in this room.", "The decision has to work for [every family], including the ones who don't speak up.", "The group I represent is one part of a larger whole, and the whole has to come first.", "I think we have to ask what serves [the program / the course] as a whole.", "This committee exists to serve [the state / the school], not any sector within it.", "The people who'll live with this decision aren't all represented here.", "If we decide for one part, the whole pays for it later.", "Our responsibility runs to [the association's members], not to any committee within it.", "That's the interest I think we're here to protect."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["So here is my honest judgement, and why.", "My view is this: [view]. ||", "Here's where I've landed, and the reasons are [reason one] and [reason two].", "I think [group] is right about [X] and, I'd respectfully say, wrong about [Y].", "My proposal is [specific proposal], and here's what it protects.", "On the figures, [the assumption] doesn't hold, because [reason].", "I'd support it, with one change: [change].", "I can't support it as it stands, but I could support [alternative].", "My judgement is that [decision], and I'd put that as a motion if the chair is willing.", "That isn't the view I was sent with, and I want to be open about that."]}, {"tag": 4, "lines": ["And if I've got it wrong, I'll answer for that.", "I'll report back to [group] exactly what I've said here.", "If the review shows I was wrong, I'll be the first to say so.", "I'd rather answer for [caution] than for [the alternative risk].", "That's on me, and I'll bring it back to this table if it doesn't work.", "If [group] wants a different representative for that, they're entitled to one.", "I'm happy for the minutes to record my view, and my reasons.", "Hold me to it at the next meeting.", "I'll put my name to the recommendation and stand behind it.", "If I'm wrong, the cost should fall on my judgement, not on [the students / the festival]."]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Department meeting", "prompt": "Your instrumental staff want you, as TIC, to refuse a request from the Principal to host a feeder primary band day, because the proposed date lands in exam week. You think the day is valuable and the date can move. Honour their concern, give your judgement, and propose hosting it the week after exams with Year 10 student leaders running the sectionals.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 1, "room": "Parent music support group", "prompt": "The parent committee expects you, as the staff representative, to endorse buying a new marquee for outdoor concerts. You think an enclosed trailer for instrument transport matters more, because instruments are being damaged in private cars. Speak to the committee and propose the trailer this year, the marquee next.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 2, "room": "School council (Dijon in the chair)", "prompt": "Parent members want council to write formally to the Department opposing a system-wide change to student reporting, and expect you as chair to sign tonight. You think council owes it to the school to seek the staff and community view first. Speak to council and propose a short survey and a special meeting within three weeks.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 2, "room": "University course advisory committee", "prompt": "You represent schools on a university course advisory committee. School heads of music want you to oppose a new compulsory music technology unit because their students lack equipment. You think the unit is right. Honour their concern, give your judgement, and propose an equipment loan scheme funded by the faculty for the first two years.", "seconds": 120}, {"level": 3, "room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee", "prompt": "A sector colleague tells the committee, in front of senior officers, that 'teachers expect' it to reject any change to the timing of practical exams. You were nominated by teachers and you disagree. One senior officer is sceptical of you, and the colleague interrupts twice. Give your judgement openly and propose a one-year pilot of the new timing in volunteer schools, with teacher feedback reported to the committee.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "Festival association board (Dijon as Treasurer)", "prompt": "The president asks the board to approve, without discussion, a large payment to a production company owned by a relative of a fellow director. Members look away and the president is irritated by questions. Honour the work behind the arrangement, give your judgement on the conflict of interest, and propose that the director declare the interest and leave the room, and that the board obtain a second quote before approving.", "seconds": 200}]}, {"id": "gf-appellate", "examples": [{"title": "Keeping the Saturday places", "room": "Conservatorium partnership committee", "prompt": "The conservatorium proposes ending the free Saturday ensemble places it offers public-school students, because the program is over budget. He asks the committee to keep the places for one more year on a revised cost model.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "What I'm asking the committee to do is one specific thing: to retain the twelve public-school places in the Saturday program for *2027*, on a revised cost model.", "note": "The order comes first and arrives already bounded by number, year and condition. A limited ask sounds reasonable before a word of argument is heard, and the committee listens to everything after it as support for a known request."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "I accept, without reservation, that the program is over budget, and that the conservatorium can't be asked to carry that indefinitely. The finance paper is *right* on the numbers.", "note": "He concedes the opponent's evidence, not merely the general difficulty, and names the document by title. Agreeing with their figures removes any need for them to defend those figures, so their attention is free for his distinction."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "But the overrun doesn't come from the public-school places. || It comes from tutor costs in the advanced stream, which those twelve students don't *attend*. Cutting the places removes the program's purpose and leaves most of the cost where it is.", "note": "The distinction is factual and locates the cost precisely, which is how counsel distinguishes an authority on its facts. The held pause before the second sentence lets the concession stand on its own, so it reads as sincere rather than as a set-up."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "And the reason to keep them is the *pipeline*. These are the students who audition for the conservatorium in Year 12, from schools with no other route in. If the places go, the conservatorium narrows its own intake to families who can pay, which is the opposite of what this partnership was founded to do.", "note": "One ground, argued in the other party's interest: their intake and their founding purpose. An appeal to a body's own stated aims is far harder to refuse than an appeal to the speaker's preferences."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "So the order I'm asking for is this: that the committee retains the twelve places for 2027, and that the program coordinator brings a revised tutor budget for the advanced stream to the *March* meeting.", "note": "The return repeats the opening order almost word for word, then adds an owner and a date. The repetition signals the end of the argument, and the added detail hands the minute-taker a sentence ready to copy."}], "weak": {"text": "Thanks, look, I just wanted to say a few things about the Saturday program. I know the budget is a real issue and I'm not across all the numbers, but I do think it's really important for our kids, and a lot of them have gone on to do amazing things. I suppose I'm a bit worried that if we cut the places we'd be sending the wrong message about access, and access is something we all care about. Maybe there's a way we could look at it differently, or perhaps revisit it later in the year? I don't know, I just think it would be a shame to lose it.", "why": ["There is no order: the committee never hears what decision is sought, where the strong version states it in the first sentence.", "Saying 'I'm not across the numbers' concedes his own competence, not a point; the strong version concedes the finance paper's figures exactly.", "The cost argument is never met; the strong version distinguishes it by showing where the overrun actually sits.", "It ends on a feeling and a deferral rather than a minutable order with an owner and a date."]}}, {"title": "A variation, not a new grant", "room": "Arts funding panel", "prompt": "The festival's guest artist has withdrawn. He asks the panel to approve a variation moving the artist's fee to regional workshops; the panel officer has noted that variations after commencement are rarely approved.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "What I'm asking the panel to approve is a *single* variation: moving the guest artist fee in our grant to three regional workshops, within the same budget and the same acquittal date.", "note": "He uses the panel's own vocabulary, 'variation' and 'acquittal', which tells a funding body that the request fits its processes. Stating what does not change, budget and date, shrinks the decision before it is argued."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "I accept that variations after a project has begun are rarely approved, and for good *reason*. A panel that approves changes freely loses the ability to fund what it actually assessed.", "note": "He concedes the rule and then states its rationale better than the officer did. Articulating the purpose of the rule is the strongest form of concession, and it sets up the distinction, which will turn on that purpose."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "But this variation doesn't change what you *assessed*. || The application was scored on regional reach, and the guest artist was the means, not the outcome. The workshops deliver the outcome you funded, to more students, for the same money.", "note": "Classic distinction by purpose: the rule protects what was assessed, and what was assessed is untouched. The means and outcome antithesis is short enough to be repeated in the panel's deliberation without him."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "The reason to approve it is that the alternative is worse for the *panel*. If the variation is refused, the fee is returned unspent, the regional component disappears, and the acquittal reports a project that did less than you paid for.", "note": "The ground is framed as the decision-maker's own risk, not the applicant's need. Three short consequences in sequence give the refusal a concrete cost the panel would have to own."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "So the variation I'm asking for is this: that the panel approves reallocating the guest artist fee to three regional workshops, with the acquittal date *unchanged*, and a revised schedule supplied within a fortnight.", "note": "The order returns in the same terms, with one undertaking added that costs the panel nothing. Offering a deliverable with a deadline makes approval look like good administration rather than leniency."}], "weak": {"text": "Hi everyone, thank you so much for having us back, we really appreciate the panel's support over the years. As you know, our guest artist unfortunately had to pull out, which was really disappointing for everyone. We've been thinking about what we could do instead and we had a few ideas, regional workshops being one of them, which we think could be really valuable. I understand there might be some rules around changing things, and we totally respect that. We'd just be really grateful if the panel could consider being a bit flexible in this case, given the circumstances, because the students would really benefit.", "why": ["It opens with thanks that carry no content, where the strong version opens with the exact variation sought.", "It asks for 'flexibility' rather than a defined decision the panel could approve.", "It acknowledges 'some rules' but never distinguishes them; the strong version shows the rule's purpose is untouched.", "The ground is sympathy, not the panel's own assessment criteria and acquittal risk."]}}, {"title": "Amending the tour subsidy", "room": "Music parents' association committee", "prompt": "Last month the parent committee replaced a discretionary tour subsidy with a flat amount per student. He asks it to amend the resolution so families who cannot pay at all are not excluded.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "What I'm asking the committee to do is one specific thing: to *amend* last month's resolution so that a hardship pool sits alongside the flat subsidy.", "note": "Naming the procedural form, an amendment rather than a reversal, tells the committee its earlier decision survives. People who voted for the flat subsidy can support him without admitting error."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "I accept, without reservation, that the flat subsidy is *fairer* than what it replaced. The old system was opaque, and families were right to complain about it.", "note": "He concedes the merit of the very decision he is asking them to amend, and validates the complaint that produced it. The room relaxes because it is being agreed with before it is asked anything."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "But the flat subsidy solves a fairness problem and creates an *access* one. || For most families, the same amount for every child is fair. For the few families who couldn't send a child at all, the same amount is the same as nothing.", "note": "The distinction is a balanced antithesis, fairness problem and access problem, which reframes the issue without attacking the resolution. The final clause turns on repetition of 'the same', a plain device that lands harder than any adjective."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "And the reason to amend it now is *timing*. Tour deposits are due in five weeks. If we wait until the next meeting, those families will already have told their children no, and a pool we create later will fund an empty seat.", "note": "The single ground is urgency, made concrete with a date and an image. 'Fund an empty seat' is the kind of short, physical phrase a parent committee repeats when it decides."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "So the amendment I'm asking for is this: that ten per cent of the tour subsidy is set aside as a hardship pool, allocated *confidentially* by the Principal, with the flat subsidy otherwise unchanged.", "note": "The close is drafted as minutable text: proportion, mechanism, decision-maker and what stays the same. 'Confidentially' answers the unspoken worry about families being identified, without making a speech about it."}], "weak": {"text": "I know we already voted on this, and I'm not trying to reopen anything, but I've had a few conversations with parents and I think there might be some unintended consequences. Fairness is obviously really important and I think everyone agrees with that. But there are some families who are really doing it tough at the moment, and it would be awful if anyone missed out. I wonder if there's something we could do, maybe a fund or something, I'm not sure exactly how it would work. Anyway, just something to think about before the deposits are due.", "why": ["It apologises for reopening the decision instead of naming the amendment it seeks.", "Its concession is a generality about fairness; the strong version concedes that the specific resolution was an improvement.", "The proposal is buried and unformed ('a fund or something'), with no proportion, mechanism or decision-maker.", "The deadline, which is the real ground, appears as an afterthought in the last sentence."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["What I'm asking the committee to do is one specific thing: [the order].", "I'm asking for a single decision today: [the decision].", "The decision I'm seeking is narrow: [the order], and nothing more.", "Let me say at the outset what I'm asking for: [the order].", "There's one thing I'd like the board to decide: [the order], for [period].", "My request is that the council [action], by [date].", "I'll be brief, and I'll start with the ask: [the order].", "What I'd like minuted, by the end of this item, is [the order].", "I'm not asking the panel to revisit its whole decision, only [the narrow point].", "The order I'd seek is limited to [scope]: [the order]."]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["I accept, without reservation, that [the strongest objection].", "[The executive officer] is right that [point], and I don't contest it.", "Let me concede the point at once: [point].", "The finance paper is correct on [the figures].", "There's no answer to [the objection] as a general rule, and I won't pretend there is.", "That's a fair criticism, and our first draft deserved it.", "If I were in the Board's position, I'd have raised [the concern] too.", "I agree that [principle] matters, and nothing I'm asking for undermines it.", "The rule is clear, and [the school] didn't meet it.", "I'll grant the premise entirely: [premise]."]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["But this case is different in one material respect: [difference].", "But [the objection] doesn't reach this case, because [reason].", "The concern is about [X]; what I'm asking for concerns [Y].", "That's true of [the general class]; it isn't true of [this instance].", "The Board's objection was confined to [component], and [component] can be fixed.", "The rule exists to prevent [mischief]. Nothing like that happened here.", "The cost sits in [place], not in [the thing I'm defending].", "If this were [the feared case], I'd agree. It isn't, because [fact].", "What distinguishes this is [fact], which none of the earlier cases had.", "The precedent assumed [condition]. Here, [condition] doesn't hold."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["And the reason the committee should make this decision is this: [ground].", "The single strongest reason to act is [ground].", "And the reason to act now is [timing].", "The reason to approve it is that the alternative is worse for [the body].", "What decides it, I think, is [ground].", "This serves the [body]'s own purpose, which is [stated purpose].", "If we don't, [concrete consequence] by [date].", "Put simply, the cost of [the order] is small against the cost of refusing it.", "The people who would carry this, [group], have told us they're ready.", "The reason is the students: [specific effect on them]."]}, {"tag": 4, "lines": ["So the order I'm asking for is this: [order, owner, date].", "So, for the minutes: that [body] [action], with [owner] reporting by [date].", "To return to where I began: [the order, in the same words].", "The motion I'd put, then, is that [wording].", "So the decision is simply [the order], and I'd ask the committee to make it today.", "If it helps the secretary, the words would be: [wording].", "So I'd ask the council to [order], and to review it at [meeting].", "That's the whole of the request: [the order], nothing broader.", "So the variation sought is [variation], with [condition] unchanged.", "I'll leave it there: [the order], reported to [the next meeting]."]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Department meeting", "prompt": "The Year 10 recital is booked for Week 7 of Term 4, which now clashes with Year 10 exams. Ask colleagues to move it to Week 5. Concede that Week 7 is traditional and that the hall booking was hard to get; distinguish on the exam clash; propose that you rebook the hall and notify families by Friday.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 1, "room": "Festival association board", "prompt": "A regional school's entry arrived two days late because of a postal failure they can document. Ask the board to accept it without the late fee. Concede the deadline is clear and applies to everyone; distinguish on the documented cause; propose that the board accepts the entry and adds an online submission option for next year.", "seconds": 60}, {"level": 2, "room": "School council", "prompt": "Council has endorsed converting the main music room into a staff workroom on Monday afternoons. Ask council to vary the decision. Concede that staff need the space; distinguish that Monday is the only slot for the senior ensemble; propose a timetable swap with the drama studio, which the drama staff have agreed to, reported back by the business manager next meeting.", "seconds": 135}, {"level": 2, "room": "University academic committee", "prompt": "The committee is about to reaffirm that all entry auditions must be live. Ask it to accept a recorded Year 12 performance for regional applicants only. Concede that live auditions are more reliable; distinguish on the cost and distance for regional families; propose a one-year trial with a live callback for borderline cases.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 3, "room": "SCSA Board subcommittee", "prompt": "You are presenting the resubmitted course. A Board member interrupts within a minute to say that performance assessment is inherently unreliable and no model can fix it. Hold your structure: concede the reliability concern without retreat, distinguish moderated assessment from the original draft, and ask for an order approving the course for consultation with external sampling of a fixed proportion of performances.", "seconds": 210}, {"level": 3, "room": "Principal's office (Associate Principal and business manager present)", "prompt": "Instrumental tuition hours are to be cut by four a week to meet a school-wide deficit. Ask for the hours to be restored. The business manager will interrupt with the deficit figure. Concede the deficit fully; distinguish that the tuition hours are partly funded by hire fees that would also be lost; propose restoring the hours with a hire-fee review reported to the executive in Term 1.", "seconds": 180}]}, {"id": "gf-count", "examples": [{"title": "Improvisation as an option", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "prompt": "He wants the committee to recommend improvisation as an optional alternative to the Year 12 composition task. There are fourteen members, and two university musicologists are likely sceptics.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "(Before) Fourteen members. Three teachers will back it outright. The two musicologists are wary; they'll see improvisation as *soft* on theory. The assessment officer will ask about marking. The rest are neutral, and will follow whoever sounds most certain.", "note": "He maps each member by position and by reason, because the reason predicts what will move them. Grouping the neutrals honestly, as followers of certainty, tells him the live contest is with three people, not fourteen."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "(Before) I rang the musicologists first, *separately*. One needed the task to require a written analysis of what the student played. The other wanted it limited to students with a declared jazz or contemporary focus. The assessment officer needed exemplars before any vote.", "note": "He starts with the hardest votes and speaks to them apart, so neither performs scepticism for the other. The question he asks is what they need, not whether they agree, which produces specific conditions he can work with."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "(Before) So the proposal changed. Improvisation becomes an *option*, not a replacement, with a written commentary attached, and I'll bring three marked exemplars. The second restriction I couldn't accept, and I told her so, and why.", "note": "Every adjustment answers a named need, which is why the proposal now belongs partly to its critics. Declining one request openly, before the meeting, keeps the process honest and protects him from the charge of a stitch-up."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "(Before) Three teachers firm, one musicologist now firm, the officer leaning, one opposed, eight neutral who've seen it and raised *nothing*. That's enough to put it, and the opposed member has been heard, not ambushed.", "note": "The count separates firm from leaning and treats consulted silence as information. His test for proceeding is twofold: the numbers, and whether anyone will meet the proposal for the first time in the room."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "(In the room) The assessment officer's request for exemplars has made this far *stronger*, and they're in the papers. | The written commentary was a colleague's idea, and it answers the theory concern better than I could have. || I'd simply move that the committee recommends improvisation as an optional Year 12 task, with commentary, for consultation next term.", "note": "The live contribution is three sentences and gives the credit away, which converts the critics into co-authors in front of the room. The held pause before the motion separates gratitude from the ask, and the motion is short enough to minute verbatim."}], "weak": {"text": "I've been thinking a lot about improvisation, and I really think it's time we had a conversation about it as a committee. In jazz, which is my world, improvisation is basically composition in real time, so it's not really that different. I know some people might have concerns about assessment and theory, and that's totally fair, I'm sure we can work through all of that. I'd love to hear what everyone thinks today, and then maybe we could put something together for a future meeting? I just think it's something students really respond to, and we'd be missing an opportunity.", "why": ["The committee hears the idea for the first time in the room, so the sceptics defer; the strong version had already met each of them.", "Objections are waved at ('I'm sure we can work through all that') instead of answered in advance with commentary and exemplars.", "He makes the long advocacy speech himself, where the strong version is three sentences that credit others.", "With no count there is nothing to decide, so it ends in 'a future meeting' rather than a motion."]}}, {"title": "A sliding scale for instrument hire", "room": "School council (he chairs)", "prompt": "He wants council to endorse a sliding scale for instrumental hire fees. There are ten members: the Principal, two staff representatives, five parents and two community members.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "(Before) Ten members. The Principal supports it in principle but won't get ahead of the *business manager*. Both staff representatives are for. Of the five parents, two are for, one is worried about intrusive means-testing, and two haven't said. The community members usually follow the Principal.", "note": "The map records influence as well as position: the Principal's vote sits behind someone who is not even a member. Seeing that chain tells him the most important conversation is with a person outside the room."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "(Before) I met the business manager *first*, because the Principal's vote sits behind hers. She needed it revenue-neutral. The worried parent needed families never to disclose income to the school. The two quiet parents I simply asked, and one had a good question about siblings.", "note": "Order of conversations follows the chain of influence. Simply asking the quiet members is the point Caro's Johnson understood best: people who are consulted rarely oppose, and they often improve the proposal."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "(Before) So the scale is based on concession card status, which the school already holds, so nobody discloses *anything* new. A sibling discount goes in. The top band rises slightly to keep it neutral, and the business manager has checked the arithmetic.", "note": "Each change maps to one person's need, and the verification is borrowed from the sceptic herself. A figure checked by the business manager carries authority that the chair's own figure would not."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "(Before) The Principal is now firm, so the community members will follow. Eight likely, one leaning, one *unknown*. I'll put it, and as chair I'll ask a parent to move it rather than move it myself.", "note": "He decides not only whether to proceed but who should carry it. A chair who moves his own motion spends his impartiality; a parent mover makes it the council's proposal."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "(In the room) This comes with the business manager's figures, and I'm *grateful* to her. | The sibling discount was a parent's suggestion, and a good one. || I'll ask the member who raised it to move the motion as circulated.", "note": "As chair he advocates nothing; he attributes. The short acknowledgements tell the council the work is done, and handing the motion to a parent lets the room own the decision."}], "weak": {"text": "So the next item is the instrument hire fees. I've put a paper together, which I hope people have had a chance to read. Basically the idea is a sliding scale, so families who can pay more do, and families who are struggling pay less. I haven't run the final numbers past finance yet, but I'm fairly confident it works out. I know there might be some sensitivity around asking families about income, so we'd need to think about that. Does anyone have any thoughts? If people are broadly comfortable, maybe we could vote on it tonight?", "why": ["The business manager and the worried parent first meet the proposal at the table, where the strong version had already heard and met both.", "The figures are unchecked and the disclosure issue is left open, so the obvious objections are still live.", "The chair argues his own paper and then asks to vote without knowing the count.", "Nobody else carries it; the strong version gives the motion to a parent and the credit to the people who shaped it."]}}, {"title": "Booking the rehearsal rooms", "room": "Department meeting", "prompt": "He wants the department to replace informal room claiming with a shared booking calendar. There are six staff, and one long-serving colleague has held the same room on the same afternoon for a decade.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "(Before) Six staff. The two newer teachers want it; they lose rooms every week. The longest-serving teacher has had Room 4 on Tuesdays for a *decade*. One senior colleague dislikes any new software. One is neutral. The Performing Arts coordinator will want to be consulted, not informed.", "note": "The map names the interest behind each position, including the coordinator's need for process rather than outcome. Seeing who wants to be consulted is as important as seeing who will vote."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "(Before) I briefed the coordinator first, before anyone else had the detail. Then the longest-serving teacher, in her room rather than *mine*. What she needed was to keep Tuesdays, which is fair; her ensemble has nowhere else to fit. The colleague who dislikes software needed it to work on paper as well.", "note": "Meeting a colleague on her own ground is a small courtesy with a large effect: it signals that he is asking, not summoning. Calling her need 'fair' in his own planning keeps him from treating her as an obstacle."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "(Before) So standing bookings carry over for any ensemble that rehearses weekly, Tuesdays included. A printed weekly sheet goes on the office door beside the calendar. Only the *unclaimed* slots become bookable.", "note": "The adjustments protect what each person valued and narrow the change to where the problem actually is. A reform that only touches unclaimed slots is hard to oppose, because nobody loses anything they already have."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "(Before) Four firm, one leaning, one neutral, which is fine. I'll raise it, but I'll ask the *coordinator* to introduce it.", "note": "The count is short because the work has been done. Asking the coordinator to introduce it gives the proposal institutional weight and honours the need she expressed."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "(In the room) I'll be brief, because I've spoken to each of you. | The standing bookings were a colleague's point, and they've made this *fairer* to the people who've built ensembles here. || I'd propose we start in Week 1 of Term 1 and review it at the end of the term.", "note": "Opening with 'I've spoken to each of you' is open about the consultation, which disarms any suspicion of a side deal. The review date makes the proposal reversible, which is often the final concession a cautious room needs."}], "weak": {"text": "Just quickly, I wanted to raise the issue of room bookings, because it's been getting a bit chaotic and I know some people have been frustrated. I was thinking we could move to a shared online calendar, which a lot of schools use now, and it would make everything more transparent. I know some people have had their regular rooms for a long time, and I'm not trying to take anything away from anyone, but I do think we need a fairer system overall. Maybe we can all just try it for a bit and see how it goes?", "why": ["The long-serving colleague hears in a staff meeting that her room is in question; the strong version met her first, in her own room.", "'I'm not trying to take anything away' reassures without a mechanism; the strong version carries standing bookings over.", "Nobody was mapped or counted, so he cannot know whether to proceed.", "It ends with 'try it for a bit', with no start date, review point or person introducing it."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["(Before) [Number] votes. Who are they, and where does each one sit today?", "(Before) Firm for: [members]. Firm against: [members]. Everyone else, I don't yet know.", "(Before) [Member] will follow [other member], so the real conversation is with [other member].", "(Before) Where does [member] stand, and more to the point, why?", "(Before) Who would feel slighted if this passed without them being asked?", "(Before) Who carries the room on anything to do with [topic]?", "(Before) Who's online, who's new, and who hasn't spoken on this at all?", "(Before) What did [member] say the last time something like this came up?", "(Before) Whose budget, timetable or reputation does this touch?", "(Before) If I put it tomorrow, who would vote no, and what would they say?"]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["I'd value your view before Thursday. What would you need to see in this?", "Before this goes to the committee, I wanted you to see it first.", "What would make this hard for you to support?", "You've seen more of these than I have. Where would it come unstuck?", "I'm not asking for your vote; I'm asking what's wrong with it.", "Is there anything in this you couldn't live with?", "Who else should I talk to before it goes in the papers?", "If [colleague] raised [concern], would you share it?", "What would you want the minutes to say if this went ahead?", "I'd rather hear the objection now, over coffee, than on Thursday."]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["If I built that in, would it work for you?", "Suppose the cost were neutral. Would that change your view?", "What if [element] became an option rather than a requirement?", "I can do [A], but not [B], and here's why.", "Would a trial for [period], with a review at [meeting], meet your concern?", "I've changed [clause] after our conversation; does the new wording do it?", "If [member] kept [authority], would that answer it?", "Your point about [issue] is now in the paper, with your name on it, if you're happy with that.", "I couldn't accept [request], and I wanted you to hear that from me before the meeting.", "Would it help if [someone else] moved it, rather than me?"]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["(Before) Do I have the numbers, or do I hold it?", "(Before) Firm: [n]. Leaning: [n]. Opposed: [n]. Unknown: [n].", "(Before) That's enough to put it, and everyone opposed has been heard.", "(Before) Not yet. I'll hold it to [the next meeting] and talk to [member] again.", "(Before) If [member] stays opposed, does anyone follow them?", "(Before) Would I rather lose it narrowly or defer it and win it well?", "(Before) [Member] has gone quiet since our conversation. Quiet isn't yes.", "(Before) Who will move it, and who will second it?", "(Before) Will anyone hear this for the first time in the room? Then I'm not ready.", "(Before) If the vote is close, can it be a trial rather than a decision?"]}, {"tag": 4, "lines": ["I think [member] put it well; I'd simply add one thing.", "I've spoken to most of you about this, so I'll be brief.", "[Member]'s point about [issue] has made this a better proposal.", "The paper reflects a number of your suggestions, and I'm grateful for them.", "I'll leave the detail to [member], who knows it better than I do.", "I'd move that we [order], with a review at [meeting].", "I don't think I need to add much to what's in the papers.", "If there are concerns I haven't heard, I'd genuinely like to hear them now.", "I'm happy to second [member]'s motion.", "This is no longer really my proposal; most of the people in this room have shaped it."]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Department meeting (before)", "prompt": "You want a common attendance and lateness policy across all department ensembles. Five staff: two are for, one runs her ensemble entirely her own way, one is new, one is indifferent. Map them aloud, say what you would ask each privately and what you might adjust (for example, letting each director set the consequence while the department sets the record-keeping), count, then give a contribution of no more than three sentences.", "seconds": 90}, {"level": 1, "room": "Festival association board (before)", "prompt": "You want to add a junior division for Year 7 and 8 bands. Seven friendly members; the secretary worries the program will run over time. Map, plan the conversations, adjust (perhaps a separate morning session), count and give a short in-room contribution that credits the secretary.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 2, "room": "School council (before)", "prompt": "You want council to endorse a standard concert dress for all ensembles, with a hire scheme so families need not buy it. Parent members are split on cost and on whether the school should be prescriptive. Plan who you speak to and in what order, what you adjust, whether you have the numbers, and who should move it. Then give the in-meeting contribution.", "seconds": 135}, {"level": 2, "room": "Conservatorium partnership committee (before)", "prompt": "You want a joint Year 11 masterclass series taught by conservatorium staff in your school. The dean's nominee is supportive, but one head of department sees it as outreach that takes staff time from tertiary teaching. Plan the conversations, adjust (perhaps counting the sessions as recruitment rather than teaching load), count, and deliver the short contribution.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 3, "room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee (before)", "prompt": "You want the committee to adopt your moderation model for the resubmission. Two senior academics are hostile, the executive officer has advised against it, and one member is likely to raise it with the Board directly. Plan aloud the order of conversations and the adjustments. Then give your contribution on the day, during which an academic interrupts to say the proposal has been 'settled in the corridors'. Answer that openly and briefly, then move.", "seconds": 210}, {"level": 3, "room": "School executive meeting (before)", "prompt": "You want the executive to support creating a Level 3 leadership position for Music. The Principal is broadly supportive, the Associate Principal is sceptical about precedent for other learning areas, and the business manager is opposed on cost. Plan the conversations and adjustments, count, then give the in-room contribution. The Associate Principal interrupts to ask whether you have been lobbying members individually. Answer honestly and keep it short.", "seconds": 180}]}, {"id": "gf-question-put", "examples": [{"title": "Moving the festival to May", "room": "Festival association annual general meeting (he chairs the item)", "prompt": "Members have debated a resolution to move the festival from August to May from 2028. He is chairing the item and must bring it to a decision.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "The question before the meeting is that the festival be held in *May* rather than August from 2028, as set out in the resolution circulated with the notice of meeting.", "note": "He states the question in the exact words of the motion and anchors it to the circulated text. Pointing to the notice reminds members that the decision is properly before them, which matters under an incorporated association's rules."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "I'll take speakers in turn, and I'll alternate where I can. | The mover has spoken in favour. Is there a speaker *against*?", "note": "Announcing alternation tells the minority it will be heard, which lowers the temperature before it rises. Asking directly for a speaker against shows impartiality rather than claiming it."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "(Later) We've had three speakers each way. Does anyone who hasn't yet spoken wish to?", "note": "Counting the speakers aloud signals that debate is near its end without cutting anyone off. Inviting only those who 'haven't yet spoken' gently closes the floor to repeat speakers."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "Before I put the question, let me summarise. || Against, we've heard that May clashes with school camps, and that regional schools travel more easily in August. In favour, we've heard that August now sits on top of Year 12 practical exams, which costs the festival its senior bands. Both are matters of *fact*, not preference, and members will weigh them differently.", "note": "He summarises the case against first, in parallel structure with the case for, so neither side sounds favoured. Calling both 'matters of fact' dignifies the losing side in advance and keeps the vote about judgement rather than loyalty."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "The question is that the festival be held in May rather than August from 2028, as set out in the resolution. | All those in favour? | *Against*? | Abstentions?", "note": "The question is read again in identical words, so nobody can later claim they voted on something different. The short breaths between the three calls give each group its own moment."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "The resolution is *carried*, twenty-three to eleven, with two abstentions. The minutes will record the vote, and the board will report on venue arrangements for May at its next meeting.", "note": "Declaring the numbers, not just the result, protects the decision from later dispute. Attaching the next action and its owner turns the vote into work."}], "weak": {"text": "OK, so I think we've had a pretty good discussion. There are obviously different views, and I think they're all valid. I guess what I'm hearing is that most people are kind of in favour, although there are some concerns about camps and so on. Shall we just go with May, then? Does anyone strongly object? No? Great, I think we're agreed, so we'll move forward with that and work out the details later, probably at the next board meeting. Thanks, everyone. Next item.", "why": ["The question is never stated in the words of the resolution, so nobody knows exactly what was decided.", "The summary leans ('most people are kind of in favour') and reduces the other side to 'camps and so on'.", "Silence is taken for consent; there is no call for those in favour, against and abstaining.", "Nothing minutable is declared: no numbers, no record of the resolution, no owner for the next step."]}}, {"title": "Two showcases, not one concert", "room": "Department meeting (he chairs)", "prompt": "Staff have discussed replacing the single end-of-year concert with showcases in Terms 2 and 4. The discussion has started to repeat itself.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "The question before us is that, from 2027, the department replaces the single end-of-year concert with two *showcases*, one in Term 2 and one in Term 4.", "note": "Even in an informal room he turns a discussion into a question with a date and a precise change. Colleagues who have been arguing about different things are suddenly arguing about the same sentence."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "I'll take people in turn. | We've heard from the jazz staff in favour. Does anyone want to speak against? (Later) I'll come to our *newest* colleague, who hasn't had a chance yet.", "note": "The language is lighter than at a board, but the discipline is the same: turns, a call for the other side, and a deliberate invitation to the quietest person. Bringing in a new colleague by role shows the chair is watching the whole table."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "Let me pull this together before we decide. || The concern is *workload*: two concerts means two sets of logistics, and Term 4 is already heavy. The case for is that one concert in December gives the junior ensembles a single performance a year, which is too few. The motion doesn't settle who runs the logistics, and I'll come back to that.", "note": "He puts the objection first and in its strongest form, then the case for in a single sentence. Naming what the motion does not decide keeps the vote clean and promises the doubters a second conversation."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "So the question is that, from 2027, the department replaces the end-of-year concert with showcases in Term 2 and Term 4. | Those in favour? | Against? | Anyone *abstaining*?", "note": "The question is repeated with only trivial change, which in a department meeting is enough to make it formal. Asking for abstentions acknowledges that colleagues may be undecided without being opposed."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "That's carried, five to one. I'll minute it, and I'll bring a draft logistics roster to the first meeting of Term 1, so the workload concern is *dealt* with before anything is booked.", "note": "He declares the count honestly, including the one against, and converts the dissent into the first action item. The minority sees its concern carried forward, which is how a chair keeps a small team intact after a divided vote."}], "weak": {"text": "Right, I think we've talked about this enough, and I think most of us want to give the two-showcase idea a go. I know workload is an issue, and I hear that, it's definitely something we'll keep an eye on. But I think overall it's a positive change for the kids. So unless anyone feels really strongly, let's say we'll do that next year and sort out the details as we go, probably over the summer. Is everyone happy with that? Great.", "why": ["He never states the question, so the change, its start date and its terms are undefined.", "The chair advocates ('a positive change for the kids') instead of summarising both sides fairly.", "'Unless anyone feels really strongly' pressures dissent into silence rather than taking a count.", "The workload concern is waved at rather than turned into an action with an owner and a date."]}}, {"title": "A letter to the Minister", "room": "Conference business session (he chairs)", "prompt": "At a state music educators' conference, members have debated a motion, already amended from the floor, that the network write to the Minister supporting specialist music programs. Time is short.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "The question before the meeting is the motion *as amended*: that the network writes to the Minister supporting continued funding for specialist music programs in public schools, and invites the professional association to co-sign.", "note": "Saying 'as amended' and then reading the full text prevents the commonest confusion in a large room, members voting on the version they remember. Reading it in full costs fifteen seconds and saves an argument afterwards."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "Speakers through the chair, please, and two minutes each. | I have a speaker against at the microphone on the left; then I'll come back to the right. (Later) I'll take one more speaker each way, and then I'll put the *question*.", "note": "Time limits and physical signposting give a large room confidence that the chair has a plan. Announcing the last two speakers closes debate in advance, so nobody feels cut off when it ends."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "Let me summarise before I put the question. || The concern against is that a letter from a teachers' network can look like self-interest, and that we should speak for all music education, not only selective programs. The case for is that specialist programs are the ones currently under review, and that a co-signed letter carries more weight than either body alone. The amendment has broadened who *signs*; it hasn't broadened what the letter covers.", "note": "The final sentence is the chair's most useful service: it tells members precisely what the amendment did and did not change. That clarity, offered without opinion, is what makes a summary authoritative."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "The question is the motion as amended. All those in favour, please raise your *cards*. | Against? | Abstentions?", "note": "In a large room the method of voting is stated as part of the question. Keeping the call to its fixed form signals that the decision is now procedural, not rhetorical."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "The motion as amended is *carried*, clearly, with a small number against. The minutes will record it as carried on a show of cards, and the executive will circulate the draft letter to members before it's sent.", "note": "Where a count is not needed, he says so honestly with 'clearly' and records the method. Promising circulation before sending reassures the minority that the wording, at least, is still theirs."}], "weak": {"text": "OK, we're running a bit short on time, so I think we might need to wrap this up. There's been a lot of good discussion and some really passionate views on both sides. I think the general feeling in the room is that we should write some kind of letter, maybe with the association, although I know a few people were worried about how it would look. Can I get a quick show of hands for the letter? Yep, that looks like most people. Fantastic, we'll take that as a yes and get something drafted.", "why": ["Time pressure replaces procedure: the motion as amended is never read, so members vote on 'some kind of letter'.", "The summary praises the debate instead of stating the two cases and what the amendment changed.", "Only those in favour are called; there is no call for those against or abstaining.", "The declaration ('we'll take that as a yes') is not minutable and promises no circulation of the draft."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["The question before the meeting is that [motion, exact words].", "The motion, as circulated, is that [wording].", "So that we're all debating the same words, the motion reads: [wording].", "Moved by [member], seconded by [member], that [wording].", "The question before us is the motion as amended, which now reads: [wording].", "We're deciding one thing on this item: whether [wording].", "Before debate, I'll read the motion exactly: [wording].", "The amendment is to insert [words] after [words]. We'll deal with that first.", "This is a recommendation to [body], not a decision of our own; the wording is [wording].", "For those online, the motion is on screen, and reads: [wording]."]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["I'll take speakers in turn. Is there a speaker against?", "Through the chair, please.", "I'll alternate for and against where I can.", "I have [member] in favour; then I'll come to [member].", "Does anyone who hasn't yet spoken wish to?", "I'll take one more speaker each way, and then I'll put the question.", "[Member], I'll come to you; [member] has the floor at the moment.", "Can we keep to the motion? [The other matter] is listed later on the agenda.", "Two minutes each, please, so that everyone who wants to speak can.", "I'd like to hear from someone online before we go further."]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["Before I put the question, let me summarise where the debate has landed.", "Against, we've heard [concern]. In favour, we've heard [case].", "Let me start with the case against, so that it's fairly put: [concern].", "Both points are reasonable, and members will weigh them differently.", "The motion doesn't decide [related matter]; that stays open.", "Have I put that fairly? I'll correct it if not.", "The disagreement, as I hear it, is about [X], not about [Y].", "The concern about [issue] can be met by [action], whichever way this goes.", "Nobody has disputed [fact]; the question is what follows from it.", "That's the shape of the debate. I'll now put the question."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["The question is that [wording]. All those in favour? Against? Abstentions?", "I'll read the question once more before we vote: [wording].", "Those in favour, please raise your hands. | Thank you. Those against?", "Members online, please use the hand function, and I'll count you separately.", "We'll vote on the amendment first. The question is that the words [words] be inserted.", "I'll now put the substantive motion, as amended.", "On the voices: those in favour say aye. | Those against, no.", "Could the secretary count, please? I'll take the hands again.", "Is anyone abstaining? I'd like the minutes to be exact.", "There being no further speakers, I'll put the question."]}, {"tag": 4, "lines": ["The motion is carried. The minutes will record [result and action].", "Carried, [n] to [n], with [n] abstentions.", "The motion is lost. It can come back at [meeting] in a new form if members wish.", "The amendment is carried, so the motion now reads: [wording].", "[Member] has asked for their vote against to be recorded, and it will be.", "The secretary will minute the decision in the words of the motion.", "That's carried; [owner] will report on progress at [meeting].", "For clarity, that decision means [consequence], and nothing more.", "Carried unanimously. Thank you; we'll move to item [n].", "The result is close, so I'll ask for a count to confirm it."]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Department meeting (chair)", "prompt": "Staff have discussed moving the weekly department meeting from Monday to Wednesday afternoons, mainly because of Monday ensemble rehearsals. One colleague prefers Monday because Wednesday is her duty day. State the question, take a speaker each way, summarise, put it and declare it, with the duty-day concern carried into an action.", "seconds": 60}, {"level": 1, "room": "Festival association board (chair)", "prompt": "The board is adopting a revised volunteer code of conduct. It is uncontroversial, but one member wants to speak for a second time on a minor wording point. Handle the repeat speaker courteously, summarise, put the question and declare the result.", "seconds": 90}, {"level": 2, "room": "School council (chair)", "prompt": "The motion is to endorse the school's revised behaviour policy. The staff representative and a parent member disagree about mobile phones in class, and the parent moves an amendment to add a review after two terms. Deal with the amendment first, then put the substantive motion as amended, summarising fairly each time.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 2, "room": "Conservatorium partnership steering group (chair)", "prompt": "The motion is that the school and the conservatorium share audition panels for the Year 7 intake from next year. A university member raises a point of order that the paper was circulated late. Rule on it (offer to defer or to proceed by consent), then take debate, summarise and put the question.", "seconds": 135}, {"level": 3, "room": "Festival association annual general meeting (chair)", "prompt": "A contested motion would end free festival entry for life members. A former president interrupts twice, demands to speak again, and disputes your count. An online member says their vote was not seen. Keep the debate through the chair, summarise without leaning, take a counted vote with the online members counted separately, and declare a result that will survive the minutes.", "seconds": 210}, {"level": 3, "room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee (member)", "prompt": "Two academics are talking past each other about the timeline for resubmission, and the chair has lost the thread. Offer the chair exact wording for a recommendation, with a working group, an owner and a reporting date. When the chair invites you to summarise the two positions, do it fairly, starting with the side you disagree with. The executive officer then contests your wording; respond without abandoning the substance.", "seconds": 180}]}, {"id": "gf-quaker", "examples": [{"title": "A sponsorship offer", "room": "School council (he chairs)", "prompt": "A local liquor retailer has offered to sponsor the performing arts program. Some members think it inconsistent with the school's values; others think the program cannot afford to refuse. A vote would split the council.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Before we start on this item, can we take a moment? | It's the kind of question where *how* we decide matters as much as what we decide, and I'd rather we were slow than divided.", "note": "Asking for a moment changes the tempo of the meeting deliberately. The antithesis 'slow than divided' names the risk without accusing anyone, and gives members permission to think before they speak."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "I'd ask each of us to speak *once*, to the question of whether accepting this offer sits with the school's values, rather than replying to each other. I'll go round the table, and anyone may pass.", "note": "He frames the question as one of values, not money, which sets the level at which members will speak. Going round the table, with the right to pass, stops the most fluent members dominating and draws in the quiet ones."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "(After contributions) Thank you. Let me try to put the sense of the council into words. || I'll write it as I go, so give me a *moment*.", "note": "'The sense of the council' is a deliberately unfamiliar phrase that tells members this will not be a vote. Writing in the room, in silence, gives the draft visible care and keeps anyone from filling the pause with a second speech."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "Here's what I've written. 'The council thanks the company for its offer and notes the generosity behind it. Members were of one mind that the performing arts program needs additional funding, and of more than one *mind* about this source. The council does not accept the offer at this time, and asks the Principal and the business manager to report at the next meeting on alternative sponsors.'", "note": "The minute records agreement and disagreement honestly in a matched pair, 'of one mind' and 'of more than one mind', so both groups find themselves in it. 'At this time' leaves a door open, and the practical next step travels inside the minute with owners and a date."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "Does that capture where we are? | If a word is wrong, let's change the *word*, not reopen the item.", "note": "The invitation to refine is genuine, but its terms are set: wording, not positions. That single sentence moves the contest from speeches to a page, where educated members are far more willing to agree."}], "weak": {"text": "OK, this one's a bit tricky, so let's just go around and everyone have their say. ... Right, so there are clearly some strong feelings on both sides, and I think everyone's made really good points. Personally I can see both sides. I suppose we could vote on it, but I don't really want to split the council over this. Maybe we just leave it for now and come back to it next meeting when people have had more time to think? I'll let the company know we're still considering it.", "why": ["There is no settling moment, so members arrive at the item in the tempo of the previous one.", "Contributions are invited but not framed; the strong version sets the question as one of values and asks for one contribution each.", "Nobody drafts anything, so the meeting has no object to agree to and the chair defers.", "The outcome is an unminuted holding position instead of a written decision with a next step and owners."]}}, {"title": "When the partnership ended", "room": "Department meeting (he chairs)", "prompt": "The conservatorium has ended its Saturday partnership with the department at short notice. Staff are angry, and some want to complain publicly.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Before we talk about the conservatorium, can we pause? | Several of us have spent years on this partnership, and I'd like our response to be one we're still *comfortable* with in a year.", "note": "He acknowledges the investment in the room before asking for restraint, so the pause reads as respect, not suppression. The time horizon, 'in a year', invites colleagues to speak to their later selves."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "Could each of us speak once, to what we want the department's response to be, rather than to what anyone else has just said? I'll start with whoever has worked with the program longest, and I'll speak *last*.", "note": "Speaking last is the chair's most important discipline in this method: it prevents his view anchoring the room. Beginning with the longest-serving colleague honours experience and usually sets a measured tone."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "(After contributions) Let me try to write down where we've arrived. || Bear with me for a minute; I'd rather get the words right than *fast*.", "note": "The held pause and the plain apology for slowness signal that the draft is being written carefully, not pre-written. A visible minute of drafting makes the eventual text feel produced by the meeting."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "Here's the draft. 'The department regrets the conservatorium's decision and the short notice given. It records its *thanks* to the conservatorium staff who taught in the program. The department will not respond publicly. The TIC will write to the head of school seeking a meeting before the end of term, and will propose an alternative arrangement for the affected students by Week 2 of next term.'", "note": "The minute lets the anger stand in one factual phrase, 'the short notice given', and then turns to gratitude, which keeps the relationship alive. The decision not to respond publicly is stated plainly, and the practical solution arrives with an owner and two dates."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "Is that right, or does something need to change? | One of you wanted stronger language about the notice period. I've kept it factual, but if the room *wants* it stronger, say so.", "note": "He names the one dissenting preference and offers it back to the room, which shows the draft was honest about what it left out. A minority that sees its view named is far more likely to accept the minute."}], "weak": {"text": "So, obviously, we all heard about the conservatorium. Honestly, I'm as frustrated as anyone, I think the way they've handled it is pretty poor. I know some of you want to say something publicly, and I get that. I don't know if that's the best idea, but I'm open to it. I'll probably send them an email at some point. In the meantime we need to work out what to do with the kids. Does anyone have any thoughts? Let's just have a chat about it.", "why": ["The chair leads with his own anger, which anchors the room before anyone else has spoken; the strong version speaks last.", "'Let's just have a chat' invites replies and repetition instead of one contribution each to a defined question.", "No minute is drafted, so the public-response question is left open and the room's anger has nowhere to settle.", "The practical step ('an email at some point') has no recipient, purpose or date."]}}, {"title": "After the coach broke down", "room": "Music parents' association general meeting (he attends as TIC)", "prompt": "The tour coach broke down and students waited four hours at a roadside. Parents are angry with the coach company and partly with the school. The president asks him to help word the association's response.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "President, before we go further, could we take a moment? | Feelings are running high, with good *reason*, and I think we'll decide better if we slow down.", "note": "As a guest and a school representative, he asks permission through the president rather than taking the chair's role. 'With good reason' validates the anger before asking for calm, which is the only order in which calm is granted."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "Could I suggest each family speaks *once*, to what they'd want the association to do, rather than responding to each other? Some of the parents here weren't on the phone that night, and we should hear them too.", "note": "He frames the question forward, what the association should do, rather than backward, what went wrong. Naming the parents who were not involved widens the room beyond the angriest voices."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "(After contributions) If it helps, President, I've tried to put the sense of the meeting into *words* for the minutes.", "note": "'If it helps' offers the draft as a service, not a takeover. As a member, he holds the pen without holding the chair, which is exactly where this method gives a non-chair influence."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "'The association acknowledges the distress caused to students and families when the tour coach broke down, and thanks the staff who stayed with the students. It asks the school to seek a full written explanation from the coach company and share it with families by the end of the month. The association will not use this company for future tours until that explanation has been received and *considered* at its next meeting.'", "note": "The minute names the distress first, then the staff, then the action, which mirrors the order in which the room needed to be heard. The decision about the company is conditional rather than final, which satisfies the angry without committing the association to a judgement it cannot yet make."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "It's the meeting's minute, not *mine*. | Does it say what people want it to say, or should we change it?", "note": "Disowning the draft is the decisive move: it invites refinement and makes acceptance an act of the meeting. The plain question 'does it say what people want it to say' keeps the test on accuracy, not on him."}], "weak": {"text": "Can I just say, from the school's point of view, we're really sorry this happened, it was obviously a terrible experience for everyone. The staff did an amazing job in really difficult circumstances. I've already spoken to the company and they've assured me it was a one-off mechanical fault. I understand people are upset, and I'm happy to answer any questions. I'm not sure what the association wants to do formally, but maybe we could look at using a different company next time? Anyway, I'm here if anyone wants to talk.", "why": ["He defends the school and relays the company's assurance before the families have been heard; the strong version slows the room first.", "There is no invitation to speak once to a forward question, so the meeting will become replies to him.", "No minute is drafted, so the association has nothing to accept and nothing on record.", "The suggestion about the company is offered casually rather than written into a conditional decision with a date."]}}], "bank": [{"tag": 0, "lines": ["Before we begin, can we take a moment, because this matters.", "Could we pause before this item? I'd rather we were slow than divided.", "Let's take a breath before we start; it's been a long agenda.", "This one deserves more care than the items before it.", "I'd like us to come to this fresh, so let's leave the last item where it is.", "Before anyone speaks, let me say what we're actually deciding: [question].", "There's no rush on this. We have [time] for it, and we'll use it.", "I'd like our response to be one we're still comfortable with in a year.", "Can we take a minute of quiet? | Thank you.", "Some of us are closer to this than others, and that's all right."]}, {"tag": 1, "lines": ["I'd ask everyone to speak once, to the question, rather than reply to each other.", "I'll go round the table, and anyone may pass.", "Could we each say what we'd want the [body] to do, in a sentence or two?", "Please speak to the question, not to the last speaker.", "I'll speak last, so that I don't steer anyone.", "Let's hear from those online first; it's easy to lose them.", "If something's already been said, it's enough to say you share it.", "I'll leave a short pause after each contribution, so we hear each one.", "There'll be time to respond to the draft; for now, just your view.", "Has anyone not yet spoken who would like to?"]}, {"tag": 2, "lines": ["Let me try to put the sense of the meeting into words for the minutes.", "Give me a moment; I'd rather get the words right than fast.", "I'll write it as I go, and then read it back.", "What I'm hearing is closer to agreement than it may feel. Let me try to write it.", "I'll try to capture both what we agree on and where we differ.", "Could [the secretary] and I draft this together while we pause?", "I'm going to put the next step in, with a name and a date.", "If I can't get it into words, that tells us we're not there yet.", "Let me draft something, and you can tear it up if it's wrong.", "I'll keep it short; a long minute hides disagreement."]}, {"tag": 3, "lines": ["Here is what I've written.", "I'll read it slowly, word for word: '[minute]'.", "'The [body] notes [fact] with [response].'", "'Members were of one mind that [shared point], and of more than one mind about [contested point].'", "'The [body] resolves to [action], with [owner] reporting at [meeting].'", "'The [body] records its thanks to [people].'", "'The [body] does not [act] at this time, and asks [owner] to [alternative] by [date].'", "I'll read it once more, now that you've heard it once.", "Could it go up on the screen, so those online can see the words?", "That's the whole draft. It's four sentences."]}, {"tag": 4, "lines": ["Does that capture where we are, or does it need changing?", "If a word is wrong, let's change the word, not reopen the item.", "It's the meeting's minute, not mine.", "Is there anyone who couldn't live with that wording?", "[Member], you raised [point]; have I been fair to it?", "Let me read it back with that change: '[revised minute]'.", "I'm hearing that we're content. Shall we let it stand?", "If we can't agree the wording tonight, let's minute that, and return to it.", "Is that agreed? | Thank you. That's our minute.", "I'm happy to change any word of it."]}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Department meeting (chair)", "prompt": "Staff mildly disagree about whether music staff should wear a common concert dress at performances. Settle the item, hear each colleague once, then draft and read a minute: perhaps a shared standard for major concerts only, with the TIC circulating a description by the end of the month.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 1, "room": "Feeder primary schools' music coordinators (chair)", "prompt": "The group is deciding how to acknowledge a primary music teacher who is retiring after many years with the combined primary concert band. Everyone is warm, but ideas differ. Run the item to a minute that names what will happen at the combined concert and who will arrange it.", "seconds": 60}, {"level": 2, "room": "School council (chair)", "prompt": "A parent has complained that lyrics in a student band's concert piece were inappropriate. Members are divided between artistic freedom and community standards. Slow the room, hear each member once, and draft a minute that records both values honestly and asks the TIC to bring a short repertoire guideline to the next meeting.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 2, "room": "Festival association board (member)", "prompt": "The board has learned that this year's festival made a loss. Long-serving members blame the new venue; newer members blame falling entries. Suggest single contributions, then offer a draft minute that records the loss without assigning blame and asks the treasurer and the president to report on both causes before any venue decision.", "seconds": 135}, {"level": 3, "room": "SCSA reference group (member, hybrid meeting)", "prompt": "A senior academic insists on a vote and calls the discussion vague; the executive officer wants nothing minuted until consultation closes. Members online keep being talked over. Propose single contributions with those online heard first, then offer a draft minute that records what is agreed, states what is deferred to consultation, and names who will report and when. Both the academic and the officer will push back on words.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "Conservatorium partnership committee (chairing the item)", "prompt": "There is an allegation that an audition panel member had an undeclared conflict of interest. Two members interrupt repeatedly, and one threatens to resign if the matter is not referred externally. Settle the room, hold the single-contribution rule under pressure, and draft a minute that neither prejudges the allegation nor buries it, with a named process, an owner and a date.", "seconds": 210}]}, {"id": "nt-1", "examples": [{"title": "The analysis weighting", "input": "Assoc Prof Marguerite Hale (university, on Zoom): I've looked at the Unit 3 and 4 performance requirements against what first-year students arrive with, and the issue isn't that the repertoire is too hard. It's that the written component rewards description over analysis. We're getting students who can tell you what happens in a score, but not why. If I could change one thing, I'd shift the weighting of the response section toward analytical questions, even at some cost to breadth.", "output": "ROOM: MH  written rewards description  'what happens, not why'  !\nROOM: MH  Δ response weighting → analytical, at cost of breadth\nME: ✓ MH + our Yr 12 response samples #  ★", "weak": "Prof Hale (on Zoom) said she had compared the Unit 3 and 4 performance requirements with what first-year students bring, and that the repertoire is not too hard but the written component rewards description rather than analysis. She suggested changing the weighting of the response section.", "why": "The record version is accurate and useless at your turn: it has no phrase to credit, no symbol marking the proposed change, and no line for your own move. The speaking version is a third of the length and already points at what you will add."}, {"title": "The tour that is optional on paper", "input": "Ms Fiona Lacey (parent representative, school council): Families have asked me to raise the Melbourne tour. The cost has gone up again, it's now over two thousand dollars, and some of them feel the tour has become the thing that decides whether you're really in the program. Nobody is saying cancel it. But I'd like council to understand that for a lot of families it is not optional in any practical sense, even if it is optional on paper.", "output": "ROOM: FL (parent)  tour # $2k+ ↑  'optional on paper'  !\nROOM: FL  not cancel; tour = marker of belonging\nME: ? subsidy pathway + 2nd tier local tour  ★", "weak": "Fiona Lacey raised families' concerns about the Melbourne tour, which now costs more than $2000. Some families feel it is not really optional. She is not asking for it to be cancelled. Council should be aware.", "why": "Writing 'Council should be aware' records her intent but gives you nothing to do. The strong version keeps her exact phrase, the figure, the limit she set ('not cancel') and a fragment of the answer you can bring."}, {"title": "Feedback that is box-ticking", "input": "Mr Owen Prescott (festival association board): We keep saying adjudicator feedback is the heart of the festival, and then we send back a form with three lines and a grade. It's box-ticking. I sat with a director from a country school last year who drove six hours for that form. I'd like us to pay adjudicators for an extra fifteen minutes per band so they can give spoken feedback on the day.", "output": "ROOM: OP  ✗ fb form  'box-ticking'  # 3 lines + grade\nROOM: OP  Δ pay +15 min per band, spoken fb  # country dir 6 hrs\nME: # cost it: 15 min per band at adjudicator rate → I can table it  ★", "weak": "Owen thinks the adjudicator feedback forms are not good enough (three lines and a grade). Story about a country school director who drove six hours. Proposes paying adjudicators more for spoken feedback, extra fifteen minutes per band. Discussion followed.", "why": "The weak note keeps the anecdote as narrative and loses the function of each piece. The strong note sorts it: objection, figure, proposed change, and the treasurer's natural move, which is to price it."}, {"title": "The Thursday clash", "input": "Ms Priya Nandakumar (strings, department meeting): The Year 8 sectionals clash with the maths extension block on Thursdays, and I'm losing about a third of my violas every week. I've spoken to the maths coordinator. They're willing to move, but only if we give them a firm alternative slot by the end of term. Otherwise it rolls over into next year exactly as it is.", "output": "ROOM: PN  Yr 8 sect ✗ maths ext Thu  # 1/3 violas out\nROOM: PN  maths will move IF firm slot by end T4  !\nME: @ me: alt slot to maths coord by wk 8  ★", "weak": "Priya: Year 8 sectionals clash with maths extension on Thursdays. Losing violas. She has spoken to the maths coordinator. Need to find another time.", "why": "'Need to find another time' drops the condition and the deadline, which are the only parts that create an action. The strong version holds the figure, the condition and an owner, so you can close the item yourself."}, {"title": "A case the region will recognise", "input": "The principal (meeting in her office): I support the specialist program, you know that. But the intake numbers from the department assume a steady state, and we're projecting growth across the whole school. If music wants to keep its current class sizes, I need a case by the end of November that shows what the program costs per student and what it returns, in terms the regional office will recognise.", "output": "ROOM: Principal  ✓ program, but whole-school growth  !\nROOM: Principal  @ case by end Nov  'terms the regional office will recognise'\nME: # cost per student, retention Yr 7 to 12, results → 1 page  ★", "weak": "The principal said she supports the program but numbers are growing. She wants a business case by the end of November about cost and benefits.", "why": "'Cost and benefits' is your paraphrase; 'terms the regional office will recognise' is her criterion for success, and quoting it back when you deliver the case tells her you built it to her brief."}, {"title": "Direct and sometimes uncomfortable", "input": "Professor Daniel Strand (conservatorium, partnership committee): We'd welcome more of your students in the pre-tertiary program, but I'll be frank. Our concern isn't talent. It's that they arrive with very little experience of studio teaching, where feedback is direct and sometimes uncomfortable. A short transition session in Term 4 might do more for them than any amount of audition preparation.", "output": "ROOM: DS  ✓ more students; ! not talent\nROOM: DS  'direct and sometimes uncomfortable'  Δ transition session T4\nME: ≈ DS: Yr 12 masterclass series could host it  ★", "weak": "Prof Strand said they would like more of our students. The issue is that students are not used to the studio model. Suggested a transition session in Term 4 instead of audition preparation. Positive overall.", "why": "'Positive overall' is a minute-taker's judgement. The speaking note keeps the reassurance (not talent), the phrase and the offer, and the ≈ shows you already have a vehicle that builds on his idea."}, {"title": "Everyone surveys participants", "input": "Ms Rosalind Achebe (arts funding panel): The application is strong on artistic merit. The evaluation section is thin. It tells us you'll survey participants. Everyone surveys participants. What I'd want to see is a measure of what changes for the regional schools after the festival, six months on, not on the day.", "output": "ROOM: RA  ✓ merit  ✗ evaluation  'everyone surveys participants'\nROOM: RA  Δ measure change in regional schools, 6 months on\nME: ? re-entry rate 2028 + director follow-up call as measure  ★", "weak": "Panel feedback: application good on artistic merit, evaluation needs work. They want more than a survey. Look at longer term outcomes for regional schools.", "why": "The weak version softens her into 'needs work'. The exact line is sharper than anything you would paraphrase, and answering it directly ('rather than surveying participants') shows the panel you heard the objection itself."}, {"title": "It lives in someone's head", "input": "Mr Callum Reyes (music parent committee): The uniform hire system is a mess. Last year a jacket went missing for four months. I'm not blaming anyone. I'm saying the whole system lives in someone's head. If we wrote it down, a checklist, a sign-out sheet, whatever, the next committee wouldn't inherit the same chaos.", "output": "ROOM: CR  uniform hire 'lives in someone's head'  # jacket 4 mths\nROOM: CR  Δ written checklist + sign-out\nME: ✓ @ CR drafts, I review, next mtg  ★", "weak": "Callum raised problems with uniform hire, including a jacket that went missing for four months. He said he wasn't blaming anyone. Suggested a checklist or sign-out sheet.", "why": "Noting 'he wasn't blaming anyone' preserves the social texture but not the substance. The strong note keeps his diagnosis in his words and converts his idea into an owned action, which is what you will propose."}, {"title": "What actually transfers", "input": "A regional music teacher (from the floor, state music education conference): You've described a lovely program, but you're in a metropolitan school with specialist status. I've got one music room and it's a hundred kilometres to the nearest bass teacher. What in what you've said actually transfers?", "output": "ROOM: Delegate (regional)  ? 'what actually transfers'  !\nROOM: # 1 room, 100 km to bass teacher\nME: ★ 2 transfer: online sectionals, shared charts; 1 doesn't: staffing", "weak": "Question from a regional teacher about whether our program applies to regional schools with fewer resources. One music room, a long way from specialist teachers.", "why": "On a conference stage the note is your answer plan. Keeping her figures lets you answer her situation, not a generic one, and the ME line already numbers your reply before you stand to respond."}, {"title": "Not for 2027", "input": "Mr Thomas Ferreira (SCSA officer, assessment committee): Just to be clear on process. Any change to the weighting has to go through the consultation period, which means it cannot affect 2027. The earliest is 2028 implementation, and the Board will want to see the change modelled against last year's results before it agrees to anything.", "output": "ROOM: TF (SCSA)  Δ weighting → consult ∴ not 2027  !\nROOM: TF  earliest 2028; Bd wants model vs 2026 results  #\nME: ? who models it, by when  @  ★", "weak": "Thomas explained the process: changes need consultation so the earliest is 2028. The Board would also want modelling.", "why": "Procedural statements are where the real constraints sit. The ∴ records the consequence, and the ME line turns the officer's caveat into the practical question nobody has asked yet: who does the modelling."}, {"title": "It's the run sheet", "input": "Ms Annika Vos (deputy principal, presentation evening planning): For the presentation evening I'd like the music items cut from four to two. Last year we finished at nine forty and families with younger children left before the major awards. It isn't a judgement on the music. It's the run sheet.", "output": "ROOM: AV (DP)  Δ music items 4 → 2  # finished 9:40\nROOM: AV  'not a judgement on the music … the run sheet'\nME: ≈ AV: keep 3, cut changeovers  # 6 min per change  ★", "weak": "Annika wants to cut the music items from four to two because last year went too late. She said it wasn't personal.", "why": "'She said it wasn't personal' reports a feeling; her phrase concedes the real ground, that the issue is time, which lets you answer with time (changeovers) instead of defending the music."}, {"title": "All activity and no outcome", "input": "Dr Sunil Mehta (community member, school council): Before we approve the annual report, I'd note that the section on student wellbeing is all activity and no outcome. We ran twelve programs. Did anything get better? I'm not asking for a research study. Just one indicator we commit to tracking next year.", "output": "ROOM: SM  ✗ AR wellbeing  'all activity and no outcome'  # 12 programs\nROOM: SM  Δ 1 indicator tracked 2027, not a study\nME: ✓ SM; @ exec proposes indicator by Feb mtg  ★", "weak": "Dr Mehta commented on the wellbeing section of the annual report, saying it lists programs but not outcomes. He asked for an indicator to be tracked next year. Report to be approved.", "why": "As chair you need to resolve his concern before the vote. The strong note keeps his limit ('not a study'), which makes the request easy to accept, and the @ line is the resolution you will put."}], "quiz": [{"input": "Dr Lim (SCSA committee): The Board's concern is really assessment load in Unit 3. I'd say it's weight, not width. Victoria went the other way and trimmed the content.", "q": "Which line would serve you best when you speak?", "options": ["Dr Lim said the Board's concern was assessment load in Unit 3 and that Victoria trimmed content instead.", "SL  Bd = assess load U3  'weight, not width'  ≈ Vic trimmed content", "Board concerns: assessment, Unit 3, Victoria."], "answer": 1, "why": "It carries the speaker, the issue, the exact phrase to credit and the comparison, in a third of the words of the record version."}, {"input": "You are fifteen minutes into a council item. The last four minutes have been a parent repeating the concerns already raised, with no new figure or position.", "q": "What should you write for those four minutes?", "options": ["A summary of what the parent said, so the record is complete.", "Each repeated concern again, ticked to show emphasis.", "Nothing, or at most a ⟲ beside the earlier line."], "answer": 2, "why": "Speaking notes change only when the meeting changes; repetition earns a ⟲ at most."}, {"input": "You are deciding what kind of page to keep at a festival association board meeting where the secretary is taking minutes.", "q": "Which test should your page pass?", "options": ["A three-second glance tells me whom to credit, what is being decided and what I will add.", "An absent member could reconstruct the discussion from it.", "It captures every motion in the order it was moved."], "answer": 0, "why": "The minutes serve the absent; your page serves you at the moment you speak."}, {"input": "Ms Okonjo (department meeting): Look, I'm happy to run the Year 9 elective again, but not on Fridays period 6. That's where it dies.", "q": "Which note line is best?", "options": ["MO happy to run Yr 9 elective but not Fri P6.", "MO  ✓ Yr 9 elective  ✗ Fri P6  'that's where it dies'", "Yr 9 elective discussed; timing issues raised by Ms Okonjo."], "answer": 1, "why": "The symbols separate her agreement from her objection, and the phrase gives you something exact to credit when you propose a new slot."}, {"input": "During a principal's leadership meeting you notice you have written eleven full sentences in twenty minutes.", "q": "What is the likeliest cost?", "options": ["Nothing, provided the sentences are accurate.", "Your notes will be too short to share with colleagues afterwards.", "You have missed emphasis and phrases while writing, and you will arrive at your turn with a transcript and no move."], "answer": 2, "why": "Transcribing spends attention on content you could reconstruct and loses the structure and the creditable phrase."}, {"input": "Professor Aldana (partnership committee): We can offer twelve places, not twenty, and only if your staff co-supervise.", "q": "Which element must survive into your note above all?", "options": ["The figure and the condition: # 12 not 20, IF co-supervise.", "That the professor seemed reluctant.", "The name of the program the places belong to."], "answer": 0, "why": "Figures and conditions are the parts that change what the room can decide, and they cannot be reconstructed later."}, {"input": "Your page from an SCSA meeting has six ROOM lines and no ME lines at the point the chair turns to you.", "q": "What has gone wrong with the method?", "options": ["Nothing; ME lines are optional.", "You recorded the room but never marked your own move, so the page cannot launch you.", "You should have had more ROOM lines."], "answer": 1, "why": "A speaking page exists to point at your contribution; without ME lines it is only a shorter set of minutes."}, {"input": "Mr Haddad (arts funding panel, online): I'll keep this short. The budget's fine. The risk register is fiction.", "q": "Which line would you write?", "options": ["Budget ok; risk register needs more detail.", "Mr Haddad thinks the budget is fine but he is concerned about the risk register.", "NH  ✓ budget  ✗ risk register  'fiction'  !"], "answer": 2, "why": "One word of his is sharper than your paraphrase, and the ! tells you the risk register is where the application will be won or lost."}], "drills": [{"level": 1, "kind": "write", "input": "Ms Leonie Fraser (school council, finance subcommittee): The canteen contract comes up in March. I'd rather we didn't roll it over by default again. Three years ago we did exactly that and lost the healthy menu clause. Can we at least go to quote?", "task": "Write no more than two speaking lines for this contribution: initials, keyword, symbols, one exact phrase. Then one ME line.", "model": "ROOM: LF  ✗ roll over 'by default'  # 3 yrs ago lost healthy menu clause\nROOM: LF  Δ go to quote, March\nME: ✓ @ business mgr: quote spec incl. menu clause, Feb mtg  ★"}, {"level": 1, "kind": "speak", "input": "Mr James Whitlock, the Year 10 coordinator, has just spoken about the recital date at a department meeting. Your note reads: 'JW  ✗ Yr 10 recital in wk 9  'the week everything is due'  # 4 assessments'  and below it  'ME: Δ move to wk 7, same venue ★'.", "task": "Glance at the note for three seconds, then speak your contribution: credit the speaker with the phrase and make the proposal in two sentences.", "seconds": 30, "model": "James is right that week nine is *the week everything is due*; four assessments land in it. I'd propose we move the Year 10 recital to week seven, same venue, and I'll confirm the booking by Friday."}, {"level": 2, "kind": "write", "input": "Dr Carmen Silva (university, partnership committee): The placement students tell us the most valuable thing they do is watch an experienced teacher run a rehearsal. Not plan one, watch one. They learn more from one well-run Tuesday rehearsal than from a week of lectures. So I'd ask whether your senior ensembles could take two observers each term.\nMr Gareth Bloom (school deputy): We can host them, but the working with children checks and the induction take time. Last semester three students arrived without their paperwork and spent the first day in the library. If we do this, the university has to own the compliance side.", "task": "Convert this into a speaking page of no more than five lines: one or two ROOM lines per speaker with initials, symbols and one exact phrase each, and one or two ME lines.", "model": "ROOM: CS  'not plan one, watch one'  Δ 2 observers per senior ensemble per term\nROOM: GB  ✓ host, but WWC + induction  # 3 arrived without paperwork\nROOM: GB  'the university has to own the compliance side'  !\nME: ≈ CS + GB: yes to 2 observers IF uni confirms checks 2 wks prior\nME: @ CS's office checklist, @ me induction 1 page  ★"}, {"level": 2, "kind": "speak", "input": "Ms Harriet Coombe (festival association board): Sponsorship is down for the third year running. We are asking the same five businesses every year and wondering why they're tired of us. Two of them told me privately this year that they only renew out of loyalty to the old committee.\nMr Joel Antunes (board): The issue is we offer them nothing but a logo on a program nobody keeps. I'd stop calling it sponsorship and start calling it a partnership with something they can actually use, like a band at their staff function. One of the businesses we lost last year now sponsors a netball carnival, because the carnival gives them a stall.", "task": "Write a three-line speaking note in your head or on paper, then speak for up to 60 seconds: credit both speakers by name with their exact phrases and add one practical step.", "seconds": 60, "model": "Harriet put it exactly: we keep asking the same five businesses and *wondering why they're tired of us*. Joel has given us the answer, which is that a logo on a program nobody keeps is not an offer. I'd like to act on that before the next round. I propose that the treasurer and Joel draft a one-page partnership offer, with a performance at the partner's own event as its centrepiece, and that we take it to two new businesses before the end of February. Then we report back on what they said, not just whether they signed."}, {"level": 3, "kind": "write", "input": "SCSA Arts reference group, hybrid, item: moderation of the Year 12 performance exam.\nMs Rachel Dunstan (chair): We've had feedback from schools that the moderation day is too compressed. Today I'd like a view on whether we extend it or change its format.\nDr Patrick Oyelaran (university, on Zoom): The issue isn't time. It's that markers are calibrating on the day rather than before it. Calibration should come first. If the markers arrive already aligned, the day takes care of itself. Most of the disagreement I saw last year was settled in the first hour, which tells me it should have been settled before anyone arrived.\nMs Bronwyn Teale (teacher representative): I agree, and while we're on it, the online portal for uploading recordings has been terrible this year. Two of my students' files were rejected twice. I've emailed about it three times. Nobody seems to own it. Honestly, it's made the whole recording process stressful for the students.\nMr Hamish Corr (SCSA officer, quietly): For what it's worth, the 2025 data showed most of the marker variance came from three of the twelve criteria. If calibration focused only on those three, it could be done in an hour online beforehand.\nMs Teale: And the portal...\nChair: Let's note the portal separately.", "task": "Write a speaking page of no more than eight lines, using a Q line, ROOM lines with initials, symbols and phrases, a mark for the drift, and ME lines that would launch a contribution. Make sure the quiet fact survives.", "model": "Q: extend moderation day, or change format?\nROOM: PO  'calibration should come first'  Δ calibrate before the day\nROOM: BT  ✓ PO; portal ✗ # 2 files rejected twice (drift)\nROOM: HC (SCSA)  # 2025: variance from 3 of 12 criteria  !\nROOM: HC  → 1 hr online calibration on those 3, beforehand\nROOM: Chair  ⟲ portal noted separately\nME: ≈ PO + HC: format, not length; pre-day calibration on 3 criteria  ★\nME: @ SCSA pilot 2027; portal @ to secretariat, separate item"}, {"level": 3, "kind": "speak", "input": "School council, item: proposal to introduce a school-wide device levy.\nMr Ian Prowse (chair of finance): The levy would be one hundred and twenty dollars a year. It's lower than every school near us. I don't think we should be shy about it. Families will understand it if we explain it.\nMs Delphine Arcand (parent representative): Lower than other schools isn't the point. The point is what families get for it. At the moment the answer is a vague promise of 'updated technology'. And honestly the last time we had a levy it disappeared into general revenue and nobody could tell us where. I'd want to see a list, in writing, of what the money buys.\nMr Ravi Kaur (community member): That reminds me, the school website still lists the old uniform supplier. Someone should fix that, and the phone number for the front office is wrong as well.\nChair: Thank you, Ravi, I'll pass that on.\nMs Arcand: Fine, but on the levy...\nMs Tess Naughton (business manager, quietly): Just so council knows, the current laptop fleet reaches end of warranty in Term 2 next year. About four hundred machines. Replacing them at current prices would cost far more than the levy raises in a year, so the levy is really a contribution, not the whole cost.", "task": "Take a speaking note as you read, then speak for up to 90 seconds as the council member who brings the item back to a decision. Credit two speakers by name with their exact phrases, use the quiet fact, and propose a resolution.", "seconds": 90, "model": "Thank you. I'd like to bring us back to the levy, and the website point can go to the office. Ian's case is that we shouldn't be *shy* about a hundred and twenty dollars, and he's right that it compares well. Delphine's point is the stronger one, though: the question is not price but *what families get for it*, and last time the money vanished into general revenue. || Tess has just given us the answer to Delphine. About four hundred laptops reach end of warranty in Term 2. So I'd propose three things. One, the levy is tied by resolution to the device fleet and nothing else. Two, the business manager reports to council each year on how it was spent. Three, we tell families in plain terms what it buys: replacement of machines reaching end of warranty from Term 2. Wording for the minutes: council approves a device levy of one hundred and twenty dollars, hypothecated to the device fleet, with an annual expenditure report to council."}]}, {"id": "nt-2", "examples": [{"title": "Ensemble fees at council", "input": "School council, item: 2027 ensemble levy. Ms Judith Ng (P&C): The levy has gone up two years running and families are noticing. Mr Arjun Pillai (business manager): The increase covers bus hire to rehearsals and nothing else; I can show the figures. Chair: So do we approve the levy tonight or send it back?", "output": "Q: Approve 2027 ensemble levy tonight, or send back?\nROOM: JN (P&C)  levy ↑ 2nd yr  'families are noticing'\nROOM: AP (bus mgr)  ↑ = bus hire only  # can show figures\nME: ≈ JN + AP: problem is visibility, not amount  ★\nME: Δ approve + 1-page breakdown to families\nGAP: nobody has said how families are told", "weak": "Levy up 2 yrs, JN says families noticing, I think it's mostly a communication problem. AP: bus hire only, figures available. I could suggest sending families a breakdown? Chair asked approve or send back.", "why": "Mixing reactions into the record blurs who said what and buries the decision at the end. On the ruled page the Q line is the first thing you see, your reaction sits in its own column, and the GAP line names the absence your proposal fills."}, {"title": "Repertoire list review", "input": "SCSA syllabus committee, item: prescribed repertoire list. Dr Felix Marchetti (university, on Zoom): The list hasn't been refreshed since 2018 and it shows; it is almost entirely pre-1950. Ms Carol Yeung (teacher rep): Teachers like the list because it's stable; every change means new resources. Chair: We need a recommendation to the Board on whether to review it in this cycle.", "output": "Q: Recommend a list review this cycle, yes or no?\nROOM: FM (Zoom)  list unchanged since 2018  # almost all pre-1950\nROOM: CY  ✓ stability  'every change means new resources'\nME: ✓ both: review, but staged # 20% per cycle  ★\nME: ? resource support from SCSA for new works\nGAP: no one has proposed how to cushion the resource cost", "weak": "Q: repertoire list. Felix (Zoom) list is old, all pre-1950, needs updating (agree, ours is the same). Carol: teachers want stability, resources problem. Maybe a staged review? Board recommendation needed.", "why": "The weak Q line names a topic, not a decision. The strong Q line is a yes or no, which is what a recommendation to the Board needs, and the GAP line identifies the cost nobody has addressed."}, {"title": "A new venue for the festival", "input": "Festival association board, item: 2027 venue. Mr Marcus Rowe (board): The concert hall would lift the profile, but we'd be renting prestige. Ms Leah Brandt (board): The current venue loses us two schools a year because of parking. The chair: I'd like a decision by the November meeting.", "output": "Q: Move venue for 2027? Decide by Nov\nROOM: MR  ✗ concert hall  'renting prestige'\nROOM: LB  ✗ current venue  # 2 schools lost per yr, parking\nROOM: Chair  ∴ decide Nov mtg  !\nME: ★ costed 3rd option: current venue + shuttle\nGAP: nobody has costed per student, or asked the schools", "weak": "Venue: Marcus doesn't want the concert hall (too expensive?). Leah says parking is losing us 2 schools. I wonder about a shuttle bus. Decide in Nov.", "why": "With the columns separated, both objections appear side by side, which shows you that they are not opposed and leaves space for a third option. The GAP line names two absences, the per-student cost and the schools' view."}, {"title": "Year 11 elective numbers", "input": "Department meeting, item: Year 11 elective offer. Mr Toby Caldwell (HOLA): Numbers for the General course are twelve, below the line timetabling uses. Ms Sian Hartley (teacher): The twelve are the students who most need it; half of them aren't in the specialist program. Mr Caldwell: Timetabling needs our answer this week.", "output": "Q: Run Yr 11 General with 12, or not? Answer this wk\nROOM: TC (HOLA)  # 12, below timetable line\nROOM: SH  'the students who most need it'  # 1/2 non-specialist\nROOM: TC  @ answer this wk  !\nME: ? composite class with Yr 12 General  ★\nGAP: no one has named the case to put to timetabling", "weak": "Year 11 General only 12 students, Sian very keen to keep it, half non-specialist. Toby says timetabling wants an answer. Could we combine with Year 12? Probably not.", "why": "'Probably not' is a reaction contaminating the record and pre-empting your own idea. Kept in the ME column as a question, the composite class becomes a proposal you can test."}, {"title": "Instrument replacement in the principal's office", "input": "Principal's office, item: capital request. The principal: I have room for one major music purchase next year, not three. Mr Ross Ellery (business manager): The tubas are past repair, the timpani are safe for another two years, and the grand piano can be serviced. The principal: Tell me which one.", "output": "Q: Which ONE major purchase in 2027?\nROOM: Principal  # 1 not 3  !\nROOM: RE (bus mgr)  tubas past repair; timpani 2 yrs; piano service\nME: ✓ tubas ∴ obvious on condition  ★\nME: # tuba quote + CB1/CB2 tuba numbers\nGAP: no one has said what the piano service costs", "weak": "The principal can only fund one item. Ross: tubas worst, timpani ok 2 yrs, piano needs service. Go for tubas, have quotes. Piano?", "why": "The Q line in capitals on 'ONE' keeps you from arguing for all three, and the GAP shows the cheap item that may be fundable outside the capital request."}, {"title": "The pre-tertiary pathway", "input": "University and school partnership committee, item: pre-tertiary pathway. Professor Ingrid Solberg (university): We can guarantee audition places, not entry. Mr Kwame Asante (deputy principal, another school): Parents hear 'pathway' and think 'guarantee'. The language matters. Chair: Are we endorsing the pathway document today?", "output": "Q: Endorse pathway document today?\nROOM: IS (uni)  audition places, not entry  !\nROOM: KA (DP)  'parents hear pathway and think guarantee'\nME: ≈ KA: Δ title + 1 plain sentence on what is guaranteed  ★\nME: ✓ endorse subject to wording\nGAP: nobody has proposed the wording", "weak": "Ingrid can only guarantee audition places. Kwame worried about parents misunderstanding the word pathway, I agree, should change title. Endorsing today?", "why": "The right column is a draft of your contribution: a conditional endorsement and a named fix. The GAP line tells you that drafting the wording is the practical move nobody has made."}, {"title": "Funding panel deliberation", "input": "Arts funding panel, item: regional music residencies application. Ms Tamsin Ward (panel): The artists are excellent and the schools are committed. Dr Ahmed Rafiq (panel, online): The budget assumes a vehicle the applicant doesn't own. Chair: Fund, fund with conditions, or decline?", "output": "Q: Fund, fund with conditions, or decline?\nROOM: TW  ✓ artists + schools committed\nROOM: AR (online)  ✗ budget assumes vehicle not owned  !\nME: ★ fund with condition: transport costed before release\nME: # check travel line in budget\nGAP: no one has asked the applicant", "weak": "Tamsin very positive about artists and schools. Ahmed: budget problem with a vehicle. Could fund with conditions. Chair wants decision.", "why": "The chair's question already offers three options; writing them on the Q line lets you answer in the chair's terms. The GAP points to a simple procedural step: seek clarification from the applicant."}, {"title": "The parent body and the concert date", "input": "Music parent committee, item: winter concert date. Mrs Olga Petrenko (president): Week six clashes with the athletics carnival. Mr Simon Faulds (treasurer): Week eight means the hall costs more because it's the weekend rate. Mrs Petrenko: We need a date to give the school by Friday.", "output": "Q: Winter concert: wk 6 or wk 8? Date to school by Fri\nROOM: OP  ✗ wk 6 = athletics carnival\nROOM: SF  ✗ wk 8 = weekend hall rate  #\nME: ? wk 7 weeknight  ★\nGAP: no one has checked week 7", "weak": "Week 6 clashes with athletics. Week 8 more expensive. I'll suggest week 7. Need date by Friday.", "why": "The weak note is brief but has no question and no gap, so it does not show that both options have been ruled out by the room itself. The strong page makes your third option look inevitable rather than contrary."}, {"title": "Conference program committee", "input": "State conference program committee, item: keynote. Dr Moira Kent (academic): A keynote from overseas would draw registrations. Mr Liam Osei (teacher rep): Teachers tell us they come for the practical workshops, not the keynote. Chair: Do we spend the keynote budget on one international speaker or three local ones?", "output": "Q: Keynote budget: 1 international or 3 local?\nROOM: MK  international → registrations\nROOM: LO  'they come for the practical workshops'\nME: ≈ LO: 1 local keynote + 2 workshop leaders  ★\nME: # last yr's registration survey?\nGAP: no one has cited the evidence", "weak": "Moira: international keynote. Liam: teachers want workshops (true!). Choose between one international and three local. Survey data?", "why": "The exclamation 'true!' is your reaction sitting in the record. Moved to the ME column it becomes a structured counter-proposal, and the GAP names the evidence that would settle the question."}, {"title": "Leadership team timetable", "input": "Principal's leadership meeting, item: 2027 timetable structure. Mr Neil Brannigan (deputy): Moving to five periods a day means longer lessons for every subject. Ms Hana Sato (HOLA science): Longer practicals help us; I suspect they hurt music and languages. The principal: I want concerns from each learning area by the next meeting.", "output": "Q: Five periods a day: concerns from each LA by next mtg\nROOM: NB  5 periods → longer lessons\nROOM: HS  ✓ science  'hurt music and languages'\nROOM: Principal  @ concerns by next mtg  !\nME: Δ ensemble rehearsals + instrumental withdrawal model\nME: ★ offer music's impact note, 1 page\nGAP: no one has raised instrumental lesson withdrawal", "weak": "5 periods per day = longer lessons. Hana thinks it will hurt music (yes, big problem for us, IMSS lessons). Principal wants concerns by next meeting.", "why": "On the weak page the most important fact for you, IMSS withdrawal lessons, is buried in a bracket. In the strong page it is the GAP, which is exactly the point you should bring that nobody else in the room can."}, {"title": "SCSA assessment table", "input": "SCSA assessment committee, item: weighting of the aural component. Ms Penelope Hart (SCSA officer): We have consistent feedback that the aural section is too heavily weighted. Dr Isaac Mercer (university, on Zoom): Reduce it and you signal that listening is optional. Chair: The question is whether we recommend any change for consultation.", "output": "Q: Recommend aural weighting change for consultation?\nROOM: PH (SCSA)  feedback: aural too heavy\nROOM: IM (Zoom)  ✗ reduce  'listening is optional'\nME: ≈ IM: keep weight, Δ question types  ★\nME: # our aural results by question type\nGAP: no one has separated weighting from question design", "weak": "Penelope: aural too heavily weighted. Isaac disagrees, says listening would seem optional. Maybe the issue is the question types, not the weight? Recommend for consultation?", "why": "The weak note contains the same insight, but as a passing question inside the record. The ruled page promotes it to the GAP line, which is the frame of your contribution."}, {"title": "Chairing the council on a complaint", "input": "School council (you chair), item: community complaint about weekend band rehearsals. Mr Graham Toft (community member): The neighbours have a point; Sunday morning brass is a lot. Ms Erin Moss (staff rep): The rehearsals are already finished by eleven. The principal: Council needs to decide whether to ask the school to change anything.", "output": "Q: Ask the school to change anything? (yes/no + what)\nROOM: GT  ✓ neighbours  'Sunday morning brass is a lot'\nROOM: EM  # finish by 11\nROOM: Principal  ∴ council decides\nME: Δ start 10 not 9, windows closed, letter to neighbours  ★\nGAP: no one has spoken to the neighbours", "weak": "Complaint about Sunday rehearsals. Graham sympathetic. Erin says they finish by 11. Need to decide. I think a start time change and a letter.", "why": "As chair you will summarise before the decision, so the page must show positions cleanly and keep your view separate until you choose to give it. The GAP is a step the council can take tonight."}], "quiz": [{"input": "You are ruling a page before an SCSA committee item headed 'Response to stakeholder feedback'.", "q": "What should go on the Q line before the item opens?", "options": ["Nothing; wait until the real question emerges.", "The agenda title, written provisionally, ready to be rewritten.", "Your own proposal, so you remember it."], "answer": 1, "why": "Start with the nominal question and treat it as provisional; your proposal belongs in the ME column."}, {"input": "Ms Varga (festival board): Let's not pretend the volunteer roster works. We had three people doing the work of twelve.", "q": "Where does your thought 'I have last year's roster numbers' belong?", "options": ["In the ROOM column beside Ms Varga's line.", "On the GAP line.", "In the ME column."], "answer": 2, "why": "Evidence you hold is yours, and keeping it in ME preserves a clean record of who said what."}, {"input": "Forty minutes into a school council item, everyone has argued about the cost of a new bus. Nobody has mentioned who would drive it.", "q": "Which line would you write?", "options": ["GAP: who drives it (licences, staff hours)?", "ROOM: bus discussed at length.", "ME: ✗ bus."], "answer": 0, "why": "The GAP line records the absence, and absences are where practical contributions live."}, {"input": "Your page from a department meeting reads: 'TC says numbers are low but I think the real issue is timetabling (as usual) and SH agrees with me.'", "q": "What is the main fault?", "options": ["It is too short.", "Reactions and record are mixed, so you cannot see cleanly who said what.", "It uses no symbols."], "answer": 1, "why": "Separation of columns is what lets you credit cleanly and see your own draft at a glance."}, {"input": "The chair of a partnership committee says: 'So, what we're really deciding is whether the university co-badges the program at all.' That was not the agenda wording.", "q": "What should you do with the Q line?", "options": ["Leave it; the agenda governs.", "Erase it and write the chair's version.", "Rewrite it with the chair's version and keep the old one visible."], "answer": 2, "why": "The real question has surfaced; keeping the old version visible shows you the shift, which is useful material."}, {"input": "You are at an arts funding panel with four applications and twenty minutes each.", "q": "How should you lay out your page?", "options": ["A fresh ruled block for each application, each with its own Q and GAP.", "One continuous column of notes for the whole session.", "Only a GAP line per application."], "answer": 0, "why": "Each decision needs its own frame, or the questions bleed into each other."}, {"input": "A colleague asks why you bother with a narrow ME column when you could write your thoughts in the margin.", "q": "What is the best reply?", "options": ["It looks more organised to others.", "The ME column becomes the draft of my contribution while the meeting runs.", "It is the format the minute-taker uses."], "answer": 1, "why": "The column's function is to accumulate your move as you listen, so you arrive at your turn with a draft."}, {"input": "Halfway through a principal's leadership meeting you notice an absence: no one has said who would tell parents about the change.", "q": "When should you write the GAP line?", "options": ["At the end of the meeting, when you can see everything.", "Before the meeting, as a prediction.", "Now, the moment you notice the absence."], "answer": 2, "why": "The GAP stays blank until you genuinely notice an absence, then you write it immediately, before it passes."}], "drills": [{"level": 1, "kind": "write", "input": "Mr Desmond Lyle (department meeting): I'd like us to stop running the instrument audit in Term 4. It's the busiest term we have. Term 1 is quieter and the new students are still settling, so the instruments are mostly in the store anyway. Last year two of us spent the final week of term counting music stands instead of writing reports.", "task": "Rule a page and fill it: a Q line, one or two ROOM lines, one ME line and a GAP line.", "model": "Q: Move instrument audit from T4 to T1?\nROOM: DL  ✗ audit T4  'busiest term we have'\nROOM: DL  Δ T1: instruments mostly in store\nME: ✓ + hire returns due end T4 anyway  ★\nGAP: who does the T1 audit, and by when?"}, {"level": 1, "kind": "write", "input": "Ms Gemma Royce (festival association board): The sponsorship report says we have four partners. Two of them haven't paid this year, and one hasn't answered an email since June. The printer needs final copy in three weeks. I'd like us to decide tonight whether they stay in the printed program.", "task": "Rule a page and fill it: Q, ROOM, ME and GAP. Keep your reaction strictly in the ME column.", "model": "Q: Unpaid partners in the printed program, yes or no, tonight\nROOM: GR  # 4 partners, 2 unpaid  !\nME: # print deadline 3 wks; invoice dates?  ★\nME: Δ yes if paid by print deadline, else removed\nGAP: has anyone called the two partners?"}, {"level": 2, "kind": "write", "input": "School council, item: proposed change to the music camp.\nMs Abigail Fenn (parent representative): Camp is four days and parents tell me it's the best thing their children do all year, but the cost has climbed to nearly six hundred dollars, and that's before the bus. I'd like council to ask whether it could be three days instead.\nMr Victor Lam (staff representative): Three days loses the final concert, which is the whole point of camp; the students rehearse for three days and perform on the fourth. If cost is the problem, the venue is the problem. There's a school campsite at half the price, but it only takes a hundred students.", "task": "Rule a page and fill it: a Q line, one or two ROOM lines per speaker with symbols and one exact phrase each, two ME lines and a GAP line.", "model": "Q: Ask school to shorten camp to 3 days?\nROOM: AF  ✓ camp 'best thing their children do'  # nearly $600 + bus\nROOM: AF  Δ 3 days\nROOM: VL  ✗ 3 days: loses final concert  'the whole point of camp'\nROOM: VL  'the venue is the problem'  # half price, 100 cap\nME: ≈ AF + VL: problem = cost, not length  ★\nME: ? numbers: how many attend? If 100 or fewer, cheaper site works\nGAP: no one has given attendance numbers"}, {"level": 2, "kind": "speak", "input": "SCSA syllabus committee, item: the extended composition task.\nDr Naomi Feld (university, on Zoom): The task asks for eight minutes of original music. In my experience first-year composers can't sustain eight minutes with real craft. I'd rather see four minutes done properly. Students pad to reach the length. Length is not a proxy for ambition.\nMs Kirsten Holt (teacher representative): Four minutes is fine for composers, but the task is also taken by students whose strength is arranging or production. For them the length gives room to show a process. If we shorten it, the portfolio needs to carry that process instead, and I'd want the criteria to say so.", "task": "Rule and fill a page as you read. Then speak for up to 90 seconds from the page: name the question, credit both speakers with their phrases, and fill the gap.", "seconds": 90, "model": "The question, as I understand it, is whether we recommend shortening the extended composition task. Naomi put it well: *length is not a proxy for ambition*. Kirsten's point is the necessary counterweight: for arrangers and producers, length is currently where the process shows, and if we shorten it the portfolio has to carry that process. || What nobody has yet said is how. I'd propose two things. First, four minutes for the composition itself. Second, a short process log as a required part of the portfolio, with three dated drafts, so the assessment sees the decisions, not just the result. I'm happy to draft the log template with Kirsten for the next meeting."}, {"level": 3, "kind": "write", "input": "Festival association board, hybrid, item: whether to add a primary schools division in 2027.\nMs Lorraine Beck (chair): We've had requests from six primary schools. The question tonight is whether we add a primary division next year or wait. We also have a draft schedule from the artistic director.\nMr Declan Shaw (board member): I'm all for it. The future of the festival is in those primary bands. If we don't grow the pipeline, the high school divisions shrink in ten years. Every high school director I know started in a primary band.\nDr Esther Kwan (board member, on Zoom, with a slight delay): Sorry, I missed the start. Can I just say again that the website still has last year's dates? Parents are emailing me, and the contact form bounces.\nMr Shaw: And on the website, the photos are from 2019, which is embarrassing.\nMs Beck: Can we come back to the division?\nMr Pablo Ruiz (board member, quietly): The venue contract only gives us the hall until four on the Saturday. A primary division would have to run Friday, and primary schools have told us before that Friday daytime is hard for their parents to attend. So whatever we decide, the day matters more than the principle.\nMs Beck: So, add it or wait?", "task": "Rule a page as you read: Q line, ROOM lines with initials, symbols and phrases, drift marked, ME lines and a GAP line. No more than ten lines.", "model": "Q: Add primary division 2027, or wait?\nROOM: LB (chair)  # 6 primary schools asking\nROOM: DS  ✓ add  'the future of the festival'  → pipeline\nROOM: EK (Zoom)  website old dates (drift)\nROOM: DS  ≈ EK: photos 2019 (drift)\nROOM: Chair  ⟲ division\nROOM: PR  # hall only till 4 Sat ∴ Friday; ! parents can't attend Fri\nME: ✓ DS on principle; PR's fact decides the format  ★\nME: Δ 2027 pilot: Sat morning, 6 schools, short program; full division 2028\nGAP: no one has asked the six schools what day works; @ website to secretary"}, {"level": 3, "kind": "speak", "input": "Principal's leadership meeting, item: whether to reduce Year 7 specialist music intake from two classes to one.\nThe principal: The department has told me our 2027 enrolments are over capacity. Something has to give, and I'm asking every specialist program the same question, and I'd like each of you to be frank.\nMr Clive Harmon (deputy principal): Music is the most expensive specialist program per student. It's hard to argue for two classes when the general classes are at thirty-two, and the parents of the general classes notice.\nMs Joanne Pak (HOLA English): I'd just say that last time we cut a program, the communication to parents was awful, and we spent a term on complaints, with parents ringing the front office for weeks. I'm not against the change; I'm against doing it badly. We still haven't fixed the newsletter process either.\nMr Harmon: That's true about the newsletter.\nMs Rina Alvarez (business manager, quietly): The music specialist intake is partly funded by the department's specialist program allocation. If the intake drops below a full class, the allocation drops too, so the saving per student is much smaller than it looks. In rough terms, most of what we think we'd save comes back off the allocation.\nThe principal: Dijon?", "task": "Take a ruled page as you read. Then speak for up to 120 seconds: name the real question, credit two speakers by name with exact phrases, bring the quiet fact forward, and propose a way forward with an owner and date.", "seconds": 120, "model": "Thank you. The question, as I hear it, is not whether music gives something up, but what the school actually saves if it does. Clive is right that music is *the most expensive specialist program per student*, and I won't pretend otherwise. But Rina has just told us something that changes the arithmetic: the intake is partly funded by the specialist allocation, so if it drops, *the allocation drops too*, and the saving is much smaller than it looks. || Joanne's warning about communication is also one we should heed, because any change to a selective program reaches families who have already auditioned. So I'd propose three steps. One, Rina and I model the net saving of one class against two, including the allocation, by the next meeting. Two, if the net saving is small, we look at reducing music's elective allocation in Years 9 and 10 instead, where the cost falls. Three, whatever we decide, Joanne and I draft the parent communication before anything is announced. I'm happy to have that minuted as an action for the next meeting."}]}, {"id": "nt-3", "examples": [{"title": "Moderation stays face to face", "input": "Ms Teresa Alvarez (teacher representative, SCSA committee): I'd strongly resist moving the moderation meeting online. It was the only thing last year that teachers said actually changed their marking, and we had forty-two schools attend. Reading the scripts together in one room is what did it.", "output": "ROOM: TA  ✗ moderation online  'actually changed their marking'  # 42 schools  !", "weak": "TA doesn't want marking calibration to go virtual because teachers found it useful; lots of schools came.", "why": "The weak line replaces her nouns ('moderation meeting') with yours ('marking calibration'), loses the figure, and paraphrases the phrase. Using the room's own vocabulary lets you and the minute-taker attach your point to her thread."}, {"title": "The split venue", "input": "Mr Jonah Delacroix (festival association board): I agree with Martin that the concert hall is too dear, but I don't think the answer is staying put. What if we split the festival across two venues over two days: big bands at the hall on the Saturday, concert bands at the current venue on the Friday?", "output": "ROOM: JD  ≈ MR ✗ hall cost  Δ split: big bands hall Sat, concert bands current Fri\nME: ? Fri attendance for concert bands  # 2026 entries by division  ★", "weak": "JD -- agrees w/ MR about cost >> idea of 2 venues 2 days (big bands Sat hall / CB Fri current) ~~ good?", "why": "Improvised marks ('--', '>>', '~~') have to be decoded later, and '~~ good?' mixes your reaction into his line. The fixed set says it exactly: ≈ builds on, ✗ objection, Δ change proposed, and your question moves to the ME line."}, {"title": "Professional learning in Term 1", "input": "Dr Lucy Abernethy (deputy director, curriculum, on Zoom): The new course needs a professional learning program before implementation, not after. Our experience with the last syllabus was that teachers met the document for the first time in February. That is a moderation burden we created ourselves.", "output": "ROOM: LA (Zoom)  Δ PL before implementation  'a moderation burden we created ourselves'  !\nME: ≈ LA → PL T4 2027 for 2028 start  ★", "weak": "Lucy says teachers need training before the course starts, otherwise marking becomes harder, which happened last time.", "why": "'Training' and 'marking becomes harder' are your words. 'PL' and 'moderation burden' are hers and the system's, and quoting them signals fluency in the room's language."}, {"title": "The hardship fund", "input": "Mr Andrew Tully (staff representative, school council): For what it's worth, there's a hardship fund that covers instrument hire. It had eleven thousand dollars in it at the end of last year and I don't think more than a handful of families have ever applied.", "output": "ROOM: AT  ! hardship fund covers hire  # $11k, few applicants\nME: ★ fund exists, nobody knows → Δ letter + enrolment form tick box", "weak": "Andrew mentioned a fund for families in hardship that could help with instruments. Hasn't been used much. Check the amount.", "why": "'Check the amount' means the figure was lost while you wrote words. The # captures it at once, and the ★ marks this as your moment, because the absence is a communication problem you can solve."}, {"title": "A promise, not a menu", "input": "Dr Helen Okafor (university, SCSA committee, on Zoom): Whatever we resubmit, I'd hope we keep the principle that a syllabus is a promise, not a menu. Schools shouldn't be able to choose the easy half of the outcomes and leave the hard half.", "output": "ROOM: HO  'a syllabus is a promise, not a menu'  ✗ schools choosing easy half\nME: ≈ HO → assessment must cover all outcomes  ★", "weak": "Helen: syllabus should be consistent, schools shouldn't pick and choose.", "why": "'Consistent' and 'pick and choose' flatten a memorable phrase into a generality. The quote marks hold the words you will credit; the ≈ shows that your assessment point will be built on hers."}, {"title": "The cost per student", "input": "Mr Martin Rees (treasurer of a partner organisation, festival board): The quote for the concert hall is fourteen thousand dollars more than this year. That's a lot of money for a better foyer. Nobody chooses a festival for the foyer. We'd be renting prestige.", "output": "ROOM: MR  ✗ concert hall  # +$14k  'renting prestige'\nME: # $14k → cost per student entered  ? vs 2026  ★", "weak": "Martin thinks the concert hall is too expensive and is mainly about image. Extra cost quite large.", "why": "'Quite large' replaces a figure you could have used. With the number held, your ME line can do the arithmetic that reframes it, and per-student cost is the language a funding body understands."}, {"title": "Action with owner", "input": "The chair (department meeting): Right, so Kate, could you draft the revised Year 9 assessment outline and circulate it by the end of week four, and we'll sign it off at the week five meeting? Thanks, Kate, that's yours then. Let's make sure it includes the moderation dates.", "output": "ROOM: Chair  @ KM: Yr 9 assess outline, circ end wk 4  → sign off wk 5", "weak": "Kate to do Year 9 assessment thing soon.", "why": "An @ line is only useful if it holds the owner, the task in the room's terms, and the date. Those three are what you will repeat when you summarise, or when you chase it."}, {"title": "Came back to it", "input": "Ms Wendy Halloran (parent representative, school council, later in the item): I want to come back to what Andrew said about the hardship fund twenty minutes ago. If families don't know it exists, then the problem we've been discussing all night isn't the fee at all.", "output": "ROOM: WH  ⟲ AT hardship fund  ∴ problem not the fee  !\nME: ✓ WH + AT  → my ★ moment now", "weak": "Wendy agreed with Andrew from before about the fund.", "why": "The ⟲ shows the room returning to an earlier point, which is a strong signal the real question is shifting. Linking WH to AT gives you two names to credit in one sentence."}, {"title": "Initials for a stranger", "input": "A woman in the second row (state network meeting, not yet introduced): I'm from a district high school. Our problem is not repertoire, it's that we have one teacher covering music for Kindergarten to Year 12. [Later the chair addresses her as 'Bridget'.]", "output": "ROOM: DHS teacher (now: Bridget)  ✗ repertoire not issue  'one teacher, K to 12'  !\nME: ? shared senior ensemble online across DHSs  ★", "weak": "Lady in 2nd row: problem with staffing in small schools.", "why": "Use the role until you have the name, then correct it at once, so you can credit her as Bridget. 'Staffing in small schools' is a category; 'one teacher, K to 12' is the fact."}, {"title": "Agree, object, ask", "input": "Professor Graham Lindqvist (conservatorium, partnership committee): I support the masterclass idea in principle; the students would gain enormously from it. I don't support it running in school hours, because our staff teach then. And I'd want to know who's insuring the instruments when they travel.", "output": "ROOM: GL  ✓ masterclass  ✗ school hours  ? instrument insurance in transit\nME: Δ after-school 4 to 6 pm  @ me: check school insurance  ★", "weak": "Graham generally likes the masterclass idea but has some concerns about timing and insurance.", "why": "'Some concerns' collapses three different moves into one. The symbols separate agreement, objection and question, which tells you precisely what your reply must answer."}, {"title": "Therefore", "input": "Ms Jacinta Moore (SCSA principal consultant): If the committee wants the Board to consider this in March, the documentation must be with the secretariat by the end of January. Nobody does good work in the first fortnight of January, so the real deadline is the end of the school year.", "output": "ROOM: JM (SCSA)  Bd March → docs end Jan  ∴ real deadline end of school yr  !\nME: @ drafting group + dates before Dec  ★", "weak": "Jacinta: deadline end of January for March Board meeting. Holidays a problem.", "why": "'Holidays a problem' records the observation and misses the conclusion. The ∴ carries her logic in one character, and the conclusion is the constraint every proposal must meet."}, {"title": "Two speakers, one line each", "input": "Mr Ben Hollis (school council): The car park is dangerous at pick-up. Last Thursday a car reversed within a metre of a Year 7 student. Ms Sandra Ito (school council): It's dangerous because the concert finishes at the same time as the swimming carnival buses arrive. It's the same three Thursdays every term.", "output": "ROOM: BH  ! car park dangerous at pick-up  # Yr 7, 1 metre\nROOM: SI  ≈ BH  → cause: concert end = carnival buses arrive\nME: Δ concert ends 15 min later  ★", "weak": "Car park safety discussed. Ben and Sandra both concerned. Something about the swimming carnival.", "why": "One line per speaker keeps attribution clean, and the ≈ shows Sandra builds on Ben by naming a cause. 'Something about' is what you write when you were writing rather than listening."}], "quiz": [{"input": "Dr Fiona Gale (SCSA): The Board's issue is comparability of the school-assessed portfolio.", "q": "Which note uses the speaker's own nouns?", "options": ["FG  Bd: marking consistency of folio", "FG  Bd = 'comparability' of school-assessed portfolio  !", "FG  Board worried about fairness of projects"], "answer": 1, "why": "Her term 'comparability' is the Board's technical concept; paraphrasing it as fairness or consistency loses the thread the minutes will use."}, {"input": "You want to mark that a speaker's point builds on what an earlier speaker said.", "q": "Which symbol do you use?", "options": ["≈", "→", "⟲"], "answer": 0, "why": "≈ means same as or builds on; → is a consequence and ⟲ is a return to an earlier point."}, {"input": "Mid-meeting you invent a new mark, a wavy underline, to mean 'contested'.", "q": "What is the main problem?", "options": ["It takes too long to draw.", "Other people may see it.", "A symbol you must decode later is worse than a word."], "answer": 2, "why": "The value of a fixed set is that it is read without thought; improvised marks cost attention at exactly the wrong moment."}, {"input": "Mr Paolo Russo (board): I'll move that the treasurer prepares a costed option by the February meeting.", "q": "Which line is best?", "options": ["PR motion: treasurer to cost options", "@ Treasurer: costed option, Feb mtg  (PR moved)", "PR  ✓ costing"], "answer": 1, "why": "An action line needs the @ owner, the task and the date; the mover's initials keep the credit."}, {"input": "Ms Emma Lowe (council): We spent thirty-eight thousand on the oval lights and they're used twice a year.", "q": "What must not be lost in your note?", "options": ["# $38k and 'twice a year'", "That she seemed annoyed.", "The word 'oval'."], "answer": 0, "why": "Figures are the part you cannot reconstruct, and the two together carry her argument."}, {"input": "You have an urge to speak right now, but the chair has just opened the item and two people are already in the queue.", "q": "What should you write?", "options": ["Your full contribution, so you are ready.", "Nothing; wait for your turn to plan.", "★ in your ME column beside the line your contribution will answer."], "answer": 2, "why": "★ fixes where you intend to enter, which stops you entering too early or not at all."}, {"input": "Dr Arun Nair (university, on Zoom): We tried a version of this at another university; it collapsed because no one owned the timetable.", "q": "Which line captures the function of his point best?", "options": ["AN  tried elsewhere, failed", "AN  ! precedent: collapsed  'no one owned the timetable'  → @ owner needed", "AN  had a bad experience at another uni"], "answer": 1, "why": "It keeps his phrase and uses → to show what follows for your proposal: an owner."}, {"input": "A speaker's contribution at a funding panel runs three minutes: a story, a figure, an objection and a closing phrase.", "q": "How many lines should it take?", "options": ["About one line per thirty seconds, six lines.", "One line per sentence she says.", "One or two lines: objection, figure, phrase."], "answer": 2, "why": "Write when something changes; a story with one figure and one objection is one or two lines, not a transcript."}], "drills": [{"level": 1, "kind": "write", "input": "Ms Delia Brooks (assistant principal, leadership meeting): I'm not against the music tour. I'm against it being in Term 3, week two, when the Year 12s have their mock exams. Move it to the holidays or the end of Term 2 and I'll sign it.", "task": "Convert this into a single note line using the fixed symbol set, her nouns, and one exact phrase.", "model": "ROOM: DB (AP)  ✓ tour  ✗ T3 wk 2 = Yr 12 mocks  Δ holidays or end T2  'I'll sign it'"}, {"level": 1, "kind": "write", "input": "Write out the full symbol set from memory with a meaning for each. Then convert: Mr Tim Fairweather (council): The new uniform supplier costs eighteen per cent more, and I'd like the business manager to get two more quotes by the March meeting.", "task": "List the thirteen symbols with meanings, then write one note line for Mr Fairweather.", "model": "→ leads to, so; ✓ agree; ✗ object; ? question to raise; ★ my moment; ≈ same as, builds on; Δ change proposed; ∴ therefore; ! important; '…' exact words to credit; @ action and owner; # number or figure; ⟲ came back to an earlier point.\nROOM: TF  ✗ new supplier # +18%  @ bus mgr: 2 more quotes, March mtg"}, {"level": 2, "kind": "write", "input": "SCSA syllabus committee, item: the ensemble performance requirement.\nDr Rosa Lindgren (university, on Zoom): I'd keep the ensemble requirement exactly as it is. It's the only place in the course where students are assessed on listening to each other in real time, and that's the professional skill we most want.\nMr Harvey Quinn (teacher representative, regional school): In principle, yes. In practice, I have two Year 12 music students this year. Two. There's no ensemble for them to be assessed in. Last year one of them recorded with a community band, and the marker queried whether it counted. I'd like a ruling.", "task": "Write no more than five lines using only the fixed symbols, initials, the speakers' own nouns and one phrase each, then an ME line with ★.", "model": "ROOM: RL (Zoom)  ✓ keep ensemble req  'listening to each other in real time'\nROOM: HQ (regional)  ✓ principle  # 2 Yr 12 students  ✗ no ensemble\nROOM: HQ  community band queried  ? ruling  !\nME: ≈ RL + HQ: keep req, Δ define eligible ensembles incl. community  ★\nME: @ SCSA: written ruling before 2027 exams"}, {"level": 2, "kind": "speak", "input": "Festival association board, item: the adjudicator panel for 2027.\nMs Nadia Harcourt (board): We've used the same four adjudicators for six years. They are excellent and the schools trust them, but the festival is starting to sound like their taste. Three schools have told us informally that they now choose repertoire to suit particular judges.\nMr Lewis Brand (board): Changing all four at once would be a mistake. Schools would read it as a vote of no confidence. If we rotate, rotate one a year. These are people who have given us years of goodwill, and good adjudicators are harder to recruit than people think.", "task": "Write a two to four line symbol note as you read. Then speak for up to 60 seconds from it, using both speakers' own words.", "seconds": 60, "model": "Nadia's point is one I share: four adjudicators for six years, and the festival is *starting to sound like their taste*. Lewis is right that changing all four would read as a vote of no confidence. So the two points meet in one proposal: rotate one adjudicator a year, starting in 2027, and publish the rotation as policy so that no individual change reads as a judgement. I'd suggest the chair and I draft that policy for the February meeting."}, {"level": 3, "kind": "write", "input": "University and schools partnership committee, hybrid, item: the teacher education placement model for music.\nProfessor Anita Ferraro (university, in the room): We're proposing to move music placements from a four-week block to one day a week across the semester. The research on distributed practice is persuasive, and our students tell us the block is a performance, not an apprenticeship. We'd want to start in Semester 1.\nMs Claire Dobson (principal, in the room): One day a week is harder for schools. Our mentors would have a placement student every Tuesday for fifteen weeks. That's a lot of supervision for a teacher who already has a full load, and we don't get relief funding for it. I'm not saying no. I'm saying it isn't free.\nMr Rhys Penhallow (deputy principal, on Zoom, with a delay): Sorry, can I go back to the parking? The students last semester were getting fined in the staff car park.\nMs Dobson: Yes, that was a nightmare.\nDr Mei Tanaka (university placement officer, quietly): For information, the university already pays a mentor stipend per placement day. It's the same total whether it's a block or distributed, so the funding question is really about relief, not payment.\nProfessor Ferraro: So can we agree to pilot it?", "task": "Write a note page of no more than nine lines using only the fixed symbols. Mark the drift, keep each speaker's own nouns, and make sure the quiet fact is flagged with !.", "model": "Q: Pilot 1 day per wk music placements?\nROOM: AF  Δ 4-wk block → 1 day per wk  'a performance, not an apprenticeship'\nROOM: DC  ✗ load on mentors  # 15 Tuesdays  no relief funding\nROOM: RP (Zoom)  car park fines (drift)\nROOM: DC  ≈ RP (drift)  ⟲ AF\nROOM: MT  ! mentor stipend same total either way  ∴ issue = relief, not payment\nME: ≈ AF + DC + MT: pilot with 2 schools, stipend pooled for relief days  ★\nME: @ MT: costing; @ schools: 2 mentors each\nGAP: no one has asked the mentors"}, {"level": 3, "kind": "speak", "input": "School council, item: proposed change to the school's homework policy.\nMr Felix Grant (chair of the policy subcommittee): The draft policy limits homework to an hour a night in Years 7 to 9. It's evidence-informed, it's in line with neighbouring schools, and frankly it's overdue. We've consulted staff and the student council.\nMs Ruth Achterberg (parent representative): I support it, but the music students also have instrument practice. If the school counts practice as homework, a lot of them are already over the limit. My daughter does an hour of cello most nights. If it doesn't, we're telling them practice doesn't matter. Families will take whichever reading suits them.\nMr Owen Patel (community member): While we're on the subject of overloaded students, I've been meaning to raise the bus timetable. Half the kids from my street can't get home before five, and the bus shelter on the corner has no roof.\nMr Grant: That's a fair point but a different item. Let's put it on the next agenda, Owen.\nMs Fay Liddell (staff representative, quietly): The specialist music program already has a practice expectation in its handbook, thirty minutes a day. It was approved by council in 2022. It also says classroom teachers should be made aware of it, though I'm not sure they are.", "task": "Write a symbol note as you read. Then speak for up to 120 seconds from it: credit two speakers in their own words, use the quiet fact, and propose wording for the policy.", "seconds": 120, "model": "Thank you, Felix. I support the draft, and I'd like to resolve the point Ruth raised, because she's put the dilemma exactly: if practice counts as homework, the music students *are already over the limit*, and if it doesn't, *we're telling them practice doesn't matter*. || Fay has just given us the way through. Council already approved a practice expectation of thirty minutes a day for the specialist program in 2022. So the policy doesn't need to invent anything; it needs to refer to what council has already decided. I'd propose one added clause: 'Instrumental practice set under a specialist program handbook approved by council is recognised separately from homework under this policy, and teachers will take it into account when setting homework.' That keeps the hour, protects practice, and avoids a second policy. Owen's bus point deserves its own item, and I'd suggest it goes on the next agenda."}]}, {"id": "nt-4", "examples": [{"title": "Weight, not width", "input": "Dr Simon Lim (SCSA committee, in the room): When I read the Board's letter, the issue isn't the amount of content. It's how much each task counts for. If I had to put it in a phrase, the problem is weight, not width. We could keep every outcome and still fix it.", "output": "NOTE:\nROOM: SL  'weight, not width'  ✓ keep outcomes\nME: ≈ SL → reweight U3 tasks  ★\n\nSPOKEN:\nSimon put it in four words: *weight, not width*. | If he's right, and I think he is, then we can answer the Board without losing a single outcome, by reweighting two Unit 3 tasks.", "weak": "As Simon was saying, the Board seems to be worried about how the assessment is balanced rather than the amount of content, so maybe we could look at that.", "why": "The weak version credits a paraphrase, which sounds like a summary. Quoting his own compression signals respect and lets you borrow its authority for a move that follows in the same breath."}, {"title": "Priced out by stealth", "input": "Ms Karen Pryor (P&C president, school council): Nobody has decided to make the program unaffordable. It's just that every year there's another ten dollars here, another bus there, and families who were fine in Year 7 are quietly leaving by Year 10. They're being priced out by stealth.", "output": "NOTE:\nROOM: KP  'priced out by stealth'  # leaving by Yr 10\nME: ✓ KP → annual cost cap + 1 published total  ★\n\nSPOKEN:\nKaren described families being *priced out by stealth*, and I think that's exactly the mechanism. | Stealth is the problem, so visibility is the remedy: I'd propose we publish one total annual cost for the program and cap increases at CPI.", "weak": "Karen made an important point about costs gradually going up and families leaving. I agree with Karen that cost is an issue.", "why": "'I agree with Karen' is credit without a move, which is applause. The strong version turns her metaphor into the logic of the proposal: stealth answered by visibility."}, {"title": "The partial phrase", "input": "Professor Eleanor Marsh (university, on Zoom, audio breaking up): The danger with a portfolio is that it rewards the students with the most... [audio drops] ...not the most musical ones. We need to assess the decisions, not the polish. Polish can be bought.", "output": "NOTE:\nROOM: EM (Zoom)  portfolio rewards '… not the most musical'  'assess the decisions, not the polish'  !\nME: ≈ EM → process log, 3 dated drafts  ★\n\nSPOKEN:\nEleanor's audio dropped for a moment, but the line that came through clearly was that we should *assess the decisions, not the polish*. I'd like to give that a mechanism: a process log with three dated drafts.", "weak": "Eleanor said portfolios reward the students with the most resources and money rather than the most musical ones, so we should assess the decisions, not the polish.", "why": "The weak version fills the dropped words from imagination and then credits her with them. The strong note marks the gap with an ellipsis and credits only the phrase that was actually heard."}, {"title": "Too long to be a phrase", "input": "Mr Darren Holt (festival association board): We need to remember that for a lot of these country schools, coming to the festival is the one time in the year that their students hear another school's band play, and that is what the whole thing is for.", "output": "NOTE:\nROOM: DH  country schools  'the one time … hear another school's band'\nME: ≈ DH → keep listening sessions in schedule  ★\n\nSPOKEN:\nDarren reminded us that for country schools this is *the one time* their students hear another school's band. | That's the argument for protecting the listening sessions when we compress the schedule.", "weak": "ROOM: DH 'for a lot of these country schools, coming to the festival is the one time in the year that their students hear another school's band play'", "why": "A twenty-five word quote is a paraphrase in disguise and cannot be retrieved under pressure. Six or eight words, with an ellipsis for the connective tissue, are enough to credit accurately."}, {"title": "Listening for the pause", "input": "Ms Beatrice Odum (deputy director general, at a conference panel): We've built a system that is very good at measuring what students know. [She pauses.] We are much less good at measuring what they can make. And the arts are about what they can make.", "output": "NOTE:\nROOM: BO (DDG)  'what they can make'  ! (pause before)\nME: → assessment of making: performance + composition evidence  ★\n\nSPOKEN:\nThe Deputy Director General said that we are much less good at measuring *what students can make*. | Music is one of the few places where the system already does that, and I'd like to offer the panel two examples of how.", "weak": "The DDG talked about the importance of the arts and creativity in the curriculum and how we need to measure more than knowledge.", "why": "A speaker's pause before a phrase usually marks the line they prepared. Catching it verbatim, before writing anything else, gives you the hinge of your question from the floor."}, {"title": "Initials before the quote", "input": "Festival association board, item: sponsorship renewal. Two board members speak in quick succession. Mr Amir Haddad: Businesses give because they get something. Sponsorship isn't charity; it's a transaction. Ms Phoebe Lane: And at the moment we're offering a logo on a program nobody keeps.", "output": "NOTE:\nROOM: AH  'isn't charity; it's a transaction'\nROOM: PL  ≈ AH  'a logo on a program nobody keeps'\nME: ★ partnership offer: band at partner's event\n\nSPOKEN:\nAmir reminded us that sponsorship *isn't charity, it's a transaction*, and Phoebe told us what we're currently transacting: *a logo on a program nobody keeps*. | So let's change the offer.", "weak": "ROOM: 'isn't charity, it's a transaction'  'logo on a program nobody keeps'  sponsorship", "why": "Two orphaned quotes on one line cannot be credited with confidence, and crediting the wrong person is worse than not crediting. Initials before each quote keep attribution fixed."}, {"title": "Credit followed by the move", "input": "Dr Nikhil Rao (university, partnership committee, on the pre-tertiary pathway): Most of the students who apply to us come from four schools. I don't think talent is that concentrated. A pathway that only the confident students know about isn't a pathway, it's a secret.", "output": "NOTE:\nROOM: NR  'isn't a pathway, it's a secret'\nME: ≈ NR → Δ info session for all Yr 10 families, T3  ★\n\nSPOKEN:\nNikhil said a pathway that only the confident students know about *isn't a pathway, it's a secret*. | I'd like to make it public: an information session for every Year 10 music family in Term 3, run jointly with the university.", "weak": "Nikhil made a great point there, a pathway that only some students know about is basically a secret. Great point.", "why": "Praise without a move is applause, and repeated praise sounds anxious. The strong version credits once and turns his diagnosis into a concrete action in the next sentence."}, {"title": "Verbatim beats elegant", "input": "Ms Janet Farrow (school council, community member, plain-spoken): Look, I'm not a musician. But when my grandson's band played at the assembly, it was the first time I'd seen the whole hall go quiet for students. That's worth paying for.", "output": "NOTE:\nROOM: JF  'the whole hall go quiet for students'  ✓ worth paying for\nME: ≈ JF → assembly performances in the annual plan  ★\n\nSPOKEN:\nJanet described seeing *the whole hall go quiet for students*. | I'd like council to make that a regular part of the school year: one ensemble at every whole-school assembly, written into next year's plan.", "weak": "As Janet so eloquently put it, music creates a profound sense of community and attention within the school.", "why": "Improving a plain speaker's words into your own polish erases her and sounds patronising. Her exact, unadorned phrase is more vivid than the paraphrase and honours her contribution."}, {"title": "The phrase as summary", "input": "Chair (SCSA committee): We've heard three views. Dr Okafor said the syllabus is a promise, not a menu. Mr Ferreira said the timeline is the real constraint. And Ms Teale said teachers need the documents a term before, not a week before.", "output": "NOTE:\nROOM: HO  'a promise, not a menu'\nROOM: TF  'the timeline is the real constraint'\nROOM: BT  'a term before, not a week before'\nME: ≈ all 3 → 1 timeline delivering full syllabus + docs T3  ★\n\nSPOKEN:\nThe three phrases the chair has just repeated fit together. A syllabus is *a promise*; the timeline is *the real constraint*; and teachers need the documents *a term before*. So the proposal writes itself: a timeline that delivers the whole syllabus, and its documents, by Term 3.", "weak": "I agree with all three speakers. There are some good points about the syllabus, timing and teacher preparation.", "why": "Three exact phrases woven into one sentence is the classic shape of a strong summary; a list of topics is not. Each credit also becomes a premise of your proposal."}, {"title": "Correcting a misheard phrase", "input": "Arts funding panel, deliberating on a regional residency application. Ms Laila Hussein (online) makes her point and you write: 'LH 'quality over quantity''. A moment later she repeats herself: 'As I said, three hundred students seeing a band once is reach. Reach without depth is just marketing.'", "output": "NOTE:\nROOM: LH (online)  'reach without depth is just marketing'  (not 'quality over quantity')\nME: → our 3-visit model per school  ★\n\nSPOKEN:\nLaila's phrase was that *reach without depth is just marketing*. | That's exactly why the residency visits each school three times rather than touring once.", "weak": "As Laila said, quality over quantity, and I agree.", "why": "When a speaker repeats a point, they usually say it in their prepared form; overwrite your first capture. Crediting a cliché she did not say would be both inaccurate and weaker."}, {"title": "Crediting the quiet expert", "input": "SCSA assessment committee, item: moderation of the performance exam. After forty minutes of debate about extending the moderation day, Mr Hamish Corr (SCSA officer, briefly and quietly near the end): For what it's worth, nearly all the marker variance last year came from three criteria out of twelve.", "output": "NOTE:\nROOM: HC  ! 'three criteria out of twelve'  # variance\nME: ≈ HC → calibration on those 3 only, online, before the day  ★\n\nSPOKEN:\nHamish mentioned, almost in passing, that nearly all the variance came from *three criteria out of twelve*. | I think that's the most important thing said today, because it means calibration can be focused, short and done online before the moderation day.", "weak": "Someone mentioned that most of the variance came from a few criteria, so perhaps we could focus on those.", "why": "'Someone mentioned' wastes the most useful fact in the meeting and the chance to lift a quiet expert's standing. Naming him and his exact figure gives the fact the weight the room missed."}, {"title": "A phrase to answer, not adopt", "input": "Festival association board, item: proposed primary schools division. Mr Marcus Rowe (board member, contrarian): Honestly, the primary division is mission creep. We are a high school festival. We do one thing well. If we add primary bands, we'll do two things adequately.", "output": "NOTE:\nROOM: MR  ✗ primary div  'mission creep'\nME: ? test against our constitution's objects: 'school students' or 'high school'?  ★\n\nSPOKEN:\nMarcus called the primary division *mission creep*, and it's a fair test to apply. | So let's apply it to our objects. If the constitution speaks of school students rather than high school students, then on our own terms the primary division isn't creep; it's the mission. I'd suggest we read the clause into the minutes before we vote.", "weak": "I don't agree with Marcus that it's mission creep. I think it's important for the future.", "why": "Quoting a contrarian's exact phrase and then testing it against evidence answers him on his own ground, with courtesy and with a test the room can check. Contradiction alone only restates disagreement."}], "quiz": [{"input": "Dr Alice Ferro (university): We keep treating the exam as the course. It isn't. The exam is the receipt, not the purchase.", "q": "What should you write first?", "options": ["AF  'the receipt, not the purchase'", "AF  exam ✗ course", "AF  argues exam overemphasised"], "answer": 0, "why": "Write the exact phrase the instant you hear it; the keyword can be reconstructed later, the phrase cannot."}, {"input": "You caught 'a ladder with the bottom rungs …' before a cough drowned out the end.", "q": "How should you credit it?", "options": ["Complete it with the likeliest ending, 'missing', and credit the full phrase.", "Credit what you heard, or the point in your own words without quote marks.", "Attribute it to the chair, who repeated the idea."], "answer": 1, "why": "Crediting someone with words they did not say is worse than not crediting them."}, {"input": "Ms Gita Roy (council): It's not a fee, it's a filter.", "q": "Which spoken line uses the credit best?", "options": ["Gita made a really good point about fees. I agree with her.", "As Gita said, fees can stop some families joining, which is a concern.", "Gita said it's *not a fee, it's a filter*. | So let's measure who it filters out: I'll bring hire and withdrawal data by family postcode to the next meeting."], "answer": 2, "why": "Verbatim credit followed at once by your own move is leadership; paraphrase or praise alone is not."}, {"input": "A speaker's contribution includes this sentence: 'And I think, in the end, what we're really talking about here is whether the students who need us most can actually get into the room.'", "q": "Which capture is best?", "options": ["The whole sentence in quote marks.", "'students who need us most … into the room'", "'access issue'"], "answer": 1, "why": "Six to eight words are retrievable; the ellipsis keeps it honest; 'access issue' is your label, not their words."}, {"input": "Two people are speaking in quick succession at a board meeting and you have caught two good phrases.", "q": "What is the essential habit?", "options": ["Put the initials immediately before each quote.", "Write both phrases and assign them afterwards.", "Keep only the better phrase."], "answer": 0, "why": "An orphaned quote cannot be credited safely; initials before the quote fix attribution as you write."}, {"input": "Mr Lars Eklund (partnership committee) says one sharp line, 'calendar, not curriculum', and spends the next four minutes elaborating.", "q": "What does the four minutes probably add to your note?", "options": ["A full line for each example he gives.", "Most of the substance; the phrase is a decoration.", "Very little; the phrase is his compression, so add a figure or condition only if one appears."], "answer": 2, "why": "Articulate speakers compress their argument into one prepared phrase; the elaboration rarely needs recording."}, {"input": "You plan to open by crediting Dr Okafor's phrase 'a promise, not a menu'.", "q": "What should the next sentence do?", "options": ["Explain why her phrase was so apt.", "Make your own move that builds on the phrase.", "Credit a second speaker."], "answer": 1, "why": "Credit without a move is applause; the phrase is the hinge into your proposal."}, {"input": "A plain-spoken parent on a council says, 'The kids light up when the band plays at assembly.'", "q": "How should you credit her?", "options": ["As Mrs Kemp put it, music fosters engagement and wellbeing.", "Several people have noted music's impact on students.", "Mrs Kemp said the kids *light up* when the band plays."], "answer": 2, "why": "Her own words are more vivid than any elevated paraphrase, and quoting them honours rather than translates her."}], "drills": [{"level": 1, "kind": "write", "input": "Ms Odette Barnes (department meeting): I don't mind the extra concert. What I mind is that every new event arrives as a favour and leaves as a tradition. Nobody ever takes anything off the calendar. Last year we added two and removed none.", "task": "Write one note line with her initials and the phrase you would credit, then one ME line with your move.", "model": "ROOM: OB  ✓ concert  'arrives as a favour and leaves as a tradition'  # 2 added, 0 removed  !\nME: ≈ OB → Δ rule: 1 event added, 1 reviewed for removal  ★"}, {"level": 1, "kind": "speak", "input": "Mr Stuart Kemble (school council): We talk about the music program as if it belongs to the music department. It belongs to the school. When the bands play at the ANZAC service, nobody thinks of it as a department activity.", "task": "Capture his phrase, then speak two sentences: one crediting him by name with his exact words, one making your own move.", "seconds": 30, "model": "Stuart said the music program *belongs to the school*, not to the department. | I'd like council to act on that by listing the ANZAC service and the presentation evening performances as whole-school commitments in the annual plan."}, {"level": 2, "kind": "write", "input": "Arts funding panel, deliberation on a youth orchestra application.\nDr Ursula Kemp (panel): The artistic plan is ambitious, and I like that. My worry is that the application describes the orchestra the applicant wishes it had. The rehearsal schedule assumes forty players and their own report says they had twenty-six last year.\nMr Harold Tran (panel, online): I'd fund it. Ambition is the point of a development grant. But I'd want a condition, something like 'a plan for the players you have, not the players you hope for'. Let them show us the twenty-six version. That's the right kind of risk for us to take.", "task": "Capture one exact phrase from each speaker, attached to initials, and write an ME line that uses both.", "model": "ROOM: UK  'the orchestra the applicant wishes it had'  # 40 assumed vs 26 actual\nROOM: HT (online)  ✓ fund + condition  'the players you have, not the players you hope for'\nME: ≈ UK + HT → fund, conditional on 26-player schedule; growth as stretch target  ★"}, {"level": 2, "kind": "speak", "input": "SCSA syllabus committee, item: the jazz and contemporary context.\nDr Wilhelmina Ashe (university, on Zoom): Students study jazz in this course as if it were a museum. They learn names and dates. They rarely learn that improvisation is a discipline with its own grammar. Ask a student what a ii V I does and they'll tell you its name.\nMr Corey Mansfield (teacher representative): I'd agree, but teachers will need help. Many of us were trained classically. If you want improvisation assessed, give us exemplars, not just outcomes. Recordings at a few standards, so we can hear what a pass sounds like.", "task": "Capture both phrases. Then speak for up to 75 seconds: credit each speaker by name with their exact words and make a proposal that answers both.", "seconds": 75, "model": "Wilhelmina's image is exact: students meet jazz *as if it were a museum*, names and dates, rather than as a discipline *with its own grammar*. And Corey has put the condition on fixing it: *give us exemplars, not just outcomes*. || I'd propose we do both in one step. First, an outcome that assesses improvisation as grammar: the use of chord tones, motif and response over a set form. Second, a bank of six annotated exemplar recordings at three standards, published with the syllabus. I'd be glad to work with Wilhelmina on the outcome wording and with Corey on the exemplars for the next meeting."}, {"level": 3, "kind": "write", "input": "School council, hybrid, item: whether to endorse a proposed partnership with a private music academy to deliver after-school lessons on site.\nMs Delphine Royce (principal): The academy has approached us. They'd use our rooms after hours, pay a modest fee, and offer lessons our IMSS staff don't cover, like drum kit and voice. It would also bring in a little income for the music department, which we could use for repairs.\nMr Barry Coleman (parent representative): I'm wary. We'd be letting a business sell to our families on our premises, with our logo on the flyer. That's not a partnership, it's a shopfront. And when parents are unhappy with the academy's teachers, they'll complain to us.\nMs Helen Voss (community member, on Zoom): Sorry, I was frozen for a minute. Has anyone looked at the hall booking system? My choir couldn't get a booking this year at all. We ended up in a church hall.\nMr Coleman: That's a whole other conversation.\nMs Harriet Lowe (staff representative, quietly): The Department's policy on use of school facilities requires a formal hire agreement and public liability cover for any commercial user. We'd also have to make sure it doesn't compete with the instrumental service.\nThe principal: So are we comfortable to endorse in principle?", "task": "Write a note page that captures one exact phrase per substantive speaker, attached to initials, marks the drift, and holds the quiet fact. Then add an ME line that would open your contribution with a credit.", "model": "Q: Endorse academy partnership in principle?\nROOM: DR (principal)  rooms after hours, fee  # kit + voice not covered by IMSS\nROOM: BC  ✗  'not a partnership, it's a shopfront'\nROOM: HV (Zoom)  hall bookings (drift)  ⟲ BC\nROOM: HL  ! Dept policy: hire agreement + public liability  'doesn't compete with' IMSS\nME: ≈ BC + HL: endorse only as facility hire, no school logo, kit + voice only  ★\nME: open: 'Barry called it a shopfront; Harriet's point tells us how to make it something else.'\nGAP: no one has asked IMSS"}, {"level": 3, "kind": "speak", "input": "Festival association board, item: response to a complaint from a school about an adjudicator's written comments.\nMs Bernadette Kalu (chair): A director has complained that an adjudicator's comments on her Year 8 band were harsh. She's copied in her principal and would like a reply this week. I'd like us to decide tonight whether we respond formally.\nMr Quentin Hale (board member): I've read the comments. They're blunt, but they're accurate. I've adjudicated for twenty years, and the kindest thing you can do for a young band is tell it the truth. They'll be a better band for it next year. If we start softening adjudication to protect feelings, we become a participation certificate with a trophy table.\nMs Ivy Tran (board member, online, lagging): Sorry, could someone tell me whether this is the same school that complained about the parking? Because that was also a complaint about tone, if I remember rightly...\nMr Hale: Different school, I think.\nMr Roland Pike (board member, quietly): Just one thing. Our adjudicator guidelines say comments for junior divisions should be framed around next steps. That's in the brief we sent them in July. These comments don't have any next steps at all; they're a list of faults.\nMs Kalu: Views?", "task": "Capture one phrase from each of two speakers and the quiet fact. Then speak for up to 120 seconds: credit two speakers by name with exact phrases and propose the board's response.", "seconds": 120, "model": "Thank you, Chair. I think we can respond well and quickly. Quentin is right that we must not become *a participation certificate with a trophy table*; accuracy is the whole value of adjudication. But Roland has just reminded us of something that settles the question: our own brief says comments for junior divisions are *framed around next steps*. || So the issue is not whether the comments were too honest, but whether they followed our brief. I'd propose three things. One, the chair writes to the director acknowledging the complaint, confirming that the comments were accurate, and noting that junior comments should include next steps. Two, the artistic director adds two next-step suggestions to the written feedback for that band, with the adjudicator's agreement. Three, we restate the junior division guideline at the adjudicators' briefing next year. If the board agrees, I'd suggest the chair's letter goes this week."}]}, {"id": "nt-5", "examples": [{"title": "The vehicle, not the content", "input": "SCSA committee, item: 'Response to Board feedback on the draft course'. After ten minutes, Ms Diane Pereira (SCSA director) says: What we really need to decide is the vehicle. Do we revise and resubmit this as a course for 2028, or do we reframe it as an endorsed program schools can take up now? The content arguments follow from that.", "output": "Q (was): Response to Bd feedback\nQ: Resubmit for 2028, or reframe as endorsed program now?\nROOM: DP  'what we really need to decide is the vehicle'  !\nME: ★ answer the vehicle Q first, content second", "weak": "Q: Response to Board feedback\nROOM: DP talked about options for the course, resubmitting or endorsed program.", "why": "A senior reframing ('what we really need to decide is') is the clearest signal that the real question has surfaced. Rewriting the Q line, and keeping the old one visible, shows you the distance the room has travelled and who moved it."}, {"title": "Consultation grievance", "input": "SCSA committee, same item, later. Mr Keith Walsh (teacher representative): Can I just say the consultation on this course was rushed from the start. Mr Robert Tan (university): I agree, and it was the same in 2019 with the last revision; we had three weeks. Mr Walsh: Exactly, and nobody ever reports back on what happened to the submissions.", "output": "ROOM: KW  consultation 'rushed from the start'  (drift)\nROOM: RT  ≈ KW  # 2019: 3 wks  (drift)\nROOM: KW  no report back on submissions  (drift)\nME: ✓ minute it; then restore Q: vehicle  ★", "weak": "ROOM: KW consultation rushed\nROOM: RT agrees, 2019 three weeks\nROOM: KW no reporting back\nME: agree, consultation is a big issue, raise this", "why": "The weak page treats a legitimate grievance as the substance of the item, and your ME line is drawn into it. Marking it as drift lets you honour it briefly and then bring the room back."}, {"title": "The restoring move", "input": "Mr Walsh has just finished saying that nobody ever reports back on submissions, and two members on Zoom are nodding. Your notes show eight minutes of drift about consultation (KW, RT) after Ms Pereira's reframing: 'what we really need to decide is the vehicle'. The chair looks around for the next speaker.", "output": "NOTE:\nQ: Resubmit 2028, or endorsed program now?\nROOM: DP  'decide is the vehicle'\nROOM: KW, RT  consultation (drift)\nME: ★ restore Q, credit DP\n\nSPOKEN:\nThese are important points about consultation, and I'd like them minuted so they don't get lost. | On the question in front of us, though, Diane put it exactly: what we really need to decide is *the vehicle*. Resubmit for 2028, or reframe as an endorsed program now. I'd like to speak to that.", "weak": "I think we're getting off track here. Can we get back to the actual agenda item?", "why": "The weak version restores the question by making two colleagues wrong. The strong one acknowledges the drift as valuable, routes it to the minutes, credits the reframing, and states the real question in one sentence."}, {"title": "The paper says fees", "input": "School council, item: 'Instrument hire fee for 2027'. Ms Carla Diaz (parent): The fee isn't really the problem. Ms Yvonne Bright (staff): Agreed. Most families who leave the program never mention money; they say they didn't know what help was available. Chair: So is the question the fee or the support?", "output": "Q (was): Set 2027 hire fee\nQ: Do families know what support exists, and who tells them?\nROOM: CD  'the fee isn't really the problem'\nROOM: YB  ≈ CD  families 'didn't know what help was available'  !\nROOM: Chair  ? fee or support\nME: ★ answer both: hold fee + Δ support letter at enrolment", "weak": "Q: Set 2027 hire fee\nROOM: CD, YB: fee not really the issue, it's about support\nME: hire fee options: hold or increase CPI?", "why": "Two speakers disagreeing with the paper's framing is a signal the real question has moved. The weak page keeps answering the nominal question, so your contribution would arrive one question behind the room."}, {"title": "The anecdote that becomes a tangent", "input": "Festival association board, item: 2027 adjudicator panel. Mr Gordon Fyfe (board): That reminds me of the year we had the adjudicator from Melbourne who missed his flight and judged the whole morning by phone. Ms Naomi Kirk: Oh, that was 2014, wasn't it? Mr Fyfe: 2015. The phone kept dropping out in the ballads. [Laughter. Four minutes pass.]", "output": "ROOM: GF  2015 phone adjudicator story  (drift)\nROOM: NK  ≈ GF  (drift, 4 min)\nME: ★ re-enter: 'which is why backup adjudicator' → restore Q", "weak": "ROOM: GF story about Melbourne adjudicator 2015, missed flight, judged by phone, phone dropping out\nROOM: NK thought 2014", "why": "Recording the anecdote's content wastes the page. Marking its start and length tells you there is an opening, and the anecdote itself offers a graceful bridge back: the case for a reserve adjudicator."}, {"title": "Two speakers disagree about something not on the paper", "input": "University partnership committee, item: 'Renewal of the pre-tertiary agreement'. Professor Ian Moffat (conservatorium): The agreement should be renewed as is. Dr Hannah Leigh (education faculty): As is, it only serves performers. Half the students who go on to study music with us become teachers, and nothing in the agreement touches that pathway.", "output": "Q (was): Renew pre-tertiary agreement?\nQ: Renew as is, or widen it to include the teaching pathway?\nROOM: IM  ✓ renew as is\nROOM: HL  ✗ 'only serves performers'  # half become teachers  !\nME: ≈ HL → Δ schedule 2: teaching pathway, same agreement  ★", "weak": "Q: Renewal of pre-tertiary agreement\nROOM: IM yes renew\nROOM: HL thinks it should include teaching too\nME: renew?", "why": "When two speakers disagree about something the paper did not mention, the real question is usually the point of disagreement. Naming it lets you propose a way to resolve it rather than vote on a question the room has already left."}, {"title": "The chair's summary uses new words", "input": "Principal's leadership meeting, item: 'Term 4 reporting timeline'. After discussion the principal summarises: So what I'm hearing is that the issue is really workload in week eight, when reports, concerts and the Year 12 farewell all land at once. Most of you have said the dates are fine in themselves. Is that fair?", "output": "Q (was): Term 4 reporting timeline\nQ: How do we relieve week 8 (reports + concerts + Yr 12 farewell)?\nROOM: Principal  'workload in week eight'  !\nME: Δ move Yr 10 concert to wk 6  # 1 event out of wk 8  ★", "weak": "Q: Term 4 reporting timeline\nROOM: Principal summarised: workload concerns in week 8.", "why": "When a chair's summary uses words the agenda did not, the chair has told you the real question. Rewriting the Q line in the chair's words means your proposal will sound like an answer to the chair."}, {"title": "Policy debate that does not need deciding today", "input": "Department meeting, item: 'Year 7 instrument allocation'. Mr Felix Brandt (teacher): Honestly, the whole selective model is questionable. Should a public school be auditioning eleven-year-olds at all? Ms Lena Fox (teacher): That's a big question, and I have views on it too, but... Mr Brandt: Well, someone has to ask it.", "output": "Q: Allocate Yr 7 instruments by Friday\nROOM: FB  selective model 'questionable'  (drift: policy, not today)\nROOM: LF  ✓ big Q, but…  ⟲ allocation\nME: ✓ FB's Q → agenda for planning day; ★ restore allocation", "weak": "Q: Year 7 allocation\nROOM: FB questions the selective model, should we audition at all?\nME: good question, I have thoughts on this\nROOM: LF also has views", "why": "A broad policy question is real but does not need deciding today. The skilled move is to give it a home (a later agenda) rather than either debating it now or dismissing the person who raised it."}, {"title": "Drift in a hybrid meeting", "input": "Festival association board, hybrid. Item: 'Budget for 2027'. Mr Ethan Carr (on Zoom, delayed): Sorry, I missed the last part; are we still on the venue? Because I wanted to add something on the venue. Chair: We finished venue. Mr Carr: Right, but very quickly, the venue's parking...", "output": "Q: Approve 2027 budget\nROOM: EC (Zoom, delay)  ⟲ venue parking  (drift: closed item)\nROOM: Chair  ✗ venue finished\nME: @ offer: EC's parking point to secretary for venue file  ★ then budget", "weak": "ROOM: EC parking at venue\nME: parking is an issue", "why": "Online members on delay often reopen closed items; the ⟲ and 'closed item' label make the pattern visible. A practical routing offer restores the question and keeps the online member included."}, {"title": "The question narrows", "input": "Arts funding panel, deliberation. Item: 'Application 14, regional youth jazz project'. Ms Ruth Gallagher (panel): I don't think anyone doubts the project. The question is whether a first-time applicant can manage forty thousand dollars. Dr Owen Price (panel): Then the question is really the amount, not the merit.", "output": "Q (was): Fund App 14?\nQ: Fund App 14 at full $40k, or staged?\nROOM: RG  ✓ project  ? first-time applicant, $40k  !\nROOM: OP  'the amount, not the merit'\nME: Δ stage: $25k now, $15k on acquittal of stage 1  ★", "weak": "Q: Fund Application 14?\nROOM: RG good project, worried about money\nROOM: OP agrees", "why": "Sometimes the real question is a narrower version of the nominal one. Rewriting the Q line to name the amount makes a staged grant the obvious answer rather than a compromise."}, {"title": "Noticing the old question come back", "input": "School council, item: 'Car park safety'. Twenty minutes earlier the room agreed the real issue was the concert finishing as the swimming carnival buses arrived. Now Mr Lionel Bach (community member) says: So are we going to ask the department for more parking bays or not?", "output": "Q (was): More parking bays?\nQ: Separate concert end from bus arrival\nROOM: LB  ⟲ 'more parking bays'  (back to old Q)\nME: ★ remind: we agreed timing, credit SI; Δ concert end 15 min later", "weak": "ROOM: LB wants more parking bays\nME: ask department?", "why": "Rooms often slide back to the nominal question. Keeping the old Q visible lets you recognise the regression at once and restore the agreed question by citing who reframed it."}, {"title": "Restoring as chair", "input": "You chair the school council. Item: 'Annual report approval'. Ms Gemma Thorne (parent) has spent five minutes on the canteen menu, which appears in one sentence of the report. Two other members have joined in. The report must be approved tonight to meet the department's deadline.", "output": "NOTE:\nQ: Approve 2026 annual report\nROOM: GT  canteen menu  (drift, 5 min)\nROOM: 2 others ≈ GT  (drift)\nME: ★ route menu to next agenda; restore approval\n\nSPOKEN:\nThank you, Gemma; the canteen deserves its own item, and I'll put it on the next agenda with the canteen manager invited. || For tonight, the question is whether council approves the annual report. Are there any concerns about the report itself before I put it?", "weak": "We're running out of time, so can we move on from the canteen please. Any other comments?", "why": "As chair, the restoring move should give the drift a home with a date and a person, then state the question as a decision. 'Any other comments?' invites the drift to resume."}], "quiz": [{"input": "Agenda item: 'Feedback on the Year 11 assessment outline'. The deputy director says, 'What we're actually deciding is whether schools can set their own task order.'", "q": "What should you do with the Q line?", "options": ["Rewrite it as 'Can schools set their own task order?' and keep the old version crossed through.", "Leave it; the agenda item governs.", "Add the deputy director's comment to the ROOM column only."], "answer": 0, "why": "A senior reframing is the clearest signal the real question has surfaced, and the old version stays visible as material."}, {"input": "You are twenty minutes into an item at a partnership committee, listening for the moment the agenda wording stops describing the decision.", "q": "Which of these is the strongest signal that the real question is surfacing?", "options": ["A speaker repeats a point already made.", "Two speakers disagree about something the paper did not mention.", "The meeting runs over time."], "answer": 1, "why": "Disagreement outside the paper's framing usually marks the decision the room actually has to make."}, {"input": "During a board item on sponsorship, two members spend five minutes on the history of a former sponsor's falling out with the association.", "q": "How should this appear on your page?", "options": ["A full summary, since history may be relevant.", "Nothing at all.", "A short line marked (drift), with a ⟲ when the room returns."], "answer": 2, "why": "Marking where drift begins and ends shows you the opening and who took the room away."}, {"input": "A colleague has raised a legitimate grievance about consultation that has run for six minutes.", "q": "Which restoring move is best?", "options": ["'These are important points and I'd like them minuted. On the question in front of us, which I take to be X...'", "'Can we stay on topic, please?'", "'I agree completely, and I'd add another concern about consultation...'"], "answer": 0, "why": "It honours the drift, gives it a home in the minutes, and restates the real question without making anyone wrong."}, {"input": "The chair summarises: 'So the issue is really who owns the follow-up, not the event itself.' The agenda said 'Review of the open night'.", "q": "What does this tell you?", "options": ["The chair has drifted.", "Nothing; chairs paraphrase.", "The real question is ownership of the follow-up; rewrite Q in the chair's words."], "answer": 2, "why": "A chair's summary in words the agenda did not use usually names the real question."}, {"input": "A member raises whether the school should have a selective music program at all during an item on next year's instrument allocation.", "q": "What is the most skilful response?", "options": ["Debate it now, since it is fundamental.", "Acknowledge it and propose a home for it on a later agenda, then restore the allocation question.", "Ignore it and continue."], "answer": 1, "why": "A real but undecidable-today question needs a home, not a debate or a dismissal."}, {"input": "Twenty minutes after the room agreed the issue was timing, someone asks again whether to request more parking bays.", "q": "What is your page for at this moment?", "options": ["Showing you the old Q, so you can recognise the regression and cite who reframed it.", "Recording the parking request as a new point.", "Noting the speaker's frustration."], "answer": 0, "why": "Keeping the old Q visible lets you see when the room slides back and restore the agreed question with a credit."}, {"input": "You are about to make your proposal. The room has drifted and returned twice, and the real question differs from the agenda.", "q": "What should come first in your contribution?", "options": ["Your proposal, before you lose the floor.", "A summary of the drift.", "The real question in one sentence, then the proposal."], "answer": 2, "why": "Naming the real question first means everyone after you, and the minutes, must answer it, and your proposal reads as the answer."}], "drills": [{"level": 1, "kind": "write", "input": "Agenda item: 'Review of concert ticket pricing'. Ms Penny Arnott (music parent committee): I don't think the price is what's keeping people away. It's that the concerts are on Wednesday nights. Working parents can't make it from the city by six thirty.", "task": "Write the Q (was) line, the rewritten Q line, and one ROOM line with her phrase.", "model": "Q (was): Review ticket pricing\nQ: Move concerts off Wednesday 6:30 pm?\nROOM: PA  'the price is not what's keeping people away'  # 6:30 Wed, working parents"}, {"level": 1, "kind": "speak", "input": "Music parent committee. Mr Graham Hurst has spent six minutes on the tour company's insurance claim from 2023, and two members have joined in. Your page: 'Q: Approve the 2027 tour itinerary' and below it 'ROOM: GH  tour company's insurance history (drift, 6 min)'. The chair glances at you.", "task": "Give the restoring move in no more than three sentences: acknowledge the drift without dismissing it, give it a home, and restate the question.", "seconds": 30, "model": "Graham's point about the company's insurance history matters, and I'd suggest the business manager checks it and reports back before we sign anything. | For tonight, the question is whether we approve the itinerary itself. I'm happy to speak to that."}, {"level": 2, "kind": "write", "input": "SCSA syllabus committee, item: 'Clarification of the composition outcome'.\nMs Martina Oakes (teacher representative): Teachers keep asking whether a song with chords and lyrics counts as a composition for this outcome. The current wording says 'an original work', which doesn't settle it.\nDr Gavin Petrie (university, on Zoom): I'd go further. The question isn't really whether songs count. It's whether the outcome assesses the product or the process. If it's the process, a song is fine, provided the student can show the decisions. If it's the product, we need a much tighter definition of what we're marking.\nMs Oakes: That's fair. And it also affects how the portfolio is moderated.", "task": "Write the Q (was) and Q lines, two to four ROOM lines with phrases, and one ME line that answers the real question.", "model": "Q (was): Clarify composition outcome (do songs count?)\nQ: Does the outcome assess product or process?\nROOM: MO  'an original work' doesn't settle it\nROOM: GP (Zoom)  'the product or the process'  !\nROOM: MO  ≈ GP → moderation of portfolio\nME: ★ process: songs count with decision evidence; Δ add 'and the decisions that shaped it' to the outcome"}, {"level": 2, "kind": "speak", "input": "School council, item: 'Proposal to ban mobile phones at school concerts'.\nMr Hugh Delaney (community member): Families film the entire concert on their phones and the glow is distracting for the performers. At the last concert I counted more screens than performers. I'd like a ban.\nMs Sonya Reid (parent representative): A ban won't be enforced, and parents will resent it. What families actually want is a recording of their child. If the school provided a good recording, most of them wouldn't bother filming. Some schools already do this and the filming has largely stopped. Honestly, while we're at it, the concert programs last term had three students' names misspelt.", "task": "Note the nominal question, the real question and the drift. Then speak for up to 75 seconds: restore the real question, credit Ms Reid's reframing, and propose something.", "seconds": 75, "model": "Sonya's point about the programs is a fair one, and I'll take it back to the music office so the proofing is fixed for next term. || On the item itself, I think Sonya has reframed it well. The question is not really whether to ban phones; it's that *what families actually want is a recording of their child*. If we give them that, Hugh's problem largely solves itself. So I'd propose that the school records each major concert and makes the recording available to families within a week, and that the program includes a short note asking families to keep phones down during performances. That's a request, not a ban, and we can review it at the end of next year."}, {"level": 3, "kind": "write", "input": "Principal's leadership meeting, hybrid, item: 'Staffing of the 2027 instrumental program'.\nThe principal: The department has given us the 2027 IMSS allocation. It's the same hours as this year. The question on the paper is how we distribute them across instruments. I need a recommendation by the end of the month.\nMr Sean O'Rourke (deputy principal): Before we get into distribution, I think we should talk about whether the school should be buying in extra private tuition at all. Other schools manage without it. We're the only school in the network paying for it.\nMs Paula Grieve (HOLA, on Zoom): That's a bigger conversation. And can I just say the Zoom link for this meeting was wrong again; I had to ring the office. Second time this term.\nMr O'Rourke: Yes, that keeps happening.\nThe principal: Let's note the link.\nMs Tara Nguyen (business manager, quietly): One thing that may matter: the hours are the same, but the 2027 Year 7 intake has eleven more brass players than this year, and brass is where we have no extra private tuition at all. The brass teacher's timetable is already full.\nMr O'Rourke: Well, that rather makes my point about private tuition. We'd need more of it.\nThe principal: Or the opposite.", "task": "Write a note page of no more than nine lines: Q (was), the real Q, ROOM lines with drift marked and ⟲, the quiet fact with !, and ME lines that would launch you.", "model": "Q (was): Distribute 2027 IMSS hours across instruments\nQ: How do we cover +11 brass with the same hours?\nROOM: Principal  # same hours as 2026\nROOM: SO  ? buy in private tuition at all  (drift: policy)\nROOM: PG (Zoom)  Zoom link wrong  (drift)  ⟲ Principal\nROOM: TN  ! # +11 brass Yr 7, no private tuition in brass\nROOM: SO  'makes my point' / Principal 'or the opposite'\nME: ≈ TN: real Q = brass; Δ shift 2 hrs from low-demand area + group lessons in brass  ★\nME: SO's policy Q → separate item, @ principal; link @ office"}, {"level": 3, "kind": "speak", "input": "SCSA committee, hybrid, item: 'Response to Board feedback on the draft course'.\nMs Rosalind Firth (chair): The Board's letter is on the screen. I'd like us to agree a response.\nDr Adrian Cole (university, on Zoom): I've read it twice, I've seen this pattern on accreditation panels interstate, and I'd say the Board is not objecting to the content at all. It is objecting to the comparability of the school-assessed component. So the question isn't 'how do we respond', it's 'how do we make the portfolio comparable across schools'.\nMs Joy Barlow (teacher representative): I hear that, but I have to say I'm still upset about how the decision was communicated. We found out from a newsletter. Teachers in my network were ringing me before I knew.\nMr Keith Arnold (teacher representative): Same. And it's not the first time. It happened with the last syllabus too.\nMs Firth: That's noted.\nMr Desmond Yap (SCSA officer, quietly): For what it's worth, the Board approved a similar portfolio in another learning area last year once it included external moderation of a sample. That was the change that satisfied them. I can circulate the details after the meeting.\nDr Cole: That's very helpful.\nMs Firth: Where does that leave us? Can we be practical?", "task": "Take notes as you read: Q (was), the real Q, drift and the quiet fact. Then speak for up to 120 seconds: acknowledge the drift, credit Dr Cole's reframing in his words, use Mr Yap's fact, and propose the response with an owner and date.", "seconds": 120, "model": "Thank you, Chair. Joy and Keith have raised something that matters: hearing about the decision from a newsletter is not how this committee should learn of a Board outcome, and I'd ask that we minute that and that the chair raises it with the secretariat. || On the question in front of us, Adrian has given us the right one. The Board is not objecting to the content; the question is *how do we make the portfolio comparable across schools*. And Desmond has just told us how the Board has answered that question before: in another learning area, *external moderation of a sample* was the change that satisfied it. || So I'd propose three steps. One, we add external moderation of a sample of portfolios to the assessment design, using the model the Board has already approved. Two, Desmond and I prepare a one-page comparison of that model with our draft for the next meeting. Three, the response letter to the Board addresses comparability directly and nothing else, so it reads as an answer to their letter, line by line. Wording for the minutes: the committee will resubmit with external moderation of a sample, and a draft response will be tabled at the next meeting."}]}, {"id": "nt-6", "examples": [{"title": "The pathway that nobody staffs", "input": "Professor Lena Varga (conservatorium, in the room): We all accept the diagnosis. Students from public schools arrive at audition underprepared in aural and keyboard, and the ones from private studios do not. I'd call it a preparation gap, not a talent gap. The research on access is consistent, and frankly the solution is obvious: earlier contact between our staff and the schools. We should be doing far more of it.", "output": "ROOM: LV  'a preparation gap, not a talent gap'  ✓\nROOM: LV  → earlier contact uni ↔ schools\nME: who? when? how many? cost?\nGAP: who runs the contact, which schools, from which term", "weak": "LV: public school students underprepared aural/keyboard, private studio students better. Prep gap not talent gap. Research consistent. Need earlier contact between uni and schools.", "why": "The weak note records the diagnosis faithfully and stops there. The strong note runs the checklist and finds that 'far more of it' names no owner, no schools and no start date, which is where a practical proposal belongs."}, {"title": "Council values without a vehicle", "input": "Mrs Priya Raman (parent representative, school council): I think everyone at this table wants the same thing. We want families from every suburb to feel the music program is for them, not just for those who already have a piano at home. It's about belonging. I'd love to see the school take that seriously in its communications and its culture over the next few years.", "output": "ROOM: PR  'belonging'  ✓ all agree\nROOM: PR  → comms + culture, 'next few years'\nME: vehicle? existing: Yr 6 info night, feeder band\nGAP: no vehicle or date; which existing event carries it?", "weak": "PR wants families from all suburbs to feel welcome. Belonging. Communications and culture. Agree.", "why": "Agreement on a value is not a decision. The strong note asks through which existing structure the value would be delivered and finds that nobody has named one, so the gap points straight at the Year 6 information night and the feeder band."}, {"title": "The funding panel that loved the idea", "input": "Mr Daniel Osei (arts funding panel, online): I'm genuinely enthusiastic. Regional jazz workshops are exactly the sort of thing this fund exists for. The artistic case is strong, the partners are credible. My only hesitation is that I can't yet picture it on the ground, you know, what a Year 9 kid in Geraldton actually gets on a Tuesday.", "output": "ROOM: DO(Z)  ✓ artistic case, partners\nROOM: DO(Z)  'what a Year 9 kid in Geraldton actually gets'  !\nME: student experience = the missing layer\nGAP: the session as the student lives it: hours, tutor, instrument, follow-up", "weak": "DO supportive. Strong artistic case. Some hesitation about practicalities.", "why": "'Some hesitation about practicalities' loses the exact gap the speaker handed over. The strong note keeps his phrase and converts it into the checklist item, student experience, which tells Dijon precisely what to describe."}, {"title": "SCSA: three speakers, no timeline", "input": "Dr Ruth Hallam (SCSA committee chair): So we've heard from Associate Professor Lim on the coherence of the content, from Ms Bianchi on the assessment weighting, and from Mr Tait on the moderation burden. There seems to be broad support for revising the practical component. Shall we note that and come back to it?", "output": "ROOM: RH  summary: Lim coherence, Bianchi weighting, Tait moderation\nROOM: RH  'note that and come back to it'  ✗ drift to referral\nME: when? who drafts? which meeting?\nGAP: no owner, no date: 'come back' to what, when\nME: ★ small drafting group, report next meeting", "weak": "Chair summarised. Broad support for revising practical. Will come back to it.", "why": "The chair's 'come back to it' is the classic point at which an item dies. The strong note flags it with ✗ and writes the missing who and when, so Dijon can turn agreement into a drafting group with a date."}, {"title": "The department meeting that diagnosed itself", "input": "Ms Carla Nguyen (classroom music teacher): The Year 8 composition unit isn't working. Half of them never finish, the software crashes, and we spend the last two weeks chasing submissions. Honestly it's become a unit about deadlines, not about music. I think everyone's felt it this term.", "output": "ROOM: CN  Yr 8 comp ✗  'a unit about deadlines, not about music'\nROOM: CN  # half unfinished, software crashes\nME: ✓ felt it too\nME: resource? the laptops; vehicle? Term 1 planning day\nGAP: no fix proposed; who redesigns the task, before Term 1", "weak": "Year 8 comp unit not working, software crashes, students not finishing. Agree.", "why": "The weak note becomes a shared complaint. The strong note keeps the creditable phrase and finds that nobody has said who redesigns the unit or when, which turns sympathy into a task for the planning day."}, {"title": "Festival board: the sponsor question", "input": "Mr Gareth Pryce (festival association board member): We're too reliant on one sponsor. If they walk, we lose a third of the budget. I've said this for two years. We need to diversify our revenue, full stop. Grants, ticketing, maybe a donor circle. Other festivals do it.", "output": "ROOM: GP  ! one sponsor ≈ 1/3 budget\nROOM: GP  'diversify our revenue'  ⟲ 2 yrs\nME: who leads? which stream first? target #?\nGAP: three ideas, no priority, no owner, no target figure", "weak": "GP: over-reliant on one sponsor. Diversify revenue: grants, ticketing, donor circle. Other festivals do it.", "why": "A list of options is still analysis. The strong note sees that nobody has chosen one stream, set a figure or named a lead, and as treasurer that is exactly the gap Dijon can fill."}, {"title": "Principal's office: wellbeing in touring", "input": "Ms Anne Whitfield (deputy principal): I'm supportive of the Melbourne tour in principle, but I'm worried about student wellbeing on a five-day trip. There's anxiety, there's medication, there's kids who've never been away. I just want us to be confident that we've thought it through properly.", "output": "ROOM: AW  ✓ in principle  ! wellbeing 5 days\nROOM: AW  anxiety, medication, first time away\nROOM: AW  'thought it through properly'\nME: what would make her say yes? → a named plan\nGAP: no named wellbeing lead, no medication protocol, no parent briefing date", "weak": "AW worried about wellbeing on tour. Anxiety, medication. Wants it thought through.", "why": "'Thought it through' is a request for a mechanism, not a reason to stop. The strong note asks what would make the decision-maker say yes and writes the three missing items she would need to see."}, {"title": "The university committee and the rubric", "input": "Associate Professor Simon Kerr (university academic committee, online): I'd question whether the ensemble rubric discriminates at all. Everyone gets a 6 or a 7. It's a ceiling, not a scale. I don't have a solution, I just think we should acknowledge it.", "output": "ROOM: SK(Z)  ✗ ensemble rubric  'a ceiling, not a scale'\nROOM: SK(Z)  # 6s and 7s only\nME: ≈ our Yr 11 practical bands; evidence: descriptor rewrite 2024\nGAP: he names the fault, no one proposes a test: trial revised descriptors on one cohort", "weak": "SK: rubric doesn't discriminate, all 6 or 7. No solution offered.", "why": "The speaker explicitly leaves the mechanism open. The strong note links his fault to Dijon's own evidence and writes the gap as a testable action, so the contribution completes his point rather than correcting it."}, {"title": "Parent body and the instrument fleet", "input": "Mr Tom Hadley (music parents' association president): Every year we raise money and every year the instruments still look tired. I'm not sure parents know where the money goes. I think we need more transparency, really, so people feel their effort matters.", "output": "ROOM: TH  'more transparency'  ✓\nROOM: TH  → parents unsure where money goes\nME: vehicle? AGM report, term newsletter\nME: evidence: # instruments bought 2026\nGAP: no format or date for reporting back to parents", "weak": "TH says instruments look tired, parents don't know where money goes, need transparency.", "why": "'More transparency' is a value until it has a format and a date. The strong note finds the missing vehicle in existing structures and adds Dijon's own figures as evidence."}, {"title": "Regional network: the same problem everywhere", "input": "Ms Leanne Ford (regional music network convenor): What strikes me is that every school around this table has said the same thing. Staffing is thin, the specialist teachers are isolated, there's no one to moderate with. I'd almost say we're all solving it alone. Which is a bit absurd, given we're in the same room.", "output": "ROOM: LF  'we're all solving it alone'  !\nROOM: LF  ≈ all schools: thin staffing, isolation, no moderation\nME: ★ shared moderation day; who hosts? when?\nGAP: diagnosis shared by all; no one has offered to host or set a date", "weak": "LF: all schools have the same issues, staffing, isolation, moderation. Solving it alone.", "why": "When a room agrees on a diagnosis, the gap is almost always ownership. The strong note sees that nobody has offered to host, which is the cheapest and most decisive contribution available."}, {"title": "Turning the gap into inclusive language", "input": "Situation: four speakers on the SCSA committee have argued well for reducing the number of assessment tasks. Nobody has said how the reduction would be communicated to teachers already planning for next year. Your GAP line reads: 'comms to teachers? timing vs planning'. You are called.", "output": "What we haven't yet settled is how teachers hear about this before they finish planning for next year. If the committee agrees, I'd suggest three things: a one-page summary to schools by the end of Term 4, a note in the next newsletter, and a short online briefing in Week 2 of Term 1.", "weak": "I think nobody has really considered the teachers here. They'll have already planned and this will just land on them.", "why": "The weak version turns a gap into an accusation that the room overlooked something. 'What we haven't yet settled' includes the room and the numbered answer shows that the gap is the beginning of a proposal, not a complaint."}, {"title": "Gap versus objection", "input": "Situation: at a school council meeting, three members support extending the music program's after-school rehearsal time. Your notes read: 'ROOM: KB ✓ more time ✗ | ROOM: JA ✓ ✗ | ROOM: MM ✓ ✗'. You realise you have marked ✗ against everyone because nobody has mentioned supervision and the late bus.", "output": "ROOM: KB ✓ more time\nROOM: JA ✓\nROOM: MM ✓\nME: not ✗: they're right, just incomplete\nGAP: supervision roster + late bus for students from the north\nME: ★ 1 staff roster  2 bus enquiry  3 trial Term 1", "weak": "ROOM: KB ✓ more time ✗\nROOM: JA ✓ ✗\nROOM: MM ✓ ✗\nME: everyone has missed supervision and buses", "why": "A column of ✗ marks positions you against a room that is broadly right. Rewriting the ✗ marks as a single GAP line positions you as the person who completes their proposal."}], "quiz": [{"input": "Dr Fiona Leigh (SCSA committee): We all agree the listening paper over-rewards recall. It should reward analysis. I think that's the direction we want.", "q": "Which GAP line would serve you best?", "options": ["Listening paper over-rewards recall; should reward analysis", "Who drafts the revised questions, and which exam year is first affected?", "✗ FL: analysis is hard to mark reliably"], "answer": 1, "why": "It names the missing owner and timing, the practical layer nobody has supplied, rather than restating the agreement or opposing it."}, {"input": "Mr Ben Corrigan (festival board): Volunteers are burning out. We need to look after them better. It's a culture thing.", "q": "Which item from the silent checklist is most clearly unanswered?", "options": ["What the volunteer actually experiences on the day, and what would change it", "Whether volunteers matter to the festival", "Whether the board agrees culture is important"], "answer": 0, "why": "The value is already agreed; the missing layer is the concrete experience and mechanism that would change it."}, {"input": "Your notes after four speakers on a university partnership committee show three ✗ marks next to other people's initials.", "q": "What does this most likely mean?", "options": ["You have found three gaps", "You are holding a counterargument and may position yourself against the room", "The room is divided and should vote now"], "answer": 1, "why": "Repeated ✗ marks usually signal an objection; a gap is something missing, written once at the foot of the page."}, {"input": "You have found the gap: nobody has said who will run the bridging workshops.", "q": "Which opening frames it best?", "options": ["Nobody here has considered who will actually run this.", "I'm worried this won't happen unless someone runs it.", "What we haven't yet settled is who runs the workshops, so let me offer three answers."], "answer": 2, "why": "It includes the room and moves at once to a numbered answer, which completes their thinking rather than correcting it."}, {"input": "Ms Olivia Brandt (principal's leadership meeting): The data shows boys drop out of instrumental music in Year 9. We need to understand why.", "q": "Which ME line best prepares a gap-based contribution?", "options": ["Agree, worrying trend", "Understand why: through what? exit survey Yr 9, when? Term 2", "Boys drop out Yr 9"], "answer": 1, "why": "It runs vehicle and timing against the speaker's call for understanding and prepares a concrete mechanism."}, {"input": "After three speakers on a parent body, the room agrees to 'improve communication with new families'.", "q": "Where should the GAP line go and how long should it be?", "options": ["At the foot of the page, one plain line", "In the ROOM column, as a full summary of each speaker", "At the top, replacing the question"], "answer": 0, "why": "The method places one plain GAP line at the foot of the page, distinct from the question at the top."}, {"input": "Dr Hamid Qureshi (arts funding panel): The application is strong, but I'd like to know what success looks like in two years.", "q": "Which checklist item is he naming?", "options": ["Who does it", "Through which existing vehicle", "Measured how"], "answer": 2, "why": "'What success looks like' asks for the measure, which is part of the practical layer the gap usually contains."}, {"input": "A department meeting has diagnosed a problem with Year 10 practical exam timing for twenty minutes.", "q": "When is a gap usually identifiable?", "options": ["Only once the chair asks for it", "When a checklist item remains unanswered after three or four speakers", "After the first speaker"], "answer": 1, "why": "The first unanswered item after three or four speakers is usually the gap; earlier, someone may still supply it."}], "drills": [{"level": 1, "kind": "write", "input": "Ms Jess Albright (Head of Performing Arts): I think the Year 7 concert format is tired. Families sit through two hours for their child's four minutes. We should make it more of an event, something people actually look forward to.", "task": "Write two ROOM lines, run the silent checklist in one ME line, and write one GAP line.", "model": "ROOM: JA  Yr 7 concert format tired  # 2 hrs for 4 min\nROOM: JA  'something people actually look forward to'\nME: who? when? vehicle: Term 2 concert; experience: family arrival to departure\nGAP: no new format proposed, no owner, no date"}, {"level": 1, "kind": "speak", "input": "Mr Ray Lindqvist (school council member): Our music room is overcrowded at lunchtime. I walked past on Tuesday and there were about thirty kids in there, some practising, some just sitting on the floor with their instruments. Kids want to practise and there's nowhere to go. It's a good problem to have, but it is a problem.", "task": "Name the gap in one inclusive sentence and offer a two-part answer.", "seconds": 30, "model": "Ray's right that it's *a good problem to have*. What we haven't yet settled is where those students can go. Two options: open the two practice rooms with a duty roster on Tuesdays and Thursdays, and trial a booking sheet from Week 3."}, {"level": 2, "kind": "write", "input": "Associate Professor Mei Tan (conservatorium, online): The bridging program we ran last year had excellent outcomes for the twelve students who attended. The problem is the twelve. We had capacity for forty, and the staff were ready for forty. I suspect the schools didn't know about it, or knew too late.\nMr Patrick Doyle (Department of Education officer, in the room): That matches what I hear. Information goes to principals and stops there. Music teachers find out in Week 8. And by then the families have made plans. I'd say the program has a distribution problem, not a design problem.", "task": "Write a ROOM line per speaker with their exact phrase or figure, one ME checklist line, a GAP line, and a three-part numbered proposal.", "model": "Q: How do we fill the bridging program?\nROOM: MT(Z)  # 12 of 40 places  ? schools didn't know\nROOM: PD  'a distribution problem, not a design problem'  ≈ MT\nME: who sends? to whom? when? vehicle: TIC network list\nGAP: no direct channel to music teachers, no send date\nME: ★ 1 send to TICs direct  2 by Week 2 Term 1  3 PD + uni coordinator own it"}, {"level": 2, "kind": "speak", "input": "Ms Ingrid Shaw (festival board chair): The adjudicator feedback forms are inconsistent. Some adjudicators write a careful paragraph on every band, some write three words, and one last year wrote nothing at all on two forms. Directors have noticed.\nMr Kofi Mensah (board member, online): And the bands read them. I've watched students pass the sheet around the bus. For a lot of these kids it's the only written feedback they'll get on their playing all year. We owe them better than three words. Ms Shaw: So I'd like us to agree, in principle at least, that the current forms aren't serving the bands. I don't want to spend the whole meeting on it.", "task": "Credit both speakers, name the gap inclusively and propose a numbered fix.", "seconds": 60, "model": "Ingrid has named the inconsistency, and Kofi reminded us why it matters: for many of these students it's *the only written feedback they'll get on their playing all year*. What we haven't yet settled is what a good form looks like. So, three things. First, a structured form with three headings: sound, ensemble, one next step. Second, a short briefing note to adjudicators with the invitation. Third, I'll draft both for the March board meeting."}, {"level": 3, "kind": "write", "input": "Dr Ruth Hallam (SCSA committee chair): We're on the Board's feedback on the new course. Let's hear views.\nAssociate Professor Lim (university, in the room): The Board's objections are, in the main, about assessment load. They're not about the content. I'd encourage us to read their letter as a request for revision, not a rejection of the idea. The content is coherent and the academic community supports it.\nMs Sara Bianchi (independent school teacher, online): I agree. I'd add that the content is ambitious, and that's good, but ambition costs teaching time. Teachers in smaller schools will feel this most. I've heard from colleagues in two regional schools who are already anxious. And actually, on a related matter, I've been meaning to raise the issue of exam timetabling clashes with the drama practical, which happened again this year. It's a long-running problem.\nMr Ian Tait (public school, in the room, quietly): Just on the load question. I counted the tasks in our draft. It's nine across the pair of units. The current course has six. That's the number the Board will be looking at.\nDr Hallam: Thank you. So we have some support for revising. Shall we note the feedback and return to it at our next meeting?", "task": "Write a full note page: Q line, ROOM lines for each speaker with symbols and exact phrases, mark the drift, ME checklist line, GAP line, and a starred three-part proposal.", "model": "Q: Revise and resubmit, or let it lapse?\nROOM: RL  'a request for revision, not a rejection of the idea'  ✓\nROOM: SB(Z)  ≈ RL  ! small/regional schools, teaching time\nROOM: SB(Z)  drift: exam timetabling, drama clash  → park, other item\nROOM: IT  # 9 tasks vs 6 current  ! the Board's number\nROOM: RH  'note and return'  ✗ referral, no owner\nME: who redrafts? by when? to what target? how tested with small schools?\nGAP: no target task number, no drafting owner, no date, no small-school check\nME: ★ 1 target six tasks (IT's figure)  2 drafting group RL + SB + me, report next meeting  3 SB's two regional colleagues review draft"}, {"level": 3, "kind": "speak", "input": "Mr Gareth Pryce (festival board): The sponsor renewal is in doubt. They've told us informally that their marketing budget is under review, and the person we deal with has gone quiet since September.\nMs Ingrid Shaw (chair): We've been here before. Three years ago the same thing happened and they renewed in the end. I think we keep our nerve and keep talking to them.\nMr Kofi Mensah (online, slight lag): Sorry, coming back to Gareth's point, I think we have to be honest that hope isn't a budget line. If they go, the March festival shrinks, and we'd be making that decision in January with no time to replace them.\nMs Helen Ruiz (board member, previously quiet): For what it's worth, I checked the grant calendar. The community arts grant round we'd be eligible for closes in mid-February, and the next state arts round opens in December. So there is a window, but it's short.\nMr Pryce: And while we're at it, I still think the venue contract is overpriced. We've never renegotiated it in five years.\nMs Shaw: Let's not open that tonight. Treasurer, any thoughts? (Mr Mensah's hand is still raised on screen. The secretary is waiting to minute something.)", "task": "Speak as treasurer. Credit Kofi's phrase and Helen's fact, park the venue point politely, name the gap and propose a numbered plan with owners and dates.", "seconds": 120, "model": "Thank you, Ingrid. I agree we keep talking to the sponsor, and I'd also take Kofi's point that *hope isn't a budget line*. Helen has given us the crucial fact: there is a funding window, but it closes in mid-February. || What we haven't yet settled is who prepares the alternative, and by when. So, three things. First, I'll prepare a revised festival budget at two levels, with and without the sponsor, for the December meeting. Second, Helen and I draft a grant application to the December round, with a decision on whether to submit by the end of January. Third, Ingrid continues the sponsor conversation and asks them for a firm answer by the end of February. Gareth's venue point is a good one and deserves its own item; perhaps we list it for February. For the minutes: that the treasurer prepare a two-scenario budget by the December meeting, and that a grant application be prepared in parallel."}]}, {"id": "nt-7", "examples": [{"title": "Predicting the real question", "input": "Situation: the agenda for Thursday's SCSA committee reads 'Item 4: Board feedback on the proposed course (for discussion)'. The chair, Dr Ruth Hallam, emailed that the Board's letter 'raises concerns about assessment load and implementation'. You have twenty minutes in the car park.", "output": "PRE-MEETING  SCSA CAC, Item 4\nLikely Q: revise and resubmit for 2028, or let it lapse?\n1 Load is fixable, content isn't the issue\n2 Implementation: pilot schools exist\n3 Resubmit by Term 2 2027\nStanding: TIC, specialist program, 20 yrs classroom\nMinutes: 'That the committee revise the assessment program and resubmit by ...'", "weak": "Item 4 Board feedback\n- assessment load?\n- implementation?\n- what do we think?\n- maybe mention my school", "why": "The agenda says 'for discussion', but the real question is whether the course survives. The strong page predicts that question and arrives with a destination and minute wording, so Dijon can steer rather than react."}, {"title": "Six words or fewer", "input": "Situation: before a school council meeting on the music program budget, your draft first point reads: 'I want to make the point that the instrument hire fees have not been reviewed for a long time and are now significantly below what comparable programs charge, which is affecting our ability to replace instruments.'", "output": "1 Hire fees unreviewed, below comparable\n   # last reviewed 2019; ours $X vs $Y nearby", "weak": "1 I want to make the point that the instrument hire fees have not been reviewed for a long time and are now significantly below what comparable programs charge", "why": "A point in six words can be seen at a glance mid-meeting; a sentence has to be read. The evidence goes beneath it as a figure, so the point and its proof sit in separate, scannable lines."}, {"title": "Evidence you can defend", "input": "Situation: preparing for a university partnership committee, your notes include: 'Research shows students who do bridging programs are much more likely to succeed at uni.' Associate Professor Mei Tan, who will be present, is a specialist in music education research.", "output": "2 Bridging works locally\n   # 12 attended 2026; 9 auditioned; 7 offered (our records)\n   ✗ cut 'research shows': can't name the study", "weak": "2 Bridging programs work\n   research shows much more likely to succeed", "why": "In front of a researcher, an unsourced 'research shows' invites a question you cannot answer. A precise local figure from your own records is defensible and, in that room, more persuasive."}, {"title": "Standing chosen for the room", "input": "Situation: you are presenting to an arts funding panel on regional jazz workshops. Your instinct is to open with your full background: teaching since 2005, TIC Music, 26 years as a professional bassist, council chair, festival treasurer, SCSA committee member.", "output": "Standing: festival treasurer; run the regional band program\n(not: SCSA, council chair, teaching history)", "weak": "Standing: teaching since 2005, TIC Music at MLSHS, professional bassist 26 yrs, chair of school council, treasurer of the festival association, SCSA committee member", "why": "A funding panel needs to know you can deliver and account for the money. One line of standing chosen for that question carries more authority than a full biography, which can read as insecurity."}, {"title": "Drafting the minutes in advance", "input": "Situation: before a department meeting on Year 8 composition, you have decided what you want: the unit redesigned over the holidays, with a shorter final task of sixteen bars and two checkpoints so that students finish. Ms Carla Nguyen has said she would help. You have not yet written how you want the decision recorded, and the head of learning area tends to minute items as 'discussed'.", "output": "Minutes: 'That the Year 8 composition task be reduced to a 16-bar piece with two checkpoints, redesign by CN and DS before Term 1 Week 1.'", "weak": "Minutes: agree to look at the Yr 8 unit", "why": "Drafting the exact wording in advance forces you to decide what you actually want. 'Look at' records nothing; a draft with scope, owners and date gives the chair a resolution they can simply adopt."}, {"title": "The festival board pre-meeting page", "input": "Situation: the festival association board meets on Tuesday. You expect the chair, Ms Ingrid Shaw, to raise sponsor renewal, because the sponsor's marketing manager has gone quiet. As treasurer you know the sponsor provides about a third of income and that reserves would cover one reduced festival. You have heard there is a grant round open, but you have not checked its closing date.", "output": "PRE-MEETING  Festival board, Tues\nLikely Q: what do we do if the sponsor goes?\n1 Two-scenario budget, not panic\n   # sponsor ≈ 1/3 income; reserves cover one reduced festival\n2 Grant round as parallel track\n   # check closing date before Tues\n3 Ask sponsor for firm date\nStanding: treasurer\nMinutes: 'That the treasurer present a two-scenario budget at the next meeting.'", "weak": "Sponsor:\n- worried\n- we need other money\n- reserves?\n- talk to Ingrid", "why": "The strong page converts anxiety into three numbered positions with evidence and a note to verify the date before speaking. The weak page records worry, which in a board meeting becomes hedging."}, {"title": "Verifying before you cite", "input": "Situation: preparing for the SCSA committee, you remember reading that another state reduced its senior music assessment tasks recently, but you cannot recall which state, which document or which year. A senior officer from the Authority, who knows the interstate syllabuses well, will be present. You do know for certain that your own draft has nine tasks against six in the current course.", "output": "3 Other jurisdictions have reduced load\n   ? verify: state, document, year  → if not found by Wed, cut\n   fallback: # our draft 9 tasks vs current 6", "weak": "3 NSW cut their tasks a couple of years ago, I think", "why": "An inaccurate interstate citation in front of policy people damages credibility far more than omitting it. The strong page marks it for verification with a deadline and provides a fallback fact that is certainly true."}, {"title": "Attaching prepared points with ≈", "input": "Associate Professor Lim (SCSA committee, in the room): For what it's worth, I read the Board's letter as fundamentally about assessment load. If we deal with that, the rest follows. Your pre-meeting page has as point 1: 'Load is fixable, content isn't the issue'.", "output": "1 Load is fixable, content isn't the issue  ≈ RL\nROOM: RL  'fundamentally about assessment load'  ✓", "weak": "ROOM: RL  thinks it's about load. Same as my point.", "why": "Marking ≈ RL against your prepared point means that when you speak, point 1 arrives as 'building on Associate Professor Lim', which sounds responsive and credits a senior ally."}, {"title": "When the meeting moves away from your plan", "input": "Situation: you arrived at a principal's leadership meeting prepared to argue for a Melbourne tour. Within ten minutes, Ms Anne Whitfield (deputy principal) reframes it entirely: 'The question isn't whether we tour. It's whether we have the staffing to supervise it.' None of your three points addresses staffing.", "output": "Likely Q: tour or not?  Δ actual Q: staffing to supervise\n1 Educational case  → park, only if asked\n2 Costed, parents support  → keep, brief\n3 Δ NEW: staffing: 4 staff named, ratio 1:10\nMinutes: 'That the tour proceed subject to a confirmed staffing plan by ...'", "weak": "1 Educational case\n2 Costs\n3 Parents support\n(keep going with plan)", "why": "The pre-meeting page is a launch pad, not a script. Marking Δ against the predicted question lets you re-order and replace points the moment the real question changes, rather than delivering a prepared speech to the wrong issue."}, {"title": "The parent body: one strong fact", "input": "Situation: the music parents' association will discuss whether to fund new percussion. Mr Tom Hadley (president) has said parents want to know where money goes. You know that last year's fundraising bought two tubas and a bass clarinet, and that the snare drums are fifteen years old.", "output": "PRE-MEETING  Parents' assoc\nLikely Q: fund percussion this year?\n1 Last year's money is visible\n   # 2 tubas, 1 bass clarinet, in use daily\n2 Percussion is the next need\n   # snares 15 yrs old\n3 Report back each term\nStanding: TIC, manages the fleet\nMinutes: 'That the association fund percussion up to $X, with a report to parents each term.'", "weak": "Percussion\n- need new ones, they're old\n- thank parents for tubas etc\n- transparency", "why": "The strong page anticipates the president's concern and answers it with concrete purchases before making the new request. Each point has one fact a parent can remember and repeat."}, {"title": "A conference panel question", "input": "Situation: you are on a conference panel on specialist music programs in public schools. The moderator, Dr Claire Moreau, has said she will ask each panellist 'what one policy change would make the biggest difference'. You have three minutes to answer.", "output": "PRE-MEETING  Conference panel\nLikely Q: one policy change?\n1 Protect specialist staffing allocation\n   # our program: staffing set annually, not multi-year\n2 Example: one cohort, one consequence\n3 The ask: three-year staffing commitment\nStanding: TIC, public specialist program since 2021\nClose: 'Three years is the length of a student's commitment; staffing should match it.'", "weak": "Policy change?\n- funding\n- staffing\n- recognition of music\n- maybe say something about SCSA", "why": "The weak page offers four candidate answers to a question that asks for one. The strong page commits to a single ask, supports it with a fact from your own program and prepares a closing sentence that can be quoted."}, {"title": "Car park version, two minutes", "input": "Situation: you have forgotten to prepare for a regional music network meeting and have two minutes before walking in. You know the item is shared moderation for Year 11 practical exams, that several small schools have nobody to moderate with, and that your school has the rooms, two grand pianos and recording equipment to host a day in Term 1.", "output": "Q: shared moderation, who hosts?\n1 We'll host, Term 1\n   # our rooms, two pianos, recording kit\nMinutes: 'That MLSHS host a moderation day in Term 1 Week 6.'", "weak": "Moderation: offer something?", "why": "Even a two-minute pre-meeting page should contain the likely question, one point with one fact and a minute. That is enough to make a decisive contribution; the rest can be attached in the room."}], "quiz": [{"input": "Agenda: 'Item 3: Review of ensemble program (noting)'. You know the principal is considering cutting one ensemble.", "q": "What should your 'Likely Q' line say?", "options": ["Review of ensemble program (noting)", "Which ensemble, if any, is cut, and on what criteria?", "Ensembles are important"], "answer": 1, "why": "The pre-meeting page predicts the real question, which may differ from the agenda wording."}, {"input": "Draft point: 'I think we need to look at the possibility of offering more lunchtime rehearsals because there seems to be demand from students.'", "q": "Which version belongs on the pre-meeting page?", "options": ["More lunchtime rehearsals: demand exists\n   # 34 names on waitlist", "Lunchtime rehearsals: look at possibility", "I think we need to look at offering more lunchtime rehearsals because of demand"], "answer": 0, "why": "The point is six words or fewer with a defensible figure beneath it."}, {"input": "You are preparing for a university academic committee chaired by a dean.", "q": "Which standing line is best chosen for that room?", "options": ["Teacher since 2005, TIC, bassist, council chair, treasurer, SCSA member", "None: standing sounds arrogant", "TIC of a specialist program that sends students to your BMus"], "answer": 2, "why": "It gives the single credential that makes your point relevant to that committee."}, {"input": "You remember a 'study' showing that ensemble participation improves attendance, but cannot name it.", "q": "What should the page say?", "options": ["Research shows ensembles improve attendance", "? verify source by Wed, else cut; fallback: our ensemble attendance figures", "Studies show attendance gains"], "answer": 1, "why": "Only defensible evidence goes on the page; unverified claims are marked and replaced with local facts."}, {"input": "During the meeting, Ms Bianchi (online) makes a point that matches your prepared point 2.", "q": "What do you write?", "options": ["≈ SB(Z) next to point 2", "✗ SB: she took my point", "Nothing; you will make the point anyway"], "answer": 0, "why": "Marking ≈ with initials lets your prepared point arrive as building on a named colleague."}, {"input": "Ten minutes in, the chair reframes the question in a way none of your prepared points address.", "q": "What is the best move on your page?", "options": ["Deliver your three points as planned", "Stay silent on this item", "Mark Δ on the question line and re-order or replace points"], "answer": 2, "why": "The page is a launch pad, not a script; Δ records the change and lets you adapt."}, {"input": "Your draft minutes line reads: 'Committee to consider the matter further.'", "q": "What is wrong with it?", "options": ["Nothing; it is suitably neutral", "It records no decision, owner or date", "It is too long"], "answer": 1, "why": "Draft minute wording should state the decision you want, with owners and a date."}, {"input": "You have two minutes before a meeting and no page.", "q": "What is the minimum worth writing?", "options": ["Likely question, one point with one fact, a minute", "Your full standing and three points", "Nothing; two minutes is not enough"], "answer": 0, "why": "Those three lines give you a destination, evidence and wording, which is enough to contribute decisively."}], "drills": [{"level": 1, "kind": "write", "input": "Situation: next week's department meeting will discuss the Year 9 camp. You think the camp should move from Term 3 to Term 1, because Term 3 clashes with the jazz festival and Year 9 students lose two rehearsals in the fortnight before they perform. The head of learning area has said the camp company can offer Term 1 dates.", "task": "Write a pre-meeting page: likely question, one point of six words or fewer with evidence, and draft minutes.", "model": "Likely Q: keep camp in Term 3 or move it?\n1 Move camp to Term 1\n   # Term 3 costs Yr 9 two festival rehearsals\nMinutes: 'That the Year 9 camp move to Term 1 from 2027.'"}, {"level": 1, "kind": "write", "input": "Situation: you will speak at a parent information evening for families of incoming Year 7 specialist music students. You expect questions about the three-year commitment, because two families asked about withdrawal at the audition. You know that each student has an instrumental tutor who checks in each term, and that skills compound across Years 7 to 9.", "task": "Write a likely question, two points of six words or fewer with one fact each, and a closing sentence you will say.", "model": "Likely Q: what are we committing to?\n1 Three years, by design\n   # skills compound across Years 7 to 9\n2 Support exists if it's hard\n   # instrumental tutor check-in each term\nClose: 'The commitment is three years because that is how long it takes to become a musician who chooses to stay.'"}, {"level": 2, "kind": "write", "input": "Situation: the school council meets on Monday. Item 5 is 'Facilities update'. You have heard from the business manager that the performing arts building maintenance has been deferred again, for the third year. You know the main rehearsal room's air conditioning failed four times in Term 1, and that two ensemble rehearsals were cancelled. You are not sure whether temperatures were recorded. You chair the council, but on this item you want to speak as a staff member, which requires care. The principal will report first, and is sympathetic but constrained by the regional budget. Two parents on council have children in the affected ensembles and may raise it themselves. Council meetings run to a strict ninety minutes.", "task": "Write a full pre-meeting page: likely real question, three points with evidence, a standing line suitable for this room and your dual role, and draft minutes.", "model": "PRE-MEETING  School council, Item 5\nLikely Q: does PA maintenance stay deferred again?\n1 Deferral now affects teaching\n   # air-con failed 4× Term 1; 2 rehearsals cancelled\n2 Health and safety, not comfort\n   # room temperature records? ? check with business manager\n3 Ask for a date, not money\nStanding: step out of chair for this item; speak as TIC\nMinutes: 'That council request a maintenance timeline for the performing arts building at its next meeting.'"}, {"level": 2, "kind": "speak", "input": "Situation: in ten minutes you speak to a university partnership committee. Your pre-meeting page reads:\nLikely Q: continue the bridging program in 2027?\n1 It works locally  # 12 attended, 7 offered places\n2 Reach is the problem  # 40 places, 12 filled\n3 Send direct to TICs, Term 1 Week 2\nStanding: TIC, sent 5 of the 12\nMinutes: 'That the program continue, with invitations sent directly to music TICs by Week 2.'\nThe chair has said the program's future is 'genuinely open'. Around the table: the conservatorium's head of access, a Department officer, two TICs from other schools and the chair. You have been told you will have about a minute.", "task": "Speak from the page alone for sixty seconds, using the standing line once and ending on the minute wording.", "seconds": 60, "model": "I'd support continuing the program, and I can speak to it directly: five of last year's twelve came from our school. || Two things. First, it *works*. Of the twelve who attended, seven were offered places. Second, the problem is reach: forty places, twelve filled. Invitations went to principals and reached music staff late. So my suggestion is simple: send them directly to music TICs, by Week 2 of Term 1. For the minutes, perhaps: that the program continue in 2027, with invitations sent directly to music TICs by Week 2."}, {"level": 3, "kind": "write", "input": "Situation: the SCSA committee meets on Thursday to respond to the Board's rejection of the new course. The Board's letter gives two reasons: the assessment load is excessive, and implementation in small and regional schools has not been demonstrated. You know the draft has nine assessment tasks against six in the current course. You believe that four schools, including yours, would pilot the course, but only yours has confirmed in writing. You think, but are not certain, that one university has written in support. The chair, Dr Ruth Hallam, tends to refer items back without decision, usually with the phrase 'return to it next time'. Associate Professor Lim is likely to argue for resubmission. Ms Bianchi, who is online from an independent school, has expressed concern about teaching time and has regional contacts. Mr Tait, from a public school, is quiet but precise about numbers. You want the committee to agree a resubmission timeline and set up a small drafting group, and you would be willing to serve on it. Before the last meeting the chair circulated a paper by email the night before, which most members had not read. The meeting is hybrid; about half the members will be online, and the Authority's officer usually speaks last.", "task": "Write a complete pre-meeting page: likely real question, three points with defensible evidence (marking anything unverified), predicted allies with ≈ placeholders, a standing line for this room, a note on the chair's tendency, and draft minutes.", "model": "PRE-MEETING  SCSA CAC, Board response\nLikely Q: revise and resubmit, or let it lapse?\n1 Load: cut to six tasks\n   # draft 9 vs current 6  ≈ IT likely\n2 Implementation: pilot in four schools\n   # 4 schools incl. ours  ? confirm the other three by Wed\n3 Resubmit by Term 2 2027\n   ? uni letter of support: verify before citing, else omit\nAllies: RL (resubmit) ≈ 1, 3; SB(Z) ≈ 2 if regional schools included\nChair: RH refers back → have a date and owners ready\nStanding: TIC, public specialist program; would teach it\nMinutes: 'That a drafting group (RL, SB, DS) revise the assessment program to six tasks and confirm pilot schools, reporting by [date], for resubmission by Term 2 2027.'"}, {"level": 3, "kind": "speak", "input": "Situation: you walked in with a pre-meeting page (1 load: cut to six tasks; 2 pilot in four schools; 3 resubmit by Term 2 2027; minutes: a drafting group reports next meeting) and could not verify the university letter of support.\nAssociate Professor Lim (in the room): I've read the Board's letter three times. It is, in substance, a request for revision, not a rejection. They say the content is coherent. They object to the load and to implementation. Both are fixable.\nMs Sara Bianchi (online, a second of lag): Sorry, I was muted. I agree with that. My worry is teaching time. Two colleagues in regional schools rang me after the letter came out, and they're anxious. If this lands on a one-teacher department, it will be very hard to deliver. And while I have the floor, the exam timetable clash with the drama practical happened again this year, and I really do think someone needs to raise it with the Authority.\nMr Ian Tait (in the room, quietly): Just a number. I counted. The draft has nine assessment tasks across the pair of units. The current course has six. Whatever else we do, that is the figure the Board will look at first.\nDr Ruth Hallam (chair): Thank you all. It sounds as though there's general goodwill. Perhaps we note the feedback and return to it next time, when people have had a chance to reflect. Dijon?", "task": "Speak for ninety seconds. Attach your prepared points to the speakers who touched them, omit the unverified letter, park the timetabling drift, respond to the chair's referral and land on your minute wording.", "seconds": 90, "model": "Thank you, Ruth. I'd like to build on three colleagues. Associate Professor Lim has given us the right reading: this is *a request for revision, not a rejection*. Ian has given us the number the Board will look at: nine tasks against six. And Sara has told us who will feel it most, which is smaller and regional schools. || So rather than return to it next time, could I suggest three things now. First, we revise the assessment program to six tasks. Second, we include a regional school among the pilots; Sara, perhaps your colleagues would be willing. Third, a small drafting group, Associate Professor Lim, Sara and myself, reports back next meeting with a revised draft, so that we can resubmit by Term 2 2027. Sara's timetabling point deserves its own item, and I'd be glad to see it listed. For the minutes: that a drafting group revise the assessment program to six tasks and confirm pilot schools, reporting at the next meeting."}]}, {"id": "nt-8", "examples": [{"title": "Choosing the two circles", "input": "Note page, regional network meeting:\nROOM: LF (convenor)  'we're all solving it alone'  !\nROOM: GW (large school)  ✓ shared moderation, 'economies of scale'\nROOM: PK (small school, quiet)  # 3 students, no one to moderate with\nROOM: GW  'efficiency'  ⟲\nGAP: no host, no date", "output": "ROOM: (LF 'we're all solving it alone')  →5\nROOM: GW  ✓ shared moderation  →2\nROOM: (PK # 3 students, no one to moderate with)  →5\nGAP: no host, no date  ★ →6", "weak": "ROOM: (LF 'we're all solving it alone')\nROOM: (GW 'economies of scale')\nROOM: (GW 'efficiency')", "why": "The weak version circles the most prominent speaker twice. The strong version credits the convenor's phrase and the quiet small-school fact, which is the most persuasive evidence in the room and makes that speaker an ally."}, {"title": "One line per slot", "input": "Situation: school council, item on the uniform review. Your note page has fourteen lines, including six ME lines: three possible thanks (to the chair, to Karen for raising it, to the families who wrote in), two possible pieces of standing (staff representative, TIC Music) and a proposal for a Term 1 trial with family notice. You have twenty seconds before the chair calls you.", "output": "ME: thanks chair for time on agenda  →1\nROOM: (KP 'ask before we buy')  →2\nME: parent of none; TIC, run the program  →3\nME: all want families included  →4\nROOM: (MT ! notice before Jan order)  →5\nGAP: trial + notice  ★ →6\nME: @ survey Term 2  →7", "weak": "Every ME line underlined, three thanks options marked 1a, 1b, 1c, two standing lines marked 3, proposal not starred.", "why": "Numbering one line per slot turns a dense page into a route. Alternatives cost seconds at exactly the moment you have none, and the unstarred proposal leaves no destination."}, {"title": "Star first when time is short", "input": "Situation: an SCSA committee meeting has run forty minutes over. Dr Ruth Hallam (chair) says, 'Dijon, briefly, we're almost out of time.' You have perhaps five seconds before speaking. Your page is unmarked. The GAP line at the foot reads 'no owner, no date for redraft', and you know a three-person drafting group reporting at the next meeting would solve it.", "output": "GAP: no owner, no date for redraft  ★\nSpoken: 'Briefly, then. One proposal: a drafting group of three reports next meeting, so that the redraft has an owner and a date.'", "weak": "Spoken: 'Thanks, Ruth. I'd just like to say I agree with much of what's been said, particularly Associate Professor Lim's comments, and I think there are some important issues around load and, um, implementation...'", "why": "With no time, the star is the only action worth taking. A contribution with a destination survives having no opening; a courteous opening without a destination is what the chair cuts off."}, {"title": "Credit with the exact words", "input": "Situation: SCSA committee, Board feedback item. Your page has two circled lines:\n(JR 'the Board has given us a map, not a verdict')\n(TN 'nine tasks is a year of rehearsal for nothing')\nDr James Reid spoke first, online; Mr Tom Nash spoke once, quietly, from the end of the table. You are about to reach slot 2 and slot 5.", "output": "James put it well: the Board has given us *a map, not a verdict*. And Tom's line will stay with me: nine tasks is a year of rehearsal for nothing.", "weak": "As James and Tom both said, the Board's letter is quite constructive and the assessment is too heavy.", "why": "Paraphrase dissolves the credit; the exact words show you were listening and give both speakers ownership of your argument. That is why the circle is around the phrase, not the speaker."}, {"title": "Standing from the pre-meeting page", "input": "Situation: arts funding panel, regional jazz workshops. You have marked your note page in the twenty-second routine, but it has no ME line about your standing. Your pre-meeting page, beside it, reads: 'Standing: festival treasurer; run the regional band program'. The chair has just said she will come to you after the current speaker.", "output": "Pre-meeting: Standing: festival treasurer  →3\n(draw the arrow across; no new line written)", "weak": "Writes a new standing line on the note page during the twenty seconds: 'I am the treasurer and have run regional programs for...'", "why": "Some slots are already filled on the pre-meeting page. Numbering across both pages takes a second; writing a new line takes ten and loses the thread of the room."}, {"title": "The route spoken", "input": "Marked page, festival board:\nME: thanks Ingrid  →1\nROOM: (KM 'hope isn't a budget line')  →2\nME: treasurer  →3\nME: all want March to go ahead  →4\nROOM: (HR # grant window closes mid-Feb)  →5\nGAP: who prepares plan B, by when  ★ →6\nME: @ two-scenario budget, Dec meeting  →7", "output": "Thank you, Ingrid. Kofi's right that *hope isn't a budget line*. Speaking as treasurer, | I think we all want the March festival to go ahead in full. Helen has told us the grant window closes in mid-February, so the question is who prepares the alternative, and when. || My proposal: I prepare a two-scenario budget, with and without the sponsor, for the December meeting, and Helen and I scope the grant in parallel. For the minutes: treasurer to present a two-scenario budget in December.", "weak": "Thanks Ingrid. I agree with a lot of what's been said, especially about the sponsor. As treasurer I'm a bit worried, to be honest. I think we probably need to think about other options, grants maybe, and I'm happy to help with that if people want.", "why": "The strong version follows the numbers in order and lands on the starred proposal. The weak version has the same raw material but no route, so it drifts into hedging and an offer of help instead of a proposal."}, {"title": "Moving briskly through slots one to five", "input": "Situation: department meeting. You have marked the page. Your proposal is that the Year 8 composition task be cut to sixteen bars with two checkpoints. In your last meeting you spent ninety seconds on thanks and context before reaching the proposal.", "output": "Thanks, Carla. You named it exactly: *a unit about deadlines, not about music*. I've taught it four times and felt the same. We all want them to finish something they're proud of. Half didn't finish this year. So, one change: sixteen bars, two checkpoints, and you and I redesign it before Term 1.", "weak": "Thanks, Carla, and thanks everyone for a really honest discussion. I think it's really important that we reflect on these things as a department, and I know we've all been under a lot of pressure this term with reports and the concert, and I suppose the composition unit has been part of that...", "why": "Knowing the destination lets each of slots one to five take a single sentence. The weak version is warm but has not yet reached slot four after the time the strong version took to finish."}, {"title": "Crediting the quiet expert", "input": "Note page, principal's leadership meeting on the Melbourne tour:\nROOM: AW (deputy)  ! wellbeing  'thought it through properly'\nROOM: BP (business manager)  ✓ cost manageable\nROOM: RJ (school psychologist, spoke once)  'name one adult per anxious child'\nROOM: AW  ⟲ wellbeing", "output": "ROOM: (AW 'thought it through properly')  →2\nROOM: BP ✓ cost  →5\nROOM: (RJ 'name one adult per anxious child')  →5 ★ →6", "weak": "ROOM: (AW 'thought it through properly')\nROOM: (BP ✓ cost manageable)", "why": "The psychologist's single line is the crucial mechanism, and building the proposal on it makes the deputy's concern answerable. Crediting the quiet expert is both generous and strategically sound."}, {"title": "Numbering a hybrid page", "input": "Note page, university committee, with online members:\nROOM: SL, MT, DS   Z: SK, HO\nROOM: SK(Z)  'a ceiling, not a scale'\nchat: HO  # 87% of grades 6 or 7?\nROOM: MT  ✓ revise descriptors\nGAP: no trial cohort named", "output": "ROOM: (SK(Z) 'a ceiling, not a scale')  →2\nchat: (HO # 87% of grades 6 or 7?)  →5  ? confirm figure aloud\nROOM: MT ✓ revise  →4\nME: our 2024 descriptor rewrite  →3\nGAP: no trial cohort named  ★ →6", "weak": "ROOM: (MT ✓ revise descriptors)\nGAP: no trial cohort\n(chat line ignored)", "why": "Circling an online speaker and a chat point credits the people least likely to be heard. Marking the chat figure with ? reminds you to attribute it to her rather than state it as fact."}, {"title": "When the gap has moved", "input": "Situation: SCSA committee. Your starred GAP line reads 'no owner, no date for redraft'. While you were marking your page, Mr Ian Tait said: 'Why don't three of us redraft it and bring it back next meeting?' He did not mention a target number of tasks or which meeting. The chair is turning to you.", "output": "GAP: no owner, no date  ★ →6  Δ IT proposed it\nNew ★: add the target (six tasks) + date to IT's proposal\nSpoken: 'I'd like to support Ian's proposal and add two details: that the group work to a target of six tasks, and that it reports at the March meeting.'", "weak": "Spoken: 'I was going to suggest that a small group redraft the assessment and bring it back next meeting.'", "why": "When someone takes your star, move it to what is still missing and build on them by name. Repeating their idea as your own costs goodwill; completing it gains an ally."}, {"title": "Closing with an action", "input": "Situation: festival board, item on adjudicator feedback forms. Your page has two circled phrases, slots →1 to →6 numbered, and a ★ on the proposal for a short standard form. There is no line numbered →7. You are willing to draft the form yourself. The chair, Mr Gareth Pryce, is calling you now.", "output": "ME: @ DS draft form, March board  →7 (written in 3 seconds)\nSpoken close: 'I'm happy to draft the form for the March meeting.'", "weak": "Spoken close: 'Anyway, that's just a thought.'", "why": "The close is the slot most often abandoned under pressure. An action with an owner and date gives the chair something to record; a self-deprecating close invites the room to let the idea go."}, {"title": "Rehearsal against an old page", "input": "Situation: at home, you take a page of notes from last month's parent association meeting on the instrument fleet. It has eleven lines, including Mr Tom Hadley's 'more transparency' and Ms Kim Lee's 'the kids notice when it's new', and a GAP line about reporting to parents. You set a twenty-second timer.", "output": "0 to 5 s: circle two phrases (TH 'more transparency'; KL 'the kids notice when it's new')\n5 to 15 s: number →1 to →7, one line each\n15 to 20 s: ★ GAP 'no report format or date'\nThen: 90 s spoken, following numbers only", "weak": "Reads the whole page again slowly, then speaks without marking it.", "why": "The routine only works under pressure if it is a habit. Timed rehearsal on old pages builds the order (circle, number, star) into reflex, which is what remains available when the chair turns to you."}], "quiz": [{"input": "Your page shows four phrases from the chair, two from a senior academic and one from a quiet teacher who gave the key figure.", "q": "Which two should you circle?", "options": ["Two from the chair", "One from the senior academic and the quiet teacher's figure", "The two longest phrases"], "answer": 1, "why": "Credit for usefulness: ideally one senior voice and one quieter speaker whose fact carries the case."}, {"input": "You have only five seconds before speaking.", "q": "Which single action matters most?", "options": ["Number slot 1 so your thanks is polished", "Circle a phrase to credit", "Star the gap and the first part of your proposal"], "answer": 2, "why": "A contribution with a destination survives a weak opening; the star gives you the destination."}, {"input": "You have three possible thanks lines on your page.", "q": "How many should you number?", "options": ["One", "All three, as options", "None; thanks is optional"], "answer": 0, "why": "One line per slot; alternatives cost time at the moment you have least."}, {"input": "Circled phrase: (AL 'a request for revision, not a rejection')", "q": "Which spoken credit uses it best?", "options": ["As Associate Professor Lim suggested, the letter is fairly positive.", "Associate Professor Lim gave us the right reading: this is a request for revision, not a rejection.", "I think someone said it's not a rejection."], "answer": 1, "why": "It names the speaker and quotes the exact phrase, which is the point of circling it."}, {"input": "Your standing is already on the pre-meeting page.", "q": "What do you do during the twenty seconds?", "options": ["Rewrite it on the note page", "Skip slot 3", "Number it →3 where it is"], "answer": 2, "why": "Numbering across both pages takes a second and keeps your eyes on the room."}, {"input": "Before you speak, another member proposes your starred idea.", "q": "What is the best move?", "options": ["Make your proposal anyway as originally planned", "Support them by name and move your star to what is still missing", "Say nothing on this item"], "answer": 1, "why": "Completing their proposal gains an ally and keeps your contribution distinct."}, {"input": "Your marked page has →1 to →6 but nothing at →7.", "q": "What should you add in the last seconds?", "options": ["An @ action with owner and date", "A second thanks", "A summary of all speakers"], "answer": 0, "why": "The close gives the chair something to minute; an action with owner and date is the strongest close."}, {"input": "You want the routine to hold up under pressure.", "q": "Which practice is most useful?", "options": ["Rereading the lesson before each meeting", "Writing longer notes so there is more to choose from", "Timed twenty-second marking of old pages, then speaking from the numbers"], "answer": 2, "why": "Under pressure you rely on habit, which only timed rehearsal builds."}], "drills": [{"level": 1, "kind": "write", "input": "Ms Carla Nguyen (department meeting): The Year 8 composition unit has become a unit about deadlines, not about music. Half of them never finished, and the ones who did mostly rushed it in the last lesson. The software crashed twice in Week 7. I don't think we can run it the same way again next year.", "task": "Write three ROOM or ME lines and a GAP line, then mark them in twenty seconds: circle one phrase, number the slots you can, and star.", "model": "ROOM: (CN 'a unit about deadlines, not about music')  →2\nROOM: CN # half unfinished  →5\nME: we all want them to finish something  →4\nGAP: no redesign, no owner  ★ →6"}, {"level": 1, "kind": "speak", "input": "Situation: school council, item on lunchtime practice space. You have marked your page in twenty seconds:\nROOM: (RL 'a good problem to have')  →2\nME: we want them practising  →4\nGAP: nowhere to go at lunch  ★ →6\nME: @ DS roster from Week 3  →7\nThe chair, who knows time is short, says: 'Dijon, just quickly.'", "task": "Speak from the numbers only, in order.", "seconds": 30, "model": "Ray's right, it's *a good problem to have*. We all want them practising. The gap is space: they have nowhere to go at lunch. So I'll put together a duty roster for the two practice rooms, starting Week 3."}, {"level": 2, "kind": "write", "input": "Mr Daniel Osei (arts funding panel, online): I'm enthusiastic about the regional jazz workshops. The artistic case is strong and the partners are credible. But I can't yet picture what a Year 9 kid in Geraldton actually gets on a Tuesday. That's what I'd need to see before I could score it highly.\nMs Wendy Park (panel member, in the room, briefly): Last round we funded two similar projects. The one that succeeded had a local teacher attached to every visit. The other didn't, and attendance fell away after the second session. Mr Osei (again, after a pause): And I'd want to know who is accountable locally if something goes wrong on a visit. The chair then says the panel has ten minutes left on this application and asks you, as the applicant's representative, to respond.", "task": "Write a note page of five to seven lines, then mark it: circle two phrases, number one line per slot (add ME lines for slots 1, 3, 4 and 7), and star the proposal.", "model": "ME: thanks panel for the question  →1\nROOM: (DO(Z) 'what a Year 9 kid in Geraldton actually gets')  →2\nME: festival treasurer; run regional program  →3\nME: we all want it to last beyond visit 2  →4\nROOM: (WP local teacher on every visit = success)  →5\nGAP: no named local teacher per site  ★ →6\nME: @ confirm local teacher each site before funding release  →7"}, {"level": 2, "kind": "speak", "input": "Situation: principal's leadership meeting on the Melbourne tour. Your marked page:\nME: thanks Anne for raising it  →1\nROOM: (AW 'thought it through properly')  →2\nME: led 3 interstate tours  →3\nME: all want the kids safe and the tour to go  →4\nROOM: (RJ 'name one adult per anxious child')  →5\nGAP: no wellbeing plan  ★ →6  1 RJ's pairing 2 medication protocol 3 parent briefing\nME: @ DS + RJ plan by 20 Nov  →7\nThe deputy principal, Ms Anne Whitfield, has just said she is 'not yet persuaded'. The school psychologist, Ms Rachel James, who gave you the pairing idea, is in the room.", "task": "Speak for sixty seconds following the numbers, crediting both circled phrases with the exact words.", "seconds": 60, "model": "Thank you, Anne, for raising it. You're right that we need to have *thought it through properly*. I've led three interstate tours, and I think we all want the same thing: the students safe, and the tour going ahead. Rachel gave us the mechanism: *name one adult per anxious child*. || So my proposal is a wellbeing plan in three parts. First, Rachel's pairing, with every student who needs it assigned a named adult. Second, a written medication protocol. Third, a parent briefing before the deposit is due. Rachel and I will bring the plan to this meeting by 20 November."}, {"level": 3, "kind": "write", "input": "Dr Ruth Hallam (SCSA committee chair): Let's take views on the Board's letter. James, you first.\nDr James Reid (university, online): Thank you. I'd say simply that the Board has given us a map, not a verdict. They've told us where the problems are. Our job is to fix them and resubmit, not to relitigate the course's rationale.\nMs Amy Moore (independent school, in the room): I agree, though I'd be careful. The Board's main objection is the assessment load, and they're right. We built a course that would be wonderful in a school with three music teachers. Most schools have one. And honestly, while we're on it, I've never understood why the Authority's consultation window is always in Term 4, when teachers are drowning in reports. It happened again with the drama review.\nMr Tom Nash (public school, in the room, quietly): Nine tasks is a year of rehearsal for nothing. Students in the current course do six. If we go to the Board with nine again, we'll get the same letter.\nDr Hallam: Good. So, a degree of consensus that the load is the issue. I wonder whether we should ask the Authority's officers for guidance before we do anything else. Dijon, you've been waiting.", "task": "Write a full note page (Q, ROOM lines with symbols, the drift, ME lines, GAP), then mark it in twenty seconds: two circles, one line per slot →1 to →7, and the star.", "model": "Q: Resubmit for 2028?\nME: thanks Ruth  →1\nROOM: (JR(Z) 'the Board has given us a map, not a verdict')  →2\nROOM: AM  ✓ load  'three music teachers ... most schools have one'  !\nROOM: AM  drift: Term 4 consultation window  → park\nROOM: (TN 'nine tasks is a year of rehearsal for nothing')  # 9 vs 6  →5\nROOM: RH  Δ ask officers first  ✗ delay, no owner\nME: TIC, one-teacher reality in many public schools  →3\nME: all want it to reach students in 2028  →4\nGAP: who redrafts to six, by when; officers can be consulted in parallel  ★ →6\nME: @ drafting group JR, AM, DS, report next meeting  →7"}, {"level": 3, "kind": "speak", "input": "Situation: SCSA committee, Board response item. You have marked your page in twenty seconds. Your route is: →1 thanks Ruth; →2 James Reid's 'a map, not a verdict'; →3 TIC in a public school, one-teacher reality; →4 all want it reaching students in 2028; →5 Tom Nash's 'nine tasks is a year of rehearsal for nothing', nine against six; ★ →6 drafting group to six tasks; →7 report next meeting. The chair suggested asking the Authority's officers for guidance first. Amy Moore drifted to the Term 4 consultation window. Since you marked the page, Ms Moore has added: 'And let's not forget regional schools, where it's not even one music teacher, sometimes it's a visiting specialist once a fortnight.' The chair says: 'Dijon, you've been waiting.' Dr Reid is online; Ms Moore and Mr Nash are in the room. The meeting has fifteen minutes left, and two other members are in the queue after you. Mr Nash has said nothing since his one line about the tasks, and has his arms folded. Dr Reid's video has frozen twice during the item, so he may have missed part of the discussion. The chair's suggestion, in her words, was: 'I wonder whether we should ask the Authority's officers for guidance before we do anything else.' The secretary has asked for clear wording on any decision.", "task": "Speak for ninety seconds following your numbers. Credit both circled phrases exactly, fold in Ms Moore's new point at slot 5 without losing your route, accept the chair's suggestion as a parallel step rather than a delay, park the drift and land on the starred proposal and the action.", "seconds": 90, "model": "Thank you, Ruth. James has given us the frame: *a map, not a verdict*. Speaking as a TIC in a public school, I'd add that the one-teacher department is the norm, and Amy's just reminded us that in regional schools it may be a visiting specialist once a fortnight. I think we all want this course in front of students in 2028. Tom gave us the number that decides it: *nine tasks is a year of rehearsal for nothing*. Students currently do six. || So, a proposal in two parts. First, a small drafting group, James, Amy and myself, revises the assessment program to six tasks and reports to the next meeting. Second, Ruth, your suggestion of guidance from the Authority's officers is a good one, and we could seek it in parallel, so it informs the draft rather than delays it. Amy's point about the consultation window deserves its own item. For the minutes: that a drafting group revise the assessment program to six tasks and report at the next meeting."}]}, {"id": "nt-9", "examples": [{"title": "The thirty-second roster", "input": "Situation: the SCSA committee is about to open Item 4. In the room: Dr Ruth Hallam (chair), Associate Professor Lim, Mr Ian Tait, Ms Kate Wells and you. On screen: Ms Sara Bianchi, Dr Henry Oduya, Ms Priya Rao (camera off) and Mr Matt Weir (joined late).", "output": "ROOM: RH (chair), RL, IT, KW, DS (me)   Z: SB, HO, PR (cam off), MW (late)", "weak": "Present: Ruth, Lim, Ian, Kate, me, plus people online", "why": "'Plus people online' is exactly how online members get overlooked. A roster with initials lets you credit an online speaker by name, and noting camera off and late arrival tells you who may need a point repeated."}, {"title": "The chat as a speaker", "input": "Dr Henry Oduya (online, SCSA committee) typed in the chat while Associate Professor Lim was speaking about the Board's letter: 'For context, enrolments in the current course have been broadly steady, but the smaller schools are where it's fragile. Happy to share figures.' Nobody in the room looked at the chat, and Lim carried on without pausing.", "output": "chat: HO  small schools 'where it's fragile'  # figures offered  @ HO to share?", "weak": "(nothing written: you were listening to Lim)", "why": "Points made only in chat are routinely lost from the minutes. Recording them with a chat: prefix lets you surface the point aloud and credit the person who typed it, which earns goodwill from the online room."}, {"title": "Marking an online speaker", "input": "Ms Sara Bianchi (online, SCSA committee, audio breaking up): I'll keep it brief because the audio's patchy. I support revision, and I agree with what's been said about load. But implementation has to be tested in a school without a specialist music team, or we're testing the easy case. Sorry, I'll go back on mute.", "output": "ROOM: SB(Z)  ✓ revise  'or we're testing the easy case'  !", "weak": "ROOM: SB  supports revision, implementation", "why": "The (Z) marker reminds you that this voice will be easy for the physical room to forget, and keeping her exact phrase gives you a precise credit that will carry over a poor connection."}, {"title": "Late replies and ⟲", "input": "Sequence on a hybrid SCSA call: Associate Professor Lim argues for resubmission because the content is coherent. Ms Kate Wells (in the room) raises the timing of consultation. Mr Ian Tait gives the task count, nine against six. Then Dr Henry Oduya (online, after a pause): 'Yes, I'd agree with that, the content is coherent and we should resubmit.'", "output": "ROOM: HO(Z)  ⟲ RL (lag)  ✓ resubmit, content coherent", "weak": "ROOM: HO  ✓ agrees with IT on task count?", "why": "Online members often respond two speakers late. Marking ⟲ to the speaker he is actually answering prevents you mistaking a delayed agreement for a new position, or crediting it to the wrong point."}, {"title": "Tracking the hands queue", "input": "Situation: on a university partnership committee, Associate Professor Simon Kerr (online) has raised his virtual hand for the third time on this item. The chair has taken two in-room speakers since your hand went up. There is no visible queue.", "output": "ROOM: SK(Z)  hand ×3\nME: hand up (room + screen) 10:42; queue ≈ #3 after SK, MT\n★ prepare now", "weak": "Waits, hand half-raised in the room only, and starts preparing when called.", "why": "Tracking the queue lets you predict when you will be called and convert your notes before then. Raising your hand on screen as well as in the room ensures the chair sees you in both places."}, {"title": "Signposting for the online room", "input": "Situation: you are about to make a contribution with two proposals on a hybrid SCSA call. Two online members have mentioned patchy audio. Your draft opening is: 'I wonder whether, given what Associate Professor Lim has said about the Board's reading of the load, and bearing in mind Sara's concern, which I share, about smaller schools, we might consider both a reduction and a pilot.'", "output": "Two things, briefly. First, the load: we cut to six tasks, as Ian suggested. Second, the pilot: we include a school without a specialist team, which was Sara's point.", "weak": "I wonder whether, given what Associate Professor Lim has said about the Board's reading of the load, and bearing in mind Sara's concern, which I share, about smaller schools, we might consider both a reduction and a pilot.", "why": "Elegant subordinate clauses collapse over a weak connection. Announcing the number of points and marking each one with 'First' and 'Second' carries across lag and dropped audio."}, {"title": "Crediting someone on screen", "input": "Dr Mei Tan (online, conservatorium): We filled twelve of forty places in the bridging program, and seven of the twelve were offered places. It's a distribution problem, not a design problem.\nMr Patrick Doyle (in the room, at some length): Information goes to principals and stops there, and by the time music teachers hear, families have made plans. I've seen it with three programs now.", "output": "Mei made the point on screen that this is *a distribution problem, not a design problem*, and Patrick's account of how information stops at the principal's desk explains why.", "weak": "As Patrick said, the information isn't getting through, and I think that's the real issue.", "why": "The physical room tends to remember the in-room speaker. Leading with the online speaker's exact phrase, then linking the in-room point to it, signals unusual attentiveness and balances the two rooms."}, {"title": "Verifying a chat figure", "input": "Ms Priya Rao (SCSA committee, in the chat, camera off, while the room discusses assessment load): 'Didn't NSW cut its senior music tasks to five recently?' Nobody responds, and nobody in the room appears to have seen it. You are not certain whether it is accurate, and an Authority officer is present who would know.", "output": "chat: PR  ? # NSW tasks to 5  → unverified, do not state as fact\nME: if used: 'Priya asked in the chat whether another jurisdiction has reduced tasks; it may be worth the officers confirming.'", "weak": "ME: NSW cut to 5 → use as evidence", "why": "A figure in the chat is a question until it is checked. Surfacing it as Priya's question credits her without putting an unverified interstate claim into the minutes under your name."}, {"title": "The chair who forgets the screen", "input": "Mr Gareth Pryce (festival board chair, in the room): Right, I think we've heard from everyone. Let's move to a decision. Meanwhile your notes show that Mr Kofi Mensah (online) has had his hand up for two minutes and has typed 'can I come in briefly?' in the chat.", "output": "ROOM: KM(Z)  hand 2 min  chat: 'can I come in briefly?'  !\nME: say before vote: 'Gareth, Kofi has his hand up online.'", "weak": "ROOM: moving to vote", "why": "Tracking the screen lets you protect an online colleague's turn with one short sentence. It is a small act of chairing from the floor that the online room notices and remembers."}, {"title": "Two rooms, two summaries", "input": "Situation: a school council meets in hybrid format. In the room the mood is in favour of a uniform trial. Online, two parents have typed concerns in the chat about cost to families, which no one in the room has acknowledged.", "output": "ROOM: in room ✓ trial  (KP, AH, MT)\nchat: JL, SR  ! cost to families  (not yet heard)\nGAP: cost raised online, unanswered in room\nME: ★ name the chat point; propose hardship provision in trial", "weak": "ROOM: consensus for trial", "why": "A hybrid meeting is two rooms pretending to be one. Noting that the in-room consensus does not include the chat reveals a gap, and naming it prevents the decision being reopened by people who felt unheard."}, {"title": "Camera-off speakers", "input": "Dr Lucy Fenn (online, camera off, university partnership committee; the first time she has spoken in ninety minutes): I'll just say, as someone who has moderated the practical exam for several years, that the Board's concern about marker reliability is well founded. We see it every year. That's all from me.", "output": "ROOM: LF(Z, cam off)  ! moderator  'well founded, we see it every year'  ★ credit", "weak": "ROOM: someone online agreed about reliability", "why": "A quiet, camera-off expert with direct evidence is easy to lose entirely. Capturing her role and exact words gives you the most authoritative support in the meeting, and crediting her draws her into the discussion."}, {"title": "Building the forty-five-second opening", "input": "Situation: your hybrid SCSA note page reads:\nROOM: RL  'a request for revision, not a rejection'\nROOM: SB(Z)  'or we're testing the easy case'\nchat: HO  small schools 'where it's fragile'  # figures offered\nGAP: no small-school pilot named  ★\nTwo online members have mentioned poor audio. The chair, Dr Ruth Hallam, says: 'Dijon, you're next.'", "output": "Thank you, Ruth. Associate Professor Lim has given us the reading: *a request for revision, not a rejection*. Sara made the point on screen that otherwise *we're testing the easy case*, and Henry noted in the chat that small schools are where it's fragile. So, two things. First, we include a school without a specialist team in the pilot. Second, Henry, could you share those figures with the drafting group?", "weak": "Thanks, Ruth. I think there's been a lot of good discussion today, both here and online. I agree it's a revision rather than a rejection, and I think we need to think about smaller schools, which a few people have mentioned.", "why": "'A few people have mentioned' erases the online contributors. The strong version credits one in-room speaker, one on screen and one in chat, then signposts two proposals clearly enough to carry over the link."}], "quiz": [{"input": "A hybrid meeting is about to begin. You have one minute.", "q": "What should you write first?", "options": ["Your proposal", "A roster of in-room and online members with initials", "The chair's name"], "answer": 1, "why": "The roster makes online members visible on your page and lets you credit them by name."}, {"input": "Dr Henry Oduya types a useful figure in the chat while someone else is speaking.", "q": "How should it appear on your page?", "options": ["chat: HO  # figure", "ROOM: HO said something", "Not recorded; chat is informal"], "answer": 0, "why": "The chat is treated as a speaker, with a chat: prefix, so the point can be surfaced and credited."}, {"input": "An online member agrees with 'that point' two speakers after it was made.", "q": "What mark do you use?", "options": ["≈ with the previous speaker", "Δ", "⟲ with the speaker he is actually answering"], "answer": 2, "why": "⟲ records a lagged reply to an earlier point rather than a new position."}, {"input": "You are about to make two proposals; online audio is poor.", "q": "Which opening carries best?", "options": ["Building on several threads in the discussion, I wonder whether a combination of approaches might serve us.", "Two things, briefly. First... Second...", "I'll keep this short, but there's quite a lot to say."], "answer": 1, "why": "Explicit numbered signposting survives lag and dropped audio."}, {"input": "Someone types in the chat: 'Didn't Victoria change this last year?' Nobody confirms it.", "q": "How should you use it?", "options": ["State it as evidence for your case", "Ignore it", "Credit it as their question and suggest it be confirmed"], "answer": 2, "why": "An unverified chat claim is a question; surfacing it as such credits the person without putting an inaccuracy in the minutes."}, {"input": "The in-room chair is about to move to a vote; an online member has had a hand up for two minutes.", "q": "What is the best contribution?", "options": ["Say briefly that the member has a hand up online", "Wait for the vote", "Type in the chat that they should speak up"], "answer": 0, "why": "One short sentence protects their turn and balances the two rooms."}, {"input": "There is no visible queue and you have raised your hand in the room.", "q": "What else should you do?", "options": ["Nothing more", "Raise your hand on screen as well and note your position", "Interject when there is a pause online"], "answer": 1, "why": "Raising your hand in both places and tracking your place lets you prepare before you are called; interjection across lag is hazardous."}, {"input": "In the room, three people support a proposal. In the chat, two people have raised an unaddressed concern.", "q": "What does your GAP line say?", "options": ["Consensus reached", "Chat is off-topic", "Concern raised online, unanswered in the room"], "answer": 2, "why": "The two rooms may hold different views; naming the online concern prevents later reopening."}], "drills": [{"level": 1, "kind": "write", "input": "Situation: a hybrid festival board meeting is about to open. In the room: Mr Gareth Pryce (chair), Ms Ingrid Shaw, Ms Helen Ruiz and you. Online: Mr Kofi Mensah and Mr Ben Corrigan, whose camera is off. Before the first item, Kofi types in the chat: 'Reminder that the venue deposit is due 15 December.' Nobody in the room reads it aloud.", "task": "Write the roster line and the chat line.", "model": "ROOM: GP (chair), IS, HR, DS (me)   Z: KM, BC (cam off)\nchat: KM  ! # venue deposit due 15 Dec  @ treasurer"}, {"level": 1, "kind": "speak", "input": "Ms Sara Bianchi (SCSA committee, online, after her link drops and returns): Sorry, the link dropped, I missed the last minute. My point is just that a pilot in well-staffed schools tells us nothing about whether the course can be delivered where it matters. If all four pilot schools have specialist teams, we'd be testing the easy case.", "task": "Credit her by name as an online speaker with her exact phrase, then signpost one proposal.", "seconds": 30, "model": "Sara made the point on screen that otherwise *we'd be testing the easy case*. So one proposal: that at least one pilot school be a school without a specialist music team."}, {"level": 2, "kind": "write", "input": "Dr Mei Tan (conservatorium, online, slight lag): We had forty places in the bridging program and filled twelve. The outcomes were excellent for those twelve. I think it's a reach issue, not a quality issue.\nMr Patrick Doyle (Department of Education officer, in the room): Yes. Information goes to principals and stops there. Music teachers find out in Week 8, if at all, and the families have already made holiday plans.\nDr Mei Tan (online, a few seconds later): Sorry, just to come back to my point, I'd add that seven of the twelve were offered places.\n(Chat, Ms Jo Lam, camera off: 'Could invitations go via the TIC network list?')", "task": "Write a roster line, ROOM lines marked for online and lag, the chat line, and a GAP line.", "model": "ROOM: PD, DS (me) + others   Z: MT, JL (cam off)\nROOM: MT(Z)  # 12 of 40 filled  'a reach issue'\nROOM: PD  ≈ MT  info stops at principals, 'Week 8, if at all'\nROOM: MT(Z)  ⟲ own point  # 7 of 12 offered places  !\nchat: JL  ? invitations via TIC network list  ★ credit\nGAP: no direct channel or send date to music teachers"}, {"level": 2, "kind": "speak", "input": "Situation: university partnership committee, hybrid; the link is unreliable. Your note page reads:\nROOM: MT(Z)  'a reach issue'  # 12 of 40, 7 offered\nROOM: PD  info stops at principals  'Week 8, if at all'\nchat: JL  ? via TIC network list\nGAP: no direct channel, no date  ★\nThe chair says: 'Dijon, then we'll need to move on. Can those online hear us?' Two people type 'just' in the chat. Dr Mei Tan is online; Mr Patrick Doyle is beside you; Ms Jo Lam is online with her camera off. Somebody's microphone is producing an echo, and the chair asks everyone online to mute unless speaking.", "task": "Speak for forty-five seconds: credit Mei on screen, Patrick in the room and Jo's chat point, then signpost two proposals.", "seconds": 45, "model": "Mei made the point on screen that this is *a reach issue*: twelve of forty places filled, and seven of the twelve offered places. Patrick has told us why: the information reaches music teachers in Week 8, *if at all*. And Jo suggested in the chat that we use the TIC network list, which I'd support. So, two things. First, invitations go directly to music TICs through that list. Second, they go out by Week 2 of Term 1."}, {"level": 3, "kind": "write", "input": "Dr Ruth Hallam (chair, in the room): Welcome to those online. We're on the Board's response. I'll take hands in the room and on screen, in order.\nAssociate Professor Lim (in the room): The letter is a request for revision, not a rejection. The two reasons are load and implementation, and both can be addressed.\nDr Henry Oduya (online, hand raised, slight lag): Thank you, Ruth. I'd agree, and I'd add that implementation needs a regional test. In regional schools it's often one teacher or a visiting specialist. Also, slightly off the item, I'd like us to discuss at some point how SCSA consults during Term 4, because it's always the worst time for teachers.\n(Chat, Ms Priya Rao, camera off: 'Our school would pilot. We're 400 km from Perth, one music teacher.')\nMr Ian Tait (in the room, quietly): The draft has nine tasks. The current course has six.\nDr Henry Oduya (online, ten seconds later): Yes, and I'd agree that revision rather than rejection is the right reading.\n(Hands: Ms Sara Bianchi online has had her hand up since Lim spoke; your hand is up in the room.)\nDr Hallam: I'll come to Dijon, then Sara. (The chair has not noticed Ms Rao's chat message.)", "task": "Write a full hybrid note page: roster, Q, ROOM lines with (Z), ⟲, chat lines, the drift, the hands queue and your position, and a GAP line with a star.", "model": "ROOM: RH (chair), RL, IT, DS (me)   Z: HO, SB, PR (cam off)\nQ: Revise and resubmit, or let it lapse?\nROOM: RL  'a request for revision, not a rejection'  load + implementation\nROOM: HO(Z)  ✓  ! regional test, 'one teacher or a visiting specialist'\nROOM: HO(Z)  drift: SCSA Term 4 consultation → park\nchat: PR  ★ offers pilot, # 400 km, one teacher\nROOM: IT  # 9 tasks vs 6  !\nROOM: HO(Z)  ⟲ RL (lag)  ✓ not new\nQueue: DS next, then SB(Z) (hand since RL)\nGAP: no pilot list incl. regional school; no drafting owner or date  ★"}, {"level": 3, "kind": "speak", "input": "Situation: hybrid SCSA meeting. Your note page reads:\nROOM: RH (chair), RL, IT, DS (me)   Z: HO, SB, PR (cam off)\nQ: Revise and resubmit, or let it lapse?\nROOM: RL  'a request for revision, not a rejection'  load + implementation\nROOM: HO(Z)  ✓  ! regional test, 'one teacher or a visiting specialist'\nROOM: HO(Z)  drift: SCSA Term 4 consultation → park\nchat: PR  ★ offers pilot, # 400 km, one teacher\nROOM: IT  # 9 tasks vs 6  !\nROOM: HO(Z)  ⟲ RL (lag)  ✓ not new\nQueue: DS next, then SB(Z) (hand since RL)\nGAP: no pilot list incl. regional school; no drafting owner or date  ★\nThe chair says: 'Dijon, then Sara.' Two online members have mentioned patchy audio, and Ms Rao's camera is still off. Dr Oduya has just unmuted as though to speak again, then muted. The chair glances at the clock: about ten minutes remain on the item. Ms Bianchi's hand has been raised for six minutes, and Associate Professor Lim is nodding at you to go ahead. You have already raised your hand on screen as well as in the room. Earlier, Dr Oduya's own lagged reply repeated agreement with Associate Professor Lim's reading, and you marked it ⟲ rather than as a new point. The Authority's officer, in the room, has said nothing yet.", "task": "Speak for ninety seconds: credit one in-room speaker, one online speaker and the chat point by name, treat Henry's lagged reply correctly, park his drift, signpost a numbered proposal clearly for the online room, and leave Sara her turn.", "seconds": 90, "model": "Thank you, Ruth, and I'll be brief so we can come to Sara. Associate Professor Lim has given us the reading: *a request for revision, not a rejection*. Ian has given us the number: nine tasks against six. Henry made the point on screen that implementation needs a regional test, where it's often *one teacher or a visiting specialist*. And Priya has just offered, in the chat, exactly that: her school is four hundred kilometres from Perth, with one music teacher, and would pilot. || So, three things. First, we revise to six tasks. Second, Priya's school joins the pilot list, so we test the hard case. Third, a small drafting group reports to the next meeting. Henry's point about consultation timing deserves its own item. For the minutes: that a drafting group revise the assessment program to six tasks and confirm pilot schools, including a regional school, by the next meeting. Thank you; over to Sara."}]}, {"id": "nt-10", "examples": [{"title": "The queue with the screen included", "input": "Situation: you chair the school council. Hands go up in this order: Mrs Karen Pope (in the room), Mr Ahmed Hassan (in the room), Mr Rob Bell (online), Ms Mai Tran (in the room). Mr Bell's hand appeared on screen before Ms Tran's in the room.", "output": "QUEUE: KP  AH  (Z) RB  MT", "weak": "QUEUE: KP  AH  MT  (online later)", "why": "Putting online members in the queue in the order they raised their hands stops the room being privileged by proximity, and it is visible fairness the online members will notice."}, {"title": "Position marks for balance", "input": "Mrs Karen Pope: Before we buy anything, we should ask families. I'd want a full consultation. 'Ask before we buy.'\nMr Ahmed Hassan: I'd rather trial the new jacket in Term 1 and see.\nMr Rob Bell (online): I support Ahmed. A trial is consultation, in a sense.", "output": "ROOM: KP  Δ full consultation  'ask before we buy'\nROOM: AH  Δ trial jacket T1\nROOM: RB(Z)  ✓ AH  'a trial is consultation, in a sense'  ≈ bridge?", "weak": "Karen: wants consultation. Ahmed: trial. Rob: agrees with Ahmed.", "why": "Marking each position with Δ or ✓ lets the chair see the balance at a glance. Noting Rob's phrase as a possible bridge shows where a compromise might be tested before calling the vote."}, {"title": "Actions need owners and dates", "input": "School council, uniform review, near the end of the item.\nMr Ahmed Hassan: I'm happy to draft something about the trial, if that helps.\nMs Mai Tran: And I can let families know, once we've decided. It would need to go out before the end of term.\nMrs Karen Pope: Someone should look into suppliers too. I don't know who.", "output": "@ AH  draft trial proposal  by 28 Nov\n@ MT  family notice  by 5 Dec\n@ ? suppliers  → ask KP to own, date?", "weak": "Actions: trial draft, family notice, suppliers", "why": "An action without an owner and a date is an aspiration, and the minutes will show it. The ? against suppliers reminds the chair to assign it before the item closes."}, {"title": "The summary before the vote", "input": "Chair's page:\nQ: Uniform review: full consult or trial?\nROOM: KP  Δ full consultation  'ask before we buy'\nROOM: AH  Δ trial jacket T1\nROOM: MT  ! families need notice pre-Jan order\nCHAIR: ≈ AH + MT → trial T1, survey T2", "output": "We've heard three positions. Karen has asked that we *ask before we buy*; Ahmed favours a trial of the new jacket in Term 1; and Mai has reminded us that families need notice before the January order. || The motion before us is a trial in Term 1, with a survey of families in Term 2, which answers Karen's point. Ahmed to draft by 28 November, Mai to brief families by 5 December.", "weak": "OK, so I think we've had a good discussion. There seems to be general support for some kind of trial, and obviously we'll consult families. Shall we go with that?", "why": "The weak summary is vague and tilted, so Karen may reopen it. The strong one restates the positions by name with Karen's exact phrase, shows how the motion answers her, and gives owners and dates before the room decides."}, {"title": "Fair to the losing side", "input": "Festival board, item on festival dates. Four members support moving the festival to May for venue availability. Mr Gareth Pryce opposes: 'May collides with semester exams and reports, and we'll lose the junior bands. Directors simply won't bring them.' You are chairing and about to put the question.", "output": "CHAIR summary: 'Four members favour May for venue availability. Gareth has argued that May will cost us the junior bands, because schools are stretched. Before we vote, it's worth noting his point is about participation, which we all care about. The motion is May, with a commitment to survey junior band directors before confirming.'", "weak": "CHAIR summary: 'Most people want May. Gareth disagrees. Let's vote.'", "why": "Crediting the minority by name, in their strongest form, is what makes a summary fair. It also lowers the chance the decision is reopened, because the dissenting member has been heard accurately."}, {"title": "Restating the real question", "input": "Situation: you chair a department meeting. The item is 'Year 8 composition'. After twenty minutes the discussion has covered software crashes, the laptop trolley, assessment deadlines, a parent complaint about one student's mark and whether composition belongs in Year 8 at all. Two colleagues are looking at the clock. Ten minutes remain.", "output": "Q: Year 8 comp: redesign for 2027, or replace?\nCHAIR: drift: laptops (→ IT item), parent complaint (→ privately)\nSummary opening: 'Let me bring us back to the question: do we redesign the Year 8 composition unit for next year, or replace it?'", "weak": "Summary opening: 'So, lots of issues with Year 8 composition. Where do we go from here?'", "why": "A chair's notes hold the question so the room can return to it. Routing the drift items explicitly makes room for the decision without dismissing the people who raised them."}, {"title": "Testing a compromise", "input": "Chair's page, music parents' association, item on funding new percussion:\nROOM: TH  Δ fund percussion now\nROOM: KL  ✗ wait for audit  'show us the last spend first'\nROOM: SN  ✓ TH\nROOM: KL  ⟲ audit, second time, more sharply\nYou are chairing. Tom Hadley wants a vote now. Kim Lee is visibly frustrated.", "output": "CHAIR: 2 Δ vs 1 ✗, KL repeated → test compromise\nSpoken: 'Could I test something before we vote? Fund the snare drums now, which Tom has asked for, and table a report of last year's spending at the next meeting, which answers Kim's point. Would that work for both of you?'", "weak": "Spoken: 'We seem to have a majority, so let's just vote.'", "why": "The position marks showed a minority that was repeating itself, a sign it felt unheard. Testing a compromise built from both positions often produces a decision that holds."}, {"title": "Form of words before the decision", "input": "Situation: you chair the festival board. After discussion of the sponsor's uncertain renewal, the board has settled on asking the treasurer to prepare two budgets for the December meeting, one with the principal sponsor and one without. The secretary is minuting. You are about to call for a vote.", "output": "CHAIR: motion: 'That the treasurer present two budget scenarios, with and without the principal sponsor, at the December meeting.'\nSpoken: 'The motion is, and I'll read it so the secretary has it exactly: that the treasurer present two budget scenarios, with and without the principal sponsor, at the December meeting.'", "weak": "Spoken: 'All in favour of doing the budget thing?'", "why": "Stating the exact form of words lets the room decide on something precise and the secretary minute it accurately. Under the association's rules a formal motion may also need a mover and seconder; follow the governing document."}, {"title": "Recording the decision", "input": "Situation: you chair the school council. After your summary, the council votes 7 to 2 in favour of a uniform trial in Term 1 with a survey of families in Term 2. Mrs Karen Pope asks that her preference for a full consultation be noted in the minutes. Ahmed and Mai have agreed actions and dates.", "output": "DECISION: trial T1 + survey T2  carried 7 to 2\nCHAIR: KP request: preference for full consultation noted in minutes\n@ AH  draft by 28 Nov   @ MT  notice by 5 Dec", "weak": "DECISION: trial agreed", "why": "The chair's page should hold the outcome, any request to note a dissent, and the actions together, so the minutes can be checked against it and nothing is lost between the meeting and the record."}, {"title": "Chairing the quiet expert in", "input": "Situation: you chair a regional music network meeting on shared moderation. Ms Pam Kerr, from a small school, has not spoken. During the break she told you she has only three Year 11 practical students and nobody to moderate with. The queue has Mr Glen Wade, Ms Leanne Ford and Mr Ben Ng (online).", "output": "QUEUE: GW  LF  (Z) BN\nCHAIR: invite PK: small-school case, # 3 students\nSpoken: 'Before we go on, Pam, your school's situation is very relevant here. Would you be willing to say a word about it?'", "weak": "Waits for Ms Kerr to raise her hand, which she doesn't.", "why": "A chair's notes can include who has not yet spoken and why their view matters. Inviting the quiet expert, with a specific reason, brings the crucial fact into the room on the record."}, {"title": "When a speaker dominates", "input": "Chair's page, university partnership committee, rubric item:\nQUEUE: SK(Z) ✓✓✓  MT  PD  LF\nROOM: SK(Z) third contribution, 'the rubric is a ceiling, not a scale'\nROOM: MT, PD, LF not yet heard on this item\nAssociate Professor Simon Kerr has just raised his virtual hand again.", "output": "CHAIR: SK ×3, three not heard → take MT, PD, LF before SK again\nSpoken: 'Thanks, Simon, that's very clear. I'd like to hear from Mei, Patrick and Leanne before we come back to you.'", "weak": "Spoken: 'Simon, go ahead.'", "why": "The queue with ticks shows the imbalance at a glance. Naming who comes next acknowledges the dominant speaker while protecting the room's other voices."}, {"title": "Referral with substance", "input": "Situation: you chair the school council. The item is extending after-school rehearsal hours. Members broadly support it, but the staff representative has raised supervision and a parent has pointed out that the late bus leaves at 4.10. Nothing has been costed. The room is not ready to decide, and the next meeting is on 3 March.", "output": "DECISION: referred  →  @ DS cost supervision roster  @ business manager bus options  both by next meeting, 3 Mar\nSpoken: 'I think we're not ready to decide tonight, but I don't want to lose it. Let's refer it with two actions: I'll cost a supervision roster, and the business manager will bring bus options, both for our March meeting.'", "weak": "Spoken: 'Let's come back to this one.'", "why": "A referral is only useful if the next meeting will be better informed. Attaching owners, tasks and a date turns 'come back to it' into a decision with a path."}], "quiz": [{"input": "You chair a hybrid meeting. Two people online raised hands before one person in the room.", "q": "How should your queue read?", "options": ["In-room speaker first, then online", "In the order hands were raised, with online members marked", "Online members at the end of each round"], "answer": 1, "why": "Order of hands, with online marked, prevents proximity from deciding who speaks."}, {"input": "Ms Mai Tran says: 'I can let families know once we decide.'", "q": "Which action line is best?", "options": ["Families to be informed", "@ MT  family notice", "@ MT  family notice  by 5 Dec"], "answer": 2, "why": "Every action needs an owner and a date."}, {"input": "Three members support a proposal; one opposes and has repeated the same point twice.", "q": "What does the repetition most likely signal to the chair?", "options": ["The member feels unheard; consider testing a compromise or reflecting their point accurately", "The member should be ruled out of order", "The vote should be called immediately"], "answer": 0, "why": "Repetition usually means a point has not been acknowledged; a fair summary or a compromise often resolves it."}, {"input": "You are about to summarise before a vote.", "q": "Which summary is best?", "options": ["'We've had a good discussion, there's broad support, shall we go ahead?'", "'Most of us want the trial, Karen doesn't, let's vote.'", "'Karen asked that we ask before we buy; Ahmed proposed a Term 1 trial; Mai raised the January deadline. The motion is a Term 1 trial with a Term 2 survey.'"], "answer": 2, "why": "It restates positions by name, fairly, and gives the exact form of words."}, {"input": "One online member has spoken three times; three in-room members have not spoken.", "q": "What is the chair's best move?", "options": ["Name the next three speakers before returning to the online member", "Mute the online member", "Continue in order of hands"], "answer": 0, "why": "Naming who comes next acknowledges the dominant voice and protects the others."}, {"input": "The room is not ready to decide.", "q": "Which referral is best?", "options": ["'Let's come back to this.'", "'Referred to the next meeting, with DS to cost the roster and the business manager to bring bus options by 3 March.'", "'Perhaps everyone could think about it.'"], "answer": 1, "why": "A useful referral has owners, tasks and a date."}, {"input": "Your chair's page has a right-hand column.", "q": "What should it be headed and hold?", "options": ["ME: your own arguments", "MINUTES: verbatim record", "CHAIR: balance of the room, compromises to test, decisions"], "answer": 2, "why": "When chairing, the right column holds the meeting's memory and the chair's moves, not personal advocacy."}, {"input": "A board member asks that their dissent be noted after a vote.", "q": "What goes on your page?", "options": ["Nothing; the vote is what matters", "The decision, the vote, the request to note dissent, and the actions", "Only the dissent"], "answer": 1, "why": "Holding outcome, dissent and actions together lets the minutes be checked against the chair's record."}], "drills": [{"level": 1, "kind": "write", "input": "Music parents' association, end of the percussion item.\nMr Tom Hadley (president): I'll get quotes for the snare drums. Probably three suppliers.\nMs Kim Lee: I can put together last year's spending for the newsletter, if someone tells me when it goes out.\nMs Sue Nair: Someone should thank the band for the fundraising concert. It raised more than last year.", "task": "Write three action lines, each with an owner and a date, flagging any that needs an owner assigned.", "model": "@ TH  snare drum quotes  by 20 Feb\n@ KL  2026 spending summary, newsletter  by 6 Mar\n@ ? thank-you to band  → ask SN to own, by next rehearsal"}, {"level": 1, "kind": "speak", "input": "Department meeting, item on the Year 9 camp. Your chair's page:\nQ: Move Year 9 camp to Term 1?\nROOM: JA  ✓ Term 1, festival clash\nROOM: BC  ✗ Term 1 too early for friendships\nCHAIR: majority ✓\nMs Jess Albright argued that the festival clash costs Year 9 two rehearsals. Mr Brendan Cole said that in Term 1 students hardly know each other.", "task": "Give a fair two-sentence summary, crediting both positions by name, then state the motion.", "seconds": 30, "model": "Jess has argued for Term 1 to avoid the festival clash; Brendan has made the point that Term 1 may be too early for friendships to have formed. The motion is that the Year 9 camp move to Term 1 from 2027, with the program adjusted to include more team-building."}, {"level": 2, "kind": "write", "input": "Ms Ingrid Shaw (festival board, in the room): The adjudicator forms are inconsistent. Some write a paragraph, some write three words. I'd like a standard form.\nMr Kofi Mensah (online): I support that. For a lot of these kids it's the only written feedback on their playing all year. But I'd want it short, or the adjudicators won't use it.\nMs Shaw: I'm happy to draft one.\nMr Kofi Mensah: And I'd be glad to test it with two of the adjudicators before March. Ms Shaw (after a pause): Does anyone else want to come in on this? No? Then I think we're close to a decision.", "task": "You are chairing. Write a chair's page: queue, Q, ROOM lines with position marks and phrases, a CHAIR line on the balance, a motion, and actions with owners and dates.", "model": "QUEUE: IS  (Z) KM\nQ: Adopt a standard adjudicator feedback form for 2027?\nROOM: IS  Δ standard form  ! inconsistency\nROOM: KM(Z)  ✓ IS  'the only written feedback on their playing all year'  Δ keep it short\nCHAIR: consensus; KM's 'short' = design condition\nMOTION: 'That a short standard adjudicator feedback form be adopted for the 2027 festival.'\n@ IS  draft form  by 31 Jan\n@ KM  test with two adjudicators  by 28 Feb"}, {"level": 2, "kind": "speak", "input": "Situation: you chair the festival board. Your page on the adjudicator form reads:\nQUEUE: IS  (Z) KM\nQ: Adopt a standard adjudicator feedback form for 2027?\nROOM: IS  Δ standard form  ! inconsistency\nROOM: KM(Z)  ✓ IS  'the only written feedback on their playing all year'  Δ keep it short\nCHAIR: consensus; KM's 'short' = design condition\nMOTION: 'That a short standard adjudicator feedback form be adopted for the 2027 festival.'\n@ IS  draft form  by 31 Jan\n@ KM  test with two adjudicators  by 28 Feb\nYou are about to put the motion. Mr Ben Corrigan (online) has just typed in the chat: 'Agree with all of this. Will the form be ready before adjudicators are briefed?' Adjudicators are briefed in early March. The secretary needs the exact wording.", "task": "Give the chair's summary before the vote in under forty-five seconds: question, positions by name with Kofi's phrase, the motion read exactly, owners and dates.", "seconds": 45, "model": "The question is whether we adopt a standard feedback form for 2027. Ingrid has raised the inconsistency in the current forms, and Kofi has reminded us why it matters: for many of these students it's *the only written feedback on their playing all year*. Kofi's condition is that it be short. || The motion is: that a short standard adjudicator feedback form be adopted for the 2027 festival. Ingrid to draft by 31 January; Kofi to test it with two adjudicators by 28 February."}, {"level": 3, "kind": "write", "input": "You chair the school council. Item: extending after-school rehearsal hours for the specialist music program.\nMrs Karen Pope (parent representative, in the room): I support it in principle. The students want it, and the results speak for themselves. My only concern is that we're always adding things without asking what families can manage.\nMr Ahmed Hassan (community member, in the room): I'm fully in favour. Two extra afternoons. If it's good for the students, we should do it, and frankly the school should find a way.\nMr Rob Bell (staff representative, online): I'd urge caution. Staff supervision is already stretched. If we add two afternoons, we need a roster, and that's a staffing question for the principal, not just goodwill. Also, I'd like the council at some point to talk about the state of the staff car park, which floods every winter.\nMs Mai Tran (parent representative, in the room, quietly): Just one thing. The late bus to the northern suburbs leaves at 4.10. If rehearsals finish at 5, a lot of those families have no way to get their children home.\nMr Ahmed Hassan: Well, parents can drive.\nMrs Pope: Not all of them, Ahmed.\n(Chat, Ms Jo Lam, online parent, camera off: 'We're one of those families. 4.10 is our only option.')", "task": "Write a full chair's page: queue with online members, Q, ROOM lines with position marks and exact phrases, chat line, drift routed, a CHAIR line on the balance and a compromise to test, the decision you expect, and actions with owners and dates.", "model": "QUEUE: KP  AH  (Z) RB  MT  chat: JL(Z)\nQ: Extend after-school rehearsals, and on what conditions?\nROOM: KP  ✓ in principle  'what families can manage'\nROOM: AH  ✓✓ two afternoons  'the school should find a way'\nROOM: RB(Z)  ✗ for now  ! supervision roster = staffing question\nROOM: RB(Z)  drift: car park flooding → future item\nROOM: MT  ! # late bus 4.10, rehearsals to 5  'no way to get their children home'\nchat: JL  ≈ MT  'one of those families'\nROOM: AH  'parents can drive'  ✗ KP 'not all of them'\nCHAIR: ✓ in principle all; two conditions unmet: supervision, transport\nCHAIR: test: trial one afternoon Term 1, finish by 4.00 for bus, roster costed\nDECISION expected: referred with actions, or trial carried on conditions\n@ DS + principal  cost supervision roster  by 3 Mar\n@ business manager  late bus options  by 3 Mar\n@ secretary  list car park for April meeting"}, {"level": 3, "kind": "speak", "input": "Situation: you chair the school council on extending after-school rehearsals. It is 8.40 pm and the meeting is due to close at 8.45. Your page reads:\nQUEUE: KP  AH  (Z) RB  MT  chat: JL(Z)\nQ: Extend after-school rehearsals, and on what conditions?\nROOM: KP  ✓ in principle  'what families can manage'\nROOM: AH  ✓✓ two afternoons  'the school should find a way'\nROOM: RB(Z)  ✗ for now  ! supervision roster = staffing question\nROOM: RB(Z)  drift: car park flooding → future item\nROOM: MT  ! # late bus 4.10, rehearsals to 5  'no way to get their children home'\nchat: JL  ≈ MT  'one of those families'\nROOM: AH  'parents can drive'  ✗ KP 'not all of them'\nCHAIR: ✓ in principle all; two conditions unmet: supervision, transport\nCHAIR: test: trial one afternoon Term 1, finish by 4.00 for bus, roster costed\n@ DS + principal  cost supervision roster  by 3 Mar\n@ business manager  late bus options  by 3 Mar\n@ secretary  list car park for April meeting\nMr Hassan is pressing for a vote on two afternoons. Ms Lam has typed 'thank you for listening' in the chat. The staff representative has his camera on and is waiting to see whether supervision is mentioned.", "task": "Give the chair's summary in under ninety seconds: restate the question, credit each position by name fairly (including the chat), test the compromise, route the drift, read the form of words, and name owners and dates.", "seconds": 90, "model": "Let me summarise, as we're near time. The question is whether we extend after-school rehearsals, and on what conditions. || Everyone supports it in principle. Ahmed has argued that *the school should find a way*. Karen has asked us to consider *what families can manage*. Rob has reminded us that supervision is a staffing question, not just goodwill. And Mai has given us the fact that decides the timing: the late bus leaves at 4.10, and Jo has confirmed in the chat that for her family it's the only option. || So could I test this: a trial of one extra afternoon in Term 1, finishing by 4.00 so students can make the bus, subject to a costed supervision roster. If that's acceptable, the form of words is: that council support in principle a trial of one additional rehearsal afternoon in Term 1, finishing by 4.00 pm, subject to a supervision roster being confirmed at the March meeting. I'll cost the roster with the principal, and the business manager will bring bus options, both by 3 March. Rob, the car park is listed for April. Are we agreed?"}]}, {"id": "scaffold", "examples": [{"title": "The Board's four reasons, answered", "room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee, hybrid, 14 members: 8 in the room, 6 on Zoom", "prompt": "The Executive Director, the Principal Consultant and the senior project officer have just reported that the SCSA Board rejected the course the committee spent two years developing, for four reasons: overlap with existing ATAR and VET offerings, assessment load, regional delivery and an unclear pathway after Year 12. Jason has spoken from Zoom about other states. The chair turns to you.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Thank you, Chair. || I want to thank the Executive Director, the Principal Consultant and the project officer, for giving us the Board's reasons *in sequence*, | in the order the Board weighed them.", "note": "Specific thanks credits a named act rather than a general performance. Crediting the sequence also quietly fixes the Board's four reasons as the agreed object of discussion, which is the ground on which his proposals are strongest."}, {"tag": 0, "text": "And I'd thank Jason for reminding us that other states have *been here*. | I'll come back to that.", "note": "A thanks with a promised callback is a plant: it attaches him to the room's most admired speaker and builds a small expectation the room will wait to see paid off."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "Two years is a long time to give to anything. || This is a *hard* morning for the writers, and I don't think we should hurry past that.", "note": "Short declaratives, no adjectives of consolation. Naming the cost plainly, then declining to rush, signals emotional accuracy, which an educated room treats as a precondition for trusting the practical judgement that follows."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "I'll speak as someone who would be delivering this course on a Monday morning. || I've taught senior music since 2005, I've led a specialist program since 2021, and I've played bass professionally for twenty-six years, | so I've read the Board's reasons as a *delivery* problem.", "note": "A tricolon of credentials, converted at once into a lens. Authority stated as a reading frame ('so I've read...') is heard as relevance, not self-promotion, and it primes the room to hear what follows as practical."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "I think everyone here, | and I'd include the *Board* in this, | wants the same thing: a senior course that a student in Karratha can take as readily as a student in the city, and that leads somewhere when they leave.", "note": "The parenthetical inclusion of the Board is the key move: it builds a coalition with the decision-maker, so no later proposal can be heard as the committee against the Board. The concrete place name makes the purpose impossible to dispute."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "As I heard it, the Board hasn't rejected the *idea*. || It has given us four objections: overlap, assessment load, regional delivery and pathways. | And four objections are four *tasks*.", "note": "Antithesis (idea against objections) followed by redefinition. Listing the objections in the leaders' own order lets the room's memory of the report carry his structure for him, and the epigram 'four objections are four tasks' converts grief into a work plan."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "In the Executive Director's words, this is a judgement on *readiness*, not quality. || That is a design brief, | and from where I sit the regional point in particular is well founded: a school with one music teacher cannot run seven tasks and an ensemble.", "note": "Evidence drawn from the leaders' own phrase is unanswerable in the room because it is theirs. The concession on the regional point (a form of prolepsis) shows he has weighed the Board's case, which makes his answer to it more credible."}, {"tag": 5, "text": "So I'd like to propose a four-part response, | one part per objection, | and I'll call it the *readiness* response. || One: a one-page differentiation map, setting the course against the ATAR and VET music offerings unit by unit, so any overlap is shown, or cut. Two: school-based assessment reduced from seven tasks to four, by combining performance and composition into one portfolio.", "note": "Naming the package gives the room a handle to repeat ('the readiness response'), and borrowing 'readiness' from the Board's own reasoning makes the proposal sound like compliance rather than resistance. Numbering mirrors the structure of the problem, so each fix is filed against its objection."}, {"tag": 5, "text": "Three: a regional delivery model built with SIDE, with recorded ensemble submissions accepted, so a school with one music teacher can offer it. And four: a pathways statement, co-signed by a university and an industry partner, showing the Board where these students go. || That last one is where Jason's point about other states belongs: | we can borrow the evidence, rather than argue it.", "note": "The callback pays off the plant from the opening: the admired speaker's contribution is folded into his proposal, so the room hears them as one argument, and Jason's supporters become his. Each item carries a concrete object (map, portfolio, model, statement) that can be minuted."}, {"tag": 6, "text": "So, Chair: | four objections, | four answers, | and nothing in the Board's paper we can't meet. || The Board hasn't asked us to change what this course is *for*. | It has asked us to show that it's *ready*.", "note": "The summary is a tricolon with parallel nouns, then an antithesis built to be quoted. Placing the line last uses the recency effect: it is the sentence most likely to be repeated after the meeting and in the minutes."}, {"tag": 6, "text": "If it helps the minutes, the wording might be: | that a working group of five redraft the course against the Board's four reasons and report to the next meeting, with a view to resubmission at the Board's first meeting next year. || I'm happy to convene the assessment piece.", "note": "Offering minute wording turns a contribution into a resolution and saves the chair work, which chairs reward. Volunteering for one named piece attaches an owner and makes the proposal his in the record, not merely in the room."}], "weak": {"text": "Thanks everyone, that was really clear, and obviously it's disappointing for all of us. I suppose I just wanted to say I've had a look at some of the issues and I think some of them are probably fixable? Like the assessment, there are maybe too many tasks, we could combine a couple, and for the regional schools there might be something with SIDE or recordings, and the overlap thing, someone could do a map or something. I'm sort of thinking out loud. I just think it's really important we don't give up, because it's a really good course and the kids deserve it. So, yeah, maybe we could look at some of that at some point.", "why": ["Thanks without content: the strong version named three leaders and the precise thing they did.", "No standing, so the ideas arrive from nowhere; the strong version set up a delivery lens before any solution.", "The solutions are hedged and buried mid-sentence; the strong version named, numbered and mapped them to the Board's four reasons.", "It ends on a generality with no owner; the strong version closed on a quotable line, minute wording and his name on a task."]}}, {"title": "Three days on a two-day budget", "room": "Board of the association that runs the state schools' jazz festival, hybrid, 9 members: 5 in the room, 4 on Zoom", "prompt": "The president and the festival director have just reported that the principal sponsor has withdrawn three months before the festival. A board member has moved to cut the festival from three days to two. As treasurer, you speak before the vote.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Thank you, President. || I want to thank you for telling the board *before* the rumour did, | and the festival director for having already rung the venue and the adjudicators before she walked in tonight.", "note": "Thanking a person for timing and for an action already taken is more credible than thanking them for a report. It also signals that options are already being worked, which lowers the temperature before the vote."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "Losing a principal sponsor at this point is serious. || For a lot of these bands, | this festival *is* the year.", "note": "The plain admission ('is serious') is followed by a short sentence with stress on the verb. Stating the stakes from the students' side keeps the acknowledgement off the board's embarrassment and on the people the board exists for."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "I've been through the accounts line by line as treasurer, | and I've also stood on that stage with a band, and beside one as a director. || So I'd like to talk about which of our costs are *fixed*, and which ones we chose.", "note": "Two kinds of standing in one breath, financial and artistic, so neither faction can dismiss him. The final sentence converts standing into the distinction that will structure the rest of the contribution."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "I don't think anyone at this table wants a band that has rehearsed since February to be told it won't play. || That's the one outcome we should all be trying to avoid.", "note": "Common ground stated as a negative image is harder to disagree with than an abstract purpose, and it makes the motion to cut a day feel like a move toward that outcome without his having to say so."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "The motion treats this as a choice between two days and three. || I'd put it differently: | the question is whether the third day costs what we think it does.", "note": "Reframing the binary the motion has set up. Naming the motion's frame before replacing it is fair to its mover and makes the new question sound like a refinement, not an attack."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "Our two largest lines are venue hire and adjudicator fees. || The sponsor covered most of the venue, | but the Saturday heats don't *need* the main venue; they need a hall, a piano and a PA.", "note": "Evidence from the accounts, compressed into one memorable contrast. The concrete triplet (hall, piano, PA) makes the requirement sound small and solvable, which is exactly the inference he wants the board to draw."}, {"tag": 5, "text": "So I propose a three-day festival on a two-day budget, in three parts. || One: we move the Saturday heats to a host school, at no hire cost; I've already had one principal say yes in principle. Two: we offer the individual awards, best soloist, best rhythm section, best original chart, to smaller sponsors by name.", "note": "The title is an oxymoron that sticks, and it states the benefit and the constraint at once. 'Yes in principle' is evidence of feasibility delivered as a by-the-way, which is more persuasive than an assertion."}, {"tag": 5, "text": "And three: we authorise a capped draw on reserves to cover the gap, repaid over the next two festivals. || That's the *treasurer* recommending it, | not just the bass player.", "note": "Putting the riskiest item last and attaching his formal role to it is a deliberate use of standing at the moment it is needed. The light self-reference releases tension without undercutting the seriousness."}, {"tag": 6, "text": "So: one fixed cost moved, | one sponsor replaced by several, | and a safety net we pay back. || We don't need to choose between the budget and the bands.", "note": "A tricolon summary in falling length, then a closing antithesis that answers the motion directly. The board leaves with a line that makes voting for the cut feel like choosing against the bands."}, {"tag": 6, "text": "If the mover is willing, I'd ask that the motion lie on the table, | and that the treasurer bring a revised budget to a special meeting in a fortnight, with the festival director approaching host schools by Friday.", "note": "A procedural close: asking that the motion lie on the table is courteous to the mover and avoids defeating it. Two owners and two dates turn the alternative into a decision the board can minute tonight."}], "weak": {"text": "Look, I understand why people want to go to two days, it's a lot of money. But I've had a look at the budget and I don't think it's quite as bad as it seems. There's probably a few things we could do. We could maybe use a school for some of it, and there are other sponsors out there, and we do have some reserves. I'd just hate to see the kids miss out, they work so hard. So I'm not sure about the motion. Maybe we could hold off and I could look at the numbers a bit more?", "why": ["It opens by conceding the other side's frame; the strong version thanked, acknowledged and then replaced the binary with a better question.", "The treasurer's authority is never used; the strong version stated it and attached it to the riskiest item.", "The three ideas are vague and unnumbered; the strong version named the package and gave each part a concrete action.", "It ends with a request for time and no owner or date; the strong version proposed a procedure, two owners and two dates."]}}, {"title": "Keeping the pathway open", "room": "Schools partnership steering committee of a university conservatorium, in person, 10 members", "prompt": "The Head of School has announced that the weekly masterclass and early-entry pathway for senior school students will end next year because of budget pressure on studio teaching hours. Two other school representatives have expressed dismay. You speak next.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Thank you, Head of School. || I'd like to thank you for bringing this to the committee *before* the budget is final, | and for being so open about where the pressure is: studio hours, not goodwill.", "note": "Thanking someone for consulting early rewards the behaviour he wants repeated. Restating their own diagnosis ('studio hours, not goodwill') shows he has listened precisely and sets up the cost problem he is about to solve."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "This program is how a lot of our students first walk into a building like this one. || Ending it would be *felt* in schools well beyond this table.", "note": "An acknowledgement from the far end of the pathway, stated concretely. It endorses the dismay already expressed without repeating it, which keeps the room's emotion intact while moving it forward."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "I've sent students through this program every year since 2021, | and for twenty-six years I've worked as a professional player in the scene your graduates go into. || So I see it from both ends.", "note": "Dual standing, as feeder and as working professional, gives him a claim no one else at the table can make. 'Both ends' is a compact spatial image that licenses him to speak for the whole pipeline."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "I think we all want the same thing here: the strongest school musicians in this state choosing to study here, | rather than in the eastern states.", "note": "Common ground framed as institutional self-interest is the most persuasive kind for a university. It aligns the schools' wish with the Head of School's recruitment targets, so his proposal will sound like help, not a plea."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "The cost, as I understand it, is studio hours: | thirty weeks of staff time in this building. || The value is in the contact, | not the calendar.", "note": "Reducing the problem to its cost driver, then an antithesis (contact against calendar) that separates what is valuable from what is expensive. The alliteration makes the distinction easy to carry."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "And there's a model for this already in the faculty: your second-year pedagogy students need placement hours, | and at the moment they're finding them one school at a time.", "note": "Evidence from the institution's own practice is the hardest to dismiss. Pointing to an unmet need on the university's side turns the problem into an exchange rather than a cost."}, {"tag": 5, "text": "So I'd propose we *redesign* the program rather than end it, in three moves. || One: the weekly masterclass becomes a termly intensive, four Saturdays a year, which cuts staff hours by most of the cost. Two: the intensives are hosted in rotation by partner schools, so there's no room cost to the faculty.", "note": "'Redesign rather than end' is the antithesis the whole contribution has been building toward. Numbered moves let the Head of School test each one separately, which is how a budget holder actually decides."}, {"tag": 5, "text": "And three: the pedagogy students teach the junior sessions as an assessed placement, | supervised by your staff and by us. || That takes up the point both my colleagues made about the younger students losing out.", "note": "Attaching the third move to what the other school representatives said converts their dismay into support for his solution, and gives the Head of School a coalition rather than three complaints."}, {"tag": 6, "text": "So: fewer staff hours, | no room cost, | and a placement problem solved on your side. || The pathway stays open; | it just changes *shape*.", "note": "The summary lists the benefits in the budget holder's terms, not the schools'. The closing line is short, metaphorical and reassuring: it makes the change sound like adaptation rather than loss."}, {"tag": 6, "text": "If the Head of School is open to it, | I'd be glad to draft a variation to the partnership agreement with her office by the end of term, for this committee to see at its next meeting.", "note": "Offering to do the drafting with the decision-maker's office, not on his own, respects her authority while taking the work off her desk. A date and a destination complete the action."}], "weak": {"text": "I'm really disappointed to hear this, I have to say. The program has been so valuable for our kids and I'd hate to lose it. I understand money's tight, it's tight everywhere. I wonder whether there's a way to keep some of it going, maybe not every week? And maybe schools could help out with rooms. I know the pedagogy students need placements too, so maybe there's something there. I just think it would be a real shame for the whole state if it went, honestly.", "why": ["It leads with his own disappointment; the strong version thanked the Head of School for consulting early and named her diagnosis.", "The solutions are posed as wonderings; the strong version called the package a redesign and numbered three moves.", "It argues from the schools' loss; the strong version framed common ground as the university's own recruitment interest.", "It closes on regret; the strong version summarised in the budget holder's terms and offered a draft with a date."]}}, {"title": "Two lines, one review", "room": "Principal's office, school executive meeting: the Principal, the Associate Principal, the timetabling Deputy and the Business Manager", "prompt": "The Deputy has proposed collapsing the two Year 11 music lines into one in 2027 to free a line for a new elective. The Principal invites your response.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Thank you, Principal. || And thank you to the Deputy for sending the line structure through on Friday, | it meant I could look at the actual clashes rather than guess at them.", "note": "Thanking for a document, and saying why it helped, credits the colleague whose proposal he is about to modify. It also announces, without claiming it, that he has done the homework."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "I know the timetable is a puzzle with no spare pieces, | and every line the Deputy protects is one someone else loses. || I don't underestimate that.", "note": "Acknowledging the proposer's constraint before his own. In a small room with seniors, empathy for the person holding the problem is what earns the right to change the solution."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "I've built the specialist music numbers in this school since 2021, | so I can tell you where these students come from and what they're likely to do.", "note": "Standing kept to a single sentence, because in the principal's office a long credential would sound defensive. The claim is about knowledge of data, which is the currency of this room."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "We all want a timetable that serves the most students well, | and that doesn't quietly close a pathway we've spent years building.", "note": "The first clause is the Deputy's goal, the second is his. Joining them in one sentence makes the shared purpose include both, so the executive cannot accept the first half without the second."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "The question as I see it isn't one line or two. || It's whether we have evidence that one line would hold the cohort. | And at the moment we don't.", "note": "A reframe from structure to evidence, which moves the discussion onto ground where he holds the data. The short final sentence is a quiet concession of uncertainty that makes his position more, not less, credible."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "Last time we merged a senior music class, in a different form, we lost students to a clash with a science line in the first fortnight. || One line puts every ATAR musician into the same clash.", "note": "Practice evidence, specific to this school, stated without drama. The second sentence draws the inference sharply, so the risk is visible without his labouring it."}, {"tag": 5, "text": "So I'd propose three things. || One: we keep two lines in 2027, with a formal review at the end of the year against an enrolment threshold the executive sets. Two: I run the Year 11 ensemble component before school, which frees one period a week in the second line for the Deputy to use.", "note": "Offering a concession of his own (the before-school period) in the proposal itself is a reciprocal move: it gives the Deputy something real, which makes refusing the rest harder. Letting the executive set the threshold hands them control of the review."}, {"tag": 5, "text": "And three: I'll bring the 2027 preference data by week six of Term 1, | so the decision for 2028 is made on numbers, not on my advocacy.", "note": "Binding himself to evidence, and explicitly discounting his own advocacy, is a credibility move: it tells seniors he will accept the result of the test he is proposing."}, {"tag": 6, "text": "So: two lines for one year, | a period back for the timetable, | and a decision on evidence. || I'd rather we review a line we kept than try to rebuild one we closed.", "note": "The summary is a falling tricolon; the final line is an antithesis with an asymmetry built in (review against rebuild), which states the irreversibility argument without lecturing."}, {"tag": 6, "text": "If that's acceptable, I'll put it in a one-page note to the Deputy and the Associate Principal by Friday.", "note": "A small, immediate deliverable to the two people who own the timetable. In a principal's office the close should be modest in form and exact in content."}], "weak": {"text": "I can see why we'd want to do it, but I'm really worried about it, to be honest. The music kids have a lot of clashes already and if we go to one line I think we'll lose some. We've worked really hard to build the program. Maybe we could keep two lines for now and just see how it goes? I could probably do something before school if that helps at all. I don't know. I just think it would be a big loss.", "why": ["It leads with worry; the strong version thanked the Deputy for the document and acknowledged the timetabling constraint.", "The risk is asserted, not shown; the strong version cited a specific past clash and its result.", "The concession is mumbled; the strong version built it into a numbered proposal as a reciprocal offer.", "It ends on loss; the strong version closed on a decision rule, an asymmetry line and a note by Friday."]}}, {"title": "The fee, explained and eased", "room": "Music parents' association general meeting, in person, about 60 parents", "prompt": "The Business Manager has explained the increase in the instrumental program fee. Several parents have spoken angrily about affordability, one saying families are being priced out of the program. The president asks you to respond.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Thank you, President. || I want to thank the Business Manager for going through the costs line by line, | and the parents who've spoken tonight for saying it *straight*. It's much easier to fix a problem when people name it.", "note": "Thanking the angry parents alongside the officer reframes their anger as a contribution, which lowers it. The general sentence after the thanks gives the room a principle it can agree with immediately."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "A fee rise lands at a kitchen table, | not in a budget. || And for some families in this room it's landed hard.", "note": "Antithesis with a domestic image. The plain second sentence accepts the most serious claim made tonight without contesting it, which is the only way to keep a parent room listening."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "I've run this program since 2021, | and I've been teaching since 2005. || I've never once seen a student leave because they stopped loving music; | I have seen them leave because of money.", "note": "Standing used to vouch for the parents' concern rather than to defend the school. The paired sentences form an antithesis that tells the room he is on the families' side of the problem."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "I think everyone here, | the school included, | wants the same thing: that no child stops playing because of what's in the family account.", "note": "Including the school in the common purpose prevents the meeting from dividing into parents against administration, and makes the solutions that follow sound like the school keeping its own promise."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "The question isn't really whether the fee is fair; | the Business Manager has shown us where it goes. || It's whether it's *payable*, | and payable is a design question.", "note": "A reframe from fairness, which cannot be settled tonight, to payability, which can. Repeating 'payable' (anadiplosis) pins the new word in the room's mind and leads it toward practical answers."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "Other schools' music programs handle this with instalments and a quiet hardship process, | and I've seen that work in my previous school, | with nobody having to stand up in a meeting like this to ask.", "note": "Comparative and practice evidence together, kept modest and specific. The final clause names the dignity cost of the current arrangement without accusing anyone."}, {"tag": 5, "text": "So I'd like to propose three things. || One: the fee can be paid in four termly instalments, starting next year. Two: a confidential hardship fund, raised by this association and administered by the Principal's office, so no family's name ever comes to this table.", "note": "Numbered, concrete and administratively realistic. Placing administration with the Principal's office answers the privacy concern before anyone raises it, which is prolepsis applied to logistics."}, {"tag": 5, "text": "And three: we expand the instrument hire pool, | because for a beginner on a brass instrument the hire cost can be larger than the fee itself. || That picks up exactly the point the parent at the back made about her son's tuba.", "note": "Attaching the third item to a named parent's story makes her an author of the solution and tells the room it was heard. The small comic specificity of the tuba releases tension without making light of the issue."}, {"tag": 6, "text": "So: | spread the cost, | protect the families who need it, | and take the instrument out of the equation. || The fee pays for the program; | our job is to make sure every child can stay *in* it.", "note": "A three-verb summary followed by an antithesis that separates the school's cost from the community's obligation. The final stressed preposition is the word the room will take home."}, {"tag": 6, "text": "If the meeting agrees, | the association's treasurer and I will bring a one-page fee statement, with the instalment schedule and the hardship process, to the next meeting.", "note": "A shared owner (the association's treasurer and himself) makes the outcome joint property of the parents and the school, and a single page is a deliverable the room can picture."}], "weak": {"text": "Look, I hear you, and I know it's a lot of money. The fee really does go to the program though, it's not like anyone's making money out of it. We are looking at ways to help. If anyone's struggling they can always come and talk to me or the office. We might be able to do payment plans. And there's instrument hire too. I really don't want anyone to leave the program. Please just get in touch if you need to.", "why": ["It opens defensively; the strong version thanked the angry parents and accepted their strongest claim.", "It defends the fee rather than reframing the question; the strong version moved the room from fairness to payability.", "The help offered requires families to identify themselves; the strong version proposed a confidential fund administered away from the meeting.", "It ends with an invitation, not a decision; the strong version proposed three measures, two owners and the next meeting."]}}, {"title": "Funding the music, fixing the budget", "room": "State arts funding assessment panel, deliberation session, hybrid, 6 assessors with the program officer and panel chair", "prompt": "A regional youth orchestra's application has scored highly on artistic merit and community reach but poorly on budget, which was incomplete. Another assessor on Zoom has argued it cannot be funded as submitted. The chair asks for your view before the panel ranks it.", "parts": [{"tag": 0, "text": "Thank you, Chair. || I'd like to thank the program officer for the eligibility summary, | and my colleague on Zoom for being so precise about *where* the budget falls short. That precision is what makes it fixable.", "note": "He thanks the colleague he is about to disagree with, for the exact thing he will use. Turning an objection into an asset in the first breath is a classic concessive opening and makes the disagreement collegial."}, {"tag": 1, "text": "Assessing a strong application with a weak budget is the hardest call this panel makes. || Whatever we decide, | a group of young players two days' drive from here will feel it.", "note": "Acknowledging the difficulty of the panel's own task, then the human consequence. The geographic detail keeps the applicant real without becoming a plea."}, {"tag": 2, "text": "I've been treasurer of a not-for-profit music association for some years now, | so I've written budgets like this one, | usually at eleven at night, by a volunteer.", "note": "Standing that explains the error instead of excusing it. The specific, wry image of the volunteer at eleven at night gives the panel a sympathetic and accurate account of how incomplete budgets happen."}, {"tag": 3, "text": "We all want the same two things here: public money spent accountably, | and funding going to the strongest work. || This application tests whether we can have both.", "note": "Both values stated as shared, so neither he nor his colleague owns the moral high ground. The second sentence turns the case into a test of the panel's competence, which panels like to pass."}, {"tag": 4, "text": "As I read it, the artistic case is not in question; | the budget is *incomplete*, | not *unsound*. || The missing items are venue hire and travel, | and both are quoted elsewhere in the support material.", "note": "A precise distinction (incomplete against unsound) that changes the category of the problem. Evidence drawn from the application itself shows the information exists, which moves the issue from risk to paperwork."}, {"tag": 5, "text": "So I'd propose we rank it on merit, | and recommend it for funding *subject to conditions*. || One: a revised budget within thirty days, using the figures already in their support material. Two: the program officer offers the applicant a call and the standard template, if the guidelines allow it.", "note": "The proposal separates ranking from release, which lets the sceptical assessor agree to the first without conceding the second. 'If the guidelines allow it' defers to the officer's authority and avoids an error of procedure."}, {"tag": 5, "text": "And three: funds released in two tranches, | the second on acquittal of the first. || That meets my colleague's point head-on: | nothing is paid against a budget we haven't seen.", "note": "The third condition is designed to answer the opposing assessor's objection exactly, and he says so. Naming the fit explicitly converts an opponent into a co-author."}, {"tag": 6, "text": "So: rank it on the music, | release it on the paperwork, | and protect the money at every step. || We can be rigorous *and* fair to a volunteer board.", "note": "A parallel summary that separates the two judgements in its grammar, followed by a conjunction-stressed line that refuses the false choice between rigour and fairness."}, {"tag": 6, "text": "If the panel agrees, | the recommendation might read: funded in full, subject to a revised budget within thirty days and release in two tranches. || I'd ask the program officer to confirm the conditions are within the guidelines before we close the session.", "note": "Draft recommendation wording plus a named check by the right officer within the meeting. The close is procedural and exact, which suits a panel that will be audited."}], "weak": {"text": "I take the point about the budget, it's definitely not great. But the music is really strong and the community reach is excellent, and I'd hate to see them miss out over a technicality. Small organisations often struggle with this stuff, they're mostly volunteers and they don't have a finance person. Could we maybe fund it anyway and ask them to fix the budget later on? I'm not sure what the rules are around that. I just think it's a really deserving application.", "why": ["It calls the colleague's objection a technicality; the strong version thanked her for its precision and used it.", "It argues from sympathy; the strong version distinguished an incomplete budget from an unsound one, with evidence from the application.", "The proposal is a single vague question; the strong version set three numbered conditions, the last built to answer the objection.", "It leaves procedure unknown; the strong version drafted the recommendation and asked the program officer to confirm it in the session."]}}], "prompts": [{"level": 1, "room": "Music department meeting, in person, 7 staff", "prompt": "A colleague proposes cancelling the Term 4 concert because it clashes with Year 12 exam leave. You think it can be saved: move it to week 3, shorten the program to ninety minutes, and have the Year 10 ensembles cover the slots the Year 12 soloists would have filled. Thank the colleague for raising the clash early.", "seconds": 90}, {"level": 1, "room": "Festival association volunteer subcommittee, hybrid, 6 members", "prompt": "The volunteer coordinator reports a shortfall of stage hands for the festival weekend. Propose a two-shift roster, a sign-up form sent through every participating school's music teacher, and a certificate of service students can use towards their community hours. The coordinator has already found half the people needed.", "seconds": 75}, {"level": 1, "room": "University music education advisory group, in person, 8 members", "prompt": "The unit coordinator asks how schools can make placement weeks more useful for pre-service teachers. Propose a structured observation schedule (one ensemble rehearsal, one theory lesson, one instrumental lesson per day), a named mentor for each student, and a short reflection template the university can assess.", "seconds": 90}, {"level": 1, "room": "Feeder primary schools' music meeting, in person, 6 primary music teachers", "prompt": "The teachers report that the combined primary concert band rehearsals on Wednesday afternoons are losing students to sport. Propose moving to a fortnightly Saturday morning rehearsal at your school for Term 1, with your senior students as section tutors, and a short survey of families before the change is final.", "seconds": 60}, {"level": 2, "room": "School council, in person, 11 members", "prompt": "The Business Manager proposes removing the transport budget for ensembles travelling to the state jazz festival and regional tours. A parent member supports the cut. Propose keeping the budget for 2027 but sharing buses with another school's ensembles on the same days, a small per-student contribution with a hardship exemption, and a report to council on actual costs at the end of Term 2.", "seconds": 120}, {"level": 2, "room": "SCSA syllabus review panel, hybrid, 12 members", "prompt": "Teachers on the panel report that external moderation of practical recordings is taking far longer than the Authority estimated. An officer defends the current process. Propose a standard recording specification, a sample of three recordings per school rather than the full cohort, and a moderator training session before submissions open, with the officer's team leading it.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 2, "room": "Instrumental music service reference group, hybrid, 10 members", "prompt": "The service manager announces a reduction in instrumental teacher hours for secondary schools next year. Principals in the room are concerned. Propose allocating hours by ensemble participation as well as enrolment, a shared teacher model for schools within ten minutes of each other, and a mid-year review, while acknowledging the manager's budget constraint.", "seconds": 150}, {"level": 2, "room": "Conservatorium and schools audition committee, in person, 7 members", "prompt": "Conservatorium auditions for school students have been scheduled in the same week as ATAR practical exams. The conservatorium's coordinator says the dates are fixed by room bookings. Propose a split audition window with recorded first rounds, a shared calendar circulated to schools in February, and a contact person at each school.", "seconds": 120}, {"level": 3, "room": "SCSA Curriculum Advisory Committee, hybrid, with a Board member attending in person", "prompt": "At the next meeting, a Board member attends and says frankly that the Board is unlikely to see a resubmission favourably within two years. An academic on Zoom interrupts to argue the committee should stand by the original design. Propose the readiness response: a differentiation map, assessment reduced to four school-based tasks, a regional delivery model with SIDE, and a co-signed pathways statement, with a working group reporting by a set date. Expect to be interrupted once.", "seconds": 210}, {"level": 3, "room": "School council you chair, hybrid, 12 members, a member has moved a motion", "prompt": "A community member has moved that council review the equity of selective entry to the specialist music program, and has implied the program serves families who can afford private lessons. Step out of the chair briefly to speak. Propose a review with terms of reference covering intake data by postcode and prior tuition, a no-prior-lessons audition pathway, and a report back by the end of Term 2. Expect a hostile follow-up question.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "Funder's acquittal meeting, hybrid: the funder's senior manager on Zoom, the festival president and you in the room", "prompt": "The funder's senior manager questions why the festival's grant acquittal shows a variance in venue costs and suggests the next grant may be at risk. As treasurer, explain without defensiveness. Propose a reconciled statement within fourteen days, a standing variance note in future acquittals, and a mid-year check-in with the funder's program officer. Expect a sharp interruption about the deadline.", "seconds": 180}, {"level": 3, "room": "University course advisory committee, in person, chaired by the Dean, 14 members", "prompt": "The Dean proposes merging music education into a generic arts education stream to reduce unit offerings. Senior academics are divided. As an external member, propose retaining two music-specific method units within the merged stream, a shared first-year core, and placement in specialist music schools, with a review against graduate employment in three years. Expect the Dean to press you on cost.", "seconds": 150}]}]};
